window.RV_LOADED['ib-history-hl'] = {"name":"IB History HL","topics":[{"name":"20th Century Wars","subtopics":[{"name":"Causes","questions":[{"ref":"HI0187","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0187\n\nDefine the term arms $\\lq$race' and explain how the arms race contributed to the tensions leading up to the First World War.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nDefinition for **one** mark:\n- A competition between nations to build up military power, often involving rapid advancements in weaponry and technology, with the goal of achieving superiority over rivals.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following explanations for **one** mark each\n- The arms race heightened fear and mistrust between nations, particularly as Germany and Britain each sought to outmatch the other's naval and military capabilities. This growing hostility fostered a climate in which diplomacy was weakened and alliances were hardened, increasing the risk of war.\n- It contributed to a sense of inevitability of conflict, as the unprecedented military buildup across Europe created an atmosphere where war seemed not just possible, but unavoidable. The sheer scale of military resources and preparedness made peaceful resolutions appear ineffective, as nations increasingly prioritized military solutions over diplomacy. This perception encouraged governments to adopt more aggressive foreign policies and defensive alliances, as they believed war was imminent and that being unprepared would leave them vulnerable to attack. Ultimately, the arms race transformed Europe into a heavily militarized and polarized continent, where even minor crises could escalate into full-scale conflict.\n- The arms race accelerated alliances and deepened divisions, as countries felt pressured to align with others for security. This alliance system created a “domino effect,” where conflict between two countries could easily draw in their allies, turning a local dispute into a broader war as countries sought to uphold the obligations of their alliances.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAn arms race is a competition where countries increase their military strength to surpass potential adversaries ($\\checkmark$). Leading up to World War I, the arms race fueled distrust between nations, especially between Germany and Britain, who viewed each other as escalating threats due to their naval buildup ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the massive investment in militaries made war feel inevitable, and nations adopted aggressive policies to protect their interests, which further destabilized Europe ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0188","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0188\n\nDescribe **two** ideological differences between the Nationalists and Communists that contributed to the Chinese Civil War.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with detailed description:\n- Political Structure and Ideology: The Nationalists (Guomindang) advocated for a centralized government with a focus on national unity and stability through a capitalist framework. They sought to modernize China based on nationalist principles and opposed radical social reforms. In contrast, the Communists envisioned a revolutionary transformation of society, aiming for a classless, socialist state based on Marxist-Leninist ideology, with an emphasis on equality and anti-imperialism.\n- Social Support Base: The Nationalists relied heavily on the support of urban elites, including landowners, industrialists, and intellectuals who benefited from capitalist development. Conversely, the Communists drew their base from rural peasants and the working class, promising land reforms and social equality, which attracted widespread support among those disenfranchised by the existing social order.\n- Foreign Influence and Alliances: The Nationalists sought to establish China as an independent, unified nation without direct alignment with foreign powers, resisting Soviet influence in particular. By contrast, the Communists aligned with the Soviet Union, adopting aspects of Soviet-style governance and economic policy, which included receiving material and ideological support from the USSR.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Nationalists promoted a centralized government based on capitalist ideals, appealing to urban elites and landowners who supported a stable, hierarchical society ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, the Communists pursued a socialist, classless society grounded in Marxist-Leninist ideology, attracting support from peasants and the working class who desired radical social change ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, while the Nationalists sought to minimize foreign influence and maintain independence ($\\checkmark$), the Communists aligned closely with the Soviet Union, gaining both ideological inspiration and material support, which strengthened their revolutionary goals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0189","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0189\n\nExplain **two** factors that led to the escalation of the Vietnam War.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The belief in the domino theory suggested that if one country in Southeast Asia fell to communism, others in the region would follow. This perspective convinced U.S. policymakers that Vietnam was critical to stopping the spread of communism, leading to increased American military and financial support to South Vietnam to resist the communist North.\n- In 1964, U.S. naval vessels were allegedly attacked by North Vietnamese forces in the Gulf of Tonkin. This incident led to the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, which granted President Johnson the authority to escalate military involvement in Vietnam, resulting in the deployment of large numbers of U.S. troops and airstrikes.\n- The Geneva Accords divided Vietnam into North and South at the 17th parallel and called for elections to reunify the country. These elections were never held, as the U.S. and South Vietnam feared a communist victory. This failure deepened tensions and led to a protracted conflict between the communist North and U.S.-backed South.North Vietnam received significant military and logistical aid from the Soviet Union and China, allowing it to sustain prolonged resistance against South Vietnam and the United States. This external support encouraged the U.S. to escalate its military operations to counter the influence of global communism.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne factor that escalated the Vietnam War was the domino theory, which suggested that if one country in Southeast Asia fell to communism, neighbouring nations would follow ($\\checkmark$). This belief drove the United States to intensify its involvement to prevent North Vietnam's communist regime from overtaking South Vietnam. The theory heightened Cold War tensions and justified increased military action, including troop deployments and airstrikes, to contain communism in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother factor was the Gulf of Tonkin Incident in 1964, where a supposed attack on U.S. naval vessels by North Vietnamese forces led to the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution. This gave President Lyndon B. Johnson the authority to escalate U.S. military operations in Vietnam without a formal declaration of war ($\\checkmark$). The resolution marked the beginning of full-scale American intervention, dramatically increasing troop numbers and intensifying the conflict ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0190","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0190\n\nDescribe **two** factors that contributed to the outbreak of the Falklands War in 1982.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Argentina's long-standing territorial claim over the Falkland Islands (known as Islas Malvinas in Argentina), which it argued had been illegitimately occupied by Britain since the early 19th century, created a historical grievance.\n- Economic and political instability in Argentina under the military junta. Facing widespread unrest and a declining economy, the junta sought to rally national support and distract from domestic issues by reclaiming the Falklands.\n- Britain's reduced military presence in the South Atlantic. The withdrawal of the HMS Endurance gave Argentina the impression that Britain was not committed to defending the islands, encouraging the decision to invade.\n- The failure of diplomatic negotiations over sovereignty. Years of inconclusive talks between Britain and Argentina exacerbated frustrations and pushed Argentina to take military action.\n- Nationalism in Argentina. The junta fueled nationalist fervour, portraying the Falklands as a matter of national pride, which pressured leaders to act decisively.\n- Miscalculation by Argentina. The Argentine government believed that Britain would not respond militarily to an invasion and assumed a diplomatic resolution could be achieved after taking the islands.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne factor that contributed to the outbreak of the Falklands War was Argentina's historical claim to the Falkland Islands, which it referred to as the Islas Malvinas. The Argentine government believed that the islands had been unjustly taken by Britain in the 19th century, and this territorial dispute remained a source of tension for decades ($\\checkmark$). Another factor was the political instability in Argentina during the rule of the military junta, which faced widespread public discontent due to a struggling economy and human rights abuses. The junta sought to use the reclamation of the islands as a way to divert attention from domestic issues and bolster nationalist sentiment among the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0191","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0191\n\nExplain **two** economic causes of the Spanish Civil War.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Land inequality: The concentration of land ownership in the hands of a small elite left rural peasants impoverished and without opportunities for economic advancement. This created widespread discontent, particularly in southern Spain, where landless labourers faced poor living conditions and were drawn to leftist ideologies advocating for land reform.\n- High unemployment: Economic hardships during the Great Depression worsened already high unemployment rates, particularly among the urban working class. This led to growing support for socialist and anarchist movements, which clashed with the conservative government and landowning elite, further polarising Spanish society.\n- Economic divide between rural and urban areas: The economic divide between rural and urban areas in Spain created significant social tensions. Rural populations remained trapped in poverty due to reliance on traditional agriculture and lack of investment, while urban areas experienced limited industrialization that benefited a small working class. This divide deepened resentment among rural communities, who felt excluded from economic progress, and urban workers, who demanded better wages and conditions.\n- Economic polarisation between regions: Wealthier regions like Catalonia and the Basque Country, which were more industrialised, resented the central government for syphoning resources without providing adequate autonomy. This regional economic disparity fueled separatist sentiments, adding to the divisions that contributed to the war.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne economic cause of the Spanish Civil War was the extreme inequality in land ownership. In southern Spain, much of the land was controlled by wealthy elites, leaving peasants landless and in poverty. This inequality created significant unrest among rural workers, many of whom turned to left-wing movements that promised land reform and social justice ($\\checkmark$). Another cause was Spain's slow industrial development, which created stark economic disparities between rural and urban areas. Workers in industrialised regions demanded better wages and conditions, while rural populations felt neglected, increasing tensions between different social groups and regions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0602Q2","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0602Q2\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the reasons for the start of the revolutionary war in Cuba?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Slum ($\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*Bohio\"*) dwellings in Havana, Cuba in 1954 outside the Havana baseball stadium. An advertisement for a nearby casino stands in the background.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Causes/images/HI0602Q2.jpg\" alt=\"HI0602Q2\">\n\n[Source: No author (1954). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved March 18, 2025 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Cuba_(1902%E2%80%931959)#/media/File:HavanaSlums1954.jpg. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Some people in Havana were very poor.\n\n - There was a stark contrast between the wealth of the buildings, like casinos and baseball stadiums, and the living conditions of many Cubans.\n\n - There was a US economic presence in Cuba (Westinghouse logo).\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source B, we can see that a sector of the Cuban population was ready for revolution as their living conditions were horrible while others were enjoying great wealth. This is evident in the shanties people live in compared to the grand building of the baseball stadium  ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, U.S. economic influence is evident, as seen in the Westinghouse logo, showing how foreign investment was not benefiting the average people's lives  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0602Q3","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0602Q3\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source D for a historian studying the causes of the Chinese Civil War.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Telegram to the British Government reporting on an editorial titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Without Democracy There Will Not Be Peaceful Reconstruction” published in the Yenan newspaper Emancipation Daily in 1945, commemorating the 34th anniversary of the Chinese Revolution of 1911. Yenan (Yan'an) was the capital of the Communist-controlled area of China, and Emancipation Daily was a newspaper produced by the Chinese Communist Party.\n\n[Source: Excerpt from a British telegram reporting on an editorial called $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*Without Democracy There Will Not Be Peaceful Reconstruction”* published in $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*Emancipation Daily”*. (1945). The National Archives. Retrieved March 18, 2025 from https://nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-chinese-civil-war/the-chinese-civil-war-source-2a/.  UK Open Government Licence. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n\n - The source contains insight as it was made in 1945, when the Chinese Civil War started its second phase. (origin)\n\n - The source summarizes the ideas of an editorial in a newspaper of the Chinese Communist Party, letting the reader know the communist position regarding the future of the country. \n\n - The source gives the Communist perspective, mentioning their desire for unity and a democratic government (content)\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n\n - The editorial is favorable to the Communist position, so it presents a one-sided view. (origin)\n\n - The source seeks to inform the British government of the most relevant ideas contained in the editorial, so it might leave out important information.  Since the editorial is one-sided it seeks to persuade readers that the Communist wants to fulfill the aims of the 1911 Revolution, leaving out communist ideology (purpose) \n\n - The source does not provide the perspective of the GMD/KMT, and due to its date of publication, it also ignores the outcome of this conflict (content)\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource D's origin is valuable as it was created in 1945, which allows it to have insight, as this is the year the Chinese Civil War entered its second phase ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$)\n\n. The purpose is also valuable since it summarizes the ideas of an editorial from a newspaper affiliated with the Chinese Communist Party, giving readers a clear understanding of the Communist stance on the country's future ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content reflects the Communist perspective, emphasizing their desire for national unity and a democratic government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, Source D also has some limitations. Its origin is limited as it is biased in favor of the Communist position, summarizing information belonging to a CCP newspaper ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).  Since it seeks to inform the British government about key ideas from the editorial, its purpose is also limited, since this desire to be concise may lead it to omit important details ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Additionally, because the editorial is intended to persuade readers that the Communists aim to fulfill the goals of the 1911 Revolution, it leaves out aspects of Communist ideology that might have been more questionable to the Chinese people ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The source also lacks the perspective of the GMD and, given its publication date, does not know that a war did end up breaking out ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0602Q4","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0602Q4\n\nWhat, according to Source C, were the causes of the Chinese Civil War?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a summary of the Chinese Civil War by the National Archives of Great Britain. \n\n[Source: *The Chinese Civil War*. The National Archives. Retrieved March 18, 2025 from https://nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-chinese-civil-war/. UK Open Government Licence. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - There were ideological differences between the GMD and the CCP\n\n - The groups that controlled the GMD were afraid the CCP would eliminate their power. \n\n - Peasants wanted land reform and better living conditions, indicating that there was an economic reason for the war\n\n - The rural masses had so far been ignored, and the CCP appealed to them. \n\n - The defeat of the Japanese meant there was no longer an unifying factor\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source C, there were many causes for the Chinese Civil War. One of them was that peasants wanted land reform and better living conditions ($\\checkmark$), something that had been ignored by the GMD but promised by the CCP ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, once the Japanese were defeated, there was no other reason to remain united in the face of a common enemy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0602Q6","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0602Q6\n\nDescribe the United States' economic influence in Cuba in the 1950s.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - By the late 1950s, U.S. financial interests had a great share in Cuba`s production, including 90 percent of Cuban mines, 80 percent of its public utilities, 50 percent of its railways, and 40 percent of its sugar production.\n\n - US capital was also important in the financial sector. It represented 25 percent of its bank deposits, roughly $1 billion in total. \n\n - A 1934 trade agreement with the USA eliminated all tariffs on US goods. This made the development of domestic industry impossible for Cuba.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe US practically dominated the Cuban economy in the 1950s. U.S. capital owned 90$\\%$ of its mines, 80$\\%$ of its public utilities, 50$\\%$ of its railways, and 40$\\%$ of its sugar production ($\\checkmark$). It was difficult for Cuba to liberate itself from US economic influence due to a 1934 trade agreement with the United States that eliminated all tariffs on U.S. goods, making it nearly impossible for Cuba to develop its own industries ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0602Q8","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0602Q8\n\nExplain the impact of the Second Sino-Japanese War on the GMD/KMT government in China.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following, each with supporting explanation/evidence:\n\n - Tax shortages due to the loss of control in wealthier coastal cities.\n\n     - Since Japan controlled the wealthy coastal cities, peasants had to pay for the war with higher taxes. Because this is not enough, the government prints money, generating inflation. \n\n - The GMD presents almost no resistance while the CCP fights against the invaders. \n\n     - Faced with the Japanese invasion, the GMD retreated to Chongqing, in central China. At the same time, the CCP stronghold was established in Yanan, next to the Japanese controlled areas of northern China, from where the Communists used their guerrilla tactics to weaken the invaders.\n\n - Lost of GMD/KMT control in Northern China\n\n     - Since the GMD is under attack by Japan, the CCP takes advantage of the situation and starts controlling most of Northern China.\n\n - Changing perception of Jiang Jieshi after decision to retreat\n\n     - The GMD/KMT's decision to retreat makes Jiang seem like a traitor and the CCP the defender of the nation.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Second Sino-Japanese War weakened the GMD so much that it made the second phase of the Chinese Civil War possible. Since Japan's attack led to its control of the wealthy coastal cities, the GMD had to raise taxes on the peasants to finance itself and the war ($\\checkmark$).  However, as this revenue was insufficient due to the poverty of the rural sector, the government resorted to printing money, leading to severe inflation, which reached 234$\\%$ in 1944 alone ($\\checkmark$). \n\nGMD policy of retreating to the interior and moving the capital to Chongqing also hurt the legitimacy of the government ($\\checkmark$). While the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) was actively resisting the Japanese invasion through guerrilla warfare, the Nationalist Government appeared as having given up on its duty to defend the nation against foreign aggression ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1048","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1048\n\nCompare and contrast the role of territorial disputes in the outbreak of **two** wars, each chosen from a different region.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- The primary focus of the responses should be the role of territorial disputes in the start of two wars. Other factors should also be addressed to refer to the “role” element of the question.\n- The command term calls for both similarities and differences in the role of territorial disputes in the start of wars. A running comparison structure, rather than a structure organized by wars allows for better analysis, but both can earn marks.\n- There are many wars of the 20th century in which territorial disputes were a key cause. Some possible examples include:\n    - **The Falklands/Malvinas War (1982):** Arose from a long-standing territorial dispute between Argentina and the United Kingdom over sovereignty of the Falkland Islands (Islas Malvinas). Argentina claimed the islands as part of its national territory, while Britain had taken back control in 1833. Other issues included both British and Argentine domestic politics, national pride/identity, miscommunication/willingness to escalate by the UK. After years of unfruitful diplomatic negotiations, Argentina's military government invaded in 1982 to assert its claim, prompting a British military response to retake the islands.\n    - **The Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905):** Driven by rival imperial ambitions in Northeast Asia, particularly over control of Manchuria and Korea. Russia sought to expand its influence in the region after gaining leases in Manchuria, while Japan viewed these areas as vital to its own security and economic interests. Other issues included imperial competition over control of economic resources and national prestige. Failure to reach a diplomatic compromise led Japan to launch a surprise attack on Russian forces in 1904.\n    - **The Chaco War (1932-1935):** A conflict between Bolivia and Paraguay centered on disputed territory in the Chaco Boreal region. Both nations claimed the area, which was sparsely populated but believed to hold oil reserves and offered access to the Paraguay River. Other issues included economic pressures due to the Great Depression, national rivalry/prestige, and the role of foreign influence (foreign oil interests). The dispute escalated into full-scale war as each sought to secure control of the region.\n    - **The Indo-Pakistani wars (1947-48, 1965, 1971, 1999):** All linked to territorial disputes, especially over Kashmir, whose accession to India at partition in 1947 was contested by Pakistan. Other issues included religious/ideological divides, national identity and legitimacy, Cold War issues/external influences, and internal domestic instability.\n\n**Indicative content for First World War (1914-1918) and the Second Sino-Japanese War (1937-1945):**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Role of territorial disputes}}$:\n- First World War\n    - The results of the Franco-Prussian War and France's loss of the provinces of Alsace and Lorraine generated a feeling of resentment and the desire to recover the territories lost in the Treaty of Frankfurt (1871).\n    - The incorporation of Bosnia into the Austro-Hungarian Empire (1908) generated resentment in Serbia, which wanted this region for its access to the sea and because its population was mostly ethnically Serb.\n- Second Sino-Japanese War\n    - Expansionist policies of Japan.\n    - The creation of the puppet state of Manchukuo by Japan  (1932) remained unrecognized by Chinese authorities.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Role of other causes}}$:\n- First World War\n    - Ideology: Nationalism was the motivation for the assassination of Archduke Franz-Ferdinand. Militarism encouraged the Arms Race among the Great Powers. Imperialism increased rivalries between England and Germany and France and Germany.\n    - Failure of diplomacy/Alliance system: The system of alliances turned a war between Austro-Hungary and Serbia into a European conflict (the July Crisis).\n    - Economic rivalry: Competition for colonies and markets, particularly between the UK, France and Germany, heightened tensions and mutual suspicion.\n- Second Sino-Japanese War\n    - Expansionism/resource needs: Japan's resource scarcity drove its goal to secure access to raw materials. Trade embargoes, such as the 1941 oil embargo, also increased pressure.\n    - Ideology: Japanese militarism and Shinto led to the belief in the superiority of the nation and justified international aggression. The ideological division in China between the Kuomintang and the Chinese Communist Party contributed to Japanese aggression against what was perceived as a weak adversary due to internal conflict.\n    - Failure of diplomacy: The League of Nations' inability to halt Japanese aggression in Manchuria in 1931 encouraged further expansion.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTerritorial disputes have often led to armed conflict throughout history. Two wars in which this is evident are WWI (1914-1918) and the Second Sino-Japanese War (1937-1945). It is also true that territorial disputes alone are not enough to explain the outbreak of both wars. Other causes, such as ideology and the failure of diplomacy, were fundamental for the start of these wars ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). This essay will compare and contrast the role of territorial disputes as a cause of the First World War and the Second Sino-Japanese War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nIn both wars, territorial disputes were the initial material cause of the conflicts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). In the case of WWI, France's defeat during the Franco-Prussian War and the subsequent loss of the provinces of Alsace and Lorraine motivated its desire for revenge against Germany, both due to the humiliation suffered and to recover its lost territories ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Similarly, in the case of the Second Sino-Japanese War, Japan's desire to maintain its control over Manchuria through the creation of the puppet state of Manchukuo made it want to protect this land ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The Chinese government refused to recognize this occupation and rejected Japan's expansionist ambitions to dominate East Asia. In both cases, the territories in question were rich in raw materials, giving them economic importance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). A difference resides in the fact that while France wanted to recover territory that had been taken from it, Japan wanted to protect land that it had taken from China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn both cases, territorial conflicts were a consequence of the growing nationalism that characterized the second part of the 19th century and the first part of the 20th ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In World War I, nationalism was a driving ideological force ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In France, the desire to recover Alsace-Lorraine became a matter of national honor. In the Balkans, Serbian nationalism sought to unite all South Slavs, challenging Austro-Hungarian authority over Bosnia ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Nationalism became closely linked to militarism as a way to expand or recover the European powers` territories. Both militarism and nationalism increased tension in Europe, making it susceptible to war in the face of any minor conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This was the case with the assassination of Franz Ferdinand by a Serbian terrorist nationalist group that sought to incorporate Bosnia into Serbia ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Japan also shared this militaristic ideology. Coupled with the state-sponsored religion, Shintoism and its belief in the superiority of the Japanese nation and the divine nature of the Emperor who could do no wrong ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), these beliefs encouraged Japanese aggression. They led to both the invasion of Manchuria and the invasion of mainland China in 1937.\n\nThe importance of ideology differs in the outbreak of both wars. All European powers that participated in the war shared the same ideologies of militarism and nationalism. In the case of the Second Sino-Japanese War, China was mired in the middle of a civil war between the contrasting ideologies of the Chinese Communist Party and the Kuomintang ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This civil dispute encouraged the Japanese government as it sought to capitalize on China's weakness, exacerbated by the ideological conflict it was experiencing.\n\nDespite the importance of territorial disputes and the ideologies that drove the outbreak of war, they do not suffice to explain the wars ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Alsace and Lorraine issue had existed since 1871, while WWI didn't start until 43 years later. The same is true for the Second Japanese War that began in 1937, while Japan had maintained control over Manchuria since 1931 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).  In both conflicts, however, we can recognize the failure of diplomacy as a key factor  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). In World War I, the rigid system of alliances turned a regional conflict between Austria-Hungary and Serbia into a continent-wide war. Austria-Hungary's participation in the Triple Alliance with Germany and the existence of the Russo-French Alliance since 1894 set the stage for escalating conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The lack of diplomatic flexibility these alliances created turned territorial tension into total war  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). To avoid a repetition of this diplomatic failure, the League of Nations was established in 1920. This organization should have stopped Japanese aggression in Manchuria. However, the failure to take decisive action and the fact that the Assembly of the League only ordered Japan to leave almost two years after the invasion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) encouraged Japan to keep expanding at the expense of its neighbors. In both wars, diplomacy failed to de-escalate the situation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, territorial disputes were important as causes of both WWI and the Second Sino-Japanese War as they constituted the initial material reason for conflict. However, nationalism and militarism were in part responsible for the territorial disputes as well as an increase in tension ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This tension could have been resolved through diplomacy, but in both cases, this failed to materialize positively. Therefore, the role of territorial disputes in the outbreak of both wars studied is very similar ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1246","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1246\n\n“Political factors were the most significant cause of 20th-century wars.” With reference to **two** wars, to what extent do you agree with the statement?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n- Clearly address the claim by evaluating whether political factors were the most significant cause of war.\n- Analyse and compare political factors with other economic, social, ideological or military causes of war, using specific evidence from two relevant wars to support the argument being made.\n- Arrive at a balanced, well-supported conclusion supported by evidence on the statement in the question.\n\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write\nabout in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive.\nPossible suitable examples include:\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{First World War}}$:\n\n**Political factors**\n- The alliance system with the Triple Alliance vs Triple Entente created blocs, turning local crises into continental war.\n- Nationalism, especially in the Balkans, destabilised empires and directly led to the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand.\n- Weak diplomatic crisis management during the July Crisis escalated tensions instead of containing them.\n\n**Militarism**\n- Arms race between major powers such as  Britain and Germany increased tension and suspicion.\n- Fixed war plans like the Schlieffen Plan created pressure for rapid mobilisation and  reduced diplomatic flexibility.\n- Military leaders held significant influence over political decision-making in the days following the assassination of the Austrian ArchDuke.\n\n**Economic and imperial rivalry**\n- Competition for colonies intensified hostility particularly between Britain, France and Germany.\n- Naval rivalry damaged Anglo-German relations.\n- Economic competition increased distrust but rarely caused direct confrontation.\n\n**Long-term social and ideological tensions**\n- Decline of multi-ethnic empires such as the Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian Empire created instability.\n- Nationalist movements challenged declining imperial authority.\n\n**Overall evaluation**\n- These factors created a climate of tension but required political decisions and diplomatic failure to result in war.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Second World War}}$:\n\n**Political factors**\n- The Treaty of Versailles created political resentment and instability in Germany that was exploited by Hitler.\n- Failure of collective security due to the weakness of the League of Nations, allowed aggression to go unchecked.\n- The policies of Appeasement followed by Britain and France emboldened Hitler and made war more likely.\n- Totalitarian leadership and expansionist foreign policies of Germany, Italy and Japan drove the conflict.\n\n**Economic factors**\n- The Great Depression caused mass unemployment and social unrest particularly in Germany leading to Hitler’s ascension to power. In Japan, it led to expansionist policies with the Manchurian crisis.\n- Economic hardship undermined democratic governments and increased support for extremist parties.\n- Desire for economic self-sufficiency or autarky encouraged territorial expansion by Germany.\n\n**Ideological factors**\n- Fascist ideology promoted militarism, expansion and rejection of international cooperation.\n- Ideological hostility between fascism, communism and democracy increased global tensions.\n- Nazi racial ideology justified aggressive expansion for Lebensraum or living space.\n\n**Military factors**\n- Rearmament by Germany, violated the Treaty of Versailles and altered the balance of power.\n- Lack of military preparedness in Britain and France encouraged Appeasement.\n- Early expansionist successes with Anschluss and the Munich Pact reinforced aggressive foreign policies.\n\n**Social factors**\n- Propaganda mobilised public support for war and expansion.\n\n**Overall evaluation**\n- These factors explain why aggressive regimes gained support but political failures and decisions shaped partially by economic factors allowed war to occur.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Chinese Civil War (1927-1949)}}$:\n\n**Political factors**\n- Weakness and failures of the Guomindang (GMD) government with corruption and authoritarian rule undermined popular support.\n- Reliance on urban elites and landlords limited mass appeal.\n- Fundamental ideological and political conflict between Nationalism put forward by the Guomindang (GMD) and Communism supported by the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) over China's future.\n- The CCP offered an alternative political vision centred on peasant mobilisation and reform.\n- Inability to sustain cooperation after the First United Front (1914-1927) led to renewed conflict.\n- Mao Zedong's leadership strengthened CCP unity, discipline and adaptability.\n- CCP's effective political mobilisation and use of propaganda contrasted with GMD inefficiency.\nCCPs political control over the Red Army ensured loyalty and coherence.\n\n**Social factors**\n- Peasant grievances over land ownership and exploitation created widespread unrest and GMDs failure to address social inequality weakened its position.\n- CCP land reform policies gained strong rural support.\n\n**Military factors**\n- CCP's use of guerrilla warfare offset GMD numerical and technological advantages\n- Red Army discipline and treatment of civilians contrasted with GMD brutality.\n- Strategic errors by Chiang Kai-shek weakened Nationalist campaigns.\n\n**Economic factors**\n- Hyperinflation in Nationalist-controlled areas destroyed savings and living standards.\n- Economic mismanagement reduced confidence in the GMD government\n- War fatigue and economic hardship pushed civilians towards the CCP\n\n**Foreign intervention**\n- The Japanese invasion in 1937 disrupted the civil war and weakened the GMD more than the CCP.\n- Soviet support and captured Japanese weapons benefited the CCP after 1945.\n- US aid to the GMD was inconsistent and poorly utilised.\n\n**Overall evaluation**\n- While social, military and economic factors explain how the CCP won, political leadership and ideology explain why the conflict developed.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Spanish Civil War (1936-1939)}}$:\n\n**Political factors**\n- There was political polarisation and instability as the Second Spanish Republic that came to power in 1931 faced deep divisions between the left and right.\n- Frequent government changes and weak coalitions undermined effective governance and radicalisation of politics made compromise increasingly impossible.\n- There was a breakdown of democratic institutions and the failure to maintain law and order led to political violence and assassinations.\n- The Ideological conflict was reflected in a political struggle between Republicanism, Socialism, Anarchism, and Fascism.\n- Fear of communist revolution drove conservative and elite support for the Nationalists\n- With the lack of respect for electoral outcomes and the right-wing opposition to the Popular Front victory in 1936, military intervention became seen as a political solution.\n- Politicisation of the army and resentment of Republican reforms led Franco and other generals to launch a coup to overthrow the elected government.\n- The failure of the coup to secure quick victory escalated into full civil war.\n\n**Social factors**\n- Deep class divisions between landowners and landless peasants.\n- Anti-clericalism versus Catholic conservatism heightened tensions.\n- Social unrest made political compromise more difficult\n\n**Economic factors**\n- Unequal land distribution and rural poverty caused long-term grievances.\n- The impact of the Great Depression increased unemployment and hardship.\n- Economic reforms of the Second Republic provoked resistance from elites.\n\n**Military factors**\n- The army's traditional power and autonomy encouraged intervention in politics.\n- Army reforms initiated by the Spanish Second Republic deeply alienated the conservative military elite, particularly the \"Africanists\" serving in Morocco. This  fostered intense anger that led to the military conspiracy and the eventual coup attempt in July 1936.\n\n**Foreign intervention**\n- German and Italian support strengthened the Nationalists\n- Soviet aid and International Brigades supported the Republicans\n- Non-Intervention policy of Britain and France weakened the Republican government\n\n**Overall evaluation**\n- Political divisions and the collapse of democratic legitimacy were central to the outbreak of war while social and economic tensions undermined the stability of society, contributing to political divisions and the eventual outbreak of the military coup.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Falklands War (1982)}}$:\n\n**Political factors**\n- Long-standing territorial sovereignty dispute between Britain and Argentina over ownership of the Falkland Islands.\n- Argentina viewed the islands as rightfully Argentine territory, the Las Malvinas that was  occupied by Britain.\n- The invasion was a direct political attempt to resolve the dispute by force.\n- There were domestic political pressures in Argentina as the ruling military junta faced declining legitimacy, economic crisis and public unrest.\n- The invasion was used as a nationalist distraction to restore political authority with decision-making being concentrated among military leaders with little civilian oversight.\n- Argentine leaders underestimated Britain’s willingness to defend the islands with the belief that the UK would negotiate rather than fight, reflecting flawed political judgement.\n- Failure of diplomatic efforts after the invasion led directly to war\n- British political response under Margaret Thatcher’s government viewed defence of the Falklands as essential to national prestige and credibility.\n- Political commitment to sovereignty and international law justified military action while domestic political support strengthened resolve to reclaim the islands.\n\n**Economic factors**\n- Severe economic decline in Argentina increased pressure on the junta.\n- While the Falklands themselves had limited economic value at the time, economic motives acted mainly through domestic political instability.\n\n**Military factors**\n- Argentine confidence was based on military control of the islands and Britain's distance.\n- However, British naval capability enabled a military response.\n\n**Nationalism and identity**\n- Argentine nationalism strongly associated with the claim to the Falklands.\n- British public opinion rallied around defence of sovereignty.\n\n**International context**\n- Cold War dynamics limited external intervention.\n- The US attempted mediation but ultimately supported Britain.\n- International law and UN resolutions framed the conflict.\n\n**Overall evaluation**\n- While economic and military factors shaped context and outcomes, it was political leadership, sovereignty claims and diplomatic failure that were the most significant causes of war.\n\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe twentieth century was marked by internal conflicts, driven by a complex interplay of political, social and economic factors. Political causes, particularly ideological divisions and struggles for power, were undeniably central to many wars. This is evident in both the Chinese Civil War (1927-1949) and the Spanish Civil War (1936-1939), where clashes between competing political ideologies played a decisive role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, political conflict was intensified by social and economic grievances, which radicalized populations and undermined political stability  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).Therefore, while political factors were the most significant cause for the outbreak of both wars, they were shaped by underlying social and economic tensions  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nIn China, political factors were central to both the initial outbreak of civil war in 1927 and the renewed conflict after 1945 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Following the fall of the Qing dynasty in 1911, China lacked a stable central government, leading to competition among political groups to determine China's future. The Chinese Communist Party (CCP), founded in 1921, promoted a revolutionary Marxist ideology, while the Guomindang (GMD) sought to establish a centralized nationalist state. Although the two parties formed the First United Front in 1923, this alliance was tactical rather than ideological. Its collapse in 1927 directly caused the outbreak of the first phase of the civil war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Chiang Kai-shek's decision to purge Communists during the Shanghai Massacre in April 1927, in which thousands of CCP members were killed, demonstrated the depth of political hostility and marked the transition from political rivalry to armed conflict. This was a deliberate political decision aimed at preserving GMD authority and directly triggered the outbreak of civil war. This event shows that political decisions and ideological fear were immediate triggers of war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe resumption of full-scale civil war after 1945 was also politically driven, and the defeat of Japan played a crucial role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Japanese surrender in August 1945 created a power vacuum in large areas of northern China. Both the CCP and the GMD rushed to occupy territory formerly under Japanese control, particularly Manchuria. Although negotiations, such as the Marshall Mission, attempted to form a coalition government, ideological differences and mutual suspicion made compromise impossible ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The failure of these talks demonstrated that political rivalry over control of the Chinese state, intensified by Japan's defeat, was the immediate cause of the renewed civil war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, the intensity of political conflict was shaped by social and economic conditions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). China in the 1920s was overwhelmingly rural, with widespread peasant poverty, high rents and landlord dominance. The GMD's failure to implement meaningful land reform alienated large sections of the rural population, while the CCP's promise of land redistribution made revolutionary politics appealing. The political conflict was shaped by the CCP's social and economic policies in the Jiangxi and Yan'an soviets, which helped politicize large sections of the population. In the Jiangxi Soviet (1931-1934), the CCP implemented radical land reform, confiscating land from landlords and redistributing it to poor peasants. Rent reductions, the abolition of usury and campaigns against landlord oppression directly addressed rural economic grievances. Additionally, the GMD's reliance on urban elites and military force, rather than mass support, weakened its legitimacy and hardened political divisions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Thus, while political conflict caused the outbreak of war, social and economic inequality intensified polarization and contributed to its inevitability ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nSimilarly, political factors were crucial in causing the Spanish Civil War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The establishment of the Second Spanish Republic in 1931 fundamentally altered Spain's political structure, ending the monarchy and introducing reforms aimed at secularization, military reduction and regional autonomy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These changes polarized Spanish politics, as left-wing parties supported reform while conservatives, monarchists and much of the army viewed the Republic as a threat to traditional values and national unity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The Republic introduced political reforms that aimed to reduce the power of traditional institutions, which provoked strong opposition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Regional autonomy was granted to Catalonia in 1932, with plans for Basque autonomy soon after. While this satisfied regional nationalists, it angered conservatives and the army, who viewed autonomy as a threat to Spanish unity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This deepened political polarization and weakened support for the Republic among key conservative institutions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Political reforms targeting the Church and the army further destabilized Spain. The Republic reduced the Church's influence by secularizing education, legalizing divorce and removing state funding for religious institutions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These measures alienated devout Catholics and conservative groups, who increasingly viewed the Republic as anti-Spanish and anti-Christian ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Military reforms, led by Manuel Azaña, reduced the number of officers in the army and forced many into early retirement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). While intended to modernize the army, these reforms humiliated senior officers and contributed to growing resentment within the military, directly increasing support for a coup ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nPolitical polarization in Spain was, however, simultaneously driven by deep-seated social and economic problems ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Land ownership in Spain was highly unequal, particularly in the south, where large estates (latifundia) dominated, and landless peasants faced chronic unemployment. The Republic's attempts at land reform were slow and limited, frustrating workers while alarming landowners. The 1932 land reform laws resulted in only 60,000 peasants being resettled by 1934. This failure angered landless workers, particularly in Andalusia, leading to strikes, land seizures and support for anarchist groups such as the CNT. Industrial workers were also frustrated by unemployment and poor conditions, and the perceived ineffectiveness of reform pushed many toward political extremism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These grievances intensified class conflict and translated directly into political instability. These social tensions strengthened anarchist and socialist movements on the left and authoritarian responses on the right. As a result, political compromise became increasingly impossible, making violent confrontation more likely. This instability convinced conservative elites and military leaders that peaceful political solutions were no longer possible, leading directly to the military uprising in July 1936 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, political factors were the most significant cause of both the Chinese and Spanish Civil Wars, as ideological conflict and key political decisions triggered the outbreak of violence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In China, the breakdown of cooperation between the GMD and the CCP, and Jiang Jieshi's anti-Communist purge, directly initiated the war. In Spain, political polarization following the establishment of the Second Republic and furthered by Republican reforms led directly to military rebellion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Ideological conflict and struggles for power directly triggered violence; however, these political divisions were fueled by underlying socio-economic inequalities. Without these contributing factors, political tensions alone may not have escalated into full-scale civil war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Thus, while political factors were the dominant cause of the outbreak of both wars, they were shaped and influenced by broader social and economic conditions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1474","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1474\n\nTo what extent was ideology the main cause of **two** 20th-century wars?[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n \n\n**General Guidance:**\n\n- Address $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ideology” as a causal factor, explaining how political doctrines, belief systems, and competing visions of state organization shaped motivations for conflict.\n\n- Explain causation as a multi-layered process, showing how ideology interacted with political instability, leadership decisions, economic pressures, and international dynamics.\n\n- The strongest answers will compare the two wars in an integrated way rather than separately and evaluate the relative importance of ideology in both with other factors, reaching a balanced judgment.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n\n- Russian Civil War (Europe)\n\n    - Ideology could be explored through the clash between Bolshevik revolutionary doctrine and the anti-communist visions of liberals, conservatives, and nationalists.\n\n    - The Bolsheviks framed the war as a defense of the revolution and Marxist state-building, giving ideology a strong mobilizing role; the Whites opposed Bolshevism on ideological grounds of national unity and traditional order.\n\n    - Ideology's role in justifying extreme measures (e.g., the Red Terror, propaganda, political repression) could be weighed.\n\n    - Counterarguments might include non-ideological causes: state collapse, economic breakdown, military disintegration, and wartime exhaustion after 1914.\n\n    - Regional, ethnic, and nationalist movements pursuing goals that were not primarily ideological could complicate the picture, as could contingent triggers such as the Czechoslovak Legion revolt (May 1918).\n\n    - Foreign intervention (Britain, France, Japan, the US) could be assessed as strategically rather than ideologically motivated.\n\n- Korean War (Asia)\n\n    - Ideology could be explored through the confrontation between communism in the North and anti-communist nationalism in the South, with the 1945 division creating two states with mutually exclusive claims to the peninsula.\n\n    - Kim Il Sung's invasion could be linked to ideological goals of revolutionary reunification; Stalin's approval and Soviet equipment, and later Chinese and US intervention, could be used to show ideology shaping international escalation.\n\n    - Propaganda, purges, and mass mobilization on both sides could illustrate the war's ideological framing.\n\n    - Counterarguments might include personal ambition, strategic calculations, and instability along the artificial 38th-parallel border.\n\n    - Acheson's January 1950 defensive-perimeter speech could be considered a non-ideological factor that may have encouraged the invasion.\n\n- Mexican Revolution (Americas)\n\n    - Competing ideological visions of constitutionalism, land reform, and national modernization shaped revolutionary factions' motivations.\n\n    - Zapatista agrarian ideology framed the conflict as a struggle for social justice and for indigenous rights.\n\n    - The collapse of the Porfirian regime could be weighed against ideology as a rival explanation for the outbreak of the conflict.\n\n    - Counterargument: much of the fighting after Díaz's fall was driven by personalist and regional power struggles among revolutionary leaders (Villa, Carranza, Obregón, Zapata) rather than a coherent ideological program, so factional rivalry and caciquismo might be weighed against ideology as competing explanations.\n\n- Algerian War of Independence (Africa)\n\n    - The FLN framed the conflict ideologically as a struggle for national liberation and for anti-colonial self-determination.\n\n    - French counterinsurgency relied on ideological narratives, such as the defense of Algérie française and France's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$civilizing mission,\" to justify continued control and to resist decolonization.\n\n    - Ideology shaped international support, as the FLN used anti-colonial discourse to secure legitimacy abroad.\n\n    - Counterargument: settler-colonial economic structures such as land dispossession, pied-noir (European settler) control of the best agricultural land, and the socioeconomic exclusion of the Muslim majority could be weighed as non-ideological causes predating any nationalist doctrine.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n \n\n$\\\\$\n\n \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n \n\nThe extent to which ideology caused twentieth-century wars has been widely debated by historians. This essay examines the Russian Civil War and the Korean War to evaluate whether ideology was the main cause in each case. ($\\checkmark$ focus) While both conflicts were shaped by ideological confrontation, they also emerged from structural instability, geopolitical pressures, and leadership decisions. A balanced assessment requires analyzing ideological motivations alongside non-ideological factors to determine their relative significance. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nIdeology played a central role in the outbreak of the Russian Civil War. The Bolsheviks sought to consolidate power after the October Revolution by implementing a revolutionary program based on Marxism, class struggle, and the creation of a socialist state. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Their opponents, known collectively as the Whites, represented a diverse coalition of liberals, conservatives, monarchists, and nationalists who rejected Bolshevik ideology and viewed the new regime as illegitimate. This ideological polarization created a political environment in which compromise became impossible. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe Bolsheviks framed the conflict as a struggle for the survival of the revolution, using propaganda, class rhetoric, and the promise of social transformation to mobilize support. The Whites, in turn, justified their resistance through ideological appeals to national unity, anti socialism, and the restoration of traditional authority. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Ideology therefore provided both sides with a clear narrative that defined enemies, justified violence, and shaped military and political strategies.\n\nHowever, ideology alone cannot explain the outbreak of the Russian Civil War. The collapse of the Russian Empire during the First World War produced severe economic dislocation, food shortages, military fragmentation, and administrative breakdown. These structural conditions made conflict highly likely regardless of ideological differences. Regional and ethnic groups also pursued autonomy for reasons not primarily ideological, and foreign intervention by Britain, France, Japan, and the United States was motivated more by strategic concerns than ideological commitment. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) As historian Orlando Figes argues, the war emerged from a combination of ideological confrontation and the collapse of state authority, while Richard Pipes emphasizes the ideological extremism of the Bolsheviks as the decisive trigger. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Taken together, these interpretations suggest that ideology was a major cause, but not the sole or dominant one.\n\nIn contrast, ideology was the primary cause of the Korean War. After 1945, Korea was divided into two states with fundamentally incompatible ideological systems. The North, under Kim Il Sung, embraced communism, revolutionary nationalism, and alignment with the Soviet Union. The South, under Syngman Rhee, adopted anti communist nationalism, capitalist development, and alignment with the United States. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Both governments claimed to be the legitimate ruler of the entire peninsula, and both viewed reunification as essential to national destiny.\n\nKim Il Sung's decision to invade the South in June 1950 was driven by ideological goals of revolutionary reunification and the belief that socialism must triumph in Korea. Soviet and Chinese support was also ideologically motivated, as both states saw the conflict as part of a broader struggle against Western capitalism. The United States intervened to defend the liberal capitalist order and prevent the spread of communism in Asia. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Ideology therefore shaped not only the motivations of the Korean leadership but also the internationalization of the conflict.\n\nAlthough ideology was dominant, non-ideological factors also contributed to the outbreak of the Korean War. Kim Il Sung sought personal prestige and strategic advantage, believing that the South was politically weak and militarily vulnerable. The division of Korea at the 38th parallel was an artificial boundary created hastily in 1945, producing long-term instability. The United States initially signaled limited commitment to defending South Korea, which may have encouraged Kim to act. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Historians such as Bruce Cumings emphasize structural and regional factors, presenting the conflict as a civil war rooted in divisions that predated 1950, while William Stueck situates it within the international confrontation between the superpowers. Cumings's interpretation explains the depth of Korean animosities, but the timing and scale of the June 1950 invasion, which were dependent upon Soviet approval and equipment, are better explained by the ideological confrontation Stueck describes, suggesting ideology remained decisive even if the conflict had civil roots. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nComparing the two wars reveals important differences in the role of ideology. In the Russian Civil War, ideology was significant but operated within a context of state collapse, wartime exhaustion, and social fragmentation. Ideology provided motivation and justification, but the structural crisis of 1917 to 1918 made conflict almost inevitable. In the Korean War, ideology was the dominant cause because the conflict emerged directly from the confrontation between communism and capitalism during the early Cold War. The internationalization of the conflict further demonstrates how ideology shaped alliances, interventions, and escalation. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe comparison also shows that ideology can function differently depending on historical context. In Russia, ideology divided domestic actors and shaped internal conflict. In Korea, ideology structured the entire geopolitical environment, transforming a regional dispute into a global confrontation. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) This difference is essential for evaluating the extent to which ideology was the main cause in each case.\n\nOverall, ideology was a major cause in both wars, but its significance varied. In the Russian Civil War, ideology was important but interacted with structural collapse, economic crisis, and wartime conditions. In the Korean War, ideology was the primary cause, shaping motivations, alliances, and the scale of the conflict more decisively than any other factor. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion) Therefore, ideology was the main cause to a greater extent in the Korean War than in the Russian Civil War, demonstrating that ideological confrontation can be decisive in some twentieth-century conflicts but not universally dominant.\n\n \n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:-:|\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus) |\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge) |\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis) |\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion) |"}]},{"name":"Effects","questions":[{"ref":"HI0118","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0118\n\nExplain **one** similarity between the political impact of WWI and WWII.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following ideas with its corresponding evidence.\n- The creation of international organisations for peace keeping: the League of Nations (1920) and the United Nations (1945)\n- The end of an authoritarian system of government for some of the countries of Europe: Monarchy in Germany, Russia and the Austro-Hungarian Empire after WWI and Fascism and Nazism in Italy and Germany after WWII.\n- The strengthening of Communism with the establishment of the first Communist State in the former Russian Empire after WWI and the establishment of Communist regimes in many countries of Europe after WWII (Poland, Czechoslovakia, Romania, Hungary, German Democratic Republic, Yugoslavia, Albania, Bulgaria)\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth WWI and WWII had important political impacts. One of them was the realisation that a conflict of such magnitude should be avoided. To achieve this international organisations were created with the objective of solving conflicts peacefully with the principle of collective security ($\\checkmark$). The League of Nations was created after WWI as stipulated in the Treaty of Versailles while the United Nations was created in 1945 a few months before the surrender of Japan ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0119","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0119\n\nEvaluate the impact of WWI on the role of women.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nSince the command term is “evaluate”, the response must contain **both positive and negative** results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a **conclusion.**\n\nPositive results:\n- The war allowed women to take up occupations traditionally monopolised by men, especially in the war industries.\n- Women gained the right to vote in Germany, Russia, Austria, Britain, Czechoslovakia, the Netherlands, Poland, Sweden, the US and Belgium\n- Their wages were higher than traditional women's work.\n- In Britain, some professions opened up to women after the war; they could now train to become architects and lawyers and were allowed to serve on a jury.\n\nNegative results:\n- Women were supposed to receive equal wages to men for similar jobs, but rarely did.\n- With demobilisation and the smaller demand for goods that characterised the post war years, most women lost the jobs they had done during the war.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- Responses are likely to conclude that the overall results of WWI were positive for the role of women in opening up new possibilities in the economic and political spheres.\n- The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWWI had many positive impacts on the role of women although many of them were short lived. Due to the lack of men available because of mobilisation and the increased demand caused by the war, women were needed in industry to perform what were previously viewed as men's jobs. This opened up new possibilities for employment and getting better wages than in traditional “women's jobs” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{positive}$). However, in their new jobs, they were paid less than men even though they performed the same tasks ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{negative}$). The end of the war meant the return to “home life” for most women, so new job opportunities were not a lasting impact of WWI on women ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{negative}$).\n\nOn the positive side, even though new job opportunities did not last long for most positions, there were some exceptions. Women in Great Britain were allowed to train to become architects and lawyers after the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{positive}$) and in most countries, their contribution to war effort made it evident women should have a say in government. That is why, in many countries such as the UK and the US, the right to vote was extended to women right after the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{positive}$).\n\nOverall, WWI had a positive impact in opening up new possibilities for women in the economic and political spheres. Their participation in the war effort and in the world of work made it hard to sustain the idea that women should be limited to home life. That is why, even though the economic opportunities were short lived, they paved the way for women to get the right to vote which had been denied to them previously ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0120","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0120\n\nIdentify **two** territorial changes brought about by WWII.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Division of Germany between France, England, the USSR and the US which would lead to a long term division into the Federal Republic of Germany and the German Democratic Republic.\n- Division of Korea into one controlled by the USSR and one controlled by the US which would lead to a long term division into the Democratic People's Republic of Korea and the Republic of Korea.\n- US occupation of all of Japan until 1954 and of Okinawa until 1972.\n- Redrawing of the Eastern frontiers of Germany.\n- Redrawing of the frontiers of Poland and the USSR.\n- USSR annexes the Southern Sakhalin and Kuril Islands from Japan\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSome territorial changes after WWII were the occupation of Germany by France, England, the USSR and the US which would lead to a long term division into the Federal Republic of Germany and the German Democratic Republic ($\\checkmark$) and the fact that Germany lost territory to Poland while Poland ceded territory to the USSR  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0122","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0122\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the main objectives of the occupation forces?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “The British Occupation and the Making of Democracy in Italy and Germany, 1943-1949.” published by *The Historical Journal*, 2024.|\n\n:box[The basic rationale was to save material resources, while securing the stability of the occupation by leaving the day-to-day running to those who were seen by the local population as legitimate rulers. As we argue in this article, these choices of the occupiers helped produce a particular model of democracy that was characterized by a continuity in authoritarian personnel of the state administrations, a strong top-down political culture, and a technocratic process of decision-making embedded within corporatist structures that widely limited participation of the population in democratic procedures.(...)The assumption that both Italians and Germans were destined to live under authoritarian rule meant that the British failed to take seriously local democratic initiatives from below and harbored a deep-seated distrust towards building a more participatory form of democracy in the occupied territories. The emphasis on creating functioning states, rather than on large-scale democratization, was exacerbated by the fact that at the beginning, British priority lay emphatically with securing health and food supplies and rebuilding basic local infrastructure.(...)\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/Screeshot%202-01-14%20t%2012.04.37.png\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">Democracy was successfully consolidated in Germany and Italy in the coming decades. However, it was a particular model of democracy that had emerged from war and occupation. These were democracies that were inclusive to the extent that they delivered tangible material benefits to wide sectors of the population. The ruling elites’ preference for restoration over renewal, stability over experiments, and compromise over conflict, however, meant that they left little room for democratic practices that would have allowed broader parts of the population to participate in the political process.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: Erlichman C, Corduwener, P. (2024). *The British Occupation and the Making of Democracy in Italy and Germany, 1943–1949*. The Historical Journal. 67 (3), pages 538-560. Retrieved October 19, 2024 from https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/historical-journal/article/british-occupation-and-the-making-of-democracy-in-italy-and-germany-19431949/E578543831217F9775480E2F0A2D2747. CC-BY 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Save material resources\n- Secure the stability of the occupation by leaving the running of the administration to those seen as legitimate rulers\n- Create functioning states\n- Secure health and food supplies and rebuilding basic local infrastructure\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe main objectives of the occupation forces, according to Source A, were to not spend a lot of resources on the occupation itself ($\\checkmark$); to make sure people of the occupied countries had the necessary food and health supplies ($\\checkmark$) and to make sure the occupied populations accepted the situation by leaving the running of the day to day to those seen as legitimate rulers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0123","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0123\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the Allies' plans for Germany at the end of WWII?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the \"Speech of Hope\" by James F. Byrnes, United States Secretary of State (September 6, 1946). This speech was given in Stuttgart, Germany.|\n\n:box[When the ruthless Nazi dictatorship was forced to surrender unconditionally, there was no German government with which the Allies could deal. The Allies had temporarily to take over the responsibilities of the shattered German state, which the Nazi dictatorship had cut off from any genuine accountability to the German people. The Allies could not leave the leaders or minions of Nazism in key positions, ready to reassert their evil influence at first opportunity. They had to go.<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/Screeshot%202-01-14%20t%2012.04.37.png\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">But it never was the intention of the American Government to deny to the German people the right to manage their own internal affairs as soon as they were able to do so in a democratic way, with genuine respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.(...) The Potsdam Agreement wisely provided that administration of the affairs of Germany should be directed toward decentralization of the political structure and the development of local responsibility. This was not intended to prevent progress toward a central government with the powers necessary to deal with matters which would be dealt with on a nation-wide basis. But it was intended to prevent the establishment of a strong central government dominating the German people instead of being responsible to their democratic will. ]\n\n[Source: Byrnes, J. F.  (1946, September 6). *Speech of Hope*. German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved October 17, 2024 from https://ghdi.ghi-dc.org/docpage.cfm?docpage_id=2992.  Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- To temporarily take over the responsibilities of the  German state.\n- To remove the Nazis from key positions\n- To decentralize the political structure and allow for the development of local responsibility.\n- To prevent the establishment of a strong central government that ignores the will of the people\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAfter the defeat of Germany, the Allies agreed to fix the lack of a state organization that the Nazis had left behind ($\\checkmark$). The Allies also promised to remove Nazis from important positions in the administration ($\\checkmark$). In the longer run, they planned for Germany to have a national government that would respect the will of the people ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0124","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0124\n\nContrast what sources A and B reveal about the establishment of a postwar administration in Germany after WWII by the Allies.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A**  $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “The British Occupation and the Making of Democracy in Italy and Germany, 1943-1949.” published by *The Historical Journal*, 2024.|\n\n:box[The basic rationale was to save material resources, while securing the stability of the occupation by leaving the day-to-day running to those who were seen by the local population as legitimate rulers. As we argue in this article, these choices of the occupiers helped produce a particular model of democracy that was characterized by a continuity in authoritarian personnel of the state administrations, a strong top-down political culture, and a technocratic process of decision-making embedded within corporatist structures that widely limited participation of the population in democratic procedures.(...)The assumption that both Italians and Germans were destined to live under authoritarian rule meant that the British failed to take seriously local democratic initiatives from below and harbored a deep-seated distrust towards building a more participatory form of democracy in the occupied territories. The emphasis on creating functioning states, rather than on large-scale democratization, was exacerbated by the fact that at the beginning, British priority lay emphatically with securing health and food supplies and rebuilding basic local infrastructure.(...)\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/question%3Aaf08e681-b78c-4200-8eb3-584070ab58f6\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">Democracy was successfully consolidated in Germany and Italy in the coming decades. However, it was a particular model of democracy that had emerged from war and occupation. These were democracies that were inclusive to the extent that they delivered tangible material benefits to wide sectors of the population. The ruling elites’ preference for restoration over renewal, stability over experiments, and compromise over conflict, however, meant that they left little room for democratic practices that would have allowed broader parts of the population to participate in the political process.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: Erlichman C, Corduwener, P. (2024). *The British Occupation and the Making of Democracy in Italy and Germany, 1943–1949*. The Historical Journal. 67 (3), pages 538-560. Retrieved October 19, 2024 from https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/historical-journal/article/british-occupation-and-the-making-of-democracy-in-italy-and-germany-19431949/E578543831217F9775480E2F0A2D2747. CC-BY 4.0.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the \"Speech of Hope\" by James F. Byrnes, United States Secretary of State (September 6, 1946). This speech was given in Stuttgart, Germany.|\n\n:box[When the ruthless Nazi dictatorship was forced to surrender unconditionally, there was no German government with which the Allies could deal. The Allies had temporarily to take over the responsibilities of the shattered German state, which the Nazi dictatorship had cut off from any genuine accountability to the German people. The Allies could not leave the leaders or minions of Nazism in key positions, ready to reassert their evil influence at first opportunity. They had to go.<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/question%3A1781c243-2af6-4b3d-981b-f677036df903\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">But it never was the intention of the American Government to deny to the German people the right to manage their own internal affairs as soon as they were able to do so in a democratic way, with genuine respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.(...) The Potsdam Agreement wisely provided that administration of the affairs of Germany should be directed toward decentralization of the political structure and the development of local responsibility. This was not intended to prevent progress toward a central government with the powers necessary to deal with matters which would be dealt with on a nation-wide basis. But it was intended to prevent the establishment of a strong central government dominating the German people instead of being responsible to their democratic will. ]\n\n[Source: Byrnes, J. F.  (1946, September 6). *Speech of Hope*. German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved October 17, 2024 from https://ghdi.ghi-dc.org/docpage.cfm?docpage_id=2992.  Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence from the sources:\n\nContrasts:\n- In Source A, decentralization was done to make sure there was “stability of the occupation by leaving the day-to-day running to those who were seen by the local population as legitimate rulers” while in Source B decentralization was inteded to “prevent the establishment of a strong central government dominating the German people instead of being responsible to their democratic will”\n- In Source A it says that the Allies allowed the “continuity in authoritarian personnel of the state administrations,” while in Source B Byrnes says “The Allies could not leave the leaders or minions of Nazism in key positions”\n- In Source A it says that the Allies had “The assumption that both Italians and Germans were destined to live under authoritarian rule” while in Source B Byrnes promises democracy when he says “it never was the intention of the American Government to deny to the German people the right to manage their own internal affairs as soon as they were able to do so in a democratic way”\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources A and B talk about how the Allied powers tried to get democracy established in Germany after the war. However, they have very different ideas. Source A says that decentralization was needed to make sure there was “stability of the occupation by leaving the day-to-day running to those who were seen by the local population as legitimate rulers” while in Source B decentralization was done to “prevent the establishment of a strong central government dominating the German people instead of being responsible to their democratic will” ($\\checkmark$). Also, Source A says that to facilitate this stability they had to allow the “continuity in authoritarian personnel of the state administrations,” which is contradicted in Source B when Byrnes says “The Allies could not leave the leaders or minions of Nazism in key positions” ($\\checkmark$). This difference can be explained by the perceptions the Sources say the Allies had of the Germans. While Source B says that Germans would be able to manage themselves in a “democratic way” eventually, Source A says the Allies assumed “that both Italians and Germans were destined to live under authoritarian rule” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0125","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0125\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the situation of Germany after WWII. [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Speech of Hope\" by James F. Byrnes, United States Secretary of State (September 6, 1946). This speech was given in Stuttgart, Germany.|\n\n \n\n:box[When the ruthless Nazi dictatorship was forced to surrender unconditionally, there was no German government with which the Allies could deal. The Allies had temporarily to take over the responsibilities of the shattered German state, which the Nazi dictatorship had cut off from any genuine accountability to the German people. The Allies could not leave the leaders or minions of Nazism in key positions, ready to reassert their evil influence at first opportunity. They had to go.<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/Screenshot%202025-02-17%20at%2020.42.35.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-02-17 at 20.42.35\">\n\nBut it never was the intention of the American Government to deny to the German people the right to manage their own internal affairs as soon as they were able to do so in a democratic way, with genuine respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.(...) The Potsdam Agreement wisely provided that administration of the affairs of Germany should be directed toward decentralization of the political structure and the development of local responsibility. This was not intended to prevent progress toward a central government with the powers necessary to deal with matters which would be dealt with on a nation-wide basis. But it was intended to prevent the establishment of a strong central government dominating the German people instead of being responsible to their democratic will. ]\n\n[Source: Byrnes, J. F.  (1946, September 6). *Speech of Hope*. German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved October 17, 2024 from https://ghdi.ghi-dc.org/docpage.cfm?docpage_id=2992. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nIf only values or limitations are discussed, the student cannot get more than **two** marks. There must be at least one reference to origin, purpose and content in either value or limitations to reach four marks.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n |  | $\\ce{VALUE}$ |$\\ce{LIMITATIONS}$|\n | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| ORIGIN | The source is made in 1946, right at the end of WWII so it has insight into the situation. It is also made by one of the men that was in charge of the future of Germany at the end of the war so he knows what he is talking about. | It is a speech and we do not know the intended audience so it's difficult to determine the purpose. $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/Screeshot%202-01-14%20t%2012.04.37.png\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">It lacks hindsight so Byrnes does not know if what he proposes is what actually ended up happening. |\n| PURPOSE| To inform about the intentions of the US government regarding the government of Germany. Since he seeks to inform, we can assume at least part of what he says is true.  |To persuade the Germans that Germany will have democracy eventually even if it does not seem like that in 1946. Since the objective is to persuade, we can expect some information to be left out and other to be exaggerated so as to convince the audience.   |\n| CONTENT | Identifies what the US wants to do with Germany now that the war is over. For example the “decentralization of the political structure and the development of local responsibility” |Byrnes gives his own interpretation of what the Allies intended for Germany but the Allies also included countries like the USSR that did not have the same objectives as the Americans. The points of view of the other Allies, and of the Germans themselves are missing in this source. |\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B is valuable in its origin since it is The source was written in 1946 at the end of WWII, so it has insight into the situation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). It was also written by one of the men that was in charge of the future of Germany at the end of the war, so we can assume a high degree of knowledge and expertise on the topic ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, precisely because it is from 1946, it lacks hindsight and does not yet know about, for example, the establishment of a dictatorship in Eastern Germany ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nThe purpose is limited since it seeks to persuade the Germans that Germany will have democracy eventually even if it does not seem like that in 1946. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$).\n\nThe content is valuable since it identifies what the US wants to do with Germany at the end of WWII ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the content is also limited as Byrnes gives his own interpretation of what the Allies intended for Germany but the Allies also included countries like the USSR that did not have the same objectives as the Americans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0126","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0126\n\nWhat does the Source suggest about peace negotiations after the Russo-Japanese War?\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cartoon by Clifford Berryman, which appeared in the Washington Post on August 6, 1905. The Cartoon shows President Theodore Roosevelt offering peace pipes to Japan and Russia at mediation talks after Japan had defeated Russia in the Russo - Japanese War. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Effects/images/HI0126.jpg\" alt=\"HI0126\">\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1905, August 6). *Untitled*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved October 17, 2024 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/6010605.  Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Russia was left badly hit by the war and thus was in a weaker position to negotiate.\n- Japan still had the power to do more damage if it required indemnities, as seen in the club next to the Japanese man.\n- Roosevelt was happy and calm during the peace negotiations which indicates the war was beneficial for the US.\n- Japan looks sure of itself while Russia looks worried at the results of the peace negotiations.\n- Neither of them seem to want to make peace as none is taking the pipe and they both have their arms crossed.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that Russia was left badly hit by the war and thus was in a weaker position to negotiate ($\\checkmark$). We can see this in the injured face of the Russian character and the smug expression of the Japanese character implying that Japan expects negotiations to favor it ($\\checkmark$). In fact, Japan has the power to do more damage to Russia since it can require compensation for the war, as seen in the club labeled “indemnities” next to the Japanese character ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0291","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0291\n\nExplain **two** economic impacts of WWI on Germany. \n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - The War hurt Germany's trade. \n\n     - Germany imported about 30 percent of its food before the war but Britain's naval blockade meant that by  December 1915, Germany imported half of what it imported in 1913. During the war it lost most of its merchant ships and this hurt its post war international trade\n\n - The War hurt the German government's finances. \n\n     - Germany spent \\$19.9 billion on the war effort. This caused a huge government debt. The German government's internal debt was the same as the German total national income. The Weimar Republic chose to print money to pay for the domestic debts it had incurred to pay for the war. In part, this led to the hyperinflation of 1923.\n\n - The Treaty of Versailles' clauses required Germany to lose areas with key resources.\n\n     - Alsace-Lorraine and Upper Silesia were important industrial areas; the Saarland`s rich coal production was to go to France for 15 years. \n\n - The Treaty of Versailles required Germany to pay reparations of £6.600 billion.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWWI had adverse effects on the German government's finances ($\\checkmark$). The government was left bankrupt as it had spent \\$19.9 billion on the war effort, resulting in a domestic debt so big that it was equivalent to the nation's total income ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe economic consequences of the Treaty of Versailles further weakened the German economy ($\\checkmark$). The Treaty called for key industrial areas to be transferred to other countries. For example, Alsace-Lorraine was returned to France and Upper Silesia was given to the newly created state of Czechoslovakia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0324","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0324\n\nExplain the reasons for the differing treatment of Japanese Americans and Japanese Argentinians.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - The Japanese threat was much bigger to the US due to its geographical location and the amount of people of Japanese origin. \n\n     - The Japanese-born population of Argentina in 1940 was only 5000 people. In the US, around 120,000 Japanese and Japanese Americans were interned.  \n\n - The US had a long history of racism against its Asian population, while Argentina did not\n\n     - Since passage of the Immigration Act of 1924 (the Johnson-Reed Act), Japanese were not allowed to migrate to the US. In Argentina, there were no laws that excluded migrants from any country. \n\n - Argentina remained neutral throughout most of the war, declaring war on Japan much later than the US. \n\n     - Argentina declared war on Japan in March 1945, 5 months after the Japanese surrender, and only when it was already clear the war was almost over. There was not that much time to implement measures against the Japanese community.\n\n - Japan attacked the US, while Japan and Argentina had friendly relations. \n\n     - The Japanese attacked the US naval base at Pearl Harbor, while in 1940, Japan elevated its diplomatic representation in Argentina to the embassy level. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWhile the United States followed a harsh policy of internment of people of Japanese origin who were living on its West coast, Argentina only confiscated the goods of businesses that were subsidiaries of Japanese companies. There are many reasons for the different ways in which each country dealt with what it considered the domestic Japanese threat during WWII. \n\nIn the case of the US, the threat of Japanese aggression was much greater for the United States than for Argentina due to geographical proximity and demographic factors ($\\checkmark$). In 1940, Argentina had only 5,000 Japanese-born residents, whereas in the U.S., approximately 120,000 Japanese and Japanese Americans who were concentrated along its western coast ($\\checkmark$). The U.S. also had a long history of racism against its Asian population while this was not the case in Argentina ($\\checkmark$). Since 1924, Japanese migration to the U.S. had been legally prohibited whereas in Argentina, there were no laws that excluded migrants from any country ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0611","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0611\n\nIdentify **two** ways that World War I impacted civilian life in Great Britain.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Women began to work in the positions that had previously been held by men.\n\n - The government imposed rations on the country in order to maintain food supplies.\n\n - Agriculture suffered, leading to price increases.\n\n - Conscription made it mandatory for men to serve in the armed forces.\n\nPropaganda was used to encourage patriotism.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWorld War I greatly impacted British civilians. In 1916, the government made conscription in the military mandatory for all men, causing a notable drop in the workforce ($\\checkmark$). As a result, women were expected to work in the factories and farms in jobs that had previously been considered men's work ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0651","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0651\n\nDescribe **four** impacts of World War I on civilians in Germany.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n- The German civilians were rationing their food like the other countries and experienced a massive food shortage on the homefront. This led to what was called the Turnip Winter, where most people were forced to eat only turnips.\n- The German government used propaganda to convince the people at home to support the war effort. This propaganda came in the form of posters and cartoons that portrayed the allies as evil or greedy.\n- Germany had a compulsory military service procedure in place even before the war, though the service time was extended during the war. This led to less men on the homefront and in the factories and farms.\n- Women were expected to work in the factories and on the farms in order to produce food and weapons for the war.\n- Food shortages and devastating military losses eventually led to political upheaval. The Kaiser was overthrown and a new government was created.\n- Social unrest and decline in civilian morale occurred due to military losses and general dissatisfaction with the war effort.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGermany, like most other countries during World War I, was in a total war. Every aspect of civilian lives revolved around the war effort. This could be seen most clearly through the policy of compulsory military service for men. While many men served voluntarily due to the nationalist and militaristic attitude of the country at the time, all men between 20 and 45 were expected to serve ($\\checkmark$). Large numbers of men enlisting in the German military led to an increased number of women in the workforce. These women were responsible for working in agriculture and the factories, producing food and weapons for the war effort ($\\checkmark$). The German civilians were also forced to ration their food while experiencing food shortages due to a British naval blockade. This led to many people dying of starvation and a loss of support for the war effort ($\\checkmark$). All of these problems led to political upheaval at home in the last few months of the war. After some devastating military losses, civilians and members of the military revolted against the leadership leading to the abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II and the eventual exit from the war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0742","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0742\n\nExplain **three** ways World War One contributed to the collapse of political systems in Europe.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with supporting evidence/explanation:\n\n - Military defeat of Central Powers. \n\n     - The defeat of Germany, Austria-Hungary, and the Ottoman Empire directly led to the fall of their monarchies. E.g. The abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II in 1918 ended the German Empire and led to the Weimar Republic.\n\n - Economic collapse and hardship. \n\n     - Massive wartime spending caused inflation, food shortages, and unemployment, undermining public support for governments. In Russia, wartime food shortages and industrial breakdown caused mass unrest in urban centres like Petrograd. This economic crisis was a key factor behind the February Revolution of 1917, which forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate and ended centuries of Romanov rule.\n\n - Revolutionary movements. \n\n     - Discontent sparked socialist and communist revolutions, particularly in states that suffered heavy losses. In 1917, the Russian Revolution overthrew the Tsarist regime and eventually led to the rise of the Bolsheviks.\n\n - Ethnic nationalism and the breakup of empires. \n\n     - The Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman Empires disintegrated as ethnic groups demanded self-determination. The Austro-Hungarian Empire collapsed into separate nation-states like Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, and Hungary.\n\n - Weakening of monarchies. \n\n     - Monarchies were blamed for the war and its devastating effects, leading to their collapse or reduction in power. The collapse of the Romanov dynasty in Russia marked the end of centuries of imperial rule.\n\n - Spread of democratic ideas and republicanism. \n\n     - War weariness and disillusionment with autocracy fostered support for parliamentary systems. The German Empire transitioned into the Weimar Republic, a democratic state, after 1918.\n\n - Failure of postwar settlements to ensure stability. \n\n     - The Treaty of Versailles and other peace treaties created resentment and a political vacuum rather than stability. Hungary lost two-thirds of its territory under the Treaty of Trianon, fueling political instability and irredentism.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWorld War One played a central role in the collapse of several long-standing monarchies ($\\checkmark$). The military defeat of the Central Powers, which included Germany, Austria-Hungary, and the Ottoman Empire, directly triggered the downfall of their monarchies. In Austria, the empire's defeat led to the collapse of the Habsburg monarchy in 1918 and the disintegration of Austria-Hungary into a series of independent nation-states. This marked the end of centuries of imperial rule under the Habsburgs and the creation of the Austrian Republic ($\\checkmark$).\n\nEconomic collapse further undermined political stability. The massive financial burden of prolonged warfare caused inflation, widespread unemployment, and food shortages, weakening public confidence in existing regimes ($\\checkmark$). In Russia, these pressures sparked major unrest in cities like Petrograd, where shortages and industrial breakdown during the war played a major role in the February Revolution of 1917. This forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate and ended the Romanov dynasty, leading to a period of revolution and unrest ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, World War One supercharged ethnic nationalism, particularly within the Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman Empires. As the war drew to a close, numerous ethnic groups within these multi-ethnic empires seized the opportunity to demand independence ($\\checkmark$). This resulted in the break-up of Austria-Hungary into new states such as Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, and Hungary, as well as the dismantling of the Ottoman Empire, further contributing to the collapse of imperial political systems in Europe ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0743","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0743\n\nDescribe changes to the role and status of women in **two** countries as a result of World War Two.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following. Changes to both role and status must be described. \n\n - United States: WWII labour shortages drew over 19 million American women into industrial jobs, from shipyards to aircraft factories, symbolized by $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rosie the Riveter.” This expanded workforce role gave women more independence and made them more visible in public life, fueling calls for gender equality. However, after the war ended, most women were pressured back into domestic life, which slowed progress but planted the seeds for future women's rights movements.\n\n - Soviet Union: Under total war mobilization, Soviet women served as snipers, pilots (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Night Witches”), medics, and factory and farm workers. Women's work in combat and industry during the war briefly raised their social status and showed that gender equality was possible. After the war, many were pushed back into traditional roles, but their efforts were praised as patriotic, supporting government messages about shared sacrifice.\n\n - Britain: With many men conscripted, British women joined the Auxiliary Territorial Service, Women's Land Army, civil defense units, and worked in munitions and transport industries. Their important contributions made them more visible in society and showed they could succeed in non-traditional roles. These wartime experiences helped speed up social changes, including the 1944 Education Act and the development of the welfare state, cementing women's position in post-war society.\n\n - Germany: The Nazis promoted the idea that women were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal but different,” elevating their status as complementary partners in the Volksgemeinschaft. Initially confined by Nazi ideology to “Kinder, Küche, Kirche,” German women were later drafted into weapons factories, auxiliary services, and labour battalions as manpower decreased. This expansion of roles when against the original expectations set by the state. \n\n - France: During occupation, French women engaged in resistance work, acting as couriers, intelligence agents, and sabotage operatives, and worked in factories to sustain the war economy. Their bravery in the Liberation elevated their symbolic status. In 1944, France granted women the vote, directly recognizing their contributions. Post-war, these political rights and increased respect marked a lasting shift in women's social status.\n\n - Japan: Mobilized for total war, Japanese women worked in munitions factories and served as nurses, though many also endured forced sexual slavery as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$comfort women.” Despite this expanded role, pre-war gender norms largely persisted. After surrender, U.S. occupation reforms introduced a new constitution granting women legal equality and political rights, initiating democratic participation. However, traditional views still shaped their status in society. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Nazis advanced the belief that women were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal but different,” portraying them as essential, complementary figures within the Volksgemeinschaft (people's community). ($\\checkmark$) Under Nazi ideology, women were initially encouraged to remain at home, fulfilling the roles of wife, mother, and homemaker (Kinder, Küche, Kirche), a change for many who had previously been employed outside the home. However, as the war intensified and male enlistment drained the workforce, the regime was forced to conscript women into war industries, auxiliary military services, and labour roles. While women's roles expanded during the war, Nazi policies conflicted with these wartime necessities, as they emphasized traditional gender roles. Women's participation in the workforce was begrudgingly accepted but seen as temporary. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nDuring the German occupation, many French women played a critical role in resistance networks, serving as couriers, intelligence agents, and taking up industrial work. The need for resistance forced women into public roles, which was a shift from their pre-war positions ($\\checkmark$). The French Resistance elevated the symbolic status of women, as their contributions to the liberation of France were recognized after the war. In 1944, French women were granted the right to vote, marking a significant political change. This was a major milestone in raising the status of women in French society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0745","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0745\n\nOutline the political repercussions of WWII for **three** countries.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Germany. Defeat led to occupation by Allied powers and eventual division. West Germany became a capitalist democracy (FRG), while East Germany became a Soviet-aligned socialist state (GDR), cementing Cold War divisions.\n\n - Soviet Union. Victory in WWII legitimized Stalin's regime and enabled Soviet domination of Eastern Europe, expanding communist ideology and creating the Eastern Bloc. The war increased the USSR's global influence.\n\n - United States. WWII marked the U.S.'s transition from isolationism to active global leadership, founding the UN and NATO. Domestically, the presidency gained power over foreign and defense policy.\n\n - Japan.The U.S. abolished Japan's military regime and imposed a pacifist constitution in 1947, ending imperial rule and initiating democratic government and economic recovery.\n\n - France. The Vichy regime's collaboration with Nazis discredited existing institutions. After liberation, Charles de Gaulle's leadership helped rebuild a new democratic structure (the Fourth Republic), though instability led to the Fifth Republic in 1958.\n\n - United Kingdom. The cost of war and global realignment sped up the process of decolonization. The Labour Party's victory in 1945 signaled public support for post-war reconstruction and led to the creation of the NHS and other social reforms signalling a political shift toward welfare state policies.\n\n - China. Resumption of civil war, leading to communist victory in 1949. The war with Japan weakened the Nationalist government and allowed Mao's communist forces to gain support and ultimately establish the People's Republic of China.\n\n - Italy. Mussolini's defeat and civil war between fascists and anti-fascists led to the fall of Fascism, the abolition of the monarchy and the creation of a democratic republic following a 1946 referendum.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor the Soviet Union, victory significantly strengthened Stalin's political authority and lifted the USSR's status globally. The Soviet Union emerged as a superpower, using its military presence in Eastern Europe to establish pro-Soviet communist regimes. This led to the formation of the Eastern Bloc and expanded the reach of communist ideology, laying the groundwork for the Cold War. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThe United States shifted from a policy of isolationism to a central role in global affairs. It took the lead in establishing key international institutions such as the United Nations and the NATO. Domestically, the war expanded presidential powers, particularly in areas of defense and foreign policy, setting a precedent for future executive leadership in international matters. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nFollowing its defeat, Japan underwent major political change under American occupation. The US dismantled Japan's militarist government and introduced a new pacifist constitution in 1947, which renounced war and banned the maintenance of armed forces for offensive purposes. The emperor's role became purely symbolic, and Japan was rebuilt as a parliamentary democracy, which helped bring political stability and economic recovery after the war. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0747","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0747\n\nWhat is the message of Source A?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech to Parliament by British Prime Minister David Lloyd George titled “NOT VENGEANCE—DISCOURAGEMENT.” Given 3 July 1919. |\n\n:box[But it is said, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Are you not punishing Germany for the crime of her rulers?\" Well, I am sorry to have to answer this, but I must. If Germany had been committed to this War against the will of her people, I say at once we ought to have taken that into account in the terms of peace. But was that so? . . . The nation approved, the nation applauded; the nation had been taught to approve and to applaud. . . . It was an enthusiasm which was at its highest when German troops were marching through Belgium. Supposing that the German Chancellor had returned from the Peace Conference with a Germanic Peace—Belgium added to the Fatherland, the mines of Briey added to the mineral wealth of the Rhineland, the British Fleet surrendered. . . . the British Dependencies added to the German Colonies, huge indemnities imposed upon France and Great Britain for a war they never provoked. . . .\\\n\\\nThat was what Germany was after. But suppose these things had happened. . . . The German people as a whole [would have received] these terms with delirious joy. I should have been glad had it been possible to say that this was a war which had been entered into against the will of the German people. But it was not, and therefore it is essential that nations must know, if they enter into unprovoked wars of aggression . . . what may lie in store for them when defeat falls upon their arms. I therefore have no hesitation in challenging anyone, either inside [or] outside this House, to point to a single Clause in this Treaty that is not in accordance with the stern and highest demands of justice and fair play.\n]\n\n[Source: Lloyd George, D. (1919, July 3). *Not vengeance-Discouragement.* HC Deb 03 July 1919 vol 117 cc1218-22. Hansard. Retrieved April 29, 2025 from\nhttps://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1919/jul/03/not-vengeance-discouragement#S5CV0117P0_19190703_HOC_328. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Germany was widely supported by its own people in starting the war. “The nation approved, the nation applauded; the nation had been taught to approve and to applaud.”\n- Treaty of Versailles is justified because Germany would have imposed worse terms if it had won. “ the British Fleet surrendered... the British Dependencies added to the German Colonies”\n- The German people cannot be separated from the actions of their leaders. “I should have been glad had it been possible to say that this was a war which had been entered into against the will of the German people. But it was not.”\n- Harsh consequences are necessary to discourage future aggression.\n “It is essential that nations must know, if they enter into unprovoked wars of aggression... what may lie in store for them when defeat falls upon their arms.”\n- The treaty reflects justice, not revenge. “I… challenge anyone… to point to a single Clause in this Treaty that is not in accordance with the stern and highest demands of justice and fair play.”\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of Source A is that the Treaty of Versailles was justified, reflecting “justice and fair play”, rather than being an act of revenge ($\\checkmark$). Lloyd George further argues that the German people, not just their leaders, supported the war: “The nation approved, the nation applauded” and so deserved to face the consequences of defeat ($\\checkmark$). Finally, he warns that nations “if they enter into unprovoked wars of aggression” should expect harsh treatment ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0748","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0748\n\nTo what extent does the message of Source A reflect contemporary reactions to the Treaty of Versailles?\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech to Parliament by British Prime Minister David Lloyd George titled “NOT VENGEANCE—DISCOURAGEMENT.” Given 3 July 1919. |\n\n:box[But it is said, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Are you not punishing Germany for the crime of her rulers?\" Well, I am sorry to have to answer this, but I must. If Germany had been committed to this War against the will of her people, I say at once we ought to have taken that into account in the terms of peace. But was that so? . . . The nation approved, the nation applauded; the nation had been taught to approve and to applaud. . . . It was an enthusiasm which was at its highest when German troops were marching through Belgium. Supposing that the German Chancellor had returned from the Peace Conference with a Germanic Peace—Belgium added to the Fatherland, the mines of Briey added to the mineral wealth of the Rhineland, the British Fleet surrendered. . . . the British Dependencies added to the German Colonies, huge indemnities imposed upon France and Great Britain for a war they never provoked. . . .\\\n\\\nThat was what Germany was after. But suppose these things had happened. . . . The German people as a whole [would have received] these terms with delirious joy. I should have been glad had it been possible to say that this was a war which had been entered into against the will of the German people. But it was not, and therefore it is essential that nations must know, if they enter into unprovoked wars of aggression . . . what may lie in store for them when defeat falls upon their arms. I therefore have no hesitation in challenging anyone, either inside [or] outside this House, to point to a single Clause in this Treaty that is not in accordance with the stern and highest demands of justice and fair play.\n]\n\n[Source: Lloyd George, D. (1919, July 3). *Not vengeance-Discouragement.* HC Deb 03 July 1919 vol 117 cc1218-22. Hansard. Retrieved April 29, 2025 from\nhttps://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1919/jul/03/not-vengeance-discouragement#S5CV0117P0_19190703_HOC_328. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- The response uses the source effectively as evidence and refers to it clearly.\n- The response uses accurate and relevant “own knowledge” as evidence to support arguments alongside source knowledge.\n- Both the source and own knowledge must be used. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of four marks.\n- Effective synthesis of own knowledge and source material to receive the full marks.\n- To receive full marks, the response must contain examples that support the policies and claims, as well as examples that do not. These do not need to be evenly balanced.\n- To receive full marks, the response should argue to a reasoned conclusion based on the evidence presented.\n\nPossible evidence from Source A:\n- The treaty reflects justice, not revenge.\n- Harsh consequences are necessary to discourage future aggression.\n\nPossible arguments from own knowledge:\n- Reactions consistent with the Source:\n    - Many in Britain supported punishing Germany harshly. Lloyd George had won the 1918 election with the slogan: “Make Germany Pay.”\n    - French Prime Minister Georges Clemenceau pushed for a tough treaty, arguing Germany must be weakened to prevent future wars: “Germany must be made to pay for the damage she has done.”\n    - U.S. Senator Henry Cabot Lodge, although critical of the League of Nations, supported strong measures against Germany: “The German people must feel the consequences of the war they supported.”\n    - Popular British newspapers (e.g., The Times) largely echoed Lloyd George's sentiment that the German people were complicit and must face consequences.\n\n- Reactions inconsistent with the Source:\n    - Thought the Treaty too harsh:\n        - U.S. President Woodrow Wilson promoted a more lenient and idealistic approach, especially with his Fourteen Points: “Peace without victory.”\n        - British economist John Maynard Keynes in The Economic Consequences of the Peace (1919), warned that the treaty was too harsh and would lead to economic collapse and future conflict: “The treaty includes no provisions for the economic rehabilitation of Europe.”\n        - Germans saw the treaty as a national humiliation. The War Guilt Clause (Article 231) especially enraged them, as they felt they were being unfairly blamed: “A diktat, not a peace.”\n        - Some British MPs, intellectuals and media saw the treaty as overly vindictive and short-sighted, risking future instability rather than securing peace.\n        - David Lloyd George. Despite defending the Treaty of Versailles in his 1919 speech, Lloyd George later expressed concerns that the treaty was too harsh and might lead to future conflict. “We shall have to revise the Treaty. There will be no peace in Europe if Germany is to be kept permanently crippled... this Treaty, as it stands, will bring war within a generation.”\n    - Thought the treaty too lenient\n        - Marshal Ferdinand Foch (French military leader) said the treaty was “not a peace. It is an armistice for twenty years,” believing it failed to weaken Germany enough to prevent another war.\n        - Many French civilians, veterans and politicians believed the treaty failed to fully dismantle Germany's military and industrial power and wanted stronger guarantees of French security.\n        - Winston Churchill (British politician and wartime minister), though he supported harsh terms, he felt the treaty didn't go far enough in breaking German militarism and could lead to future danger.\n        - Belgian Government felt betrayed by the treaty, as Belgium received little in the way of reparations or territorial gain, despite suffering early invasion and occupation.\n        - Some British media (e.g., the Morning Post) argued the treaty allowed Germany to remain too strong and that a future war was inevitable.\n        - Polish nationalists and leaders believed the treaty failed to secure Poland's eastern borders and allowed Germany to remain a future threat to Polish independence.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- The strongest answers are likely to argue that the Source did reflect some reactions, but is not fully consistent with most contemporary reactions.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A reflects some contemporary reactions to the Treaty of Versailles. The source presents the treaty as a just and necessary punishment rather than an act of revenge, arguing that “nations must know, if they enter into unprovoked wars of aggression, what may lie in store for them when.” This reflects a widely held view in Britain at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{source}$). Lloyd George himself had won the 1918 election promising to “Make Germany Pay,” and many British citizens saw harsh terms as justified ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). Similarly, U.S. Senator Henry Cabot Lodge supported a tough approach, believing the German people needed to face the consequences of the war they had supported ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$).\n\nHowever, other significant voices challenged this view. U.S. President Woodrow Wilson, author of the Fourteen Points, promoted a more forgiving peace, warning against imposing humiliating conditions that might breed resentment. His call for a “peace without victory” directly contrasts with the speech's insistence that Germany's national enthusiasm for the war justifies the treaty's severity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). Moreover, Lloyd George himself later contradicted the message of his 1919 speech, warning that “this Treaty, as it stands, will bring war within a generation,” showing his growing concern that the treaty's terms were too harsh and potentially destabilizing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). In addition, some believed the treaty was not strict enough. French military leader Marshal Foch stated that the treaty failed to ensure lasting security by not sufficiently weakening Germany ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). Polish leaders also felt the treaty did not do enough to guarantee national safety ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Source A reflects the views of those who saw the treaty as fair punishment, especially in Britain, it does not represent the full range of contemporary reactions. There were significant voices calling for both a more lenient and a more severe peace, making the source only partially reflective of the wider international response to the Treaty of Versailles ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0749","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0749\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Resolution to parliament by Herbert Samuel (former Home Secretary), motion to allow women to be eligible as Members of Parliament, 23 October 1918. |\n\n[Source: Samuel, H. (1918, 23 October). *Women As Members Of Parliament.* HC Deb 03 July 1919 vol 110 cc814-5. Hansard. Retrieved April 31, 2025 from https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/1918-10-23/debates/c5284686-3207-4f38-9119-906afe488f00/WomenAsMembersOfParliament. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The idea that women are inferior or secondary to men is being challenged: society has “given up the old theory that woman's interests were not on a level with man's.”\n- Women's political engagement has evolved: “The War, however, has brought great changes.”\n- As a result of the war, women have gained public and political awareness: “it has made women familiar with many aspects of public life.”\n- Women have become engaged with “industrial employment, prices, international politics,” suggesting they are equipped to participate in governance.\n- The war has shifted public opinion in women’s favour: “many of us have been ready during the War to modify our previous view,” showing that the war catalyzed support for women's political rights.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the source argues that traditional views of women as inferior to men are being rejected, by saying that many had “given up the old theory that woman's interests were not on a level with man's\" ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, it emphasizes that World War I brought “great changes,” helping women become more engaged in politics and public life ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the source argues that, as a result of their wartime experiences, women gained greater awareness of national issues and proved their capability beyond the domestic sphere, saying “it has made women familiar with many aspects of public life”($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0750","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0750\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for an historian studying changes to the role and status of women as a result of World War One.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Resolution to parliament by Herbert Samuel (former Home Secretary), motion to allow women to be eligible as Members of Parliament, 23 October 1918. |\n\n[Source: Samuel, H. (1918, 23 October). *Women As Members Of Parliament.* HC Deb 03 July 1919 vol 110 cc814-5. Hansard. Retrieved April 31, 2025 from https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/1918-10-23/debates/c5284686-3207-4f38-9119-906afe488f00/WomenAsMembersOfParliament. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The origin is valuable as:\n    - It is delivered by Herbert Samuel, a senior British politician (former Home Secretary) and key policymaker during WWI.\n    - The speech is dated October 1918, during the final weeks of World War I, providing a contemporary view of attitudes as the war concluded.\n    - It is an official parliamentary debate, the source offers insight into how government leaders assessed the changing status of women due to the war.\n- The purpose is valuable as:\n    - It is intended to justify women's right to stand as MPs, so it openly discusses the transformation of women's roles during the war.\n    - It offers a clear political acknowledgement that the war changed societal perceptions of women's capabilities and contributions.\n    - It demonstrates that wartime service influenced legislative reform, making it valuable for tracing connections between the war and shifts in women's status.\n- The content is valuable as:\n    - It acknowledges pre-war gender inequality (“woman was not a noun but an adjective”), showing how far perceptions had shifted.\n    - It describes how war drew women into public life and new responsibilities, “industrial employment, prices, international politics,” highlighting expanded roles.\n    - It reflects the growing political agency of women due to wartime contributions, connecting social roles to political rights.\n    - It could be useful for showing how elite male politicians interpreted and responded to women's wartime involvement.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The origin is limited as:\n    - It focuses on political eligibility post-war rather than on the full range of women's wartime roles (e.g. nursing, munitions work).\n    - It represents an elite political opinion, not the views or lived experiences of ordinary women or working-class voices.\n    - It provides a British context only; does not provide a wider European perspective on women in WWI.\n- The purpose is limited as:\n    - It is persuasive speech, it may overstate progress to win support for a political motion.\n    - It focuses more on political enfranchisement than on the daily realities or hardships faced by women during the war.\n    - It may exclude counter arguments or dissenting views, leading to a one-sided portrayal.\n- The content is limited as\n    - It lacks specific examples of roles women undertook (e.g. nursing, factory work, voluntary services).\n    - It does not mention women's struggles, unequal pay, dangerous working conditions, or opposition to their involvement.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is valuable because it comes from a speech delivered in October 1918, near the end of World War I. This timing gives historians a useful contemporary perspective on how attitudes toward women were shifting as the war drew to a close ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose also adds value, as the speech supports legal reform, directly linking women's wartime contributions to political change, and helps illustrate how their role in society was being reconsidered ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. The origin offers insight only into the British experience, without reflecting the broader European context or differences in women's roles across other nations during the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). In terms of content, the speech remains general and does not provide concrete examples of the specific jobs or responsibilities women took on, such as work in munitions factories, military nursing, or volunteer organizations, limiting its usefulness for understanding the full scope of their wartime roles ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0751","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0751\n\nDiscuss the impact of one twentieth-century war on the role of women in **two** countries. [15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nCandidates must demonstrate a clear understanding of the question’s demands. In this case, candidates should offer a considered and balanced review of the ways in which women's roles and/or status changed, and the reasons behind those changes, as a result of two twentieth-century wars.\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n- Clearly address how women's roles and/or status changed during and after each war. Students could choose to look at both roles and status, just roles or status, or role in one war and status in another.\n- Identify and explain the reasons for these changes.\n- Evaluate the extent and nature of change, including any limitations or reversals of progress after the wars, showing an understanding of short-term vs. long-term impacts.\n- Use a well-structured argument with relevant historical examples and differing perspectives integrated into the answer.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- World War I (1914-1918). In countries like Britain and France, women gained employment in munitions factories, public transport, and nursing roles as men went to the front. This challenged traditional gender norms and increased public visibility of women's work. In Britain, their wartime contributions were part of the reason women over 30 gained the vote in 1918.\n- World War II (1939-1945). WWII expanded women's roles even further. In the US, women joined the workforce in large numbers (symbolized by “Rosie the Riveter”) and served in auxiliary military roles. Soviet women served as snipers, pilots, and medics. The war elevated women's status temporarily, but most returned to domestic roles post-war, though the experience helped inspire postwar feminist movements.\n- Chinese Civil War (1927-1949). Women's roles changed significantly in Communist-held areas. The Chinese Communist Party promoted gender equality in theory, encouraging women to take part in land reform, guerrilla warfare, and party organization. However, traditional roles remained strong, and many reforms were more symbolic than fully transformative until later under Mao.\n- Vietnam War (1955-1975). In North Vietnam, women were heavily involved in both military and logistical efforts. The Viet Cong used women as spies, fighters, and transport workers along the Ho Chi Minh Trail. Propaganda celebrated women's contributions, yet their status post-war did not improve substantially, and traditional roles reasserted themselves after reunification.\n- Algerian War of Independence (1954-1962). Women played an important role in Algeria's anti-colonial struggle, especially in urban guerrilla warfare, carrying weapons and planting bombs for the FLN. Though praised for their bravery, the post-independence government sidelined them politically, and their legal and social status did not see major improvements.\n- Spanish Civil War (1936-1939). On the Republican side, women briefly took up roles in militias and civil society. Feminist groups gained visibility, and some women fought on the front lines. However, after Franco's victory, women's rights were rolled back severely, and their social status became tied once again to motherhood and domesticity under authoritarian rule.\n- Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905). This war had little direct impact on women's roles, as it was fought largely by professional male soldiers. While it contributed to political unrest in Russia that eventually led to the 1905 revolution, there was no significant shift in women's status or employment during the war itself.\n- Iran-Iraq War (1980-1988). In Iran, women were mobilized in support roles such as nursing, logistics, and civil defense. The war also reinforced the regime's ideological messaging about women's roles within an Islamic framework. While their participation was notable, it did not lead to increased rights or public status; rather, the regime maintained tight control over gender norms.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe First World War significantly altered the role and status of women in both Europe and the Pacific. These changes were primarily driven by the need to replace male labor in industry, agriculture, and other public sectors during wartime. Although some of these changes were temporary, the war helped shift public perceptions of women's capabilities and led to lasting social and political change, particularly in countries like Britain and Australia ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nIn Europe, particularly Britain, WWI created urgent demand for labor as millions of men were conscripted. This led to large-scale recruitment of women into roles previously reserved for men. By 1918, over one million British women were employed in munitions factories under the Ministry of Munitions. Known as “munitionettes,” these women performed dangerous work and were vital to the war effort. They also entered jobs in transport, government clerical roles, and agriculture. This unprecedented expansion into the workforce not only challenged traditional gender roles but also began to shift societal attitudes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Though many of these women were pushed out of jobs after the war, their wartime contributions were widely recognized. For instance, the Representation of the People Act 1918 granted the vote to women over 30 in Britain, partly due to their visible wartime service ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This marked a significant step forward in women's political status ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn addition to employment, the war in Europe altered women's self-perception. The experience of economic independence and social contribution led many women to question the limitations placed on them pre-war. Women's organizations such as the Women's Social and Political Union (WSPU) temporarily suspended suffrage campaigns during the war, supporting national unity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). After the war, however, their efforts resumed with renewed strength, and the wartime sacrifices were used as powerful arguments for equality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The war thus helped link female citizenship with national service, reinforcing calls for suffrage and social recognition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn the Pacific, particularly Australia, the war had similar impacts, though on a smaller scale. As Australian men enlisted in large numbers, with over 400,000 serving overseas, women stepped in to fill gaps in the workforce. Women worked in clerical roles, manufacturing, and especially in nursing, with the Australian Army Nursing Service (AANS) playing a key role. Around 2,300 nurses served overseas in Egypt, France, and other war zones ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Though fewer women worked in heavy industry compared to Britain, the visibility of their contributions increased significantly. Women also formed patriotic groups like the Australian Women's National League, which raised funds and supported troops abroad ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These roles reinforced women's capacity to contribute to national efforts and challenged the domestic limitations traditionally placed on them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nAdditionally, Australia saw political shifts influenced by wartime gender roles. Unlike Britain, Australian women already had the right to vote federally since 1902, but the war further strengthened their participation in civic life. For instance, women actively engaged in public debates over conscription during two national referenda in 1916 and 1917  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Though conscription was defeated both times, women's participation in public discourse reinforced their political agency. The war also raised awareness of women's capacity for leadership and organization, laying the groundwork for future reforms in areas such as employment and education ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, it's important to recognize that in both Europe and Australia, many wartime changes were temporary. After the war, returning soldiers were prioritized for employment, and many women were forced out of their wartime jobs. Governments encouraged a return to traditional domestic roles through propaganda and policy. For example, in Britain, the Restoration of Pre-War Practices Act (1919) required women to leave jobs so men could return. Similarly, in Australia, women's wartime roles were not sustained in the post-war labor force ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Despite these reversals, the long-term impact was evident ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The war had exposed women's abilities in public life, expanded their social roles, and laid the foundation for further rights movements in the 20th century ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).\n\nIn conclusion, WWI brought significant, if uneven, changes to the role and status of women in both Europe and the Pacific. In Britain, women's expanded workforce roles and political recognition marked a turning point in gender equality. In Australia, women's contributions in nursing, fundraising, and civic engagement strengthened their public visibility and confirmed their political importance. While many gains were rolled back after the war, the conflict helped reshape societal views of gender and laid the groundwork for future progress in women's rights and status  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1245","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1245 \n\nExamine the political effects of **two** wars, each chosen from a different region.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- All wars have political consequences. Students must choose two wars from different world regions and clearly explain the connections between the wars and the political situation that followed.\n\n- Some possible examples include:\n    -  **First World War (1914-1918):** The First World War caused the collapse of the German, Austro-Hungarian, Ottoman, and Russian empires. These empires broke up into independent republics. Due to the harsh realities of the war and the punishments imposed by the peace treaties, many of these republics, including the Russian and German ones, went through a period of civil war and political instability.\n    - **Second World War (1939-1945):** The Second World War led to the occupation of Germany and Japan, dismantling their existing political systems. In the case of Germany and Korea, they were divided into two new countries, each representing the opposing sides of the Cold War. In Japan, its political system was modified by limiting the emperor's power to a symbolic role.\n    - **Vietnam War (1955-1975):** The Vietnam War resulted in the collapse of South Vietnam and the establishment of a unified communist state. In the United States, the war caused immediate political division and declining public trust in government leadership.\n    - **Spanish Civil War (1936-1939):** The Spanish Civil War ended with the victory of Francisco Franco over the Second Spanish Republic and the establishment of an authoritarian dictatorship. Political opponents were repressed or exiled.\n    - **Iran-Iraq War (1980-1988):** In Iran, the conflict strengthened the Islamic Republic by consolidating the power of Ayatollah Khomeini, who used the conflict as an excuse to eliminate opposition to his rule. The same can be said for Saddam Hussein in Iraq and his increased repression of dissenters.\n    - **Chinese Civil War (1927-1949):** The war led to the control by the Chinese Communist Party, led by Mao Zedong, over the People's Republic of China.  The Guomindang escaped to the island of Taiwan, where it established a one-party state under the leadership of Chiang Kai-shek. The two parties would seek recognition as the legitimate government of all of China. This was a key issue in Cold War tensions with the Communist bloc recognizing the government set up in Beijing and the anti-communist countries recognizing the government from Taipei.\n\n**Indicative content for the Falklands/Malvinas War (1982) and the Russian Civil War (1917-1922):**\n\nFalklands/Malvinas War\n- For Argentina…\n    - The collapse of the dictatorship of the military Junta and a return to democracy. The defeat was the last straw for a regime that entered the fight to distract the population from the government's other failings.\n    - The war would motivate a peaceful resolution of the territorial disputes with Chile in 1984.\n    - The war ended decades of the Argentine Armed Forces` supervision of or direct control over the government.\n- For the United Kingdom…\n    - The strengthening of the government of Margaret Thatcher, who boosted her popularity through her handling of the war. This allowed her to be reelected as Prime Minister in 1983.\n- For both: Diplomatic relations between the two countries remained severed until the Madrid Accords of 1989 and 1990.\n\nRussian Civil War\n- Victory of the Bolsheviks over rival political parties. All other parties were eliminated, and the USSR became a one-party state.\n- Creation of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR) to give the appearance of self-determination to some of the different nations within, but in reality as a means of centralizing all power in Moscow.\n- The war legitimized the use of violence and repression to eliminate opposition.\n- Trotsky's role in the war put him in a favorable position for the struggle for power that followed Lenin's death.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWars are constants in history that affect most, if not all, aspects of a nation's development. This essay will focus on the political effects of two wars, the  Malvinas War (1982) between Argentina and the United Kingdom, and the Russian Civil War (1917-1922) between the Bolsheviks and the Whites (a loose alliance of all anti-Bolshevik forces in Russia)  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While the defeat of Argentina in the war accelerated the collapse of its military dictatorship and facilitated a return to democratic rule, the Russian Civil War enabled the Bolsheviks to consolidate their power and establish a one-party state based on centralization and repression of dissidents ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). Together, these cases demonstrate how wars can act either as catalysts for political liberalization or as foundations for long-term authoritarian control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nIn Argentina, the Malvinas War led to the collapse of the military dictatorship and a return to democracy. The ruling Junta had launched the war partly as a nationalist distraction from economic crisis, repression, and declining legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, defeat by the United Kingdom in June 1982 destroyed the regime's credibility and triggered widespread public protests like the ones that took place on June 15, a day after Argentina surrendered ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This loss of legitimacy forced the resignation of de facto president Galtieri, who was even removed from the Armed Forces. His successor, Bignone, decreed that democratic and clean elections would be held in October of the following year ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These elections ended seven years of military dictatorship.\n\nHowever, unlike other times in Argentina's history when a military dictatorship would be followed by civilian rule supervised by the armed forces, the Malvinas War would have such a negative impact on their reputation that the military never again had an accepted role in government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This was especially so because of the report, commissioned by the same armed forces to investigate what had gone wrong in the conflict. Known as the Rattenbach Report, it established that the military junta was guilty of driving the nation to a war for which it was not prepared and of handling it in an unprofessional manner. In short, it blamed the armed forces themselves for the defeat and the negative consequences it had on the economic and social fabric of the nation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This was the last straw for a population that had already seen their terrible handling of the economy and continued human rights abuses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Evidence of the end of the power of the Argentine military was the trial of the junta under the government of Alfonsín, where the leaders were sentenced to prison terms ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). President Alfonsín faced three military uprisings while attempting to hold Argentina's military leaders accountable for their crimes. However, the wide support of the Argentine population for this accountability proved that there was no going back to military control. Defeat in war ended up having positive consequences for Argentina's political stability while hurting the power of the institution that started it ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nOn the other hand, in the case of the Russian Civil War, the Bolshevik victory consolidated the Party's power, leading to political stability but also to the end of democracy in the country until the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991. Since other political parties joined the Whites, the Bolsheviks successfully sought to eliminate them. This was first achieved in 1918 with the closure of the Constituent Assembly ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This democratically elected assembly was supposed to organize the future government of Russia and had the Socialist Revolutionaries in a clear majority. However, it was only allowed to meet for one day. The Civil War was a perfect excuse to portray all opposition to the Bolsheviks as an opposition to the Revolution. For example, when the Kronstadt Rebellion of 1921 demanded a return to a “workers' democracy” as in the first months of 1917, Trotsky labelled it as a plot by the Whites and proceeded to suppress it ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This rebellion forced Lenin to realize the fragility of the regime's support among the peasantry and workers and thus the need to centralize decision-making even further ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The little democracy that existed inside the Bolshevik party itself was also eliminated with the 1921 ban on factions decided by the 10th Party Congress ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This ensured that decisions would not be discussed by anyone outside the Party's top leadership positions, solidifying Bolshevik power.\n\nThe Russian Civil War further centralized control over the country, culminating in the creation of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR) in 1922. This was a way to appear to grant self-determination to the various nationalities within the former Russian Empire while, at the same time, centralizing real authority in Moscow ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). At the start of the Civil War, to garner the support of the different nationalities, the Bolsheviks had promised the right of the peoples of Russia to self-determination, including secession ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, part of the Southern front during the civil war saw the Bolsheviks fighting in the Ukrainian War of Independence, denying the Ukrainian People's Republic's right to secede ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In practice, the federal system established in 1922 was designed to provide a feeling of autonomy (constitutions, flags, local ministries) that concealed the absolute power of the centralized Communist Party in Moscow ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Moreover, the experience of civil war legitimized violence and repression. The Red Terror and its persecution of perceived opponents through the Cheka (secret police), counted 200,000 victims by 1921 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).Censorship and executions were tools to win the civil war, but they became accepted practices of the Soviet state in the long term.  The experiences of the Civil War either forced or allowed the Bolsheviks to centralize control over the people of Russia for years to come ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the Malvinas War and the Russian Civil War illustrate how war can act as a turning point for a country's government system. In Argentina, military defeat shattered the legitimacy of an already weakened authoritarian regime, permanently discrediting the Armed Forces and enabling a sustained transition to democracy. By contrast, Bolshevik victory in the Russian Civil War allowed the regime to equate political opposition with counterrevolution, using the conditions of war to justify repression, eliminate pluralism, and institutionalize centralized one-party rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). While both wars brought political stability, the form that stability took differed fundamentally: democratic consolidation in Argentina versus authoritarian entrenchment in Soviet Russia ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). These cases demonstrate that whoever wins the war will strengthen their political position and the loser will see it weakened, both in the short and in the long term ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1475","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1475\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Peacemaking after 20th-century wars created more problems than it solved.\" Discuss with reference to two wars, each chosen from a different region.[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n \n\n**General Guidance:**\n\n- Strong answers should clearly address the question by evaluating whether peacemaking after the two selected wars created more problems than it solved. \n\n- Responses should analyse both the successes and limitations of the peace settlements, considering the extent to which they resolved existing problems or gave rise to new ones. Answers that rely on lengthy description of the terms of the peace settlements, rather than evaluating their impact, are unlikely to score well. \n\n- The strongest answers will maintain a running comparison of the effectiveness of peacemaking following the two selected wars, supporting their arguments with specific and relevant evidence and assessing both the short- and long-term consequences of the settlements. \n\n- Conclusions will vary depending on the wars selected, but all should arrive at a balanced, well-supported judgement on the extent to which peacemaking created more problems than it solved.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n\n- **First World War (1914-1918)**\n\n    - Responses should consider the extent to which the Paris Peace Settlement, including the Treaty of Versailles, successfully addressed the political, territorial and economic issues arising from the First World War. Candidates may examine the impact of territorial adjustments, disarmament, reparations and the creation of the League of Nations in promoting peace and stability, while also considering criticisms such as resentment among the defeated powers, unresolved territorial disputes, economic instability and the growth of revisionist movements.\n\n- **Second World War in the Pacific (1941-1945)**\n\n    - Responses should consider the extent to which peacemaking following the Second World War in the Pacific successfully addressed the political, territorial and economic consequences of the conflict in Asia. Candidates may examine the Allied occupation and reconstruction of Japan, constitutional and political reforms, the San Francisco Peace Treaty, the restoration of Japanese sovereignty and the promotion of regional stability, while also considering unresolved territorial disputes, the division of Korea, the Chinese Civil War, the growth of Cold War tensions in Asia and the legacy of Japanese wartime actions.\n\n- **Iran-Iraq War (1980-1988)**\n\n    - Responses should consider the extent to which peacemaking following the Iran-Iraq War resolved the political, territorial and military issues that had fuelled the conflict. Candidates may examine the effectiveness of the United Nations' role, including UN Security Council Resolution 598, in ending the conflict and restoring the pre-war status quo through ceasefire arrangements, the exchange of prisoners of war and the eventual acceptance of existing borders, while also considering the enormous economic costs of the conflict, continued hostility between Iran and Iraq and the wider impact of the war on regional stability. Responses should assess both the immediate and longer-term consequences of peacemaking before reaching a judgement on whether it created more problems than it solved.\n\n- **Indo-Pakistan Wars (1965 and 1971)**\n\n    - Responses should consider the extent to which peacemaking following the Indo-Pakistan Wars of 1965 and 1971 resolved the political, territorial and military issues that had contributed to conflict. Candidates may examine the effectiveness of the Tashkent Declaration (1966) and the Simla Agreement (1972) in restoring diplomatic relations, facilitating the return of territory and prisoners of war and establishing frameworks for the peaceful resolution of disputes, while also considering the continuing dispute over Kashmir, ongoing political and military rivalry, cross-border tensions and the regional consequences of the creation of Bangladesh.\n\n- **Irish War of Independence (1919-1921)**\n\n    - Responses should consider the extent to which peacemaking following the Irish War of Independence resolved the political and constitutional issues that had contributed to the conflict. Candidates may examine the effectiveness of the Anglo-Irish Treaty (1921) in establishing the Irish Free State, granting dominion status and reducing violence, while also considering the impact of partition, the oath of allegiance to the British Crown, the continued status of Northern Ireland and the divisions that led to the Irish Civil War.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n $\\\\$\n\n \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n \n\nPeacemaking after major wars is intended to resolve the causes of conflict and establish conditions for long-term stability. However, the success of peace settlements has varied depending on the aims and methods adopted by the victorious powers ($\\checkmark$ focus). The contrasting experiences of the First World War and the Second World War in the Pacific demonstrate that peacemaking could either intensify instability or encourage recovery and cooperation ($\\checkmark$ organization). The Treaty of Versailles and wider Paris Peace Settlement contributed to resentment and instability in Germany and Europe. In contrast, peacemaking after the Second World War in the Pacific focused more on reconstruction, political reform, and integration, particularly through the transformation of Japan ($\\checkmark$ organization). Therefore, while peacemaking after the First World War created significant problems that contributed to future conflict, the experience of the Pacific War demonstrates that settlements could create lasting stability when they addressed the needs of both security and reconstruction ($\\checkmark$ focus).\\\n\n\\\n\nThe differing aims of post-war settlements were central to their outcomes, as the restrictive, security-focused approach after the First World War contrasted sharply with the reconstruction and reintegration strategy adopted in the Pacific after 1945 ($\\checkmark$ focus). Following the First World War, the Allied powers sought both to prevent future German aggression and to establish a new European order, but many of the measures adopted were perceived as punitive by Germany. The Treaty of Versailles imposed territorial losses, military restrictions, and reparations, while Article 231 placed responsibility for the war on Germany. Although these measures aimed to protect European security, they contributed to resentment among Germans who viewed the settlement as a national humiliation ($\\checkmark$ analysis). By contrast, after the Second World War in the Pacific, the Allied occupation of Japan sought to eliminate militarism while creating a stable democratic state. The United States-led occupation introduced political reforms, economic reconstruction, and a new relationship between Japan and the international community ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). Therefore, the consequences of peacemaking differed because Versailles attempted to secure peace largely through restriction, whereas the Pacific settlement sought stability through transformation ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\\\n\n\\\n\nThe political consequences of peacemaking further demonstrate that settlements based on exclusion and punishment were less effective than those focused on integration and cooperation ($\\checkmark$ focus). After the First World War, the creation of the League of Nations represented an attempt to establish a new international system based on collective security. However, its effectiveness was limited by the initial exclusion of defeated nations, the absence of the United States, weaknesses in enforcement, and the reluctance of major powers to act decisively against aggression in the 1930s. Furthermore, dissatisfaction with the settlement contributed to the growth of revisionist movements, particularly the rise of Hitler and the Nazi Party, who promised to overturn Versailles ($\\checkmark$ analysis). In contrast, political reform in Japan after 1945 produced greater stability. The 1947 constitution established parliamentary democracy, expanded civil rights, and limited the influence of the military. Unlike Germany after 1919, Japan was gradually reintegrated into the international community and became a major ally of the United States during the Cold War ($\\checkmark$ analysis). Thus, while both settlements attempted to prevent future conflict, the political settlement after the Pacific War was more successful because it encouraged acceptance of the new order rather than creating widespread opposition to it ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\\\n\n\\\n\nHowever, neither settlement completely eliminated international tensions, demonstrating that even effective peacemaking could leave unresolved problems ($\\checkmark$ focus). The territorial changes following the First World War attempted to apply self-determination but often created new disputes by leaving significant minority populations dissatisfied. Germans living outside Germany, particularly in areas such as the Sudetenland, later became a source of tension exploited by Hitler ($\\checkmark$ examples). Similarly, peacemaking in the Pacific left unresolved issues that contributed to later instability in Asia. The division of Korea after the Japanese defeat created competing communist and non-communist states and contributed to the outbreak of the Korean War. In addition, unresolved territorial issues, particularly the Kuril Islands dispute and tensions surrounding Taiwan, demonstrated that the post-war settlement did not eliminate all regional conflicts ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). However, whereas unresolved issues after the First World War largely reflected weaknesses within the peace settlement itself, many of the tensions that emerged in East Asia were increasingly shaped by the wider Cold War context ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\\\n\n\\\n\nThe economic consequences of peacemaking also reveal important differences between the two settlements ($\\checkmark$ focus). The reparations imposed on Germany after the First World War contributed to economic difficulties and became a powerful political issue exploited by opponents of democracy, particularly during the economic crises of the 1920s and 1930s ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). Although economic instability was not caused solely by Versailles, the settlement provided a focus for resentment and weakened confidence in the Weimar Republic. By contrast, economic recovery was central to the post-war settlement in Japan. Occupation reforms and economic assistance helped Japan rebuild its industrial economy and develop into a prosperous democratic state ($\\checkmark$ perspectives). This signifies that peacemaking was more successful when it created conditions for recovery rather than reinforcing grievances among defeated powers ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\\\n\n\\\n\nThe consequences of peacemaking, therefore, depended more on the nature of the settlement imposed and the extent to which it addressed the underlying causes of conflict and created conditions for lasting stability($\\checkmark$ focus). The Treaty of Versailles created significant problems because its emphasis on restrictions and punishment contributed to resentment, instability, and revisionist ambitions, leading to the outbreak of the Second World War. In contrast, the settlement after the Pacific War was more successful because it combined demilitarisation with reconstruction and political integration ($\\checkmark$ perspectives). Therefore, peacemaking was most likely to create further problems when it sought to manage defeat, as with the Paris peace treaties after the First World War, rather than to establish a sustainable basis for cooperation, as the San Francisco Peace Treaty and the preceding occupation reforms did in the Pacific ($\\checkmark$ conclusion).\n\n \n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:-:|\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus) |\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge) |\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark$ analysis) |\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion) |"}]},{"name":"Practices & Their Impact on Outcome","questions":[{"ref":"HI0248","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0248\n\nExplain **three** similarities in the impact of the airplane on the outcome of WWI and WWII.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of at least **three** of the below with supporting details, examples and/or explanation:\n- In both conflicts, new technology was developed which increased the importance of aircraft to the war's outcome.\n    - WWI: interrupter gear, which allowed  machine guns to be fired forward through the propeller\n    - WWII: Camera technology was greatly improved, making photo-reconnaissance aircraft more effective.\n- In both conflicts, airplanes were used for surveillance\n    -  WWI: airplanes were vital because they could both prevent enemy reconnaissance and gather information about the enemy.\n    - WWII: Aircraft were used to identify troop movements and targets for bombing.\n- Aircraft were used extensively by all main actors in both conflicts.\n    - During WWI, this included the British Royal Flying Corps, the Imperial German Army Air Service, and the French Air Service, which many considered to be the world's first air force.\n    - The Royal Air Force and the Luffwaffe being the most important in the European theater and the Imperial Japanese Army Air Force and the  United States Army Air Forces in the Pacific theatre during WWII.\n- In both wars, strategic bombing was used against infrastructure.\n    - In WWI, strategic bombing targeted railroads and, eventually, factories in the enemy’s rear from 1915.\n    - During WWII on the European front, the allies used strategic bombing to force Germany to prioritize anti-aircraft strategy.  In the Pacific theater, strategic bombing, specifically incendiary bombs, was used during 1945 to devastate Japan’s urban areas which were often made of wood.\n- In both wars, strategic bombing was used to terrorize civilians.\n    - During WWI, the Germans heavily bombed southeastern England while the British responded by attacking German cities.\n    - During WWII the Germans were responsible for the eight month bombing campaign against British urban centres known as “the Blitz.”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn both wars, technological innovations were necessary to make airplanes more useful for victory in war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). In the case of WWI this is true of the interrupter gear, which allowed machine guns to be fired forward through the propeller, allowing aircraft to go beyond the task of reconnaissance. In the case of WWII, technological developments in the camera allowed airplanes to increase their preciseness in gathering information about the enemy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).  This highlights one of the main uses of airplanes in both wars: gathering information about the enemy's position ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). A great example of this during WWI is when a French aviator spotted the German First Army wheeling in front of Paris - and not around Paris as the Schlieffen Plan had intended, giving the French army an opportunity for a counteroffensive. This caused the failure of the Schlieffen Plan and stopped the German advance on the Western Front in 1914. During WWII, the reconnaissance aircraft Straight Flush flew over Hiroshima to analyse weather conditions an hour before the Enola Gay dropped the atomic bomb on Hiroshima ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth wars also used the airplane for strategic bombing to destroy enemy infrastructure and morale ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). During WWI, bombers like Germany's Gotha targeted railroads and factories, causing civilian casualties in cities such as London. However, due to technological limitations, the impact of such bombers was very limited. Gotha bombs caused only about 3,000 casualties in raids against London and southeast England ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Germany's Blitz over British industrial centers during WWII had the same objectives, and while the air raids failed to crush civilian morale or push the British toward peace negotiations, they were far deadlier ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0249","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0249\n\nDescribe **two** technological developments in weaponry and their impact on the outcome of WWI.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following mentioning both description and impact:\n\n**Poison gas**: gas used in the First World War including tearing agents, asphyxiants, and blistering agents. Chlorine was the first common gas used by both sides. Mustard gas was the most common blistering agent. Impacts included:\n- Death or debilitation of soldiers. Death might only come some days or even weeks after inhalation, but it removed soldiers from many battles indefinitely.\n- Ability to clear trenches easily\n- Slowed advances even for the side using the gas as they could not advance into it.\n- Psychological impacts on soldiers\n- Pushed the development of gas masks, making poison gas less effective.\n- It did not help break the stalemate because it was dependent on the wind for distribution.\n\n**Tanks**: A vehicle that could withstand machine gun fire and travel through the rough terrain of no man's land (the territory between enemy trenches) could be the perfect solution to the stalemate. The British first developed them and used them during the Battle of the Somme in 1916. The Germans produced only 20 tanks while the French, on the other hand, produced over 4,000 of various models and the British about 2,600. The impact was limited because:\n- Since they could only travel 6km/hr and broke down frequently.\n- The Allies used them mostly as shields for advancing soldiers but since they were so slow and broke down often, they slowed the general advance giving the enemy time to counter-áttack.\n- Their armour plating was not strong enough to resist artillery.\n\n**Submarines**: The submarine, or U-boat, was useful for the Germans since they did not have the numerical advantage of the British Royal Navy. The German Navy initially had about 30 U-boats which they used against the Royal Navy and against merchant ships that could supply the Allies. Impacts on the outcome of the war included:\n - “Unrestricted”  submarine warfare hurt the Allies` supplies for their armed forces and the home front. The German submarines sank over 15 million tonnes of shipping throughout the war and, at one point in 1917, reduced Britain to only six weeks’ reserve of food for the whole country.\n- Unrestricted submarine warfare was diplomatically dangerous. Some of the ships that sank belonged to neutral countries. The sinking of the US ship Lusitania by torpedo attack and the loss of 1,000 lives, including 128 Americans, led to strong protests from the USA. This is one of the reasons the US ended up declaring war on the Central Powers.\n- By 1918, sonar had been developed, and the French also used echo ranging, which allowed U-boat detection. In 1918, the Germans lost 69 U-boats which they were unable to replace.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPoison gas was first used by the Germans in 1915. There were different types of poison gases used during the war. They could either be tearing agents, asphyxiants or blistering agents. The most common one used was chlorine ($\\checkmark$). While poison gas did have a psychological impact and caused about 1 million casualties, since it depended on the wind to be carried it was not a reliable weapon and could often backfire if the wind blew in the direction of the army using the gas. Its effects were not very important for the outcome of the war ($\\checkmark$).\n\nTanks were another technological development that, when first used, carried with them a lot of hope to break the stalemate of the war since the tank could, in theory, move easily through “no man's land” and invade the enemy trench without fear of bullets or barbed wire . They were first introduced by the British during the Battle of the Somme in 1916 ($\\checkmark$). However, since they were very slow travelling at no more than 6 km/hr and broke down frequently, they gave the enemy plenty of time to prepare and even aim at the tanks which were not strong enough to resist artillery fire. At the Battle of the Somme, their only real impact was the panic they initially caused on the Germans ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0251","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0251\n\nDescribe the British Blockade and explain how it impacted the outcome of WWI.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**One** mark for description of the blockade:\n- Britain used its massive surface fleet to turn back all ships bound for Germany, forcing it to rely on its own production and that of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.\n\n**Two** marks for explanation of impact:\n- From 1914-1918, 800 000 German civilians died of undernourishment and related disorders. This made it difficult to sustain the army.\n- Harsh living conditions incentivized revolution against the Kaiser which started in October 1918 with a sailors` mutiny. This political instability led the German leadership to ask for an armistice.\n- Left the Army with limited supplies. The blockade cut supplies of nitrates to Germany which were vital for explosives for the army as well as fertilisers for the farmers.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe British blockade on Germany was one of the factors that determined the outcome of the war. Britain used its control of the seas to turn back all ships that wanted to supply Germany ($\\checkmark$). This led to severe shortages that affected the German civilian population by causing around 800 000 from malnourishment and related illnesses. This meant German soldiers were also undernourished and did not have enough energy to fight properly ($\\checkmark$). Food shortages also led to horrible living conditions that incentivized revolution against the Kaiser, the main proponent of the war. In October 1918 there was a mutiny of sailors that would later spread and end with the abdication of the Kaiser. This political instability led the German leadership to ask for an armistice ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0613","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0613\n\nDescribe Germany's Schlieffen Plan and explain why it failed.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following or similar description of the Schlieffen Plan:  \n\n - The Schlieffen Plan was Germany's plan for fighting a two front war against Russia and France. It was developed by the German military in 1905. The plan was dependent on invading France first, conquering them, and then moving across Germany to fight Russia in the east.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The plan called for invading France through Belgium, a neutral nation. By invading Belgium, Germany violated the Treaty of London which guaranteed Belgian neutrality and drew the British into the war.\n\n - While the Belgians ultimately could not keep the Germans out of France, they did fight back harder than Germany expected. This delayed their advance, helped diminish their resources, and allowed the Russias to mobilize quicker.\n\n - Russia was able to mobilize much quicker than the Schlieffen Plan accounted for. Because of this, the Germans were forced to split their troops sooner than expected.\n\n - The plan was outdated. It did not accurately consider new forms of technology and the updated Russian infrastructure that allowed them quicker mobilization.\n\n - Revisions made to the plan between 1905 and 1914 diluted its effectiveness by sending fewer troops to the initial attacks in Belgium and France.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Schlieffen plan was designed by the German military in 1905 in case they had to fight a two front war against France and Russia. The plan called for the Germans using most of their forces to defeat France before Russia could mobilize ($\\checkmark$). There were several issues with the plan in practice. For one, by invading Belgium, they broke a neutrality agreement with Great Britain. This is the action that brought Great Britain into the war ($\\checkmark$). In addition, they underestimated the amount of time they would need to get through Belgium and conquer France. The Belgians fought harder than they were expecting and by the time they reached France, they had fully mobilized and stopped the German progression ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the Russian military was able to mobilize much more quickly than the Germans expected. This forced the Germans to split their troops sooner, resulting in the two front war they were hoping to avoid ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0634","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0634\n\nDefine guerrilla warfare and describe **two** of its features.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor definition, the following or similar:\n- Guerrilla Warfare refers to a form of irregular warfare where smaller groups of combatants or irregular troops, use unconventional tactics to fight a larger, more traditional military force.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor key features, any **two** of the following:\n- Typically involves a significant imbalance of power, where the guerrilla forces are much smaller and less equipped compared to the regular army or occupying force. This imbalance forces guerrillas to use unconventional methods and tactics to counter the superior strength of their opponents.\n- Guerrilla forces avoid direct confrontation with larger, well-equipped armies. Instead, they use ambushes and surprise attacks to harass enemy forces, strike vulnerable targets (such as supply lines or command posts), and quickly vanish before retaliation can occur.\n- Guerrilla fighters often seek to exploit difficult or inaccessible terrain, such as forests, mountains, or urban areas, to hide, launch attacks, and retreat. This allows them to sustain resources.\n- Guerrillas frequently seek to gain the support of local communities, who provide them with resources, intelligence, shelter, and recruits. Without such support, guerrilla fighters would have limited success in their operations.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGuerrilla warfare is a type of unconventional combat in which small, irregular units engage a larger, conventional military force using nontraditional strategies ($\\checkmark$). One of its key features is the imbalance of power: guerrilla fighters are typically outnumbered and outgunned by professional armies. This imbalance of power encourages them to adopt creative and irregular tactics to overcome their disadvantage ($\\checkmark$). Another key feature is the avoidance of direct, large-scale battles. Guerrilla fighters rely on ambushes and surprise strikes to disrupt enemy operations, targeting weaknesses like supply routes then retreating swiftly to avoid counterattacks ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0643","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0643\n\nEvaluate the reasons for the adoption of guerrilla warfare and its significance in the outcome of **one** 20th-century war.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nCandidates must demonstrate a clear understanding of the question's requirements. In this case, candidates should evaluate the reasons why guerilla warfare was adopted and assess its significance for the war's outcome.\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n- Clearly define guerrilla warfare and explain why it was adopted in the selected war.\n- Evaluate the reasons for its adoption, considering military, political, and/or geographical factors.\n- Assess its significance in the war's outcome (whether it led to victory, prolonged resistance, or ultimately led to defeat).\n- Use a well-structured argument with relevant historical examples and differing perspectives integrated into the answer.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- **Chinese Civil War (1927-1949)**: The CCP lacked conventional military strength compared to the KMT; guerrilla warfare allowed them to survive, mobilize peasant support, and counter superior KMT forces. Guerrilla tactics secured rural support and facilitated the CCP's transition to conventional warfare, culminating in victory by 1949.\n- **Vietnam War (1955-1975)**: The Viet Cong (NLF) lacked resources compared to U.S. and South Vietnamese forces; dense jungle terrain favoured guerrilla tactics. Guerrilla warfare weakened U.S. resolve, prolonged the war, and ultimately played a very significant role in American withdrawal and Communist victory in 1975.\n- **Cuban Revolution (1956-1959)**: Castro's forces were outnumbered by Batista's army; mountainous terrain allowed for guerrilla warfare and small-scale ambushes. Guerrilla tactics, combined with propaganda and peasant support, undermined Batista's rule, leading to the revolution's success.\n- **Algerian War of Independence (1954-1962)**: The FLN (National Liberation Front) lacked conventional military strength; urban and rural guerrilla warfare countered superior French forces. Guerrilla resistance, coupled with international pressure, led to French withdrawal and Algerian independence in 1962.\n- **Afghan-Soviet War (1979-1989)**: The Mujahideen lacked advanced weaponry against Soviet forces; Afghanistan's rugged terrain was ideal for guerrilla tactics. Guerrilla resistance, supported by U.S. funding and arms, prolonged the war and contributed to the Soviet withdrawal in 1989.\n- **Spanish Civil War (1936-1939)**: Republican forces, especially in rural areas, used guerrilla tactics against Franco's better-equipped Nationalists. Guerrilla warfare helped prolong resistance, but ultimately, lack of external support and military disadvantages led to Republican defeat.\n- **World War II in Europe (1939-1945)**: Resistance movements across Nazi-occupied Europe, such as the French Resistance, Yugoslav Partisans, and Soviet Partisans, lacked conventional military strength against the German army. Guerrilla warfare was necessary to disrupt Axis supply lines, sabotage infrastructure, and gather intelligence. Partisan warfare significantly weakened Nazi control in occupied territories, diverted German military resources, and supported Allied advances. In Yugoslavia, Tito's Partisans played a key role in liberating the country without direct Allied military intervention. In the Soviet Union, partisan warfare hindered German advances, particularly during the Battle of Stalingrad.\n\nThe above material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses; however, it is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive and no set answer is required. Credit should be awarded for other possible examples of guerrilla warfare when relevant and used appropriately to answer the question.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Chinese Civil War (CCW) (1927-1949) was fought between the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) and the Kuomintang (KMT). While conventional military strategies played a decisive role in the final stages of CCP victory, guerrilla warfare proved crucial in shifting the balance of power towards the CCP ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Through guerrilla warfare, the CCP could at first survive KMT onslaughts and eventually thrive by mobilizing the rural populace to create a strategic advantage. Nevertheless, the Sino-Japanese War (1937-1945), Second United Front, and KMT mismanagement were also vital in the outcome of the war, as they alleviated pressure on the communists, allowing them to consolidate their power in key areas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$).\n\nGuerrilla warfare is an unconventional form of warfare reliant on fast-moving mobile forces, ambush tactics, and the support of the local populace ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Drawing on his study of historical Chinese and Marxist-Leninist theories, Mao adapted these principles to suit Chinese conditions. He particularly emphasized the mobilization of the peasantry. Mao famously stated, \"The enemy advances, we retreat; the enemy camps, we harass; the enemy tires, we attack\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nThe adoption of guerrilla warfare by the CCP was a crucial strategic response to military pressure exerted by Chiang Kai-shek and played a decisive role in the party's survival and eventual success ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The CCP first adopted guerrilla warfare on a large scale during the Jiangxi Soviet (1931-1934). This followed Chiang Kai-shek's brutal suppression of suspected communists from 1927 onwards. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) Chiang's campaigns highlighted the weaknesses of the CCP in urban areas and forced their strategic shift towards rural strongholds ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Initially, in Jiangxi, the CCP successfully employed ambushes, sabotage, and large-scale peasant mobilization ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). However, following a tactical shift by Chiang and a power shift away from Mao in the Jiangxi Soviet, guerrilla warfare was briefly abandoned. This led to devastating defeats for the CCP, culminating in the Long March (1934-35). During the march, Mao regained his influence over the party and re-established the primacy of guerrilla warfare ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This change in tactics allowed the CCP to survive and establish a new soviet in Yan'an ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nYan'an became a key military and ideological training center for the CCP; however, it is unlikely it would have lasted without the outbreak of the Second Sino-Japanese War (1937-1945) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The war diverted KMT attention, allowing the CCP to expand its rural influence without interference ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The Sino-Japanese War was to be hugely beneficial for the CCP. Through guerrilla warfare, the CCP avoided large-scale battles, maintained and grew their forces, and portrayed themselves as national defenders ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This strengthened their appeal to the rural population ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Through successful propaganda campaigns and popular policies, they gained widespread support from much of the Chinese peasantry (about 90% of the population) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In contrast, the KMT focused on traditional methods of warfare, leading to huge drains on their resources. By the end of the war, the CCP's numbers swelled to over a million, while KMT forces declined to around 3 million ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nThe CCP's strategic use of guerrilla warfare laid the foundation for the transition to large-scale conventional offensives, which proved critical in securing victory. With the end of the Sino-Japanese War and the full resumption of civil war, the KMT continued its reliance on conventional warfare. This proved highly ineffective in rural China, where the CCP had garnered peasant support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Serious logistical and communication issues further overstretched the KMT ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). As a result of the CCP's growth and confidence, by 1946, Mao shifted towards more conventional large-scale offensives. Guerrilla warfare was still used in areas of CCP weakness, but increasingly, the CCP launched coordinated troop movements, strategic encirclements, and full-scale battles against major KMT strongholds ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This turned the KMT stronghold strategy against them using siege tactics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). By 1948, with over 4 million troops, the CCP won victories in the Liaoshen, Huaihai, and Pingjin Campaigns, leading to final victory in 1949 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nHistorians such as Rana Mitter argue that rather than guerrilla warfare, KMT internal weaknesses, corruption, and mismanagement were more important in the war's outcome. However, this underestimates the importance of guerrilla warfare in CCP success. Guerrilla warfare enabled CCP survival, allowing them to transition to conventional offensives. Without this, the CCP would not have built its military strength or secured rural support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nGuerrilla warfare was crucial to CCP victory, as it neutralized KMT firepower and secured popular support. However, it was not the sole factor. The Sino-Japanese War, the Second United Front, and KMT mismanagement also played a significant role. Nevertheless, guerrilla warfare laid the foundation for CCP survival, allowing later conventional offensives to secure final victory ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0652","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0652\n\nSummarize the reasons for United States' involvement in World War I.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- The German practice of unrestricted submarine warfare targeted any ships that entered the war zone. This resulted in the sinking of American merchant ships and the Lusitania, a British cruise liner, resulting in the deaths of 128 Americans. Though the Germans briefly stopped this practice for a short time after the Lusitania, they started up again, angering the American public.\n- The Zimmerman Telegram was sent by the German Foreign Secretary  to the German minister in Mexico and proposed an alliance between Germany and Mexico against the United States. The telegram offered to help Mexico reclaim Texas, Arizona, and New Mexico from the United States. The telegram was intercepted by the British who shared their intel with the United States. Resentment towards the Mexicans from earlier wars combined with growing resentment for the Germans.\n- United States President Woodrow Wilson hesitated to declare war on Germany despite the Zimmerman Telegram and unrestricted submarine warfare. However, public opinion shifted greatly against the Germans because of these slights, allowing Wilson to abandon the US policy of neutrality.\n- The American government gave several loans to the Allied powers, giving them a reason to be vested in their victory. Even American bankers and investors like J.P. Morgan helped secure funding for the Allied powers.\n- In his declaration of war, President Woodrow Wilson claimed that democratic ideals had to be protected, appealing to the United States' belief systems.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe United States entered the war in 1917 after holding onto its policy of neutrality for several years. Germany's policy of unrestricted submarine warfare was the German practice of using submarines to sink any boats in the warzone without warning. This led to several civilian ships being destroyed, including the Lusitania, a British passenger liner with over 100 Americans on it. While the Germans briefly stopped this practice to maintain a diplomatic relationship with the United States, they started again in 1917, angering the Americans ($\\checkmark$). In 1917, a German Foreign secretary sent a telegram to their ambassador to Mexico. This note suggested an alliance between Mexico and Germany if the United States joined the war. In return, Germany promised Mexico that they would help reclaim land from the United States. This telegram was intercepted by the British and shared with President Woodrow Wilson, heightening tensions with Germany ($\\checkmark$). Together, these two events shifted public opinion away from neutrality and toward war. This shift in public opinion was an important part of Wilson's declaration of war, as he was unlikely to have gotten involved if the public were overwhelmingly against it ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the United States had a vested financial interest in an Allied victory. They had given several large loans to the Allied countries, and American financiers such as J.P. Morgan also gave many private loans, so the US involvement would also protect their investments ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0654","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0654\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the impact of Russia's revolution on the war effort?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A cartoon titled “Kerensky to the Rescue” by American political cartoonist Clifford Berryman in 1918. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Practices%20%26%20Their%20Impact%20on%20Outcome/images/HI0654.png\" alt=\"HI0654\">:IDXXXXXXXXXX\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1917, November 13). *Kerensky to the Rescue*. National Archives PICRYL. Retrieved from https://picryl.com/media/kerensky-to-the-rescue-a14bb6 on March 25, 2025. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Bolsheviks were trying to pull the Russians away from the fighting.\n- The title and the drawing suggest that Kerensky's provisional government was looking to continue the fighting on the eastern front.\n- The leash represents Germany's influence on the Bolsheviks. This suggests that Germany is supporting the Bolsheviks because they know it will weaken the Allies in the war.\n- The bear looks confused, showing that the people of Russia may be confused on the best way forward.\n- The bear is holding onto a sign that reads “Liberty” and it may be worried about whether Kerensky or the Bolsheviks are their best option for keeping liberty.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A shows a bear labeled Russia being dragged away from the fighting by the Bolsheviks. This suggests that if the communists were to win the revolution, the Russians would be leaving the war ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also shows the leash on the Russians being held by the Bolsheviks has a tag on it that reads “Made in Germany.” This suggests that the Bolsheviks are supported by the Germans and that they would benefit from the Bolsheviks winning and Russia being removed from the war ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also shows Kerensky, the leader of the Russian provisional government, chasing after the bear with worry. This, along with the cartoon's title, suggests that if Kerensky's side wins the revolution, they will continue to fight ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0655","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0655\n\nHow, according to Source B, did Russian public opinion lead to them leaving World War I?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from “About Russia, Its Revolutions, Its Development, and Its Present,” written by Michal Reiman, a Czech historian, in 2016. |\n\n[Source: Reiman, M. (2016). *About Russia, Its Revolutions, Its Development and Its Present* (pp. 13-24). Peter Lang AG. Retrieved March 25, 2025 from  http://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv2t4dn7.4. Open Access.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Bolsheviks received financial support from Germany because the Bolsheviks did not want to continue the war. When the Bolsheviks won, it was natural they sought peace with Germany.\n    - Evidence: “Even liberal and left-wing historiography that was not outright Bolshevik, and that emphasized the spontaneity of the revolutionary outburst itself, attributed its subsequent radicalization to Bolshevik actions. Some pointed to the Germans and ‘German’ money supplied to the Bolsheviks.”\n- Food shortages made the war unpopular so the people supported the side that was more likely to leave the war.\n    - Evidence: “As noted, government policy gave rise to the grudges of workers and soldiers that served as the flashpoint for the revolution; so did the despotic attitude of the owners of Putilov, St Petersburg’s largest machine works, and the shortage of bread in the stores of the city’s working-class districts.\"\n- The public supported leaving the war because they did not see the Russians gaining anything even if there was an Allied victory.\n    - Evidence: “In addition to the aforementioned causes, the unsuccessful conduct of the war had sapped the country's capacity. By 1917, a major segment of Russian society knew that Russia had already lost the war, and that even a potential victory by the Allies would not bring the promised profit nor see Russia restored to its pre-war geographic extent.”\n- The Allies only wanted to accept unconditional defeat and rejected peace negotiations. This convinced the Russians that their best way out was an armistice.\n    - Evidence: “Russians became convinced that only the rapid conclusion of an armistice and peace would save the country from catastrophe. This gives some insight into why the Russian provisional government resisted separate peace negotiations with Germany. The Allies rejected peace with Germany and the Triple Alliance on any basis other than the latter's defeat.”\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that most Russians and especially the Russians who supported the Bolsheviks wanted to leave the war effort. The Bolsheviks and their supporters received financial aid from Germany, making it natural that they would look for a settlement with them following a victory in the revolution ($\\checkmark$). Many workers and soldiers were frustrated by the food shortages at home and on the front lines, making them support an armistice ($\\checkmark$). In addition, many Russians did not see the benefit in staying in the fight much longer, as they were not winning any land and they were unlikely to benefit from continuing in the war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0656","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0656\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the end of World War I.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A cartoon titled “Kerensky to the Rescue” by American political cartoonist Clifford Berryman in 1918. |\n\n:aeee50b1-47ad-46e1-ac1f-8bc6<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Practices%20%26%20Their%20Impact%20on%20Outcome/images/HI0656.png\" alt=\"HI0656\">\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1917, November 13). *Kerensky to the Rescue*. National Archives PICRYL. Retrieved from https://picryl.com/media/kerensky-to-the-rescue-a14bb6 on March 25, 2025. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- This primary source was published in The Evening Star, a Washington, D.C. newspaper in 1917 during the war. The artist was Clifford Berryman, a well-known cartoonist from this time period.\n- The cartoon's purpose is to inform The Evening Star readers about how the revolution in Russia was influencing the war effort. This is shown by the Bolsheviks pulling the bear labeled “Russia” away from the front.\n- The cartoon is explicitly labeled to make the content clear for a historian studying the end of the war and gives a clear picture of how the Russian Revolution would impact World War I.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- While the source is a primary source from a trusted publication, it was published in the United States and is naturally sympathetic to their allies.\n- The cartoon's purpose may also be to persuade U.S. public opinion against the Bolsheviks by making a connection between them and the Germans.\n- The cartoon only focuses on the Russian Revolution's impact on the end of the war while there are many other factors that will contribute to the armistice.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is valuable as it is a primary source published during the war in a reputable newspaper ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose may be limited as it is from an American newspaper and is catering to the American perspective, which sides with the allies and does not consider other factors ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The purpose of the cartoon is valuable to historians as it seeks to inform readers about how the revolution in Russia may affect the war in Europe, giving a historian a good idea of how the allies viewed this event ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). However, the content is limited as it only shows the possible impact of the Russian Revolution when there were many other factors that could have contributed to the end of the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0657","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0657\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the Allied response to the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A New Year greeting from the United States Ambassador to Russia, David R. Francis. This was published on January 13, 1918 and sent to the Secretary of State, Robert Lansing.|\n\n[Source: *Foreign Relations of the United States: 1918 The Conclusion of the Peace of Brest Litovsk.* (1918, January 13). The Avalon Project. Retrieved March 25, 2025 from https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/bl15.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- A primary source written by an American diplomat living in Russia during this time is a good indicator of the Allied response to Russia's separate peace.\n- The source's purpose is to convey that the U.S. and their allies in the war do not believe that Russia leaving the war would be beneficial to them in the long run.\n- The content is valuable as it gives some explicit concerns that the Allies may have about Russia's separate peace, such as their exclusion from a longer more permanent peace solution and the possibility of Germany dominating their new government.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- The primary source is written by an American who would want to keep Russian soldiers in the war to keep the soldiers divided between two fronts in order to better the Allies' odds.\n- The content is limited, however, because it only considers the American perspective. The historian would likely want to explore the feelings of multiple Allied countries.\n- The purpose is limited as it is only reaffirming statements that had already been made by President Woodrow Wilson.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable to a historian looking at the allied response to the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk because it is a primary source written by an American diplomat living in Russia at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, the content of the source is limited because it only considers the American perspective and none of the other allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). The content of this source is valuable as it considers specific reasons why the treaty would not benefit Russia in the long-term. Specifically, the document states that the Russians would be left out of a more permanent peace settlement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). Still, the purpose of the document is limited as it is just a reiteration of statements that had previously been made by President Wilson and does not add any new thoughts or ideas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0744","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0744\n\nExplain the role of foreign powers in the outcome of **two** 20th-century civil wars.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - Russian Civil War (1917-1923). Britain, France, Japan, and the USA supported the anti-Bolshevik Whites. Intervention failed to unite or effectively support the Whites; the Bolsheviks discredited them as tools of foreign powers.\n\n - Spanish Civil War (1936-1939). Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy supported Franco's Nationalists, the USSR supported the Republicans. German and Italian air and ground support gave the Nationalists a decisive military edge, especially in air power and logistics.\n\n - Greek Civil War (1946-1949). Britain and later the USA supported the royalist government while the USSR and Yugoslavia indirectly aided communist forces. U.S. military and economic aid under the Truman Doctrine ensured government victory and containment of communism in Greece.\n\n - Chinese Civil War (1927-1949). USSR backed the CCP, especially after WWII while  the USA supported the Nationalists (Kuomindang). Soviet occupation of Manchuria post-WWII handed the CCP control of key areas and Japanese weapons. U.S. support was insufficient and undermined by Nationalist corruption.\n\n - Vietnam War (1955-1975). USA and other anti-communist nations supported South Vietnam while the USSR and China supported North Vietnam and the Viet Cong.\n\n - North Vietnamese victorious due to prolonged guerrilla warfare, strong local support, and continued Soviet/Chinese material aid. U.S. public opposition led to their withdrawal.\n\n - Angolan Civil War (1975-2002). USSR and Cuba supported MPLA while the USA and South Africa backed UNITA. Cuban troops and Soviet equipment enabled MPLA to gain and hold power. Cold War rivalry prolonged the war.\n\n - Afghan Civil War (1978-1992). USSR invaded in 1979 to support the communist government. The USA, Pakistan, and Saudi Arabia supported the Mujahideen.\n\n - U.S.-backed insurgency, with CIA and ISI support, ultimately forced Soviet withdrawal in 1989, contributing to the communist regime's collapse in 1992.\n\n - Nicaraguan Contra War (1981-1990). USA funded and trained Contra rebels to fight the Sandinista government while the USSR and Cuba supported the Sandinistas.\n\n - U.S. aid destabilized the Sandinista regime. The ongoing conflict led to economic hardship, influencing the 1990 election defeat of the Sandinistas.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn the Russian Civil War (1917-1923), foreign powers including Britain, France, Japan, and the United States intervened to support the anti-Bolshevik White forces, providing limited military and financial support. ($\\checkmark$) However, their involvement was poorly coordinated and failed to unify the White movement. The Bolsheviks portrayed the Whites as puppets of imperialist powers. This, combined with effective Bolshevik organization and control of central regions, contributed to Red Army victory and the establishment of the Soviet regime. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nIn the Vietnam War (1955-1975), the United States, alongside other Western allies, supported the government of South Vietnam with troops, funds, and military equipment in an effort to contain communism. In contrast, the Soviet Union and China supplied North Vietnam and the Viet Cong with weapons, training, and logistical support. ($\\checkmark$) Despite U.S. military superiority, North Vietnam's use of guerrilla tactics, its strong ideological motivation, and consistent foreign aid enabled it to persist. Increasing anti-war protests in the U.S. led to a gradual withdrawal, and in 1975, the North achieved reunification, marking a communist victory. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0823","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0823\n\nAnalyse **three** reasons for international involvement in the Vietnam War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n- U.S. containment policy and the domino theory: The United States was committed to preventing the spread of communism in Asia, especially after the Chinese Communist Revolution (1949) and the Korean War (1950-1953). The Domino Theory suggested that if one country in Southeast Asia fell to communism, others would quickly follow. Vietnam was seen as the critical first domino.\n- Support from the USSR and China for North Vietnam. Both the Soviet Union and the People's Republic of China provided economic aid, weapons, and training to the North Vietnamese as part of Cold War rivalry. Their support stemmed from a desire to promote global communism and challenge U.S. influence.\n- The USSR also used Vietnam to assert its leadership in the communist world, particularly during the Sino-Soviet split, creating competition with China over influence in Hanoi.\n- U.S. strategic interests in Southeast Asia. The U.S. saw Vietnam as strategically vital for maintaining influence over Southeast Asia, which included proximity to key allies (e.g., the Philippines, Thailand) and control of trade routes. Establishing a military presence through bases and alliances helped counterbalance growing communist influence in Asia. Vietnam was also seen as a test of American credibility; failure to act would weaken U.S. standing globally.\n- SEATO commitments. The Southeast Asia Treaty Organisation (SEATO), formed in 1954, was intended to block further communist gains in Southeast Asia. Although South Vietnam was not a member, the U.S. and its allies used SEATO as a justification for intervention under the principle of collective defence. Australia, New Zealand, and the Philippines were among SEATO members who contributed troops, citing treaty obligations.\n- French colonial legacy. Vietnam was a former French colony (part of French Indochina), and France's defeat at Dien Bien Phu (1954) drew international attention to the region. The Geneva Accords (1954), which temporarily divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel, internationalised the conflict by creating a fragile and contested peace settlement. The U.S. stepped in to support South Vietnam after France’s withdrawal, viewing the conflict as part of the broader fight against communism.\n- Global superpower rivalry and Cold War alignments. The Vietnam War became a proxy war in the broader Cold War context, with countries aligning based on ideological and strategic interests. Australia and South Korea, for example, supported the U.S. due to alliance obligations and anti-communist alignment. The Soviet Union and China, meanwhile, supported North Vietnam to counter U.S. hegemony and extend their influence in the developing world.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, international involvement in the Vietnam War was driven by the United States' containment policy and belief in the Domino Theory ($\\checkmark$). After the rise of communism in China and the outbreak of the Korean War, the U.S. feared that if Vietnam fell to communism, other Southeast Asian countries would follow. As a result, Vietnam became a key battleground in America's broader Cold War strategy to prevent the global spread of communism ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the Soviet Union and China supported North Vietnam to advance their ideological interests and to counter U.S. influence in Asia ($\\checkmark$). Both provided military and economic aid to Hanoi, aiming to promote global communism. This support was also shaped by Cold War competition, with the USSR and China each trying to assert their influence in the region, particularly during the Sino-Soviet split ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, U.S. involvement was also justified through the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO) ($\\checkmark$). Although South Vietnam was not a formal member, SEATO allowed countries like the U.S., Australia, and the Philippines to frame their military intervention as part of a collective defense strategy against communist expansion in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0824","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0824\n\nDiscuss the political and economic effects of the Vietnam War on Vietnam.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation. Both political and economic effects must be addressed in the response.\n\nPolitical Effects:\n- Unification under a communist government in 1976: After the fall of Saigon in 1975, North and South Vietnam were formally unified into the Socialist Republic of Vietnam in 1976. The North imposed its political system on the South, centralising power in Hanoi and ending the brief period of political plurality in the South.\n- Repression of political dissent: The new government established re-education camps where former South Vietnamese military officers, civil servants, and intellectuals were imprisoned, sometimes for years. The suppression of former political and religious groups in the South created lasting resentment and drove many to flee the country, contributing to the refugee crisis.\n- Strained international relations, especially with China (Sino-Vietnamese War, 1979): Vietnam's invasion of Cambodia in 1978 to oust the Khmer Rouge, which China backed, led to the brief but intense Sino-Vietnamese War in 1979. This conflict worsened relations with China and left Vietnam diplomatically isolated within the communist bloc for a time.\n- Centralised, one-party rule: The Communist Party of Vietnam (CPV) became the sole legal political party, enforcing strict ideological control and suppressing political freedoms. There was no allowance for democratic elections or dissent, with censorship and state surveillance continuing into the post-war period.\n\nEconomic Effects\n- Massive destruction of infrastructure and farmland: Years of intense bombing by the U.S., especially in the North and along the Ho Chi Minh Trail, destroyed key infrastructure like roads, bridges, railways, and irrigation systems. The use of chemical defoliants like Agent Orange also left large areas of farmland infertile and caused long-term health and environmental damage.\n- Loss of human capital: The war caused the deaths of an estimated 2 to 3 million Vietnamese, severely impacting the labour force. A significant number of skilled workers, professionals, and students were killed, imprisoned, or fled the country, creating a brain drain.\n- Economic isolation in the 1970s and 1980s: Vietnam's close ties with the USSR and occupation of Cambodia led to sanctions and hostility from Western powers, notably the U.S. As a result, Vietnam remained economically dependent on Soviet aid and struggled with stagnation, food shortages, and poor living standards.\n- Gradual economic reforms, Đổi Mới, from 1986: Facing an economic crisis, the Communist Party launched Đổi Mới (“Renovation”), introducing market-oriented reforms. This included decentralising economic planning, encouraging private enterprise, and allowing some foreign investment, which led to a gradual economic recovery and growth.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe first political effect of the Vietnam War was the complete unification of North and South Vietnam under a single communist regime in 1976 ($\\checkmark$). Following the capture of Saigon in 1975, the North established control over the entire country, dissolving the former government of South Vietnam and centralising authority in Hanoi. This marked the end of political diversity in the South, as the Communist Party became the sole governing force across the nation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe second political effect was the widespread suppression of opposition in the aftermath of unification ($\\checkmark$). The new regime targeted former South Vietnamese officials, intellectuals, and religious leaders by detaining them in re-education camps. These camps were intended to indoctrinate individuals with communist ideology, but often involved harsh conditions and long-term imprisonment. The repression contributed to mass emigration, as many sought to escape persecution, leading to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$boat people\" refugee crisis ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAn economic effect of the war was the extensive damage to Vietnam's infrastructure and environment ($\\checkmark$). Prolonged bombing campaigns destroyed roads, bridges, and railways, while large-scale use of chemical agents such as Agent Orange severely contaminated farmland and forests. These consequences not only devastated agricultural productivity but also caused lasting health issues among the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0825","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0825\n\nAnalyse **three** examples of international involvement in the Soviet-Afghan War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** points with supporting explanation:\n- United States: Through Operation Cyclone, the CIA covertly funded and armed the Mujahideen as part of its Cold War containment strategy, aiming to weaken Soviet influence. Support included advanced Stinger surface-to-air missiles, which significantly hindered Soviet air superiority by targeting helicopters and low-flying aircraft. This involvement escalated the conflict and indirectly contributed to the rise of Islamist militant groups, including the Taliban and al-Qaeda in later decades.\n- Pakistan: The Inter-Services Intelligence (ISI) served as the intermediary for U.S. and Saudi funds and weapons, distributing them to various Mujahideen factions. Pakistan hosted millions of Afghan refugees and training camps near the Durand Line, helping to build a deep cross-border insurgency. Its role enhanced its strategic importance to the West, especially during the Reagan era, though it also fueled extremism within its borders.\n- Saudi Arabia: Co-financed the jihad with the U.S., often matching American funding dollar-for-dollar. Promoted the spread of Wahhabi ideology among Mujahideen fighters, viewing the war as a religious struggle against atheist communism. Several Saudi volunteers, including Osama bin Laden, participated, strengthening transnational jihadist networks.\n- China: Opposed Soviet expansionism and sought to counter Moscow’s influence in Asia; thus, it supplied weapons, including small arms and mines, to the Mujahideen via Pakistan. This marked a rare instance of tacit alignment with the U.S. during the Cold War, despite China's communist system. China’s support was also motivated by fears of Soviet influence in neighbouring Central Asia and Xinjiang.\n- United Nations: Passed non-binding resolutions condemning the Soviet invasion, but no concrete actions (e.g., sanctions or peacekeeping) were taken due to Cold War divisions in the Security Council. The UN provided humanitarian support to Afghan refugees but lacked the authority or consensus to influence the course of the war.\n- NATO countries: Generally aligned with the U.S. stance, issuing diplomatic condemnations of Soviet aggression. Some NATO members, such as the UK, also provided indirect logistical and intelligence support, though without committing troops. The war deepened the East-West divide, reinforcing anti-Soviet policies in the West during the final decade of the Cold War.\n- Soviet bloc: Warsaw Pact allies (e.g., East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Bulgaria) publicly supported the USSR, framing the war as a defence of socialism against U.S.-backed “terrorists.” However, most did not send troops due to political caution and lack of popular support. The war contributed to growing dissent and disillusionment in Eastern Europe regarding the Soviet leadership, especially as the war dragged on without a clear victory.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the United States became heavily involved in the Soviet-Afghan War as part of its broader Cold War agenda to contain Soviet expansion ($\\checkmark$).Through a covert operation known as Operation Cyclone, the CIA provided billions of dollars in weapons, training, and logistical support to the Afghan Mujahideen. A key component of this support was the supply of advanced Stinger missiles, which enabled resistance fighters to shoot down Soviet helicopters and disrupt air dominance ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Pakistan played a central role in facilitating the Mujahideen resistance. The country's intelligence agency, the ISI, acted as a conduit for American and Saudi aid, distributing funds and weapons to various Afghan factions ($\\checkmark$). Pakistan also hosted extensive networks of refugee camps and guerrilla training centres along its border, which not only supported the war effort but also entrenched militant ideologies within Pakistan's territory. This involvement elevated Pakistan's strategic position during the Cold War, though it also laid the foundation for future instability in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, China supported the Afghan resistance as part of its geopolitical effort to counterbalance Soviet power in Central and South Asia ($\\checkmark$). Despite being a communist state itself, China feared Soviet encroachment in neighbouring areas such as Xinjiang and sought to undermine Moscow's influence. It supplied the Mujahideen with light weapons, landmines, and ammunition, often through Pakistani channels. This indirect collaboration with the U.S. highlighted the complexity of Cold War alliances and reflected China's broader strategy to assert regional autonomy and challenge Soviet dominance ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0826","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0826\n\nEvaluate the consequences of the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan in 1989.\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** points with supporting explanation:\n- Military: The withdrawal marked the end of a decade-long conflict that had cost the USSR over 15,000 troops and substantial military resources. It was widely regarded as a humiliating defeat for a superpower, having failed to subdue a guerrilla insurgency despite superior military technology. The Soviet military's poor performance exposed weaknesses in its logistics, morale, and adaptability, damaging the Red Army's reputation.\n- Political (Soviet Union): The failure in Afghanistan undermined Soviet global credibility and confidence in the Communist system. It contributed to internal disillusionment with the leadership of the Communist Party and Mikhail Gorbachev’s reforms (Perestroika and Glasnost). The strain of the war, combined with economic problems, helped accelerate the collapse of the USSR in 1991.\n- Afghanistan (domestic impact): The withdrawal created a power vacuum, as the Soviet-backed Najibullah government collapsed by 1992. Rival Mujahideen factions turned on each other, leading to a brutal civil war (1989-1996), particularly in Kabul, where competing warlords vied for control.\n- Taliban emergence: The chaos and instability enabled the rise of the Taliban, who promised order and Islamic governance and seized power by 1996. The Taliban’s emergence was partly enabled by continued foreign support, especially from Pakistan’s ISI, and disillusionment with factional infighting.\n- Regional impact (South Asia): The war left millions of Afghan refugees in Pakistan and Iran, placing enormous social and economic strain on the region. The Pakistan-Afghanistan border became a hub for arms smuggling, drug trafficking, and radical Islamist activity, destabilising both countries.\n- Global consequences (Islamist militancy): The war became a rallying point for global jihadist movements. Many foreign fighters, including Osama bin Laden, were radicalised and gained combat experience. Veterans later participated in conflicts in Bosnia and Chechnya, and eventually formed the core of al-Qaeda, leading to future global terrorism.\n- U.S. perception and policy: The Soviet withdrawal was seen in Washington as a Cold War victory, confirming the effectiveness of U.S. covert intervention. However, the U.S. disengaged quickly, failing to invest in post-war reconstruction or governance, which allowed chaos to take root. This short-term success had long-term consequences, as abandoned fighters and arms stockpiles later empowered extremist groups.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall significance and long-term impact of the Soviet withdrawal, linking the evaluated consequences to broader historical or global developments.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Soviet military withdrawal in 1989 marked a significant and costly failure for one of the world's superpowers ($\\checkmark$). After nearly a decade of fighting, the USSR had lost over 15,000 soldiers and expended vast military resources, only to withdraw without securing a clear victory. The Soviet forces' inability to defeat a guerrilla resistance movement, despite possessing superior weaponry, exposed deep flaws in their military strategy, including poor troop morale, ineffective logistics, and a lack of adaptability. This withdrawal seriously damaged the credibility of the Red Army and eroded its prestige on the global stage ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the political repercussions for the Soviet Union were equally severe. The defeat in Afghanistan undermined Soviet influence abroad and intensified domestic dissatisfaction with the Communist regime ($\\checkmark$). Public support for continued military engagement eroded, and the war became symbolic of the broader failures of the Soviet system. The conflict also placed significant strain on the Soviet economy, compounding the difficulties facing Mikhail Gorbachev as he attempted to implement reforms such as Perestroika and Glasnost. These combined pressures played a key role in accelerating the decline and eventual collapse of the Soviet Union by 1991 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, in Afghanistan itself, the Soviet exit created a dangerous power vacuum. With no strong central government in place, various Mujahideen factions turned on each other, plunging the country into a brutal civil war ($\\checkmark$). Amid the chaos, the Taliban emerged as a dominant force, promising to restore order through strict Islamic governance. Their rise to power by 1996 was aided by continued foreign backing, particularly from Pakistan, and widespread disillusionment with the corruption and infighting among rival warlords ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan had far-reaching consequences, including military humiliation for the USSR, political instability at home, and long-term regional instability that paved the way for the Taliban's rise, consequences that would shape global geopolitics for decades to come ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0827","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0827\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from “H.J.Res. 1145, Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia.” 88th Congress of the United States, August 7th, 1964. Also known as the “Gulf of Tonkin Resolution.” |\n\n[Source: United States Congress. (1964, August 7). H.J.Res. 1145, *Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia*. 88th Congress, 2nd session. Public Law 88-408. U.S. Government Publishing Office. Retrieved June 11, 2025 from https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-78/pdf/STATUTE-78-Pg384.pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nOrigin\n- Value:\n    - Official U.S. government document (Joint Resolution by the 88th Congress, 1964), providing an authoritative primary source on U.S. legislative and executive justification for escalation in Vietnam.\n    - Reflects the formal legal basis granted to President Johnson to expand military action, showing how U.S. policy was legitimized domestically.\n- Limitations:\n    - Reflects only the U.S. government's official stance, so it may omit internal debates, dissent, or alternate perspectives within the U.S. political system.\n    - Produced shortly after the Gulf of Tonkin incidents, it may contain biased or incomplete information about the actual events, limiting its reliability on factual accuracy.\nPurpose\n- Value:\n    - Intended to justify U.S. military escalation by portraying North Vietnam as the aggressor threatening international peace, showing how the U.S. framed its intervention as defensive and lawful.\n    - Demonstrates U.S. commitment to Cold War alliances (Southeast Asia Treaty Organisation) and the wider goal of containing communism.\n    - Helps historians understand the rhetoric and reasoning used to gain congressional and public support for the war.\n\n- Limitations:\n    - Purpose is inherently political and persuasive; designed to rally support and legitimize U.S. military action, so it may exaggerate or oversimplify North Vietnamese aggression.\n    - Omits discussion of U.S. strategic interests beyond stated ideals (e.g., economic, geopolitical), limiting insight into the full scope of motivations.\nContent\n- Value:\n    - Provides direct evidence of the U.S. claim that North Vietnam attacked American naval vessels in international waters, a key justification for military action.\n    - Specifies the extent of presidential authority granted to use \"all necessary measures,\" illustrating the legal framework for escalating U.S. involvement.\n    - Emphasizes U.S. self-portrayal as a defender of freedom and international peace, useful for analyzing Cold War ideological narratives.\n\n- Limitations:\n    - The claim of repeated attacks on U.S. vessels has been contested or re-evaluated by later historians, raising questions about factual accuracy.\n    - Does not acknowledge the Vietnamese perspective or broader regional complexities, limiting its scope for comprehensive understanding.\n    - The language is vague about what \"all necessary measures\" entails, leaving room for varied interpretation and use of power.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs an official U.S. government document, specifically a Joint Resolution passed by  Congress in 1964, it serves as an authoritative source that reflects the formal legislative and executive rationale for escalating American military involvement in Vietnam. It offers direct insight into the political mindset of U.S. leaders at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).\n\nSecondly, the content of the resolution is valuable in that it presents the United States as a defender of freedom and international peace. This self-portrayal helps historians understand the ideological framing of the Cold War, particularly how the U.S. justified its intervention in Southeast Asia in terms of protecting democratic values and resisting communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the purpose of the document introduces limitations. Since it was intended to rally public and congressional support for military action, it is inherently political and persuasive in nature. As such, it may exaggerate or simplify the threat posed by North Vietnam, portraying the U.S. as purely defensive and omitting the complexities of the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$).\n\nFinally, its origin also limits its reliability. Written shortly after the Gulf of Tonkin incident, the resolution may reflect incomplete or biased accounts of those events, which were later shown to be more ambiguous than initially reported. This reduces the source's usefulness for establishing an accurate account of what happened, but highlights how the events were used to justify escalation\n($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$)."},{"ref":"HI0829","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0829\n\nUsing evidence from Source B, describe how Ho Chi Minh uses foreign political ideals to justify Vietnam's independence.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam by Ho Chi Minh, the first President of Vietnam, September 2, 1945. |\n\n[Source: Ho Chi Minh. (1945, September 2). Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. Retrieved June 13, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/ho-chi-minh/works/1945/declaration-independence.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Reference to the U.S. Declaration of Independence (1776): Ho quotes “All men are created equal... Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness” to appeal to American democratic values and show that Vietnamese independence is based on the same universal principles.\n- Reference to the French Declaration of the Rights of Man (1789): Ho cites “All men are born free and with equal rights...” to highlight the hypocrisy of France’s actions in Vietnam, contrasting French ideals with their colonial oppression.\n- Appeal to post-World War II Allied principles (Tehran and San Francisco Conferences): Ho emphasizes that Vietnam’s struggle aligns with Allied declarations supporting self-determination and equality of nations, suggesting that Vietnam deserves international recognition just like other decolonizing countries.\n- Alignment with anti-fascist efforts: He claims that the Vietnamese people fought “side by side with the Allies against the Fascists,” arguing that their contribution to the global struggle for freedom entitles them to national sovereignty.\n- Use of Enlightenment values to delegitimize colonialism: By grounding his argument in widely respected Western political ideals, Ho frames colonialism as outdated and unjust, reinforcing Vietnam's moral right to independence.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHo Chi Minh uses foreign political ideals to justify Vietnam's independence by aligning his arguments with well-known democratic principles. Firstly, he references the U.S. Declaration of Independence (1776), quoting that “All men are created equal... Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness,” to appeal to American values and emphasize that the Vietnamese desire for independence is rooted in the same ideals ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, he draws on the French Declaration of the Rights of Man (1789), citing that “All men are born free and with equal rights” to expose the contradiction between France's professed ideals and its colonial rule over Vietnam ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Ho refers to post-World War II Allied commitments made “at Tehran and San Francisco”, a reference to the Tehran and San Francisco Conferences, highlighting principles of self-determination and equality among nations. In doing so, he argues that Vietnam's independence is consistent with international agreements and should therefore be recognized globally ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0831","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0831\n\nTo what extent did the goals outlined in Source C (NSDD-166) influence the outcome of the Soviet-Afghan War?\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan” given by US President Ronald Reagan, 1985, and declassified in 2008.|\n\n[Source: Reagan, R. (1985, March 27). *National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan.* Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. Retrieved June 14, 2025 from https://www.reaganlibrary.gov/public/archives/reference/scanned-nsdds/nsdd166.pdf Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a concluding sentence:\n\nGoals that were largely achieved (high influence):\n- Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan (1989): NSDD-166's ultimate goal was the removal of Soviet forces, which was achieved after sustained Mujahideen resistance and mounting costs for the USSR. U.S. covert support (weapons, funding, intelligence) played a critical role in weakening Soviet military efforts.\n- Soviet military and political strain: The directive aimed to “demonstrate to the Soviet Union that its long-term strategy is not working”. Prolonged conflict and rising casualties demoralized Soviet troops and weakened domestic support. The war became known as the “Soviet Union’s Vietnam,” contributing to internal dissent and disillusionment with leadership.\n- Support for indigenous resistance (Mujahideen): NSDD-166 emphasized preventing the defeat of the Afghan resistance, U.S. and Pakistani support helped the Mujahideen sustain a prolonged insurgency. Stinger missiles provided by the CIA in the mid-1980s significantly improved Mujahideen capacity to neutralize Soviet air superiority.\n- Soviet international isolation: The directive aimed to “promote Soviet isolation in the Third and Islamic worlds”, many Muslim-majority countries condemned the occupation and supported the Afghan cause diplomatically and materially. The conflict damaged Soviet relationships in the Non-Aligned Movement and broader Islamic world.\n- Denying Afghanistan as a Soviet base: The resistance and chaos meant Afghanistan never became a stable Soviet ally or base of operations.\n\nGoals partially achieved or with mixed results:\n- Demonstrating Soviet imperialism to the Third World: The U.S. succeeded in framing the USSR as an occupying force, shifting some countries' perspectives away from viewing the USSR as a natural ally. Still, some regimes continued to support or remain neutral toward the Soviets due to shared political or economic interests.\n\nGoals not fully achieved or with long-term unintended consequences:\n- Afghanistan's stability and independence: Although the Soviets withdrew, Afghanistan descended into civil war among Mujahideen factions. The rise of the Taliban and the harbouring of al-Qaeda in the 1990s were long-term outcomes contrary to U.S. interests.\n- U.S. long-term control over outcomes: NSDD-166 focused on defeating the USSR, but lacked a coherent post-withdrawal plan; the resulting power vacuum led to instability. U.S. disengagement after 1989 meant little support for post-Soviet reconstruction or political settlement.\n- Blowback and radicalization: Some Mujahideen fighters later became involved in global jihadist movements, including al-Qaeda. U.S. funding and training of extremists during the war had long-term unintended consequences.\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall extent to which the US achieved their goals, linking the goals to regional developments.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the directive's primary objective, the removal of Soviet forces from Afghanistan, was met to a high degree. The Soviet withdrawal in 1989 followed years of sustained resistance by the Mujahideen, supported by U.S. covert aid in the form of weapons, funding, and intelligence. This significantly weakened Soviet military effectiveness and made continued occupation unsustainable ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, NSDD-166 successfully aimed to “demonstrate to the Soviet Union that its long-term strategy is not working.” The protracted conflict placed a major strain on Soviet military resources and morale, contributing to public disillusionment and internal dissent. The war earned the nickname “the Soviet Union's Vietnam,” underscoring the extent to which it undermined Soviet confidence and political cohesion ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the goal of restoring Afghan independence and stability was met to a lesser degree. Although Soviet forces left the country, Afghanistan soon descended into civil war between rival Mujahideen factions. The eventual rise of the Taliban and the emergence of al-Qaeda in the 1990s represented long-term consequences that ultimately ran counter to U.S. interests in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while NSDD-166 played a decisive role in achieving key U.S. objectives, namely, the Soviet withdrawal and the weakening of Soviet geopolitical influence, its longer-term aim of fostering a stable and independent Afghanistan was not fully realised. The directive reflected Cold War priorities, but its legacy highlights the complexity of foreign intervention and the unintended consequences that can follow even seemingly successful policy outcomes ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0904","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0904\n\nEvaluate the limitations of the military strategies of the United States in the Vietnam War (1956-1975).\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation and a reasoned conclusion:\n\nLimited effectiveness in the short term or tactically:\n- Operation Rolling Thunder (1965-1968): Intended to weaken North Vietnamese morale and supply lines. Destroyed some infrastructure but failed to stop supplies along the Ho Chi Minh Trail. Gave North Vietnam time to strengthen air defenses.\n- Superior firepower and air mobility: Use of helicopters (e.g., UH-1 Huey) allowed fast deployment in jungle terrain. Enabled rapid troop movement and medical evacuation. Provided temporary tactical advantages in battles like Ia Drang (1965).\n- Search and destroy missions: Aimed to kill Viet Cong forces and reduce enemy presence. Occasionally successful in eliminating enemy units. Produced high body counts, seen as a success metric by U.S. military leadership.\n\nLargely ineffective or counterproductive in the long term:\n- Overreliance on attrition warfare: Strategy focused on body counts rather than holding territory. Misjudged the Viet Cong's and North Vietnamese Army's (NVA) willingness to endure losses. Failed to break enemy morale.\n- Guerrilla warfare by the Viet Cong undermined U.S. strategy: The Viet Cong avoided direct confrontations and used ambushes and booby traps. U.S. troops were unprepared for asymmetric warfare in dense jungle terrain. U.S. technology and firepower were often ineffective against elusive guerrilla tactics.\n- Alienation of the civilian population: Bombing campaigns and destruction of villages pushed civilians toward supporting the Viet Cong. Tactics like the use of napalm and Agent Orange caused long-term environmental and human harm. Events like the My Lai Massacre (1968) damaged U.S. credibility and intensified anti-war sentiment.\n- Failure to cut off the Ho Chi Minh Trail: Despite intense bombing, the supply route through Laos and Cambodia remained operational. North Vietnam adapted with effective camouflage and constant rebuilding efforts.\n- Tet Offensive (1968): Militarily, the U.S. and South Vietnamese forces repelled the attacks. Strategically, it was a major propaganda victory for the North. Shattered public perception of U.S. progress in the war and eroded political will.\n- Vietnamization policy (1969 onwards): Attempted to train and equip South Vietnamese forces to take over combat roles. Lacked long-term effectiveness due to corruption, poor morale, and inadequate training in the ARVN (Army of the Republic of Vietnam). Viewed as a way for the U.S. to exit rather than achieve victory.\n- Strategic Hamlet Program (1962-1964): U.S.-backed counterinsurgency initiative to isolate civilians from Viet Cong influence and build loyalty to the South Vietnamese regime. Often involved forced relocations, generating resentment among peasants. The Viet Cong were often able to infiltrate the hamlets or exploit them for propaganda purposes. Reflected broader failures in U.S. understanding of Vietnamese society and nationalism.\n\nBroader strategic failures:\n- Lack of understanding of Vietnamese nationalism and culture: U.S. policy framed the conflict as a Cold War battle against communism. Underestimated the strength of nationalist motivations among North Vietnamese and the Viet Cong. Failed to win the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hearts and minds\" of the South Vietnamese population.\n- Limited domestic support over time: Initial support eroded due to rising casualties, media coverage, and lack of clear progress. Anti-war protests and the credibility gap pressured politicians to seek withdrawal. Strategy was unsustainable without domestic political backing.\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, U.S. strategies such as Operation Rolling Thunder and helicopter deployment offered short-term tactical gains but failed to achieve strategic objectives. ($\\checkmark$). Bombing campaigns did not stop supply routes like the Ho Chi Minh Trail, and although helicopters provided mobility, they could not secure lasting control over territory or defeat guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the U.S. strategy of attrition, focused on body counts, misjudged the determination of the Viet Cong and North Vietnamese Army ($\\checkmark$) Despite high casualties, enemy morale remained strong. This approach ignored the nature of guerrilla warfare and resulted in costly battles that failed to produce meaningful long-term outcomes ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, tactics such as search-and-destroy missions, napalm, and Agent Orange alienated civilians and boosted Viet Cong support ($\\checkmark$). Events like the My Lai Massacre further damaged U.S. credibility. Later, Vietnamization failed due to ARVN weakness, becoming a withdrawal strategy rather than a path to sustainable victory ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, despite technological advantages, U.S. military strategies were ultimately ineffective. They failed to adapt to guerrilla warfare, alienated the civilian population, and could not build a stable South Vietnamese force. This lack of strategic flexibility and cultural understanding contributed directly to the U.S. failure in Vietnam ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0905","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0905\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of the military strategies of the North Vietnamese and the Viet Cong in the Vietnam War (1956-1975).\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a conclusion:\n\nHighly effective guerrilla warfare tactics:\n- Use of guerrilla tactics by the Viet Cong: Avoided direct confrontation with superior U.S. forces. Relied on hit-and-run attacks, ambushes, booby traps, and sniper fire. Created a constant psychological strain on U.S. and ARVN troops. Frustrated conventional military strategy and prevented U.S. forces from gaining territorial control.\n- Blending in with the civilian population: Viet Cong operated without uniforms, making it hard to distinguish them from non-combatants. Enabled surprise attacks and minimized exposure to enemy firepower. Led to U.S. mistrust of civilians, fueling brutal countermeasures that alienated local populations.\n- Effective use of underground tunnel networks: Complex tunnel systems (e.g., Cu Chi tunnels) used for hiding, moving, and storing supplies. Enabled Viet Cong to launch attacks and disappear before U.S. forces could retaliate. Tunnels also served as living quarters, hospitals, and command centers.\n\nStrong strategic coordination and supply lines:\n- Ho Chi Minh Trail: Vital supply route through Laos and Cambodia connecting North to South. Despite heavy U.S. bombing, it was constantly repaired and expanded, often by hand. Enabled the steady flow of troops, weapons, and supplies to Viet Cong forces in the South. Demonstrated logistical resilience and commitment.\n- North-South coordination between NVA and Viet Cong: North Vietnam provided training, leadership, and strategic planning. Allowed for major joint offensives like the Tet Offensive (1968) and Easter Offensive (1972).\n\nPsychological and ideological effectiveness:\n- Strong ideological motivation: Framed the war as a struggle for national liberation from foreign imperialists. Gained popular support among peasants and urban nationalists alike. Morale remained high despite massive casualties.\n- Propaganda and political education: Used to motivate fighters and civilians to endure hardship.n Portrayed the U.S. and the South Vietnamese government as corrupt and oppressive.\n\nStrategic offensives and timing:\n- Tet Offensive (1968): Coordinated surprise attacks on over 100 cities and military bases during Vietnamese New Year. Temporarily seized U.S. Embassy in Saigon, shocking American public. Military defeat for the Viet Cong, but strategic success, undermining U.S. claims of progress. Turned American public opinion against the war and led to policy shifts (Vietnamization).\n- Easter Offensive (1972): Large-scale conventional invasion by the NVA. Tested Vietnamization and strained ARVN capabilities. Although repelled with U.S. air support, it showed that North Vietnam could mount conventional offensives and wear down Southern defenses.\n- Final Ho Chi Minh Campaign (1975): Rapid and well-coordinated offensive that led to the fall of Saigon. Took advantage of the collapse in ARVN morale and reduced U.S. support. Demonstrated strategic patience and long-term planning by North Vietnamese leadership.\n\nLimitations and costs of their strategy:\n- High casualty rates: North Vietnam and the Viet Cong were willing to accept extremely high losses. The war effort depended on mass mobilization and sacrifice, which could have failed without sustained morale and ideological commitment.\n- Dependence on Soviet and Chinese support: Relied heavily on military aid from communist allies. While effective, it showed they couldn't have sustained large-scale warfare without foreign backing.\n\nConclusion:\n- Strengths: Adaptability, resilience, ideological commitment, use of terrain and local knowledge, long-term strategic thinking, and political unity. Effectively undermined U.S. military superiority and outlasted American political will.\n- Weaknesses: Extremely high casualties, dependency on external aid, and occasional military failures (e.g., post-Tet heavy losses).\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Viet Cong avoided direct battles, instead relying on guerrilla tactics such as ambushes, booby traps, and hit-and-run attacks ($\\checkmark$). These methods wore down U.S. and ARVN forces both physically and psychologically. Their ability to blend with civilians made them difficult to detect, disrupting U.S. operations and complicating counterinsurgency efforts. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nSecondly, the Ho Chi Minh Trail was a crucial logistical success, enabling North Vietnam to sustain the Viet Cong in the South ($\\checkmark$). Despite intense U.S. bombing, it was constantly rebuilt, showing remarkable organization and endurance. Close coordination between the Viet Cong and North Vietnamese Army also allowed for major joint offensives when needed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, while the North suffered high casualty rates, their fighters remained committed due to strong nationalist and ideological motivation ($\\checkmark$). The willingness to endure enormous losses demonstrated the depth of their resolve. This moral strength allowed them to continue fighting long after the U.S. expected them to collapse, ultimately outlasting their opponent ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, North Vietnamese and Viet Cong military strategies were highly effective. Their use of guerrilla tactics, logistical resilience, and ideological commitment allowed them to counter superior U.S. firepower. Despite heavy losses, their adaptability and endurance proved decisive in achieving reunification and forcing the withdrawal of American forces ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0907","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0907\n\nDescribe **three** examples of guerrilla tactics used by the Viet Minh against French forces during the French Indochina War (1946-1954).\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nNature of Guerrilla Warfare\n- Avoidance of conventional battles: Viet Minh rarely engaged French forces in large-scale open combat unless the odds were favorable. Focused on wearing down the enemy over time.\n- Small, mobile combat units: Operated in small, flexible groups that could strike quickly and retreat before French forces could respond.\n- Hit-and-run attacks: Targeted French patrols, outposts, and convoys to cause continuous disruption and psychological stress.\n- Ambushes and surprise raids: Took advantage of terrain and local knowledge to ambush isolated or vulnerable French units. Often occurred in remote or jungle-covered areas.\n- Use of booby traps and mines: Simple yet effective traps slowed down French movement and demoralized troops.\n\nUse of Terrain and Local Support\n- Familiarity with the terrain: Viet Minh fighters used Vietnam's dense forests, mountains, and river systems to their advantage.\n- Extensive tunnel networks and hidden supply routes: Enabled fighters to hide, store weapons, and launch attacks undetected.\n- Blending in with local populations: Fighters wore no uniforms and relied on the support of rural villagers for shelter, supplies, and information. Made it difficult for the French to distinguish between combatants and civilians.\n- Use of local militias ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people's war\"): Mobilized ordinary citizens to assist in guerrilla operations and create a climate of constant resistance.\n\nTactics of Attrition and Psychological Warfare\n- Gradual weakening of French forces (war of attrition): Persistent guerrilla attacks slowly wore down French morale and resources. Forced the French to spread their troops thin across large rural areas.\n- Psychological impact on French soldiers: Constant threat of ambush or booby traps led to fatigue and fear. Contributed to a growing sense that the war was unwinnable.\n- Disruption of French supply lines and communication: Guerrilla fighters often attacked roads, bridges, and convoys to isolate French outposts and restrict movement\n\nEvolution Over Time and Strategic Impact\n- Gradual shift to more conventional tactics: By the early 1950s, especially under General Giap, Viet Minh forces began incorporating larger, more coordinated attacks (e.g., Dien Bien Phu), but guerrilla tactics remained foundational.\n- Enabled long-term resistance: Guerrilla warfare allowed the Viet Minh to sustain their campaign over eight years despite being outgunned and out-financed.\n- Forced the French to overextend: The French were drawn into remote areas where they were vulnerable and lacked local support.\n- Helped pave the way for key victories: Constant pressure from guerrilla warfare contributed to weakening French positions leading up to the decisive Battle of Dien Bien Phu in 1954.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Viet Minh relied on guerrilla tactics that deliberately avoided large-scale battles unless they had a clear advantage. Instead, they operated in small, mobile units capable of launching hit-and-run attacks and surprise raids. These tactics kept French forces constantly off-balance, making it difficult for them to engage the enemy directly or maintain territorial control ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the Viet Minh exploited their terrain and local support. Familiarity with Vietnam's challenging geography, its forests, rivers, and mountains, allowed the Viet Minh to launch attacks and disappear quickly. They used simple tunnel systems and hidden supply routes to store arms and regroup in secret. By blending in with local villagers and relying on their support for supplies and intelligence, they made it nearly impossible for the French to distinguish between fighters and civilians ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the Viet Minh aimed to wear down French strength over time. Frequent attacks on French patrols, infrastructure, and supply routes strained resources and forced the French to spread their troops across vulnerable areas. The constant threat of ambushes and traps had a powerful psychological effect, lowering morale and contributing to growing doubts about the war's viability($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0908","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0908\n\nOutline the significance of the Battle of Dien Bien Phu (1954) for both the Viet Minh and French colonial rule in Indochina.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**Four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both the Viet Minh and French perspectives are addressed.\n\nSignificance for the Viet Minh:\n- Decisive military victory: Demonstrated the Viet Minh's ability to defeat a major colonial power in a large-scale conventional battle. Boosted morale and validated General Vo Nguyen Giap's military strategy of transitioning from guerrilla warfare to set-piece battles.\n- Proof of military and logistical capability: Successfully transported heavy artillery and maintained supply lines in remote mountainous terrain. Showed strategic sophistication and determination.\n- Strengthened Ho Chi Minh's political position: Gave the Viet Minh greater international legitimacy as the primary representative of Vietnamese nationalism and resistance. Bolstered Ho Chi Minh's role as a leader of a future independent Vietnam.\n- Catalyst for diplomatic negotiations: Forced France to the negotiating table, leading directly to the Geneva Conference. Enabled the Viet Minh to secure control over North Vietnam as part of the Geneva Accords.\n- Symbol of anti-colonial resistance: Inspired nationalist and anti-imperialist movements throughout Asia and Africa. Became a rallying point for the global decolonization movement.\n\nSignificance for French Colonial Rule:\n- End of French military presence in Indochina: Marked the collapse of France's attempt to reassert colonial control in Vietnam after WWII. Led to France's withdrawal from Indochina entirely.\n- Psychological and political shock in France: Humiliating defeat that damaged national pride and exposed the limits of colonial power. Contributed to political instability in the French Fourth Republic.\n- Loss of colonial credibility: Undermined France's reputation as a colonial power and hastened the decline of its empire. Signaled to other colonies that colonial regimes could be defeated militarily.\n- Trigger for the Geneva Accords (1954): Forced France to negotiate a ceasefire and temporary division of Vietnam at the 17th parallel. Marked a transition from colonial conflict to Cold War confrontation in Southeast Asia.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFor the Viet Minh, the battle was a decisive military victory that showcased their growing strength and strategic capability. Under General Vo Nguyen Giap, the Viet Minh surrounded and overwhelmed a well-defended French base in a remote valley, demonstrating their ability to conduct large-scale operations and transport heavy artillery through difficult terrain ($\\checkmark$). This victory boosted morale and solidified Ho Chi Minh's political leadership, while also forcing France to the negotiating table at the Geneva Conference ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFor France, the defeat was a devastating blow to its colonial ambitions. It exposed the limits of French military power and led to the end of French involvement in Vietnam ($\\checkmark$). The loss also shocked the French public, contributed to political instability at home, and hastened the decline of the French empire ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1047","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1047\n\nTo what extent did foreign intervention determine the outcome of **two** 20th-century wars?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- The primary focus of the responses should be the role of foreign intervention, but counterarguments should also be present to address the “extent” element.\n- **Two** factors should be analyzed to answer this question, besides foreign intervention. Possibilities include the role of technology, Ideology and the mobilization of human and economic resources.\n- A comparative analysis must be attempted since the question asks for two examples.\n- The role of foreign intervention is present in civil wars, so these are also viable choices.\n\n**Indicative content for Russia and Cuba:**\n\nForeign intervention\n- In Russia\n    - Foreign intervention was supportive of the Whites and it provided both men and supplies\n    - Foreign intervention ended up undermining the Whites as they were accused of being “puppets” of the imperialist countries of the West as well as Japan, an enemy of Russia since the Russo-Japanese War.\n- In Cuba\n    - The US provided weapons to the Batista regime until 1958, which were subsequently stolen by the M-26-7 in raids and used by the guerrillas\n    - The US supplied Cuba with planes, ships, tanks, and other technology, such as napalm, which was used against the rebels. In 1958, it stopped supporting the regime and introduced an arms embargo against Batista's regime.\n    - According to Tad Szulc, the United States began funding the 26th of July Movement around October 1957 and ended around mid-1958, though this is somewhat contested.\n\nMobilization of human and economic resources\n- In Russia\n    - Conscription introduced in 1918 by the Bolsheviks\n    - War Communism was used to supply the Red Army. This was effective in supplying the troops but generated a famine that led to rebellions.\n    - Political officers in each Red Army unit were tasked to provide “political education” and make sure of the loyalty of soldiers and officers\n    - Former Tsarist officers were appointed to lead the units of the Red Army.\n    - The use and application of conventional warfare\n- In Cuba\n    - M-26-7 members in Cuba formed secret underground cells to help support Castro's return. Frank Paìs established one such group in Santiago de Cuba. These secret groups began stockpiling weapons and ammunition as well as printing anti-regime propaganda\n    - In Mexico, Castro had secretly trained a fighting force of 82 volunteers who would sail with him on his mission to liberate Cuba (known as the Granma Expedition), which was funded by a variety of anti-Batista sources.\n    - Up until 1958, Castro's guerrilla forces remained quite small, sometimes fewer than 200 men, while the Cuban army and police force had a manpower of around 37,000.\n    - The use and application of guerrilla warfare\n\nRole of technology\n- In Russia\n    - Conventional battles with war hardy soldiers but few weapons due to lack of domestic production\n    - Tachanka\n    - Railroads were crucial during the Civil War, as they were the fastest way to transport supplies and troops. The Reds controlled railroad hubs, which helped them connect the areas under their control.\n    - Artillery saw limited use, as ammunition was in limited supply due both lack of production capacities and logistical difficulties.\n- In Cuba\n    - Radio Rebelde transmitted around Cuba using a mobile antenna that could be hidden and reassembled covertly.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nForeign intervention in civil wars is a common phenomenon as countries support opposing sides in the conflict either for ideological purposes and/or to keep a degree of influence over the country at war that had been enjoyed so far. This was the case in both the Russian Civil War (1917-1922) and the Cuban Revolution (1953-1959) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). While foreign intervention in both cases provided temporary advantages to counterrevolutionary forces, it often proved counterproductive ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The mobilization of human and economic resources, as well as the use of technology, were far more critical to determine both the victory of the 26th of July Movement (M-26-7) against Batista in Cuba and of the Bolsheviks (Reds) in their struggle against the Whites in Russia ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$). This essay will seek to determine the relative importance of each of these factors ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nEven though foreign intervention was aimed at helping win the war, its impact on the outcome of the war was the opposite of what was intended by the countries that assisted both the Whites and Batista ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Russia, the Whites received foreign support from the Allied powers, who provided weapons and even troops.  England sent £100 million to buy supplies and the Czechoslovak Legion, for example, consisted of upwards of 50,000 men who at times were able to control the entire Trans-Siberian railway and several major cities in Siberia. At the peak of its intervention, there were roughly 70,000 Japanese Imperial troops in the Russian Far East  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). However, this assistance undermined the White cause, as Bolshevik propaganda painted them as “puppets” of foreign imperialists, particularly Japan, which had already humiliated Russia in the Russo-Japanese War of 1904-1905 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Thus, the intervention backfired, consolidating Bolshevik legitimacy among Russians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Cuba, the United States had supported Batista's control over the island since 1933. In the eyes of Cubans, Batista's dictatorship was tied to US economic domination of their country ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). When the revolutionary war started in 1953, it was natural for the US to supply him with weapons, planes, ships, and even napalm, which was used against Castro's guerrillas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Like the Russian Civil War, this foreign support ended up undermining the legitimacy of Batista ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Part of the appeal of the M-26-7 was precisely its call for the reduction of US economic dominance and the enjoyment of natural resources by the Cuban people. Moreover, by 1958, Washington imposed an arms embargo, effectively cutting Batista off from further resources. With this embargo, Batista had little hope for success, especially if the U.S. was covertly aiding Castro's 26th of July Movement for a brief period between 1957 and 1958, as reported by American journalist Tad Szulc ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In both cases, foreign intervention weakened the side they were supporting rather than strengthening it, as reliance on external powers gave both the Reds and Castro the opportunity to gain broader support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nA factor that was much more important for the outcome of both revolutions was the mobilization of human and economic resources ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Russia, the Bolsheviks drew on the massive population of the former empire by introducing conscription in 1918. This allowed the Red Army to grow from a few thousand men to half a million by August 1918 and 5 million in 1920 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The Whites, who did not control the state apparatus that had remained from Tsarist times, could not muster such a force and relied on volunteers and drafts on the sparsely populated areas they controlled  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). At the height of its power, the number of soldiers barely surpassed 1 million. By comparison, the M-26-7's fighting force was always smaller than its rival's. Up until 1958, Castro's forces remained quite small in numbers, sometimes fewer than 200 men, while the Cuban army and police force had a manpower of around 37,000 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). It would seem that this disadvantage in numbers would spell out defeat. However, while much of the fighting in the Russian Civil War involved conventional battles where numbers were important, Cuba's Revolutionary War was fought using guerrilla tactics. In this case, knowledge of the terrain as well as support of the local population would determine the outcome of the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).  Besides, the mobilization of human resources implies not only the amount of active combatants but also the mobilization of the population in general in support of the war. In the case of Russia, this was done through the policy of War Communism which, even though it ended up having disastrous consequences for the Russian people and had to be abandoned in 1921, was able to keep the Red Army supplied ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In Cuba, support for the rebels was organized by secret groups in the cities that provided resources, printed anti-regime propaganda, and prepared the way for popular uprisings in support of Castro's cause ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Sabotage and the dissemination of propaganda were key parts of the M-26-7's strategy to destabilize the Batista regime. Urban organizations like the Student Revolutionary Directoriate were key for this ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In both the Russian Civil War and Cuban Revolution, support of the people, whether coerced through conscription and War Communism by the Reds or voluntarily organized like the support for the M-26-7, was key in determining the outcome ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nFinally, technology had a limited impact on the result of both wars ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In both cases, the countries were relatively poor and predominantly rural so technology was not widely available ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In the case of Russia, it can be argued that the Reds' control of most of the railroad system for a great part of the war allowed them considerable advantage, especially because White armies were often far away from each other, and couldn't coordinate effectively due to the lack of communication between them. The tachanka, a horse-drawn cart with a mounted machine gun, and some use of artillery also contributed to the Red Army's effectiveness, despite shortages of modern equipment and ammunition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In Cuba, by contrast, guerrilla warfare replaced conventional strategies. Castro's forces compensated for their lack of heavy weapons by using innovative technology, particularly through Radio Rebelde, which broadcast propaganda across the island and bolstered support in rural and urban areas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Many of the weapons the US had provided to Batista were eventually stolen and recycled by the rebels, turning Batista's own resources against him. Thus, while Russian success was linked to conventional military infrastructure like railroads, Cuban success derived from adapting guerrilla tactics to a technologically uneven battlefield ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, in both the Russian Civil War and the Cuban Revolutionary War foreign intervention did little to determine the outcome of the conflicts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Its small impact was also the opposite of what was intended by the intervening powers and the forces they supported in the conflict. The mobilization of human and economic resources was much more important both for the victory of the Reds, who far outweighed the Whites both in men and resources, and for the victory of the 26th of July Movement, which was able to harness the support of the population as well as to make the most of their limited resources through guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). Technology also had a limited role in the wars, being more important in the case of Russia through control of the railways by the Reds, who were able to capitalize on one of the weaknesses of the Whites ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"HI0023","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0023\n\nDefine *Blitzkrieg* and explain how its tactics contributed to Germany’s early successes in the Second World War. [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny of the following (**up to one**) for definition: \n\n- A military tactic meaning “lightning war” involving fast, coordinated attacks using infantry, tanks, and air power.\n\nA strategy designed to quickly overwhelm the enemy through rapid, concentrated strikes.\n\nAny of the following (**up to two**) for explanation:\n\n- *Blitzkrieg* allowed Germany to catch enemies by surprise, leading to swift victories and minimal German casualties.\n\nThe tactic overwhelmed unprepared and slower-moving armies, such as Poland in 1939 and France in 1940.\n\n- *Blitzkrieg*'s coordination between airstrikes and ground forces disorganised enemy defences, making counter attacks difficult.\n\nIt helped Germany capture key territories quickly, destabilising opposing nations' war efforts in the early stages of the war.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n\n*Blitzkrieg* is a military tactic that involves fast, sudden attacks combining air strikes, tanks, and ground troops to overwhelm the enemy before they can effectively respond ($\\checkmark$). This tactic was crucial to Germany’s early victories because it took their opponents by surprise, such as in the invasions of Poland and France ($\\checkmark$). The speed and coordination between air and ground forces disrupted enemy defences, preventing them from organising counterattacks ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0066","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0066\n\nBased on the information in the source, identify **three** reasons why Senate Republicans rejected the Treaty of Versailles.\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the webpage of the United States Senate. This extract describes information pertaining to the Senate’s refusal to ratify the Treaty of Versailles on November 19, 1919.   |\n\n[Source: No author. (2015). Senate Rejects the Treaty of Versailles. United States Senate. Retrieved September 19, 2024 from https://www.senate.gov/about/powers-procedures/treaties/senate-rejects-treaty-of-versailles.htm. Public domain.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Wilson's decision to exclude senators from the Paris Peace Conference angered Senate Republicans, particularly Lodge.\n- Despite Lodge's committee making reservations to the treaty, Wilson refused to compromise, further alienating Senate Republicans.\n- Wilson and Lodge's long-standing dislike for each other contributed to Lodge's opposition.\n- Wilson's idealistic approach of a “peace without victory” contrasted with Lodge's desire for Germany's unconditional surrender, creating division.\n- Wilson's open hostility towards Lodge and other Senate Republicans, calling them \"contemptible, narrow, selfish, poor little minds,\" likely deepened the animosity and reduced the chances of cooperation, contributing to the rejection of the treaty.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Senate Republicans rejected the Treaty of Versailles mainly due to three factors. First, the long-standing personal rivalry between Wilson and Lodge fueled mistrust and hindered cooperation ($\\checkmark$). Second, Wilson's exclusion of senators from the peace negotiations frustrated the Senate, particularly when he made the results public without consulting them ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, their differing visions for post-war peace, with Wilson seeking a more lenient settlement and Lodge demanding stricter terms, widened the divide, leading to the treaty's rejection ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0072","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0072\n\nWhat does the source suggest about the consequences of British and French actions for Czechoslovakia?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A speech by Winston Churchill in the British House of Commons 5 October 1938.\n\n[Source: Churchill, W. (5 October 1938). Disaster of the First Magnitude. America's National Churchill Museum. Retrived on 12 October 2024 from https://www.nationalchurchillmuseum.org/disaster-of-the-first-magnitude.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Churchill suggests that Czechoslovakia has been abandoned by the Western democracies, leading to its suffering.\n\n - He describes Czechoslovakia as being politically mutilated and economically devastated after the Munich Agreement.\n\n - Churchill predicts that Czechoslovakia will soon lose its independence and be absorbed into Nazi Germany.\n\n - He criticizes Britain and France for failing to stop the rise of Nazi power, which he believes directly contributed to Czechoslovakia's plight.\n\n - Churchill emphasizes that Britain and France's policy of appeasement has led to a major disaster, impacting Czechoslovakia and all of Central and Eastern Europe.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer:}}$\n\n\\\n\nChurchill suggests that Czechoslovakia has suffered greatly because of the failure of the Western democracies, particularly Britain and France, to support it properly ($\\checkmark$). He states that the country is now \"politically mutilated\" and facing economic devastation due to the loss of territory and resources ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Churchill predicts that Czechoslovakia will soon lose its independence entirely, being absorbed into the Nazi regime as a consequence of British and French inaction ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0075","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0075\n\nWhat is the message conveyed by the source?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | From David Low, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Increasing Pressure\" Published in the London Evening Standard 18 Feb 1938. The given labeled Britain is saying “Why should we take a stand about someone pushing someone else when it’s all so far away…”\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0075.png\" alt=\"HI0075\">\n\n]\n\n[Source: Low, David. (18 February 1938). *Increasing Pressure*. London Evening Standard. Retrieved 29 October 2024  from https://wwwarchive.cartoons.ac.uk/GetMultimedia.ashx?db=Catalog&type=default&fname=LSE2494.jpg. Usage request sent October 30, 2024.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The cartoon conveys that Nazi Germany, represented by the soldier, is increasingly pressuring neighboring countries, foreshadowing Germany's plans for territorial expansion. This is particularly relevant to Austria and Czechoslovakia, which were soon to fall under German influence.\n\n - The cartoon criticizes Britain and France's policy of appeasement, suggesting that by failing to take action, they allow Hitler to apply unchecked pressure on European states, ultimately enabling Nazi aggression.\n\n - The cartoon warns of an inevitable escalation toward conflict due to Germany's actions. The increasing pressure implies that continued appeasement may lead to war, as Hitler's ambitions will not be satisfied without aggressive expansion, which will destabilize Europe.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe cartoon by David Low conveys the message that Germany, symbolized by the large soldier pushing against smaller nations, is aggressively exerting pressure on neighboring European countries. This illustrates Nazi Germany's expansionist ambitions, particularly aimed at countries like Austria and Czechoslovakia, and shows the growing dominance and threat posed by Hitler's regime ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the British figures on the left, holding a basket labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$British Empire\" and discussing the issue as something distant, suggest a critique of British inaction. This implies Low's disapproval of the British policy of appeasement, as they watch passively instead of intervening, which ultimately allows Hitler to continue his aggressive tactics unchecked ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0076","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0076\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the values and limitations of this source for a historian studying Britain's policy of appeasement.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from a letter from Nevile Henderson, British Ambassador in Germany, September 6th, 1938. Henderson has been described by historians as a supporter of appeasement.\n\n[Source: Henderson, Nevile. (6 September 1938) *European situation: Sudeten-German problem*. Code 18 File 1941 (papers 9619 - 9884). (FO 371/21737). Retrieved from https://nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/chamberlain-and-hitler. UK Open Government Licence.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n\n - The source's origin is valuable because it comes from Nevile Henderson, the British Ambassador to Germany, who had direct contact with Nazi officials and firsthand insight into British-German relations. His position allows historians to understand the perspectives and recommendations of a senior diplomat actively involved in Britains policy of appeasement.\n\n - The purpose of the source, to advise the British government on handling Hitler through positive media portrayal, provides valuable insight into the strategies employed in appeasement. It reveals a preference for influencing Hitler diplomatically rather than through confrontation, which is crucial for understanding British efforts to avoid war.\n\n - The content of the source, suggesting that the British press avoid criticizing Hitler and instead focus on presenting him positively, is valuable as it captures the British diplomatic mindset at the time. It illustrates the hope that diplomatic gestures might encourage Hitler toward peace, offering insight into appeasement strategies.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n\n - As an ambassador, Henderson's perspective may reflect his personal biases and diplomatic objectives, which could skew his assessment. His views might not represent the entire British government's stance, making it a limited source for understanding broader British policy beyond his own pro-appeasement viewpoint.\n\n - The purpose is also a limitation, as it reflects an attempt to sway government and press strategy rather than an objective analysis of Hitler's intentions. Henderson's goal to portray Hitler as a potential $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$apostle of Peace\" may reveal wishful thinking rather than a realistic assessment, thus potentially misrepresenting the Nazi threat.\n\n - The content is limited as it reflects only Henderson's optimistic perspective on influencing Hitler through appeasement. His suggestion to avoid criticizing Hitler directly may overlook the true extent of Hitler's aggression, thus potentially downplaying the risks of appeasement for contemporary historians.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of this source, written by Nevile Henderson, the British Ambassador to Germany, is valuable because Henderson had firsthand experience with Nazi officials and was deeply involved in British-German relations. His perspective provides a unique window into the British diplomatic approach at the time, especially as he was a senior figure actively trying to influence the course of events ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the document—to recommend that the British press portray Hitler positively—reveals a strategic attempt to use media as a tool to steer Hitler toward peace, offering insight into the broader rationale behind appeasement policies ($\\checkmark$ VP). Furthermore, the content, which suggests avoiding direct criticism of Hitler and instead presenting him as an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$apostle of Peace,” reflects British hopes that diplomacy and public messaging might discourage Nazi aggression, shedding light on Britain's cautious approach during this period ($\\checkmark$ VC). \n\nHowever, the source has limitations due to its origin, as Henderson's personal position and pro-appeasement stance may color his views, meaning this source might not fully represent other, perhaps more skeptical, voices within the British government ($\\checkmark$ LO). The purpose of advising the government to use the press to influence Hitler could be seen as overly optimistic, as it reflects Henderson's belief that media portrayal could sway Hitler's actions, which might not align with the reality of Hitler's ambitions ($\\checkmark$ LP). Lastly, the content's suggestion to avoid criticizing Hitler directly could underestimate the true threat of Nazi Germany, as Henderson's approach may reflect wishful thinking about Hitler's potential for peace rather than a realistic assessment, thereby presenting a potentially misleading view of the risks of appeasement ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0079","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0079\n\nExplain why Jiang Jieshi said the phrase, “The Japanese are a disease of the skin; the Communists are a disease of the heart.”\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of the following ideas:\n- He thought that the Japanese were not a real threat to China, the real problem were the Communists.\n- He felt it was not necessary to fight the Japanese, they would go away on their own. The GMD must eliminate the CCP first.\n- He said this because the Japanese had invaded Manchuria in 1931 and the GMD had not tried to repel the invasion.\n- He said this because he was trying to eliminate the CCP during the first phase of the Chinese Civil War (1927-1937).\n- He said this to justify the GMD`s inaction in the face of Japanese aggression.\n- He had received military training in the USSR and knew that Communism posed a threat to Chinese traditional culture.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nJiang Jieshi decided to concentrate his efforts on fighting the CCP ($\\checkmark$) during the first phase of the Chinese Civil War ($\\checkmark$) even though the Japanese had invaded Manchuria in 1931 and were extending their control ($\\checkmark$). This is because he thought the real threat to China's existence were the Communists ($\\checkmark$) since they wanted to change Chinà`s social structure ($\\checkmark$). His policy of eliminating the CCP instead of fighting the Japanese brought him widespread criticism so with this phrase he was trying to explain his priorities ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0080","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0080\n\nEvaluate the significance of the Long March for the Chinese Communist Party (CCP).\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nSince the command term is “evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than **four** marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion.\n\nThe following ideas may be present in the answer to address significance:\n\nPositive results:\n- It allowed the CCP to survive the first phase of the Chinese Civil War.\n- It brought the CCP close to the areas subject to the Japanese invasion.\n- It brought the CCP to an area with limited GMD presence so they could start implementing their policies in the areas under their control.\n- It left Mao as the only surviving leader of the CCP.\n- It put an end to Jiang Jieshi`s encirclement campaigns.\n- It served as great propaganda material having covered 9,600km of China's most inhospitable territory in little over a year.\n\nNegative results:\n- The CCP had to retreat in the face of GMD pressure.\n- It led to the loss of more than 90% of the Communists that had been fighting the GMD in the Jiangxi Soviet.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- Responses are likely to conclude that the overall results of the Long March were positive for the CCP especially in the long-term.\n- The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Long March was a threshold moment in the history of the CCP as it allowed the CCP to survive ($\\checkmark$) and start implementing the reforms that would bring them the peasants' support ($\\checkmark$). Going north put them in a position where they would have to fight the Japanese and thus be perceived as saviors of the nation ($\\checkmark$) especially as the GMD was not viewed as doing its job of defending the country from foreign aggression. Meanwhile, the GMD now had to think of a new strategy to fight the CCP since the encirclement campaigns had failed ($\\checkmark$). The Long March also cemented Mao's position within the CCP as most of his rivals died during the trek ($\\checkmark$) and, in later years, it would serve as the basis of propaganda that portrayed Mao as its hero ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the CCP was accepting that it could not defeat the GMD in the south and was essentially running away with the Long March ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, it caused considerable damage to CCP's forces with nearly 90% lost to either death or abandonment ($\\checkmark$).\n\nEven though the Long March was the result of initial weakness and it meant a huge loss of life for the CCP  it still had an overall positive impact. The March placed the Party on its path to victory under the undisputed leadership of Mao and in a new territory where they could implement their policies as well as defend the nation from Japanese aggression ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0081","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0081\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of the source for a historian studying the Long March.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\\\$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  *Excerpt from Report by Mao Tse-tung at a Communist Party Conference*, December 27th, 1935.  |\n\n:box[The Long March is a manifesto. It has proclaimed to the world that the Red Army is an army of heroes, while the imperialists and their running dogs, Chiang Kai-shek [Jiang Jieshi] and his like, are impotent. It has proclaimed their utter failure to encircle, pursue, obstruct and intercept us. The Long March is also a propaganda force. It has announced to some 200 million people in eleven provinces that the road of the Red Army is their only road to liberation. Without the Long March, how could the broad masses have learned so quickly about the existence of the great truth which the Red Army embodies? The Long March is also a seeding-machine. In the eleven provinces it has sown many seeds which will sprout, leaf, blossom, and bear fruit, and will yield a harvest in the future. In a word, the Long March has ended with victory for us and defeat for the enemy. Who brought the Long March to victory? The Communist Party. Without the Communist Party, a long march of this kind would have been inconceivable. (...) A new situation arose as soon as the Long March was over. In the battle of Chihlochen the Central Red Army and the Northwestern Red Army, fighting in fraternal solidarity, shattered the traitor Chiang Kai-shek's campaign of \"encirclement and suppression\" against the Shensi-Kansu border area and thus laid the cornerstone for the task undertaken by the Central Committee of the Party, the task of setting up the national headquarters of the revolution in northwestern China. \\\n]\n\n[Source: Mao, Tse-tung (1935, December 27th). *On Tactics Against Japanese Imperialism*. Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved September 26th, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-1/mswv1_11.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nIf only values or limitations are discussed, the response cannot earn more than **two** marks. There must be at least one reference to origin, purpose and content in either value or limitations to reach **four** marks.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n |  | Value |Limitations|\n | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Origin** | Mao has participated in the Long March and therefore has insight. | Mao was responsible for taking the decision to undergo the Long March and therefore is biased in its favor.|\n| **Purpose** | To inform the Communist Party Conference about the impacts of the Long March. Since the purpose is to inform, Mao is giving reliable information.  |To persuade the Communist Party Conference about how positive for the Revolution the Long March was. Since the purpose is to persuade, he is exaggerating certain points and leaving out others as can be seen in the content.  |\n| **Content** | It lets us know about the immediate impacts of the Long March, like the fact that it served as a way of spreading communist ideas. It also lets us know that the Communists are not planning to set up their headquarters in northwestern China. |It does not mention any of the negative impacts of the Long March. For example, it does not mention the number of casualties. For a historian studying the Long March, the source leaves out the events of the March itself. |\n\n[4 marks]\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThis source is valuable in its origin since it`s made by one of the leaders of the Communist forces during the Long March itself so he has insight into the situation ($\\checkmark$). However, since Mao was the one that decided to undertake the Long March, he is inclined in favor of it ($\\checkmark$). Since he is speaking to the Communist Party Conference the purpose is limited as he is trying to convince the Conference that the Long March was a good idea ($\\checkmark$). However, since he is speaking at a Conference of people that belong to his own party, and are on his side during the ongoing civil war, he needs to tell them what actually happened so the Party can make informed decisions ($\\checkmark$) so this element of the purpose does provide some value to an historian studying the Long March. When talking about content, while the source does mention many of the positive consequences of the Long March ($\\checkmark$), like it being a great propaganda tool, it leaves out the negative consequences of the March and so a historian studying the Long March will not get the full picture of its impacts ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0082","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0082\n\nCompare and contrast what sources A and B reveal about the Long March.\n[6 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\\\$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  *Excerpt from Report by Mao Tse-tung at a Communist Party Conference*, December 27th, 1935.  |\n\n:box[“The Long March is a manifesto. It has proclaimed to the world that the Red Army is an army of heroes, while the imperialists and their running dogs, Chiang Kai-shek [Jiang Jieshi] and his like, are impotent. It has proclaimed their utter failure to encircle, pursue, obstruct and intercept us. The Long March is also a propaganda force. It has announced to some 200 million people in eleven provinces that the road of the Red Army is their only road to liberation. Without the Long March, how could the broad masses have learned so quickly about the existence of the great truth which the Red Army embodies? The Long March is also a seeding-machine. In the eleven provinces it has sown many seeds which will sprout, leaf, blossom, and bear fruit, and will yield a harvest in the future. In a word, the Long March has ended with victory for us and defeat for the enemy. Who brought the Long March to victory? The Communist Party. Without the Communist Party, a long march of this kind would have been inconceivable. (...) A new situation arose as soon as the Long March was over. In the battle of Chihlochen the Central Red Army and the Northwestern Red Army, fighting in fraternal solidarity, shattered the traitor Chiang Kai-shek's campaign of \"encirclement and suppression\" against the Shensi-Kansu border area and thus laid the cornerstone for the task undertaken by the Central Committee of the Party, the task of setting up the national headquarters of the revolution in northwestern China.” \\\n]\n\n[Source: Mao, Tse-tung (1935, December 27th). Selected works of Mao Tse-tung. Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved September 26th, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-1/mswv1_11.htm. Public domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\\\$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Propaganda poster from around the 1930`s. The image depicts Mao and it reads: “The Red Army does not fear the trials of the Long March”. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0082.jpg\" alt=\"HI0082\">:IDXXXXXXXXXX\n[Source: Unknown author (No date). The Red Army does not fear the trials of the Long March. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved September 27, 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Red_Army_Does_Not_Fear_the_Trials_of_the_Long_March,_People%27s_Republic_of_China,_undated,_lithograph_-_Jordan_Schnitzer_Museum_of_Art,_University_of_Oregon_-_Eugene,_Oregon_-_DSC09524.jpg. Public domain.","markScheme":"|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor compare, any combination of the following similarities:\n- In both, the Long March is used for propaganda purposes.\n- In both, Communists are the protagonists.\n- In both the future looks promising.\n\nFor contrast, any combination of the following differences:\n- In Source A, Mao does not mention his part in the Long March while in Source B he is depicted as the main character.\n- In Source A, Mao mentions 2 different Red Armies while in Source B the Red Army is identified as one thing.\n- While Source A mentions a clear enemy, Source B only mentions “trials” that seem to be associated with the difficulty of the terrain.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources A and B talk about the Long March as material for propaganda ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B is a propaganda poster itself and in Source A Mao says “The Long March is also a propaganda force” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).  Both sources also show the protagonists were the Communists as Mao says “Who brought the Long March to victory? The Communist Party.” and the poster is all in Red, in clear allusion to Communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$. $\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The future also appears promising in both sources ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) as Mao is seen in the poster looking ahead with satisfaction while in Source A it says the Long March “In the eleven provinces it has sown many seeds which will sprout, leaf, blossom, and bear fruit, and will yield a harvest in the future” indicating that the CCP will have many new followers in the future ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources are also different in the importance they give to Mao ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While in the poster he is the main character, in source A, Mao does not mention himself ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). On the contrary, Mao mentions the existence of 2 different Red Armies while source B mentions the Red Army as 1 entity in its caption ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The poster also talks about “trials” in its caption without mentioning what they were ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). In the image, the problem seems to be the mountains they had to cross while in Source A Mao says they had to fight against “the traitor Chiang Kai-shek's campaign of ‘encirclement and suppression’” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0116","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0116\n\nExplain **two** limitations of the support provided to the Republicans by the Soviet Union during the Spanish Civil War. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Soviet support was politically motivated and caused internal divisions\n\n    - The USSR gave aid mainly to strengthen communist control within the Republican side, not to support broader unity.\n\n    - Soviet advisors influenced the Spanish Communist Party, leading to conflicts with other Republican factions such as anarchists and socialists.\n\n    - This ideological interference weakened Republican unity and military coordination.\n\n- Soviet aid was inconsistent and declined over time\n\n    - Although the USSR sent weapons, tanks, and military advisors, the support lacked long-term consistency.\n\n    - By 1938, Stalin reduced and eventually withdrew Soviet aid.\n\n    - This left the Republicans under-equipped during the final stages of the war, while the Nationalists continued to receive steady support from Germany and Italy.\n\n- Reliance on Soviet aid limited Republican autonomy\n\n    - The Republicans became dependent on Soviet supplies and political direction.\n\n    - This undermined their domestic and international credibility, particularly among Western democracies who feared communist influence.\n\n    - It also led to purges of non-communist left-wing figures within the Republican camp, further weakening their effectiveness.\n\n- Weapons and equipment were outdated or limited in quantity\n\n    - Much of the equipment sent by the USSR was second-hand or outdated.\n\n    - In contrast, Germany and Italy provided Franco with more modern aircraft, tanks, and artillery.\n\n    - This technological gap made it difficult for Republican forces to compete on the battlefield.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne major limitation of Soviet support was that it was politically motivated and caused internal division within the Republican side. Stalin used his aid to increase the influence of the Spanish Communist Party, which led to tensions and infighting with other left-wing factions such as anarchists and socialists ($\\checkmark$). This disunity weakened the Republicans' ability to coordinate militarily and politically ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother limitation was that Soviet aid was inconsistent and declined over time ($\\checkmark$). Although the USSR initially sent weapons and advisors, by 1938 Stalin had reduced support significantly and eventually withdrew aid completely. This left the Republicans poorly equipped in the later stages of the war, while Franco continued receiving strong and consistent support from Germany and Italy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0117","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0117\n\nDescribe the immediate political impacts of WWI.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Russian Revolutions: Russia's poor performance in WWI and the impact the war had on civilians was a catalyst for the February/March Revolution that brought down the tsar as well as for the October Revolution that brought down the Provisional Government\n- Establishment of the Weimar Republic in Germany: faced with Germany`s imminent defeat, the Kaiser decided to abdicate and a Republic was set up to replace him. The German ruling class was hoping to negotiate a lenient peace now that the man responsible for many of the factors that led to WWI was no longer in power.\n- Break up of the Austro-Hungarian Empire: the strain of war proved too much for an already embattled multiethnic empire. The Paris Peace Conference and the treaties that followed with the defeated Austria and Hungary included the creation of new countries from the lands that had formerly been ruled by the Habsburg monarch.\n- Creation of Yugoslavia, Czechoslovakia and Poland: following Wilson's 14 points, the Paris Peace Conference decided to create these 3 nation-states based on the principle of self-determination.\n- Creation of the League of Nations as an organisation that would solve future conflicts. This was established in the Treaty of Versailles.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Great War, as WWI was known before WWII, had many immediate political effects. One of the most important for the future of the world was the October Revolution in Russia where the Bolsheviks were able to bring down the Provisional Government and eventually set up the world's first Communist state ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother big change was the breakup of the strong power of central Europe, the Austro-Hungarian Empire. The strain of war proved too much for an already embattled multiethnic empire ($\\checkmark$). The Paris Peace Conference decided to create new nation-states with some lands from the former Austro-Hungarian Empire as well as some land taken from the German Empire. These were the countries of Czechoslovakia, Poland and Yugoslavia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0121","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0121\n\nProvide **two** arguments to justify the following statement: “the results of WWI were the main cause of WWII”.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nArguments need evidence to back them up. **Two** of the following arguments with its supporting evidence:\n- The Treaty of Versailles left Germans humiliated and with a deep resentment towards the Allies. Evidence of this is the fact that part of Hitler's popularity before and during his government was due to the fact that he promised to destroy the Treaty of Versailles and in fact did so in the years prior to the start of WWII.\n- The Paris Peace Conference created a power vacuum in central Europe with the creation of new small states. Evidence of this is that it was very easy for Nazi Germany to annex Austria, conquer Czechoslovakia and aim for Poland which was the short term cause of the war. With each new conquest, Germany became stronger and thus ready for war.\n- Japan was not satisfied by the gains it had obtained from the Paris Peace Conference. It desired further territory in China. Evidence: Japan invaded China during the Second Sino-Japanese War which would become the Pacific Front during WWII.\n- The horrors of the Great War made many politicians in France and the UK willing to use the policy of appeasement with Hitler. Evidence: in the Munich Agreement, also known as the Munich Betrayal, France, Italy, and the UK agreed Germany could take part of Czechoslovakia. Hitler was encouraged by this Agreement and kept trying to undo the loss of land imposed by the Treaty of Versailles by taking the Polish Corridor and thus sparking WWII.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe horrors of the Great War made many politicians in France and the UK willing to use the policy of appeasement with Hitler thus encouraging him to be more ambitious ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{argument}$). We can see this in the Munich Agreement, also known as the Munich Betrayal, where France, Italy, and the UK agreed Germany could take part of Czechoslovakia. Hitler was encouraged by this Agreement and kept trying to undo the loss of land imposed by the Treaty of Versailles by taking the Polish Corridor and thus sparking WWII ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\nAnother reason why the impact of WWI was one of the main causes of WWII is because the Paris Peace Conference created a power vacuum in central Europe. The fact that the Austro-Hungarian Empire was replaced by three new and small states made Germany's expansion very easy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{argument}$).  We can see this in the fact that Hitler annexed Austria in 1938 without firing a single shot and the same is true for the conquest of Czechoslovakia. By taking over these countries, Germany became stronger and thus more capable of conquering lands ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0141","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0141\n\nDescribe the actions Hitler took to default on the terms of the Treaty of Versailles.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three**  of the following:\n\n - He announced the Non-repayment of reparations in 1933.\n\n - Hitler started rearmament in Germany in 1934/35.\n\n - He introduced compulsory military service 1935 and increased the strength of the  German army from the 100,000/ men permitted by the Treaty of Versailles. \n\n - He remilitarized the Rhineland in March 1936\n\n - Carried out the Anschluss with Austria 1938\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nHitler worked to fulfil his promise of revising the terms of the Treaty of Versailles when he came to power in 1933. He first announced that Nazis would not resume payments of the reparations that had earlier been suspended during the Depression ($\\checkmark$). During the next two years in 1934 to 1935, he increased military spending and started a programme of rearmament, again breaking the terms of the Treaty of Versailles ($\\checkmark$). This together with the introduction of conscription and an increase in the strength of the German army from the 100,000/ permitted by the treaty were the initial steps taken by Hitler to revise the terms of the Treaty of Versailles ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0142","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0142\n\nDescribe the process by which Hitler built his relationship with Italy between 1933 and 1939.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n - Hitler's visits to Italy in 1934 and 1938.\n\n -  German and Italian involvement in the Spanish Civil War in 1936 to help Franco the leader of the Nationalist mutiny \n\n - Signing the Treaty of Friendship in November 1936 to establish the Rome-Berlin Axis. \n\n - Italy joined Germany and Japan in the Anti-Comintern Pact in November 1937. \n\n - Italy and Germany signed the Pact of Friendship and Alliance referred to as the Pact of Steel in 1939. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWhen Hitler came to power in 1933, he initially focused on building links with Mussolini, the fascist ruler of Italy since 1922. This attempt to build a relationship resulted in state visits in 1934 and later in 1938.  Both visits helped consolidate their relationship ($\\checkmark$). German and Italian support to Franco, in the Spanish Civil War in 1936, laid the foundation for the signing of the Treaty of Friendship in November of the same year, to establish the Rome-Berlin Axis ($\\checkmark$). This relationship was further consolidated in November 1937, when Italy joined Germany and Japan in the Anti-Comintern Pact ($\\checkmark$). The two countries continued with their relationship by signing the Pact of Friendship and Alliance referred to as the Pact of Steel in 1939 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0143","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0143\n\nExplain the impact of British appeasement policies on Hitler's foreign policy. [4 marks]","markScheme":"Any combination of **four** of the following:\n\n - Britain's acceptance of Hitler's initial foreign policies in 1934-1935 such as revising the terms of the Treaty of Versailles, a declaration of rearmament and implementing conscription gave Hitler the initial confidence that he would not be opposed.  \n\n - The signing the Anglo-German Naval pact of 1935 approving the expansion of the German Navy which would be limited to 35 percent of the size of the British navy; hence condoning the violation of the terms of the Treaty of Versailles. This permitted German rearmament. \n\n - Permitting Hitler's remilitarization of the Rhineland, another violation of the terms of the Treaty of Versailles, strengthened Hiter's growing confidence that he would face no opposition from major European powers. \n\n - Britain's acceptance of Anschluss, the annexation of Austria, in March of 1938 and which was forbidden by the Treaty of Versailles, gave Hiter the confidence to expand into Czechoslovakia next. \n\n - The signing of the Munich Agreement in September 1938 permitted Hitler to occupy the Sudeten area of Czechoslovakia making it clear that he would be unopposed in his quest to expand Germany's existing borders.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBritish policy of appeasement helped Hitler embark on his expansionist foreign policy. Lack of opposition to initial German rearmament and expansion of the German army permitted Hitler to strengthen his defence forces ($\\checkmark$). Germany rearmament was further validated with the signing of the Anglo-German Naval pact in 1935  that approved the expansion of the German Navy which would be limited to 35 percent of the size of the British navy. This British action condoned the violation of the terms of the Treaty of Versailles and officially permitted German rearmament ($\\checkmark$). The lack of response from Britain (and France) when Hitler sent troops to take over Austria in March 1938, resulted in acceptance of Anschluss that was forbidden by the Treaty of Versailles. This gave Hiter the confidence to expand into Czechoslovakia next and increased his demands for Sudetenland, the German speaking area of Czechoslovakia ($\\checkmark$). The  highlight of the appeasement policy was the signing of the Munich Agreement in September 1938, permitting Hiter to occupy the Sudeten area of Czechoslovakia. This further convinced Hitler that he would be unopposed in his quest to continually expand Germany's existing borders ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0144","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0144\n\nDescribe the advantages Germany gained by signing the Nazi-Soviet Pact in 1939. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\n- Allowed Germany to invade Poland without the risk of a two-front war \n\n- Received economic aid from the USSR which would negate the impact of any Anglo-French blockade \n\n- The control of Poland would be a launching pad for Hitler’s later goal of conquering the USSR\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe signing of the Nazi-Soviet Pact with Stalin permitted Germany to invade Poland without the risk of a two-front war as there would be no opposition from the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). It also allowed Germany to receive economic aid from the USSR which would help negate the impact of any Anglo-French blockade ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0145","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0145\n\nDescribe the advantages Germany gained by its involvement in the Spanish Civil War in 1936. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\n - Hitler used his involvement in the Spanish Civil war to test out new and improved armed forces.'\n\n - The fighting tactic of Blitzkrieg or lightning war was first tested in the Spanish Civil War. \n\n - The Condor Legion of the Luftwaffe (German air force) tested out the effectiveness of civilian aerial bombing at Guernica. \n\n - By helping Franco come to power, Germany could benefit from the presence of another right wing dictator in Europe.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nGerman involvement in the Spanish Civil War provided Hitler with the advantage of testing out his new armed forces including Blitzkrieg tactics or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$lightning war\" which was first tested during the fighting in the Spanish Civil War ($\\checkmark$). The Condor Legion of the Luftwaffe (German air force) also tested the effectiveness of civilian aerial bombing at the infamous bombing of Guernica before its eventual use during the Second World War ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0146","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0146\n\nWhat, according to Source B, was the British response to Mussolini’s actions in Abyssinia?  [3 marks] :br\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Bertram Maxwell, a professor of history and political science in the academic book, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$International Relations (1939)\", pp 395-396.\n\n[Source: Maxwell, B. W. (1939). *International Relations*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 1, 2024  from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b589970. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The British government prevented Abyssinia from acquiring arms, hoping to gain Mussolini's friendship. \n\n - The British government hoped Mussolini would accept the Hoare-Laval Pact\n\n - The British people's reaction however was to support the principles of the League of Nations to help prevent Mussolini's aggression against Abyssinia. \n\n - The British government hence were forced to reprimand Mussolini.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, the British response to Mussolini's actions in Abyssinia was to prevent Abyssinia from acquiring arms in the hope of gaining Mussolini's friendship ($\\checkmark$). They also put forward the Hoare-Laval pact which they hoped would help them deal with the crisis and appease Mussolini ($\\checkmark$). However the British people's reaction was to support the principles put forward by the League and to help  prevent Mussolini's aggression against Abyssinia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0147","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0147\n\nWhat does Source A reveal about the impact of the Abyssinian crisis? [2 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A political cartoon titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Abyssinian Breezes” by David Low, January 1936.  The caption reads: “It's an ill wind that blows nobody good.” \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0147.png\" alt=\"HI0147\">\n\n[Source: Low, D. (1936, January 20). *Abyssinian Breezes* from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Low Again.\" Internet Archive. Retrieved November 1, 2024 from https://archive.org/details/371201-low-again-1930s-political-satirical-cartoons. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following:\n\n - The Abyssinian crisis or the Abyssinian breeze is encouraging the growth of Japanese and German militarism depicted by the Japanese and German soldiers marching through the windmills. \n\n - The Abyssinian breeze is “an ill wind that blows nobody good” implying that what is happening in Abyssinia is bad for everyone.\n\n - The Abyssinian breeze is threatening to destabilize peace which is depicted by the threat to the kite being flown peacefully by the woman. \n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A shows that the Abyssinian crisis or the Abyssinian breeze is encouraging the growth of Japanese and German militarism. This is depicted by marching Japanese and German soldiers going through two windmills that are fueled by the Abyssinian breeze ($\\checkmark$).  Source A also shows that the Abyssian breeze or the Abyssinian crisis  is threatening peace as it is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$an ill wind that blows nobody good.\" This is shown by the destabilizing of the kite being peacefully flown by the Abyssinian breeze ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0148","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0148\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the impact of the Abyssinian crisis. [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | George Martelli, a journalist and historian, writing in the academic book \"Italy Against The World: the first complete impartial account of Italy's repudiation of the League $\\&$ her conquest of Abyssinia by an English author with an intimate knowledge of the facts\" (1938).\n\n[Martelli, George (1938). *Italy Against The World; the first complete impartial account of Italy's repudiation of the League; her conquest of Abyssinia, by an English author with an intimate knowledge of the facts*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 1, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015014439452. Public Domain..]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The origin of the source is valuable as the author of Source C, George Martelli, is both a journalist and historian, hence trained in the skills of historical analysis. \n\n - The purpose of the source is valuable as its focus is solely on the Abyssinian crisis as evident from the title of the book. \n\n - The content, the source is valuable as it details the impact of public opinion on the British government which forced the government to abandon their initial appeasement of Mussolini. \n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The purpose of the source is limited as the book focuses on the Abyssinian crisis and so does not discuss the wider impact of the crisis on the events in  - Europe, such as the remilitarization of the Rhineland that ultimately contributed to the Second World War. \n\n - The origin of the source is limited as it was written in 1938 and so does not include the long term impact of the Abyssinian crisis on world peace. \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of Source C is valuable as the author, George Martelli, is both a journalist and historian. As a historian he is trained in the skills of historical analysis and as a journalist he would have access to policy makers who shaped the crisis ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is also valuable as its focus is solely on the Abyssinian crisis as evident from the title of the book ($\\checkmark$ VP). Lastly the content of the source holds value as it details the impact of public opinion on the British government which forced it to abandon the initial policy of appeasement of Mussolini ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nThe purpose of the source is limited because it focuses on the Abyssinian crisis itself and so does not discuss the wider impact of the crisis on the events in Europe, such as the remilitarization of the Rhineland, that ultimately contributed to the Second World War ($\\checkmark$ LP). In addition, the origin of the source is limited as it was written in 1938 and so does not include the long term impact of the Abyssinian crisis on world peace ($\\checkmark$ LO)."},{"ref":"HI0149","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0149\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and D reveal about the impact of the League of Nations on the Abyssinian Crisis. [6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Bertram Maxwell, a professor of history and political science in the academic book, *International Relations* (1939), pp 395-396.\n\n[Source: Maxwell, Bertram Wayburn (1939). *International Relations*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 1, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b589970. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | William Ebenstein, political scientist writing in the academic book *Fascist Italy* (1939), pp. 290-291.\n\n[Source: Ebenstein, William. (1939). *Fascist Italy*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved on November 2, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015004908987. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor comparison, any **two** to **three** of the following similarities:\n\n - Both sources state that the League’s response was to impose sanctions. \n\n - Both sources state Mussolini was not worried about the reaction of the League and was willing to oppose the League with force if necessary. \n\n - Both sources state that the League failed to impose a sanction or embargo  on oil which was the only effective means to stop the Abyssinian aggression. \n\nFor contrast, any **three** to **four** of the following differences:\n\n - Source B discusses the British policy of appeasing Mussolini and the Hoare Laval pact which is not mentioned in Source D.\n\n - Source B also mentions that restrictions were imposed on both Abyssinia and Italy while Source D only discusses restrictions imposed on Italy. \n\n - Source D mentions that if opposed, Italy was willing to leave the League, which is not mentioned in Source B\n\n - Source B mentions the role of the general public in supporting action by the League against Mussolini which is not mentioned in Source D.\n\n [6 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources B and D state that the League' initial response was to impose sanctions ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the sanctions passed by the League were not carried out\", while Source D states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it seemed for a time that the League was serious about stopping Italy from continuing her war of conquest and began to impose sanctions against Italy\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both sources also state Mussolini was not worried about the reaction of the League and was willing to oppose the League with force if necessary ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mussolini threatened that if an oil embargo were declared he would fight\" while Source D quotes Mussolini as saying , $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$If the satanic oppression of the imperialists and of the bloodthirsty sects of Europe have their own way, Europe will inevitably fall into the most unjustified and terrible war that humanity has ever seen.\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Finally, both sources discuss the role of a potential oil embargo on Italy during the crisis ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B says that because $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$no oil embargo was ever declared on Mussolini” Italy was able to use gas and airplanes to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$subdue the Abyssinian nation which fought desperately to retain its independence\" and source D says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the League did not dare to impose the only sanction, the oil embargo, which would have brought the Fascist government to reason\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nSource B discusses the British policy of appeasing Mussolini and the Hoare Laval pact which is not mentioned in Source D ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B mentions that restrictions were imposed on both Abyssinia and Italy while Source D only discusses restrictions imposed on Italy ($\\checkmark$ evidence). In Source B it is mentioned Britain and France  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not only did nothing to help Abyssinia, but rather prevented her from acquiring the necessary arms for defense\" while Source D states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$League was serious about stopping Italy from continuing her war of conquest and began to impose sanctions against Italy\" ($\\checkmark$ difference and evidence). Finally Source D mentions that if opposed, Italy was willing to leave the League saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ Mussolini threatened that Italy would leave the League if it dared to execute faithfully the provisions of the Covenant\", which is not mentioned in Source B ($\\checkmark$ difference)."},{"ref":"HI0150","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0150\n\nUsing the information in Sources B and C, discuss the impact of public opinion on British policy towards Mussolini.\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Bertram Maxwell, a professor of history and political science in the academic book, *International Relations* (1939), pp 395-396.\n\n[Source: Maxwell, Bertram Wayburn (1939). *International Relations*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 1, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b589970. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | George Martelli, a journalist and historian, writing in the academic book \"Italy Against The World: the first complete impartial account of Italy's repudiation of the League & her conquest of Abyssinia by an English author with an intimate knowledge of the facts\" (1938).\n\n[Source: Martelli, George (1938). *Italy Against The World; the first complete impartial account of Italy's repudiation of the League; her conquest of Abyssinia, by an English author with an intimate knowledge of the facts*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 1, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015014439452. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** or more of the following with supporting discussion for **four** marks maximum:\n\n - Source B states the British attempt at appeasing Mussolini as proposed by the Hoare Laval Pact was undermined by the British public who believed that the League should follow its principles and stop Italian aggression in Abyssinia. Mussolini hence feared that public opinion in England might demand the closing of the Suez Canal and deeply resented the negativity of public opinion shown in the British press.\n\n - Source C states that the British policy towards Mussolini was hugely influenced by public opinion. Hence in the General Election in November 1935, the National Government of Britain was reelected for showing their perceived support of the League. However, the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$floating vote”, a group of people whose support was unpredictable, shifted their opinion and this public opposition forced the Government to reject the Hoare-Laval plan and its author for appeasing Italy and Mussolini's aggression. . \n\n - Source C also states that after the rejection of the Hoare-Laval Plan, those that believed in sanctions were unable to follow it up as they no longer had the support of the large mass of middle opinion (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$floating vote”, who now were beginning to be “bored” with the situation and thus making it difficult for the League to follow through with effective sanctions on Italy. \n\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding statement for **one** mark that aligns with the evidence and discussion presented:\n\nThe best responses will include evidence from both Sources B and C and provide specific evidence of the impacts of British public opinion on its policies toward Mussolini.\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBritish policy towards Mussolini's demands and their attempt at appeasement as put forward by the Hoare-Laval pact was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$undermined by the honest British people who believed that the principles of the League should be carried out” as stated in Source B ($\\checkmark$). Mussolini hence feared that public opinion in England might demand the closing of the Suez Canal as stated in the source and deeply resented the reprimands of public opinion in the British press ($\\checkmark$). This impact of British public opinion was also apparent when $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$at the General Election in November 1935, the government was elected as a reward for their perceived support of the League” (Source C). The source states that the group of undecided voters (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$floating vote”) had been cast for the National Government, as a “reward for its support of the League'. However the government loses this support “in December with the revelation of the Hoare-Laval pact and public opinion swung violently away and forced the Government to repudiate the Hoare-Laval plan and its author” ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSource C also states that those who supported sanctions found that they failed to keep the pressure on Mussolini because after the rejection of the Hoare-Laval Plan, the British public became less interested in the crisis in Abyssinia. This lack of interest made it difficult for the League to follow through with effective sanctions ($\\checkmark$). \n\nSources B and C display the power of British public opinion which put pressure on the British government to support the League's sanctions against Italy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0192","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0192\n\nAccording to Source A, what were the United States' goals in Asia in 1949?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US National Security Council document NSC 48/2 entitled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Position of the United States With Respect to Asia.” Written in 1949.\n\n[Source: National Security Council. (December 23, 1949). *NSC 48/2 - The Position of the United States with Respect to Asia*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1949v07p2/d387. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The U.S. aimed to help Asian nations develop into stable, self-sustaining states aligned with the United Nations Charter.\n\n - The U.S. sought to develop sufficient military power in non-Communist Asian nations like South Korea to prevent Communist expansion.\n\n - A key goal was to eliminate or significantly reduce Soviet power in Asia, ensuring the USSR could not threaten regional peace or U.S. security.\n\n - The U.S. planned to provide military, economic, and political aid to strategic areas, particularly South Korea, to bolster non-Communist regimes.\n\n - The U.S. committed to resisting Communist encroachment by strengthening the forces of freedom and maintaining the independence of allied nations.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source A, the United States pursued multiple objectives in Asia in 1949. One key goal was to support the creation of stable, independent nations that aligned with the principles of the United Nations Charter ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the U.S. aimed to strengthen the military capabilities of non-Communist countries, such as South Korea, to act as a barrier against Communist expansion ($\\checkmark$). Another major objective was to curb Soviet influence in the region, preventing the USSR from disrupting security and stability in Asia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0193","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0193\n\nAccording to Source B, what were the objectives of the United States in Indochina during the French occupation?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US Department of State $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1949.” \n\n[Source: Department of State (September 27, 1948). *Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1948*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v06/d33. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The U.S. sought to limit Communist influence in Southeast Asia, particularly in Indochina, where Ho Chi Minh’s Communist-led movement threatened the stability of the region and U.S. interests.\n\n - A U.S. goal was to assist France, a key ally, in maintaining control over Indochina to prevent further economic strain on France, which was vital to European recovery after World War II.\n\n - The U.S. aimed to achieve stability in Southeast Asia by encouraging France to address the nationalist aspirations of the Vietnamese people, as ending the conflict was seen as crucial to reducing anti-Western sentiment.\n\n - The U.S. sought to avoid becoming directly involved in the conflict by pressing France to resolve the situation through reforms and negotiations rather than relying solely on military solutions.\n\n - The U.S. recognized the importance of maintaining French influence in Indochina, both to sustain a Western-aligned presence in the region and to counter Communist expansion.\n\n - The U.S. aimed to foster non-Communist nationalist movements in Indochina as an alternative to Ho Chi Minh's Communist-dominated leadership, though this goal was hampered by a lack of viable alternatives.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, the United States had several objectives in Asia in 1949. One key aim was to curb the spread of Communism, especially in Indochina, where Ho Chi Minh's leadership posed a direct threat to stability and U.S. strategic interests ($\\checkmark$). Another goal was to bolster France’s position in the region, as maintaining French control was seen as critical to preventing further economic strain on a vital European ally recovering from World War II ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the U.S. prioritized fostering stability in Southeast Asia by urging France to meet the nationalist demands of the Vietnamese, which would help reduce anti-Western sentiment and ease tensions in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0194","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0194\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the causes of the Vietnam War.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US Department of State $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1949.” \n\n[Source: Department of State (September 27, 1948). *Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1948*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v06/d33. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:: \n\n - Origin:\n\n     - Produced by the U.S. Department of State in 1948, the source provides an official American perspective on the early Cold - War tensions in Indochina. It reflects U.S. assessments of the causes of the conflict and offers insight into early American concerns about Communist expansion.\n\n - Purpose:\n\n     - The document's purpose is to advise U.S. policymakers on the situation in Indochina, particularly how instability and Communist activity were contributing to conflict. It is useful because it reveals how the United States assessed the role of Communism in the causes of the war, helping historians understand why the U.S. eventually intervened.\n\n- Content:\n\n     - The source highlights key causes of the Vietnam War, including French failures to maintain control, the growing strength of Communist forces, and nationalist opposition to colonial rule.\n\n    - It provides insight into how the U.S. viewed the conflict as a Cold War struggle rather than a colonial independence movement, which influenced later American actions in Vietnam.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - Origin:\n\n     - As a U.S. government document written in 1948, the source is biased toward portraying Communism as the primary cause of conflict, potentially overlooking other nationalist motivations behind the war. It predates key developments such as the escalation of the First Indochina War (1946-1954), meaning it provides only an early snapshot of the causes rather than a full picture of the conflict's evolution.\n\n - Purpose:\n\n     - The document's purpose was to guide U.S. foreign policy, meaning it may exaggerate the threat of Communism while downplaying the significance of anti-colonial nationalism as a cause.\n\n - Content:\n\n     - The source primarily reflects French and American viewpoints, offering little insight into Vietnamese nationalist perspectives beyond their Communist affiliations.\n\n     - It oversimplifies the causes of the war by presenting it as a Cold War struggle rather than an independence movement, failing to explore the broader social, political, and economic factors that contributed to the conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIts origin as an official U.S. Department of State document ($\\checkmark$ VO) written in 1948 ensures it provides a contemporary perspective on the U.S.'s analysis of French colonial struggles and growing Communist influence in Indochina. This makes it particularly valuable for understanding how American policymakers perceived the situation early in the conflict. The purpose of the source, to guide U.S. foreign policy decisions ($\\checkmark$ VP), offers insight into the strategic calculations behind U.S. concerns over Ho Chi Minh’s leadership and the need to balance support for a French ally with the broader goal of containing Communism. Additionally, the content is highly detailed, describing key causes such as the failure of French negotiations with Ho Chi Minh, their military ineffectiveness, and the establishment of puppet governments, which bolstered Ho's prestige and weakened French credibility ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source has notable limitations. While the U.S. Department of State is a reliable origin, the American-centric nature of the document reflects a strong Cold War bias, as it exaggerates Communist influence while downplaying the nationalist motivations of the Vietnamese people ($\\checkmark$ LO). Furthermore, since the source was written in 1948, it lacks knowledge of later developments in the First Indochina War and the escalation of U.S. involvement, limiting its utility for a full understanding of the conflict ($\\checkmark$ LO). In terms of purpose, while it aims to analyze the situation, its role in shaping U.S. policy may have led to selective interpretation or omission of details that could weaken the argument for containment ($\\checkmark$ LP). Finally, the content, though informative, does not include Vietnamese perspectives, particularly those of non-Communist nationalists or ordinary citizens, and it neglects to address the role of international actors such as China or the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0195","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0195\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about Nazi foreign policy in the late 1930s?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon by cartoonist Clifford Berryman titled “It's A Good Act But It's Hard On The Spectators” published in the Washington Evening Star in August of 1939. It shows Hitler balancing the world on the tip of a rifle balanced on the tip of his nose as spectators, including Uncle Sam, John Bull, and a caricature of France, watch. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0195%28i%29.jpg\" alt=\"HI0195(i)\">\n\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (August 20, 1939). *It's A Good Act But It's Hard On The Spectators*. Washington Evening Star. Retrieved on January 12, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/6012197. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The source suggests that Nazi foreign policy was aggressive and aimed at destabilizing the global order, as symbolized by Hitler balancing the world on a rifle.\n\n - The cartoon portrays Nazi foreign policy as reckless and precarious, symbolized by the delicate act of balancing the world, highlighting the risks inherent in Hitler's strategies.\n\n - The nervous spectators, including Uncle Sam, John Bull (Britain), and France, reflect widespread international concern about Hitler's aggressive and unpredictable foreign policy.\n\n - By placing the world literally at the tip of a rifle, the cartoon suggests that Nazi foreign policy directly endangered global peace and stability.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe source suggests that Nazi foreign policy in the late 1930s was aggressive and destabilizing ($\\checkmark$). This is depicted by Hitler balancing the world precariously on the tip of a rifle, symbolizing the recklessness of his actions, while the anxious spectators, representing nations like the U.S., Britain, and France, reflect widespread fear of the consequences of his policies ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0196","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0196\n\nAccording to Source D, what role does Mao believe the peasant movement will play in the Chinese revolution?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a 1927 report by Mao Tse-Tung about the role of peasants within the Communist Party.  \n\n[Source: Mao Tse-Tung. (March 1927). *Report on an Investigation of the Peasant Movement in Hunan, March 1927, in Selected Works of Mao Tse-tung, vol. I* (3rd printing; Peking: Foreign Languages Press, 1975), pp. 23-29. Retrieved January 13, 2025 from https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1927mao.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Mao sees the peasant movement as the central driving force of the revolution, describing it as a \"mighty storm\" that will reshape Chinese society.\n\n - Mao believes the movement will destroy the power of imperialists, warlords, corrupt officials, and local tyrants, paving the way for liberation\n\n - He highlights the peasant associations as the new centers of authority, replacing the feudal structures that had dominated rural areas for centuries.\n\n - Mao states that the movement will challenge every revolutionary party and individual, forcing them to either lead, follow, or oppose it, with clear consequences.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source D, Mao portrays the peasant movement as the driving force behind the Chinese revolution, likening it to a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mighty storm\" that will dismantle the oppressive structures of feudalism and imperialism ($\\checkmark$). He also emphasizes the critical role of peasant associations, which have become the new centers of authority in rural areas, symbolizing the collapse of the old gentry-dominated order ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Mao describes the movement as a test for revolutionaries, compelling them to take a clear stance by leading, supporting, or opposing it, with significant consequences for their revolutionary legitimacy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0213","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0213\n\nDescribe the relationship between the *zaibatsu* (Japanese business conglomerates) and the rise of Japanese militarism.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following **three** relationships should be described:\n- Economic Backing\n    - The zaibatsu provided key economic support for Japan's military buildup, enabling rapid industrialization and armament.\n- Mutual Benefit\n    - They gained from imperial conquests, reinforcing their support for militarist policies and fueling Japan’s expansionist ambitions.\n- Accelerated Militarism\n    - Their support for the military accelerated Japan’s aggressive expansionist policies in Asia, contributing to the outbreak of World War II.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe connection between the zaibatsu and the military was significant in mainly three ways. First, financial support, as the zaibatsu helped fund Japan's military development and industrial growth, strengthening its armed forces ($\\checkmark$). Second, shared interests, as the zaibatsu gained profits from Japan's territorial expansions while backing militaristic policies that aligned with their economic goals ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, increased militarism, as the zaibatsu's wealth allowed Japan to pursue aggressive territorial expansion in Asia, which played a key role in the onset of World War II ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0215","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0215\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about China's role in the Allied war effort and its relationship with the United States?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “All we are and all we have” by  Chiang Kaishek in a message to  President Franklin D. Roosevelt on the day after Pearl Harbor, dated December 9, 1941. |\n\n[Source: Kai-shek, C . (1942). *All We Are and All We Have* Internet Archive. Retrieved November 18, 2024, from https://archive.org/details/AllWeAreAndAllWeHaveSpeechesAndMessagesSincePearlHarbor/page/n4/mode/1up. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- China is fully committed to fighting alongside the United States and the Allies against the Axis powers, showing its dedication to the shared war effort.\n- Emphasizes the strong bond between China and the U.S., with China expressing gratitude for American support, which was vital in resisting Japan.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that China was fully committed to fighting with the US against Japan who they viewed as a “common” enemy ($\\checkmark$). In Source B Chiang also expresses China's gratitude for the help the U.S. had given. This highlights especially, the U.S. support that helped China resist Japan before America joined the war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0250","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0250\n\n\"Nuclear weapons were necessary for the US to win the war with Japan.\" Provide **three** arguments that support this perspective.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following arguments with its supporting evidence.\n- Nuclear weapons were necessary to avoid American casualties.\n    - American leaders were afraid that an invasion of the Japanese home islands would require similar US casualties.\n- Conventional strategic bombing was not enough to force Japan to surrender.\n    - Conventional bombing against Japanese urban centers had caused huge losses to the Japanese, but it was not enough to cause their surrender.\n- The use of atomic weapons would mean a faster end to the war.\n    - The Japanese emperor surrendered 6 days after the bombing of Nagasaki.\n- The use of atomic weapons would actually mean fewer overall casualties.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMany at the time, including the US high command and President Truman argued that atomic bombs had to be used to end the war as quickly as possible to save American lives ($\\checkmark$). The previous battle for Okinawa, the last stepping stone before reaching the Japanese home islands, had resulted in 65,000 U.S. casualties, or 35% of the American forces deployed. If those numbers held, it was expected an invasion of the Japanese home islands would result in around 250,000 American casualties  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The death toll for the Japanese of the use of conventional weapons would not be much better ($\\checkmark$). After only 1 air raid in March of 1945 over Tokyo the death toll had been 80.000 Japanese civilians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The US government also feared that the Japanese would not surrender since they had not done so yet despite air raids that had destroyed 50% of Japanese urban areas ($\\checkmark$). In this context, the use of atomic weapons appeared to offer a solution that minimized further loss of life for the Americans while delivering a shock that would make the Japanese surrender. In fact, the Japanese Emperor called for surrender on 15 August, 9 days after the bombing of Hiroshima ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0252","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0252\n\nDefine the Lend-Lease Policy and describe **two** of its features.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**One** mark for definition similar to:\n- The Lend-Lease Act set up a scheme through which the US sent aid to the Allies during the Second World War.\n\nAny **two** of the following features:\n- Immediate payment for war material was not required as the US was “lending” the materials to the Allies.\n- The act that started the policy was passed in March of 1941, before the US officially entered WWII as a belligerent.\n- It was introduced as a result of a conversation Churchill had with Roosevelt warning him that Britain could not keep paying for US war materials and would therefore not be able to continue to keep its stance against the Nazis.\n- The US had Lend-Lease agreements with a number of Allied countries. For example, with the Soviet Union. It ended up supplying it with 18 000 airplanes; 1,500 tanks and more than 500 ships.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lend-Lease policy was adopted by the US to be able to supply the Allies during WWII ($\\checkmark$). The first act that created the policy was passed before the US entered the war as a belligerent  ($\\checkmark$) but it allowed the British to be able to resist the Nazis without having to pay for American war materials ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0256","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0256\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the decision to use atomic weapons on Japan.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Harry Truman and the Bomb\" by Lee Lacy, Assistant Professor at the US Army Command and General Staff College, published August 5 , 2014.|\n\n[Source: Lacy, L. (2014, August 5). *Harry Truman and the Bomb*. The National Archives - Pieces of History. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://prologue.blogs.archives.gov/2014/08/05/harry-truman-and-the-bomb/. Public domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Months of the War with Japan\" by MacEachin, D. J., former Deputy Director for Intelligence at the US Central Intelligence Agency, published 1998. |\n\n:box[…for all practical purposes the decision on whether to use the nuclear weapon against Japan had already been reached by the time the President arrived in Potsdam. On this point virtually all scholars who have studied the issue seem to concur, however much they may disagree on the motives for its use and whether its use was justified. …\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-01-29%201.17..png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-01-29 1.17.\">Nor should the evidence be construed as indicating that concern over casualties was the only factor exerting critical force on the A-bomb decision. What the evidence does indicate is that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions, (2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam, and before the acquisition of intelligence showing much-larger-than-expected Japanese forces on Kyushu [the southernmost Japanese home island]. Nonetheless, it is certainly plausible that the buildup disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance. …\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-01-29%20at%201.17..png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-01-29 at 1.17.\">For those favoring a bomb-and-blockade approach or even just a postponement of any invasion, Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands. On the other hand, if Japan's surrender did not take place until after the Soviets had been in the Pacific war for some length of time, and if there were no US forces on Japanese territory (because no US invasion had occurred), how could the United States and its Allies acquire the control over occupation that they were seeking? Unless the mere entry by the USSR somehow caused an immediate surrender on the unconditional terms being demanded by Washington, the Potsdam experience was likely to reinforce the tendency among at least some US officials to see any gain resulting from Soviet entry as also carrying a serious potential cost--the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: MacEachin, D. J. (1998, December). *The Final Months of the War With Japan.* CIA's Center for the Study of Intelligence. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/The-Final-Months-of-the-War-With-Japan-Monograph.pdf . Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources agree that one reason to use atomic weapons was to avoid American casualties. Source B: “an amphibious invasion of the Japanese mainland was unthinkable. Neither were the estimated millions of American lives that would be lost if mainland Japan was invaded.”  “Source C: “the evidence does indicate that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions”.\n\nFor contrast, any **two to four** of the following differences:\n- While Source B stresses the unity of the Allies, Source C portrays one of the Allies, the USSR, as a rival. Source B: “He hoped the ultimatum would coincide with a successful test of the atomic bomb to demonstrate the resolve of the Allies to Japan” Source C: “minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage”\n- The sources disagree on who made the decision to use the atomic bomb. Source B states “No doubt the weight of the world was on Truman’s shoulders, and the final decision was not easy. Finally, he concluded it was his decision, alone” while Source C speaks of “US leadership” generally.\n- Besides avoiding American casualties, which is mentioned in both sources as a reason for the use of atomic weapons, Source C also identifies two other reasons “(2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam.”\n- Source B seems to imply that Truman had no other choice but to use atomic weapons saying, “On July 28, Japan announced its intention to continue the war. There was no alternative—Truman had to take action to end the war” while Source C presents another possibility: “Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands.\"\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources agree that one reason to use nuclear weapons was to end the war quickly and avoid further American casualties ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B explains that “an amphibious invasion of the Japanese mainland was unthinkable. Neither were the estimated millions of American lives that would be lost if mainland Japan was invaded” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) and Source C gives a similar motivation when it says: “the evidence does indicate that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also contain many contrasts regarding the decision to drop the atomic bombs on Japan. While Source B stresses Truman´s desire to show the unity of the Allies against Japan by saying that “He hoped the ultimatum would coincide with a successful test of the atomic bomb to demonstrate the resolve of the Allies to Japan,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) Source C identifies the USSR as a rival of the US saying that the decision had to do with the desire to reduce “the USSR's postwar leverage” and there is not mention of a joint decision regarding the use of the bomb ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference }$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThey also disagree on who made the decision ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While Source B states, “No doubt the weight of the world was on Truman's shoulders, and the final decision was not easy. Finally, he concluded it was his decision, alone.” In contrast, Source C speaks of “US leadership” as a group of many who participated in the decision-making ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nA final difference between the sources is that Source B only talks about a desire to avoid casualties as the motivation behind the use of the atomic bombs, while Source C identifies many other motivations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) such as,  “(2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). This helps explain another difference, the fact that Source B says that Truman had “no alternative,” while Source C explores the possibility of obtaining the surrender of Japan with the entry of the USSR to the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0257","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0257\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the reasons for the use of atomic weapons on Japan?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Months of the War with Japan\" by MacEachin, D. J., former Deputy Director for Intelligence at the US Central Intelligence Agency, published 1998. |\n\n:box[…for all practical purposes the decision on whether to use the nuclear weapon against Japan had already been reached by the time the President arrived in Potsdam. On this point virtually all scholars who have studied the issue seem to concur, however much they may disagree on the motives for its use and whether its use was justified. …\n\\\n\\\nNor should the evidence be construed as indicating that concern over casualties was the only factor exerting critical force on the A-bomb decision. What the evidence does indicate is that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions, (2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam, and before the acquisition of intelligence showing much-larger-than-expected Japanese forces on Kyushu [the southernmost Japanese home island]. Nonetheless, it is certainly plausible that the buildup disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance. …\n\\\n\\\nFor those favoring a bomb-and-blockade approach or even just a postponement of any invasion, Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands. On the other hand, if Japan's surrender did not take place until after the Soviets had been in the Pacific war for some length of time, and if there were no US forces on Japanese territory (because no US invasion had occurred), how could the United States and its Allies acquire the control over occupation that they were seeking? Unless the mere entry by the USSR somehow caused an immediate surrender on the unconditional terms being demanded by Washington, the Potsdam experience was likely to reinforce the tendency among at least some US officials to see any gain resulting from Soviet entry as also carrying a serious potential cost--the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: MacEachin, D. J. (1998, December). *The Final Months of the War With Japan.* CIA's Center for the Study of Intelligence. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/The-Final-Months-of-the-War-With-Japan-Monograph.pdf . Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To avoid casualties\n- To end the war quickly\n- To minimize the USSR's postwar leverage: they wanted to avoid the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n- To not have to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender\n- Knowledge of military buildup in Kyushu disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom the source, we can learn that the atomic weapons were used on Japan because the United States wanted to end the war as quickly as possible ($\\checkmark$). Also, they wanted to avoid American casualties ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, there was the worry that if the USSR participated in the defeat of Japan, it would impose its hegemony over the Far East just like it had done in Eastern Europe ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0292","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0292\n\nDescribe **three** economic impacts of WWII on the United States.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - WWII effectively ended the Great Depression in the United States. While the unemployment rate was 15$\\%$ in 1940, it fell to 2$\\%$ in 1945. \n\n - The Price Administration Office limits the price of 90$\\%$ of products and this results in an average inflation of only 3.5$\\%$\n\n - A system of strict rationing was introduced in 1942 to keep up war production and supply of the Allies` troops\n\n - The US spent \\$341 billion, including $50 billion for lend-lease supplies that went to its allies. \n\n - The US economy emerged from the war as the dominant economy of the world. It held two-thirds of the world's gold supply and produced over 60$\\%$ of the world's industrial output. \n\n - America's economic influence during the war and immediately after it allowed the US to make its allies accept tariff and monetary agreements that opened much of the world, especially the colonial empires of Europe, to American trade and investment.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWWII had a positive impact on the US economy. The necessities of war put an end to the decade-long Great Depression. Evidence of this is the fact that the unemployment rate was 15$\\%$ in 1940, and it fell to $2\\%$ in 1945 ($\\checkmark$). The US also emerged from the war as the dominant economy of the world, producing about 60$\\%$ of the world's industrial output by 1945 ($\\checkmark$). This economic might and the fact that it had assisted its allies economically as well as militarily both during and after the war gave US leaders the possibility of influencing US aid recipients to accept tariff and monetary agreements that opened much of the world, especially the colonial empires of Europe, to American trade and investment ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0293","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0293\n\nExplain the impact of WWII on minorities in the United States.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - Around 1.5 million African Americans moved in search of the jobs that opened up in the war industries. In their new homes, they had substandard housing, and experienced discrimination from neighbouring communities. This occasionally led to race riots such as the one that occurred in Detroit in 1943\n\n - Roosevelt, by decree, prohibited war industries from discriminating when hiring employees. This was the first time race discrimination was outlawed. Thanks to this, while African Americans suffered around 50$\\%$ unemployment in 1940, and usually found work only in low-level positions, by 1945 African Americans made up 8$\\%$ of all war production workers.\n\n - The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) was an organization that pressed for racial equality. It successfully pressured the US government to allow African American men to participate in combat, although in segregated units. \n\n - NAACP membership went from 50,000 members in 1940 to 450,000 in 1946. Many of the new members were returning servicemen determined to fight for their civil rights. \n\n - In 1948, Truman prohibited segregation in the Armed Forces.\n\n - 1942-Executive order to remove people of Japanese descent from the West coast. About 120,000 were sent to internment camps even though ⅔ were American citizens. They were ordered to dispose of their property in very little time=lost their jobs and businesses\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThere was an improvement in the access to equal rights for some minorities in the United States. In great part, this was due to the need to increase their contribution to the war effort. President Roosevelt issued a decree prohibiting war industries from discrimination based on race when hiring employees. This is the first time race discrimination was outlawed in the US. Thanks to this, by 1945, African Americans made up 8$\\%$ of all war production workers which helped them improve their economic situation ($\\checkmark$). Other roles were also opened up for African Americans, most notably in the Armed Forces. The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) played a key role in successfully advocating to convince the government to allow African American men to participate in combat, even if they did so in segregated units ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOther minorities, however, were greatly disadvantaged by the war situation. The clearest example is that of Japanese Americans who lived on the West Coast who, due to an executive order of 1942, were sent to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$internment camps” ($\\checkmark$).  Approximately 120,000 persons, two-thirds of whom were American citizens, were sent to internment camps having to leave their jobs and business, losing their freedom until 1945 and their economic stability for many more years ($\\checkmark$). \n\n$\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"HI0294","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0294\n\nDescribe **two** of the main clauses of the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Russia agreed to end its participation in WWI. \n\n - Russia recognized the independence of Ukraine, Georgia and Finland; gave up Poland and the Baltic states of Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia to Germany and Austria-Hungary; and ceded Kars, Ardahan and Batum to Turkey. These lands comprised 34$\\%$ of the former empire's population, 54$\\%$ of its industrial land, 89$\\%$ of its coalfields, and 26% of its railways. \n\n - It required Russia to pay Germany war reparations of 6 billion German marks. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWith the Treaty of Brest Litovsk, Russia agreed to end its participation in WWI ($\\checkmark$). It also lost substantial territory, having to recognize the independence of Ukraine, Georgia and Finland and giving up its Polish lands and the Baltic states to Germany and Austro-Hungary ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0295","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0295\n\nEvaluate the impact of WWII on the role of women in the United States.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. \n\nPositive results: \n\n - Creation of Women's Army Corps (WACs), US Naval Women's Reserve (WAVES), Women's Air Force Service Pilots (WASPs) and Women's Coast Guard Reserve. In total, 200,000 serve in war,\n\n - 6 million enter the workforce, 57$\\%$ more than before WWII\n\n - The absence of husbands made women heads of the family.\n\nNegative results: \n\n - Female stereotypes are reinforced in the Armed Forces despite women's new roles. For example, in 1944 a contest was organized under the title:  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Who is the prettiest in the Armed Forces?”\n\n - They have no way to take care of their children if there are no grandparents/aunts: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$8-hour orphans” the media calls them, criticizing the mothers\n\n - With demobilisation and the smaller demand for goods that characterised the post war years, most women lost the jobs they had done during the war: 2.5 million women stopped working outside the home. \n\n - Women still earned an average of 60$\\%$ less than men.\n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall results of WWII were short lived for the role of women in opening up new possibilities in the economic sphere.  \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative.\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWWII had a considerable impact on the role of women in the United States. On the positive side, 200,000 women were able to join the Armed Forces participating in almost all tasks except combat ($\\checkmark$). Women were even part of the Air Force though the Women's Air Force Service Pilots (WASPs)  that had the job of transporting material and men. The war also made it possible for many women to join the workforce now that more than 10 million men were overseas fighting ($\\checkmark$). In 1945 there were  57$\\%$ more women working outside the home than before WWII.\n\nHowever, most of these advances were lost once the war ended. Female sections of the Armed Forces were no longer needed and in fact, even during the war itself female stereotypes were reinforced despite the new positions allowed to women ($\\checkmark$). For example, in 1944 a beauty contest was organized under the title  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Who is the prettiest in the Armed Forces?” showing that women were still valued for their looks and not for their contribution to the war effort. The same can be said of their role in the job market. After 1945 with demobilisation and the smaller demand for goods that characterised the post war years, most women lost the jobs they had done during the war: 2.5 million women stopped working outside the home ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIn conclusion, WWII allowed more women to enter the job market and to participate in the Armed Forces than ever before. However, once the war ended many doors were shut to women. The Armed Forces became again a place for men and many women lost the jobs they had since it was necessary to accommodate the returning soldiers. The changes to the role of women were therefore short lived.  What cannot be denied is that millions of women were not limited to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$home life” for at least some years and the experiences they had changed the lives of many ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0296","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0296\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the negotiations of the Treaty of Brest Litovsk? \n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Cartoon by Clifford Berryman, June 11, 1918. It refers to the negotiations that ended with the signing of the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk. The German character has a paper that reads $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$deed to Russian lands” and the small character, identified as Lenine, says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$That's right. The barrel is his”. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0296A.png\" alt=\"HI0296A\">\n\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1918, June 11). *Prussian Peace Payment*. National Archives NextGen Catalog. Retrieved December 14, 2024 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/6011427. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - That Russia was giving up a lot of land as symbolized by paper labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Deed to Russian lands”\n\n - That Russia was being left poor as symbolized by how skinny the character labeled as Russia looks and by the fact that he does not even have any clothes\n\n - That Russia no longer had the capacity to fight, while the German character did\n\n - Lenin participated in the negotiations, and he was on the German side as he even wants Russia to give up the barrel\n\n - The Germans kept demanding more from Russia. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source A we can learn that Russia had to give up a lot of land to Germany under the Treaty of Brest Litovsk. This is shown by the German character holding a paper labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Deed to Russian lands” ($\\checkmark$). We can also learn that Lenin participated in the negotiations and that he was on the German side of the debate as he even wanted Russia to give up the barrel, which seems to be the only thing Russia had left ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0297","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0297\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the objectives of the men at the Paris Peace Conference?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$US History” by P. Scott; Corbett, Ventura and Volker Janssen,  2015.\n\n[Source: Scott P.; Corbett, V. and Volker J. *US History*. (2015). Retrieved December 16 10, 2024 from https://openstax.org/books/us-history/pages/23-4-from-war-to-peace. CC-BY 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Wilson wanted a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$peace without victory.”\n\n - Great Britain, France, and Italy wanted revenge and to protect themselves from potential future German aggressions\n\n - Great Britain, France, and Italy wanted to strengthen their colonial empires \n\n - Great Britain and France wanted monetary reparations and German lands. Italy also wanted more territory in Europe\n\n - Japan wanted to get concessions in Asia, \n\n - Fear of Russia's Bolshevik revolution made them want to secure their strategic interests\n\n - Except for Wilson, they did not have $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$world peace” as an objective\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source B, we can learn that the men at the Paris Peace Conference had different objectives. In the case of Wilson, his priority was to obtain peace ($\\checkmark$). In the case of Great Britain, France and Italy, they wanted to get revenge on the Germans ($\\checkmark$) and to increase their colonial empires at the expense of Germany ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0298","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0298\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying the results of the Paris Peace Conference. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Economic Consequences of the Peace” by John Maynard Keynes, 1920. The author was part of the British delegation to the Versailles Peace Conference. He believed the demands on Germany were too harsh so he resigned his government position and wrote this book explaining his reasons.\n\n[Source: Keynes, J. M. (1920). *The Economic Consequences of the Peace*. Retrieved December 16 10, 2024 from https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/keynes-the-economic-consequences-of-the-peace. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following: \n\n - The source contains insight as it was made in 1920 by a man who participated in the Paris Peace Conference as part of the British delegation (origin).\n\n - The source wants to explain the economic consequences of the Paris Peace Conference and why he thinks the treaty's impacts will be negative (purpose).\n\n - The source describes the real motivations of the negotiators and the reasons why the author thinks the results of the Paris Peace Conference are negative (content).\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n\n - The source lacks hindsight as it was made in 1920 and does not know the actual consequences of the peace treaties (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to defend his position against the results of the Paris Peace Conference (purpose)\n\n - The source does not provide the perspective of other people involved in the negotiations. It also contains some exaggerations such as when he refers to Clemenceau's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Carthaginian Peace” (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBecause Source C was created in 1920 by a man who was part of the British delegation at the Paris Peace Conference, it provides first hand insight into what happened at the conference ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is also valuable since Keynes seeks to explain the economic consequences of the Paris Peace Conference and give his view on why he thinks the impacts of the Treaty will be negative ($\\checkmark$ VP). From the source we can learn what the author saw as the real motivations of the negotiators and this makes the content valuable ($\\checkmark$ VC). \n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the results of the Paris Peace Conference. Regarding the origin, the source lacks hindsight as it was made in 1920 when the consequences of the peace treaties were still unknown ($\\checkmark$ LO). Moreover, the author wants to defend his position against the results of the Paris Peace Conference and justify his having resigned as a member of the British delegation therefore, he says only critical things about the Conference ($\\checkmark$ LP). This also makes the content limited since it makes the author use some exaggerations to prove his point, like when he refers to Clemenceau´s  “Carthaginian Peace” ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0299","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0299\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the Paris Peace Conference. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$US History” by P. Scott; Corbett, Ventura and Volker Janssen,  2015.\n\n[Source: Scott P.; Corbett, V. and Volker J. (2015). *US History*. Retrieved December 16 10, 2024 from https://openstax.org/books/us-history/pages/23-4-from-war-to-peace. CC-BY 4.0.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Economic Consequences of the Peace” by John Maynard Keynes, 1920. The author was part of the British delegation to the Versailles Peace Conference. He believed the demands on Germany were too harsh so he resigned his government position and wrote this book explaining his reasons.\n\n[Source: Keynes, J. M. (1920). *The Economic Consequences of the Peace*. Retrieved December 16 10, 2024 from https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/keynes-the-economic-consequences-of-the-peace. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison|($\\checkmark$ similarity)\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)\n\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive and no set answer is required.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n\n - They identify similar selfish objectives of some of the members at the Conference. \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sought to obtain some measure of revenge against Germany for drawing them into the war, to secure themselves against possible future aggressions from that nation, and also to maintain or even strengthen their own colonial possessions. Great Britain and France in particular sought substantial monetary reparations, as well as territorial gains, at Germany's expense. Japan also desired concessions in Asia, whereas Italy sought new territory in Europe. Finally, the threat posed by a Bolshevik Russia under Vladimir Lenin, and more importantly, the danger of revolutions elsewhere, further spurred on these allies to use the treaty negotiations to expand their territories and secure their strategic interests, rather than strive towards world peace.“ \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Their preoccupations, good and bad alike, related to frontiers and nationalities, to the balance of power, to imperial aggrandizements, to the future enfeeblement of a strong and dangerous enemy, to revenge, and to the shifting by the victors of their unbearable financial burdens on to the shoulders of the defeated.”\n\n - They both identify Wilson's main aim as peace. \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the American president's plans for a “peace without victory.” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$For a Peace of magnanimity or of fair and equal treatment, based on such $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ideology\" as the Fourteen Points of the President”\n\n - They both recognize the spread of the Russian Revolution as an underlying preoccupation of the negotiators. \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the threat posed by a Bolshevik Russia under Vladimir Lenin, and more importantly, the danger of revolutions elsewhere, further spurred on these allies “\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the same people who fear such a union are even more afraid of the success of Bolshevism” \n\n - They both recognize that there were disagreements between Wilson's objectives and the other leaders. \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$delegates at the conference had deep misgivings about the American president's plans” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two rival schemes for the future polity of the world took the field,—the Fourteen Points of the President, and the Carthaginian Peace of M. Clemenceau”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n\n - The sources disagree about the results of the Treaty for the victorious nations: \n\n     - Source C speaks of the future destruction of France and Italy if Germany is destroyed due to their $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$being so deeply and inextricably intertwined with their victims by hidden psychic and economic bonds.” \n\n     - Source B states that with the Treaty, the victorious nations are securing $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$their strategic interests”.\n\n - The sources identify different threats regarding Russia: \n\n     - Source B identifies $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the threat posed by a Bolshevik Russia under Vladimir Lenin” while in \n\n     - Source C there is are 2 potential threats, that of Bolshevik Russia and the potential  “union of forces between Russia and Germany”\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B and C agree on several issues regarding the Paris Peace Conference. Both recognize that there was a disagreement between the aims of Wilson and the aims of the other members of the conference ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B shows this when it says that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$delegates at the conference had deep misgivings about the American president's plans” and Source C speaks of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two rival schemes for the future polity of the world took the field,—the Fourteen Points of the President, and the Carthaginian Peace of M. Clemenceau”  ($\\checkmark$ evidence). For Wilson, they both identify that his only objective was peace and not to demand anything from the ones who lost the war ($\\checkmark$ similarity). We can see this in Source B when it states Wilson wanted  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$peace without victory” and in Source C which speaks of Wilson's desire $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$For a Peace of magnanimity or of fair and equal treatment” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). The sources recognize that this was not the case for the other delegates at the Conference. Source B says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Britain and France in particular sought substantial monetary reparations, as well as territorial gains, at Germany's expense”and Source C mentions that the winners wanted to place $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$their unbearable financial burdens on to the shoulders of the defeated” ($\\checkmark$ similarity and evidence).\n\nOn the other hand, the sources identify different perceived future problems for the nations of Europe. While Source B recognizes that the negotiators saw $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the threat posed by a Bolshevik Russia under Vladimir Lenin”, Source C adds the possibility of a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$union of forces between Russia and Germany” as something that worried many of the negotiators ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence). Source C is also much more extreme regarding the consequences of the Conference by predicting the future destruction of France and Italy if Germany is destroyed due to their $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$being so deeply and inextricably intertwined with their victims by hidden psychic and economic bonds.” Source B, on the other hand, mentions that the winners were able to use the treaties to secure “their strategic interests” ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0301","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0301\n\nIdentify **two** social divisions in Spain before the outbreak of the Spanish Civil War and explain how they contributed to the conflict.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny two of the following for identification:\n\n- The deep divide between rural peasants and landowners\n\n- Tensions between urban working classes and conservative elites\n\n- Conflict between the Catholic Church and anti-clerical movements\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\nAny of the following explanations (must correspond to the identification given):\n\n- The resentment of rural peasants toward wealthy landowners created support for leftist factions, polarizing society further.\n\n- Urban workers' demands for rights clashed with conservative elites who supported the Nationalists, deepening class divides.\n\n- Anti-clerical policies from the left alienated conservative and Catholic groups, consolidating their support for the Nationalists.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne social division before the Spanish Civil War was the tension between rural peasants and wealthy landowners ($\\checkmark$). Many peasants resented the lack of land reforms and poor working conditions, which pushed them to support leftist factions advocating for redistribution. This increased polarization as landowners aligned themselves with the Nationalists to protect their interests ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother key division was between the Catholic Church and anti-clerical factions ($\\checkmark$), as the Church was closely aligned with conservative landowners, while leftist groups saw it as an institution that upheld inequality. The increasing hostility between these groups contributed to the polarization that led to civil war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0302","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0302\n\nCompare and contrast the role of nationalism as a cause for the First and Second World Wars\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following with at least **one** comparison (similarity) and one difference (contrast). Answers which only address similarities OR differences can achieve a maximum of **three** marks.\n\nFor comparison, any **two** of the following for similarities:\n\n - In both wars, nationalism fueled tensions and rivalries. In WWI, nationalism created rivalries among European powers (e.g., France seeking revenge against Germany for the loss of Alsace-Lorraine). In WWII, nationalism drove aggressive expansion (e.g., Nazi Germany's vision of a Greater Germany)\n\n - Nationalism motivated territorial ambitions in both wars. In WWI, Balkan nationalism fueled disputes, such as Serbia's desire to unite South Slavic peoples. In WWII, territorial ambitions were central to expansionist nationalism, such as Japan's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Asia for Asians” policy and Germany's pursuit of Lebensraum.\n\n - Nationalism shaped alliance systems in both wars. In WWI, alliances like the Austro-Hungarian partnership with Germany were motivated by nationalist goals. In WWII, nationalism was key to forming the Axis powers, including the alliance of Germany, Italy, and Japan.\n\n - Nationalism played a role in mobilizing public opinion in both wars. In WWI, propaganda emphasized patriotic duty and loyalty to the nation. In WWII, nationalist rhetoric was used to justify aggressive policies and maintain public support for the war effort.\n\nFor contrast, any **two** of the following for differences:\n\n - Nationalism was often defensive in WWI but aggressive in WWII. In WWI, Serbian nationalism sought to protect sovereignty and oppose Austro-Hungarian dominance. In WWII, nationalism was more aggressive, as seen in Nazi Germany's Lebensraum policy and Japan's imperial ambitions in Asia.\n\n - Nationalism in WWI was not as ideologically driven as in WWII. In WWI, it focused on independence and territorial disputes, such as Balkan nationalism. In WWII, nationalism was deeply tied to ideologies like fascism and racial superiority, exemplified by Nazi Germany's belief in Aryan supremacy and Japan's cultural superiority.\n\n - Nationalism in WWI was largely confined to Europe, while in WWII it had a global reach. In WWI, nationalism was concentrated in the Balkans and among European powers. In WWII, it influenced conflicts worldwide, including Japan's Pan-Asianism and anti-colonial nationalism in Asia and Africa.\n\n - Colonies were less significant in the nationalism of WWI compared to WWII. In WWI, colonial nationalism played a minor role in the causes of the war. In WWII, Axis powers exploited colonial nationalist movements to weaken the Allies (e.g., Japan fostering anti-colonial sentiments in Southeast Asia).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nNationalism was a driving force in both the First and Second World Wars, creating rivalries and fueling territorial ambitions. For example, in WWI, nationalism intensified tensions between France and Germany, as France sought to reclaim Alsace-Lorraine. Similarly, in WWII, nationalism inspired Nazi Germany's expansionist policies, such as its aim to establish a Greater Germany ($\\checkmark$ similarity ). Additionally, nationalism influenced the formation of alliances in both wars. In WWI, nationalist goals aligned countries like Austria-Hungary and Germany, while in WWII, the Axis powers were formed through shared nationalist and expansionist ideologies ($\\checkmark$ simiarlity).\n\nHowever, nationalism in WWI was more focused on defense and sovereignty, such as Serbian nationalism resisting Austro-Hungarian control. In contrast, WWII nationalism was more aggressive, with nations like Japan pursuing imperial domination in Asia ($\\checkmark$ difference). Furthermore, nationalism in WWI lacked a strong ideological foundation, focusing instead on independence and territorial disputes. In WWII, it was deeply linked to ideologies like fascism and racial superiority, as seen in Nazi Germany's policies and Japan's belief in cultural dominance ($\\checkmark$ difference)."},{"ref":"HI0303","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0303\n\nDescribe **two** political causes of the Korean War.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The division of Korea after WWII led to the country being split along the 38th parallel, with North Korea led by Kim Il Sung, a communist leader, and South Korea led by Syngman Rhee, an anti-communist leader. Both claimed to be the legitimate government, leading to increasing tensions and border conflicts\n\n - Cold War tensions between the United States and the Soviet Union turned Korea into a proxy battleground, as both superpowers sought to expand their influence and ideologies in Asia, making war more likely.\n\n - The Soviet Union and China encouraged North Korea's leader, Kim Il Sung, to invade the South, providing military aid and believing the United States would not intervene, increasing North Korea's willingness to launch an attack.\n\n - The United States' policy of containing communism led to its commitment to defending South Korea, ensuring that any invasion by the North would result in direct American involvement, increasing the scale of the conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne political cause of the Korean War was the division of Korea at the 38th parallel after World War II, which resulted in two opposing governments. Kim Il Sung led the communist North, while Syngman Rhee led the anti-communist South, and both claimed to be the rightful rulers of all Korea, leading to rising tensions ($\\checkmark$). Another cause was the broader Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union. Korea became a key battleground in their ideological struggle, with each superpower supporting its respective Korean ally, making conflict increasingly inevitable ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0304","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0304\n\nCompare and contrast the role of foreign intervention in the causes of the Spanish Civil War and the Korean War.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nAt least **two** arguments, **one** comparison and one contrast, both with accompanying evidence\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n\n - In both wars, foreign powers played a crucial role in escalating tensions before the conflict began. In the Spanish Civil War, international support for different factions increased polarization, while in the Korean War, Cold War superpowers influenced the division of Korea and its growing hostilities.\n\n - Both conflicts were shaped by the ideological rivalry between communism and anti-communism. In Spain, the USSR supported the Republicans, while fascist powers aided Franco's Nationalists. In Korea, the Soviet Union and China backed North Korea, while the United States supported South Korea.\n\n - In both cases, foreign intervention contributed to transforming internal struggles into larger conflicts with international dimensions. Spain became a battleground for European fascist and communist forces, while Korea became a proxy war between the U.S. and the USSR.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n\n - In Korea, both sides received direct military intervention, with the U.S. and UN forces actively fighting for South Korea and China directly sending troops to support North Korea. In contrast, in Spain, only the Nationalists received direct military support in the form of German and Italian troops, while Soviet aid to the Republicans was limited to weapons, advisors, and volunteers.\n\n - The Spanish Civil War remained a civil conflict within one country, whereas the Korean War became an international war, involving multiple nations under the UN command and full-scale military operations.\n\n - The timing of foreign involvement differed. In the Spanish Civil War, foreign powers intervened after the war had already begun, supporting one side or the other as the conflict developed. In contrast, in Korea, foreign involvement was a direct cause of the war, as the division of Korea was largely driven by U.S. and Soviet policies after World War II, long before fighting started.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nForeign intervention played a significant role in the lead-up to both the Spanish Civil War and the Korean War. In both conflicts, foreign involvement intensified pre-existing divisions within the country, making reconciliation between opposing factions nearly impossible ($\\checkmark$ compare). Similarly, both wars became battlegrounds for broader ideological struggles, with the Spanish Civil War featuring support from the USSR for the Republicans and assistance from fascist Germany and Italy for Franco, while in Korea, the Soviet Union and China backed North Korea and the U.S. supported South Korea ($\\checkmark$ evidence ).\n\nHowever, the nature of direct involvement differed. In the Korean War, both sides received full military support, with the U.S. and its UN allies directly fighting for South Korea and China sending troops to assist North Korea. In contrast, in the Spanish Civil War, only Franco's Nationalists received direct foreign military assistance, with German and Italian forces actively participating, while Soviet aid to the Republicans was limited to arms, advisors, and volunteers ($\\checkmark$ contrast). Another key difference was the timing of foreign involvement. In Spain, external support was provided only after the war had begun, as foreign powers chose sides once the conflict was underway. Meanwhile, in Korea, foreign influence was a direct cause of the war, as the division of the peninsula between North and South was largely driven by U.S. and Soviet policies in the post-World War II period, long before fighting officially started ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0305","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0305\n\nDescribe **two** political causes of the First Chinese Civil War (1927-1937).\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The collapse of the First United Front (1924-1927) led to tensions between the Kuomintang (KMT) and the Chinese Communist Party (CCP). Chiang Kai-shek turned against the Communists in the Shanghai Massacre of 1927, leading to open conflict.\n\n - The authoritarian leadership of Chiang Kai-shek alienated various groups, including warlords and left-wing factions, leading to internal opposition within China.\n\n - The failure of the Nationalist government to implement social and economic reforms weakened its legitimacy, pushing peasants and workers to support the Communists.\n\n - The influence of Soviet and foreign ideologies contributed to tensions, as the USSR supported the CCP while the KMT sought Western backing, deepening ideological divisions.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne political cause of the First Chinese Civil War was the breakdown of cooperation between the Kuomintang and the Chinese Communist Party, which culminated in Chiang Kai-shek's violent purge of Communists in 1927, known as the Shanghai Massacre ($\\checkmark$). Another cause was the inability of the Nationalist government to address key social and economic issues, which led many disillusioned citizens, particularly peasants, to turn toward Communist leadership as an alternative ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0322","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0322\n\nEvaluate the impact of WWII on the Mexican economy.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. To respond to an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate” command term, it is valid to say that the whole goal was not reached as something negative. \n\nPositive results:\n\n - US loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million to the Mexican government\n\n - US guaranteed to purchase Mexican silver \n\n - The government encouraged the population to increase its productivity to assist the Allied cause. This led to an 8$\\%$ increase in GDP per year between 1940 and 1945, \n\n - The needs of the war and the war itself encouraged industrialization. Mexico replaced European and American production in Central America and the West Indies. For example, textile exports increased from less than 1$\\%$ of Mexico's total exports in 1939 to 32$\\%$ in 1945-46.\n\n - US assistance led to improved infrastructure (highways and ports) which in turn helped economic development. For example, in December 1942 the US sent a Technical Mission to help rehabilitate Mexico's railway system. \n\n - Thanks to the Bracero Program, which was revived in 1942, Mexico received remittances from the Mexican citizens that were given short term work permits to the US. For example, in 1944, 62,200 Mexican citizens were allowed to work in the US thanks to this program. As part of their contracts, braceros agreed to have ten percent of their wages withheld and placed in a fund controlled by the Mexican government.\n\nNegative results:\n\n - Inflation increased an average 18.5$\\%$ during each of the war years. \n\n - Large government contracts gave the opportunity for increased corruption\n\n - Mexico became dependent on the US market. By 1945 sent 90$\\%$ of its exports to the United States.\n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall economic effects of WWII were positive for Mexico.\n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative ones as well. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWorld War II presented significant economic benefits to Mexico. First, the US was willing to assist Mexico's economic development so it could produce the necessary war materials for the war effort. In that light, the US granted the Mexican government a loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million ($\\checkmark$). The higher demand the war generated as well as the government's mobilization of the economic capabilities to support the war led to a significant growth in Mexico's economy. Between 1940 and 1945 it had a sustained 8% average annual GDP growth ($\\checkmark$). A great part of this growth had to do with the industrial sector. As US and European consumer goods production was virtually halted, Mexico stepped in to cover that demand in the markets of Central America and the West Indies. This is evident in the fact that the textile industry was now a key component of Mexico's exports. Whereas in 1939 textile goods were only 1% of total exports, in 1945-1946 they were 32% of the total ($\\checkmark$). \n\nHowever, the war also brought economic challenges. Like in most countries, war leads to inflation. This was also the case in Mexico, where the war years had an average inflation rate of 18.5$\\%$ ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Mexico became heavily dependent on the U.S. market, with 90% of its exports directed to the United States by 1945 ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, its economic prosperity became tied to the United States.\n\nDespite higher inflation and dependence, the overall economic impact of WWII on Mexico's economy was positive. The important growth in its GDP and the development of its industries outweigh the negative consequences ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0325","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0325\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of World War II on the United States labor movement.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Roosevelt supported trade unions during the Great Depression and, in turn, trade unions did not organize strikes that might hamper the war effort. \n\n - In 1943, Congress passed the Smith-Connally Act (or War Labor Disputes Act) to ensure continued wartime production. It allowed the president to seize productions of wartime industries. Roosevelt would use it to intervene in labor disputes and ensure the production of goods to support the war effort\n\n - With the end of the war and the return of 10 million demobilised soldiers, the national government passed the Taft-Hartley Act. This measure prohibited strikes unless they were first authorized by the government and reserves, clearly limiting the power of trade unions. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nRoosevelt supported the work of trade unions through most of the Great Depression and unions responded in support by pledging not to strike during the war to assist in the final victory ($\\checkmark$). However, in light of a few exceptions, Congress passed the Smith-Connally Act (War Labor Disputes Act) in 1943, granting the president the authority to seize control of industries critical to the war ($\\checkmark$). Roosevelt utilized this power to intervene in labor disputes and maintain the steady production of goods necessary to support the war."},{"ref":"HI0326","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0326\n\nDescribe **three** diplomatic impacts of World War II on Canada.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Relations between the United States and Canada became closer. The Permanent Joint Board on Defense was created in 1940 to formulate a joint strategy for the military defence of North America. \n\n - While it had traditionally been represented by British diplomats (as Canada was part of the Commonwealth) during the war, Canada established formal, independent relations with Brazil, Argentina, and Chile.\n\n - The people of Canada made a tremendous contribution to the war effort which raised Canada's international profile. Almost 10% of the Canadian population had served in the Armed Forces during the war. \n\n - Canada made a significant contribution to the war effort. For example, more than half of its production of war material went to the UK. Because of this contribution, Canada was given a place as one of the founding members of the United Nations after the war ended.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nRelations between the United States and Canada grew closer during World War II. In 1940, a Permanent Joint Board on Defense was set up so that both countries could coordinate their military strategy for North America ($\\checkmark$). The significant contributions of the Canadian people to the war effort, such as the fact that almost 10$\\%$ of the whole population served in the Armed Forces,  improved the country's international profile. Many now saw it as second only to the great superpowers and thus a key global player. Evidence of this is the fact that it would become a founding member of the United Nations ($\\checkmark$). This increased international role of Canada translated itself into more diplomatic representation. Before the war, it had been mainly represented by British diplomats as it was part of the Commonwealth. However, during the war, it established independent diplomatic missions in Brazil, Argentina, and Chile ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0342","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0342\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Nuclear weapons were NOT necessary for the US to win the war with Japan.\" Provide **three** arguments that support this perspective.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following possible arguments with supporting evidence:\n- The US was keen on showing the USSR its superior armament as the alliance created to fight in WWII was turning into the rivalry that would characterize the Cold War\n    - At Potsdam there were already many disagreements with Stalin regarding the new world order. Truman did not want Soviet entry into the war against Japan since that would allow the Soviet Union to increase its its influence in East Asia just like it had done in Eastern Europe\n- The Japanese were willing to negotiate the peace as long as their Emperor was not made to pay the price for Japan's involvement in WWII.\n    - The US demanded an “unconditional surrender” and the Japanese feared this would mean an end to their constitutional monarchy. In fact, after the war, the US allowed the Emperor Hirohito to remain as sovereign, however, it had never clarified this when asking for peace.\n- The USSR had promised to join the war against Japan at the Yalta Conference and that was enough to bring the Japanese to their knees.\n- Japan was virtually defeated by August of 1945. Military advisers told Truman that Japan that the US naval blockade of the home islands meant that its war effort would wane quickly. Thanks to the blockade and the continued conventional bombing, Japan was expected to surrender soon.\n    - The Japanese had sent Stalin a telegram in July 1945 asking for peace.\n- What the US really wanted was vengeance for the attack on Pearl Harbour and for the horrible treatment of Japanese prisoners of war.\n    - Following the liberation of the Allied prisoner-of-war camps in 1944 and 1945, accounts of horrific conditions and barbaric treatment by the Japanese guards fed a desire for vengeance that has started in 1941\n- Making the bomb had cost thousands of hours of research and development as well as billions of dollars. The government had to show the resources had been well spent\n    - The Manhattan Project cost approximately \\$2 billion by 1945 (or over \\$30 billion in 2023 USD)\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSince the dropping of the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, there has been considerable debate on whether these horrific events were really necessary for the US to win the war with Japan. Many arguments support the notion that it was actually not necessary. In fact, many of Truman's military advisers, like General Eisenhower, told him that nuclear weapons were not needed since Japan's surrender was imminent and that it could not hold out much longer due to the US`s blockade of the Japanese home islands ($\\checkmark$). This blockade prevented the empire's soldiers from returning home and accessing the resources necessary to keep up the war effort. In fact, in July of 1945, Japan had already requested the USSR to act as a mediator for peace negotiations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe main issue that prevented a Japanese surrender was the future of Emperor Hirohito, the political and religious leader of the nation. If the US had guaranteed his position, as it did once the war was over, the Japanese government might have been willing to surrender sooner ($\\checkmark$). However, the US insisted on demanding an “unconditional surrender” even in the Potsdam Declaration of July 1945, and this scared the Japanese, who saw their emperor as a living god ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nMany historians point to the fact that the US wanted to use its atomic weapons not so much to end the war but to prove its superiority to the USSR since the end of WWII meant the conclusion of the alliance against the Axis and the start of the rivalry that would characterize the Cold War ($\\checkmark$).  At the Potsdam Conference, disagreements between Truman and Stalin over the post-war world order were already evident. Truman wanted to end the war as quickly as possible so that the Soviets would not have to set foot on Japan, allowing them to extend Soviet influence in East Asia, much like it had in Eastern Europe ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0343","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0343\n\nDescribe the Manhattan Project.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- President Roosevelt assembled the greatest minds in physics and chemistry in 1942 so they would discover how to initiate the chain reaction of nuclear fission and harness it into a weapon,\n- The scientific efforts were directed by Dr Robert Oppenheimer and included scientists from around the world.\nIt employed over 125,000 people and cost about 2 billion dollars.\n- By the summer of 1945 the project test an atomic weapon in New Mexico.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1942, President Roosevelt brought together the greatest minds in physics and chemistry to discover how to initiate a nuclear fission chain reaction and harness it as a weapon ($\\checkmark$). This was a secret endeavor known as the Manhattan Project. Over the years, it employed over 125,000 people and cost approximately 2 billion dollars ($\\checkmark$). By the summer of 1945, the project had successfully tested an atomic weapon in New Mexico, paving the way for the use of atomic bombs against Japan ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0429","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0429\n\nExplain **two** economic causes of the Spanish Civil War. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following causes with supporting details/explanation:\n\n- Agrarian inequality and rural poverty deepened social divisions\n\n    - Spain's economy remained dominated by agriculture, with land ownership concentrated in latifundios, leaving most rural workers landless and in poverty.\n\n   - The Second Republic's land reforms (1931-1936) were limited and slow, failing to meet peasants' expectations while alienating conservative landowners.\n\n    - Rural uprisings and land seizures led to violent confrontations, pushing many landowners to support Franco and the military to protect their interests.\n\n- Economic instability and the Great Depression increased political polarisation \n\n    - Pre-existing structural weakness and low industrialisation were worsened by the Great Depression, causing soaring unemployment and widespread poverty.\n\n    The government’s poor economic management frustrated both the working class (who saw reforms as insufficient) and the elites (who saw them as threatening).\n\n    - By 1936, economic hardship and class tensions had eroded faith in democratic compromise, contributing directly to support for armed uprising.\n\n- Government mismanagement of reforms and finances undermined economic stability.\n\n    - Republican reforms in land, labour, education, and the military were seen as too moderate by the left and too radical by the right, satisfying neither.\n\n    - Inconsistent implementation and internal divisions within the government created a perception of incompetence and increased frustration.\n\n    - Widespread dissatisfaction with reform efforts led to declining confidence in the Republic, making it vulnerable to authoritarian alternatives.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne economic cause of the Spanish Civil War was agrarian inequality  ($\\checkmark$). A significant portion of Spain's land was concentrated in the hands of a small elite, particularly in the south, where large estates (latifundios) left many peasants landless and in poverty. Attempts at land reform by the Second Republic (1931-1936) were slow and ineffective, increasing tensions between rural workers and wealthy landowners, leading to strikes and violence, which ultimately led to many landowners supporting Franco and the military in order to protect their interests ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother economic cause was the economic instability caused by the Great Depression ($\\checkmark$). Spain's already fragile economy suffered further decline, resulting in high unemployment and reduced wages for both industrial and agricultural workers. This deepened class divisions, with the working class supporting left-wing movements advocating for economic reform, while business elites and landowners backed right-wing groups resisting change. The economic crisis radicalised both sides, increasing polarisation and contributing to the outbreak of war in 1936 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0430","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0430\n\nDescribe **two** reasons the Catholic Church supported Franco and the Nationalists during the Spanish Civil War. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Opposition to Republican anti-clerical reforms\n\n    - The Second Republic (1931-1936) introduced secular laws that weakened the Catholic Church's influence, such as banning religious education, confiscating Church property, and legalising divorce.\n\n    - These measures were viewed as attacks on traditional values. Many churches were burned and priests assaulted, deepening hostility toward the Republican government.\n\n    - The Church saw Franco as someone who would restore its authority and reverse secularisation.\n\n- Fear of left-wing violence and persecution\n\n    - The Republican side included radical left-wing groups such as anarchists and communists who promoted atheism and were involved in violent acts against the Church.\n\n    - Religious sites were desecrated, clergy were executed, and symbols of Catholicism were destroyed.\n\n    - The Church aligned itself with the Nationalists as a means of survival and protection against what it saw as a campaign to eliminate religion.\n\n- Franco's pro-Catholic image and policies\n\n    - Franco portrayed himself as a staunch defender of Catholicism and traditional moral values.\n\n    - His regime restored religious privileges, reintroduced Catholic education, and elevated Catholicism as the moral foundation of Spanish identity.\n\n    - The Church supported Franco in return for the promise of social and political restoration.\n\n- Religious framing of the war as a crusade\n\n    - The Nationalists used the language of a religious crusade, presenting the war as a battle to save Spain from atheism and moral decay.\n\n    - This appealed to the Church's sense of spiritual mission and helped frame support for Franco as a moral imperative.\n\n    - The Church gave ideological and moral backing to the Nationalists, contributing to their strong support base.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason the Catholic Church supported Franco and the Nationalists was its opposition to the anti-clerical policies of the Second Republic ($\\checkmark$). The Republican government had introduced reforms that restricted the Church's influence, such as banning religious education in schools, confiscating Church property, and legalising divorce. Many priests and religious figures were persecuted, leading the Church to align itself with Franco, who promised to restore its authority($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reason was the Nationalists' portrayal of the war as a religious crusade against communism and atheism ($\\checkmark$). Left-wing groups within the Republican side, including anarchists and communists, viewed the Church as a symbol of oppression and attacked clergy members, burning churches and monasteries. Franco and the Nationalists positioned themselves as defenders of Catholicism, gaining strong Church support in return ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0431","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0431\n\nIdentify **two** key events in Spain between 1931 and 1936 and explain how they increased tensions leading to the war.  [6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n- Proclamation of the Second Republic (April 1931) - King Alfonso XIII abdicated, and Spain became a republic. The new progressive government introduced reforms that angered conservatives, including land reform, military restructuring, and anti-clerical policies. The shift from monarchy to a republic immediately polarised Spanish society.\n\n- Anti-Clerical Reforms and Church Burnings (1931-1932) - The government restricted the Church's power by banning religious education and nationalising Church property. Leftist groups attacked churches and monasteries, burning them down in 1931, which led to conservative outrage and deepened divisions between secular and religious factions.\n\n- Failed Military Coup by General Sanjurjo (1932) - Right-wing general José Sanjurjo attempted a coup to overthrow the Republic, but it failed. This event demonstrated that conservative factions and the military were willing to use force to oppose the government, increasing political instability.\n\n- Land Reforms and Peasant Uprisings (1932-1933) - The government attempted to redistribute land from wealthy landowners to poor peasants, but landowners resisted, and reforms were slow. Peasants began staging uprisings, and the failure to effectively implement reforms led to further radicalisation of both landowners, who supported the right, and peasants, who turned to more extreme leftist movements.\n\n- Right-Wing Victory in the 1933 Elections (CEDA Government) - The right-wing Confederación Española de Derechas Autónomas (CEDA) won the elections and began rolling back progressive reforms. This angered left-wing workers, anarchists, and socialists, who viewed it as a betrayal of the Republic's values and responded with strikes and revolts.\n\n- Asturias Miners' Revolt (October 1934) - Left-wing workers in Asturias staged an armed revolt against the right-wing government in response to CEDA's policies. The uprising was brutally suppressed by government forces under Francisco Franco, resulting in thousands of deaths and imprisonments. This deepened class divisions and helped elevate Franco as a key figure for right-wing forces.\n\n- Popular Front Victory in the 1936 Elections - In February 1936, the left-wing Popular Front coalition won the elections, prompting mass strikes, land occupations, and violent street clashes between left- and right-wing militias. Right-wing groups, including Franco and other military officers, saw this as a threat and began actively planning a coup.\n\n- Assassination of José Calvo Sotelo (July 1936) - Right-wing politician José Calvo Sotelo was assassinated by leftist police officers in retaliation for the killing of a Republican officer. His murder was seen as a major provocation by the right, and shortly after, Franco and the military launched a coup on July 17, 1936, triggering the outbreak of the Spanish Civil War.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Asturias Miners' Revolt of October 1934 was an armed uprising by left-wing workers in response to the right-wing CEDA government's reversal of progressive reforms ($\\checkmark$). The miners seized key areas in Asturias, but the government, led by Francisco Franco, brutally suppressed the rebellion using the army and Moroccan troops ($\\checkmark$). Thousands were killed, and mass imprisonments followed, deepening class divisions and increasing hostility between left-wing and right-wing factions in Spain ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAnother key event was the assassination of José Calvo Sotelo in July 1936, a prominent right-wing monarchist politician ($\\checkmark$). He was killed by leftist police officers in retaliation for the murder of a Republican officer, and his death was perceived by many on the right as a symbol of the Republic's inability to maintain law and order ($\\checkmark$). The assassination radicalised conservative factions and was seen as the final spark that justified military intervention ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0432","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0432\n\nDescribe **two** reasons why the Republicans were defeated in the Spanish Civil War.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Internal Divisions and Lack of Unity\n\n    - The Republican side was politically fragmented, with communists, socialists, anarchists, and moderate Republicans frequently clashing.\n\n    - The May Days of 1937 in Barcelona saw open fighting between communists and anarchists, severely weakening the Republican war effort.\n\n    - Franco's Nationalists were united under a single military command, while the Republicans lacked centralised leadership.\n\n- Limited and Unreliable Foreign Support\n\n    - The Republicans relied heavily on Soviet aid, which was inconsistent and politically motivated.\n\n    - Britain and France enforced the Non-Intervention Agreement, preventing democratic nations from assisting the Republic.\n\n    - The International Brigades provided some support, but their numbers were insufficient to counter the Nationalists' foreign aid.\n\n- Superior Nationalist Foreign Aid\n\n    - Germany (Hitler) and Italy (Mussolini) provided Franco with planes, tanks, and troops, significantly strengthening the Nationalist forces.\n\n    - Germany's Condor Legion carried out the bombing of Guernica (1937), showcasing the military superiority of the Nationalists.\n\n    - Portugal's António Salazar supported Franco by allowing supplies and troops to pass through Portuguese territory.\n\n- Military Weaknesses of the Republicans\n\n    - The Republicans had fewer professional officers and lacked proper military coordination.\n\n    - Many soldiers were militia fighters rather than trained military personnel.\n\n    - The Nationalists had a more structured and disciplined army, led by experienced military leaders like Franco, Mola, and Yagüe.\n\n- Economic Difficulties\n\n    - The Republicans lacked access to crucial resources such as weapons, fuel, and food.\n\n    - Nationalists controlled important agricultural and industrial areas, such as Andalusia (food supply) and the Basque Country (industry).\n\n    - Inflation, shortages, and corruption further weakened the Republican economy.\n\n- Effective Nationalist Propaganda and Organisation\n\n    - Franco presented himself as the defender of Catholicism and Spanish tradition, gaining strong support from the Catholic Church and conservatives.\n\n    - Nationalists controlled key media outlets and used propaganda to rally support, while the Republicans struggled to maintain a cohesive message.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason for the Republican defeat in the Spanish Civil War was internal divisions among left-wing factions. The Republicans were split between communists, socialists, anarchists, and moderates, which led to infighting and weakened their ability to fight effectively ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reason was the lack of strong foreign support. While Franco received consistent aid from Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy, the Republicans relied on limited Soviet assistance and were further weakened by the Non-Intervention Agreement, which prevented democratic nations like Britain and France from helping them ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0433","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0433\n\nOutline **two** ways German and Italian support helped Franco's Nationalists during the Spanish Civil War. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Germany (Hitler) provided aircraft, tanks, and troops, giving Franco air superiority. The Condor Legion (Germany) bombed key Republican cities, including Guernica (April 1937), damaging morale and infrastructure.\n\n- Italy (Mussolini) sent 70,000 troops, artillery, and air support, which helped Franco win major battles like Guadalajara (1937).\n\n- Portugal's Salazar provided logistical support and allowed Nationalist troops to move freely through Portugal.\n\n- Germany and Italy provided military supplies on credit, ensuring Franco had continuous access to weapons.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne way foreign intervention affected the outcome of the Spanish Civil War was through Germany and Italy's military support for Franco's Nationalists. Germany's Condor Legion provided advanced aircraft and carried out devastating bombings, most famously the destruction of Guernica in April 1937, which damaged Republican morale and infrastructure ($\\checkmark$). Italy sent approximately 70,000 troops, tanks, and artillery, helping the Nationalists win critical battles such as Guadalajara ($\\checkmark$). This consistent and large-scale support gave Franco a significant military advantage."},{"ref":"HI0530","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0530\n\nDiscuss the significance of foreign aid in the Spanish Civil War.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following arguments/examples of foreign aid:\n\n - Italy and Germany sent a substantial number of troops,weapons, and money to assist the Nationalists, which led to effective bombings of Republican-controlled cities.\n\n - Portugal supported Franco and allowed open borders which aided the nationalists when they needed new supplies.\n\n - Stalin gave financial support and some weapons to the Republic. However, Stalin used this support to gain power among the Spanish communists and did not communicate well with the Republican leadership, making this aid less effective.\n\n - The International Brigade, a group of volunteer soldiers, fought for the Republicans but were hampered by communication issues and a lack of strong leadership.\n\n - France and Britain were determined not to intervene in Spain. Their influence was enough to ensure that the Republicans did not receive financial support from nations that otherwise may have supported their effort.  However, they were unable to deter Germany and Italy from intervening on behalf of the Nationalists.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses should consider the role of foreign aid from various countries and in support of different sides of the conflict.\n\n - The strongest responses are likely to conclude that some foreign aid played a more important role in the conflict and/or that particular sides of the conflict benefited more from foreign aid.\n\n \\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nForeign aid had a positive impact on the Nationalists and a negative impact on the Republicans during the Spanish Civil War. The Nationalists received financial and military aid from Italy and Germany. The military aid came in the form of organized troops and new advanced weaponry. This allowed the Nationalists to lead several successful campaigns, including the bombing of Guernica ($\\checkmark$). While Portugal did not offer soldiers, they were sympathetic to Franco’s cause and allowed the Nationalists to cross their borders. This was helpful to the Nationalists in that it allowed them to resupply when needed ($\\checkmark$). The Republicans did receive help from international allies, like the Soviet Union. However, this help was not as substantial. Stalin used his help to try to gain control over the Spanish communists, causing distrust with Republican leadership ($\\checkmark$). Even more detrimental to the Republicans was the aid they did not receive. France and Great Britain were determined not to get involved in the Spanish Civil War and stopped most of the support that the Republicans could have received from outside sources, such as the International Brigade. However, because Italy and Germany ignored the League of Nations' Non-Intervention Committee, the Nationalists still received aid ($\\checkmark$). Foreign aid from Germany and Italy played a significant role in helping the Nationalists, while foreign aid for the Republicans did little to support their efforts ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0531","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0531\n\nDescribe **two** domestic political effects of the Spanish Civil War on Spain.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Francisco Franco emerged from the war as the victor and developed a military dictatorship with fascist leanings. This led to a foreign policy that was sympathetic to the Axis powers, though Spain remained neutral in World War II.\n\n - Franco claimed that Nationalist victory was a victory against communism and waged a domestic battle against republicans who survived the war. Many were killed in the years following the war.\n\n - The 1939 Law of Political Responsibility allowed the government to seize the property and land of former republicans.\n\n - The Church saw a resurgence in power because they were allied with the Nationalists and all citizens were required to assimilate to Nationalist views.\n\n - Labor unions were dismantled and, as a result, many people saw their wages reduced.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrancisco Franco's victory in the Spanish Civil War led to the development of a military dictatorship with fascist leanings. This led to a domestic battle against Republicans who survived the war. In the years following the Nationalist victory, many Republicans were killed or forced to assimilate to Nationalist values ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the Church saw a resurgence in power in Spain under Franco's leadership and often teamed with him to espouse Nationalist and Catholic ideals for Spain ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0532","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0532\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about General Francisco Franco's influence during the war?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | \n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A propaganda poster titled El Generalisimo by Pedero, a poster artist for a socialist trade union during the Spanish Civil War. The title depicts Francisco Franco as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$*el generalisimo*” or supreme commander whose cape is being carried by three characters representing the military, business owners and the Catholic Church\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0532.png\" alt=\"HI0532\">\n\n[Source: Pedero. (1937). *El Generalisimo.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved February 21, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_General%C3%ADsimo.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny three of the following\n\n - Franco is larger than other characters, which implies that he is considered to be extremely powerful.\n\n - Franco is shown with a skull face, suggesting that he is considered deadly or to be feared.\n\n - The swastika on his uniform suggests that his power is backed by Germany.\n\n - The men trailing behind him, holding his cape and representing the military, the capitalists, and the Catholic Church, suggest that all three groups supported Franco and his policies.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn this poster, General Franco is larger than the men following him, suggesting that he is the most powerful among them ($\\checkmark$) The swastika on his chest indicates that his power might come from foreign sources like the Nazis ($\\checkmark$). The three men trailing behind him look like they represent the Church, the military and the wealthy and that they are backing him and riding his coattails to gain more power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0533","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0533\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the reasons for the Republican defeat in the Spanish Civil War?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B**  $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt written by Leon Trotsky in 1939 about the results of the Spanish Civil War.\n\n[Source: Trotsky, L. (1939, January). *The Tragedy of Spain.* Originally published in the “Russian Bulletin of the Opposition”. Retrieved February 21, 2025  from https://marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1939/01/spain.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Franco had a clear plan throughout the war that the Spanish bourgeoisie could support. \n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Franco's great superiority lies in this, that he has a clear and definite program: to safeguard and stabilize capitalist property, the rule of the exploiters, and the domination of the church; and to restore the monarchy.”\n\n - The Popular Front (Republicans) betrayed their own messaging, leading to distrust from the workers and peasants. \n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But the honorable Republicans did everything in their power to trample, to besmirch, or simply to drown in blood the cherished hopes of the oppressed masses. As a result, we have witnessed during the last two years the growing distrust and hatred of the Republican cliques on the part of the peasants and workers.”\n\n - The Republicans and their supporters became indifferent to the actions of their leadership. \n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Despair or dull indifference gradually replaced revolutionary enthusiasm and the spirit of self - sacrifice. The masses turned their backs on those who had deceived and trampled upon them. That is the primary reason for the defeat of the Republican troops.”\n\n - By trusting Stalin and embracing capitalism, the Republicans betrayed their messaging and lost the support of the people. \n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Under the label of the Popular Front, they set up a joint stock company. Under the leadership of Stalin, they have assured the most terrible defeat when all the conditions for victory were at hand.”\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Leon Trotsky, the Republicans were defeated for betraying their own messaging, for losing the support of the people and because the Nationalists had clearer goals and messaging. The excerpt states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$dull indifference gradually replaced revolutionary enthusiasm” among the Republicans and that this is because of their frustration with their leadership  ($\\checkmark$). The leadership betrayed their own messaging by embracing capitalism and setting up “a joint-stock company.” This caused the peasants and workers to distrust the Republicans ($\\checkmark$. In addition, Franco and the Nationalists remained clear and steady in their program. This led to capitalists supporting them as well as them getting the support of strong capitalist nations ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0534","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0534\n\nAccording to Source C, what are the goals of Franco's new government?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The US Consul (diplomat) in Seville, Spain, Charles A Bay, sent the following telegram to the US Secretary of State, Cordell Hull upon hearing a radio broadcast. \n\n[Source: Bay, C. A. (1936, October 4). 852.00/3349: Telegram - The Consul at Seville (Bay) to the Secretary of State. From $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Foreign Relations of the United States Diplomatic Papers, 1936, Europe, Volume II.” U.S. Department of State. Retrieved February 21, 2015 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1936v02/d480. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Maintenance of foreign affairs, except with Russia\n\n - The elimination of the popular vote\n\n - To establish a new government based on totalitarian concepts\n\n - A revision of the tax system\n\n - Respect for the Catholic Church\n\n - Independence of the peasant\n\n - That labor would be guaranteed under his leadership\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to the US Consul to Seville, Franco was looking to establish a government based on totalitarian principles ($\\checkmark$). As such, he wanted to eliminate the popular vote, saying that this would better serve the population ($\\checkmark$). Also, Franco wanted to revise the tax system ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0587","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0587\n\nContrast the impact of conscription in the United States and Canada during World War II.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - In Canada, the issue of conscription generated a division in public opinion between the English speakers and the Quebecois, while in the US, it was generally less debated by the public, especially after Pearl Harbor. \n\n      - Although 70$\\%$ of Canadians agreed to conscription in the plebiscite organized by the government, in Quebec only 20$\\%$ of voters agreed. \n\n     - While there was some debate about the need for the draft in the US before Pearl Harbor, after it, it largely disappeared. Voluntary enlistment increased such that President Roosevelt had to issue an Executive Order (9279) to slow down enlistments in order to protect the US workforce.\n\n - The impact of conscription in the Canadian war effort was negligible, while in the US, it resulted in most of the combatants. \n\n     - Most Canadian combatants were volunteers. Only after 1944 did the Canadian government have to resort to conscription. Until the surrender of Germany only 2500 conscripts saw battle. In the US only about 40$\\%$ of servicemen were volunteers. The majority of the 10 million strong Armed Forces were draftees. \n\n - The governments dealt differently with those who were conscripted and refused to fight.\n\n     - In Canada, there was a mutiny of soldiers who refused to be sent overseas in Terrace, British Columbia. The mutiny was quickly put down, and those involved were sent to other military bases. In the US, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$conscientious objectors” were allowed to serve as medics or work in the Civilian Public Service. The idea was to keep them out of public view. Members of the African American Nation of Islam refused to serve due to political reasons. They, including their leader Elijah Mohammad, were convicted on charges of draft dodging. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn the war itself, the impact of conscription was quite different ($\\checkmark$). One of the reasons that Prime Minister King was able to order conscription so late in the war was the large number of volunteers that made conscription unnecessary. This changed only after 1944 so, in total, only 2500 Canadian conscripts saw any action before Germany's surrender. In the US, the majority of servicemen, about 60$\\%$, were actually draftees ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBoth countries dealt differently with those who were conscripted but refused to fight ($\\checkmark$). When in Canada there was a mutiny in Terrace, British Columbia ,of soldiers who refused to be sent overseas, it was quickly put dow,n and those involved were sent to other military bases suffering no long-term sanctions. However, in the US, while $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$conscientious objectors” were allowed to serve as medics or work in the Civilian Public Service, this was not so for those who had political reasons to oppose conscription ($\\checkmark$). The most notable case is that of members of the African American Nation of Islam who refused to serve and fight for a country that did not grant them equal rights. They, including their leader Elijah Mohammad, were convicted on charges of draft dodging and sent to prison ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0588","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0588\n\nDescribe the workings of one US government agency that helped organize the economy for the Second World War.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** details of **one** of the following government agencies:\n\n - Office of Production Management (OPM, 1941-1942)\n\n     - Created by Roosevelt in January 1941, before the passage of the Lend-Lease Act and the declaration of war by the US. \n\n     - It aimed to regulate the production and supply of materials necessary for national defense.\n\n     - It was not always successful in avoiding supply chain bottlenecks.\n\n - The War Production Board (WPB, 1942-1945)\n\n     - It replaced the Office of Production Management after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbour. \n\n     - Its job was to organize the production of war materials and ensure war industries had the necessary raw materials as well as to help .\n\n     - It could not always ensure a constant flow of raw materials such as aluminium but it was partly responsible for a great increase in production of war materials. For example, production of aircraft in 1944 was 5 times that of 1941. \n\n - War Manpower Commission (WMC, 1942-1945)\n\n     - It was set up by President Roosevelt in1942 due to tensions between the military and the war industry. \n\n     - Its aim was to balance the manpower needs of agriculture, industry and the armed forces. \n\n     - It was responsible for publicity campaigns aimed at women to convince them to join the war industries or essential civilian services\n\n     - It set up a Training Program to train unskilled workers to cover the shortages caused by those who had enlisted. \n\n     - It was able to train more than 1.5 million workers thus covering the shortage of skilled labor. \n\n - Office of Price Administration (OPA, 1941-1947)\n\n     - It was set up to control prices and rents to avoid inflation\n\n     - It placed maximum prices on 90% of retail food prices and residential rents. \n\n     - It introduced rationing to control demand of scarce goods. It rationed things like tires, gasoline, fuel oil, coal, firewood, nylon, silk, and shoes.\n\n     - Its measures resulted in an average inflation of 3.5% for the years 1942-1946. \n\n - Office of War Mobilization (OWM, 1943-1945)\n\n     - Managed the coordination among various wartime agencies to ensure smooth production.\n\n     - Helped prioritize military contracts and ensure resources were used efficiently.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Office of Price Administration was established to control prices and rents in order to prevent inflation during World War II ($\\checkmark$). It set maximum prices on 90$\\%$ of retail food items and residential rents while also introducing rationing to manage the demand for scarce goods ($\\checkmark$). Thanks to the action of the Office of Price Administration, the average inflation rate was only 3.5$\\%$ between 1942 and 1946 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0590","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0590\n\nDescribe Brazilian participation in WWII.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Brazil's navy helped combat German U-boats in the south and central Atlantic Ocean. \n\n - The USA built airfields in Brazil to be used for the war effort. For example, the US air base at Parnamirim was the largest single US air base outside its own territory. From the base, supplies were sent to North Africa or the China-Burma-India theatre. Because of its significant contribution to the war, the air base at Parnamirim was known as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Trampoline to Victory.\"\n\n - Brazil provided key materials for the US war effort, such as iron ore, chrome, manganese, nickel, bauxite, and rubber.\n\n - Brazil was the only Latin American country to send part of its army, 25,000 men, into combat overseas. In 1944, the Brazilian Expeditionary Force (BEF) and the Air Force's First Fighter Group were sent to fight in Italy. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince its entry into the war in 1942, Brazil played an important logistical role in the Allied war effort. The government of Vargas also allowed the US to build airfields in Brazil, including Parnamirim, which became the largest U.S. air base outside the continental United States. This base became known as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Trampoline to Victory\"  due to its importance for transporting supplies to North Africa and the Pacific theater ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Brazil provided essential raw materials to the US war effort, such as iron ore, chrome, manganese, nickel, bauxite, and rubber ($\\checkmark$). Brazil was also the only Latin American country to deploy troops overseas, sending 25,000 Brazilian Expeditionary Force (BEF) soldiers to fight in Italy in 1944 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0591","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0591\n\nDescribe US participation in WWII.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Initially, the US participated by providing the Allies with supplies through the Cash and Carry policy and then the Lend-Lease policy. For example, the Lend-Lease Act granted the USSR 18,000 airplanes, 1,500 tanks, and more than 500 ships.\n\n - After 1941, the US was responsible for carrying out the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$island hopping” strategy in the Pacific to defeat Japan. \n\n - In both the European theater and the Pacific theater, it was responsible for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$carpet bombing” of major cities. Estimates about damage to urban areas in Japan vary, but many indicate well over 40$\\%$ of urban areas were destroyed with a higher percentage in Tokyo. American troops participated in major battles on the Italian and Pacific fronts and in the Normandy invasion. In all, they sent about 16 million soldiers, 1 million of whom were casualties. \n\n - The US was responsible for effectively ending the war with the use of atomic bombs against Japan. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe United States initially supported the Allies by supplying resources through the Cash and Carry policy and later the Lend-Lease Act. By the Lend-Lease Act, 18,000 airplanes, 1,500 tanks, and over 500 ships were provided to the USSR  ($\\checkmark$). After officially entering the war in December of 1941, American forces were responsible for large-scale $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$carpet bombing\" campaigns in both Europe and the Pacific, with devastating effects. In Tokyo alone it is estimated that around 60$\\%$ of the city was destroyed.  ($\\checkmark$). Ultimately, the U.S. brought the war to an end by forcing Japan to surrender after the use of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0592","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0592\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the treatment of Japanese Americans during WWII?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | During a visit to the Gila River camp, where his family is confined, Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto shows his Purple Heart to his brother, Susumu. October 31, 1943.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0592%28A%29.png\" alt=\"HI0592(A)\">\n\n[Source: No title. (1945, October 31). Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2024 from https://fdrlibrary.org/documents/356632/390886/Japanese+American+Internment+Photographs.pdf/56c62fad-dd2a-4325-9fef-76ca2ff1ffa6.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Whole families were confined to camps\n\n - Even though Japanese Americans were willing to risk their lives for their country, they were still treated as criminals. \n\n - Even harmless and innocent people were confined. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source A, we can learn that whole families were confined to camps, even with their little children, such as the one portrayed in the photograph ($\\checkmark$). We can also learn the unfairness of the situation as, despite the willingness of Japanese Americans to risk their lives for their country, people like Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto, awarded the Purple Heart, were still treated as threats or criminals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0593","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0593\n\nWhat, according to Source C, was the impact of internment on Japanese Americans?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An explanation of Executive Order 9066 published by the National Archives (2022).\n\n[Source: Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (2022, January 24). *Milestone Documents. National Archives*. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift //archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- They lost personal liberties. \n\n- Due to the speed at which the orders were implemented, many were unable to sell their property quickly. \n\n- They were treated as criminals as the camps were built in isolated places and surrounded by guards. \n\n- They had fewer rights than criminals since they could not even appeal their incarceration.\n\n- Even though they were treated as enemies of the country, some were still drafted into the army. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source C, we can learn that internment for Japanese Americans was a traumatic experience. Being confined, they lost many of their personal liberties ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, due to the rapid implementation of the order, many were forced to sell their property as quickly as possible, often unable to get a reasonable price ($\\checkmark$). Perhaps the worst consequence was that they were treated as criminals, put in camps built in the middle of nowhere, and surrounded by guards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0594","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0594\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | During a visit to the Gila River camp, where his family is confined, Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto shows his Purple Heart to his brother, Susumu. October 31, 1943.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/594.PNG\" alt=\"594\">\n\n[Source: No title. (1945, October 31). Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2024 from https://fdrlibrary.org/documents/356632/390886/Japanese+American+Internment+Photographs.pdf/56c62fad-dd2a-4325-9fef-76ca2ff1ffa6.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source contains insight as the picture was taken in 1943, a year after the internment of people of Japanese origins started.  (origin).\n\n - The photograph was made to show the reality of what Japanese Americans were living. \n\n - From the source we can learn that even small children were confined and that you could get out of the camps to serve in the army. We also learn that despite the injustice suffered, Japanese Americans stood out for their bravery in combat defending the US (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source lacks hindsight. The photographer could not know how the lives of Japanese Americans would change in the upcoming years (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to criticize the internment camps as it highlights the injustice of a man who has risked his life for his country while his country was depriving his family of their freedom (purpose).\n\n - The source is only one small window into the conditions in which those in the internment camps lived. It portrays only one moment in time for two people. Because it's a photograph, it cannot show the reasons for the establishment of internment camps (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A has a valuable origin since it has insight as the photograph was taken in 1943, a year after the internment of Japanese Americans began ($\\checkmark$ VO). Its purpose is also valuable since it seeks to show the reality of the internment camps, showing that even young children were confined ($\\checkmark$ VP). The source shows that some internees were allowed to leave the camps to serve in the army. From the purple heart earnt by Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto we can also learn that, despite the injustice they endured, Japanese Americans demonstrated remarkable bravery in combat, defending the United States ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Its origin is limited since it lacks hindsight, as the photographer could not predict how the lives of Japanese Americans would change in the following years ($\\checkmark$ LO). The purpose is limited as it seeks to criticize the internment camps by emphasizing the injustice of a man risking his life for a country that was simultaneously depriving his family of freedom ($\\checkmark$ LP). Moreover, as it is the image of only 2 individuals in one moment in time, the content we learn is limited. For example, it does not provide context regarding the reasons behind the establishment of internment camps ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0595","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0595\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the reasons for Roosevelt's Executive Order confining people of Japanese descent to internment camps.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Paul Sparrow, Director of the FDR Library, in an introduction to the exhibition *Images of Internment* held at the Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum (2017).\n\n[Source: Sparrow, P. M. (2017, February 17). *Images of Internment*. Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2025  from https://fdr.blogs.archives.gov/2017/02/17/images-of-internment/.  Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An explanation of Executive Order 9066 published by the National Archives (2022).\n\n[Source: Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (2022, January 24). *Milestone Documents. National Archives*. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift //archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ contrast)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive, and no set answer is required.\n\nFor comparison, any **one to four** of the following similarities: \n\n - They both identify racism as a cause of why people of Japanese descent were targeted\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$entrenched and widespread racism of that time” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans.” \n\n - Both refer to the attack on Pearl Harbor as the catalyst of the executive order\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The 'dastardly' surprise attack on Pearl Harbor, and Japan's brutal military expansion throughout Asia, enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time.”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the Empire of Japan attacked the United States at the Pearl Harbor Naval Base in Hawaii. The attack launched a rash of fear about national security”\n\n - Both refer to the threat to and fear for national security  \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The attack launched a rash of fear about national security”\n\n - Both identify the West Coast as the place where demands for internment were especially coming from\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The President was under tremendous pressure from military commanders, members of congress and political and business leaders in California, Oregon and Washington”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fear about national security, especially on the West Coast.”\n\nFor contrast, any one to two of the following differences:\n\n - The sources differ on who was calling for internment. \n\n     - Source B speaks of military leaders, Congress, newspapers, and the military: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The President was under tremendous pressure from military commanders, members of congress … Leading newspaper columnists demanded action.” \n\n     - Source C only mentions lobbyists from Western states who put pressure on politicians.$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lobbyists from western states, many representing competing economic interests or nativist groups, pressured Congress and the President”\n\n - They differ on the reasons for the internment. \n\n     - Source B mentions racism and military reasons for confinement: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack or even invade the West Coast …The 'dastardly' surprise attack on Pearl Harbor, and Japan's brutal military expansion throughout Asia, enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time.” \n\n     - Source C also adds economic competition as a factor: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$economic competition, distrust over cultural separateness, and long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans.”\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSources B and C give similar reasons for Roosevelt's Executive Order confining people of Japanese descent to internment camps. First, they both identify racism as a factor in the targeting of Japanese Americans ($\\checkmark$ similarity), with Source B referring to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$entrenched and widespread racism of that time” and Source C describing how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both also mention the attack on Pearl Harbor as the catalyst for Executive Order 9066 (✓ similarity), with Source B emphasizing how the attack $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time,” while Source C states that the attack $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$launched a rash of fear about national security” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Furthermore, both sources highlight the fear for national security as one of the reasons for the order ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack”  while Source C says that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the attack launched a rash of fear about national security” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nHowever, the sources differ on what groups were calling for the internment of people of Japanese descent ($\\checkmark$ contrast). Source B mentions pressure from military leaders, Congress and how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$leading newspaper columnists demanded action.” In contrast, Source C highlights how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lobbyists from western states, many representing competing economic interests or nativist groups, pressured Congress and the President” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). The role of lobbyists helps explain why Source C includes “economic competition, distrust over cultural separateness, and long-standing anti-Asian racism” as reasons for internment, while Source B stays focused on the military aspect, saying,  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack or even invade the West Coast” ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0596","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0596\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII.\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Paul Sparrow, Director of the FDR Library, in an introduction to the exhibition *Images of Internment* held at the Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum (2017).\n\n[Source: Sparrow, P. M. (2017, February 17). *Images of Internment*. Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2025  from https://fdr.blogs.archives.gov/2017/02/17/images-of-internment/.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source contains hindsight as it was made in 2017 so the author is able to analyze events detached from the passions of WWII. He is also the Director of the FDR Library so he is likely an expert on FDR's actions.  \n\n - The text seeks to give a short explanation of the internment of Japanese Americans since it is the introduction of an exhibit on the topic that was organized by the Library and Museum (purpose). \n\n - From the source we can learn the reasons why Roosevelt gave the executive order as well as some information on those who supported and those who opposed the measure (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source lacks some insight as it was made by someone who did not live the events and was not privy to the feelings at the time. Moreover, since the author is the Director of the FDR Library and Museum, he is biased in favor of Roosevelt (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to give only a brief introduction so the details given are few. It also wants to portray FDR in a positive light stressing that it was not really his fault that the executive order was given (purpose).\n\n - The source's nature as a brief introduction means it has a limited amount of information about the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Furthermore, it contains some exaggerations regarding Roosevelt calling him $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of our greatest champions of human rights” (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B is valuable for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Since it was created in 2017, the origin contains hindsight and is thus able to analyze events without the emotional intensity of World War II. Additionally, the author is the Director of the FDR Library, so he should be an expert on Roosevelt's actions ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is valuable since it provides a brief explanation of the internment of Japanese Americans, as it is part of an exhibit organized by the Library and Museum ($\\checkmark$ VP). The content allows us to learn some of the reasons Roosevelt issued Executive Order 9066, as well as information on those who supported and opposed the measure ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. The origin is limited since the author did not experience the events firsthand, it lacks insight into the emotions of the time. Furthermore, as he is the Director of the FDR Library, the author has a bias in favor of Roosevelt ($\\checkmark$ LO). This is apparent in the content of the source where the author includes some exaggerations such as calling Roosevelt $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of our greatest champions of human rights” ($\\checkmark$ LC).The purpose is also limited since the brevity required for an introduction to an exhibition necessarily means that a lot of information must be left out. We can also see from the text that the Director of the FDR Library understandably seeks to portray Roosevelt in a positive light, downplaying his responsibility for the executive order ($\\checkmark$ LP)."},{"ref":"HI0609","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0609\n\nDescribe the German policy of Weltpolitik and identify **two** of its goals.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following or similar description of Weltpolitik:\n\n - Weltpolitik was Kaiser Wilhelm II's goal to expand Germany's influence in the world. The policy reflected Wilhelm's belief that Germany should be considered a world power based on their military and industrial strength.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Growth of the German colonial empire in Africa and Asia\n\n - Establishing economic spheres of influence in Africa and Asia\n\n - Expansion of the German navy\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWeltpolitik was a German foreign policy that was established in the 1890s. It was based on the belief that Germany should be a world power and needed to expand its influence ($\\checkmark$). Kaiser Wilhelm II believed that in order to be a world power, the German's needed to expand their colonial holdings in Africa and Asia ($\\checkmark$). Also, Germany made it a goal to expand their navy in order to better support their army and their international territories ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0610","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0610\n\nDescribe the Congress of Berlin and give **two** examples of how it shifted the balance of power in Europe.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following or similar description of the Congress of Berlin:\n\n- The Congress of Berlin was a meeting of the most powerful nations in Europe to discuss the balance of power in Europe following the  Russo-Turkish War. The meeting happened in 1878 and was convened by German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The Treaty of Berlin was signed at the end of the conference and limited the amount of control Russia would have in the Balkans. This limited Russian access to the Black Sea and slowed any naval aspirations they may have had, shifting power away from Russia.\n\n - Nations such as Romania, Serbia and Montenegro were granted independence.\n\n - Bosnia and Herzegovina were occupied by Austria-Hungary, making Austria-Hungary more powerful and limiting Russian influence in the region.\n\n - The Ottoman Empire maintained its status as a sovereign empire which limited European expansion in the region.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Congress of Berlin was a meeting of the European powers in the late 19th century. The purpose of the meeting was to reassess the treaty that was made at the end of the Russo-Turkish War to maintain the balance of power in Europe ($\\checkmark$). The Treaty of Berlin was signed at the end of the conference. Though Russia defeated the Ottoman Empire in the war, the conference limited Russia's access to the Balkan region. Specifically, Russia could not use the Black Sea to expand its military ($\\checkmark$). In addition, several nations in the Balkans were granted their independence, but Austria-Hungary occupied Bosnia and Herzegovina ($\\checkmark$). The treaty successfully limited the most powerful nations in Europe from gaining too much power at the expense of the Balkans and the Ottoman Empire."},{"ref":"HI0612","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0612\n\nSummarise the events of the July Crisis of 1914.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Germany offered Austria-Hungary its full support in the event of a war with Serbia (the “blank check”) (1-6 July).\n\n - Austria-Hungary sent Serbia an ultimatum, which included the provision that Austria-Hungary would be in charge of the investigation into the assassination (22 July).\n\n - Serbia agreed to most of Austria-Hungary's terms but did not want them in charge of the investigation (23 July).\n\n - Austria-Hungary mobilized for war after Serbia rejected the ultimatum (24-25 July).\n\n - Russia offered to support Serbia after Austria-Hungary mobilized (24-25 July).\n\n - Great Britain attempted to broker peace, but Germany and Austria-Hungary rejected the appeals (28 July).\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFollowing the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, Europe faced a major diplomatic crisis that is referred to as the July Crisis. During this time, war could still have been avoided. However, diplomacy failed. Following the assassination, Germany agreed to support Austria-Hungary if they went to war with Serbia fully ($\\checkmark$). This emboldened Austria-Hungary to send Serbia an ultimatum that they would likely reject as it would allow Austria-Hungary power in the assassination investigation ($\\checkmark$). Austria-Hungary mobilized for war with Serbia after its ultimatum was rejected ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0614","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0614\n\nAccording to Source A, what were the conditions of the alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany?    [3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |An excerpt of articles from the Dual Alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany,1879. |\n\n[Source: *The Dual Alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany (1879, October 7)*. The Avalon Project. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/dualalli.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- Germany and Austria-Hungary would fight for each other if either was attacked by Russia.\n\n- Germany would not support another country that attacked Austria-Hungary, nor would Austria-Hungary support another country that attacked Germany, remaining at least neutral.\n\n- If a country supported by Russia attacked Germany and Austria-Hungary, then Germany and Austria-Hungary would be obligated to support each other.\n\n- They could reassess the terms of their alliance every five years and that if no further negotiations are requested in the fifth year, the treaty will automatically renew for three more years.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Dual Alliance between Germany and Austria-Hungary stated that Germany and Austria-Hungary would go to war for each other if either of them were attacked by Russia ($\\checkmark$). They also agreed that they would remain neutral in the event that either of them were attacked by another European power ($\\checkmark$). However, if Russia was supporting the country that attacked them, then Germany and Austria-Hungary would become involved to support the other ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0615","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0615\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the troubles in the Balkans?   [2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon titled “The Boiling Point” published in the British magazine *Punch* in 1912 which shows the great powers of Europe, Germany, Great Britain, Austria-Hungary, Russia, and France, sitting on a boiling pot labeled “Balkan Troubles”.|\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/hi0614.png\" alt=\"hi0614\">]\n\n[Source: Raven-Hill, L. (1912). *The Boiling Point*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 11, 2025  from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balkan_troubles1.jpg  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- The boiling pot symbolizes that troubles in the Balkans may be ready to “boil over” or explode.\n\n- The men sitting on the top of the pot indicate that they are trying to stop the pot from boiling over or to control trouble in the area.\n\n- The worried faces on the men show that they are not sure how much longer they can control the situation.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe pot is labeled Balkan Troubles. It is shown to be close to boiling over which indicates that the problems in the Balkans may be getting worse. ($\\checkmark$) The men sitting on the lid represent some of the powerful nations in Europe, such as Germany and Great Britain. By sitting on the lid, the cartoon indicates that they are trying to stop the pot from boiling over, or keep control of the problems in the Balkans. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0616","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0616\n\nAccording to Source C, how did the Balkan wars influence militarism in Europe?  [3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a 1914 journal article titled “The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliance and the Future of the Ottoman Empire” by Norman Dwight Harris, a professor of European History at Northwestern University. |\n\n[Source: Harris, N. D. (1914). *The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliances and the Future of the Ottoman Empire.* The American Political Science Review, 8(1), 105-116. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from http://www.jstor.org/stable/4617011. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- Austria-Hungary added 31,000 people to their military in five years.\n\n- Austria-Hungary and Germany added over $67 million to their military budget.\n\n- Germany recruited more than 38,000 people to the military and promised to add 136,000 more.\n\n- France and Russia vowed to increase their military by similar amounts.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Document C, the Balkan Wars inspired Austria- Hungary to increase their military by over 30,000 people ($\\checkmark$). Germany also added more than 38,000 soldiers to their ranks with the promise of adding thousands more ($\\checkmark$). France and Russia planned to add to their ranks in order to keep up with Austria-Hungary and Germany ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0617","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0617\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying the impact of the Balkan Wars on the European powers. [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a 1914 journal article titled “The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliance and the Future of the Ottoman Empire” by Norman Dwight Harris, a professor of European History at Northwestern University. |\n\n[Source: Harris, N. D. (1914). *The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliances and the Future of the Ottoman Empire.* The American Political Science Review, 8(1), 105-116. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from http://www.jstor.org/stable/4617011. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor **value**, any of the following:\n\n- The source is secondary as it was written by an American professor who was removed from the wars and was written within a few years of the wars.\n\n- The source connects the growing militarism in Europe to the wars in the Balkans, specifically connecting it to the need for a strong Austro-Hungarian military to respond to Russian and Pro-Slavic militaries near their Southern borders.\n\n- The purpose of the source is to highlight how European powers responded to the wars in the Balkans. \n\nFor **limitations**, any of the following:\n\n- While the source is secondary, it was written before World War I broke out and cannot analyze how these events led to the war.\n\n- The source briefly mentions the need for the Ottoman Empire but does not go into detail on how the Balkan Wars strengthened or weakened the empire.\n\n- The source refers to a “status quo” in the Mediterranean but does not go into detail about what that means.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C is valuable to a historian as a secondary source that informs the reader about how the European powers were affected by the Balkan Wars ($\\checkmark$ VO). However, the source was written in 1914 and is limited in how deeply the effects were analyzed as they could not consider World War I nor its aftermath ($\\checkmark$ LO). The source is valuable for making connections between the problems in the Balkans and the growing militarism in Europe ($\\checkmark$ VP) but is limited in its treatment of the Ottoman Empire. The document mentions that the Ottoman Empire was struggling but does not detail how the Balkan Wars affected the stability of the empire ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0618","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0618\n\nUsing evidence from Sources A and C, describe the growing tensions between Germany and Russia between 1871 and 1914.    [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |An excerpt of articles from the Dual Alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany,1879. |\n\n[Source: *The Dual Alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany (1879, October 7)*. The Avalon Project. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/dualalli.asp. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a 1914 journal article titled “The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliance and the Future of the Ottoman Empire” by Norman Dwight Harris, a professor of European History at Northwestern University. |\n\n[Source: Harris, N. D. (1914). *The Effect of the Balkan Wars on European Alliances and the Future of the Ottoman Empire.* The American Political Science Review, 8(1), 105-116. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from http://www.jstor.org/stable/4617011. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with at least **one** reference to each document:\n\n- Russia's victory against the Ottoman Empire in 1878 inspired the Dual Alliance between Austria-Hungary and Germany which states that they would come to each other's aid if Russia attacked either of them.\n\n    - Source A: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Should, contrary to their hope, and against the loyal desire of the two High Contracting Parties, one of the two Empires be attacked by Russia the High Contracting Parties are bound to come to the assistance one of the other with the whole war strength of their Empires, and accordingly only to conclude peace together and upon mutual agreement.”\n\n- Germany and Austria-Hungary were so concerned about the possibility of war with Russia that they included a clause in their Dual Alliance that if Russia were to support any nation that might invade or attack them, then they would need to come to each other's aid as well.\n\n    - Source A: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Should, however, the attacking party in such a case be supported by Russia, either by an active cooperation or by military measures which constitute a menace to the Party attacked, then the obligation stipulated in Article 1 of this Treaty, for reciprocal assistance with the whole fighting force, becomes equally operative, and the conduct of the war by the two High Contracting Parties shall in this case also be in common until the conclusion of a common peace.”\n\n- Germany and Austria Hungary invested millions of dollars and added troops to their militaries and Russia responded by doing the same.\n\n    - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\\$67,290,000.00, in addition to the regular funds, will be expended by Austria-Hungary on her military service, and her army increased by 31,000 within five years. It has been already raised by 58,000 officers and men during the past two years, within which period Germany has added over 38,000 to her military forces with the official proposal to recruit 136,000 more officers and men inside of the next few years. Russia and France are planning to equalize these additions by similar increases in their own armies.”\n\n- Germany and Austria-Hungary's policies were inspired by the presence of Slavic regiments that were supported by Russia.\n\n    - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Among the considerations which moved the statesmen of Berlin and Vienna to adopt this new military policy, none was more potent than the fact that the presence of a Slavic alliance in the Balkans sustained by Russia will hereafter neutralize a large portion of the Austrian army in the event of the outbreak of hostilities between the great powers of Europe, for a number of regiments will have perforce to be permanently stationed near the southern boundary of the Austrian Empire.”\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A implies growing tensions between Germany and Russia, saying that if $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of the two Empires be attacked by Russia\" or if the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$attacking party\" was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$supported by Russia\", both Germany and Austria-Hungary would be required to come to each other's aid ($\\checkmark$). This alliance for mutual defense suggests growing tensions in that both empires felt that an attack by Russia was highly likely, if not imminent ($\\checkmark$). \n\nSource C states that much of Germany and Austria-Hungary's military growth was inspired by growing presence of Russia in the Balkans and specifically the fear that these Slavic regiments would be able to quickly mobilize along the Austro-Hungarian border ($\\checkmark$). During this period, Germany and Russia also saw significant growth in their military. Russia had been behind the other European powers for a long time, but following their victory in the Ottoman Empire and their increased influence in the Balkans, they began to increase the numbers of their standing army. However, after the Balkan Wars in 1912, Germany and Austria-Hungary added troops and money to their military budget. According to Source C, Russia and France would $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equalize these additions by similar increases in their own armies” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0635","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0635\n\nEvaluate the claim that “Chinese Communist Party (CCP) members were the true defenders of China during the Sino-Japanese War” (1937-45).\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nSince the command term is “evaluate,” the response must contain both agreement and disagreement to reach more than three marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion.\n\nThe following ideas may be present in support of the claim:\n- The CCP's guerrilla tactics disrupted Japanese operations, stretched their forces thin, and helped them resist complete occupation, which significantly contributed to China's defenses.\n- Land reform and promises to peasants won the CCP's widespread rural support, boosting recruitment, intelligence, and resources for resisting Japan.\n- The CCP joined the KMT in the Second United Front, where they set aside ideological conflict to coordinate effective resistance against Japan.\n- CCP control in rural areas allowed for agricultural stability, militia organization, and sustained resistance in Japanese-occupied zones.\n\nThe following ideas may be present to reject the claim:\nThe KMT defended China's key cities and industrial centres, preserving vital infrastructure despite heavy losses.\n- The KMT secured major U.S. support through diplomacy, gaining aid and international recognition as a key Allied power, securing China's role as one of the \"Big Four\" Allied powers.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- The strongest answers are likely to argue that both the CCP and KMT fulfilled crucial yet distinct roles in China's defense.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe claim that the CCP were the true defenders of China during the Sino-Japanese War has merit. First, their effective guerrilla tactics disrupted Japanese operations in rural areas, tying down large enemy forces ($\\checkmark$). Second, the CCP's land reform policies gained peasant support, allowing them to recruit fighters and gather crucial intelligence ($\\checkmark$). Third, their willingness to cooperate with the KMT through the Second United Front showed a commitment to defending China over party rivalry ($\\checkmark$). However, the KMT also played a critical role in China's defense by securing vital international aid through diplomatic channels, gaining U.S. military support, and boosting China's global standing ($\\checkmark$). Overall, while the CCP made significant contributions to resistance efforts, both parties played essential but different roles in defending China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0636","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0636\n\nDescribe the Xi'an Incident of December 1936 and explain its importance in the outcome of the Chinese Civil War.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor description, the following or similar:\n- The Xi'an Incident of December 1936 was a pivotal moment in the Chinese Civil War. Prior to the event, Chiang Kai-shek and the Kuomintang (KMT) were more focused on eradicating the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) than resisting Japanese aggression. However, frustration within the KMT, in particular General Zhang Xueliang, led to Chiang's dramatic kidnapping while he was in Xi'an. This forced Chiang to negotiate with the CCP to change his priorities. The result was the formation of the Second United Front: a temporary alliance between the KMT and CCP aimed at resisting Japanese invasion.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor importance, any **two** of the following:\n- The ending (or at least limiting) of KMT-CCP hostilities (for a time) and formation of a united front against the Japanese took the pressure off CCP at Yan'an. This allowed the CCP to expand its influence, gaining credibility by allowing them to be recognized as a partner in the fight against the Japanese. The temporary ceasefire between the KMT and CCP allowed the communists to rebuild and strengthen their position.\n- Mao's position within the CCP became more solidified as he was seen as a key player in pushing for the alliance with the KMT. With a stronger leadership position and growing popularity, Mao was able to strengthen the CCP's organizational capabilities and expand its base of support. Mao's vision of a peasant-based revolutionary struggle began to take hold, and this contributed to the CCP's eventual success in the civil war.\n- The KMT's internal divisions were exacerbated by the Xi'an Incident. Chiang's leadership was weakened, and although he remained in power, his ability to effectively lead the KMT against both the CCP and Japan was reduced. As a result, the KMT was less united and less effective in the years following the incident.\n- The CCP used the time during the Second United Front to further strengthen its position and prepare for the resumption of the civil war after the defeat of Japan. The strategic and political gains made by the CCP during the united front were crucial in its ability to eventually defeat the KMT and establish the People's Republic of China in 1949.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Xi'an Incident was a major turning point in 1936 when Chiang Kai-shek was kidnapped by his own generals because they were frustrated that he wasn't doing enough to stop the Japanese. Before this, the KMT was more focused on fighting the CCP than dealing with Japan. Instead of killing Chiang, the CCP used the situation to push for an alliance, the Second United Front, so both sides could fight Japan together. ($\\checkmark$) This event had significant consequences. The ceasefire between the two parties allowed the CCP to regroup and expand its base from Yan'an, gaining valuable time to build popular support through land reforms and resistance efforts. ($\\checkmark$) Additionally, the CCP gained political legitimacy by being recognized as a co-defender of the nation. ($\\checkmark$) Meanwhile, Chiang's forced compromise exposed internal divisions within the KMT and undermined his leadership authority. Although he remained in power, his ability to maintain a united front within his own party weakened, affecting the KMT's effectiveness in the later stages of the war. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0637","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0637\n\nDescribe **three** reasons the hostilities between the CCP and KMT recommenced in 1946.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Deep-rooted ideological conflict between the CCP's communism and the KMT's nationalism made long-term cooperation impossible.\n- With Japan defeated in 1945, the shared threat that had united the CCP and KMT during the Second United Front was gone, reigniting hostilites.\n- Both parties sought to control post-war China, each claiming to represent the true government, leading to competition for power.\n- US-mediated negotiations (e.g. Marshall Mission) failed due to mutual distrust and incompatible political goals.\n- Skirmishes broke out in contested regions like Manchuria, where both sides rushed to occupy former Japanese territory.\n- The U.S. supported the KMT with weapons and aid, while the Soviets allowed CCP access to Japanese arms in Manchuria, escalating tensions.\n- Agreements made during the war, including political cooperation and troop withdrawal, were frequently violated by both sides.\n- The KMT's declining popularity and internal issues made the CCP more confident in pursuing its goal of controlling China.\n- The CCP had grown in numbers and support during the war, giving greater cofidence them to challenge the KMT militarily.\n- After Japan's surrender there was a political vacuum in previously occupied rigions, the rush to control these areas led to direct military confrontations between CCP and KMT forces.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHostilities between the CCP and KMT recommenced in 1946 due to entrenched ideological differences, with the CCP promoting communism and the KMT defending Chinese nationalism, making long-term cooperation unlikely. Attempts at peaceful resolution, such as the US-led Marshall Mission, failed due to mutual distrust and ideologically incompatible political goals. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, the defeat of Japan in 1945 removed the common enemy that had temporarily united them under the Second United Front, reigniting their antagonism. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, the KMT's internal corruption, declining public support, and poor governance further weakened its position, giving Mao and the CCP greater confidence in pushing for control of China. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0649","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0649\n\nDescribe the First Moroccan Crisis (1905-1906) and explain how it increased tensions among the European powers.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following description or similar:\n- The First Moroccan Crisis occurred in 1905 after Kaiser Wilhelm II publicly recognized the sultan of Morocco as a sovereign leader in an attempt to lessen France's imperial influence.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **three** of the following to explain how it increased tensions:\n- Morocco was under the French sphere of influence in the early 1900s (later becoming a protectorate of France). By publicly recognizing the sultan as a sovereign leader, Germany was undermining French influence in the country.\n- When the Kaiser declared the sultan sovereign, he was directly challenging France's efforts to build an empire and the Entente Cordiale between Great Britain and France, forcing Great Britain to take a stand in support of France.\n- The German chancellor demanded an international conference to settle the dispute between France and Germany. When France said there was no need, the chancellor threatened war.\n- When the conference was held, only Austria-Hungary backed Germany. This greatly increased tensions between the alliances in Europe.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe First Moroccan Crisis was an attempt by Kaiser Wilhelm to weaken France's influence in Morocco ($\\checkmark$). Though Morocco was an independent state with a sultan leading it, France held a lot of power there. Kaiser Wilhelm was looking to weaken France while also testing Britain and France's new entente ($\\checkmark$). In 1905, Kaiser Wilhelm publicly recognized the sultan as the leader of Morocco. This undermined French influence in the country. When France criticized the move, the Kaiser demanded a conference to settle the dispute. France refused a conference, and Germany threatened war ($\\checkmark$). When the conference was finally held, Britain and all other European countries except Austria-Hungary backed France's claims ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0650","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0650\n\nDescribe the Entente Cordiale (1904) between Great Britain and France and explain its diplomatic significance.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following or similar:\n- The Entente Cordiale, or the Anglo-French Agreement, was a diplomatic understanding between Great Britain and France to settle their colonial differences and encourage future cooperation between the nations.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n- France and Great Britain had been rivals for centuries. This agreement marked a shift in European diplomatic relations.\n- This ended Great Britain's “splendid isolation” period which had limited its involvement in continental European affairs.\n- With France and Great Britain on the same side, Germany could not benefit from their hostility.\n- Paved the way for the Triple Entente as France already had an alliance with Russia.\n- Allowed France to more effectively resist Germany when there was conflict in colonial issues.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Entente Cordiale was an agreement between Great Britain and France to encourage cooperation between their nations and to end their tensions over colonial holdings ($\\checkmark$). This was a major shift in European diplomacy as the British and French had been rivals for centuries, fighting against each other in several major wars ($\\checkmark$). Also, agreeing to the entente ended Britain's self-imposed isolation from continental European affairs, which they had been practicing since the end of the Napoleonic Wars ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the easing of Anglo-French colonial tensions allowed France to focus its attention on its colonial conflicts with Germany, thus increasing tensions between the two major colonial powers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0782","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0782\n\nDefine imperialism and describe **three** ways that imperialism could strengthen an empire.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following or a similar description of imperialism:\n- Imperialism is the policy of expanding a nation's power and influence by taking over other nations through diplomacy or force.\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Empires looked for colonies that had access to natural resources that they were able to profit from.\n- Empires used their colonies to expand their markets and make more money.\n- Empires gained prestige for having control over large pieces of territory.\n- Empires were able to build military bases in their colonies making it easier for them to protect their holdings and mobilize for war.\n- Colonists were used for cheap labor or were recruited to the military.\n- Empires were able to spread their cultures and way of thinking to many people around the world.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nImperialism is the expansion of an empire by taking over other territories ($\\checkmark$). Imperialism can benefit industrial empires needing cheap raw materials ($\\checkmark$). Empires also use the people living in their colonies for cheap labor ($\\checkmark$). Colonies can also use empires to expand their military reach. They did this by building military and naval bases in their colonized areas ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0783","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0783\n\nDescribe how Germany's industrial growth after 1871 influenced its domestic and foreign policy.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Industrial growth led to the need for more resources and markets which led to Germany looking to increase their colonial holdings.\n- Germany used the influx of money from their industrial growth to fund their expanding military, specifically their navy. Germany was looking to compete with Great Britain’s superior navy.\n- At home, Germany saw the creation of workers’ unions and social welfare programs in response to rapid urbanization and difficult working conditions.\n- The middle class gained some political power while the Junkers saw a decrease in power.\n- Germany's increase in global power led to more conflict with other European powers, such as Great Britain, France, and Russia. As a result, Germany became involved in conflicts that offered a chance to undermine their enemies.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGermany grew as an industrial power after its unification and this led to significant changes in their domestic life and foreign policies. They saw the rise in power of unions and social programs to help the working class ($\\checkmark$). Also, Germany used their excess wealth to invest in their military in order to compete with the strength of the British Navy ($\\checkmark$). Germany also became more interested in expanding their empire in order to reach new markets and gain cheaper natural resources to further their industrial growth ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0784","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0784\n\nDescribe **three** ways military conflict weakened the Ottoman Empire. [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Conflicts with Russia and the Serbians led to a significant loss of territory in Europe and around the Black Sea. This led to a loss of trade routes and a decline in prestige, especially among the Europeans.\n- Military losses contributed to frustrations on the homefront and internal conflict. This led to further distrust of the central government.\n- Nationalist uprisings led to the loss of Greece, Serbia, and Bulgaria.\n- Europeans purposely involved themselves in conflicts that involved the Ottoman Empire in order to weaken them and spread their influence in the region.\n- Mistreatment of minorities led to uprisings and a distrust of authority.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Ottoman Empire engaged in conflicts with people within and outside of their empire that would significantly weaken their power and influence. Nationalist uprisings within the empire led to the loss of Greece, Serbia, and Bulgaria and weakened their influence within Europe ($\\checkmark$). Also, the Ottoman government often mistreated minorities living in the empire which led to a distrust of authority ($\\checkmark$). Military losses against Russia decreased their access to the Black Sea and important trade routes, hurting their wealth and military reach ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0785","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0785\n\nDescribe **three** reasons for the 1884 Berlin Conference.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Berlin Conference was held in order for the Europeans to avoid conflict over areas in Africa that multiple countries tried to claim.\n- The Berlin Conference was held in order to maintain the balance of power in Europe and ensure that no one country became significantly more powerful than the others.\n- The most powerful nations wanted to ensure that the country who gained a territory was able to control it.\n- European nations wanted to clarify rules about free trade in Africa,\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Berlin Conference of 1884 was held in order to avoid conflict between European nations over land in Africa ($\\checkmark$). Most European countries, and specifically Germany, wanted to make sure that Great Britain and France did not gain more power and influence in Africa, fearing a shift in the balance of power ($\\checkmark$). The Europeans also wanted to clarify rules about free trade throughout Africa in order to ensure that no one nation controlled all of the waterways ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0786","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0786\n\nIdentify **two** effects of the 1884 Berlin Conference.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Africa was divided up by the European powers who did not consider previously standing ethnic boundaries.\n- Many African kingdoms and nations ceased to exist without the input of the people who lived there.\n- The boundaries that were established put together people of different ethnicities and cultures. This led to conflict and war in some areas.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Berlin Conference had a profound effect on Africa and its people. The Europeans created boundaries that had not previously existed. This forced people who had different languages and cultures into one area, causing conflict ($\\checkmark$). Also, many African kingdoms ceased to exist without the input of the people ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0787","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0787\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about Belgium's relationship with the Congo Free State?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon originally published in 1906 in the British magazine Punch. The cartoon shows a Congolese worker tangled with a snake that has the face of King Leopold II of Belgium. The first caption reads $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In the rubber coils” and the second, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$SCENE - The Congo $`$Free' State.”\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0787_A.png\" alt=\"HI0787_A\">\n\n[Source: Sambourne\\, E. L. (1906). *In the Rubber Coils*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved on May 15, 2025 from  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punch_congo_rubber_cartoon.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - By putting free in quotation marks, the artist is implying that the Congo is not actually free under Belgian rule.\n\n - The crown and face on the head of the snake suggests that King Leopold of Belgium II is the main person harming the people of the Congo.\n\n - The snake is wrapped around a person and the person is struggling to keep the snake from harming him. This suggests that Belgium is not considering the wellness of the people of the Congo.\n\n - People are watching and running away in horror which implies that they see the bad things happening but couldn't do anything to stop it.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A suggests that Belgium is abusive to the people who live in their colonies. This is implied by the snake being wrapped around the Congolese figure ($\\checkmark$). People are looking on in horror and that implies that there was not much they could do to defend themselves against the Belgians ($\\checkmark$). Also, the title of the cartoon has the word free in quotation marks and this gives the impression that the artist does not believe the people of the Congo are free at all ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0788","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0788\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about how Europeans viewed imperialism in China?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$In China - the cake of kings and emperors.” The caricatures in the cartoon show a Chinese man standing in the background with representatives around the table from Great Britain, Germany, Russia, France, and Japan.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI0787_B.png\" alt=\"HI0787_B\">\n\n[Source: Meyer\\, H. (1898). *In China - The Cake of Kings and Emperors.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved on May 15, 2025 from  from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China,_the_cake_of_kings_and_emperors,_Le_Petit_Journal_1898.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - China is a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$treat” to be divided among the powerful countries sitting at the table, represented by the slicing of a cake.\n\n - China is helpless against the others, represented by the Chinese character standing in the background, looking panicked or helpless.\n\n - Britain may be worried about Germany infringing on its colonial holdings, represented by Queen Victoria protectively holding her piece while staring at Germany.\n\n - Germany is looking to gain control over territory in China and is worried about Great Britain's reaction, represented by Kaiser Wilhelm II cutting himself a slice while staring at Great Britain. \n\n - France, Russia, and Japan are interested in gaining more influence in China, but also that they may be more passive than Germany and Great Britain, as they are simply looking on rather than taking action\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B suggests that the Europeans see China as an opportunity for them to grow their empires. This is implied by the presence of France, Russia, Germany and Great Britain at the table ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also suggests that Germany and Great Britain were fighting over territory ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also suggests that China was helpless in stopping these countries from carving them up ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0789","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0789\n\nUsing evidence from Source C, explain Rudyard Kipling's justification for imperialism.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the poem $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The White Man's Burden,” written by Rudyard Kipling in 1899.\n\n:box[\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nSend forth the best ye breed--\\\n\nGo bind your sons to exile\\\n\nTo serve your captives' need;\\\n\nTo wait in heavy harness,\\\n\nOn fluttered folk and wild--\\\n\nYour new-caught, sullen peoples,\\\n\nHalf-devil and half-child.\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nIn patience to abide,\\\n\nTo veil the threat of terror\\\n\nAnd check the show of pride;\\\n\nBy open speech and simple,\\\n\nAn hundred times made plain\\\n\nTo seek another's profit,\\\n\nAnd work another's gain.\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nThe savage wars of peace--\\\n\nFill full the mouth of Famine\\\n\nAnd bid the sickness cease;\\\n\nAnd when your goal is nearest\\\n\nThe end for others sought,\\\n\nWatch sloth and heathen Folly\\\n\nBring all your hopes to nought.\\\n\n\\\n\n[…]\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nAnd reap his old reward:\\\n\nThe blame of those ye better,\\\n\nThe hate of those ye guard--\\\n\nThe cry of hosts ye humour\\\n\n(Ah, slowly!) toward the light:--\\\n\n\"Why brought he us from bondage,\\\n\nOur loved Egyptian night?\"\n\n]\n\n[Source: Kipling\\, R. (1899). *The White Man's Burden*. Retrieved May 15, 2025 from  from https://kiplingsociety.co.uk/poem/poems_burden.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n - It is the responsibility of the white man, or Europeans, to go to far away lands in order to help civilize people that they view as savage, wild or child-like.\n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Take up the White Man's burden--\\\n\n     Send forth the best ye breed--\\\n\n     Go bind your sons to exile\\\n\n     To serve your captives' need;\\\n\n     To wait in heavy harness,\\\n\n     On fluttered folk and wild--\\\n\n     Your new-caught, sullen peoples,\\\n\n     Half-devil and half-child\n\n - Going to these nations is not for the benefit of the Europeans but for the benefit of the people in these countries.\n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Take up the White Man's burden--\\\n\n     In patience to abide,\\\n\n     To veil the threat of terror\\\n\n     And check the show of pride;\\\n\n     By open speech and simple,\\\n\n     An hundred times made plain\\\n\n     To seek another's profit,\\\n\n     And work another's gain.”\n\n - Europeans should conquer these lands in order to make sure that the people are fed and healthy.\n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Take up the White Man's burden--\\\n\n     The savage wars of peace--\\\n\n     Fill full the mouth of Famine\\\n\n     And bid the sickness cease;”\n\n - Europeans will likely see their work squandered by the lazy people they are trying to help but it is the right thing to do.\n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$And when your goal is nearest\\\n\n     The end for others sought,\\\n\n     Watch sloth and heathen Folly\\\n\n     Bring all your hopes to nought.”\n\n - There is no real reward for saving the colonists as they will still yearn for their past, even though their lives are better.\n\n     - Evidence: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Take up the White Man's burden--\\\n\n     And reap his old reward:\\\n\n     The blame of those ye better,\\\n\n     The hate of those ye guard.”\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C promotes the idea that imperialism is justified because of the cultural supremacy of the Europeans, or white people ($\\checkmark$), describing the people of other places as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$half-devil and half-child” to imply European superiority ($\\checkmark$). He also frames imperialism as a sacrifice, saying that there is no benefit for the colonizers, while the places being colonized will be fed and cured of diseases ($\\checkmark$). He also claims that their responsibility is thankless, as the colonists are too lazy to take advantage of all that they have to offer ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0790","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0790\n\nContrast the views of imperialism shown in Sources A and C.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon originally published in 1906 in the British magazine Punch. The cartoon shows a Congolese worker tangled with a snake that has the face of King Leopold II of Belgium. The first caption reads $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In the rubber coils” and the second, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$SCENE - The Congo $`$Free' State.”\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/790.JPEG\" alt=\"790\">\n\n[Source: Sambourne\\, E. L. (1906). In the Rubber Coils. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved on May 15, 2025 from  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punch_congo_rubber_cartoon.jpg. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the poem $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$*The White Man's Burden,”* written by Rudyard Kipling in 1899.\n\n:box[\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nSend forth the best ye breed--\\\n\nGo bind your sons to exile\\\n\nTo serve your captives' need;\\\n\nTo wait in heavy harness,\\\n\nOn fluttered folk and wild--\\\n\nYour new-caught, sullen peoples,\\\n\nHalf-devil and half-child.\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nIn patience to abide,\\\n\nTo veil the threat of terror\\\n\nAnd check the show of pride;\\\n\nBy open speech and simple,\\\n\nAn hundred times made plain\\\n\nTo seek another's profit,\\\n\nAnd work another's gain.\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nThe savage wars of peace--\\\n\nFill full the mouth of Famine\\\n\nAnd bid the sickness cease;\\\n\nAnd when your goal is nearest\\\n\nThe end for others sought,\\\n\nWatch sloth and heathen Folly\\\n\nBring all your hopes to nought.\\\n\n\\\n\n[…]\\\n\n\\\n\nTake up the White Man's burden--\\\n\nAnd reap his old reward:\\\n\nThe blame of those ye better,\\\n\nThe hate of those ye guard--\\\n\nThe cry of hosts ye humour\\\n\n(Ah, slowly!) toward the light:--\\\n\n\"Why brought he us from bondage,\\\n\nOur loved Egyptian night?\"\n\n\\\n\n]\n\n[Source: Kipling\\, R. (1899). The White Man's Burden. Retrieved May 15, 2025 from  from https://kiplingsociety.co.uk/poem/poems_burden.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Source C implies that the colonists would benefit from being colonized while Source A shows that the people are mistreated.\n\n     - Source A shows a Congolese worker struggling against the coils of Belgium. \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Go bind your sons to exile To serve your captives' need”\n\n - Source A implies that Belgium is using the Congo in order to benefit from their natural resources, specifically rubber. Source C implies that the white man would not benefit at all.\n\n     - Source A refers to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rubber coils” implying the importance of that resource to Belgium\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$To seek another's profit, And work another's gain.”\n\n - Source A shows that the colonists feared the Belgians, specifically King Leopold II, because of their mistreatment. Source C says that the colonists would be resentful because the Europeans were their betters.\n\n     - Source A shows colonists running away in fear\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The blame of those ye better, The hate of those ye guard-”\n\n - Source A does not show any benefits to colonization while Source C says that the colonizers would help stop famine.\n\n     - Source A only focuses on the negative aspects of imperialism in the Congo.\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Fill full the mouth of Famine And bid the sickness cease.”\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C portrays a selfless version of imperialism where Western nations are colonizing countries for their own good while Source A shows that nations were using their colonies for resources ($\\checkmark$). Also Source A implies that colonists are being mistreated, as evidenced by the snake coiling around the worker, while Source C only focuses on the benefits of imperialism ($\\checkmark$). Source C focuses on the fact that the colonists were likely to resent westerners for trying to modernize them. This is shown when Kipling writes that white men will get the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hate of those they guard.” However,  Source A shows that the colonists feared the westerners who would mistreat them, illustrated by the fearful looks of the workers as they ran from the snake ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0791","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0791\n\nUsing evidence from Sources A and B, describe **two** negative effects of imperialism in Asia and Africa.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon originally published in 1906 in the British magazine Punch. The cartoon shows a Congolese worker tangled with a snake that has the face of King Leopold II of Belgium. The first caption reads $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In the rubber coils” and the second, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$SCENE - The Congo $`$Free' State.”\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/791imageA.JPEG\" alt=\"791imageA\">\n\n[Source: Sambourne\\, E. L. (1906). In the Rubber Coils. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved on May 15, 2025 from  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punch_congo_rubber_cartoon.jpg. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$In China - the cake of kings and emperors.” The caricatures in the cartoon show a Chinese man standing in the background with representatives around the table from Great Britain, Germany, Russia, France, and Japan.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/791imageB.JPEG\" alt=\"791imageB\">\n\n[Source: Meyer\\, H. (1898). *In China - The Cake of Kings and Emperors.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved on May 15, 2025 from  from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China,_the_cake_of_kings_and_emperors,_Le_Petit_Journal_1898.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence from the sources:\n\n - Source A shows that the colonists in the Congo were being mistreated by Belgium. This is an example of how the colonizing countries would sometimes exploit the colonists.\n\n     - Evidence: The colonists look frightened with two running away in fear and one wrapped up in the snake's coils.\n\n - Source A refers to the importance of rubber. This implies that Belgium was using the Congo for their resources.\n\n     - Evidence: The title mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rubber coils” and the coils of a snake are wrapped around the Congolese worker.\n\n - Source B implies that China was not welcome at the table which is an example of how countries in Asia and Africa were left out of decision making. \n\n     - Evidence: The Chinese man is standing in the background with his hands in the air.\n\n - Source B suggests that there is tension between the nations sitting at the table which is consistent with the idea that imperialism was a cause of conflict.\n\n     - Evidence: The Queen and the Kaiser are glaring at each other and seem to be fighting over territory.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A implies that imperialism could lead to mistreatment of colonists in order to exploit them ($\\checkmark$). This is represented by the colonists showing expressions of fear and running from the snake ($\\checkmark$). Source B suggests that the countries who were colonizing land in Asia would fight over these territories ($\\checkmark$). This can be clearly seen when you look at the cartoons representing Germany and England. They are looking at each other and seem to be fighting over the same piece of cake ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0830","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0830\n\nAccording to Source C, what was the ultimate goal of U.S. policy in Afghanistan?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan” given by US President Ronald Reagan, 1985, and declassified in 2008.|\n\n[Source: Reagan, R. (1985, March 27). *National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan.* Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. Retrieved June 14, 2025 from https://www.reaganlibrary.gov/public/archives/reference/scanned-nsdds/nsdd166.pdf Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Removal of Soviet forces: The ultimate goal was “the removal of Soviet force from Afghanistan.”\n- Restoration of Afghan independence: The U.S. aimed to restore Afghanistan's “independent status,” free from Soviet control or influence.\n- Undermine the Soviet long-term strategy: The U.S. aimed to demonstrate that the Soviet Union's long-term strategy of subjugating Afghanistan would ultimately fail.\n- Create pressure for Soviet policy change: By worsening the situation for Soviet forces, the U.S. aimed to “build pressure on the Soviet Union to adjust its policies” in a manner favourable to U.S. interests and the Afghan people.\n- Prevent Soviet consolidation of power in the region: The U.S. aimed to stop the Soviets from using Afghanistan as a “secure base” to project power into the surrounding region, especially Iran and Pakistan.\n- Support an Indigenous resistance movement: Preventing the defeat of Afghan resistance fighters was key to U.S. goals to avoid signalling that Soviet military intervention could succeed.\n- Promote Soviet political isolation: A goal was to “promote Soviet isolation in the Third and Islamic worlds” by ensuring continued condemnation of Soviet actions in Afghanistan.\n- Deny strategic regional advantages to the USSR: The U.S. sought to prevent the Soviet Union from using Afghanistan as a launching point for influence over post-Khomeini Iran or as a means of destabilising Pakistan.\n- Demonstrate U.S. resolve in Cold War conflicts: Implicitly, this goal also served to show American commitment to containing Soviet expansion and supporting anti-communist movements globally.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, according to the source, the ultimate goal of U.S. policy in Afghanistan was “the removal of Soviet force from Afghanistan” ($\\checkmark$). Second was the restoration of the country's “independent status”($\\checkmark$). Finally, the United States sought to undermine the Soviet Union's broader ambitions by demonstrating that its long-term strategy for conquering Afghanistan would not succeed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0915","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0915\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kim Il Sung. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.\" Speech before the Officers and Men of Unit No.256 of the Korean People's Army. October 23, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Kim, I. S. (1953, October 23). *On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.* Kim Il Sung Works. marxists.org. Retrieved June 25, 2025 from  https://www.marxists.org/archive/kim-il-sung/cw/08.pdf\nPublic Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Kim calls the war a “great victory,” claiming North Korea forced the U.S. to sign the armistice, redefining success as resistance rather than reunification.\n- The army “repulsed the enemy's invasion.” Kim labels the U.S. the “ringleader of world imperialism,” and says the war “shattered the myth of the might of U.S. imperialism.”\n- Kim says the war “tempered” the people and state, presenting it as a unifying struggle that built resilience and loyalty under the Workers' Party.\n- The speech claims a “great contribution to global peace and security,” asserting that North Korea's struggle boosted its international reputation and moral standing.\n- The mention of “tasks” suggests the fight isn't over. The tone promotes readiness, discipline, and long-term ideological commitment by the army and people.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of Source A is that North Korea emerged from the Korean War with honour and strength. Although Kim Il-Sung acknowledges that reunification was not achieved, he describes the war as a “great victory” because North Korea resisted the combined forces of the United States and its allies and forced them to agree to an armistice ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Kim frames the conflict as part of a larger anti-imperialist struggle. He claims the Korean People's Army “repulsed the enemy's invasion” and portrays the United States as the “ringleader of world imperialism.” According to Kim, the war exposed U.S. aggression and destroyed its reputation for military superiority ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the speech praises the unity and endurance of the North Korean people, military, and state institutions. Kim argues that the war strengthened national resilience and loyalty under the leadership of the Workers' Party, presenting the conflict as a transformative moment for domestic unity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0916","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0916\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying South Korean attitudes to the armistice of 1953.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor **value**, any of the following:\\\n\\\nOrigin\n- Written by Syngman Rhee, President of South Korea, on May 30, 1953, addressed to U.S. President Dwight D. Eisenhower. The Source offers a direct insight into the official South Korean leadership's position on the armistice.\n- Since it is addressed to Eisenhower and not intended for public consumption, it likely reflects Rhee’s true diplomatic concerns and political strategy, rather than propaganda.\n- Demonstrates the tensions between South Korea and the U.S., even as allies, highlighting differing priorities regarding peace and reunification.\n\nPurpose\n- Intended to convince or pressure the United States to reconsider or adjust its approach to armistice negotiations. Reveals South Korea's diplomatic posture and negotiation strategy, especially Rhee's strong emphasis on reunification and anti-communism.\n- Aimed to assert South Korea's sovereignty and raise objections to any peace that would leave the North Korean regime and Chinese troops intact. Highlights the political stakes and frustrations felt by the South Korean leadership at being sidelined in decisions affecting Korean sovereignty.\n\nContent\n- Rich in detail about the official South Korean narrative of the war and its aftermath.\n- Shows the ideological dimension of South Korean leadership, strong nationalism, anti-communism, and fear of long-term division.\n- Useful for understanding the reasons behind Rhee's resistance to the armistice, including his willingness to act unilaterally (e.g., releasing POWs).\n\nFor **limitations**, any of the following:\\\n\\\nOrigin:\n- Reflects Rhee's personal views, not necessarily representative of the broader South Korean population or all political groups.\n- As a diplomatic message, it may still be somewhat calculated to influence American policy, rather than purely expressing national sentiment.\n- Limited in scope; it does not reveal how ordinary South Koreans, opposition politicians, or civil society viewed the armistice.\n\nPurpose\n- The persuasive tone of the letter means some arguments may be exaggerated to achieve diplomatic aims.\n- The purpose may have influenced the way Rhee frames the situation, possibly downplaying compromises South Korea might have been willing to accept privately.\n\nContent\n- Does not provide statistical data, social context, or public opinion, limiting its usefulness for understanding broader societal views.\n- Focused mainly on high-level diplomatic concerns, leaving out discussion of how the armistice might impact ordinary citizens.\n- Offers a one-sided perspective, there's no engagement with counter arguments or internal dissent.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs a top-secret letter from South Korea's president to his U.S. counterpart, the origin increases its value. It was not intended for public release, which likely makes it more candid and reflective of Rhee's true diplomatic stance. This gives historians rare access to his personal reasoning and frustrations, especially his strong rejection of an armistice ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, the origin also has limitations as it represents only Rhee's views, not those of South Korean citizens or opposition voices ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nThe purpose of the letter was to persuade the United States to change its approach to the armistice, pushing for stronger action against communism and reunification of Korea. This has value as it clearly demonstrates South Korea's frustration at being sidelined in key negotiations. For historians, this purpose helps explain diplomatic tensions between allies and shows how deeply South Korea valued sovereignty and reunification ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nIn terms of content, Rhee criticises the proposed ceasefire as a form of surrender, arguing it would be “a death sentence without protest.” He stresses the importance of punishing aggression, unifying the country, and protecting the security of free nations. This is valuable for understanding the ideological basis of Rhee's policies, including his fierce anti-communism and nationalism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0917","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0917\n\nUsing Source C and your own knowledge, discuss why Syngman Rhee was dissatisfied with the 1953 armistice negotiations.\n[5 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a conclusion. Both the source and own knowledge must be addressed.\n\nFrom the source:\n- Belief that the armistice amounted to surrender: Rhee viewed the U.S./UN proposal as “appeasing” and warned it “cannot avoid the appearance of surrender,” suggesting it rewarded aggression rather than punished it.\n- Fear of permanent division of Korea: He opposed any agreement that would leave Chinese forces in the North, which he believed would solidify Korea's division and undermine national reunification.\n- Described the armistice as a “death sentence” for Korea: Rhee warned that accepting the armistice would mean “an acceptance of a death sentence without protest,” showing he feared long-term vulnerability or abandonment.\n- Excluded from meaningful participation in negotiations: Rhee complained that the Republic of Korea (ROK) had been “patiently waiting” for others to discuss proposals while South Korea had not had a real say in its own future.\n- Emphasised justice and punishment for aggressors: He insisted that the war should end with the punishment of North Korea and Communist China, and that only reunification would ensure peace and collective security.\n\nOwn Knowledge:\n- Deep mistrust of Communist China and North Korea: Rhee believed any truce leaving communist forces in Korea would only delay future aggression and allow them time to regroup.\n- Uncompromising commitment to reunification under ROK rule: Rhee's ultimate goal was a unified Korea led by the South; he saw the armistice as abandoning this objective.\n- Strong nationalist ideology and anti-communism: Rhee’s political identity was rooted in fierce anti-communism and Korean nationalism, making him unwilling to accept any solution that preserved communist rule in the North.\n- Frustration with U.S. priorities: Rhee felt the U.S. was prioritising global Cold War stability over Korean national interests, especially after Eisenhower showed willingness to end the war even with Korea divided.\n- Attempted to sabotage armistice talks: In June 1953, Rhee released 27,000 North Korean POWs unilaterally to disrupt negotiations and pressure the U.S. into making stronger commitments.\n- Used the crisis to demand a U.S.–ROK mutual defense treaty: Rhee's resistance to the armistice was also a negotiation tactic to secure long-term military guarantees from the U.S., which he eventually achieved in October 1953.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Provide a clear summary judgment that accurately reflects the evidence provided about Rhee's dissatisfaction/opposition to the armistice.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, Rhee rejected any agreement that would allow Chinese Communist forces to remain in Korea, warning it would be “an acceptance of a death sentence without protest.” Second, he criticised the United Nations' proposal as overly conciliatory, stating it appeared to be an act of “appeasement” that could lead to disaster ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBeyond the source, Rhee's broader frustration stemmed from being sidelined in negotiations that directly affected his country's future. Despite leading South Korea, he was excluded from key decisions made by the U.S. and UN ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAdditionally, his goal of unifying Korea under Southern leadership was completely undermined by a ceasefire that left the peninsula divided. Rhee feared this division would become permanent, and he saw the armistice as a betrayal of Korean sovereignty ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, Rhee opposed the armistice because it not only failed to secure Korean reunification but also left his country vulnerable to future threats, all while excluding him from shaping the outcome ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0918","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0918\n\nCompare and contrast the views presented in Source B and Source C regarding the 1953 Korean War armistice.\n[6 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kim Il Sung. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.\" Speech before the Officers and Men of Unit No.256 of the Korean People's Army. October 23, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Kim, I.S. (1953, October 23). *On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.* Kim Il Sung Works. marxists.org. Retrieved June 25, 2025 from  https://www.marxists.org/archive/kim-il-sung/cw/08.pdf\nPublic Domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both leaders were dissatisfied with the outcome of the war:\n    -  Rhee (C): Opposed the armistice as it meant accepting division and the continued presence of communists in the North; “a death sentence without protest.”\n    - Kim (B): Acknowledged that reunification had not been achieved; “we have not yet reunified the country.”\n- Both frame the war as part of a larger ideological struggle:\n    - Rhee (C): Viewed the conflict as a front in the fight against communism, part of global Cold War dynamics.\n    - Kim (B): Described the war as a battle against “US imperialism” and claimed it exposed the “brutal nature of imperialism” worldwide.\n- Both used the war to promote national pride and justify their leadership:\n    - Rhee (C): Presented South Korea’s resistance as morally correct and rooted in justice.\n    - Kim (B): Claimed the armistice was a “great victory,” enhancing the prestige of the Korean people, the army, and the party.\n- Both maintained claims to legitimacy over the entire Korean peninsula:\n    - Neither leader accepted the legitimacy of the other side's government.\n    - Both saw the armistice as a temporary condition, not a permanent settlement.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences\n- Attitude Toward the Armistice\n    - Rhee (C): Strongly opposed the armistice; saw it as appeasement and betrayal.\n    - Kim (B): Publicly embraced the armistice as a strategic victory, even though military goals were unmet.\n- Perceived Outcome of the War\n    - Rhee (C): Believed the war had not achieved its goals, particularly Korean reunification under the South.\n    - Kim (B): Claimed a political and ideological victory by resisting the U.S. and “forcing” the enemy to sign the agreement.\n- Framing of the Enemy\n    - Rhee (C): Focused on the threat of communist expansion and Chinese military presence in Korea.\n    - Kim (B): Focused on U.S. imperialism as the main aggressor, calling it the “ringleader” of global oppression.\n- Vision for the Future\n    - Rhee (C): Urged continued resistance to communism and advocated for Korean reunification through stronger action.\n    - Kim (B): Positioned the post-war state as stronger, more unified, and ready to continue defending socialism.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth leaders were unhappy that the war ended without reunification ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Rhee refused to accept any peace that left North Korea intact, calling such a deal “a death sentence without protest” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Similarly, Kim acknowledged that “we have not yet reunified the country,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) suggesting that complete victory was not achieved. This reveals a shared goal of national reunification, although under very different regimes.\n\nAlso, Rhee and Kim both presented the war as part of a broader ideological struggle, tied to Cold War rivalries. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) Rhee saw the armistice as appeasement in the face of communist expansion, while Kim celebrated the war as a blow against “US imperialism,” stating that it “shattered the myth of the might of U.S. imperialism” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nFurthermore, neither Rhee nor Kim accepted the legitimacy of the other Korean government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Rhee argued that settling the war without unifying Korea would be “a death sentence without protest,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) implying that anything short of reunification under his government was unacceptable. Kim similarly described the outcome as unfinished, stating, “we have not yet reunified the country,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) suggesting that the DPRK still aimed to assert control over the entire peninsula. Both treated the armistice as a temporary arrangement rather than a lasting peace ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also differ. Rhee viewed the proposed armistice as a betrayal of Korean independence and openly rejected it. He feared it would legitimise continued foreign occupation and communist rule in the North. In contrast, Kim portrayed the armistice as a diplomatic and military success, claiming that North Korea had forced the U.S. to sign on its terms, calling it “a great victory” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Rhee saw defeat; Kim declared triumph ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI1061","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1061\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the reaction of the local population to the German army entering the Sudetenland?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the diary of Wehrmacht soldier Herbert Wetzig, October 10, 1938. Wetzig describes the takeover of the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia. |\n\n[Source: Wetzig, H. (1938, October 10) *Soldier's Diary Entries on the Takeover of the Sudetenland (1938)*, published in German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved 21 October 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/soldiers-diary-entries-on-the-takeover-of-the-sudetenland-1938 Public Domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The local population welcomed the German soldiers with joy and enthusiasm.\n- People threw flowers and gave food, drink, and cigarettes to the soldiers.\n- The reception was described as deeply emotional and genuine, not forced.\n- The local people expressed a sense of relief and happiness (“true joy,” “indescribable joy”).\n- There was a sense of unity between the German army and local Sudeten Germans (“brothers and sisters of ours”).\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source shows that the local people were excited to see the German soldiers. They gathered in crowds and cheered so loudly that the music could barely be heard ($\\checkmark$). People also gave the soldiers food, drinks, and cigarettes, which suggests they were welcoming and generous ($\\checkmark$). The writer says the joy was “true” and unlike anything they had experienced before, which shows the emotional response of the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1062","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1062\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, assess the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the annexation of the Sudetenland in 1938.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the diary of Wehrmacht soldier Herbert Wetzig, October 10, 1938. Wetzig describes the takeover of the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia. |\n\n[Source: Wetzig, H. (1938, October 10) *Soldier's Diary Entries on the Takeover of the Sudetenland (1938)*, published in German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved 21 October 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/soldiers-diary-entries-on-the-takeover-of-the-sudetenland-1938 Public Domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n\n- Origin:\n    - Written by a German soldier (Herbert Wetzig) in October 1938, it provides immediate insight into the annexation.\n    - A primary source created during the event, offering direct observation and lived experience.\n- Purpose:\n    - Intended as a private diary, not propaganda, which may increase its reliability.\n    - Reflects personal emotions and experiences, giving historians a sense of how soldiers perceived the annexation.\n- Content:\n    - Describes cheering crowds, people offering gifts, and a celebratory atmosphere, which shows popular support among Sudeten Germans.\n    - Emphasizes unity and emotional connection between German troops and local populations (“brothers and sisters”), revealing how the annexation was framed.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n\n- Origin:\n    - The author was a soldier in the Nazi military, likely influenced by Nazi ideology or expectations.\n    - Only reflects the German perspective; it excludes the views of Czech citizens or Sudeten Germans opposed to the annexation.\n- Purpose:\n    - Although private, the diary may reflect internalized propaganda or selective memory shaped by nationalist thinking.\n    - The emotional tone may exaggerate or romanticize events, reducing objectivity.\n- Content:\n    - Omits any signs of resistance, fear, or opposition and presents only one, celebratory narrative.\n    - Lacks a broader political or strategic context; focuses on feelings rather than factual analysis of the annexation’s causes or consequences.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable because it is a primary account written by a German soldier who participated in the 1938 annexation of the Sudetenland, giving historians direct insight into the perceptions of those involved at the time  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  Since it is a private diary, not an official propaganda piece, it is more likely to express genuine emotions and personal views, which may make it more reliable in reflecting the soldier's true experience ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content vividly describes cheering crowds, people giving gifts to soldiers, and a sense of emotional unity between the German army and the local population. This shows how at least some Sudeten Germans reacted positively to the annexation and felt a connection to Germany ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source is limited because it only presents the perspective of a German soldier and does not include any views from Czech citizens or Sudeten Germans who may have opposed the annexation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Even though it is a diary, the author may have internalized Nazi propaganda or held nationalistic views, which could distort how he interpreted local reactions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The content only describes enthusiastic support and does not mention fear, resistance, or the political context of the annexation, which limits its usefulness in understanding the full picture of how the event was experienced by all communities involved ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1063","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1063\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about Hitler's intentions for Germany's foreign policy in 1939?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a secret speech given by Adolf Hitler to his military commanders on February 10, 1939. |\n\n[Source: Hitler, A. (10 February 1939). *Hitler's Secret Speech to Military Commanders.* Published in German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved 29 November 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitlers-secret-speech-to-military-commanders-february-10-1939 Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Hitler intended for Germany to expand militarily.\nHe had been planning foreign policy moves well before 1939 — since 1933.\n- The regime's previous actions (e.g., remilitarization, annexations) were part of a long-term strategy, not random or defensive.\n- Despite public claims of peace, Hitler secretly prepared for war.\n- He saw war as necessary or inevitable for Germany’s future.\n- He wanted the military commanders to understand and support the broader plan\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that Hitler viewed Germany's foreign policy since 1933 as part of a calculated and long-term plan rather than a series of isolated decisions ($\\checkmark$). It reveals that, despite public declarations of peaceful intentions, the regime was actively preparing for war in secret ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Hitler's aim in addressing his military commanders appears to be to justify his expansionist ambitions and secure their support for future military action ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1064","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1064\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, assess the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying German foreign policy in early 1939.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a secret speech given by Adolf Hitler to his military commanders on February 10, 1939. |\n\n[Source: Hitler, A. (10 February 1939). *Hitler's Secret Speech to Military Commanders.* Published in German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved 29 November 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitlers-secret-speech-to-military-commanders-february-10-1939 Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n\n- Origin:\n    - Delivered by Adolf Hitler, the head of state, on 10 February 1939 — providing a direct insight into Nazi leadership decision-making.\n    - A private speech to military commanders, making it less likely to be shaped by public propaganda motives.\n- Purpose:\n    - Intended to inform and prepare top military leaders, suggesting a more honest or strategic tone than public speeches.\n    - Useful for understanding how Hitler justified future military action to those in command.\n- Content:\n    - Declares that Germany's foreign policy since 1933 was part of a broader, long-term strategy of expansion and rearmament.\n    - Contrasts public claims of peace with secret preparations for war — offering a clearer picture of Nazi intent.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n\n- Origin:\n    - As a speech by Hitler, it reflects the views and agenda of one individual, potentially distorting the broader decision-making process.\n    - Offers only the Nazi leadership perspective; excludes military dissent, civilian opinions, or international views.\n- Purpose:\n    - Though private, Hitler may have aimed to persuade or unify military leaders, possibly overstating confidence or inevitability.\n    - May have simplified or distorted the complexity of Germany's position to maintain control or loyalty.\n- Content:\n    - Focuses on intentions, not actions — it doesn't confirm that all planned steps were carried out.\n    - Lacks details on how other countries were reacting or what specific policies would follow — limiting broader historical understanding.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B is useful because it was spoken directly by Hitler just months before World War II, offering a rare glimpse into his mindset and priorities at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). Since the speech was delivered behind closed doors to high-ranking military officials, it was not meant to shape public opinion, making it more likely to reflect real intentions and strategies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).  In terms of content, it shows that Hitler viewed past actions — like rearmament and territorial expansion — as steps in a broader plan, helping historians understand that Germany's aggressive policies were calculated and long-standing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nStill, the source has its limits. Being a speech from Hitler, it only presents his point of view and doesn't include the perspectives of other Nazi leaders or military figures who may have disagreed ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).  Even in a private setting, he may have aimed to rally support among his generals by presenting a confident and simplified version of events ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$).  The speech also doesn't give practical details or include any reactions from foreign governments, so it doesn't fully explain how this policy fit into the larger international context ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1065","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1065\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about changes in defence spending by major powers between 1930 and 1938?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Defence Expenditure of the Major Belligerent Nations of WW II, 1930-1938. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/20th%20Century%20Wars/Unassigned/images/HI1065.png\" alt=\"HI1065\">\n[Source: Wikimedia Commons. *Data taken from The World in 1939,* AJ Toynbee & FT Ashton-Gwatkin (1952). Retrieved November 30, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graph_top7_def_expd_1930-38.png CC-SA 3.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Germany's defence spending rose dramatically, especially after 1933.\n- By 1938, Germany had the highest military expenditure among the countries shown.\n- Japan's spending peaked around 1936, then decreased.\n- The USSR's spending steadily increased and remained high throughout the period.\n- The United Kingdom's defence spending began rising more noticeably after 1935.\n- Most countries increased their defence budgets in the late 1930s, reflecting growing tensions.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe chart shows that Germany's defence spending rose sharply after 1933, surpassing all other countries by 1938 ($\\checkmark$). It also indicates that most major powers, including the United Kingdom and the USSR, began significantly increasing their military budgets in the mid- to late 1930s, likely in response to growing international tensions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1164","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1164\n\nDiscuss **three** economic effects of the USSR's invasion of Afghanistan.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Massive Military Expenditure\n    - The war cost the USSR an estimated US $8-10 billion per year, diverting funds from civilian sectors.\n    - Sustained defence spending (up to $30\\%$ of GDP) deepened economic stagnation and weakened domestic growth.\n- Resource Drain and Opportunity Costs\n    - Huge quantities of weapons, fuel, and materials were consumed, reducing industrial output and export capacity.\n    - Investment in modernisation and consumer goods was neglected, worsening inefficiency in the command economy.\n- Human and Labour Impact\n    - Around 15,000 soldiers died, and many more were injured or traumatised, increasing welfare costs.\n    - The loss of young workers and veterans' reintegration challenges further strained productivity and the economy.\n- Impact on the Soviet Bloc Economy\n    - The USSR sent large-scale economic aid to Afghanistan, burdening the wider communist economic system (COMECON).\n    - Eastern European allies faced reduced trade and resources as Soviet spending prioritised the war effort.\n- Decline in Oil Revenue and Global Economic Pressure\n    - Falling oil prices and Western sanctions (trade and grain embargoes) reduced Soviet export income.\n    - International isolation, symbolised by the 1980 Olympic boycott, limited investment and economic engagement with the West.\n- Impact on Global Economic Relations\n    - The invasion ended détente and reignited the Cold War arms race, forcing further unsustainable military spending.\n    - The USSR's competition with the U.S. under Reagan's rearmament policies deepened its financial crisis.\n- Long-Term Domestic Economic Consequences\n    - The war exposed the inefficiency of central planning and increased public discontent.\n    - Gorbachev labelled the war an “economic and political burden,” using it to justify reforms, but the damage was lasting.\n    - By the late 1980s, the financial strain and loss of legitimacy contributed to the collapse of the Soviet economy and the USSR (1991).\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979-1989) was one of the most costly foreign interventions in Soviet history, imposing enormous financial costs on the Soviet Union, estimated between US \\$ 8-10 billion per year throughout the 1980s ($\\checkmark$). Occurring at a time of stagnation in the Soviet economy, it contributed to structural weaknesses and to the USSR's decline, with military operations diverting funds from civilian industries, agriculture, and welfare programs. This aggravated public dissatisfaction and revealing severe limits to central planning ($\\checkmark$). Withdrawal in 1989 did not reverse the damage as the war had weakened state finances, eroded public trust, and undermined the legitimacy of Soviet leadership ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1165","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1165\n\nExplain **three** political effects of the USSR's invasion of Afghanistan in December 1979.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n- Collapse of Détente and Renewed Cold War Tensions\n    - The invasion ended détente, prompting U.S. condemnation and sanctions.\n    - The Carter Doctrine (1980) and Western rearmament signalled a return to confrontation. Tthe “Second Cold War.”\n- Soviet Diplomatic Isolation.\n    - The USSR lost support among non-aligned and Muslim-majority nations, facing repeated UN condemnation.\n    - Even allies like India and Yugoslavia distanced themselves; China denounced the invasion as imperialist.\n- U.S. Proxy War Response\n    - The U.S. launched Operation Cyclone, arming Afghan Mujahideen through Pakistan.\n    - Afghanistan became a major Cold War battleground, worsening global tensions and Soviet losses.\n- Domestic Political Impact in the USSR\n    - The war became unpopular as casualties mounted and censorship failed to hide the truth.\n    - It divided Party leaders, undermined faith in the regime, and contributed to leadership instability in the 1980s.\n- Weakening of the Communist Bloc\n    - The invasion strained relations with China and Eastern Europe, revealing cracks in the Soviet alliance system.\n    - The USSR's moral and ideological authority declined as the war dragged on without victory.\n- Gorbachev's Rise and Policy Shift\n    - The war's failure pushed Gorbachev to pursue reform and withdraw Soviet troops (1989).\n    - Marked a turn away from military interventionism toward diplomacy and openness with the West.\n- Contribution to the USSR's Collapse\n    - The conflict exposed Soviet weakness and undermined communist legitimacy.\n    - Together with economic strain, it accelerated the decline and dissolution of the USSR (1991).\n- Broader Regional Effects\n    - Left Afghanistan unstable, empowering Mujahideen fighters who later formed the Taliban and al-Qaeda.\n    - Strengthened Pakistan's role as a U.S. ally and reshaped power dynamics in the Middle East and South Asia.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe invasion led to the end of détente and a renewal of the Cold War ($\\checkmark$). The U.S. and its allies condemned the Soviet action as aggressive and expansionist, ending the period detente. The Carter Doctrine (1980) promised to defend the Persian Gulf from the Soviets, while the U.S. also imposed economic sanctions, cancelled SALT II, and boycotted the 1980 Moscow Olympics. Western nations also increased defence spending, marking the start of the “Second Cold War”($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the invasion left the USSR politically isolated ($\\checkmark$). Many non-aligned and Muslim-majority countries condemned the intervention, and the United Nations repeatedly criticised Soviet actions. Long-standing partners like India and Yugoslavia were also uneasy, while China denounced the move as imperialist ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the war's failure contributed to significant political change within the USSR ($\\checkmark$). By 1985, Gorbachev recognised the conflict as a political disaster and introduced glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) to reform Soviet politics and foreign policy. His decision to withdraw Soviet troops in 1989 marked a shift away from military interventionism toward diplomacy. The war accelerated the eventual collapse of the USSR in 1991 ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"Authoritarian States (20th Century)","subtopics":[{"name":"Aims & Results of Policies","questions":[{"ref":"HI0088","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0088\n\nExplain the impacts of **one** educational reform implemented in Cuba by Fidel Castro’s government\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following ideas with supporting details:\n\nLiteracy Campaign\n- Mobilised volunteers to teach literacy in rural areas\n- Literacy rose to over 96%, but education remained under state control which limited intellectual freedom.\n\nEducation Reform\n- Broadened education access for many Cubans\n- State curricula promoted socialism through propaganda which limited critical thinking\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of Castro's most significant social reforms was the Literacy Campaign of 1961, which aimed to eliminate illiteracy by mobilising students and volunteers to teach in rural areas ($\\checkmark$). The campaign was highly successful, raising literacy rates to over 96%. ($\\checkmark$) However, education became state-controlled, promoting socialist ideology and limiting intellectual freedom. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0219","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0219\n\nExplain the successes and failures of Fidel Castro's social policies in Cuba.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor successes, any of the following:\n- Improved literacy: The 1961 National Literacy Campaign reduced illiteracy from 23% to 4%, earning recognition from UNESCO.\n- Improved public health: The creation of a universal healthcare system made Cuba a model in Latin America, achieving a life expectancy comparable to developed nations by 1980.\n- Increased social equality: The government implemented housing reforms, providing subsidized or free housing to many citizens. By the 1980s, homelessness was virtually eliminated, and access to basic utilities like water and electricity improved significantly.\n\nFor failure, any of the following:\n- Resource shortages: The economic embargo and excessive centralization led to shortages of essential goods, especially during the Special Period in the 1990s following the collapse of the USSR.\n- Restrictions on political freedom and speech: Social policies were accompanied by censorship and repression, including the imprisonment of dissenters during the 1960s and 1970s.\n- Economic dependency: Cuba's reliance on the USSR for subsidies and trade made its economy vulnerable. When the USSR collapsed, the resulting economic crisis in the 1990s severely impacted social programs, reducing their quality and accessibility.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFidel Castro's social policies in Cuba achieved notable successes. The 1961 National Literacy Campaign dramatically reduced illiteracy from 23% to 4%, earning global recognition ($\\checkmark$). Universal healthcare established Cuba as a model in Latin America, with life expectancy rivaling that of developed nations by 1980 ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, housing reforms provided subsidized or free housing, eliminating homelessness and improving access to essential utilities like water and electricity by the 1980s ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, these achievements were accompanied by significant failures. The economic embargo and centralization caused severe resource shortages, particularly during the Special Period in the 1990s ($\\checkmark$). Social policies also came at the cost of political freedoms , as censorship and repression targeted dissenters, especially in the 1960s and 1970s ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Cuba's dependence on the USSR made its economy fragile, and the Soviet collapse undermined the quality and sustainability of its social programs ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0240","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0240\n\nDescribe the features of Stalin's first 5-year plan (1928-1932). \n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Increased production of coal and iron.\n\n - There were major infrastructure projects completed, such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal and the Dnieprostroi Dam.\n\n - Major industrial areas were constructed, such as the Magnitogorsk Industrial city. \n\n - Agricultural collectivisation was implemented to centralise the control of land resources and food production in order to facilitate industrial growth.\n\n - Forced labor stationed in gulags were used widely as extra manpower to help complete the projects in the 1st 5-year plan. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe USSR was able to rapidly industrialize its economy under the 1st 5-year plan. This included the increased production of coal and iron ($\\checkmark$). There were also major construction projects involved, such as the White Sea-Baltic Canal and the Dnieprostroi Dam  ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, major industrial areas such as Magitorgosk were built to facilitate more production of iron and steel ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0241","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0241\n\nDiscuss the successes and failures of Stalin's first 5-year plan.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor **successes**, any of the following:\n\n - Collectivisation allowed the USSR to have more centralised control over agricultural resources and land.\n\n - Due to collectivisation, the USSR launched the dekulakization to eradicate the kulaks, which were considered a threat.\n\n - The Gosplan was able to mobilise more resources to industrialize the USSR's economy.\n\n - The USSR was able to transform itself from an agrarian economy to industrial economy. \n\nThe 1st 5-year plan laid the foundation for the future 5-year plans.\n\nFor **failures**, any of the following:\n\n - The 1st 5-year plan had drawbacks in poor planning. \n\n - Due to a strong emphasis on heavy industries, little consumer goods were produced. \n\n - The rural society was disrupted since the kulaks were eliminated as a class enemy and peasants were forced to work in state collective farms in which they could not produce food for themselves. \n\n - The collective farms were poorly managed and were inefficient. Peasants had little incentive to produce food since the food supplies would be immediately given to the state.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\nFor concluding remarks:\n\n - Responses should consider both successes and failures, but this need not be balanced between the two.\n\n - The best responses will have a conclusion that synthesises all the main arguments and present a concluding which provides a justification on whether the 5-year plans were mostly a success or failure. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe 1st 5-year plans of the USSR were successful to a large extent in radically transforming the USSR into a major industrial power. \n\nDuring the 1st 5-year plan, the USSR greatly increased its industrial production capacity in iron and steel, most notably in Magitorosk ($\\checkmark$), which became the USSR's center for steel production. The development of industrial cities also helped expand the Soviet Union's capacity in heavy industry. For example, the USSR was able to produce up to 4.8 million tonnes of steel in 1928 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nDespite the successes of the 1st 5-year plan, there were also some drawbacks. The industrialisation of the USSR was made possible by the use of forced labor that was made available from the countryside after the USSR forced rural households into collective farms ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the result of rapid transition to heavy industry had negative effects on the countryside. Around 5 million people in the countryside died as a result of the dekulakization campaigns. Agricultural production feel since the peasants would destroy their own livestock rather than having them be sent to collective farms ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, though there some minor setbacks in the 1st 5-year plans, the 1st 5-year plans were mostly successful in transforming the USSR's economy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0280Q1","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0280Q1\n\nExplain **two** ways in which national minorities were impacted by Stalin's leadership and/or policies. \n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following: \n\n - Russification/Korenizatsiya ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Indigenization\") \n\n     - Stalin promoted the Russian language and culture as a unifying force across the USSR. In the 1920s and early 1930s, Stalin initially supported policies that encouraged the development of local languages, cultures, and leadership within minority regions. This was intended to gain the loyalty of national minorities and integrate them into the Soviet system. \n\n - Deportation: \n\n     - Stalin used mass deportations to weaken ethnic groups he considered disloyal or a security threat. During World War II, groups like the Crimean Tatars, Chechens, Ingush, and Volga Germans were accused of collaborating with Nazi Germany and forcibly relocated to remote areas such as Siberia and Central Asia. These deportations often involved brutal conditions, leading to widespread death and suffering.\n\n - Purges: \n\n     - Stalin targeted minority elites and intellectuals through purges, accusing them of nationalism or counter-revolutionary activity. Prominent examples include the purges of Ukrainian intellectuals during the 1930s and the repression of leaders in other minority regions.\n\n - Creation of National Republics: \n\n     - Stalin formally recognized the USSR's multi-ethnic composition by establishing national republics and granting them nominal autonomy. Each republic had its own constitution and was allowed to promote local languages and cultures, but this autonomy was tightly controlled by Moscow.\n\n - Collectivization and Industrialization: \n\n     - Stalin's economic policies often disproportionately affected minority regions. Collectivization, for example, caused widespread famine in Ukraine during the 1932-1933 Holodomor, which many scholars view as a deliberate act of repression against Ukrainian nationalism.\n\n - Infrastructure Development: \n\n     - While Stalin's industrialization projects brought economic development to some minority regions and the accompanying infrastructure needed to support industrialisation, they often prioritized the interests of the central government over local populations, further marginalizing minority groups.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne way national minorities were dealt with under Stalin was through forced deportations ($\\checkmark$). Stalin targeted ethnic groups he deemed disloyal or a threat to Soviet unity, particularly during World War II. Entire populations, such as the Crimean Tatars, Chechens, Ingush, and Volga Germans, were accused of collaborating with enemy forces and forcibly relocated to remote areas like Siberia and Central Asia ($\\checkmark$). \n\nStalin also eliminated minority ethnic intellectuals branding them as nationals or enemies of the State ($\\checkmark$). For example, in Ukraine there was a crackdown on intellectuals in the 1930s driven by his belief that they might undermine the Soviet regime or prevent the assimilation of various ethnic groups into Russian culture ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0280Q5","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0280Q5\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source A for an historian studying the domestic policies of the Soviet Union.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$ Constitution (Fundamental law) of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,” Kremlin, Moscow, December 5, 1936.\n\n[Source: Stalin, J.V. (1936, December 5). *Works Vol. 14. Red Start Press Ltd, 1978*. Transcribed by Salil Sen for Marxists Internet Archive, 2008. Retrieved December 14th, 2024 from  https://marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1936/12/05.htm.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n\n - The source is an official document from the 1936 Constitution of the USSR, providing a primary perspective on the legal framework and state ideology under Stalin's rule.\n\n - The document's purpose is to articulate the rights and responsibilities of Soviet citizens and present the state as a guarantor of social welfare, equality, and progress.\n\n - The content of the source indicates a clear enumeration of rights (e.g. education, gender equality and equality amongst various races and cultures) as well as the secular nature of the Soviet government. \n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n\n - The document's propagandistic nature may exaggerate or misrepresent the extent to which these rights were implemented in practice. Its purpose to glorify the USSR undermines its reliability as an objective representation of living conditions or individual freedoms.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A, being an official document from the 1936 Soviet Constitution, offers valuable insights for historians as an official text  ($\\checkmark VO$),  it provides a primary perspective on the legal framework and state ideology, directly reflecting the goals and policies of the Soviet government  ($\\checkmark VP$). Its endorsement by the Soviet state ensures that it represents the regime's vision for the USSR, particularly its emphasis on social welfare, equality, and progress ($\\checkmark VC$). The constitution enumerates specific rights such as the right to work, rest, and education, signalling the state's prioritization of these areas  ($\\checkmark VC$). \n\nHowever, the source also has significant limitations. Authored under Stalin's regime, it is inherently biased, serving as propaganda to legitimize the government and promote the image of the USSR as a utopian socialist state  ($\\checkmark LO$). This propagandistic nature means the document likely exaggerates or misrepresents the actual implementation of these rights, presenting a rosy picture of Soviet life that was far from reality  ($\\checkmark LP$). The highly controlled political environment in which the document was created also means it doesn't reflect genuine public opinion or opposition views, limiting its usefulness as a complete or objective representation of Soviet domestic policies  ($\\checkmark LC$)."},{"ref":"HI0329","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0329\n\nDescribe **one** of Fidel Castro's motivations to align Cuba with the Soviet Union.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following:\n- Strategic motivation: To protect Cuba against retaliation from the US after Castro nationalised foreign industries.\n- Economic motivation: To provide Cuba with economic support after the US embargo on Cuban sugar, as sugar was Cuba's largest export.\n- Ideological motivation: embrace of Marxist-Leninist principles, especially socialism and anti-imperialism.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCastro chose to align with the Soviet Union because he needed economic support. The nationalising of foreign industries has resulted in the US imposing an embargo on Cuban sugar which was Cuba's largest export commodity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0441","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0441\n\nDescribe how the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$New Economic Policy” (NEP) helped the Bolsheviks consolidate their power.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The NEP allowed Russia's economy to recover from the devastation caused by War Communism.\n\n - The NEP restored support for the Bolsheviks from the peasants due to the ending of forced grain acquisition.\n\n - However, the NEP also introduced NEPmen, which were people that benefited from the private trade during the NEP period, some Bolsheviks saw this as a step backwards from the revolution. \n\n - Some Bolsheviks also saw the emerging NEPmen as a challenge to the Bolshevik state. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe NEP, to a more considerable extent, was successful in helping the Bolsheviks to consolidate their power. One of the notable successes of the NEP is that it restored Russia's economy, which had been devastated by War Communism in the early phases of the Russian Civil War ($\\checkmark$). Since the NEP was able to briefly restore the economy and especially end forced grain acquisition in the countryside, the NEP helped to restore support for the Bolseviks in the countryside ($\\checkmark$). Despite the successes of the NEP, it also met some challenges both within and outside the party. Some Bolsheviks, such as Trotsky, criticized the NEP for taking a step backwards from the revolution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0442","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0442\n\nDescribe the features of War Communism.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following points:\n\n - Peasants were required to give their agricultural produce to the state.\n\n - Forced grain requisition was conducted when peasants could not hand over food supplies to the state.\n\n - Peasants that resisted the requisition squads were executed.\n\n - Food rationing was introduced to control food supplies to ensure that the Red Army has more of the food supplies in order to defeat the White Army.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe first feature of War Communism is that food rationing was implemented to ensure that food supplies were given to the Red Army in order to defeat the White Army ($\\checkmark$). To enable this, peasants were required to give their agricultural produce to the state ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, force grain requisition was implemented as a last report if peasants could not give in their food supplies to the state ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0443","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0443\n\nExplain two causes for the failure of War Communism.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Decline in industrial production\n\n     - Industrial production fell by 20$\\%$ from 1913 levels since the civil war brought damage to key industrial areas and conscription of men from both sides meant that there were fewer workers.\n\n     - The Bolsheviks nationalised factories after they took power, but the management of the factories were inefficient, which led to poor productivity.\n\n     - Moreover, due to war communism, most of the resources were diverted to support the military and military supplies were produced instead. Hence production of consumer goods declined and the availability of raw materials declined as a result.\n\n - Decline in agricultural production\n\n     - The forced grain requisition discouraged peasants to produce food since they would be confiscated, which led to a drastic decline in agricultural production. For example, grain production fell by 40 million tons in 1920. \n\n     - The sharp decline in agricultural production also led to a massive famine that killed up to 5 million people across the country.\n\n - Urban Depopulation:\n\n     - The civil war led to massive urban depopulation since the war greatly affected the urban areas and nearby countryside.  For example, Petrograd lost 70$\\%$ of its population while Moscow lost 50% of its population. \n\n - Mass Resistance \n\n     - The Kronstadt Rebellion erupted because sailors and soldiers at the Kronstadt naval base disagreed with war communism. Soldiers were dissatisfied since they received fewer food supplies under War Communism. Also, the Bolsheviks suppressed any dissidents during this time period, which further angered the soldiers that once supported the Bolsheviks. \n\n     - The forced grain requisition measures further alienated the peasants in the countryside, which led to many uprisings in the countryside such as the Tambov uprising. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWar Communism was a failure mainly due to the two following factors. Firstly, War Communism led to a decline in agricultural production ($\\checkmark$). The forced grain acquisition measures used by the Bolsheviks only discouraged peasants to produce food since they would be confiscated. For example, grain production fell by 40 million tons in 1920 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, War Communism also led to more mass resistance ($\\checkmark$). One of the most notable resistant movements was the Kronstadt rebellion when soldiers at the Kronstadt naval base rebelled against the Bolsheviks due to their disagreement with the economic policies ($\\checkmark$). The increase in mass resistance only showed that War Communism was becoming more unpopular over time."},{"ref":"HI0445","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0445\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about Stalin's view on the future of agriculture in the USSR?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**  $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech by Joseph Stalin titled “Industrialisation of the country and the Right Deviation in the CPSU.” Published in November 19, 1928. |\n\n[Source: Stalin, J (1928, November). *Industrialisation of the country and the Right Deviation in the C.P.S.U.(B.).* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved March 16, 2025 from http://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1928/11/19.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Soviet Union cannot exist on two foundations, with one foundation in modern large-scale industry and the other on backward agriculture.\n- The Soviet Union would have to gradually and systematically transform agriculture to a scale equivalent to large-scale industries.\n- The transformation of agriculture would ensure the victory of the Soviet Union.\n- Stalin warned of the return of capitalism if the transformation of agriculture failed.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that the existing problem of the Soviet Union is that it cannot exist on two foundations, with one foundation in modern large-scale industries and another foundations on small-scale backwards agricultural production for a long time ($\\checkmark$). Stalin also stated that the Soviet union would have to gradually and systematically transform the country's agriculture on a new technical basis to reach the level of socialist industry  ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, Stalin warned that capitalism would return if the transformation of agriculture fails  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0498","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0498\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of Castro's agrarian reforms in Cuba.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Reforms led to the rise of State Farms and Cooperatives: As the state centralized agriculture, Cuba saw the decline of individual smallholders\n- Short-term increase in agricultural production: After the agrarian reforms, Cuba saw a rise in the production of sugarcane\n- Increase in social justice and welfare: more people gained access to land due to land distribution reforms, leading to a reduction in rural poverty and an improvement in the standard of living.\n- Long-term economic challenges: centralisation and strict state control led to inefficiencies and shortages of certain foods, as the working populace had few incentives to be productive.\n- Economic isolation, caused by the removal of US influence from businesses, also led to supply problems.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe impact of Castro's agrarian reforms was that, as the state centralised agriculture, Cuba saw the decline of individual smallholders and instead a rise in State farms and Cooperatives ($\\checkmark$). In the long term, this led to economic challenges, as strict state control resulted in inefficiencies and production shortages in certain areas ($\\checkmark$).  The move to nationalism and the removal of US influences in Cuba also led to supply problems for certain goods and economic isolation for Cuba ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0638","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0638\n\nCompare and contrast the role and status of women in **two** authoritarian states.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n- Clearly address both ***role*** (what women did) and ***status*** (their rights, position, and recognition in society) in each state.\n- Identify both **similarities** and **differences**, showing how ideology shaped women's roles/status differently in each context.\n- Explain the impact of policies on women's lives and how ideological goals influenced the treatment of women.\n- Acknowledge contradictions in each regime.\n- Use a well-structured argument with relevant historical examples and differing perspectives integrated into the answer.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- **Maoist China (1949-1976)**: Women were mobilized as key contributors to the socialist economy, working in factories, communes, and participating in mass campaigns as part of the state's push for industrial and agricultural transformation. Although legally granted equality through reforms like the 1950 Marriage Law, women often remained in subordinate positions socially and politically. Although women were given more power, it was often just for show.\n- **Nazi Germany (1933-1945)**: Women were expected to fulfil domestic roles as mothers and homemakers, responsible for raising racially pure Aryan children and supporting the family unit as the foundation of the Nazi state. Their status was framed as equal but different, with motherhood glorified and rewarded through honours like the Mother's Cross; however, they were excluded from political and leadership roles, reinforcing a subordinate position in public life.\n- **Fascist Italy under Mussolini (1922-1943)**: Women were encouraged to focus on producing large families to bolster the Italian population and support the state's expansionist goals, while also participating in regime-sponsored social organizations like the Fascist Women's Groups. Despite official praise for their maternal role, women were legally and politically marginalized, denied voting rights, and subjected to restrictive laws limiting access to education and professional advancement.\n- **Francoist Spain (1939-1975)**: Women were expected to maintain traditional Catholic values by serving as obedient wives and mothers, with their education geared toward domestic duties and moral instruction. Their legal and civil rights were severely restricted under laws such as the “Fuero del Trabajo,” which reasserted male authority in the family, positioning women as legally and socially subordinate to men.\n- **The USSR under Stalin (1924-1953)**: Women were integral to the industrial workforce and agricultural collectivization, often taking on roles as factory workers, doctors, and engineers while also raising families under difficult living conditions. Although the Soviet state proclaimed gender equality and granted legal rights, including access to education and employment, real power remained male-dominated, and traditional expectations of women’s domestic responsibilities persisted.\n- **North Korea under Kim Il-Sung and successors (from 1948 onward)**: Women were mobilized for both production and ideological work through the Women’s Union, expected to contribute to socialist construction while maintaining household duties and educating children in loyalty to the regime. While granted nominal equality in the constitution, women have remained largely absent from senior political roles and often face institutional discrimination, with Confucian gender norms continuing to influence social hierarchies.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMao Zedong's China (1949-1976) and Hitler's Nazi Germany (1933-1945) both implemented state-directed policies that defined the role and status of women according to their ideological visions. While Mao promoted gender equality and sought to integrate women into the workforce as part of socialist transformation, Nazi Germany reinforced traditional gender roles, confirming motherhood as women's highest duty. Despite these contrasting ideologies, both regimes ultimately aimed to control and mobilize women to serve state interests. This essay will compare and contrast the role and status of women in each regime through analysis of policy, economic participation, political engagement, and societal expectations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nIn both regimes, women's roles were shaped by ideological aims ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In China, Communist ideology framed gender equality as essential to socialist development. Mao's famous saying, “Women hold up half the sky,” symbolized the party's commitment to elevating women's status. The 1950 Marriage Law granted women divorce rights, banned arranged marriages, and established legal equality within marriage. These reforms aimed to dismantle feudal patriarchy and integrate women into revolutionary society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In contrast, Nazi ideology emphasized a complimentary gender model. The Nazi regime proclaimed that men and women were equal but different, with women's roles confined to the domestic sphere. The “three Ks, ”Kinder, Küche, Kirche (Children, Kitchen, Church), summarized their expected contribution. However, the regime attempted to raise the status of women by glorifying motherhood as a patriotic duty. Programs like the Mother's Cross honoured women for bearing multiple children, and motherhood was framed as vital to preserving the Aryan race ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).\n\nEconomic participation showed how different political ideas played out, but it also exposed some clear contradictions between what was said and what actually happened ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Mao actively promoted female labour as part of the broader goal of mass mobilization. Women joined agricultural cooperatives, factories, and communes, with the state providing childcare services to support working mothers. This participation contributed to their visibility and role in national production, though often in lower-paid or less influential positions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Thus, while their economic role expanded, the promise of full equality in status was undermined by persistent gender hierarchies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Nazi Germany, women were initially discouraged from joining the workforce, with policies like the marriage loan program incentivizing them to leave jobs and focus on domestic life. However, the demands of total war created a policy reversal. During World War II, labour shortages forced the Nazi regime to recruit women into industrial and agricultural work, contradicting its earlier ideals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example }$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These developments highlight how both regimes set aside their gender ideals when economic demands took priority, relying on women in practical ways to help achieve state objectives ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$).\n\nState policies in both regimes often imposed a double burden on women, intensifying rather than alleviating their workload. In Maoist China, the communal system was supposed to relieve women of household responsibilities through shared kitchens and nurseries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$). In reality, these facilities were frequently under-resourced, and women were still expected to fulfill domestic duties after hours ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This dual expectation increased their labour burden while failing to elevate their status in the home or workplace ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Similarly, Nazi women were idealized as homemakers and mothers, but wartime demands compelled them to assume additional responsibilities in the workforce. Despite this shift, women were rarely granted recognition or decision-making power, underscoring the limitations of their status in Nazi society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In both regimes, women were expected to contribute extensively to the state while receiving limited personal autonomy or recognition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nPolitical participation offers another point of contrast($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Maoist China encouraged women to take part in political life as part of mass mobilization efforts. Organizations such as the All-China Women's Federation aimed to involve women in policymaking and propaganda work ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$). Some women, like Soong Ching-ling, held prominent symbolic positions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$). Nevertheless, real political power remained overwhelmingly in the hands of male leaders, and female involvement often served more to legitimize party policies than to enable meaningful representation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Nazi Germany, women were explicitly excluded from formal politics. Their participation was confined to groups like the National Socialist Women's League (NSF), which promoted traditional roles and loyalty to the regime ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$). Although women were seen as theoretically equal and essential to the Volksgemeinschaft (people's community), they had no political agency ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In both cases, the promise of status through political involvement was undermined by male-dominated structures and symbolic roles ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nUltimately, both regimes adapted women's roles to serve their political and economic goals, but did so through different ideological lenses that shaped the perceived status of women. Maoist China advanced the idea of gender equality, integrating women into public life and economic production. However, patriarchal norms persisted, and women's actual status, although improved, remained constrained. Nazi Germany, in contrast, upheld traditional gender roles but attempted to enhance the status of women within those roles, particularly through the glorification of motherhood. Yet, when ideological goals conflicted with necessity, both regimes adapted their policies to prioritize state survival ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).  Despite their differences, neither regime sought true empowerment for women; instead, both adapted gender roles to meet the needs of the state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0639","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0639\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about the roles of women in Mao's China and Hitler's Germany.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Introductory note by Mao Zedong to \"Women Have Gone to the Labour Front\" (1955), The Socialist Upsurge in China's Countryside, Chinese ed., Vol. I. Reprinted in 1964 in “Quotations of Chairman Mao”. |\n\n[Source: Mao, Z. (1964, January 1). *Quotations from Mao Tse Tung*, Chapter 31. Marxists.org.  Retrieved March 31 from httpsnolink://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/works/red-book/ch31.htm. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Hitler's Speech to the National Socialist Women's League, September 8, 1934. |\n\n[Source: Hilter, A. (1934, September 8). Hitler’s Speech to the National Socialist Women’s League. German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-speech-to-the-national-socialist-women-s-league-september-8-1934. Public domain.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n$\\\\$\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both Communist China and Nazi Germany saw women as crucial to their national vision.\n    - Source A: Women were needed in the workforce to advance socialism and industrialization.\n    - Source B: Women were expected to focus on homemaking, which was seen as the foundation of the state.\n- Both regimes controlled and dictated women's roles to align with state objectives.\n    - Source A: Women were expected to work and contribute equally to the economy, with policies enforcing their participation.\n    - Source B: Women were encouraged to focus on motherhood and household duties through propaganda and policy.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Equal vs. Complementary.\n    - Source A: Advocated for full gender equality, promoting women as equal workers and contributors.\n    - Source B: Defined men and women as having separate but complementary roles, with men as workers and women as caretakers.\n- Workforce vs. Domesticity.\n    - Source A: Women's liberation was tied to their participation in production and economic growth.\n    - Source B: Women were primarily responsible for maintaining the home and raising children to support the state.\n- Progressive vs. Traditional View of Women's Role.\n    - Source A: Encouraged the transformation of gender roles through socialist policies.\n    - Source B: Reinforced traditional values, discouraging social change in gender roles.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources emphasize the role of women in nation-building ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A states that it is “of the utmost importance to arouse the broad masses of women to join in productive activity,” indicating that women's active participation in economic production was essential to the development of a socialist society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Source B similarly presents women as essential to the nation's success, claiming that “the greater world is built on the foundation of this smaller world,” suggesting that women's domestic role was vital for national stability ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In both cases, the state defines the ideal role of women as contributing to the broader aims of the regime, whether through labor or family life ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, the sources contrast significantly in how they define women's roles. Source A emphasizes equality in public life and labor, asserting that “men and women must receive equal pay for equal work in production” and that “genuine equality between the sexes can only be realized” through socialist transformation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). This reveals Maoist China’s ideological commitment to gender equality in the public and economic spheres. In contrast, Source B reinforces traditional gender roles, asserting that “her world is her husband, her family, her children, and her home” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Rather than advocating equality, it describes gender roles are seen as different but complementary ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0641","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0641\n\nTo what extent does the message of Source B reflect the policies and expectations regarding women in Nazi Germany?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Hitler's Speech to the National Socialist Women's League, September 8, 1934. |\n\n[Source: Hilter, A. (1934, September 8). Hitler’s Speech to the National Socialist Women’s League. German History in Documents and Images. Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-speech-to-the-national-socialist-women-s-league-september-8-1934. Public domain.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- The response uses the source effectively as evidence and refers to it clearly.\n- The response uses accurate and relevant “own knowledge” as evidence to support arguments alongside source knowledge.\nBoth the source and own knowledge must be used. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Effective synthesis of own knowledge and source material to receive the full marks.\n- To receive full marks the response must contain examples that support the policies and claims, as well as examples that do not. These do not need to be evenly balanced.\n\nPossible evidence from Source B:\n- The speech emphasized women's “smaller role”, their primary role being in the home, supporting their husbands and raising the next generation of racially “pure” Germans.\n- It reinforces the idea that men and women have distinct yet complementary roles, with men leading in politics and warfare while women maintain the domestic sphere.\n\nPossible arguments from own knowledge:\n- Elements consistent with Nazi policies toward women:\n    - Nazi policies that supported this view:\n        - The Law for the Encouragement of Marriage (1933).\n        - Kinder, Küche, Kirche (children, kitchen, church)\n        - The Cross of Honour of the German Mother (1938).\n        - Restrictions on Employment: Women were discouraged from pursuing higher education and professional careers, and many female civil servants and teachers were dismissed from their jobs in the early 1930s.\n- Elements inconsistent with Nazi policies toward women (possible own knowledge):\n    -  While the speech promotes a clear vision of domestic roles, Nazi gender policies were inconsistent and adapted to state needs:\n        - During the early years, policies aimed at reducing female employment in favour of men. However, as World War II progressed, women were increasingly recruited into the workforce and auxiliary military roles, contradicting earlier rhetoric.\n        - The speech does not mention that not all women were included in this vision (Jewish, Roma, disabled, and politically dissident women were excluded and often persecuted).\n        - The forced sterilization program under the 1933 Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring contradicted the idea of protecting “all” German women and instead targeted those deemed unfit to reproduce.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- The strongest answers are likely to argue that the Source generally aligns with Nazi policies but is not fully consistent.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of Source B aligns closely with Nazi policies and expectations toward women, particularly in reinforcing traditional gender roles. The source emphasizes that “the woman's is a smaller world” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{source}$) centered around “her husband, her family, her children, and her home” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{source}$), which mirrors the Nazi ideal of Kinder, Küche, Kirche (children, kitchen, church) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). Nazi propaganda glorified motherhood and domesticity, promoting women as the backbone of the Volksgemeinschaft (people's community). For example, policies such as the Law for the Encouragement of Marriage (1933) gave loans to newlywed Aryan couples, with portions forgiven for each child born, incentivising domestic motherhood ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$).\n\nHowever, inconsistencies also emerged, especially during the Second World War. Despite the initial emphasis on domesticity, the regime was forced to mobilize women into the workforce and auxiliary roles due to labour shortages. For example, by 1943, conscription of women into wartime industries had begun, contradicting the earlier vision of the woman's “smaller world” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$). Similarly, the Nazi regime sometimes allowed women to serve in organizations like the NS-Frauenschaft (Nazi Women's League) to spread ideology, showing that the public-private divide was not always rigid in practice ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Source B strongly reflects the ideological stance of the Nazi regime toward women in the 1930s, particularly regarding their domestic roles, practical demands during wartime led to contradictions, showing the regime's policies were not entirely consistent with the idealized vision expressed in the source ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0696","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0696\n\nContrast **two** policies toward women in the German (1933-1939) and Cuban (1959-1975) authoritarian states.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following differences and supporting explanation/evidence:\n- Women in Germany should stay at home, whereas women in Cuba were able to engage in wider social activities.\n    - Germany: KKK (Children, Kitchen and Church) principles.\n    - Cuba: Creation of Federation of Cuban Women (FMC) in 1960, led by revolutionary leader Vilma Espín.\n- Emotional condition of women in Germany and political militancy of women in Cuba.\n    - Germany: Women's specific role in self-sacrifice and suffering in Adolf Hitler's speech before the National Socialist Women's League (September 1934).\n    - Cuba: The Family Code (1975) enshrined the equal civil and political standing of women with respect to men.\n- Reproductive roles of women aligned with the Aryan race in Germany and significant contributions of women to education policies in Cuba.\n    - Germany: Lebensborn program (December 1935) through which SS members were to have children with racially Aryan pure women. Further, the creation of the Cross of Honour of the German Mother (December 1938) incentivised women to have children against rewards in the form of bronze, silver and gold crosses.\n    - Cuba: Women's active role as teachers in Cuba's Literacy Campaign (1961).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAlthough women in both authoritarian states played a significant role in their societies, they were relegated in Germany to childbearing of the Aryan population through propaganda and incentive schemes between 1935 and 1938 ($\\checkmark$). By contrast, women were integral to the Cuban Revolution and maintained an active role in the political realm after Fidel Castro's rise to power in 1959. Led by Vilma Espín, the creation of the Federation of Cuban Women in 1960 further enhanced their status as political equals with men and allowed them to uphold their rights ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, women in Germany were subject to sex-related indoctrination with reference to deeply ingrained roles of housewives and emotional beings responsible for raising children and cooking ($\\checkmark$). For their part, women in Cuba were integrated into social policies by serving as teachers in the nationwide literacy and numeracy campaign in 1961, which paved the way for strengthening the capabilities of youngsters and workers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0698","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0698\n\nWhat, according to Source A, was the relationship between the health of Cubans and the health of the Revolution?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech by Ernesto $\\lq$Che' Guevara on the importance of medicine to the Cuban Revolution, 1960. |\n\n:box[You must have seen children [in the Sierra Maestra] whose physical constitutions appeared to be those of eight or nine-year-olds, yet almost all of whom are thirteen or fourteen. They are the most authentic children of the Sierra Maestra, the most authentic offspring of hunger and misery. They are the creatures of malnutrition.\\\n\\\nIn this tiny Cuba, with its four or five television channels and hundred of radio stations, with all the advances of modern science, when those children arrived at school for the first time at night and saw the electric light bulbs, they exclaimed that the stars were very low that night. And those children, some of whom you must have seen, are learning in collective schools skills ranging from reading to trades, and even the very difficult science of becoming revolutionaries.\\\n\\\nThose are the new humans being born in Cuba. They are being born in isolated areas, in different parts of the Sierra Maestra, and also in the cooperatives and work centres. All this has a lot to do with the theme of our talk today, the integration of the physician or any other medical worker, into the revolutionary movement. The task of educating and feeding youngsters, the task of educating the army, the task of distributing the lands of the former absentee landlords to those who laboured every day upon that same land without receiving its benefits, are accomplishments of social medicine which have been performed in Cuba.\\\n\\\nThe principle upon which the fight against disease should be based is the creation of a robust body; but not the creation of a robust body by the artistic work of a doctor upon a weak organism; rather, the creation of a robust body with the work of the whole collectivity, upon the entire social collectivity.\\\n]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Guevara, E. (1960, August 19). *On Revolutionary Medicine*. Translated by Beth Kurti. Che Guevara Internet Archive (marxists.org), 1999. Retrieved April 7, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/guevara/1960/08/19.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Contrast with the lack of policies of pre-Castro regimes.\n- Re-interpretation of nationalist ideological underpinnings with reference to Castro's Five Revolutionary Laws.\n- Examination of the health conditions of Cubans in certain regions of the country.\n- Analysis of health in the context of wider social policies of Castro's emerging regime.\n- Interpretation of the reference to “social medicine” in light of the pillars of Cubania and national unity.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source A, medicine in the Cuban Revolution addressed the disregard for social inequality of previous regimes, particularly in marginalised localities in the southeastern region such as Sierra Maestra ($\\checkmark$). Also, medicine in the Cuban Revolution tackled the malnutrition condition of youngsters in a well-rounded manner by paving the way for socioeconomic conditions for access to food and placing health at the centre of policymaking ($\\checkmark$). Finally, health-related measures integrated into wider social policies linked to the Cuban Revolution, such as education and land distribution, constituted the guiding principle of social medicine ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0699","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0699\n\nExplain what is meant by the claim that “the fight against disease should be based [on the principle of] the creation of a robust body” in Source A.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech by Ernesto $\\lq$Che' Guevara on the importance of medicine to the Cuban Revolution, 1960. |\n\n:box[You must have seen children [in the Sierra Maestra] whose physical constitutions appeared to be those of eight or nine-year-olds, yet almost all of whom are thirteen or fourteen. They are the most authentic children of the Sierra Maestra, the most authentic offspring of hunger and misery. They are the creatures of malnutrition.\\\n\\\nIn this tiny Cuba, with its four or five television channels and hundred of radio stations, with all the advances of modern science, when those children arrived at school for the first time at night and saw the electric light bulbs, they exclaimed that the stars were very low that night. And those children, some of whom you must have seen, are learning in collective schools skills ranging from reading to trades, and even the very difficult science of becoming revolutionaries.\\\n\\\nThose are the new humans being born in Cuba. They are being born in isolated areas, in different parts of the Sierra Maestra, and also in the cooperatives and work centres. All this has a lot to do with the theme of our talk today, the integration of the physician or any other medical worker, into the revolutionary movement. The task of educating and feeding youngsters, the task of educating the army, the task of distributing the lands of the former absentee landlords to those who laboured every day upon that same land without receiving its benefits, are accomplishments of social medicine which have been performed in Cuba.\\\n\\\nThe principle upon which the fight against disease should be based is the creation of a robust body; but not the creation of a robust body by the artistic work of a doctor upon a weak organism; rather, the creation of a robust body with the work of the whole collectivity, upon the entire social collectivity.\\\n]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Guevara, E. (1960, August 19). *On Revolutionary Medicine*. Translated by Beth Kurti. Che Guevara Internet Archive (marxists.org), 1999. Retrieved April 7, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/guevara/1960/08/19.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny of the following explanations with supporting reasoning and synthesis of **two** source material inputs:\n- Analysis of the socioeconomic causes of the Cuban Revolution.\n    - “You must have seen children [in the Sierra Maestra] whose physical constitutions appeared to be those of eight or nine-year-olds, yet almost all of whom are thirteen or fourteen.”\n    - “They are the most authentic children of the Sierra Maestra, the most authentic offspring of hunger and misery.”\n- Connections between the M-26-7 guerrilla’s calls for equality and the policies implemented by Fidel Castro between 1959 and 1960.\n    - “And those children, some of whom you must have seen, are learning in collective schools skills ranging from reading to trades, and even the very difficult science of becoming revolutionaries.”\n- Revolutionary objectives in common of Fidel Castro and Ernesto Guevara regardless of their different ideological stances.\n    - “The task of educating and feeding youngsters, the task of educating the army, the task of distributing the lands of the former absentee landlords to those who laboured every day upon that same land without receiving its benefits (...)”\n    - “(...) the creation of a robust body with the work of the whole collectivity, upon the entire social collectivity.”\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe reference to disease employed by Ernesto “Che” Guevara reflects the firm commitment of Fidel Castro and his fellow Cuban Revolution militants to bringing about profound changes in the country ($\\checkmark$). In the context of the source, the wide range of socioeconomic inequalities faced by marginalised communities in the southeastern region is mainly attributed to the lack of opportunities for integrated human development during previous regimes ($\\checkmark$). For example, malnutrition due to the difficulties in access to food and an illiterate workforce speaks to the need for well-rounded policies built on health, education and land distribution ($\\checkmark$). The latter, coupled with a nationalist call for national unity and self-sufficiency for the common good, would laid solid groundwork for the solution of protracted socioeconomic issues and the building of a strong Cuban society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1046","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1046\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of domestic economic policies in solving the problems faced by **two** authoritarian states, each from a different region.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance for responses\n- Candidates should examine the domestic economic policies of at least two authoritarian states from different regions (e.g., Stalin, Mao, Hitler, Mussolini, Castro, etc.).\n- Responses must identify the problems these regimes faced (e.g., economic backwardness, unemployment, dependence on foreign powers, inflation, agrarian underdevelopment) and analyse how far economic policies solved them.\n- Strong responses will distinguish between aims, short-term successes, long-term results, and unintended consequences.\n- Evaluation should consider both effectiveness in solving immediate problems and whether policies supported regime survival.\n- A substantiated conclusion should directly answer “to what extent” economic policies were effective.\n\n**Indicative Content:**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Key Problems Faced}}$\n- Stalin (USSR): Backward, agrarian economy; fear of foreign invasion; need for rapid industrialisation.\n- Mao (China): Desire to modernise and catch up with the USSR; rural underdevelopment; eliminate landlordism.\n- Hitler (Germany): Mass unemployment from Great Depression; resentment of Versailles reparations; desire for autarky.\n- Mussolini (Italy): Postwar economic instability, inflation, unemployment; goal of “corporatist state”; need for prestige.\n- Castro (Cuba): Dependence on U.S. sugar exports; economic inequality; underdevelopment.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Policies and Their Effectiveness}}$\n\nStalin\n- Five-Year Plans: Rapid industrialisation, major growth in heavy industry (steel, coal, oil). By late 1930s, USSR became a major industrial power.\n- Collectivisation: Aimed to modernise agriculture and feed cities, but caused famine (1932-33) and millions of deaths; nevertheless, it secured state control over agriculture.\n -Overall: Effective in building industry and military strength, less so in improving living standards or agricultural efficiency.\n\nMao\n- First Five-Year Plan (1953-57): Soviet-style industrialisation with some success in steel, coal, and electricity.\n- Great Leap Forward (1958-62): Attempted to combine industrialisation with communes; disastrous famine caused 30-40 million deaths.\n- Overall: Mixed; initial industrial gains undermined by catastrophic agricultural failure.\n\nHitler\n- Public works (autobahns), rearmament, and conscription cut unemployment dramatically.\n- Four-Year Plan (1936): Autarky partially achieved; successes in synthetic fuel/rubber but Germany remained dependent on imports.\n- Overall: Effective in solving unemployment and preparing for war, but long-term sustainability is questionable.\n\nMussolini\n- “Battle for Grain” boosted wheat production, reducing imports but harming other crops.\n- Public works and corporatism stabilised the economy but failed to create real growth.\n- “Battle for the Lira” (1926) overvalued currency, harming exports.\n- Overall: Limited success; prestige more than economic transformation.\n\nCastro\n- Agrarian Reform (1959): Land redistribution aimed at reducing inequality, initially popular but reduced efficiency.\n- Nationalisation of industries and reliance on Soviet aid stabilised the economy but increased dependence.\n- Sugar harvest targets (1970 “Ten Million Ton Harvest”) failed.\n- Overall: Some gains in literacy, healthcare, and social equality, but long-term economic weakness persisted.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Possible Arguments for Agreement (economic policies were effective)}}$\n- Hitler: solved unemployment and restored national pride.\n- Stalin: turned the USSR into an industrial power capable of defeating Nazi Germany.\n- Mao (early years): some success with First Five-Year Plan.\n- Castro: improved social equality and services despite weak growth.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Possible Arguments for Disagreement (economic policies were ineffective)}}$\n- Stalin: collectivisation was disastrous for agriculture.\n- Mao: The Great Leap Forward was a catastrophic failure.\n- Mussolini: policies prioritised image over substance.\n- Castro: dependence on the USSR left the economy vulnerable.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAuthoritarian leaders often presented economic transformation as essential for consolidating power and proving their regimes' legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Italy, Benito Mussolini aimed to stabilise the fragile post-First World War economy and build prestige for his Fascist regime. In China, Mao Zedong sought to modernise a backward agrarian society and construct a socialist economy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Both leaders implemented ambitious policies framed in ideological or nationalist terms, but while Mussolini achieved little beyond propaganda victories, Mao combined limited industrial progress with disastrous agricultural failures ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nAgricultural policy was central to both regimes, but exposed their weaknesses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Mussolini's Battle for Grain (1925) sought to make Italy self-sufficient by raising wheat production. Wheat yields rose, and imports declined, allowing Mussolini to boast of success ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Yet the policy distorted farming: land was shifted away from traditional crops like olives and citrus, damaging exports and worsening Italy's trade balance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Mao initially implemented land reforms that redistributed land from landlords to peasants, undermining old elites and winning rural support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).However, this was followed by collectivisation, culminating in the establishment of People's Communes during the Great Leap Forward (1958-62). These aimed to boost efficiency but instead disrupted farming practices, reduced incentives, and contributed to a catastrophic famine that killed tens of millions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). While Mussolini exaggerated modest gains for propaganda, Mao's agricultural policy was far more transformative and ultimately disastrous ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIndustrialisation was another key area where both regimes sought legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Mussolini promised to modernise Italy through corporatism and public works, but results were limited. The “corporate state” largely functioned as a mechanism for state control rather than genuine collaboration between employers and workers, and industrial growth remained modest compared with other European states ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Public works projects such as draining the Pontine Marshes were more symbolic than economically transformative ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Mao, by contrast, pursued Soviet-style industrialisation with his First Five-Year Plan (1953-57), which achieved significant gains in coal, steel, and electricity output ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This represented genuine progress in modernising China's economy. Yet his subsequent Great Leap Forward aimed to accelerate development through backyard steel furnaces and mass mobilisation. Steel production figures were inflated, quality was poor, and agricultural collapse undermined the entire policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In industrialisation, Mussolini's efforts were shallow and prestige-driven, while Mao's were initially successful but collapsed under ideological excess ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nPropaganda and ideology deeply shaped both leaders' economic approaches ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).  Mussolini framed his policies as “battles”: the Battle for Grain, the Battle for Land, the Battle for the Lira, which projected him as a decisive leader transforming Italy's fortunes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Yet these campaigns were more about image than results. The Battle for the Lira (1926), which revalued the currency, created short-term prestige but damaged exports and slowed growth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Mao's economic policies were also steeped in ideology: the Great Leap Forward reflected his belief in mass mobilisation and revolutionary willpower overcoming material constraints ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).Propaganda, such as giant rallies, posters, and the cult of Mao as “Great Helmsman,” pushed unrealistic quotas and silenced dissenting voices ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In both cases, propaganda served to mask economic failures, but while Mussolini's campaigns inflated minor achievements, Mao's propaganda helped drive catastrophic mismanagement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the economic policies of Mussolini and Mao reveal both the ambitions and failures of authoritarian economic management ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$). Mussolini's “battles” were superficial and prestige-driven, leaving Italy economically weak and unprepared for war, contributing directly to the regime's collapse in 1943. Mao achieved limited industrial progress in the First Five-Year Plan, but the catastrophic famine of the Great Leap Forward exposed the dangers of ideology-driven economics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Neither leader solved their countries' fundamental problems, but authoritarian control allowed them to claim success and, in Mao's case, maintain political power despite devastating failure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1141","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1141\n\nExplain **three** aims of Castro's domestic economic policies.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanations:\n- To secure economic independence for Cuba: Castro wanted Cuba to be free of foreign influence, especially from the USA, so he aimed to nationalise key Cuban industries like sugar production.\n- To develop a centrally planned economy: Castro wanted his government to control the economic decisions to ensure they served national interests and the Cuban people. They aimed to establish agriculture under state or cooperative ownership.\n- To promote rapid economic growth: Castro wanted the economy to be more modernised and industrialized, so he invested money in manufacturing and technology. This would help to improve productivity in agriculture and sugar exports.\n- To improve the standard of living of all Cubans: Implemented policies of land reform, redistribution of wealth, and access to free healthcare and education for all.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of the most important aims of Castro's economic policies was to secure economic independence for Cuba ($\\checkmark$). In order to be independent Castro wanted Cuba to be free of foreign influence, especially from the USA and so he aimed to nationalise key Cuban industries like sugar production ($\\checkmark$). He aimed to improve the standard of living of all Cubans to remove the inequalities that had existed under the Batista regime ($\\checkmark$). To do this, he implemented land reform policies and redistribution of wealth, and aimed to ensure access to free healthcare and education for all Cubans ($\\checkmark$). A further aim was to drive rapid economic growth ($\\checkmark$). As such, Castro invested in manufacturing and technology to improve agricultural and sugar production, which was Cuba's primary source of wealth ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1142","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1142\n\nOutline **three** short-and long-term impacts of Castro's nationalisation policy for foreign investors.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following, including at least one short-term and one long-term impact:\n- **Short-term impacts:**\n    - Castro took control of foreign owned businesses and property by passing laws that took away the legal rights of foreigners. They could no longer benefit from their assets in Cuba. Affected land, banks, oil refineries, commercial enterprises.\n    - This caused a diplomatic crisis especially with the USA and an embargo on the import of sugar from Cuba.\n- **Long-term impacts:**\n    - Deterred any new foreign investment in Cuba - If no legal protection of assets existed investment was considered too high risk. Led to Cuba becoming isolated\n    - Loss of foreign investment and expertise.\n    - Economic sanctions imposed by the USA also discouraged other International investors as they feared loss of US support and markets.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne short-term impact of Castro's policy of nationalisation on foreign investors was that American and European businesses no longer had any legal rights in Cuba and could no longer benefit from their assets ($\\checkmark$). This led to an immediate diplomatic crisis, especially in the USA, and they placed an embargo on the import of sugar from Cuba ($\\checkmark$). In the long term, this led to economic isolation as it deterred any new foreign investments in Cuba, as there was no legal protection for their assets ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1143","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1143\n\nDescribe **two** social policies implemented by Fidel Castro to improve the rights of women in Cuba.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Federation of Cuban Women (1960):The main organisation for promoting women's rights, gender equality, integrating women into the workforce, expanding educational opportunities, and promoting legal reforms for equality.\n- Maternity Law of 1963:Ensured that women were paid maternity leave and their jobs were protected during maternity leave.\n- National Literacy Campaign 1961: Although not exclusively a women's policy, this campaign significantly helped women overcome illiteracy and participate more fully in education and skilled work.\n- Free and Universal Healthcare Policies: Included reproductive health rights such as contraception and abortion services, crucial for women's autonomy and health.\n- The Family Code (1975): A legal code that covers marriage, divorce, paternity, adoption and parental responsibilities. It suggested that men and women were to take equal responsibility for the upbringing of children and domestic labour.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne social policy that was introduced to improve the rights of women in Cuba was the establishment of the Federation of Cuban Women in 1960. The organisation was set up to promote women's rights and gender equality. Castro believed in the importance of integrating women into the workforce, expanding their educational opportunities, and promoting legal reforms for equality ($\\checkmark$). The Maternity Law of 1963 helped women retain equality in the workforce as it ensured that they were paid maternity leave and their jobs were protected during maternity leave ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1144","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1144\n\nIdentify **two** economic problems Cuba faced by 1962.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Industrialisation had not been as effective as hoped: Factories had been built to produce goods, but many could have been imported more cheaply, so industrial production was inefficient.\n- In the countryside, peasants resisted cooperative farming. Farmers were forced to sell their products to the state at low prices and thus had little incentive to produce a surplus.\n- Lack of support from the middle class: policies that promoted equality between the classes led to many people with specialised skills leaving the workforce, which had a negative impact on Castro's economic plans.\n- By 1962 industrial and agricultural production was not as successful as Castro had aimed for, leaving Cuba in an economic crisis and the nation suffering from food shortages.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBy 1962, Castro realised that some of his plans for improving the Cuban economy had not been as effective as hoped. Industrialization had brought about change, but the cost of building factories outweighed the cost of importing certain products from abroad ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the redistribution of land had caused dissatisfaction amongst the middle classes as it did not economically benefit them, and this led to approximately 250,000 Cubans leaving the island. This resulted in a reduction in knowledge and specialised skills in the workforce ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1145","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1145\n\nExplain why healthcare policies were a priority for Castro's government.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Supported policies of social justice and addressed inequality: Good healthcare and knowledge of basic hygiene were important to improve health issues in poorer/rural communities that previously had no access to healthcare.\n- Comprehensive health care was made a part of every citizen's constitutional rights in 1979, so the government was expected to provide free health services, including preventative medical care.\n- Improving public health: They were committed from the beginning to helping improve people's living standards, including vaccination campaigns and controlling infectious diseases.\n- Political reasons: Delivering high-quality healthcare demonstrated the benefits of socialism and helped to maintain popular support.\n- A healthy population was seen as essential for Cuba's economic development goals.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCastro's government was focused on removing social injustices and making healthcare accessible and free for all Cubans as a way of addressing some of the social inequalities in society ($\\checkmark$). A healthy population was seen as essential for Cuba's economic development goals, as economic reforms and policies placed high importance on the rural workforce to aid Cuba's economic recovery ($\\checkmark$). Politically, it was also a means of ensuring popular support as success in health care was equated with the success of Cuba's socialist system ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1146","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1146\n\nExplain the significance of Castro's cultural policies for authoritarian control.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Cultural change had to happen alongside social and economic change to build his idea of a new society.\n- The 1961 speech \"Words to the Intellectuals\" set the tone for cultural policy, supporting artistic freedom within revolutionary boundaries. Saw culture as integral to national identity, education and social development\n- Access for all - Castro supported the arts through education and trained art teachers nationwide to promote interest and participation in the arts.\n- Founded new institutions (1959) to support the arts - the Cuban Institute of Cinematographic Art and Industry (ICAIC), the National Theater, National Symphony Orchestra, and Casa de las Américas to promote cultural unity and cooperation among Latin American and Caribbean countries.\n- Rejected Soviet-style Socialist Realism censorship in favour of a more open, diverse artistic environment.\n- Castro also maintained control over cultural expression and did not allow it to become a platform for political dissent.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFor Castro to achieve his ideals of a new society, the role of culture was a crucial factor; he believed that cultural change had to occur alongside social and economic change for his vision to be realized ($\\checkmark$). He was keen to support the arts and founded new institutions in 1959 to support them. He established the Cuban Institute of Cinematographic Art and Industry (ICAIC), the National Theater, National Symphony Orchestra, and Casa de las Américas to promote cultural unity and cooperation among Latin American and Caribbean countries ($\\checkmark$). Castro was aware of how the arts could both promote and undermine his rule, and so maintained careful control over cultural expression to prevent it from becoming a platform for political dissent ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1147","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1147\n\nDescribe **three** aims of Fidel Castro's Agrarian Reform Act of May 1959.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To redistribute land to peasants and have more equality in land ownership\n- To increase the living standards of the rural poor - land ownership titles meant that they no longer had to pay rent\n- To promote further agricultural development - the act encourages cooperation, and intensive production to increase productivity.\n- Encouraging growth of the agriculture industry to reduce Cuba's reliance on sugar production\n- To increase the government's control over resources  - the law banned foreign ownership of rural property.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Agrarian Reform Act of 1959 was implemented to improve welfare in Cuba. Land redistribution was a key factor of the reform act and this gave the peasants greater equality in society ($\\checkmark$). Peasants were no longer expected to pay rent on land so it also improved the standard of living for many of the rural poor in Cuba ($\\checkmark$). Agricultural development was prioritised and working cooperatively in order to achieve intense production and increased productivity which in turn was also linked to an increase in living standards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1148","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1148\n\nDescribe **two** aims of Fidel Castro's policies toward racial minorities in Cuba after 1959.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Aimed to reduce the social and economic gaps between racial groups\n- Castro aimed to extend his Universal free education policy to include all minorities. Increased literacy helped people gain employment.\n- He aimed to outlaw Racial discrimination in public places, education, and employment, promoting equal access to jobs and training for all races.\n- He aimed to improve healthcare in marginalized communities.\n- Aimed to promote equality in social programs, including housing and employment benefits for previously excluded groups.\n- He aimed to increase the number of $\\lq$non-whites' in positions of power, including government jobs.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe situation for racial minorities improved under Castro as he aimed for social equality and economic parity in Cuba. His educational policies ensured that minority groups had access to education, which helped increase literacy rates and provide people with more job opportunities ($\\checkmark$). Jobs that had previously been deemed inaccessible to minority groups were now open, including jobs in the government and positions of power. This gave minority groups more political power and increased their awareness of their economic situation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1149","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1149\n\nDescribe the aims of Castro's $\\lq$Proclamation against discrimination' in 1959.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nInclude **both** of the following points:\n- A policy aimed at reducing inequality between racial groups in Cuba.\n- A policy to highlight the success of his new socialist state, where all races were included in his reforms.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCastro's $\\lq$Proclamation against discrimination' in 1959 was aimed at reducing the social and economic gaps between racial groups. Because inequality contradicted his vision of a successful socialist state, he ensured that all races were included in his reforms. The inclusion of minorities also helped limit opposition to his rule ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1151","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1151\n\nAccording to Source B, how did the United States economically dominate Cuba?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**  | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the article looking at the economic relationship between the United States and Cuba prior to the Cuban Revolution. |\n\n[Source: Thomas, H. (1978, November).*The U.S. And Castro, 1959-1962.* American Heritage. Retrieved October 28, 2025 from https:www.americanheritage.com/us-and-castro-1959-1962. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- 60% of Cuban sugar production was controlled by the US\n- American companies controlled public utilities such as electricity and telephones\n- U.S. companies had large landholdings in Cuba\n- Cuban tobacco was marketed through U.S. merchants\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, the United States economically dominated Cuba as it controlled 60% of its sugar production  ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, American companies also controlled many of Cuba's public utilities, such as the electricity and phone companies ($\\checkmark$). Finally, US companies also had large landholdings in Cuba, which gave them access to resources and greater economic control ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1152","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1152\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the benefits Cuba received from its relationship with the United States?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the article looking at the economic relationship between the United States and Cuba prior to the Cuban Revolution. |\n\n[Source: Thomas, H. (1978, November).*The U.S. And Castro, 1959-1962.* American Heritage. Retrieved October 28, 2025 from https:www.americanheritage.com/us-and-castro-1959-1962. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Cuba gained freedom from Spain sooner due to American intervention in 1898\n- The Cuban standard of living rose to half that of the United States by 1925\n- Yellow fever was conquered through work of U.S. military doctors\n- A railway was built along the length of the island by a U.S. company\n- Upper-class Cuban children could be educated in the United States\n- American investments transformed the Cuban economy\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, American investments transformed the Cuban economy. Cuba benefited from a rise in the standard of living to half that of the United States by 1925 ($\\checkmark$). A second benefit was to the infrastructure of Cuba, as a US company built a railway along the length of the island ($\\checkmark$). Finall,y the links between the two countries meant that the children of upper-class Cubans could be educated in the US ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1153","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1153\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for historians studying the causes of conflict between Cuba and the United States in 1959-1960\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A**| $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from an interview by Fidel Castro, in 1959, discussing early revolutionary reforms and U.S. opposition. |\n\n[Source: Porter, C. O. (1963). *An interview with Fidel Castro.* Retrieved October 28, 2025 from chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/ https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP75-00149R000600370005-4.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The source is an extract from a speech by Fidel Castro, who played a key role in the politics of this time and had direct knowledge of the motivations behind Cuban policies\n- The source provides insight into events from a Cuban leadership perspective and explains the Cuban revolution to a wider audience.\n- The source allows Castro to justify his actions, especially the confiscation of US assets, as a defensive measure, suggesting that the cause of the conflict is due to aggressive action from the US.\n- It gives specific examples of reforms that were implemented and the US response to these, which led to the confiscation of US assets.\n- Identifies the actions of the US, for example, the economic blockade, which highlight the reasons for Castro's response.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- The author of the source is Castro, and so offers only a Cuban perspective of events.\n- Castro is using the speech to justify the increasing conflict with the US, defend his actions and policies, and gain sympathy for his cause.\n- The source looks to portray Cuba as the victim and the US as the aggressor in these events - the aim is to elicit sympathy from the public, and so it oversimplifies the situation.\n- IT makes no mention of US concerns about the influence of communism, which they saw as a legitimate cause for intervention as well as the talk of an alliance with the USSR\n- The source frames the causes as being unavoidable actions rather than ideological choices.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is an extract from a speech by Fidel Castro, who played a pivotal role in the politics of this era, so its value lies in the fact that he had direct knowledge of the motivations behind Cuban policies of that time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  The limitations of the origin are that the source is written by Castro, so it only offers the reader a Cuban perspective of events ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose of the source is a value as it allows one to understand Castro's justification for his actions, especially the confiscation of US assets. It suggests that they are a defensive measure and that the cause of the conflict is due to aggressive action from the US ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).  The limitation of the source's content is that the justifications provided as causes do not mention US concerns about the influence of communism, which they saw as a legitimate cause for intervention. This omission limits the greater context for the conflict between the US and Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1154","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1154\n\nBriefly compare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about the impacts of the relationship that existed between Cuba and the USA.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from an interview by Fidel Castro, in 1959, discussing early revolutionary reforms and U.S. opposition. |\n\n[Source: Porter, C. O. (1963). *An interview with Fidel Castro.* Retrieved October 28, 2025 from chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/ https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP75-00149R000600370005-4.pdf. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the article looking at the economic relationship between the United States and Cuba prior to the Cuban Revolution. |\n\n[Source: Thomas, H. (1978, November).*The U.S. And Castro, 1959-1962.* American Heritage. Retrieved October 28, 2025 from https:www.americanheritage.com/us-and-castro-1959-1962. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- To achieve full marks, the answer must include both a point of comparison (similarity) and a point of contrast (difference).\n- A minimum of **two** clear, valid arguments (with at least one similarity and one difference) along with supporting evidence should be given to achieve the full **four** marks.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both Source A and B highlight the history of the US presence in Cuba\n    - Source A shows that they held established economic interests in Cuba as the revolution now planned “to confiscate American enterprises.” in Cuba\n    - Source B states that “Had it not been for American intervention in 1898, Cuba would not so soon have become free from Spain”\n- Both sources show that the role of sugar production was a key economic factor in the relationship between the US and Cuba\n    - Source A suggests that by “the cutting of the sugar quota ”America was behaving aggressively and trying to control Cuba\n    - Source B shows that “Investments in sugar mills gave U.S. companies control of 60 per cent of Cuban sugar production by the 1920's.” and were crucial to the US economic position in Cuba\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- The sources disagree on the impact of the relationship between the US and Cuba\n    - Source A states that this accelerated the revolution and describes the US as  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the enemy of the peoples\" and \"an ally of military, reactionary organizations\"\n    - Source B states that the relationship is positive and modernised Cuba  - $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The building by a U.S. company of a railway along the length of the island was an achievement from which Cuba will always benefit\"\n\n- Source A and B disagree on the impact of the economic involvement of the US in Cuba\n    - Source A  shows that Castro saw the US economic strategies as exploitative and this justified the revolution - “So all those aggressions accelerated the revolutionary process.”\n    - Source B states that US investments in Cuba were positive as they “transformed the Cuban economy and gave it the shape that it now has” and downplayed any complaints that Cuba had.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources A and B focus on the role that sugar production played on Cuba's economy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A suggests that the US, by $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the cutting of the sugar quota”, was trying to use its economic power to control Cuba. Source B shows the importance of sugar by stating that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$investments in sugar mills gave U.S. companies control of 60 per cent of Cuban sugar production by the 1920s” and were crucial to the US economic position in Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the two sources differ in their perspectives regarding the impact of the relationship between the US and Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A states that this relationship was negative and accelerated the revolution and describes the US as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the enemy of the peoples\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$an ally of military, reactionary organizations.\" Source B states that the relationship is positive and modernised Cuba, saying that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the building by a U.S. company of a railway along the length of the island was an achievement from which Cuba will always benefit\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI1155","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1155\n\nWhat does source C suggest about the US perspective on its assets in Cuba in 1960?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cartoon by Jim Berryman published November 3, 1960 titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In English or Spanish, It Means What It Says.\" |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Aims%20%26%20Results%20of%20Policies/images/HI1155.png\" alt=\"HI1155\">\n[Source: Berryman. J. (1960). *In English or Spanish, It Means What It Says.* Retrieved November 5, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/5743286. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The source shows that the US is determined to protect its strategic position in Cuba - a clear message in both the caption and the sign on the ship to keep its hands off US property.\n- The cartoon highlights the significance of this area as a strategic location, marked by the label $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$GUANTANAMO\" directly beneath the ship in the water.\n- The cartoon also highlights the military strength of the US during this period of increasing tensions. This is evident in the size of the US ship and the fact that Castro appears frustrated when confronted with the instructions on the ship.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C is showing that the US is determined to protect its strategic interests in Cuba. This is seen in the cartoon by the clear message in both English and Spanish of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hands off” and supported by the caption which says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In English or Spanish, It Means What It Says\" ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe cartoon also highlights the military strength of the US during this period of increasing tensions. This is shown by the size of the US ship and the fact that Castro looks frustrated when confronted with the instructions on the ship ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1244","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1244\n\nTo what extent did the social policies of 20th-century authoritarian states have a significant impact? Discuss with reference to **two** states, each chosen from a different region.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n\n**USSR - Lenin (1917-1924), Power Struggle Era (1924-1928), and Stalin (1918-1953)**\n\nWomen and Family\n- Under Lenin, the Communist Party attempted to liberate women from domestic and patriarchal structures. The 1917 Decree on Civil Marriage, Children and Registry Books made divorce easier to obtain, while abortion was legalised in 1920. The Zhenotdel worked to promote literacy, workplace participation, and social equality for women. However, these reforms remained limited in practice, especially in rural areas.\n- During the Power Struggle era, early Bolshevik gender reforms remained in place. State provision of childcare, canteens, and social support expanded slowly, and this period represented the last phase of genuine Soviet gender liberalism before later Stalinist reversals. However, funding remained inconsistent and many women continued to shoulder traditional domestic responsibilities.\n- Under Stalin, social policy shifted sharply. The 1936 Family Law made divorce more difficult and abortion illegal (except for medical necessity). Traditional family roles were promoted, and pro-natalist measures, including maternal awards such as the “Mother Heroine”, encouraged large families. As a result, earlier gains in female emancipation were substantially rolled back.\n\nEducation\n- Under Lenin, spreading literacy was a central priority. The 1919 Decree on the Liquidation of Illiteracy initiated mass literacy campaigns, and free schooling expanded dramatically. Adult programmes (likbez) educated millions, laying the foundation for a more educated workforce.\n- During the Power Struggle era, literacy campaigns continued, and unified labour schools expanded across the country. Polytechnic education was encouraged, and the state began to standardise curricula. These developments prepared the ground for later centralised educational structures.\n- Under Stalin, education became more formalised and disciplined. Ten years of compulsory schooling were introduced; curricula emphasised science, engineering, and Russian history. However, fees were imposed on upper secondary and university education from 1940, limiting access for poorer students and contributing to the formation of a privileged elite.\n\nReligion\n- Under Lenin, the Communist Party promoted state atheism and sought to undermine the Russian Orthodox Church. The 1918 Decree on the Separation of Church and State stripped the Church of privileges, and church property was confiscated during the Civil War. Religious persecution intensified during famine relief campaigns, although suppression was uneven across regions.\n- During the Power Struggle era, anti-religious activity increased. The League of Militant Atheists, founded in 1925, expanded atheist propaganda and supported the continued closure of churches. Urban religious institutions were more easily dismantled, but rural areas often resisted these measures.\n- Under Stalin, the campaign against religion deepened. The destruction of rural churches frequently provoked unrest, while urban churches were closed with little resistance. Only during the Second World War were restrictions relaxed slightly, allowing some churches to reopen for morale. Overall, religious life was severely restricted.\n\nLanguage / Culture / Nationality Policy\n- Under Lenin, the government introduced korenizatsiya (“indigenisation”) to encourage minority languages and cultures. Minority-language schools, theatres, and publications expanded, and local elites were promoted to reduce ethnic resistance.\n- During the Power Struggle era, korenizatsiya reached its peak. Minority-language education continued, and cultural institutions flourished. Although tensions emerged between centralising pressures and local autonomy, no mass deportations occurred, and cultural diversity remained relatively protected.\n- Under Stalin, these policies were reversed. Russian culture and language were promoted as dominant, and Russian became mandatory in non-Russian schools. Entire minority groups, such as Chechens, Crimean Tatars, and Volga Germans, were deported from their homes during the war. Despite these pressures, minority identities persisted, especially in rural areas.\n\n**China - Mao (1949-1976):**\n- Women and family: To counter the Chinese male-dominated society, the Communist Party of China (CCP) advocated for female equality. In 1950, Mao introduced the New Marriage Law, which banned arranged marriages and entitled women to divorce. However, in the Muslim western provinces, female members were still subject to the orders of husbands, fathers and brothers.\n- Religion: The CCP sought to replace religious worship with loyalty to the Communist Party and the state. Buddhism, Confucianism, and major foreign religions were forbidden from being openly practised. However, in 1976, the Muslim proportion still formed a large minority of the Xinjiang population.\n- Health: The CCP promoted modern medicine and disease prevention in rural areas. The Rural Co-operative Medical Scheme was established, which increased the number of “barefoot doctors” and built township health centers and county hospitals. Mao wanted a basic literacy standard for peasants and workers.\n- Education: Basic education was provided to all peasant families up to the primary level, with a heavy emphasis on engineering and technical training to support industrialisation. However, Mao did not allow higher education institutions to flourish.\n\n**Germany - Hitler (1933-1945):**\n- Women and family: The Nazis were determined to boost the birth rate, so they wanted women to embrace motherhood. Fewer women were allocated university places, and fewer professions offered positions for women. However, in 1942, the pressure of war obliged the Nazis to demand that women go into the factories. The Nazis wanted to get rid of the mentally and physically disabled. In 1939, they initiated the programme of $\\lq$mercy killing'. Children with severe disabilities were murdered by starvation or lethal injections. However, public rumours and Catholic opposition led Hitler to halt the programme in 1941.\n- Religion: Hitler wanted to replace Christianity with a faith reflecting Nazi values. He united all the Protestant Churches together into one official Reich Church under a pro-Nazi Reich Bishop. However, many Protestant pastors formed an opposition group, the Confessional Church.\n- Culture: Hitler promoted the concept of *Volksgemeinschaft*, a new collective national identity. The concept of $\\lq$Blood and Soil' portrayed the peasantry as the nation's most racially pure element and as the symbol of traditional German values. However, it failed to seduce the working class from their established identity.\n\n**Tanzania - Nyerere (1961-1985):**\n- Health: Nyerere aimed to make health a basic right for all Tanzanians. He invested public money in health programmes that trained doctors, built health centres, and vaccinated children against various diseases.\n- Education: Nyerere's education policy aimed to increase the literacy rate. He significantly expanded school provision for children, established teacher training colleges, and founded the Institute of Curriculum Development. However, the problem of overly large class sizes in schools persisted.\n- Women and family: Nyerere strongly advocated for women's full equality. He took a series of legal initiatives that required women to receive equal pay, outlawed discrimination against women in employment, and granted women equal rights to stand for parliament. However, traditional male dominance was still ingrained in Tanzanian society.\n- Culture/community: Nyerere promoted *Ujamaa*, a co-operative, communalist approach to the development of Tanzania. The programme of “villagization,” which grouped peasants into collective farms, led to a significant drop in agricultural production. However, the programme provided free, compulsory education in every village.\n\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCommunist social policies in the Soviet Union under Lenin and Stalin and in China under Mao had mixed and uneven impacts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While Lenin's reforms significantly transformed society, particularly in advancing women's rights and literacy, Stalin's policies reversed or limited many of these gains ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Mao's reforms in China similarly produced partial change, as progressive laws often failed to eliminate traditional hierarchies or create full equality in practice ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Overall, while Communist social policies under these authoritarian regimes aimed at profound transformation, their actual impact was often mixed, producing both significant change and notable continuity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn the Soviet Union under Lenin, social policies had a significant impact. One element of these policies was Lenin's attempt to liberate women from their traditional roles of homemaking and child-rearing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Decree on Divorce, issued in 1917, made divorce easier, allowing marriages to be dissolved at the request of either or both spouses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In addition, the Decree on Women's Healthcare, adopted in 1920, legalised abortion, permitting them to be done freely and without charge in a state hospital ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These measures were important in allowing female emancipation from family and motherhood ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). However, Stalin began to stress the value of the family and changed the earlier Bolshevik social policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The 1936 Divorce Decree made divorce more difficult by requiring both spouses to appear to register a divorce and increasing divorce costs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The 1936 Decree on the Prohibition of Abortions made abortion illegal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) unless the pregnancy threatened the woman's life or health ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The strictures of freedom under Stalin and the emphasis on family allowed little room for female liberation despite previous progress under Lenin ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nLenin wanted to improve educational outcomes by spreading literacy among a largely illiterate population in the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In 1919, he signed the decree “On elimination of illiteracy among the population of Soviet Russia” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This decree launched a mass literacy campaign and expanded access to schooling, while millions of illiterate adults were enrolled in special literacy schools across the country ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Formal education was made a priority in the Soviet Union, and these foundations contributed to a major rise in literacy, from 23 per cent in 1914 to 75 per cent in 1937 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Under Stalin, schooling became more systematised, with the state gradually moving toward a ten-year compulsory programme ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, in 1940, fees were charged for the last three years (ages fifteen to eighteen) of non-compulsory secondary schooling and university education ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The payment of fees challenged the notion of an egalitarian education system since an elite of privileged people developed for those able to pay for extra education ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). University graduates had access to the key areas of Soviet administration, so this limitation also prevented many poorer citizens from accessing positions of power and influence within the new Soviet system ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nMao also attempted to change women's roles in Chinese society through policymaking. To counter women's subordinate role in Chinese society, the Chinese Communists advocated for female equality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In 1950, the New Marriage Law was introduced, ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) which forbade concubinage, discontinued arranged marriages and entitled women to divorce their partners ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The role of women improved as many women used their new freedom to divorce and remarry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). However, female subordination was the norm in the Muslim western provinces ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$), where female members were subject to the orders of husbands, fathers and brothers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The new marriage laws were unable to end traditional notions of female inferiority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In addition, Mao made a priority of promoting women within the Communist Party ranks ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). However, by the 1959 National People's Congress, women still made up only around 23 per cent of delegates, and it remained rare for women to be appointed to senior government or Party positions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Despite the Communist Party's public advocacy for women's rights, few women were promoted within the Communist Party and change to traditional roles was slow and limited ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nMao's policy toward religion had a substantial impact on Chinese society, as the Chinese Communist Party sought to replace religious worship with loyalty to the Communist Party and the state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). During a major anti-religious campaign that began in 1950 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), Buddhism, Confucianism, Christianity and Islam were forbidden to be openly practiced. Priests, monks and nuns were prohibited from wearing their distinctive dress, churches and mosques were closed and their property seized, and foreign clergy were expelled from China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These measures effectively outlawed formal expressions of belief and aimed to weaken organisations that might challenge Party authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). However, religious life did not disappear entirely. In 1950, patriotic churches, led by Chinese Protestants, emerged in China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Such churches were only tolerated if clergy openly supported the regime and accepted state control over appointments and worship practices ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The fact that some churches were permitted to remain open, provided they posed no threat to state security, shows that the CCP's success in transforming religious behaviour was uneven and depended on negotiated cooperation rather than total eradication ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nOn the one hand, social policies in the Soviet Union under Lenin/Stalin and China under Mao did little to change the lives of women ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$). In the Soviet Union, the restrictions on the right to divorce and abortion allowed little room for female independence, and in China, obedience of wives to their husbands continued in all rural areas, while few women were promoted within the ranks of the Communist Party ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). However, this is not to say that the social policies in the two countries were not important ; rather, they reveal the tension between revolutionary ideals and the limited, often uneven, social change that authoritarian regimes were willing, or able, to achieve ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."}]},{"name":"Consolidation & Maintenance of Power","questions":[{"ref":"HI0071","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0071\n\nExplain **three** legal methods used by Mao Zedong to consolidate his power after 1949.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following **two** marks each:\n\n- Mao relied on legal frameworks, such as the 1954 Constitution, which centralised power under the Communist Party and legally established a one-party state.\n\n- Mao used legal methods to outlaw opposition parties and create a one-party state, ensuring that the Communist Party had absolute control over China's political system.\n\n- Mao enacted land reform laws to legally redistribute land from landlords to peasants, which gained him significant support from rural populations.\n\n- The Marriage Law introduced social reforms, such as rights for women and easier access to divorce, aligning the Communist Party with social progress, which increased popular support and helped Mao integrate the Party's influence into daily life.\n\n- Mao used legal structures like the judicial system to suppress opposition initially, but increasingly relied on extra-legal means such as violent repression, arbitrary arrests, and executions during purges.\n\n- Nationalising industries allowed Mao to bring economic control under state authority, reducing the influence of private business owners and centralising economic power within the Communist Party.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne legal method Mao used to consolidate his power was the 1954 Constitution, which centralised authority under the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$). This allowed Mao to shape the political and legal systems according to the Party's needs, ensuring that no other groups could challenge his control ($\\checkmark$). Another method was the implementation of land reform laws, which redistributed land from landlords to peasants ($\\checkmark$). This weakened the power of the landowning class while building loyalty among rural populations, securing Mao's influence in the countryside ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, by creating a one-party state, Mao eliminated any political opposition ($\\checkmark$). This meant that all political decisions were controlled by the Communist Party, making it easier for Mao to maintain his dominance without facing any organised resistance ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0200","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0200\n\nDescribe **four** of Mao's initial mass mobilisation campaigns to consolidate power between 1950 and 1952.[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- Resist America and Aid Korea Campaign in 1950 directed against foreigners resulting in their expulsion and the closing of Christian churches. \n\n- The Suppression of Counter-Revolutionaries Campaign from 1950-51 targeting the GMD, criminal gangs and religious sects with public executions.\n\n- Speak Bitterness Campaign (Land Reform) in 1950 encouraged peasants to turn on landlords, subjecting them to a public 'trial' and executing 2 million of them. \n\n- The Three-Antis Campaign in 1951 against corruption, waste and obstruction that was directed against communist and non-communists, targeting state officials, police and communist party cadres. \n\n- The Five-Anti Campaign in 1952 against bribery, tax-evasion, theft of state property, cheating in government contracts and economic espionage which led to the death of 2-3 million people. [4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's consolidation of power was the result of a series of mass mobilisation campaigns which used propaganda, self-criticism, rectification and purges. The first of these, the *Resist America and Aid Korea Campaign* in 1950 was directed against foreigners resulting in their expulsion and the closing of Christian churches.This ensured the country was closed to all foreigners except Russians ($\\checkmark$) In the countryside, he launched the Speak Bitterness Campaign in 1950 which encouraged peasants to turn on landlords, subjecting them to a public 'trial' and executing 2 million of them. This ensured the removal of substantial opposition and gained him the support of the large rural peasant population of China ($\\checkmark$). The period 1950-51 also saw the launch of *The Suppression of Counter-Revolutionaries Campaign* that targeted the Guomindang (GMD), criminal gangs and religious sects with denunciations and  public execution. This helped him remove the last remnants of the party controlling China before he came to power together with any religious opposition and ensuring law and order ($\\checkmark$) Mao continued in 1951 with *The Three-Antis Campaign* against corruption, waste and obstruction. This was directed against communist and non-communists, targeting state officials, police and communist party cadres and ensured people were always careful to prove they were followers of Mao ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0201","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0201\n\nOutline the goals of the Hundred Flowers Campaign launched in 1956 and explain its impact on Mao's maintenance of power.[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following for the goals of the Campaign:\n\n- Mao lifts censorship restrictions and encourages criticism of the Communist Party.\n\n- February 1957 he announced $`$Let a Hundred Flowers blossom and a  \n\nhundred schools of thought contend' as a policy to encourage constructive criticism.[1 mark]\n\nAny combination of the following for impact on maintenance of power. \n\n- The Central government receives a deluge of letters and complaints. \n\n- Criticism appears in magazine articles, in rallies in the street and on posters on the $`$Democracy Wall' in Beijing University.\n\n- Mao calls off the campaign and reimposes censorship. \n\n- He launched the Anti-Rightist Campaign in 1957 where half a million Chinese were subject to persecution, imprisonment, public shooting and re-education in labour camps. Removing another layer of opposition. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nHaving established his power by 1956 Mao launched the Hundred Flowers Campaign with the goal of encouraging constructive criticism of the working of the Communist Party. He said $`$letting a Hundred Flowers blossom and a hundred schools of thought contend' would promote progress and a flourishing socialist culture ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe impact of the Hundred Flowers Campaign was a deluge of criticism in letters sent to the Central government ($\\checkmark$). This was followed by criticism of the privileged lifestyle of party members which was just one amongst other issues that appeared in magazine articles, on rallies in the street and from students in Beijing University who expressed their opinion on posters on the $`$Democracy Wall' ($\\checkmark$). All this was deemed by Mao to go beyond $`$healthy criticis' and he called off the campaign and reimposed censorship ($\\checkmark$). This was followed in 1957 by the Anti-Rightist Campaign in 1957 where half a million Chinese were subject to persecution, imprisonment, public shooting and re-education in labour camps and helped remove another layer of opposition ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0202","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0202\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of the failure of the Great Leap Forward in 1958 on Mao's maintenance of power.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n\nThe failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese. \n\n- Mao faced mild criticism from his Defence Minister, Peng Dehuai who was dismissed. \n\n- The crisis weakened Mao who would then step down as Head of State in 1959 but continue as Head of the Communist Party. \n\n- The aftermath of the crisis was now handed to Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping who introduced some elements of private ownership of land,  going against Mao's Communist teachings.[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese but Mao refused to admit responsibility ($\\checkmark$). Internal criticism was minimal, such as from his Defence Minister, Peng Dehuai, who was simply dismissed ($\\checkmark$). The crisis, however, weakened Mao who stepped down as Head of State in 1959 but continued as Head of the Communist Party, thus maintaining substantial power in China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0203","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0203\n\nDescribe actions taken by Mao to regain political control of China during the Cultural Revolution in 1966.[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n\n- Mao encouraged the formation of the Red Guards consisting of bands of radical students.\n\n- The Red Guards were ordered to attack the $`$four olds' - old thought, culture, practice and customs and remove $`$bad elements' from society.\n\n- Mao removed his opponents from the party and retook absolute control of party hierarchy. \n\n- Among those targeted were Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping who ran the government since the time Mao had stepped down as Head of State. \n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe $`$Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution' marked Mao's attempt to regain political control following the failure of the Great Leap Forward and consequent weakening of his power. He initially encouraged the formation of the Red Guards consisting of bands of radical students who were ordered to attack the $`$four olds' - old thought, culture, practice and customs and remove $`$bad elements' from society ($\\checkmark$). He also proceeded to remove his opponents from the party and retook absolute control of party hierarchy ($\\checkmark$). Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, who had run the government since the time Mao had stepped down as Head of State, were both removed from office amongst the chaos caused by the Red Guards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0204","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0204\n\nDescribe how Mao’s Continuous Revolution Theory helped him retain power.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following:\n\n- The concept of continuous revolution emphasises revolution as a process that continues even after a party achieves power in order to ensure a constant process of renewal to avoid complacency and corruption. \n\n- Mao used this theory of a constant process of renewal to attack opponents on the basis of complacency and corruption. \n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's theory of continuous revolution was a cornerstone of his thought and stated that revolution was as a process that continues even after a party achieves power as there needed to be constant renewal to avoid complacency and corruption ($\\checkmark$). This was the theory used by Mao to justify his many initial mobilisation campaigns and later the purges carried out in the Hundred Flowers Campaign and the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0205","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0205\n\nWhat according to Source C was the impact of the Cultural Revolution on Communist Party leadership? [3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal Problems of Communism (1966).  |\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly….There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum….It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge* from *Problems of Communism*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nParty officials were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly. \n\n- Communist Party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum.\n\n- Their resistance resulted in Liu and Teng being demoted in party ranking at the Plenum. \n\n- Lin Piao was soon proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and chosen heir in place of Liu.\n\n- Chou En-lai was confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source C, the party officials amongst the Communist leadership were told they should accept criticism aimed at them ($\\checkmark$). When an objection was raised against this by Communist Party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping], they were demoted ($\\checkmark$). Lin Piao (Lin Biao) acquires power as a result of this and is seen as Mao's heir apparent ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0206","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0206\n\nWhat does Source A reveal about Mao's status in China at the time of the Cultural Revolution? [2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A political cartoon titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The New Religion\" by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. October 13, 1966.  |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Consolidation%20%26%20Maintenance%20of%20Power/images/HI0206.png\" alt=\"HI0206\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, E. S. (1966, October 13). *The New Religion.* Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/resource/ppmsc.07977/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\nMao is worshipped as a deity by his followers in China. \n\n- The prayers and doctrine of this religion are recited from the book on Mao's Thought. \n\n- The symbols of the religion are the hammer and sickle that signify Communism.\n\n- The entire nation, comprised of different sections of society, follow this religion.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A suggests that Mao has been elevated to the status of a religious deity that is the subject of worship by all his followers in China ($\\checkmark$). Mao's worshippers recite the prayers and doctrine of the religion he embodies from the book on Mao's Thought ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0207","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0207\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source D for an historian studying the impact of the Cultural Revolution. [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Conversation between the Party of Labor of Albania Delegation, headed by Beqir Balluku, and Mao Zedong on 5 October 1968. |\n\n[Source: *Conversation between the Party of Labor of Albania Delegation, headed by Beqir Balluku, and Mao Zedong*. (1968, October 5). Wilson Center Digital Archive. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://digitalarchive.wilsoncenter.org/document/300036. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any **one or two** of the following:\n\n- The origin of the source is valuable as it is the recording of a conversation between China's Communist leader Mao Zedong and the leader of the visiting Albanian delegation in 1968, during the Cultural Revolution. \n\n- The purpose of the source is valuable as Mao's aim is to discuss and explain the reasons for and impact of the Cultural Revolution. \n\n- The content of the source is valuable as Mao shares details of infighting, at times involving family members and shares his reasoning regarding its revolutionary impact. \n\nFor limitations, any **one or two** of the follwoing:\n\n- The origin of the source is limited as the discussion in 1968 does not give the historian studying the Cultural Revolution an idea of how the Revolution impacted China in the years till the death of Mao Zedong in 1976. \n\n- The purpose of the source is limited as the discussion is between Mao who is the architect of the Cultural Revolution and hence would only present what he sees as the positive impact of the Revolution. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of Source D is valuable as it is the recording of a conversation between China's Communist leader Mao Zedong who was the main force behind the Cultural Revolution and the leader of the visiting Albanian delegation, recorded in 1968 just two years after the launch of the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is also valuable as Mao's aim is to discuss and explain the reasons for and impact of the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ VP). Lastly, the content of the source holds value as Mao shares details of chaotic infighting, at times involving family members, and shares his reasoning regarding the necessity of the Cultural Revolution for Chinese society ($\\checkmark$ VC). \n\nThe origin of the source is limited as the discussion in 1968 does not give the historian studying the Cultural Revolution an idea of how it impacted China after 1968 and until the death of Mao Zedong in 1976 ($\\checkmark$ LO). Furthermore, the origin and purpose of the source are limited because the discussion is with Mao himself and hence would tend to present the events of the Revolution in a positive light ($\\checkmark$ LO and LP)."},{"ref":"HI0208","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0208\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution on China. [6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Report titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Chinese Communist Problem\" from the Asian Affairs Bureau of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Japan. November 18, 1967. |\n\n[Source: Asian Affairs Bureau/Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Japan. (1967, November 18). *The Chinese Communist Problem*. Wilson Center Digital Archive. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://digitalarchive.wilsoncenter.org/document/300640. CC-BY.]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal *Problems of Communism* (1966).|\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly... There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum... It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge from Problems of Communism*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor **comparison** any **three** to **four** of the following **similarities**:\n\n- Both Sources state that Liu Shaoqi had been overthrown or demoted during the Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Both sources state that Mao was now aligned with Lin Piao\n\n- Both sources mention the fighting and chaos that broke out during the Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Both sources mention the attempts made to bring the chaos and disturbance under control.\n\n- Both sources state that the military played a role in bringing the chaos under control.\n\n- Both sources mention that despite the attempts to bring the chaos under control, the situation still is problematic.\n\n- Both sources mention the importance of agricultural production.\n\nFor **contrast**, any **two** to **three** of the following **differences**:\n\n- The Sources emphasize different impacts of the Revolution on leadership. Source C mentions that Chou Enlai retained his position which is not mentioned in Source B while Source B discusses how Liu and Teng lost power.\n\n- Source C mentions the Red Guards and details the destruction caused by them. It also mentions the Red Guards were banished to the countryside in order to bring the chaos under control neither of which is mentioned in Source B.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth Source B and C state that Liu Shaoqi had been overthrown or demoted during the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions that  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Liu Shaoqi, Deng Xiaoping, and others” were overthrown while Source C states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nBoth sources also mention the fighting and chaos that broke out during the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a large number of organizations calling themselves the Mao-Lin faction repeatedly confronted and fought one another, whether within Party and government organizations or within those of workers and peasants,” while Source C mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nBoth sources state that the military played a role in bringing the chaos under control ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the military, brought in to bring the situation under control,” while Source C mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their (Red Guard) activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).  \n\nBoth sources mention the need to maintain agricultural production as an issue that helped drive the reduction of the chaos of the Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B emphasizes the value of agricultural production and the need to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$secure the autumn harvest” ($\\checkmark$ evidence) and Source C explains that some Red Guards were even $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nOne significant contrast between Sources B and C is their emphasis on the impacts of the Red Guards. Source C describes the Red Guards and details the destruction caused by them as well as mentioning that they were sent to the countryside in order to bring the chaos under control while Source B does not mention the role of the Red Guards or their impact at all ($\\checkmark$ difference). Lastly while both sources discuss the impacts of the Cultural Revolution on Chinese leadership, Source C simply mentions that Chou Enlai retained his position and alignment with Mao, while Source B does not mention Chou and instead emphasizes that Liu and Teng were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$overthrown” ($\\checkmark$ difference)."},{"ref":"HI0209","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0209\n\nUsing the information in Sources A and C, discuss the destabilizing impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution on Chinese society. [6 marks] \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A political cartoon titled *$`$The New Religion'* by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. October 13, 1966. |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Consolidation%20%26%20Maintenance%20of%20Power/images/HI0206.png\" alt=\"HI0206\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, E.S. (1966, October 13). *The New Religion*. Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/resource/ppmsc.07977/. Public domain.] \n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal *Problems of Communism* (1966). pp13-14. |\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly….There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum... It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge from Problems of Communism.* HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two or more** of the following with supporting discussion for **five** marks maximum:\n\n- Source C discusses the impact on the Communist Party and states that there was a purge of the senior Communist party membership with CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing being removed from power as a result of their resistance to the directives aimed at public humiliation of important members. The source also mentions the consolidation of Lin Piao's position as Mao's presumed successor. Prime Minister Chou Enlai was the only senior official who retained his position through this chaotic period.  \n\n- Source A portrays the complete supremacy of Mao achieved as a result of the Cultural Revolution. The Cultural Revolution had resulted in the elevation of Mao to the position of a national deity to be worshiped by the entire nation. Mao's Thought was the holy book of the religion and the associated symbols of communism - the hammer and sickle were now the symbols of the religion linked to Mao in the Cultural Revolution.  \n\n- Source C also states the destabilizing impact on society as a result of the terror unleashed by the Red Guards who attacked anyone regarded as bourgeoisie, destroyed places of worship and artifacts linked to culture.\n\n- Source C also mentions the impact on education as schools and universities remained closed impacting the education of an an entire generation. \n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding statement for **one** mark that aligns with the evidence and discussion presented:\n\n- The best responses will include evidence from both Sources A and C and provide specific evidence of the destabilizing impact of the Cultural Revolution on Chinese society.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe destabilizing impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution was felt by all sections of society. Source C discusses the impact of the Revolution on the Communist Party and states that there was a purge of the senior Communist party membership with Communist party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] being removed from power as a result of their resistance to the directives aimed at public humiliation of important members ($\\checkmark$). It also mentions the consolidation of Lin Piao's position as Mao's presumed successor with Prime Minister Chou Enlai being the only senior official who retained his position through this chaotic period ($\\checkmark$).\n\n \n\nFurther evidence of the destabilizing impact of the Revolution is evident in Source A which portrays the complete control that Mao achieved as a result of the Cultural Revolution suggesting that Mao was raised to the position of a national deity to be worshiped ($\\checkmark$). $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao's Thought” had now assumed the status of the holy book of the religion associated with him. The symbols of communism, the hammer and sickle, were now the symbols of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$religion” linked to Mao in the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$).\n\n \n\nIn addition, as mentioned in Source C, all sections of society faced the terror unleashed by the Red Guards as they attacked anyone regarded as bourgeoisie and destroyed places of worship and artifacts linked to culture ($\\checkmark$). Source C further mentions the destabilizing impact on education as schools and universities remained closed for the entire period of the Revolution, depriving an entire generation of education in order to emphasize $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Red Guard activities” instead ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe chaos unleashed by Mao by launching the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution to further his doctrine of continuous revolution and to regain his power and position within the Communist Party caused waves of disruption that would bring upheaval to the lives of a generation of people in China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0312","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0312\n\nDescribe **two** actions Fidel Castro took that helped consolidate his power in the first year of his government.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following actions with its supporting description:\n- Creation of the National Institute of Agrarian Reform (INRA) in May 1959.\n    - INRA absorbed the Office of Revolutionary Plans and Coordination and was directly subordinated to Castro, allowing him to control agrarian reforms and secure leadership over the provisional government.\n- Suspension of free elections starting in 1959\n    - Castro justified the suspension as a measure to prevent counterrevolution and protect the revolution. This eliminated potential challenges to his political authority.\n- The establishment of revolutionary trials and public executions in 1959\n    - According to Castro, these trials and executions ensured \"revolutionary justice\" and eliminated Batista's former allies, consolidating his authority and removing opposition to the regime.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn May 1959, Fidel Castro created the National Institute of Agrarian Reform (INRA) ($\\checkmark$). This key institution absorbed the Office of Revolutionary Plans and Coordination, ensuring Castro's direct control over agrarian reforms and the provisional government ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAdditionally, in 1959, he suspended free elections ($\\checkmark$) to prevent the risk of a counterrevolution, justifying this measure as necessary to protect the revolution. These decisions consolidated his power by eliminating political challenges and securing his leadership over reforms ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0313","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0313\n\nIdentify **two** major reforms implemented by Fidel Castro between 1959 and 1962 and explain how they contributed to the consolidation of his power.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following reforms each with its supporting explanation/connection to Castro's consolidation of power:\n- Agrarian Reform\n    - The 1959 Agrarian Reform Law redistributed land from large landowners and foreign companies to peasants and state-run cooperatives.\n    - The Second Agrarian Reform Law (1963) further limited private land ownership to a maximum of 67 hectares and nationalized the remaining large estates.\n- Rent and Wage Reforms\n    - Implemented wage increases for urban and rural workers, improving their purchasing power.\n    - Rent reductions significantly lowered housing costs, making homes more affordable for the lower classes.\n    - State control over wages and prices aimed to prevent inflation and ensure equitable access to basic goods.\n    - Gained massive support from the working class, weakened landlords, and the bourgeoisie, who lost income from rent and economic control\n- Social Reforms\n    - The 1961 Literacy Campaign reduced illiteracy from 23% to 4%. Schools were established in rural areas, and free education was expanded at all levels.\n    - A universal healthcare system was introduced, providing free medical care to all citizens.\n- Other economic reforms\n    - The Cuban government nationalized banks, foreign companies (including U.S.-owned firms), oil refineries, and electricity companies. Eliminated U.S. economic influence and weakened the Cuban bourgeoisie, dismantling opposition\n    - The market economy was replaced with a state-controlled, centrally planned economy. Reinforced Cuban independence and strengthened Castro's anti-imperialist rhetoric, boosting public support\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne major reform implemented by Fidel Castro between 1959 and 1962 was Agrarian Reform. The 1959 Agrarian Reform Law redistributed land from wealthy landowners and foreign companies to peasants and cooperatives, while the Second Agrarian Reform Law (1963) further restricted private land ownership to 67 hectares, nationalizing larger estates ($\\checkmark$). This reform weakened the landowning elite and U.S. economic influence while securing the support of rural workers who benefited from land redistribution. Additionally, by centralizing agricultural production under state control, Castro strengthened his authority over the economy, reducing opposition from private landowners ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother significant reform was education and healthcare reform. The 1961 Literacy Campaign dramatically reduced illiteracy rates, expanding access to education, especially in rural areas. At the same time, universal healthcare was introduced, ensuring free medical services for all Cubans ($\\checkmark$). These reforms fostered widespread support from marginalized sectors, as they improved living conditions and reinforced Castro's image as a leader committed to social equity. Moreover, education became a tool for ideological indoctrination, promoting revolutionary ideals and discouraging dissent, which further secured his grip on power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0314","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0314\n\nDescribe **three** key features of Castro's Rectification Campaign *(rectificación de errores y tendencias negativas)* in 1986.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following features:\n- A rejection of economic liberalization that Castro felt had gone too far and was undermining centralized planning.\n- The campaign banned material incentives, increased state oversight, and reinforced the ideological commitment to labor to curb corruption and restore centralized control.\n- Increased government oversight of production and distribution to reduce reliance on market mechanisms and enforce socialist principles.\n- Promoted voluntary labor, moral incentives, and socialist values to strengthen ideological commitment and unity.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Rectification Campaign of 1986 was characterized by a rejection of economic liberalization, an anti-corruption drive, and a push for greater labor discipline in Cuba. Castro sought to reverse market-oriented policies that had weakened centralized planning, emphasizing a return to socialist economic principles ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the government cracked down on corruption among officials who had exploited market freedoms for personal gain, reinforcing state control and ideological purity ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, the campaign encouraged voluntary labor and moral incentives to boost productivity and foster revolutionary commitment among workers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0315","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0315\n\nDescribe the US intervention in Cuba known as Operation Mongoose (1960-1963) and explain **two** of its impacts.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor description, the following or similar:\n- Operation Mongoose 1960-1963 was a covert U.S. operation led by the CIA\n- Aimed at destabilizing and overthrowing Castro's government through sabotage, propaganda, and assassination attempts\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following impacts\n- Legitimizing the narrative of an external threat: U.S. actions allowed Castro to consolidate his control by justifying the need to protect the revolution against a hostile foreign power.\n- Justifying internal repression: The perceived threat enabled the implementation of strict security measures, such as the creation of the secret police, which facilitated the elimination of opponents.\n- Strengthening Cuban nationalism: U.S. interventions, seen as a direct attack on Cuban sovereignty, rallied the population around Castro as the defender of the nation.\n- Distracting from internal issues: The narrative of an external threat helped Castro shift focus away from internal economic and social difficulties, reinforcing his legitimacy.\n- Discrediting opposition: U.S. interventions allowed Castro to associate internal opposition with imperialism, reducing their popular support.\n- Increasing dependence on the Soviet Union: Operation Mongoose and other U.S. aggressions pushed Cuba into closer alignment with the USSR, bolstering its economic and military capacity.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOperation Mongoose (1960-1963) was a covert operation led by the U.S. government and the CIA, aimed at destabilizing and ultimately overthrowing Fidel Castro's government. The operation involved sabotage, economic disruption, propaganda campaigns, and even assassination attempts ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOne key impact of Operation Mongoose was the strengthening of Cuban nationalism ($\\checkmark$). U.S. actions were portrayed by the Cuban government as direct attacks on Cuban sovereignty, which allowed Castro to rally popular support by positioning himself as the defender of the revolution and the nation. This narrative united the population against a common enemy and reinforced national pride ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA second impact was the increased dependence on the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). Faced with repeated U.S. aggression, Castro sought closer ties with the USSR for protection and support. This led to stronger economic and military alliances, including the placement of Soviet missiles on Cuban soil, ultimately intensifying Cold War tensions and deepening Cuba's alignment with the communist bloc ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0316","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0316\n\nExplain **two** impacts of the Bay of Pigs Invasion on Castro's consolidation of power.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following impacts, each with supporting explanations:\n- Strengthened Castro's Image\n    - The victory against the U.S. made Castro appear as a strong leader, boosting his popularity and legitimacy. He used the failed invasion as propaganda to showcase the strength of the revolution and his leadership in defending Cuba against imperialism.\n- Created a Unifying Enemy\n    - The invasion gave Castro a clear external threat, helping him unite the Cuban people under his leadership. By portraying the U.S. as an aggressor seeking to overthrow the revolution, he fostered a sense of national unity and justified continued revolutionary measures.\n- Pushed Cuba Closer to the USSR\n    - The attack led Cuba to seek Soviet support, increasing military and economic aid that strengthened Castro's position. In response to the invasion, the USSR expanded its assistance, providing weapons, economic resources, and eventually nuclear protection, which bolstered Castro's regime.\n- Justified Further Political Repression and Elimination of Opposition Within Cuba\n    - Castro used the invasion as justification for cracking down on internal dissent, arguing that opposition groups could be working with the U.S. The government increased surveillance, imprisoned suspected counter-revolutionaries, and suppressed political freedoms under the pretext of national security.\n- Weakened U.S. Influence and Advanced Nationalism in Cuba\n    - The failure of the invasion discredited U.S. efforts to control Cuba, allowing Castro to position himself as the leader of an independent, anti-imperialist state. The event fueled nationalist sentiment, reinforcing Cuban sovereignty and diminishing support for U.S.-backed opposition groups.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Bay of Pigs Invasion strengthened Castro's image as a powerful and victorious leader. When the U.S.-backed exiles failed to overthrow him in April 1961, Castro used the moment to showcase his ability to defend Cuba against a foreign threat ($\\checkmark$). This victory allowed him to present the revolution as successful and resilient, increasing his popularity among Cubans. As a result, he consolidated his power by reinforcing national unity and silencing opposition under the justification of defending the revolution against imperialist aggression ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA different impact of the invasion was that it pushed Cuba into a closer alliance with the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). The failure of the U.S. attack convinced Castro that he needed stronger international support to protect his government from future interventions. In response, the USSR increased its military and economic aid to Cuba, providing weapons, advisors, and eventually nuclear protection. This alliance gave Castro the external backing he needed to strengthen his regime and secure his position both domestically and internationally, reinforcing his control over the island ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0372","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0372\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for an historian studying the consolidation of power of the Chinese authoritarian state.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address by Mao Zedong on the role of the Chinese Communist Party in the early 1950s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Mao, Z. (1951, February 18). *Main Points of the Resolution Adopted at the Enlarged Meeting of the Political Bureau of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China.* Retrieved October 18, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-5/mswv5_12.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, a maximum of **two** marks is awarded.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The source is valuable in terms of origin because it is an official address by Mao himself as the leader of the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) and the regime.\n- The value of the source's purpose is that Mao's purpose is to inform both high-ranking officials and members of the CCP about the measures the regime is taking to strengthen the party internally.\n- The content is valuable because it clearly outlines the main policy actions for the CCP's consolidation.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- It is limited in terms of origin because it is a speech shortly after the CCP's rise to power, which does not allow for tracing the effects of the policy.\n- The source's purpose is limited to debriefing members of the CCP on the policy plan for consolidation and does not account for an evaluation of the scope of the policy.\n- The content is limited to a policy outline on party building and consolidation and does not account for its actual impact in cities and rural towns.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable in terms of origin because it is an internal speech by Mao himself as head of state and the leader of the Chinese Communist Party (CCP), which positions him as an authoritative actor in the consolidation of the Chinese authoritarian state  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). With reference to the purpose, the source is valuable because it showcases the direction in which the Chinese regime is planning to strengthen the CCP  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). Mao's firsthand account makes the source's content valuable as it contains the layout of the policy for the party's buildup and consolidation  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source's origin is limited because it is historically framed shortly after the CCP rose to power  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Along these lines, the content is limited because Mao's address does not account for either the implementation or the impacts of the policy in urban centres and rural towns ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0373","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0373\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and C reveal about the consolidation of power of the Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A**  | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address by Mao Zedong on the role of the Chinese Communist Party in the early 1950s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Mao, Z. (1951, February 18). *Main Points of the Resolution Adopted at the Enlarged Meeting of the Political Bureau of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China.* Retrieved October 18, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-5/mswv5_12.htm. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  CIA's report on the status of media control by the Castro regime by the early 1980s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Central Intelligence Agency. (1983, December). *Cuba: Castro's Propaganda Apparatus and Foreign Policy.* Directorate of Intelligence, p. 3. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP84S00897R000200050008-4.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources examine the significance of centralisation of state functions in the consolidation of power of the Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states.\n- Both sources stress that the control of individuals through the reinforcement of ideological principles was essential in strengthening the regimes' influence on Chinese and Cuban societies.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While Source A focuses on the significance of political control through the consolidation of party structures in China, Source C suggests that the media was at the core of the consolidation of the Cuban authoritarian state.\n- Source A outlines the importance of domestic policy to the consolidation of the Chinese regime. In contrast, Source C emphasises the interplay of ideology, propaganda and foreign policy as a primary factor of the consolidation of Castro’s regime in Cuba.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Sources A and C argue that the centralisation of state functions under Mao's and Castro's regimes was paramount for the consolidation of power from above ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A emphasises the importance of establishing a hierarchical party system that reflects the Chinese Communist Party's (CCP) principles. Similarly, Source C underscores the influence of the state-controlled media outlets on Cuban society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Moreover, both sources stress that ideological principles were at the core of the measures the Chinese and Cuban states implemented to consolidate their power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). While Source A illustrates this through the references to national unity under communism, Source C describes the anti-US views of Castro's regime framed in nationalism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, both sources differ in their levels of analysis regarding the consolidation of power in the Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states. While Source A refers to the domestic policies in China, Source C examines the relationship between Cuba's foreign policy and the regime's influence on society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A is primarily focused on domestic policies targeting CCP units across cities and rural towns, engaging the industrial workforce and former party members, respectively. By contrast, Source C examines the effects of Cuban nationalist anti-US ideology in media propaganda within the framework of Cuba's foreign policy toward the USA ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0374","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0374\n\nExplain the relationship between China's policies of benevolence and domestic policies in Source B.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| **Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address by Mao Zedong on the importance of domestic policies in China's leading role in the Korean War. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Mao, Z. (1953, September 12). *Our Great Victory in the War to Resist U.S. Aggression and Aid Korea and Our Future Tasks.* Retrieved October 18, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-5/mswv5_34.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- China's policies of benevolence under Mao's regime stressed an ideological principle of anti-imperialism against the historical legacy of Japanese invasion attempts and achievements. Chinese people were thus expected to contribute to a major cause of their own nation through hard work and high levels of productivity.\n- China's policies of benevolence were established through communist alliances in the early 1950s, aligning with development programs aimed at modernising the domestic economic system (e.g., heavy industry and infrastructure construction).\n- China's policies of benevolence supported nations affected by US imperialism and thus encouraged the Chinese people to support the efforts of the regional cause in the frame of the Korean War.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nChina's policies of benevolence were deeply grounded in Mao's and the CCP's firm commitment to the ideological principles of anti-imperialism, communism and nationalism ($\\checkmark$). This is reflected in the source's references to Mao's incitement of national unity and implementation of social policies of collectivisation of agricultural outputs aimed at outweighing the long-term impacts of past imperial legacies, such as the Japanese invasions ($\\checkmark$).\n\nMoreover, the commitment to communism positioned China's policies of benevolence at the centre of communist alliances at the international level ($\\checkmark$). As illustrated in the source, China's active involvement in the Korean War led to the intensification of domestic policies for economic modernisation built on the equipment of heavy industry and the construction of infrastructure against the threats of the USA in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0375","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0375\n\nDescribe **three** characteristics of the Cuban state's control of the media mentioned in Source C.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  CIA's report on the status of media control by the Castro regime by the early 1980s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Central Intelligence Agency. (1983, December). *Cuba: Castro's Propaganda Apparatus and Foreign Policy.* Directorate of Intelligence, p. 3. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP84S00897R000200050008-4.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The media was controlled by a main administrative body linked to the Cuban regime (e.g., the Communist Party's Political Bureau).\n- The control of the media was overarching, including professional associations, publishing houses, newspapers, and radio stations.\n- The Cuban state limited control over independent media outlets.\n- The Cuban state's control of the media had support from foreign organisations and allies.\n- The central government, led by Castro, used the media outlets as platforms for spreading and leveraging nationalist anti-US propaganda internationally.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source C, the Cuban state was able to control the media through its central institutional apparatus. The Communist Party's Political Bureau was essential in establishing top-down directives for media outlets under the state's control ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Castro's regime was able to strengthen the reach of the media because it was supported by foreign networks aligned with the Cuban central government ($\\checkmark$). Nonetheless, the Cuban state's control of the media was partly limited with respect to independent outlets ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0376","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0376\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources C and E reveal about the consolidation of power of the Cuban authoritarian state.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  CIA's report on the status of media control by the Castro regime by the early 1980s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Central Intelligence Agency. (1983, December). *Cuba: Castro's Propaganda Apparatus and Foreign Policy.* Directorate of Intelligence, p. 3. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP84S00897R000200050008-4.pdf. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source E** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Castro's speech on the role of doctors in the Cuban public medical service in the early 1960s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Castro, F. (1964, September 11). *Cuba's Record in Medical Care.* Marxists.org. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/history/etol/newspape/world-outlook/v02n33-oct-02-1964-wo.pdf#page=6 p. 7-8. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources examine the role and significance of different institutions under the Cuban regime in consolidating the power of the authoritarian state (e.g., the Communist Party’s Political Bureau and the Ministry of Public Health).\n- Both sources suggest that the Castro regime was influential amongst different social groups, such as the national and international audiences and people in rural areas.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While Source C emphasises the Cuban state’s consolidation of power through the regime’s control of the media, Source E analyses the extent of the regime’s control through social policies tailored to vulnerable groups in rural areas.\n- Source C highlights the significance of foreign policy in the Cuban state’s consolidation of power, facilitated by the support of international media networks. In contrast, Source E emphasises that securing social support for the implementation of social policies in the country was crucial for strengthening the regime’s legitimacy domestically.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Sources C and E highlight the significance of a strong institutional apparatus in the consolidation of power of the Cuban authoritarian state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C argues that the Communist Party's Political Bureau played a key role in controlling the information released by media outlets. Source E illustrates the Cuban state's power by analysing the lasting impact of the Ministry of Public Health's social policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, both sources differ on the extent of the influence of the state apparatus ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While Source C addresses the influence of Cuban media on public audiences, Source E highlights the positive impact of health measures on vulnerable groups in rural areas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, Source C focuses on the Cuban state's consolidation of power from an international perspective, whereas Source E underscores the importance of strengthening societal relations at the local level ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source C emphasises the interplay between the Cuban regime's international networks and the spread of an anti-US nationalist ideology as key to consolidating the state's power among the broader public. By contrast, Source E highlights the strong bond public health doctors established with peasants as essential in increasing the legitimacy of the state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0440","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0440\n\nState the impacts of the Treaty of Brest Litovsk to the Bolshevik's consolidation of power.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following: \n\n - The Bolsheviks could focus on fighting against the White Army in the civil war.\n\n - The Bolsheviks were able to keep the promise in the April Theses by offering peace to the war weary citizens and soldiers. \n\n - By signing the treaty with the Central Powers, the Bolsheviks established themselves as the legitimate successor government to the Provisional Government and Tsarist Government.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Treaty of Brest Litovsk was significant to the Bolshevik's consolidation of power due to several reasons. Firstly, by signing the treaty with the Central powers, the Bolshevhiks established themselves as the legitimate successor to the Provisional and Tsarist Government ($\\checkmark$). By withdrawing from the war, the Bolsheviks could focus their resources in fighting against the White Army in the Russian Civil War ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0444","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0444\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about Trotsky's perspective on the Treaty of Brest Litovsk?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$History of the Russian Revolution to Brest-Litovsk,\" written by Trotsky, published in 1918. |\n\n[Source: Trotsky, L (1918, February). *History of the Russian Revolution to Brest-Litovsk.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1918/hrr/ch04.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Russia must not be involved in a conflict far beyond her strength.\n- Trotsky believes that Russia should buy time for the success of other socialist revolutions in the West and expects they would come to Russia's aid if the other revolutions were successful.\n- The well-being of the revolution is the highest priority.\n- The treaty would also allow the Bolsheviks to buy time to reconstruct their army and other internal issues.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A suggests that Russia should not be involved in a conflict that is far beyond her own strength ($\\checkmark$). Another message is that Trotsky believes that Russia should use the treaty to buy time in order for other countries' socialist revolutions to be successful, so that those countries could come to Russia's aid ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, the source also believes that the revolution should take the highest priority ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0446","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0446\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the state of the Russian Revolution up to 1918.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$History of the Russian Revolution to Brest-Litovsk,\" written by Trotsky, published in 1918. |\n\n[Source: Trotsky, L (1918, February). *History of the Russian Revolution to Brest-Litovsk.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/trotsky/1918/hrr/ch04.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The origin of the source is a top-level Bolshevik member that was directly involved in many events of the Russian Revolution, providing direct insight into the state of the Revolution.\n- The content of the source provides insight into the Bolshevik leadership's perspective on the events leading up to the treaty.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The content of the source provides only Trotsky's perspective and thus provides no insight into alternative or contrasting Bolshevik views of the treaty.\n- The content only provides information up to 1918 as the events are still unfolding.\n- Trotsky is trying to justify his decision to sign the treaty, which suggests that his analysis might be subjective.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable as it was written by Trotsky himself, a top Bolshevik leader who participated in the events and thus had access to inside information during the Russian Revolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  The source's content offers insight into the Bolshevik Party's perspective on the events leading up to the signing of the Treaty of Brest Litvosk ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nThe source's content is limited because it is strictly from Trotsky's perspective, which implies that other Bolshevik leaders' perspectives are not shown ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). The source's purpose is also limited as Trotsky is a firm believer in World Revolution, so his documentation of the events may have a very subjective narrative and be used to promote his beliefs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0463","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0463\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the methods used by Castro to consolidate his power in Cuba?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the speech by Commander Camilo Cienfuegos at the Agramonte Theater in the city of Camagüey on October 21, 1959, to report on the betrayal of Commander Huber Matos Benítez.\n\n[Source: Cienfuegos\\, C. (1959, October 21). *Discurso del Comandante Camilo Cienfuegos en el Teatro Agramonte de la ciudad de Camagüey, para informar sobre la traición del Comandante Huber Matos Benítez.* Instituto de Historia de Cuba. Retrieved February 17, 2025, from https://spreaker.com/episode/camilo-en-camaguey-denuncia-la-traicion-de-hubert-matos--19722615. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny of the following:\n\n - Portraying loyalty to Castro as a measure of revolutionary commitment. The speech frames betrayal of Castro as a betrayal of the Cuban Revolution, reinforcing his position as the sole legitimate leader.\n\n - Marginalizing political opposition - By labeling Huber Matos a traitor, the speech discourages dissent and strengthens Castro's control by eliminating potential rivals.\n\n - Emphasizing mass support for the revolution - Reference to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$massive demonstrations\" legitimizes Castro's rule, portraying him as having the backing of the Cuban people.\n\n - Framing the revolution as a struggle between loyalty and treason - The idea that the revolution has only two paths, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$victory or death,\" discourages opposition and solidifies Castro's authority.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nCamilo Cienfuegos' speech supports Castro's consolidation of power by portraying loyalty to Castro as essential to the revolution, marginalizing political opposition, and framing the revolution as a struggle between loyalty and treason ($\\checkmark$). He presents the betrayal of Castro as a betrayal of the Cuban Revolution, reinforcing the idea that true revolutionaries must remain faithful to him. By labeling Huber Matos a traitor, he discredits political opposition and discourages dissent ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, his statement that the revolution has only $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$two paths: victory or death\" creates a sense of urgency and absolute commitment, making opposition appear disloyal and dangerous to the revolutionary cause ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0464","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0464\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying Fidel Castro's consolidation and maintenance of power in Cuba. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract is from a letter written by Ernesto $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Che” Guevara to Fidel Castro, which Castro publicly read during his speech on October 3, 1965, at the “Chaplin” Theater in Cuba.\n\n[Source: Castro, F. (1965, October 3). *Speech delivered by Commander in Chief Fidel Castro Ruz during the presentation of the Central Committee of the Cuban Communist Party at the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Chaplin” Theater*. Retrieved  from https://fidelcastro.cu/en/discursos/speech-delivered-during-presentation-central-committee-cuban-communist-party-chaplin. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of two marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n\n - The source is a primary document, a personal letter from Ernesto $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Che\" Guevara, a key revolutionary figure and close ally of Fidel Castro. As such, it provides firsthand insight into the internal dynamics of the revolutionary leadership. The letter was read during a period when Castro was consolidating his power by eliminating internal opposition and securing ideological control, making it valuable for understanding the role of political loyalty in this process.\n\n - The letter, publicly read by Castro, was intended to demonstrate unwavering loyalty among top revolutionary figures, reinforcing the legitimacy of his rule and the ideological commitment of the Cuban government.\n\n - Guevara's statements emphasize his dedication to the revolution and Cuba's foreign policy, portraying Castro's leadership as inspirational and morally justified, which helps explain how Castro maintained ideological unity.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following: \n\n - As a personal letter from a loyal revolutionary, it is highly biased in favor of Castro, omitting any criticism or internal conflicts within the revolutionary leadership.\n\n - Although written as a private letter, Castro's public reading suggests it was used as propaganda, meaning it was likely framed to strengthen his leadership rather than offer an objective reflection of events.\n\n - The source does not include the perspectives of those who opposed Castro's leadership, limiting its usefulness for understanding broader political struggles during consolidation.\n\n - The letter focuses on Guevara's personal commitment and ideological stance but does not provide concrete details on policies or specific mechanisms Castro used to maintain power.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B is valuable for studying Castro's consolidation of power because it is a primary source from Che Guevara ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$), a key revolutionary, offering firsthand insight into the loyalty within the leadership. Additionally, its purpose, being publicly read by Castro, suggests it was used to reinforce ideological unity and legitimize his rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nHowever, the source has limitations. It is highly biased ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$), as it presents only Guevara's perspective, omitting internal conflicts or critiques of Castro's leadership. Furthermore, its public reading indicates a propaganda function, making it less reliable ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$) for understanding the complexities of Castro's consolidation of power."},{"ref":"HI0465","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0465\n\nAccording to Source C, what role did U.S. foreign policy play in shaping Castro's strategy for consolidating and maintaining power?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Fidel Castro’s October 28, 1962, declaration to US President John F. Kennedy during the Cuban Missile Crisis. \n\n[Source: Castro\\, F. (1962, October 29). *Los Cinco Puntos (The Five Points)*. Translation by S. Garces. Published in Revolución, Monday, October 29, 1962. Cited in Granma, December 30, 2011. Retrieved from https://granma.cu/granmad/2011/12/30/pdf/pagina06.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following: \n\n - The source presents the U.S. as an ongoing threat, justifying Castro's authoritarian measures under the guise of national defense.\n\n - Castro portrays the U.S. as an imperialist aggressor, rallying nationalist support and uniting Cubans behind the revolution.\n\n - By listing multiple hostile actions from the U.S., Castro emphasizes the necessity of Soviet support, helping to maintain his regime.\n\n - The source's demand for the return of Guantánamo Bay reinforces Castro's narrative of resisting foreign control, strengthening his nationalist legitimacy.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C suggests that U.S. foreign policy was an ongoing threat through actions such as the economic blockade and military interventions, which allowed Castro to justify his authoritarian control under the guise of national defense ($\\checkmark$). By emphasizing external aggression by the United States, he could suppress dissent and maintain strict political control. Additionally, Castro strategically used anti-U.S. sentiment to unite the Cuban population behind his leadership. The source frames the U.S. as an imperialist aggressor, reinforcing the idea that the revolution was necessary to defend Cuban independence. This helped Castro maintain public support and solidify his position ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the demand for the return of Guantánamo Bay underscores Castro's commitment to Cuban sovereignty, further legitimizing his rule. By positioning himself as the leader fighting against foreign domination, he strengthened nationalist pride and reinforced his control over the state ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0466","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0466\n\nWith reference to origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the methods Fidel Castro used to maintain power in Cuba.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the speech by Commander Camilo Cienfuegos at the Agramonte Theater in the city of Camagüey on October 21, 1959, to report on the betrayal of Commander Huber Matos Benítez.\n\n[Source: Cienfuegos\\, C. (1959, October 21). *Discurso del Comandante Camilo Cienfuegos en el Teatro Agramonte de la ciudad de Camagüey, para informar sobre la traición del Comandante Huber Matos Benítez.* Instituto de Historia de Cuba. Retrieved February 17, 2025, from https://spreaker.com/episode/camilo-en-camaguey-denuncia-la-traicion-de-hubert-matos--19722615. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following: \n\n - Provides insight from Camilo Cienfuegos, a close ally of Castro who helped him maintain power.\n\n - The public speech's purpose is to demonstrate how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$betrayal” would be treated, highlighting methods used to reinforce Castro's authority.\n\n - Shows how Castro's government framed dissent as betrayal to maintain control.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following: \n\n - Camilo Cienfuegos, not Castro himself, gave the speech, so it may not wholly reflect Castro's perspective.\n\n - The source shows only one example of how internal dissent was treated, thus limiting our understanding of the range of tactics used to maintain Castro's regime.\n\n - The source treats all disagreement as betrayal, making it hard to tell the difference between honest criticism and real acts of treason, and how Castro may have treated various cases differently.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A provides insight from one of Castro's key supporters, providing insight into the role of Castro's closest allies in his maintenance of power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The content of the source provides insight into how Castro's government justified repression, portraying opposition as betrayal to solidify control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). It also shows how public rhetoric was used as a tool of legitimacy and to discourage dissent ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). \n\nThe source provides insight into only one method, internal purges, and how they were used to maintain power. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Also, the source cites only two examples of internal dissent in Matos and Pedro Luis, so it cannot provide insight into the full extent of internal opposition that Castro was managing to maintain power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0467","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0467\n\nIdentify **two** strategies used by Castro to consolidate and maintain power according to Source B.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract is from a letter written by Ernesto $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Che” Guevara to Fidel Castro, which Castro publicly read during his speech on October 3, 1965, at the “Chaplin” Theater in Cuba.\n\n[Source: Castro, F. (1965, October 3). *Speech delivered by Commander in Chief Fidel Castro Ruz during the presentation of the Central Committee of the Cuban Communist Party at the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Chaplin” Theater*. Retrieved  from https://fidelcastro.cu/en/discursos/speech-delivered-during-presentation-central-committee-cuban-communist-party-chaplin. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following, each with supporting evidence from the source: \n\n - Building a cult of personality around Castro: Castro reads Guevara's words to present himself as a guiding figure and a role model, reinforcing his image as the central leader of the Revolution.\n\n - Expansion of the Revolution: The Revolution was not confined to Cuba; it was promoted as a global movement, solidifying Castro's leadership among international revolutionary groups\n\n - Encouraging absolute loyalty to the revolutionary cause: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Wherever I may end up I will feel the responsibility of being a Cuban revolutionary, and I will act as one.” This reinforces the idea of unwavering ideological commitment.\n\n - Portrayal of the state as the provider for the people: Guevara expresses confidence that the revolutionary state will provide for and educate his family, reinforcing the idea of a paternalistic government ensuring citizens' well-being.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B reflects Castro's attempt to use Guevara's words to help build his cult of personality and maintain power ($\\checkmark$). Guevara reinforces Castro's cult of personality by portraying him as a mentor and indispensable leader, stating, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I am thankful to you for your teachings and your example.” By publicly reading the letter, Castro strengthened his authority and secured loyalty among his followers ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe letter also highlights Castro and Guevara's desire to expand the revolution globally, with Guevara saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I have at all times been identified with the foreign policy of our Revolution.” ($\\checkmark$) This underscores the Revolution's international scope, solidifying Castro's influence abroad while uniting Cubans under a broader ideological mission ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0603","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0603\n\nDescribe the 100 Flowers Campaign and explain how it facilitated the Great Leap Forward.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor description, the following or similar:\n- In the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), Mao Zedong encouraged intellectuals to voice their opinions on government policies. Initially, there was little response; however, eventually, there was widespread criticism of the Communist Party.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any **three** of the following connections to the Great Leap Forward:\n- Mao was able to identify critics\n- It strengthened Mao's authority; critics were purged through the resultant Anti-Rightist campaign\n- The suppression of perceived $\\lq$rightists' set a precedent for the suppression of intellectuals\n- The brief period of open discussion demonstrated the power of mass mobilisation, something Mao picked up on.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), Mao encouraged intellectuals to voice their opinions on government policies. After a slow start, there was widespread criticism of the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$). This facilitated the Great Leap Forward firstly by enabling Mao to identify his critics ($\\checkmark$). Once identified, critics were purged through the Anti-Rightist campaign, leaving no one to protest against Mao’s unrealistic goals in the GLF ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the brief period of open discussion demonstrated the power of mass mobilisation in state-led initiatives, something Mao picked up on and developed during the GLF ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0604","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0604\n\nOutline the events of the Lushan Conference of 1959.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The conference was called to address growing concerns within the Party over agricultural failures, food shortages and economic problems as a result of collectivisation and unrealistic production targets.\n- Peng Dehuai (Minister of Defence) wrote a letter to Mao outlining the problems that he saw with the Great Leap Forward.\n- Rather than addressing Peng's criticisms, Mao attacked Peng (and his supporters) accusing him of rightist and bourgeois tendencies.\n- Most key leaders, fearing for their positions, sided with Mao, and the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lushan conference was called to address growing concerns within the Party over agricultural failures, food shortages and economic problems as a result of collectivisation and unrealistic production targets ($\\checkmark$). Peng Dehuai (Minister of Defence) wrote a letter to Mao outlining the problems that he saw with the Great Leap forward ($\\checkmark$). Rather than addressing Peng's criticisms, Mao attacked Peng (and his supporters) accusing him of rightist and bourgeois tendencies ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked."},{"ref":"HI0605","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0605\n\nExplain the significance of the Lushan Conference of 1959 to Mao's consolidation of power.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Peng and his supporters were purged.\n- Opposition to Mao's policies were practically eliminated from the party as criticism of Mao could lead to persecution.\n- The Great Leap Forward continued unchecked, with disastrous effects.\n- Lin Biao, a staunch supporter of Mao, replaced Peng as Minister of Defence. With Lin Biao in charge of the military, the military and ideology took priority over pragmatism, also confirming the PLA as a key power base for Mao.\n- Lin Biao's growing influence would lead to the militarisation of Mao's Cult of Personality.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lushan Conference was significant as opposition to Mao's policies were practically eliminated from the party, as criticism of Mao could lead to persecution ($\\checkmark$). Thus, the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked, leading to the escalation of famine and economic collapse ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Lin Biao, a loyal supporter of Mao, replaced Peng as Minister of Defence. With Lin Biao in charge of the military, the military and ideology took precedence over pragmatism and cemented the People's Liberation Army as a key power base for Mao ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0606","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0606\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  “Bumper harvest after bumper harvest, leap forward after leap forward!” Poster designed by Wu Linsheng and published in May 1959. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Consolidation%20%26%20Maintenance%20of%20Power/images/HI0606.jpg\" alt=\"HI0606\">\n[Source: Linsheng, W (1958, May). *Bumper harvest after bumper harvest, leap forward after leap forward!* Private collection hosted on ChinesePosters.net. Retrieved March 7, 2025 from https://chineseposters.net/posters/pc-1958-026. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Substantial gains are being made in the wheat, cotton and corn harvests (as seen in the graph and the crops shown behind it)\n- These gains will continue (bumper harvest after bumper harvest, great leap after great leap), also shown through the title and trajectory of the rocket\n- Farmers in China should be optimistic about their future.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of the poster is that substantial gains are being made (as seen in the graph) ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, these gains will continue as shown in the caption “bumper harvest after bumper harvest, great leap after great leap” and also shown by the trajectory of the rocket ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0607","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0607\n\nAccording to Source A, why did the Great Leap Forward fail?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Comrade Peng Dehuai's Letter to Chairman Mao” (July 14, 1959). Peng Dehuai was a general, politician, and Minister of Defence from 1954 to 1959. The letter was given to Mao by Peng during the Lushan Conference of 1959. |\n\n[Source: Dehuai, P. (1984). *Comrade Peng Dehuai's Letter to Chairman Mao* (July 14, 1959). Memoirs of a Chinese Marshal - The autobiographical notes of Peng Dehuai (1898-1974). Retrieved March 7, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/peng-dehuai/1984/memoirspengdehuai.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Peng criticises the overestimation of and boasting about production figures, particularly in relation to food.\n- He points out that these unrealistic statistics led to a premature focus on the iron and steel industries without proper planning.\n- Peng notes the lack of serious analysis of the equipment that would be needed in steel production.\n- Peng suggests that a lack of workforce and purchasing power limited the development of industry\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, Peng criticises the overestimation of production figures, particularly in relation to food ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, he points out that these unrealistic statistics led to a premature focus on the iron and steel industries without proper planning ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Peng notes the lack of serious analysis of the equipment that would be needed in steel production ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0767","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0767\n\nWhat is the message of Source A?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | \n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School,” sent August 1, 1966, by Mao Zedong. Originally published in *Long Live Mao Tse-tung Thought*, a Red Guard publication. \n\n[Source: Mao\\, Z. (1966, August 1). *A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School.* Retrieved May 12, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-9/mswv9_60.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Mao enthusiastically supports the Red Guards' denunciations of landlords, bourgeois elements, imperialists, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists” as enemies of the revolution. \n\n - Mao encourages mass action against perceived enemies of socialism. He reinforces the slogan $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It is right to rebel against reactionaries”. \n\n - Mao calls for unity and re-education. Whilst supporting rebellion, Mao cautions the Red Guards to unite with others who can still be part of the revolution. \n\n - Mao frames the the Cultural revolution as part of a universal proletarian mission, referencing Marx's idea that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the proletariat must emancipate not only itself but all mankind,”\n\n - By addressing the students directly, Mao signals that they are now the vanguard of the revolution. (Thus bypassing traditional party structures)\n\n - By endorsing the Red Flag Combat Group and specific posters, Mao validates extreme, sometimes violent methods as legitimate revolutionary acts. Thus radicalism is not only permitted, it is praised by the highest authority.\n\n - The source contains an implied criticism of the CCP establishment. In referring to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$landlords, bourgeois, imperialists, revisionists and their running dogs” Mao is suggesting the existing party leadership is suspect, and he is placing his trust in mass mobilisation rather than official structures.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe first message of Source A is that Mao strongly endorses the Red Guards' denunciation of landlords, bourgeois elements, imperialists, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists,” whom he identifies as enemies of the revolution. He expresses approval of their big-character posters and revolutionary speeches, signaling his personal support for their militant approach. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, he actively encourages mass action against perceived enemies of socialism, reaffirming the slogan, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It is right to rebel against reactionaries.” By doing so, Mao grants the Red Guards legitimacy to continue their revolutionary efforts, thereby intensifying the Cultural Revolution. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, Mao urges unity and the re-education of those who have made errors. While backing rebellion, he advises the Red Guards to work with all those who can still contribute to the revolution. He emphasizes that individuals who have made serious mistakes should be guided toward ideological reform and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$become new men,” reflecting a Marxist view that transformation, not just punishment, is the ultimate goal. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0768","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0768\n\nUsing evidence from Source A, explain Mao's concerns about the direction of the Chinese Communist Party.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | \n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School,” sent August 1, 1966, by Mao Zedong. Originally published in *Long Live Mao Tse-tung Thought*, a Red Guard publication. \n\n[Source: Mao\\, Z. (1966, August 1). *A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School.* Retrieved May 12, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-9/mswv9_60.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Implies internal threats; $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$those who have made serious mistakes.” Suggesting Mao feared corruption and complacency among cadres.\n\n - The message is directed at students, suggesting Mao saw youth as more ideologically pure and less corrupted.\n\n - Mao encourages the students in their role of confronting and correcting perceived Party deviations.\n\n - Calls for rectification and re-education rather than purges alone.\n\n - Emphasizes ongoing revolutionary struggle to prevent revisionism.\n\n - Shows Mao's belief that the Party needed to stay connected to the masses.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A implies that Mao believed there were internal threats within the CPP. His reference to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$those who have made serious mistakes” suggests that he was concerned about the ideological weakness, corruption, or complacency of certain party members. This indicates a fear that some cadres were drifting away from revolutionary principles and embracing more bureaucratic or capitalist tendencies. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, rather than calling for their immediate removal or punishment, Mao emphasizes the need for rectification and re-education, showing that he hoped to ideologically reform these individuals instead of relying solely on purges. This reflects a broader concern that the party was losing its revolutionary spirit and needed to return to its Marxist-Leninist roots through mass mobilization and ideological struggle. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, the fact that Mao directs this message to the Red Guards rather than party officials further reveals his mistrust of the existing party leadership and his reliance on youth to reinvigorate the revolution. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI1045","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1045\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Without charismatic leadership, authoritarian states could not survive.\" With reference to two authoritarian states, to what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**Guidance for responses**\n- Candidates should examine the role of charismatic leadership in both the consolidation and maintenance of authoritarian states, and weigh it against other factors (coercion, propaganda, legal methods, ideology, and foreign policy).\n- Responses should use comparative case studies\n- High-level responses will recognise that charisma was a major factor in some cases (Hitler, Mao, Castro), but less decisive in others (Stalin, Mussolini's later years).\n- A substantiated conclusion should evaluate how far charisma was necessary for survival, and whether regimes relied more on structural methods of control.\n\n**Indicative Content**\n\nRole of Charismatic Leadership\n- Hitler: Charisma central in building loyalty; Führerprinzip demanded obedience; dynamic oratory and personal authority legitimised the Nazi regime.\n- Mussolini: Projected his Il Duce image as decisive and modernising; propaganda portrayed him as Italy's saviour. Charisma weakened as military and economic failures mounted.\n- Mao: Revolutionary charisma; mobilised peasants during the civil war; built as the “Great Helmsman” through the Little Red Book and mass rallies. Charisma waned after the Great Leap Forward, revived during the Cultural Revolution.\n- Castro: Highly charismatic orator; symbol of resistance against U.S. imperialism; sustained legitimacy through personal magnetism, even during crises like the Bay of Pigs.\n- Stalin: Not naturally charismatic, but his image was carefully constructed into a cult of personality — “Vozhd” (leader), paternal figure, wartime hero. Propaganda presented him as infallible, and this artificial charisma was vital in legitimising his authority alongside terror and bureaucracy.\n\nForce and Terror\n- Stalin: NKVD (People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs), purges, show trials, Gulags — institutionalised terror more important than personal charisma.\n- Hitler: SS, Gestapo, concentration camps; Night of the Long Knives secured loyalty and eliminated rivals.\n- Mao: Suppression of Counter-Revolutionaries, Anti-Rightist Campaign, Red Guards during Cultural Revolution.\n- Castro: Committees for the Defense of the Revolution (CDRs), imprisonment/exile of opponents, and revolutionary tribunals.\n- Mussolini: Squadristi violence pre-1922, later OVRA secret police, imprisonment, and exile for opponents.\n\nPropaganda and Indoctrination\n- Stalin: Socialist Realism in art and literature, rewriting of history (e.g., erasing Trotsky), glorification through posters and films, and cult reinforcement in education.\n- Hitler: Goebbels' Ministry of Propaganda controlled press, radio, film; mass rallies at Nuremberg; Hitler Youth indoctrinated children; anti-Semitic campaigns like Der Stürmer.\n- Mao: “Little Red Book,” mass rallies, posters glorifying Mao; Cultural Revolution slogans; propaganda theatre and art depicting Mao as the “Great Helmsman.”\n- Castro: Long public speeches broadcast on radio/TV; literacy campaign (1961) doubled as ideological training; media tightly controlled to promote socialism and anti-U.S. nationalism.\n- Mussolini: Controlled press and cinema (LUCE newsreels); education reforms emphasised Fascist values; slogans like “Mussolini is always right”; rallies and architectural projects (e.g., EUR in Rome) projected power\n\nLegal and Political Methods\n- Hitler: Enabling Act (1933) eliminated parliamentary opposition; Gleichschaltung aligned institutions with Nazi control.\n- Stalin: The 1936 Constitution proclaimed democratic rights but masked one-party dictatorship; show trials gave purges a veneer of legality.\n- Mao: One-Party Constitution; CCP dominance institutionalised; campaigns like the Hundred Flowers quickly reversed to suppress opposition.\n- Castro: The 1976 constitution codified one-party rule; the nationalisation of the press and industries placed opposition under legal restrictions.\n- Mussolini: Acerbo Law (1923) ensured Fascist majority in parliament; emergency decrees post-Matteotti Crisis (1924) legalised dictatorship.\n\nForeign Policy Success/Failure\n- Hitler: Early triumphs (Rhineland, Anschluss, Munich) boosted support; initial WWII victories strengthened the regime; later defeats undermined survival.\n- Stalin: His leadership during WWII and victory over Nazi Germany reinforced his authority, and the expansion of USSR influence legitimised his rule.\n- Mao: Korean War (1950-53) enhanced legitimacy as defender of Communism; later, foreign isolation weakened position after the Great Leap Forward.\n- Castro: Bay of Pigs (1961) failure for the U.S. made Castro a hero; Cuban Missile Crisis (1962) enhanced global stature despite risks.\n- Mussolini: Foreign failures (Abyssinia's cost, WWII disasters) weakened charisma and led to regime collapse in 1943.\n\nComparative Points\n- Charismatic leadership was a central factor in some regimes (Hitler, Mao, Castro) but manufactured or less effective in others (Stalin, Mussolini).\n- In all cases, charisma had to be reinforced by force, propaganda, legal methods, and institutions.\n- Foreign policy successes often boosted charisma, while failures exposed its limits (Mussolini, Hitler after 1943).\n- Authoritarian states could survive without genuine charisma if repression and propaganda were strong enough (Stalin).\n\nPossible Arguments for Agreement\n- Charismatic leaders gave regimes legitimacy and mass loyalty.\n- The cult of personality often inspired unity and mobilised populations.\n- Charisma helped states survive moments of crisis (Castro post-Bay of Pigs, Mao during the Cultural Revolution).\n\nPossible Arguments for Disagreement\n- Some leaders (Stalin) survived mainly through repression and bureaucracy, not personal charisma.\n- Charisma often faltered when paired with policy failures (Mussolini, Mao post-Great Leap Forward).\n- Structural controls (force, propaganda, institutions) were more reliable for survival than charisma alone.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCharismatic leadership was a powerful tool for consolidating and maintaining authoritarian states, but it was not always decisive ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Adolf Hitler relied heavily on personal charisma to secure and sustain Nazi rule in Germany, while Joseph Stalin in the USSR survived through repression, propaganda, and bureaucratic control, with his “charisma” largely manufactured rather than natural ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This essay will argue that while Hitler's Germany illustrates the centrality of charisma, Stalin's USSR shows that authoritarian regimes could survive without it, provided force, propaganda, and institutions were strong enough ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nAdolf Hitler offers the clearest case of charismatic leadership underpinning an authoritarian state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Hitler's dynamic oratory and ability to channel resentment over the Treaty of Versailles and the economic crisis of the 1930s were central to the Nazi rise to power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). His personal authority was embedded in the Führerprinzip, which demanded absolute obedience to him as leader ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Joseph Goebbels' propaganda ministry reinforced this image through mass rallies at Nuremberg, control of radio and film, and constant presentation of Hitler as Germany's saviour ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Charisma legitimised the regime by linking loyalty to the state with loyalty to Hitler personally ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nForce and terror, however, were also essential in sustaining Hitler's charismatic authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The SS and Gestapo eliminated political opponents, while concentration camps like Dachau imprisoned communists, social democrats, and others who resisted Nazi rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The Night of the Long Knives in 1934 eliminated Ernst Röhm and the SA leadership, removing rivals and securing the loyalty of the army ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These actions reinforced Hitler's personal authority but show that charisma worked in combination with coercion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Even when his charisma began to falter during military defeats after 1943, the Nazi state endured through repression and propaganda, demonstrating that Hitler's charisma was necessary but not sufficient for survival.\n\nJoseph Stalin provides a striking contrast, as his survival relied less on natural charisma and more on institutional control and fear ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Unlike Hitler, Stalin was not a powerful orator, and his rise after Lenin's death in 1924 owed more to bureaucratic skill and political manoeuvring than personal magnetism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Nevertheless, a cult of personality was carefully constructed around him, portraying him as the “Vozhd” (leader), father of the nation, and wartime hero. Propaganda glorified his image in posters, literature, and Socialist Realist art, while history was rewritten to erase rivals such as Trotsky ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Stalin's “charisma” was thus artificial, a product of state propaganda rather than genuine mass appeal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nTerror, more than charisma, ensured Stalin's survival ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The NKVD carried out purges during 1936-38, executing or imprisoning hundreds of thousands of party members, military officers, and ordinary citizens. Show trials gave a façade of legality, while the Gulag system enforced obedience through fear ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These methods eradicated real and imagined opposition, consolidating Stalin's personal control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). During the Second World War, Stalin's leadership and the USSR's eventual victory further legitimised his rule, but it was the machinery of repression, not charisma, that kept him in power before and after the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nPropaganda also played a critical role in both regimes, reinforcing or compensating for charisma($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Germany, Goebbels' propaganda glorified Hitler while vilifying Jews and political enemies, uniting Germans around the leader ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In the USSR, propaganda saturated schools, art, and media with Stalin's image, promoting him as Lenin's true heir and the architect of Soviet progress ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). While Hitler's charisma was genuine and amplified by propaganda, Stalin's “charisma” was manufactured almost entirely by propaganda ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nA comparison of Germany and the USSR highlights both the importance and the limits of charismatic leadership ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Hitler's survival as dictator depended heavily on his personal appeal, amplified by propaganda and sustained by repression. Without his charisma, the Nazi regime would likely have been weaker and less stable. Stalin, by contrast, demonstrates that authoritarian rule could endure without genuine charisma, relying instead on terror, bureaucracy, and a manufactured cult of personality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In conclusion, charismatic leadership was central to the survival of some authoritarian states, but not universally essential. Force, propaganda, and institutional control provided a more durable foundation, and even where charisma mattered, it was never sufficient on its own ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1111","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1111\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of the Soviet Union's economic assistance to Mao's maintenance of power in China.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Reconstruction of the country after the war through loans and supplies.\n- Implementation of the Five-Year Plan aimed at fast-paced economic development.\n- Mao's prioritisation of political developments aligned with communist principles limited the progress of the Soviet Union's economic assistance in the long-term.\n- Soviet Union planners did not allocate resources on needs-based, thereby paving the way for inefficient administration.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThrough direct loans and the provision of essential supplies, the Chinese state was able to enhance the reconstruction of the country after the war ($\\checkmark$). This paved the way for a robust economy, which Mao could use to further his political revolutionary agenda ($\\checkmark$). However, the inefficient administration of resources by Soviet Union planners in China, coupled with Mao's disagreement over ideological principles in the USSR, challenged the extent of the alliance between both nations and limited the reach of the economic recovery of the country in the long-term ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1112","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1112\n\nDescribe **two** ways the Chinese government's military spending contributed to its maintenance of power during the 1960s.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It enabled the strengthening of the Chinese state's capabilities during the Cultural Revolution.\n- It was supported by the Soviet Union's technical support, allowing it to advance more quickly.\n- It helped the military forces regain control and restore social order in rural areas affected by rebels in 1967 and 1968.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nDuring the 1960s, the Chinese state increased military spending to strengthen its ability to address the country's political turmoil during the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the strengthening of the military helped the public forces tackle public order issues in rural areas, which have worsened due to violent opposition from non-state actors ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1113","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1113\n\nDescribe **three** ways Chinese foreign policy affected its consolidation of power in the 1970s.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- China's confrontation with China in the context of the Sino-Soviet Split partly influenced Mao's violent undertakings during the Cultural Revolution against suspected anti-communist individuals and groups.\n- China's foreign policy, centred on the balance of power and cooperation, paved the way for modernisation policies.\n- China's foreign policy, focused on foreign investment, helped the state implement socioeconomic policies tailored to the peasants and reestablish its legitimacy in rural areas.\n- China's alignment with the USA in the 1970s allowed it to attempt to exert control over Taiwan, but it had a limited impact on its consolidation of power.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nChina's foreign policy focused on the balance of power in the Asian region, and transnational cooperation was translated into modernisation policies, thereby establishing conditions for establishing a most robust economic system ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the shift from ideological confrontation with the USA to economic measures linked to openness to trade allowed the Chinese regime to regain legitimacy amongst peasants ($\\checkmark$). However, China's foreign policy also had shortcomings. Although the US support in the 1970s allowed China to resume its attempt to control Taiwan, it had a limited impact on its consolidation of power as the Taiwanese government remained strong ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1114","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1114\n\nDescribe **one** way Castro's education policies influenced his consolidation of power in Cuba.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nEither of the following:\n- The literacy program enhanced the state's presence in remote rural areas, which helped legitimise the Cuban state's commitment to context-based social policies.\n- The increase in educated people allowed for upskilling the capabilities of the workforce and forming citizens in accordance with the Cuban Revolution's ideals.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFurther to the ideals in his revolutionary addresses in the late 1950s, Castro's implementation of a literacy program allowed him to bridge the gap between people living in rural areas and the government, further legitimising his government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1115","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1115\n\nDescribe **two** ways the use of force by the Cuban state influenced its consolidation of power during the 1960s.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Suppression of opposition, including former officials from the Batista government and anti-communist groups, through execution and exile.\n- Defence of the state from foreign influence in the face of the Bay of Pigs and Operation Mongoose.\n- Establishment of labour camps for Cubans reluctant to enrol in the military (Military Units to Aid Production).\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nShortly after the initial triumph of the Cuban Revolution in 1959, the military suppressed any source of opposition from pro-Batista and pro-USA backgrounds through execution and exile ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the use of force was paramount for furthering the anti-imperialist revolutionary ideal. For instance, Castro's commitment to defending the state's sovereignty from foreign influence was successful in hindering the US-led attacks in the Bay of Pigs and through Operation Mongoose ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1116","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1116\n\nDescribe three ways *Radio Rebelde* was significant in the consolidation of the Cuban authoritarian state.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Radio Rebelde was state-controlled by 1962.\n- Radio Rebelde was a platform for propaganda, including Castro's speeches, the government's successes and the revolution's values.\n- As infrastructure in remote rural areas improved, Radio Rebelde's reach increased.\n- Radio Rebelde was one of the only state-approved media outlets through which official narratives were broadcast and views from the opposition were banned.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBecause it was fully controlled by the Castro regime by 1962, Radio Rebelde helped establish a state-led propaganda platform through which Castro could disseminate his ideas and the achievements of his government ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, Castro built on the legacies of early Cuban Revolution stages in Sierra Maestra to position Radio Rebelde as one of the few official media outlets ($\\checkmark$). In doing so, the Cuban state enhanced its consolidation process through the broadcast of the regime's announcements and propaganda and the banning of media companies that opposed the government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1117","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1117\n\nDescribe **two** ways the Cuban authoritarian state treated its opposition during the 1960s.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Suppression of anti-communist rebels early on during the Castro regime (e.g. Escambray Rebellion).\n- Execution in the early 1960s of former members of the Batista regime.\n- The systematic control over political prisoners from opposition groups within and outside the regime.\n- The institutionalisation of a one-party system with the Constitution of 1976.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Cuban authoritarian state engaged in the suppression of opposition groups in violent confrontations and the execution of Batista officials in the early 1960s ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the Cuban state systematically hindered elections and banned political parties from participating in politics throughout the 1960s. This was further materialised with the establishment of a one-party system under the 1976 Constitution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1118","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1118\n\nCompare and contrast the treatment of opposition in the Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop a total of **three** similarities and differences, all supported by clear **evidence**.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states established a one-single party system and banned political parties.\n- Both states suppressed anti-regime groups through violence (e.g. Chinese nationalists and former GMD members in the early 1950s and Cuban anti-communist and pro-USA individuals in the early 1960s).\n- Governments in both states expressed openness to listening to the disagreements of the opposition, but dissent was met with censorship and persecution (Hundred Flowers Campaign in 1956 and the Anti-Rights Movement in 1957 in China and the relative freedom of the press and expression in Cuba).\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While the Chinese authoritarian state used deliberate and systematic violence to hinder any opposition (e.g. the Great Leap Forward 1958-1961), the Cuban authoritarian state engaged in specific targeted attacks against and imprisonment of opposition groups more gradually in the early 1960s.\n- The Chinese state created the Red Guards as a parallel force to violently attack individuals from the opposition, whereas the Cuban state usually used locally-based organisations such as the Committees for the Defense of the Revolution to both provide social services in the communities and inform of anti-revolutionary actions.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states hindered any political opposition in formal spaces through the ban of elections and political parties ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). The Chinese government established the Chinese Communist Party as the leading partisan institution across the country in the early 1950s by creating distinct regional units in cities and the countryside. Similarly, the Cuban state formalised a single-party system under the Cuban Communist Party in the Constitution of 1976 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nMoreover, both states used violence against opposition groups that challenged the Chinese and Cuban regimes' authorities, particularly shortly after Mao and Castro rose to power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). For instance, nationalists and former GMD members were executed or sent to labour camps in the early 1950s, and pro-Batista and anti-communist Cubans were executed and exiled in the early 1960s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the treatment of opposition also differed in the Chinese and Cuban authoritarian states regarding the extent of the use of force ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While the Chinese state created the Red Guards as a parallel force mainly comprised of students to systematically attack right-aligned businesspeople and intellectuals, the Cuban state established the Committees for the Defense of the Revolution as a hybrid organisation responsible for engaging in counterintelligence activities and providing social services in communities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI1119","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1119\n\nDescribe **two** ways the Soviet Union's economic assistance impacted Castro's maintenance of power in Cuba.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Open the market for sugar cane in the Soviet Union at convenient prices.\n- Commercial treaty between the USSR and Cuba for the modernisation of the Cuban agricultural productive system (1966).\n- Increased Cuba's economic dependence on the Soviet Union.\n- Limitations of Cuba's single sugar product in the Soviet Union's market.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Soviet Union's and Cuba's ideological proximity allowed them to start building a strong economic bond in the early 1960s. This was mainly reflected in the opening of Soviet markets for Cuban sugar imports and the signing of a formal commercial treaty in 1966, which helped Castro's government reinvest the resources in the strengthening of the agricultural productive system and implementation of literacy education policies ($\\checkmark$). However, Cuba heavily relied on the Soviet Union's assistance. As the agricultural production's boost of the 1960s slowed down in the 1970s, domestic policies did not meet the economic needs in the country. The Cuban state's capabilities were further weakened in the face of the limitations of exporting sugar as one of the only food products ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1120","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1120\n\nCompare and contrast the role and significance of Fidel Castro and Ernesto $\\lq$Che' Guevara in the consolidation and maintenance of power in Cuba.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **three** similarities and differences, all supported by clear **evidence**.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both Castro and Guevara spearheaded nationwide campaigns promoting nationalism and anti-imperialism such as the nationalisation of US-owned companies and rejection of US interventions.\n- Both Castro and Guevara were committed to strengthening the social fabric of Cuba through tailor-made programs for vulnerable groups.\n- Both Castro and Guevara positioned Cuba in the international arena through pro-revolution and anti-imperialist public speeches and active participation in revolutionary attempts in countries in the Americas and Africa.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Castro was the main political and military leader of Cuba after rising to power as commander in chief of the armed forces, whereas Guevara was responsible for taking on key ministerial posts and carrying out a wide range of measures in land and literacy programs.\n- While Castro was aligned with the USSR early on, Guevara viewed the Cuban-Soviet relations as detrimental to Cuba’s opposition to foreign influence and consolidation of the revolution.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Fidel Castro and Ernesto $\\lq$Che' Guevara contributed to the consolidation and maintenance of power in Cuba as main leaders of the revolution. This entailed the promotion of nationalism and anti-imperialism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). For example, the nationalisation of US-owned companies allowed for major independence in the national production system, particularly in the 1960s. In terms of international relations, Castro and Guevara capitalised on the failed Bay of Pigs invasion attempt to further anti-US sentiments and gain support of Cuban fellow nationals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nMoreover, Castro and Guevara were able to strengthen the Cuban state's position domestically through their active participation if foreign affairs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). For instance, Castro's anti-imperialist speeches in international media outlets and at the United Nations were significant in legitimising his leadership and revolutionary discourse in Cuba. Similarly, Guevara's role in supporting anti-US revolutions in Central America and Africa were paramount for positioning Cuba's foreign policy in the international arena and maintaining a coherent stance with and reinforcing domestic policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, both leaders played different roles and had different impacts on the consolidation and maintenance of power in Cuba. While Castro centralised the institutional power through his roles as Prime Minister and chief in commander of the armed forces, Guevara's role in ministerial posts was paramount to the implementation of social measures ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Castro spearheaded the control of Cuba's political developments through the elimination of opposition sectors and centralisation of power as elections and political parties were banned. By contrast, Guevara was essential in strengthening the state's power amongst vulnerable groups through the implementation of land and literacy programs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI1121","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1121\n\nAccording to Source A, what legal methods were used to consolidate power in the Chinese authoritarian state?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address by Mao Zedong on the role of the Chinese Communist Party in the early 1950s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Mao, Z. (1951, February 18). *Main Points of the Resolution Adopted at the Enlarged Meeting of the Political Bureau of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China.* Retrieved October 18, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-5/mswv5_12.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Institutionalisation of party building.\n- Concentration of power under the Central Committee.\n- Training of officers for administrative tasks.\n- Distinction of party building in cities and rural areas\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to the source, the power of the Chinese authoritarian state through legal methods was mainly achieved by institutionalising the party across the country ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the Chinese regime organised party units more consistently by controlling industrial workers in the cities and enrolling longstanding rural party members on the wider party structures ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the source says that the concentration of power under the Central Committee allowed the implementation of top-down guidelines for organisations at different levels ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1123","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1123\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the significance of legal methods in the consolidation and maintenance of power of the Cuban authoritarian state.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  CIA's report on the status of media control by the Castro regime by the early 1980s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Central Intelligence Agency. (1983, December). *Cuba: Castro's Propaganda Apparatus and Foreign Policy.* Directorate of Intelligence, p. 3. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP84S00897R000200050008-4.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, a maximum of **two** marks is awarded.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The source is valuable in terms of origin because it is a government agency's internal document, which suggests a candid account of the political developments in Cuba.\n- The value of the source's purpose is that it informs on the relationships between Castro's regime and the control of the media apparatus in Cuba, drawing upon information gathered by US intelligence services.\n- The content is valuable because it outlines different perspectives on how the CIA characterises the influence of Castro within pro-government and independent media outlets.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- It is limited in terms of origin because it offers the CIA's one-sided perspective on the extent of the Cuban state's control of the media.\n- The source's purpose is limited to outlining the extent of Castro's control over the media with respect to Cuba's anti-US campaign, thereby overlooking the consolidation of power domestically.\n- The content is limited to aspects related to how Castro used the media to influence the dissemination of anti-US propaganda and does not account for the wider effects of the Cuban state's power in society.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable in terms of origin because the tone of the report is candid as it is meant for the government's internal use, which allows for using it as one perspective on the extent of Cuban state's consolidation of power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). With reference to purpose, the value of the source is supported by the informative nature of the internal report meant for guiding the governmental course of action regarding Castro's control over media outlets ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nMoreover, the content is valuable because the CIA accounts for different proofs that speak to a wide range of perspectives on the relationships between Castro and both pro-regime and independent outlets ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the source's content is also limited because it mainly addresses how Castro controls the media as a platform for attacking the USA through propaganda. This focus does not allow to identify the Cuban state's power in wider sectors of society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1124","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1124\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources C and D reveal about the effects of leadership in the consolidation of power of the Cuban authoritarian state.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | CIA's report on the status of media control by the Castro regime by the early 1980s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Central Intelligence Agency. (1983, December). *Cuba: Castro's Propaganda Apparatus and Foreign Policy.* Directorate of Intelligence, p. 3. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/docs/CIA-RDP84S00897R000200050008-4.pdf. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Castro's address on the public's concerns about the limitations of the media in Cuba. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Castro, F. (1961, June 30). *Palabras a los Intelectuales [Words to the Intellectuals].* Translated by D. Alzate. Retrieved October 20, 2025 from https://www.presidencia.gob.cu/media/filer/public/2022/05/07/palabras_a_los_intelectuales_1961.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources emphasise the significant influence of Castro on Cuba's political development after the Cuban Revolution.\n- Both sources suggest that Castro's leadership allowed him to establish specific policies on the use of media.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While Source C stresses Castro's control of all media outlets in Cuba, Source D highlights the government's openness to creating other publishing companies.\n- Source C underscores the detrimental effects of Castro's leadership in spreading anti-US sentiments within Cuba and beyond, whereas Source D states that the Cuban government promotes freedom of expression in light of revolutionary values.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources agree that Castro's leadership was significantly influential in the development of Cuban institutions after he rose to power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). For instance, Source C highlights the leading role of the Communist Party's Political Bureau in structuring different news outlets. Similarly, Source D describes the Cuban National Council as an overarching institution responsible for administering and overseeing media outlets ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources differ on the extent of the function of the Cuban institutions responsible for controlling the media. While Source C stresses that Castro had absolute control over the news outlets, Source D highlights that the government was committed to diversifying the media ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). For example, Source C illustrates that Castro frequently reviewed and approved of the Granma's editorials before their publication. By contrast, Source D describes the legislative options for expanding the printing companies as evidence of openness in the media ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nMoreover, both sources disagree on how the Cuban government used the media outlets as propaganda platforms. Source C underscores Castro's consolidation of power through anti-US sentiments both nationally and internationally, whereas Source D states that freedom of expression was at the core of the Cuban state under the revolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source C describes a wide range of news outlets in Cuba and international networks which served as platforms for Castro's campaigning against the USA and consolidation of his power. Meanwhile, Source D underscores the importance of promoting the artistic spirit in writers and artists ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI1125","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1125\n\nAccording to Source E, what was the significance of public health provision in the consolidation of power of the Cuban authoritarian state?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source E** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Castro's speech on the role of doctors in the Cuban public medical service in the early 1960s. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Castro, F. (1964, September 11). *Cuba's Record in Medical Care.* Marxists.org. Retrieved October 15, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/history/etol/newspape/world-outlook/v02n33-oct-02-1964-wo.pdf#page=6 p. 7-8. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Increased enrollment of doctors in the public health system.\n- Establishment of the Rural Medical Service to address the needs of the rural population.\n- Satisfaction of public health recipients across the country.\n- Increase in the construction of public health facilities.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to the source, the public health provision in Cuba helped consolidate the power of the authoritarian state by strengthening the health system throughout the country. The needs of the population were met through the creation of a robust Rural Medical Service that reached people in distant rural areas ($\\checkmark$). This was further enhanced through the expansion of the health system, particularly the construction of new hospitals and dispensaries, all of which were paramount for increasing the satisfaction of the services’ recipients and legitimising the public health policies of the Cuban state ($\\checkmark$). The source also says that the provision helped increase the number of doctors available to provide services in the public health system ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1243","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1243\n\n“Propaganda was the most significant factor in the maintenance of power in authoritarian states.” Discuss with reference to **two** states, each chosen from a different region.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organisation) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- **Germany** - Hitler (1933-1945): There were Nazi posters and flags all over Germany, as well as photographs of Hitler. In factories, people had to listen to Nazi propaganda through radios and loudspeakers. Children were indoctrinated in school through the curriculum, which supported Nazi ideas. People would hear this propaganda so often that many people would end up accepting it as the truth. Hitler was portrayed as the infallible *Führer*, while propaganda promoted nationalism, anti-Semitism, and the idea of a racially unified Volksgemeinschaft. Mass rallies, films like “Triumph of the Will,” and cheap radios (*Volksempfänger*) ensured widespread dissemination.\n    - Other factors to consider: general terror and repression, SS powers to remove political opponents, Gestapo powers to hunt out enemies of Nazism and Nazi control of the courts and the judges. Responses may also mention economic recovery via rearmament and public works programs, which bolstered public support for Hitler.\n- **USSR** - Stalin (1924-1953): Stalin developed a cult of personality around himself. Images of Stalin were everywhere - photographs, statues, posters and pictures. Stakhanovites, Soviet workers of the 1930s, used posters, slogans and radio broadcasts to celebrate communist successes in the Five-Year Plans. It was all this propaganda that made people loyal to Stalin, allowing him to control the Soviet Union. Education, posters, newspapers like *Pravda*, and socialist realism reinforced ideological conformity.\n    - Other factors to consider: the Great Terror, NKVD violence and executions, use of the Purges to get rid of rivals and use of gulags for slave labour.\n- **China** - Mao (1949-1976): Mao used mass media to spread Maoist ideas and promote the Cult of Personality. Mao's Five-Year Plan propaganda used posters to motivate the workforce. The commune system enabled Mao to establish schools, exposing children to Maoist propaganda and ideology. All this brainwashed people into worshipping Mao, and this helped keep Mao in power.\n    - Other factors to consider: mass mobilisation combined with the removal and persecution of landlords and persecution of political enemies during the Hundred Flowers Campaign, Cultural Revolution, and mobilisation of young Chinese in the Red Guards.\n- **Cuba** - Castro (1959-2008): The *Granma* newspaper disseminated anti-imperialist and pro-socialist propaganda. Castro's government used a “Golden Age” of poster art to promote revolutionary figures and public health campaigns. The Cuban Institute of Friendship with the Peoples educated the world about the Cuban Revolution. Fidel Castro used speeches, education, state media, and symbolism to promote nationalism, anti-imperialism, and social equality. Propaganda framed economic hardship as the cost of resisting U.S. aggression, especially after the Bay of Pigs invasion and during the Cold War.\n    - Other factors to consider: Castro's charismatic leadership, Revolutionary Tribunals for former Batista officials and Committees for the Defense of the Revolution to monitor neighbourhoods. Other social achievements in healthcare and education reinforced propaganda and enhanced popular support in the early part of Castro's rule.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOn the one hand, Hitler's Germany and Mao's China both used propaganda to maintain power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Germany, the Nazi regime organised mass rallies, controlled radio broadcasting and indoctrinated young people to make people loyal to Hitler and the Nazis ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In China, the Communist Party used posters, the commune system and the Little Red Book to spread Maoist ideas and promote the cult of Mao. On the other hand, both regimes used other methods to maintain power, so to suggest propaganda was the most significant factor would be an overstatement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Propaganda, force, terror, and economic policies played a critical role in maintaining power in both regimes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nPropaganda was an important tool for the Nazis to spread their ideology quickly and effectively to all parts of German society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Firstly, Goebbels, Minister of Propaganda, brought all German broadcasting under Nazi control by creating the Reich Radio Company ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). There was a great extension of the audience, helped by the mass production of very cheap radios called $\\lq$The People's Receiver' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). By 1939, 70% of German homes had a radio. There were also loudspeakers installed in the streets. By controlling radio stations, the Nazis could transmit Hitler's speeches and ideas about German expansion into Eastern Europe and the inferiority of the Jews until these ideas came to be accepted as normal by the German people ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Secondly, Goebbels organised mass rallies to commemorate historic days in the Nazi calendar, such as Hitler's birthday, Party Foundation Day and Heroes' Remembrance Day ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These large rallies were very popular and helped strengthen loyalty to the Nazi state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Nazi propaganda was also embedded in education where curricula reinforced racial ideology, nationalism and loyalty to Hitler ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$), ensuring long-term ideological conformity among German youth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The German people did not hear any dissenting voices, making it difficult to challenge what they were told ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, propaganda cannot completely account for the Nazi's maintenance of power, as fear, coercion, and economic recovery under the regime also helped them dominate and gain support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Firstly, the Nazis used the state police, the Gestapo, and the SS to make people afraid of them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Gestapo agents had sweeping powers to spy on Germans by tapping telephones, intercepting mail and using information from a network of informers. They could arrest people and send them to concentration camps without trial ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This meant that most Germans were frightened to speak out against the regime, so there was little opposition to Nazi policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Secondly, Nazi economic policy from 1933 reduced unemployment ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The Law to Reduce Unemployment was renewed and expanded, and the Reich Labour Service was expanded to employ 19-to 25-year-olds. As a result, state investment was directed towards job-creation schemes such as reforestation, land reclamation, the construction of autobahns, housing and public facilities. As a result of these policies, there was dramatic job growth. From the registered peak of 5.6 million unemployed in 1932, the official figure showed that it had declined to 1.6 million ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Hitler promised and delivered lower unemployment, which helped to ensure popularity among industrial workers and increase loyalty to the regime ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nPropaganda was also important in maintaining the Communist Party's rule in China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Mao used mass media, most notably posters, to help workers reach production targets in the first Five-Year Plan, a programme of industrial development launched in 1953 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Posters promoted collective effort, selflessness and faith in Mao's vision. Posters were simple and effective at communicating with China's millions of peasants, who were largely illiterate. Secondly, Mao used the commune system to propagate Communist ideas and his own cult of personality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). After 1958, Mao set up schools and Party headquarters in 70,000 communes and collective farms across rural China. Communist Party personnel controlled educational policies, exposing children to Maoist propaganda and teaching them to worship Mao and fear Western ideas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). During the Cultural Revolution, Mao promoted an extensive cult of personality through posters, newspapers, and the Little Red Book ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), portraying himself as the infallible leader and source of ideological truth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Propaganda projected the image of Mao as the saviour of the nation and the great benefactor of the people; as a result, Mao became little less than a god to the great mass of the Chinese people ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, similar to Hitler and Germany, propaganda alone cannot explain Mao's maintenance of power in Communist China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Firstly, the Chinese Communist Party persecuted landlords to reduce their power and redistribute land to peasants, ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) thus gaining their support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The Agrarian Reform Law of 1950 created collective farms. The property of landlords was confiscated and redistributed among former tenants, further increasing peasant support of the regime. At the same time, the People's Courts persecuted landlords by putting them on public trial and denouncing them as enemies of the people, resulting in the deaths of one million landlords ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Secondly, Mao purged Communist Party members during the 1957 Hundred Flowers Campaign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). He invited Communist Party members to criticise government and party policies, which led to bitter denunciations of Mao. In response, Mao implemented policies against rival politicians, intellectuals, students, artists, and professors. Those who spoke out were condemned as $\\lq$rightists' and thousands were arrested and imprisoned, ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) allowing stronger Communist Party control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).Faced with Mao's determination to remove anyone regarded as a threat to his authority, Communist Party members dropped all thought of serious opposition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the use of force rather than propaganda was the most important factor in maintaining power for Hitler in Germany and for Mao in China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$). In Germany, the SS and the Gestapo dealt with any sign of opposition from the Left, made up of communists and socialists. In China under Mao, the People's Liberation Army removed anyone regarded as a threat to Mao's authority, landlords during the anti-landlord campaign and Communist Party members condemned as $\\lq$anti-socialists' during the anti-rightist campaign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). While propaganda was important to the maintenance of power of both, it was secondary to the power of fear and coercion to suppress opposition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)."},{"ref":"HI1473","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1473\n\nCompare and contrast the nature and treatment of opposition in **two** authoritarian states.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**General Guidance:**\n\\\nStrong answers should clearly address the question by comparing and contrasting both the nature of opposition and the ways in which opposition was treated in two authoritarian states. Answers that rely on lengthy description of opposition are unlikely to score well. The strongest answers will maintain a running comparison of the two states and support arguments with specific, relevant evidence. Conclusions will vary depending upon the states chosen, but all should arrive at a balanced, well-supported conclusion that evaluates the extent to which the nature and treatment of opposition were similar or different between the two authoritarian states.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n- **Nazi Germany under Hitler (1933-1945)**\n    - Opposition in Nazi Germany came from a range of political, religious, social and military groups, including Communists, Social Democrats, trade unionists, religious leaders, youth organisations and some members of the armed forces. While these groups opposed the regime for different reasons, they were united in their rejection of aspects of Nazi rule.\n    - Hitler responded by eliminating political opposition through legal measures, censorship, propaganda, surveillance and the use of the Gestapo and concentration camps. As the regime became more radical, particularly during the Second World War, repression intensified and many opponents faced imprisonment or execution.\n- **Mao's Communist China (1949-1976)**\n    - Opposition in Mao's China came from a range of political, social and ideological groups, including Nationalist supporters, landlords, intellectuals, religious groups, party critics and individuals accused of being $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$counter-revolutionaries.\" Opposition was often linked to resistance to Communist policies or criticism of Mao's leadership, although perceived opposition could also emerge from within the Communist Party itself.\n    - Mao responded through political campaigns, including land reform, the Anti-Rightist Movement and the Cultural Revolution, using public denunciations, imprisonment, forced labour, purges and widespread political persecution to eliminate real and perceived opponents. These campaigns often encouraged ordinary citizens to participate in identifying and exposing enemies of the revolution.\n- **Mussolini's Fascist Italy (1922-1943)**\n    - Opposition in Fascist Italy came from a variety of political and social groups, including socialists, communists, liberal politicians, trade unionists, Catholic critics and anti-fascist intellectuals. Much of the early opposition challenged Mussolini's destruction of parliamentary democracy and the growing influence of Fascism, although organised resistance declined as the regime consolidated power.\n    - Mussolini responded through a combination of intimidation, legal measures and political control. Fascist squads (Blackshirts) used violence against opponents, while censorship, the banning of political parties, the OVRA secret police, imprisonment, exile and propaganda were used to suppress dissent and strengthen the Fascist state.\n- **Castro's Cuba (1959-2008)**\n    - Opposition in Castro's Cuba came from a range of political, social and economic groups, including supporters of the Batista regime, political opponents, wealthy elites affected by revolutionary reforms, religious critics, dissidents and pro-democracy activists. Many opponents challenged the establishment of a one-party socialist state, while others opposed restrictions on political freedoms, economic reforms or the increasing influence of the Communist Party.\n    - Castro responded by consolidating one-party rule and restricting political opposition through censorship, state control of the media, surveillance by security organisations, imprisonment, exile and limitations on political organisation. These measures were intended to prevent organised resistance and reinforce the authority of the revolutionary government.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAll authoritarian states suppress opposition, but the nature of that opposition and the methods used to eliminate it reflect each regime's ideology and circumstances ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Nazi Germany under Adolf Hitler, opposition largely meant identifiable political, religious and racial enemies, whereas in Mao Zedong's China it was defined ideologically, extending to anyone deemed insufficiently revolutionary ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Consequently, Hitler built a centralised system of persecution, while Mao relied on mass political campaigns to identify enemies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nThe nature of opposition differed in each state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Nazi Germany, early opposition came from the Communist Party (KPD), the Social Democratic Party (SPD) and trade unions, which opposed the destruction of democracy after 1933. Religious figures, including members of the Confessing Church and Bishop Clemens von Galen, criticised aspects of Nazi policy, and student groups such as the White Rose opposed the regime during the war. Some conservative military officers also turned against Hitler, culminating in the failed July Bomb Plot of 1944 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Though differing in motivation, these groups were systematically dismantled by the regime, preventing a united resistance movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn contrast, opposition in Mao's China was often determined by ideological loyalty rather than organised political activity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Former Kuomintang supporters, landlords and wealthy peasants were identified as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$class enemies\" following the Communist victory in 1949, while many businessmen and industrialists were targeted during the Three-Anti Campaign (1951) and Five-Anti Campaign (1952). Intellectuals who criticised government policies during the Hundred Flowers Campaign became victims of the Anti-Rightist Campaign, while the Cultural Revolution expanded the definition of opposition to include teachers, writers, party officials and anyone accused of promoting \"bourgeois\" ideas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).Unlike Nazi Germany, where opposition generally came from identifiable political or religious groups, Mao's campaigns meant that almost anyone could be labelled a counter-revolutionary, creating widespread fear throughout society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nBoth regimes relied on repression to eliminate opposition, but the methods they employed reflected their different political systems ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Nazi Germany, Hitler rapidly dismantled democracy following the Reichstag Fire in February 1933. The Reichstag Fire Decree suspended civil liberties, while the Enabling Act allowed Hitler to legislate without parliamentary approval. By July 1933, political parties were banned, trade unions dissolved and Germany a one-party state. The Gestapo and SS, under Heinrich Himmler, monitored opponents through surveillance, informants and arbitrary arrest. Early concentration camps such as Dachau (March 1933) initially housed political prisoners, notably communists and trade unionists. Censorship and propaganda directed by Joseph Goebbels promoted ideological conformity and discouraged dissent ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).\n\nMao also used state repression but frequently combined it with mass political mobilisation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Land Reform campaigns targeted landlords through public $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$struggle sessions\", while the Anti-Rightist Campaign punished critical intellectuals. During the Cultural Revolution, Mao encouraged the Red Guards to attack perceived enemies of the revolution, leading to public humiliation, imprisonment and forced labour for millions. Unlike Hitler's reliance on institutions such as the Gestapo, Mao deliberately encouraged ordinary citizens to denounce neighbours, colleagues and even family, making repression a mass social movement as well as a state policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).\n\nDespite these differences, both regimes shared important similarities. Hitler and Mao portrayed opponents as enemies of the nation or the revolution and justified repression as essential for protecting the state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Both relied on propaganda, censorship and surveillance to discourage dissent and eliminate alternative political organisations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In each case, repression was not simply defensive but formed part of a broader effort to reshape society according to the regime's ideological goals and strengthen the authority of the leader ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe scale and purpose of repression, however, differed significantly ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Nazi Germany, repression was closely linked to the creation of a Volksgemeinschaft based on Nazi racial ideology. Political opponents were imprisoned or executed, but persecution increasingly extended to groups considered racially or biologically inferior, particularly Jews, Roma and people with disabilities. During the Second World War, repression intensified as the regime sought to eliminate both political and racial enemies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Thus, opposition was suppressed not only to secure Hitler's political authority but also to facilitate the racial transformation of German society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn contrast, Mao's repression aimed to maintain continuous revolutionary change rather than racial purification ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Mao believed that class struggle continued even after the Communist victory in 1949 and that enemies could emerge within both society and the Communist Party. Consequently, campaigns such as the Anti-Rightist Movement and the Cultural Revolution repeatedly identified new $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$counter-revolutionaries\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$capitalist roaders\", resulting in millions being persecuted, imprisoned or sent to labour camps. Unlike Hitler, who had largely destroyed organised political opposition by the mid-1930s, Mao repeatedly revived campaigns against perceived enemies to reinforce his personal authority and revolutionary ideology ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Consequently, repression in Mao's China was cyclical and ideological, whereas in Nazi Germany it became increasingly focused on enforcing political obedience and implementing racial policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the nature of opposition differed: Hitler faced identifiable political and racial enemies, while Mao defined opposition through ideological loyalty ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). So did its treatment: Hitler relied on centralised institutions such as the Gestapo and concentration camps, Mao on repeated mass campaigns ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Both consolidated power through repression, but their contrasting ideological objectives shaped whom each regime persecuted and how ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$).\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"},{"ref":"HI1626","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1626\n\nWhat message is conveyed by Source A?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| $\\textbf{Source A}$ |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| | | $\\mbox{}\\\\[-30pts]$A propaganda poster, “Intellectuals Turn to Labour: Get Toughened in Practical Struggles, Get Rooted into the Masses of Workers and Peasants,” created by Ha Qiongwen in 1964, shortly before the Cultural Revolution (1966-1976). It reflects Mao Zedong's belief that intellectuals had been influenced by bourgeois ideas and needed to be ideologically reformed through manual labour alongside workers and peasants. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Consolidation%20%26%20Maintenance%20of%20Power/images/HI1626.jpg\" alt=\"HI1626\">\n[Source: Ha Qiongwen. (1964). *Intellectuals turn to labour: Get toughened in practical struggles, get rooted into the masses of workers and peasants.* Retrieved July 7, 2026 from https://www.flickr.com/photos/thomasfisherlibrary/8161103196/in/photostream/. No known restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Intellectuals should leave their privileged positions and work alongside workers and peasants.\n- Physical labour would strengthen the revolutionary commitment of intellectuals by making them tougher and more dedicated to communist ideals through practical struggle.\n- Intellectuals working alongside workers and peasants would help them understand ordinary people's lives and become more closely connected to the masses.\n- The poster is throwing positive light on Mao's policy of sending educated people to the countryside to be re-educated by the masses.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe image caption indicates that man is an intellectual who has left his privileged position to work alongside workers and peasants in order to better understand their practical struggles and strengthen his commitment to communist ideals ($\\checkmark$). By showing the challenging physical labour, it also implies that intellectuals have much to learn from the working class and peasants, which would help them become more integrated into socialist society ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the red paper in the man's pocket indicates that he is undergoing reeducation in Maoist ideology ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1627","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1627\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about Mao's view of the challenges facing socialist China in 1957?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| $\\textbf{Source B}$ |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| | | $\\mbox{}\\\\[-30pts]$This excerpt is taken from a speech by Mao Zedong at the Chinese Communist Party's National Conference on Propaganda Work in 1957, later published in *Quotations from Chairman Mao Tse-Tung*. It was delivered during the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), when Mao initially encouraged criticism of the Communist Party before reversing course and launching the Anti-Rightist Campaign. The source reflects Mao's belief that bourgeois and anti-Marxist ideas remained a threat to socialism and justified the continued ideological struggle to strengthen Communist Party control. |\n\n[Source: Brians, P. et al. (2002, June 6). *Reading About the World*, Volume 2. Via Internet Archive Wayback Machine. Originally published by Harcourt Brace Custom Books. Retrieved July 7 2026 from https://web.archive.org/web/20020606015507/http://www.wsu.edu:8080/~wldciv/world_civ_reader/world_civ_reader_2/mao.html.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Mao believed that bourgeois and petty-bourgeois ideas continued to exist within Chinese society and threatened the development of socialism.\n- He argued that economic transformation alone was not enough, as the struggle between socialist and capitalist ideas was still ongoing.\n- Mao felt that political and ideological victory had not yet been secured, even though socialist ownership of the means of production had been established.\n- He warned that anti-Marxist beliefs could spread and weaken the socialist system if they were not actively challenged.\n- Mao believed that constant criticism and ideological education were necessary to ensure that communist principles remained dominant and that China stayed on the socialist path\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that Mao believed the main challenge facing socialist China was ideological rather than economic ($\\checkmark$). Although socialism had largely transformed ownership of the means of production, Mao argued that bourgeois and anti-Marxist ideas still survived in society ($\\checkmark$). He therefore believed that a long-term ideological struggle was necessary to ensure that socialist and communist values would prevail ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1628","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1628\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about Mao's attitude towards intellectuals in China.\n[6 marks]\n\n| $\\textbf{Source A}$ |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| | | $\\mbox{}\\\\[-30pts]$A propaganda poster, “Intellectuals Turn to Labour: Get Toughened in Practical Struggles, Get Rooted into the Masses of Workers and Peasants,” created by Ha Qiongwen in 1964, shortly before the Cultural Revolution (1966-1976). It reflects Mao Zedong's belief that intellectuals had been influenced by bourgeois ideas and needed to be ideologically reformed through manual labour alongside workers and peasants. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Consolidation%20%26%20Maintenance%20of%20Power/images/HI1628.jpg\" alt=\"HI1628\">\n[Source: Ha Qiongwen. (1964). *Intellectuals turn to labour: Get toughened in practical struggles, get rooted into the masses of workers and peasants.* Retrieved July 7, 2026 from https://www.flickr.com/photos/thomasfisherlibrary/8161103196/in/photostream/. No known restrictions.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| $\\textbf{Source B}$ |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| | | $\\mbox{}\\\\[-30pts]$This excerpt is taken from a speech by Mao Zedong at the Chinese Communist Party's National Conference on Propaganda Work in 1957, later published in *Quotations from Chairman Mao Tse-Tung*. It was delivered during the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), when Mao initially encouraged criticism of the Communist Party before reversing course and launching the Anti-Rightist Campaign. The source reflects Mao's belief that bourgeois and anti-Marxist ideas remained a threat to socialism and justified the continued ideological struggle to strengthen Communist Party control. |\n\n[Source: Brians, P. et al. (2002, June 6). *Reading About the World*, Volume 2. Via Internet Archive Wayback Machine. Originally published by Harcourt Brace Custom Books. Retrieved July 7 2026 from https://web.archive.org/web/20020606015507/http://www.wsu.edu:8080/~wldciv/world_civ_reader/world_civ_reader_2/mao.html.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The **strongest answers** will identify and develop at least one similarity and one difference, followed by a further point of either similarity or difference. Each point must be supported by clear evidence from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources show that Mao believed intellectuals needed ideological reform to become loyal supporters of socialism and communism.\n- Both sources indicate that intellectual reform requires an active and continuing process.\n- Both sources imply that intellectuals cannot remain detached from the wider population.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source A emphasises reforming intellectuals through physical labour and integration with workers and peasants, whereas Source B stresses criticism and ideological debate.\n- Source A presents intellectual reform in a positive and constructive manner, whereas Source B adopts a more hostile and threatening attitude towards ideas associated with intellectuals.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSources A and B both reveal that Mao was suspicious of intellectuals and believed they needed to be transformed to become true supporters of socialism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A indicates that intellectuals should be $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rooted into the masses of workers and peasants,\" and in Source B, Mao says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$We still have to wage a protracted struggle against bourgeois and petty-bourgeois ideology” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth sources indicate that intellectual reform requires an active and continuing process($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A instructs intellectuals to participate in “practical struggles” and become $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$toughened\", presenting true reform as something achieved through sustained experience and effort, while Source B describes ideological struggle as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$protracted” because bourgeois and anti-Marxist ideas would $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$continue to exist for a long time\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nSource A presents intellectual reform in a positive and constructive manner, whereas Source B adopts a more hostile and threatening attitude towards ideas associated with intellectuals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A portrays the intellectual actively learning and working alongside a peasant. The instruction is to become $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$toughened” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rooted\", suggesting that intellectuals could be successfully remoulded and incorporated into socialist society. Whereas in Source B hostile expressions are used, such as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$poisonous weeds\" and “ghosts and monsters\", and there are warnings that such ideas must not be allowed to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$spread unchecked\". This reveals a more hostile attitude and presents certain intellectual ideas as a continuing danger to socialism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |"}]},{"name":"Emergence","questions":[{"ref":"HI0151","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0151\n\nCompare and contrast the use of force in Castro and Hitler's rise to power.  [6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nIf only similarities or contrasts are identified, 4 is the highest mark.\n\nAny **6** of the following:\n\nFor compare:\n- Both attempted coups unsuccessfully: Munich Putsch of 1923 against the government of Bavaria and 26 July 1953 on the Moncada Barracks against the dictatorship of Batista.\n- Both used armed groups to help them get to power: the Sturmabteilung (SA) and the Schutzstaffel (SS) were paramilitary groups controlled by Hitler which terrorized political opponents while the guerrilla group 26th of July Movement was Castro's guerrilla army during the civil war.\n- In both cases, the failed coups pushed both leaders into notoriety.\n- The trials they were both subjected to as a result of the failed coups provided a useful platform from which to explain their ideas.\n\nFor contrast:\n- Hitler got to power through legal means by being appointed chancellor by President Hindenburg while Castro was victorious in a civil war.\n- Hitler's violence was directed against a democratic government that guaranteed people's liberties while Castro's was directed against the dictatorship of Batista that had been brutalizing the population of Cuba since the 1930's.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn both Hitler and Castro's rise the use of force was an important factor . Both leaders tried unsuccessfully to overthrow the government to get to power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Hitler did so in 1923 during the Munich Putsch against the government of Bavaria while Castro tried to control the Moncada Barracks on the 26th of July, 1953 as the first step in his plan to replace the dictatorship of Batista ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In both cases, although they were unsuccessful in their initial goals, the coups made them household names  and allowed them a platform from which to express their ideas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). In the trials that followed, Hitler was able to defend himself by declaring the treachery of the Weimar Republic and his fervent nationalism while Castro presented his Moncada Program during his defense ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). After the failure of the coups, both leaders relied on armed groups to help them get to power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$): the SA and the SS were paramilitary groups controlled by Hitler which terrorized political opponents while the guerrilla group 26th of July Movement was Castro's guerrilla army during the civil war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, there were also some differences regarding their use of force. Most notably, Hitler's use of force was not decisive in his getting to power as he eventually did so legally while in the case of Castro his victory in the civil war is what placed him in the position of leader of Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). In part this had to do with the fact that Hitler's violence was used against a democratic government that defended people's rights while Castro's was directed against the dictatorship of Batista that had been brutalizing the population of Cuba since the 1930s. Therefore, in the case of Hitler, the use of force did not greatly garner him the people's support while Castro's did ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0152","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0152\n\nCompare and contrast Castro's Moncada Programme with Hitler's 25 Points.[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\nIf only similarities or contrasts are identified, 3 is the highest mark.\n\nFor **compare**:\n- Land reforms:\n    - Castro: The ceding of land to tenant farmers, sharecroppers, and squatters who occupy small parcels of land.\n    - Hitler: farmers should be given their land.\n- Company Profits:\n    - Castro: Profit sharing for industrial workers.\n    - Hitler: Division of profits in heavy industries.\n- Corruption:\n    - Castro: End of corruption and confiscation of property from those found guilty of it.\n    - Hitler: We demand struggle without consideration against those whose activity is injurious to the general interest. Common national criminals are to be punished with death.\n\nFor **contrast**:\n-   Form of government:\n    -   Castro's demand for the re-establishment of the democratic and republican Constitution of 1940 in contrast to Hitler's idea of Führer - the idea that there should be a single leader with complete power.\n- Form of proposal:\n    - Castro proposed the laws during his defense trial after the attack on the Moncada Barracks of 26 July, 1953 while Hitler's 25 Points were the Nazi Party's program.\n\n[4 marks]\n\\\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMuch of the ideology that helped Castro gain power was detailed in his Moncada Program. The same is true for Hitler and the Nazi Party's 25 Point program. Though the 2 leaders had radically different ideas in some aspects, they were similar in others.\n\nBoth proposals emphasize the need for land redistribution to farmers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$)\nand profit sharing for industrial workers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Both ideologies also advocate for the elimination of corruption in the government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n They differ however, in the punishment that those guilty of corruption should receive ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While Castro calls for the confiscation of their property, the Nazis call for capital punishment ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, a stark difference exists in what the two leaders believed the future government should look like ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Castro advocated for a return to the democratic and republican Constitution of 1940. In contrast, the Nazi Party Program promoted the concept of a Führer, or a single leader with authoritarian power, rejecting democratic principles ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0153","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0153\n\nDescribe **three** measures Perón implemented as Secretary of Labor which helped him rise to power.\n\\\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **3** of the following:\n- Rural Workers' Code: established new living and working conditions for rural workers as well as medical care, paid annual vacation leave and adequate housing.\n- Creation of Labor Courts: Employees now had a way to resolve their conflicts with employers legally.\n- Paid annual vacation leave for all workers.\n- One extra month's pay per year (*aguinaldo*).\n- Government recognition of trade unions.\n- Dismissal Law: established that every worker had the right to receive a compensation proportional to their seniority in the event of being dismissed without cause.\n- Social Security in case of being unemployed.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs Secretary of Labor during the dictatorship of the Grupo de Oficiales Unidos (GOU), Perón implemented a series of reforms that would garner him the support of the workers. Some of these were the introduction of paid annual vacation leave for all workers ($\\checkmark$).  He also implemented a policy granting workers 13 monthly salaries a year ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Perón formally recognized trade unions. This gave trade unions greater power of negotiation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0154","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0154\n\nExplain **one** of the ways in which World War II contributed to Perón's rise to power. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **1** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n- It generated political instability\n    - WWII was one of the reasons for the 1943 coup that aimed at preventing Argentina´s declaration of war against the Axis powers. Perón was one of the leaders of the group that perpetrated the coup, the Grupo de Oficiales Unidos (GOU). Being a member of the GOU allowed him to pass measures that would garner him support. WWII created additional political instability from 1943-1945 due to US sanctions on the government to force it to declare war on the Axis. This instability facilitated Peron's rise.\n- It allowed Perón to appeal to a nationalist ideology\n    - After being faced with pressure from the US to declare war on the Axis powers, Perón appealed to Argentinian independence which  resonated with the population that resented the US's bullying tactics.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWWII allowed Perón to appeal to the population`s nationalist sentiment ($\\checkmark$). The population was resentful of U.S. pressures to declare war on the Axis powers. By resisting what many viewed as American bullying tactics, Perón positioned himself as a defender of Argentina’s sovereignty. These tactics included everything from a trade embargo to pressuring the governments of other Latin American countries as well as Great Britain to not recognize the government of President Farrell ($\\checkmark$). This is clearly seen in one of his campaign slogans “Braden o Perón” referring to the name of the US ambassador to Argentina in 1945 ($\\checkmark$) and asking people to vote for the US or for the representative of Argentinian interests."},{"ref":"HI0158","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0158\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying the emergence of Hitler. [4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from Bruno Heilig,“Why the German Republic Fell\" (published in 1945). Heilig was an Austrian journalist, who worked for thirty years as editor or foreign correspondent of leading newspapers in Austria, Germany, Hungary and the Balkans. He was a prisoner in German concentration camps. |\n\n:box[“Yet why had Germany taken the road from individual political liberty through mass hysteria to the surrender of all liberty and the despotic \"leadership\" of one man? Was there a link between the economic and the political collapse? Emphatically, yes. For as unemployment grew, and with it poverty and the fear of poverty, so grew the influence of the Nazi Party, which was making its lavish promises to the frustrated and its violent appeal to the revenges of a populace aware of its wrongs but condemned to hear only a malignant and distorted explanation of them. …\\\n\\\nIn the first year of the crisis the number of Nazi deputies to the Reichstag rose from (12) to 107. A year later this figure was doubled. In the same time the Communists captured half of the votes of the German Social Democratic Party and the representation of the middle class practically speaking disappeared. In January 1933 Hitler was appointed Reichskanzler; he attained power, as I said before, quite legally. All the forms of democracy were observed.(...)\n\\\n\\\nMoney also was the chief weapon the enemies of democracy applied to overthrow democracy. Germany's masters, the owners of agricultural and industrial land, the Junkers [landowning aristocrats of Eastern Germany] and the Ruhr industrialists, had no actual love for Nazidom as such, but they were willing to use it to destroy the hated Republic. \"A mere aristocracy of wealth will never struggle while it can hope to bribe a tyrant,\" which is just how the German landlords behaved. Nazidom was financed as everyone knows by the heavy industry in the first place, but the Junkers also contributed to the millions of marks which were paid to the leaders of the Nazi Party.”\n]\n\n[Source: Heilig, B. (1938). *Why the German Republic Fell*.  Retrieved October 24, 2024, from Hanover College History Department. https://history.hanover.edu/courses/excerpts/111heil.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nIf only values or limitations are discussed, the student cannot get more than **two** marks. There must be at least one reference to origin, purpose and content in either value or limitations to reach **four** marks.\n\n |  | $\\ce{VALUE}$ |$\\ce{LIMITATIONS}$|\n | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| ORIGIN | Since the source was published in 1945 and the author lived in Central Europe at the time of Hitler's rise, he has insight into the situation. $\\\\$ He is a journalist so is  knowledgeable about the topic. | Because the author was placed in a concentration camp,  he is writing from an anti-Nazi, anti-Hitler perspective.|\n| PURPOSE| He wants to explain why the Weimar Republic was replaced by Hitler. As a journalist, his aim is to inform. | |\n| CONTENT | He gives data proving how the Nazis increased support after the Great Depression. He explains the connection between economic problems and descent into despotism. He explains the role of big industrialists and the Junkers in helping Hitler`s rise.  |He uses strong language that proves he is exaggerating his point like when he writes the Germans were “condemned to hear only a malignant and distorted explanation.”|\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source has value in its origin because it was published in 1945 and the author lived in Austria and Germany. As a result, he has insight into the situation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, a limitation of the origin is that Heilig is against Hitler as we can see from the fact that he was put in a concentration camp ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nThe value of the purpose is that Heilig is a journalist who wants to explain to the public how Germans went from democracy to Hitler's rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nThe value of the content is that the source gives data proving how support for the Nazis increased due to the economic crisis. This data is used to prove his explanation of the connection between economic problems and descent into despotism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, a limitation of the content has to do with the fact that, as the writer experienced the whole process, he uses emotional language like: “condemned to hear only a malignant and distorted explanation” . This emotional language limits objectivity. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)"},{"ref":"HI0196","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0196\n\nAccording to Source D, what role does Mao believe the peasant movement will play in the Chinese revolution?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a 1927 report by Mao Tse-Tung about the role of peasants within the Communist Party.  \n\n[Source: Mao Tse-Tung. (March 1927). *Report on an Investigation of the Peasant Movement in Hunan, March 1927, in Selected Works of Mao Tse-tung, vol. I* (3rd printing; Peking: Foreign Languages Press, 1975), pp. 23-29. Retrieved January 13, 2025 from https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1927mao.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Mao sees the peasant movement as the central driving force of the revolution, describing it as a \"mighty storm\" that will reshape Chinese society.\n\n - Mao believes the movement will destroy the power of imperialists, warlords, corrupt officials, and local tyrants, paving the way for liberation\n\n - He highlights the peasant associations as the new centers of authority, replacing the feudal structures that had dominated rural areas for centuries.\n\n - Mao states that the movement will challenge every revolutionary party and individual, forcing them to either lead, follow, or oppose it, with clear consequences.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source D, Mao portrays the peasant movement as the driving force behind the Chinese revolution, likening it to a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mighty storm\" that will dismantle the oppressive structures of feudalism and imperialism ($\\checkmark$). He also emphasizes the critical role of peasant associations, which have become the new centers of authority in rural areas, symbolizing the collapse of the old gentry-dominated order ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Mao describes the movement as a test for revolutionaries, compelling them to take a clear stance by leading, supporting, or opposing it, with significant consequences for their revolutionary legitimacy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0281","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0281\n\nDescribe **four** characteristics of the period of 1930 to 1943 in Argentina that gave it the nickname $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$La Década Infame” (The Infamous Decade).\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following characteristics of the period:\n\n - Social Inequality\n\n     - Argentina was marked by a stark divide between wealthy landowners and the working class, with widespread poverty and limited opportunities for social mobility.\n\n - Corruption and Fraud\n\n     - Corruption and the practice known as \"fraude patriótico\" (patriotic fraud) were widespread as political elites, particularly the conservative oligarchy, manipulated elections to maintain power and prevent the rise of opposition groups using vote-rigging and coercion.\n\n - Political Instability \n\n     - The combination of military interference since the 1930 Coup and fraudulent elections eroded political stability, fostered social unrest, and laid the groundwork for further political crises, eventually leading to the rise of Peronism in the 1940s.\n\n - Economic Dependence on Britain and foreign influence\n\n     - Argentina relied heavily on Britain for trade and investment, giving foreigners significant control over the economy like Roca-Runciman Treaty. \n\n - Repression of Opposition\n\n     - Political dissenters, including socialists, labor unions, and members of the Radical Civic Union (UCR), were targeted through censorship, arrests, and the suppression of protests.\n\n     Strikes faced violent crackdowns, socialist leaders were imprisoned or exiled, and repression silenced opposition, fueling resentment that contributed to the rise of Peronism.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe political context in Argentina until the time of the Revolución del 43 on June 4  was characterized by the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Década Infame\" (Infamous Decade), which spanned from 1930 to 1943 because it was a period of political instability ($\\checkmark$) and one of corruption and electoral fraud ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, Argentina's economy was heavily influenced by Britain, with treaties like Roca-Runciman granting significant control to foreign interests ($\\checkmark$). Meanwhile, opposition groups, including socialists and labor unions, faced harsh repression through censorship, arrests, and violent crackdowns, fueling discontent that paved the way for Peronism.($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0282","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0282\n\nIdentify **two** reasons for the formation of the Grupo de Oficiales Unidos (GOU) in Argentina in 1943.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following: \n\n - To remove the existing corrupt government led by Ramón Castillo\n\n - To challenge foreign economic dominance and promote Argentina's sovereignty\n\n - To address concerns about military vulnerability due to Brazil's growing power\n\n - To end communist influence in the labor movement\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe GOU was formed in 1943 to tackle two major issues. First, there was a growing concern over Argentina's military weakness, especially in comparison to Brazil's rising power ($\\checkmark$). Second, there was a need to end the widespread political corruption that had dominated the country during the previous Infamous Decade ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0283","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0283\n\nDescribe **two** key events between 1943 and 1946 in Juan Domingo Perón's political career that led him to power.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following: \n\n - Perón emerged during the military coup of June 4, 1943, which overthrew President Ramón Castillo. Initially, General Arturo Rawson briefly took power but was soon replaced by Pedro Pablo Ramírez, under whose government Perón emerged as a key figure. He became part of the military government as a member of the Grupo de Oficiales Unidos (GOU), founded on March 10th, 1943 by 19 military members including Coronel Peron.\n\n - Perón was appointed to key positions, including Secretary of Labor and Social Welfare in 1943-1944. He gained popularity by improving workers' rights, and introducing social reforms like wage increases, pensions, and paid vacations.\n\n - When Ramírez was removed in early 1944, Edelmiro Farrell took over as President, and Perón rose to Vice President and Minister of War, consolidating his influence in the military and among workers.\n\n - After being forced to resign on October 9th and briefly imprisoned in October 1945,  large-scale, worker-led demonstrations (17 de Octubre de 1945) demanded his release. This event solidified Perón's support from labor unions. He won the 1946 presidential election as the candidate of the Labor Party, beginning his first term as President.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nJuan Domingo Perón's political career between 1943 and 1946 began with the military coup of June 4, 1943, which removed President Ramón Castillo from power. Perón emerged as a key figure within the new government through his role in the Grupo de Oficiales Unidos (GOU) ($\\checkmark$). Another key event came in October 1945 after Perón's brief imprisonment sparked massive worker-led protests on October 17, solidifying his position as a popular leader. This growing support culminated in his victory in the 1946 presidential election as the candidate of the Labor Party, beginning his first term as President ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0284","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0284\n\nExplain **two** reasons middle-class Argentines grew dissatisfied with Perón's government measures and how this contributed to rising tensions in Argentina.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following reasons with supporting explanation:\n\n- Dissatisfaction with a military government and Perón's growing influence:\n\n     - Many middle-class groups, including political parties, were frustrated with the military government and Perón's growing influence because they felt excluded from decision-making processes under military rule. They demanded a return to democracy.\n\n - Reintroduction of religious education in state schools:\n\n     - Teachers were angered by the reintroduction of religious education in state schools, as it was seen as a step backward for secular education, creating tension within the education sector.\n\n - State intervention in universities:\n\n     - Students and professionals opposed state intervention in universities, undermining academic freedom and autonomy. This intervention alienated the middle sectors and fueled their dissatisfaction with Perón's government policies.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason for the dissatisfaction of the middle sectors with Perón's government was the political frustration caused by the continuation of military rule ($\\checkmark$). Many political parties, such as the Radical Civic Union (UCR), demanded a return to democracy after years of military control and were angered by Perón's growing influence over government decisions, especially through his roles as Minister of War and Secretary of Labor ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reason was the state intervention in universities, which limited academic freedom and autonomy ($\\checkmark$). For example, Perón's government placed university administrators under state control, replacing independent leaders with government-aligned figures. Students and professionals opposed these actions, seeing them as a threat to intellectual independence. These factors deepened the resentment of the middle sectors and heightened tensions with Perón's government. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0285","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0285\n\nExplain **two** ways the influence of landowners (*estancieros*) and business owners contributed to the request for Perón's resignation in 1945.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following ways with supporting explanation:\n\n - Opposition from Landowners (*Estancieros*)\n\n     - Perón's Estatuto del Peón introduced better working conditions for rural laborers, which angered landowners as they viewed these reforms as expensive and a threat to their control over the workforce. This economic burden and perceived erosion of their authority led to their opposition against Perón.\n\n - Resistance from Business Owners and Professionals\n\n     - Business owners and professionals opposed the Ley de Asociación de Profesionales, which allowed workers to form unions and strengthened Perón's influence over the labor force. They saw this as a move that undermined their economic interests and increased Perón's political power, prompting them to join the anti-Peronist movement.\n\n - Collective Protest (March of September 1945)\n\n     - The anger of landowners, business owners, and the middle class culminated in the March of September 1945 with a large-scale demonstration against Perón's policies. This collective pressure, along with public protests, forced the government to request Perón's resignation on October 9, 1945, showcasing the united strength of his opposition.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe reactions to Perón's reforms caused strong opposition from landowners and business owners. Landowners were upset by the Estatuto del Peón, which improved rural workers' conditions, as they saw it as too costly and a loss of control over labor ($\\checkmark$). At the same time, business owners and professionals opposed the Ley de Asociación de Profesionales because it allowed workers to form unions, which they believed gave Perón too much power and hurt their interests ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThese frustrated groups - landowners, business owners, and the middle class - came together and organized the March of September 1945, demanding Perón's removal ($\\checkmark$). The protests created so much pressure that the government forced Perón to resign on October 9, 1945 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0286","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0286\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about Juan Domingo Perón?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Commemorative poster for the second anniversary of the Día de la Lealtad (Loyalty Day, 17 October), first celebrated in 1947. The word in the caption translates to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unmovable”.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Emergence/images/HI0286A.png\" alt=\"HI0286A\">\n\n[Source: *File: 17 de octubre - Inconmovible Afiche de 1947*. (1945, October 17).  Museo del Bicentenario. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved September 27, 2024, from https://es.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archivo:Museo_del_Bicentenario_-_Afiche_Inconmovible.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The poster suggests Perón would not back down, depicting he and his supporters during the mass demonstration on October 17, 1945, as unshakable, using the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Inconmovible” (unmovable).\n\n - The obelisk symbolizes Perón's firm and resistant leadership, while the waves represent opposition that cannot weaken him.\n\n - It suggests that Perón still has the loyalty of his supporters two years after the demonstrations of 1945 and marks $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Loyalty Day”, a key moment in the Peronist movement.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe poster emphasizes Perón's leadership as strong and unshakable by using the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Inconmovible,” which suggests that his rise to power and the movement's foundation cannot be weakened ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the obelisk serves as a symbol of stability and resilience, portraying Perón as a leader who stands firm despite opposition, shown by the waves, while reinforcing the idea that October 17, 1945, was a defining and enduring moment for the Peronist movement even two years later ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0287","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0287\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about the communist perspective of Perón and the events of October 17, 1945?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A cartoon published by the communist publication Orientación that depicts its view of the demonstrations of October 17, 1945 in the Plaza de Mayo, Buenos Aires. On October 24, he following image appeared on its cover. The caption of the image reads: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The colonel displayed his cast of thugs and criminals, already familiar to the country from the events of the 17th and 18th. The most regrettable thing is that, alongside this cast, he managed to mislead a few honest workers lacking experience and political insight.\" The fishing rod is labled “Borlenghi” referring to a General Confederation of Labor (Confederación General del Trabajo or CGT) union leader, Ángel Borlenghi, drawing in workers labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$tough guys.” Also it can be seen Perón holding a sword with the initials $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$T y P\" (referring to the Secretaría de Trabajo y Previsión).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Emergence/images/HI0286C.png\" alt=\"HI0286C\">\n\n[Source: Sigfredo. (1945, October 24). *Political Cartoon from Orientación.* Accessed via *El Partido Comunista de Argentina y los orígenes del peronismo. Un análisis desde su estrategia de Frente Popular* by Gabriel Piro Mittelman (2021) in Nuevo Mundo Mundos Nuevos. Retrieved January 8, 2025 from https://journals.openedition.org/nuevomundo/85504. Image in public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - It portrays Perón's supporters as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$maleantes\" (criminals) and aims to discredit the movement and Perón's leadership, suggesting that it lacked legitimacy and was supported by undesirable sectors of society.\n\n - Workers are shown as tricked, symbolized by a fishing rod labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Borlenghi,\" referring to the General Confederation of Labor (CGT) leader who backed Perón. The communist press suggests that these workers lacked political experience and were unable to recognize Perón's true intentions, portraying them as victims of false promises.\n\n - Perón is drawn in a dictator-like pose, while the police are blamed for organizing the event. Perón holding a sword with the initials $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$T y P\" (referring to the Secretaría de Trabajo y Previsión). This visual suggests that his leadership style is authoritarian and militaristic. The sword symbolizes control and power, reinforcing the communist press's criticism of Perón as a manipulative leader who seeks to dominate the working class.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe communist press's perspective in Source C shows Perón's supporters as criminals and outsiders, making the demonstration look disorganized and illegitimate ($\\checkmark$). It also suggests that workers were misled, using a fishing rod labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Borlenghi\" to show they were tricked by false promises ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0288","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0288\n\nWhy, according to Source B, does Perón view the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$collective movement” started on October 17, 1945 as a significant event for Argentina?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a speech delivered by Juan Domingo Perón from the “Casa Rosada” to a large crowd gathered in the Plaza de Mayo, Buenos Aires, Argentina on October 17, 1945.\n\n[Source: Perón, J. D. (1945, October 17). *Discurso de Juan Domingo Perón el 17 de octubre de 1945: Habla desde el balcón de la Casa Rosada en Plaza de Mayo (Translation: Speech by Juan Domingo Perón on October 17, 1945: Speaking from the balcony of the Casa Rosada in Plaza de Mayo)*. Radio Universidad Nacional de La Plata. Retrieved September 27, 2024, from https://sedici.unlp.edu.ar/handle/10915/27206. CC BY 2.5.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following ideas: \n\n - Perón sees the movement as the rebirth of a workers' consciousness, which he believed was essential for the greatness and immortality of Argentina.\n\n - He sees the event as a sign of trust and loyalty and a rejection of those who thought “the people” would betray him and the movement.\n\n - Perón sees the movement as his acceptance as the leader of the working masses, showing a deep emotional connection by symbolically embracing them like family.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nPerón sees the massive congregation in Plaza de Mayo in October 17, 1945, as the rebirth of workers' consciousness, which he believes is essential for Argentina's strength and success ($\\checkmark$). He views the gathering as a sign of the workers' loyalty and trust, encouraging him forward as the leader of Argentina ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0289","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0289\n\nCompare and contrast the perspectives about Perón shown in Sources B and C.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a speech delivered by Juan Domingo Perón from the “Casa Rosada” to a large crowd gathered in the Plaza de Mayo, Buenos Aires, Argentina on October 17, 1945.\n\n[Source: Perón, J. D. (1945, October 17). *Discurso de Juan Domingo Perón el 17 de octubre de 1945: Habla desde el balcón de la Casa Rosada en Plaza de Mayo (Translation: Speech by Juan Domingo Perón on October 17, 1945: Speaking from the balcony of the Casa Rosada in Plaza de Mayo)*. Radio Universidad Nacional de La Plata. Retrieved September 27, 2024, from https://sedici.unlp.edu.ar/handle/10915/27206. CC BY 2.5.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A cartoon published by the communist publication Orientación that depicts its view of the demonstrations of October 17, 1945 in the Plaza de Mayo, Buenos Aires. On October 24, he following image appeared on its cover. The caption of the image reads: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The colonel displayed his cast of thugs and criminals, already familiar to the country from the events of the 17th and 18th. The most regrettable thing is that, alongside this cast, he managed to mislead a few honest workers lacking experience and political insight.\" The fishing rod is labled “Borlenghi” referring to a General Confederation of Labor (Confederación General del Trabajo or CGT) union leader, Ángel Borlenghi, drawing in workers labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$tough guys.” Also it can be seen Perón holding a sword with the initials $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$T y P\" (referring to the Secretaría de Trabajo y Previsión).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Emergence/images/HI289guapos.png\" alt=\"HI289guapos\">\n\n[Source: Sigfredo. (1945, October 24). *Political Cartoon from Orientación.* Accessed via El Partido Comunista de Argentina y los orígenes del peronismo. Un análisis desde su estrategia de Frente Popular by Gabriel Piro Mittelman (2021) in Nuevo Mundo Mundos Nuevos. Retrieved January 8, 2025 from https://journals.openedition.org/nuevomundo/85504. Image in public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor full marks, both similarities and differences must be addressed.\n\nFor comparison, any **one to three** of the following similarities:\n\n - Both sources recognize the significant role of workers in the events of October 17, 1945.\n\n - Both portray the demonstration as an important, large-scale event that marked a key moment in Argentina's history.\n\n - Both acknowledge Perón as the central figure directly tied to the workers' mobilization and its impact on Argentine politics.\n\nFor contrast, any **two to four** of the following differences:\n\n - The cartoon depicts the workers as being deceived by leaders like Borlenghi, suggesting they lacked awareness, while Perón praises their loyalty and political consciousness.\n\n - The cartoon describes the event as chaotic and staged to manipulate people and using  phrases like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mislead a few honest workers\" mock the legitimacy of the demonstrations, suggesting that Perón and his allies exploited inexperienced workers for their own gain. Perón's speech portrays the events as a profound and genuine moment of national pride. Words like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rebirth,\" $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$workers' consciousness,\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$great and immortal Nation\" emphasize a vision of unity and progress led by the working class. \n\n - The cartoon portrays Perón as a dictator holding a sword, linking him to authoritarianism. Perón presents himself as a humble leader serving the workers.\n\n - The cartoon's language, as indicated in the caption, is critical and disparaging, using terms like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$thugs,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$criminals,” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mislead” to portray the event as chaotic and reflects skepticism toward Perón and his policies. While Perón Perón's speech uses positive and inclusive language to celebrate the event as a demonstration of genuine worker empowerment. Using words such as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rebirth,\" $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$workers' consciousness,\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$great and immortal Nation\" emphasize a vision of unity and progress led by the working class. The speech's tone seeks to reinforce loyalty and trust in his leadership, presenting the demonstrations as a legitimate and organic expression of popular will.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth the cartoon and Perón's speech agree on the central role of workers as key participants in the events at the Plaza de Mayo in October of 1945 ($\\checkmark$ similarity), though their interpretation of that role differs ($\\checkmark$ difference). \n\nIn Source B, Perón refers to the event as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rebirth of workers' consciousness,\" highlighting it as a spontaneous and historic movement that can $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$make the Nation great and immortal\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence). He uses emotive language, expressing personal pride by stating his desire to \"embrace all of them deeply against my heart, as I would embrace my mother\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence), portraying the workers, as honorable and central to Argentina's future. In contrast, the cartoon frames the workers as manipulated by leaders like Borlenghi, describing them as a \"cast of thugs and criminals\" and \"a few honest workers lacking experience and political insight\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence), emphasizing deceit and coercion ($\\checkmark$ difference).  Furthermore, the cartoon describes the event as disorderly and orchestrated for political manipulation, ($\\checkmark$ difference) as reflected in the imagery of a fishing rod used to reel in workers ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Perón, by contrast, presents it as a unifying and spontaneous demonstration of loyalty and trust ($\\checkmark$ difference), emphasizing that the people $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$do not deceive those who do not betray them” ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0290","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0290\n\nWhat does Source D suggest about the demonstrations in the Plaza de Mayo in Buenos Aires on October 17, 1945?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Image taken during the large-scale protest in Plaza de Mayo on October 17th 1945.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Emergence/images/HI0286D.png\" alt=\"HI0286D\">\n\n[Source: No author. (1945, October 17). File: *Archivo General de la Nación Argentina 1945 Buenos Aires Plaza de Mayo el 17 de octubre, pies en el agua.jpg*. Archivo General de la Nación Argentina via Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved January 3, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archivo_General_de_la_Naci%C3%B3n_Argentina_1945_Buenos_Aires_Plaza_de_Mayo_el_17_de_octubre,_pies_en_el_agua.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The image suggests the centrality of Plaza de Mayo as a political symbol in Argentine history.\n\n - The image suggests this was a large-scale, mass mobilization.\n\n - The image suggests that many of the attendees were young people.\n\n - The image suggests that participants viewed the demonstration as a patriotic event, evidenced by the flag present in the photo.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource D suggests that many people attended the gather in the Plaza de Mayo in support of Perón ($\\checkmark$). The flag in the image also indicates that the participants felt their demonstration was a form of patriotism for Argentina ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0378","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0378\n\nDescribe **two** causes of the Kornilov Affair.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Kornilov wanted to get rid of the Bolsheviks.\n- Kornilov wanted to get rid of the Provisional Government.\n- Kornilov wanted to restore order in the Russian army.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSupport for the Bolshevik Party began to grow when Lenin argued that there should be a Socialist revolution. Kornilov, head of the army, was a conservative army officer. He ordered his troops to march on Petrograd to address the Bolshevik revolutionary threat ($\\checkmark$).\n\nPetrograd Soviet Order No. 1 called for each military unit to elect a committee to manage the unit. Soldiers' committees had weakened the authority of the officers, which led to desertions and ill-discipline. Kornilov sent troops to Petrograd to remove the Soviet and restore order in the army ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0379","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0379\n\nExplain **two** challenges facing the Provisional Government after March 1917.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- The issue of dual power with the Petrograd Soviet.\n    - The Provisional Government was obliged to share power with the Petrograd Soviet, which had the support of workers and soldiers. The Petrograd Soviet published Order No. 1, which gave it control over the army.\n- The failure of the Summer Offensive led to the July Days.\n    - In June 1917, Kerensky ordered the Summer Offensive, which was a massive failure and defeat. It sparked armed demonstrations and strike action in Petrograd against the Provisional Government.\n- Peasants demanded that land was redistributed.\n    - The Provisional Government refused to give land to the peasants and asked them to wait for the election of the Constituent Assembly. However, some soldiers returned and used their weapons to seize land from the landlords.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Provisional Government was not against the idea of land redistribution but wanted to do it within the framework of the law. Therefore, they asked the peasants to wait for the elections of the Constituent Assembly. However, many of the peasants who deserted from the army returned home and seized land from the landlords ($\\checkmark$). The government sent out punishment brigades to try to stop the land seizures but failed to control the peasants ($\\checkmark$).\n\nTwo Russian governments coexisted as a result of the February Revolution, the Provisional Government and the Petrograd Soviet, which represented workers, sailors and soldiers ($\\checkmark$). The Petrograd Soviet issued Soviet Order Number 1 in March 1917 which ordered soldiers and sailors to not follow orders from the Provisional Government if they contradicted the Soviet ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0439","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0439\n\nExplain the reasons the Provisional Government was unpopular after it took power in Russia in March of 1917. \\\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following reasons with supporting explanation:\n\n - Failure of the Russian offensive during WWI:\n\n     - The Provisional Government continued to fight in the First World War to fulfill Russia's commitment in the Triple Entente. Moreover, the Kerensky government hoped to use the campaign to restore national unity and pride. However, the offensive failed since the Germans and Austrians were able to advance further into Russian territory and deliver more damage to the Russian forces. The failure of the offensive further discredited the Provisional Government as a result. \n\n - Suppression of dissidents during the July Days\n\n     - The July Days started spontaneously when soldiers and workers protested against the Provisional Government due to the failed Kerensky office and dissatisfaction against poor government administration. The Provisional Government then suppressed dissidents in the July Days, which further damaged the Provisional Government's credibility.  \n\n - Actions during the Kornilov Affair\n\n     - The Kornilov Affair further revealed the incompetence of the Provisional Government when Kornilov brought his troops to Petrograd to restore order. The Provisional Government did not have enough troops to defend Petrograd so they asked the Bolsheviks for assistance and armed them. This shows that the Provisional Government was incapable of defending any threat, while the Bolsheviks gained more popularity as they were seen as heroes defending the revolution. \n\n - Limited power during the Dual Power (Dvoyevlastiye) period\n\n     - The Provisional Government was unable to resolve many socio-economic problems in Russia due to the dual-power structure where the Petrograd Soviet was more dominant and had the ability to enforce policies. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Provisional Government in Russia did not withdraw from the First World War, and the failure of the Kerensky Offensive in July 1917 was unpopular among the masses, especially the military ($\\checkmark$). The Kerensky government hoped to use the campaign to restore national unity and pride. However, the offensive failed since the Germans and Austrians were able to advance further into Russian territory and deliver more damage to the Russian forces ($\\checkmark$).\n\nMoreover, the Provisional Government was incompetent, which was exemplified in the Kornilov Affair when Kornilov led his troops into Petrograd to restore order ($\\checkmark$). The Provisional Government did not have enough troops to defend Petrograd, so they asked the Bolsheviks for assistance and armed them. This shows that the Provisional Government was incapable of defending any threat ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0447","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0447\n\nCompare and contrast what sources Source D and E reveal about the dictatorship of the proletariat.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from Democracy and Dictatorship, written by Lenin, published in 1918. |\n\n[Source: Lenin, V. (1918). *Democracy and Dictatorship.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved February 11, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/lenin/works/1918/dec/23.htm. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source E** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from Marx and the Dictatorship of the Proletariat written by Julius Martov, a Menshivik member. Published in 1918. |\n\n[Source: Martov, J. (1918). *Marx and the Dictatorship of the Proletariat*. Marxist Internet Archive. Retrieved February 11, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/martov/1918/xx/marxdp.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources emphasise the importance of the dictatorship of the proletariat in the revolution. Source D: “emancipate humanity” and Source E: “historic struggle.”\n- Both sources emphasise that the capitalists and non-proletariat were the main threat. Source D: “ the oppression of capital” and Source E: “except the class against which the historic struggle of the proletariat is directed.”\n- Both sources targeted the rich as a threat: Source D, “Democracy for the Rich,” and Source E, “Rich Capitalists.”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source D argues that the dictatorship of the proletariat should eradicate all the bourgeois elements from society in order to “emancipate humanity.” Source E suggests that some “healthy elements” of society can still be retained, except for the “rich capitalists.”\n- Source D states that oppression is a political problem arising from “bourgeois democracy” and “democracy for the rich.” Source E states that the threat is primarily the “rich capitalists.”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources emphasised the importance of the dictatorship of the proletariat in the Russian Revolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source D says the dictatorship of the proletariat can “emancipate humanity from oppression…” and Source E says it is engaged in a “historical struggle” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Another similarity is that both sources emphasised capitalists and non-proletariats being the main threat to the revolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source D references “the oppression of capital,” and Source E says the struggle of the proletariat is directed at “the $\\lq$rich capitalists.’” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources differ in their description of the role of the dictatorship of the proletariat ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source D thinks that the dictatorship of the proletariat should eradicate all the bourgeois elements from society to “emancipate humanity.” In contrast, Source E thinks that some “healthy elements” of society can still be retained, except for the “rich capitalists” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Another difference between the sources is that they differ in their description of the nature of the oppression or the threats they faced ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source D states that oppression is a political problem that comes from “bourgeois democracy” and “democracy for the rich.” In contrast, Source E states that the threat is primarily the “rich capitalists” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0746","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0746\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Weak political systems were the most significant factor in the emergence of authoritarian states.” Discuss with reference to **two** states. \n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nCandidates must demonstrate a clear understanding of the question's requirements. In this case, candidates should offer a considered and balanced review of the statement that weak political systems were the most significant factor in the emergence of authoritarian states.\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n\n - Clearly address the role of weak political systems and consider the ways in which they affected the conditions in which authoritarian states emerged.\n\n - Identify other factors important in the emergence of the authoritarian states chosen.\n\n - Offer a considered and balanced review of the hypothesis that economic factors were the most significant determinant of the conditions in which authoritarian states emerged.\n\n - Use a well-structured argument with relevant historical examples and differing perspectives integrated into the answer.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n\n - **Germany** - Adolf Hitler (1933-1945): The Weimar Republic was a fragile democracy established after WWI, plagued by hyperinflation, political extremism, proportional representation that led to unstable coalition governments, and emergency powers under Article 48. Hitler exploited public disillusionment with democratic inefficiency, especially during the Great Depression, and used the Reichstag Fire and the Enabling Act to dismantle democratic structures. Other factors included Nationalist resentment over the Treaty of Versailles, economic crisises and Nazi propaganda..\n\n - **China** - Mao Zedong (1949-1976): After the fall of the Qing dynasty, China's Republican government under the KMT struggled with warlords, corruption, and failure to implement effective national governance, especially in rural areas. Mao and the CCP capitalized on the KMT's inability to unify China or address peasant concerns, offering land reform, anti-Japanese resistance, and ideological alternatives.  Other factors included Japanese invasion and CCP grassroots mobilization helped the Communists gain legitimacy and support.\n\n - **Italy** - Benito Mussolini (1922-1943): Post-WWI Italy had a liberal parliamentary monarchy that failed to deliver economic recovery, territorial gains (the “mutilated victory”), or political stability, resulting in frequent government changes. Mussolini exploited public anger and fear of socialism by forming paramilitary Blackshirts and securing support from conservative elites and the king, who appointed him Prime Minister. Other factors included Nationalist sentiment, elite support, and Mussolini's charisma and propaganda skills.\n\n - **Russia/USSR** - Lenin (1917-1924): The Provisional Government was weak, politically divided, and failed to address key issues such as ending Russia's involvement in World War I, land reforms, and economic hardship, which led to widespread dissatisfaction. Lenin and the Bolshevik Party capitalized on the Provisional Government's failures by promising \"peace, land, and bread.\" Lenin led the October Revolution (1917), which successfully overthrew the Provisional Government and established Bolshevik rule. Other factors included Lenin's skillful use of propaganda, control over the Soviet press, and his leadership in the October Revolution enabled him to rally support, particularly from soldiers and workers. \n\n - **Spain** - Francisco Franco (193-1975): The Second Spanish Republic (1931–1939) was deeply divided between left and right, leading to social unrest, political assassinations, and economic instability. Franco led a military rebellion that capitalized on the Republic's weaknesses, eventually winning the Civil War and establishing a military dictatorship. Other factors included support from Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy, as well as disunity amongst the republicans.\n\n - **Argentina** - Juan Peron (1946-1955, 1973-1974): Argentina's democratic institutions were undermined by political instability, military interference, and economic inequality during the 1930s and 1940s. Peron used his military background and popular support among workers to gain power, presenting himself as a stabilizing, nationalist force. Other factors include his populist appeal and ability to use mass media and labour unions.\n\n - **Cuba** - Fidel Castro (1959-2008): Batista's Cuba was a U.S. backed authoritarian regime plagued by corruption, repression, and inequality, with growing unpopularity. Castro used guerrilla warfare, populist rhetoric, and anti-imperialist sentiment to rally support and overthrow Batista. Other factors included widespread public dissatisfaction, U.S. support for Batista, and Castro's charismatic leadership.\n\n - **Egypt** - Gamal Abdel Nasser (1954-1970): Egypt under King Farouk was a nominal constitutional monarchy, but it was plagued by corruption, inefficiency, British influence, elite dominance, electoral manipulation, and a lack of popular trust in its institutions. Nasser and the Free Officers Movement capitalized on dissatisfaction with Farouk's rule, particularly after Egypt's defeat in the 1948 Arab-Israeli War, using the monarchy's failure to address key issues to justify the 1952 coup. Other factors include anti-imperialist sentiment, rising Arab nationalism, and Nasser's charisma and control over the media and military.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn both Germany under Adolf Hitler and China under Mao Zedong, weak political systems played a central role in the emergence of the authoritarian regimes. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$)\n\n In Germany, the Weimar Republic's structural instability, constitutional flaws, and inability to manage economic crises enabled Hitler's rise. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) In China, the Kuomintang's (KMT) failure to establish effective national governance and address rural issues created space for the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) to gain support and seize power. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  This essay will first examine how the weaknesses of the Weimar system facilitated the Nazi seizure of power. It will then assess the KMT's ineffective political leadership and how it contributed to the CCP's rise. Finally, it will acknowledge other contributing factors while reinforcing that political weakness was the most significant cause in both cases. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$) \n\nIn Germany, the weakness of the Weimar Republic was a fundamental factor in Hitler's rise to power. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) Established in 1919 after Germany's defeat in World War I, the Weimar system was created in crisis and remained fragile throughout its existence. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) It relied on proportional representation, which led to unstable coalition governments and frequent changes in leadership, over a dozen different governments between 1919 and 1933, each struggling to maintain political cohesion. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) This instability made it difficult to pass decisive legislation or confront growing threats such as extremism. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) Moreover, Article 48 of the Weimar Constitution, which allowed the president to rule by decree in times of emergency, was increasingly abused. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  By the early 1930s, Germany was essentially governed by presidential decree, paving the way for authoritarianism. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) \n\nEconomic crises further exposed the fragility of Weimar democracy and increased the public's desire for strong leadership. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)  The Great Depression hit Germany especially hard, with unemployment peaking at over six million in 1932. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  The Weimar government appeared powerless to stop the economic collapse, deepening popular disillusionment. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)  Hitler and the Nazi Party capitalized on this discontent by promising national rejuvenation, economic recovery, and a return to traditional values. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) Their rise was not solely due to their message, but also due to the political vacuum following the fall of Muller's Great Coalition in 1930, which was followed by a period where Chancellors increasingly relied on Article 48, rather than consulting parliament. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) Hitler's appointment as Chancellor in 1933, followed by the Enabling Act, which gave him dictatorial powers, was the culmination of the Weimar system's failures. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) Without these political weaknesses, the Nazis would likely have faced much stronger resistance in their path to power. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) \n\nThe rise of Mao Zedong and the CCP was also facilitated by a weak political system under the KMT. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) After the fall of the Qing dynasty in 1911, China entered a prolonged period of instability marked by warlordism, civil war, and foreign invasion. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  The Republic of China under the KMT, particularly after Chiang Kai-shek took control in the late 1920s, was never able to unify the country or establish effective governance. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) While the Nationalist government made some progress in modernizing urban areas, it remained corrupt, authoritarian, and disconnected from the rural majority. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)  This inability to create a stable and inclusive political system left large parts of the population alienated and open to alternative forms of leadership. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) \n\nThe KMT's inability to address land reform, rural poverty, and Japanese aggression eroded its legitimacy and contributed to the CCP's rise. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)  During the Second Sino-Japanese War (1937-1945), the KMT focused on defending cities and preserving military strength, often at the expense of the rural population. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  In contrast, the CCP used guerrilla tactics to resist the Japanese in occupied territories and implemented land reforms to win over the peasantry. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) The failure of the KMT to mobilize mass support and govern effectively during this critical period revealed the weakness of its political structure. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) This enabled the CCP to portray itself as the true defender of China and laid the foundation for its eventual victory in the Chinese Civil War (1946-49). ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) The collapse of the KMT regime was not solely a result of Communist strength, but also a reflection of its own systemic weaknesses. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) \n\nHowever, while weak political systems were central in both Germany and China, other factors also played important roles. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) In Germany, nationalist resentment over the Treaty of Versailles, widespread anti-communist sentiment, and the Nazi Party's skillful propaganda machinery contributed to Hitler's appeal. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  Similarly, in China, the CCP's ideological flexibility, grassroots mobilization, and military strategy were critical to their success. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)  Nevertheless, these factors became effective only because the existing political systems failed to provide stability, legitimacy, and effective government. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) \n\nIn conclusion, weak political systems were the most significant factor in the emergence of authoritarian states in both Germany and China. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)  The inability of the Weimar Republic and the KMT regime to manage political, economic, and social crises created opportunities for authoritarian leaders to seize power. (✓ analysis) While other factors contributed to their success, they were secondary to the collapse or failure of existing political structures. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)"},{"ref":"HI1126","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1126\n\nOutline **three** methods used by the Nazis to increase their electoral support up to 1932.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Nazis made widespread use of propaganda.\n- The Nazis blamed several groups for Germany's suffering.\n- Hitler attacked the Treaty of Versailles.\n- The Nazis used violence against the opposition.\n- Hitler made public speeches across Germany.\n- Hitler provided solutions to the problems of the Great Depression.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Great Depression significantly impacted the lives of the German people. In 1929, approximately 1.4 million Germans were unemployed. By 1933, this had increased to 6 million. The Nazis appealed to the unemployed by promising jobs in road-building and public works ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the Nazis used propaganda to attack the government and criticise its inability to get Germany out of the depression. Radio broadcasts, millions of election posters, rallies, parades, and marches carried out the Nazi message in every town and home in Germany ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the Nazi leadership focused attention on the communist threat; as a result, they encouraged the use of violence against the communists. The *Sturmabteilung* (SA) frequently attacked communists and disrupted Communist Party meetings ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1127","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1127\n\nExplain **one** reason the economy of the Weimar Republic collapsed after 1929.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- The German economy depended on US loans.\n    - In 1924 the Dawes Plan provided an American loan of 800 million gold marks to help kick-start the German economy. This initiative brought about a marked economic revival: inflation fell, industry expanded, and exports increased.\n- The  US stock market crashed in 1929.\n    - Wall Street, New York was the main American stock market where company shares were bought and sold. In October 1929 the panic selling of shares in this market led to a crash which marked the beginning of the Great Depression.\n- The German economy depended on US loans.\n    - American business failure put pressure on the banking system and many of the loans offered to Germany in the period since 1924 were recalled. This meant that the German economy suffered a severe blow.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1924, through the Dawes Plan, Germany was loaned 800 million marks by the USA to invest in industry and commerce. The Dawes Plan was a success because the economy began to recover. By 1930, Germany was one of the world's leading exporters of manufactured goods. However, the German economy was dangerously dependent on the continuation of American loans ($\\checkmark$). Shortly after the Wall Street Crash of October 1929, the American economy went into recession, triggering an economic crisis in Germany. Industrial production declined sharply because American loans and investment dried up, and the industrialised nations reduced their imports. Prices and wages fell, and the number of bankruptcies increased ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1128","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1128\n\nExplain **two** ways the economic problems caused by the Great Depression contributed to Hitler's rise to power.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- The Nazis promised to solve unemployment.\n    - The Great Depression caused an economic crisis in Germany. In Germany, there were wage cuts, short-time working, unemployment, homelessness, and poverty on a scale never seen before. In desperation, voters changed support to the party that promised full employment, the Nazis.\n- Wealthy industrialists supported Hitler.\n    - The economic depression also led to an increase in support for the Communists. They argued that the root cause of Germany's problems was capitalism, and this frightened the middle and upper-class Germans. Wealthy industrialists began to support the Nazi Party and gave financial support to the Nazis to help them in their election campaigns.\n- The Nazis blamed the Weimar Republic.\n    - The Depression caused massive unemployment and hardship in Germany in the early 1930s. The main political parties, like the Social Democrats, were blamed for this, and people began to turn to the Nazis, who had always criticised the Weimar Republic and its governments.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Great Depression caused an economic crisis in Germany. By January 1932, unemployment stood at 6.1 million. For those who lost their job, there was poverty, hunger, and homelessness. Many people lost faith in the Weimar Republic ($\\checkmark$), which seemed to offer no solutions to the problems of rising unemployment and growing poverty, and began to see salvation in the solutions offered by the Nazi Party. Nazi Party members delivered messages that appealed to the unemployed and industrial workers by aiming to overcome the depression and providing work on road building and public works ($\\checkmark$). As the economic crisis deepened, support for the Communists also rose. In the general election of September 1930, the Communists polled more than 4.5 million votes, making them the third-largest party in the Reichstag. Business leaders feared the Communists due to their plans to introduce nationalization and state control of businesses ($\\checkmark$). The Nazis turned this to their advantage by playing on the fears of communism. They portrayed the Communists as scheming revolutionaries. Business leaders felt the Nazis would combat this threat, and some began to put money into Nazi election campaigns ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1129","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1129\n\nExplain **two** ways Goebbels' propaganda campaign contributed to Hitler's rise to power.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Goebbels directed propaganda in accordance with people's social and economic interests.\n    - Specific leaflets were produced for different social groups, and Nazi speakers paid particular attention to the worries and concerns of the individual clubs and societies they addressed.\n- Goebbels exploited modern technology.\n    - Goebbels hired airplanes for the practical purpose of transporting Hitler to as many places as possible, but also to project a statesman figure.\n- Goebbels played on the idea of $\\lq$scapegoats'.\n    - Nazism focused on several identifiable groups which were denounced and blamed for Germany's suffering: the $\\lq$November criminals', Communists, and Jews.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGoebbels tailored the Nazi propaganda message to fit a whole range of people ($\\checkmark$). Specific leaflets were produced for different social groups, and Nazi speakers delivered messages tailored to appeal to their target audiences. For example, workers were promised jobs, farmers higher prices, and shopkeepers protection against competition. Nazi policies were flexible, so many Germans could find something that appealed to them ($\\checkmark$). The German people supported the Nazis not because they shared Nazis' views but because they shared the Nazis' fears. This was achieved by Goebbels who played on the idea of $\\lq$scapegoats' as Nazi propaganda denounced and blamed several groups for Germany's problems ($\\checkmark$).Firstly, Nazi propaganda whipped up fear and hatred of the Communists. It was seen as a threat to traditional values and the ownership of property and wealth. Secondly, it exploited the long-established history of anti-Semitism in Germany and held the Jews responsible for all Germany's suffering. Thirdly, Nazism blamed the $\\lq$November criminals' for German acceptance of the Armistice. These politicians became representative of all aspects associated with the Weimar Republic ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1130","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1130\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of the Great Depression on Germany.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Germany saw unemployment rocket to 25% of the working population.\n- Increased homelessness and malnutrition.\n- Portrayed Hitler as a saviour who would rebuild Germany.\n- Increased anti-communist fears.\n- Decrease in support for Weimar parties.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGermany's industrial recovery was built on borrowing from US banks. When the US stock market crashed in October 1929, the banks lending money to Germany needed it back, causing a lack of funds for Germany's postwar recovery ($\\checkmark$). Many Germans lacked faith in the Weimar government, as it seemed to have no clear idea about how to address the problems of rising unemployment and growing poverty, increasing distrust in its leadership ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1131","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1131\n\nDescribe **four** factors that helped the Bolshevik seizure of power.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- The Provisional Government decided to continue fighting in the First World War.\n- The Provisional Government did not address the land issue.\n- Lenin's promises were more attractive than the Provisional Government's policies.\n- The Petrograd Soviet undermined Kerensky's authority.\n- Kerensky failed to deal with Kornilov, and this strengthened Lenin's position.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nRussia's continued involvement in the First World War led to higher casualties, closure of factories and further food and fuel shortages. Lenin, leader of the Bolsheviks, was able to set the Bolsheviks up as the only anti-war party against the Provisional Government ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Provisional Government refused to solve the land issue and asked peasants to wait for the election of the Constituent Assembly, which led to many peasants seizing land from their landlords. The Bolsheviks recognized the land seizures, which improved their previously weak support in the countryside ($\\checkmark$).\n\nLenin offered an end to the unpopular war, the transfer of agricultural land from the nobility to the peasants and food for the hungry. These were issues which Kerensky failed to address. Support for the Bolsheviks increased quickly, particularly in the soviets and in the army ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Petrograd Soviet controlled the armed forces. By September 1917, the Bolsheviks had control of the Petrograd Soviet, and Trotsky was its chairman. He formed the Military Revolutionary Committee and Red Guard units, which he used to seize power in November 1917 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1132","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1132\n\nDescribe **three** consequences of the July Days on the rule of the Provisional Government.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Kerensky used troops to crush the rebellion.\n- Kerensky took over the Provisional Government.\n- Kerensky arrested Bolshevik leaders.\n- Kerensky reintroduced the death penalty.\n- Kerensky appointed Kornilov as the head of the army.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn July 1917 workers and soldiers of Petrograd staged armed demonstrations against the Provisional Government. The government was forced to bring in troops to end the riots. The July Days resulted in the formation of a stronger Provisional Government led by Kerensky, aimed at restoring order ($\\checkmark$).\n\nTo restore military discipline and crush revolutionary elements, the Provisional Government became more hardline in approach. Kerensky, the new leader, reintroduced the death penalty on the front lines for rebelling, deserting and disorderly soldiers ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn August 1917, Prime Minister Kerensky appointed Kornilov as Supreme Commander of the Russian army.  Led to the Kornilov Affair. In September 1917, Kornilov attempted a military coup against the Provisional Government and the Petrograd Soviet ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1133","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1133\n\nExplain the significance of the Kornilov Affair in the Bolsheviks' rise to power in 1917.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- The Bolsheviks organised an army called the Red Guard.\n    - Kornilov took troops to restore order by marching on Petrograd which panicked Kerensky. He released Bolshevik prisoners and organised an army, the Red Guards.\n- The Bolsheviks kept their arms after the defeat of Kornilov.\n    - Kerensky also armed the Bolshevik Red Guard with rifles and ammunition, which they kept. These weapons were used by the Bolsheviks in the November Revolution.\n- The Affair increased political support for the Bolsheviks in the soviets.\n    - The Bolsheviks removed the threat of Kornilov. By the end of September 1917, the Bolsheviks had control of the Petrograd Soviet and Leon Trotsky was its chairman.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn September 1917, the army leader Kornilov decided to send troops into Petrograd to remove the Petrograd Soviet and the Provisional Government ($\\checkmark$). Kerensky was forced to free imprisoned Bolshevik leaders and arm them to halt the coup. Trotsky, a recent recruit to the Bolsheviks, organized an armed Red Guard of 25,000 volunteer soldiers to defend Petrograd ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Red Guard dug trenches and fortified the city. They also persuaded Kornilov's soldiers to stand down ($\\checkmark$). The Bolsheviks gained credit for leading resistance among the workers. By the end of September, they had control of the Petrograd Soviet and Trotsky was elected chairman. He formed the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet to plan and execute the October Revolution. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1134","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1134\n\nDescribe **two** ways Trotsky contributed to the downfall of the Provisional Government.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Chaired the Petrograd Soviet from September 1917.\n- Sided with Lenin in favour of an armed uprising against the Provisional Government.\n- Used the Military Revolutionary Committee to organise the seizure of power.\n- Ordered the Red Guards to take control of Petrograd’s bridges and railway stations.\n- Led the counterattacks against Cossack regiments after the insurrection.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTrotsky, as chairman of the Petrograd Soviet, took the initiative in the formation of a Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet. He used this organisation to carry out an armed insurrection against the Provisional Government ($\\checkmark$).\n\nTrotsky, as chairman of the Petrograd Soviet, organised the Red Guards, an armed workers’ group. On 6 and 7 November, the Red Guards led by Trotsky took control of Petrograd's post offices, bridges, railways and the State Bank ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1135","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1135\n\nOutline **three** key events leading to the Bolshevik seizure of power in November 1917.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- On November 2, the Petrograd Soviet sets up a Military Revolutionary Committee.\n- On November 6, Red Guards took control of Petrograd’s post offices, bridges and the State Bank.\n- On November 7, Red Guards took control of railways\n- On November 7, the Bolsheviks stormed the Winter Palace.\n- On November 8, an announcement was made to the Russian people.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOn November 6, the Red Guard, led by Leon Trotsky, took control of post offices, bridges and the State Bank ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOn 7 November, without much opposition, the Bolsheviks stormed the Winter Palace and arrested the ministers of the Provisional Government ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOn 8 November, the Petrograd Soviet proclaimed the Provisional Government was replaced by an administration headed by Lenin ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1150","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1150\n\nExplain **three** methods Fidel Castro used to establish authoritarian control in Cuba.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Creation of a one-party state:\n    - Castro banned opposing political parties, so there was no political competition.\n- Eliminated opposition\n    - He placed loyal revolutionaries in positions of power and command - political opponents were imprisoned or executed, and independent labour unions and human rights organisations were not allowed.\n- Established the Committees for the Defense of the Revolution in 1960\n    - This was a system of neighborhood surveillance that encouraged people to monitor others and report acts of disloyalty. Created an environment of fear and distrust.\n- Censorship and propaganda:\n    - All news outlets were state-controlled, and the media was used to glorify the revolution and Castro's leadership. Access to foreign media and information was also restricted.\n- Ideological indoctrination\n    - Educational programmes were used as a way of promoting the ideas of the revolution.\n- State control of the economy\n    - Nationalisation of industries and agriculture gave the state increased power.\n- Use of fear\n    - Public arrests, censorship, and the disappearance of opposition increased the public's fear and enabled greater governmental control.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTo establish authoritarian control over Cuba, Castro had to gain political and economic control, and also control over Cuban culture and the media. The establishment of a one-party state was central to his success ($\\checkmark$). The banning of political opposition helped him achieve control and was a key factor in allowing further measures to be implemented ($\\checkmark$). Castro placed loyal revolutionaries in positions of power and command, which enabled him to control any opponents ($\\checkmark$). Political opponents were imprisoned or executed, and independent labor unions and human rights organisations were not allowed ($\\checkmark$). Ideological indoctrination played a key role in establishing his ideas ($\\checkmark$). By creating educational programs, Castro was able to promote the ideas of the revolution, and success here was also supported by increasing literacy rates among the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1299","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1299\n\nExplain two factors that influenced Mao’s emergence as an authoritarian leader in China.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation: \n\n- Economic problems\n\n    - Hyperinflation, corruption, and mismanagement under the Nationalist government destroyed savings and trust, pushing public opinion toward the Communist Party. Mao’s early land reform policies in the CCP-controlled areas helped legitimize him as a leader and earn trust.\n\n- Conflict\n\n    - The Second Sino-Japanese War allowed the CCP to expand its influence and gain military experience while simultaneously weakening the Nationalist army and government.\n\n- Warlordism/political instability\n\n    - Warlordism in the period preceding Mao’s control gave the central government limited authority and made alternative governance appealing in the eyes of the public.\n\n- Persuasion and coercion\n\n    - Mao’s Yan’an Rectification Movement pushed for ideological uniformity and Mao’s charismatic speeches gained him personal fame and loyalty, helping establish his cult of personality. Mao also used purges, such as the Futian Incident, to help remove dissenters and rivals.\n\n- Mao’s leadership\n\n    - Mao’s leadership through the Long March (1934-1935) helped cement him as a heroic and resilient leader. His use of guerrilla tactics to outmaneuver the GMT also allowed the CCP to survive and expand.\n\n- Use of force\n\n    - Red Army actions to defeat the GMT during the civil war, as well as his use of party purges and executions.\n\n- Propaganda\n\n    - Mao used propaganda to establish his culty of personality both before his complete control and after. Control of party newspapers, education and art in Yan’an helped promote his achievements.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nA critical factor behind Mao’s rise was his use of conflict and violence, both military and political, in China ($\\checkmark$). Both the war against Japan (1937–45) and the subsequent Civil War created conditions that allowed the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) to expand its influence. During the Japanese invasion, the Nationalist government bore the bulk of the fighting, suffered major losses, and saw its authority erode. At the same time, the CCP utilised the war years to consolidate its base areas, recruit followers, and present itself as a patriotic and disciplined force that could protect ordinary citizens. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nA second significant factor was the weakness of the existing political system, which struggled to provide effective national governance ($\\checkmark$). The Nationalist regime faced chronic corruption, factional disputes, and limited control outside major cities. Many rural regions remained under the influence of local warlords, undermining the authority of the central government. This lack of political cohesion made it easier for the CCP to offer itself as a credible alternative ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1300","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1300\n\nExplain how the Long March contributed to Mao’s emergence as an authoritarian leader in China.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nAny of the following:\n\n- Allowed Mao to outmaneuver rival factions inside the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) after early defeats.\n\n- Strategic proposals like the use of guerrilla tactics proved effective and allowed him to gain greater control of decision-making.\n\n- Helped establish Mao’s cult of personality and elevated his image above other prominent figures in the CCP. \n\n- Propaganda, including poetry, songs, posters and later films, about the march was used extensively both before his full control in China and throughout his time as leader.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\nThe Long March contributed to Mao’s emergence by enabling him to outmaneuver rival factions within the Chinese Communist Party. As other leaders were discredited by early failures, Mao’s strategic ideas, especially his emphasis on guerrilla tactics, proved more effective, allowing him to take control of key decisions ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the Long March became central to the creation of Mao’s cult of personality. Party propaganda, through stories, songs, and later films, portrayed him as the inspirational figure who had guided the Communists to survival, helping solidify his authority as an unquestioned leader. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI1472","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1472\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The impact of war was the most important condition in the emergence of authoritarian states.\" Discuss with reference to two states, each chosen from a different region.[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n \n\n**General Guidance:**\n\n- Answers should frame $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$impact of war” as a condition for emergence (destruction of existing institutions, militarization of politics, social polarization, etc.) and will likely explore $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mergence” as a process rather than a single event (breakdown of previous systems and the construction of new authoritarian structures)\n\n- The best answers will compare two states from different regions in an integrated way (how war shaped each case differently) and evaluate relative importance by contrasting war with other conditions (economic crisis, ideological factors, political culture, etc.) to reach a balanced judgment (war may be decisive in some cases, but only contributory in others).\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n\n- Spain, Franco, and the Spanish Civil War (Europe)\n\n    - The Spanish Civil War (1936-1939) destroyed the democratic Second Republic and created a power vacuum that enabled Franco's rise.\n\n    - The war allowed Franco to eliminate political rivals, unify Nationalist factions, and establish himself as the sole military and political leader.\n\n    - The conflict generated extreme polarization, legitimizing authoritarianism as a means to restore $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$order” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$national unity.”\n\n    - Military victory provided Franco with ideological legitimacy, framing his rule as the outcome of a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$crusade” against communism and regional separatism.\n\n    - The postwar reconstruction of the state was entirely shaped by wartime dynamics, enabling centralized control, repression, and the institutionalization of authoritarian rule. \n\n    - Impact: In Spain, war was the most important condition for the emergence of Franco's authoritarian state.\n\n- China, Mao, and the Chinese Civil War (Asia)\n\n    - Mao's emergence was closely tied to prolonged conflict, including the Second Sino-Japanese War and the Chinese Civil War.\n\n    - The war against Japan weakened the Nationalist government, eroded its legitimacy, and allowed the CCP to expand its territorial control and popular support.\n\n    - The CCP used wartime conditions to build parallel institutions, mobilize peasants, and present itself as a disciplined alternative to the corruption of the Nationalists.\n\n    - The civil war (1946–1949) provided Mao with opportunities to consolidate leadership within the CCP and demonstrate military effectiveness.\n\n    - Victory in the civil war enabled the creation of a new political order centered on Mao's authority and justified through revolutionary struggle. \n\n    - Impact: In China, war was a central condition for Mao's emergence, although ideology and social mobilization also played significant roles.\n\n- Cuba, Castro, and the Cuban Revolutionary War (Americas)\n\n    - The failure of Batista's military responses during the armed struggle weakened state authority and increased support for the revolutionary movement.\n\n    - The experience of guerrilla warfare allowed Castro to build organizational cohesion and present the 26 July Movement as a legitimate alternative to the collapsing regime.\n\n    - The victory in 1959 created a power vacuum that enabled the rapid centralization of authority and the construction of new authoritarian structures.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n \n\n$\\\\$\n\n \n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n \n\nThe Spanish Civil War and Chinese Civil War provide contrasting opportunities to explore the role of war in the emergence of authoritarian rule ($\\checkmark$ focus). For Franco's emergence in Spain, war played a decisive role, whereas in China, the civil war was transformative, though not exclusive. The argument, therefore, demands an analysis of both the destructive and constructive effects of war, as well as the extent to which prewar conditions shaped authoritarian outcomes. This essay will compare Spain and China to assess the relative importance of war in their authoritarian trajectories and argue that war was essential to Franco's rise but only one of several significant conditions in Mao's emergence. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nIn Spain, the Spanish Civil War destroyed the democratic Second Republic, creating the structural vacuum that enabled Franco's ascent. The war produced extreme polarization, militarized politics, and legitimized authoritarian solutions to perceived national chaos. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Franco's rise depended on the wartime unification of Nationalist factions, which enabled him to eliminate rivals and establish himself as the sole military and political leader. The conflict also provided ideological legitimacy, as Franco framed his victory as a crusade against communism, secularism, and regional separatism. The postwar reconstruction of the state was shaped entirely by wartime dynamics, enabling centralized control, repression, and the institutionalization of authoritarian rule. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The impact of war in Spain was therefore not merely contributory but foundational. Without the Civil War, Franco would not have gained the authority, legitimacy, or institutional control required to establish an authoritarian regime. The war created the conditions for the collapse of the previous system, the consolidation of military power, and the ideological justification for authoritarianism. This makes war the most important condition in the emergence of Franco's state, outweighing other factors such as prewar political instability or economic tensions. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nChina presents a contrasting case. Mao's emergence did not originate solely in war but in a combination of ideological mobilization, social structures, and political fragmentation that preceded major conflict. Before full-scale war, the Chinese Communist Party had already developed organizational discipline, a distinct ideological program, and a base of rural support. The weakness of the Nationalist government, marked by corruption, factionalism, and limited control over rural areas, created structural conditions that facilitated Mao's rise. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The CCP's early experiences in guerrilla warfare and the Long March strengthened Mao's leadership position and allowed him to consolidate authority within the party before the decisive phases of conflict. War nevertheless played a transformative role. The Second Sino-Japanese War weakened the Nationalist government militarily and politically, eroding its legitimacy and enabling the CCP to expand its influence. The CCP used wartime conditions to build parallel institutions, mobilize peasants, and present itself as a disciplined alternative to the failing Nationalist regime. The Chinese Civil War further strengthened Mao's position, as military victories allowed him to eliminate rivals and demonstrate strategic effectiveness. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Wartime mobilization also reinforced ideological cohesion, as Mao framed the struggle as both a national liberation effort and a revolutionary transformation of Chinese society. However, war was not the only significant condition in Mao's emergence. Ideology played a central role, providing a coherent framework that justified social mobilization and political restructuring. The CCP's emphasis on land reform, peasant participation, and anti-corruption campaigns created a broad base of support that was not solely dependent on wartime dynamics. Structural weaknesses in the Nationalist government, including economic crisis, inflation, and administrative collapse, also contributed to Mao's rise. These factors interacted with wartime conditions but were not created by them. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nFranco's portrayal of the Civil War as a crusade against communism can be read either as a genuine ideological conviction or as a retrospective justification for an opportunistic seizure of power. The convenient early deaths of rival Nationalist generals Sanjurjo and Mola, which cleared Franco's path to sole command, support the justification reading: ideology legitimized power already seized rather than caused its seizure. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Similarly, official CCP narratives present the united front against Japan, and the mobilization of the peasantry as principled national liberation, yet the CCP used the same period to expand its territorial base and strengthen its position relative to the Nationalists — suggesting nationalist framing coexisted with calculated strategic self-interest. Evaluating these competing readings suggests that in both cases, war supplied not only the material conditions for authoritarian consolidation but also a rhetorical resource that leaders shaped after the fact. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nThe comparison also highlights differences in leadership dynamics. Franco's authority stemmed directly from wartime command, whereas Mao's leadership developed through a combination of ideological innovation, party consolidation, and military experience. In Spain, the war unified factions under a single leader, whereas in China, Mao had already established his position within the CCP before the decisive phases of conflict. The ideological role of war also differed: Franco used war to justify national reconstruction, whereas Mao used war to reinforce revolutionary ideology that predated the conflict. These differences underscore the need to evaluate war within broader historical contexts rather than assume its universal primacy. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nIn conclusion, war was the most important condition in the emergence of Franco's authoritarian state, but only one of several significant conditions in Mao's rise. The Spanish Civil War laid the structural, ideological, and military foundations for Franco's rule, making war essential to the authoritarian outcome. In China, prewar ideological development, rural mobilization, and structural weaknesses in the Nationalist government played more significant roles, with war serving primarily to accelerate and consolidate existing tendencies. The impact of war, therefore, cannot be generalized across cases. Its relative importance depends on the specific historical context and the interplay of structural and ideological factors. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:-:|\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus) |\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge) |\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark$ analysis) |\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion) |"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"HI0022","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0022\n\nCompare and contrast the economic policies of Nazi Germany under Adolf Hitler and Communist China under Mao Zedong.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both regimes implemented state-controlled economic policies to rapidly transform their nations' economies.\n- Both leaders pursued massive infrastructure projects to boost industrialization (e.g., Hitler's Autobahn; Mao's Great Leap Forward).\n- Both economies emphasised the role of agriculture, with Hitler focusing on achieving autarky (self-sufficiency) and Mao attempting to collectivise agriculture.\n- Both leaders used their economic policies to consolidate political power and strengthen the state.\n- Both Hitler and Mao's policies led to widespread human suffering due to mismanagement and repression (e.g., famine in China, forced labour in Germany).\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Hitler's economic policies aimed to prepare Germany for war and included rearmament, while Mao's policies were primarily focused on domestic transformation, particularly during the early years.\n- Hitler's economic approach was capitalist but highly controlled by the state, allowing some private enterprise under strict state regulation. Mao's policies were rooted in communist ideology, focusing on complete state control of both agriculture and industry.\n- Hitler's policies initially brought economic recovery and success in reducing unemployment, while Mao's Great Leap Forward led to economic disaster and widespread famine.\n- Mao's economic reforms were more ideologically driven (aiming for true communism), while Hitler's were largely pragmatic, designed to strengthen the nation militarily and economically for future conflict.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Nazi Germany under Hitler and Communist China under Mao Zedong implemented state-driven economic policies to rapidly transform their nations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). For example, both leaders launched major infrastructure projects, such as the Autobahn in Germany and Mao's Great Leap Forward, to stimulate industrial growth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, both Hitler and Mao sought to restructure agriculture, although Hitler focused on self-sufficiency, while Mao pushed for collectivisation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the economic goals of the two leaders differed. Hitler’s policies were designed to prepare Germany for war through rearmament and military expansion, while Mao’s early policies were centered on domestic reforms, particularly collectivization and industrialization ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While Hitler’s approach allowed for some controlled private enterprise, Mao’s policies were based on strict communist principles, which rejected private ownership and sought complete state control over both agriculture and industry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) Additionally, while Hitler's policies initially led to economic growth and lower unemployment, Mao’s Great Leap Forward resulted in economic collapse and widespread famine ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference and evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0052","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0052\n\nDescribe **two** of the aims of Mussolini’s ‘Battle for Births’ policy.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- A policy to increase the population of Italy 20 million by 1950.\n\n- A policy to increase the population to make more soldiers and workers\n\n- A policy to produce a new generation faithful to fascist Italy. \n\n- A policy to increase the population in order to populate Italy’s colonial holdings in Africa.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe ‘Battle for Births’ was a policy created by Mussolini to increase the population of Italy with a target of 20 million by 1950 ($\\checkmark$). The motivations for this increased population was to supply workers for industry and agriculture ($\\checkmark$) as well as to populate new colonial holdings in Africa ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0053","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0053\n\nExplain **two** methods used to achieve the goals of Mussolini’s ‘Battle for Births’.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following numbered points with at least 1 bullet point included:\n\n$1.$ Incentives and Rewards from the government\n\n- Families with many children were given tax breaks\n\n- Fathers with more than six children were given medals\n\n- Mothers were given ‘honorary titles’ \n\n- Mothers were seen as ‘heroines of the nation’\n\n- Marriage was encouraged with financial incentives\n\n$2.$ Penalties\n\n- Higher taxes on unmarried men and childless couples\n\n- Lack of promotions in jobs or limited career potential\n\n- Educational opportunities limited for women\n\n$3.$ Social pressure/loss of social status\n\n- Propaganda stereotyped woman’s place in the house - pressure to conform\n\n- Unmarried or childless women seen as ‘less than’ \n\n$4.$ Reproductive Rights limited\n\n- Abortion illegal\n\n- Birth control illegal\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThere were several methods which Mussolini used to achieve his ‘Battle for Births’. The first approach was through incentives and/or rewards ($\\checkmark$) from the Italian government including tax breaks for families with many children ($\\checkmark$). \n\nMussolini also used punitive measures to make people afraid of not having children ($\\checkmark$). In contrast to the incentives given to married couples with many children, unmarried people and childless couples paid higher taxes ($\\checkmark$). Job promotions and careers were often given to men who had many children ($\\checkmark$) and women were punished by limiting their educational opportunities in order to encourage them to get married and have children ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0054","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0054\n\nDescribe the Lateran Treaty and discuss its impacts on Italy and the Catholic Church.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **one** of the following points for description:\n\n- A treaty between Italy (the Kingdom of Italy, under Mussolini) and the Catholic Church in 1929\n\n- A treaty which settled the rift between fascist Italy and the Catholic Church which began after Italian unification (1870)\n\n- A series of agreements between fascist Italy (Mussolini) and the Church (the Pope) that was beneficial to both parties.\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following for discussion:\n\n- Impact on fascist Italy:\n\n  - Legitimacy and support of the Church.\n\n    - This was important because many people in Italy were torn between allegiance to the state and/or the Church\n\n    - Helped Mussolini gain more support of the people\n\n    - Strengthened his rule as the Church supported many of Mussolini's domestic policies\n\n- Impact on  the Catholic Church\n\n  - Recognised the Church and the Pope's sovereignty in Vatican City.\n\n  - Made Roman Catholicism the official state religion, therefore giving the Church more power in society - schools were run by the Church for example.\n\n  - Financial compensation settled the 1870 dispute over the loss of the Papal States that occurred with the unification of Italy, as Italy agreed to pay the Church a sum of money.\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Lateran Treaty was an agreement between fascist Italy led by Mussolini and the Catholic Church in 1929 which was intended to benefit both Mussolini and the Church ($\\checkmark$). \n\nMussolini gained legitimacy in the eyes of the country and the international community after healing the conflict between the state and the church through the signing of the Lateran Treaty ($\\checkmark$). This lessening of tension between the two subsequently made it easier for people to support the fascist regime as well ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Church also gained several advantages from the treaty.  Firstly, there was financial compensation in which the Italian government agreed to compensate the Church for the loss of the Papal States in 1870 ($\\checkmark$). The Roman Catholic Church was also given an official status as the state religion and this gave them control over many social aspects of society such as schools ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0055","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0055\n\nEvaluate the extent to which the Catholic Church in Italy was a collaborator in the fascist government of Mussolini.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe response must include both points of view between collaborator and not collaborator.  One point is awarded for evaluation and a maximum of **five** points for ‘evidence’ presented.  There is to be a mix of any of the pro and contra points.\n\nAny combination of the following as evidence:\\\n\nCollaborator:\n\n- By signing the Lateran Treaty, the Church endorsed Mussolini’s government and gave some people more confidence in the regime.\n\n- By signing the Lateran Treaty, the Church accepted money from the fascist regime.\n\n- By signing the Lateran Treaty, the Church accepted the position of being the state Church in a fascist regime and thereby had a large extent on carrying out fascists policies.\n\n- The church reinforced the ‘moral’ order that Mussolini wanted to carry out - particularly in matters where the Church and the fascist state were in agreement (Battle for Births, for example).\n\n- The fascist government put the schools in the hands of the Church which ensured that the students’ education was aligned with both the Church’s values and the state’s values.\n\nNot a collaborator:\n\n- Ideological differences, Mussolini’s fascist goal of totalitarianism is not the same as ‘spiritual authority’. The Church collaborated with the government when it was in its own interests (see above) but remained independent when Mussolini’s aims did not align with Church values.\n\n- Disagreements over Youth policies - in 1931 the Church criticised the government’s attempts to monopolise Italian youth and limit the Church’s role in education. Therefore, the Church’s willingness to stand up to Mussolini when its autonomy was threatened, was demonstrated.\n\n- The Church opposed Mussolini’s anti-Semitic Laws (1938). \n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nAny evaluation statement that aligns with the evidence provided:\n\n- Students may argue that the Church collaborated when it suited them to do so. \n\n- The strongest answers will argue a combination of the Church collaborating when Mussolini’s aims aligned with the Church’s but also recognise that the Church was capable of actively opposing the regime when contradicted their aims. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nTo a large extent, the Catholic Church collaborated with Mussolini’s fascist regime ($\\checkmark$). When the Church signed the Lateran Treaty, they showed support for the government and this impacted the religious population in Italy as they saw it as a show of support for Mussolini ($\\checkmark$). This had a positive effect for him as people were more supportive of the fascist government.  Additionally, the Church also received money from the government which made them complicit in the fascist government ($\\checkmark$). The Church also benefited from being put in charge of the schools which meant that the State and the Church’s values became one and the same ($\\checkmark$). The Church did object to the state trying to force youth movements on the children as they were seen as competition with Church organised youth groups and many people remained in the Church groups ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the Church was opposed to Mussolini’s anti-Semitism laws which came in 1938 ($\\checkmark$). Therefore it can be argued that the Church collaborated when the fascist regime’s goals aligned with the Church's but was capable of opposing the regime when it contradicted the Church’s aims ($\\checkmark$ evaluation) ."},{"ref":"HI0056","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0056\n\nWhy were the Anti-Semitic Laws of 1938 not strongly enforced until 1943? \n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following:\n\n- Many early leading Fascists were Jewish and about 30% of all Jewish Italians belonged to the Fascist Party\n\n- Many local officials in charge of carrying out the laws simply ignored them\n\n- The Catholic Church was publicly against the laws\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAlthough Mussolini created the Anti-Semitic Laws in 1938 in an attempt to ally Italy more strongly with Hitler, the laws were mostly ignored by the local authorities since the Church came out publicly against these them ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0058","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0058\n\nAccording to Source B, what were the aims of the ONMI?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Italian Social Policy for Mother and Child during the World War II (1943–1945)” written by Domenica la Banca, an historian and political scientist, and published in 2016 in the *Prague Economic and Social History Papers (Prager wirtschafts- und sozialhistorische Mitteilungen)*. |\n\n:box[One of the welfare institutions the regime was most proud about was [The National Agency for Maternity and Childhood or Opera nazionale per la protezione della maternità e dell’infanzia, ONMI]. It was considered as the highest expression of the State’s care for Italian women and children. The ONMI, founded in 1925 and located within the Ministry of Interior for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$physical and moral improvement of the race”, was mandated to: protect and assist pregnant women, especially those who were poor or single; nursing and weaned infants up to five years old from needy families; children who are physically or psychologically abnormal; and minors under eighteen years of age who are materially and morally abandoned, deviant or delinquent.]\n\n[Source: La Banca, D. (2016). *Italian Social Policy for Mother and Child during the World War II (1943-1945)*. CEJSH The Central European Journal of Social Sciences and the Humanities. Retrieved October 11th, 2024, from https://dspace.cuni.cz/bitstream/handle/20.500.11956/97014/1513250_domenica_la_banca_35-49.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y. Creative Commons.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- To improve the moral and physical conditions of Italians\n\n- To protect women and children\n\n- To take care of babies and small children from poor families\n\n- To take care of children who are physically or mentally disabled\n\n- To protect minors under 18 who are poor, abandoned or “delinquent”\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe ONMI was a welfare institution whose aim was to take care of women and children. They set up organisations to take care of pregnant women and babies ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, they aimed to take care of children living in poverty ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0060","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0060\n\nWith reference to its **content**, analyse the **value** of Source C for an historian researching the impacts of Mussolini's policies on families. \n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mussolini’s Speech of the Ascension\" from May 26, 1927.|\n\n[Source: Mussolini, Benito, (1925, May 26th).  *Speech of the Ascension*. Biblioteca Fascista. Retrieved October 12th, 2024 from https://bibliotecafascista.blogspot.com/2012/03/speech-of-ascension-may-26-1927.html. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- The source announces the creation of a National Agency to protect women and children. This is a value as it gives the historian a concrete date from which to date their research on the effectiveness of this agency.\n\n- The source indicates Mussolini's intentions in protecting the family and $`$race' which is a value to the historian as it gives insight into Mussolini's motivations for these policies. \n\n- The source also gives the historian information about the former and current overseers of the agency, Minister of the Interior, Federzoni, and Blanc, who were appointed by Mussolini to manage the agency and its policies. This is a value to the historian in establishing cooperation between Mussolini and his Ministers. \n\n- The source also gives an indication about how the government dissolved other agencies and could be a value to the historian in establishing the ways in which Mussolini took over the government and consolidated power. \n\n- The source also gives the information about the financial situation at the time and indicates steps Mussolini would take to address the financial need. This is useful to the historian evaluating the impact of Mussolini's policies concerning women and children on the Italian economy and finances. \n\n- The source may also indicate the pressure that would be put on women in the future to have children through taxes on “bachelors” and “childless marriages”. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe content of this speech is of value to the historian as it hints at Mussolini's ideas on race and on how the fascist government could protect the future of Italy ($\\checkmark$). The source also gives the historian valuable information about the lack of money the state had and Mussolini's solutions as to how to raise money by taxing bachelors and childless couples ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, the source may be valuable as it indicates the pressure women were put under by the fascist state to have children ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0061","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0061\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about Mussolini's domestic aims for Italy. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Italian Social Policy for Mother and Child during the World War II (1943–1945)” written by Domenica la Banca, an historian and political scientist, and published in 2016 in the Prague Economic and Social History Papers (Prager wirtschafts- und sozialhistorische Mitteilungen). |\n\n:box[One of the welfare institutions the regime was most proud about was [The National Agency for Maternity and Childhood or Opera nazionale per la protezione della maternità e dell’infanzia, ONMI]. It was considered as the highest expression of the State’s care for Italian women and children. The ONMI, founded in 1925 and located within the Ministry of Interior for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$physical and moral improvement of the race”, was mandated to: protect and assist pregnant women, especially those who were poor or single; nursing and weaned infants up to five years old from needy families; children who are physically or psychologically abnormal; and minors under eighteen years of age who are materially and morally abandoned, deviant or delinquent.]\n\n[Source: La Banca, D. (2016). *Italian Social Policy for Mother and Child during the World War II (1943-1945)*. CEJSH The Central European Journal of Social Sciences and the Humanities. Retrieved October 11th, 2024, from https://dspace.cuni.cz/bitstream/handle/20.500.11956/97014/1513250_domenica_la_banca_35-49.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y. Creative Commons.] \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mussolini’s Speech of the Ascension, May 26, 1927.|\n\n[Source: Mussolini, Benito, (1925, May 26th).  *Speech of the Ascension*. Retrieved October 12th, 2024 from https://bibliotecafascista.blogspot.com/2012/03/speech-of-ascension-may-26-1927.html. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor comparison, any **two to three** of the following similarities:\n\n- Both sources reflect Mussolini's desire to increase the population\n\n- Both sources highlight the state's intervention in personal and family matters\n\n- Both sources mention the ONMI as a key tool in achieving Mussolini's demographic goals\n\nFor contrast, any **two to three** of the following differences:\n\n- Source B give more detailed information about the ONMI whereas Source D gives Mussolini's ideological point of view\n\n- Source B focuses on the services provided to mothers and families while Source D focuses on the finances needed to provide these services\n\n- Source B focuses more on assisting people, mostly women and children whereas Source D focuses on the racial purity and the need to grow the population\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources discuss Mussolini's domestic aims and include his goal of increasing the population ($\\checkmark$ similiarity). Source B discusses programs designed to cope with larger families and Source D is more clear as Mussolini refers to the need for a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demographic boom” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both sources also show that Mussolini's government should intervene in the personal lives of the people ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B says that the ONMI is, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...the highest expression of the State's care for Italian women and children.” and Source D elucidates the idea that the State should intervene in people's personal lives, for example suggesting taxes on certain family structures including bachelors and childless marriages ($\\checkmark$ evidence). The idea that the ONMI was created to socially engineer Italy is expressed in both Source B and D ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B is more concrete about the structures put in place via the ONMI and Source D is from Mussolini himself announcing the ONMI and his intentions for state involvement in the lives of the people ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nIn terms of contrasts, Source B is more explicit about the services being provided by the ONMI such as assistance to pregnant women, infants and children in need while Source D is more focused on ideological concepts like racial purity and linking the ONMI to that purpose ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source D also gives a more focus on the financial aspects on how this project can be funded saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hence the tax on bachelors, and perhaps in the future there will be a tax on childless marriages.” Source B, in contrast, is not explicit about the funding but rather the purpose for the programs and is focused on the types of services being offered ($\\checkmark$ difference and evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0067","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0067\n\nIdentify and explain one legal method used by Hitler to consolidate power in Germany. \n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne mark for identifying any of the following:\n\n- The Enabling Act (1933)\n\n- The Reichstag Fire Decree (1933)\n\n- The Law Against the Formation of New Parties (1933)\n\n[1 marks]\n\nOne mark for a correct explanation of the legal method used\n\n- The Enabling Act allowed Hitler to pass laws without Reichstag approval, effectively bypassing the democratic process and giving him dictatorial powers.\n\n- The Reichstag Fire Decree suspended civil liberties and enabled the arrest of political opponents, particularly Communists, thereby eliminating opposition to Nazi rule.\n\n- The Law Against the Formation of New Parties made the Nazi Party the only legal political party in Germany, ensuring that no other parties could challenge Hitler’s power and solidifying a one-party state.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne method used by Hitler to consolidate power was the Enabling Act ($\\checkmark$). It was a law that allowed him to bypass the Reichstag entirely, enabling him to create and enforce laws independently, which quickly dismantled democratic structures and solidified his control over Germany ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0068","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0068\n\nCompare and contrast how propaganda was used by Hitler and Mao Zedong to maintain their authority.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAward up to **four** marks for a response that includes comparisons and contrasts between the use of propaganda by Hitler and Mao Zedong. Students should include at least one similarity and one difference to achieve full marks. **One** mark for each accurate similarity/difference.\n\nFor similarities:\n\n- Both Hitler and Mao utilised propaganda to create a cult of personality, portraying themselves as infallible leaders. Hitler was depicted as the savior of Germany through films, speeches, and posters, while Mao was presented as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Great Helmsman” whose thoughts, such as those in the Little Red Book, were treated as guiding principles.\n\n- Both regimes exerted strict control over the media to spread their messages. Hitler used state-controlled media like radio broadcasts and the press to propagate Nazi ideology, while Mao's government tightly controlled newspapers and broadcast media to ensure that only Communist messages were heard.\n\n- Both leaders focused their propaganda efforts on youth to ensure long-term loyalty. Hitler targeted young people through the Hitler Youth organisation, using it to instill Nazi ideology from a young age. Similarly, Mao used the Red Guards, made up largely of students, to spread his messages during the Cultural Revolution and reinforce his ideology among the youth.\n\nFor Differences\n\n- Hitler's propaganda often emphasised themes of Aryan superiority, anti-Semitism, and the need for living space (Lebensraum), targeting specific external and internal enemies like Jews and Communists. In contrast, Mao's propaganda focused more on class struggle, socialist ideals, and mobilising the masses for internal campaigns like the Great Leap Forward and the Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Hitler's propaganda relied heavily on modern mass media, such as cinema and large-scale rallies (e.g., Nuremberg rallies), which helped create a highly orchestrated image of Nazi power. Mao, while also using mass rallies, heavily utilised grassroots methods like distributing the Little Red Book to spread his ideology among peasants and students, ensuring that his thoughts reached all levels of society.\n\n- Hitler's propaganda often centred on grand public spectacles like mass rallies at Nuremberg, designed to showcase the power and unity of the Nazi regime. These events were highly choreographed and meant to project strength. Mao, on the other hand, emphasised mass participation, encouraging the public to engage directly with his propaganda through campaigns like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Speak Bitterness” sessions and distributing the Little Red Book, which aimed to engage people at a grassroots level.\n\n- Hitler's propaganda largely remained consistent throughout his time in power, focusing on the central themes of racial purity, nationalism, and anti-Semitism. Mao's propaganda, however, adapted to different political needs, such as shifting from promoting the success of the Communist Party after 1949 to attacking counter-revolutionaries during the Cultural Revolution, which required a shift in messaging to suit changing campaigns.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth Hitler and Mao used propaganda as a means to foster a cult of personality, portraying themselves as indispensable and almost divine figures ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Each leader maintained tight control over the media landscape in their respective countries—Hitler manipulated radio broadcasts and the press to spread Nazi ideology, while Mao controlled newspapers and radio to ensure a consistent Communist message ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Despite these similarities, the focus of their propaganda efforts differed: Hitler's messaging concentrated on racial superiority and the demonization of Jews, while Mao prioritised themes of class struggle and the promotion of socialist ideals ($\\checkmark$ difference). Moreover, while Hitler often used dramatic, large-scale public events like the Nuremberg rallies to display the unity and power of the Nazi state, Mao relied on more direct engagement with the population, such as the distribution of the Little Red Book and encouraging public participation in revolutionary activities ($\\checkmark$ difference)."},{"ref":"HI0069","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0069\n\nDescribe the significance of the Reichstag Fire Decree in Hitler's consolidation of power.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three**  of the following:\n\n- The Reichstag Fire Decree allowed for the suspension of fundamental civil rights such as freedom of speech, assembly, and the press. This suppression made it easier for the Nazis to silence critics and opposition, reducing the ability of any group to organise against them.\n\n- The decree granted the Nazis the power to arrest and detain political opponents, particularly Communists and socialists, without trial or due process. This enabled Hitler to eliminate opposition parties and secure more control over the political landscape.\n\n- By authorising the use of police and paramilitary forces like the SA and SS, the decree laid the foundation for a police state, allowing the Nazis to use force and intimidation to enforce their rule and maintain power.\n\n- The decree created an environment of fear and eliminated political opposition, which paved the way for the passing of the Enabling Act in March 1933. This act effectively gave Hitler dictatorial powers, allowing him to legislate without Reichstag approval.\n\n- The decree provided Hitler with a legal basis to act with emergency powers indefinitely, which he used to bypass democratic processes. This was a crucial step towards establishing his dictatorship and consolidating his control over Germany.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Reichstag Fire Decree was pivotal in consolidating Hitler's power by allowing him to arrest and imprison political rivals, especially Communists, without trial, thereby eliminating immediate threats to his authority ($\\checkmark$). It also suspended basic civil liberties like freedom of speech, making it difficult for opposition groups to challenge the Nazi regime ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the decree created the conditions needed for the passing of the Enabling Act, which ultimately granted Hitler the dictatorial powers he needed to control Germany without interference from the Reichstag ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0070","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0070\n\nExplain how the use of force differed between Hitler's regime and Mao Zedong's regime in maintaining power.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n- Hitler used highly organised paramilitary groups like the SA and SS to eliminate political opponents and enforce Nazi policies, while Mao relied on loosely organised, ideologically driven Red Guards to target perceived enemies during the chaotic Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Hitler's force was aimed at eliminating external political opponents like Communists and Jews, while Mao used force primarily against internal enemies within the Communist Party and intellectuals.\n\n- Hitler's use of force extended beyond Germany in military conflicts and expansionism, while Mao focused on internal consolidation through purges and land reforms.\n\n- Hitler framed violent actions as legal measures for national security, using laws like the Reichstag Fire Decree, whereas Mao bypassed formal legal processes, using mass mobilisation to carry out violence.\n\n- Hitler's use of force maintained strict control and stability through fear, while Mao's use of force during the Cultural Revolution caused long-term social upheaval and instability.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nHitler used highly organised paramilitary forces like the SS and SA to suppress opposition and enforce Nazi policies, whereas Mao relied on the chaotic Red Guards during the Cultural Revolution, who targeted intellectuals and internal enemies with less centralised control ($\\checkmark$). Hitler's use of force primarily focused on eliminating external political enemies such as Communists and Jews, while Mao concentrated on internal ideological control, aiming to purify the Communist Party and eliminate counter-revolutionary elements ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Hitler's use of force contributed to maintaining a stable, if repressive, political order in Germany, while Mao's reliance on mass mobilisation and violence during the Cultural Revolution led to social chaos and long-term instability in Chinese society ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Hitler framed many of his violent actions as legally justified through measures like the Reichstag Fire Decree, whereas Mao's actions often bypassed formal legal processes, especially during the Cultural Revolution when mass violence was encouraged outside of legal channels($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0097","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0097\n\nOutline **four** factors that helped Mussolini rise to power.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of any **four** of the following points:\n\n- The war left Italy economically devastated, with high inflation and unemployment.\n\n- Italians felt their demands at the Paris Peace Conference had not been met, as they were not granted the port of Fiume and Northern Dalmatia, leading to anger against the ruling liberal government.  \n\n- The Italian government was threatened by the growing popularity of the Italian Socialist Party and the two years of industrial militant action - the *biennio rosso*. \n\n- Socialist action threatened the industrialists and middle class who turned to right wing groups such as Mussolini's *Fascio di Combattimento* to break up the strikes, leading to his increased popularity. \n\n- Mussolini was able to gain the support of the Catholic Church by removing left-wing elements from his party which helped secure the backing of the new Pope Pius XI. \n\n- He also gained the support of the Queen mother and the King's powerful cousin, the Duke of Aosta by ending his attacks on the monarch. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMussolini rose to power as he was able to capitalize on the anger amongst the Italians who were suffering from both the death toll and the economic devastation that resulted from the First World War ($\\checkmark$). The feeling of humiliation as a result of  the inability of the Italian government at the Paris Peace Conference, to get control of the port of Fiume and Dalmatia, also compounded the growing anger directed at the ruling liberal government ($\\checkmark$).  In addition the Italians faced high inflation and unemployment and these economic hardships faced by the workers led to working class opposition that  manifested in strikes led by the Socialist Party during the two years of *biennio rosso* further weakening the government ($\\checkmark$). This  helped Mussolini gain the support of industrialists and middle class as he offered the use of his Blackshirts to crush the workers movement, thus paving his way to power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0098","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0098\n\nCompare and contrast the factors that helped Mussolini and Hitler rise to power.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor comparison, any **two to four** of the following similarities:\n\n- Both dictators capitalized on the anger generated by the Paris Peace Treaties. \n\n- Both dictators focused their propaganda on nationalist principles that aimed to strengthen their countries. \n\n- Both dictators were aided by the economic unrest facing their countries- Hitler by the Great Depression and Mussolini by the economic impact of the First World War and the industrial unrest of the *biennio rosso* years. \n\n- Both leaders emphasized their anti-communist viewpoints in propaganda and violent actions against communist groups. \n\nFor contrast, any **two to four** of the following differences:\n\n- While Mussolini used the threat of a forced take over of government with his Blackshirts' use of violence and the attempted coup with the March on Rome, Hitler sought to contest elections to help the Nazi party gain power.\n\n- Hitler used propaganda against particular races as evident in his anti-semetic rhetoric. This tactic was not resorted to by Mussolini who instead focused  on an unifying theme, emphasizing the greatness of the Roman Empire. \n\n- Mussolini sought the support of the Catholic Church in his rise to power by removing elements hostile to the church from his group and dropping anti-clerical aspects of the fascist programme, unlike Hitler who did not feel the need to seek  support from the church. \n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWhen comparing factors that helped Mussolini and Hitler rise to power, both leaders benefited from the anger felt by the citizens of Italy and Germany against the governments that signed the Paris Peace Treaties ($\\checkmark$ similarity). The Italians were disappointed at the rejection of their demand for the port of Fiume and the Dalmatian coast, while German anger was directed at the terms of the Treaty of Versailles that deprived them of land and resources together with \\$33 billion reparations  imposed for war damages ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both Mussolini and Hitler capitalized on the economic suffering of their people and promised them a better economic future ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Mussolini included measures to deal with Italian inflation and unemployment resulting from World War I in his fascist programme, while Hiter's promise of a strong and prosperous Germany ensured an increase in popular support for the Nazis following the economic disaster of the Great Depression ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both dictators gained support of the middle and industrial classes as a result of their strong anti-communist rhetoric ($\\checkmark$ similarity). The Blackshirts and the SA targeted communists in Italy and Germany with violent attacks that served to weaken the existing democratic governments and help their rise to power ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nThere were also several contrasting factors that contributed to the rise to power of both dictators. Mussolini relied on the use of force with the attacks by the Blackshirts on communists and organizing the March on Rome with the aim of putting pressure on the government. He was then able to take advantage of the resulting chaos in government to position himself for the post of prime minister on the invitation of the King. However in contrast Hitler was made Chancellor not due to the threat of Nazi violence but because of the gains made by the Nazi party in the elections of July and November 1932 ($\\checkmark$ difference and evidence). Another contrast in the rise to power of the two dictators was their attitude with regard to Jews ($\\checkmark$ difference). Hitler's anti-semitism was a bedrock of his ideology and contributed to garnering support in his rise to power while Mussolini did not use anti-semitism to help him gather support ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Lastly, both leaders differed in their approach to the Catholic Church ($\\checkmark$ difference). While Mussolini dropped the anti-clerical stance he had initially adopted from his fascist programme in order to ensure support from the Catholic church to help his rise to power, Hitler did not modify his programme to gain support from the Church ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0155","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0155\n\nExplain **one** of the ways in which WWII contributed to Mao's rise to power. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **1** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n- It weakened the Guomindang (GMD) economically, militarily and morally which contributed to the Chinese Communist Party (CCP)'s victory during the Chinese Civil War.\n- It allowed Mao to seem like the liberator of the nation since the CCP fought a guerrilla war against the Japanese invaders.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Guomindang (GMD) were Mao`s enemy during the Chinese Civil War and the Japanese invasion during WWII seriously weakened it leaving Mao with a discredited adversary ($\\checkmark$). WWII meant the GMD had to retreat from the coastal cities, the wealthiest parts of China which provided the most revenue. As a result, the GMD was forced to increase taxation on the peasants and to print money to finance itself. This caused resentment among the peasants and an inflation rate of about 25% per month during 1944 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0157","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0157\n\nCompare and contrast what sources A and C reveal about the reasons for Hitler's rise to power. [6 marks] :br\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from Waldman, M. D. (1962). “Sieg Heil!: the Story of Adolf Hitler.\"Waldman was head of the American Jewish Committee until the war's end in 1945. |\n\n[Source: Waldman, M. D. (1962). *Sieg heil!: the story of Adolf Hitler*.  Retrieved October 24, 2024, from https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.49015000255076&seq=115. Public domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from Bruno Heilig,“Why the German Republic Fell\" (published in 1945). Heilig was an Austrian journalist, who worked for thirty years as editor or foreign correspondent of leading newspapers in Austria, Germany, Hungary and the Balkans. He was a prisoner in German concentration camps. |\n\n:box[Yet why had Germany taken the road from individual political liberty through mass hysteria to the surrender of all liberty and the despotic $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$leadership\" of one man? Was there a link between the economic and the political collapse? Emphatically, yes. For as unemployment grew, and with it poverty and the fear of poverty, so grew the influence of the Nazi Party, which was making its lavish promises to the frustrated and its violent appeal to the revenges of a populace aware of its wrongs but condemned to hear only a malignant and distorted explanation of them. …\\\n\\\nIn the first year of the crisis the number of Nazi deputies to the Reichstag rose from (12) to 107. A year later this figure was doubled. In the same time the Communists captured half of the votes of the German Social Democratic Party and the representation of the middle class practically speaking disappeared. In January 1933 Hitler was appointed Reichskanzler; he attained power, as I said before, quite legally. All the forms of democracy were observed.(...)\n\\\n\\\nMoney also was the chief weapon the enemies of democracy applied to overthrow democracy. Germany's masters, the owners of agricultural and industrial land, the Junkers [landowning aristocrats of Eastern Germany] and the Ruhr industrialists, had no actual love for Nazidom as such, but they were willing to use it to destroy the hated Republic. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A mere aristocracy of wealth will never struggle while it can hope to bribe a tyrant,\" which is just how the German landlords behaved. Nazidom was financed as everyone knows by the heavy industry in the first place, but the Junkers also contributed to the millions of marks which were paid to the leaders of the Nazi Party.\n]\n\n[Source: Heilig, B. (1938). *Why the German Republic Fell*.  Retrieved October 24, 2024, from Hanover College History Department. https://history.hanover.edu/courses/excerpts/111heil.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- They both identify economic factors as important in Hitler's rise. In Source A it says that “The greatest obstacle to Adolf´s ascendancy, however, was the continued success of the (Weimar) Government in both political and economic directions.” When this changed with the Great Depression Source C says “as unemployment grew, and with it poverty and the fear of poverty, so grew the influence of the Nazi Party”\n- They both identify heavy industry as financing Hitler`s emergence. Source A says “The young Fuehrer won the admiration, goodwill and generous support of the outstanding leaders of heavy industry” while Source C says “Nazidom was financed as everyone knows by the heavy industry”\n- Both sources identify messages of revenge as being used to persuade the German people. Source A refers to the \"November criminals\" who had stabbed Germany in the back by signing the Treaty of Versailles and the \"Jewish controlled Republic of Betrayal” and Source C mentions that the Nazis made a “ violent appeal to the revenges of a populace”.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source C mentions the leaders of heavy industry and agriculture as financing Hitler when it  says “the Junkers also contributed to the millions of marks which were paid to the leaders of the Nazi Party” while Source A ignores the role played by the big landowners (the “Junkers”).\n- Source A gives great importance to propaganda as a reason why Hitler became so influential while Source C points out his successes  in the democratic process.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSources A and C identify similar ideas when talking about the reasons for Hitler's rise ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). They both identify economic factors as important in Hitler's rise. In Source A it says that “The greatest obstacle to Adolf's ascendancy, however, was the continued success of the (Weimar) Government in both political and economic directions.” So for Hitler the fact that the Weimar Republic was doing well economically, made his rise more difficult but, as Source C says, when this changed “as unemployment grew, and with it poverty and the fear of poverty, so grew the influence of the Nazi Party” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). They also both identify heavy industry as financing Hitler's emergence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A says “The young Fuehrer won the admiration, goodwill and generous support of the outstanding leaders of heavy industry” while Source C says “Nazidom was financed as everyone knows by the heavy industry” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Finally both sources identify messages of revenge as being used to persuade the German people ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A refers to the \"November criminals\" who had stabbed Germany in the back by signing the Treaty of Versailles and the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jewish controlled Republic of Betrayal” and Source C mentions that the Nazis made a “ violent appeal to the revenges of a populace” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also present contrasts. Most notably, while Source C mentions the leaders of heavy industry as financing Hitler, it also says “the Junkers also contributed to the millions of marks which were paid to the leaders of the Nazi Party” while Source A ignores the role played by the big landowners ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Moreover, Source A gives great importance to propaganda as a reason why Hitler became so influential while Source C points out his successes within the democratic process to explain how he reached a position of power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0159","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0159\n\nWhat does Source B reveal about the Reichstag during the Weimar Republic? [3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Number of Deputies Elected to the Reichstag during the Weimar Republic.|\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Date|Jan 1919|Jun 1920|May 1924|Dec 1924|May 1928|Sept 1930|July 1932|Nov 1932|Mar 1933|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Total Deputies | 423 | 459 | 472 | 493 | 491 | 577 | 608 | 584 | 647 |\n| SPD $\\\\$ Social Democrats | 165 | 102 | 100 | 131 | 153 | 143 | 133 | 121 | 120 |\n| USPD $\\\\$ Independent Socialists | 22 | 84 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| KPD $\\\\$ Communists | 0 | 4 | 62 | 45 | 54 | 77 | 89 | 100 | 81 |\n| Centre Party $\\\\$ (Catholics) | 91 | 64 | 65 | 69 | 62 | 68 | 75 | 70 | 74 |\n| BVP $\\\\$ Bavarian Peoples Party | 0 | 21 | 16 | 19 | 16 | 19 | 22 | 20 | 18 |\n| DDP $\\\\$ Democrats | 75 | 39 | 28 | 32 | 25 | 20 | 4 | 2 | 5 |\n| DVP $\\\\$ Peoples Party | 19 | 65 | 45 | 51 | 45 | 30 | 7 | 11 | 2 |\n| Wirtschafts Partei $\\\\$ Economy Party | 4 | 4 | 10 | 17 | 23 | 23 | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n| DNVP $\\\\$ Nationalists | 44 | 71 | 95 | 103 | 73 | 41 | 37 | 52 | 52 |\n| NSDAP $\\\\$ Nazis | 0 | 0 | 32 | 14 | 12 | 107 | 230 | 196 | 288 |\n| Othters | 3 | 5 | 19 | 12 | 28 | 49 | 9 | 11 | 7 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from *Weimar Republic, Elections* (2002). Virginia Wesleyan University (formerly Virginia Wesleyean College). Retrieved October 24, 2024 from https://web.archive.org/web/20021202035105/http://facultystaff.vwc.edu/~dgraf/weim.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **2** of the following:\n- There were many political parties present in the Reichstag, some with very few members\n- There were many different ideologies present in the Reichstag\n- Some of the parties represented in the Reichstag were actually antidemocratic like the KPD and the Nazi Party.\n- Part of the Reichstag was divided along sectarian identity lines like the Catholics and the Bavarians\n- Until 1932, the Social Democrats were the most important party of the Reichstag.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom Source B we can learn that the Reichstag during the Weimar Republic was made up of members from many different parties ($\\checkmark$). We can also see that Until 1932, the Social Democrats were the most important party of the Reichstag ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0160","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0160\n\nWhat, according to Source A, was the impact of Hugenberg on Hitler's rise? [2 marks] :br\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from Waldman, M. D. (1962). “Sieg Heil!: the Story of Adolf Hitler.\"Waldman was head of the American Jewish Committee until the war's end in 1945. |\n\n[Source: Waldman, M. D. (1962). *Sieg heil!: the story of Adolf Hitler*.  Retrieved October 24, 2024, from https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.49015000255076&seq=115. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- Hitler's presence in Hugenberg's newspaper and films made Hitler a familiar figure\n- That same presence revealed to industrial leaders that Hitler was very good at propaganda and an agitator they could use.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHugenberg allowed Hitler to become a well known figure among the German public thanks to his use of newspapers and films to spread his message ($\\checkmark$). This media presence also allowed Hitler to be noticed by industrial leaders who used him as a political tool ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0270","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0270\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of Mao's implementation of $`$collective ownership of the means of production' on the agricultural sector of China from 1950-1957. [6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- June 1950 Land Reform Programme: \n\n    - Mao launched a programme of land reform with the Agrarian Reform Law, which confiscated and redistributed the property of rural landlords. Land ownership remained private, but the former landlords who survived the attacks of the $`$speak bitterness campaign', which encouraged peasants to turn on their landlord, now held only as much land as the poorest peasant.  \n\n- 1951-1953 cooperative farming:\n\n    - The Communist party cadres encouraged peasants to farm the land cooperatively, with profits distributed according to the land, tools, animals and work contributed by individual peasants. The land was still individually owned while resources were pooled for better grain production. By 1953, almost 40% of peasant farms belonged to mutual aid teams or cooperatives. From 1953 on, the state was the sole purchaser of grain.    \n\n- 1954-1955 Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives (APCs):\n\n    - The peasants were encouraged to create higher-level Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives. Land was now pooled and no longer individually owned with profits being distributed according to work contributed by each peasant. Individual peasant farmers were unable to obtain bank loans, buy seed and fertilizer or get help during harvest time. \n\n    - By 1957 over 96% of peasant families were part of Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives and peasants were obliged to sell fixed quotas to the state. \n\n- 1958: Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives become $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Communes”.\n\n    - APCs were amalgamated into much larger units called Communes where there were no more private farms. \n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn June 1950, Mao launched his land reform programme with the passing of the Agrarian Reform Law. This implemented the confiscation of property from rural landlords and its redistribution amongst the landless peasants ($\\checkmark$). This was accompanied by the $`$speak bitterness campaign', in which party cadres encouraged the peasants to subject the landlords to a $`$people's trial', where many lost their lives as they were subject to the anger of the mob ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe next step in Mao's agrarian reforms was from 1951-1953, when the Communist party cadres encouraged peasants to farm their land cooperatively, with profits being distributed according to the land, tools, animals and work contributed by individual peasants ($\\checkmark$). Land was still individually owned while resources were pooled for better grain production ($\\checkmark$). \n\nBy 1954-1955, the peasants were now encouraged to create higher-level Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives. Land was now pooled and no longer individually owned with profits being distributed according to work contributed by each peasant ($\\checkmark$). Individual peasant farmers who held out were unable to obtain bank loans, buy seed and fertilizer or get help during harvest time ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0271","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0271\n\nOutline the main features of Mao's $`$Great Leap Forward' in 1958. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- The Great Leap Forward was the mass mobilisation of society launched in 1958 and aimed at making China a world superpower that would challenge the USA and overtake Britain in fifteen years. \n\n- In urban areas there was an increase in State-Owned Enterprises while in the countryside, the Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives (APCs)  were amalgamated into Communes. \n\n- Each Commune consisted of 40-100 villages. There were approximately 53000 communes in China. Private farming was to cease and all aspects of production were now centrally directed by the Communist Party. \n\n- Each commune would also contain factories to provide work for women and children. \n\n- Communes were self-contained units that had nurseries for young children, old people's homes, health clinics, communal canteens and unisex dormitories for the inhabitants of the commune, who were encouraged to abandon personal possessions. \n\n- Rural peasants were now also expected to contribute to industrial growth and the production of steel and were encouraged to build and operate brick furnaces in their backyards to smelt crude steel. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward was the mass mobilisation of society launched in 1958 and aimed at making China a world superpower that would challenge the USA and overtake Britain in fifteen years ($\\checkmark$). In urban areas, there was an increase in State-Owned Enterprises, while in the countryside, the Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives (APCs)  were amalgamated into Communes ($\\checkmark$). As a result, private farming was to cease, and all aspects of production were now centrally directed by the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$). Communes were self-contained units with nurseries for young children, old people's homes, health clinics, communal canteens and unisex dormitories for the inhabitants ($\\checkmark$). One final feature of the Great Leap Forward was that rural peasants were also expected to contribute to industrial growth and steel production, including building and operating brick furnaces in their backyards to smelt crude steel ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0272","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0272\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of the Great Leap Forward on China. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- Failure of agricultural systems:\n\n    - Communal farming failed as Chinese farmers were not able to adapt to the demands of large scale farming. Farmers left their fields fallow as the focus of propaganda was on keeping the backyard furnaces running. Many farmers melted their tools to produce steel, much of which was unusable. \n\n    - The $`$four pests campaign' endorsed by Mao further damaged agriculture. The four pests campaign $`$outlawed' and was aimed at the eradication of flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows. The killing of sparrows led to an explosion of caterpillars and other insects that destroyed crops.\n\n    - The focus on producing crude steel in the backyard furnaces led to neglect of animal husbandry and growing of vegetables. \n\n- Famine and mass death\n\n    - Government increased the share of grain collected from the communes from 17% to 28%, leaving the communes with insufficient food for its members and leading to starvation. \n\n    - 1959 to 1961 (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Three Bitter Years”) witnessed the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward and were exacerbated by the drought that affected northern and central China in 1960 and floods in Southern China. \n\n- Loss of some power for Mao and new Chinese leadership\n\n    - The failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in Mao stepping down as the Chairman of the People's Republic of China while retaining his position as Head of the Communist Party. \n\n    - Liu Shaoqi, as the new Head of State along with Deng Xiaoping, now introduce elements of private ownership in order to stabilise the situation. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward failed to meet its agriculture or steel production targets ($\\checkmark$). Communal farming failed as Chinese farmers could not adapt to the demands of large-scale farming. Farmers left their fields fallow as propaganda focused on keeping the backyard furnaces running. The $`$four pests campaign', endorsed by Mao, aimed to eradicate flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows. The killing of sparrows led to an explosion of caterpillars and other insects that destroyed crops ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFocusing on producing crude steel in backyard furnaces to increase steel production in China was also a failure of the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$). As peasants were told they would get rich quickly if they concentrated on iron and steel production, many farmers melted their tools to produce steel, much of which was unusable. This further impacted agricultural production and, at the same time, failed to contribute to an increase in steel production ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThis resulted in $`$three bitter years' from 1959 to 1961, which witnessed the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese from the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward and from the natural disasters that hit China at the same time - a drought that affected northern and central China in 1960 and floods in Southern China ($\\checkmark$). In the end, the failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in Mao stepping down as the Chairman of the People's Republic of China while retaining his position as Head of the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0273","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0273\n\nOutline **four** steps Mao took to support women who, as he stated, $`$hold up half the sky'.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- In 1950 Female infanticide was abolished, and foot binding was outlawed. \n\n- The 1950 Marriage Law forbade arranged and child marriages, the payment of dowry, and concubinage. Official marriage registration was introduced, and divorce by mutual consent or on the complaint of either party was permitted. Those subjected to arranged marriage were allowed to petition for divorce. \n\n- In 1950 women were given the same property rights as men.\n\n- 1953 Election Law gave women the right to vote. \n\n- Women were encouraged to train for jobs, and some joined the government and the People's Liberation Army. \n\n- During the Great Leap Forward communal nurseries, communal eating and shared cleaning supported women's entry into the workforce. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao famously proclaimed, $`$women held up half the sky', to emphasise the equal role played by women in achieving the goals of the Chinese Revolution. To ensure equal status for women, the Communist state banned female infanticide and foot binding in 1950 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe 1950 Marriage Law further forbade arranged and child marriages, the payment of dowry, and concubinage. Official marriage registration was introduced, and divorce by mutual consent or on the complaint of either party was permitted. Those subjected to arranged marriage were allowed to petition for divorce, and in 1950, women were also given the same property rights as men ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn 1953, the Election Law gave women the right to vote, leading to greater participation in political matters ($\\checkmark$). \n\nDuring the Great Leap Forward, the establishment of communal nurseries, shared eating arrangements for all commune members and shared, centralised cleaning of all living areas lessened the domestic burden placed on women and supported their entry into the workforce, leading to greater independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0274","marks":"40","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0274\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of Mao's Communist Revolution on minority groups within China. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- Annexation of Tibet and subsequent famine:\n\n    - Tibet was forcibly made part of the People's Republic of China, and Chinese economic policies were imposed.\n\n    - These new economic policies resulted in a famine in which a quarter of the Tibetan population died. This was because of Chinese policies that forced the Tibetans to grow wheat and maize, which was not their staple diet. The high ground used by the Tibetans for grazing their yak was forcibly farmed, resulting in a shortage of milk, cheese, and yak hair, which were required for clothes and tents. \n\n- Supervision of ethnic minorities and culture:\n\n    - In 1950, ethnic minorities were asked to self-identify and were promised autonomy. 400 ethnic groups that did so were organised into 50 groups and placed under military supervision. \n\n- Religious suppression:\n\n    - Buddhist culture was suppressed in Tibet. In 1959 the uprising in Tibet against the outlawing of the Lama Faith and Tibetan culture was brutally crushed. \n\n    - In 1974 during the Cultural Revolution, the People's Liberation Army forced mosques to close and burnt religious books.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's communist revolution resulted in the suppression of Tibet's Buddhist culture, and Tibet was forcibly made part of the People's Republic of China ($\\checkmark$). The Lama Faith was outlawed, and the resultant uprising against this in 1959 was brutally crushed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nChinese economic policies in Tibet resulted in a famine in which a quarter of the Tibetan population died ($\\checkmark$). This was because Chinese policies forced the Tibetians to grow non-staple crops like wheat and because grazing land was forcibly farmed, resulting in a shortage of milk, cheese and yak hair needed for clothes and tents ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0275","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0275\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the reasons that prompted Mao to launch the Great Leap Forward? [3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research report titled *Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China* published by Canadian Center of Science and Education. July 10, 2019|\n\n[Source: Jung, Hsiung-Shen & Chen, Jui-Lung. (2019, July 10). *Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China*.Asian Culture and History - . Canadian Center of Science and Education. Retrieved December 14, 2024 from https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v11n2p58 . CC-BY 4.0. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** the following:\n\n- The Great Leap Forward was a result of the deterioration of the relations between the Communist Parties of China and the Soviet Union. \n\n- Mao launched the Great Leap Forward as he initially wanted to catch up with the United States in iron and steel production in 50 to 60 years.\n\n- However after meeting Krushchev in 1957 he decided that China could surpass Britain in 15 years. \n\n- He was also convinced that China's economy would develop faster and better than that of the Soviet Union. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, Mao launched the Great Leap Forward as a result of the deteriorating relations between the Communist Parties of China and the Soviet Union and as a way to  lessen Chinese dependance on the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). He was also initially prompted by the desire to catch up with the United States in iron and steel production in 50 to 60 years ($\\checkmark$). After meeting Krushchev in 1957, Mao decided that China could surpass Britain in 15 years with the mass mobilisation of the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0276","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0276\n\nWhat does Source A reveal about the impact of the Great Leap Forward on China? [2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A political cartoon titled, “By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot” by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. March 9, 1961. |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Unassigned/images/hi0276.JPEG\" alt=\"hi0276\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, Edmund S.(1961, March 9). *By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot*. Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/item/2016687288/. Public domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- The Chinese people have been hard hit by the Great Leap Forward.\n\n- Many deaths have resulted from hunger.\n\n- Mao and his colleagues are cynical and unconcerned about the human cost of the program and are still promoting it. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A shows that the Chinese people have suffered as a result of the Great Leap Forward as many deaths have resulted from hunger as evidenced by the graves and the words $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$victims of hunger” ($\\checkmark$). The source also shows that Mao and his colleagues are cynical and unconcerned about the human cost of the program and are still promoting it as the title of the cartoon reads, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot,” with reference to plots for the graves ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0278","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0278\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the impact of the Great Leap Forward on China. [6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research report titled Causes, *Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China* published by Canadian Center of Science and Education. July 10, 2019|\n\n[Source: Jung, Hsiung-Shen & Chen, Jui-Lung. (2019, July 10). Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China.Asian Culture and History - . Canadian Center of Science and Education. Retrieved December 14, 2024 from https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v11n2p58 . CC-BY 4.0. ]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research article titled *Peasants and the Great Leap Forward* by Marissa Warren, from  the University of Nebraska at Kearney. Published in 2017 in the Undergraduate Research Journal.|\n\n[Source: Warren, M. (2017). *Peasants and the Great Leap Forward*. Undergraduate Research Journal: Vol. 21 , Article 9. Retrieved December 12, 2024 from https://openspaces.unk.edu/undergraduate-research-journal/vol21/iss1/9. Open access.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nFor comparison, any **three** to **four** of the following **similarities**:\n\n- Both sources argue that grain exports were emphasized during the Great Leap Forward.\n\n- Both sources mention that the policies of the Great Leap Forward drove a famine and contributed to the deaths.\n\n- Both sources state that farmers were forced to work on producing iron and steel and on growing grain.\n\nBoth sources state that millions of Chinese died as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward.\n\nFor contrast, any **two** to **three** of the following differences:\n\n- Source B states that the reduction in grain production was a result of $`$increasing output of iron and steel' while Source C states it was $`$due to the policy on communal dining halls, (as) peasants no longer had private plots to grow food'.\n\n- Source C mentions accounts of cannibalism as a result of famine and the collapse of the healthcare system, which is not mentioned in Source B.\n\n- Both Sources mention quantities of deaths caused by the Great Leap Forward, but Source B cites the number $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$40 million” nationwide while Source C focuses on the quantities in Xinyang Region ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over a million”) and Guangshan ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one in four people”).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources mention the increased emphasis on small-scale steel production ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B describes $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$reduced grain production due to increasing output of iron and steel” in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$small blast furnaces,” while Source C says peasants worked “backyard furnaces to create steel” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nBoth sources also state that grain export continued during the famine and contributed to the deaths ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$deficient food supply due to over-export,” while Source C states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and members of the CCP were obsessed with their export markets and thus, even during widespread famine, continued to export most of their grain” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nBoth sources also state that millions of Chinese died as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over 40 million people starved to death statistically” while Source C states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$by 1960, over a million people were dead due to the widespread famine” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). However, The focus of the deaths mentioned in both sources differs ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B cites the number of deaths at $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$40 million” nationwide while Source C focuses on the quantities in Xinyang Region ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over a million”) and Guangshan ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one in four people”) ($\\checkmark$ evidence).   \n\nThe sources disagree on what drove reduced grain production ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B states that the reduction in grain production resulted from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$increasing output of iron and steel” while Source C states it was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$due to the policy on communal dining halls, (as) peasants no longer had private plots to grow food” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Finally, Source C mentions accounts of cannibalism as a result of famine and the collapse of the healthcare system, which is not mentioned in Source B ($\\checkmark$ difference). \n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$"},{"ref":"HI0279","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0279\n\nAccording to Source D, what challenges did China's mass mobilization (the Great Leap Forward) still face in 1958?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Masses Can Do Anything” an extract from a statement by Mao Zedong to a reporter of the Hsin Hua Agency on 29 September 1958 following a tour of the country.|\n\n[Source: Mao Tse-tung. (September 29, 1958). The Masses Can Do Anything. Selected Works of Mao-Tse-tun on Marxists.org. Retrieved December 12, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-8/mswv8_16.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\n- There were places where the work of mobilizing the masses had not been properly carried out and where the tasks required, the reasons for them and the methods had still not been made clear to the masses.\n\n- There were a few Communist party leaders who were unwilling to undertake a large-scale mass movement in the industrial sphere and who disparaged the mass movement on the industrial front as $`$a rural style of work' and $`$a guerrilla habit'.\n\n- To ensure that agriculture was not neglected or sacrificed while focusing on iron and steel production on a large scale.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source D, Mao stated that the Great Leap Forward mass mobilization faced challenges as there were places where the tasks required for the movement, the reasons for them, and the methods had not been made clear to the masses ($\\checkmark$). Another challenge was that there were a few Communist party leaders who were unwilling to undertake a large-scale mass movement in the industrial sphere and who disparaged the mass movement on the industrial front as $`$a rural style of work' and $`$a guerrilla habit' ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0642","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0642\n\nExplain how Source C conveys socialist ideals promoted by the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) during the 1950s.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Chinese propaganda poster from 1953. The caption reads, “A free and independent marriage is good, there is great happiness in unified production.\" |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Authoritarian%20States%20%2820th%20Century%29/Unassigned/images/HI0642.jpg\" alt=\"HI0642\">\n[Source: Cheng, B, (1953, February). *A free and independent marriage is good, there is great happiness in unified production.* Private collection hosted on ChinesePosters.net. Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://chineseposters.net/themes/marriage-law Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence/explanation:\n- Emphasis on greater gender equality and women's liberation:\n    - The phrase “A free and independent marriage is good” reflects the Marriage Law of 1950, which aimed to eliminate forced marriages, arranged marriages, and feudal family structures. The CCP promoted marriage based on mutual love and equality, aligning with its broader goal of gender equality and women's liberation.\n- Connects marriage to the collective good of society:\n    - The phrase, “There is great happiness in unified production” ties marriage to communal labor and socialist production, reinforcing the idea that happiness comes from working together for the collective good.\n- Emphasizes the role of productivity of both men and women together:\n    - The Great Leap Forward (1958-1962) emphasized collective farming and industrial production, and propaganda often depicted men and women working enthusiastically in agriculture and industry.\n- The critical role of agricultural laborers\n    - Agricultural labor was a key theme in Communist propaganda, portraying work in the fields as fulfilling and joyful rather than labor-intensive and harsh. The smiling young man and woman harvesting represents the CCP's vision of a harmonious, productive socialist society.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe poster reflects key socialist ideals, beginning with the promotion of free and independent marriage ($\\checkmark$). The phrase “A free and independent marriage is good” aligns with the Marriage Law of 1950, which sought to abolish forced and arranged marriages, as well as feudal family structures. The CCP emphasized marriage based on mutual love and equality, supporting its broader vision of gender equality and women's liberation ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the poster highlights economic productivity and collective labor ($\\checkmark$). The phrase “There is great happiness in unified production” connects marriage to communal labor and socialist production, reinforcing the notion that true happiness comes from working together for the benefit of society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0842","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0842\n\nList **three** weaknesses of recently unified Italy (1870).\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Regional economic differences, wealthy north and poorer south\n- Weak, liberal democracy run by elites\n- Lack of a national identity as $\\lq$Italians'.\n- The new state caused conflict between government and the Church\n- Centralization and elite rule during unification set a precedent for top-down governance\n- Lack of modernisation\n- Limited suffrage - only wealthy literate men can vote\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWhen Italy was unified in 1870, it became a country, but the people lacked an identity as “Italians” ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, there was a lack of industrialisation and economic development, especially in the southern part of the country ($\\checkmark$). This led to economic disparity in which southern Italy was much poorer ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0843","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0843\n\nExplain the impact of **two** weaknesses of recently unified Italy (1870) on Mussolini's rise to power.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following weaknesses:\n- Regional economic differences, the wealthy north and the poorer south\n- Weak, liberal democracy run by elites\n- Lack of a national identity as $\\lq$Italians'.\n- The new state caused conflict between the government and the Church\n- Centralization and elite rule during unification set a precedent for top-down governance\n- Lack of modernisation\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following explanations connected to the relevant weakness:\n- The North-South divide made national unity fragile and allowed Mussolini to present Fascism as a unifying force\n- The alienation of the South (due to historical neglect and repression) made parts of the population more receptive to strong leadership promising order and national pride.\n- Mussolini criticized the failures of parliamentary democracy and promised a new, stronger system.\n- Mussolini was able to use the resentment people felt towards the elites to gain support from the people.\n- A lack of national identity allowed Mussolini the opportunity to create one based on fascism and nationalism, Roman symbolism, and militaristic pride\n- The people were used to top-down governance, so Mussolini’s increasing fascist control went unchallenged\n- Since unification, ongoing tensions between Church and State had alienated devout Catholics from politics.\nMussolini's regime promised modernization, land reform, and employment, especially appealing to those neglected by the earlier liberal state.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMussolini was able to exploit several of Italy's weaknesses.  Italy was unified in 1870, and there was a lack of national identity  ($\\checkmark$).  As a result, Mussolini was able to help create an identity based on fascist nationalism and pride in Italy's Roman past ($\\checkmark$).  Furthermore, the government was weak and run by a few elites ($\\checkmark$). Mussolini exploited this by harnessing the people's distrust and dislike for the elite government, which, from their perspective, had failed them ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0844","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0844\n\nOutline **two** consequences of the First World War that helped Mussolini come to power.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- After the war, massive inflation, debt, and unemployment created widespread public discontent. Mussolini promised to resolve these issues.\n- The war normalised violence to some extent, and Mussolini was able to exploit this with the rise of his fascist squads (Blackshirts)\n- Italy's minimal gains from the Treaty of Versailles led to discontent, which Mussolini exploited by appealing to those who wanted to $\\lq$restore' Italy's greatness.\n- The war made people lose faith in the liberal government, which made Mussolini's call for strong, authoritarian leadership more appealing.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMussolini was able to exploit several results of WWI. First, Italy was disappointed in the amount of land it gained in the Treaty of Versailles, feeling it was disproportionately less than other countries. This allowed Mussolini to use this to appeal to people's pride and promise a return to a $\\lq$great' Italy ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, after WWI, people began to lose faith in the government due to its failures, making Mussolini's call for authoritarian rule seem to offer people a change ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0845","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0845\n\nDiscuss the significance of Mussolini’s March on Rome (October 1922) in his rise to power.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two to four** of the following for significance with supporting details and analysis:\n- Demonstration of strength and intimidation tactics\n    - The march showcased Mussolini's ability to mobilize large numbers of supporters, particularly among the military.\n    - The march displayed the growing power and organization of the Fascist movement and created an atmosphere of fear and instability that Mussolini exploited.\n- Political maneuvering and the catalyst for a legal transfer of power\n    - The march led King Victor Emmanuel III to invite Mussolini to form a government, marking the transition from a democratic system to Fascist rule\n    - Mussolini used the march as leverage to demand political power, and the threat of violence and potential civil war pressured the government to capitulate, helping him to position himself as the only figure capable of restoring order\n- Symbolic importance\n    - The march became a powerful propaganda tool for the Fascist regime, and it was mythologized as a heroic revolution in Fascist narratives\n- International impact\n    - The march attracted attention worldwide, raising Mussolini's profile as well as inspiring similar tactics by other far-right movements in Europe\n- Turning point\n    - The event marked the end of the liberal parliamentary era in Italy, and it set the stage for Mussolini's consolidation of power in the following years\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall significance of the March on Rome, linking the arguments clearly to Mussolini's rise to power.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMussolini's March on Rome was significant for several reasons.  Firstly, it showed the world and Italy how much support he had, particularly from the 30,000 militant participants in the march ($\\checkmark$). The pressure this show of support created also increased Mussolini's political power to the point that it forced the King to form a government to avoid the threat of civil war ($\\checkmark$).\n\nInternationally, the march drew great attention and gave Mussolini more credibility in the eyes of Italians and the world ($\\checkmark$). Despite the relatively small number of actual participants, the march would be used as a symbol of Mussolini's power and as a tool for Fascist propaganda during his rule ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, the march seemed relatively small at the time, with some in Italy still thinking Mussolini could be controlled. However, in the end, it marked the end of Italy's parliamentary power and signalled a turning point towards a Fascist, authoritarian regime ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0846","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0846\n\nDescribe how Mussolini used the Black Shirts to create fear in the population.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Black Shirts were used to attack political rivals/parties generally and directly (e.g. the assassinations of political rivals like Giacomo Matteotti)\n- Mussolini called the Black Shirts out to suppress socialist activities and attack strikers\n- The Black Shirts were prominent in The March on Rome in order to show power and intimidate\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMussolini used the Black Shirts to attack other rival political parties and individuals, such as the assassination of socialist leader Giacomo Matteotti ($\\checkmark$). He also used them to break up worker strikes and other activities that were deemed socialist or contrary to Fascist ideals ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"Conflict & Intervention","subtopics":[{"name":"Kosovo","questions":[{"ref":"HI0514","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0514\n\nDescribe **two** constitutional or political reforms introduced in Serbia between 1989 and 1994 and their impact on Kosovar Albanians.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following for the description of the Reform:\n\n- Serbia amended the constitution to remove Kosovo's status as an autonomous province.\n\n- Kosovo's legislature and government were abolished, and all governance decisions were centralized in Belgrade.\n\n- Kosovo's judicial system was merged into Serbia's, eliminating local Kosovar Albanian judges and courts.\n\n- The Serbian government shut down Albanian-language schools and universities.\n\n- Large numbers of Kosovar Albanian teachers, doctors, and civil servants were removed from their jobs.\n\n- Serbia banned Kosovo's independent political parties and arrested key activists.\n\nAny **two** of the following for the impact of Kosovar Albanians:\n\n- Kosovar Albanians lost political self-governance, with Serbia assuming full control over Kosovo's administration.\n\n- Kosovar Albanians had no official representation or political influence in decision-making.\n\n- Kosovar Albanians were subjected to a legal system that discriminated against them, making it difficult to seek justice\n\n- Kosovar Albanian students were forced to either learn in Serbian or attend underground schools.\n\n- Economic hardship increased among Kosovar Albanians, and essential services in Albanian-speaking communities suffered.\n\n- Kosovar Albanians were deprived of legal political avenues to advocate for their rights, leading to underground resistance movements.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reform introduced by Serbia was the revocation of Kosovo's autonomy in 1989, which ended Kosovo's special status within Yugoslavia ($\\checkmark$). This meant that all major political decisions were now controlled by the Serbian government, and Kosovar Albanians lost all self-governance ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reform was the closure of Albanian-language schools in 1991, which prevented Kosovar Albanian students from being educated in their native language ($\\checkmark$). As a result, many Albanians were forced to study in Serbian or attend underground schools, making education less accessible ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0515","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0515\n\nIdentify **two** actions taken by the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) during the Kosovo conflict.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Guerrilla attacks were carried out against Serbian police and military forces.\n\n- Serbian officials and Albanians accused of collaboration were targeted for assassination.\n\n- International recognition and military support were actively sought, particularly from NATO.\n\n- Key regions of Kosovo were seized in an attempt to establish territorial control.\n\n- Kosovar Albanians, including those from the diaspora, were recruited to expand the KLA's forces.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nKLA fighters launched guerrilla attacks against Serbian security forces to challenge Serbian rule ($\\checkmark$). They also sought international support from NATO to strengthen their position in the conflict ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0516","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0516\n\nExplain the significance of **two** ways in which the international community responded to the Kosovo crisis.   [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAward **one** mark for each way and **one** mark for explaining the significance.\n\nAny of the following:\n\n- NATO bombing campaigns against Serbia\n\n    - NATO launched an aerial bombing campaign in 1999 to pressure Serbia into withdrawing from Kosovo. The campaign forced Serbia to end its campaign against Kosovar Albanians and demonstrated the willingness of Western powers to intervene militarily in ethnic conflicts\n\n- The United Nations deployed the Kosovo Force (KFOR) and established UNMIK\n\n    - KFOR's peacekeeping mission helped prevent further violence and maintained stability in the region following the conflict. UNMIK's administration of Kosovo laid the institutional and political groundwork for Kosovo's transition to self-governance and its eventual unilateral declaration of independence in 2008, though this status remains contested.\n\n- The international community attempted to negotiate a settlement through the Rambouillet talks.\n\n    - The failure of these negotiations exposed the limits of diplomacy in resolving deep-rooted ethnic conflicts. The breakdown of talks justified, in the eyes of NATO and its allies, the necessity of military action and accelerated the decision to launch airstrikes against Serbia.\n\n- The ICTY indicted Slobodan Milošević and other Serbian leaders for war crimes.\n\n    - The indictments sent a clear message that leaders could be held personally accountable for crimes against humanity and war crimes. This reinforced the role of international law in addressing serious human rights violations and helped establish legal precedents for prosecuting sitting or former heads of state.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne way the international community responded to the Kosovo crisis was through NATO's bombing campaign against Serbia in 1999 ($\\checkmark$). This intervention forced Serbian forces to pull out of Kosovo, bringing an end to the violence against Kosovar Albanians. It also marked a new phase in international relations, as NATO acted without direct authorization from the United Nations, raising important questions about the use of military force for humanitarian purposes ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother example was the deployment of the United Nations Kosovo Force (KFOR), along with the establishment of the UN administration known as UNMIK ($\\checkmark$). These measures helped restore order and prevent further clashes between ethnic groups in the aftermath of the war. They also played a key role in managing Kosovo's transition toward self-government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0517","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0517\n\nExplain **two** ways in which the Račak massacre influenced the course of the Kosovo conflict.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n- It intensified diplomatic condemnation of Serbia by the international community\n\n    - The massacre sparked outrage among Western governments and human rights organizations, leading to increased pressure on Milošević's regime and strengthening calls for intervention to protect the Kosovar Albanian population.\n\n- It increased recruitment and support for the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA).\n\n    - Anger over the killings encouraged more Kosovar Albanians to join the KLA, boosting its capacity to resist Serbian forces and escalating the armed conflict within Kosovo.\n\n- It served as evidence in war crimes indictments against Serbian leaders.\n\n    - The massacre was cited by the International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia (ICTY) in its prosecution of Slobodan Milošević and others, helping establish international legal accountability for crimes committed during the conflict.\n\n- The massacre prompted NATO to launch an aerial bombing campaign against Serbia.\n\n    - The killings at Račak were widely seen as proof of systematic Serbian human rights abuses, providing the key justification for NATO's 1999 intervention. This escalated the conflict from a regional crisis to an international military operation aimed at ending the violence.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne way the Račak massacre influenced the Kosovo conflict was by prompting NATO to launch an aerial bombing campaign against Serbia \n\n($\\checkmark$). The massacre was seen as clear evidence of systematic atrocities, which convinced NATO that military intervention was necessary to prevent further human rights abuses and protect Kosovar Albanians \n\n($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother way it influenced the conflict was by intensifying diplomatic condemnation of Serbia from the international community \n\n($\\checkmark$). Widespread outrage following the massacre increased pressure on Milošević's government and strengthened calls for intervention, isolating Serbia diplomatically and paving the way for international action \n\n($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0518","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0518\n\nOutline Slobodan Milošević's significance in the Kosovo conflict.   [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Milošević revoked Kosovo's autonomy in 1989, removing political rights from Kosovar Albanians and increasing tensions that led to demands for independence.\n\n- He ordered military crackdowns on Kosovar Albanian civilians and the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA), escalating the conflict into widespread violence. The brutality of Serbian forces contributed to NATO's decision to intervene.\n\n- Milošević oversaw ethnic cleansing campaigns targeting Kosovar Albanians, resulting in mass displacement and widespread human rights abuses.\n\n- His refusal to accept peace proposals at the Rambouillet talks in 1999 led directly to NATO's military intervention.\n\n- Milošević was indicted for war crimes by the International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia (ICTY), marking a key moment in holding political leaders accountable for crimes committed during conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMilošević was significant in the Kosovo conflict because he revoked Kosovo's autonomy in 1989, which increased ethnic tensions and helped trigger the conflict ($\\checkmark$). Shortly after, he ordered brutal crackdowns against Kosovar Albanians with Serbia's armed forces ($\\checkmark$). He also refused to agree to the peace terms at the Rambouillet negotiations, leading to NATO's bombing campaign against Serbia in 1999 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0519","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0519\n\nDefine the term “ethnic cleansing” and describe how it was applied during the Kosovo conflict.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor definition, the following or similar:\n\n- The systematic and forced removal of an ethnic or religious group from a specific area.\n\n- A policy aimed at removing an ethnic group through methods such as intimidation, violence, murder, or deportation in order to achieve ethnic homogeneity.\n\n- The deliberate expulsion or elimination of a particular ethnic group from a territory.\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor description, and **two** of the following:\n\n- Serbian forces carried out the mass expulsion of Kosovar Albanians from their homes and villages.\n\n- Many Kosovar Albanians were forcibly deported to neighboring countries such as Albania, Macedonia, and Montenegro.\n\n- Villages, homes, and places of cultural or religious significance (e.g., mosques) were deliberately destroyed to prevent return.\n\n- The Serbian military and paramilitary forces committed widespread human rights abuses, including killings and sexual violence, as part of the ethnic cleansing campaign.\n\n- The campaign led to a massive refugee crisis, with approximately 800,000 Kosovar Albanians fleeing Kosovo.\n\n- Intimidation and fear tactics were used to terrorize Kosovar Albanians into abandoning their homes.\n\n- Some populations were concentrated in controlled areas before being expelled.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nEthnic cleansing is when an ethnic group is systematically removed from a particular area, often by using violence or threats ($\\checkmark$). In Kosovo, Serbian forces drove hundreds of thousands of Kosovar Albanians from their homes, forcing them to cross into neighboring countries and turning them into refugees ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, predominantly Kosovar Albanian villages and homes were targeted and destroyed to discourage a return in the future ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0520","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0520\n\nCompare and contrast the roles of Slobodan Milošević and Ibrahim Rugova in the Kosovo conflict.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels |\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)$\\hspace{2em}$|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own $\\newline$ knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nFor comparison, any of the following:\n\n- Political Leadership - Both Milošević and Rugova were prominent political leaders during the Kosovo conflict, representing their respective ethnic groups.\n\n- Influence Over Their Populations - Both exercised significant influence over their respective communities—Milošević over the Serbs and Rugova over Kosovar Albanians.\n\n- Central Figures in the Conflict's Narrative - Both became symbolic leaders in the Kosovo conflict and were central to the way the conflict was perceived domestically and internationally.\n\n- Long-Term Impact on Kosovo's Future - Both played significant roles in shaping the political outcomes of the Kosovo conflict, through war (Milošević) and post-war leadership (Rugova).\n\n- Use of Nationalist Rhetoric - Both leaders appealed to national identity to gain support—Milošević promoted Serbian nationalism; Rugova championed Kosovar Albanian nationalism through calls for independence.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following:\n\n- Approach to Achieving Political Goals - Milošević relied on military force, repression, and ethnic cleansing to maintain control over Kosovo. Rugova consistently advocated for nonviolent resistance and diplomatic solutions.\n\n- Relationship with the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) - Milošević viewed the KLA as a terrorist group and sought to eliminate them through military means. Rugova initially distanced himself from the KLA's armed struggle, favoring peaceful negotiation, though later he recognized their role in the independence movement.\n\n- Political Ideology - Milošević promoted authoritarianism and Serbian nationalist dominance, while Rugova emphasized democracy, self-determination, and peaceful coexistence.\n\n- Impact on the Escalation or De-escalation of Conflict - Milošević's policies escalated the conflict through repression and war crimes. Rugova aimed to de-escalate tensions by promoting peaceful resistance.\n\n- International Perception and Legitimacy - Milošević was widely condemned by the international community and eventually indicted for war crimes. Rugova was respected internationally for his commitment to peaceful solutions and was recognized as Kosovo's legitimate leader post-conflict.\n\n- Outcomes After the Conflict - Milošević lost power in Serbia and was put on trial for war crimes by the ICTY. Rugova was elected Kosovo's president after the conflict and helped guide the territory toward statehood.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth Milošević and Rugova were central figures in the Kosovo conflict, each serving as the political leader of their respective ethnic groups ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Milošević led Serbia, promoting Serbian nationalism, while Rugova headed the Democratic League of Kosovo (LDK), advocating for Albanian autonomy ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both leaders used national identity to unify their populations ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Milošević invoked Serbia's historic and cultural claims to Kosovo, while Rugova promoted Kosovar Albanian identity through civil society and symbolic state-building ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both men played significant roles in shaping the trajectory of the conflict ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Milošević's policies escalated tensions and drew NATO's attention, while Rugova's peaceful resistance gained international sympathy and diplomatic support ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nBoth leaders had a different approach to achieving political goals ($\\checkmark$ difference). Milošević relied on military force, repression, and ethnic cleansing to maintain control over Kosovo. Rugova consistently advocated for nonviolent resistance and diplomatic solutions ($\\checkmark$ evidence) Their relationships with the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) also differed ($\\checkmark$ difference). Milošević targeted the KLA as a terrorist group and used violence to suppress it, while Rugova distanced himself from its armed tactics but later acknowledged its role in Kosovo's struggle ($\\checkmark$ evidence) Their international reputations and outcomes also contrasted ($\\checkmark$ difference). Milošević was indicted by the International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia (ICTY), while Rugova was internationally recognized for his peaceful leadership and elected as Kosovo's first post-war president ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0521","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0521\n\nDescribe **three** reasons the Kosovo Liberation Army (KLA) gained support among Kosovar Albanians. [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- Many Kosovar Albanians became frustrated with Ibrahim Rugova's policy of nonviolent resistance. After years of peaceful protests and diplomacy, there was little progress toward independence, making armed struggle seem like a more effective option.\n\n- Widespread repression by Serbian police and military forces, including arbitrary arrests, killings, and the destruction of villages, led many Albanians to believe they needed armed protection. The KLA was seen as the only group actively resisting Serbian aggression and defending Albanian communities.\n\n- The KLA promoted Kosovar Albanian nationalism and the goal of establishing an independent Kosovo. Their commitment to achieving self-determination through direct action appealed to many Albanians who wanted to break away from Serbian rule.\n\n- As Serbian forces escalated violence, the KLA gained support by presenting itself as the protector of Albanian civilians.  Their image as defenders against oppression made them heroes in the eyes of many Albanians. \n\n- The KLA's early successes in guerrilla attacks against Serbian targets raised their profile. These actions demonstrated that armed resistance could challenge Serbian forces and gave many Albanians hope for achieving political change.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMany Kosovar Albanians began to support the KLA because they had become disillusioned with Ibrahim Rugova's nonviolent strategies after peaceful resistance failed to stop Serbian repression ($\\checkmark$). The KLA also gained popularity by presenting itself as a force capable of protecting the Albanian population from the violence carried out by Serbian security forces ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the KLA gained some support after its early success against Serbian targets using guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0522","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0522\n\nDescribe the significance of the United Nations Interim Administration Mission in Kosovo (UNMIK) after the conflict ended in 1999.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- Established Interim Governance - UNMIK took over the administration of Kosovo after Serbian withdrawal, providing an interim government that maintained law and order during the transitional period.\n\n- Laid the Foundations for Self-Governance and Democracy - It helped to build democratic institutions, including establishing provisional governing bodies and organizing elections, which paved the way for Kosovo's future independence.\n\n- Maintained Security and Stability - Alongside NATO's Kosovo Force (KFOR), UNMIK was responsible for maintaining peace and security, preventing renewed violence between ethnic groups.\n\n- Protected Human Rights and Promoted Reconciliation - UNMIK was tasked with protecting minority rights, promoting reconciliation between Serbs and Albanians, and ensuring the rule of law in a deeply divided society.\n\n- Facilitated Kosovo's Transition Toward Independence - UNMIK oversaw Kosovo's transition from a war-torn region under Serbian control to a territory with functioning self-governing institutions, ultimately leading to Kosovo's unilateral declaration of independence in 2008.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nUNMIK was significant because it established interim governance in Kosovo after the withdrawal of Serbian forces, providing stability and ensuring that essential administrative functions, such as law enforcement and civil services, continued during a highly unstable period ($\\checkmark$). It also played a central role in building the framework for democratic self-governance by creating provisional institutions and organizing free elections, which were crucial steps toward Kosovo's political development and eventual declaration of independence ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, UNMIK worked to protect human rights and promote reconciliation between ethnic Albanians and Serbs by supporting legal reforms, rebuilding trust in public institutions, and addressing minority rights concerns in the post-conflict environment ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0523","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0523\n\nExplain **two** consequences of the NATO bombing campaign against Serbia during the Kosovo conflict.    [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanations:\n\n- Serbian Military Withdrawal from Kosovo:\n\n    - NATO's bombing campaign forced Serbia to withdraw its military and police forces from Kosovo, ending the violent repression of Kosovar Albanians. This paved the way for NATO's Kosovo Force (KFOR) and UNMIK to take over peacekeeping and governance in the region.\n\n- Strained Relations Between NATO and Russia\n\n    - Russia strongly opposed NATO's intervention, viewing it as a violation of international law and its own influence in the Balkans. This created lasting tensions between Russia and the West, contributing to a deterioration in post-Cold War relations.\n\n- Destruction of Serbia's Infrastructure and Economy\n\n    - NATO airstrikes caused extensive damage to Serbia's infrastructure, including bridges, factories, and power plants. This worsened Serbia's economic situation, increased domestic discontent, and weakened Milošević's regime.\n\n- Return of Kosovar Albanian Refugees\n\n    - After Serbia's forces withdrew, many Kosovar Albanians returned to Kosovo, reversing the mass displacement caused by the conflict. However, this also led to ethnic tensions and attacks on the remaining Serb minority, contributing to instability in the region.\n\n- Political Downfall of Slobodan Milošević\n\n    - The NATO bombing campaign undermined Milošević's authority at home. His failure to maintain control over Kosovo and Serbia's economic collapse contributed to his eventual overthrow in 2000 and his subsequent trial for war crimes at the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne consequence of NATO's bombing campaign was that Serbia was forced to withdraw its troops from Kosovo ($\\checkmark$), bringing an end to the ethnic cleansing of Kosovar Albanians and allowing NATO and UN forces to establish peacekeeping and civil administration ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother consequence was the worsening of NATO's relations with Russia, which opposed the intervention and viewed it as a breach of international law ($\\checkmark$). This contributed to increased geopolitical tensions and damaged cooperation between Russia and the West in the years that followed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0524","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0524\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying foreign intervention in Kosovo.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpts from United Nations Security Council Resolution 1244, adopted 10 June 1999. |\n\n[Source: *Resolution 1244*. (1999). UN Security Council. Retrieved March 18, 2025 from https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/274488?ln=en&v=pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The source is an official United Nations Security Council Resolution (1244), adopted by the most powerful international decision-making body. It represents the consensus (or at least the formal agreement) of major global powers at the time, including NATO members and Russia.\n- The purpose was to authorize foreign intervention, establish peacekeeping and administrative roles, and lay out a framework for restoring peace and autonomy in Kosovo. As a legal document, it clearly outlines what the international community intended to achieve, making it valuable for understanding the goals and legal justification for intervention.\n- The content provides specific details on the mandates for both military (KFOR) and civilian (UNMIK) intervention—demands for Yugoslav withdrawal, the demilitarization of the KLA, and the establishment of an interim administration. It also reflects the legal basis for intervention, representing the scope and nature of foreign intervention.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- It reflects the official stance of the international community, not necessarily the reality on the ground or the perspectives of local actors such as Kosovar Albanians, Serbs, or other stakeholders.\n- As a document produced at the start of the intervention, it does not provide information about what actually happened afterward, including the long-term outcomes, challenges, or unintended consequences of foreign intervention.\n- The purpose was to justify intervention, so its language may be idealistic and focused on what should happen, rather than acknowledging the practical difficulties, opposition, criticisms, or potential consequences of intervention.\n- The content provides broad goals and mandates, but lacks details on implementation, operational challenges, or local reactions to the intervention.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is a United Nations Security Council resolution adopted in June 1999, at the time when foreign intervention was being formalized. This makes it valuable because it reflects the consensus of major powers, including NATO members and Russia, providing insight into the international community’s agreed approach to ending the conflict and legitimizing intervention ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose of the resolution was to authorize international security and civil presences, specifically KFOR and UNMIK, and to establish a legal framework for foreign intervention, providing insight into the goals of the intervention, including the aim to protect civilians, restore order, and create conditions for self-governance in Kosovo ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content outlines specific mandates, such as the withdrawal of Yugoslav forces, the demilitarization of the KLA, and the creation of a secure environment for refugees and international agencies, which is useful for understanding the planned scope and nature of foreign intervention ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, because it is a formal UN resolution, it reflects only the official stance of the UN and major powers, excluding the perspectives of local actors or critics of intervention ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose of legitimizing intervention means the document focuses on presenting the intervention in a positive light, and does not acknowledge potential problems, such as challenges in enforcing peace or post-conflict tensions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0525","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0525\n\nUsing Source B, describe three purposes of the obligations outlined in the Kumanovo Agreement.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the Kumanovo Military Technical Agreement between the International Security Force ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$KFOR\") and the Governments of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia and the Republic of Serbia. Signed 9 June 1999. |\n\n[Source: *Military Technical Agreement between the International Security Force (“KFOR”) and the Governments of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia and the Republic of Serbia.* (1999, June 9).   Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://unmik.unmissions.org/kumanovo-military-technical-agreement Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To stop fighting and prevent further conflict by restricting FRY and Serbian forces from entering Kosovo or designated safety zones without permission from KFOR.\n- To authorize KFOR to use force if necessary to enforce the agreement and maintain security.\n- To protect KFOR personnel during their mission in Kosovo.\n- To support the international civil presence by helping create a stable environment.\n- To permit only local police in the Ground Safety Zone.\n- To clarify that any future return of Serbian personnel would require a separate agreement.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B explains that one purpose of the agreement was to bring an end to hostilities by ensuring that Serbian and FRY troops could not return to Kosovo or nearby security zones unless they received direct approval from KFOR ($\\checkmark$). It also gave KFOR the authority to take “necessary force” to make sure the terms of the agreement were followed, showing that peacekeepers were meant to have active enforcement powers ($\\checkmark$). Another goal was to help establish conditions in which international civilian organizations could safely begin their work in Kosovo, such as rebuilding governance and supporting displaced populations ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0526","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0526\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about the international response to the Kosovo conflict in 1999.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpts from United Nations Security Council Resolution 1244, adopted 10 June 1999. |\n\n[Source: *Resolution 1244* (1999) / adopted by the Security Council at its 4011th meeting, on 10 June 1999 Retrieved March 18, 2025 from https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/274488?ln=en&v=pdf Public Domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the Kumanovo Military Technical Agreement between the International Security Force ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$KFOR\") and the Governments of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia and the Republic of Serbia. Signed 9 June 1999. |\n\n[Source: *Military Technical Agreement between the International Security Force (“KFOR”) and the Governments of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia and the Republic of Serbia.* (1999, June 9).   Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://unmik.unmissions.org/kumanovo-military-technical-agreement Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor comparison, a minimum of **two** of the following with supporting evidence:\n- Both authorize international intervention in Kosovo.\n    - Source A gives a broad mandate for international civil and security presence.\n    - Source B details the operational terms under which KFOR will act.\n- Both emphasize the role of KFOR.\n    - Source A gives KFOR a peacekeeping mandate.\n    - Source B allows KFOR to use “necessary force” to enforce the agreement and secure the area.\n- Both aim to restore security and support post-conflict transition.\n    - Source A outlines provisional governance via UNMIK.\n    - Source B describes establishing a safe environment for the civil presence to function.\n\nFor contrast, a minimum of **two** of the following with supporting evidence:\n- Source A frames intervention as a collective, legal response, while Source B is a military capitulation.\n    - Source A establishes international authority through the UN\n    - Source B reflects a surrender of authority by Yugoslav forces.\n- Source A includes language reaffirming Yugoslav sovereignty, while Source B focuses on military withdrawal.\n    - Source A tries to balance autonomy and territorial integrity\n    - Source B prioritizes immediate demilitarization and enforcement.\n- Source B is more specific and tactical, while Source A is broader in scope.\n    - Source A discusses governance, autonomy, and long-term political goals\n    - Source B is operational-focused on troop movement, enforcement, and field logistics.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSources A and B both show that the international community intervened in Kosovo to restore peace and security in 1999 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A authorizes an international civil and security presence through the UN, while Source B outlines the conditions under which KFOR will take control of security and ensure compliance on the ground ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both emphasize KFOR's central role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A gives it peacekeeping authority, while Source B allows KFOR to use “necessary force” to enforce the agreement and protect personnel ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, both sources show that a secure environment was needed to support post-conflict recovery ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A links this to UNMIK and provisional institutions, and Source B describes KFOR creating conditions for a civil implementation presence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, Source A establishes international authority through a multilateral UN resolution, while Source B reflects a military surrender by the Yugoslav government, agreeing to terms dictated by NATO ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A represents diplomatic consensus; Source B is a binding military concession ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Also, Source A reaffirms Yugoslavia's sovereignty while granting autonomy to Kosovo, whereas Source B contains no reference to sovereignty and focuses entirely on the withdrawal and restrictions placed on Serbian forces ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0527","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0527\n\nUsing Source C, describe three reasons President Clinton gives to justify international action in Kosovo.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from United States President Bill Clinton, March 19, 1999, White House Press Conference. |\n\n[Source: *White House Press Conference* (March 19, 1999). US Department of State. Retrieved April 25, 2025 from https://1997-2001.state.gov/policy_remarks/1999/990319_clinton_kosovo.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The memory of past atrocities such as the massacre at Racak must not be repeated. Clinton describes Racak as the murder of civilians based on their identity.\n- Serbian forces are currently mobilizing near Kosovo, posing a direct threat. Clinton references 40,000 troops massing near villages like Racak.\n- The Kosovar Albanians have already chosen peace, but remain vulnerable. He says they are “without protection” despite their peaceful choice.\n- Inaction will lead to further massacres. “If we and our allies do not have the will to act, there will be more massacres.”\n- Strong international resolve can stop violence and save lives. “Action and resolve can stop armies.”\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C shows that President Clinton believed immediate international intervention was necessary to prevent further atrocities like those seen in Racak, where civilians were brutally killed ($\\checkmark$). He also emphasizes that Kosovar Albanians had committed to peace, yet remained exposed to violence from Serbian forces ($\\checkmark$). Clinton argues that only decisive action from the international community could halt future bloodshed and protect innocent lives from a repeat of past horrors ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0684","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0684\n\nWhat, according to Source T, were the reasons for NATO's military actions in Kosovo?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source T** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\\\$ |  Excerpt from “The situation in and around Kosovo” a statement issued at the Extraordinary Ministerial Meeting of the North Atlantic Council held at NATO Headquarters, Brussels, April 12, 1999. |\n\n[Source: North Atlantic Treaty Organization. (1999, April 12). *The situation in and around Kosovo: Statement issued at the Extraordinary Ministerial Meeting of the North Atlantic Council held at NATO Headquarters*, Brussels. Retrieved August 10, 2025 from https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/official_texts_27435.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- It felt that the actions of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia (FRY) had violated UN resolutions and undermined its values.\n- It was acting in support of the goals of the international community.\n- The actions of the FRY and President Milosevic created a massive humanitarian catastrophe and threatened to destabilise the surrounding region.\n- The Yugoslav government had committed violations of human rights and used indiscriminate force, pushing NATO to act.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source T, NATO intervened in the Kosovo conflict because it was concerned about the potential humanitarian consequences of the conflict ($\\checkmark$). NATO also intervened because it felt that the FRY had committed human rights violations ($\\checkmark$). Finally, NATO took action in Kosovo because it felt it was acting in support of the views of the international community ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0685","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0685\n\nWhat does Source R suggest about NATO's actions in Kosovo?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source R** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Editorial cartoon by Etta Hulme, published in the Fort Worth Star-Telegram in 1999. There are two soldiers standing in rubble, and surrounded by smoke. One soldier is labeled, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$NATO.\" The bottom of the cartoon reads, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$An autonomous Kosovo protected by NATO troops.\" |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Conflict%20%26%20Intervention/Kosovo/images/HI0685%20new.png\" alt=\"HI0685 new\">\n[Source: Hulme, E. (1999). *An autonomous Kosovo protected by NATO troops.* Originally published in the Fort Worth Star-Telegram. Retrieved August 10, 2025 from https://sites.libraries.uta.edu/ettahulme/image/20111739. Used by permission.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- After the bombings, there was nothing left for NATO to protect, though Kosovo was now autonomous.\n- The bombings had a devastating impact on Kosovo.\n- The area was left without a population.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource R suggests that, though the NATO bombings helped Kosovo defend its “autonomy”, as shown by the caption “An autonomous Kosovo protected by NATO troops” ($\\checkmark$), the area was devastated by the bombings, shown by the troops standing in an empty area of burning rubble, so there was not much left to defend ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0686","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0686\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source T for an historian studying international responses to the crisis in Kosovo.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source T** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\\\$ |  Excerpt from “The situation in and around Kosovo” a statement issued at the Extraordinary Ministerial Meeting of the North Atlantic Council held at NATO Headquarters, Brussels, April 12, 1999. |\n\n[Source: North Atlantic Treaty Organization. (1999, April 12). *The situation in and around Kosovo: Statement issued at the Extraordinary Ministerial Meeting of the North Atlantic Council held at NATO Headquarters*, Brussels. Retrieved August 10, 2025 from https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/official_texts_27435.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- Provides direct insight into the perspective of NATO's response.\n- Reveals how NATO sought to present its role and actions to the international community.\n- It provides concrete evidence of NATO's stated objectives in its actions and its interpretation of “international community” goals.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- Only represents NATO's perspective, not other international responses or their perspective on NATO's actions.\n- The portrayal of the crisis is selective and self-justifying to support NATO's actions.\n- Omits mention of criticism from UN member states, humanitarian NGOs critical of bombing, or countries advocating alternative solutions.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource T comes from NATO, one of the most critical international organizations responding to the crisis in Kosovo, providing direct insight into their actions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, this could also be considered a limitation, because it provides information only about NATO's response, not other international responses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nA limitation of the source's purpose is that NATO is attempting to justify its actions, which likely indicates that they are painting their decision in a positive light, biasing their account ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). That being said, the purpose is to provide NATO's final rationale for its actions in response to the crisis, providing insight into their decision-making ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nThe source's content is limited because it does not represent, share, or respond to the criticisms of its actions at the time, such as those from the UN ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0687","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0687\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources Q and S reveal about international responses to the crisis in Kosovo.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source Q** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\\\$ | An excerpt from “NATO's Response to the Kosovo Crisis” by Shalini Chawla, a research scholar at the Manohar Parrikar Institute for Defence Studies and Analyses, and published in the journal *Strategic Analysis* in September 2000. |\n\n[Source: Chawla, S. (2000, September). *NATO's Response to the Kosovo Crisis.* Originally published in Strategic Analysis. Retrieved August 10, 2025 from https://ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu/olj/sa/sa_sep00chs01.html. Used by permission.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source S** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\\\$ |  An excerpt from “Developing Effective Intervention: A Case Study of Genocidal Moments in Sebrenica and Kosovo” by student writer Caleb Bryan. Published May 2021 in the peer-reviewed University of Denver Undergraduate Research Journal. |\n\n[Source: Bryan, C (2021, May). *Developing Effective Intervention: A Case Study of Genocidal Moments in Srebrenica and Kosovo.* University of Denver Undergraduate Research Journal. Retrieved August 10, 2025, from https://duurjportal.com/index.php/duurj/article/view/94/77. Open access.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources identify NATO as a primary international actor in the crisis.\n- Both acknowledge NATO's humanitarian justification for its actions.\n- Both mention the harm to civilians caused by NATO's actions.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source Q is more critical of NATO's actions, suggesting ethical and legal flaws, while Source S is more balanced. It acknowledges harm but suggests that the end goal of pressuring Milosevic was achieved.\n- Source Q focuses more on a legal critique of NATO's actions, while Source S addresses primarily political and military consequences.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Source Q and Source S agree that NATO's intervention in Kosovo primarily involved an extensive air campaign aimed at stopping the Serbian campaign of ethnic cleansing and protecting Kosovo Albanians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nSource Q states that NATO engaged in a “massive air campaign involving nearly 1,000 aircraft\" to avoid friendly casualties, while Source S notes that NATO “launched an air campaign that attacked Yugoslavian military targets\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, both sources recognize the political outcome of this intervention: the capitulation of Yugoslavian President Milosevic ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). According to Source Q, “Yugoslavia submitted to most of NATO's demands,” and Source S confirms that “on June 10, 1999, the Yugoslavian president finally capitulated,” leading to the withdrawal of Serbian forces and the return of Kosovo Albanians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nDespite these similarities, the sources differ significantly in their assessment of NATO's intervention regarding legality and effectiveness ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source Q emphasizes the lack of a clear legal basis, explaining that “NATO air strikes violated well-established international laws” and that the operation was “neither based on a Security Council decision...nor pursued in collective self-defence,” highlighting serious ethical concerns, including “high civilian casualties.” In contrast, Source S does not focus on legality but frames the intervention as effective because it influenced political change in Serbia, noting that NATO bombings “made Milosevic's war in Kosovo unpopular” and sparked opposition rallies calling for his resignation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Furthermore, while Source Q critiques NATO's failure to prevent a humanitarian disaster or ensure long-term stability ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$), Source S stresses that “NATO bombs brought an end to the killing in Kosovo” and facilitated the return of displaced Kosovars, portraying the intervention as a successful resolution to the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."}]},{"name":"Rwanda","questions":[{"ref":"HI0047","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0047\n\nDescribe **two** consequences of the Rwandan Genocide.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Approximately 800,000 to 1 million Tutsis and moderate Hutus were killed in just 100 days, leading to widespread trauma and long-term demographic impacts.\n\n - A refugee crisis emerged as hundreds of thousands of Hutus fled to neighbouring countries, such as the Democratic Republic of Congo, fearing retaliation from the new government. Many refugee camps became bases for armed groups, prolonging regional instability.\n\n - Infrastructure was destroyed, agricultural production was disrupted, and a significant portion of the working-age population was killed, leaving Rwanda's economy in ruins.\n\n - Trust between ethnic groups was shattered, making reconciliation efforts difficult and prolonging tensions in Rwandan society.\n\n - The International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda (ICTR) was established by the United Nations to prosecute key figures responsible for genocide, setting an international legal precedent for addressing crimes against humanity.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne major consequence of the Rwandan Genocide was the massive loss of life, with approximately 800,000 to 1 million Tutsis and moderate Hutus killed over a span of just 100 days. This large-scale violence devastated families and communities across the country ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAnother consequence was a severe refugee crisis, as hundreds of thousands of Hutus fled to neighbouring countries such as the Democratic Republic of Congo, fearing retaliation from the new Tutsi-led government. Many of these refugee camps later became bases for armed groups, further destabilising the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0499","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0499\n\nExplain how ethnic divisions contributed to the outbreak of the 1994 conflict in Rwanda.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following:\n\n - The ethnic divisions between the Hutu and Tutsi were solidified during Belgian colonial rule, where the Tutsi were favoured for administrative roles. This led to resentment among the Hutu majority, who felt excluded.\n\n - After independence, the Hutu majority reversed this dynamic, cementing discrimination against the Tutsi through policies that excluded them from leadership and public life. This long-standing inequality deepened divisions and made large-scale violence more likely.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBelgian colonial rule in Rwanda deepened ethnic divisions by favouring the Tutsi minority for administrative roles while marginalising the Hutu majority ($\\checkmark$). This created resentment among the Hutu, who felt excluded from political and economic opportunities ($\\checkmark$). After Rwanda gained independence in 1962, the Hutu majority reversed this dynamic by implementing policies that discriminated against the Tutsi, excluding them from leadership positions and public life ($\\checkmark$). These long-standing inequalities fuelled ethnic tensions, ultimately contributing to large-scale violence, including the 1994 genocide ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0500","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0500\n\nIdentify **two** immediate outcomes of the assassination of President Habyarimana in 1994.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following: \n\n - The assassination triggered the genocide in Rwanda, with mass killings starting almost immediately in Kigali. Roadblocks were set up, and Hutu militias began targeting Tutsi civilians and moderate Hutu within hours of the crash.\n\n - The interim government accused foreign powers, including Belgium and France, of involvement in the plane crash, causing diplomatic tension. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe assassination of President Habyarimana led directly to the outbreak of the Rwandan genocide, with violence erupting in Kigali almost immediately. Hutu militias quickly established checkpoints and began targeting Tutsi individuals and moderate Hutu ($\\checkmark$\n\n). Additionally, the transitional government accused external powers like Belgium and France of involvement, causing diplomatic tension ($\\checkmark$\n\n)."},{"ref":"HI0501","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0501\n\nDescribe the role played by media and propaganda in enabling the genocide.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n - The media contributed to the spread of propaganda by promoting the Hutu Power ideology, spreading hate speech, and dehumanising the Tutsi population. This created an atmosphere of hostility and reinforced ethnic divisions.\n\n - The media helped with the organisation of violence by mobilising listeners and giving specific instructions on actions, such as what to do at roadblocks. This made the killings more systematic and widespread.\n\n - Constant repetition of hate-filled messaging led to the amplification of hate speech, influencing large portions of the population to support or participate in the genocide.\n\n - Propaganda played a key role in fuelling conflict by dehumanising the Tutsi. For example, the Kangura magazine published the Hutu Ten Commandments, which labelled Tutsi as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$enemies\" and promoted hostility towards them.\n\n - The government also spread messages through media outlets, such as the radio station Radio Télévision Libre des Mille Collines (RTLM), which referred to Tutsi as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cockroaches\" and called for their extermination. This continuous promotion of hate normalised violence and primed the population for genocide.\n\n - Political and military elites used propaganda to frame the genocide as self-defence\n\nThe RPF was accused of shooting down President Habyarimana's plane, an incident that was used as a pretext for stating the genocide\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe media was key in facilitating the Rwandan genocide by spreading divisive messages and encouraging violent actions. Radio Télévision Libre des Mille Collines (RTLM) incited violence by labelling Tutsi as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cockroaches” and calling for their extermination continuously both before and during the genocide ($\\checkmark$). This radio station was also nicknamed $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hutu Power Radio” and was used to spread Hutu Power, an ethnic supremacist ideology ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSimilarly, Kangura magazine reinforced hostility by publishing the Hutu Ten Commandments, which depicted Tutsi as enemies, liars, and traitors, promoting discrimination ($\\checkmark$). The media also facilitated violence by providing instructions, such as directing attackers to roadblocks and identifying targets, making killings more systematic ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0502","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0502\n\nDescribe **two** reasons France supported the Hutu-led government during the genocide.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following: \n\n - France aimed to maintain its influence in the region and feared a victory by the Tutsi-led RPF, which they associated with English-speaking powers like Uganda, the UK and the USA.\n\n - The French government viewed the Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF), led by Paul Kagame, as an extension of Anglophone influence, given its connections to Uganda and support from the United States and the UK. France saw this as a direct challenge to Francophone hegemony in the region.\n\n - France sought to maintain its historical and geopolitical influence in Central Africa, particularly in Rwanda, as part of its Miterrand's Françafrique policy, which sustained French dominance over its former colonies and allies and ensured they remained within the Francophone sphere.\n\n - The Hutu-led government under President Juvénal Habyarimana had long-standing ties to France. France provided economic aid, military training, and weapons to the regime, reinforcing its authority and ensuring Rwanda remained aligned with French interests.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrance supported the Hutu-led government because it sought to preserve its influence in the region and prevent the rise of the Tutsi-led RPF, which was seen as aligned with English-speaking countries like Uganda and the USA  ($\\checkmark$). The French government viewed the RPF as part of an \"Anglophone invasion\" that could undermine Francophone dominance in Africa ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0503","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0503\n\nDescribe **three** ways in which the international community failed to respond effectively to the Rwandan Genocide.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - UNAMIR was given a limited mandate that focused on maintaining peace rather than taking offensive actions to stop the killings. This lack of authority prevented the mission from intervening effectively.\n\n - Key international actors were fully informed of the atrocities occurring in Rwanda but chose not to classify it as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$genocide\" to avoid legal obligations to act under the UN Convention on Genocide.\n\n - Countries like the USA, France, and the UK showed a lack of political will to commit troops or resources, fearing the political and military costs of involvement.\n\n - France was not only reluctant to intervene but also actively supported the Hutu-led government during the genocide, contributing to the problem rather than resolving it.\n\n - The United States and other countries cited the financial burden of potential interventions, such as the high cost of jamming hate radio broadcasts.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe international community failed to act effectively during the Rwandan genocide in several key ways. The UN Security Council reduced the size of UNAMIR from 2,500 to a token force of 270 peacekeepers, even after General Dallaire had warned of impending genocide ($\\checkmark$). Major powers, including the USA, France, and the UK, showed a lack of commitment, avoiding intervention due to concerns over political repercussions, financial costs, and potential military risks ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the refusal to classify the violence as genocide allowed countries to avoid their legal obligations under the UN Genocide Convention, further delaying any meaningful action to stop the killings ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0504","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0504\n\nExplain **two** factors that increased tensions between Hutus and Tutsis as a result of Belgian rule.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Belgian policies introduced a system of forced labour, which disproportionately affected the Hutu majority. This economic inequality deepened ethnic tensions.\n- European colonial powers propagated racial theories that described the Tutsis as inherently superior due to their physical features and supposed intelligence, further institutionalising divisions.\n- Belgians collaborated with the Church to promote Christianity, which also served to reinforce ethnic stratification, as Tutsis were favoured for education and leadership roles while Hutus were marginalised. The colonial emphasis on cash crops like coffee led to economic policies that favoured Tutsi landowners, sidelining Hutu farmers and creating resource-related conflicts.\n- The shift from indirect rule under Germans to direct rule by Belgians centralised power, which they allocated to the Tutsi elite, further isolating the Hutu majority from political participation.\n- Ethnic classification was formalised in 1933 with identity cards that labelled individuals as Hutu, Tutsi, or Twa, preventing social mobility and institutionalising ethnic divisions.\n- In the 1950s, Belgium reversed its support for the Tutsi elite and began favouring the Hutu majority, responding to growing anti-colonial sentiment and aiming to maintain influence after Rwanda's independence. This shift inflamed ethnic tensions, leading to violent uprisings and setting the stage for future conflicts.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne factor that increased tensions between Hutus and Tutsis during Belgian rule was the system of forced labour introduced by the Belgians. The Hutu majority were often forced to work on cash crop plantations, such as coffee farms, under harsh conditions ($\\checkmark$). Meanwhile, Tutsis benefited economically as they were favoured by the colonial administration, exacerbating resentment among the Hutu population ($\\checkmark$). Another significant factor was Belgium's reliance on the Tutsi elite to administer the colony. The Tutsis were given preferential treatment, including access to education and political power while the Hutus were excluded from these opportunities ($\\checkmark$). This inequality created feelings of marginalisation and fuelled ethnic hostilities that persisted even after independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0505","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0505\n\nDescribe the role of the Catholic Church in shaping Rwandan society under Belgian rule.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- The Catholic Church collaborated with colonial authorities, working closely with the Belgian administration to reinforce social and political structures.\n- The Catholic Church favoured the Tutsi elite, as missionaries viewed Tutsis as more “civilised” and supported their dominance in leadership roles.\n- It promoted Christianity, encouraging large-scale conversion, including compulsory conversion for the Tutsi elite, with Christianity becoming a key part of colonial rule.\n- It influenced education, as the Catholic Church helped establish Western-style schools, which mainly benefited Tutsis and deepened ethnic divisions.\n- The Catholic Church played a role in the 1959 Hutu Revolution by supporting the shift in Belgian policy from favouring the Tutsi elite to backing the Hutu majority. Many clergy members aligned with Hutu political movements, helping to legitimise their rise to power.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Catholic Church played a key role during Belgian rule by collaborating with colonial authorities to maintain and reinforce the social hierarchy. Missionaries viewed Tutsis as more advanced than Hutus and supported their continued dominance in leadership positions ($\\checkmark$). Christianity was widely promoted, with mass conversions encouraged and made mandatory for the Tutsi elite, embedding religion into colonial governance ($\\checkmark$). The Church also played a major role in education by establishing Western-style schools, but these primarily served Tutsis, further deepening divisions between ethnic groups ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, in the late 1950s, the Church played a role in the shift of Belgian support from the Tutsi elite to the Hutu majority, backing the 1959 Hutu Revolution by aligning with Hutu political movements and legitimising their rise to power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0506","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0506\n\nIdentify **three** ways the First Republic in Rwanda (1963-1973) discriminated against the Tutsis.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The government restructured political power to ensure Hutu dominance, excluding Tutsis from leadership roles.\n- Tutsis were discriminated against in employment, being denied access to key positions in government and other sectors.\n- Ethnic violence targeted Tutsis, particularly in response to attacks from Inyenzi fighters (clarified from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$refugees\"), leading to massacres, including violence in 1963–1964 following attacks from Burundi.\n- Anti-Tutsi propaganda promoted Hutu superiority and justified their exclusion from society.\n- The government did not intervene effectively to stop violence against the Tutsi, allowing massacres to take place.\n- Education policies favoured Hutu students, limiting access to education for the Tutsi through ethnic quotas.\n- The government redistributed land from Tutsi landowners to Hutu citizens, further economically marginalizing the Tutsi population.\n- Tutsi refugees who fled violence were denied the right to return, effectively exiling them and preventing them from reclaiming property or rights.\n- The Parmehutu (Party of the Hutu Emancipation Movement) played a significant role in reinforcing these discriminatory policies, advocating for Hutu supremacy in politics and society.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe First Republic disadvantaged the Tutsi in multiple ways. Firstly, the government restructured political institutions to ensure Hutu dominance, preventing Tutsis from holding significant positions ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, Tutsis faced discrimination in employment, as many were excluded from government jobs and key professions ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, violence against the Tutsi increased, particularly following attacks by Inyenzi fighters, leading to massacres that were either ignored or not effectively addressed by the authorities ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0507","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0507\n\nIdentify **two** reasons Hutu extremists opposed the Arusha Accords.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The agreement allowed the return of Tutsi refugees, which extremists opposed because they feared it would shift the political balance or, in rural areas in particular, that Tutsi might reclaim land and resources previously low.\n- They promoted a multiparty system that threatened the dominance of the Mouvement Révolutionnaire National pour le Développement (MRND).\n- Some feared that integrating RPF forces would weaken Hutu control over the military.\n- Propaganda portrayed the Accords as a foreign-imposed solution favouring the RPF and that President Juvénal Habyraimana had betrayed Hutu interests by signing them.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHutu extremists opposed the Accords because they threatened Hutu political control both in their promotion of a multiparty system ($\\checkmark$) and because they allowed Tutsi refugees to return, which they feared would lead to a shift in the economic and social balance of power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0508","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0508\n\nExplain **two** ways the Hutu Ten Commandments sought to marginalise the Tutsi population.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting details:\n- Social and political exclusion\n    - The Hutu Ten Commandments explicitly called for Hutu to dominate Rwanda’s public institutions, banning Tutsi from positions of power or influence.\n    - For example, Tutsis were systematically removed from government jobs and leadership roles, reinforcing Hutu political control.\n- Economic discrimination\n    - The Ten Commandments reinforced the idea that Tutsi were \"dishonest\" in business and should not be trusted in professional relationships, ensuring economic exclusion and systemic marginalisation.\n    - Hutu employers were encouraged to hire only Hutus, leading to systemic economic exploitation of Tutsis.\n- Promotion of anti-Tutsi hate and violence\n    - Propaganda encouraged hostility toward Tutsis, fuelling public support for discriminatory policies and, later, acts of violence.\n    - The commandments declared that any Hutu who married or befriended a Tutsi would be labeled a \"traitor\" to their own ethnic group, cutting social ties and deepening the division between the Hutus and Tutsis.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe commandments declared that Tutsi were not to be trusted in public or business life, urging Hutu to dominate leadership positions and avoid working with Tutsi ($\\checkmark$). This policy created economic and institutional barriers for the Tutsi, pushing them to the fringes of society ($\\checkmark$). They also forbade any personal relationships between Hutu and Tutsi, such as marriages or friendships, with violators being branded as traitors ($\\checkmark$). This measure deepened the social isolation of the Tutsi and increased hostility between the two groups ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0509","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0509\n\nAccording to Source A, how did French troops in Rwanda impact the genocide?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An extract from *South African History Online,* which outlines the nature and impact of the French military intervention in Rwanda during the 1994 genocide. |\n\n[Source: South African History Online (1994, 21 August). *The last French troops pull out of Rwanda.* Retrieved March 3, 2025 from: https://www.sahistory.org.za/dated-event/last-french-troops-pull-out-rwanda. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny of following:\n- They were deployed with the purpose of protecting individuals on “genocide lists” (though the source implies they did not)\n- They carried out human rights abuses including rape of genocide survivors\n- They claimed to have saved lives through Operation Turquoise\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe French troops trained militias and gave them weapons ($\\checkmark$), and carried out human rights abuses ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0510","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0510\n\nUsing the information in Source A and your own knowledge, discuss the extent to which French military intervention in the Rwandan genocide caused more harm than good.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An extract from *South African History Online,* which outlines the nature and impact of the French military intervention in Rwanda during the 1994 genocide. |\n\n[Source: South African History Online (1994, 21 August). *The last French troops pull out of Rwanda.* Retrieved March 3, 2025 from: https://www.sahistory.org.za/dated-event/last-french-troops-pull-out-rwanda. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFrom Source A:\n- Good:\n    - The French claimed to have saved thousands of lives by establishing a “safe zone” in south-west Rwanda.\n- Harm:\n    - French troops were accused of supporting the genocidal regime by training militias and supplying them with weapons.\n    - French soldiers committed human rights abuses including raping survivors.\n\nFrom own knowledge:\n- Good:\n    - Although geographically limited, Operation Turquoise did provide some short-term protection for Tutsi and moderate Hutu civilians by setting up protected camps and evacuating thousands of people who would otherwise have been killed in the genocide\n    - The intervention helped deliver humanitarian aid and stabilise parts of the country during the final stages of the genocide.\n- Harm:\n    - Operation Turquoise allowed many perpetrators of the genocide to flee into neighbouring countries, especially Zaire, helping them escape justice.\n    - France had strong political and military ties with the Hutu-led government before and during the genocide, raising questions about bias and motives.\n    - The intervention began late, after much of the genocide had already occurred, limiting its effectiveness and making it appear symbolic or politically driven.\n    - The mission is described as “highly controversial,” suggesting widespread international and local criticism.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall argument, linking the evidence clearly to the good/harm caused by French troops.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTo a large extent, French military intervention in Rwanda caused more harm than good. Source A states that French troops were accused of helping the genocidal regime by training militias and supplying them with weapons ($\\checkmark$).  It also mentions that French soldiers committed serious human rights abuses, including the rape of survivors ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, while it provided some temporary protection for civilians who were potential targets of the genocide and delivered humanitarian aid, Operation Turquoise allowed many perpetrators of the genocide to escape into neighbouring Zaire, helping them avoid justice ($\\checkmark$). France's long-standing ties with the Hutu-led government further raised doubts about the neutrality of their intervention ($\\checkmark$). Although Source A notes that France claimed to have saved thousands of lives by establishing a “safe zone,” the overall impact was limited and came too late to stop the genocide ($\\checkmark$). In conclusion, both the source and historical context show that despite some humanitarian efforts, the French intervention did more harm than good ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0511","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0511\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the role of women in Hutu extremist ideology?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “Hutu Ten Commandments,” published in 1990 in the Rwandan extremist magazine *Kangura*. |\n\n[Source: Wikipedia (n.d). *Hutu Ten Commandments.* Retrieved April 30, 2025 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hutu_Ten_Commandments. CC BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Women's value was defined primarily by their domestic roles and loyalty, as the source highlights that they were seen as better wives, mothers, and secretaries, and that their value was defined by their domestic roles and loyalty, not by holding public or political power\n- Women were expected to act as ideological enforcers within the private sphere, as the source urges Hutu women to be “vigilant” and to “bring your husbands, brothers and sons back to reason.”\n- Hutu women were portrayed as morally and ideologically superior to Tutsi women, as seen in the claim that Hutu women were “more suitable and conscientious,” reinforcing ethnic and gender hierarchies.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that Hutu extremist ideology saw women's primary value in their domestic roles, as the source describes them as better wives, mothers, and secretaries than Tutsi women ($\\checkmark$).  The source also describes Hutu women as “more suitable and conscientious,” portraying them as better wives, mothers, and secretaries than Tutsi women, reinforcing both gender and ethnic hierarchies ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0512","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0512\n\nUsing the information in Source B, identify and explain **two** ways in which Hutu people were encouraged to exclude or discriminate against Tutsi people.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “Hutu Ten Commandments,” published in 1990 in the Rwandan extremist magazine *Kangura*. |\n\n[Source: Wikipedia (n.d). *Hutu Ten Commandments.* Retrieved April 30, 2025 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hutu_Ten_Commandments. CC BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n-  Marriage and personal relationships:\n    - Hutu men were warned not to marry or even employ Tutsi women (as secretaries or concubines).\n    - Any Hutu who did so was labelled a “traitor,” framing personal relationships with Tutsi women as acts of betrayal.\n- Business and economic activity:\n    - Hutu were forbidden from going into business with Tutsi — including partnerships, loans, or investing in Tutsi-owned businesses.\n    - Helping Tutsi get public contracts, bank loans, or import licences was also condemned. This aimed to cut Tutsi out of the economy.\n- Gender roles and loyalty:\n    - Hutu women were urged to be vigilant and to influence their husbands, brothers, and sons to follow anti-Tutsi ideology.\n    - This extended the ideology into private and family life.\n- Employment and power:\n    - Strategic jobs (political, military, education) were to be reserved for Hutu only, excluding Tutsi from public and professional life.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A shows that Hutu were told not to marry Tutsi women ($\\checkmark$). Any Hutu who did so was labelled a traitor, which discouraged personal relationships and promoted ethnic separation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt also states that Hutu should not go into business with Tutsi or help them get contracts or loans ($\\checkmark$). This was meant to push Tutsi out of the economy and prevent them from gaining financial power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0513","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0513\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying Hutu ideology in Rwanda before the 1994 genocide.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “Hutu Ten Commandments,” published in 1990 in the Rwandan extremist magazine *Kangura*. |\n\n[Source: Wikipedia (n.d). *Hutu Ten Commandments.* Retrieved April 30, 2025 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hutu_Ten_Commandments. CC BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The source was published in 1990 in Kangura, a Hutu Power magazine, making it a primary source that reflects the extremist ideology circulating in Rwanda shortly before the 1994 genocide.\n- It provides detailed insight into the social and political goals of Hutu extremists, including ethnic exclusion, propaganda, and identity formation.\n- The source outlines specific expectations placed on Hutu men and women, revealing how hate ideology was promoted in both public and private life.\n- It reflects efforts to mobilise support and unify Hutu people around an anti-Tutsi agenda, offering valuable context for understanding the causes of the genocide.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- As a piece of extremist propaganda, the source is highly biased and not a balanced or objective account of events.\n- It does not reveal how widely these ideas were accepted, resisted, or implemented in practice across Rwandan society.\n- The source represents only the viewpoint of one group (Hutu extremists) and does not include the perspectives of moderate Hutu, Tutsi, or international actors.\n- While it reflects intent and ideology, it does not provide direct evidence of the actions that followed or how these ideas translated into violence.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is Kangura, a Hutu Power magazine published in 1990 by Hutu extremists. It is valuable because it serves as a primary source from the period leading up to the Rwandan genocide, offering direct insight into the ideology being promoted at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, its limitation lies in the fact that it reflects only the views of extremist groups and does not reveal how these ideas were received or challenged by the broader Rwandan population ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nThe purpose of the source was to promote Hutu supremacist ideology and to mobilise support for the exclusion and marginalisation of Tutsi people. This is valuable as it illustrates how propaganda was strategically used to encourage ethnic division, define loyalty, and prepare the population for violence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nThe content of the source is valuable because it contains explicit instructions to exclude Tutsi individuals from social, political, and economic life, including marriage, employment, and education. It also calls on Hutu women to help enforce ideological loyalty within families. This provides historians with a clear example of how hatred and division were encouraged in everyday interactions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0979","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0979\n\nIdentify **two** terms of the Arusha Peace Agreement signed in August 1993.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Integration of RPF into national armed forces (50% Tutsi)\n- Establishment of broad-based transitional government (BBTG, Hutu-Tutsi power-sharing), including reduction in the powers of the president and the creation of a national assembly\n- Ceasefire between RPF and government forces\n- Repatriation and resettlement of refugees\n- Rule of law to be established based on democracy and pluralism with guarantee of human rights and civil liberties as well as an independent judiciary\n- UN to oversee the transition (UN Assistance Mission for Rwanda, UNAMIR) and implementation\n- Establishment of Commissions for National Unity and Reconciliation, Reform of the Judiciary and Demobilization and Reintegration of former combatants\n- The transitional period would end after two years with free and fair democratic elections.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne term of the Arusha Peace Agreement was the integration of the Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF) into the national armed forces, with 50% of the positions allocated to Tutsi members ($\\checkmark$). Another term was the establishment of a broad-based transitional government that would include power-sharing between Hutu and Tutsi representatives ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0980","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0980\n\nExplain **two** reasons why the Rwandan government considered the formation of RTLM *(Radio Télévision Libre des Mille Collines)* in 1993 to be a strategic political tool.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- RTLM was used to spread anti-Tutsi propaganda, framing the Tutsi minority as a threat to national security and encouraging fear and hatred among the Hutu population.\n- The station was a platform to promote the ideology of Hutu Power, reinforcing ethnic divisions and attacking political moderates and peace efforts such as the Arusha Accords.\n- It provided a means of communication to a largely illiterate population, making it an effective tool to reach rural communities with state-sanctioned messaging.\n- It disguised anti-Tutsi propaganda as entertainment through music, satirical political commentary, and conspiracy theories, making the content more accessible, especially among the less-educated portions of the population.\n- RTLM helped the government counteract opposition media, such as the pro-RPF newspaper Kanguka, ensuring state control over public discourse.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne reason the Rwandan government considered RTLM as a political tool was to disseminate anti-Tutsi propaganda that incited fear and hatred ($\\checkmark$). By consistently portraying Tutsi civilians as enemies and traitors, RTLM helped psychologically prepare the Hutu population to accept, support, or even participate in ethnic violence ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA second reason was that radio was the most accessible medium for the majority of the Rwandan population, especially those who were illiterate ($\\checkmark$). The government used RTLM to promote Hutu Power ideology broadly and to control the national narrative, effectively countering opposition voices such as those from RPF-aligned media ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0981","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0981\n\nExplain **two** ways the Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF) challenged the authority of the Habyarimana regime between 1990 and 1994.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The RPF launched a military invasion from Uganda in October 1990, initiating a civil war that exposed the weakness of the Rwandan Armed Forces and forced the regime to rely on foreign military support.\n- The RPF maintained a guerrilla war in northern Rwanda, destabilising key regions and creating a sense of national insecurity that undermined public confidence in Habyarimana's leadership.\n- The RPF's presence and influence pressured the government into negotiations, leading to the signing of the Arusha Accords in 1993, which proposed power-sharing and military integration—both seen as threats by Habyarimana's hardline supporters.\n- The RPF was supported by Tutsi exiles, whose return challenged the government's ethnic nationalism and revived demands for refugee repatriation and political reform.\n- The RPF's military professionalism and discipline, especially under Paul Kagame after 1990, made it a credible alternative to the government's disorganised and politicised armed forces.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe RPF challenged the regime by launching an armed invasion from Uganda in 1990 ($\\checkmark$). This triggered a civil war that forced the Rwandan government to divert resources to defence and exposed their reliance on foreign allies like France and Zaire for military support ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe RPF also challenged the regime through their involvement in political negotiations, particularly the Arusha Peace Agreement in 1993 ($\\checkmark$). The RPF's demands for power-sharing and inclusion in the national army threatened Habyarimana's control and provoked opposition from Hutu extremists within his own government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0982","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0982\n\nExplain **two** ways foreign powers were involved in the Rwandan Civil War between 1990 and 1994.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- France sent troops to Rwanda in 1990 under Opération Noroît, officially to protect French nationals, but in practice to support the Habyarimana regime against the RPF. France also provided weapons and training to the Rwandan Armed Forces throughout the conflict.\n- Uganda served as the base from which the RPF launched its initial invasion in October 1990. Although Uganda officially denied involvement, the RPF operated with the logistical advantages of Ugandan territory.\n- Belgium, as part of the UN peacekeeping mission (UNAMIR), contributed troops tasked with monitoring the ceasefire and implementation of the Arusha Accords. However, they withdrew their forces after 10 Belgian soldiers were killed in April 1994.\n- The United Nations, through UNAMIR, was involved in overseeing the peace process after the Arusha Accords in 1993, but was underfunded, limited in mandate, and unable to prevent escalating violence.\n- Zaire provided support to the Habyarimana government by offering military assistance, though on a smaller scale than France.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne way foreign powers were involved was through French military support ($\\checkmark$). In 1990, French troops entered Rwanda supposedly to protect French citizens, but they also provided backing to the Rwandan government by helping train its army and supplying weapons ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother example is Uganda's indirect role in supporting the RPF ($\\checkmark$). The rebel group launched its invasion from Ugandan territory, and while the Ugandan government denied official involvement, it allowed the RPF to organise and operate across the border ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0983","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0983\n\nOutline the role played by the Interahamwe between 1990 and 1994.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Interahamwe functioned as a paramilitary militia loyal to the ruling MRND party, formed in the early 1990s.\n- They were involved in targeted attacks against Tutsi civilians and political opponents even before the official start of the genocide.\n- The group helped intimidate moderate Hutus, opposition parties, and anyone supporting the Arusha Accords.\n- They played a key role in training and arming local youth for future mass violence.\n- They were central to spreading Hutu Power ideology, participating in rallies and distributing propaganda.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of the roles played by the Interahamwe was to spread extremist propaganda ($\\checkmark$). The Interahamwe participated in rallies and worked alongside media outlets like RTLM to promote Hutu Power ideology and increase ethnic tension in the lead-up to the genocide ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0984","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0984\n\nExplain **two** consequences of the assassination of President Habyarimana in April 1994.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The assassination triggered the start of the Rwandan Genocide, with mass killings beginning in Kigali just hours after the plane was shot down.\n- It led to the murder of moderate Hutu politicians, including Prime Minister Agathe Uwilingiyimana, who was assassinated the following day.\n- The event provided Hutu Power extremists with a pretext to implement their pre-planned genocide, as it created chaos and removed the president who had signed the Arusha Accords.\n- It caused a breakdown in the peace process outlined in the Arusha Agreement, which was abandoned immediately after the assassination.\n- It led to conflicting international responses, with suspicion cast on different actors, including the RPF, French forces, and Hutu extremists.\n- The incident sparked diplomatic fallout, including tensions between France and Rwanda over responsibility for the crash and the failure to prevent escalation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne major consequence of the assassination was the outbreak of the genocide itself ($\\checkmark$). Within hours of the crash, killings of Tutsi civilians and moderate Hutus began in Kigali, marking the start of mass violence across the country ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother consequence was the complete breakdown of the Arusha peace process ($\\checkmark$). The assassination removed the president who had supported the negotiations, allowing Hutu extremists to reject power-sharing and pursue violent control instead ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0985","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0985\n\nExplain **two** ways the international response to President Habyarimana's death contributed to the worsening crisis in Rwanda.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Delayed international reaction allowed extremist violence to escalate unchecked in the critical early hours and days.\n- Belgian peacekeepers were quickly disarmed and later withdrawn, weakening the UN presence (UNAMIR) and sending a message that international forces would not intervene.\n- France's role at the crash site raised suspicions, with allegations that French troops interfered with the investigation, which fuelled mistrust and diverted attention.\n- No credible investigation followed, due to the disappearance of the black box and political sensitivities, which allowed extremist narratives to dominate.\n- UN's restricted mandate (under Chapter VI) meant peacekeepers were not authorised to use force to stop the violence.\n- International debate over responsibility (RPF vs Hutu extremists) created division and inaction among global actors, including at the UN Security Council.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne way the international response worsened the crisis was through inaction ($\\checkmark$). The UN and foreign governments hesitated to react quickly after the crash, giving extremists time to organise and begin the killings without resistance ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother problem was the withdrawal of Belgian peacekeepers after some of their soldiers were killed ($\\checkmark$). This significantly weakened the UN's ability to maintain order and sent a message that the international community would not intervene ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0986","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0986\n\nDiscuss the role of social and economic factors that contributed to the outbreak of genocide in Rwanda.\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**A full-mark response should:**\n1. Include at least **two** of the following with supporting details.\n2. Address both social **and** economic factors.\n3. Include a concluding statement that aligns with the discussion.\n\n- Poverty and land pressure\n    - Rwanda's high population density and reliance on subsistence agriculture created land scarcity\n    - As the economy worsened in the early 1990s, resentment toward the Tutsi minority—who were perceived by some as economically privileged or “foreign”—intensified.\n- Impact of economic crisis and structural adjustment\n    - Rwanda's economy suffered from a drop in coffee prices (its main export), leading to widespread unemployment and rising cost of living\n    - IMF-imposed structural adjustment policies reduced public sector spending, intensifying hardship and fuelling ethnic scapegoating\n- Refugee return fears\n    - The planned return of hundreds of thousands of Tutsi exiles under the Arusha Accords generated fear among Hutu farmers and elites who were concerned about losing land or political influence.\n- Historical social divisions\n    - Long-standing discrimination against the Tutsi minority, dating back to colonial rule and reinforced by post-independence Hutu regimes, had led to ethnic stereotyping and polarisation.\n    - These divisions were exploited by Hutu Power ideology in the early 1990s.\n- Illiteracy and reliance on media\n    - High illiteracy rates meant many Rwandans were vulnerable to hate propaganda spread by radio stations like RTLM and magazines like *Kangura*, which blamed Tutsis for the country's suffering.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\nConcluding statement:\n- Should include an overall analysis of the role of the factors discussed in the outbreak of the genocide.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nA key economic factor was the severe downturn in Rwanda's economy in the early 1990s. Falling global coffee prices led to widespread poverty, especially in rural areas ($\\checkmark$). This created frustration and fear, which extremist politicians exploited by blaming Tutsis for the country's problems ($\\checkmark$). Along with the struggling economy, many Hutu farmers feared that if Tutsi exiles returned to Rwanda under the Arusha Accords, they would lose access to land and political influence ($\\checkmark$). This fear of continued economic struggles helped justify violence in the minds of some ordinary citizens ($\\checkmark$). However, social divisions also played a role. For decades, Tutsis were portrayed as a separate, untrustworthy group ($\\checkmark$). These long-standing ethnic stereotypes, reinforced by colonial policies and Hutu-led governments, laid the groundwork for genocidal thinking ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe combination of economic desperation with long-standing ethnic tensions created a volatile environment that enabled extremist ideologies to grow, sparking mass violence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0987","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0987\n\nIdentify **two** ways in which the withdrawal of UNAMIR troops in April 1994 worsened the genocide.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The withdrawal reduced the number of peacekeepers able to protect civilians.\n- It left vulnerable people, such as the Tutsi at the ETO school and Don Bosco School, unprotected.\n- It signalled to the perpetrators that the international community would not intervene.\n- It allowed the killers to operate with greater freedom and less fear of being stopped.\n- It showed international priorities lay in evacuating foreigners, not protecting Rwandans.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe withdrawal of UNAMIR troops meant that safe zones were no longer secure for civilians because of fewer peacekeepers ($\\checkmark$). It also gave the impression that the international community had abandoned Rwanda ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0988","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0988\n\nIdentify **two** short-term consequences of the assassination of Prime Minister Agathe Uwilingiyumana in April 1994.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Collapse of the constitutional line of succession\n- Extremists could install an interim government\n- Sparked further killings of moderate Hutus\n- Showed that even high-level officials weren't safe from violence\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe assassination of Prime Minister Agathe Uwilingiyumana allowed extremist leaders to form an interim government without opposition ($\\checkmark$). It also sent a clear message that even senior Hutu politicians who opposed violence could be targeted ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0989","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0989\n\nAccording to Source A, what were the key limitations of the UNAMIR mission?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from a Master's thesis by Michael L. Artbauer which examines the failure of UNAMIR in Rwanda. Written in 2001, the extract outlines the constraints placed on the mission by the UN Security Council, especially the United States. |\n\n:box[The size of the UNAMIR force contingent was an immediate source of debate. A UN military expert recommended a force size of 8,000 men, General Dallaire asked for 4,500, and the United States recommended a force of 500. It was not until October 5, that a force of approximately 2,500 was approved for the mission. The UNAMIR force next suffered from a delay in funding. They arrived ill equipped to serve in their role and were undersupplied once the genocide commenced [...] The Security Council decided to place clear limits on the UNAMIR mandate. As a result, the mandate plainly deviated from the role as envisioned in the actual Arusha Accords. Several key considerations had been weakened, including notably the premise that UNAMIR would guarantee overall security in Rwanda. The mandate restricted UNAMIR from assisting in the search and recovery of arms caches and the neutralisation of armed gangs throughout the country as called for in the Arusha Accords. UNAMIR as established, notes Des Forges, could only $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$contribute to security, and not throughout the country, but only in the city of Kigali.\"]\n\n[Source: Artbauer, M. L. (2001). *Rwanda Reconsidered* (thesis). Calhoun: The NPS Institutional Archive. Retrieved July 24, 2025, from http://hdl.handle.net/10945/10937. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Limited mandate\n    - UNAMIR was not authorized to guarantee overall security in Rwanda, but rather only in the capital city of Kigali. It could not stop armed gangs or search for/recover weapons around the country.\n- Lack of resources and delays in funding\n    - The UNAMIR mission was underfunded and severely understaffed, hindering its operational capacity on the ground.\n- Limited forces\n    - Despite clear evidence of escalating violence, the UN Security Council failed to expand the mission or authorise additional troops.\n- Bureaucratic constraints\n    - The mission was delayed by administrative procedures and political indecision, which reduced its ability to act quickly and effectively.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nUNAMIR had a limited mandate, which meant it was not authorised to use force except in self-defence, preventing effective intervention during the genocide($\\checkmark$). The mission was also under-resourced and understaffed, making it difficult to monitor or halt the violence ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the UN Security Council failed to provide reinforcements, even after clear evidence of mass killings, further weakening the mission's ability to respond ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0990","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0990\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the role of the UN Security Council in the Rwandan genocide?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from a Master's thesis by Michael L. Artbauer which examines the failure of UNAMIR in Rwanda. Written in 2001, the extract outlines the constraints placed on the mission by the UN Security Council, especially the United States. |\n\n:box[The size of the UNAMIR force contingent was an immediate source of debate. A UN military expert recommended a force size of 8,000 men, General Dallaire asked for 4,500, and the United States recommended a force of 500. It was not until October 5, that a force of approximately 2,500 was approved for the mission. The UNAMIR force next suffered from a delay in funding. They arrived ill equipped to serve in their role and were undersupplied once the genocide commenced [...] The Security Council decided to place clear limits on the UNAMIR mandate. As a result, the mandate plainly deviated from the role as envisioned in the actual Arusha Accords. Several key considerations had been weakened, including notably the premise that UNAMIR would guarantee overall security in Rwanda. The mandate restricted UNAMIR from assisting in the search and recovery of arms caches and the neutralisation of armed gangs throughout the country as called for in the Arusha Accords. UNAMIR as established, notes Des Forges, could only $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$contribute to security, and not throughout the country, but only in the city of Kigali.\"]\n\n[Source: Artbauer, M. L. (2001). *Rwanda Reconsidered* (thesis). Calhoun: The NPS Institutional Archive. Retrieved July 24, 2025, from http://hdl.handle.net/10945/10937. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Security Council approved a much smaller force than recommended (only 2,500 instead of 4,500 or 8,000).\n- The Security Council delayed funding, leaving UNAMIR ill-equipped and unable to act effectively.\n- The Security Council restricted the mission's mandate, allowing it to operate only in Kigali and not throughout Rwanda.\n- The Security Council weakened the mission's authority, making it unable to conduct arms recovery or stop armed gangs.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe UN Security Council played a limiting role in the genocide by authorizing only 2,500 troops—far fewer than requested—reducing the mission's strength ($\\checkmark$).  It also restricted UNAMIR's mandate to Kigali, preventing action across Rwanda ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0991","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0991\n\nUsing Source A and your own knowledge, discuss the extent to which the failure of UNAMIR was due to the limitations imposed by the Security Council.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from a Master's thesis by Michael L. Artbauer which examines the failure of UNAMIR in Rwanda. Written in 2001, the extract outlines the constraints placed on the mission by the UN Security Council, especially the United States. |\n\n:box[The size of the UNAMIR force contingent was an immediate source of debate. A UN military expert recommended a force size of 8,000 men, General Dallaire asked for 4,500, and the United States recommended a force of 500. It was not until October 5, that a force of approximately 2,500 was approved for the mission. The UNAMIR force next suffered from a delay in funding. They arrived ill equipped to serve in their role and were undersupplied once the genocide commenced [...] The Security Council decided to place clear limits on the UNAMIR mandate. As a result, the mandate plainly deviated from the role as envisioned in the actual Arusha Accords. Several key considerations had been weakened, including notably the premise that UNAMIR would guarantee overall security in Rwanda. The mandate restricted UNAMIR from assisting in the search and recovery of arms caches and the neutralisation of armed gangs throughout the country as called for in the Arusha Accords. UNAMIR as established, notes Des Forges, could only $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$contribute to security, and not throughout the country, but only in the city of Kigali.\"]\n\n[Source: Artbauer, M. L. (2001). *Rwanda Reconsidered* (thesis). Calhoun: The NPS Institutional Archive. Retrieved July 24, 2025, from http://hdl.handle.net/10945/10937. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA full-mark response should:\n- Include evidence from Source A, either directly quoted or paraphrased\n- Provide evidence from outside of Source A (own knowledge)\n- Provide a concluding sentence aligned with the evidence and analysis\n\nFrom Source A:\n- UNAMIR was given a restricted mandate, authorising it only to act in self-defence, which prevented it from taking proactive measures during the genocide.\n- The Security Council failed to respond adequately, even after warnings of mass killings.\n- Reinforcement requests were ignored, and the Council avoided invoking the term “genocide” to circumvent international legal obligations under the Genocide Convention.\n- The actions of dominant powers (e.g. the United States and France) reflected strategic political interests rather than humanitarian concern.\n\nOwn knowledge:\n- Belgian troop withdrawal following the murder of ten peacekeepers early in the conflict, reducing UNAMIR's operational capacity.\n- Lack of political will among influential states, particularly the US, which was deeply affected by the failure in Somalia in 1993.\n- Limited international media coverage and insufficient public pressure meant Rwanda did not become a policy priority for most governments.\n- Structural and logistical challenges, including UN bureaucracy, further delayed an effective response.\n- The genocide unfolded rapidly, making intervention even more difficult.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall argument, stating whether the Security Council's limitations were the principal cause of UNAMIR's failure or one of several significant contributing factors.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Security Council assigned UNAMIR a highly restrictive mandate, permitting the use of force solely in self-defence, which left peacekeepers unable to act decisively in the face of mass violence ($\\checkmark$). Despite receiving reports of atrocities, the Council refused to deploy additional troops, leaving the mission under-resourced and overwhelmed ($\\checkmark$). It also deliberately avoided characterising the crisis as “genocide”, thereby evading legal obligations under the 1948 Genocide Convention ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe mission's failure, however, was not solely the result of Security Council decisions. The withdrawal of Belgian forces following the murder of ten of their peacekeepers further undermined the mission's capacity ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the United States, deeply affected by its failed intervention in Somalia, opposed any substantial engagement in Rwanda, reflecting a wider absence of political will among key powers ($\\checkmark$).\n\nWhile the Security Council's constraints critically undermined UNAMIR's effectiveness, the mission ultimately collapsed due to the intersection of political apathy, troop withdrawals, and the broader unwillingness of the international community to intervene ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0992","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0992\n\nWith reference to the origin, purpose and content, assess the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the international response to the Rwandan genocide\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A confidential telegram sent on 15 April 1994 by the US Department of State to its mission at the United Nations in New York. The telegram provided official guidance on the immediate withdrawal of UNAMIR personnel from Rwanda during the early days of the genocide. |\n\n[Source: U.S. Department of State. (1994, April 15). *Talking Points for UNAMIR Withdrawal.* National Security Archive. Retrieved July 24, 2025, from https://nsarchive2.gwu.edu//NSAEBB/NSAEBB53/index.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor values, any combination of the following:\n- The origin of the source is a confidential cable from the US Department of State and it offers a firsthand and contemporary view of American foreign policy during the genocide, revealing official attitudes at a critical moment.\n- The purpose is to inform and instruct US diplomats at the United Nations and it provides insight into the diplomatic language used to justify peacekeeping withdrawal, which is valuable for understanding political decision-making.\n- The content reveals the United States' firm opposition to maintaining UNAMIR and its prioritisation of troop safety, illustrating how national interest influenced international policy.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The origin of the source is US government communication and it reflects an official narrative and is unlikely to include criticism of US policy or acknowledgment of misjudgements.\n- The purpose to justify troop withdrawal makes the source one-sided, as it seeks to legitimise a controversial stance and avoids deeper moral or legal accountability.\n- The content omits any reference to the genocide itself, including the systematic targeting of Tutsi civilians, and avoids use of the word “genocide,” which significantly limits historical understanding.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source, a confidential cable from the US Department of State written in April 1994, adds value as it provides a real-time reflection of the views and strategic thinking of a major global actor during the Rwandan genocide ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The source's content illustrates how US national interests influenced its foreign policy decisions in the case of the genocide ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the origin also limits the usefulness of the source, as it reflects the official position of the US government and is unlikely to include any criticism of American foreign policy or its failure to act ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose of the source is limited as it seeks to legitimise the actions of its allies rather than viewing their actions more objectively ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0993","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0993\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the United States’ position on the UNAMIR mission in Rwanda?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A confidential telegram sent on 15 April 1994 by the US Department of State to its mission at the United Nations in New York. The telegram provided official guidance on the immediate withdrawal of UNAMIR personnel from Rwanda during the early days of the genocide. |\n\n[Source: U.S. Department of State. (1994, April 15). *Talking Points for UNAMIR Withdrawal.* National Security Archive. Retrieved July 24, 2025, from https://nsarchive2.gwu.edu//NSAEBB/NSAEBB53/index.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The United States firmly opposed maintaining a UN peacekeeping force in Rwanda because it believed that UNAMIR could not operate effectively or safely under the circumstances.\n- The source suggests the US prioritised the safety of peacekeepers over continued humanitarian intervention.\n- The US felt the situation in Rwanda did not justify a military presence and that negotiations should be handled politically, not by peacekeeping forces.\n- The US commended the efforts of those who had participated in UNAMIR but endorsed their withdrawal.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that the United States firmly opposed the continuation of the UNAMIR mission in Rwanda, arguing that there was no viable role for peacekeepers in the ongoing conflict ($\\checkmark$). It also implies that the US prioritised the safety of peacekeeping personnel, as it commended troop-contributing nations for their efforts and supported their decision to withdraw ($\\checkmark$).  Furthermore, the source indicates that the US believed any future international involvement should focus on political negotiations led by a representative of the Secretary-General, not through continued peacekeeping operations ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"History of Asia and Oceania","subtopics":[{"name":"Cold War Conflicts in Asia","questions":[{"ref":"HI0192","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0192\n\nAccording to Source A, what were the United States' goals in Asia in 1949?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US National Security Council document NSC 48/2 entitled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Position of the United States With Respect to Asia.” Written in 1949.\n\n[Source: National Security Council. (December 23, 1949). *NSC 48/2 - The Position of the United States with Respect to Asia*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1949v07p2/d387. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The U.S. aimed to help Asian nations develop into stable, self-sustaining states aligned with the United Nations Charter.\n\n - The U.S. sought to develop sufficient military power in non-Communist Asian nations like South Korea to prevent Communist expansion.\n\n - A key goal was to eliminate or significantly reduce Soviet power in Asia, ensuring the USSR could not threaten regional peace or U.S. security.\n\n - The U.S. planned to provide military, economic, and political aid to strategic areas, particularly South Korea, to bolster non-Communist regimes.\n\n - The U.S. committed to resisting Communist encroachment by strengthening the forces of freedom and maintaining the independence of allied nations.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source A, the United States pursued multiple objectives in Asia in 1949. One key goal was to support the creation of stable, independent nations that aligned with the principles of the United Nations Charter ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the U.S. aimed to strengthen the military capabilities of non-Communist countries, such as South Korea, to act as a barrier against Communist expansion ($\\checkmark$). Another major objective was to curb Soviet influence in the region, preventing the USSR from disrupting security and stability in Asia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0193","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0193\n\nAccording to Source B, what were the objectives of the United States in Indochina during the French occupation?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US Department of State $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1949.” \n\n[Source: Department of State (September 27, 1948). *Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1948*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v06/d33. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The U.S. sought to limit Communist influence in Southeast Asia, particularly in Indochina, where Ho Chi Minh’s Communist-led movement threatened the stability of the region and U.S. interests.\n\n - A U.S. goal was to assist France, a key ally, in maintaining control over Indochina to prevent further economic strain on France, which was vital to European recovery after World War II.\n\n - The U.S. aimed to achieve stability in Southeast Asia by encouraging France to address the nationalist aspirations of the Vietnamese people, as ending the conflict was seen as crucial to reducing anti-Western sentiment.\n\n - The U.S. sought to avoid becoming directly involved in the conflict by pressing France to resolve the situation through reforms and negotiations rather than relying solely on military solutions.\n\n - The U.S. recognized the importance of maintaining French influence in Indochina, both to sustain a Western-aligned presence in the region and to counter Communist expansion.\n\n - The U.S. aimed to foster non-Communist nationalist movements in Indochina as an alternative to Ho Chi Minh's Communist-dominated leadership, though this goal was hampered by a lack of viable alternatives.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, the United States had several objectives in Asia in 1949. One key aim was to curb the spread of Communism, especially in Indochina, where Ho Chi Minh's leadership posed a direct threat to stability and U.S. strategic interests ($\\checkmark$). Another goal was to bolster France’s position in the region, as maintaining French control was seen as critical to preventing further economic strain on a vital European ally recovering from World War II ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the U.S. prioritized fostering stability in Southeast Asia by urging France to meet the nationalist demands of the Vietnamese, which would help reduce anti-Western sentiment and ease tensions in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0194","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0194\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the causes of the Vietnam War.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a US Department of State $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1949.” \n\n[Source: Department of State (September 27, 1948). *Department of State Policy Statement on Indochina, September 27, 1948*. Office of the US Historian. Retrieved December 3, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v06/d33. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:: \n\n - Origin:\n\n     - Produced by the U.S. Department of State in 1948, the source provides an official American perspective on the early Cold - War tensions in Indochina. It reflects U.S. assessments of the causes of the conflict and offers insight into early American concerns about Communist expansion.\n\n - Purpose:\n\n     - The document's purpose is to advise U.S. policymakers on the situation in Indochina, particularly how instability and Communist activity were contributing to conflict. It is useful because it reveals how the United States assessed the role of Communism in the causes of the war, helping historians understand why the U.S. eventually intervened.\n\n- Content:\n\n     - The source highlights key causes of the Vietnam War, including French failures to maintain control, the growing strength of Communist forces, and nationalist opposition to colonial rule.\n\n    - It provides insight into how the U.S. viewed the conflict as a Cold War struggle rather than a colonial independence movement, which influenced later American actions in Vietnam.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - Origin:\n\n     - As a U.S. government document written in 1948, the source is biased toward portraying Communism as the primary cause of conflict, potentially overlooking other nationalist motivations behind the war. It predates key developments such as the escalation of the First Indochina War (1946-1954), meaning it provides only an early snapshot of the causes rather than a full picture of the conflict's evolution.\n\n - Purpose:\n\n     - The document's purpose was to guide U.S. foreign policy, meaning it may exaggerate the threat of Communism while downplaying the significance of anti-colonial nationalism as a cause.\n\n - Content:\n\n     - The source primarily reflects French and American viewpoints, offering little insight into Vietnamese nationalist perspectives beyond their Communist affiliations.\n\n     - It oversimplifies the causes of the war by presenting it as a Cold War struggle rather than an independence movement, failing to explore the broader social, political, and economic factors that contributed to the conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIts origin as an official U.S. Department of State document ($\\checkmark$ VO) written in 1948 ensures it provides a contemporary perspective on the U.S.'s analysis of French colonial struggles and growing Communist influence in Indochina. This makes it particularly valuable for understanding how American policymakers perceived the situation early in the conflict. The purpose of the source, to guide U.S. foreign policy decisions ($\\checkmark$ VP), offers insight into the strategic calculations behind U.S. concerns over Ho Chi Minh’s leadership and the need to balance support for a French ally with the broader goal of containing Communism. Additionally, the content is highly detailed, describing key causes such as the failure of French negotiations with Ho Chi Minh, their military ineffectiveness, and the establishment of puppet governments, which bolstered Ho's prestige and weakened French credibility ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source has notable limitations. While the U.S. Department of State is a reliable origin, the American-centric nature of the document reflects a strong Cold War bias, as it exaggerates Communist influence while downplaying the nationalist motivations of the Vietnamese people ($\\checkmark$ LO). Furthermore, since the source was written in 1948, it lacks knowledge of later developments in the First Indochina War and the escalation of U.S. involvement, limiting its utility for a full understanding of the conflict ($\\checkmark$ LO). In terms of purpose, while it aims to analyze the situation, its role in shaping U.S. policy may have led to selective interpretation or omission of details that could weaken the argument for containment ($\\checkmark$ LP). Finally, the content, though informative, does not include Vietnamese perspectives, particularly those of non-Communist nationalists or ordinary citizens, and it neglects to address the role of international actors such as China or the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0303","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0303\n\nDescribe **two** political causes of the Korean War.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The division of Korea after WWII led to the country being split along the 38th parallel, with North Korea led by Kim Il Sung, a communist leader, and South Korea led by Syngman Rhee, an anti-communist leader. Both claimed to be the legitimate government, leading to increasing tensions and border conflicts\n\n - Cold War tensions between the United States and the Soviet Union turned Korea into a proxy battleground, as both superpowers sought to expand their influence and ideologies in Asia, making war more likely.\n\n - The Soviet Union and China encouraged North Korea's leader, Kim Il Sung, to invade the South, providing military aid and believing the United States would not intervene, increasing North Korea's willingness to launch an attack.\n\n - The United States' policy of containing communism led to its commitment to defending South Korea, ensuring that any invasion by the North would result in direct American involvement, increasing the scale of the conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne political cause of the Korean War was the division of Korea at the 38th parallel after World War II, which resulted in two opposing governments. Kim Il Sung led the communist North, while Syngman Rhee led the anti-communist South, and both claimed to be the rightful rulers of all Korea, leading to rising tensions ($\\checkmark$). Another cause was the broader Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union. Korea became a key battleground in their ideological struggle, with each superpower supporting its respective Korean ally, making conflict increasingly inevitable ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0330","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0330\n\nWith reference to its content and tone, what does Source A suggest about the United States' view of its role in Southeast Asia during the Cold War?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a transcript of an interview of US Secretary of State Dean Rusk by NBC correspondent Elie Abel, broadcast over nationwide television on August 5, 1964. |\n\n[Source: Office of the Secretary of Defense Vietnam Task Force. (1964, August 24). *Justification of the War, Public Statements, Volume II: D-The Johnson Administration.* United States - Vietnam Relations 1945-1967. Pentagon Papers Part V, Vol II, D. Retrieved October 26, 2025 from https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/research/pentagon-papers/Pentagon-Papers-Part-V-A-Vol-IID.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with explanation and a conclusion:\n\nTo promote and preserve a peaceful international order\n- Rusk states that U.S. involvement in Southeast Asia was part of a “general policy since World War II” aimed at creating “a decent world community” where nations could live peacefully and leave their neighbours alone.\n- Emphasises a moral and idealistic purpose. The U.S. as a global guardian of peace, order, and cooperation.\n- Suggests continuity with broader U.S. postwar goals such as the United Nations Charter and containment of aggression.\n\nTo defend nations in Southeast Asia from external aggression (“pressures from the North”)\n- Identifies the central problem as communist pressure and interference from North Vietnam (“the north”).\n- Claims peace could be achieved if northern forces “left their neighbours to the south alone.”\n- Portrays the U.S. as defending smaller, independent states (like South Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia) from communist expansion or domination.\n- Reflects the Cold War containment mindset: the U.S. must stop aggression wherever it arises to preserve global stability.\n\nTo uphold international law and protect U.S. rights\n- Justifies military action in the Gulf of Tonkin as necessary because U.S. ships were “under attack… in international waters in which we have a perfect right to be.”\n- Frames intervention as a defensive and legal response protecting freedom of navigation and U.S. personnel.\n- Emphasises legitimacy and restraint, not aggression, to present U.S. actions as law-abiding within the international system.\n\nTo demonstrate U.S. credibility and deter further communist aggression\n- Argues that if the U.S. had not responded to attacks, “Hanoi and those who might be standing behind Hanoi” could have misjudged U.S. resolve.\n- Warns that a failure to act might make the U.S. seem a “paper tiger.”\n- Reinforces the Cold War doctrine of credibility: showing strength to prevent further challenges from communist powers (including China and the USSR).\n- Suggests that global perception of U.S. power was as important as the specific regional conflict.\n\nTo ensure stability and self-determination for Southeast Asian nations\n- Asserts that if interference from the North stopped, “these peoples of that area could have their peace and could have a chance to work out their own lives in their own way.”\n- Positions U.S. involvement as supporting independence and local autonomy, not colonial control.\n- Reflects Cold War-era belief that American intervention protected freedom and self-rule against totalitarianism.\n\nTo prevent the escalation of communist influence (Domino Theory context)\n- Though not explicitly stated in the extract, Rusk's reasoning aligns with containment and domino logic: preventing one state's fall to communism to protect others in the region.\n- His framing of “pressures from the north” reflects the U.S. belief that Vietnam's security was tied to global Cold War balance.\n- Implies that Southeast Asia was a testing ground for U.S. resolve against communism. Failure here could invite further aggression elsewhere.\n\nTo align with postwar U.S. moral identity\n- Rusk's tone underscores that the U.S. was acting as a benevolent power, seeking peace rather than conquest.\n- Portrays involvement as a moral duty, consistent with America's self-image as leader of the free world.\n- Reinforces the Cold War narrative that U.S. intervention served universal ideals, not narrow national interests.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that the United States viewed its role in Southeast Asia as moral and idealistic ($\\checkmark$). Rusk describes U.S. policy since World War II as aimed at building “a decent world community” where nations “leave their neighbours alone.” This reflects a belief that the U.S. was acting as a guardian of peace and international order, consistent with its self-image as leader of the “free world” ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt also presents the U.S. as defensive and law-abiding, claiming that American ships were “under attack… in international waters in which we have a perfect right to be” ($\\checkmark$). The language suggests a sense of restraint and legitimacy, portraying intervention as protection, not aggression ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, the tone is confident, moral, and paternal. The U.S. saw itself not as an aggressor but as a responsible global power, defending peace, legality, and freedom in Southeast Asia during the Cold War ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0331","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0331\n\nWhat, according to Source B, does the war in Vietnam reveal about the nature of the United States' involvement in the Cold War?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | John F. Kerry. Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations, 22 April 1971. |\n\n[Source: Kerry, J. F. (1971, April 22). *Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations.* Retrieved October 25, 2025 from: https://voicesofdemocracy.umd.edu/kerry-statement-before-the-senate-committee-speech-text/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- U.S. involvement in the Cold War was based on distortion and misinformation, as veterans describe the official justification as a “terrible distortion” and a “mystical war against communism.”\n- It portrays the war as morally corrupt, claiming America “lost her sense of morality” and accepted atrocities like My Lai.\n- Implies that U.S. leaders misunderstood the conflict, calling Vietnam “a civil war” and “an effort by a people… seeking their liberation,” not a front in a global fight against communism.\n- The U.S. is depicted as hypocritical, since linking the deaths of soldiers to “the preservation of freedom” is called “the height of criminal hypocrisy.”\n- The war is shown to have damaged the United States internally, with such hypocrisy having “torn this country apart.”\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, the war in Vietnam revealed that the United States' involvement in the Cold War in Asia was based on distortion and false justification. The veterans describe official claims of a “mystical war against communism” as a “terrible distortion,” suggesting U.S. leaders misrepresented the conflict ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThey also imply that America had become morally corrupted, saying the country “lost her sense of morality” and accepted atrocities like My Lai without question ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the source portrays U.S. actions as hypocritical and self-destructive, condemning attempts to link soldiers' deaths to “the preservation of freedom” as “the height of criminal hypocrisy,” which had “torn this country apart\" ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0345","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0345\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It was French weakness that enabled the Viet Minh to win the war in Indochina between 1946 and 1954.” To what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n- Candidates will consider the merits or otherwise of the statement that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It was French weakness that enabled the Viet Minh to win the war in Indochina between 1946 and 1954.”\n- Candidates may choose to structure their response chronologically by discussing events and policies prior to 1946, as long as these are clearly connected to the question and help explain why the Viet Minh ultimately won the war.\n- Candidates may agree, partly agree or disagree with the statement.\n\nIndicative content:\n\nFrench Weaknesses (supporting the claim)\n\n- Military weaknesses\n    - Underestimation of Viet Minh tactics: French forces, trained mainly in conventional warfare, struggled to adapt to the Viet Minh's guerrilla tactics.\n    - Overextension of forces: French troops were spread thin across rugged terrain and unfamiliar territory.\n    - Inadequate local support: French reliance on colonial troops and lack of strong Vietnamese allies undermined military cohesion.\n    - Dien Bien Phu (1954): A catastrophic strategic blunder; the French miscalculated the Viet Minh's capabilities, leading to a decisive and humiliating defeat.\n- Political and strategic failures\n    - Failure to win “hearts and minds”: French colonial repression and racial discrimination alienated the local population.\n    - Inflexible colonial policy: Refusal to offer genuine political reforms or autonomy until it was too late (e.g., the Bao Dai solution lacked credibility).\n    - French political instability: Frequent government changes in Paris undermined a coherent long-term strategy in Indochina.\n- Economic limitations\n    - High cost of war: The French economy, already weakened by WWII, could not sustain a prolonged conflict overseas.\n    - Dependency on U.S. funding: From 1950, the U.S. financed up to 80% of the French war effort, highlighting France's inability to bear the cost alone.\n- Morale and public opinion\n    - Declining morale among French troops and public: Growing war-weariness and lack of public support for the conflict at home.\n    - Perception of a lost cause: By the early 1950s, even French officials had come to view the war as unwinnable.\n\nViet Minh Strengths (challenging the claim)\n\n- Leadership and organisation\n    - Ho Chi Minh: Charismatic leader who united diverse nationalist forces and provided ideological direction.\n    - Vo Nguyen Giap: Brilliant military strategist; orchestrated both guerrilla warfare and conventional campaigns like Dien Bien Phu.\n- Nationalist motivation\n    - Popular support: Strong sense of nationalism and resentment of colonial rule; many Vietnamese viewed the war as a liberation struggle.\n    - Effective propaganda: Promoted an image of defending Vietnamese independence from foreign imperialism.\n- Military strategy and tactics\n    - Guerrilla warfare: Used terrain knowledge, hit-and-run attacks, and ambushes to wear down French forces.\n    - Transition to conventional warfare: Gradual shift to larger-scale offensives in the early 1950s as Viet Minh forces gained strength.\n    - Logistical achievements: Impressive supply and troop movements through jungle terrain, exemplified during the siege of Dien Bien Phu.\n- Support networks and external aid\n    - Chinese support: Following the establishment of the PRC in 1949, the new government provided weapons, training, and sanctuary.\n    - Soviet support: Provided ideological and material support, though more limited than China's.\n\nContextual and Contributing Factors\n- Impact of World War II\n    - Japanese occupation (1940-45): Weakened French authority and exposed the Vietnamese to alternative power structures.\n    - August Revolution (1945): Demonstrated Viet Minh's capacity to mobilise and briefly govern.\n- Failure of post-WWII diplomacy\n    - Breakdown of negotiations (e.g., Fontainebleau 1946): French refusal to grant independence led to armed conflict.\n    - Return of French colonial forces: Seen by many Vietnamese as a betrayal of self-determination after WWII.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Viet Minh victory in the First Indochina War (1946-1954) marked a decisive end to nearly a century of French colonial rule in Vietnam. While French weakness, militarily, politically, and economically, played a crucial role in this outcome, it would be inaccurate to attribute the Viet Minh's success solely to French failure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The strengths of the Viet Minh, including their leadership, nationalist appeal, military adaptability, and external support, were equally, if not more, significant in shaping the course and conclusion of the war. The Viet Minh's victory resulted from a combination of French weaknesses and Viet Minh strengths, with the latter ultimately proving decisive ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nFrench military weaknesses were evident throughout the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Trained primarily for conventional European warfare, French forces struggled against the guerrilla tactics employed by the Viet Minh. The French army was overextended and unfamiliar with the terrain, which gave the Viet Minh a clear advantage ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Their inability to adapt was most notably demonstrated in the 1954 Battle of Dien Bien Phu, where French commanders underestimated Viet Minh capabilities and failed to account for their ability to transport artillery through dense jungle ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The defeat was catastrophic, resulting in over 10,000 French casualties and forcing France to negotiate peace terms at Geneva ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This highlights a failure of French military strategy, but it also underscores the effective planning and determination of the Viet Minh ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nFrench political weaknesses further undermined their war effort ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The French government's reluctance to grant meaningful political concessions to the Vietnamese alienated potential allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The attempt to establish the State of Vietnam under Emperor Bao Dai was widely seen as a puppet regime lacking legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Meanwhile, the French Fourth Republic was plagued by political instability, with frequent changes in leadership weakening the consistency and effectiveness of colonial policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). French inability to win over the Vietnamese population, combined with ongoing repression, forced labour, and the memory of colonial abuses, meant that the war lacked popular support both in Indochina and increasingly in France ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Along with its political weakness, France was also economically weak and unable to wage a long and costly colonial war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Having suffered severe losses during the Second World War, France was dependent on American financial support from as early as 1950. By 1954, the United States was funding approximately 80% of the French war effort ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This not only demonstrated the unsustainability of French commitment but also revealed the war's growing reliance on foreign backing rather than national resolve ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, focusing solely on French weaknesses neglects the vital role played by the Viet Minh's strengths ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Under the leadership of Ho Chi Minh and military commander Vo Nguyen Giap, the Viet Minh successfully mobilised a broad-based nationalist movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Ho's ability to blend nationalism with Marxist ideology allowed the movement to appeal to both traditional anti-colonial sentiment and newer revolutionary ideals. The Viet Minh were seen not only as military leaders but as legitimate representatives of Vietnamese independence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This nationalist spirit of the Vietnamese people played a key role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The French had ruled Vietnam since the mid-nineteenth century, and their return after the Japanese occupation during the Second World War was widely resented ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The Viet Minh successfully portrayed themselves as liberators fighting against foreign domination, while the French appeared as reactionary colonialists attempting to reassert outdated imperial authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This unevenness in purpose meant the Viet Minh were fighting a people's war, while France was fighting to preserve its empire, a cause that lacked moral and political support both domestically and internationally ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nMilitarily, the Viet Minh were innovative and adaptive ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Initially relying on guerrilla tactics, they gradually transitioned to more conventional operations as their capabilities grew ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Their ability to use the terrain to their advantage, organise supply lines through rugged terrain, and mobilise rural populations made them formidable opponents ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The siege at Dien Bien Phu was the culmination of years of tactical learning and logistical sophistication ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Moreover, the Viet Minh received significant foreign military support, especially after the Communist victory in China in 1949 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Chinese provided training, weapons, and sanctuary across the border, giving the Viet Minh a safe rear base and enabling them to escalate their campaign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Although Soviet support was more limited, the broader context of the Cold War ensured that the Viet Minh were part of a larger ideological struggle, bolstering their international standing and material resources ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the statement that \"French weakness enabled the Viet Minh to win the war\" is only partly accurate. While French military, political, and economic weaknesses contributed to their defeat in the First Indochina War, these factors alone do not fully explain the outcome. The Viet Minh's success was also driven by effective leadership, nationalist legitimacy, tactical ingenuity, and crucial foreign assistance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0346","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0346\n\nDiscuss the role of Khmer Rouge ideology in the rise and fall of Pol Pot.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\n - Candidates are expected to give a balanced and well-supported explanation of the role of Khmer Rouge ideology in both why Pol Pot came to power and why he eventually lost power.\n\nIndicative content:\n\nRise of Pol Pot \n\nCore ideological principles\n\n - Maoist agrarian socialism: Revolution led by peasants rather than the industrial working class; rural life idealised as morally pure and economically self-sufficient.\n\n - Radical egalitarianism: Abolition of private property, money, religion, and social classes; urban-rural divide erased.\n\n - Nationalism and anti-imperialism: Framed the revolution as a defence of Cambodian sovereignty against US imperialism, Vietnamese dominance, and Western cultural influence.\n\n - Self-reliance: Rejection of foreign economic and political dependence.\n\nPol Pot's role in promoting ideology\n\n - Personal conviction in the purity of rural society; belief that revolutionary transformation required immediate, total change.\n\n - Propaganda portrayed Phnom Penh as decadent and peasants as the backbone of the nation.\n\n - Oversaw ideological training for cadres to ensure uniformity of message.\n\nIdeology as a mobilising tool\n\n - Promised land redistribution and equality to the rural poor.\n\n - Framed US bombing (1969-73) and Lon Nol's government as evidence of capitalist oppression.\n\n - Portrayed Khmer Rouge as protectors of Cambodian culture and sovereignty.\n\nInteraction with other factors\n\n - Political instability: Weak leadership and corruption under Sihanouk and Lon Nol created fertile ground for revolutionary ideology to take root.\n\n - Vietnam War: Regional conflict, US bombing, and displacement amplified anti-imperialist sentiment.\n\n - Sihanouk alliance (1970): Pragmatic political move justified ideologically as part of a united anti-imperialist struggle.\n\n - Chinese support: Provided weapons, training, funding, and Maoist ideological legitimacy, strengthening Khmer Rouge's capacity.\n\nFall of Pol Pot \n\nIdeology-driven domestic policies\n\n - $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Year Zero”: Erasure of Cambodia's past; evacuation of cities; abolition of money and markets; banning of religion.\n\n - Collectivisation and agricultural targets: Driven by ideological rejection of private property and adoption of Maoist economic models; led to famine.\n\n - Cultural purification: Targeting intellectuals, religious leaders, and ethnic minorities as ideological enemies.\n\nConsequences of ideological policies\n\n - Economic collapse: Disruption to agriculture and trade.\n\n - Mass death: 1.5-2 million deaths from execution, famine, disease, and overwork.\n\n - Loss of popular support: Harsh policies alienated rural and urban populations alike.\n\nInternal party purges\n\n - Ideological purity tests: Leaders accused of deviation or pro-Vietnamese sympathies executed (*e.g., S-21 prison*).\n\n - Purges removed skilled administrators and military leaders, undermining governance and security.\n\n - Climate of fear stifled dissent, preventing policy correction.\n\nIdeology and foreign policy\n\n - Ultra-nationalism & anti-Vietnamese hostility: Historical animosity amplified by the belief that Vietnam sought to dominate Cambodia.\n\n - Justification of aggressive cross-border raids into  Vietnam (1977-78) as defensive actions against imperialism.\n\n - Refusal to compromise due to ideological conviction.\n\nInteraction with other factors\n\n - Vietnamese military superiority: Overwhelmed Khmer Rouge forces during 1978-79 invasion.\n\n - Loss of international legitimacy: Atrocities reduced global support, though China continued backing Khmer Rouge for geopolitical reasons.\n\n - Diplomatic isolation: Limited allies willing to intervene militarily.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nKhmer Rouge ideology, a radical fusion of Maoist agrarian socialism, Cambodian nationalism, and anti-imperialism, was central to Pol Pot's political career. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) It shaped the movement's rise by providing a revolutionary vision that mobilised support in the countryside, exploited political instability, and aligned with foreign backers. At the same time, it played a decisive role in his downfall, as its uncompromising application underpinned catastrophic social and economic policies, fuelled internal purges, and provoked a war with Vietnam. This essay will examine how Khmer Rouge ideology, under Pol Pot's direction, contributed to his rise to power and later to his fall. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organisation}$)\n\nPol Pot's rise was closely tied to the appeal of Khmer Rouge ideology to Cambodia's rural majority. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) Influenced by Maoist principles, Pol Pot believed that the peasantry, not the urban working class, would be the driving force of revolution. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$) He idealised rural life as pure and uncorrupted, contrasting it with the perceived decadence of Phnom Penh. This ideological stance resonated with peasants neglected by Sihanouk's monarchy and alienated by the corruption of Lon Nol's republic. Under Pol Pot's leadership, the Khmer Rouge promised land redistribution, equality, and the removal of exploitative urban elites. Propaganda portrayed the revolution as a struggle for both national independence and social liberation, giving it an appeal that rival movements lacked. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$)\n\nKhmer Rouge ideology also shaped how Pol Pot exploited the turmoil of the Vietnam War. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) The US bombing of Cambodia between 1969 and 1973 caused massive displacement and destruction, which Pol Pot used to frame the Lon Nol regime as a puppet of imperialist powers. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$) His anti-imperialist and nationalist ideology helped position the Khmer Rouge as the defenders of Cambodian sovereignty against both Western and Vietnamese influence. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) After Lon Nol's coup removed Sihanouk in 1970, Pol Pot pragmatically allied with the former monarch despite ideological differences, justifying this as a necessary anti-imperialist united front. This move broadened the Khmer Rouge's support base and was consistent with Pol Pot's ability to adapt ideological presentation for strategic gain. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$)\n\nForeign backing reinforced both the ideological and practical capacity of Pol Pot's movement. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) Alignment with Maoist China brought weapons, training and political legitimacy. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$) Pol Pot admired China's Cultural Revolution and incorporated its themes of self-reliance, collectivism, and cultural purification into the Khmer Rouge programme. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$) Beijing's endorsement bolstered Pol Pot's authority within the party and provided a model for the radical transformation he envisioned for Cambodia. Without this external support and the disorganisation of Lon Nol's forces, Pol Pot's capture of Phnom Penh in April 1975 would have been far more difficult. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)\n\nHowever, once in power, Pol Pot's rigid application of Khmer Rouge ideology became the main driver of the regime's collapse. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) Declaring $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Year Zero,” he sought to erase Cambodia's past and rebuild it according to ideological principles. Cities were emptied, markets abolished, religion banned, and private property eliminated. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$) These measures, grounded in his belief that only a classless, rural society could achieve true revolution, caused massive disruption to agriculture and trade. Pol Pot's push for rapid collectivisation and unrealistic rice production targets, inspired by Mao's Great Leap Forward, resulted in famine and mass starvation. As many as two million Cambodians, roughly a quarter of the population, died through execution, forced labour, disease, and malnutrition. These policies alienated the population, undermined the economy, and left the regime militarily and politically weakened. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysi}$s)\n\nPol Pot's leadership style, secretive, paranoid, and intolerant of dissent, amplified the destructive impact of ideology. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) His insistence on ideological purity led to widespread purges within the Khmer Rouge. Party members, military leaders, and even close associates were accused of betraying revolutionary principles and executed. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$)This climate of fear eroded trust within the leadership, removed experienced officials, and further weakened the state's capacity to govern. Pol Pot's personal authority was so closely tied to the ideology that questioning the programme was equated with treason, ensuring that policy mistakes were repeated rather than corrected. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)\n\nKhmer Rouge ideology also dictated an aggressive foreign policy that directly contributed to the regime's downfall. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) Pol Pot's ultra-nationalist and anti-Vietnamese stance was rooted in historical animosity and reinforced by his conviction that Vietnam sought to dominate Cambodia. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$) This belief justified repeated cross-border raids into Vietnam in 1977 and 1978, which Pol Pot described as defensive actions against imperialism. In reality, these provocations brought retaliation from a militarily superior neighbour. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) Vietnam's full-scale invasion in December 1978, supported by the Soviet Union, swiftly toppled the Khmer Rouge. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$) Phnom Penh fell in January 1979, forcing Pol Pot and his followers to retreat to the Thai border. While external military power was decisive in the regime's overthrow, the decision to provoke Vietnam was driven by ideological hostility that Pol Pot personally championed. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)\n\nIn conclusion, Khmer Rouge ideology was both the foundation of Pol Pot's rise and the cause of his fall. In the rise, it provided a revolutionary vision that mobilised rural support, justified strategic alliances, and aligned the movement with powerful foreign backers. In the fall, its uncompromising application produced disastrous domestic policies, deepened internal divisions, and provoked an unwinnable war with Vietnam. While political instability, foreign intervention, and military factors all played a role, they were consistently interpreted and acted upon through the lens of Khmer Rouge ideology as enforced by Pol Pot. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$) Ultimately, the ideology that had secured his rise also ensured his downfall. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion}$)"},{"ref":"HI0660","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0660\n\nDescribe the content of NSC-68 and explain its influence on US foreign policy in Asia.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor describe, the following or similar:\n\n- A document created to report on US foreign policy which stated that Soviet expansionism was the cause of the continuation of the Cold War and reported that the USSR was aiming to dominate both Europe and Asia.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following for explanation:\n\n - Outlined possible courses of action against the perceived increase in threat from the USSR including: a return to isolationism, continued diplomatic negotiations with the USSR and a justification for the buildup of weapons to deter Soviet aggression and protect US interests.\n\n - Introduced the idea of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rolling back\" communism and a stronger response against communist expansion.\n\n - Increased US defense spending and influenced the expansion of long-term US military presence in Asia including bases in Japan, Korea and the Pacific\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nNSC-68 was the document created by the US National Security Council when asked to report on Cold War policy. The report looked at the role of the 2 superpowers and claimed that the USSR was aiming to extend its control to have domination over Europe and Asia ($\\checkmark$). This fear of the increasing spread of communism made US foreign policy stress the need to ensure that non-communist regimes were a more attractive alternative than communism and that US policy should use military measures to combat the threat of communism ($\\checkmark$). The policy marked a change from containing to rolling back communism as it meant the US was now prepared to push back communist groups trying to take over non-communist countries and, therefore, marked the beginning of a more aggressive US foreign policy in Asia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0662","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0662\n\nDescribe **three** ways the Korean War was significant in the context of the Cold War. \n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following: \n\n - The Korean War intensified the Cold War and showed that the US was willing to use force to contain communism.\n\n - It became the first $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hotspot\" of the Cold War: until this crisis, there had been no direct fighting between the superpowers.\n\n - It was the first time the Cold War had spread to Asia: up until this point, US policy had called for intervention in Japan and the Philippines but not Korea.\n\n - Managing the crisis and war became the first real test for the newly formed United Nations to resolve the situation effectively.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Cold War was a period of tension between nations that did not involve direct military action. The Korean War is significant because it marked a change in this status quo as Korea saw the first fighting in the Cold War. ($\\checkmark$) It was led by the UN and was the first real test for the UN in its role as a peacekeeping organisation. ($\\checkmark$) This crisis also showed how the Cold War had spread into Asia which was now seen as being a target for Soviet expansionism. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0665","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0665\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the actions of General MacArthur during the Korean Crisis?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Political cartoon titled $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$MacArthur, 1951. $`$Not a General's Job'” by cartoonist D.R. Fitzpatrick.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Cold%20War%20Conflicts%20in%20Asia/images/HI0665%28A%29.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0665(A)\">\n\n[Fitzpatrick, D.R. (1951). *MacArthur, 1951. Not a General's Job.*  Granger. Retrieved March 30, 2025 from  https://granger.com/. Copyright Granger. Used by permission.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The source shows that MacArthur has the power and influence to push the USA further into the conflict.\n\n - Pushing the US into further action in Korea could lead to a much more global conflict ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$World War II in Asia”). \n\n - The caption/title suggests that MacArthur's actions were inappropriate and that he should not have the power to take any further military action.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A suggests that General MacArthur, as an individual, could push America further into the conflict, shown by his ability to tip the hat, representing the US, over the cliff into $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$World War III in Asia\" ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also suggests that if he succeeds in pushing America further into the conflict, he will be responsible for starting $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$World War III in Asia\" as seen by the caption ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0666","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0666\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the values and limitations of Source C for an historian studying Truman's decision to involve the USA in Korea in 1951.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a radio address titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Report to the American People on Korea” by US President Harry Truman on April 11, 1951.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nIt is right for us to be in Korea now. It was right last June. It is right today.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nI want to remind you why this is true.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom all over the world. If they were to succeed, the United States would be numbered among their principal victims. It must be clear to everyone that the United States cannot—and will not—sit idly by and await foreign conquest. The only question is: What is the best time to meet the threat and how is the best way to meet it?\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe best time to meet the threat is in the beginning. It is easier to put out a fire in the beginning when it is small than after it has become a roaring blaze. And the best way to meet the threat of aggression is for the peace-loving nations to act together. If they don't act together, they are likely to be picked off, one by one. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nIf history has taught us anything, it is that aggression anywhere in the world is a threat to peace everywhere in the world. When that aggression is supported by the cruel and selfish rulers of a powerful nation who are bent on conquest, it becomes a dear and present danger to the security and independence of every free nation. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe aggression against Korea is the boldest and most dangerous move the Communists have yet made.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe attack on Korea was part of a greater plan for conquering all of Asia. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n]\n\n[Source: Truman\\, H. (1951, April 11). *Report to the American People on Korea.* US National Archives via the Miller Center. Retrieved May 11, 2025 from https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/april-11-1951-report-american-people-korea. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n\n - It is a speech by President Truman that gives us a direct insight into the political perspective of the time.\n\n - It aims to reassure the American public that the US will take action against aggressive countries and provides justification for US involvement in Korea.\n\n - The source shows the public the potential danger if the US does not act. There could be long-term consequences for their security, and it might lead to World War III.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following: \n\n - The source is a speech to the American people and shows only the US perspective of the threat.\n\n - The source was written during the crisis by the person who created the containment policy, which was being applied to Korea, so it seeks to support that policy.\n\n - The source uses emotive language that portrays the threat as dangerous to America's freedom.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of this source can be considered both a value and a limitation to an historian studying Truman's decision to involve the USA in Korea. The source is a speech by President Truman, so it's value lies in that it gives us a direct insight into the political perspective that existed in America at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The limitations of the origin are because it is addressed solely to the American people and shows only a US perspective of the threat ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). \n\nThe content of the source shows the public perception of the potential danger to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the security and independence of every free nation\" and that America needs to act to prevent a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$third World War\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the language of the source is very emotive. It portrays the situation as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$monstrous\" and that the blame lies with the communists, which indicates a clear bias toward the US perspective ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). \n\nThe purpose is to reassure the American public that the US will act quickly and protect the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$threat to peace\" that the crisis poses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The limitation of purpose is that it is stated by Truman, who, as the creator of the containment policy, is also looking to support and justify that policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0667","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0667\n\nBriefly compare and contrast what sources A and C reveal about US involvement in the Korean Crisis in 1951.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Political cartoon titled $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$MacArthur, 1951. $`$Not a General's Job'” by cartoonist D.R. Fitzpatrick.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Cold%20War%20Conflicts%20in%20Asia/images/HI0665%28A%29.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0665(A)\">\n\n[Source: Fitzpatrick, D.R. (1951). *MacArthur, 1951. $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$Not a General's Job.\"*  Granger. Retrieved March 30, 2025 from  https://granger.com/. Copyright Granger. Used by permission.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a radio address titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Report to the American People on Korea” by US President Harry Truman on April 11, 1951.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nIt is right for us to be in Korea now. It was right last June. It is right today.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nI want to remind you why this is true.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom all over the world. If they were to succeed, the United States would be numbered among their principal victims. It must be clear to everyone that the United States cannot—and will not—sit idly by and await foreign conquest. The only question is: What is the best time to meet the threat and how is the best way to meet it?\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe best time to meet the threat is in the beginning. It is easier to put out a fire in the beginning when it is small than after it has become a roaring blaze. And the best way to meet the threat of aggression is for the peace-loving nations to act together. If they don't act together, they are likely to be picked off, one by one. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nIf history has taught us anything, it is that aggression anywhere in the world is a threat to peace everywhere in the world. When that aggression is supported by the cruel and selfish rulers of a powerful nation who are bent on conquest, it becomes a dear and present danger to the security and independence of every free nation. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe aggression against Korea is the boldest and most dangerous move the Communists have yet made.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe attack on Korea was part of a greater plan for conquering all of Asia. [...]\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n]\n\n[Source: Truman\\, H. (1951, April 11). *Report to the American People on Korea.* US National Archives via the Miller Center. Retrieved May 11, 2025 from https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/april-11-1951-report-american-people-korea. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n- To achieve full marks, the answer must include both a point of comparison (similarity) and a point of contrast (difference). \n\n- A minimum of two clear, valid arguments along with supporting evidence should be given to achieve the full **four** marks.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities: \n\n- Both sources agree that the crisis could lead to a third World War.\n\n    - Source A: ‘World War III in Asia’ is written in the cartoon\n\n    - Source C: Truman states ‘‘We are trying to prevent a third world war…”\n\n- Both sources suggest that the USA is able to influence events in Asia\n\n    - Source A: MacArthur can be seen in the cartoon pushing the US over the edge towards war in Asia.\n\n    - Source C: Truman is clear that the US should be involved when he say,s ‘the best way to meet the threat of aggression is for the peace-loving nations to act together’\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences: \n\n- The sources disagree about the main aggressor in the conflict.\n\n    - Source A suggests that it is the US and MacArthur as they are seen to be pushing for war\n\n    - Source C suggests that it is the communists who are acting aggressively and a threat to peace.\n\n- The sources disagree about who is responsible for the US’s involvement in Korea:\n\n    - Source A suggests that it is MacArthur who is pushing the US further into the conflict\n\n    - Source C suggests that the US is becoming involved in the conflict with the President’s support to protect itself\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources A and C focus on the USA's role in the Korean Crisis. They both agree that the crisis could cause a ‘third World War’ ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{similarity}$). Source A shows MacArthur potentially pushing the US into “World War III in Asia,” while in Source C, Truman states that the US is “trying to prevent a third world war” ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{evidence}$).  \n\nHowever, the two sources differ in their perspectives regarding who holds the most responsibility for this ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{difference}$). Source A suggests that by giving MacArthur too much power, the US is becoming the main aggressor and pushing the US further into the conflict. In contrast, Source C argues that “the Communists in the Kremlin” are seeking to “stamp out freedom” and that this move in Korea “is the boldest and most dangerous” yet ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0822","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0822\n\nIdentify **two** key causes of the Vietnam War.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The division of Vietnam along the 17th parallel (Geneva Accords, 1954) created a temporary North and South, which led to long-term political tension.\n- The rise of communism in North Vietnam under Ho Chi Minh and the U.S. policy of containment led to ideological conflict.\n- The South Vietnamese government under Ngo Dinh Diem alienated large portions of the population through political repression, nepotism, and anti-Buddhist policies, fueling support for the Viet Cong.\n- U.S. support for Ngo Dinh Diem's anti-communist regime in South Vietnam, despite its unpopularity. The U.S. gradually escalated its involvement by sending advisors, weapons, and aid from 1955 onwards, leading to direct military engagement.\n- Failure to hold national elections in 1956, as agreed in Geneva, prevented peaceful reunification. Elections were blocked by Diem and the U.S., fearing a likely communist victory, which escalated tensions.\n- Colonial legacy and nationalism: Vietnamese resistance to French colonial rule evolved into a broader nationalist struggle for independence and unification, led by Ho Chi Minh. The First Indochina War (1946–1954) laid the groundwork for further conflict, as it failed to resolve the division or satisfy Vietnamese national aspirations.\n- Ideological conflict- Capitalism vs Communism: The Cold War rivalry between the U.S. and USSR made Vietnam a proxy battleground between capitalist and communist ideologies.\n- Influence of the Domino Theory: U.S. policymakers believed that the fall of South Vietnam to communism would lead to the spread of communism throughout Southeast Asia.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne key cause of the Vietnam War was the division of Vietnam at the 17th parallel following the Geneva Accords in 1954, which created political tension between the North and South ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA second cause was the rise of communism under Ho Chi Minh in the North, which clashed with the United States' policy of containment during the Cold War ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0828","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0828\n\nAccording to Source A, what justification does the United States give for military action in Southeast Asia?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from “H.J.Res. 1145, Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia.” 88th Congress of the United States, August 7th, 1964. Also known as the “Gulf of Tonkin Resolution.” |\n\n[Source: United States Congress. (1964, August 7). H.J.Res. 1145, *Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia*. 88th Congress, 2nd session. Public Law 88-408. U.S. Government Publishing Office. Retrieved June 11, 2025 from https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-78/pdf/STATUTE-78-Pg384.pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Defense against attack: U.S. naval vessels were allegedly “deliberately and repeatedly attacked” by North Vietnamese forces while “lawfully present in international waters.”\n- Violation of international law: The source claims these attacks violated the Charter of the United Nations and international law, framing the response as a defense of international norms.\n- Threat to international peace: The attacks are described as a “serious threat to international peace,” suggesting U.S. action is required to maintain global stability.\n- Collective defense: The resolution emphasizes that the U.S. is assisting “nations joined with them in the collective defense of their freedom,” linking the action to alliances like SEATO.\n- Support for freedom: It states that the U.S. desires that the “peoples [of Southeast Asia] should be left in peace to work out their own destinies,” portraying U.S. involvement as defending self-determination.\n- No territorial ambitions: The U.S. claims to have “no territorial, military or political ambitions” in the region, presenting its actions as altruistic.\n- Presidential authority: The resolution supports the President's power to “take all necessary measures” to repel attacks and prevent further aggression, reinforcing executive responsibility to act swiftly in defense.\n- Vital national interest: The U.S. declares the region to be of “vital national interest and to world peace,” justifying intervention on strategic and global stability grounds.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the United States claims that its naval vessels were “deliberately and repeatedly attacked” by North Vietnamese forces while “lawfully present in international waters.” This suggests that military action was justified as a direct act of self-defense ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the resolution argues that these attacks violated “the principles of the Charter of the United Nations and of international law.” This positions U.S. involvement as a legitimate response aimed at upholding international legal norms ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the source describes the attacks as “a serious threat to international peace,” implying that U.S. intervention was necessary to maintain global stability and prevent further aggression in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0830","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0830\n\nAccording to Source C, what was the ultimate goal of U.S. policy in Afghanistan?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan” given by US President Ronald Reagan, 1985, and declassified in 2008.|\n\n[Source: Reagan, R. (1985, March 27). *National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan.* Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. Retrieved June 14, 2025 from https://www.reaganlibrary.gov/public/archives/reference/scanned-nsdds/nsdd166.pdf Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Removal of Soviet forces: The ultimate goal was “the removal of Soviet force from Afghanistan.”\n- Restoration of Afghan independence: The U.S. aimed to restore Afghanistan's “independent status,” free from Soviet control or influence.\n- Undermine the Soviet long-term strategy: The U.S. aimed to demonstrate that the Soviet Union's long-term strategy of subjugating Afghanistan would ultimately fail.\n- Create pressure for Soviet policy change: By worsening the situation for Soviet forces, the U.S. aimed to “build pressure on the Soviet Union to adjust its policies” in a manner favourable to U.S. interests and the Afghan people.\n- Prevent Soviet consolidation of power in the region: The U.S. aimed to stop the Soviets from using Afghanistan as a “secure base” to project power into the surrounding region, especially Iran and Pakistan.\n- Support an Indigenous resistance movement: Preventing the defeat of Afghan resistance fighters was key to U.S. goals to avoid signalling that Soviet military intervention could succeed.\n- Promote Soviet political isolation: A goal was to “promote Soviet isolation in the Third and Islamic worlds” by ensuring continued condemnation of Soviet actions in Afghanistan.\n- Deny strategic regional advantages to the USSR: The U.S. sought to prevent the Soviet Union from using Afghanistan as a launching point for influence over post-Khomeini Iran or as a means of destabilising Pakistan.\n- Demonstrate U.S. resolve in Cold War conflicts: Implicitly, this goal also served to show American commitment to containing Soviet expansion and supporting anti-communist movements globally.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, according to the source, the ultimate goal of U.S. policy in Afghanistan was “the removal of Soviet force from Afghanistan” ($\\checkmark$). Second was the restoration of the country's “independent status”($\\checkmark$). Finally, the United States sought to undermine the Soviet Union's broader ambitions by demonstrating that its long-term strategy for conquering Afghanistan would not succeed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0878","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0878\n\nIdentify **three** aims of the Malayan Communist Party (MCP) during the Malayan Emergency.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Establish a communist state in Malaya: The MCP aimed to overthrow the existing colonial government and replace it with a Marxist-Leninist regime modeled on the Chinese Communist Party.\n- Remove British colonial rule: The MCP sought to end British imperial control over Malaya and achieve national independence through revolutionary means rather than negotiated reform.\n- Gain control over trade unions and mobilize workers: By infiltrating and dominating trade unions, the MCP hoped to organize strikes and build a mass base of support among urban workers, crucial to their revolutionary strategy.\n- Undermine capitalist and imperialist influences: The MCP opposed foreign-owned businesses and Western economic dominance, aiming to dismantle the capitalist system and redistribute resources among the local population.\n- Inspire broader communist movements in Southeast Asia: The MCP's struggle was seen as part of a larger regional communist uprising; they hoped success in Malaya would encourage similar revolutions in neighbouring countries like Indonesia and Thailand.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the MCP aimed to establish a communist state in Malaya by toppling the British-backed colonial government and replacing it with a Marxist-Leninist regime inspired by the Chinese Communist Party ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the MCP sought to eliminate British colonial rule altogether, pursuing national independence through armed revolution rather than political negotiation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the party aimed to gain control over trade unions and mobilize the working class by infiltrating labor movements, organizing strikes, and building widespread support among urban workers to strengthen their revolutionary campaign ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0880","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0880\n\nIdentify **two** key failures of Prince Sihanouk's rule before the rise of the Khmer Rouge.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Economic mismanagement: Sihanouk's regime suffered from growing corruption and inefficiency, especially in state-run enterprises. Misuse of resources and lack of economic reform led to stagnation, worsening inequality and weakening popular support, particularly among rural populations.\n- Neutrality policy issues: Sihanouk's attempt to remain neutral during the Vietnam War backfired. His tolerance of North Vietnamese activity in Cambodia angered the U.S., while communists at home viewed him as compromising, increasing domestic and external tensions.\n- Political repression: Sihanouk tightly controlled political life, banning opposition parties and suppressing dissent. This fostered resentment, especially among students and leftist groups, some of whom later joined or supported the Khmer Rouge movement.\n- Over-reliance on personal rule/authoritarianism: Sihanouk concentrated power on himself, sidelining institutions and creating a patronage-based system. His rule became increasingly out of touch with everyday Cambodians, eroding legitimacy and leaving the country vulnerable to radical alternatives.\n- Failure to anticipate rural discontent: Sihanouk underestimated the depth of rural grievances, particularly over land inequality and government neglect. His regime’s failure to address these issues allowed the Khmer Rouge to gain support by promising justice and reform.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, under Sihanouk, widespread corruption and poorly run state enterprises damaged the economy ($\\checkmark$). The lack of reform and uneven wealth distribution led to economic stagnation and growing dissatisfaction, especially among Cambodia's rural majority ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Sihanouk limited political freedoms by outlawing opposition groups and cracking down on dissent ($\\checkmark$). This alienated many, particularly urban students and leftists, some of whom later supported or joined the Khmer Rouge ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0881","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0881\n\nExplain how **three** Khmer Rouge ideologies shaped its policies from 1975 to 1979.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** points with supporting explanation:\n\nAgrarian Utopianism:\n- The Khmer Rouge believed that Cambodia should return to a mythical, pre-industrial golden age where all citizens were peasants working collectively on the land.\n- This vision of a classless, agrarian society led to the forced evacuation of urban populations to the countryside shortly after they seized Phnom Penh in April 1975.\n- Cities were considered corrupt and exploitative; people living in them were viewed as tainted by capitalism.\n- The regime abolished private property, money, and markets in its effort to eliminate inequality and individualism.\n\nMaoist Communism:\n- Inspired by China's Cultural Revolution and Mao's Great Leap Forward, the Khmer Rouge emphasized self-reliance (autarky) and rural collectivism.\n- The population was forced into agricultural communes, where food production was centrally planned and strictly monitored.\n- Foreign aid and influence were rejected as imperialist and corrupting; Cambodia withdrew from international organizations and expelled most foreign diplomats.\n- The push for rapid agricultural transformation led to overwork, starvation, and mass deaths, especially during failed attempts to meet unrealistic rice production quotas.\n\nAnti-Intellectualism:\n- The Khmer Rouge saw educated people as a threat to their revolutionary goals, associating knowledge with elitism, Western values, and counterrevolution.\n- Teachers, doctors, engineers, and anyone with a university education were singled out for execution or harsh re-education.\n- Even speaking a foreign language, wearing glasses, or appearing “bourgeois” could lead to arrest or death.\n- This ideological hatred of intellectuals devastated Cambodia's professional class and crippled future development.\n\nExtreme Nationalism and Xenophobia\n- The Khmer Rouge promoted a radical form of Cambodian nationalism, claiming to protect the country from foreign contamination, especially from Vietnam.\n- Ethnic minorities such as the Cham Muslims, ethnic Vietnamese, and ethnic Chinese were systematically targeted—many were massacred or forcibly assimilated.\n- The regime sought to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$purify\" Cambodia by eliminating non-Khmer cultural and religious influences, including Islam and Buddhism.\n- Tensions with Vietnam, driven by both historical enmity and ideological differences, eventually led to border skirmishes and the Vietnamese invasion in 1979.\n\nYear Zero Ideology\n- The Khmer Rouge envisioned 1975 as “Year Zero,” the start of a completely new society unburdened by past traditions, values, or institutions.\n- This led to the destruction of libraries, schools, religious buildings, and historical records, in an attempt to erase Cambodia's cultural memory.\n- Religion was outlawed, and Buddhist monks were defrocked, forced into labor, or killed.\n- Families were broken apart, with children placed in indoctrination centers and taught to report on their parents.\n\nTotalitarian Control and Revolutionary Vigilance\n- The Khmer Rouge promoted the idea that class enemies could be found anywhere, even among loyal citizens.\n- People were encouraged to spy on neighbours, and dissent—even accidental—was met with immediate arrest or execution.\n- The infamous Tuol Sleng (S-21) prison symbolised this paranoia; thousands were tortured and forced to confess to imaginary crimes before being executed.\n\nMillenarianism and Revolutionary Purity\n- The regime believed that violence was necessary to purify society and bring about revolutionary transformation.\n- Anyone resisting the regime or failing to meet its expectations was labelled a traitor or enemy of the people, justifying mass killings.\n- The scale of violence was partly driven by a belief that the old world had to be completely destroyed before a perfect society could be built.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, their belief in agrarian utopianism led to the forced evacuation of urban populations. Cities were viewed as corrupt, and the regime aimed to transform all Cambodians into rural peasants working collectively on the land. This vision prompted the mass relocation of people from Phnom Penh and other cities to the countryside, where they were made to work in agricultural communes ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecond, the Khmer Rouge drew heavily on Maoist communist principles, particularly the ideas of self-reliance and collectivised agriculture. Inspired by Mao's Great Leap Forward, the regime rejected foreign influence and imposed centrally planned food production. People were forced into communes with strict controls over their labour and output, leading to widespread starvation and suffering due to unrealistic production targets and poor planning ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the regime's Year Zero ideology aimed to wipe out all remnants of Cambodia's past in order to build a new revolutionary society. The Khmer Rouge attempted to erase the country's cultural identity by destroying libraries, religious sites, schools, and historical records. This campaign extended to banning religion, dismantling traditional family structures, and re-educating the population according to revolutionary ideals. Together, these ideological beliefs justified the regime's radical and often violent transformation of Cambodian society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0882","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0882\n\nIdentify **three** causes of the Korean War.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Korea was divided along the 38th parallel in 1945, with the USSR occupying the North and the U.S. the South, creating two rival governments with conflicting ideologies.\n- Plans for nationwide elections failed in 1948, leading to the establishment of two separate states, communist North Korea and capitalist South Korea, each claiming legitimacy over the entire peninsula.\n- The Korean peninsula became a Cold War flashpoint, as the U.S. and USSR promoted opposing systems, deepening hostility between the two Koreas and fuelling global ideological confrontation.\n- Kim Il-Sung sought to reunify Korea through military force, believing the South Korean government was weak and that unification under communism was both achievable and inevitable.\n- With backing from Stalin and later tacit support from Mao, North Korea gained confidence to attack, receiving weapons, training, and strategic approval to launch the invasion in 1950.\n- The United States was determined to stop the spread of communism in Asia, fearing that a communist victory in Korea would lead neighbouring countries to fall, like dominoes, to communism\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of the leading causes of the Korean War was the division of Korea along the 38th parallel at the end of World War II. This split the country into two zones, with the Soviets occupying the North and the Americans the South, setting the stage for growing political differences ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother important factor was the failure to hold nationwide elections in 1948. Instead, two separate governments were established, North Korea became a communist state under Kim Il-Sung, while South Korea was led by Syngman Rhee and followed a capitalist model. Both sides claimed to be the rightful government of all Korea ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the broader Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union added to the tension. With each superpower backing its respective Korean ally, the peninsula became a hotspot in the global struggle between communism and capitalism ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0883","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0883\n\nHow does Pol Pot present the role of the Communist Party of Kampuchea in shaping society in Source A?\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a speech by Pol Pot, leader of Cambodia at the time, titled “Long Live the 17th Anniversary of the Communist Party of Kampuchea,” September 29, 1977. |\n\n[Source: Pot. P. (1977, September 29) *Long Live the 17th Anniversary of the Communist Party of Kampuchea.* Marxists.org. Retrieved June 19, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/pol-pot/1977/cpk-17th-aniv.pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Party is presented as the supreme guiding force: Pol Pot frames the Party as the central authority in both governance and moral development. Its leadership is portrayed as essential to managing contradictions in society and guiding the revolutionary path; “under the leadership of the Party.”\n- The Party leads the educational and revolutionary transformation process: Education is not portrayed as neutral or optional, but as a tool for ideological transformation. The Party directs efforts to reshape individuals' thinking and behaviour to align with revolutionary goals. “It is important to consistently carry out thorough-going educational work.”\n- The Party supervises criticism and self-criticism, implying control over personal beliefs and behaviour. Individuals are expected to monitor and correct themselves with the Party's help. This system of “criticism and self-criticism” is a form of surveillance and ideological discipline.\n- The Party demands total ideological and social obedience to its goals: Pol Pot stresses the need for the people to adopt a revolutionary lifestyle, eliminating personal desires in favour of collective progress as defined by the Party; “gradually eliminating the idea of private ownership”, “each of us becomes a revolutionary of the new Kampuchea”.\n- The Party assumes responsibility for personal transformation. Pol Pot suggests that the Party's role is not only to shape society but also to reshape the very character of its people, turning them into ideal socialist citizens; “to continue to build the revolutionary strength of the people.”\n- The Party integrates itself into every aspect of national life (education, lifestyle, and national reconstruction): There is no distinction between state, society, and individual development; the Party permeates all layers of daily existence, from labour to thought.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, Pol Pot depicts the Party as the supreme guiding authority, responsible not only for political leadership but also for the moral direction of the people. He emphasises that societal contradictions can only be resolved “under the leadership of the Party,” indicating that no progress or unity is possible without its control ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Pol Pot highlights the Party's role in leading the ideological transformation of individuals. He does not view education as neutral, but rather as a means to reshape citizens' beliefs and behaviours in line with revolutionary values. This is reflected in his insistence that “it is important to consistently carry out thorough-going educational work,” making it clear that the Party oversees both learning and re-education as tools of social control ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, Pol Pot calls for complete ideological and social conformity to the Party's goals. He outlines the need to “gradually eliminate the idea of private ownership,” showing how collective ideals must replace individual identity and desires. He describes the ideal citizen as someone who “becomes a revolutionary of the new Kampuchea,” suggesting that personal worth is measured entirely by loyalty to the Party's vision ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0884","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0884\n\nExplain **two** ways Source A could help a historian understand the ideology behind the Khmer Rouge's policies.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a speech by Pol Pot, leader of Cambodia at the time, titled “Long Live the 17th Anniversary of the Communist Party of Kampuchea,” September 29, 1977. |\n\n[Source: Pot. P. (1977, September 29) *Long Live the 17th Anniversary of the Communist Party of Kampuchea.* Marxists.org. Retrieved June 19, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/pol-pot/1977/cpk-17th-aniv.pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** points with supporting explanation:\n- Maoist influence and ongoing revolution: The source reflects Maoist ideology, particularly the concept of permanent revolution. Pol Pot describes “contradictions among the people” caused by lingering class traits, which must be corrected through “criticism and self-criticism.” This illustrates the regime's emphasis on ongoing re-education to maintain ideological purity and revolutionary discipline.\n- Justification for eliminating class enemies: Pol Pot's focus on eradicating “old class character” explains why the Khmer Rouge targeted intellectuals, minorities, and religious groups. The regime saw such individuals as incapable of transformation and threats to revolutionary unity. The speech implies social cleansing was necessary to create a truly classless, obedient society.\n- Ideological purity over individual identity: The source emphasises total submission to collective ideology. Pol Pot urges citizens to “become a revolutionary of the new Kampuchea,” abandoning personal ambition for the revolution. The promotion of “collectivist and socialist ownership” reveals the regime's demand that individual identity be replaced with loyalty to the Party's vision.\n- Radical restructuring of society: Pol Pot's call to “eliminate the idea of private ownership” helps explain radical Khmer Rouge policies like abolishing money, destroying religious institutions, and relocating city dwellers. The goal was to erase all remnants of the old society and build a self-sufficient, ideologically pure agrarian utopia under Party control.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, Pol Pot's emphasis on ongoing ideological transformation reflects the Maoist belief in continuous revolution and mass re-education ($\\checkmark$). The source speaks of “contradictions among the people” due to lingering “vestiges of our old class character,” which he argues must be corrected through “education, study, criticism and self-criticism.” This reflects Maoist methods of ideological purification and social engineering, where people are constantly pushed to re-align their thoughts and behaviours to fit the revolutionary ideal ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the speech demonstrates a worldview in which those representing pre-revolutionary social status or thinking are seen as threats ($\\checkmark$). Pol Pot suggests that all Cambodians must undergo ideological transformation, but those who cannot or will not, such as intellectuals, religious leaders, and ethnic minorities, were often labelled enemies of the revolution. The mention of “deep-rooted” class characteristics and the need to “eliminate the idea of private ownership” supports the view that the Khmer Rouge sought to remove all elements associated with class hierarchy and capitalist values, often through violent means ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0885","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0885\n\nIn Source B, how does Truman justify US involvement in Korea?\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a radio address given by US President Harry Truman titled “Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East,” broadcast from the White House on April 11, 1951. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, April 11). *Radio Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East.* Truman Library. Retrieved June 20, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/78/radio-report-american-people-korea-and-us-policy-far-east. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Preventing a third World War: Truman argues that the U.S. is acting in Korea to avoid a larger global conflict: “In the simplest terms, what we are doing in Korea is this: We are trying to prevent a third world war.” Suggesting early intervention stops wider wars before they escalate.\n- Containment of communism: Truman frames Communism as a global threat that must be stopped: “The Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom all over the world.” “They want to control all Asia from the Kremlin.” Describes Korea as one front in a global ideological struggle.\n- Lessons from History: Truman uses historical analogy to justify early action: “It is easier to put out a fire in the beginning... than after it has become a roaring blaze.” Compares current inaction to the failures of the 1930s: “If they had acted in the beginning... there probably would have been no World War II.”\n- Collective security & UN cooperation: Justifies U.S. action as part of a broader international responsibility: “The best way to meet the threat of aggression is for the peace-loving nations to act together.” “This is the basic reason why we joined in creating the United Nations.” Implies that failing to act would undermine the UN and global security.\n- Precedent from other Cold War conflicts: Cites successful past interventions to show Korea fits a pattern: “That is what we did in Greece... The United Nations supported Greek resistance.” “Another big Communist threat... was the Berlin blockade... it was settled because free men would not back down.”\n- Direct Communist threat to Asia: Argues Korea is part of a larger Communist plan for conquest. Cites secret Communist speech: “Java, Indochina, Malaya, India, Tibet, Thailand, Philippines, and Japan are our ultimate targets... The United States is the only obstacle.” Claims the goal is “the long-awaited world revolution.”\n- Defence of freedom and democracy: Positions the U.S. as protector of free nations: “Aggression anywhere... is a threat to the peace everywhere in the world.” Suggests America has a moral duty to resist tyranny and oppression.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, Truman justifies U.S. involvement in Korea by presenting it as a necessary step to prevent another global conflict. He argues that intervention is meant to stop the outbreak of a larger war, stating, “In the simplest terms, . . . We are trying to prevent a third world war\"($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, he portrays communism as a global threat that must be contained. Truman claims the Soviet Union is leading a coordinated effort to destroy freedom worldwide, saying “The Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy.” Thus, Korea is just one part of a wider plan to “control all Asia from the Kremlin”($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Truman draws historical parallels. He refers to the failures of the 1930s to justify early intervention, arguing that if democratic nations had confronted dictators sooner, “there probably would have been no World War II.” By invoking this example, he presents U.S. action in Korea as both urgent and informed by the mistakes of the past ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0886","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0886\n\nEvaluate the accuracy of Truman's portrayal of communist expansionism in Source B.\n[7 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a radio address given by US President Harry Truman titled “Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East,” broadcast from the White House on April 11, 1951. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, April 11). *Radio Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East.* Truman Library. Retrieved June 20, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/78/radio-report-american-people-korea-and-us-policy-far-east. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both accuracies and overstatements or inaccuracies must be addressed in the response.\n\nOverstatements and Inaccuracies in Truman's Portrayal\n- Truman claims a unified global communist strategy directed by Moscow\n    - Quote: “The Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom all over the world.”\n    - Historical accuracy:\n        - Today, we know that Soviet and Chinese interests were not fully aligned and that Mao and Stalin had strategic disagreements and mutual distrust.\n        - Stalin was initially hesitant to support North Korea’s invasion, only giving approval after the U.S. withdrew troops from South Korea and signalled little interest in defending it (Dec 1949–Jan 1950).\n        - This shows that the communist bloc was not a single, unified command structure, as Truman suggested.\n- Truman claims the Korean War was part of a plan to conquer all of Asia\n    - Quote: “The attack on Korea was part of a greater plan for conquering all of Asia.”\n    - Historical accuracy:\n        - There is no definitive evidence of a master plan for all of Asia being orchestrated by Moscow or Beijing.\n        - The Korean War was primarily initiated by Kim Il-Sung, who persistently lobbied Stalin and Mao for support to invade the South. While both leaders eventually gave their support, it was reluctant and calculated, not part of a pre-agreed global strategy.\n- Truman quotes a ‘secret intelligence report’ to suggest a broader communist conspiracy\n    - Quote: “Java, Indochina, Malaya, India, Tibet, Thailand, Philippines, and Japan are our ultimate targets ... We must unify the people of Asia and crush the United States.”\n    - Historical accuracy:\n        - This rhetoric reflects Cold War fear and propaganda more than documented strategic planning. While communist movements existed in some of these regions, they were often nationalist in nature and not always controlled by Moscow or Beijing. Modern scholarship shows these movements had local motivations rather than being directed parts of a unified global offensive.\n\nElements of Accuracy in Truman's Portrayal\n- Stalin did approve North Korea's invasion\n    - Though not the mastermind Truman claimed, Stalin did ultimately give Kim Il-Sung the go-ahead:\n        - This fits with Truman's claim that the Soviet Union backed the aggression. Furthermore, Stalin provided military equipment, advisors, and planning assistance.\n        - His involvement remained behind the scenes to avoid provoking U.S. retaliation directly against the USSR.\n- China entered the war decisively\n    - After the U.S. crossed the 38th parallel, Mao sent over 300,000 troops to support North Korea (late 1950).\n        - This demonstrates that the communist bloc did act together to defend North Korea, even if their reasons differed. However, Mao’s intervention was partly to protect China’s borders and demonstrate strength after the revolution, not just to serve Soviet interests.\n- Communist ideology did promote global revolution\n    - Marxist-Leninist ideology did advocate the spread of revolution.\n        - The Soviet Cominform (Communist Information Bureau) aimed to coordinate global communist parties. However, in practice, Soviet and Chinese actions were often constrained by national interests.\n\nConclusion\n- Truman's portrayal contains some truth, communist powers did support North Korea and were ideologically hostile to the U.S.\n- However, his depiction of a monolithic, centrally coordinated plan for Asian conquest was exaggerated.\n- Modern historical research reveals a more complex picture with local leaders (like Kim) having agency. Furthermore, Stalin and Mao acting cautiously and were not always aligned.\n- Truman's framing served a political purpose: to rally public support and present the war as a clear-cut moral struggle, but it oversimplified the geopolitics behind the Korean War.\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTruman's portrayal of communist expansionism in Source B is only partially accurate. He describes a coordinated, global communist strategy led from Moscow, claiming, “The Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom all over the world.” However, this view oversimplifies the situation. Soviet and Chinese interests in Korea were not fully aligned ($\\checkmark$). Stalin and Mao had serious strategic disagreements and mutual distrust ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThat said, elements of Truman's interpretation do reflect reality. Stalin did ultimately approve the invasion and supported North Korea with weapons, advisors, and military planning ($\\checkmark$). While he stayed behind the scenes to avoid direct confrontation with the U.S., his involvement fits Truman's claim that the USSR backed North Korean aggression ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFurthermore, communist ideology did promote global revolution. The Soviet Cominform aimed to coordinate communist movements internationally, and Marxist-Leninist theory supported the spread of revolution ($\\checkmark$). However, in practice, Soviet and Chinese decisions were often shaped by national interests rather than a unified master plan ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, Truman was right to see communism as a threat, but his portrayal of a centrally coordinated campaign to dominate Asia was exaggerated. Historical research reveals a more complex reality involving cautious leadership, ideological rivalry, and local initiative, particularly from Kim Il-Sung ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0887","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0887\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the motivations of the USA and the USSR in Korea.\n[6 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from a radio address given by US President Harry Truman titled “Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East,” broadcast from the White House on April 11, 1951. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, April 11). *Radio Report to the American People on Korea and on U.S. Policy in the Far East.* Truman Library. Retrieved June 20, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/78/radio-report-american-people-korea-and-us-policy-far-east. Public Domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from an interview of Joseph Stalin titled “When is War Not Inevitable?” published in *Pravda*, February 16, 1951. |\n\n[Source: Stalin, J. (1951, February 16). *When is War Not Inevitable?* Pravda. Marxist.org. Retrieved June 21 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1951/02/16.htm. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both leaders claim to support peace, presenting their nation as fundamentally defensive, claiming to be resisting aggression rather than initiating conflict.\n    - Stalin: “As for the Soviet Union, it will continue... to pursue the policy of averting war and maintaining peace.”\n    - Truman: “We are trying to prevent a third world war.”\n- Both accuse the other side of deception, claiming moral high ground and framing the other as dishonest and manipulative.\n    - Stalin: “They are trying to use... lies, to deceive [their peoples], and to depict the new war as defensive.”\n    - Truman: Refers to Communist rhetoric as manipulative: “$\\lq$Liberation' in $\\lq$commie' language means conquest.”\n- Both portray the Korean conflict as part of a larger global struggle, situating the Korean War within a broader ideological and geographical context.\n    - Stalin: Speaks of a global conflict between “peace-loving” peoples and “aggressive” capitalist elites.\n    - Truman: Describes Korea as “part of a greater plan for conquering all of Asia.”\n- Both leaders use ideological language to frame motivations to demonize the other system and justify their own actions.\n    - Truman: Uses anti-communist rhetoric: “monstrous conspiracy,” “cruel and selfish rulers,” and “world revolution.”\n    - Stalin: Uses anti-capitalist rhetoric: “criminal machinations of the warmongers,” “reactionary governments,” and “super-profits.”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Truman blames the Soviet Union for expansionism; Stalin blames capitalist elites\n    - Truman: “The Communists in the Kremlin are engaged in a monstrous conspiracy to stamp out freedom.”\n    - Stalin: “They [capitalist elites] need war to obtain super-profits... These are the billionaires and millionaires...”\n- Truman justifies U.S. military intervention; Stalin condemns it\n    - Truman: Frames U.S. action in Korea as necessary and moral: “Aggression anywhere... is a threat to the peace everywhere.”\n    - Stalin: Depicts U.S. actions as part of an imperialist conspiracy: “They regard war as an item of income which gives colossal profits.”\n- Stalin claims Soviet policy is peace-based; Truman sees USSR as aggressor\n    - Stalin: “The Soviet Union... firmly to pursue the policy of averting war and maintaining peace.”\n    - Truman: “They want to control all Asia from the Kremlin.”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Truman and Stalin claim that their nations are acting in the interest of peace. Truman states that U.S. involvement in Korea aims to “prevent a third world war,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). while Stalin asserts that the Soviet Union seeks to “maintain peace” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Each presents their actions as defensive, arguing that they are responding to external aggression rather than initiating conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nBoth leaders also accuse the opposing side of manipulation and dishonesty. Stalin claims that Western governments use “lies to deceive their peoples” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). and falsely portray their war efforts as defensive. Similarly, Truman dismisses communist rhetoric as deliberately misleading, stating that in “commie” language, “liberation means conquest” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Each side positions itself as morally just while portraying the other as deceptive and dangerous ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, their portrayals of the causes of the Korean War sharply contrast. Truman blames the Soviet Union, describing a “monstrous conspiracy” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) to destroy global freedom. In contrast, Stalin places the blame on capitalist elites in the West who, he claims, seek war for “super-profits.” Where Truman sees communist ideology as the central threat, Stalin sees imperialist greed as the true cause of conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$).\n\nFinally, their attitudes toward U.S. intervention diverge sharply. Truman justifies military action as a moral duty, warning that “aggression anywhere” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) threatens global peace. Stalin condemns this involvement, framing it as part of a capitalist agenda that treats war as a profitable venture. While Truman casts the U.S. as a global protector, Stalin portrays it as the primary warmonger ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0906","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0906\n\nDescribe the role of Ho Chi Minh in the struggle for Vietnamese independence between 1946 and 1954.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nPolitical Leadership and Vision\n- Founder and leader of the Viet Minh (1941): Created the League for the Independence of Vietnam to fight Japanese occupation and later French colonialism.\n- Symbol of national unity and anti-colonial resistance: Embodied the struggle for independence and appealed to Vietnamese nationalism. Seen by many as the legitimate leader of a unified Vietnam.\n- Clear ideological framework: Combined Vietnamese nationalism with Marxist-Leninist ideology. Positioned the struggle as both anti-imperialist and revolutionary.\n\nDiplomatic Efforts\n- Declaration of Independence (1945): Proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in Hanoi after Japan's surrender. Quoted the U.S. Declaration of Independence and the French Rights of Man to appeal for international support.\n- Attempted negotiations with France (1946): Initially sought a peaceful resolution with French leaders at the Fontainebleau Conference. Efforts failed, leading to the outbreak of war in December 1946.\n- Maintained international relations with key allies: Gained recognition and support from the Soviet Union and China, strengthening the Viet Minh's position.\n\nMilitary and Strategic Direction\n- Oversaw the Viet Minh's military resistance against the French: Provided leadership and direction for the armed struggle during the First Indochina War. Promoted a strategy of protracted guerrilla warfare to wear down French forces.\n- Encouraged political and military mobilisation: Inspired mass participation from peasants and workers.\n- Supported the spread of revolutionary education and land reform in Viet Minh-controlled areas.\n- Backed key military leaders like General Vo Nguyen Giap: Allowed capable subordinates to lead military operations, such as the planning of Dien Bien Phu.\n\nPropaganda and Ideological Influence\n- Effective use of propaganda: Portrayed the Viet Minh as a liberation force fighting for the people. Depicted the French as oppressive imperialists and rallied support for the resistance.\n- Promoted the idea of an independent, unified Vietnam: Framed the conflict as a continuation of Vietnam's long history of resisting foreign domination (e.g., Chinese, French, Japanese).\n\nContribution to Victory and Legacy\n- Key figure behind the eventual Viet Minh victory at Dien Bien Phu (1954): Though not a military commander, his leadership enabled the long-term strategy that led to victory. The defeat forced the French to negotiate and led to the Geneva Accords.\n- Influenced the Geneva negotiations: Ho's government gained partial international recognition. Although Vietnam was temporarily divided, the North was under Viet Minh control, which laid the foundation for further conflict and eventual reunification.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHo Chi Minh played a vital political role as the founder of the Viet Minh in 1941 and later as the symbolic leader of Vietnamese resistance during the First Indochina War (1946-1954). He represented national unity and anti-colonialism, gaining widespread support as the legitimate leader of a future independent Vietnam ($\\checkmark$),\n\nSecondly, although not a military commander, Ho Chi Minh guided the overall direction of the Viet Minh's armed struggle. He advocated a strategy of prolonged guerrilla warfare to weaken French forces and supported capable military leaders, especially General Vo Nguyen Giap, who led key campaigns against the French ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Ho's long-term leadership contributed directly to the Viet Minh's success at Dien Bien Phu in 1954, a turning point in the war. This defeat forced the French to the negotiating table, resulting in the Geneva Accords, which marked a major step toward Vietnamese independence and secured the North under Viet Minh control ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0909","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0909\n\nOutline the internal political developments in Afghanistan between 1978 and 1979 that prompted Soviet intervention.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Saur Revolution (April 1978): The communist People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan (PDPA) overthrew President Mohammed Daoud Khan. Daoud and his family were executed, ending the Republic of Afghanistan and replacing it with a Marxist regime.\n- Emergence of PDPA rule under Nur Muhammad Taraki: Taraki became President and implemented radical socialist reforms (e.g. land redistribution, women's rights, secularisation). These reforms clashed with traditional tribal, Islamic, and rural values, causing widespread resentment.\n- Factionalism within the PDPA: PDPA was split into two rival factions: Khalq (dominant, led by Taraki and later Amin) and Parcham (led by Babrak Karmal). Internal purges and repression targeted Parcham members and other opponents.\n- Brutal repression of dissent: Mass arrests, torture, and executions by the secret police (AGSA). Tens of thousands of political prisoners were taken; many were religious leaders and tribal elders.\n- Growing armed resistance and instability: Anti-communist insurgencies (mujahideen) began to emerge across rural areas. Civil unrest spread rapidly, threatening the PDPA's control of the countryside.\n- Taraki-Amin power struggle (1979): Deputy Prime Minister Hafizullah Amin gained increasing control and eventually overthrew and killed Taraki in October 1979. Amin's leadership was viewed as erratic, brutal, and unpredictable, even by the Soviets.\n- Soviet distrust of Amin: The USSR feared Amin might turn to the West or lose control of the country to Islamist forces. Reports suggested Amin had secret contacts with the U.S., deepening Soviet paranoia.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Saur Revolution in April 1978 brought the communist People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan (PDPA) to power. The new regime, led by Nur Muhammad Taraki, introduced radical reforms such as land redistribution and the promotion of women's rights, which sparked widespread resistance from conservative and rural communities ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, growing internal divisions within the PDPA, especially between the Khalq and Parcham factions, led to political instability and purges, weakening the government's legitimacy and control ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the rise of Hafizullah Amin following his overthrow and killing of Taraki in 1979 alarmed the Soviet leadership. They viewed Amin as unpredictable and feared he might seek support from the West or fail to contain the rising Islamist insurgency. These developments convinced the USSR that intervention was necessary to preserve a friendly regime on its southern border ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0910","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0910\n\nOutline the short- and long-term consequences of the Soviet invasion for Afghanistan's government.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both the short- and long-term consequences are addressed.\n\nShort-Term Consequences (1979-1989)\n- Overthrow of Hafizullah Amin: Soviet forces assassinated President Hafizullah Amin in December 1979 and replaced him with Babrak Karmal, a member of the rival Parcham faction of the PDPA.\n- Installation of a Soviet-backed puppet regime: Babrak Karmal's government lacked popular legitimacy and was heavily reliant on Soviet military and political support. Many Afghans saw the government as a foreign-imposed regime.\n- Escalating civil conflict and growing opposition: The PDPA government faced widespread resistance from mujahideen fighters, tribal leaders, and Islamic groups. The government's control was largely limited to urban centres, while rural areas fell under insurgent influence.\n- Expansion of secret police and repression: The regime intensified surveillance, imprisonment, and torture of suspected dissidents, fuelling further unrest and resistance.\n- Dependence on Soviet military presence: The survival of the Afghan government became tied to the presence of approximately 100,000 Soviet troops deployed during the war. Without Soviet backing, the regime lacked the capacity to maintain control.\n\nLong-Term Consequences (Post-1989)\n- Collapse of the PDPA regime (1992): Following the Soviet withdrawal in 1989, the Afghan government, under President Mohammad Najibullah (Karmal's successor), survived briefly but collapsed in 1992 when Soviet aid ceased.\n- Power vacuum and civil war: The fall of the PDPA regime led to a violent power struggle among mujahideen factions, plunging Afghanistan into a brutal civil war.\n- Loss of centralised government authority: The idea of a strong, centralised Afghan government eroded, with warlords and militias dominating various regions.\n- Discrediting of communist ideology: The association of the PDPA with foreign occupation and internal repression led to a long-term rejection of communist politics in Afghan governance.\n- Foundation for Taliban rise (1990s): The chaos and governance vacuum left in the aftermath of the PDPA's collapse created conditions for the Taliban's emergence and eventual takeover in 1996.\n- Enduring political instability: The invasion and its consequences triggered a decades-long cycle of conflict, weakening Afghanistan's political institutions and fostering chronic instability.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn the short term, the Soviets removed President Hafizullah Amin and installed Babrak Karmal, a more compliant leader from the rival Parcham faction of the PDPA ($\\checkmark$). However, Karmal's regime lacked popular support and quickly became dependent on Soviet military and economic aid to stay in power ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn the long term, after the Soviet withdrawal in 1989, the Afghan government under Najibullah collapsed by 1992, leading to a violent civil war between mujahideen factions ($\\checkmark$). This prolonged instability undermined central authority and created conditions that ultimately enabled the rise of the Taliban in the mid-1990s ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0912","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0912\n\nDescribe economic and political impacts of the Korean War on South Korea (ROK).\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both political and economic impacts are addressed.\n\nPolitical:\n- Strengthened authoritarian leadership under President Syngman Rhee. Wartime chaos allowed Rhee to consolidate power and suppress political opposition.\n- Increased dependence on the United States for security and governance. U.S. military and financial aid tied South Korea closely to Western interests.\n- Establishment of a pro-Western, anti-communist regime, shaping Cold War alignment.\n- Democratic reforms delayed as national security concerns dominated politics.\n- Permanent division of Korea at the 38th parallel. No peace treaty signed; both states claimed to be the sole legitimate government of Korea.\n- Ongoing militarization and hostility. Heavily fortified DMZ created; tensions and propaganda persisted for decades.\n\nEconomic:\n- Widespread destruction of infrastructure and industry. Seoul changed hands four times; cities, railways, and factories were heavily damaged.\n- High civilian casualties and displacement strained the economy. Over 1 million South Koreans killed and millions more displaced.\n- Initial economic stagnation due to war damage and instability.\n- Massive U.S. economic aid led to post-war recovery and eventual growth. The U.S. funded reconstruction and provided food, fuel, and equipment.\n- Militarization of the economy began, laying the foundations for later industrialization.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPolitically, the war strengthened the authoritarian leadership of President Syngman Rhee, who used the conflict to suppress opposition and consolidate power ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, South Korea became heavily reliant on the United States for military and economic support, solidifying its alignment with the West and delaying democratic reforms due to ongoing security concerns ($\\checkmark$).\n\nEconomically, the war caused widespread destruction across South Korea, with cities like Seoul changing hands multiple times and infrastructure left in ruins ($\\checkmark$). However, the large-scale U.S. aid that followed enabled reconstruction and eventually contributed to economic recovery, laying the groundwork for South Korea's later industrial development ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0913","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0913\n\nDescribe economic and political impacts of the Korean War on North Korea (DPRK).\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both political and economic impacts are addressed.\n\nPolitical:\n- Solidification of Kim Il-Sung's regime. War enabled the purging of rivals and consolidation of absolute power.\nIncreased dependence on Soviet and Chinese support. USSR and China helped rebuild the North and entrenched its communist orientation.\n- Adoption of strict totalitarian control. Surveillance, repression, and cult of personality intensified after the war.\n- Permanent division of Korea at the 38th parallel. No peace treaty signed; both states claimed to be the sole legitimate government of Korea.\n- Ongoing militarization and hostility. Heavily fortified DMZ created; tensions and propaganda persisted for decades.\n\nEconomic:\n- Severe war destruction. U.S. bombings devastated nearly all major cities and infrastructure.\n- High civilian and military losses, along with widespread displacement, placed a heavy burden on the economy. Massive human casualties(estimated at over 1 million) and widespread population displacement, severely strained the labor force and hindered post-war recovery.\n- Post-war reconstruction supported by Soviet and Chinese aid. North Korea initially recovered faster than the South in the 1950s.\n- State-controlled economy entrenched. Reconstruction focused on heavy industry under central planning.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPolitically, the conflict enabled Kim Il-Sung to consolidate his authority by eliminating internal rivals and strengthening his grip on power ($\\checkmark$). The war also deepened North Korea's dependence on the Soviet Union and China, reinforcing its alignment with communist ideology and entrenching a totalitarian regime marked by surveillance and repression ($\\checkmark$).\n\nEconomically, North Korea suffered extensive destruction due to heavy U.S. bombing, which devastated cities, factories, and infrastructure ($\\checkmark$). Despite these challenges, post-war reconstruction was made possible through substantial aid from the USSR and China, allowing the country to initially recover quickly by focusing on heavy industry under a centrally planned, state-controlled economy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0914","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0914\n\nTo what extent is Source A useful in explaining the justification for the United States' use of the Domino Theory to guide its foreign policy in Asia during the Cold War?\n[5 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the final section of “The Communist Manifesto” (1848) by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, which is often titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Position of the Communists in Relation to the Various Existing Opposition Parties.\" |\n\n[Source: Marx, K. & Engels, F. (1848). *The Communist Manifesto.* Retrieved June 24, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/1848/communist-manifesto/. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a conclusion. Both values and limitations should be addressed.\n\nValue of the Source:\n- The source clearly expresses the internationalist aims of communism, supporting the idea that revolution would not remain isolated in one country.\n- It explains the ideological motivation behind communist support for revolutionary movements globally, an idea central to the Domino Theory, which assumed that if one nation fell to communism, others nearby would follow.\n- This aligns with U.S. concerns in Asia (e.g., Vietnam, Korea, Laos, Cambodia), where communism was seen not as a national phenomenon but part of a coordinated global threat.\n\nLimitations of the Source:\n- The source is theoretical and ideological, written in 1848, long before the Cold War or Asian decolonisation movements, so it lacks specific reference to the context of 20th-century Asia.\n- It does not account for nationalist motivations in Asian communist movements (e.g., Vietnamese resistance to colonialism), which were often locally driven rather than directed by Marxist ideology alone.\n- The source reflects Marx and Engels’ original vision, which was later interpreted differently by leaders like Mao or Ho Chi Minh, who adapted Marxism to fit local realities.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall assessment of the source's usefulness.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the source is valuable because it explicitly states that communists “support every revolutionary movement against the existing social and political order of things.” This suggests that communism aims to be international in scope, actively encouraging revolution beyond national borders. This ideological stance supports the premise of the Domino Theory, that if one country in a region became communist, others would likely follow, justifying U.S. involvement in countries like Korea and Vietnam to prevent this spread ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the phrase “they labour everywhere for the union and agreement of the democratic parties of all countries” reinforces the idea that communists did not see revolutions as isolated events, but as interconnected efforts to dismantle global capitalism. From an American Cold War perspective, this confirmed fears of coordinated subversion, and thus the U.S. believed early intervention in one country could prevent a regional cascade ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, a key limitation of the source is that it was written in 1848, long before the Cold War or the rise of communist movements in Asia. While it outlines the original goals of Marxist ideology, it does not address the specific historical and nationalist motivations that shaped revolutions in places like Vietnam or China. Leaders like Ho Chi Minh combined communist ideals with nationalist aims, seeking independence from colonial powers rather than merely participating in a global proletarian revolution ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, the source is useful in showing the ideological foundation for the Domino Theory by articulating communism's revolutionary and international aims. However, its usefulness is limited by its lack of reference to Cold War geopolitics and the diverse motivations behind Asian communist movements. Therefore, it helps explain the extent to which U.S. policy was ideologically driven, but not the full complexity of the region's Cold War conflicts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0915","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0915\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n[3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kim Il Sung. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.\" Speech before the Officers and Men of Unit No.256 of the Korean People's Army. October 23, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Kim, I. S. (1953, October 23). *On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.* Kim Il Sung Works. marxists.org. Retrieved June 25, 2025 from  https://www.marxists.org/archive/kim-il-sung/cw/08.pdf\nPublic Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Kim calls the war a “great victory,” claiming North Korea forced the U.S. to sign the armistice, redefining success as resistance rather than reunification.\n- The army “repulsed the enemy's invasion.” Kim labels the U.S. the “ringleader of world imperialism,” and says the war “shattered the myth of the might of U.S. imperialism.”\n- Kim says the war “tempered” the people and state, presenting it as a unifying struggle that built resilience and loyalty under the Workers' Party.\n- The speech claims a “great contribution to global peace and security,” asserting that North Korea's struggle boosted its international reputation and moral standing.\n- The mention of “tasks” suggests the fight isn't over. The tone promotes readiness, discipline, and long-term ideological commitment by the army and people.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of Source A is that North Korea emerged from the Korean War with honour and strength. Although Kim Il-Sung acknowledges that reunification was not achieved, he describes the war as a “great victory” because North Korea resisted the combined forces of the United States and its allies and forced them to agree to an armistice ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Kim frames the conflict as part of a larger anti-imperialist struggle. He claims the Korean People's Army “repulsed the enemy's invasion” and portrays the United States as the “ringleader of world imperialism.” According to Kim, the war exposed U.S. aggression and destroyed its reputation for military superiority ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the speech praises the unity and endurance of the North Korean people, military, and state institutions. Kim argues that the war strengthened national resilience and loyalty under the leadership of the Workers' Party, presenting the conflict as a transformative moment for domestic unity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0916","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0916\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying South Korean attitudes to the armistice of 1953.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor **value**, any of the following:\\\n\\\nOrigin\n- Written by Syngman Rhee, President of South Korea, on May 30, 1953, addressed to U.S. President Dwight D. Eisenhower. The Source offers a direct insight into the official South Korean leadership's position on the armistice.\n- Since it is addressed to Eisenhower and not intended for public consumption, it likely reflects Rhee’s true diplomatic concerns and political strategy, rather than propaganda.\n- Demonstrates the tensions between South Korea and the U.S., even as allies, highlighting differing priorities regarding peace and reunification.\n\nPurpose\n- Intended to convince or pressure the United States to reconsider or adjust its approach to armistice negotiations. Reveals South Korea's diplomatic posture and negotiation strategy, especially Rhee's strong emphasis on reunification and anti-communism.\n- Aimed to assert South Korea's sovereignty and raise objections to any peace that would leave the North Korean regime and Chinese troops intact. Highlights the political stakes and frustrations felt by the South Korean leadership at being sidelined in decisions affecting Korean sovereignty.\n\nContent\n- Rich in detail about the official South Korean narrative of the war and its aftermath.\n- Shows the ideological dimension of South Korean leadership, strong nationalism, anti-communism, and fear of long-term division.\n- Useful for understanding the reasons behind Rhee's resistance to the armistice, including his willingness to act unilaterally (e.g., releasing POWs).\n\nFor **limitations**, any of the following:\\\n\\\nOrigin:\n- Reflects Rhee's personal views, not necessarily representative of the broader South Korean population or all political groups.\n- As a diplomatic message, it may still be somewhat calculated to influence American policy, rather than purely expressing national sentiment.\n- Limited in scope; it does not reveal how ordinary South Koreans, opposition politicians, or civil society viewed the armistice.\n\nPurpose\n- The persuasive tone of the letter means some arguments may be exaggerated to achieve diplomatic aims.\n- The purpose may have influenced the way Rhee frames the situation, possibly downplaying compromises South Korea might have been willing to accept privately.\n\nContent\n- Does not provide statistical data, social context, or public opinion, limiting its usefulness for understanding broader societal views.\n- Focused mainly on high-level diplomatic concerns, leaving out discussion of how the armistice might impact ordinary citizens.\n- Offers a one-sided perspective, there's no engagement with counter arguments or internal dissent.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs a top-secret letter from South Korea's president to his U.S. counterpart, the origin increases its value. It was not intended for public release, which likely makes it more candid and reflective of Rhee's true diplomatic stance. This gives historians rare access to his personal reasoning and frustrations, especially his strong rejection of an armistice ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, the origin also has limitations as it represents only Rhee's views, not those of South Korean citizens or opposition voices ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nThe purpose of the letter was to persuade the United States to change its approach to the armistice, pushing for stronger action against communism and reunification of Korea. This has value as it clearly demonstrates South Korea's frustration at being sidelined in key negotiations. For historians, this purpose helps explain diplomatic tensions between allies and shows how deeply South Korea valued sovereignty and reunification ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nIn terms of content, Rhee criticises the proposed ceasefire as a form of surrender, arguing it would be “a death sentence without protest.” He stresses the importance of punishing aggression, unifying the country, and protecting the security of free nations. This is valuable for understanding the ideological basis of Rhee's policies, including his fierce anti-communism and nationalism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0917","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0917\n\nUsing Source C and your own knowledge, discuss why Syngman Rhee was dissatisfied with the 1953 armistice negotiations.\n[5 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a conclusion. Both the source and own knowledge must be addressed.\n\nFrom the source:\n- Belief that the armistice amounted to surrender: Rhee viewed the U.S./UN proposal as “appeasing” and warned it “cannot avoid the appearance of surrender,” suggesting it rewarded aggression rather than punished it.\n- Fear of permanent division of Korea: He opposed any agreement that would leave Chinese forces in the North, which he believed would solidify Korea's division and undermine national reunification.\n- Described the armistice as a “death sentence” for Korea: Rhee warned that accepting the armistice would mean “an acceptance of a death sentence without protest,” showing he feared long-term vulnerability or abandonment.\n- Excluded from meaningful participation in negotiations: Rhee complained that the Republic of Korea (ROK) had been “patiently waiting” for others to discuss proposals while South Korea had not had a real say in its own future.\n- Emphasised justice and punishment for aggressors: He insisted that the war should end with the punishment of North Korea and Communist China, and that only reunification would ensure peace and collective security.\n\nOwn Knowledge:\n- Deep mistrust of Communist China and North Korea: Rhee believed any truce leaving communist forces in Korea would only delay future aggression and allow them time to regroup.\n- Uncompromising commitment to reunification under ROK rule: Rhee's ultimate goal was a unified Korea led by the South; he saw the armistice as abandoning this objective.\n- Strong nationalist ideology and anti-communism: Rhee’s political identity was rooted in fierce anti-communism and Korean nationalism, making him unwilling to accept any solution that preserved communist rule in the North.\n- Frustration with U.S. priorities: Rhee felt the U.S. was prioritising global Cold War stability over Korean national interests, especially after Eisenhower showed willingness to end the war even with Korea divided.\n- Attempted to sabotage armistice talks: In June 1953, Rhee released 27,000 North Korean POWs unilaterally to disrupt negotiations and pressure the U.S. into making stronger commitments.\n- Used the crisis to demand a U.S.–ROK mutual defense treaty: Rhee's resistance to the armistice was also a negotiation tactic to secure long-term military guarantees from the U.S., which he eventually achieved in October 1953.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Provide a clear summary judgment that accurately reflects the evidence provided about Rhee's dissatisfaction/opposition to the armistice.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, Rhee rejected any agreement that would allow Chinese Communist forces to remain in Korea, warning it would be “an acceptance of a death sentence without protest.” Second, he criticised the United Nations' proposal as overly conciliatory, stating it appeared to be an act of “appeasement” that could lead to disaster ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBeyond the source, Rhee's broader frustration stemmed from being sidelined in negotiations that directly affected his country's future. Despite leading South Korea, he was excluded from key decisions made by the U.S. and UN ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAdditionally, his goal of unifying Korea under Southern leadership was completely undermined by a ceasefire that left the peninsula divided. Rhee feared this division would become permanent, and he saw the armistice as a betrayal of Korean sovereignty ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, Rhee opposed the armistice because it not only failed to secure Korean reunification but also left his country vulnerable to future threats, all while excluding him from shaping the outcome ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0918","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0918\n\nCompare and contrast the views presented in Source B and Source C regarding the 1953 Korean War armistice.\n[6 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kim Il Sung. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.\" Speech before the Officers and Men of Unit No.256 of the Korean People's Army. October 23, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Kim, I.S. (1953, October 23). *On a Historic Victory in the Fatherland Liberation War and the Tasks of the People's Army.* Kim Il Sung Works. marxists.org. Retrieved June 25, 2025 from  https://www.marxists.org/archive/kim-il-sung/cw/08.pdf\nPublic Domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from a letter from the President of the Republic of Korea, Syngman Rhee, to US President Eisenhower with a “top secret” classification. May 30, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Rhee, S. (1953, May 30). *The President of the Republic of Korea (Rhee) to President Eisenhower.* Office of the Historian. Retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v15p1/d565. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both leaders were dissatisfied with the outcome of the war:\n    -  Rhee (C): Opposed the armistice as it meant accepting division and the continued presence of communists in the North; “a death sentence without protest.”\n    - Kim (B): Acknowledged that reunification had not been achieved; “we have not yet reunified the country.”\n- Both frame the war as part of a larger ideological struggle:\n    - Rhee (C): Viewed the conflict as a front in the fight against communism, part of global Cold War dynamics.\n    - Kim (B): Described the war as a battle against “US imperialism” and claimed it exposed the “brutal nature of imperialism” worldwide.\n- Both used the war to promote national pride and justify their leadership:\n    - Rhee (C): Presented South Korea’s resistance as morally correct and rooted in justice.\n    - Kim (B): Claimed the armistice was a “great victory,” enhancing the prestige of the Korean people, the army, and the party.\n- Both maintained claims to legitimacy over the entire Korean peninsula:\n    - Neither leader accepted the legitimacy of the other side's government.\n    - Both saw the armistice as a temporary condition, not a permanent settlement.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences\n- Attitude Toward the Armistice\n    - Rhee (C): Strongly opposed the armistice; saw it as appeasement and betrayal.\n    - Kim (B): Publicly embraced the armistice as a strategic victory, even though military goals were unmet.\n- Perceived Outcome of the War\n    - Rhee (C): Believed the war had not achieved its goals, particularly Korean reunification under the South.\n    - Kim (B): Claimed a political and ideological victory by resisting the U.S. and “forcing” the enemy to sign the agreement.\n- Framing of the Enemy\n    - Rhee (C): Focused on the threat of communist expansion and Chinese military presence in Korea.\n    - Kim (B): Focused on U.S. imperialism as the main aggressor, calling it the “ringleader” of global oppression.\n- Vision for the Future\n    - Rhee (C): Urged continued resistance to communism and advocated for Korean reunification through stronger action.\n    - Kim (B): Positioned the post-war state as stronger, more unified, and ready to continue defending socialism.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth leaders were unhappy that the war ended without reunification ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Rhee refused to accept any peace that left North Korea intact, calling such a deal “a death sentence without protest” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Similarly, Kim acknowledged that “we have not yet reunified the country,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) suggesting that complete victory was not achieved. This reveals a shared goal of national reunification, although under very different regimes.\n\nAlso, Rhee and Kim both presented the war as part of a broader ideological struggle, tied to Cold War rivalries. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) Rhee saw the armistice as appeasement in the face of communist expansion, while Kim celebrated the war as a blow against “US imperialism,” stating that it “shattered the myth of the might of U.S. imperialism” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nFurthermore, neither Rhee nor Kim accepted the legitimacy of the other Korean government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Rhee argued that settling the war without unifying Korea would be “a death sentence without protest,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) implying that anything short of reunification under his government was unacceptable. Kim similarly described the outcome as unfinished, stating, “we have not yet reunified the country,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) suggesting that the DPRK still aimed to assert control over the entire peninsula. Both treated the armistice as a temporary arrangement rather than a lasting peace ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also differ. Rhee viewed the proposed armistice as a betrayal of Korean independence and openly rejected it. He feared it would legitimise continued foreign occupation and communist rule in the North. In contrast, Kim portrayed the armistice as a diplomatic and military success, claiming that North Korea had forced the U.S. to sign on its terms, calling it “a great victory” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Rhee saw defeat; Kim declared triumph ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI1163","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1163\n\nDescribe **three** political aims of the USSR's invasion of Afghanistan in December 1979.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Maintain a Pro-Soviet Regime\n    - The USSR aimed to keep a friendly communist government in Afghanistan to secure its southern border and protect the Central Asian republics.\n    - Intervention was meant to stop Western or Islamist influence and prevent civil war near Soviet territory.\n- Contain Islamic and Western Influence\n    - After the Iranian Revolution (1979), Moscow feared the spread of Islamic activism and Western-backed instability.\n    - The invasion sought to suppress Islamist insurgency and defend socialist ideology from U.S. and regional influence.\n- Assert Soviet Power and Credibility\n    - The USSR wanted to show it could defend its allies and maintain control over the socialist bloc.\n    - Reflected the Brezhnev Doctrine, justifying intervention to protect socialism from counterrevolution.\n- Protect Strategic Interests\n    - Afghanistan's position offered potential access to the Indian Ocean and Middle Eastern trade routes.\n    - A stable, pro-Soviet Afghanistan would help safeguard Soviet geopolitical influence in Central Asia.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPolitically, the invasion aimed to maintain a pro-Soviet regime on its southern border and prevent Afghanistan from collapsing into civil war or falling under Western or Islamist influence ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, it aimed to contain the spread of Islamic and Western influence as Moscow feared a regional rise in Islamic activism that could inspire separatism within the Soviet Union's Muslim-majority republics ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Brezhnev's leadership sought to project strength and control amid growing global and domestic challenges ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1164","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1164\n\nDiscuss **three** economic effects of the USSR's invasion of Afghanistan.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Massive Military Expenditure\n    - The war cost the USSR an estimated US $8-10 billion per year, diverting funds from civilian sectors.\n    - Sustained defence spending (up to $30\\%$ of GDP) deepened economic stagnation and weakened domestic growth.\n- Resource Drain and Opportunity Costs\n    - Huge quantities of weapons, fuel, and materials were consumed, reducing industrial output and export capacity.\n    - Investment in modernisation and consumer goods was neglected, worsening inefficiency in the command economy.\n- Human and Labour Impact\n    - Around 15,000 soldiers died, and many more were injured or traumatised, increasing welfare costs.\n    - The loss of young workers and veterans' reintegration challenges further strained productivity and the economy.\n- Impact on the Soviet Bloc Economy\n    - The USSR sent large-scale economic aid to Afghanistan, burdening the wider communist economic system (COMECON).\n    - Eastern European allies faced reduced trade and resources as Soviet spending prioritised the war effort.\n- Decline in Oil Revenue and Global Economic Pressure\n    - Falling oil prices and Western sanctions (trade and grain embargoes) reduced Soviet export income.\n    - International isolation, symbolised by the 1980 Olympic boycott, limited investment and economic engagement with the West.\n- Impact on Global Economic Relations\n    - The invasion ended détente and reignited the Cold War arms race, forcing further unsustainable military spending.\n    - The USSR's competition with the U.S. under Reagan's rearmament policies deepened its financial crisis.\n- Long-Term Domestic Economic Consequences\n    - The war exposed the inefficiency of central planning and increased public discontent.\n    - Gorbachev labelled the war an “economic and political burden,” using it to justify reforms, but the damage was lasting.\n    - By the late 1980s, the financial strain and loss of legitimacy contributed to the collapse of the Soviet economy and the USSR (1991).\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979-1989) was one of the most costly foreign interventions in Soviet history, imposing enormous financial costs on the Soviet Union, estimated between US \\$ 8-10 billion per year throughout the 1980s ($\\checkmark$). Occurring at a time of stagnation in the Soviet economy, it contributed to structural weaknesses and to the USSR's decline, with military operations diverting funds from civilian industries, agriculture, and welfare programs. This aggravated public dissatisfaction and revealing severe limits to central planning ($\\checkmark$). Withdrawal in 1989 did not reverse the damage as the war had weakened state finances, eroded public trust, and undermined the legitimacy of Soviet leadership ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1165","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1165\n\nExplain **three** political effects of the USSR's invasion of Afghanistan in December 1979.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n- Collapse of Détente and Renewed Cold War Tensions\n    - The invasion ended détente, prompting U.S. condemnation and sanctions.\n    - The Carter Doctrine (1980) and Western rearmament signalled a return to confrontation. Tthe “Second Cold War.”\n- Soviet Diplomatic Isolation.\n    - The USSR lost support among non-aligned and Muslim-majority nations, facing repeated UN condemnation.\n    - Even allies like India and Yugoslavia distanced themselves; China denounced the invasion as imperialist.\n- U.S. Proxy War Response\n    - The U.S. launched Operation Cyclone, arming Afghan Mujahideen through Pakistan.\n    - Afghanistan became a major Cold War battleground, worsening global tensions and Soviet losses.\n- Domestic Political Impact in the USSR\n    - The war became unpopular as casualties mounted and censorship failed to hide the truth.\n    - It divided Party leaders, undermined faith in the regime, and contributed to leadership instability in the 1980s.\n- Weakening of the Communist Bloc\n    - The invasion strained relations with China and Eastern Europe, revealing cracks in the Soviet alliance system.\n    - The USSR's moral and ideological authority declined as the war dragged on without victory.\n- Gorbachev's Rise and Policy Shift\n    - The war's failure pushed Gorbachev to pursue reform and withdraw Soviet troops (1989).\n    - Marked a turn away from military interventionism toward diplomacy and openness with the West.\n- Contribution to the USSR's Collapse\n    - The conflict exposed Soviet weakness and undermined communist legitimacy.\n    - Together with economic strain, it accelerated the decline and dissolution of the USSR (1991).\n- Broader Regional Effects\n    - Left Afghanistan unstable, empowering Mujahideen fighters who later formed the Taliban and al-Qaeda.\n    - Strengthened Pakistan's role as a U.S. ally and reshaped power dynamics in the Middle East and South Asia.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe invasion led to the end of détente and a renewal of the Cold War ($\\checkmark$). The U.S. and its allies condemned the Soviet action as aggressive and expansionist, ending the period detente. The Carter Doctrine (1980) promised to defend the Persian Gulf from the Soviets, while the U.S. also imposed economic sanctions, cancelled SALT II, and boycotted the 1980 Moscow Olympics. Western nations also increased defence spending, marking the start of the “Second Cold War”($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the invasion left the USSR politically isolated ($\\checkmark$). Many non-aligned and Muslim-majority countries condemned the intervention, and the United Nations repeatedly criticised Soviet actions. Long-standing partners like India and Yugoslavia were also uneasy, while China denounced the move as imperialist ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the war's failure contributed to significant political change within the USSR ($\\checkmark$). By 1985, Gorbachev recognised the conflict as a political disaster and introduced glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) to reform Soviet politics and foreign policy. His decision to withdraw Soviet troops in 1989 marked a shift away from military interventionism toward diplomacy. The war accelerated the eventual collapse of the USSR in 1991 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1176","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1176\n\nExplain **three** ways the Korean War (1950-53) affected Taiwan's strategic importance to the United States.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\nThe Korean War's outbreak and regional implications:\n- The North Korean invasion of South Korea (June 1950), supported by the USSR and later China, transformed US perceptions of communist expansion in Asia.\n- The war appeared to confirm the “domino theory”, that communism was spreading aggressively across Asia.\n- The US concluded that Taiwan could be the next target in this pattern of communist aggression.\n\nShift in US policy toward Taiwan:\n- President Harry Truman ordered the US Seventh Fleet into the Taiwan Strait (June 1950) to prevent PRC invasion and ROC attacks on the mainland.\n- This marked a decisive reversal of earlier non-intervention policy, placing Taiwan under de facto US protection.\n- The Korean War thus elevated Taiwan from a Chinese civil war remnant to a strategic Cold War asset.\n\nStrategic importance within the containment policy\n- Taiwan became a crucial link in the US containment network in East Asia, alongside Japan, South Korea, and the Philippines.\n- Its geographical position (off China's coast, near major sea lanes) made it a forward base for intelligence, naval operations, and surveillance of mainland China.\n- The ROC regime was now portrayed as part of the “Free World”, defending Asia from communist encroachment.\n- The island's defence became symbolic of US resolve to confront communism after the perceived failure to “save” China in 1949.\n\nDiplomatic and military outcomes\n- The US began supplying economic and military aid to the ROC, strengthening Chiang Kai-shek's regime.\n- In 1954, the Sino-American Mutual Defense Treaty formalised US commitment to defend Taiwan and the Pescadores Islands.\n- Taiwan's inclusion in the US-led alliance system reflected the post-Korean War expansion of American global commitments.\n\nBroader Cold War implications\n- The Korean War reinforced the bipolar division of East Asia. Communist mainland China vs US-supported Taiwan.\n- It entrenched Taiwan's position as a long-term flashpoint between communist and capitalist blocs.\n- It contributed to the militarisation of the Pacific, with a permanent US naval and air presence in the region.\n- The war also hardened China's hostility toward both Taiwan and the United States, setting the stage for the First Taiwan Strait Crisis (1954-55).\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Korean War (1950-53) greatly increased Taiwan's strategic importance as the war convinced U.S. policymakers that communist expansion in Asia was coordinated, raising fears that Taiwan might be the next target of aggression after Korea ($\\checkmark$). This shifted Taiwan from a regional issue to the frontline of the global Cold War ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecond, Taiwan became a critical link in the U.S. containment network in East Asia, along with Japan, South Korea, and the Philippines. It formed part of a defensive “island chain” against the communists ($\\checkmark$). This integration reinforced the U.S. strategy of encircling communist powers and building allies along the Pacific rim ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Taiwan's geographic position made it an ideal forward base for monitoring China and projecting American military power across the Western Pacific, reinforcing U.S. strategic dominance in the region ($\\checkmark$).  The U.S. could observe the Chinese Communists and maintain a rapid response capability in future crises ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1177","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1177\n\nIdentify **one** reason the Soviet Union boycotted the United Nations vote on Korea in June 1950 and explain **two** ways this affected the outcome of the UN decision to intervene in the Korean conflict.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** reason and any **two** effects:\n\nReason for the Soviet boycott\n- In January 1950, the Soviet Union began boycotting the United Nations to protest the continued recognition of the Republic of China (ROC) (Chiang Kai-shek's government in Taiwan) instead of the People's Republic of China (PRC) (Mao Zedong's communist regime).\n- The USSR argued that the PRC was the legitimate representative of China and that the ROC had lost control of the mainland after the Chinese Civil War (1949).\n- The boycott was intended to pressure the UN to transfer China's seat to the PRC.\n- As a result, the Soviet delegate was absent when the UN Security Council convened on 25 June 1950, following North Korea's invasion of South Korea.\n\nImpact on the UN Security Council decision\n- With the USSR absent, the Security Council lacked a communist veto power, which normally could have blocked Western-led resolutions.\n- The UN Security Council passed Resolution 82 (June 25, 1950), condemning North Korea's invasion and calling for an immediate withdrawal.\n- Two days later, it passed Resolution 83, recommending that UN member states provide military assistance to South Korea to repel the attack.\n- Because of the boycott, the Soviet veto was not exercised, allowing the United States to secure UN authorisation for collective military intervention.\n- The decision effectively made the Korean War the first UN-backed military operation in history.\n\nConsequences for the Korean War\n- The US led the UN coalition, contributing the majority of troops, resources, and command leadership under General Douglas MacArthur.\n- The absence of the USSR gave the US-led forces international legitimacy, framing intervention as a collective action to uphold international peace, rather than unilateral American aggression.\n- The UN's backing allowed the US to act decisively and swiftly, stabilising the South and later advancing beyond the 38th parallel.\n- The Soviet miscalculation was widely seen as a major diplomatic blunder, since their presence could have prevented UN approval of military intervention.\n\nBroader Cold War significance\n- The episode demonstrated how Soviet diplomacy could inadvertently strengthen Western influence in international institutions.\n- The Korean War solidified the US role as leader of the “Free World”, using the UN as a tool for collective security.\n- It prompted the USSR to return to the UN Security Council in August 1950, determined never to miss another vote.\n- The crisis intensified East–West confrontation, setting the tone for future proxy wars under UN auspices.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Soviet Union boycotted the United Nations in early 1950 to protest the UN's continued recognition of the Republic of China (ROC). Moscow argued that the People's Republic of China (PRC), not the Nationalists on Taiwan, should hold China's UN seat after the Communist victory in 1949 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThis boycott had two key effects on the outcome of the UN decision. First, the UN Security Council passed Resolution 82, condemning North Korea's aggression and demanding withdrawal. Soon after, Resolution 83 authorised member states to provide military support to South Korea under UN command ($\\checkmark$). Second, the decision strengthened the U.S. position in East Asia by demonstrating its leadership within the UN and its commitment to containing communism. This, in turn, increased the strategic importance of other regional allies like Taiwan, Japan, and the Philippines within the broader Cold War security network ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1178","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1178\n\nExplain **two** impacts of the Chinese intervention in the Korean War after October 1950.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with explanation:\n\nImmediate military effects\n- The surprise Chinese offensives reversed UN gains dramatically.\n- The First Phase Offensive (October-November 1950) halted the UN advance near the Yalu River.\n- The Second Phase Offensive (November-December 1950) forced UN and South Korean troops into a rapid retreat southward.\n- Key events:\n    - The fall of Pyongyang (October 1950) was followed by its recapture by Chinese and North Korean forces in December.\n    - The Battle of Chosin Reservoir (Nov-Dec 1950) resulted in a costly US withdrawal.\n- By January 1951, the Chinese-North Korean forces had captured Seoul for the second time.\n\nChange in the nature and scope of the war\n- China's intervention transformed the conflict from a regional civil war into a major Cold War confrontation between the communist and capitalist blocs.\n- The war became a stalemate rather than a swift UN victory.\n- The intervention prevented the unification of Korea under a pro-Western government.\n- It stabilised the northern regime, ensuring the survival of North Korea as a communist state.\n- The entry of Chinese forces prolonged the war until 1953, resulting in heavy casualties on all sides.\n\nImpact on US and UN strategy\n- The intervention forced the US to abandon its goal of total victory and to adopt a limited war strategy focused on containment rather than rollback.\n- President Truman rejected General MacArthur’s proposal to bomb Chinese territory and use nuclear weapons.\n- MacArthur was dismissed in April 1951 for insubordination, marking a turning point in US civil-military relations.\n- The US increased military spending and reinforced alliances across Asia (e.g. Japan, SEATO, Taiwan).\n\nWider Cold War consequences\n- The Chinese intervention intensified East-West tensions, reinforcing the global perception of a unified communist front (China, USSR, North Korea).\n- It boosted China's international prestige as the first major communist power to confront the US militarily.\n- The USSR supported China indirectly, providing weapons, pilots, and advisers but avoiding open conflict.\n- The war contributed to the militarisation of the Cold War, including the rearmament of West Germany and formation of NATO's integrated military structure (1951).\n- It solidified the division of Korea at the 38th parallel, which persists today.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, China's intervention reversed early UN and U.S. advances into North Korea which forced a retreat to below the 38th parallel and created a prolonged stalemate ($\\checkmark$). This showed severe limits to American military power in Asia and marked the beginning of a costly, drawn-out war of attrition ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecond, Cold War divisions in Asia were intensified by the intervention  as the regional and global balance of power was altered ($\\checkmark$). The United States increased its military presence across the Pacific and strengthened its alliances with Japan, South Korea, and Taiwan. For China, the war enhanced its prestige in the communist bloc, establishing it as a major power willing to confront the West ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"The People’s Republic of China","questions":[{"ref":"HI0107","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0107\n\nIdentify **three** principles on which the USA and the People's Republic of China agreed according to Source B. [3 marks]\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\\\$  | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the \"Joint Statement Following Discussions With Leaders of the People’s Republic of China\" (also known as the Shanghai Communiqué). Published February 27th, 1972.  |\n\n[Source: No author. (1972, February 27th). *Joint Statement Following Discussion With Leaders of the People's Republic of China*. Office of the Historian. Retrieved October 6th, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v17/d203. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n-   they agreed that countries should respect each other's sovereignty by not attacking or becoming involved in the affairs of other states\n-   the international situation is experiencing “important changes and great upheavals”\n-   international disputes should be solved peacefully\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nAccording to Source B, the US and China agreed that countries should respect each other's sovereignty which includes no attacks or aggression toward other states ($\\checkmark$) as well as not getting involved in the domestic affairs of other states ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, both agree that disputes between countries should be solved peacefully ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0270","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0270\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of Mao's implementation of $`$collective ownership of the means of production' on the agricultural sector of China from 1950-1957. [6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- June 1950 Land Reform Programme: \n\n    - Mao launched a programme of land reform with the Agrarian Reform Law, which confiscated and redistributed the property of rural landlords. Land ownership remained private, but the former landlords who survived the attacks of the $`$speak bitterness campaign', which encouraged peasants to turn on their landlord, now held only as much land as the poorest peasant.  \n\n- 1951-1953 cooperative farming:\n\n    - The Communist party cadres encouraged peasants to farm the land cooperatively, with profits distributed according to the land, tools, animals and work contributed by individual peasants. The land was still individually owned while resources were pooled for better grain production. By 1953, almost 40% of peasant farms belonged to mutual aid teams or cooperatives. From 1953 on, the state was the sole purchaser of grain.    \n\n- 1954-1955 Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives (APCs):\n\n    - The peasants were encouraged to create higher-level Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives. Land was now pooled and no longer individually owned with profits being distributed according to work contributed by each peasant. Individual peasant farmers were unable to obtain bank loans, buy seed and fertilizer or get help during harvest time. \n\n    - By 1957 over 96% of peasant families were part of Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives and peasants were obliged to sell fixed quotas to the state. \n\n- 1958: Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives become $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Communes”.\n\n    - APCs were amalgamated into much larger units called Communes where there were no more private farms. \n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn June 1950, Mao launched his land reform programme with the passing of the Agrarian Reform Law. This implemented the confiscation of property from rural landlords and its redistribution amongst the landless peasants ($\\checkmark$). This was accompanied by the $`$speak bitterness campaign', in which party cadres encouraged the peasants to subject the landlords to a $`$people's trial', where many lost their lives as they were subject to the anger of the mob ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe next step in Mao's agrarian reforms was from 1951-1953, when the Communist party cadres encouraged peasants to farm their land cooperatively, with profits being distributed according to the land, tools, animals and work contributed by individual peasants ($\\checkmark$). Land was still individually owned while resources were pooled for better grain production ($\\checkmark$). \n\nBy 1954-1955, the peasants were now encouraged to create higher-level Agricultural Producers' Co-operatives. Land was now pooled and no longer individually owned with profits being distributed according to work contributed by each peasant ($\\checkmark$). Individual peasant farmers who held out were unable to obtain bank loans, buy seed and fertilizer or get help during harvest time ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0271","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0271\n\nOutline the main features of Mao's $`$Great Leap Forward' in 1958. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- The Great Leap Forward was the mass mobilisation of society launched in 1958 and aimed at making China a world superpower that would challenge the USA and overtake Britain in fifteen years. \n\n- In urban areas there was an increase in State-Owned Enterprises while in the countryside, the Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives (APCs)  were amalgamated into Communes. \n\n- Each Commune consisted of 40-100 villages. There were approximately 53000 communes in China. Private farming was to cease and all aspects of production were now centrally directed by the Communist Party. \n\n- Each commune would also contain factories to provide work for women and children. \n\n- Communes were self-contained units that had nurseries for young children, old people's homes, health clinics, communal canteens and unisex dormitories for the inhabitants of the commune, who were encouraged to abandon personal possessions. \n\n- Rural peasants were now also expected to contribute to industrial growth and the production of steel and were encouraged to build and operate brick furnaces in their backyards to smelt crude steel. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward was the mass mobilisation of society launched in 1958 and aimed at making China a world superpower that would challenge the USA and overtake Britain in fifteen years ($\\checkmark$). In urban areas, there was an increase in State-Owned Enterprises, while in the countryside, the Agricultural Producers' Cooperatives (APCs)  were amalgamated into Communes ($\\checkmark$). As a result, private farming was to cease, and all aspects of production were now centrally directed by the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$). Communes were self-contained units with nurseries for young children, old people's homes, health clinics, communal canteens and unisex dormitories for the inhabitants ($\\checkmark$). One final feature of the Great Leap Forward was that rural peasants were also expected to contribute to industrial growth and steel production, including building and operating brick furnaces in their backyards to smelt crude steel ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0272","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0272\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of the Great Leap Forward on China. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- Failure of agricultural systems:\n\n    - Communal farming failed as Chinese farmers were not able to adapt to the demands of large scale farming. Farmers left their fields fallow as the focus of propaganda was on keeping the backyard furnaces running. Many farmers melted their tools to produce steel, much of which was unusable. \n\n    - The $`$four pests campaign' endorsed by Mao further damaged agriculture. The four pests campaign $`$outlawed' and was aimed at the eradication of flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows. The killing of sparrows led to an explosion of caterpillars and other insects that destroyed crops.\n\n    - The focus on producing crude steel in the backyard furnaces led to neglect of animal husbandry and growing of vegetables. \n\n- Famine and mass death\n\n    - Government increased the share of grain collected from the communes from 17% to 28%, leaving the communes with insufficient food for its members and leading to starvation. \n\n    - 1959 to 1961 (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Three Bitter Years”) witnessed the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward and were exacerbated by the drought that affected northern and central China in 1960 and floods in Southern China. \n\n- Loss of some power for Mao and new Chinese leadership\n\n    - The failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in Mao stepping down as the Chairman of the People's Republic of China while retaining his position as Head of the Communist Party. \n\n    - Liu Shaoqi, as the new Head of State along with Deng Xiaoping, now introduce elements of private ownership in order to stabilise the situation. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward failed to meet its agriculture or steel production targets ($\\checkmark$). Communal farming failed as Chinese farmers could not adapt to the demands of large-scale farming. Farmers left their fields fallow as propaganda focused on keeping the backyard furnaces running. The $`$four pests campaign', endorsed by Mao, aimed to eradicate flies, mosquitoes, rats and sparrows. The killing of sparrows led to an explosion of caterpillars and other insects that destroyed crops ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFocusing on producing crude steel in backyard furnaces to increase steel production in China was also a failure of the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$). As peasants were told they would get rich quickly if they concentrated on iron and steel production, many farmers melted their tools to produce steel, much of which was unusable. This further impacted agricultural production and, at the same time, failed to contribute to an increase in steel production ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThis resulted in $`$three bitter years' from 1959 to 1961, which witnessed the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese from the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward and from the natural disasters that hit China at the same time - a drought that affected northern and central China in 1960 and floods in Southern China ($\\checkmark$). In the end, the failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in Mao stepping down as the Chairman of the People's Republic of China while retaining his position as Head of the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0273","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0273\n\nOutline **four** steps Mao took to support women who, as he stated, $`$hold up half the sky'.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- In 1950 Female infanticide was abolished, and foot binding was outlawed. \n\n- The 1950 Marriage Law forbade arranged and child marriages, the payment of dowry, and concubinage. Official marriage registration was introduced, and divorce by mutual consent or on the complaint of either party was permitted. Those subjected to arranged marriage were allowed to petition for divorce. \n\n- In 1950 women were given the same property rights as men.\n\n- 1953 Election Law gave women the right to vote. \n\n- Women were encouraged to train for jobs, and some joined the government and the People's Liberation Army. \n\n- During the Great Leap Forward communal nurseries, communal eating and shared cleaning supported women's entry into the workforce. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao famously proclaimed, $`$women held up half the sky', to emphasise the equal role played by women in achieving the goals of the Chinese Revolution. To ensure equal status for women, the Communist state banned female infanticide and foot binding in 1950 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe 1950 Marriage Law further forbade arranged and child marriages, the payment of dowry, and concubinage. Official marriage registration was introduced, and divorce by mutual consent or on the complaint of either party was permitted. Those subjected to arranged marriage were allowed to petition for divorce, and in 1950, women were also given the same property rights as men ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn 1953, the Election Law gave women the right to vote, leading to greater participation in political matters ($\\checkmark$). \n\nDuring the Great Leap Forward, the establishment of communal nurseries, shared eating arrangements for all commune members and shared, centralised cleaning of all living areas lessened the domestic burden placed on women and supported their entry into the workforce, leading to greater independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0274","marks":"40","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0274\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of Mao's Communist Revolution on minority groups within China. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting details:\n\n- Annexation of Tibet and subsequent famine:\n\n    - Tibet was forcibly made part of the People's Republic of China, and Chinese economic policies were imposed.\n\n    - These new economic policies resulted in a famine in which a quarter of the Tibetan population died. This was because of Chinese policies that forced the Tibetans to grow wheat and maize, which was not their staple diet. The high ground used by the Tibetans for grazing their yak was forcibly farmed, resulting in a shortage of milk, cheese, and yak hair, which were required for clothes and tents. \n\n- Supervision of ethnic minorities and culture:\n\n    - In 1950, ethnic minorities were asked to self-identify and were promised autonomy. 400 ethnic groups that did so were organised into 50 groups and placed under military supervision. \n\n- Religious suppression:\n\n    - Buddhist culture was suppressed in Tibet. In 1959 the uprising in Tibet against the outlawing of the Lama Faith and Tibetan culture was brutally crushed. \n\n    - In 1974 during the Cultural Revolution, the People's Liberation Army forced mosques to close and burnt religious books.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's communist revolution resulted in the suppression of Tibet's Buddhist culture, and Tibet was forcibly made part of the People's Republic of China ($\\checkmark$). The Lama Faith was outlawed, and the resultant uprising against this in 1959 was brutally crushed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nChinese economic policies in Tibet resulted in a famine in which a quarter of the Tibetan population died ($\\checkmark$). This was because Chinese policies forced the Tibetians to grow non-staple crops like wheat and because grazing land was forcibly farmed, resulting in a shortage of milk, cheese and yak hair needed for clothes and tents ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0275","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0275\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the reasons that prompted Mao to launch the Great Leap Forward? [3 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research report titled *Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China* published by Canadian Center of Science and Education. July 10, 2019|\n\n[Source: Jung, Hsiung-Shen & Chen, Jui-Lung. (2019, July 10). *Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China*.Asian Culture and History - . Canadian Center of Science and Education. Retrieved December 14, 2024 from https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v11n2p58 . CC-BY 4.0. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** the following:\n\n- The Great Leap Forward was a result of the deterioration of the relations between the Communist Parties of China and the Soviet Union. \n\n- Mao launched the Great Leap Forward as he initially wanted to catch up with the United States in iron and steel production in 50 to 60 years.\n\n- However after meeting Krushchev in 1957 he decided that China could surpass Britain in 15 years. \n\n- He was also convinced that China's economy would develop faster and better than that of the Soviet Union. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, Mao launched the Great Leap Forward as a result of the deteriorating relations between the Communist Parties of China and the Soviet Union and as a way to  lessen Chinese dependance on the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). He was also initially prompted by the desire to catch up with the United States in iron and steel production in 50 to 60 years ($\\checkmark$). After meeting Krushchev in 1957, Mao decided that China could surpass Britain in 15 years with the mass mobilisation of the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0276","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0276\n\nWhat does Source A reveal about the impact of the Great Leap Forward on China? [2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A political cartoon titled, “By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot” by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. March 9, 1961. |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/The%20People%E2%80%99s%20Republic%20of%20China/images/hi0276.JPEG\" alt=\"hi0276\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, Edmund S.(1961, March 9). *By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot*. Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/item/2016687288/. Public domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- The Chinese people have been hard hit by the Great Leap Forward.\n\n- Many deaths have resulted from hunger.\n\n- Mao and his colleagues are cynical and unconcerned about the human cost of the program and are still promoting it. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A shows that the Chinese people have suffered as a result of the Great Leap Forward as many deaths have resulted from hunger as evidenced by the graves and the words $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$victims of hunger” ($\\checkmark$). The source also shows that Mao and his colleagues are cynical and unconcerned about the human cost of the program and are still promoting it as the title of the cartoon reads, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$By government decree every member of the commune is entitled to a private lot,” with reference to plots for the graves ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0278","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0278\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the impact of the Great Leap Forward on China. [6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research report titled Causes, *Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China* published by Canadian Center of Science and Education. July 10, 2019|\n\n[Source: Jung, Hsiung-Shen & Chen, Jui-Lung. (2019, July 10). Causes, Consequences and Impact of the Great Leap Forward in China.Asian Culture and History - . Canadian Center of Science and Education. Retrieved December 14, 2024 from https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v11n2p58 . CC-BY 4.0. ]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Research article titled *Peasants and the Great Leap Forward* by Marissa Warren, from  the University of Nebraska at Kearney. Published in 2017 in the Undergraduate Research Journal.|\n\n[Source: Warren, M. (2017). *Peasants and the Great Leap Forward*. Undergraduate Research Journal: Vol. 21 , Article 9. Retrieved December 12, 2024 from https://openspaces.unk.edu/undergraduate-research-journal/vol21/iss1/9. Open access.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nFor comparison, any **three** to **four** of the following **similarities**:\n\n- Both sources argue that grain exports were emphasized during the Great Leap Forward.\n\n- Both sources mention that the policies of the Great Leap Forward drove a famine and contributed to the deaths.\n\n- Both sources state that farmers were forced to work on producing iron and steel and on growing grain.\n\nBoth sources state that millions of Chinese died as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward.\n\nFor contrast, any **two** to **three** of the following differences:\n\n- Source B states that the reduction in grain production was a result of $`$increasing output of iron and steel' while Source C states it was $`$due to the policy on communal dining halls, (as) peasants no longer had private plots to grow food'.\n\n- Source C mentions accounts of cannibalism as a result of famine and the collapse of the healthcare system, which is not mentioned in Source B.\n\n- Both Sources mention quantities of deaths caused by the Great Leap Forward, but Source B cites the number $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$40 million” nationwide while Source C focuses on the quantities in Xinyang Region ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over a million”) and Guangshan ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one in four people”).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources mention the increased emphasis on small-scale steel production ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B describes $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$reduced grain production due to increasing output of iron and steel” in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$small blast furnaces,” while Source C says peasants worked “backyard furnaces to create steel” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nBoth sources also state that grain export continued during the famine and contributed to the deaths ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$deficient food supply due to over-export,” while Source C states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and members of the CCP were obsessed with their export markets and thus, even during widespread famine, continued to export most of their grain” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nBoth sources also state that millions of Chinese died as a result of the policies implemented during the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over 40 million people starved to death statistically” while Source C states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$by 1960, over a million people were dead due to the widespread famine” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). However, The focus of the deaths mentioned in both sources differs ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B cites the number of deaths at $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$40 million” nationwide while Source C focuses on the quantities in Xinyang Region ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$over a million”) and Guangshan ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one in four people”) ($\\checkmark$ evidence).   \n\nThe sources disagree on what drove reduced grain production ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B states that the reduction in grain production resulted from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$increasing output of iron and steel” while Source C states it was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$due to the policy on communal dining halls, (as) peasants no longer had private plots to grow food” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Finally, Source C mentions accounts of cannibalism as a result of famine and the collapse of the healthcare system, which is not mentioned in Source B ($\\checkmark$ difference). \n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$"},{"ref":"HI0279","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0279\n\nAccording to Source D, what challenges did China's mass mobilization (the Great Leap Forward) still face in 1958?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Masses Can Do Anything” an extract from a statement by Mao Zedong to a reporter of the Hsin Hua Agency on 29 September 1958 following a tour of the country.|\n\n[Source: Mao Tse-tung. (September 29, 1958). The Masses Can Do Anything. Selected Works of Mao-Tse-tun on Marxists.org. Retrieved December 12, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-8/mswv8_16.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\n- There were places where the work of mobilizing the masses had not been properly carried out and where the tasks required, the reasons for them and the methods had still not been made clear to the masses.\n\n- There were a few Communist party leaders who were unwilling to undertake a large-scale mass movement in the industrial sphere and who disparaged the mass movement on the industrial front as $`$a rural style of work' and $`$a guerrilla habit'.\n\n- To ensure that agriculture was not neglected or sacrificed while focusing on iron and steel production on a large scale.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source D, Mao stated that the Great Leap Forward mass mobilization faced challenges as there were places where the tasks required for the movement, the reasons for them, and the methods had not been made clear to the masses ($\\checkmark$). Another challenge was that there were a few Communist party leaders who were unwilling to undertake a large-scale mass movement in the industrial sphere and who disparaged the mass movement on the industrial front as $`$a rural style of work' and $`$a guerrilla habit' ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0642","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0642\n\nExplain how Source C conveys socialist ideals promoted by the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) during the 1950s.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Chinese propaganda poster from 1953. The caption reads, “A free and independent marriage is good, there is great happiness in unified production.\" |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/The%20People%E2%80%99s%20Republic%20of%20China/images/HI0642.jpg\" alt=\"HI0642\">\n[Source: Cheng, B, (1953, February). *A free and independent marriage is good, there is great happiness in unified production.* Private collection hosted on ChinesePosters.net. Retrieved March 31, 2025 from https://chineseposters.net/themes/marriage-law Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence/explanation:\n- Emphasis on greater gender equality and women's liberation:\n    - The phrase “A free and independent marriage is good” reflects the Marriage Law of 1950, which aimed to eliminate forced marriages, arranged marriages, and feudal family structures. The CCP promoted marriage based on mutual love and equality, aligning with its broader goal of gender equality and women's liberation.\n- Connects marriage to the collective good of society:\n    - The phrase, “There is great happiness in unified production” ties marriage to communal labor and socialist production, reinforcing the idea that happiness comes from working together for the collective good.\n- Emphasizes the role of productivity of both men and women together:\n    - The Great Leap Forward (1958-1962) emphasized collective farming and industrial production, and propaganda often depicted men and women working enthusiastically in agriculture and industry.\n- The critical role of agricultural laborers\n    - Agricultural labor was a key theme in Communist propaganda, portraying work in the fields as fulfilling and joyful rather than labor-intensive and harsh. The smiling young man and woman harvesting represents the CCP's vision of a harmonious, productive socialist society.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe poster reflects key socialist ideals, beginning with the promotion of free and independent marriage ($\\checkmark$). The phrase “A free and independent marriage is good” aligns with the Marriage Law of 1950, which sought to abolish forced and arranged marriages, as well as feudal family structures. The CCP emphasized marriage based on mutual love and equality, supporting its broader vision of gender equality and women's liberation ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the poster highlights economic productivity and collective labor ($\\checkmark$). The phrase “There is great happiness in unified production” connects marriage to communal labor and socialist production, reinforcing the notion that true happiness comes from working together for the benefit of society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0644","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0644\n\nDescribe the features of the PRC's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Reunification Campaigns\" from 1949 to 1952. \n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The PRC sent the PLA (People's Liberation Army) to Guangdong province, Tibet and Xinjiang in 1950 to assert their control.\n\n - Mao was worried that remote outlying regions would revolt against the CCP. For example, Guangdong province was once the origin and stronghold of the Nationalist Party. \n\n - Mao feared that Xinjiang, which has a large Muslim population, would either declare independence or be taken by Mongolia or the Soviet Union.\n\n - Mao justified that Tibet was historically part of China. The PLA was sent to Tibet to take over the region and met minimal resistance due to the PLA having more men and experience in combat. \n\n - Shortly after the reunification campaigns, the PRC conducted a \"sinicization” campaign to forcefully assimilate Han culture into Tibet. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe PRC launched a series of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Reunification Campaigns\" from 1949 to 1952 by sending the PLA to different outlying regions to consolidate the PRC's dominance across China ($\\checkmark$). Mao feared that the outlying regions may threaten the new government. For example, Mao feared that Xinjiang, which is a northwestern province dominated by a Muslim population, might be taken over by Mongolia or the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). Mao also claimed that other outlying regions such as Tibet have been historically part of China, in which the PLA was dispatched to takeover the region without meeting any massive resistance ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0645","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0645\n\nExplain **two** consequences of the PRC's first 5-year Plan from 1952 to 1957.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following reasons with supporting explanation:\n\nIndustrialization\n\n - China was able to develop key state industries by adopting the Soviet model and using the capitalists that did not flee China to contribute to China's economic development.\n\n     - China developed its state-owned automobile industry, such as the First Automobile Works .\n\n     - The government focused on developing coal and steel industries in Northeast China by using the abundant natural resources in the area and also the facilities left by the Japanese. \n\n     - By 1956, coal production increased by 98$\\%$, industrial output increased by 25$\\%$ \n\n - Infrastructure development\n\n     - The government completed various infrastructure projects under the support of the Soviet Union during this period, such as the Nanjing Yangtze River Bridge\n\n     - The government also expanded its industrial capacity by constructing more industrial production facilities across the country, such as the Daqing Oilfield and Anshan Steel Plan. \n\n - Impact on higher education in China\n\n     - The 5-year plan required a large number of technical expertises. The government revamped the higher education program to focus on training more engineers and technicians. \n\n     - Due to the strong emphasis on technical and science education, the art subjects were discouraged.\n\n - Impact on Agriculture \n\n     - The government implemented the Agrarian Reform law that abolished land ownership by confiscating land from landlords, in which some of the landlords were executed in public trials. \n\n     - The confiscated land was redistributed to peasants. Around 300 million peasants received redistributed land by 1952.\n\n     - Collective farms were established all across the country. By 1957, there were around 800,000 collective farms built. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe PRC's 1st 5-year plan had a significant impact on China's economy by laying down the foundation for China's industrial and agricultural development. Firstly, China was successful in creating different state-owned enterprises under the support of the Soviet Union, such as the First Automobile Works ($\\checkmark$). During this period, the government was able to develop its coal and steel industries in Northeastern China by using the abundant natural sources and the resources left behind by the Japanese, which led to a significant increase in its overall industrial output by 25$\\%$ ($\\checkmark$). In addition to developing its foundation for its industries, the 1st 5-year plan also had a significant impact on China's agriculture. In order for the government to centralise its control on agricultural production, all land was confiscated from landlords and redistributed to the peasants, so that by 1952, around 300 million peasants received redistributed land ($\\checkmark$). However, the government soon implemented collectivisation by having the state take over land ownership and requiring peasants to join collective farms ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0646","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0646\n\nDescribe **four** key features of the Great Leap Forward from 1958 to 1962.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n - The Great Leap Forward aimed to boost China's steel production to surpass the UK and the US in the next 15 years. \n\n - The government required the countryside to build backyard furnaces to increase steel production. \n\n - The Chinese government also aimed to increase agricultural production dramatically by abolishing private ownership and forcing peasants into collective farms. \n\n - The traditional social structure in the rural countryside was changed dramatically since all peasants were required to live in communes, in which they had to live a communal lifestyle. \n\n - The Chinese government also conducted crop experiments by adopting Lysenkoism to increase the agricultural yield. \n\n - Local officials exaggerated food production statistics due to fear of punishment. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward aimed to boost China's steel production to surpass the UK and the US in the next 15 years ($\\checkmark$). To reach this goal, the Chinese government mobilized the rural population to produce steel using backyard furnaces, but the steel produced was of poor quality ($\\checkmark$). The Chinese government also aimed to increase agricultural production by forcing peasants to live in people's communes and abolishing private farming altogether ($\\checkmark$). Due to this, the traditional social structure in the rural countryside was changed dramatically since all peasants were required to live in communes, in which they had to live a communal lifestyle ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0647","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0647\n\nExplain **two** ways the failure of the Great Leap Forward impacted the leadership of the Chinese Communist Party.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Mao's retreat from politics\n\n     - Mao decided to retreat from politics by stepping down as the President of China in 1959, but still retaining his position as the Chairman of the Communist Party of China. \n\n     - Mao conducted self-criticism on his policies in the 7000 Cadre Conference in 1962 due to the failure of the Great Leap Forward.  \n\n - Mao's leadership came under criticism and scrutiny\n\n    - After Mao's resignation, he was criticized by General - Peng Dehuai for his failures in the Great Leap  Forward at the Lushan Conference. Mao later removed Peng from the Minister of National Defence post and installed Lin Biao as the new minister. \n\n- Rise of Deng Xiaoping and Liu Shaoqi\n\n     - Liu and Deng took over Mao's position and started implementing policies to reverse the damage done by the Great Leap Forward.\n\n     - Liu and Deng implemented policies such as re-introducing private farming and downsizing the people's communes. Peasants were allowed to trade at local markets to sell their surplus goods. \n\n     - Liu and Deng also imported grain from Australia, diverting food resources to affected regions. Grain exports were halted to direct resources to famine-affected regions.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe failure of the Great Leap Forward had different impacts on the CCP's leadership, such as Mao's leadership in the party. Due to the failure of the Great Leap Forward, Mao went through self-criticism and decided to step down as the President of China, but still kept his position as the Chairman of the CCP ($\\checkmark$). Mao was also under criticism from General Peng Dehuai at the Lushan Conference  ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAnother impact of the Great Leap Forward on the CCP leadership was the rise of Deng Xiaoping and Liu Shaoqi. Deng Xiaoping and Liu Shaoqi took over Mao's duties and started to implement policies to reverse the damage brought by the Great Leap Forward, such as reintroducing private farming by allowing peasants to trade their surplus goods in local markets  ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, they also imported grain from Australia to alleviate the damage in famine-affected regions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0648","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0648\n\nTo what extent had Mao consolidated his rule in China by 1976?\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)$\\hspace{1.2em}$|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\nPossible evidence/examples to argue that Mao **had** consolidated his rule:\n\n - Land Reforms \n\n     - The land reforms effectively transformed the rural areas by eliminating the landlords and putting the peasants into communes that were under the control of the CCP.\n\n - Three-anti and Five-anti campaigns\n\n     - The campaigns were launched after a few years of the founding of the PRC. The campaign aimed to eliminate corruption, bureaucratism, and anyone that was considered to be a state enemy. \n\n     - The campaign also targeted capitalists by putting them on show trials to confess their crimes.\n\n     - The campaigns weakened the Chinese bourgeois, especially business owners, professionals and intellectuals. \n\n     - The vacant government positions after the campaign were replaced by new younger party cadres that showed more devotion to Maoist ideology. \n\n - Hundred Flowers Campaign and Anti-Rightist movement \n\n     - Although the Hundred Flowers Campaign encouraged people to criticise the government, the fact that Mao was able to launch the Anti-Rightist movement to purge those that criticized him.\n\n - Great Leap Forward\n\n     - Officials often made exaggerated statistics on the food and steel production during the Great Leap Forward out of fear of being persecuted. Moreover, during the Lushan Conference in 1959 during the Great Leap Forward, only Peng Dehuai criticized Mao openly while other party officials kept silent or even defended Mao, this suggested that Mao had significant influence despite not being in direct control of the events.\n\n - Cultural Revolution\n\n     - The Cultural Revolution marked the height of Mao's power. During this period, the cult of Mao reached a semi-divine level. The Red Guards would carry portraits of Mao around when doing parades and public trials. The Little Red Book containing Mao's quotes was also printed and distributed to everyone. Party officials and Red Guards often chanted slogans from the book. \n\n     - During the Cultural Revolution, political opponents that once challenged Mao such as Peng Dehuai, Li Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping were being attacked by Red Guards ordered by Mao. This left Mao unchallenged at this point.\n\n     - Although Mao's health deteriorated drastically by the early 1970s, officials still feared being purged by Mao. In 1975 Mao was still able to issue orders to rehabilitate purged officials such as Deng Xiaoping back in power. \n\nPossible evidence/examples to argue that Mao **had not** consolidated his rule:\n\n - Although Mao was effectively the leader of the CCP and China, he was challenged numerous times by party officials or in campaigns such as the Hundred Flowers Campaign and the Lushan Conference. \n\n - During the Great Leap Forward, Mao was not completely aware of and in control of the devastation in the countryside since most officials lied about the statistics. \n\n - In the Lushan Conference, Mao was criticized by Peng Dehuai. After the Great Leap Forward, Mao retreated from politics and let Deng and Liu Shaoqi run the country.\n\n - During the later stages of the Cultural Revolution, Mao did not often involve himself in running the government and the movement; most of them were done by the Gang of Four. Moreover, despite the Red Guards showing loyalty to Mao, there was often infighting between different Red Guard factions to see which faction had stronger loyalty. This suggests that Mao was not in control during the Cultural Revolution. \n\n - Mao was nearly assassinated by Lin Biao, which showed that Mao’s rule was challenged even by those who had previously supported him.\n\nFurther guidance:\n\n - Some responses may divide their commentary about his consolidation into various themes or perspectives, such as political, ideological, military, social, and economic consolidation, arguing the degree to which Mao controlled one or more of them.\n\n - The strongest responses are likely to argue that Mao maintained a firm grip on power up until his death in 1976, but that it was far from fully consolidated in every aspect.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAt the time of his death in 1976, Mao had been mostly successful in consolidating his rule, but there were still points of weakness in his control. He faced substantial internal dissent as the country experienced economic hardship and instability due to his policies. Mao's political power fluctuated throughout his rule, reaching its peak during the late 1950s following his ideological and political campaigns, and again during the late 1960s through the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, the repercussions of the failures of the Great Leap Forward and internal opposition, particularly in the early 1960s and again in the 1970s as his health declined, indicated that his power was fragile by the end of his life ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nMao was able to successfully consolidate and maintain a strong control over the country through political means and ideological control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). These were achieved through Mao's political campaigns in the 1950s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). For example, in the Three-anti and Five-anti movements ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), Mao purged corrupt officials and bourgeois elements, such as intellectuals and business owners. Mao replaced the purged officials with younger party cadres who were more devoted to his ideology in government positions, which further consolidated Mao's support within the government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Mao further consolidated his rule in the Hundred Flowers Campaign and Anti-Rightist movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Although the Hundred Flowers Campaign initially enabled people to voice their criticism of the government freely, Mao immediately reversed the campaign by launching the Anti-Rightist movement and purging those who once criticised him ($\\checkmark knowledge$). These two events showed that even though Mao was initially willing to tolerate criticism, the sudden change suggested that Mao used this event to persecute his political opponents, consolidating his rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Moreover, many hesitated to challenge Mao's ideology and decision-making, further solidifying his control in the CCP ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). \n\nIn the late 1950s and early 1960s, Mao's control was greatly tested by the failures of the Great Leap Forward, a campaign for rapid industrialization and collectivization of the Chinese economy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The widespread famine and mass deaths caused by the policies of the Great Leap Forward resulted in some temporary loss of power and control for Mao in favor of more practical leaders like Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, who introduced reforms to help the country recover economically ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Though he was technically still in power, Mao retreated notably from leadership and lost some of his grip on the power he had previously held ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, by the early 1970s, Mao's control was increasingly symbolic. After 1968, the Red Guards were disbanded, and many were sent to the countryside as their chaos threatened Party stability ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Authority over the Cultural Revolution was transferred to the People's Liberation Army (PLA), and real power shifted to the Gang of Four, who claimed to act on Mao's behalf ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). As Mao's health declined after 1972, he became increasingly detached from day-to-day governance, relying on proxies who often pursued their own agendas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Meanwhile, moderate leaders like Zhou Enlai and Deng Xiaoping began to re-emerge, especially after Zhou's efforts to stabilize the economy and Deng's brief rehabilitation in 1973 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Though Mao remained the figurehead of Chinese Communism, the internal divisions within the Party, lack of coherent policy, and Mao's inability to fully control his successors suggest that his consolidation of power had weakened significantly by the time of his death ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). \n\nIn conclusion, Mao successfully consolidated his rule through purges, ideological campaigns, and personal cult-building from the 1950s into the 1960s. However, the failures of the Great Leap Forward and the chaotic legacy of the Cultural Revolution undermined the stability of his rule ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). By the 1970s, Mao remained a powerful symbol, but factionalism, declining health, and reliance on radicals like the Gang of Four meant that his direct control had weakened ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Thus, while Mao had effectively consolidated his rule in earlier years, by 1976 his grip on power was severely limited. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0762","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0762\n\nExplain **three** reasons Mao launched the Cultural Revolution in 1966.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - Reassert authority after the Great Leap Forward. \n\n     - The catastrophic failure of the Great Leap Forward (1958-62) had damaged Mao's credibility. He was sidelined by more pragmatic leaders like Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping. Mao complained that he was being treated as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$dead ancestor\" (respected but ignored). The Cultural Revolution was a way for him to return to the political centre.\n\n - Purge $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists” and restore ideological purity. \n\n     - Mao believed the CCP was becoming too bureaucratic and moving away from revolutionary principles. He feared the Soviet-style shift toward a ruling class.\n\n - Revive revolutionary enthusiasm among the youth. \n\n     - Mao saw the youth as politically pure and revolutionary. By mobilising students, he bypassed the Party apparatus and built a mass movement loyal to himself.\n\n - Protect against capitalist and traditional influences. \n\n     - Mao wanted to destroy Confucian values, capitalist behaviours, and bourgeois thinking, which he viewed as threats to socialism, and which he thought had crept back into Chinese society.\n\n - Support from radicals like Jiang Qing and the Gang of Four. \n\n     - These allies encouraged Mao's cultural and ideological goals and helped him orchestrate attacks on his political opponents through mass campaigns.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne of the key motivations in launching the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution (GPCR) was Mao's desire to reassert his authority after the disastrous outcomes of the Great Leap Forward (1958-1962), which had severely undermined his credibility within the CCP. ($\\checkmark$) In the aftermath, moderate leaders like Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping took on greater roles, pushing Mao to the sidelines. Feeling marginalized and reportedly likening himself to a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$dead ancestor\" (revered but no longer influential), Mao initiated the Cultural Revolution to regain control and reaffirm his dominance at the top of the Party hierarchy. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nAnother major factor behind the movement was Mao's determination to purge the Party of so-called $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists\" and to restore what he viewed as the ideological purity of the revolution. ($\\checkmark$) He grew increasingly concerned that the CCP was drifting to the right and adopting a Soviet-style government, which he believed betrayed the original revolutionary spirit. To counter this, Mao sought to eliminate those within the Party who he thought were deviating from Marxist-Leninist principles. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nIn addition, Mao aimed to rekindle revolutionary fervor among the population, particularly the youth. ($\\checkmark$) He regarded young people as untainted by the compromises and corruption of older generations and saw them as ideal agents of radical change. By encouraging students to challenge authority and embrace revolutionary ideals, Mao was able to sidestep the established Party structure and create a mass movement that was personally loyal to him. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0763","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0763\n\nDescribe the role of the Red Guards in the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Red Guards were often teenagers from urban schools and universities who were mobilised by Mao as revolutionary agents. He directly encouraged students to rise against $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$capitalist roaders” within the Party. \n\n - Red Guards attacked the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Olds”, which included old customs, culture, habits, and ideas. They destroyed temples, burned books, and renamed streets, aiming to erase traditional Chinese culture.\n\n - Red Guards led the violent persecution of perceived enemies, including teachers, intellectuals, former landlords, and anyone accused of being anti-revolutionary. They were humiliated, beaten, imprisoned, or killed.\n\n - Red guards led public denunciations and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$struggle sessions”. Victims were paraded, forced to confess, and publicly shamed. These events instilled fear and reinforced Maoist ideology.\n\n - Brought up on Mao's cult of personality, the Red Guards worshipped Mao, carrying his Little Red Book and treating his words as absolute truth. This helped cement Mao's supremacy over the CCP.\n\n - As the GPCR progressed, rival groups of Red Guards began to clash, leading to chaos and civil strife. Mao eventually had to intervene. As order broke down, Mao turned to the army to restore control. Many Red Guards were sent to the countryside to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$learn from the peasants\", effectively ending their political role. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Red Guards played a central and highly influential role during the early years of the Cultural Revolution, acting as Mao Zedong's chosen agents to carry out his radical ideas. Composed mostly of teenagers and young adults from urban schools and universities, the Red Guards were mobilized by Mao to challenge and uproot perceived $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$capitalist roaders\" and enemies within the Chinese Communist Party. Mao's direct endorsement gave them the authority and zeal to confront established figures and institutions in the name of revolutionary purity. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nOne of their key missions was to attack the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Olds\": old customs, old culture, old habits, and old ideas. In pursuit of this goal, Red Guards launched campaigns to erase traditional Chinese heritage, including vandalizing temples, burning historical texts, destroying cultural relics, and renaming streets and institutions. These actions were driven by the belief that old cultural elements were obstacles to the progress of socialism and needed to be eradicated. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nBy 1969, the situation had grown increasingly unstable. As a result, Mao, along with key figures like Premier Zhou Enlai and military leaders such as Lin Biao, turned to the People's Liberation Army (PLA) to suppress the Red Guards and restore order. Many of the youth were sent to the countryside in the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Down to the Countryside Movement\" to be $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$re-educated\" by peasants, effectively removing them from urban political life and ending their revolutionary activism. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0764","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0764\n\nDescribe **three** ways the status and rights of women changed in the People's Republic of China between 1949 and 1976.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The Marriage Law of 1950 was a landmark reform. It outlawed arranged, forced, and child marriages, as well as concubinage, and gave women the right to divorce, radically challenging traditional Confucian family structures.\n\n - The CCP promoted gender equality in law, encouraging women to participate equally in work and society. Women were legally recognized as equal to men in the workplace and within the family. This was evident especially during the Great Leap Forward (1958-62), where their labor in agriculture and industry was seen as vital to economic growth.\n\n - The government invested in female literacy and education, but enrollment and progression for girls lagged behind boys, especially in less developed regions.\n\n - In practice, patriarchal norms persisted, particularly in rural areas. While women worked outside the home, they were still expected to perform traditional domestic roles, which was particularly evident during the Great Leap Forward.\n\n - During the Great Leap Forward, communal living was promoted through communal kitchens and childcare facilities. These were supposed to liberate women from duties at home so women could work in fields or factories. However, in practice, these systems often increased the burden on women rather than alleviating it:\n\n     - Communal kitchens were often poorly managed and at times lacked sufficient food, especially during the famine that followed. Women had to stand in long lines to get meager food rations, then sometimes still cook or find food for their families due to shortages. Rather than relieving the burden, this added time and stress, especially when kitchens were far from home.\n\n     - The childcare services were understaffed and poorly maintained. Infants and toddlers were often neglected or got sick easily, so mothers often had to check in frequently or take care of children after long workdays. This reduced the intended $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$freedom\" to work and added emotional and physical strain.\n\n - Women were expected to match men's labour output in agriculture and construction without adjustments for physical differences or family responsibilities. This $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equality\" in labour demands often meant double workloads, doing hard labor and then managing home tasks inadequately supported by the commune.\n\n - During the Cultural Revolution (1966-76), women were depicted as revolutionary comrades, often shown in propaganda wielding rifles or working in factories, but this was more symbolic than systemic, as women still faced structural limitations.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA key milestone in the advancement of status and rights for women was the Marriage Law of 1950, which fundamentally challenged traditional Confucian values that had long ruled family life. The law banned arranged, forced, and child marriages, as well as concubinage, and granted women the right to seek divorce. These reforms gave women unprecedented legal autonomy and aimed to redefine marriage as a partnership based on equality and consent. ($\\checkmark$) \n\nThe CCP strongly promoted gender equality as part of its broader revolutionary goals. Women were encouraged to take on roles outside the home and participate fully in the workforce and public life. Legally, they were granted equal rights to men in the workplace and within the family. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nHowever, despite these legal and ideological improvements, deeply rooted patriarchal attitudes continued to shape women’s daily lives. In rural areas, especially, traditional expectations remained strong. Women who worked full-time outside the home were still expected to fulfill domestic responsibilities, effectively leaving them with a double burden. As a result, the promise of equality often translated into heavier demands rather than genuine empowerment. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0765","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0765\n\nDescribe **three** ways the Chinese Communist Party attempted to improve health care in rural areas between 1949 and 1976.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The barefoot doctor program, introduced in the 1960s, trained peasants in basic medicine and sent them to rural areas. These workers provided primary health services, first aid, and hygiene education, reflecting Mao's goal of practical, community-based care.\n\n - Health care was decentralized and organized around communes. Each commune often had a clinic or cooperative health station, integrating health into collective farming life.\n\n - Mass public health campaigns were launched to combat widespread diseases. These included vaccinations, pest control (*e.g., the $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Pests Campaign”*), and improved sanitation, contributing to major reductions in infant mortality and infectious disease.\n\n - The state emphasized preventive care and hygiene, educating people on sanitation, nutrition, and disease prevention through posters, operas, and local propaganda.\n\n - Access to health care expanded significantly in rural regions where no services previously existed, improving life expectancy and basic health indicators.\n\nDespite improvements, the system lacked trained medical staff and modern equipment, especially outside urban centers. Most barefoot doctors received only a few months of training.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne of the most influential initiatives to improve rural health was the barefoot doctor program, introduced in the 1960s. Under this scheme, local peasants received short-term medical training and were sent to villages to provide essential health services. These $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$barefoot doctors\" offered basic treatments, administered first aid, and educated communities on hygiene and disease prevention. The program was a practical, low-cost solution rooted in Mao Zedong's vision of accessible, community-based care, emphasizing prevention over specialization. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nIn addition, health care delivery was decentralized and integrated into the commune system, which structured rural life during this period. Most communes had small clinics or cooperative health stations serving the collective population. This approach embedded medical services directly into the daily lives of rural workers, making health care more readily available in otherwise underserved areas. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThe CCP also launched nationwide public health campaigns to tackle major health threats. These mass mobilizations focused on improving sanitation, controlling pests, and vaccinating against common infectious diseases. Notable efforts included the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Pests Campaign,” which targeted disease-carrying animals like rats and mosquitoes. These campaigns played a crucial role in reducing rates of infectious diseases and infant mortality, laying the foundation for long-term improvements in public health. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0766","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0766\n\nAnalyse **three** reasons Deng Xiaoping emerged as the paramount leader after Mao's death.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with supporting evidence:\n\n - Removal of the Gang of Four. The Gang of Four were arrested in October 1976 by Hua Guofeng, with backing from senior military leaders like Ye Jianying. This action removed the most radical faction within the CCP, ended the Cultural Revolution, and created space for moderates and pragmatists like Deng to return, as the party rejected the chaos and ideological extremism associated with radical Maoism.\n\n - Deng was officially restored to his positions in July 1977, including Vice Premier and later Vice Chairman of the CCP. His rehabilitation marked the beginning of his political comeback.\n\n - Deng returned to power after being purged twice during the Cultural Revolution. He worked behind the scenes to build alliances with influential party officials, military leaders, and reform-minded bureaucrats, positioning himself as a unifier and pragmatic leader.\n\n - Deng's famous motto, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It doesn't matter whether a cat is black or white, as long as it catches mice,\" reflected his practical approach and ideological flexibility. He moved away from class struggle and ideological purity, focusing instead on economic growth and national rejuvenation.\n\n - Hua, Mao's designated successor, lacked personal authority and vision. His $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers” policy (upholding whatever Mao said and did) became increasingly unpopular, and Deng used this to argue for a break from the past. Deng gradually pushed Hua into the background by gaining control over key party bodies, like the Central Military Commission and the Organization Department, which oversees personnel appointments.\n\n - Deng championed the Four Modernizations (agriculture, industry, defense, and science/technology), which offered a clear alternative to the chaos of the Cultural Revolution. These policies helped win popular support and legitimacy among the elite.\n\n - Although Deng did not initially take top formal positions, he gradually took control of the military, the Central Committee, and the Organization Department. This gave him de facto power over party appointments and direction.\n\n - Deng reinstated many competent officials who had been removed during the Cultural Revolution, creating a loyal support base and restoring experienced leadership to the CCP.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nDeng Xiaoping's emergence as the paramount leader of China was facilitated initially by the removal of the Gang of Four. ($\\checkmark$) Their arrest in October 1976 eliminated the most radical faction within the CCP and marked the end of the Cultural Revolution. This allowed for the return of more moderate figures like Deng, as the party and the country moved away from the chaos and extremism associated with radical Maoism. ($\\checkmark$) \n\nDeng, who had been purged twice during the Cultural Revolution, carefully built alliances with influential party officials, military leaders, and reform-minded bureaucrats. ($\\checkmark$) His behind-the-scenes efforts positioned him as a unifying figure, with the political skills to gain support and ultimately reclaim power. ($\\checkmark$) \n\nAnother important factor was the weak leadership of Hua Guofeng. ($\\checkmark$) Hua, Mao's designated successor, struggled with a lack of personal authority and vision. His policy of adhering to Mao's legacy, the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers,\" became unpopular, and Deng capitalized on this by gaining control over key party institutions, gradually sidelining Hua and asserting his own leadership. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0769","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0769\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$  $`$Two Whatevers' Do Not Accord With Marxism” written by Deng Xiaoping, May 24, 1977. An excerpt from a talk with two leading comrades of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China.\n\n[Source: Deng, X. (1977, May 24) *`Two Whatevers' Do Not Accord With Marxism*. Retrieved May 14, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/deng-xiaoping/1977/124.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Rejection of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers\". \n\n     - Deng rejects blind loyalty to Mao's words and policies, arguing that it blocks reform and the fair reassessment of events like the 1976 Tiananmen Incident and his own rehabilitation.\n\n - Support for reassessing Mao's legacy. \n\n     - Deng encourages an honest evaluation of Mao, noting that even Mao admitted his mistakes, and suggesting the party should weigh both his successes and failures.\n\n - Promotion of true marxist principles. \n\n     - Deng stresses that genuine Marxism (historical materialism) requires flexibility and critical analysis, not rigid adherence to past statements.\n\n - Justification for reform. \n\n     - By opposing the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers,\" Deng lays the theoretical groundwork for his shift away from Maoist ideology and toward pragmatic reform.\n\n - Implicit criticism of Mao's rule. \n\n     - Without directly attacking Mao, Deng subtly challenges the excesses of Mao's later years by referencing his errors and fallibility.\n\n - Reassertion of Deng's authority. \n\n     - Deng uses this ideological stance to challenge Hua Guofeng's conservative leadership and position himself as the leader of post-Mao China.\n\n - Alignment with popular sentiment. \n\n     - In defending the 1976 Tiananmen protests, Deng shows support for public discontent with Maoist extremism and connects with the broader mood of the people.\n\n - Call to move beyond Mao's cult of personality. \n\n     - Deng urges the party to abandon idolization of Mao and adopt a more thoughtful form of Marxism based on reason, not reverence.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Source is a clear rejection of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers\" policy, which stated that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Whatever Chairman Mao said must be followed; whatever instructions he gave must be upheld.” Deng argues this principle is unacceptable and counterproductive, especially in the context of reform and the reevaluation of past events like the Tiananmen Incident of 1976 and his own political rehabilitation. ($\\checkmark$\n\n) Secondly, the Source expresses support for a critical reassessment of Mao's legacy. Deng emphasizes that Mao himself acknowledged making mistakes, and that no historical figure, including Marx, Engels, Lenin, or Stalin, was infallible. He invokes Mao's own saying that a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$70 percent achievements, 30 percent mistakes” judgment was fair, suggesting the party must honestly assess both Mao's successes and errors. ($\\checkmark$\n\n)  Finally, the Source contains an implicit criticism of Mao's later rule. While Deng maintains a respectful tone, he undermines the idea of Mao's infallibility, particularly by referencing Mao's own admission of mistakes. This subtly challenges the Cultural Revolution and other radical policies of Mao's later years, without outright condemning Mao as a whole. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0770","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0770\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about ideological tensions within the CCP after Mao's death?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$  $`$Two Whatevers' Do Not Accord With Marxism” written by Deng Xiaoping, May 24, 1977. An excerpt from a talk with two leading comrades of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of China.\n\n[Source: Deng, X. (1977, May 24) *`Two Whatevers' Do Not Accord With Marxism*. Retrieved May 14, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/deng-xiaoping/1977/124.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following\n\n - Reveals a divide between conservatives (pro-$\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Whatevers\") and reformists.\n\n - Speaker represents the reformist side, promoting critical reassessment of Maoism.\n\n - Suggests the ideological struggle is also about political legitimacy (e.g., Tiananmen 1976 protests).\n\n - Shows that control over Mao's legacy was central to the post-Mao power struggle.\n\n - Reveals debates about historical interpretation and leadership accountability.\n\n - Implies internal debate within the Party leadership, not unified consensus.\n\n - Emphasizes the role of Marxist theory (historical materialism) as a battleground for legitimacy.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B reveals a clear ideological divide within the CCP between conservatives, who supported the $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*Two Whatevers\"* and continued strict adherence to Mao's teachings, and reformists like Deng Xiaoping, who promoted a more critical and flexible approach to Mao's legacy. Deng's rejection of the $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*Two Whatevers”* shows his belief that dogmatic loyalty was holding back progress and reform. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, the Source suggests that these ideological debates were tied to political legitimacy. Deng defends the 1976 Tiananmen protests, previously condemned by Maoists, as justified, using this to legitimize his own political comeback and challenge the verdicts of the Cultural Revolution. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, the source shows that control over Mao's legacy was central to the power struggle. Deng is not rejecting Mao entirely but is arguing for a more nuanced, realistic view, which helped pave the way for post-Mao reform and modernization. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0771","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0771\n\nTo what extent was the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution a victory for Mao but a disaster for the Chinese people?\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\nA high-scoring response should:\n\n - Demonstrate a clear understanding of the question's demands.\n\n - Clearly address how Mao used the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution (GPCR) for his own political purposes, and the extent to which he was successful.\n\n - Evaluate the effects of the GPCR on the Chinese people in terms of social, cultural, and economic impacts.\n\n - Use a well-structured argument with relevant historical examples and differing perspectives integrated into the answer.\n\nStudents could address the following content:\n\n - The context of position after the failures of the Great Leap Forward\n\n     - The Great Leap Forward (1958-62) was a catastrophic failure, leading to widespread famine and the deaths of 30-55 million people.\n\n     - Mao's credibility within the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) was seriously damaged; he was forced to take a back seat in policy-making.\n\n     - Pragmatic leaders like Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping implemented economic recovery measures, promoting a moderate and practical government.\n\n     - Mao viewed their reforms as an ideological betrayal of revolutionary communism and feared the rise of a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$new elite” within the Party.\n\n     - By the mid-1960s, Mao sought to reassert dominance and revive class struggle, launching the Cultural Revolution (1966-76) as a political and ideological offensive.\n\n - Mao's political goals and actions, and their success \n\n     - Mao's objective to reclaim dominance in the CCP following the rise of pragmatic leaders like Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping.\n\n     - Use of Red Guards, youths radicalized and mobilized to attack perceived enemies of Maoist ideology.\n\n     - Purging of political rivals and destruction of bureaucratic structures to weaken opposition.\n\n     - Reinforcement of Maoist thought through propaganda, the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Little Red Book\", and the creation of a cult of personality.\n\n     - Consequences for political institutions: paralysis of state machinery and decision-making replaced by ideological chaos.\n\n - Social and cultural impact on the Chinese people\n\n     - Widespread violence and persecution: intellectuals, teachers, and even ordinary citizens were humiliated, tortured, or killed.\n\n     - Systematic destruction of China's cultural heritage under the banner of eliminating the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Olds\".\n\n     - Schools and universities shut down; education was halted, creating a “lost generation” of uneducated youth.\n\n     - Breakdown of families and social trust as individuals were encouraged to denounce even relatives.\n\n     - Psychological consequences: fear, betrayal, and deep emotional scars that persisted long after the movement ended.\n\n - Economic consequences\n\n     - Sharp decline in productivity due to work stoppages, chaos in factories, and attacks on skilled professionals.\n\n     - Agriculture and industry neglected while political loyalty was prioritized over economic management.\n\n     - Disruption of central planning institutions and loss of technical expertise.\n\n     - Little to no innovation or economic growth during the decade; stagnation compared to earlier reforms.\n\n     - Contrasted with the economic recovery and modernization that followed under Deng Xiaoping.\n\n - The failure of Mao's long-term legacy\n\n     - After Mao's death in 1976, Deng Xiaoping rose to power and swiftly dismantled many of Mao's policies.\n\n     - Cultural Revolution leaders like the Gang of Four were arrested and publicly condemned.\n\n     - Mao's vision of continuous revolution was replaced by a pragmatic, reform-oriented agenda.\n\n     - Institutions purged during the Revolution were restored, and purged individuals were rehabilitated.\n\n     - Mao's extremism was gradually discredited, and while his image remained, his ideological goals were not sustained.\n\nPossible approaches to the essay:\n\n - A balanced perspective\n\n     - Acknowledge Mao's temporary political success in silencing opposition and regaining control.\n\n     - Emphasize the long-term failure to secure a lasting ideological legacy.\n\n     - Argue that while it was a short-term political $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$victory” for Mao, it was overwhelmingly a disaster for the Chinese people.\n\n - Emphasis on Mao's political victory\n\n     - Focus on Mao's consolidation of power and elimination of rivals.\n\n     - Argue that from a purely personal or political perspective, Mao succeeded.\n\n     - Downplay or view the suffering of the population as a consequence Mao was willing to accept for his goals.\n\n - Emphasis on disaster for the people\n\n     - Prioritize the immense social, economic, and cultural costs.\n\n     - Portray Mao's gains as short-lived or superficial.\n\n     - Conclude that no political $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$victory” can justify the human suffering caused.\n\n - Symmetry/contrast approach\n\n     - Divide the essay into two major sections: the extent of Maoss success, followed by the extent of the disaster for the people.\n\n     - This allows for direct comparison and mirrors the structure of the question.\n\n - Thematic structure\n\n     - Organize the body paragraphs around themes:\n\n         - Political impact (victory for Mao)\n\n         - Social and cultural consequences (disaster for people)\n\n         - Economic impact (largely negative for people)\n\n         - Ideological legacy (failure for Mao)\n\n     - In each theme, assess the extent of both Mao's success and the cost to the people.\n\nStrong responses should return to the central question and weigh the short-term benefits Mao may have gained against the long-term consequences for the nation. The most convincing answers will acknowledge that Mao achieved a degree of control and influence, but at a devastating cost that ultimately undermined his vision and caused immense suffering for millions of Chinese people.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao Zedong launched the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution (1966-1976) as a political campaign to reassert his ideological control and purge $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionist” elements within the Chinese Communist Party (CCP). ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) While it initially succeeded in reviving Mao's authority and reinforcing his revolutionary ideology, it inflicted immense social, cultural, and economic damage on the Chinese population and ultimately failed in its political goal as Mao's legacy was not assured and shortly after his death, pragmatism replaced ideological purity. Thus, to a large extent, the Cultural Revolution was only a limited political victory for Mao and a profound disaster for the Chinese people. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$)\n\nMao used the Cultural Revolution to successfully reassert his personal authority over the CCP and marginalize political rivals. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) By 1966, Mao's influence had waned after the failures of the Great Leap Forward. Leaders such as Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping had assumed greater control and implemented pragmatic economic reforms. ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{knowledge}$) Through the Cultural Revolution, Mao mobilized the Red Guards (youthful revolutionary groups) to denounce party officials, destroy the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Olds,” and challenge bureaucratic authority. This allowed Mao to purge rivals like Liu and Deng. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) The reassertion of Maoist ideology through mass mobilization and ideological fervor reaffirmed his supremacy at the top of the CCP hierarchy. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) However, this consolidation of power came at the cost of political stability and the effective functioning of government institutions. The party structure was paralyzed, and decision-making became chaotic. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) While Mao triumphed politically, the disorder caused long-term fragmentation within the party and created a leadership vacuum after his death. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)\n\nFor the Chinese people, the Cultural Revolution was socially and culturally devastating. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) Red Guards attacked teachers, intellectuals, and perceived $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$class enemies,” leading to widespread persecution, torture, and death. Traditional cultural artifacts, temples, and texts were destroyed in a campaign against the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four Olds.” Education systems were disrupted, with schools and universities closed for years, depriving an entire generation of formal learning. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) The movement encouraged suspicion, betrayal, and chaos in families and communities, eroding trust in social relationships. While Mao argued that this struggle would lead to greater ideological purity, in reality, it unleashed violent class-based campaigns with little legal protection for the accused. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) The trauma of public $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$struggle sessions” and the breakdown of traditional values left lasting scars on Chinese society. These outcomes show that, despite ideological intent, the social cost of the Cultural Revolution was enormous. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)\n\nEconomically, the Cultural Revolution caused significant disruption and regression. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) Industrial output declined sharply during the early years of the movement due to widespread work stoppages and purging of skilled professionals. In rural areas, agricultural production was neglected as Red Guards and revolutionary committees prioritized political activism over agricultural work. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) Although some economic recovery occurred later in the 1970s, the decade-long turmoil undermined earlier efforts to develop a stable socialist economy. There was no significant technological progress or productivity growth during this period. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) The temporary dismantling of planning institutions and the targeting of specialists and managers further weakened China's economic foundations. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) Compared to the moderate economic successes achieved under Liu and Deng in the early 1960s, the Cultural Revolution represented a dramatic reversal. This demonstrates that while Mao may have viewed political revolution as primary, the economic well-being of the people suffered gravely. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)\n\nWhile Mao achieved a short-term political triumph, the Cultural Revolution ultimately failed to secure his long-term ideological legacy. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) After Mao's death in 1976, the CCP quickly shifted course under Deng Xiaoping, prioritizing economic reform and opening to the global market over continued class struggle. Mao's radical ideology was effectively abandoned as China transitioned towards pragmatism and modernization. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) This shift is evident in the rehabilitation of many purged officials, the restoration of educational institutions, and the dismantling of communes. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) The fact that Deng, once purged by Mao, became the architect of modern China highlights how thoroughly Mao's revolutionary vision was overturned. While Mao had succeeded in dominating the party during his lifetime, his inability to establish a lasting ideological framework meant that his political victory was brief. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$) The reversal of his policies and the rejection of Cultural Revolution ideals in the post-Mao era demonstrate that his ultimate goal, to entrench Maoist thought as the guiding principle of Chinese socialism, was not achieved. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)\n\nIn conclusion, while Mao Zedong achieved a short-term political victory through the Cultural Revolution by crushing opposition and reviving his authority, he ultimately failed to secure the ideological legacy he sought. The movement proved to be a profound disaster for the Chinese people, marked by social upheaval, cultural destruction, economic decline, and human suffering. Although the revolution initially reaffirmed Mao's ideological dominance, it did so at an immense cost to China's development and stability. After his death, the rapid shift toward pragmatism and reform further underlined the failure of Mao's long-term goals. On balance, the Cultural Revolution can be viewed as a temporary political success for Mao, but a human, economic, and national tragedy for China. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)"},{"ref":"HI0772","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0772\n\nDescribe **three** key features of China's First 5-year plan from 1953 to 1957.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The economy was directed by a centralized command system managed by teh State Planning Commission.\n\n - The government aimed to create heavy industry in China, which they believed was the foundation for a socialist society. The government increased the production of coal and steel. By 1957, 115 million tonnes of coal were produced, and 5.35 million tonnes of steel were produced. \n\n - The government focused on developing the country's transportation infrastructure, such as constructing the Yangzi River Bridge at Nanjing.\n\n - The Soviet Union sent thousands of technical specialists to support industrial development. The government started 694 industrial projects, of which 150 industrial plants were built with Soviet aid.\n\n - The government created state-owned enterprises by nationalising or merging private enterprises with existing ones.\n\n - The government focused on industrial development by extracting resources from the agricultural sector, such as stockpiling food supplies by requiring peasants to sell grain at extremely low prices. \n\n - Government budget focused on industrial development, while less funding was allocated to social welfare and defence. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe First 5-year plan of the PRC from 1953 to 1957 focused on industrial development. The government focused on developing the country's industry by cooperating with the Soviet Union, in which 150 of the 694 industrial projects were assisted by the Soviet Union ($\\checkmark$). The government also abolished private enterprises and created state-owned enterprises to centralise industrial production ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the government also directed its budget to expand on the country’s transportation infrastructure, such as the construction of the Nanjing Yangzi River Bridge ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0773","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0773\n\nDescribe **two** methods used by the Communist Party to restore financial stability in China after the Chinese Civil War.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The government banned the circulation of foreign currencies.\n\n - The supply of paper money was reduced to control inflation.\n\n - The government created a new unified monetary system to centralize government control, such as tightening government budget and credit control. \n\n - A centralized banking system was created, the banking industry was nationalized, and only the People's Bank of China could operate in the country. \n\n - The wage-point system was introduced as a new unified wage system for workers based on the price of basic items such as rice and oil. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe PRC inherited a country that was ravaged by the Second Sino-Japanese War and the Chinese Civil War, which left the country with a severely disrupted economy that included hyperinflation and low industrial and agricultural output. The government first controlled inflation by banning the circulation of foreign currencies and reducing the supply of paper money ($\\checkmark$). This was followed by creating a new unified monetary system and a centralized banking system to tighten the control of the government budget and credit ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0774","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0774\n\nExplain **two** consequences of land reform on China's countryside during the 1st and 2nd 5-year Plans.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Destruction of the landlords\n\n     - The Agrarian Reform Law was passed, in which land formerly owned by landlords was distributed to peasants.\n\n     - Landlords and rich peasants were denounced in public trials and some were summarily executed in the countryside by party cadres.\n\n - Collectivisation\n\n     - Peasants were put into agricultural producers cooperatives in which peasants pooled resources and worked together on the same land.\n\n     - Large cooperative farms under state control were constructed, in which all peasants collectively own the land.\n\n     - By the end of 1956 over 96$\\%$ of peasant households were in collective farms. \n\n - People's Communes \n\n     - The People's Communes were created as part of the government's attempt to centralise the control of resources for industrialisation.\n\n     - By 1958, over 98$\\%$ of the farming population resided in communes. \n\n     - The People's Communes administration controlled the commune's agricultural and industrial resources.\n\n     - The communes provided welfare for the peasants and workers by operating schools, nurseries and public kitchens. \n\n     - The production teams within the commune were organized in a military fashion to raise output. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe first and foremost impact was the destruction of the landlord class that dominated China for over a millennium. This was achieved by the Agrarian Reform Law, which confiscated the landlord's land and redistributed it to peasants ($\\checkmark$). Also, when the Communist Party took over the countryside, the party cadres organized public trials to denounce landlords and rich peasants, in which some of them were executed ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAfter the destruction of the landlords and the land redistributed, the government put peasants into large cooperative farms that were under state control, and by the end of 1956, over 96$\\%$ of the peasant population were in collective farms ($\\checkmark$). Inside the state-owned collective farms were the People's Communes, responsible for managing the resources and labor along military lines to raise agricultural output to meet the state quota ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0775","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0775\n\nExplain **two** consequences on China's economic development from the Sino-Soviet cooperation up to 1959.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Financial support from the Soviet Union.\n\n     - The Soviet Union provided a loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$60 million annually starting in 1950. \n\n     - The loan included the construction of railways, industrial infrastructure, and materials for reconstruction. \n\n - Provision of Soviet specialists \n\n     - The Soviet Union provided up to 15000 technical advisors to China to support their industrial enterprises in 1954.\n\n     - The Soviet Union assisted China in building its industrial infrastructure through the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$156 Projects,” which involved developing industrial plants in different Chinese cities. Some examples are the Anshan Steel Plant and Nanjing Machine Tool Plant.\n\n     - The Soviet Union also assisted China in expanding its rail network, such as the Lanzhou-Xinjiang Railway, which connects Northwestern China, and the Trans-Siberian Railway in Central Asia. \n\n - Transfer of ownership of industrial plants and resources in Northeastern China\n\n     - The Soviet Union returned some of their industrial resources from the Japanese that were previously based in Manchuria to the Chinese government.\n\n     - The Soviet Union renounced its rights to use Port Arthur, which was previously leased by the Russian Empire.\n\n     - The Soviet Union returned the ownership of the Chinese Eastern Railway in 1953\n\n - Nuclear development\n\n     - The Soviet Union provided nuclear energy engineers to assist China's development in nuclear research.\n\n     - By 1958, nuclear energy development had become a top priority for the country. \n\n     - The Chinese government cooperated with the Soviet Union to establish the China Institute of Atomic Energy, which focused on designing nuclear power plants and nuclear fuel production. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nDuring the first decade of the PRC's establishment, the Soviet Union offered various forms of assistance to the PRC to assist in its economic development. During this period, the Soviet Union provided financial support by offering an annual loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$60 million ($\\checkmark$). The loan also included the construction of railways, industrial infrastructure, and reconstruction materials ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIn addition to providing financial support, the Soviet Union also sent technical specialists to assist in China's industrial development and economic recovery ($\\checkmark$). For example, the Chinese government embarked on the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$156 Projects” that aimed to develop industrial plants in different major cities with Soviet technical assistance, one of these examples being the Anshan Steel Plant ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0776","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0776\n\nExplain **two** causes for the failure of the Great Leap Forward in 1958.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Four Pests Campaign\n\n     - The aim of the campaign is to eliminate rats, flies, mosquitoes and sparrows.\n\n     - The government asked the people to kill any wild birds such as sparrows that ate crop seeds. \n\n     - The killing of sparrows led to an increase in locust and insect population that destroyed crops. \n\n - Lysenkoism\n\n     - The government was influenced by Lysenkoism and made it an official policy in 1958. Lysenkoism consisted of techniques that would increase rice and wheat production. However, Lysenko's theories were worthless and unsuitable for farming.\n\n     - Some of the methods in Lysenkoism such as deep ploughing only hindered the yields in China.\n\n - Failure of the backyard furnaces\n\n     - Most of the usable steel were only produced in industrial plants\n\n     - The steel produced in the backyard furnaces were of poor quality.\n\n     - Despite the poor quality steel being made, the local authorities did not report the problem and still continued to produce them to reach the quotas designated by the central government.\n\n - Role of local government officials\n\n     - Many government officials covered up the low agricultural output and the famine, fearing that they might be purged. Instead, the officials lied to the central government that production targets were being met. \n\n     - Some local officials even placed crops alongside railway tracks when Mao's train passed through during his inspection. \n\n     - Anyone that dared speak up against the policies imposed by the government were denounced as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rightist”, as a result, no one dared to speak up despite the failures.\n\n - Natural Disasters\n\n     - The Yellow River flooded in 1958 and affected the Henan and Shandong Region, in which over half a million acres of cultivated fields were destroyed. \n\n     - To help with the flooded areas, the officials assembled a rescue team consisting of farm labourers, which meant that the crops were left unattended and were left to rot. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Leap Forward resulted in an estimated 30 million people dead in China, making it one of the most devastating famines in modern history. The reason for the failure of the Great Leap Forward could be due to poor planning by local government officials combined with the natural disasters in 1958. Many local government officials during the Great Leap Forward were concerned with meeting the quotas assigned by the central government and therefore exaggerated the production result ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the local government officials did not dare speak up against the policies as anyone that went against the farming methods assigned by the state would be denounced as $\\checkmark$Rightists” or “Class Enemies” ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAnother factor in the failure of the Great Leap Forward was the Yellow River Flood in 1958, which flooded over a million acres of cultivated land ($\\checkmark$). The mismanagement of the flood only worsened the Great Leap Forward when officials diverted 2 million farmers from nearby provinces for the relief effort, which left the crops unattended and rotten, further decreasing the already low agricultural output ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0777","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0777\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the Land Reforms in China?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  “The life of the peasants is good after Land Reform” by Jin Meisheng, published in 1951. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/The%20People%E2%80%99s%20Republic%20of%20China/images/HI0777.jpg\" alt=\"HI0777\">\n[Source: Jin, M.S. (1951). *The life of the peasants is good after Land Reform.* Chineseposters.net. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://chineseposters.net/gallery/e15-595. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The land reforms improved the living standard of peasants.\n- Mao Zedong was shown as an influential figure behind the land reforms based on the portrait in the poster.\n- The peasants are shown to be well-dressed and well-fed.\n- The poster shows that the agricultural produce was plentiful.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source shows that the land reforms brought improvements to peasants' living conditions in the countryside ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the source also seems to suggest that Mao was highly revered based on the portrait that was displayed at the center ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0778","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0778\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the role of private enterprises and capitalists in the country?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from Mao Zedong's work titled “The only road for the transformation of capitalist industry and commerce.” Published on September 7, 1953. |\n\n[Source: Mao, Z.D. (1953, September). *The only road for the transformation of capitalist industry and commerce.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-5/mswv5_33.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Private enterprises should not be dismissed from state capitalism.\n- Further study of private enterprises is required\n- Private enterprises are an asset to the state and play a large part in the economy.\n- Capitalists should be educated in patriotism.\n- Capitalists could be trained to support the Communist Party.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that private enterprises are an asset to the state since they could play an important part in the economy ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, the source also mentions that capitalists could be educated in patriotism ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, the source suggests that further study of the role of private enterprises in the state's economic development should be conducted ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0779","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0779\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the economic outcomes during the Great Leap Forward.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech at the 6th Plenum of the Eighth Central Committee (December 19, 1958). |\n\n[Source: Mao, Z.D. (1958, December). Speech at the Sixth Plenum of the Eighth Central Committee. Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-8/mswv8_23.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The source comes from Mao himself, who was involved in most of China's economic planning in the 1950s.\n- The content shows Mao's opinion on China's economic development during the 1st and 2nd 5-year plans.\n- The content of the sources gives some data on China's economic development at that time.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The purpose of the speech is to sway the Communist Party members to support Mao's policies further; therefore, the language might be exaggerated, and the statistics exaggerated.\n- The content of the source does not provide further information on the steel production other than the data mentioned in his speech.\n- The content only addresses events up until 1958, while the events under the 2nd 5-year plan are still unfolding.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable as the speech is directly from Mao himself, who played a significant role behind China's 5-year plans through the 1950s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The source's content also shows Mao's opinion on the 1st and 2nd 5-year plan ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the source also comes with limitations. Since the purpose of the speech is to rally more support within the Communist Party, the language might be emotive and the statistics used in the speech might be exaggerated ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Moreover, the content does not provide more information about the outcome while the events under the 2nd 5-year plan were still taking place ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0780","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0780\n\nWhat does Source D suggest about the communes' impact on the peasants during the Great Leap Forward?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the Memoirs of a Chinese Marshal - The Autobiographical Notes of Peng Dehuai (1898-1974). The autobiography was written in response during the interrogations he faced during the Cultural Revolution. In this extract, Peng Dehuai recalled his meeting at the Lushan Conference in 1959 that was called to review the Great Leap Forward. |\n\n[Source: Peng, D.H. (1984). *Memories of a Chinese Marshal.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/peng-dehuai/1984/memoirspengdehuai.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The cadres wrongly believed that the communes were already considered an achievement of Communism.\n- The peasants suffered in the people’s commune.\n- The cadres were blindly collectivised from the peasants.\n- Peasant households had to resort to selling their property to survive.\n- The cadres exaggerated their achievements when reporting to the higher-level authorities.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source shows peasants suffered greatly in the communes during the Great Leap Forward ($\\checkmark$). The peasants suffered because the commune cadres collectivised aggressively from the peasants ($\\checkmark$). To make matters worse, the cadres exaggerated their achievements of the communes when reporting to the higher-level authorities ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0781","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0781\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source D for an historian studying about the effects of the Great Leap Forward.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the Memoirs of a Chinese Marshal - The Autobiographical Notes of Peng Dehuai (1898-1974). The autobiography was written in response during the interrogations he faced during the Cultural Revolution. In this extract, Peng Dehuai recalled his meeting at the Lushan Conference in 1959 that was called to review the Great Leap Forward. |\n\n[Source: Peng, D.H. (1984). *Memories of a Chinese Marshal.* Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/peng-dehuai/1984/memoirspengdehuai.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of two marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The origin of the source is written by Peng Dehuai himself, who was the defence minister of China and also a vocal critic of Mao's Great Leap Forward.\n- The content shows Peng's observation of the dismal conditions in the countryside during his tour around China.\n- The purpose of the source is to express his frustration towards the poor planning and execution of the Great Leap Forward. The extract reflects his emotion and opinion of the state of the countryside.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The memoir only showed Peng's dissatisfaction on the Great Leap Forward and did not mention other outcomes of the campaign or other party official's perspective.\n- Peng wrote the autobiography during the Cultural Revolution as a response to his interrogation. This might suggest that the content may be affected by his emotion during his trial.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable since it was written by Peng Dehuai himself, who was a vocal critic of Mao's Great Leap Forward policy and personally witnessed the disaster in the countryside when he toured around different provinces ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The content and the purpose of the source is also a value because it shows Peng's emotions and insight on the outcomes of the Great Leap Forward that were unfolding in the countryside ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC/VP}$).\n\nThe source has its limitations. In terms of the content, the memoir only showed Peng's dissatisfaction and his observations during the Great Leap Forward, other outcomes were not mentioned ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). Moreover, the origins could also be limited since Peng wrote this during the Cultural Revolution when he was subjected to continuous interrogation, in which his emotion and mental state might affect the accuracy of the memoir ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$)."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"HI0101","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0101\n\nDescribe **two** reasons for strained relations between the USSR and China during the time of Stalin's rule.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Limited Soviet support to the CCP during the Chinese Civil War.\n- Ideological differences with Maoism's emphasis on the role of the peasants instead of the proletariat.\n- Territorial disputes like the port of Vladivostok which had been taken by imperial Russia from China during the era of imperialism.\n- Lack of the USSR's participation in the Korean War in defence of Communism while Stalin did pressure Mao into sending troops\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSino-Soviet relations were strained from the start of the People's Republic of China. One stressor on the relationship was that the USSR favoured a united front against Japan instead of supporting the CCP during the Chinese Civil War ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Soviet Communism and Chinese Communism were different ideologically with Mao having adapted Communism through the realities of China ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, Mao was suspicious of the USSR`s motives in pressuring him to participate in the Korean War while the USSR took the time to rebuild itself after WWII ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0102","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0102\n\nExplain **three** reasons for the Sino-Soviet split.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny of the following **three** reasons and their explanations:\n- Ideological differences - any or all of the following:\n    - Role of the peasants: Mao felt that the peasants should be the ones to drive the Communist revolution forward while Stalin was more orthodox in expecting the proletariat to be the drivers of revolution. This was significant in that the Soviet Union stressed the need to focus revolutionary activity on the cities while Mao favoured the creation of rural bases during the long Chinese Civil War.\n    - Peaceful coexistence: Khruschev thought the USSR and the US could coexist peacefully despite having different ideologies while Mao thought it was a duty to advance the cause of Communism in the world.\n    - Cult of personality: Khruschev criticised the cult of the leader done under Stalin's rule while Mao used the cult of personality to consolidate his own power.\n- USSR's position in the Korean War: The USSR supported North Korea with material aid only.\n- USSR's position in the war between India and China: the USSR did not even diplomatically condemn India in its border conflict with China\n- Mao's idea that the USSR wanted to keep China as a junior partner: Mao resented receiving Soviet indications on how to achieve the victory of the Revolution during the period of the Chinese Civil War as well as Soviet indications on how to achieve development according to the Soviet model of the 5 Year Plans.\n- The Soviet Union's limited support in helping the Chinese build a nuclear weapon.\n- Soviet attempts to limit Mao's aggressive actions during the Taiwan Strait Crises in 1954-55 and 1958.\n\\\n [6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of the most important reasons for the Sino-Soviet split were ideological differences ($\\checkmark$). For example, Mao believed in the necessity of expanding the Revolution despite the cost and rejected Khrushchev's idea of “peaceful coexistence” with capitalist states ($\\checkmark$).  Mao also identified Khruschev as a “revisionist” deviating from true Leninism with his criticism of the cult of personality during Stalin's time ($\\checkmark$), a cult of personality which Mao greatly used in China to inspire the people to achieve the goals of the revolution and to consolidate his own power ($\\checkmark$).\n\nMao was also angered by limited Soviet support in building a Chinese nuclear weapon as well as during the Taiwan Strait Crises ($\\checkmark$). He was frustrated by  the USSR trying to restrain the CCP during the Taiwan Strait Crises because  Mao saw the opportunity to  achieve success for the Revolution in the whole of Chinese territory ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0103","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0103\n\nExplain **two** reasons for the improvement of Sino-American relations in the 1970's.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following ideas with their evidence:\n- Sino-Soviet split: border clashes in 1969 signalled to the world, including the US, that the Communist block was not monolithic and it was to negotiate with its separate members.\n- Fear of Soviet expansion: the Brezhnev Doctrine of 1968 stated that the USSR could intervene in any socialist state to “protect socialism”. This had been applied in Czechoslovakia in 1968 to quell the Prague Spring.\n- To put an end to the Vietnam war: The US had been in a state of war against North Vietnam since 1964 and there was no victory in sight in part because the People's Republic of China supported North Vietnam.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth the US and the People's Republic of China feared Soviet expansionism after the declaration of the Brezhnev Doctrine in 1968. The Brezhnev doctrine stated that the USSR could intervene in any socialist state to “protect socialism” ($\\checkmark$). This could mean further Soviet occupation of Eastern Europe, like it had done in Czechoslovakia and possible intervention in China. China at the time was going through its Cultural Revolution and Mao feared Soviet intervention to put an end to the upheavals it had caused ($\\checkmark$). The US was also eager to prevent Soviet expansionism and to negotiate with China a possible solution to the Vietnam War ($\\checkmark$). There was domestic conflict because of the protracted war and Nixon had promised in his campaign to put an honorable end to US participation in Vietnam. For that, it needed to negotiate with North Vietnam's neighbour and main supplier, China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0105","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0105\n\nList **two** of the people responsible for the improvement of Sino-American relations in the 1970s and describe their role.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAward only one mark if only names are listed with no description of role.\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Mao: leader of the People's Republic of China and one of the signatories of the Shanghai Communique.\n- Nixon: president of the US and one of the signatories of the Shanghai Communique.\n- Henry Kissinger: US Secretary of State in the 1970`s. He visited China  to iron out the difficulties in the establishment of relations between the US and the People's Republic of China prior to the visit of US president Nixon.\n- Zhou Enlai: Prime Minister of China from 1949-1976. He met with Henry Kissinger prior to the visit of US president Nixon to iron out the difficulties in the establishment of relations between the US and the People's Republic of China.\n- Glenn Cowan: member of the US Table Tennis team. His participation in the World Table Tennis Championship in 1971 and friendly encounter with Chinese counterpart Zhuang Zedong sparked the start of diplomatic relations between the 2 countries.\n- Zhuang Zedong: member of the People's Republic of China`s Table Tennis team. His participation in the World Table Tennis Championship in 1971 and friendly encounter with American counterpart Glenn Cowan sparked the start of diplomatic relations between the 2 countries.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMao Zedong and Richard Nixon ($\\checkmark$) were the leaders of the People's Republic of China and of the US respectively and they met to sign the Shanghai Communique reopening diplomatic relations between the countries while improving the security of Taiwan ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0106","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0106\n\nAccording to the source, who were **two** enemies of the People's Republic of China?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from Press Communiqué of the Tenth National Congress of the Communist Party of China, August 29th, 1973.\n\n[Source: Tenth National Congress of the Communist Party of China (1973, August 29th). *Press Communique*. Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved October 6th, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/subject/china/documents/cpc/press_commu.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The USA\n- The USSR\n- social-imperialism\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source A, two enemies of China are the USA ($\\checkmark$) and the USSR ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0108","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0108\n\nUsing Source B and your own knowledge, explain how the issue of “Two Chinas” was a problem for the normalization of Sino-American relations during the Cold War.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the Joint Statement Following Discussions With Leaders of the People’s Republic of China (also known as the Shanghai Communiqué). February 27th, 1972.\n\n[Source: *Joint Statement Following Discussion With Leaders of the People's Republic of China*. (1972, February 27th). Office of the Historian. Retrieved October 6th, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v17/d203. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n\nUse of source:\n- Source B explains the US view of the issue as a Chinese problem and recognizes the unity of the Chinese nation.\n- Source B shows there are US military forces in Taiwan.\n\nOwn knowledge:\n- The US recognized Taiwan's government as the legitimate government of all of China from 1949 until 1979.\n- There were 2 crises in the 1950's involving the strait of Taiwan in which the US provided military support to the Taiwanese side.\n- The situation of Taiwan was first dealt with diplomatically in the Shanghai Communiquè where Mao and Nixon agreed to the idea of “one China”.\n- In 1979, the United States recognized the PRC and established diplomatic relations with it as the sole legitimate government of China. The United States then withdrew its diplomatic recognition of the government of Taiwan but continues to support it militarily.\n\nTo achieve full marks, there must be a reference to source in the answer.  References must be explicit, either by identifying the name of the source or its letter.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom the end of the Chinese Civil War in 1949 the Chinese nation was ruled by 2 governments, one in Beijing controlling what is known as the People's Republic of China and one in Taiwan. Both presumed to rule over the whole Chinese nation and in that dispute, the US sided with the government of Taiwan and even stationed troops there as seen in Source B ($\\checkmark\\,  \\text{OK and sources}$). In the 1950s, the desire of the People's Republic of China to recover Taiwan had led to crises in which the US had sided with the Taiwanese and committed itself to protecting Taiwan from an invasion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{OK}$).  We can see in Source B that this issue was dealt with in the Shanghai Communiquè where both sides agreed to recognize the existence of only one China ($\\checkmark$ sources)."},{"ref":"HI0109","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0109\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of the source for a historian studying Sino-American relations.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{4em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from Press Communiqué of the Tenth National Congress of the Communist Party of China, August 29th, 1973.\n\n[Source: Tenth National Congress of the Communist Party of China (1973, August 29th). Press Communique. Marxists Internet Archive. Retrieved October 6th, 2024 from https://www.marxists.org/subject/china/documents/cpc/press_commu.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nIf only values or limitations are discussed, the student cannot get more than **two** marks. There must be at least one reference to origin, purpose and content in either value or limitations to reach **four** marks.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n |  | $\\ce{VALUE}$ |$\\ce{LIMITATIONS}$|\n | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| ORIGIN |The source is made in 1973 when Sino-American relations are starting to change so it has insight into their development. |It is a press communique of the CCP's congress so it limits itself to the official perspective of the CCP. $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202024-12-22%20at%2019.06.3.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-22 at 19.06.3\">It lacks the benefit of hindsight so it does not know the impact of this meeting in future events.|\n| PURPOSE| To inform the people of the current international situation and China's position. Since the objective is to inform, we can expect the information to be at least in part accurate.  | To persuade the population that China is in danger and they must be ready to defend it from imperialism. Since the objective is to persuade, we can expect some information to be left out and others to be exaggerated so as to convince the population. $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202024-12-22%20at%2019.06.35.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-22 at 19.06.35\">To give a favorable image of China as the one who is on the same side as the “oppressed people and nations of the whole world.”|\n| CONTENT | Identifies China's enemies as the US, the USSR. $\\\\$<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202024-12-22%20at%2019.06.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-22 at 19.06\"> Identifies China's main worry at the time that was a “surprise attack by social-imperialism” referring to the USSR.|It exaggerates when it implies there was “unity with all the countries subjected to imperialist aggression” and China. $\\\\$<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202024-12-22%20at%2019.06..png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-22 at 19.06.\"> It ignores the positive results of ping-pong diplomacy and the better relationship with the US.|\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A is valuable in its origin since it is from 1973 and so has insight into the new relationship between the US and China ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, precisely because it is from 1973, it lacks hindsight and does not yet know about the official establishment of diplomatic relations with the US in 1979 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nIts purpose is valuable because it is a press release so it wants to inform the population of the conclusion of the Party Congress ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). However, the purpose is also limited since it seeks to persuade the population that China is in danger and they must be ready to defend it ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$).\n\nThe content is valuable since it identifies China’s main enemies, the US and the USSR ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the content is also limited as it leaves out the fact that the relationship with the US is changing and improving ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0110","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0110\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of the source for a historian studying Sino-American relations.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the Joint Statement Following Discussions With Leaders of the People's Republic of China (also known as the Shanghai Communiqué). February 27th, 1972.\n\n[Source: Joint Statement Following Discussion With Leaders of the People's Republic of China  (1972, February 27th). Office of the Historian. Retrieved October 6th, 2024 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v17/d203. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nIf only values or limitations are discussed, the student cannot get more than **two** marks. There must be at least one reference to origin, purpose and content in either value or limitations to reach **four** marks.\n$\\\\$\n |  | $\\ce{VALUE}$ |$\\ce{LIMITATIONS}$|\n | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| ORIGIN | The source is made in 1973 when Sino-American relations are starting to change so it has insight into their development. | It lacks the benefit of hindsight so it does not know the impact of this meeting in future events.|\n| PURPOSE| To inform what the powers agree on. Since the objective is to inform, we can expect the information to be at least in part accurate.| |\n| CONTENT | Demonstrates the willingness to have a diplomatic relationship and some of the agreements achieved.|It ignores the impact of this agreement both on the relations between the US and the 2 Chinas and on other countries.|\n\n[4 marks]\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGiven that the source is a primary source that explicitly outlines the intentions of the US and the PRC ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$), it might be assumed that the source is of great use to a historian studying Sino-American relations. Not only does it provide a first-hand account of what the powers agree on ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$) (the need for peaceful coexistence for example) it also demonstrates the willingness to have a diplomatic relationship ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, on its own, the extent of the source's value to the historian is limited. It is described as an excerpt hence it is likely just a snapshot of the power`s intentions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Also, given the fact that it is the first document signed to reestablish Sino-American relations, it is not helpful to the historian trying to uncover the success of this new relationship ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). It is also only the decision of the two signatories and does not  indicate how other powers perceived this new relationship, for example, the USSR ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). To fully appreciate its value, this source cannot be used in isolation."},{"ref":"HI0200","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0200\n\nDescribe **four** of Mao's initial mass mobilisation campaigns to consolidate power between 1950 and 1952.[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **four** of the following:\n\n- Resist America and Aid Korea Campaign in 1950 directed against foreigners resulting in their expulsion and the closing of Christian churches. \n\n- The Suppression of Counter-Revolutionaries Campaign from 1950-51 targeting the GMD, criminal gangs and religious sects with public executions.\n\n- Speak Bitterness Campaign (Land Reform) in 1950 encouraged peasants to turn on landlords, subjecting them to a public 'trial' and executing 2 million of them. \n\n- The Three-Antis Campaign in 1951 against corruption, waste and obstruction that was directed against communist and non-communists, targeting state officials, police and communist party cadres. \n\n- The Five-Anti Campaign in 1952 against bribery, tax-evasion, theft of state property, cheating in government contracts and economic espionage which led to the death of 2-3 million people. [4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's consolidation of power was the result of a series of mass mobilisation campaigns which used propaganda, self-criticism, rectification and purges. The first of these, the *Resist America and Aid Korea Campaign* in 1950 was directed against foreigners resulting in their expulsion and the closing of Christian churches.This ensured the country was closed to all foreigners except Russians ($\\checkmark$) In the countryside, he launched the Speak Bitterness Campaign in 1950 which encouraged peasants to turn on landlords, subjecting them to a public 'trial' and executing 2 million of them. This ensured the removal of substantial opposition and gained him the support of the large rural peasant population of China ($\\checkmark$). The period 1950-51 also saw the launch of *The Suppression of Counter-Revolutionaries Campaign* that targeted the Guomindang (GMD), criminal gangs and religious sects with denunciations and  public execution. This helped him remove the last remnants of the party controlling China before he came to power together with any religious opposition and ensuring law and order ($\\checkmark$) Mao continued in 1951 with *The Three-Antis Campaign* against corruption, waste and obstruction. This was directed against communist and non-communists, targeting state officials, police and communist party cadres and ensured people were always careful to prove they were followers of Mao ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0201","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0201\n\nOutline the goals of the Hundred Flowers Campaign launched in 1956 and explain its impact on Mao's maintenance of power.[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following for the goals of the Campaign:\n\n- Mao lifts censorship restrictions and encourages criticism of the Communist Party.\n\n- February 1957 he announced $`$Let a Hundred Flowers blossom and a  \n\nhundred schools of thought contend' as a policy to encourage constructive criticism.[1 mark]\n\nAny combination of the following for impact on maintenance of power. \n\n- The Central government receives a deluge of letters and complaints. \n\n- Criticism appears in magazine articles, in rallies in the street and on posters on the $`$Democracy Wall' in Beijing University.\n\n- Mao calls off the campaign and reimposes censorship. \n\n- He launched the Anti-Rightist Campaign in 1957 where half a million Chinese were subject to persecution, imprisonment, public shooting and re-education in labour camps. Removing another layer of opposition. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nHaving established his power by 1956 Mao launched the Hundred Flowers Campaign with the goal of encouraging constructive criticism of the working of the Communist Party. He said $`$letting a Hundred Flowers blossom and a hundred schools of thought contend' would promote progress and a flourishing socialist culture ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe impact of the Hundred Flowers Campaign was a deluge of criticism in letters sent to the Central government ($\\checkmark$). This was followed by criticism of the privileged lifestyle of party members which was just one amongst other issues that appeared in magazine articles, on rallies in the street and from students in Beijing University who expressed their opinion on posters on the $`$Democracy Wall' ($\\checkmark$). All this was deemed by Mao to go beyond $`$healthy criticis' and he called off the campaign and reimposed censorship ($\\checkmark$). This was followed in 1957 by the Anti-Rightist Campaign in 1957 where half a million Chinese were subject to persecution, imprisonment, public shooting and re-education in labour camps and helped remove another layer of opposition ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0202","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0202\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of the failure of the Great Leap Forward in 1958 on Mao's maintenance of power.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n\nThe failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese. \n\n- Mao faced mild criticism from his Defence Minister, Peng Dehuai who was dismissed. \n\n- The crisis weakened Mao who would then step down as Head of State in 1959 but continue as Head of the Communist Party. \n\n- The aftermath of the crisis was now handed to Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping who introduced some elements of private ownership of land,  going against Mao's Communist teachings.[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe failure of the Great Leap Forward resulted in the death of 20 to 50 million Chinese but Mao refused to admit responsibility ($\\checkmark$). Internal criticism was minimal, such as from his Defence Minister, Peng Dehuai, who was simply dismissed ($\\checkmark$). The crisis, however, weakened Mao who stepped down as Head of State in 1959 but continued as Head of the Communist Party, thus maintaining substantial power in China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0203","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0203\n\nDescribe actions taken by Mao to regain political control of China during the Cultural Revolution in 1966.[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n\n- Mao encouraged the formation of the Red Guards consisting of bands of radical students.\n\n- The Red Guards were ordered to attack the $`$four olds' - old thought, culture, practice and customs and remove $`$bad elements' from society.\n\n- Mao removed his opponents from the party and retook absolute control of party hierarchy. \n\n- Among those targeted were Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping who ran the government since the time Mao had stepped down as Head of State. \n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe $`$Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution' marked Mao's attempt to regain political control following the failure of the Great Leap Forward and consequent weakening of his power. He initially encouraged the formation of the Red Guards consisting of bands of radical students who were ordered to attack the $`$four olds' - old thought, culture, practice and customs and remove $`$bad elements' from society ($\\checkmark$). He also proceeded to remove his opponents from the party and retook absolute control of party hierarchy ($\\checkmark$). Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, who had run the government since the time Mao had stepped down as Head of State, were both removed from office amongst the chaos caused by the Red Guards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0204","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0204\n\nDescribe how Mao’s Continuous Revolution Theory helped him retain power.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following:\n\n- The concept of continuous revolution emphasises revolution as a process that continues even after a party achieves power in order to ensure a constant process of renewal to avoid complacency and corruption. \n\n- Mao used this theory of a constant process of renewal to attack opponents on the basis of complacency and corruption. \n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nMao's theory of continuous revolution was a cornerstone of his thought and stated that revolution was as a process that continues even after a party achieves power as there needed to be constant renewal to avoid complacency and corruption ($\\checkmark$). This was the theory used by Mao to justify his many initial mobilisation campaigns and later the purges carried out in the Hundred Flowers Campaign and the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0205","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0205\n\nWhat according to Source C was the impact of the Cultural Revolution on Communist Party leadership? [3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal Problems of Communism (1966).  |\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly….There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum….It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge* from *Problems of Communism*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nParty officials were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly. \n\n- Communist Party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum.\n\n- Their resistance resulted in Liu and Teng being demoted in party ranking at the Plenum. \n\n- Lin Piao was soon proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and chosen heir in place of Liu.\n\n- Chou En-lai was confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source C, the party officials amongst the Communist leadership were told they should accept criticism aimed at them ($\\checkmark$). When an objection was raised against this by Communist Party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping], they were demoted ($\\checkmark$). Lin Piao (Lin Biao) acquires power as a result of this and is seen as Mao's heir apparent ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0206","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0206\n\nWhat does Source A reveal about Mao's status in China at the time of the Cultural Revolution? [2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A political cartoon titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The New Religion\" by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. October 13, 1966.  |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/HI0206.png\" alt=\"HI0206\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, E. S. (1966, October 13). *The New Religion.* Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/resource/ppmsc.07977/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n\nMao is worshipped as a deity by his followers in China. \n\n- The prayers and doctrine of this religion are recited from the book on Mao's Thought. \n\n- The symbols of the religion are the hammer and sickle that signify Communism.\n\n- The entire nation, comprised of different sections of society, follow this religion.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A suggests that Mao has been elevated to the status of a religious deity that is the subject of worship by all his followers in China ($\\checkmark$). Mao's worshippers recite the prayers and doctrine of the religion he embodies from the book on Mao's Thought ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0207","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0207\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source D for an historian studying the impact of the Cultural Revolution. [4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Conversation between the Party of Labor of Albania Delegation, headed by Beqir Balluku, and Mao Zedong on 5 October 1968. |\n\n[Source: *Conversation between the Party of Labor of Albania Delegation, headed by Beqir Balluku, and Mao Zedong*. (1968, October 5). Wilson Center Digital Archive. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://digitalarchive.wilsoncenter.org/document/300036. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any **one or two** of the following:\n\n- The origin of the source is valuable as it is the recording of a conversation between China's Communist leader Mao Zedong and the leader of the visiting Albanian delegation in 1968, during the Cultural Revolution. \n\n- The purpose of the source is valuable as Mao's aim is to discuss and explain the reasons for and impact of the Cultural Revolution. \n\n- The content of the source is valuable as Mao shares details of infighting, at times involving family members and shares his reasoning regarding its revolutionary impact. \n\nFor limitations, any **one or two** of the follwoing:\n\n- The origin of the source is limited as the discussion in 1968 does not give the historian studying the Cultural Revolution an idea of how the Revolution impacted China in the years till the death of Mao Zedong in 1976. \n\n- The purpose of the source is limited as the discussion is between Mao who is the architect of the Cultural Revolution and hence would only present what he sees as the positive impact of the Revolution. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of Source D is valuable as it is the recording of a conversation between China's Communist leader Mao Zedong who was the main force behind the Cultural Revolution and the leader of the visiting Albanian delegation, recorded in 1968 just two years after the launch of the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is also valuable as Mao's aim is to discuss and explain the reasons for and impact of the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ VP). Lastly, the content of the source holds value as Mao shares details of chaotic infighting, at times involving family members, and shares his reasoning regarding the necessity of the Cultural Revolution for Chinese society ($\\checkmark$ VC). \n\nThe origin of the source is limited as the discussion in 1968 does not give the historian studying the Cultural Revolution an idea of how it impacted China after 1968 and until the death of Mao Zedong in 1976 ($\\checkmark$ LO). Furthermore, the origin and purpose of the source are limited because the discussion is with Mao himself and hence would tend to present the events of the Revolution in a positive light ($\\checkmark$ LO and LP)."},{"ref":"HI0208","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0208\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution on China. [6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Report titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Chinese Communist Problem\" from the Asian Affairs Bureau of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Japan. November 18, 1967. |\n\n[Source: Asian Affairs Bureau/Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Japan. (1967, November 18). *The Chinese Communist Problem*. Wilson Center Digital Archive. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://digitalarchive.wilsoncenter.org/document/300640. CC-BY.]\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal *Problems of Communism* (1966).|\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly... There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum... It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge from Problems of Communism*. HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor **comparison** any **three** to **four** of the following **similarities**:\n\n- Both Sources state that Liu Shaoqi had been overthrown or demoted during the Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Both sources state that Mao was now aligned with Lin Piao\n\n- Both sources mention the fighting and chaos that broke out during the Cultural Revolution.\n\n- Both sources mention the attempts made to bring the chaos and disturbance under control.\n\n- Both sources state that the military played a role in bringing the chaos under control.\n\n- Both sources mention that despite the attempts to bring the chaos under control, the situation still is problematic.\n\n- Both sources mention the importance of agricultural production.\n\nFor **contrast**, any **two** to **three** of the following **differences**:\n\n- The Sources emphasize different impacts of the Revolution on leadership. Source C mentions that Chou Enlai retained his position which is not mentioned in Source B while Source B discusses how Liu and Teng lost power.\n\n- Source C mentions the Red Guards and details the destruction caused by them. It also mentions the Red Guards were banished to the countryside in order to bring the chaos under control neither of which is mentioned in Source B.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth Source B and C state that Liu Shaoqi had been overthrown or demoted during the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions that  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Liu Shaoqi, Deng Xiaoping, and others” were overthrown while Source C states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nBoth sources also mention the fighting and chaos that broke out during the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a large number of organizations calling themselves the Mao-Lin faction repeatedly confronted and fought one another, whether within Party and government organizations or within those of workers and peasants,” while Source C mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nBoth sources state that the military played a role in bringing the chaos under control ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the military, brought in to bring the situation under control,” while Source C mentions, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their (Red Guard) activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).  \n\nBoth sources mention the need to maintain agricultural production as an issue that helped drive the reduction of the chaos of the Revolution ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B emphasizes the value of agricultural production and the need to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$secure the autumn harvest” ($\\checkmark$ evidence) and Source C explains that some Red Guards were even $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nOne significant contrast between Sources B and C is their emphasis on the impacts of the Red Guards. Source C describes the Red Guards and details the destruction caused by them as well as mentioning that they were sent to the countryside in order to bring the chaos under control while Source B does not mention the role of the Red Guards or their impact at all ($\\checkmark$ difference). Lastly while both sources discuss the impacts of the Cultural Revolution on Chinese leadership, Source C simply mentions that Chou Enlai retained his position and alignment with Mao, while Source B does not mention Chou and instead emphasizes that Liu and Teng were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$overthrown” ($\\checkmark$ difference)."},{"ref":"HI0209","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0209\n\nUsing the information in Sources A and C, discuss the destabilizing impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution on Chinese society. [6 marks] \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |A political cartoon titled *$`$The New Religion'* by Edmund S. Valtman published in the Hartford Times. October 13, 1966. |\n\n:box[<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/HI0206.png\" alt=\"HI0206\">]\n\n[Source: Valtman, E.S. (1966, October 13). *The New Religion*. Library of Congress. Retrieved November 28, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/resource/ppmsc.07977/. Public domain.] \n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao and the Permanent Purge”, an article by Harry Gelman, International Relations scholar and Kremlinologist in the academic journal *Problems of Communism* (1966). pp13-14. |\n\n:box[Party officials at all levels were warned that they might come under criticism in wall posters, and that they should accept such criticism calmly….There is good reason to believe that CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] resisted this proposal when it was put forward by Mao at the August Plenum... It became apparent that Liu and Teng had been demoted in party ranking at the Plenum, with Lin Piao [Lin Biao] shortly being proclaimed Mao's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$closest comrade in arms\" and presumably his chosen heir in place of Liu. Chou En-lai [Zhou Enlai] appears to have remained faithful to Mao at the Plenum and has since been confirmed as third man in the hierarchy.\n\n\\\n\nWhat Liu and Teng undoubtedly tried to prevent at the August Plenum materialized during the ensuing weeks. On August 13, the Central Committee of the Young Communist League was reorganized without public announcement, and the YCL's propaganda organs subsequently ceased publication.\n\n\\\n\nIn the place of the old youth order came the new disorder-the Red Guards. Much has been written in the Western press about the activities of these bands of fanatical secondary-school and university students the attacks on persons regarded as bourgeois, the beating of aged persons in the streets, the seizure and destruction of works of art, the attacks on both Christian churches and Buddhist temples, the forced extortion of ancestral tablets from terrorized peasants, the desecration of graveyards, and so on.\n\n\\\n\nFinally, in September, there were some cautionary notes. The Red Guards were told not to attack factories or communes, and many of them were sent into the countryside to help the communes with the harvesting. At the same time, their organization was greatly tightened, and the formation of committees in the provinces to supervise their activities was accelerated, with party and PLA [People's Liberation Army] representatives both playing key roles. Classes in schools and universities remained suspended, however, and foreign students were sent home for a year, suggesting that Red Guard activities will dominate school life for many months to come.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Gelman, H. (1966). *Mao and the Permanent Purge from Problems of Communism.* HathiTrust Digital Library. Retrieved November 29, 2024 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.31210006686040. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two or more** of the following with supporting discussion for **five** marks maximum:\n\n- Source C discusses the impact on the Communist Party and states that there was a purge of the senior Communist party membership with CPR Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i and party Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing being removed from power as a result of their resistance to the directives aimed at public humiliation of important members. The source also mentions the consolidation of Lin Piao's position as Mao's presumed successor. Prime Minister Chou Enlai was the only senior official who retained his position through this chaotic period.  \n\n- Source A portrays the complete supremacy of Mao achieved as a result of the Cultural Revolution. The Cultural Revolution had resulted in the elevation of Mao to the position of a national deity to be worshiped by the entire nation. Mao's Thought was the holy book of the religion and the associated symbols of communism - the hammer and sickle were now the symbols of the religion linked to Mao in the Cultural Revolution.  \n\n- Source C also states the destabilizing impact on society as a result of the terror unleashed by the Red Guards who attacked anyone regarded as bourgeoisie, destroyed places of worship and artifacts linked to culture.\n\n- Source C also mentions the impact on education as schools and universities remained closed impacting the education of an an entire generation. \n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding statement for **one** mark that aligns with the evidence and discussion presented:\n\n- The best responses will include evidence from both Sources A and C and provide specific evidence of the destabilizing impact of the Cultural Revolution on Chinese society.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe destabilizing impact of the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution was felt by all sections of society. Source C discusses the impact of the Revolution on the Communist Party and states that there was a purge of the senior Communist party membership with Communist party Chairman Liu Shao-ch'i [Liu Shaoqi] and Secretary-General Teng Hsiao-p'ing [Deng Xiaoping] being removed from power as a result of their resistance to the directives aimed at public humiliation of important members ($\\checkmark$). It also mentions the consolidation of Lin Piao's position as Mao's presumed successor with Prime Minister Chou Enlai being the only senior official who retained his position through this chaotic period ($\\checkmark$).\n\n \n\nFurther evidence of the destabilizing impact of the Revolution is evident in Source A which portrays the complete control that Mao achieved as a result of the Cultural Revolution suggesting that Mao was raised to the position of a national deity to be worshiped ($\\checkmark$). $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mao's Thought” had now assumed the status of the holy book of the religion associated with him. The symbols of communism, the hammer and sickle, were now the symbols of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$religion” linked to Mao in the Cultural Revolution ($\\checkmark$).\n\n \n\nIn addition, as mentioned in Source C, all sections of society faced the terror unleashed by the Red Guards as they attacked anyone regarded as bourgeoisie and destroyed places of worship and artifacts linked to culture ($\\checkmark$). Source C further mentions the destabilizing impact on education as schools and universities remained closed for the entire period of the Revolution, depriving an entire generation of education in order to emphasize $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Red Guard activities” instead ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe chaos unleashed by Mao by launching the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution to further his doctrine of continuous revolution and to regain his power and position within the Communist Party caused waves of disruption that would bring upheaval to the lives of a generation of people in China ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0304","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0304\n\nCompare and contrast the role of foreign intervention in the causes of the Spanish Civil War and the Korean War.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ difference)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nAt least **two** arguments, **one** comparison and one contrast, both with accompanying evidence\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n\n - In both wars, foreign powers played a crucial role in escalating tensions before the conflict began. In the Spanish Civil War, international support for different factions increased polarization, while in the Korean War, Cold War superpowers influenced the division of Korea and its growing hostilities.\n\n - Both conflicts were shaped by the ideological rivalry between communism and anti-communism. In Spain, the USSR supported the Republicans, while fascist powers aided Franco's Nationalists. In Korea, the Soviet Union and China backed North Korea, while the United States supported South Korea.\n\n - In both cases, foreign intervention contributed to transforming internal struggles into larger conflicts with international dimensions. Spain became a battleground for European fascist and communist forces, while Korea became a proxy war between the U.S. and the USSR.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n\n - In Korea, both sides received direct military intervention, with the U.S. and UN forces actively fighting for South Korea and China directly sending troops to support North Korea. In contrast, in Spain, only the Nationalists received direct military support in the form of German and Italian troops, while Soviet aid to the Republicans was limited to weapons, advisors, and volunteers.\n\n - The Spanish Civil War remained a civil conflict within one country, whereas the Korean War became an international war, involving multiple nations under the UN command and full-scale military operations.\n\n - The timing of foreign involvement differed. In the Spanish Civil War, foreign powers intervened after the war had already begun, supporting one side or the other as the conflict developed. In contrast, in Korea, foreign involvement was a direct cause of the war, as the division of Korea was largely driven by U.S. and Soviet policies after World War II, long before fighting started.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nForeign intervention played a significant role in the lead-up to both the Spanish Civil War and the Korean War. In both conflicts, foreign involvement intensified pre-existing divisions within the country, making reconciliation between opposing factions nearly impossible ($\\checkmark$ compare). Similarly, both wars became battlegrounds for broader ideological struggles, with the Spanish Civil War featuring support from the USSR for the Republicans and assistance from fascist Germany and Italy for Franco, while in Korea, the Soviet Union and China backed North Korea and the U.S. supported South Korea ($\\checkmark$ evidence ).\n\nHowever, the nature of direct involvement differed. In the Korean War, both sides received full military support, with the U.S. and its UN allies directly fighting for South Korea and China sending troops to assist North Korea. In contrast, in the Spanish Civil War, only Franco's Nationalists received direct foreign military assistance, with German and Italian forces actively participating, while Soviet aid to the Republicans was limited to arms, advisors, and volunteers ($\\checkmark$ contrast). Another key difference was the timing of foreign involvement. In Spain, external support was provided only after the war had begun, as foreign powers chose sides once the conflict was underway. Meanwhile, in Korea, foreign influence was a direct cause of the war, as the division of the peninsula between North and South was largely driven by U.S. and Soviet policies in the post-World War II period, long before fighting officially started ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0603","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0603\n\nDescribe the 100 Flowers Campaign and explain how it facilitated the Great Leap Forward.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor description, the following or similar:\n- In the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), Mao Zedong encouraged intellectuals to voice their opinions on government policies. Initially, there was little response; however, eventually, there was widespread criticism of the Communist Party.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any **three** of the following connections to the Great Leap Forward:\n- Mao was able to identify critics\n- It strengthened Mao's authority; critics were purged through the resultant Anti-Rightist campaign\n- The suppression of perceived $\\lq$rightists' set a precedent for the suppression of intellectuals\n- The brief period of open discussion demonstrated the power of mass mobilisation, something Mao picked up on.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956-1957), Mao encouraged intellectuals to voice their opinions on government policies. After a slow start, there was widespread criticism of the Communist Party ($\\checkmark$). This facilitated the Great Leap Forward firstly by enabling Mao to identify his critics ($\\checkmark$). Once identified, critics were purged through the Anti-Rightist campaign, leaving no one to protest against Mao’s unrealistic goals in the GLF ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the brief period of open discussion demonstrated the power of mass mobilisation in state-led initiatives, something Mao picked up on and developed during the GLF ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0604","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0604\n\nOutline the events of the Lushan Conference of 1959.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The conference was called to address growing concerns within the Party over agricultural failures, food shortages and economic problems as a result of collectivisation and unrealistic production targets.\n- Peng Dehuai (Minister of Defence) wrote a letter to Mao outlining the problems that he saw with the Great Leap Forward.\n- Rather than addressing Peng's criticisms, Mao attacked Peng (and his supporters) accusing him of rightist and bourgeois tendencies.\n- Most key leaders, fearing for their positions, sided with Mao, and the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lushan conference was called to address growing concerns within the Party over agricultural failures, food shortages and economic problems as a result of collectivisation and unrealistic production targets ($\\checkmark$). Peng Dehuai (Minister of Defence) wrote a letter to Mao outlining the problems that he saw with the Great Leap forward ($\\checkmark$). Rather than addressing Peng's criticisms, Mao attacked Peng (and his supporters) accusing him of rightist and bourgeois tendencies ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked."},{"ref":"HI0605","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0605\n\nExplain the significance of the Lushan Conference of 1959 to Mao's consolidation of power.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Peng and his supporters were purged.\n- Opposition to Mao's policies were practically eliminated from the party as criticism of Mao could lead to persecution.\n- The Great Leap Forward continued unchecked, with disastrous effects.\n- Lin Biao, a staunch supporter of Mao, replaced Peng as Minister of Defence. With Lin Biao in charge of the military, the military and ideology took priority over pragmatism, also confirming the PLA as a key power base for Mao.\n- Lin Biao's growing influence would lead to the militarisation of Mao's Cult of Personality.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lushan Conference was significant as opposition to Mao's policies were practically eliminated from the party, as criticism of Mao could lead to persecution ($\\checkmark$). Thus, the Great Leap Forward was allowed to continue unchecked, leading to the escalation of famine and economic collapse ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Lin Biao, a loyal supporter of Mao, replaced Peng as Minister of Defence. With Lin Biao in charge of the military, the military and ideology took precedence over pragmatism and cemented the People's Liberation Army as a key power base for Mao ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0606","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0606\n\nWhat is the message of Source B?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  “Bumper harvest after bumper harvest, leap forward after leap forward!” Poster designed by Wu Linsheng and published in May 1959. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/HI0606.jpg\" alt=\"HI0606\">\n[Source: Linsheng, W (1958, May). *Bumper harvest after bumper harvest, leap forward after leap forward!* Private collection hosted on ChinesePosters.net. Retrieved March 7, 2025 from https://chineseposters.net/posters/pc-1958-026. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Substantial gains are being made in the wheat, cotton and corn harvests (as seen in the graph and the crops shown behind it)\n- These gains will continue (bumper harvest after bumper harvest, great leap after great leap), also shown through the title and trajectory of the rocket\n- Farmers in China should be optimistic about their future.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe message of the poster is that substantial gains are being made (as seen in the graph) ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, these gains will continue as shown in the caption “bumper harvest after bumper harvest, great leap after great leap” and also shown by the trajectory of the rocket ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0607","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0607\n\nAccording to Source A, why did the Great Leap Forward fail?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Comrade Peng Dehuai's Letter to Chairman Mao” (July 14, 1959). Peng Dehuai was a general, politician, and Minister of Defence from 1954 to 1959. The letter was given to Mao by Peng during the Lushan Conference of 1959. |\n\n[Source: Dehuai, P. (1984). *Comrade Peng Dehuai's Letter to Chairman Mao* (July 14, 1959). Memoirs of a Chinese Marshal - The autobiographical notes of Peng Dehuai (1898-1974). Retrieved March 7, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/archive/peng-dehuai/1984/memoirspengdehuai.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following\n- Peng criticises the overestimation of and boasting about production figures, particularly in relation to food.\n- He points out that these unrealistic statistics led to a premature focus on the iron and steel industries without proper planning.\n- Peng notes the lack of serious analysis of the equipment that would be needed in steel production.\n- Peng suggests that a lack of workforce and purchasing power limited the development of industry\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, Peng criticises the overestimation of production figures, particularly in relation to food ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, he points out that these unrealistic statistics led to a premature focus on the iron and steel industries without proper planning ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Peng notes the lack of serious analysis of the equipment that would be needed in steel production ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0767","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0767\n\nWhat is the message of Source A?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | \n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School,” sent August 1, 1966, by Mao Zedong. Originally published in *Long Live Mao Tse-tung Thought*, a Red Guard publication. \n\n[Source: Mao\\, Z. (1966, August 1). *A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School.* Retrieved May 12, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-9/mswv9_60.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Mao enthusiastically supports the Red Guards' denunciations of landlords, bourgeois elements, imperialists, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists” as enemies of the revolution. \n\n - Mao encourages mass action against perceived enemies of socialism. He reinforces the slogan $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It is right to rebel against reactionaries”. \n\n - Mao calls for unity and re-education. Whilst supporting rebellion, Mao cautions the Red Guards to unite with others who can still be part of the revolution. \n\n - Mao frames the the Cultural revolution as part of a universal proletarian mission, referencing Marx's idea that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the proletariat must emancipate not only itself but all mankind,”\n\n - By addressing the students directly, Mao signals that they are now the vanguard of the revolution. (Thus bypassing traditional party structures)\n\n - By endorsing the Red Flag Combat Group and specific posters, Mao validates extreme, sometimes violent methods as legitimate revolutionary acts. Thus radicalism is not only permitted, it is praised by the highest authority.\n\n - The source contains an implied criticism of the CCP establishment. In referring to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$landlords, bourgeois, imperialists, revisionists and their running dogs” Mao is suggesting the existing party leadership is suspect, and he is placing his trust in mass mobilisation rather than official structures.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe first message of Source A is that Mao strongly endorses the Red Guards' denunciation of landlords, bourgeois elements, imperialists, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$revisionists,” whom he identifies as enemies of the revolution. He expresses approval of their big-character posters and revolutionary speeches, signaling his personal support for their militant approach. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, he actively encourages mass action against perceived enemies of socialism, reaffirming the slogan, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It is right to rebel against reactionaries.” By doing so, Mao grants the Red Guards legitimacy to continue their revolutionary efforts, thereby intensifying the Cultural Revolution. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, Mao urges unity and the re-education of those who have made errors. While backing rebellion, he advises the Red Guards to work with all those who can still contribute to the revolution. He emphasizes that individuals who have made serious mistakes should be guided toward ideological reform and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$become new men,” reflecting a Marxist view that transformation, not just punishment, is the ultimate goal. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0768","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0768\n\nUsing evidence from Source A, explain Mao's concerns about the direction of the Chinese Communist Party.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- | \n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School,” sent August 1, 1966, by Mao Zedong. Originally published in *Long Live Mao Tse-tung Thought*, a Red Guard publication. \n\n[Source: Mao\\, Z. (1966, August 1). *A Letter To The Red Guards Of Tsinghua University Middle School.* Retrieved May 12, 2025 from https://marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-9/mswv9_60.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Implies internal threats; $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$those who have made serious mistakes.” Suggesting Mao feared corruption and complacency among cadres.\n\n - The message is directed at students, suggesting Mao saw youth as more ideologically pure and less corrupted.\n\n - Mao encourages the students in their role of confronting and correcting perceived Party deviations.\n\n - Calls for rectification and re-education rather than purges alone.\n\n - Emphasizes ongoing revolutionary struggle to prevent revisionism.\n\n - Shows Mao's belief that the Party needed to stay connected to the masses.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A implies that Mao believed there were internal threats within the CPP. His reference to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$those who have made serious mistakes” suggests that he was concerned about the ideological weakness, corruption, or complacency of certain party members. This indicates a fear that some cadres were drifting away from revolutionary principles and embracing more bureaucratic or capitalist tendencies. ($\\checkmark$) Secondly, rather than calling for their immediate removal or punishment, Mao emphasizes the need for rectification and re-education, showing that he hoped to ideologically reform these individuals instead of relying solely on purges. This reflects a broader concern that the party was losing its revolutionary spirit and needed to return to its Marxist-Leninist roots through mass mobilization and ideological struggle. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, the fact that Mao directs this message to the Red Guards rather than party officials further reveals his mistrust of the existing party leadership and his reliance on youth to reinvigorate the revolution. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0823","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0823\n\nAnalyse **three** reasons for international involvement in the Vietnam War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n- U.S. containment policy and the domino theory: The United States was committed to preventing the spread of communism in Asia, especially after the Chinese Communist Revolution (1949) and the Korean War (1950-1953). The Domino Theory suggested that if one country in Southeast Asia fell to communism, others would quickly follow. Vietnam was seen as the critical first domino.\n- Support from the USSR and China for North Vietnam. Both the Soviet Union and the People's Republic of China provided economic aid, weapons, and training to the North Vietnamese as part of Cold War rivalry. Their support stemmed from a desire to promote global communism and challenge U.S. influence.\n- The USSR also used Vietnam to assert its leadership in the communist world, particularly during the Sino-Soviet split, creating competition with China over influence in Hanoi.\n- U.S. strategic interests in Southeast Asia. The U.S. saw Vietnam as strategically vital for maintaining influence over Southeast Asia, which included proximity to key allies (e.g., the Philippines, Thailand) and control of trade routes. Establishing a military presence through bases and alliances helped counterbalance growing communist influence in Asia. Vietnam was also seen as a test of American credibility; failure to act would weaken U.S. standing globally.\n- SEATO commitments. The Southeast Asia Treaty Organisation (SEATO), formed in 1954, was intended to block further communist gains in Southeast Asia. Although South Vietnam was not a member, the U.S. and its allies used SEATO as a justification for intervention under the principle of collective defence. Australia, New Zealand, and the Philippines were among SEATO members who contributed troops, citing treaty obligations.\n- French colonial legacy. Vietnam was a former French colony (part of French Indochina), and France's defeat at Dien Bien Phu (1954) drew international attention to the region. The Geneva Accords (1954), which temporarily divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel, internationalised the conflict by creating a fragile and contested peace settlement. The U.S. stepped in to support South Vietnam after France’s withdrawal, viewing the conflict as part of the broader fight against communism.\n- Global superpower rivalry and Cold War alignments. The Vietnam War became a proxy war in the broader Cold War context, with countries aligning based on ideological and strategic interests. Australia and South Korea, for example, supported the U.S. due to alliance obligations and anti-communist alignment. The Soviet Union and China, meanwhile, supported North Vietnam to counter U.S. hegemony and extend their influence in the developing world.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, international involvement in the Vietnam War was driven by the United States' containment policy and belief in the Domino Theory ($\\checkmark$). After the rise of communism in China and the outbreak of the Korean War, the U.S. feared that if Vietnam fell to communism, other Southeast Asian countries would follow. As a result, Vietnam became a key battleground in America's broader Cold War strategy to prevent the global spread of communism ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the Soviet Union and China supported North Vietnam to advance their ideological interests and to counter U.S. influence in Asia ($\\checkmark$). Both provided military and economic aid to Hanoi, aiming to promote global communism. This support was also shaped by Cold War competition, with the USSR and China each trying to assert their influence in the region, particularly during the Sino-Soviet split ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, U.S. involvement was also justified through the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO) ($\\checkmark$). Although South Vietnam was not a formal member, SEATO allowed countries like the U.S., Australia, and the Philippines to frame their military intervention as part of a collective defense strategy against communist expansion in the region ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0824","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0824\n\nDiscuss the political and economic effects of the Vietnam War on Vietnam.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation. Both political and economic effects must be addressed in the response.\n\nPolitical Effects:\n- Unification under a communist government in 1976: After the fall of Saigon in 1975, North and South Vietnam were formally unified into the Socialist Republic of Vietnam in 1976. The North imposed its political system on the South, centralising power in Hanoi and ending the brief period of political plurality in the South.\n- Repression of political dissent: The new government established re-education camps where former South Vietnamese military officers, civil servants, and intellectuals were imprisoned, sometimes for years. The suppression of former political and religious groups in the South created lasting resentment and drove many to flee the country, contributing to the refugee crisis.\n- Strained international relations, especially with China (Sino-Vietnamese War, 1979): Vietnam's invasion of Cambodia in 1978 to oust the Khmer Rouge, which China backed, led to the brief but intense Sino-Vietnamese War in 1979. This conflict worsened relations with China and left Vietnam diplomatically isolated within the communist bloc for a time.\n- Centralised, one-party rule: The Communist Party of Vietnam (CPV) became the sole legal political party, enforcing strict ideological control and suppressing political freedoms. There was no allowance for democratic elections or dissent, with censorship and state surveillance continuing into the post-war period.\n\nEconomic Effects\n- Massive destruction of infrastructure and farmland: Years of intense bombing by the U.S., especially in the North and along the Ho Chi Minh Trail, destroyed key infrastructure like roads, bridges, railways, and irrigation systems. The use of chemical defoliants like Agent Orange also left large areas of farmland infertile and caused long-term health and environmental damage.\n- Loss of human capital: The war caused the deaths of an estimated 2 to 3 million Vietnamese, severely impacting the labour force. A significant number of skilled workers, professionals, and students were killed, imprisoned, or fled the country, creating a brain drain.\n- Economic isolation in the 1970s and 1980s: Vietnam's close ties with the USSR and occupation of Cambodia led to sanctions and hostility from Western powers, notably the U.S. As a result, Vietnam remained economically dependent on Soviet aid and struggled with stagnation, food shortages, and poor living standards.\n- Gradual economic reforms, Đổi Mới, from 1986: Facing an economic crisis, the Communist Party launched Đổi Mới (“Renovation”), introducing market-oriented reforms. This included decentralising economic planning, encouraging private enterprise, and allowing some foreign investment, which led to a gradual economic recovery and growth.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe first political effect of the Vietnam War was the complete unification of North and South Vietnam under a single communist regime in 1976 ($\\checkmark$). Following the capture of Saigon in 1975, the North established control over the entire country, dissolving the former government of South Vietnam and centralising authority in Hanoi. This marked the end of political diversity in the South, as the Communist Party became the sole governing force across the nation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe second political effect was the widespread suppression of opposition in the aftermath of unification ($\\checkmark$). The new regime targeted former South Vietnamese officials, intellectuals, and religious leaders by detaining them in re-education camps. These camps were intended to indoctrinate individuals with communist ideology, but often involved harsh conditions and long-term imprisonment. The repression contributed to mass emigration, as many sought to escape persecution, leading to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$boat people\" refugee crisis ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAn economic effect of the war was the extensive damage to Vietnam's infrastructure and environment ($\\checkmark$). Prolonged bombing campaigns destroyed roads, bridges, and railways, while large-scale use of chemical agents such as Agent Orange severely contaminated farmland and forests. These consequences not only devastated agricultural productivity but also caused lasting health issues among the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0825","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0825\n\nAnalyse **three** examples of international involvement in the Soviet-Afghan War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** points with supporting explanation:\n- United States: Through Operation Cyclone, the CIA covertly funded and armed the Mujahideen as part of its Cold War containment strategy, aiming to weaken Soviet influence. Support included advanced Stinger surface-to-air missiles, which significantly hindered Soviet air superiority by targeting helicopters and low-flying aircraft. This involvement escalated the conflict and indirectly contributed to the rise of Islamist militant groups, including the Taliban and al-Qaeda in later decades.\n- Pakistan: The Inter-Services Intelligence (ISI) served as the intermediary for U.S. and Saudi funds and weapons, distributing them to various Mujahideen factions. Pakistan hosted millions of Afghan refugees and training camps near the Durand Line, helping to build a deep cross-border insurgency. Its role enhanced its strategic importance to the West, especially during the Reagan era, though it also fueled extremism within its borders.\n- Saudi Arabia: Co-financed the jihad with the U.S., often matching American funding dollar-for-dollar. Promoted the spread of Wahhabi ideology among Mujahideen fighters, viewing the war as a religious struggle against atheist communism. Several Saudi volunteers, including Osama bin Laden, participated, strengthening transnational jihadist networks.\n- China: Opposed Soviet expansionism and sought to counter Moscow’s influence in Asia; thus, it supplied weapons, including small arms and mines, to the Mujahideen via Pakistan. This marked a rare instance of tacit alignment with the U.S. during the Cold War, despite China's communist system. China’s support was also motivated by fears of Soviet influence in neighbouring Central Asia and Xinjiang.\n- United Nations: Passed non-binding resolutions condemning the Soviet invasion, but no concrete actions (e.g., sanctions or peacekeeping) were taken due to Cold War divisions in the Security Council. The UN provided humanitarian support to Afghan refugees but lacked the authority or consensus to influence the course of the war.\n- NATO countries: Generally aligned with the U.S. stance, issuing diplomatic condemnations of Soviet aggression. Some NATO members, such as the UK, also provided indirect logistical and intelligence support, though without committing troops. The war deepened the East-West divide, reinforcing anti-Soviet policies in the West during the final decade of the Cold War.\n- Soviet bloc: Warsaw Pact allies (e.g., East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Bulgaria) publicly supported the USSR, framing the war as a defence of socialism against U.S.-backed “terrorists.” However, most did not send troops due to political caution and lack of popular support. The war contributed to growing dissent and disillusionment in Eastern Europe regarding the Soviet leadership, especially as the war dragged on without a clear victory.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the United States became heavily involved in the Soviet-Afghan War as part of its broader Cold War agenda to contain Soviet expansion ($\\checkmark$).Through a covert operation known as Operation Cyclone, the CIA provided billions of dollars in weapons, training, and logistical support to the Afghan Mujahideen. A key component of this support was the supply of advanced Stinger missiles, which enabled resistance fighters to shoot down Soviet helicopters and disrupt air dominance ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, Pakistan played a central role in facilitating the Mujahideen resistance. The country's intelligence agency, the ISI, acted as a conduit for American and Saudi aid, distributing funds and weapons to various Afghan factions ($\\checkmark$). Pakistan also hosted extensive networks of refugee camps and guerrilla training centres along its border, which not only supported the war effort but also entrenched militant ideologies within Pakistan's territory. This involvement elevated Pakistan's strategic position during the Cold War, though it also laid the foundation for future instability in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, China supported the Afghan resistance as part of its geopolitical effort to counterbalance Soviet power in Central and South Asia ($\\checkmark$). Despite being a communist state itself, China feared Soviet encroachment in neighbouring areas such as Xinjiang and sought to undermine Moscow's influence. It supplied the Mujahideen with light weapons, landmines, and ammunition, often through Pakistani channels. This indirect collaboration with the U.S. highlighted the complexity of Cold War alliances and reflected China's broader strategy to assert regional autonomy and challenge Soviet dominance ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0826","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0826\n\nEvaluate the consequences of the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan in 1989.\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** points with supporting explanation:\n- Military: The withdrawal marked the end of a decade-long conflict that had cost the USSR over 15,000 troops and substantial military resources. It was widely regarded as a humiliating defeat for a superpower, having failed to subdue a guerrilla insurgency despite superior military technology. The Soviet military's poor performance exposed weaknesses in its logistics, morale, and adaptability, damaging the Red Army's reputation.\n- Political (Soviet Union): The failure in Afghanistan undermined Soviet global credibility and confidence in the Communist system. It contributed to internal disillusionment with the leadership of the Communist Party and Mikhail Gorbachev’s reforms (Perestroika and Glasnost). The strain of the war, combined with economic problems, helped accelerate the collapse of the USSR in 1991.\n- Afghanistan (domestic impact): The withdrawal created a power vacuum, as the Soviet-backed Najibullah government collapsed by 1992. Rival Mujahideen factions turned on each other, leading to a brutal civil war (1989-1996), particularly in Kabul, where competing warlords vied for control.\n- Taliban emergence: The chaos and instability enabled the rise of the Taliban, who promised order and Islamic governance and seized power by 1996. The Taliban’s emergence was partly enabled by continued foreign support, especially from Pakistan’s ISI, and disillusionment with factional infighting.\n- Regional impact (South Asia): The war left millions of Afghan refugees in Pakistan and Iran, placing enormous social and economic strain on the region. The Pakistan-Afghanistan border became a hub for arms smuggling, drug trafficking, and radical Islamist activity, destabilising both countries.\n- Global consequences (Islamist militancy): The war became a rallying point for global jihadist movements. Many foreign fighters, including Osama bin Laden, were radicalised and gained combat experience. Veterans later participated in conflicts in Bosnia and Chechnya, and eventually formed the core of al-Qaeda, leading to future global terrorism.\n- U.S. perception and policy: The Soviet withdrawal was seen in Washington as a Cold War victory, confirming the effectiveness of U.S. covert intervention. However, the U.S. disengaged quickly, failing to invest in post-war reconstruction or governance, which allowed chaos to take root. This short-term success had long-term consequences, as abandoned fighters and arms stockpiles later empowered extremist groups.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall significance and long-term impact of the Soviet withdrawal, linking the evaluated consequences to broader historical or global developments.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Soviet military withdrawal in 1989 marked a significant and costly failure for one of the world's superpowers ($\\checkmark$). After nearly a decade of fighting, the USSR had lost over 15,000 soldiers and expended vast military resources, only to withdraw without securing a clear victory. The Soviet forces' inability to defeat a guerrilla resistance movement, despite possessing superior weaponry, exposed deep flaws in their military strategy, including poor troop morale, ineffective logistics, and a lack of adaptability. This withdrawal seriously damaged the credibility of the Red Army and eroded its prestige on the global stage ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the political repercussions for the Soviet Union were equally severe. The defeat in Afghanistan undermined Soviet influence abroad and intensified domestic dissatisfaction with the Communist regime ($\\checkmark$). Public support for continued military engagement eroded, and the war became symbolic of the broader failures of the Soviet system. The conflict also placed significant strain on the Soviet economy, compounding the difficulties facing Mikhail Gorbachev as he attempted to implement reforms such as Perestroika and Glasnost. These combined pressures played a key role in accelerating the decline and eventual collapse of the Soviet Union by 1991 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, in Afghanistan itself, the Soviet exit created a dangerous power vacuum. With no strong central government in place, various Mujahideen factions turned on each other, plunging the country into a brutal civil war ($\\checkmark$). Amid the chaos, the Taliban emerged as a dominant force, promising to restore order through strict Islamic governance. Their rise to power by 1996 was aided by continued foreign backing, particularly from Pakistan, and widespread disillusionment with the corruption and infighting among rival warlords ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan had far-reaching consequences, including military humiliation for the USSR, political instability at home, and long-term regional instability that paved the way for the Taliban's rise, consequences that would shape global geopolitics for decades to come ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0827","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0827\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from “H.J.Res. 1145, Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia.” 88th Congress of the United States, August 7th, 1964. Also known as the “Gulf of Tonkin Resolution.” |\n\n[Source: United States Congress. (1964, August 7). H.J.Res. 1145, *Joint Resolution to Promote the Maintenance of International Peace and Security in Southeast Asia*. 88th Congress, 2nd session. Public Law 88-408. U.S. Government Publishing Office. Retrieved June 11, 2025 from https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-78/pdf/STATUTE-78-Pg384.pdf. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nOrigin\n- Value:\n    - Official U.S. government document (Joint Resolution by the 88th Congress, 1964), providing an authoritative primary source on U.S. legislative and executive justification for escalation in Vietnam.\n    - Reflects the formal legal basis granted to President Johnson to expand military action, showing how U.S. policy was legitimized domestically.\n- Limitations:\n    - Reflects only the U.S. government's official stance, so it may omit internal debates, dissent, or alternate perspectives within the U.S. political system.\n    - Produced shortly after the Gulf of Tonkin incidents, it may contain biased or incomplete information about the actual events, limiting its reliability on factual accuracy.\nPurpose\n- Value:\n    - Intended to justify U.S. military escalation by portraying North Vietnam as the aggressor threatening international peace, showing how the U.S. framed its intervention as defensive and lawful.\n    - Demonstrates U.S. commitment to Cold War alliances (Southeast Asia Treaty Organisation) and the wider goal of containing communism.\n    - Helps historians understand the rhetoric and reasoning used to gain congressional and public support for the war.\n\n- Limitations:\n    - Purpose is inherently political and persuasive; designed to rally support and legitimize U.S. military action, so it may exaggerate or oversimplify North Vietnamese aggression.\n    - Omits discussion of U.S. strategic interests beyond stated ideals (e.g., economic, geopolitical), limiting insight into the full scope of motivations.\nContent\n- Value:\n    - Provides direct evidence of the U.S. claim that North Vietnam attacked American naval vessels in international waters, a key justification for military action.\n    - Specifies the extent of presidential authority granted to use \"all necessary measures,\" illustrating the legal framework for escalating U.S. involvement.\n    - Emphasizes U.S. self-portrayal as a defender of freedom and international peace, useful for analyzing Cold War ideological narratives.\n\n- Limitations:\n    - The claim of repeated attacks on U.S. vessels has been contested or re-evaluated by later historians, raising questions about factual accuracy.\n    - Does not acknowledge the Vietnamese perspective or broader regional complexities, limiting its scope for comprehensive understanding.\n    - The language is vague about what \"all necessary measures\" entails, leaving room for varied interpretation and use of power.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs an official U.S. government document, specifically a Joint Resolution passed by  Congress in 1964, it serves as an authoritative source that reflects the formal legislative and executive rationale for escalating American military involvement in Vietnam. It offers direct insight into the political mindset of U.S. leaders at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).\n\nSecondly, the content of the resolution is valuable in that it presents the United States as a defender of freedom and international peace. This self-portrayal helps historians understand the ideological framing of the Cold War, particularly how the U.S. justified its intervention in Southeast Asia in terms of protecting democratic values and resisting communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the purpose of the document introduces limitations. Since it was intended to rally public and congressional support for military action, it is inherently political and persuasive in nature. As such, it may exaggerate or simplify the threat posed by North Vietnam, portraying the U.S. as purely defensive and omitting the complexities of the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$).\n\nFinally, its origin also limits its reliability. Written shortly after the Gulf of Tonkin incident, the resolution may reflect incomplete or biased accounts of those events, which were later shown to be more ambiguous than initially reported. This reduces the source's usefulness for establishing an accurate account of what happened, but highlights how the events were used to justify escalation\n($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$)."},{"ref":"HI0829","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0829\n\nUsing evidence from Source B, describe how Ho Chi Minh uses foreign political ideals to justify Vietnam's independence.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam by Ho Chi Minh, the first President of Vietnam, September 2, 1945. |\n\n[Source: Ho Chi Minh. (1945, September 2). Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. Retrieved June 13, 2025 from https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/ho-chi-minh/works/1945/declaration-independence.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Reference to the U.S. Declaration of Independence (1776): Ho quotes “All men are created equal... Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness” to appeal to American democratic values and show that Vietnamese independence is based on the same universal principles.\n- Reference to the French Declaration of the Rights of Man (1789): Ho cites “All men are born free and with equal rights...” to highlight the hypocrisy of France’s actions in Vietnam, contrasting French ideals with their colonial oppression.\n- Appeal to post-World War II Allied principles (Tehran and San Francisco Conferences): Ho emphasizes that Vietnam’s struggle aligns with Allied declarations supporting self-determination and equality of nations, suggesting that Vietnam deserves international recognition just like other decolonizing countries.\n- Alignment with anti-fascist efforts: He claims that the Vietnamese people fought “side by side with the Allies against the Fascists,” arguing that their contribution to the global struggle for freedom entitles them to national sovereignty.\n- Use of Enlightenment values to delegitimize colonialism: By grounding his argument in widely respected Western political ideals, Ho frames colonialism as outdated and unjust, reinforcing Vietnam's moral right to independence.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nHo Chi Minh uses foreign political ideals to justify Vietnam's independence by aligning his arguments with well-known democratic principles. Firstly, he references the U.S. Declaration of Independence (1776), quoting that “All men are created equal... Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness,” to appeal to American values and emphasize that the Vietnamese desire for independence is rooted in the same ideals ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, he draws on the French Declaration of the Rights of Man (1789), citing that “All men are born free and with equal rights” to expose the contradiction between France's professed ideals and its colonial rule over Vietnam ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Ho refers to post-World War II Allied commitments made “at Tehran and San Francisco”, a reference to the Tehran and San Francisco Conferences, highlighting principles of self-determination and equality among nations. In doing so, he argues that Vietnam's independence is consistent with international agreements and should therefore be recognized globally ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0831","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0831\n\nTo what extent did the goals outlined in Source C (NSDD-166) influence the outcome of the Soviet-Afghan War?\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the “National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan” given by US President Ronald Reagan, 1985, and declassified in 2008.|\n\n[Source: Reagan, R. (1985, March 27). *National Security Decision Directive 166: U.S. Policy Toward Afghanistan.* Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. Retrieved June 14, 2025 from https://www.reaganlibrary.gov/public/archives/reference/scanned-nsdds/nsdd166.pdf Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a concluding sentence:\n\nGoals that were largely achieved (high influence):\n- Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan (1989): NSDD-166's ultimate goal was the removal of Soviet forces, which was achieved after sustained Mujahideen resistance and mounting costs for the USSR. U.S. covert support (weapons, funding, intelligence) played a critical role in weakening Soviet military efforts.\n- Soviet military and political strain: The directive aimed to “demonstrate to the Soviet Union that its long-term strategy is not working”. Prolonged conflict and rising casualties demoralized Soviet troops and weakened domestic support. The war became known as the “Soviet Union’s Vietnam,” contributing to internal dissent and disillusionment with leadership.\n- Support for indigenous resistance (Mujahideen): NSDD-166 emphasized preventing the defeat of the Afghan resistance, U.S. and Pakistani support helped the Mujahideen sustain a prolonged insurgency. Stinger missiles provided by the CIA in the mid-1980s significantly improved Mujahideen capacity to neutralize Soviet air superiority.\n- Soviet international isolation: The directive aimed to “promote Soviet isolation in the Third and Islamic worlds”, many Muslim-majority countries condemned the occupation and supported the Afghan cause diplomatically and materially. The conflict damaged Soviet relationships in the Non-Aligned Movement and broader Islamic world.\n- Denying Afghanistan as a Soviet base: The resistance and chaos meant Afghanistan never became a stable Soviet ally or base of operations.\n\nGoals partially achieved or with mixed results:\n- Demonstrating Soviet imperialism to the Third World: The U.S. succeeded in framing the USSR as an occupying force, shifting some countries' perspectives away from viewing the USSR as a natural ally. Still, some regimes continued to support or remain neutral toward the Soviets due to shared political or economic interests.\n\nGoals not fully achieved or with long-term unintended consequences:\n- Afghanistan's stability and independence: Although the Soviets withdrew, Afghanistan descended into civil war among Mujahideen factions. The rise of the Taliban and the harbouring of al-Qaeda in the 1990s were long-term outcomes contrary to U.S. interests.\n- U.S. long-term control over outcomes: NSDD-166 focused on defeating the USSR, but lacked a coherent post-withdrawal plan; the resulting power vacuum led to instability. U.S. disengagement after 1989 meant little support for post-Soviet reconstruction or political settlement.\n- Blowback and radicalization: Some Mujahideen fighters later became involved in global jihadist movements, including al-Qaeda. U.S. funding and training of extremists during the war had long-term unintended consequences.\n\nConcluding sentence:\n- Should provide a clear summary judgment that reflects the overall extent to which the US achieved their goals, linking the goals to regional developments.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the directive's primary objective, the removal of Soviet forces from Afghanistan, was met to a high degree. The Soviet withdrawal in 1989 followed years of sustained resistance by the Mujahideen, supported by U.S. covert aid in the form of weapons, funding, and intelligence. This significantly weakened Soviet military effectiveness and made continued occupation unsustainable ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, NSDD-166 successfully aimed to “demonstrate to the Soviet Union that its long-term strategy is not working.” The protracted conflict placed a major strain on Soviet military resources and morale, contributing to public disillusionment and internal dissent. The war earned the nickname “the Soviet Union's Vietnam,” underscoring the extent to which it undermined Soviet confidence and political cohesion ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the goal of restoring Afghan independence and stability was met to a lesser degree. Although Soviet forces left the country, Afghanistan soon descended into civil war between rival Mujahideen factions. The eventual rise of the Taliban and the emergence of al-Qaeda in the 1990s represented long-term consequences that ultimately ran counter to U.S. interests in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while NSDD-166 played a decisive role in achieving key U.S. objectives, namely, the Soviet withdrawal and the weakening of Soviet geopolitical influence, its longer-term aim of fostering a stable and independent Afghanistan was not fully realised. The directive reflected Cold War priorities, but its legacy highlights the complexity of foreign intervention and the unintended consequences that can follow even seemingly successful policy outcomes ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0904","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0904\n\nEvaluate the limitations of the military strategies of the United States in the Vietnam War (1956-1975).\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation and a reasoned conclusion:\n\nLimited effectiveness in the short term or tactically:\n- Operation Rolling Thunder (1965-1968): Intended to weaken North Vietnamese morale and supply lines. Destroyed some infrastructure but failed to stop supplies along the Ho Chi Minh Trail. Gave North Vietnam time to strengthen air defenses.\n- Superior firepower and air mobility: Use of helicopters (e.g., UH-1 Huey) allowed fast deployment in jungle terrain. Enabled rapid troop movement and medical evacuation. Provided temporary tactical advantages in battles like Ia Drang (1965).\n- Search and destroy missions: Aimed to kill Viet Cong forces and reduce enemy presence. Occasionally successful in eliminating enemy units. Produced high body counts, seen as a success metric by U.S. military leadership.\n\nLargely ineffective or counterproductive in the long term:\n- Overreliance on attrition warfare: Strategy focused on body counts rather than holding territory. Misjudged the Viet Cong's and North Vietnamese Army's (NVA) willingness to endure losses. Failed to break enemy morale.\n- Guerrilla warfare by the Viet Cong undermined U.S. strategy: The Viet Cong avoided direct confrontations and used ambushes and booby traps. U.S. troops were unprepared for asymmetric warfare in dense jungle terrain. U.S. technology and firepower were often ineffective against elusive guerrilla tactics.\n- Alienation of the civilian population: Bombing campaigns and destruction of villages pushed civilians toward supporting the Viet Cong. Tactics like the use of napalm and Agent Orange caused long-term environmental and human harm. Events like the My Lai Massacre (1968) damaged U.S. credibility and intensified anti-war sentiment.\n- Failure to cut off the Ho Chi Minh Trail: Despite intense bombing, the supply route through Laos and Cambodia remained operational. North Vietnam adapted with effective camouflage and constant rebuilding efforts.\n- Tet Offensive (1968): Militarily, the U.S. and South Vietnamese forces repelled the attacks. Strategically, it was a major propaganda victory for the North. Shattered public perception of U.S. progress in the war and eroded political will.\n- Vietnamization policy (1969 onwards): Attempted to train and equip South Vietnamese forces to take over combat roles. Lacked long-term effectiveness due to corruption, poor morale, and inadequate training in the ARVN (Army of the Republic of Vietnam). Viewed as a way for the U.S. to exit rather than achieve victory.\n- Strategic Hamlet Program (1962-1964): U.S.-backed counterinsurgency initiative to isolate civilians from Viet Cong influence and build loyalty to the South Vietnamese regime. Often involved forced relocations, generating resentment among peasants. The Viet Cong were often able to infiltrate the hamlets or exploit them for propaganda purposes. Reflected broader failures in U.S. understanding of Vietnamese society and nationalism.\n\nBroader strategic failures:\n- Lack of understanding of Vietnamese nationalism and culture: U.S. policy framed the conflict as a Cold War battle against communism. Underestimated the strength of nationalist motivations among North Vietnamese and the Viet Cong. Failed to win the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hearts and minds\" of the South Vietnamese population.\n- Limited domestic support over time: Initial support eroded due to rising casualties, media coverage, and lack of clear progress. Anti-war protests and the credibility gap pressured politicians to seek withdrawal. Strategy was unsustainable without domestic political backing.\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, U.S. strategies such as Operation Rolling Thunder and helicopter deployment offered short-term tactical gains but failed to achieve strategic objectives. ($\\checkmark$). Bombing campaigns did not stop supply routes like the Ho Chi Minh Trail, and although helicopters provided mobility, they could not secure lasting control over territory or defeat guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the U.S. strategy of attrition, focused on body counts, misjudged the determination of the Viet Cong and North Vietnamese Army ($\\checkmark$) Despite high casualties, enemy morale remained strong. This approach ignored the nature of guerrilla warfare and resulted in costly battles that failed to produce meaningful long-term outcomes ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, tactics such as search-and-destroy missions, napalm, and Agent Orange alienated civilians and boosted Viet Cong support ($\\checkmark$). Events like the My Lai Massacre further damaged U.S. credibility. Later, Vietnamization failed due to ARVN weakness, becoming a withdrawal strategy rather than a path to sustainable victory ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, despite technological advantages, U.S. military strategies were ultimately ineffective. They failed to adapt to guerrilla warfare, alienated the civilian population, and could not build a stable South Vietnamese force. This lack of strategic flexibility and cultural understanding contributed directly to the U.S. failure in Vietnam ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0905","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0905\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of the military strategies of the North Vietnamese and the Viet Cong in the Vietnam War (1956-1975).\n[7 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with a conclusion:\n\nHighly effective guerrilla warfare tactics:\n- Use of guerrilla tactics by the Viet Cong: Avoided direct confrontation with superior U.S. forces. Relied on hit-and-run attacks, ambushes, booby traps, and sniper fire. Created a constant psychological strain on U.S. and ARVN troops. Frustrated conventional military strategy and prevented U.S. forces from gaining territorial control.\n- Blending in with the civilian population: Viet Cong operated without uniforms, making it hard to distinguish them from non-combatants. Enabled surprise attacks and minimized exposure to enemy firepower. Led to U.S. mistrust of civilians, fueling brutal countermeasures that alienated local populations.\n- Effective use of underground tunnel networks: Complex tunnel systems (e.g., Cu Chi tunnels) used for hiding, moving, and storing supplies. Enabled Viet Cong to launch attacks and disappear before U.S. forces could retaliate. Tunnels also served as living quarters, hospitals, and command centers.\n\nStrong strategic coordination and supply lines:\n- Ho Chi Minh Trail: Vital supply route through Laos and Cambodia connecting North to South. Despite heavy U.S. bombing, it was constantly repaired and expanded, often by hand. Enabled the steady flow of troops, weapons, and supplies to Viet Cong forces in the South. Demonstrated logistical resilience and commitment.\n- North-South coordination between NVA and Viet Cong: North Vietnam provided training, leadership, and strategic planning. Allowed for major joint offensives like the Tet Offensive (1968) and Easter Offensive (1972).\n\nPsychological and ideological effectiveness:\n- Strong ideological motivation: Framed the war as a struggle for national liberation from foreign imperialists. Gained popular support among peasants and urban nationalists alike. Morale remained high despite massive casualties.\n- Propaganda and political education: Used to motivate fighters and civilians to endure hardship.n Portrayed the U.S. and the South Vietnamese government as corrupt and oppressive.\n\nStrategic offensives and timing:\n- Tet Offensive (1968): Coordinated surprise attacks on over 100 cities and military bases during Vietnamese New Year. Temporarily seized U.S. Embassy in Saigon, shocking American public. Military defeat for the Viet Cong, but strategic success, undermining U.S. claims of progress. Turned American public opinion against the war and led to policy shifts (Vietnamization).\n- Easter Offensive (1972): Large-scale conventional invasion by the NVA. Tested Vietnamization and strained ARVN capabilities. Although repelled with U.S. air support, it showed that North Vietnam could mount conventional offensives and wear down Southern defenses.\n- Final Ho Chi Minh Campaign (1975): Rapid and well-coordinated offensive that led to the fall of Saigon. Took advantage of the collapse in ARVN morale and reduced U.S. support. Demonstrated strategic patience and long-term planning by North Vietnamese leadership.\n\nLimitations and costs of their strategy:\n- High casualty rates: North Vietnam and the Viet Cong were willing to accept extremely high losses. The war effort depended on mass mobilization and sacrifice, which could have failed without sustained morale and ideological commitment.\n- Dependence on Soviet and Chinese support: Relied heavily on military aid from communist allies. While effective, it showed they couldn't have sustained large-scale warfare without foreign backing.\n\nConclusion:\n- Strengths: Adaptability, resilience, ideological commitment, use of terrain and local knowledge, long-term strategic thinking, and political unity. Effectively undermined U.S. military superiority and outlasted American political will.\n- Weaknesses: Extremely high casualties, dependency on external aid, and occasional military failures (e.g., post-Tet heavy losses).\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Viet Cong avoided direct battles, instead relying on guerrilla tactics such as ambushes, booby traps, and hit-and-run attacks ($\\checkmark$). These methods wore down U.S. and ARVN forces both physically and psychologically. Their ability to blend with civilians made them difficult to detect, disrupting U.S. operations and complicating counterinsurgency efforts. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nSecondly, the Ho Chi Minh Trail was a crucial logistical success, enabling North Vietnam to sustain the Viet Cong in the South ($\\checkmark$). Despite intense U.S. bombing, it was constantly rebuilt, showing remarkable organization and endurance. Close coordination between the Viet Cong and North Vietnamese Army also allowed for major joint offensives when needed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, while the North suffered high casualty rates, their fighters remained committed due to strong nationalist and ideological motivation ($\\checkmark$). The willingness to endure enormous losses demonstrated the depth of their resolve. This moral strength allowed them to continue fighting long after the U.S. expected them to collapse, ultimately outlasting their opponent ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, North Vietnamese and Viet Cong military strategies were highly effective. Their use of guerrilla tactics, logistical resilience, and ideological commitment allowed them to counter superior U.S. firepower. Despite heavy losses, their adaptability and endurance proved decisive in achieving reunification and forcing the withdrawal of American forces ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0907","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0907\n\nDescribe **three** examples of guerrilla tactics used by the Viet Minh against French forces during the French Indochina War (1946-1954).\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nNature of Guerrilla Warfare\n- Avoidance of conventional battles: Viet Minh rarely engaged French forces in large-scale open combat unless the odds were favorable. Focused on wearing down the enemy over time.\n- Small, mobile combat units: Operated in small, flexible groups that could strike quickly and retreat before French forces could respond.\n- Hit-and-run attacks: Targeted French patrols, outposts, and convoys to cause continuous disruption and psychological stress.\n- Ambushes and surprise raids: Took advantage of terrain and local knowledge to ambush isolated or vulnerable French units. Often occurred in remote or jungle-covered areas.\n- Use of booby traps and mines: Simple yet effective traps slowed down French movement and demoralized troops.\n\nUse of Terrain and Local Support\n- Familiarity with the terrain: Viet Minh fighters used Vietnam's dense forests, mountains, and river systems to their advantage.\n- Extensive tunnel networks and hidden supply routes: Enabled fighters to hide, store weapons, and launch attacks undetected.\n- Blending in with local populations: Fighters wore no uniforms and relied on the support of rural villagers for shelter, supplies, and information. Made it difficult for the French to distinguish between combatants and civilians.\n- Use of local militias ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people's war\"): Mobilized ordinary citizens to assist in guerrilla operations and create a climate of constant resistance.\n\nTactics of Attrition and Psychological Warfare\n- Gradual weakening of French forces (war of attrition): Persistent guerrilla attacks slowly wore down French morale and resources. Forced the French to spread their troops thin across large rural areas.\n- Psychological impact on French soldiers: Constant threat of ambush or booby traps led to fatigue and fear. Contributed to a growing sense that the war was unwinnable.\n- Disruption of French supply lines and communication: Guerrilla fighters often attacked roads, bridges, and convoys to isolate French outposts and restrict movement\n\nEvolution Over Time and Strategic Impact\n- Gradual shift to more conventional tactics: By the early 1950s, especially under General Giap, Viet Minh forces began incorporating larger, more coordinated attacks (e.g., Dien Bien Phu), but guerrilla tactics remained foundational.\n- Enabled long-term resistance: Guerrilla warfare allowed the Viet Minh to sustain their campaign over eight years despite being outgunned and out-financed.\n- Forced the French to overextend: The French were drawn into remote areas where they were vulnerable and lacked local support.\n- Helped pave the way for key victories: Constant pressure from guerrilla warfare contributed to weakening French positions leading up to the decisive Battle of Dien Bien Phu in 1954.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Viet Minh relied on guerrilla tactics that deliberately avoided large-scale battles unless they had a clear advantage. Instead, they operated in small, mobile units capable of launching hit-and-run attacks and surprise raids. These tactics kept French forces constantly off-balance, making it difficult for them to engage the enemy directly or maintain territorial control ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the Viet Minh exploited their terrain and local support. Familiarity with Vietnam's challenging geography, its forests, rivers, and mountains, allowed the Viet Minh to launch attacks and disappear quickly. They used simple tunnel systems and hidden supply routes to store arms and regroup in secret. By blending in with local villagers and relying on their support for supplies and intelligence, they made it nearly impossible for the French to distinguish between fighters and civilians ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the Viet Minh aimed to wear down French strength over time. Frequent attacks on French patrols, infrastructure, and supply routes strained resources and forced the French to spread their troops across vulnerable areas. The constant threat of ambushes and traps had a powerful psychological effect, lowering morale and contributing to growing doubts about the war's viability($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0908","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0908\n\nOutline the significance of the Battle of Dien Bien Phu (1954) for both the Viet Minh and French colonial rule in Indochina.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**Four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both the Viet Minh and French perspectives are addressed.\n\nSignificance for the Viet Minh:\n- Decisive military victory: Demonstrated the Viet Minh's ability to defeat a major colonial power in a large-scale conventional battle. Boosted morale and validated General Vo Nguyen Giap's military strategy of transitioning from guerrilla warfare to set-piece battles.\n- Proof of military and logistical capability: Successfully transported heavy artillery and maintained supply lines in remote mountainous terrain. Showed strategic sophistication and determination.\n- Strengthened Ho Chi Minh's political position: Gave the Viet Minh greater international legitimacy as the primary representative of Vietnamese nationalism and resistance. Bolstered Ho Chi Minh's role as a leader of a future independent Vietnam.\n- Catalyst for diplomatic negotiations: Forced France to the negotiating table, leading directly to the Geneva Conference. Enabled the Viet Minh to secure control over North Vietnam as part of the Geneva Accords.\n- Symbol of anti-colonial resistance: Inspired nationalist and anti-imperialist movements throughout Asia and Africa. Became a rallying point for the global decolonization movement.\n\nSignificance for French Colonial Rule:\n- End of French military presence in Indochina: Marked the collapse of France's attempt to reassert colonial control in Vietnam after WWII. Led to France's withdrawal from Indochina entirely.\n- Psychological and political shock in France: Humiliating defeat that damaged national pride and exposed the limits of colonial power. Contributed to political instability in the French Fourth Republic.\n- Loss of colonial credibility: Undermined France's reputation as a colonial power and hastened the decline of its empire. Signaled to other colonies that colonial regimes could be defeated militarily.\n- Trigger for the Geneva Accords (1954): Forced France to negotiate a ceasefire and temporary division of Vietnam at the 17th parallel. Marked a transition from colonial conflict to Cold War confrontation in Southeast Asia.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFor the Viet Minh, the battle was a decisive military victory that showcased their growing strength and strategic capability. Under General Vo Nguyen Giap, the Viet Minh surrounded and overwhelmed a well-defended French base in a remote valley, demonstrating their ability to conduct large-scale operations and transport heavy artillery through difficult terrain ($\\checkmark$). This victory boosted morale and solidified Ho Chi Minh's political leadership, while also forcing France to the negotiating table at the Geneva Conference ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFor France, the defeat was a devastating blow to its colonial ambitions. It exposed the limits of French military power and led to the end of French involvement in Vietnam ($\\checkmark$). The loss also shocked the French public, contributed to political instability at home, and hastened the decline of the French empire ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1156","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1156\n\nIdentify **three** key differences between Maoism and traditional Marxist-Leninism.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nRevolutionary Base\n- Maoism: Revolution led by the peasants in rural areas.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Revolution led by the urban working class (proletariat).\n\nNature of Revolution\n- Maoism: Believed in continuous revolution to prevent corruption.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Focused on seizing power once and consolidating socialism.\n\nRole of the Masses\n- Maoism: Emphasised mass participation and the “mass line” (listening to the people).\n- Marxist-Leninism: Relied on a centralised vanguard party to lead the people.\n\nEconomic Development\n- Maoism: Favoured self-reliance and small-scale rural industry.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Focused on state-led industrialisation and central planning.\n\nAttitude Toward Peasantry\n- Maoism: Viewed peasants as the main revolutionary force.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Considered peasants less revolutionary and sought rapid urbanisation.\n\nInternational Outlook\n- Maoism: Promoted national independence and Third World revolutions.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Promoted Soviet-led international communism.\n\nCultural and Ideological Emphasis\n- Maoism: Saw culture and thought reform as central to class struggle.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Prioritised economic structure over cultural change.\n\nRelationship with the USSR\n- Maoism: Criticised the USSR for “revisionism” and dependence on the West.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Accepted Soviet leadership and peaceful coexistence with the West (under Khrushchev).\n\nAdaptation to Context\n- Maoism: Adapted Marxism to agrarian, developing societies.\n- Marxist-Leninism: Applied mainly to industrial, urban societies.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMaoism saw the rural peasantry as the main revolutionary force capable of overthrowing feudal and capitalist systems. In contrast, Marxist-Leninism centred on the urban working class (proletariat) as the revolutionary vanguard ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, Maoism advocated mass mobilisation and political participation as the source of revolutionary energy. Marxist-Leninism stressed centralised control by a disciplined vanguard party of professional revolutionaries ($\\checkmark$). Finally, Maoism favoured small-scale, labour-intensive industry (e.g., “backyard furnaces” during the Great Leap Forward). In contrast, Marxist-Leninism favoured state-led industrialisation and heavy industry as the foundation of socialism ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1157","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1157\n\nOutline **two** impacts of Khrushchev's $\\lq$Secret Speech' (1956) on Mao Zedong.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n\nIdeological Shock and Threat to Mao's Legitimacy\n- Khrushchev's criticism of Stalin's cult of personality challenged Mao's own leadership style and authority.\n- Mao viewed de-Stalinisation as “revisionist” and feared it would weaken revolutionary ideology in China.\n\nDeterioration of Sino-Soviet Relations\n- Mao resented Khrushchev's actions as disrespectful and saw the speech as a betrayal of true Marxism.\n- This ideological rift deepened tensions, paving the way for the Sino-Soviet split (1959-1960) and the withdrawal of Soviet aid.\n\nDomestic Consequences in China\n- The speech encouraged Mao to launch the Hundred Flowers Campaign (1956) to show China's ideological unity.\n- When criticism grew, Mao reversed course with the Anti-Rightist Campaign (1957) to reassert control and loyalty to his leadership.\n\nAdditional Impacts\n- Motivated the Great Leap Forward (1958) as Mao sought to prove China's revolutionary superiority.\n- Reinforced Mao's emphasis on continuous revolution and resistance to Soviet-style reform or liberalisation.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nKhrushchev's denunciation of Stalin's cult of personality directly undermined Mao's leadership style, which also centred on personal authority and ideological purity ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, Mao saw de-Stalinisation as an attack on Marxist-Leninist orthodoxy and revolutionary principles, laying the foundation for the later Sino-Soviet split (1959-1960) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1158","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1158\n\nExplain **three** effects of Mao's victory in the Chinese Civil War (1949) on the Cold War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n\nShift in the Global Balance of Power\n- China's communist victory greatly expanded the reach of communism, shifting the balance toward the Soviet bloc.\n- The “loss of China” shocked the West and deepened Cold War divisions between communist and capitalist powers.\n\nImpact on U.S. Foreign Policy\n- The U.S. faced domestic backlash for “losing China,” leading to stronger anti-communist sentiment and McCarthyism.\n- America intensified its containment strategy in Asia, forming alliances and backing non-communist regimes in Korea, Vietnam, and Taiwan.\n\nImpact on the Soviet Union and Communist Bloc\n- The USSR gained a major new ally in Asia, forming the Sino-Soviet alliance (1950) and strengthening the communist front.\n- China's revolution inspired other Asian communist movements, though rivalry over leadership later emerged between Moscow and Beijing.\n\nImpact on the Asian Cold War\n- Mao's victory made Asia a central arena of Cold War conflict, extending superpower competition beyond Europe.\n- The U.S. adapted containment into a global policy, responding to rising communist influence in the region.\n\nLong-Term Significance\n- China's rise introduced a third major communist power, complicating the bipolar structure of the Cold War.\n- Mao's revolutionary model inspired anti-colonial and communist movements worldwide, expanding the Cold War into Asia and Africa.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nDue to Mao's victory, there was a shift in the global balance of power ($\\checkmark$) and the ideological reach of communism was dramatically expanded as the world's most populous nation came under communist control ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, US foreign policy moved toward stronger containment in Asia ($\\checkmark$). This can be seen in the formation of military alliances such as SEATO (1954) as well as support for anti-communist regimes in Korea, Vietnam, and Taiwan ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the victory contributed to the spread of the Cold War ($\\checkmark$), as it inspired communist movements in other countries in Asia, such as Vietnam, Malaya and Indonesia ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1159","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1159\n\nExplain **three** ways Nixon's visit to China in 1972 affected relations between the United States and the Soviet Union.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with relevant explanation:\n- Nixon's visit to China heightened Soviet fears of U.S.-China cooperation and strategic encirclement, pressuring Moscow to improve relations with Washington.\n- It triggered a triangular diplomacy dynamic, where the U.S. could exploit tensions between Beijing and Moscow for diplomatic advantage.\n- The USSR, worried about isolation, accelerated efforts to negotiate arms control and détente with the U.S.\n- The visit contributed directly to the signing of the SALT I Treaty (1972) and the Anti-Ballistic Missile (ABM) Treaty, as the Soviets sought to stabilise relations.\n- Soviet leaders saw Nixon's opening to China as evidence of a shifting balance of power and sought to counter it through renewed engagement in Europe and the developing world.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe visit alarmed Soviet leaders, who feared a potential U.S.-China alliance and the risk of strategic encirclement ($\\checkmark$). As a result, Moscow reassessed its foreign policy and sought improved relations with Washington. ($\\checkmark$). Second, it created a new triangular balance of power ($\\checkmark$). The US was able to exploit tensions between Beijing and Moscow to its own advantage. By engaging separately with the communist powers, the U.S. gained greater diplomatic leverage in the Cold War ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the USSR, to avoid isolation, moved quickly to strengthen ties with the United States ($\\checkmark$). This drove renewed arms control negotiations and the formalisation of détente through agreements such as the SALT I Treaty (1972) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1160","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1160\n\nExplain **three** ways Mao's intervention in the Korean War (1950-1953) influenced Cold War superpower tensions.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with explanation:\n\nEscalation of Superpower Conflict\n- The intervention reversed U.N./U.S. advances and prolonged the war, demonstrating that communist forces could effectively challenge Western military power.\n- This marked the first direct military confrontation between communist and Western forces, intensifying U.S.-Soviet rivalry.\n- Mao's willingness to confront the U.S. convinced Western leaders that the Cold War had become a truly global and militarised conflict, not confined to Europe.\n\nImpact on U.S. Foreign and Military Policy\n- The Chinese intervention shocked the U.S., prompting a major military buildup and the globalisation of containment.\n- The U.S. quadrupled defence spending (NSC-68) and accelerated rearmament, framing communism as a monolithic/unified, expansionist threat directed by Moscow and Beijing.\n- Truman's administration expanded alliances and bases across Asia (e.g., ANZUS 1951, SEATO 1954).\n- Mao's actions also led to the U.S. commitment to defend Taiwan, increasing tensions in the Taiwan Strait.\n\nImpact on Sino-Soviet Relations\n- China's intervention elevated its status within the communist bloc as a powerful revolutionary force.\n- Stalin initially encouraged Mao's involvement indirectly, but was cautious about provoking direct Soviet confrontation with the U.S.\n- Mao's sacrifices in the war gave China a sense of independence and equal standing with the USSR, but also exposed unequal alliance dynamics with the USSR provided limited support and demanded repayment for aid. As such, the war laid the groundwork for future Sino-Soviet tensions, as Mao began to resent Soviet hesitancy and dominance.\n\nMilitarisation and Division of the Cold War\n- The Korean War, sustained by China's intervention, entrenched the division of Korea at the 38th parallel, symbolising the permanent bipolar structure of the Cold War.\n- It justified the long-term U.S. military presence in Asia and NATO's expansion in Europe.\n- Both superpowers increased nuclear development and readiness, pushing the Cold War into an arms race.\n- China's role solidified Asia as a central theatre of Cold War rivalry.\n\nLong-Term Significance\n- Mao's actions positioned China as a key Cold War actor and the leading communist power in Asia.\n- The conflict hardened ideological lines between capitalism and communism, ending hopes of U.S.-China cooperation.\n- It marked the beginning of the U.S. policy of containment in Asia, later extended to Vietnam and beyond.\n- The war also legitimised Mao's domestic authority, reinforcing his revolutionary credentials and aligning China's identity with global anti-imperial struggle.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, the Cold War escalated ($\\checkmark$). Mao's decision to send the Chinese People's Volunteer Army (1950) into Korea transformed a limited conflict into a major Cold War confrontation, prolonging the war and demonstrating that communist forces could effectively challenge Western military power ($\\checkmark$). Second, China's status was elevated within the communist bloc ($\\checkmark$) as it could now be seen as a powerful revolutionary force ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the intervention prompted a major U.S. military buildup and the globalisation of containment ($\\checkmark$). The U.S. quadrupled defence spending (NSC-68) and accelerated rearmament, viewing communism as a monolithic/unified, expansionist threat directed by Moscow and Beijing ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1162","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1162\n\nDescribe two ways China's involvement in the Korean War affected its economic development.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- War diverted scarce resources away from domestic reconstruction after the civil war.\n- Military mobilisation slowed industrial recovery and increased the strain on food and raw materials.\n- Soviet military aid created short-term dependency and debt obligation.\n- The conflict stimulated the development of China's defence industries and legitimised tighter central economic control.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nChina's involvement in the Korean War diverted vital resources away from domestic recovery after the civil war, slowing economic reconstruction ($\\checkmark$). However, the conflict also accelerated the growth of defence industries and justified greater central control over the economy, strengthening state planning and industrial capacity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1626","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1626\n\nWhat message is conveyed by Source A?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| $\\textbf{Source A}$ |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| | | $\\mbox{}\\\\[-30pts]$A propaganda poster, “Intellectuals Turn to Labour: Get Toughened in Practical Struggles, Get Rooted into the Masses of Workers and Peasants,” created by Ha Qiongwen in 1964, shortly before the Cultural Revolution (1966-1976). It reflects Mao Zedong's belief that intellectuals had been influenced by bourgeois ideas and needed to be ideologically reformed through manual labour alongside workers and peasants. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20Asia%20and%20Oceania/Unassigned/images/HI1626.jpg\" alt=\"HI1626\">\n[Source: Ha Qiongwen. (1964). *Intellectuals turn to labour: Get toughened in practical struggles, get rooted into the masses of workers and peasants.* Retrieved July 7, 2026 from https://www.flickr.com/photos/thomasfisherlibrary/8161103196/in/photostream/. No known restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Intellectuals should leave their privileged positions and work alongside workers and peasants.\n- Physical labour would strengthen the revolutionary commitment of intellectuals by making them tougher and more dedicated to communist ideals through practical struggle.\n- Intellectuals working alongside workers and peasants would help them understand ordinary people's lives and become more closely connected to the masses.\n- The poster is throwing positive light on Mao's policy of sending educated people to the countryside to be re-educated by the masses.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe image caption indicates that man is an intellectual who has left his privileged position to work alongside workers and peasants in order to better understand their practical struggles and strengthen his commitment to communist ideals ($\\checkmark$). By showing the challenging physical labour, it also implies that intellectuals have much to learn from the working class and peasants, which would help them become more integrated into socialist society ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the red paper in the man's pocket indicates that he is undergoing reeducation in Maoist ideology ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"History of the Americas","subtopics":[{"name":"Civil Rights and Social Movements","questions":[{"ref":"HI0392","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0392\n\nEvaluate the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement of the 1950s\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is “evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. \n\nPositive results: \n\n - It was responsible for taking legal steps to overturn laws that promoted racial segregation. For example, Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1954) seemed to remove all constitutional sanctions for racial segregation by overturning Plessy v. Ferguson.\n\n - It helped other groups even if it did not agree with their methods. For example, it posted bail for hundreds of Freedom Riders and gave legal and financial aid to students arrested in sit-ins. \n\n - It was the initial driving force behind the Montgomery bus boycott and Little Rock Nine in 1957.\n\nNegative results: \n\n - The strategy of using the court system was slow, so other leaders of the movement, such as Martin Luther King, wanted direct action and mass mobilization. This disagreement over tactics led to a division of the movement with the creation of the SCLC and the . \n\n - The NAACP's victories in the courtrooms did not always translate into real change for the people. For example, acceptance of the Brown ruling varied. In the urban South, desegregation was introduced relatively quickly, but schools remained segregated in the Deep South, especially in rural areas. \n\n - It could not always act freely in the states of the Deep South. For example, after helping to organize the Montgomery Bus Boycott, Alabama barred the NAACP from working in the state.\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s was positive \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the reasons why they were limited. \n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) had an important role in the civil rights movement during the 1950s. Its main tactic was to fight against segregation in the courts, and this proved successful in landmark cases like Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1954). With its ruling, the Supreme Court decision overturned Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), thus removing constitutional support for segregation in education. This legal triumph was a turning point in the fight for civil rights, setting a precedent that segregation was inherently unequal ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, although it was not its main focus, the NAACP participated in other types of actions during the 1950s that contributed to the civil rights movement. For example, the NAACP initiated events like the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1956 and the integration of Little Rock Central High School in 1957 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement during the 1950s was also limited. The slow pace of change through the court system frustrated many, leading to disagreements over tactics. Leaders like Martin Luther King Jr. called for direct action and mass mobilization, resulting in the formation of organizations such as the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, a legal victory did not always mean things improved for people in real life. For example, while the Brown decision called for the desegregation of schools, its implementation varied widely. In some urban areas, schools complied relatively quickly, while some districts in the Deep South remained segregated for many more years ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, local authorities often restricted the NAACP's ability to act. For example, after its involvement in organizing the Montgomery Bus Boycott, Alabama prohibited the organization from operating within the state ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe NAACP's contributions to the civil rights movement in the 1950s were significant, as its litigation tactic successfully attacked institutional racism and inspired broader activism. However, its achievements were limited by the slow pace of change, internal divisions within the movement, and resistance in regions like the Deep South ($\\checkmark$conclusion )."},{"ref":"HI0393","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0393\n\nProvide **two** arguments to justify the following statement: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Malcolm X was a controversial figure of the civil rights movement.”\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nArguments need evidence to back them up. **Two** of the following arguments with supporting evidence: \n\n - He was accused of incentivizing the use of violence. In the televised documentary $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Hate that Hate Produced” (1959), Malcolm X said blacks should defend themselves $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$by any means necessary.\"\n\n - His relationship with the Nation of Islam (NOI) ended badly. Even though it was a small religious sect until Malcolm X became its spokesman, he was suspended in 1963 and left the organization in 1964. In February 1965, Malcolm was assassinated by NOI gunmen. \n\n - He made controversial remarks after Kennedy's assassination. When asked by reporters to comment on the assassination of President Kennedy, Malcolm X said that it was a case of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$chickens coming home to roost\" and added that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$chickens coming home to roost never did make me sad; they've always made me glad.\" For his comments, he was suspended from preaching by the NOI.\n\n - It was said by many that his influence actually set back the civil rights movement. Time Magazine wrote an article after his death describing him as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a disaster to the civil rights movement,” and Martin Luther King said of him, \"urging Negroes to arm themselves and to prepare to engage in violence, as he has done, can reap nothing but grief.\"\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nToday, Martin Luther King and Malcolm X are remembered as some of the most influential figures of the civil rights movement.  However, at the time, Malcolm X was considered a very controversial figure. As part of the Black Power movement, he rejected the peaceful protests of other organizations ($\\checkmark$) and called national attention to himself by stating in the televised documentary $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Hate that Hate Produced” (1959) that African Americans should defend themselves “by any means necessary” ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the Nation of Islam, of whom he was the main spokesman, ended up suspending his preaching ($\\checkmark$). Malcolm X disagreed with some of the behaviour and beliefs of the leader of the NOI, Elijah Muhannad, and he left the organization in 1964. In February 1965, Malcolm was assassinated by NOI gunmen ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0394","marks":"30","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0394\n\nIdentify three central beliefs of the members of the Nation of Islam about the issue of racial segregation in the United States.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny three of the following:\n\n - Elijah Muhammad was a prophet of God (Allah)\n\n - African Americans should aim for separation, not integration, with the white Americans\n\n - Elijah Muhammad preached that white people are evil \n\n - Divine intervention would resolve race problems\n\n - Was against the the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$turn the other cheek\" philosophy of Christianity as a perpetuation of enslavement\n\n - Stressed racial pride and economic self-help as a way to improve the lives of African Americans\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Nation of Islam was an organization that mixed ideas of black nationalism with elements of Islam. Some of its beliefs included the idea that its leader, Elijah Muhammad was a prophet of Allah (God)($\\checkmark$). Elijah Muhammad preached that African Americans should aim for separation instead of integration ($\\checkmark$) since he considered white people as evil ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0395","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0395\n\nDescribe the Montgomery bus boycott (1955-1956).\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The objective was to end segregation on buses in Montgomery, Alabama, the state capital.\n\n - It was initially organized by the local branch of the NAACP, and then by the newly created Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), led by Martin Luther King. \n\n - It started just after the arrest of Rosa Parks, secretary of the local branch of the NAACP, for refusing to give up her seat to a white passenger on a crowded bus. \n\n - Most of the African American community in Montgomery, roughly 50,000 people, refused to use buses until their demands were met. \n\n - As a result, Montgomery buses were desegregated after the results of the federal court case Browder v. Gayle took effect,\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe main goal of the Montgomery bus boycott was to end racial segregation on Montgomery's public buses in Alabama ($\\checkmark$). The boycott was initially organized by the local NAACP branch but gained strength under the organization of the newly established Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), headed by Martin Luther King Jr. ($\\checkmark$). Partly as a result of the boycott, Montgomery buses were desegregated although it took the decision of the federal courts with the case Browder v. Gayle for desegregation to officially take effect ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0396","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0396\n\nDescribe the Freedom Rides of 1961.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following (or a combination):\n\n - Small, racially integrated groups of activists travelled the South testing Supreme Court rulings in favor of desegregation on interstate transport and interstate bus facilities.\n\n - Initially organized by CORE (Congress of Racial Equality - an interracial American organization established by James Farmer in 1942 to improve race relations and end discriminatory policies through direct-action projects) but eventually included the participation of the  SCLC and SNCC as well\n\n - The Freedom Rides generated the violent response of white racism\n\n - They led Attorney General Robert Kennedy and the Interstate Commerce Commission to enforce the Supreme Court rulings on desegregated interstate travel in November 1961\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Freedom Rides of 1961 were a movement that consisted of small, racially integrated groups of students who traveled on buses throughout the Southern United States to test Supreme Court rulings in favor of desegregation on interstate transport and bus facilities ($\\checkmark$). It was initially organized by the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), but eventually had the support of other organizations like the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) and the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0397","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0397\n\nExplain the significance of the Montgomery bus boycott for the civil rights movement. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - It demonstrated the power of a community using direct but non-violent action. \n\n     - Most of the 50.000 members of the African American community of Montgomery participated in the boycott for more than a year. \n\n - It demonstrated the importance of the Churches in the fight for equality.\n\n     - Martin Luther King Jr.  was the minister of the Dexter Avenue Baptist Church which served as the backbone of the organization of the boycott. \n\n - This form of protest served as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$model\" for other grass-roots demonstrations. \n\n     - There were similar successful bus boycotts in 20 Southern cities. For example, the Tallahassee Bus Boycott of 1956. \n\n - It led to more Northern white support and more co-operation between Northern and Southern blacks.\n\n - It showed the economic importance of African Americans.\n\n     - White business men began to work against segregation because the boycott cost businesses around \\$1 million in sales since shoppers could not get downtown for lack of transportation. \n\n - It brought King, with his powerful rhetoric, to the forefront of the movement. In 1957 he helped to establish a new organization, the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC).\n\n - It showed the effectiveness of the NAACP strategy of working through the US legal system. \n\n     - It was only after the Supreme Court backed the federal district court in Browder v. Gayle (1956) that buses were desegregated. \n\n - Historiography debate:\\\n\n    - David Garrow (1994) says the Montgomery Bus Boycott marked the start of the civil rights movement. \n\n    - In contrast, Mark Newman (2004) says it $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$did not spark a mass movement\".\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Montgomery Bus Boycott that took place for almost a year in 1955-1956 was highly significant for the civil rights movement. One of the main reasons for this was that it showed the importance of unity of the community to achieve success in direct and non-violent action ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$). In Montgomery, almost all 50,000 members of the African American community participated in the boycott of the city buses ($\\checkmark$). Due to this, the boycott was able to prove the economic importance of the African American community since the boycott led to an estimated \\$1 million in lost sales since people could not go downtown to shop ($\\checkmark$). This economic power forced many white businessmen to start opposing segregation ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAccording to historian David Garrow (1994), the Montgomery Bus Boycott was one of the most important events of the civil rights movement as he considers it marked the start of the movement ($\\checkmark$). However, Mark Newman (2004) concludes it was not so significant since it $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$did not spark a mass movement\" as the next big mass action event was many years later, with the Freedom Rides of 1961 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0398","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0398\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the reasons for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An introduction about Lyndon B. Johnson's speech to Congress titled “The American Promise” (1965). With this speech, he called for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Published on the website of the U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany. |\n\n[Source: *U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany.* (n.d.). Lyndon B. Johnson from “Basic Readings in US Democracy.” Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://usa.usembassy.de/etexts/democrac/40.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Though the 1964 Civil Rights Act called for an end to state discrimination in voter registration, the Justice Department did not have the means to enforce it, so more measures were necessary.\n- In March, Martin Luther King organized a march on Selma, Alabama, to dramatize the need for a voting rights bill. The violence used by Alabama officials on the peaceful protesters generated a national crisis with tens of thousands of volunteers heading south to join King.\n- Johnson delivered a powerful speech to Congress that called for a respect of American democracy, urging it to pass the bill.\n- Both the public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills led to the passing of the Voting Rights Act\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to the source, the passing of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 was because the provisions of the previous Civil Rights Act of 1964 did not give enough power to the federal government to make sure ending discrimination in voter registration was achieved ($\\checkmark$). Due to this failure, Martin Luther King Jr. organized a march on Selma, Alabama to show the need for a voting rights bill. The violent response to the march by white racists led to a national crisis and showed the need for further federal government action ($\\checkmark$). Johnson was able to convince Congress to pass the Voting Rights Act thanks to a speech he gave appealing to the need to respect American democracy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0399","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0399\n\nWhat does the source suggest about the NAACP?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Poster by the National Association For The Advancement Of Colored People, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$People is what the NAACP is about.\" Created between 1965 and 1980. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/Civil%20Rights%20and%20Social%20Movements/images/HI0399.jpg\" alt=\"HI0399\">\n\n[Source: National Association for the Advancement Of Colored People (NAACP). (Between 1965 and 1980). *People is what the NAACP is about*. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/item/2015648062/.CC-BY 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It is a nationwide organization\n- It represents the people of the country\n- It needs more support\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom the source we can learn that the NAACP was a nationwide organization since we can see in the poster the map of the continental United States ($\\checkmark$). We can also learn that the NAACP needed more members since the poster calls for people to “join the NAACP” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0400","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0400\n\nWhat does the source suggest about the March on Selma?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Photograph by Stanley Wofson for the newspaper New York World Telegram & Sun, March 1965. This Photograph shows an African American man carrying a child on his shoulders with a placard telling President Johnson to go to Selma, Alabama. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/Civil%20Rights%20and%20Social%20Movements/images/presjohnson%20selma.jpg\" alt=\"presjohnson selma\">\n[Source: Wofson, S. (March, 1965). *President Johnson go to Selma now!* World Telegram & Sun. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2005685852/. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It required the president`s help\n- The situation was urgent as it calls for the president's action “now!”\n- It was peaceful as there were young children participating\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that marchers at Selma were requesting the president's help as we see one of them carrying a poster that says “President Johnson go to Selma now!”($\\checkmark$). From the inclusion of the word “now!” in the phrase, we can also infer that the situation is urgent ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0401","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0401\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of the source for an historian studying the March on Selma of 1965.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Photograph by Stanley Wofson for the newspaper New York World Telegram & Sun, March 1965. This Photograph shows an African American man carrying a child on his shoulders with a placard telling President Johnson to go to Selma, Alabama. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/Civil%20Rights%20and%20Social%20Movements/images/presjohnson%20selma%20-%20opcvl.jpg\" alt=\"presjohnson selma - opcvl\">\n[Source: Wofson, S. (March, 1965). *President Johnson go to Selma now!* World Telegram & Sun. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2005685852/. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\nOrigin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The source provides insight as it was made in 1965 by a photographer who was present at the march on Selma (origin).\n- The source seeks to show what was going on during the March on Selma since the photograph was published in the newspaper The New York World Telegram & Sun (purpose).\n- We can learn from the source that the March was organized with peaceful intentions as the man portrayed is carrying a small child. We also can see that, at least this individual, was calling for Johnson's presence (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The source has a limited perspective of what the photographer was pointing at when he took the picture. The picture was probably taken at the beginning of the march before the situation turned violent (origin).\n- The source seeks to convey the urgency of Johnson's actions by only focusing on this particular participant's sign (purpose).\n- The source portrays a very limited angle of the march and leaves out other demands of the people who were there as well as the impact of the March itself (content).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable in its origin since it has insight into the situation at Selma as the photograph was taken in 1965 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose is also valuable since the photographer wanted to document what was happening at the March for the newspaper The New York World Telegram & Sun ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content of the source is valuable as we can learn that the march was organized with peaceful intentions, as evidenced by the man depicted carrying a small child. Additionally, the photograph shows that at least one individual was calling for President Johnson's involvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the March on Selma of 1965. First, its origin is limited since it only shows the perspective the photographer chose to focus on. Moreover, the photograph was obviously taken before the situation turned violent, so it has limited information on the whole development of the March ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose is limited since the photograph seeks to emphasize the need for President Johnson to act by concentrating solely on one character’s sign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Since the picture provides a narrow view of the march, its content is limited since the other demands of the marchers are left out, as is the overall impact of the March itself ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0402","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0402\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An introduction about Lyndon B. Johnson's speech to Congress titled “The American Promise” (1965). With this speech, he called for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Published on the website of the U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany. |\n\n[Source: *U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany.* (n.d.). Lyndon B. Johnson from “Basic Readings in US Democracy.” Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://usa.usembassy.de/etexts/democrac/40.htm. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the online textbook $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$US History\" by P. Scott Corbett, Volker Janssen, John M. Lund, Todd Pfannestiel, Sylvie Waskiewicz, and Paul Vickery, 2015.|\n\n:box[Protecting African Americans' right to vote was as important as ending racial inequality in the United States. In January 1964, the Twenty-Fourth Amendment, prohibiting the imposition of poll taxes on voters, was finally ratified. Poverty would no longer serve as an obstacle to voting. Other impediments remained, however. Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance, and protests against this interference often met with violence. On March 7, 1965, a planned protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into “Bloody Sunday” when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas. Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/Civil%20Rights%20and%20Social%20Movements/images/Screenshot%202025-02-08%20t%2014.2.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-02-08 t 14.2\">Deeply disturbed by the violence in Alabama and the refusal of Governor George Wallace to address it, Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause. His proposal, the Voting Rights Act of 1965, prohibited states and local governments from passing laws that discriminated against voters on the basis of race. Literacy tests and other barriers to voting that had kept ethnic minorities from the polls were thus outlawed. Following the passage of the act, a quarter of a million African Americans registered to vote, and by 1967, the majority of African Americans had done so. Johnson's final piece of civil rights legislation was the Civil Rights Act of 1968, which prohibited discrimination in housing on the basis of race, color, national origin, or religion. \\\n]\n\n[Source: Corbett, P.S., Janssen, V., Lund, J.M., Pfannestiel, T., Waskiewicz, S., and Vickery, P. (2015). *US History.*  Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://openstax.org/books/us-history/pages/29-2-lyndon-johnson-and-the-great-society. CC-BY-SA.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources agree that there was white resistance to the voter registration of Africans Americans\n    - Source C “the continuing resistance of southern leaders kept black registration low”\n    - Source D “Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance”\n- During the March on Selma, government employees attacked the marchers.\n    - Source C “protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into “Bloody Sunday” when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas”\n    - Source D “Alabama officials brutally attacked the marchers”\n- The violence at Selma was broadcast on TV to the whole nation.\n    - Source C “Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.”\n    - Source D “police violence, shown on national television, sickened the country”\n- President Johnson was instrumental in the passage of legislation that would allow African Americans to effectively be able to register to vote.\n    - Source C “The combination of public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills brought Congress to pass the voting rights bill on August 5, 1965.”\n    - Source D “Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause.”\n- The crisis at Selma was one of the reasons that motivated Johnson's actions.\n    - Source C “In the midst of the crisis, the president delivered the following message to Congress.”\n    - Source D “Deeply disturbed by the violence in Alabama and the refusal of Governor George Wallace to address it Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause”\n- Voter registration of African Americans greatly increased after the passing of the bill.\n    - Source C “In Mississippi, the state with the worst voting registration record, enrollment of black voters jumped from 6 to 44 percent in three years.”\n    - Source D “ Following the passage of the act, a quarter of a million African Americans registered to vote”\n- The March on Selma was a planned event.\n    - Source C “Martin Luther King led a march on Selma, Alabama, to dramatize the need for a voting rights bill.”\n    - Source D “a planned protest march from Selma, Alabama”\n- The new law increased the involvement of the federal government in the local registration process.\n    - Source C “The new law, known either as the Civil Rights Act of 1965 or as the Voting Rights Act of 1965, brought an unprecedented federal intrusion into local affairs,”\n    - Source D “Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While Source C credits Johnson as well as civil rights workers and leaders, Source D only gives credit to Jonson for the passing of the Voting Rights Act.\n    - Source C “The credit belongs not only to the civil rights workers who put their bodies and their lives on the line, and to the civil rights leaders like Martin Luther King Jr., who aroused public consciousness, but in large measure to Lyndon Johnson.”\n    - Source D “His proposal, the Voting Rights Act of 1965, prohibited states and local governments from passing laws that discriminated against voters on the basis of race. Literacy tests and other barriers to voting that had kept ethnic minorities from the polls were thus outlawed.”\n- While Source C highlights the violence at Selma was shown nationwide and to the rest of the world while Source D only mentions national television.\n    - Source C “Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.”\n    - Source D “police violence, shown on national television”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C and D share many similar ideas regarding the passing of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Both say that there was significant white resistance to the voter registration of African Americans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C notes that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the continuing resistance of southern leaders kept black registration low,\" while Source D states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The sources also agree that this resistance was evident during the March on Selma where government employees violently attacked the marchers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C describes how the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into $\\lq$Bloody Sunday' when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas,” and Source D similarly states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Alabama officials brutally attacked the marchers\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The two sources also agree on the fact that the violence at Selma was seen by the whole nation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C explains, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation\" while Source D emphasizes that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$police violence, shown on national television, sickened the country\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe two sources also agree on the fact that President Johnson played a crucial role in the passage of legislation that allowed African Americans to register to vote effectively ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). According to Source C, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\nthe combination of public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills brought Congress to pass the voting rights bill on August 5, 1965.\" Similarly, Source D highlights that \"Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). However, the two sources differ in the others they credit for passage of the bill beyond the president ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source C says that along with LBJ, “the credit belongs … to the civil rights workers who put their bodies and their lives on the line, and to the civil rights leaders like Martin Luther King Jr., who aroused public consciousness.” In contrast, Source D only recognizes the impact of Johnson's “proposal”  as having put an end to local laws that “discriminated against voters on the basis of race” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0700","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0700\n\nWith reference to the expressions “RACIAL ACCORD” and “EXTREMISTS” in Source B, describe **two** characteristics of the US civil rights movement in the 1960s.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**  $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cartoon titled “A Flower Tries to Grow in Birmingham” by Jim Berryman published in 1963. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/Civil%20Rights%20and%20Social%20Movements/images/HI0700.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0700\">\n[Source: Berryman, J. (1963, May 14). *A Flower Tries to Grow in Birmingham*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved April 7, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/5743297. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following alongside own knowledge of historical context:\n- Racial accord may be understood as the initial progress of the US civil rights movement toward political equality.\n- Racial accord may be associated with growing societal pressure from non-violent protests.\n- Extremists may refer to the limited reach of legislative decisions beyond state-level.\n- Extremists may reflect the persistent violence exerted by US authorities or organizations in an attempt to hinder civil rights entitlements for African American people.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs seen in the growing flower in the cartoon, the engagement in non-violent demonstrations led by peaceful leaders such as Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. reflected initial progress toward pro-civil rights legislation for African-American people and wider social reach advocating for political equality ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAlthough peace initiatives initially gained traction in the legislature through anti-segregation agreements at the subnational level, persistent wider social violent actions such as riots and bombs by extremist groups further challenged the civil rights movement's aims ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0949","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0949\n\nExplain **two** methods used to disenfranchise African American voters in the South before 1965.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with explanation:\n- Literacy tests: Required potential voters to pass reading or civics tests that were often unfair and selectively administered.\n- Poll taxes: Imposed fees to vote, disproportionately affecting poor African Americans.\n- Grandfather clauses: Allowed those whose grandfathers could vote before the Civil War to bypass restrictions, thus excluding Black citizens.\n- Voter intimidation: Threats, violence, and job loss used to discourage voter registration or participation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, the implementation of literacy tests required individuals to demonstrate reading and comprehension skills before being allowed to vote ($\\checkmark$). In practice, the tests were applied selectively and unfairly, with white voters often being exempted or given easier versions, while Black voters faced complex or intentionally misleading questions. This created a significant barrier to voter registration ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother method was the use of poll taxes, which required citizens to pay a fee in order to cast a ballot ($\\checkmark$). These taxes disproportionately affected African Americans, who were more likely to live in poverty due to systemic economic discrimination. Since many could not afford to pay the tax, they were effectively excluded from voting ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0952","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0952\n\nExplain **three** key ways the federal government responded to resistance to school desegregation.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Judicial actions and court rulings\n    - Brown v. Board of Education II (1955): Follow-up to the original 1954 Brown decision. Directed schools to desegregate “with all deliberate speed.” Gave federal courts ongoing oversight of desegregation, though the vague language allowed Southern states to delay compliance.\n    - Court enforcement of desegregation orders: Federal courts issued injunctions and orders against school boards refusing to comply. Judges could hold local officials in contempt of court for noncompliance.\n- Executive actions and federal intervention\n    - Little Rock, Arkansas (1957): President Dwight D. Eisenhower deployed the 101st Airborne Division to escort the Little Rock Nine into Central High School after the Arkansas governor used the National Guard to block integration. Marked the first time since Reconstruction that federal troops were used to enforce African American civil rights.\n    - Department of Justice involvement: The DOJ increasingly intervened in desegregation cases, filing lawsuits and supporting civil rights plaintiffs. Used amicus curiae ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$friend of the court\") briefs to support integration.\n- Legislation\n    - Civil Rights Act of 1964: Title IV: Gave the federal government authority to file suits to desegregate public schools. Title VI: Allowed the government to withhold federal funds from school districts practising discrimination. Created the Office for Civil Rights (OCR) within the Department of Health, Education, and Welfare to monitor school compliance.\n    - Elementary and Secondary Education Act (1965): Provided federal funding to schools but tied it to compliance with desegregation mandates. Used financial incentives to encourage local school districts to implement integration.\n- Monitoring and compliance mechanisms\n    - Federal oversight of school districts: The federal government, often through the courts or the OCR, monitored desegregation plans and ensured districts made $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$good faith\" efforts to comply. Required submission of desegregation plans and regular progress reports.\n- Public statements and pressure\n    - Presidents (especially Kennedy and Johnson) used the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$bully pulpit” to support civil rights and encourage compliance with desegregation: JFK sent federal marshals to enforce court orders (e.g., University of Mississippi, 1962). LBJ pushed through legislation and linked civil rights to national unity and moral responsibility.\n    - Brown II (1955) ruling mandated that desegregation occur $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$with all deliberate speed,\" although the term was vague.\n    - Eisenhower's intervention in Little Rock (1957): Sent federal troops to enforce integration of Central High School.\n    - Civil Rights Act of 1964: Gave the federal government power to withhold funds from segregated schools.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne major way the federal government responded was through judicial action ($\\checkmark$). After the Supreme Court's decision in Brown v. Board of Education (1954), federal courts continued to oversee desegregation cases and issued orders to force school districts to comply. In Brown II (1955), the Court instructed schools to integrate with \"all deliberate speed,\" and federal judges later used their authority to hold districts accountable :span($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the federal government used executive power to enforce desegregation when state authorities refused to comply ($\\checkmark$). For example, in 1957, President Eisenhower sent federal troops to Little Rock, Arkansas, to protect nine African American students entering Central High School, directly confronting resistance from the state governor :span($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, federal legislation played a critical role :span($\\checkmark$). The Civil Rights Act of 1964 gave the federal government the power to cut funding to segregated schools and enabled the Department of Justice to bring legal action against non compliant districts. This increased pressure on local authorities to end school segregation and comply with federal law ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0953","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0953\n\nDefine redlining and explain **three** ways it was used to legitimise racial discrimination between 1954 and 1965.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor define:\n- Redlining was a form of racial discrimination in which banks and other financial institutions refused to offer services such as home loans, mortgages, or insurance to people living in certain areas, primarily because those neighbourhoods were inhabited by racial or ethnic minorities.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any **three** of the following:\n- Institutionalised housing segregation: Redlining provided a bureaucratic and financial justification for keeping African Americans confined to segregated, under-resourced neighbourhoods. Banks and federal programs refused to offer loans to Black families in redlined zones, while making loans easily available to white families in “green” or “desirable” areas.\n- Blocked access to suburbanisation: As white Americans moved to newly developed, federally subsidised suburbs, African Americans were excluded due to redlining and racially restrictive covenants. This deepened economic and social divides by denying Black families access to the same housing opportunities, wealth accumulation, and education systems.\n- Reinforced cycles of poverty: By cutting off investment in Black neighbourhoods, redlining led to urban decay, lower property values, and a lack of access to public services like quality schools, healthcare, and infrastructure. African Americans were effectively trapped in impoverished communities, unable to accumulate home equity or escape economic hardship.\n- Racial discrimination under the guise of “risk management”: Redlining framed racism as objective economic policy, legitimising racial segregation as a matter of sound business rather than overt prejudice. This provided a veneer of legality and professionalism to racial discrimination, making it harder to challenge.\n- Undermined desegregation efforts: Even after Brown v. Board of Education (1954) began to challenge school segregation, redlining reinforced residential segregation, which in turn perpetuated de facto school segregation. This limited the impact of legal victories by keeping communities and their schools racially divided.\n- Contributed to long-term wealth disparities: Homeownership is a major avenue for building wealth in the U.S.; by denying loans, redlining systematically excluded African Americans from wealth-building. These disparities have had long-lasting effects that persisted well beyond the official end of Jim Crow laws.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nRedlining was a discriminatory practice in which banks and federal housing agencies marked certain neighbourhoods, usually those with large African American populations, as “high-risk” areas on residential maps. While it appeared to be an economic assessment, redlining was in fact based on racial composition rather than financial risk, and it served to reinforce segregation and inequality ($\\checkmark$).\n\nRedlining legitimised racial discrimination by institutionalising residential segregation, preventing African American families from moving into white neighbourhoods or accessing loans to buy homes outside of redlined zones ($\\checkmark$). Second, it restricted economic mobility by cutting off opportunities for Black Americans to build wealth through homeownership, an option that remained available to most white families during the postwar suburban expansion ($\\checkmark$). Third, redlining helped justify unequal access to public services, including education, by ensuring that Black communities remained underfunded and isolated. These policies framed racial exclusion as a matter of financial prudence, masking systemic racism behind the language of economic risk ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0954","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0954\n\nIdentify **three** forms of nonviolent protest used during the Civil Rights Movement between 1954 and 1965.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Bus boycotts - e.g., Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955-56)\n- Sit-ins - e.g., Greensboro sit-ins (1960)\n- Freedom Rides - to test desegregation of interstate buses (1961)\n- Marches and demonstrations - e.g., March on Washington (1963), Selma to Montgomery marches (1965)\n- Voter registration drives - e.g., Mississippi Freedom Summer (1964)\n- Legal challenges - e.g., NAACP-supported court cases like Brown v. Board of Education (1954)\n- Use of media/publicity - strategic use of television, newspapers, and photographs to raise awareness\n- Economic boycotts - e.g., boycotting segregated businesses\n- Church-led activism and prayer pilgrimages - often coordinated by leaders like MLK\n- Non-violent civil disobedience - refusing to obey unjust laws peacefully\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, bus boycotts, most famously seen in the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1955-56, placed economic pressure on the bus system and contributed to the Supreme Court ruling that bus segregation was unconstitutional ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, sit-ins such as the Greensboro sit-in of 1960 challenged the legality and morality of segregation in public places ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, Freedom Rides drew national attention to the continued defiance of federal laws and helped prompt stricter enforcement of desegregation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0955","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0955\n\nExplain **three** ways the Student Non-Violent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) contributed to the Civil Rights Movement from 1960 to 1965.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Grassroots activism. Focused on empowering local communities, particularly young Black activists in the South.\n- Sit-ins. Helped coordinate and expand sit-ins after the Greensboro Four protest in 1960.\n- Freedom Rides. Participated in the 1961 Freedom Rides alongside CORE to challenge segregation on interstate buses.\n- Voter registration drives. Led campaigns in the Deep South, especially in Mississippi (e.g., 1964 Mississippi Freedom Summer).\n- Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party (MFDP). Helped organise this alternative political party to challenge the exclusion of Black voters in Mississippi.\n- March on Washington (1963). SNCC leader John Lewis gave a speech (moderated by other leaders for tone) representing youth activism.\n- Selma Voting Campaign (1965). Organised efforts in Selma prior to SCLC's involvement, showing its early leadership.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1961, SNCC members joined the Freedom Rides, risking violence and arrest to challenge the illegal segregation of interstate bus travel in the South ($\\checkmark$). These actions drew national attention to ongoing racial injustice and helped push federal enforcement of desegregation laws ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe SNCC also played a leading role in voter registration efforts, particularly in areas where white resistance was particularly extreme ($\\checkmark$).Its most notable campaign was the Mississippi Freedom Summer (1964), where volunteers worked to register Black voters and establish schools and community centres, despite threats and violence ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, SNCC helped form the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party (MFDP) to challenge the all-white Mississippi delegation at the 1964 Democratic National Convention ($\\checkmark$). This effort highlighted the exclusion of Black citizens from political representation and pressured the national Democratic Party to confront racism in its ranks ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0956","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0956\n\nIdentify and explain **three** main provisions of the Civil Rights Act of 1964.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Title II - Desegregation of Public Facilities. Outlawed segregation in public places (e.g., restaurants, hotels, theatres, parks).\n- Title III - Desegregation of Public Facilities by Government Agencies. Authorised the federal government to enforce desegregation in public facilities owned or operated by state and local governments.\n- Title IV - Desegregation of Public Schools. Directed the federal government to assist with the desegregation of schools, including legal support and funding.\n- Title VI - Federal Funds Cut for Discrimination. Banned discrimination by government agencies and organisations that received federal funding; violators could lose funding.\n- Title VII - Equal Employment Opportunity. Prohibited employment discrimination on the basis of race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin and established the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) to investigate complaints.\n- Voting rights enforcement (while strengthened further in the Voting Rights Act of 1965, the Civil Rights Act provided some federal oversight for voting discrimination, such as prohibiting unequal application of voter registration requirements).\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Act outlawed segregation in public places, including restaurants, hotels, theatres, and transportation terminals ($\\checkmark$). This meant that African Americans could no longer be legally denied service or access based on their race, helping to dismantle Jim Crow laws across the South ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, it banned employment discrimination based on race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin ($\\checkmark$). This was enforced through the creation of the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC), which investigated cases of unfair hiring and workplace practices ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, the Act barred discrimination by any institution receiving federal funds, including public schools and hospitals ($\\checkmark$). This allowed the federal government to withhold funds from segregated institutions, putting pressure on local authorities to comply with desegregation laws ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0957","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0957\n\nCompare and contrast the strategies and beliefs of Martin Luther King Jr and Malcolm X in achieving civil rights for African Americans.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3),\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Committed to Black empowerment and dignity. Both wanted to end white oppression and improve the social, political, and economic conditions of African Americans.\n- Challenged systemic racism. Each condemned the systemic discrimination that African Americans faced in housing, education, and employment.\n- Influential orators. Both used powerful speeches to inspire their followers and influence public opinion.\n- Supported Black pride and self-worth. Especially in later years, both emphasised African American identity and self-respect.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Philosophy of non-violence vs. self-defence\n    - King: Advocated non-violent civil disobedience, inspired by Gandhi and Christian theology.\n    - Malcolm X: Originally promoted self-defence “by any means necessary”, rejecting non-violence if attacked.\n- Views on integration\n    - King believed in racial integration and cooperation between races.\n    - Malcolm X (early): Advocated for Black separatism, believing that integration was impossible in a racist society.\n- Relationship with the U.S. political system\n    - King: Worked within the system, lobbying politicians and pushing for federal civil rights legislation (e.g., Civil Rights Act, Voting Rights Act).\n    - Malcolm X: Distrusted the system, viewing it as fundamentally corrupt; often rejected appealing to white institutions for justice.\n- Evolution over time\n    - Malcolm X's views evolved after 1964; he softened his stance on working with white allies and began advocating for global human rights.\n    - King also became more radical in his later years, focusing on economic justice and opposing the Vietnam War.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, both leaders utilised powerful public speeches to raise awareness and inspire their followers, thereby becoming national symbols of the civil rights movement ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, each sought to empower Black communities and condemned systemic racism in American society, although they approached the issue differently ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, there were important differences in their strategies and beliefs. King believed in nonviolent protest, using peaceful marches and civil disobedience to push for legal reforms such as the Civil Rights Act of 1964. In contrast, Malcolm X initially argued that African Americans should defend their rights “by any means necessary,” including violence if provoked ($\\checkmark$). Also, King welcomed white support and believed interracial cooperation was essential for progress. He worked closely with white activists and religious leaders and emphasised the shared moral responsibility of all Americans to fight racism. However, Malcolm X rejected alliances with white liberals or civil rights groups, believing that African Americans should build their independent power without interference. He viewed white involvement as potentially condescending or untrustworthy. However, this view evolved later in his life after his 1964 pilgrimage to Mecca, where he expressed greater openness to working with people of all races who supported human rights ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0958","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0958\n\nAnalyse **two** ways the Freedom Rides (1961) were significant in challenging segregation in the American South.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** with analysis.\n- Tested federal enforcement of desegregation rulings: Exposed the federal government's failure to uphold the law in Southern states and pressured the Kennedy administration to intervene and uphold civil rights rulings.\n- Exposed violent resistance in the South: Media coverage of attacks (e.g., firebombing in Anniston, beatings in Birmingham) shocked national and international audiences.\n- Increased federal involvement: Resulted in the Interstate Commerce Commission (ICC) issuing regulations (Nov 1961) banning segregation in interstate transport facilities.\n- Galvanised the Civil Rights Movement: Inspired greater activism and cooperation among groups (CORE, SNCC, SCLC) and proved young activists could take the lead in high-risk direct action.\n- Highlighted the courage of civil rights activists: Demonstrated the power of nonviolent protest even in the face of extreme danger, and raised the profile of student activism and nonviolent resistance.\n- Deepened North-South divide on civil rights: Caused tension between Southern local authorities and the federal government and forced national attention on the persistence of de facto segregation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Freedom Rides were significant because they directly challenged the federal government to enforce its desegregation rulings. Although the Supreme Court had ruled that segregation in interstate travel was unconstitutional, Southern states ignored these decisions ($\\checkmark$). When Freedom Riders were violently attacked in places like Anniston and Birmingham, it exposed the federal government's reluctance to act. This public pressure eventually led the Interstate Commerce Commission to issue regulations banning segregation in interstate bus terminals, making the Freedom Rides a turning point in federal enforcement of civil rights law ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Freedom Rides were also significant for the way they galvanised the Civil Rights Movement ($\\checkmark$). The bravery of the young Black and white activists, many of whom were students, inspired a new wave of grassroots protest. Groups like the SNCC gained momentum, and the rides demonstrated that strategic, nonviolent action, even in the face of violent opposition, could bring national attention to the issue of injustice. This helped expand the movement beyond courtrooms and into direct action campaigns across the South ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0959","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0959\n\nTo what extent does Source A explain the reasons for the Civil Rights Act of 1964?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Radio Coverage of President Lyndon Johnson's Remarks upon Signing the Civil Rights Act of 1964. |\n\n[Source: Johnson, L. B. (1964). *The Civil Rights Act of 1964: A Long Struggle for Freedom.* Retrieved July 13, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-rights-act/multimedia/johnson-signing-remarks.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both support from the Source and other factors must be addressed in the response.\n\nPoints from the source:\n- Moral imperative: Johnson appeals to morality, stating that “morality forbids” the continuation of racial discrimination.\n- Constitutional foundation: He cites the U.S. Constitution as a basis for equality; “Our Constitution… forbids it.”\n- Legacy of American values: Reference to the Declaration of Independence and belief that “all men are created equal.”\n- Historical injustice: Acknowledges that millions are denied rights “not because of their own failures, but because of the colour of their skin.”\n- Bipartisan political support: Johnson references the overwhelming support in Congress, showing political will for change.\n- Continuity with Kennedy's agenda: The bill was initially proposed by JFK, reinforcing a commitment to civil rights reform.\n\nPoints from Own Knowledge:\n- Grassroots activism: Civil rights campaigns such as the Birmingham Campaign (1963) and Freedom Summer (1964) increased public support and pressure on politicians.\n- Violent backlash and media exposure: Events like the Birmingham church bombing (1963) and police brutality against peaceful protesters drew national attention and sympathy.\n- International Cold War pressure: The U.S. faced global criticism for racial inequality, which undermined its image as a defender of democracy.\n- March on Washington (1963): Demonstrated mass support for civil rights and influenced legislative priorities.\n- Southern resistance to desegregation: Highlighted the need for strong federal legislation to override state inaction or obstruction.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- Good conclusions will likely argue that the source material only addresses a limited scope of the factors that influenced the passage of the bill.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne clear reason Johnson outlines in the speech is the moral obligation to end racial discrimination. He states, “Morality forbids it,” arguing that denying rights based on skin colour is fundamentally unjust. This moral framing positioned the legislation as not merely political, but a necessary ethical correction ($\\checkmark$). He also references the U.S. Constitution as a legal and ideological foundation for the Act, stating that “our Constitution... forbids it.” This demonstrates an appeal to shared national values and legal equality, strengthening the legitimacy of the legislation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the speech does not fully acknowledge the role of grassroots activism, which was instrumental in prompting federal action. Events like the Freedom Summer of 1964, which sought to register Black voters in Mississippi, exposed the violence and institutional racism still present in the South ($\\checkmark$). The murder of three civil rights workers during that campaign shocked the nation and increased pressure for reform. Additionally, media coverage of brutality, such as that seen in Birmingham in 1963, where police attacked peaceful demonstrators, particularly children, drew national and international condemnation. This helped build public and political support for comprehensive legislation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Johnson's speech effectively highlights the moral and constitutional justifications for the Act, it underrepresents the critical role of civil rights activism and public outrage in driving the legislation forward. Therefore, the source provides a partial explanation of the reasons behind the Civil Rights Act of 1964 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0960","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0960\n\nTo what extent does Source B support the argument that federal action was necessary to ensure civil rights progress?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | President John F. Kennedy's Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, June 11). *Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963.* Retrieved July 12, 2025 from https://www.jfklibrary.org/archives/other-resources/john-f-kennedy-speeches/civil-rights-radio-and-television-report-19630611. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both support for federal action and other important factors must be addressed in the response.\n\nPoints from the Source supporting federal action:\n- Persistent inequality despite existing rights: The source details severe racial disparities in education, income, and life expectancy, suggesting local/state efforts had failed.\n- Need for federal enforcement: References to military presence (e.g., “without having to be backed up by troops”) imply that only federal intervention could guarantee access to education.\n- Civil rights tied to national values: JFK frames civil rights as central to American identity and international credibility (e.g., “we do not ask for whites only” in Vietnam), this suggests national responsibility.\n- Failure of voluntary compliance: The fact that demonstrations were still necessary implies that federal law, rather than local goodwill, was essential.\n- Moral imperative from the presidency: As the highest executive office, JFK's public stance signals the need for top-down intervention.\n\nPoints suggesting other important factors:\n- Grassroots activism: The speech acknowledges public demonstrations, suggesting the impact of movements like sit-ins and Freedom Rides.\n- Media coverage: While not directly mentioned, JFK's timing aligns with intense media attention on Birmingham and other flashpoints.\n- Economic and social change: The rise of a Black middle class and urban migration shifted the political landscape.\n- International pressure: Cold War context (referenced in speech) created pressure for the U.S. to address domestic racial injustice for its global image.\n- Judicial progress: Earlier court cases like Brown v. Board of Education and Boynton v. Virginia were vital judicial steps outside of federal legislation.\n- Local and state variation: Some areas (e.g., in the North) had made more progress through local reforms, suggesting federal action wasn't the sole pathway.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nKennedy cites stark disparities in education, income, and employment, framing these as evidence of enduring racial injustice. His remark that it “ought to be possible... to attend any public institution… without having to be backed up by troops” directly implies that local governments and institutions could not be relied on to uphold equal rights, therefore requiring federal intervention ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, Kennedy connects civil rights to America's global reputation, referencing Vietnam and West Berlin to argue that the country cannot advocate freedom abroad while denying it at home. This alignment with national interests further justifies federal responsibility ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the speech also indirectly acknowledges the role of grassroots movements. The reference to demonstrations in the street reflects the pressure placed on the government by civil rights activists, especially those involved in the Birmingham Campaign and the upcoming Freedom Summer ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, judicial rulings such as Brown v. Board of Education and Boynton v. Virginia had already begun to erode legal segregation before federal civil rights legislation was passed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while the source supports the idea that federal action was vital to achieving civil rights progress, it also implies that activism, court decisions, and international dynamics were important contributing factors. Federal action may have been decisive, but it was not the only force driving change ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0961","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0961\n\nEvaluate the extent to which Source C represents typical attitudes of Southern white leaders in 1963.\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both typical and atypical attitudes must be addressed in the response.\n\nWays the speech represents attitudes of many Southern white leaders:\n- Commitment to segregation: Wallace's demand for “segregation now… segregation forever” echoes the positions of many Southern governors, legislators, and law enforcement officials.\n- Use of Confederate legacy: Invoking Jefferson Davis and “the Anglo-Saxon Southland” reflects common appeals to Southern identity and the “Lost Cause” narrative.\n- States' rights rhetoric: The speech frames federal civil rights legislation as “tyranny,” a view widely shared among white Southern elites who saw desegregation as federal overreach.\n- Political expedience: Many white leaders maintained segregation to gain political support among white voters who opposed integration.\n- Opposition to civil rights demonstrations: Like Wallace, many officials resisted civil rights campaigns with legislative, rhetorical, or violent means (e.g., Ross Barnett in Mississippi, Lester Maddox in Georgia).\n\nWays the speech does not fully represent all Southern white leaders:\n- Extremity of language: Wallace's explicit and theatrical rhetoric went further than many white leaders who maintained segregation more subtly or pragmatically.\n- Internal division: Some Southern white leaders were beginning to quietly moderate their stance by 1963 due to national pressure (e.g., Tennessee's Governor Buford Ellington cautiously cooperated with federal law).\n- Private reluctance: Some leaders may have privately opposed civil rights but avoided the kind of defiant posturing Wallace embraced in public.\n- Fear of federal consequences: Certain officials complied with court rulings or federal mandates not out of agreement but to avoid federal intervention (e.g., some school boards in Virginia).\n- Urban vs rural divide: White political leaders in more urbanised Southern areas were sometimes more pragmatic about gradual integration due to economic pressures.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWallace's declaration of “segregation now, segregation tomorrow, segregation forever” captures the deep resistance to racial integration that dominated Southern political life. His speech aligns with the views of other prominent figures such as Mississippi's Governor Ross Barnett, who similarly resisted desegregation, including standing in the way of James Meredith's enrollment at the University of Mississippi ($\\checkmark$). The speech’s appeal to “freedom-loving blood” and the Confederacy also mirrors the cultural and ideological defences many leaders used to justify maintaining segregation and opposing federal mandates ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, not all Southern leaders were as extreme in tone or as openly defiant. Some, like Arkansas Governor Orval Faubus, had begun to moderate their language under federal pressure by 1963, even if they still resisted integration ($\\checkmark$). Others complied with court rulings reluctantly, without the fiery appeals to Southern heritage that Wallace embraced. Furthermore, Wallace's theatrical delivery and explicit invocation of white supremacy were not universally adopted, especially by leaders in more economically progressive areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Wallace's speech was emblematic of mainstream white Southern political resistance to civil rights, its intensity and symbolism represent the most vocal and hardline segment rather than a complete picture of Southern leadership attitudes in 1963 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0962","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0962\n\nCompare and contrast the views expressed on civil rights in Sources B and C.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | President John F. Kennedy's Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, June 11). *Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963.* Retrieved July 12, 2025 from https://www.jfklibrary.org/archives/other-resources/john-f-kennedy-speeches/civil-rights-radio-and-television-report-19630611. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Themes of freedom and American identity: Both reference freedom, heritage, and what it means to be American, but they define these ideals in very different ways.\n- Strong emotional appeals: Both use rhetoric and emotive language to persuade their audiences, Wallace appeals to Southern pride, Kennedy to moral responsibility and national unity.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- View of Segregation\n    - Wallace defends it as a permanent, positive good (“segregation now, tomorrow, forever”).\n    - Kennedy condemns segregation as morally wrong and contrary to American values, calling for legislation to end it.\n- Role of Government\n    - Wallace positions the federal government as a “tyranny” imposing its will on the South.\n     -Kennedy presents the federal government as a protector of rights, upholding the Constitution for all citizens.\n- Tone and Vision of National Unity\n    - Wallace invokes division, portraying the South as culturally and politically distinct.\n    - Kennedy seeks unity, arguing that all Americans, regardless of race, should enjoy the same rights and opportunities.\n- Intended Audience and Purpose\n    - Wallace addresses a Southern, white segregationist base to solidify political power.\n    - Kennedy speaks to the entire nation, urging moral progress and legislative reform.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nA point of similarity is that both leaders refer to core American ideals, such as freedom and heritage, to support their arguments ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Wallace invokes the “Cradle of the Confederacy” and the “Anglo-Saxon Southland” to stir up Southern pride, while Kennedy appeals to the Declaration of Independence and American moral values, stating that the nation was founded on the principle that “all men are created equal” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, both use emotionally charged language to rally their audiences ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Wallace's dramatic phrasing, “draw the line in the dust”, mirrors the passionate tone of Kennedy's call for equal rights and national conscience ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the two speeches fundamentally differ in their messages ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Wallace passionately defends segregation, calling for its continuation “forever” as a symbol of Southern resistance to federal authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In contrast, Kennedy opposes segregation, declaring it unjust and emphasising the need for civil rights legislation to ensure equality. Furthermore, Wallace depicts the federal government as an oppressive force threatening Southern traditions. In contrast, Kennedy argues that it is the duty of the federal government to enforce constitutional rights for all citizens ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0963","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0963\n\nIdentify **two** historical references in Source C and explain their significance to Wallace's message.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with a conclusion. Each point requires an explanation and discussion of significance.\n\nHistorical reference 1: Jefferson Davis\n- President of the Confederate States of America during the Civil War (1861-1865).\n- Why Wallace references him:\n    - To align himself with the Confederacy's legacy of Southern resistance to federal authority.\n    - To evoke Southern pride and reinforce the image of segregation as a heritage issue, not a moral failing.\n    - To legitimise defiance of federal civil rights laws by invoking a well-known figure who also rejected federal control.\n- Significance:\n    - Sets a tone of rebellion and continuity; Wallace presents himself as a modern heir to Davis's cause.\n    - Suggests that resisting civil rights is a defence of tradition rather than extremism.\n\nHistorical Reference 2: “Cradle of the Confederacy”\n- What it refers to: Montgomery, Alabama, where the Confederacy was founded and its first capital in 1861.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To anchor his speech in a location symbolic of Southern defiance and white supremacy.\n    - To invoke nostalgia for a romanticised version of the Old South.\n- Significance:\n    - Embeds his political position in a place deeply associated with slaveholding and segregationist values.\n    - Strengthens the idea that Alabama, and the South in general, has a special mission to resist federal changes.\n\nHistorical Reference 3: “The Great Anglo-Saxon Southland”\n- What it refers to: A racialized phrase used by Wallace to describe the cultural identity of the South.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To appeal to a white, Protestant conception of Southern heritage and cultural superiority.\n    - Reinforces the idea that segregation is not just political, but deeply rooted in racial and cultural identity.\n- Significance:\n    - Frames the civil rights movement as a threat to the racial and cultural order of the South.\n    - Highlights how white identity was embedded in political resistance to integration.\n\nHistorical Reference 4: “Tyranny that clanks its chains upon the South”\n- What it refers to: An allusion to Revolutionary War-era language about British oppression.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To liken the federal government's push for desegregation to a foreign tyranny infringing on Southern “freedom.”\n    - Portrays the South as a victim of oppressive outside control, echoing the Confederate and Revolutionary tradition of resistance.\n- Significance:\n    - Helps cast federal civil rights enforcement as morally illegitimate and reminiscent of colonial oppression.\n    - Adds emotional and historical weight to the argument against federal intervention, appealing to patriotic Southern sentiment.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne reference is to Jefferson Davis, the President of the Confederacy ($\\checkmark$). Wallace states that he is standing “where once Jefferson Davis stood,” signalling to his audience that he sees himself as the successor to Davis's legacy of resisting federal authority ($\\checkmark$).  By invoking Davis, Wallace attempts to legitimise his opposition to civil rights as part of a proud Southern tradition rather than as political extremism ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA second reference is to Montgomery, Alabama, described as the “Cradle of the Confederacy”($\\checkmark$).  This phrase evokes the founding of the Confederacy and reinforces the idea that Alabama has a special historical role in defending states' rights and white supremacy ($\\checkmark$).  By anchoring his speech in this symbolic location, Wallace aligns the modern civil rights struggle with the Civil War and frames federal desegregation efforts as a continuation of historical tyranny ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0994","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0994\n\nDescribe the role of **two** prominent leaders of the Civil Rights Movement in the United States.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Martin Luther King Jr. - Baptist minister, activist, and important leader in the nonviolent protest movement for racial equality.\n\n - Rosa Parks was an activist who played a crucial role in sparking the Montgomery Bus Boycott.\n\n - Malcolm X - A Muslim leader advocating for Black empowerment and self-defense.\n\n - John Lewis - Leader of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) and later a U.S. Congressman.\n\n - Bayard Rustin - An essential organizer and advisor. One of the strategists behind the 1963 March on Washington.\n\n - Ella Baker - Organizer and leader, pivotal in SNCC and other organizations.\n\n - Thurgood Marshall - First Black Supreme Court Justice and important NAACP lawyer in cases like Brown v. Board of Education.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThurgood Marshall was a lawyer, a Supreme Court Justice, and a prominent civil rights advocate. He was also notable for being the first African American to serve on the Supreme Court. He led the NAACP Legal Defense and Education Fund. He won important Supreme Court cases, including the landmark Brown v. Board of Education, which declared racial segregation in public schools unconstitutional.  ($\\checkmark$)\n\nElla Baker was a prominent African-American civil rights activist, notable for her low-profile organizing work, which focused on empowering African-American communities through education and training.  ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI1006","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1006\n\nTo what extent did the United States governmental institutions further the rights of indigenous people between 1945 and 1980?\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\nThere are **three** fundamental responses to the question above: \n\n - A response that rejects the role of governmental institutions in helping to further the rights of indigenous people. \n\n - A response that supports the impact of governmental institutions in helping indigenous people.\n\n - A response that argues a balance of the two previous arguments. \n\nThe very strongest responses are most likely to argue the third, which takes the most balanced view. The third choice is also the most likely to score the highest amount of points in the areas of analysis and focus.\n\nPossible arguments supporting the role of the United States government in supporting indigenous peoples' rights:\n\n - Indian Civil Rights Act of 1968: Part of President Johnson's 1968 Civil Rights Act; This led to the appointment of a Native American as the head of the BIA and was designed to protect people living on the reservations from white and tribal dictatorships. Ultimately, the Indian Civil Rights Act helped people on the reservations gain access to better health and housing services, education and poverty benefits.\n\n - Indian Self-Determination Act: Under President Nixon, the special legal status of Native tribes was restored.\n\n - United States v. Wheeler: Land was returned to the Narragansetts of Rhode Island, and $\\text\\textdollar$100 million was offered to the Sioux, who demanded land instead for the dishonorable nature of the purchase of the Black Hills in South Dakota.\n\n - Boldt Decision (United States v. Washington): Ensured that tribes had the right to 50$\\%$ of the fishing along with Washington state, recognizing the two as equal partners in the maintenance of the fishing industry.\n\nOther possible reasons for increased indigenous rights that can be considered:\n\n - American Indian Movement (AIM): AIM was a militant Red Power group that looked to gain power and influence in the United States by bringing attention to the treatment of Native Americans. They monitored police discrimination against natives, opened survival schools for displaced natives to learn about tribal culture and languages, and gained publicity through protest marches, litigation, and occupations.\n\n - National Congress of American Indians: Inspired by the work of the NAACP, the NCAI worked for the survival of Native American identity by suing the state and federal government when they went back on their promises\n\n - Protest movements: groups such as the National Indian Youth Council embraced the Red Power movement, rejecting assimilation. Natives staged sit-ins to protest state governments. One famous protest was the occupation of Alcatraz in response to the loss of San Francisco's Indian Center.\n\n - Increased media coverage of Native American protests led to more empathy from the American public. The Trail of Broken Treaties, a march from San Francisco to Washington, D.C., heavily publicized the violation of longstanding treaties between the government and the Native Americans. The occupation of Wounded Knee was inspired by the murder of an indigenous man by a white man who was charged with manslaughter. This occupation led to several deaths and more attention to the Native American plight. \n\n - Books, such as Bury My Heart at Wounded Knee (1970) and House Made of Dawn (1968), raised awareness of the mistreatment of Native Americans.\n\nArguments against the impact of the United States government in supporting indigenous people: \n\n - Termination Policy: Under President Eisenhower, the government attempted to end federal recognition of tribes and transfer the jurisdiction of tribal lands to the states. This policy encouraged assimilation and relocation.\n\n - The Indian Relocation Act was meant to provide jobs to indigenous people in cities and urban areas. Job training would be provided to them. However, many were faced with discrimination and had difficulty assimilating. Over a third of the people involved with this program returned to the reservations. \n\n - The Indian Claims Commission, while awarding compensation for lost lands, meddled in reservation business. For example, the head of the commission, Dillon Myer, sold Pueblo land without permission\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn the years following World War II, several marginalized groups fought for equality in the United States. The indigenous community, often overlooked or pushed aside throughout United States history, sought to secure more independence and humane treatment. To some extent, government policies were created that helped or furthered the rights of indigenous people. However, indigenous activism most encouraged the United States to adopt these policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). \n\nUnder Presidents Truman and Eisenhower, the government enacted policies that appeared to help indigenous people but were more focused on assimilation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). For example, the Indian Claims Commission (ICC) was responsible for paying reparations to tribes for land that was taken away from them by the government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). However, the ICC would value the land at its cost from the time it was taken and would pay cash, rather than return the land to the tribes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). This shows that the government did not account for the cultural significance of the land and instead was more focused on paying a monetary value that was usually much less than what the land was worth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). While the United States government may have had good intentions, ultimately, the ICC did not help to further the rights of indigenous people. Similarly, the Relocation Act promised job training and employment to Native Americans but in urban centers rather than on reservations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). Most of the people who took advantage of these programs began to feel isolated from their communities and their cultural background, while also being unable to assimilate into their new cities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). While the program may have had good intentions, the government did not follow through with enough support to actually help the people who had moved from their homes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). Some historians, such as Vine Deloria, Jr., recognize the relocation program as disastrous and an attempt to eliminate cultural bonds ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). However, it is important to note that many Native Americans who found their way to the cities would eventually thrive and many of them were founders of the Pan-Indian movement that helped usher in actual positive change in the late 1960s and early 1970s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nThe government did eventually make some meaningful changes for the indigenous people ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). For example, the Supreme Court sided with Native Americans in two court decisions, The United States v. Washington and United States v. Wheeler ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). The United States v. Wheeler, also known as the Boldt decision, acknowledged that the tribes of Washington should be equal partners with the state regarding the fishing industry. United States v. Wheeler ordered a return of land to the Narragansett people of Rhode Island ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). In addition, when President Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act into law, it included a portion geared explicitly toward furthering the rights of Native Americans. The Indian Civil Rights Act extended constitutional rights to people living on reservations and brought better educational, job, and healthcare opportunities to the reservations as well ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). While this was received better than the Relocation Act, some argued it was a move away from the sovereignty of the tribes. Deloria stated that the ICRA specifically was like trying to force reservations into a system designed by $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$non-Indians” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). However, historian Robert Clinton acknowledged how progressive the act was in avoiding complete government control of the reservations. The evidence suggests that the government did truly make changes that would benefit the indigenous population, though not without some issues ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nHowever, it can be argued that many of these changes would not have taken place had it not been for Native American assertiveness and increased organization ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). During the 1960s and 1970s, following the negative changes made during the Truman and Eisenhower administrations, natives began to mobilize more effectively. As noted earlier, the pan-Indian movement grew from natives who had relocated to cities for career training and met with poverty ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Groups such as the American Indian Movement and the National Congress of American Indians formed to fight for more rights and to garner more attention to their cause ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). They staged sit-ins, protests, and marches to fight perceived injustices against their community. The Occupation of Alcatraz by the Indians of All Tribes was particularly effective in garnering attention to broken land treaties between the tribes and the United States government. This event directly encouraged future land restorations, such as Blue Lake in New Mexico ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). The Trail of Broken Treaties was a cross-country march that forced government officials to meet with organization leaders to address sovereignty issues following the Indian Civil Rights Act. Several historians, notably Kent Blansett, argue that the high visibility of these protests forced the hand of the government and produced more tangible change, such as Nixon's Self-Determination Act ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$).\n\nWhile the United States government made some movement toward righting wrongs with the indigenous population, the most significant change was achieved as a result of the mobilization of indigenous people following failed government policies and abuses. Their activism forced the government to address its failed assimilation policies, reshaping the relationship between the tribes and the United States ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1007","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1007\n\nEvaluate the contribution of the US Supreme Court in advancing the civil rights of African Americans between 1945 and 1980.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\nThis question requires candidates to make an appraisal of the contribution of the Supreme Court in advancing the civil rights of African Americans up to 1980. They should demonstrate a clear understanding of the impact\n\nof specific Supreme Court rulings. Candidates could argue some rulings advanced the movement, while others hindered it. \n\nFor positive contributions to civil rights, candidates may refer to:\n\n - *Shelley v. Kraemer* (1948): Stated that restrictive agreements for purchasing property were unconstitutional.\n\n - *Brown v. Topeka* Board of Education (1954): Overturned Plessy v Ferguson and can be seen as the most important contribution in pushing the Civil Rights Movement forward.\n\n - *Browder v. Gayle* (1956): Ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional and ended the Montgomery Bus Boycott.\n\n - *Cooper v. Aaron* (1958): Reaffirmed Brown and declared that states could not resist desegregation orders. A response to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$massive resistance” campaigns.\n\n - *Boynton v. Virginia* (1960): Declared segregation in interstate bus terminals was unconstitutional, helping spur the Freedom Rides (1961).\n\n - *Griffin v. County School Board of Prince Edward County* (1964): Held that Virginia's closing of schools to resist desegregation was unconstitutional, helping maintain public education opportunities.\n\n - *The Heart of Atlanta Motel v. United States* (1964): Established the constitutionality of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and ruled that Congress could prohibit racial discrimination in public accommodations via the Commerce Clause.\n\n - *South Carolina v. Katzenbach (1966)*: Upheld the Voting Rights Act of 1965 as a valid use of Congress's Fifteenth Amendment powers, allowing federal preclearance and examiners to stop states from disenfranchising Black voters. \n\n - *Loving v. Virginia (1967)*: Struck down state bans on interracial marriage.\n\n - *Green v. New Kent County* (1968): Struck down ineffective $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$freedom of choice” plans and required schools to dismantle segregation $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$root and branch.”\n\n - *Alexander v. Holmes County Board of Education* (1969): Ended the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all deliberate speed” standard and ordered the immediate desegregation of dual school systems.\n\n - *Swann v. Charlotte-Becklenburg Board of Education* (1971): Upheld busing and other $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$remedies” as a method to integrate schools, helping legitimize more aggressive desegregation measures.\n\nFor negative contributions to civil rights, candidates may refer to:\n\n - *Brown v. Board of Education II* (1955): Ordered desegregation at $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all deliberate speed.” Vague language allowed states to delay compliance for years.\n\n - *Milliken v Bradley* (1974): Prohibited cross-district busing unless it was proven that districts had deliberately segregated, limiting desegregation to within single districts and advancing school segregation in cities where cities and suburbs were racially divided.\n\n - *California v Bakke* (1978) prohibited affirmative action programs if these led to reverse discrimination. The Bakke decision could be seen as slowing down the Civil Rights Movement. \n\nCandidates' opinions or conclusions will be presented clearly and supported by appropriate evidence.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nUS Supreme Court rulings between 1945 and 1980 are often associated with advancing the civil rights of African Americans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). While some historians regard these rulings as decisive turning points, others adopt a more measured view, emphasizing the decisive role of congressional legislation as the real driver of civil rights progress. These historians tend to argue that the Supreme Court operates within a broader framework of constitutional development and rarely initiates social change independently ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives)}$. This essay evaluates the Court's contribution to advancing African American civil rights through two case studies focusing on education and voting rights ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). It argues that the Court rarely achieved significant civil rights advances in isolation. Only when its rulings were aligned with federal legislation did they become transformative ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). \n\nThe Supreme Court was able to make significant contributions in advancing civil rights for African Americans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) when its decisions were aligned with a broad framework of congressional and judicial action ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). The 1954 Brown v Topeka's Board of Education ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$) ruling became transformative once Congress, the Executive, and courts aligned behind its decision. Although the original ruling was won unanimously 9-0 and did much to weaken the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Separate but Equal\" principle outlined in Plessy v Ferguson (1896) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$), it was bitterly resisted for almost a decade. But Brown I failed to give precise instructions on how and when to desegregate schools. Brown II, in 1955, ordered federal district courts to oversee desegregation $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$with all deliberate speed\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$), but it set no deadlines and provided no enforcement mechanism. Brown II essentially left implementation to local school authorities, many of whom were hostile to integration. This lack of direction empowered resistance movements such as the Southern Manifesto (1956), and the events at Little Rock (1957) to take hold, slowing progress ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) to the extent that by 1963, an estimated 98% of Black students in parts of the Deep South were still attending segregated schools ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$).\n\nOnce Congress and federal agencies aligned behind Brown, integration accelerated sharply and produced significant gains ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). The Civil Rights Act (1964) granted Title IV, allowing the US Attorney General to file lawsuits to enforce school desegregation. Title IV also offered federal funding to help schools meet desegregation requirements. Title VI provided enforcement measures by withdrawing federal funding from schools that resisted desegregation. Moreover, the Department of Health, Education and Welfare could withhold funding from non-compliant districts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). This provided the necessary financial incentive for districts to follow the Brown ruling. \n\nMoreover ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$), when momentum picked up for Brown, the Supreme Court became more confident enforcing remedies to overturn $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$freedom of choice\" plans in Green v New Kent County (1968), to abolish $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all deliberate speed\" delaying tactics in Alexander v Holmes (1969), and to create busing, school zoning and racial ratios in Swann (1971). By 1970, 33% of Black students in the South were attending majority white southern schools, a figure that approached 43$\\%$ in the 1980s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). This demonstrates that the Supreme Court's breakthrough in advancing African American civil rights achieved real force only when Congress and federal agencies provided the political will and enforcement mechanisms to implement it. The 1954 landmark ruling stood as a principle without practical effect for over a decade. It lay dormant, waiting for legislative and administrative action to give it life ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nIn addition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization)}$, the Supreme Court's 1966 ruling against Black voting suppression in South Carolina underscores that only when paired with existing statutory framework could judicial decisions overturn disenfranchisement. The Voting Rights Act (1965) banned all discriminatory voting practices and provided preclearance requirements for changing voting procedures, including literacy tests to restrict voting. Even without Supreme Court involvement, the Act accelerated Black voter registration in Mississippi from 6.7$\\%$ in 1964 to 59.8$\\%$ in 1967 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). However, when states challenged the Act, arguing that it infringed on Tenth Amendment rights, the Supreme Court could rule with the backing of legislation against the states. In South Carolina v Katzenbach (1966) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$), the Court upheld the Voting Rights Act under the 15th Amendment, which prevented racial discrimination in voting ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). The ruling immediately validated the Act's enforcement mechanisms and set a precedent for removing legal obstacles that states could use to delay compliance. Katzenbach demonstrates that the Supreme Court was effective in advancing civil rights when it backed existing legislation. Without the Act's preclearance and enforcement powers, the ruling would have been symbolic rather than transformative ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). \n\nHowever ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$), while the power of Congress and the Supreme Court did advance African American Civil Rights ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$), states adapted to the new legislation by utilizing gerrymandering and annexation that slowed the Black enfranchisement. Moreover, voting legislation required a renewal in 1970, which abolished the remaining literacy tests.  By the late 1970s, critics argued that judicial resources were too thinly stretched to curb states' manipulation of the system to stall electoral reform.\n\nIn conclusion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion}$), the  Supreme Court contributed greatly to advancing African American Civil Rights, but its rulings were much more effective when aligned with legislation enacted by Congress. This alignment produced tangible results in education and voting rights ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). However, progress was maximized when Court rulings followed federal legislation. Advancement was delayed by a decade when the Brown ruling preceded the Civil Rights Act, yet brought immediate results when the Katzenbach ruling followed the Voting Rights Act. This suggests that the Court could open the door to equality, but only when Congress chose to walk through it did civil rights advance in a meaningful and lasting way ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)."},{"ref":"HI1023","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1023\n\n“The counterculture movement in the Americas during the 1960s and 1970s was always peaceful.” To what extent do you agree with this view?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- Students should address the demands of the question by acknowledging the strengths of the claim that the “counterculture movement was always peaceful” and counterclaims that indicate how, when, where, or why it became violent.\n- Responses should include clear examples chosen from the Americas to illustrate arguments. Examples may be taken from one or a variety of countries.\n\nIndicative content:\n- Definition and historical context of the counterculture movement in the 1960s. The counterculture movement could be broadly defined as a series of public demonstrations by different social groups, mostly youth, that opposed social inequalities, the effects of the capitalist and consumerist society on people and the environment, and the negative impacts of the Cold War on civilians.\n- The counterculture movement's firm opposition to the political polarisation and destruction of social fabric caused by the Cold War.\n- Accounts of the peaceful principles of the counterculture movement alongside specific demands for social change (e.g. anti-war, gender and racial equality, freedom of expression, socioeconomic equality, anti-imperialism).\n- Cultural manifestations of the movements in the arts:\n    - Artistic productions in California and New York.\n    - Popular events such as the Monterrey Pop (1967) and Woodstock (1969) festivals.\n    - The Onda literature movement in Mexico supporting the freedom of expression of the youth.\n    - Critique of consumerism by the Arte Destructivo visual arts movement in Argentina.\n- Violent groups in the USA:\n    - The Black Liberation Army (1970s) acted as an opposition armed group against race-based violence and systemic inequalities by bombing key government infrastructure and attacking police officers.\n    - The Symbionese Liberation Army (early 1970s) robbed banks and kidnapped wealthy people.\n    - The Weather Underground organisation (late 1960s) mainly attacked government infrastructure to express firm opposition to the Vietnam War and US imperialism.\n- Violent groups in Latin America:\n    - The People's Guerrilla Group (early 1960s) reclaimed land for peasants in rural Mexico and resisted state repression through direct confrontation.\n    - The Tupamaros (mid-1960s) robbed banks and foreign companies, kidnapped political and economic elite members, attacked police officials, and committed terrorist attacks in Uruguay.\n    - The Montoneros (1970s) engaged in targeted attacks against the government and military officials and infrastructure in attempts to subvert US direct influence in national politics.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe counterculture movement emerged in the 1960s against the backdrop of the Cold War's polarised world and grew critical of both international conflicts and social inequality in countries across the Americas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). It could be argued that this movement was essentially peaceful because it was mainly comprised of youth circles favouring a peaceful,  non-violent approach to bringing about profound changes in society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). As reflected in artistic demonstrations, the movement proved to be effective in gaining nationwide support in different countries. However, it should be acknowledged that the counterculture movement also developed as a platform for violent demonstrations, particularly in the 1970s, in response to peaceful undertakings' limited impact ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nBy definition, the counterculture movement that emerged in the 1960s in the USA was peaceful ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The values put forward by youth groups were aligned with messages of peace and love alongside non-violent demonstrations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). For instance, arts-based mobilisations served as peaceful platforms through which different groups revealed their political militancy. The creation of the Peace Tower by the Artist Protest Committee in Los Angeles in 1966 and the joint efforts of musical artists in the Woodstock festival in 1969 showcase the arts' significance in amplifying youth voices against US military actions in the Vietnam War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In Latin America, a wide range of grassroots organisations had the common goal of subverting the traditional values they felt were hindering human development. For example, the Onda literature movement in Mexico advocated for freedom of expression, especially of young individuals who wanted to create alternative fashion trends inspired by indigenous costumes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Similarly, the Arte Destructivo in Argentina in the early 1960s drew from its counterpart in London to contest the destruction of humanity and nature associated with both the nuclear arms race and the increasing materialism and consumerism in society. These manifestations demonstrate altogether that peaceful resistance through art was reflective of a growing call for cultural revolution within and across countries in the Americas and beyond ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nMoreover, groups that worked for social justice took the peaceful approach within the counterculture movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In the USA, the influences of the principle of non-violence in the Civil Rights Movement in the 1950s and the Free Speech Movement in 1964 were paramount for furthering grassroots activism in educational institutions in the country ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). With higher enrolment rates in the 1960s and 1970s, universities served as platforms for anti-establishment demonstrations of students demanding freedom of assembly and collaboration with wider causes such as racial and gender equality, environmentalism, and anti-war protests ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In Latin America, organised networks were built to address pressing issues nationally. For example, the Dutch-led organisation Solidaridad was vital in promoting both economic development in Brazilian slums and supporting human rights defenders under the dictatorial regime in El Salvador in the 1970s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Social justice tackled through concrete actions addressing people's problems was, therefore, another peaceful pillar of the counterculture movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, some organisations also attempted to achieve social changes through violent means ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Although claiming similar grounds to peaceful manifestations, revolutionary movements in Latin America resorted to violence to bring about structural sociopolitical changes in the Cold War context ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). For instance, the creation of guerrilla groups was prompted by both discontent over socioeconomic inequality and violence exerted by the US-aligned central governments ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The Mexican peasant-led People's Guerrilla Group in the early 1960s, the student-led guerrillas of Tupamaros in Uruguay, and the Montoneros in Argentina in the late 1960s carried out attacks against political and economic elites they viewed as responsible for letting foreign influence affect their nation's autonomy, overlooking policies favouring integrated development of marginal groups, and safeguarding human rights ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Similarly, some organisations in the USA further challenged a political system that was not meeting the social demands raised by the counterculture movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). During the 1970s, the sustained racial inequality was redressed by the Black Liberation Army and the white-led Weather Underground group through terrorist attacks such as bombings of governmental buildings and direct confrontation with law enforcement officials. Although most of these violent groups in Latin America and the US were short-lived, they symbolised an alternative and radicalised approach to peaceful manifestations that were regarded as unsuccessful in creating conditions for profound social changes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the counterculture movement in the 1960s and 1970s was, in principle, peaceful. The values put forward by youth groups through the arts in the USA and Latin America were popular and gained support from different social sectors. Nonetheless, the ineffectiveness of this approach was reflected in the creation of violent groups that were determined to subvert racial inequality, challenge the continued US pro-war actions in Vietnam and broader Cold War scenario, undermine the US's entrenched foreign influence, and contest the state violence and socioeconomic inequality in Latin America ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1024","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1024\n\nTo what extent did the rise of radical African American activism weaken the civil rights movement in the 1960s?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**Guidance for responses**\n- Candidates should examine the emergence of radical African American activism in the 1960s: including Malcolm X, the Black Power movement, the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) after 1965, and the Black Panther Party,  and its relationship to the mainstream civil rights leadership represented by Martin Luther King Jr. and the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC).\n- Responses should evaluate whether radical activism divided the movement, undermined national support, and created tension with nonviolent campaigns.\n- They should also consider the argument that radicalism broadened the struggle, gave voice to urban Black frustrations, and pressured the government to respond to civil rights demands.\n- High-level responses will balance both interpretations, using specific examples, and reach a substantiated conclusion on whether radicalism ultimately weakened or strengthened the movement.\n\n**Indicative Content**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Martin Luther King Jr. and the Non-violent Civil Rights Movement}}$:\n- Early successes: Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955-56), Birmingham Campaign (1963), March on Washington (1963).\n- The Civil Rights Act (1964) and the Voting Rights Act (1965) as major achievements.\n- Strategy: nonviolent direct action, federal legislation, and moral appeal to white Americans.\n- Limits: focus on the South, less effective in addressing Northern urban issues (housing, poverty, policing).\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Emergence of Radical Activism}}$:\n- Malcolm X and the Nation of Islam: Advocated Black self-determination, separatism, and rejection of white-dominated integrationist goals. Assassinated in 1965 but inspired later radicals.\n- SNCC's shift under Stokely Carmichael: From interracial cooperation to “Black Power” in 1966. Called for racial pride, economic self-sufficiency, and community control.\n- Black Panther Party (1966, Huey Newton & Bobby Seale): Emphasised self-defence, community programmes (free breakfast, health clinics), and militant rhetoric.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Ways Radicalism Weakened the Movement}}$:\n- Split from King's nonviolent approach created tensions and fractured national unity.\n- Alienated moderate white allies and northern liberals, weakening coalition-building.\n- The media's focus on militancy fed into government portrayals of Black activism as violent.\n- FBI's COINTELPRO targeted radical groups aggressively, using divisions to discredit civil rights activists.\n- King himself worried that radicalism would distract from legislative reform and moral authority.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Ways Radicalism Strengthened the Movement}}$:\n- Brought attention to urban issues like police brutality, poverty, and systemic inequality, which mainstream campaigns had ignored.\n- Black Power inspired cultural pride, identity, and self-confidence, reshaping Black political consciousness.\n- Provided practical support for communities through local programmes (Panthers).\n- Gave leverage: radicals made King's demands seem more moderate, pushing the government to negotiate with him.\n- Highlighted the limits of legislation (1964/65 Acts) in transforming daily life.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Government Responses}}$:\n- President Lyndon Johnson's administration supported King's campaigns reluctantly but saw radicals as a threat.\n- FBI surveillance (e.g., wiretapping King, infiltrating Black Panthers).\n- Repression of radicals through COINTELPRO, raids, and imprisonment.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Overall Evaluation}}$:\n- By the late 1960s, divisions within the movement were undeniable: King was assassinated in 1968, SNCC and Panthers fragmented.\n- However, radicalism also broadened the struggle beyond legal segregation to systemic racism.\n- Candidates should weigh whether the loss of unity outweighed the gains in consciousness and scope.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe civil rights movement of the 1960s is often remembered for the contrast between Martin Luther King Jr.'s nonviolent, integrationist approach and the rise of radical African American activism, represented by figures such as Malcolm X, Stokely Carmichael, and the Black Panther Party ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While mainstream campaigns achieved major legislative victories in the mid-1960s, growing frustration with continued inequality in housing, employment, and policing helped fuel radical demands for Black Power and self-determination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This essay will argue that radical activism did create divisions that weakened the civil rights coalition in terms of national unity and white support, but it also strengthened the movement by expanding its scope, energising Black communities, and forcing broader recognition of systemic racism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nIn the early 1960s, Martin Luther King Jr. and the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) embodied the mainstream civil rights strategy of nonviolent direct action ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). King's campaigns, such as the Birmingham protests in 1963, combined nonviolent resistance with moral appeals to the conscience of the nation. Later that year, the March on Washington projected unity and optimism, culminating in King's “I Have a Dream” speech ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These tactics helped secure landmark legislation: the Civil Rights Act of 1964 outlawed segregation in public facilities and employment discrimination, while the Voting Rights Act of 1965 removed barriers to Black political participation in the South ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Yet by the mid-1960s, the limits of this approach became clear. While legal segregation in the South had been dismantled, African Americans in Northern cities continued to face poverty, unemployment, inadequate housing, and police brutality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This gap between legislative progress and lived experience created fertile ground for more radical activism.\n\nMalcolm X was one of the most important early voices of radical critique ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). As a minister for the Nation of Islam, he rejected integration and instead advocated Black self-determination, racial pride, and separation from white institutions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). His speeches condemned nonviolence as ineffective, arguing that African Americans had the right to defend themselves “by any means necessary” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). While Malcolm X was assassinated in 1965, his message resonated with urban African Americans who felt alienated by King's focus on the South and frustrated by slow progress ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Although his separatism contrasted sharply with King's integrationism, his emphasis on dignity and empowerment helped lay the foundations for the Black Power movement.\n\nThe emergence of Black Power in the mid-1960s further highlighted divisions within the movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In 1966, Stokely Carmichael, leader of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC), popularised the slogan “Black Power” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This marked a shift away from interracial cooperation toward calls for racial pride, political autonomy, and economic independence. SNCC, once committed to nonviolence, began expelling white members and endorsing more militant rhetoric ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The Black Panther Party, founded the same year by Huey Newton and Bobby Seale, took this militancy further by promoting armed self-defence against police brutality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Panthers also ran community programmes such as free breakfast for children and health clinics, which won grassroots support in Black communities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). However, the rise of Black Power alienated many white liberals and moderates who had previously supported the civil rights cause ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Media coverage often focused on militancy rather than community service, feeding fears of violence and extremism.\n\nRadical activism, therefore, weakened the movement's unity at the national level ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). King himself expressed concern that Black Power rhetoric diverted attention from the shared struggle for civil rights and risked eroding public sympathy, though this was also motivated by his general disagreement with the approach of black radicals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The decline of interracial cooperation within SNCC and tensions between King's SCLC and younger activists fractured the once broad-based movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). At the same time, the Johnson administration, already strained by the Vietnam War, became less responsive to civil rights demands, particularly as riots broke out in cities like Watts (1965), Newark, and Detroit (1967) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The association of civil rights with violence, even when protests were largely reactions to police brutality, reduced political momentum for reform ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nYet to view radical activism only as a source of division would be to overlook its contributions to the movement's evolution ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Black Power emphasised issues neglected by King's campaigns, such as economic inequality, police violence, and cultural pride ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In many urban communities, the Panthers' community initiatives addressed immediate needs that federal policy ignored ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The rhetoric of Black Power inspired a broader sense of self-confidence and cultural identity, seen in the rise of Afrocentric fashion, art, and education ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Furthermore, the existence of more militant groups sometimes made King's demands seem more moderate by comparison, strengthening his bargaining position with white political leaders ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Thus, radical activism arguably complemented nonviolent strategies by broadening the agenda and reshaping the cultural landscape of Black America.\n\nGovernment responses further illustrate both the challenges and the significance of radicalism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While King's campaigns faced FBI surveillance, radical groups were targeted with far greater intensity under COINTELPRO ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The FBI infiltrated, harassed, and disrupted Black Panther chapters, and police confrontations often turned deadly, as in the killing of Panther leader Fred Hampton in 1969 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This repression weakened radical organisations, but also demonstrated how seriously the government viewed their challenge ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). By contrast, King's approach—though controversial—remained more acceptable to mainstream politics, which helped secure legislation in 1964-65 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). The starkly different responses to nonviolent and radical activism reveal the strategic dilemmas facing the movement.\n\nBy the end of the 1960s, the civil rights movement was fragmented: King had been assassinated in 1968 while preparing a campaign for economic justice, and radical groups such as SNCC and the Panthers were weakened by internal divisions and government repression ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This loss of unity suggests that radicalism did weaken the movement in the short term. Yet its legacy endured through Black cultural pride, increased political consciousness, and local community empowerment. If King's leadership was essential in dismantling legal segregation, radicals pushed the struggle further by confronting structural racism and highlighting issues like poverty and policing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In conclusion, the rise of radical African American activism both divided the movement and broadened it, weakening national unity but reshaping the struggle into a wider fight for equality. It ensured that civil rights would not be limited to access to the ballot or desegregated buses, but would also encompass demands for dignity, justice, and systemic change ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1235","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1235\n\n“Social issues were the main cause of the emergence of social movements in the Americas.” Discuss with reference to **one** social movement in the Americas after 1945.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- Strong answers will evaluate causation, weigh one category of causes (“social issues”) against others and make a judgment about relative importance\n- Responses should clearly define the “social issues” in the example chosen\n- High-level responses will focus on preconditions and triggers, but may briefly mention escalation to reinforce causation\n\nAny **one** of the following:\n- The M-26-7 Movement, which spearheaded the Cuban Revolution in the 1950s.\n    - Inequality between urban centres with opportunities for higher income, such as Habana, and rural areas in marginalised regions lacking infrastructure and conditions for economic development in southern Cuba, such as Sierra Maestra.\n    - Dependence on sugar production and foreign investment.\n    - The imperialist domination of the USA in Cuba through puppet governments.\n    - Guidance: responses may acknowledge the significance of inequality as a social factor and argue that one or more of the other political and economic factors above were more significant in the emergence of the M-26-7 in Cuba.\n- The Civil Rights movement in the USA.\n    - Racial discrimination against African Americans through social segregation and targeted systematic violence in public spaces.\n    - Structural economic inequality is reflected in the lack of job opportunities or lower wages for black people.\n    - Exclusion of African American social groups from formal political participation in elections and public fora.\n    - Guidance: A student may acknowledge the significance of racial discrimination as a social factor and argue that one or more of the other political and economic factors above were more significant in the emergence of the Civil Rights movement in the USA.\n- Feminist movements in the Americas\n    - Gender-based inequality in the workplace and institutional restrictions on the sexual freedom of women.\n    - Institutionalisation of the Cuban state’s support for women's organisations to promote gender equality through social policies.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSocial movements in the Americas after the Second World War emerged amid widespread social discontent over the war's effects on humanity and the call for change. In both the USA and Latin America, the 1950s and 1960s signalled growing concerns of distinct social groups about economic inequalities, challenges to inclusive political participation, the marginalisation of minority groups, and the traditional gender-based roles assigned to men and women ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). For instance, civil society organisations across the Americas started to raise awareness and mobilise in relation to the relegated place of women in social life despite the significance of their contributions to the development of their countries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This essay posits that social issues were a primary cause of the emergence of feminist movements in the Americas after 1945. However, it also argues that political factors were key to their early development, especially in the context of radical changes in political regimes in the region during the Cold War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nSocial issues partly caused the emergence of feminist movements in the Americas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). After the Second World War, rapid changes carried out by governments aimed at boosting economic development brought about the intensification of industrialisation in the Americas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These changes were significant in the gender-based organisation of social life across countries in the region, as women began working in factories and partially shifted away from their traditional domestic responsibilities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Nonetheless, the differentiated working conditions continued reproducing inequalities between men and women ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). This was noteworthy in the formation of influential feminist movements that called for the end of wage discrimination and gender-based exploitation. In the USA, the publication of Betty Friedan's “The Feminine Mystique” and the leadership of labour rights advocate Esther Peterson were key to advancing nationwide demands for equal pay rights in the late 1950s. Similarly, the Argentinian Front for Women's Struggle movement advanced all-encompassing demands for better conditions of women in the workplace and at home in the late 1970s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The marked hierarchical system in the workplace thus prompted the early developments of feminist movements as a response to women's exclusion from particular businesses and roles ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nMoreover, feminist movements also developed against the backdrop of institutional restrictions on the freedom of choice and expression of women ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). For example, women's organisations in the USA and Mexico began mobilising for their liberation in the late 1960s and early 1970s. One salient aspect of the call for mobilisation was linked to the significance of women's will to control their own sexual life ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Framed by demands for equality enshrined in sexual rights, the National Organization for Women in the USA and the Women in Solidarity Action in Mexico spearheaded public protests and political actions to bring about change. More specifically, these organisations sought changes in the legal framework in their countries against deeply ingrained issues such as sexual assaults at home and the lack of support in their decision to use contraceptive methods or access an abortion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The emergence of these organised movements, therefore, highlighted the negative impacts of structural conditions grounded in national policies that did not guarantee women's protection, thereby driving social issues and inspiring social movements ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, political factors were also paramount to the development of feminist movements in the Americas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). Although women's participation in decision-making at the state level was limited or non-existent by the 1950s, their growing influence in the political realm through campaigning and mass mobilisations paved the way for the emergence of feminist movements ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). For instance, the active militant role of women in the M-26-7 during the Cuban Revolution during the 1950s contributed to the movement's achievements under Fidel Castro and Ernesto $\\lq$Che' Guevara in 1959. As the Cuban leaders praised the significance of women in the socialist cause and placed gender equality at the centre of social policies, the creation of the state-led Federation of Cuban Women (FMC) under the leadership of former revolutionary Vilma Espín in 1960 represented a watershed moment ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The political will of the Cuban state allowed for the institutionalisation of the FMC as an organisation reflective of feminist principles linked to sexual education, health, and the legalisation of abortion. Although the initiative stemmed from the Cuban state's attempt to further control social groups and consolidate its power, the FMC was reflective of the significance of women in the view of the regime under Castro ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, social factors were key to the early development of feminist movements in the Americas from the 1950s onwards. The effects of the Second World War in societies across the region created conditions for increased gender inequality, which partly caused the formation of organised groups seeking better living conditions for women. This was evident in both public and private spheres as feminist movements demanded better working conditions in factories and the protection of their sexual rights. However, political factors were also significant in the emergence of feminist movements. For instance, measures such as the ones implemented by the Cuban state aimed at enhancing the role of women paved the way for the development of an institutional framework that enabled conditions for the advancement of gender equality principles reflective of feminist movements ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$)."},{"ref":"HI1236","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1236\n\nDiscuss the factors that influenced the emergence of the Hispanic American movement in the United States.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nAny combination of the following may be considered:\n- Social discrimination and segregation\n    - The Hispanic American movement emerged in the mid-20th century as a response to exclusion, exploitation, and cultural marginalization.\n    - Social dynamics, including racism, segregation, and rapid population growth, spurred grassroots organizing, as women and youth expanded their activism.\n    - Underfunded, struggling schools with high dropout rates were common in Hispanic communities and drove activism like the East LA Walkouts of 1968.\n- Economic exploitation and labor inequality\n    - Immigration policies, particularly the Bracero Program, underscored the contradictions between reliance on immigrant labor and their limited legal protections.\n    - Hispanic communities were largely dependent upon agricultural labor and unskilled industrial work, which provided poor wages, long hours, and unsafe working conditions.\n    - Economic struggles in low-wage sectors fueled union activism, most notably among the United Farm Workers (UFW), led by César Chávez and Dolores Huerta.\n- Political marginalization and lack of representation\n    - Hispanics/Latinos were underrepresented in political institutions from local to national levels\n    - Faced barriers to voting, including literacy tests and intimidation, into the 1960s.\n    - Moments of progress, such as the Civil Rights Act (1964) and the Voting Rights Act (1965), initially focused on African Americans, driving the creation of new political organizations like La Raza Unida Party (1970).\n- Cultural identity and Chicano nationalism\n    - The ideas of the Chicano Movement emphasized cultural pride, indigenous heritage, and bilingual education, thereby shaping a shared Hispanic identity.\n- Wider context of the Civil Rights and Anti-War Movements in the US\n    - Politically influenced by African American civil rights, groups such as LULAC and MAPA fought for political representation and reform.\n    - Many young Hispanic/Latino people served in Vietnam, but returned to continued marginalization, driving activism.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nEconomic and political inequality were the driving factors in the emergence of the Hispanic and Latino Civil Rights movements in the US. Under policies like the Bracero Program, employers benefited while workers remained vulnerable and powerless against abuse ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Alongside these economic struggles, Hispanic and Latino communities also faced significant political marginalization. Across local governments and national institutions, they were consistently underrepresented and had their voices excluded from decision-making processes that directly affected their lives ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This lack of representation perpetuated policies that ignored or undermined their needs, reinforcing cycles of disenfranchisement. Political invisibility and economic exploitation were not accidental, but rather the product of systemic barriers deliberately designed to limit participation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). These limits would also serve to inspire the emergence of the broader Hispanic and Latino Civil Rights Movement in the US ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nThe history of Hispanic and Latino labor in the U.S. is tied to economic exploitation and systemic inequality. Immigration policies such as the Bracero Program (1942-1964) reveal the contradictions of relying on immigrant labor while providing limited protections. Created to recruit Mexican agricultural workers during WWII, it supplied cheap labor but institutionalized disposable treatment. Braceros faced exploitative contracts, had their rights denied, and endured poor living conditions. During this era, Hispanic communities were largely dependent on agricultural labor and unskilled industrial work. These sectors offered meager wages, long hours, and unsafe working conditions. Agricultural workers faced exposure to pesticides, inadequate housing, and limited access to healthcare. Industrial jobs in factories and meatpacking plants were similarly grueling, with little opportunity for advancement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This economic reality reinforced cycles of poverty, restricted educational opportunities, and perpetuated systemic inequality across generations. Although reliance on Hispanic labor was undeniable, the economic system consistently undervalued their contributions, treating them as a cheap workforce rather than as citizens deserving of dignity and rights ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Out of these struggles emerged union activism, most notably the United Farm Workers (UFW). Founded in the 1960s by César Chávez and Dolores Huerta, the UFW became a powerful symbol of resistance to exploitation. Chávez and Huerta mobilized farmworkers to demand fair wages, safer working conditions, and recognition of their humanity. Through strikes, boycotts, and grassroots organizing, the UFW brought national attention to the plight of agricultural workers. Their activism underscored the broader struggle for labor rights in America, linking Hispanic workers' fight to the larger civil rights movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Campaigns such as the grape boycott ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) demonstrated the power of collective action to challenge entrenched systems of inequality. Although victories were often partial and hard-won, the union's work laid the foundation for future labor movements and highlighted the resilience of Hispanic communities amid economic exploitation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn the political realm, voting rights were among the most contested issues. Well into the 1960s, Hispanics and Latinos faced barriers such as literacy tests, poll taxes, and outright intimidation at the polls. These measures suppressed participation among working-class and immigrant communities, many of whom had limited access to formal education due to economic hardship ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The denial of voting rights left Hispanic communities with little influence over policies that shaped their economic and social realities. Even when eligible to vote, many were discouraged by hostile environments that treated their participation as unwelcome or illegitimate ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Nonetheless, progress emerged during the broader civil rights era, though it often focused on African American disenfranchisement. However, this progress also laid the foundation for Hispanic activists to build on ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Inspired by the era's momentum, new political organizations arose to represent Latino interests more directly. One of the most notable was the La Raza Unida Party, ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) founded in 1970. The party sought to challenge the two-party system by advocating for issues specific to Mexican Americans and other Latino groups, including education reform, labor rights, and political representation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Though its influence was geographically and temporally limited, La Raza Unida symbolized a growing awareness of the need for independent political power among Hispanic communities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe creation of such organizations reflected a broader shift: Hispanic and Latino activists recognized that economic justice could not be achieved without political empowerment. The struggles of farm workers, factory laborers, and marginalized communities were closely tied to a lack of political representation. By organizing politically, they sought to break the cycle of exploitation and disenfranchisement and to assert their right to participate fully in American democracy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Historians interpret these developments differently. Mae Ngai argues that programs such as the Bracero Program created a racialized labor hierarchy that left Mexican workers vulnerable. She links reliance on immigrant labor and the denial of rights to broader exploitation, thereby maintaining inequality and limiting upward mobility for Latinos. Conversely, David G. Gutiérrez highlights Hispanic communities' agency in resisting these systems. While acknowledging exploitation, he points to the resilience of groups such as the United Farm Workers and La Raza Unida Party, viewing their struggles as catalysts for social change tied to the civil rights movement. Their views reveal a tension: Ngai emphasizes systemic exclusion and exploitation, while Gutiérrez focuses on grassroots efforts to transform labor and politics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nThe struggles against economic exploitation and political marginalization highlight the systemic challenges that Hispanic and Latino communities faced in the mid-20th-century United States. The Bracero Program exposed the contradictions of a nation that relied on immigrant labor yet failed to protect workers. While agricultural and industrial work sustained Hispanic communities, it also brought poverty and hardship. Union leaders such as César Chávez and Dolores Huerta responded with activism, demonstrating the power of collective action to challenge exploitation. Political marginalization limited their influence on policy, but progress such as the Civil Rights and Voting Rights Acts opened new possibilities and inspired groups like La Raza Unida Party. These struggles reveal Hispanic resilience and determination to fight for economic justice and political empowerment, laying a foundation for future generations and keeping the pursuit of equality central to America ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1482","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1482\n\nExamine the impact and significance of feminist movements in one country of the Americas.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGeneral Guidance:\n- The question requires students to investigate both the concrete effects and the broader, longer-term importance of feminist movements.\n- The response must focus on one country of the Americas and strong responses will focus on clearly documented feminist activism in that country.\n- The best responses will distinguish clearly between impact (legal, political, social, economic and cultural change) and significance (how far the movements were transformative, lasting or limited) and evaluate different perspectives on how much really changed.\n- Evidence of significance may include durable legal frameworks, mass entry of women into education, work and politics, reshaped cultural norms, and international linkage.\n- Limitations may include: persistent wage gaps, under-representation in positions of power, conservative backlash, and the exclusion of poorer women and women of colour.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- **United States** - feminist movements and second-wave feminism, 1960s onward\n    - Legal: Equal Pay Act (1963); Title VII of the Civil Rights Act (1964) banning sex discrimination in employment; Title IX (1972) requiring gender equity in federally funded education; Roe v. Wade (1973) establishing a constitutional right to abortion.\n    - Political: National Organization for Women (NOW, 1966); the campaign for the Equal Rights Amendment (ERA), passed by Congress in 1972.\n    - Social/cultural: expansion of reproductive choice (the contraceptive pill; legalised abortion); consciousness-raising; changing attitudes to marriage, work and domestic roles; *Ms.* magazine (1972); figures such as Betty Friedan and Gloria Steinem.\n    - Significance: Transformative long-term shift in women’s legal status, education and workforce participation.\n    - Limitations: The Equal Rights Amendment failed ratification by the 1982 deadline (falling short of 38 states), exposing the limits of the movement and the strength of conservative backlash (such as Phyllis Schlafly’s STOP ERA campaign). Mainstream (liberal, largely white, middle-class) feminism neglected working-class women and women of colour, prompting Black feminist and intersectional responses.\n- **Mexico** - post-war suffrage and “Second Wave” from 1970 on\n    - Women gained the municipal vote (1947) and full federal suffrage (1953), first voting in national elections in 1955.\n    - Second-wave groups from the early 1970s (e.g. Mujeres en Acción Solidaria) campaigned on reproductive rights, violence against women and legal equality; a 1974 constitutional reform enshrined legal equality between men and women.\n    - The 1975 UN World Conference on Women in Mexico City increased the visibility of feminist demands and strengthened links between Mexican, Latin American and international women's movements.\n    - **Significance:** Suffrage and legal reform integrated women into formal politics. Hosting the 1975 conference gave Mexican and Latin American feminism international visibility and linked national activism to a global agenda.\n    - **Limitations:** Gains were constrained by PRI dominance, machismo and the Catholic Church; reproductive rights advanced slowly (Mexico City decriminalised abortion only in 2007), showing significant but incomplete change.\n- **Argentina** - post-war suffrage and increased presence in politics\n    - Female suffrage law (Law 13,010, 1947), championed by Eva Perón; women first voted in the 1951 national elections.\n    - The Female Peronist Party (Partido Peronista Femenino, founded 1949) mobilised women politically on an unprecedented scale.\n    - **Significance:** Rapidly incorporated women into national political life and tied women’s rights to Peronism, giving them mass political weight. Later gendered activism shows a longer trajectory via the Madres de Plaza de Mayo under the dictatorship.\n    - **Limitations:** Gains were bound to a populist regime, so were partly instrumentalised and vulnerable to political instability; autonomous feminist organisation was limited.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSince the mid-twentieth century, feminist movements in the United States have reshaped the legal, political and cultural place of women, yet the extent of that transformation remains contested. ($\\checkmark$ focus) Emerging from the frustrations of educated, middle-class women in the post-war period and drawing organisational lessons from the civil rights movement, the “second wave” of American feminism from the 1960s onwards secured a series of concrete legal victories while also provoking a powerful conservative backlash. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The movement made substantial gains in legal protections, education and employment, even though its gains were uneven and, in the case of the Equal Rights Amendment, incomplete. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nThe most measurable impact of American feminism was legislative and legal. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The Equal Pay Act of 1963 made it illegal to pay men and women different wages for equal work, and Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 prohibited employment discrimination on the basis of sex, giving women a legal weapon against workplace inequality. ($\\checkmark$ examples) In 1972, Title IX barred sex discrimination in any federally funded education programme, opening universities, professional schools and athletics to women on a far larger scale. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The following year, the Supreme Court’s decision in Roe v. Wade (1973) recognised a constitutional right to abortion, granting women unprecedented control over reproduction. ($\\checkmark$ examples)Taken together, these measures dismantled much of the formal legal architecture of discrimination, and their impact was direct and durable. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThese legal changes also translated into profound educational and economic impact. ($\\checkmark$ focus) In the decades after Title IX, women entered higher education, law, medicine and business in rapidly rising numbers, eroding the assumption that the professions were male preserves. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Access to reliable contraception and, after 1973, to legal abortion allowed many women to delay or plan childbearing, making sustained careers more achievable and reshaping patterns of work and family life. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) The result was a lasting increase in female workforce participation and a slow narrowing of the gap between men’s and women’s public roles. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nBeyond legislation, feminist movements transformed political organisation and cultural attitudes. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The founding of the National Organization for Women (NOW) in 1966, led by figures such as author and activist Betty Friedan created a durable lobbying vehicle for women’s rights. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Consciousness-raising groups, the launch of *Ms.* magazine in 1972 and the public advocacy of activists such as Gloria Steinem carried feminist ideas well beyond activist circles. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The effect was to change everyday expectations about marriage, motherhood, work and sexuality, so that assumptions taken for granted in 1950 had become widely questioned by 1980. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) In this sense the movement’s cultural impact arguably outlasted any single statute, because it altered how a generation of women understood their own possibilities. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nThe significance of these changes, however, must be weighed against clear limitations. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The campaign for the Equal Rights Amendment, passed by Congress in 1972, ultimately failed, falling short of ratification by the necessary thirty-eight states before the 1982 deadline, defeated in part by Phyllis Schlafly’s STOP ERA campaign, which mobilised conservative women who feared feminism threatened their idea of the traditional family. ($\\checkmark$ examples) This defeat revealed both the strength of the opposition and the limits of feminist consensus. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Critics also argued that mainstream feminism spoke primarily for white, middle-class women and neglected working-class women and women of colour, a critique that gave rise to Black feminist and, later, intersectional analyses. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) From this angle the movement’s impact was real but uneven, benefiting some women far more than others and leaving structural inequalities like the persistent wage gap only partly addressed. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nOn balance, the feminist movements of the United States had a transformative impact on the legal status, education and employment of women, and their deeper significance lies in the permanent shift they produced in cultural expectations about gender. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The failure of the ERA and the exclusion of many women from the movement’s early gains show that this transformation was neither complete nor evenly shared. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Yet even allowing for these limits, American feminism fundamentally redrew the boundaries of women’s public and private lives, and its significance is best understood not as a single legislative moment but as a lasting reorientation of society that continues to shape debates over gender to the present day. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)"},{"ref":"HI1483","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1483\n\nEvaluate the role of Dr Martin Luther King Jr in the civil rights movement.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|RResponse is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGeneral Guidance:\n- This question requires students to make an appraisal by weighing the strengths and effectiveness of King’s role against its limitations, and to reach a supported judgement; a narrative account of his life will not score well.\n- The strongest responses assess King’s role in relation to other factors, such as grassroots activists and organisations, rival strands of activism, and the actions of government and the courts, rather than treating him in isolation.\n- The question invites a judgement on how decisive King's contribution was: whether he was the movement's most important individual leader or one important figure within a much broader movement.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- Arguments for a central/decisive role\n    - **Leadership and strategy:** Articulated and popularised nonviolent direct action (influenced by Gandhi and the Black church), giving the movement moral authority and a unifying method. Framed the struggle in terms of American and Christian ideals, making it hard for white moderates to dismiss.\n    - **Achievements:**\n        - Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56): established King as a national leader and demonstrated the effectiveness of mass nonviolent protest. However, bus segregation was formally ended through the federal court case *Browder v. Gayle*, highlighting the importance of local activists, lawyers and the courts alongside King.\n        - Co-founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC, 1957), providing a church-based organisational network across the South.\n        - Birmingham campaign (1963): “Project C” and the Letter from Birmingham Jail; televised images of police dogs and fire hoses shocked opinion and pressured the Kennedy administration, building momentum for the Civil Rights Act.\n        - March on Washington (August 1963) and the “I Have a Dream” speech: mobilised national and international support; became the movement’s iconic image.\n        - Selma to Montgomery marches (1965): “Bloody Sunday” dramatised the denial of voting rights and helped secure the Voting Rights Act (1965).\n        - Recognition and influence: Nobel Peace Prize (1964); ability to lobby Presidents Kennedy and Johnson; skilled use of the media.\n- Arguments for a limited role\n    - The movement was bigger than King. Successes rested on grassroots organisations (NAACP, SNCC, CORE) and local activists such as Rosa Parks, E. D. Nixon, Ella Baker and Fannie Lou Hamer.\n    - **Limitations:**\n        - Setbacks: the Albany Movement (1961–62) achieved little and exposed tactical weaknesses.\n        - Limits in the North: the Chicago campaign (1966) made scant progress against de facto segregation, housing discrimination and economic inequality, revealing the limits of his southern methods.\n        - Rising radicalism: by 1965–68 King was challenged by Malcolm X’s critique, the Black Power slogan (Stokely Carmichael, 1966) and the Black Panther Party (founded 1966); many younger activists saw nonviolence as too slow and accommodationist.\n        - Broadening agenda and backlash: the turn to economic justice (Poor People’s Campaign, 1968) and public opposition to the Vietnam War (“Beyond Vietnam” speech, Riverside Church, April 1967) alienated some allies, the press and the Johnson administration.\n        - Structural factors beyond King: the courts (Brown v. Board of Education, 1954) and government (Johnson’s legislative drive); a “long civil rights movement” predated and outlasted him.\n\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nDr Martin Luther King Jr is often portrayed as the embodiment of the American civil rights movement, but evaluating his role requires weighing his undoubted achievements against the contributions of the wider movement and the limits of his approach. ($\\checkmark$ focus) As the leading advocate of nonviolent direct action from the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1955 until his assassination in 1968, King gave the movement moral authority, national visibility and a strategy that helped secure landmark legislation. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) King’s role was pivotal, but the movement’s successes were collective, also depending on grassroots activists, rival strands of protest and decisive government action, so that King is best understood as the movement’s most important leader rather than its sole author. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nKing’s most important contribution was his leadership and the strategy of disciplined nonviolence. ($\\checkmark$ focus) Emerging as spokesman for the Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56), he demonstrated that mass, peaceful protest could achieve concrete change, as the boycott ended in the desegregation of the city’s buses. ($\\checkmark$ examples)Drawing on Gandhian principles and the traditions of the Black church, he articulated a philosophy that gave the movement both a moral vocabulary and a practical method, and his founding of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) in 1957 gave that strategy a church-based organisational base across the South. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) His particular gift was to frame the struggle in terms of American and Christian ideals, making it difficult for white moderates to dismiss and allowing him to project the movement’s message to a national audience. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nKing’s effectiveness was clearest in the campaigns that produced the movement’s greatest legislative victories. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The Birmingham campaign of 1963, which deliberately provoked confrontation, generated televised images of police dogs and fire hoses turned on peaceful demonstrators that shocked national and international opinion and pushed the Kennedy administration towards civil rights legislation; from jail King issued his Letter from Birmingham Jail, a powerful justification of direct action. ($\\checkmark$ examples)The March on Washington later that year, and his “I Have a Dream” speech, mobilised support and became the movement’s defining image, helping to build momentum for the Civil Rights Act of 1964. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge)In 1965 the Selma to Montgomery marches, and the violence of “Bloody Sunday,” dramatised the denial of voting rights and helped secure the Voting Rights Act of 1965, all evidence that his role in converting protest into law was decisive. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nHis role must nonetheless be evaluated against the limits of his leadership and the breadth of the movement. ($\\checkmark$ focus) King did not create the movement single-handedly: its successes rested on grassroots organisations such as the NAACP, SNCC and CORE and on local activists including Rosa Parks, E. D. Nixon and Fannie Lou Hamer, while the organiser Ella Baker argued that “the movement made Martin rather than Martin making the movement.” ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) King’s work also suffered notable failures such as failed campaigns in Albany (1961-1962) and Chicago (1966) which had limited impact on de facto segregation, poor housing and economic inequality of the North. ($\\checkmark$ examples) By the mid-1960s his leadership was increasingly challenged from within Black America. Malcolm X offered a sharp critique of nonviolence, and the rise of the Black Power slogan and the Black Panther Party reflected a younger generation’s impatience with his gradualism. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) This suggests that King’s approach, however effective in the South, neither commanded universal support nor solved the movement’s deeper problems. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nKing’s later career and the role of government further complicate any simple assessment of his importance. ($\\checkmark$ focus) His broadening focus on economic justice, expressed in the Poor People’s Campaign of 1968, and his public condemnation of the Vietnam War in the “Beyond Vietnam” speech of April 1967, alienated sections of the press, some civil rights allies and the Johnson administration, weakening his political influence in his final years. ($\\checkmark$ examples)The movement’s legislative gains, moreover, depended on factors beyond King. For example, the Supreme Court’s ruling in Brown v. Board of Education (1954) predated his rise, and it was President Johnson who drove the Civil Rights and Voting Rights Acts through Congress. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) King’s protests created the political pressure and moral climate for reform, but converting that pressure into law required a receptive government, suggesting that his role formed part of a wider process. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nOn balance, Dr Martin Luther King Jr played a pivotal role in the civil rights movement. ($\\checkmark$ focus) His leadership, his strategy of nonviolent direct action and his unmatched ability to command national attention were central to the campaigns that produced the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, and no other single figure did more to shape the movement’s public image or its legislative success. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Yet his role should not be exaggerated: the movement’s achievements were collective, resting on grassroots organisation, sustained by activists who often disagreed with him, and dependent on government action, while his failures in the North and the rise of Black Power exposed the limits of his approach. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) King was therefore the movement’s most important and effective leader, but its success was the work of many hands. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)"},{"ref":"HI1623","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1623\n\nDefine the term Black Power and provide **two** examples.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following to define “Black Power” with **two** supporting examples:\n- **Black supremacy**\n    - Some supporters believed Black Power meant reversing historical racial domination, with Black people ruling over White people.\n    - This was a minority and controversial interpretation.\n- **Black working-class revolution**\n    - Black workers in Detroit's automobile industry saw Black Power as a movement for economic justice and workers' rights.\n    - The League of Revolutionary Black Workers organised Black workers and pushed for greater representation and better work conditions.\n- **Black pride and dignity**\n    - Although initially critical of the slogan, Martin Luther King Jr. later argued that Black Power could represent racial pride, self-respect, and empowerment.\n    - He emphasised the need for Black Americans to gain confidence, dignity, and a sense of belonging.\n- **Economic empowerment**\n    - Nathan Wright and later Richard Nixon interpreted Black Power as increasing Black ownership of businesses and economic resources.\n    - They believed economic strength would lead to greater opportunities and influence.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBlack Power was a movement that encouraged African Americans to gain political, economic, and social power, while promoting racial pride, self-respect, and independence ($\\checkmark$). Rather than having a single meaning, it was understood in different ways by different groups. One example is that Black workers in Detroit's automobile industry viewed Black Power as a struggle for economic justice and workers' rights, leading them to form the League of Revolutionary Black Workers to fight workplace discrimination and improve conditions ($\\checkmark$). Martin Luther King Jr. later interpreted Black Power as a way of developing Black pride, dignity, and a sense of belonging among African Americans. Thus, Black Power was both a call for racial empowerment and a demand for greater equality and opportunity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1624","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1624\n\nCompare and contrast the roles and significance of Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X in the U.S. Civil Rights Movement.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence**.\n\nFor **comparison**, any **two or more** of the following **similarities**:\n- **Political ideas:**\n    - Both wanted to improve the lives and rights of Black Americans. Both had large numbers of followers and were influential leaders.\n- **Influence:**\n    - Both were powerful and inspiring speakers. Both believed in the importance of organisations, preaching, and publications to spread their ideas.\n- **Later Developments and Legacy:**\n    - Both were involved in influential civil rights organisations. Their views changed over time, making them more similar in their later years. Both were controversial figures and were assassinated.\n\nFor **contrast**, any **two or more** of the following **differences**:\n- **Main Political Goal:**\n    - King sought integration of Black and White Americans, while Malcolm X initially supported separatism and Black self-determination.\n- **Political Focus:**\n    - King focused mainly on ending segregation in the South, while Malcolm X focused on problems in urban ghettos in the North.\n- **Political Views:**\n    - King increasingly supported economic equality and social justice in his later years, while Malcolm X emphasised Black nationalism and later moved toward a broader human rights approach.\n- **Methodology and Approach to Violence:**\n    - King promoted non-violent protest, while Malcolm X accepted self-defence and justified violence when necessary for protection.\n- **Attitude Towards White Americans:**\n    - King worked towards cooperation and integration with White Americans, while Malcolm X was initially more critical and distrustful of White Americans, though he became less antagonistic later in life.\n- **Achievements and Legacy:**\n    - King is generally seen as achieving more concrete civil rights successes, including helping secure the Voting Rights Act of 1965, while Malcolm X had a major impact on Black pride and Black consciousness but achieved fewer legislative changes.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMartin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X had several similarities. Both aimed to improve the lives and rights of Black Americans and became influential leaders with large followings ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Both were powerful speakers who used important civil rights organisations to reach out to people to spread their ideas. Martin Luther King Jr. utilised and collaborated with the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC), which he helped found, and the National Association for the Advancement of Coloured People (NAACP). Malcolm X was associated with the Nation of Islam (NOI) and the Organization of Afro-American Unity (OAAU) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Their views evolved over time, making them more similar in their later years ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Both men were controversial figures and were eventually assassinated ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, there were also significant differences between them. King believed in integration and sought equality between Black and White Americans, whereas Malcolm X initially supported separatism and Black self-determination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). King focused mainly on ending segregation in the South, while Malcolm X concentrated on the problems faced by Black people in northern ghettos ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). King promoted non-violent protest, but Malcolm X accepted self-defence and the use of violence when necessary ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Furthermore, King encouraged cooperation with White Americans, whereas Malcolm X was initially more critical and distrustful of them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Finally, King achieved major legislative successes, including helping secure the Voting Rights Act of 1965, while Malcolm X's greatest impact was promoting Black pride and Black consciousness ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |"},{"ref":"HI1625","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1625\n\nExplain **two** political impacts of the Voting Rights Act (1965) on American society.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Enfranchisement of Black Americans in the South\n    - Removed barriers to voting by ending literacy tests and poll taxes, making it easier for African Americans to register and vote.\n    - Greatly increased Black voter registration in the South, with Mississippi's registration rate rising to 59% by 1968 and the gap between Black and White voter registration narrowing to just 7% by 1980.\n- The rise of Black political power\n    - Increased Black political representation, as the number of African Americans elected to office in the South increased six-fold between 1965 and 1969 and doubled again by 1980.\n- Party realignment\n    - Shifted American political alignments dramatically, with the South transitioning away from the Democratic Party and towards the Republican Party. The transformation reshaped both parties' ideological identities and formed the foundation of US politics to today.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne impact of the Voting Rights Act (1965) was that it removed barriers to voting by abolishing literacy tests and poll taxes, making it easier for African Americans to vote ($\\checkmark$). Another impact was that Black voter registration increased significantly across the South, with Mississippi reaching 59% registration by 1968 ($\\checkmark$). A further impact was the increase in Black political representation, as the number of African Americans elected to office in the South increased six-fold between 1965 and 1969 ($\\checkmark$). The Civil Rights Movement reshaped American political loyalties, as many white Southern voters moved from the Democratic to the Republican Party. This shift contributed to the parties’ modern ideological identities shaping American politics even in modern times ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"The Cold War and the Americas","questions":[{"ref":"HI0024","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0024\n\nOutline **two** reasons why the Cuban Missile Crisis was significant in the context of the Cold War. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It brought the United States and the Soviet Union to the brink of nuclear war, showcasing the real danger of Cold War tensions.\n- The crisis led to the establishment of a direct communication line between Washington and Moscow, known as the “Hotline.\"\n- It resulted in a diplomatic agreement where the Soviet Union agreed to remove its missiles from Cuba in exchange for the US promising not to invade Cuba and the removal of missiles from Turkey.\n- The crisis demonstrated the importance of nuclear arms control, leading to future agreements such as the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty of 1963.\n- It strengthened Fidel Castro's position in Cuba and solidified Cuba's role as a Soviet ally in the Western Hemisphere.\n\n[2 marks]\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nThe Cuban Missile Crisis was significant because it brought the US and the Soviet Union to the brink of nuclear war, emphasizing the peril of Cold War confrontations ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, it led to important diplomatic negotiations, such as the removal of US missiles from Turkey, marking a shift towards more cautious handling of nuclear weapons by both superpowers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0045","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0045\n\nSummarize Kennan's perspective of the Soviet Union according to Source E.\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source E** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$  | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt adapted from Part 5 “Practical Deductions From Standpoint of US Policy” of George Kennan's secret telegram sent from Moscow, February 22, 1946 widely known as “The Long Telegram.”|\n\n[Source: Kennan, G. (1946, February 22). George Kennan's \"Long Telegram\". United States National Security Archive. Retrieved September 10, 2024 from https://nsarchive2.gwu.edu/coldwar/documents/episode-1/kennan.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Kennan describes the Soviet Union as “fanatical” and disconnected from “reality” in its behaviors toward the US.\n- Kennan suggests that the Soviet Union and the US cannot peacefully coexist.\n- Kennan says that the Soviet Union will work to “disrupt” US society and “destroy” the American “way of life” in the name of its own security.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn Source E, Kennan argues that the Soviet Union is “fanatical” in its beliefs and actions toward the US ($\\checkmark$) and that as a result, the US and Soviet Union will never be able to permanently make peace with each other. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0092","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0092\n\nExplain the significance of the Truman Doctrine in the context of the Cold War.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny of the following:\n- It marked the beginning of the U.S. policy of containment, which sought to stop the spread of communism during the Cold War, and laid the foundation for other US Cold War policies, such as the Marshall Plan.\n- The doctrine essentially divided the world into two blocs: the capitalist West, led by the U.S., and the communist East, led by the Soviet Union, escalating tensions between the superpowers.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe significance of the Truman Doctrine is that it marked the start of the U.S. containment strategy  during the Cold War that aimed to stop the global spread of communism ($\\checkmark$). It also laid the foundation for subsequent Cold War initiatives which sought to counter Soviet expansionism, such as the Marshall Plan ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0094","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0094\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the signing of the nuclear test treaty?\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A cartoon by Ed Valtman, a well-known editorial cartoonist from the mid-20th century, which depicts the tense negotiations between the USA and the USSR around nuclear arms control and the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/The%20Cold%20War%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0094.png\" alt=\"HI0094\">\n\n[Source: Valtman, E. (1962, April 21). *He's driving me nuts - I'm on the verge of blowing my top*. Retrieved 1 October, 2024 from https://www.loc.gov/item/2015645656/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The cartoon suggests that the USSR is to blame for the delays in the signing of the nuclear test ban treaty by labelling the Soviet figure \"Soviet Intransigence.\"\n- The Soviet figure is depicted as sitting calmly with a smug expression, suggesting an unwillingness to compromise, while the U.S. figure, labelled \"U.S. A-Tests,\" appears frustrated and visibly agitated with the Soviet figure and close to losing control.\n- The clock on the table adds a sense of urgency, implying that the delays are critical, with the Soviet Union’s stubborn stance being blamed for stalling the treaty negotiations.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn Source B, the cartoonist suggests that the Soviet Union is responsible for the delays in signing the nuclear test ban treaty by depicting the Soviet figure, labelled \"Soviet Intransigence,\" sitting calmly with a smug expression   indicating a refusal to compromise ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand,  the American figure, labelled \"U.S. A-Tests,\" is shown as frustrated and agitated and on the verge of losing control, suggesting that he blames the USSR for the lack of progress on the signing of the nuclear test treaty ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0104","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0104\n\nDescribe the Cuban Missile Crisis.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- A crisis in 1962 involving the USSR, Cuba and the US.\n- It was motivated by Castro's fear of a US invasion.\n- It was motivated by Khrushchev's desire to establish a nuclear “balance” with US missiles set up in Turkey aiming at the USSR.\n- Kennedy tried to solve it by imposing a naval blockade of Cuba.\n- In the end, Khrushcev agreed to no nuclear missiles on Cuba if the US removed its missiles from Turkey and promised to not try to invade Cuba.\n- An example of the Sino-Soviet split as Mao openly criticised Khrushchev for this handling of the crisis.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Cuban Missile Crisis is the closest the world has been to human made destruction. It happened when the USSR tried to set up nuclear missile bases in Cuba to counter the US  ($\\checkmark$) threat to its own territory with American missile bases in Turkey ($\\checkmark$). Cuba's Fidel Castro agreed to this since he was afraid the US would try to use force to bring down the revolution in Cuba after the failure of the Bay of Pigs Invasion ($\\checkmark$). The situation was solved peacefully with Kennedy agreeing to remove the bases in Turkey and to not invade Cuba ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0220","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0220\n\nWith reference to origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the perception of the Soviet threat during the Cuban Missile Crisis.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt is from President John F. Kennedy's 1962 address on the Cuban Missile Crisis. |\n\n[[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1962, October 22). Radio and television address to the American people on the Soviet arms build-up in Cuba. John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum. https://www.jfklibrary.org/learn/about-jfk/historic-speeches/address-during-the-cuban-missile-crisis. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nValues:\n- A speech by Kennedy reflects the official U.S. political stance and its intent to shape public opinion.\n- Aims to justify the U.S. response to the crisis, helping to understand the rationale behind their actions.\n- Explains the narrative of the Soviet threat, focusing on hemispheric security and the balance of power.\n\nLimitations\n- Only presents the U.S. perspective, excluding the Soviet viewpoint and providing a one-sided view of the crisis.\n- Designed to mobilize internal and external support, it does not provide an objective evaluation of the facts.\n- Its rhetorical language can make it difficult to identify precise factual information.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source, being a speech by Kennedy, reflects the official U.S. political stance and provides insight into how the U.S. government publicly articulated the Soviet threat during the crisis ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose of the speech is to justify the U.S. response, offering valuable context about the rationale behind actions taken, such as the naval blockade ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content of the source emphasizes the narrative of the Soviet threat, focusing on hemispheric security and the balance of power, shedding light on how the U.S. framed the crisis to its domestic and international audiences ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nThe purpose of the speech is to mobilize internal and external support for U.S. policies, which limits its objectivity and excludes a balanced perspective of the Soviet viewpoint ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The content employs rhetorical language, potentially exaggerating the threat and making it difficult to extract precise factual information ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). As an official speech, it lacks the depth of classified or internal communications, focusing only on the public narrative and possibly omitting strategic or controversial details ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$)."},{"ref":"HI0221","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0221\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about U.S. perceptions of the Soviet threat during the Cuban Missile Crisis.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Map of Northern America showing the full range of the nuclear missiles under construction in Cuba, used during the secret meetings on the Cuban crisis. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/The%20Cold%20War%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0221.jpg\" alt=\"HI0221\">\n[Source: CIA  (1962, October 16). *Cuban crisis map missile range.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved November 26, 2024, from https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_de_los_misiles_de_Cuba#/media/Archivo:Cuban_crisis_map_missile_range.jpg  Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt is from President John F. Kennedy's 1962 address on the Cuban Missile Crisis. |\n\n[[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1962, October 22). *Radio and television address to the American people on the Soviet arms build-up in Cuba*. John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum. https://www.jfklibrary.org/learn/about-jfk/historic-speeches/address-during-the-cuban-missile-crisis. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor full marks, both similarities and differences must be addressed.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources reflect the perception of a direct and immediate threat to the United States arising from the installation of Soviet missiles in Cuba.\n- Both emphasize the importance of Cuba's geographical proximity as a key factor in increasing the perceived danger.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source A focuses on a technical representation of the threat (the range of the missiles), whereas - Source B uses an emotional and political speech to emphasize the impact of the crisis on national security.\n- Source A was created for an internal audience with strategic purposes, while Source B was designed for a broader audience, aiming to influence public opinion and justify political actions.\n- Source A does not explicitly express moral judgments or ideological evaluations, whereas Source B directly criticizes the actions of the Soviet Union, labeling them as deceptive and provocative.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Source A and Source B reflect the U.S. perception of a direct and immediate threat posed by the installation of Soviet missiles in Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A visually highlights the range of the Soviet missiles, offering concrete evidence of their capability to reach U.S. territory, which validates the concern of a direct nuclear threat. Source B, however, provides more detailed reasoning, with Kennedy framing the missile deployment as not only a physical threat but a political one, calling it a \"deliberately provocative and unjustified change in the status quo.\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)\n\nBoth sources also highlight Cuba's geographical proximity as a critical factor that amplifies the perceived danger ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). While Source A depicts this proximity visually with the theoretical missile ranges from Cuba, Source B mentions the “buildup of Communist missiles--in an area well known to have a special and historical relationship to the United States and the nations of the Western Hemisphere” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources present contrasts in the US perception of threat as well. Source A provides a technical depiction of the threat, focusing on the missile range, while Source B employs an emotional and politically charged speech to underscore the crisis's impact on national security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) such as Kennedy’s use of the phrase “clear and present danger” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nAdditionally, Source A avoids moral or ideological commentary, whereas Source B explicitly criticizes the Soviet Union's actions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$), portraying them as the aggressor by saying their actions are “deliberately provocative and unjustified” and in “violation” of their promises ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0352","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0352\n\nExplain how the friction between Truman and Stalin represented the larger breakdown of the Grand Alliance by 1946.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence/examples: \n\n - Ideological differences: \n\n    - The U.S., under Truman, promoted democratic governance and capitalist economies, whereas Stalin sought to expand communism and establish a buffer zone of Soviet-controlled states in Eastern Europe. \n\n - Leadership styles: \n\n    - Truman's direct and assertive approach clashed with Stalin's calculated and authoritarian methods. \n\n - Disagreements over postwar arrangements: \n\n    - Disagreements over Germany and Eastern Europe widened the divide.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe breakdown of the Grand Alliance was largely due to ideological differences between Truman and Stalin. Truman believed in promoting democracy and free markets, while Stalin focused on spreading communism and securing a buffer zone of Soviet-dominated states ($\\checkmark$). For example, in Poland, Stalin disregarded the Yalta agreement by installing a pro-Soviet communist government ($\\checkmark$). Their leadership styles also contributed to rising tensions. Truman’s assertive approach, such as pushing for free elections in Eastern Europe, contrasted sharply with Stalin's authoritarian tactics intended to maintain the Soviet sphere of influence ($\\checkmark$). An example of this occurred at the Potsdam Conference in 1945 where Truman revealed the U.S. had successfully tested the atomic bomb to show American strength.  However, this increased Stalin's distrust leading him to keep the Red Army in Eastern Europe to strengthen Soviet control, breaking promises of free elections and further damaging Allied trust ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0353","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0353\n\nDescribe **three** reasons for the tension between Truman and Stalin at the Potsdam Conference in 1945.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Differing Ideologies\n\n     - Truman represented democratic capitalism, while Stalin represented communist authoritarianism, leading to clashing visions for post-war Europe.\n\n - Disagreement over Poland\n\n     - Disagreements over Poland's future government heightened tensions, with Truman demanding free elections and Stalin insisting on a pro-Soviet regime.\n\n - Reparations\n\n     - Stalin wanted heavy reparations from Germany to rebuild the Soviet Union, whereas Truman opposed excessive penalties to avoid economic instability.\n\n - Atomic Diplomacy\n\n     - Truman revealed the successful testing of the atomic bomb to Stalin, which increased Soviet suspicions and set the stage for the arms race.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason for tension at the Potsdam Conference was the disagreement over Poland, with Stalin insisting on a pro-Soviet government while Truman demanded free elections ($\\checkmark$). Another source of conflict was Truman’s decision to inform Stalin about the U.S. testing of the atomic bomb, which increased mistrust and highlighted U.S. military dominance ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, there was significant tension over reparations, as Stalin sought heavy payments from Germany, whereas Truman opposed this to avoid economic instability ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0354","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0354\n\nIdentify and explain **two** ways atomic diplomacy contributed to the origins of the Cold War and the breakdown of the Grand Alliance by 1946.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation/evidence:\n\n - Created a shift in power dynamics\n\n     - The use of the atomic bomb highlighted the U.S.'s military and technological superiority, shifting global power dynamics in its favor by 1945. \n\n - Excluded of the Soviet Union from atomic knowledge\n\n     - The U.S. intentionally excluded the Soviet Union from the Manhattan Project, even though they were allies during the war. This decision reflected deep-seated mistrust of Stalin's intentions and a desire to maintain a strategic advantage in the post-war period. \n\n - Demonstrated US military superiority\n\n     - By demonstrating the bomb's immense destructive power in Japan, the U.S. aimed to strengthen its position in future negotiations and deter Soviet aggression in Europe and Asia. \n\n - Eroded Allied trust\n\n     - The U.S. decision to reveal its nuclear capability at the Potsdam Conference heightened mistrust within the Grand Alliance. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAtomic diplomacy caused the breakdown of the Grand Alliance because it represented immense US military strength globally as well as relative to the USSR ($\\checkmark$). The use of two nuclear weapons in Hiroshima and Nagasaki publicly demonstrated to the world both the destructive power the US wielded and its willingness to use it ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe US's decision to exclude the Soviet Union from atomic knowledge demonstrated its distrust despite the alliance between the two ($\\checkmark$). The U.S. kept the Manhattan Project secret from Stalin, making him suspicious of American intentions. In response, Stalin tightened Soviet control in Eastern Europe, breaking agreements made at Yalta and worsening tensions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0659","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0659\n\nDescribe **two** organisations or policies set up to counter communist threats in the US.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC), 1938: created to investigate alleged disloyalty and unpatriotic behaviour by any US citizen, employee or organisation suspected of Communist ties.\n- National Security Act, 1947: set up to support the work of HUAC. It created the office of Secretary of Defense and also established the National Security Council and separate departments for each branch of the armed forces.\n- McCarran Act, 1950 - gave the govt the power to investigate spies, and allowed for the investigation and deportation of people accused of promoting Communism or engaging in subversive activities.\n- The Alien Registration Act, 1940: required all alien residents over 14 to file a record of their political beliefs. It also made it illegal to promote any ideas that may criticise the government or discuss overthrowing it.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1938, the fear of increased communist influence in the US led to more focus being given to what was being referred to as $\\lq$Un-American activities'.\n\nThe Alien Registration Act of 1940 was set up to counter communist threats. This required all alien residents over 14 to file a record of their political beliefs. It also made it illegal to promote any ideas that may criticise the government or talk about overthrowing the government ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, the National Security Act was set up in 1947, which created the office of Secretary of Defense and also established the National Security Council and separate departments for each branch of the armed forces ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0697","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0697\n\nExplain **three** conditions for the emergence of Augusto Pinochet's dictatorship in Chile.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation/evidence:\n- Socialist and communist labels on Salvador Allende's government.\n    - CIA's declassified documents reveal that US diplomats saw the weakening of conservative parties and rise of socialist movements in Chile (Popular Unity Party) as a similar process to the Cuban Revolution.\n    - Ideological-based fear of the USA exacerbated by Henry Kissinger's resort to the $\\lq$domino theory' and the strength of the constitutional process toward socialism in Chile.\n- Active involvement of the USA in Chilean domestic affairs shortly before and after Allende was democratically elected.\n    - Covert operations (1964-1972) under the CIA's 40 Committee during Richard Nixon's administration.\n    - Track I: undermining of the Chilean political system through political means such as bribery of congresspeople and influence of public opinion through the control of media outlets (late 1960s).\n    - Project FUBELT (Track II): promotion of coup of Allende through military means (1970).\n- Short-term impact of economic policies implemented by Salvador Allende.\n    - Inflation increased by nearly 400% between 1972 and 1973.\n    - Wages decreased 38% by 1972.\n- Chilean military's shifting alliances.\n    - Military's protection of democratic process regardless of US economic offer (October 1970).\n    - Failed coup attempt by anti-Allende military faction (June 1973).\n    - Weakening of General Prats's influence over the military despite his firm commitment to an apolitical relationship with the elected President (August-September 1973).\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe emergence of Augusto Pinochet's military dictatorship in Chile may be attributed to both international and domestic factors. In terms of international factors, the broader Cold War context in the Americas, particularly the consolidation of Fidel Castro in Cuba and the US failed attempts to weaken his regime, created conditions for growing fear in the USA of the spread of communism ($\\checkmark$). This was exacerbated by the weakening of US-aligned conservative parties in the face of the steady rise by the mid-1960s of socialist political movements such as the Popular Unity Party led by the figure of Salvador Allende ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the success of the latter in the presidential elections consolidated the move toward socialism against pro-capitalist interests of the USA in the country ($\\checkmark$). The reaction of the CIA under Nixon's administration was firmly carried out through a combination of a $\\lq$stick and carrots' approach such as bribery of congresspeople in 1970 and influence over the military between 1972 and 1973, which ultimately triggered a coup ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, domestic factors also paved the way for the rise of Pinochet. For instance, the short-term impact of inclusive economic policies in employment and national production brought about nation-wide social discontent over and deligitmisation of the central government ($\\checkmark$). By 1973, this was reflected in the increase of nearly 400% of inflation and the decrease in wages of 38%. This economic crisis contributed to widening the pre-existent domestic polarisation due to ideological disputes of communism and capitalism ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0792","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0792\n\nIdentify **three** key factors that led to the establishment of the Truman Doctrine in 1947.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- A desire to protect democracy in Europe\n- To prevent countries with economic difficulties from turning to Communism as a way forward, for example, Greece and Turkey\n- To take a leading role in world politics\n- A means of establishing the USA as a leading economic power\n- Growing fear and mistrust of the influence of Stalin and the USSR\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Truman Doctrine was established in 1947 because of the growing fear of the influence of Stalin and the USSR ($\\checkmark$). The economic situation at the end of World War II meant that the US could no longer follow a policy of isolationism and was committed to protecting democracy in Europe ($\\checkmark$). The US recognised that countries suffering from economic difficulties could be tempted to turn to Communism as it appeared to offer a way forward and a chance of renewed economic stability ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0793","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0793\n\nDefine the term $\\lq$dollar imperialism' and explain how it is connected to the Soviet Union's distrust of aid from the Marshall Plan.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor definition, the following or similar:\n- Dollar imperialism was a term used to describe US policy aimed at gaining influence and greater economic control in Europe. Typically used by the Soviet Union and other US rivals as a criticism of their aid policies.\n\nFor explanation, any **two** of the following:\n- The USSR did not see it as a way of providing financial support to Europe but rather as exerting influence over Europe and making it more dependent on the USA.\n- Communist countries were eligible to apply for Marshall Aid, but the conditions attached to it required them to allow the US access to their economic information and justify how they spent the aid. They feared this would give the US the upper hand in future negotiations.\n- Both the USA and the USSR knew that economic strength was becoming a crucial factor in the Cold War, and the country with the strongest economy would have the advantage.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nDollar imperialism was a term used by Stalin and the Soviet Union to describe the US's approach to distributing foreign aid ($\\checkmark$), which they distrusted because they viewed it as the US seeking economic control and gain in Europe rather than for the purposes of economic recovery after WWII ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe USSR also distrusted this policy, as to be eligible for Marshall Aid, they had to agree to certain conditions and allow the US access to their economic information ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0794","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0794\n\nDescribe the origins of the Red Scare before 1950.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Long history of opposition to communism: The first Red Scare began in the 1920s after the establishment of the first communist party in 1919 and the fear of increasing Communist influence in the US.\n- Harry S. Truman was staunchly anti-communist and implemented policies to repress the spread of Communist and Socialist ideologies.\n- Opposing economic ideology: The US viewed Communist economic policies as contrary to the ideas of a free market economy and freedom in general.\n- The USSR was actively expanding its influence in Eastern Europe, and many European countries appeared attracted to communism.\n- Propaganda: rumours of Soviet spies led to a culture of mistrust and fear among people\n- China became communist in 1949: Senator Joseph McCarthy used this to promote fear further, asserting there were spies in the US Government and that anyone claiming to be liberal was supporting Communist ideals, creating paranoia and suspicion.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAfter the Second World War, the USSR could be seen to expand their influence in Eastern Europe, which led to the belief that this would threaten American rights and freedoms ($\\checkmark$). President Truman was staunchly anti-communist. He saw communism as a threat and implemented policies to repress the spread of Communist and Socialist ideologies ($\\checkmark$). This fear was exacerbated by China becoming communist in 1949 and was subsequently used by Senator Joseph McCarthy to promote fear and suspicion about the influence and presence of Communism in the US government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0795","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0795\n\nHow did the Truman Doctrine change American foreign policy?\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- The doctrine indicated  that the US was no longer following a policy of isolationism and were committed to supporting democratic countries who may be being threatened by communism\n- In 1949, the U.S. and 11 other nations in North America and Europe formed NATO to guarantee security for its members\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Truman Doctrine changed American foreign policy as it meant that the US was no longer following a policy of isolationism. Focus was now on protecting democratic countries in Europe that were under threat from the influence of communism ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0796","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0796\n\nDescribe **two** of the $\\lq$show trials' that took place as a part of HUAC's campaign to expose communists in America.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Alger Hiss: A high-level official in the US State Department accused of being a spy.  In January 1950, he was found guilty of perjury for passing classified US information to the Soviet Union during the late 1930s. Hiss was not tried for treason as it was outside the allowed time limit on when legal proceedings for a crime could begin. He was sentenced to five years in prison.\n- Klaus Fuchs: was a physicist who worked on the Manhattan Project and was a spy for the Soviet Union, passing atomic secrets to them during the war. He was arrested in 1950, convicted of espionage, and sentenced to 14 years in prison.\n- Julius and Ethel Rosenberg:worked on the US Manhattan Project, a US-led atomic research project set up in 1941. They were arrested on charges of stealing atomic secrets for the Soviets and executed in 1953.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSome of the most famous trials were linked to people who worked on the Manhattan Project, an atomic research project that was set up in 1941, such as Klaus Fuchs. Fuchs was the lead physicist on this project and was accused of passing atomic secrets to the USSR during the war and sentenced to 14 years in prison ($\\checkmark$).\n\nJulius and Ethel Rosenberg also worked on the US Manhattan Project with Klaus Fuchs. They were arrested on charges of stealing atomic secrets for the Soviets and executed in 1953 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0797","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0797\n\nUsing the information provided in Source C, identify **three** reasons for opposition to the McCarran Act.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion Public Papers - Harry S Truman. Aug. 14, 1951 Excerpt. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, August 14). *Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion.* Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Retrieved May 28, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/191/address-dedication-new-washington-headquarters-american-legion. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Because it violates people's rights, damaging democracy and civil liberties\n- It violated the First Amendment, the right to free speech and assembly, by requiring groups considered Communist to register with the government and submit information about membership, finances, and activities.\n- It violated the Fifth Amendment, which states that the government cannot compel individuals to provide incriminating information about themselves.\n- Because it resembled policies of  Nazi Germany.\n- Because it violated people's rights in similar ways to previous laws, such as the Fugitive Slave Act, which were also reversed by Presidents who “used their power under the Constitution to reverse the court's rulings”.\n- It implies that registration as a Communist or Communist organization will lead to repression and wholesale persecution.\n- The Act is based on the same scare tactics that were employed by Senator Joseph McCarthy\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe poster warns people of the dangers of the McCarran Act in that it is breaching American civil liberties, saying $\\lq$preserve your democratic rights by registering protests not organizations' ($\\checkmark$). The poster also compares the Act to the actions of the Nazis who started with $\\lq$outlawing communists' and ended with $\\lq$wholescale persecution' ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the poster opposes the McCarran Act because it is a violation of the Fifth Amendment which protects individuals against self-incrimination ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0798","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0798\n\nCompare and contrast the ideas expressed in Sources B and C regarding the threat posed by Communism in 1951.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from an overview of the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) from the Harry S. Truman Presidential Library website. |\n\n[Source: *House Un-American Activities Committee - Background.*  Harry S. Truman Library and Museum, National Archives. Retrieved 28 May 2025 from www.trumanlibrary.gov/education/presidential-inquiries/house-un-american-activities-committee. Public domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion Public Papers - Harry S Truman. Aug. 14, 1951 Excerpt. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, August 14). *Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion.* Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Retrieved May 28, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/191/address-dedication-new-washington-headquarters-american-legion. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- To achieve full marks, the answer must include both a point of comparison (similarity) and a point of contrast (difference).\n- A minimum of **two** clear, valid arguments along with supporting evidence should be given to achieve the full **four** marks.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources recognize the threat posed by Communism after the Second World War.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$the frenzy of perceived threat posed by Communism'\n    - Source C: $\\lq$a wave of fear and uncertainty.'\n- Both look at the idea of civil liberties and the policies being put in place to deal with the threat of Communism.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$the lines between civil liberties and national security begging to blur'\n    - Source C: $\\lq$chipping away at our basic freedoms'\n- Both sources agree that people were afraid to stand up for their rights.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$blacklisted', seen as $\\lq$untrustworthy' and they could $\\lq$lose their job.\n    - Source C: describes the situation as a $\\lq$dictatorship' where people are living in $\\lq$terror of being denounced and slandered'\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- The sources differ in how they regard the impact of the threat.\n    - Source B: considers the American government's response to the perceived threat of communism explaining about the creation of the House of Un-American Activities which was ‘charged with identifying Communist threats to the United States'\n    - Source C focuses on criticising those in society who undermine the individual's rights in America and the $\\lq$keystone of government' in America which is about $\\lq$the liberty' of the individual and instead attacking with $\\lq$unproven accusations'\n- The sources focus on different perspectives of the idea of the perceived threat posed by communism.\n    - Source B is informative and gives the background to the perceived threat and the idea of McCarthyism and his $\\lq$campaign against alleged communist in the US government and other institutions'\n    - Source C is very critical of McCarthyism and the way he is undermining the idea of Americanism whilst $\\lq$proclaiming that they are its chief defenders'\n- The sources also disagree on the level of communist influence and impact within the US government.\n    - Source B suggests that the response by the government is $\\lq$necessary to protect national security'\n    - Source C states that the reaction is more of a witch hunt because in the US $\\lq$we have the finest and most loyal body of civil servants in the whole world.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Sources B and C show that the policies that are being put in place to deal with the threat of Communism are impacting people's civil liberties ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B talks about $\\lq$the lines between civil liberties and national security beginning to blur' and Source C suggests that they are $\\lq$chipping away at our basic freedoms' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth sources also agree that if a person has been suspected of Communist ties, they would be labeled and find it hard to have a livelihood ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\nSource B talks about employees being $\\lq$blacklisted', seen as $\\lq$untrustworthy' and they could $\\lq$lose their job. Source C talks about the $\\lq$most irresponsible kinds of accusations' and $\\lq$character assassination' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe two sources differ in how they regard the impact of the threat ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source B considers the impact in terms of the response of the American government to the perceived threat of communism, explaining about the creation of the House of Un-American Activities which was $\\lq$charged with identifying Communist threats to the United States' while Source C focuses on criticising those in society who are undermining the $\\lq$keystone of government' in America which is about $\\lq$the liberty' of the individual and instead attacking with $\\lq$unproven accusations' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe sources also disagree on the level of communist influence and impact within the US government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source B highlights the period of 1950-1954, stating that McCarthyism was $\\lq$necessary to secure national security.' In contrast, Source C states that $\\lq$we have the finest and most loyal body of civil servants in the whole world' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0799","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0799\n\nAccording to Source B, why was HUAC created?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from an overview of the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) from the Harry S. Truman Presidential Library website. |\n\n[Source: *House Un-American Activities Committee - Background.*  Harry S. Truman Library and Museum, National Archives. Retrieved 28 May 2025 from www.trumanlibrary.gov/education/presidential-inquiries/house-un-american-activities-committee. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- To counter the $\\lq$perceived threat posed by communism' that emerged after World War II\n- To $\\lq$identify Communist threats to the United States' as Cold War tensions increased.\n- Some people believed it to have been created to protect and $\\lq$secure national security' and others that it was a form of control and an attack on democracy.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B HUAC was created to $\\lq$identify Communist threats to the United States' resulting from increased Cold War tensions ($\\checkmark$). However, some people also believed HUAC was created to $\\lq$secure national security' ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0800","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0800\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying Truman's response to the threat of Communist infiltration.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion Public Papers - Harry S Truman. Aug. 14, 1951 Excerpt. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, August 14). *Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion.* Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Retrieved May 28, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/191/address-dedication-new-washington-headquarters-american-legion. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- This excerpt is from a public address at the new Washington Headquarters of the American Legion Public Papers, 1951 and shows an official governmental perspective on the response to the fear of increased communist infiltration in society\n- The speech was given at a time when the fear of communism was growing in the US, providing insight into the context of Truman's response.\n- The source shows Truman's perspective that the fear of Communism was undermining American values.\n- The address was given to show that the main focus of Truman's government is to defend people's constitutional rights, providing some justification for Truman's response.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- The source is a public speech at the Headquarters of the American Legion, so the content is focused on the President saying what this organization would expect to hear, rather than an objective message.\n- The language used is very emotional. Truman talks about Americanism being $\\lq$under attack', which is aimed at getting support for his campaigns.\n- The response does not acknowledge real concerns about the fear of communist infiltration that existed in society, so it provides a one-sided view of the threat.\n- Truman's response is also to protect his political position as he suggests that $\\lq$These people claim to be against communism. But they are chipping away at our basic freedoms just as insidiously and far more effectively than the Communists have ever been able to do.'\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is valuable as it is a public address given by President Truman himself, thus providing direct insight into his perspective about the threat of Communism at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). Because the content of the speech is based on the freedoms described in the US Bill of Rights, it helps provide the logic and justification for Truman's response ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, the language used is very emotional, with Truman saying Americanism is $\\lq$under attack' and that $\\lq$slander mongers' are trying to instill in people a $\\lq$fear of being called a communist' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). Truman is being pressured both by anti-communist critics and civil liberties advocates, so the source seeks to defend his political position more than address his objective views about the threat of Communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0801","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0801\n\nUsing the information in Source C, describe what principles constitute the idea of $\\lq$Real Americanism'.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion Public Papers - Harry S Truman. Aug. 14, 1951 Excerpt. |\n\n[Source: Truman, H. (1951, August 14). *Address at the Dedication of the New Washington Headquarters of the American Legion.* Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Retrieved May 28, 2025 from https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/191/address-dedication-new-washington-headquarters-american-legion. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- That freedom of speech should be protected regardless of whether or not we disagree with the opinions of others\n- That fair play is the basic principle that underlies the American Constitution.\n- That all accusations must be supported by evidence\n- That people should believe in the system and that the government is made up of $\\lq$the most loyal body of civil servants'\n- That no one should live in fear of being falsely accused.\n- That the innocent in society are protected.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C highlights that the principles that make up the idea of $\\lq$Real Americanism' are based on the ideas of fair play ($\\checkmark$). That people should feel safe and not live in fear of persecution or of being falsely accused ($\\checkmark$). The basic principle of Real Americanism is one based on freedom of speech, and this right should be defended $\\lq$regardless of how much we may disagree with them' ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0852","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0852\n\nExplain **two** reasons the United States supported the creation of the Organization of American States (OAS) in 1948.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Containment of communism: This aligned with Truman's Cold War containment policy, encouraging regional unity against Soviet influence.\n- Asserting US leadership in the Western Hemisphere:  Through the OAS, the US institutionalized its dominance and could influence political and diplomatic actions in the region.\n- Creating a collective security framework: The OAS provided a structure for coordinating actions against instability, especially leftist movements or coups.\n- Promoting political and economic cooperation: This helped counter Soviet influence by encouraging inter-American diplomacy and trade within a US-led bloc.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne reason the United States supported the creation of the OAS was to promote political and economic cooperation among the countries of the Americas ($\\checkmark$). By building stronger diplomatic and economic ties, the US hoped to limit Soviet influence and create a more unified, pro-Western hemisphere ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reason was to assert greater US leadership in the region ($\\checkmark$). The OAS gave the United States a formal mechanism to guide the political direction of the Americas and ensure regional policies aligned with US Cold War goals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0853","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0853\n\nDescribe **two** significant impacts of the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) during the early Cold War.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Blacklisting in Hollywood: HUAC's investigations led to the blacklisting of actors, writers, and directors accused of communist ties.\n- Limiting political expression: Created a culture of fear in which individuals avoided controversial political opinions to avoid suspicion.\n- Reinforcing anti-communist legislation: HUAC's work supported laws like the Internal Security Act, increasing government control over leftist groups.\n- Expanding federal loyalty programs: HUAC contributed to the creation of loyalty oaths and background checks for federal employees.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne significant impact of HUAC during the early Cold War was its investigation of the film industry, which resulted in the blacklisting of screenwriters, directors, and actors who were accused of having communist connections. Many of these individuals were unable to find work for years, even without clear evidence against them ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother impact was that HUAC helped to create a political climate in which Americans became increasingly afraid to express dissenting views. People were cautious about attending certain meetings or criticizing the government because they feared being investigated or labeled un-American ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0854","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0854\n\nCompare and contrast the “New Look” foreign policy of President Eisenhower with Truman's Cold War strategy in the Americas.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both aimed to contain the spread of communism in the Western Hemisphere.\n- Both used alliances and hemispheric cooperation (e.g., OAS) to support US influence in Latin America.\n- Both supported authoritarian regimes if they aligned with US anti-communist goals.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Truman emphasized conventional military aid and troop deployments (e.g., Korean War), while Eisenhower's New Look emphasized nuclear deterrence and “massive retaliation.”\n- Truman was more cautious, while Eisenhower significantly expanded CIA operations in Latin America (e.g., Guatemala 1954).\n- Truman used broad economic aid programs (e.g., Point Four), while Eisenhower emphasized strategic spending and cost-cutting in defense.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Truman and Eisenhower aimed to prevent the spread of communism in the Americas, aligning their foreign policies with the broader Cold War goal of containment ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Each president supported regimes that were anti-communist, even if they were authoritarian, as seen in US backing for conservative governments in countries like Cuba (pre-1959) and Nicaragua ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, their strategies differed significantly. Truman relied more on conventional military approaches, such as providing direct military aid and deploying U.S. forces to resist communist expansion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). In contrast, Eisenhower shifted toward a strategy of nuclear deterrence through the policy of “massive retaliation,” while also expanding the use of covert CIA operations to achieve U.S. objectives without large-scale military involvement. This is seen in the 1954 CIA-backed coup in Guatemala, which overthrew the democratically elected President Jacobo Árbenz ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThey also differed in economic strategy. Truman promoted broader developmental goals through initiatives like the Point Four Program, which aimed to support modernization in Latin America ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Eisenhower, however, prioritized economic restraint and tied foreign aid more directly to strategic objectives, such as securing military bases and backing anti-communist regimes that aligned with U.S. interests ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0855","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0855\n\nExplain **two** consequences of Vice President Richard Nixon's 1958 visit to Latin America.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Nixon's hostile reception, especially the violent protests in Caracas, exposed widespread public anger at U.S. policies. These reactions stemmed from long-standing resentment over U.S. support for authoritarian regimes and economic control in the region.\n- The attack on Nixon's motorcade in Venezuela became a global spectacle. It suggested the U.S. had lost influence and respect, damaging the image of American leadership during the Cold War.\n- The violence forced U.S. officials to reassess their approach to the region. This led to increased attention on public diplomacy and economic development, paving the way for later initiatives like the Alliance for Progress.\n- The U.S. mobilized military forces (Operation Poor Richard) in case of further threats. While the forces weren't deployed, it showed how Cold War-era Washington saw Latin unrest as a national security issue.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne consequence of Nixon's 1958 trip to Latin America was that it triggered a serious reevaluation of U.S. policy toward the region ($\\checkmark$). After facing unexpected and intense hostility, American officials began to realize that military alliances and anti-communism alone were not enough—there was a growing recognition that economic inequality and public resentment needed to be addressed through more comprehensive engagement ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother consequence was that the U.S. government prepared for a possible military intervention in Venezuela following the attack on Nixon's convoy ($\\checkmark$). Although the troops were never deployed, the swift mobilization revealed how quickly Cold War tensions could escalate and how the U.S. viewed unrest in Latin America as a potential threat to hemispheric stability ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0856","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0856\n\nExplain **three** reasons why some countries in the Americas chose not to participate in the Korean War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Desire to maintain neutrality: Mexico opted to avoid Cold War entanglements, maintaining a policy of non-intervention and neutrality in global conflicts.\n- Domestic political instability or transition: Argentina, under Juan Perón, focused on internal political consolidation and economic nationalism rather than foreign military commitments.\n- Limited military or financial capacity: Paraguay and Honduras lacked the logistical means and resources to contribute troops to a distant conflict.\n- Skepticism of U.S. foreign policy: Uruguay and others were cautious about appearing to serve U.S. interests without clear national benefit, especially in a distant war.\n- Public or political opposition: In Chile, there was no broad political consensus or public will to justify military intervention in East Asia.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSeveral countries in the Americas chose not to take part in the Korean War due to concerns about sovereignty and avoiding alignment in global power struggles ($\\checkmark$). For instance, Mexico avoided involvement because of its traditional foreign policy principle of non-intervention, which aimed to keep the country out of external military conflicts after a long history of foreign interference ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn Argentina, President Juan Perón prioritized national development and a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$third position\" foreign policy that rejected both U.S. and Soviet influence ($\\checkmark$). As such, military participation in a distant Asian war would have contradicted his nationalist agenda and risked internal political backlash ($\\checkmark$).\n\nMeanwhile, many smaller countries in Central America, such as El Salvador and Nicaragua, simply lacked the military infrastructure and financial resources to deploy troops abroad ($\\checkmark$). For them, the war in Korea had little strategic value and was seen as a distant conflict better left to the major powers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0857","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0857\n\nAccording to Source A, describe three methods used by the CIA to undermine the Arbenz government in Guatemala.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A Memorandum created by the CIA in 1975 about the actions taken by the Agency in Guatemala in the 1950s. |\n\n:box[In August 1953, the Operations Coordinating Board directed CIA to assume responsibility for operations against the Arbenz regime. Appropriate authorization was issued to permit close and prompt cooperation with the Departments of Defense, State and other Government agencies in order to support the Agency in this task. The plan of operations called for cutting off military aid to Guatemala, increasing aid to its neighbors, exerting diplomatic and economic pressure against Arbenz and attempts to subvert and or defect Army and political leaders, broad scale psychological warfare and paramilitary actions. During the period August through December 1953 a CIA staff was assembled and operational plans were prepared.\\\n\\\nFollowing are the specific operational mechanisms utilized by the Agency in the overall missions against the Arbenz government:\\\n\\\n$\\hspace{2em}$a. Paramilitary Operations. Approximately 85 members of the Castillo Armas group received training in Nicaragua. Thirty were trained in sabotage, six as shock troop leaders and 20 others as support-type personnel. Eighty-nine tons of equipment were prepared. The support of this operation was staged inside the borders of Honduras and Nicaragua. [1-1/2 lines of source text not declassified] There were an estimated 250 men in Honduras and El Salvador for use as shock troops and specialists, outside of the training personnel that had been sent to Nicaragua.\\\n$\\hspace{2em}$b. Air Operations. The planning for providing air operational support was broken down into three phases; i.e. the initial stockpiling of equipment; the delivering of equipment to advance bases by black flight; and the aerial resupply of troops in the field. Thirty days prior to D-day, a fourth phase, fighter support, was initiated. There were approximately 80 missions flown during the 14–29 June 1954 period, by various type aircraft such as C-47's, F-47's and Cessnas which were used to discharge cargo, distribute propaganda and for strafing and bombing missions.\\\n$\\hspace{2em}$c. Clandestine Communications. A clandestine radio broadcasting station was established in Nicaragua. The purpose of these broadcasts was to intimidate members of the Communist Party and public officials [Page 449]who were sympathetic to the Communist cause. The radio station, prior to D-day, broadcasted programs on why they were on the air; dramatized examples of Communist tyranny; the ideologies and aims of the Liberation Movement and what effect was intended vis-à-vis each individual who was listening; an aggressive program outlining the activities which would ultimately bring down the Communist threat, etc.\\\n$\\hspace{2em}$d. Q Program. The objective was spreading responsibility for the operation throughout as many Latin American countries as possible in order to lessen the impact of United States participation.\\\n$\\hspace{2em}$e. Indigenous agent radio operator training program. This included 13 radio operators, including seven residents and six tactical, and one cryptographer who were trained in Nicaragua from 6 March-9 June 1954.]\n\n[Source: Central Intelligence Agency (May 12, 1975) *Memorandum Prepared in the Central Intelligence Agency.* Office of the US Historian. Retrieved June 30, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54Guat/d287. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Paramilitary operations - The CIA trained approximately 85 members of the Castillo Armas group in Nicaragua in sabotage, shock leadership, and support roles. An additional 250 men were staged in Honduras and El Salvador to be used as shock troops and specialists.\n- Air operations - The CIA flew about 80 missions between June 14–29, 1954, using aircraft like C-47s and F-47s. These were used for supply drops, propaganda distribution, and for conducting bombing and strafing missions.\n- Psychological warfare (Clandestine communications) - A clandestine radio station was established in Nicaragua that broadcast propaganda to intimidate Guatemalan Communist Party members and sympathizers. Programming dramatized communist oppression and promoted the goals of the “Liberation Movement.”\n- Q Program - This was intended to involve multiple Latin American countries in the operation to reduce the appearance of direct U.S. involvement.\n- Indigenous agent training - Thirteen radio operators and one cryptographer were trained in Nicaragua between March and June 1954 to support CIA communications.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe CIA attempted to destabilize the Arbenz government by building up an armed opposition force. According to the source, dozens of Castillo Armas's supporters were trained in Nicaragua in specialized roles, including sabotage and leadership, while hundreds more were positioned in neighboring Honduras and El Salvador to serve as combat troops ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThey also relied on air support to aid the mission. Aircraft were used over a two-week period in June 1954 to deliver supplies, spread propaganda leaflets, and carry out attacks such as bombings and strafing, as part of a coordinated military effort to pressure the Guatemalan regime ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn addition to physical operations, the CIA conducted psychological warfare through a secret radio station based in Nicaragua. This station broadcast anti-communist propaganda aimed at intimidating both officials and citizens loyal to Arbenz, portraying the Liberation Movement as a powerful force and communism as a direct threat to Guatemalan society ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0858","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0858\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the aims and priorities of U.S. Cold War strategy during the Eisenhower administration.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpts from NSC 162/2 on 30 October 1953. This policy is the basis for what is known as Eisenhower's $\\lq$New Look' Policy. |\n\n[Source: National Security Council (October 30, 1953) *NSC 162/2 Note by the Executive Secretary to the National Security Council on Basic National Security Policy.* Retrieved July 16, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v02p1/d101. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nValues\n- Created by the U.S. National Security Council and approved by President Eisenhower in 1953—highly authoritative. Reflects official U.S. government thinking at a pivotal moment of Cold War policy development. Its timing offers insight into the early Cold War context and the transition from Truman to Eisenhower.\n- Designed to guide national security decisions—shows formal articulation of the New Look strategy. Reveals core aims such as reliance on nuclear weapons, deterrence, strategic bases, and cost control.\n- Clearly outlines the central tenets of the New Look strategy, including massive retaliation and economic sustainability. Useful for identifying key U.S. priorities during early Cold War tensions.\n\nLimitations\n- Reflects only elite, internal U.S. government perspectives—excludes dissent, public opinion, and international responses. May downplay risks or controversial elements for the sake of bureaucratic clarity.\n- Created at the start of the policy, so it doesn't reveal how strategies were implemented or whether they succeeded.\n- As a prescriptive planning document, it does not evaluate actual outcomes or acknowledge limitations in execution. May have been written to justify decisions rather than explore risks or failures.\n- Focused on broad strategy—omits regional details (e.g., Latin America) or concrete case studies. Does not address responses from allies or adversaries or unintended consequences.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B is valuable for a historian studying U.S. Cold War strategy because it was produced by the National Security Council and approved by President Eisenhower in 1953, offering direct insight into the thinking of top U.S. officials during the early Cold War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). Its purpose was to coordinate national security policy, making it useful for understanding the intended direction of the “New Look” strategy, especially its focus on nuclear deterrence, alliance-building, and economic sustainability ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content further supports this by clearly outlining the central pillars of the strategy, such as reliance on atomic weapons, strategic air power, and overseas bases, which were all defining features of Eisenhower's approach ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. Since it was produced at the beginning of the New Look, it does not reflect how the strategy was implemented or whether it was successful, limiting its usefulness for evaluating actual outcomes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Its purpose as a planning document means it was not intended to analyze failures or dissenting views, which makes it one-sided and potentially idealized ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The content, while clear on general strategic aims, lacks detail about how these policies played out in specific regions, such as Latin America, and provides no perspective on how allies or opponents reacted ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0859","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0859\n\nAccording to Source B, identify **three** priorities of U.S. Cold War strategy under the New Look policy.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpts from NSC 162/2 on 30 October 1953. This policy is the basis for what is known as Eisenhower's $\\lq$New Look' Policy. |\n\n[Source: National Security Council (October 30, 1953) *NSC 162/2 Note by the Executive Secretary to the National Security Council on Basic National Security Policy.* Retrieved July 16, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v02p1/d101. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Reliance on atomic capability to counterbalance Soviet power. The United States placed strategic emphasis on its atomic weapons as the primary means of deterring the Soviet Union.\n- Dependence on allied cooperation to meet defense needs. U.S. defense strategy required active participation and political support from allied nations.\n- Need for overseas bases to support strategic air power. Effective deployment of air power depended on securing military bases in allied countries.\n- Emphasis on maintaining economic strength while defending against communism. Military planning was shaped by the need to avoid excessive spending that could weaken the U.S. economy.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, one priority of U.S. strategy was to use its nuclear arsenal as the foundation of its global defense plan ($\\checkmark$). Another focus was ensuring close collaboration with friendly nations, since the U.S. couldn't maintain its position alone ($\\checkmark$). A third goal was to secure access to foreign military bases in partner countries to make its air forces effective in any future conflict ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0860","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0860\n\nWhat are **two** messages conveyed by Source C about Marshall Aid?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A 1949 cartoon about Marshall Aid by Clive Uptton. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/The%20Cold%20War%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0860.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0860\">\n[Source: Uptton, C. (1949). *Marshall Aid.* The National Archives INF 3/1295. Retrieved on July 19, 2025 from https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/cold-war-on-file/marshall-aid-cartoon/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Marshall Aid was intended to be temporary - The aid is described as something to “keep you going till 1952,” implying short-term support until Europe can recover independently.\n- The aid is productive and supportive, not controlling - The man receiving the aid (likely representing a European country) is grateful and confident he can become self-sufficient, suggesting Marshall Aid was enabling rather than dominating.\n- Marshall Aid provided essential materials to rebuild Europe - The labeled items ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Food and Raw Material”) and the farming metaphor suggest the U.S. supplied basic inputs to help Europe $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$grow\" its economy again.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne message of the cartoon is that Marshall Aid was meant to support European recovery only for a limited time, not permanently. This is shown by the man saying it will $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$keep you going till 1952\" ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother message is that the aid was empowering rather than controlling—the farmer says he'll be able to provide for himself soon, suggesting that the U.S. help was designed to make Europe self-sufficient again ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0861","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0861\n\nUsing Source D, explain **three** ways Soviet media portrayed the outbreak of the Korean War.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Extract from a British Foreign Office report on the Soviet government's management of media coverage of the Korean War, July 7th, 1950. |\n\n:box[Although the march of events in Korea is too swift and too uncertain to justify an attempt at this stage to draw any but the most tentative conclusions from them, I think that it may be not without interest to record the steps - or, rather, such of them as were visible to an observer in Moscow - which led up to the invasion of South Korea on the 25th June.\\\n\\\n2. In retrospect the ideological preparation for the use of force can be detected clearly enough. It must be borne in mind, however, that Korea is only one of the numerous fields in which Soviet propagandists have, for many months past, claimed to see evidence of the aggressive attentions of the United States; so that until the blow actually fell, there was no more reason to suspect the imminence of a Communist coup in Korea than in, say, Persia or Yugoslavia. Indeed until June, the Soviet press seemed to find relatively little interest in the Korean scene. Even the elections held in South Korea on the 30th May under the supervision of the United Nations Commission passed almost unnoticed in the Soviet Press, and it was not until a fortnight later that a minor Soviet commentator asserted that the results had confirmed that the broad masses in South Korea were resolutely condemning the “treacherous policy of the Syngman Rhee clique”.\\\n\\\n3. Then, suddenly, on the 8th June, the first move was made by the North Korean authorities in the campaign which was to reach its climax with the invasion of South Korea on the 25th June. The Central Committee of the United Democratic Fatherland Front of North Korea published an appeal for the holding of a general election on August 5th-8th August throughout the whole territory of Korea which was to lead to the “peaceful unification” of all Korea. The appeal significantly demanded that both the rulers of South Korea and the United Nations Commission should be excluded from participation in the elections. On the 17th June Mr. Dulles arrived in South Korea, enabling the official news agency of North Korea to comment a few days later that this visit had revealed clearly that the Americans intended to “frustrate at all costs peaceful unification and to cause fratricidal war” so as to consolidate their dominion in South Korea.\\\n\\\n4. As was presumably expected, the appeal of the Fatherland Front was not accepted by the South Koreans, and the North Koreans then passed to the next stage of their campaign which was the publication on the 20th June of a decree by the Presidium of the People’s Assembly of North Korea proposing that the peaceful unification of Korea should now be effected by the merging of the Assemblies of North and South Korea as prelude to the drawing up of a new constitution under which all Korean elections could be held. In this decree, it was now stated not only that the present rulers of South Korea and the United Nations Commission should not participate but also that the former should be arrested and the later expelled. The sinister proposal was also made that the armies and police forces of both countries should be reorganised into single bodies on “democratic foundations”. In effect the decree constituted a demand for the voluntary surrender of South to North Korea. As such, it stood no chance of acceptance, and can only have been intended as the final step in the preparations for the invasion of the 25th June.\\\n\\\n5. All these manoeuvres [by the North Koreans] were faithfully reported by the Soviet press, in the form of Tass messages from the North Korean capital. Comment was carefully eschewed, but the agency reports were sufficient to prepare the Soviet reader for ready acceptance of the myth that the North Koreans, so far from attacking their Southern neighbours, were themselves the victims of an unprincipled aggression launched under United States auspices.]\n\n[Source: Foreign Office (July 7, 1950) *Soviet press comment on the Korean war.* The National Archives FO 371/86738. Retrieved on July 19, 2025 from https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/cold-war-on-file/soviet-media-on-korea/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Portrayed the U.S. as the aggressor - The Soviet media suggested that the war was provoked by the United States, not North Korea—framing the North as victims of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unprincipled aggression.\"\n- Emphasized peaceful intentions by North Korea - Soviet coverage highlighted North Korean appeals for “peaceful unification” through elections and a merged assembly—despite the fact these demands were ultimately unacceptable.\n- Framed South Korean and UN leaders as obstacles to peace - The press presented South Korean leadership (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Syngman Rhee clique\") and the UN Commission as threats to peace and called for their exclusion and even arrest.\n- Justified North Korean military preparations as a reaction to U.S. provocation - The visit by U.S. envoy John Foster Dulles was used to claim the U.S. was preparing war, thus framing North Korean actions as defensive rather than aggressive.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to the source, Soviet media portrayed the conflict in a way that made North Korea appear peaceful and reasonable. They emphasized proposals like country-wide elections and the merging of assemblies, which suggested North Korea was seeking unity rather than war ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAt the same time, the Soviet press positioned the South Korean government and the United Nations as hostile actors. It reported calls for the arrest of South Korean leaders and the removal of UN officials, making them appear like obstacles to peace ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, Soviet reporting blamed the United States for the war. By focusing on John Foster Dulles's visit and suggesting it revealed an American plan to provoke conflict, Soviet outlets made it seem like the North was responding to U.S. aggression rather than initiating violence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0862","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0862\n\nDescribe President Kennedy's Alliance for Progress and identify **two** challenges in implementing the plan.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following description or similar:\n- President Kennedy's Alliance for Progress was a 10-year plan that promised aid to Latin American countries to help spur social change and commit to democracy.\n[1 marks]\n\nFor challenges, any **two** of the following:\n- The program expected change to happen too quickly which was a challenge for many Latin American governments.\n- The wealthy in Latin American countries were resistant to change that might limit their wealth.\n- Much of the funding did not reach the place it was supposed to reach because of corruption in the government.\n- Latin American nationalists were resistant to the plan as it was seen as “too American.”\n- US economic interests often conflicted with the goals of the Alliance for Progress.\n- Many believed that the Alliance was just an anti-Communist tool of the United States\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPresident Kennedy's Alliance for Progress was meant to aid Latin American countries committed to democracy and social progress ($\\checkmark$). The implementation of the program proved to be difficult in many Latin American countries. First, the program was seen by many as a way for America to push its anti-Communist beliefs onto other countries ($\\checkmark$). Also, many of the elites in Latin America were resistant to change that would limit their wealth ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0863","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0863\n\nExplain President Kennedy's reasons for creating the Peace Corps and give **two** reasons why it was or was not successful.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following reasons for creation or similar:\n- President Kennedy designed the Peace Corps in order to help developing countries and to improve the international image of the United States.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following to assess its successes:\n- Was successful:\n    - The Peace Corps helped to develop successful educational systems and train teachers in countries such as India and Ghana.\n    - The Peace Corps promoted a positive image of the United States in developing countries as most of them lived modestly.\n    - Several newly independent nations benefited from the programs offered by the Peace Corps.\n    - Volunteers came back to the United States more aware of global issues.\n- Was NOT successful:\n    - Volunteers were not always prepared or trained well for their work.\n    - Critics saw this as a ploy to spread US influence and contain communism.\n    - The success of the program was largely dependent on the reception of the host countries.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPresident Kennedy created the Peace Corps to provide help and aid to developing countries while also showing the United States in a positive light ($\\checkmark$). Like most programs, there were successes and failures within the program. The program was largely successful in helping the people of developing nations build education systems and learn English ($\\checkmark$). However, the success of the Peace Corps often depended on how the host countries perceived the volunteers. Many countries did not embrace the Peace Corps, leaving the programs unsuccessful in some places ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0864","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0864\n\nDescribe President Carter's beliefs regarding the Panama Canal.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- President Carter believed that the United States’s control over the Panama Canal was leftover from the age of imperialism.\n- Carter believed that it was important for the Canal to be neutral for international shipping regardless of who controlled the Canal. The Neutrality Treaty between Panama and the United States agreed to this.\n- Carter believed that control of the Canal should be relinquished to Panama. The Panama Canal Treaty agreed that control of the canal would be handed over to Panama in 1999.\n- He believed that giving control of the canal back to Panama would help to improve US relations in Latin America.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPresident Carter believed that Panama should control the Panama Canal and that relinquishing control of the canal would improve US relations in Latin America ($\\checkmark$). He also believed that the Canal should be neutral for international shipping, no matter who controlled the canal ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0865","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0865\n\nSummarize the reasons for and the effects of US intervention in Chile between 1970 and 1974.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following with at least **one** reason and at least **two** effects:\n- Reasons\n    - The US foreign policy was still very focused on containment, especially following the Cuban Revolution\n    - Marxist Salvador Allende's popularity was increasing, which concerned the Americans, as he would be the first democratically elected Marxist.\n    - US multinational companies had less influence in Chile and would carry even less if Allende became their leader.\n- Effects\n    - The United States supported a destabilization campaign through the CIA to undermine Allende's leadership. This would lead to a coup where Allende committed suicide before he could be captured.\n    - The military coup was led by Augusto Pinochet, who became a brutal totalitarian.\n    - The coup strengthened distrust of America in Latin America, specifically because they were seen as hypocrites who undermined a democratically elected government.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFollowing the Cuban Revolution, the United States was very focused on stopping the spread of communism in Latin America ($\\checkmark$). They were extremely concerned that Salvador Allende would win his election, becoming the first democratically elected Merxist ($\\checkmark$). After he won, the United States supported a destabilization campaign that undermined Allende's leadership, leading to a Coup by the Chilean military leader, Augusto Pinochet ($\\checkmark$). Pinochet was a violent totalitarian who was responsible for countless deaths in Chile ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0866","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0866\n\nDescribe the Cuban Missile Crisis and its influence on the Cold War.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following description or similar:\n- The Cuban Missile Crisis was a two week long standoff between the United States and the Soviet Union over the placement of ballistic missiles in Cuba. As a response to the discovery of the Missiles, President Kennedy initiated a naval blockade of Cuba.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **three** of the following for influence on the Cold War:\n- The Cuban Missile Crisis was the most significant threat of nuclear war to take place during the Cold War and both countries were able to see how quickly each side could escalate.\n- Communication between Washington and Moscow improved following this crisis in order to more effectively avoid a larger conflict.\n- Even though both sides made concessions to resolve the conflict, Nikita Khrushchev appeared to take the bigger political hit as he agreed to remove the missiles from Cuba publicly.\n- President Kennedy quietly agreed to remove missiles from Turkey and redeemed some of his social credit after the disastrous Bay of Pigs invasion.\n- Both Superpowers were motivated to pursue nuclear arms agreements.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Cuban Missile Crisis took place after the United States discovered Soviet ballistic missiles in Cuba. As a result of the missiles, the United States initiated a naval blockade of Cuba ($\\checkmark$). This crisis came close to escalating the Cold War to a nuclear war and both sides were able to see how quickly escalation could occur ($\\checkmark$). Because of this, the superpowers moved away from the policy of brinkmanship and began pursuing nuclear arms agreements  ($\\checkmark$). Also, communication between the president of the United States and the First Secretary of the Soviet Union improved following the crisis ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0867","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0867\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the nature of the war in Vietnam?\n[2 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | “Stick ‘em up!” is a cartoon by Edmund Valtman published in June 1964. In it, a caricature of President Lyndon B. Johnson is portrayed as a cowboy with a caricature of an unnamed Vietnamese soldier. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/The%20Cold%20War%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0867.jpg\" alt=\"HI0867\">\n[Source: Valtman, E. (1964). *Stick $\\lq$Em Up!* Library of Congress. Retrieved on June 29, 2025 from www.loc.gov/exhibits/pointing-their-pens-editorial-cartoons/vietnam-war.html#obj018. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- President Johnson is portrayed as a cowboy and larger than the Vietnamese soldier pictured, indicating his power.\n- President Johnson is claiming to be very powerful and clinging to hydrogen bombs, indicating that the United States has the power to win the war.\n- The concerned look on Johnson's face indicates that even though they have nuclear weapons, they are not winning the war.\n- The laughing Vietnamese soldier is labeled as “guerrilla warfare” and pointing a conventional weapon at the surprised Johnson, indicating that the United States was not prepared to fight the Vietnamese in this way.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A shows a Vietnamese soldier holding President Johnson at gunpoint using conventional weapons and guerrilla warfare. The happy look on his face indicates that the Vietnamese have taken President Johnson by surprise with their fighting methods ($\\checkmark$). It also indicates that though the United States has many nuclear weapons and could be considered the more powerful military, they are holding them rather than using them, and that is not helping them fight the Vietnamese ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0870","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0870\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from President Johnson's Message to Congress August 5, 1964 following the Gulf of Tonkin incident. |\n\n[Source: Johnson, L. B. (1964, August 5). *The Tonkin Gulf Incident,* 1964. Department of State Bulletin, August 24, 1964. Retrieved on June 29, 2025 from  https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/tonkin-g.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The origin of the source is valuable as it comes from an address by the President of the United States during the time that the United States became officially involved in the Vietnam War.\n- The purpose of the source is valuable as it is meant to convince the representatives of the U.S. government that their joining the war effort was the necessary and legal thing to do.\n- The content of the source is valuable as it explains President Nixon's justification for becoming more involved in the Vietnam War.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following\n- The purpose of the source is limited as it is a public speech being made in order to convince the U.S. government and people that they are justified in becoming more involved in war without considering alternate reasons for the attack on the U.S. ships.\n- The content of the source is limited as it is solely based on why the United States should become more involved in the war and doesn't give any context on their history in the region up to this point.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is valuable as it is a speech to Congress by the President of the United States, who clearly gives his reasons for becoming more involved in the Vietnam War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). However, the purpose may be considered limited as President Johnson is clearly trying to sway members of Congress to his way of thinking and may therefore be leaving out other bits of information ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Still, the content of the source is valuable as it explains President Johnson's justification for further military action with great detail ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). The content is also limited, however, as it only focuses on why the US should become involved and gives no detail or context about prior involvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0871","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0871\n\nUsing **two** pieces of evidence from Source C, explain how the President and some members of Congress were divided about the end of the Vietnam War.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Memorandum dated January 25, 1973 from Harry R. Haldeman, the White House Chief of Staff for President Richard Nixon, to Henry Kissinger, the President's National Security Advisor. |\n\n[Source: *Memorandum From the Assistant to the President (Haldeman) to the President's Assistant for National Security Affairs (Kissinger).* (1973, January 25). National Archives. Retrieved on June 28, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v09/d335. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting analysis:\n- “When you go to the Capitol you must at all costs give no quarter whatever to the doves. Any tilt to your remarks must be toward the side of those who have stood with us rather than to those who have always opposed us.”\n    - Hardeman references the “doves” as a group that was separate from “those who have stood with us.” This indicates a clear division between the president and some members of Congress.\n- “The simple points must be made that our opponents in the Congress and in the media wanted to end the war in Vietnam with dishonor and what amounted really to an abject surrender and defeat for the United States. We persisted in seeing it through until the war was ended with honor.”\n    - Hardeman refers to opponents in Congress and a difference in opinion on how to end the war.\n- “The difference between the Senate and House doves’ position of POWs for withdrawal and the peace we finally got is very simply that the prisoners for withdrawal proposal would have meant that the United States would get out and let the war go ahead. In other words, it would end the war for us and have the war continue for those that remained with 1,000 casualties a week at least ad infinitum.”\n    - Hardeman describes the difference between the “dove” plan for withdrawal and the final withdrawal plan.\n- “Our opponents would have ended the war in a way that would have led at the very least to a Communist coalition government for South Vietnam or a totally Communist government for South Vietnam. We have ended in a way that assures the people of South Vietnam the right to determine their own future in free, internationally supervised elections, which means that there will be no Communist government unless the people want it.”\n    - Hardeman uses language such as “our opponents” to explain how the Nixon administration’s views differed from others.\n- “Finally, our opponents with all their talk about peace were only interested in getting peace for America and would have ended our involvement in a way that would have allowed the war to continue indefinitely for the long-suffering people of Indochina. In other words, peace with honor means peace with independence for South Vietnam and peace for the people of Southeast Asia. Peace with surrender and dishonor means peace for us but a Communist government for South Vietnam and continued war.”\n    - Hardeman focuses on how their opponents did not consider long-term peace in Indochina.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C indicates that President Nixon and the “doves” had different opinions on how the United States should leave the Vietnam War ($\\checkmark$). Throughout the text, Mr. Haldeman relays President Nixon's thoughts on how Mr. Kissinger should present his plan to Congress. This includes him saying that their opponents wanted to end the war with dishonor and an “abject surrender” though Nixon’s plan brought honor to South Vietnam and the American soldiers ($\\checkmark$). Also, he relays that their opponents “with all their talk about peace were only interested in getting peace for America and would have ended our involvement in a way that would have allowed the war to continue indefinitely for the long-suffering people of Indochina” ($\\checkmark$). This implies that Nixon and Kissinger felt leaving the war would harm the people of Vietnam while some in Congress were only concerne with “peace for America” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0995","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0995\n\nDescribe the emerging sentiments in the United States that gave rise to the Red Scare.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - After World War II, American society was worried and anxious about the post-war period.\n\n - Americans feared radical ideologies, such as communism.\n\n - Labor strikes, bombings, and the Russian Revolution generated distrust towards immigrants and those who professed other ideologies.\n\n - Fear of subversive activities created a paranoia in American society and government that gave rise to the Red Scare.\n\n - Anti-immigrant sentiments led to a growing fear and suspicion toward all foreigners.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne of the main aspects was the fear of radical ideologies such as communism, as most Americans associated them with strikes and social demonstrations, and they provoked fears of a possible revolution in society. ($\\checkmark$) The Russian Revolution generated greater concern that these movements could also occur in the United States, while labor strikes and bombings further increased public anxiety. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nWidespread anti-immigrant sentiment was another key aspect, as immigrants were blamed for promoting dangerous ideas that could lead to social instability. Immigrants became prime suspects during the Red Scare, leading to calls for government anti-immigration measures and increased surveillance of foreign-born residents. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0996","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0996\n\nExplain **two** reasons for Fidel Castro's visit to Chile during Salvador Allende's presidency.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - Political solidarity: There was an ideological affinity between Castro and Allende, as both promoted socialist changes beyond the different paths by which they came to power.\n\n - Strengthening diplomatic relations: Until Allende took office, Cuba was isolated in the Americas, so it sought Chile's alliance, a feeling that was reciprocated, as Allende also needed allies on the continent.\n\n - Exchange of experiences on socialism: Castro was interested in seeing the development of the so-called \"peaceful path\" to socialism, and both wished to discuss strategies for its growth and for addressing potential future problems.\n\n - Symbolic and propaganda value: the meeting promoted regional unity of anti-imperialist forces to confront interference from foreign powers like the United States.\n\n - Regional implications: The meeting sought to inspire some leftist and anti-imperialist movements that were emerging in other Latin American countries, showing that socialism was possible on the continent.\n\n - Economic and trade cooperation: Both heads of state sought new allies and trade agreements to address the isolation that the United States was promoting toward both countries.\n\n - Popular participation and mass mobilization: Fidel Castro's extensive visit also sought to strengthen the support of workers, students, and peasants for the Allende government and the reforms it promoted.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThey need economic cooperation and the development of trade agreements due to the isolation promoted by the USA towards both countries as a strategy to undermine the socialist governments of Salvador Allende in Chile and Fidel Castro in Cuba. ($\\checkmark$) They were facing delicate financial situations that limited their economic growth and political influence. During Salvador Allende’s presidency, the US sought to destabilize his socialist government through monetary measures. In the case of Cuba, the US imposed an economic embargo on Cuba. So they needed to establish alliances that would allow them to strengthen their economies and reduce their dependence on antagonistic foreign powers. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThe meeting also aimed to inspire emerging leftist and anti-imperialist movements across Latin America. ($\\checkmark$) Both leaders wanted to send a message of hope and possibility, letting people know that creating a socialist society was realistic and achievable for Latin American nations, not just an abstract dream. This was an important message because numerous anti-imperialist movements marked the period between 1960 and 1970, challenged foreign influence—especially that of the United States—and sought to regain economic, political, and cultural sovereignty for their countries. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI1004","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1004\n\nEvaluate the social and cultural impact of the Cold War on the Americas from 1945-1953.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\nMost responses are likely to argue that the Cold War significantly impacted society and culture in the Americas, while some may also acknowledge areas of life that were less impacted. The strongest responses will provide examples of impacts in a variety of countries and/or regions, possibly including the US, Canada, and Latin America.\n\nCold War impact in the United States:\n\n - McCarthyism: Senator Joseph McCarthy led trials in an attempt to root out communism in the United States. Similar to the HUAC, these trials focused on influential people or groups. These included Hollywood actors, federal employees, the military, college professors, and labor unions, though accusations were made against many people without any evidence. This led to a culture of fear in the United States, where anyone could be accused and investigated.\n\n - Red Scare: The end of World War II and the beginning of tensions with the Soviet Union brought about a second Red Scare, or fear of the spread of communism. This fear was fueled by the actions of the HUAC and President Truman's foreign policies, as well as the looming threat of nuclear war. The threat of nuclear war, in particular, permeated the lives of all Americans as children practiced hiding under their desks in school in preparation for a nuclear attack. Many movies, books, and cartoons also included evidence of anti-communist rhetoric.\n\n - House Un-American Activities Committee: While the HUAC existed before World War II, after the war, it focused almost exclusively on rooting out communism in America. The HUAC inspired the Hollywood Blacklist, a refusal to hire people who may have had ties to subversive people and ideas. Hearings were highly publicized and instilled fear in Americans while encouraging conformity. People who were questioned often had their careers ruined.\n\n - Civil Liberties: Fear of communism and the Cold War led to restrictions on civil liberties. Because Senator McCarthy's and the HUAC's hearings were so detrimental to people, they were unlikely to question the methods being used to root out communism. Since people could accuse just about anybody of being a communist or knowing a communist, people kept quiet to stay out of it.\n\nAreas of life with less of an impact\n\n - Consumer Culture: The United States prospered following World War II, leading to a consumer culture that had not previously existed. Some historians believe that this had a greater impact on American life. Some specific examples that can be connected to this argument are increased amounts of consumer goods, suburbanization, and increased forms of escapist entertainment. While music would become more political later in the Cold War, early rock $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$n\" roll was more influenced by emerging teen culture.\n\n - Civil Rights: The Civil Rights movement in the United States gained momentum during this time and was not influenced by the Cold War in many ways. In some ways, the Cold War may have helped move along the Civil Rights movement. For example, the army was desegregated before the Korean War. Also, the United States wanted to present itself as a united front without much internal strife. However, the Cold War also encouraged assimilation in the United States, something that worked against civil rights as it eliminated the cultures of groups such as African Americans and Indigenous Americans. Regardless, most people fighting for Civil Rights were focused on domestic life in the United States and did not focus as much on foreign affairs.\n\nThe Cold War Impact in Canada:\n\n - The Red Scare did spread to Canada, particularly after the Gouzenko Affair. This event proved that the Soviet Union had established an extensive spy network in Canada. This led to several arrests and widespread fear of communism.\n\n - Similar to the United States, Canada limited civil liberties in an effort to stop any spread of communism.\n\n - Canada grew closer to the United States during this time because of their similar views on stopping communism.\n\nThe Cold War in Latin America:\n\n - Anti-Communism and Social Polarization: impacts of containment leading to accusations and suspicion of countries pushing for progressive reforms (*e.g. Arévalo in Guatemala*). Elites pushed for anti-communism (pro-capitalism), while many workers supported socialist reforms.\n\n - Cultural and Intellectual Changes: US influence toward  democracy/consumerism through educational exchange and cultural diplomacy. Surge of leftist Latin American artists critical of US dominance and imperialism in Latin America.\n\n - Militarization and Social Identity: elevation of military elites and leaders to defend against the spread of Communism. Student movements tended to embrace reformism and anti-imperialism.\n\n - Religion and Conservatism: The Catholic Church became a powerful anti-communist tool in the highly religious region, reinforcing conservatism. The Church's stance later became a seed for social division in Latin America.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe conflict between the United States and the Soviet Union changed many facets of society and culture in the Americas, particularly in the United States and Canada. On the home front, American and Canadian citizens saw a second Red Scare, brought on by the fear of the spread of communism. While some aspects of life would not be affected by the Cold War, the fear inspired by Senator Joseph McCarthy and the House Un-American Activities Committee in the United States, and the Gouzenko Affair in Canada, would lead to a significant change in culture and society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$).\n\nThe relationship between the Soviet Union and the United States was never firm, but deteriorated substantially at the end of World War II. Part of that deterioration came with the news that the United States had developed a nuclear weapon without the knowledge of its allies. Following the war, the Soviet Union began developing its own weapon, leading to an arms race for several decades ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). The knowledge that the Soviet Union was actively trying to develop a weapon of mass destruction led to a culture of fear in the United States ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). This fear permeated many areas of life. Much like fire drills, children were expected to practice what to do in the event of a nuclear strike ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Many movies, books, and cartoons included mentions of the Cold War and played on the fears of the American public. However, people continued to live their lives. The United States experienced a surge in consumerism following the war. People did not stop spending money or buying homes and cars because of the culture of fear, suggesting that not every aspect of life changed. Also, many movies made during this time were westerns and musicals and showed little connection to the Cold War. Historian Alan Brinkley suggests that the Cold War impacted politics substantially, but American values guided society during this time. This could be contradicted by the idea that consumerism, music, and movies of the time might be seen as escapism, where people used them to distract from the fears of the Cold War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$).\n\nThe most significant changes to everyday life in the US happened due to the actions of the House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) and Senator Joseph McCarthy's trials ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). While HUAC existed before World War II, during the Cold War, it shifted to rooting out communism in the United States. The wave of patriotism after World War II and the new Red Scare led to more feverish anti-communist activities within HUAC. This group targeted Americans whom they believed held influence. For example, several Hollywood directors and Broadway actors were investigated for subversive behavior ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Senator McCarthy built on the work of the HUAC. He accused State Department workers of being communist, inspiring Congress to investigate these accusations. Most of the accusations were unfounded, but could still disrupt people’s lives. Many lost their jobs or credibility. While the actions of the HUAC and Senator McCarthy were harsh, the American people were so afraid of communism that they let it happen. Most Americans in the 1950s thought that the United States had to do more to protect the people from communism, especially after the Rosenberg case and the expansion of the Soviet nuclear program ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). However, civil liberties were at risk during this time, and the freedom of speech and the right to due process were challenged directly by McCarthy and HUAC. It may be argued that these cases are extreme and that most people were not affected by the show trials; however, many Americans self-censored their ideas and their work to avoid any accusations of communist sympathy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). Federal employees were forced to sign loyalty oaths, and many people lost their jobs because of past political activity or association with people who may have been sympathetic to communism. Very few of these people were tied to actual acts of espionage ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Revisionist historian Ellen Schrecker believed that the actions taken by the government during this time significantly undermined democracy by silencing any unpopular opinions. However, Orthodox historian Richard Powers thought these steps were necessary in the face of threats and Soviet espionage ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). Regardless of the justification, it is clear that civil liberties were restricted during this time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nCanada's Red Scare began when a Russian cipher clerk defected and revealed a Soviet spy ring in Ottawa. Known as the Gouzenko Affair, this led to arrests, fear, and anti-communist fervor in Canada. Civil liberties were challenged similarly to those in the United States. Many people were imprisoned without a trial: a significant breach of the right of habeas corpus. College professors were silenced, and members of the Communist Party of Canada were monitored and, in some cases, arrested ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$ $\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Because of the similar fears of the two countries, some Canadian historians, like Harold Innis, believed that the United States media attempted to dominate Canadian culture ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). However, it can be argued that the United States and Canada had always maintained some similar cultural aspects and that Canada was committed to preserving national identity through public broadcasting and arts as a defense against the Soviet Union, leftists, and becoming too Americanized ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nThe Cold War profoundly impacted many aspects of life and culture in the Americas. While the Cold War may not have obviously changed some forms of entertainment and consumer culture, the anxieties of the Cold War permeated every aspect of life. Ultimately, the Cold War’s domestic impact lay less in transforming every aspect of culture than in embedding a sense of vigilance and conformity into everyday life that allowed for extreme changes in the government and the workplace ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1005","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1005\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Cold War significantly influenced the policies of countries in the Americas.\" With reference to **two** countries (excluding the US), to what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nGeneral Guidance:\n- This question asks students to assess the extent to which the Cold War influenced the internal and foreign policies of two countries in the region.\n- To evaluate the Cold War's influence on the internal and foreign policies of two countries, students can discuss both Cold War-related changes and those caused by other factors, or they can concentrate their analysis on just one of these elements.\n    - It is essential to note that many changes are driven by Cold War dynamics themselves. Still, others are rooted in internal situations beyond how foreign powers like the US perceive them, and it is crucial to emphasize this.\n- Each student should clearly state their opinions or conclusions and support them with enough evidence.\n\nPossible content and examples:\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Cuba}}$: alignment with the USSR resulted in the adoption of Marxist-Leninist policies (not during, but after the Cuban Revolution), including nationalization of industries, collectivization of agriculture, and the restructuring of the economy around Soviet aid and trade. Foreign policy was also influenced as Cuba supported revolutionary movements across Latin America and Africa.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Chile}}$: Allende's socialist reforms, including land redistribution and nationalization, came before Soviet involvement and reflected its own internal political processes. However, these reforms increased hostility from the US, eventually leading it to back Allende's overthrow and install General Augusto Pinochet via military coup. Under Pinochet, Chile moved away from Allende's policies and toward neo-liberal, anti-communist policies to align with the US.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Argentina and Brazil}}$: In both countries, military regimes claimed legitimacy by framing themselves as a bulwark against the threat of communism (in alignment with the US and its fears of a $\\lq$domino effect' of communism). US support, including initiatives like Operation Condor, helped coordinate the repression of groups it considered socialist, communist, or generally leftist. Policies of censorship and domestic terror were justified using anti-communist Cold War rhetoric regardless of actual motivation.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Guatemala}}$: The US backed the overthrow of democratically elected President Jacobo Arbenz after his portrayal as a communist threatened US business interests, leading to decades of authoritarian rule and civil war justified as anti-communist.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Nicaragua}}$: The Somoza dictatorship was overthrown in 1979 by the Sandinistas, who implemented socialist-inspired reforms in alignment with Cuba and the USSR. In response, the US funded the Contra insurgency, which pushed its Sandinista policymaking toward wartime defense, limiting the progress of reforms.\n- Examples of countries whose policies were generally less influenced by the Cold War (non-examples based on the phrasing of the quotation):\n    - Costa Rica, Mexico, Uruguay, Canada (significantly influenced its foreign policy, but less influence domestically).\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Cold War significantly influenced Latin America's development, shaping its internal and external affairs with the competition between the US and the USSR. Cuba and Chile are key examples of this situation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In both cases, the Cold War was the dominant factor influencing policy, but its influence interacted with domestic realities in varying ways and to different extents in each country ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nThe Cuban Revolution's victory in 1959 began a new political era as it dismantled the old political and economic systems. US pressure pushed Cuba toward socialist radicalization and a one-party regime under the Communist Party, inspired by alignment with the USSR ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Cuba nationalized several major industries and sectors, including land and banks. Furthermore, many companies, most of them owned by US investors, like sugar plantations and the Cuban Telephone Company ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$), were also nationalized. Nationalization threatened US interests, leading to increasing pressure from the US, to which the fledgling revolutionary government responded with policy changes. In response to the threat of US-backed counterrevolution, the state established a surveillance system, motivated by the need to defend against “imperialism” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Soviet alignment implied not just economic and military aid, but also centralized planning, collectivization, and nationalization of education, culture, and media ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), such as the newspaper Prensa Libre ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$), which allowed Cuba to spread revolutionary ideas within Cuba and beyond as a part of state policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn foreign policy, the Cold War made Cuba the focus of hemispheric confrontation. This was built upon the domestic policy's focus on national security and revolutionary internationalism. For example, the CIA-backed attack on the Bay of Pigs was paramount to the consolidation of Cuba's security stance in the region. Although the attack was unsuccessful, it was critical in formalizing an alliance with the USSR. Although some historians such as Kronbluh and Petras suggest that the interplay of Cuban domestic and foreign policy actions was heavily influenced by the wider Cold War tensions and the Soviet Union's interests, Cuban intellectuals stress that the national revolutionary move was the main driving force of the policies.\n\nFor instance, the Missile Crisis of 1962 and Castro's aims of spreading revolutionary processes within Latin America and other regions allowed Cuba to become a key international player. For example, its growing participation in the Non-Aligned Movement, which it had joined in 1961, and its promotion of armed insurgency in Nicaragua and Angola ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$) further positioned Cuba to engage in geopolitics independently and reflected the convergence of foreign policy and internal revolutionary ambitions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). However, the extent of these foreign policy actions was limited as Cuba received protection and aid from the Soviet Union, including sugar subsidies and preferential oil ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Ultimately, this dependence came at the expense of sacrificing national autonomy and accepting subordination to the Eastern Bloc's agenda ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nWith reference to Chile, the election of Salvador Allende (1970) brought about the implementation of structural reforms within a legal and pluralistic framework ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). In acknowledging the significance of international anti-imperialist movements, Allende's government diversified its alliances by establishing diplomatic and commercial relations with socialist countries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), including China and nations from the Non-Aligned Movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Although Allende himself was not fond of communism, in the eyes of both national opposition groups and the USA, this was evidence of alignment with the Eastern bloc.\n\nInternational pressure and domestic polarization regarding Allende's idea of “peaceful path to socialism” were intensified by the US fear of the “domino effect” in Latin America. Socialism, even when peaceful and elected democratically, put Chile in a precarious position in the Cold War context. In the short term, this resulted in the US funding of Allende's opposition in different anti-regime actions such as economic sabotage and targeted media campaigns supported by the CIA's control of El Mercurio ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).  International boycotts exacerbated economic problems, heightened social fears, and legitimized the growing militarization of politics with the radicalization of discourses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Although some historians argue that the Cold War significantly influenced Chile's political upheaval, others highlight that internal struggles among political groups were just as influential as external intervention. Nonetheless, these perspectives reveal how both foreign and domestic forces drove Chile's transformation during this era ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspective}$).\n\nMoreover, the successful 1973 military coup, which overthrew Allende, helped establish a dictatorship led by Pinochet. This regime, aligned with the doctrine of national security and anti-communist efforts, was heavily influenced by the Cold War tensions and US interests. For instance, policies of systematic repression, elimination of political parties, and introduction of neoliberal reforms were supported by the USA ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Chile's domestic policy shifted thus from pluralist inclusion to authoritarian control, influenced by Cold War dynamics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Additionally, Chile rejoined the inter-American system, cut ties with Cuba and the USSR, and became an ally of Washington in the continental “dirty war” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Within Operation Condor, Pinochet collaborated with other South American dictatorships and US agencies to suppress leftist movements. This international alliance strengthened the legitimacy of the new regime, securing financial and political support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn conclusion, the Cold War significantly influenced both Cuba and Chile's domestic and foreign policies. In Cuba, radicalization, statism with major social reforms, and dependence on the USSR emerged alongside US intervention, polarization, and repressive militarization in Chile. Both nations experienced a decrease in national autonomy, but through different routes: Cuba aligned itself with the socialist bloc, while Chile, following the coup, joined the capitalist bloc. In Cuba, the revolution absorbed external pressure and turned it into unity and international revolutionary outreach; in Chile, institutional weakness and foreign intervention led to democratic collapse and dictatorship. In both cases, foreign policy reflected domestic policy and vice versa. The experiences of Cuba and Chile demonstrate how the Cold War not only set opposing models against each other but also reshaped societies, redefined the boundaries of sovereignty, and left lasting impacts on how nations govern themselves ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1021","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1021\n\nTo what extent did the US achieve its aims in the Korean War?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**Three** aims can be analyzed to answer this question.\n- Containment: to what extent did the Korean War stop the spread of Communism?\n- Domestic Political Aims: To what extent did the Korean War improve Truman's domestic approval?\n    - By 1950 his administration was accused of having “lost China” to the Chinese Communist Party and Senator McCarthy had accused the State Department of being infiltrated by Communists. Truman needed to clear his reputation in light of the upcoming elections.\n- Help the United Nations in its peacekeeping role: To what extent did the US help or hurt the UN's mission in Korea?\n    - The United Nations was created in 1945, and the Truman administration wanted to avoid the fate of its predecessor, the League of Nations. To do that, it had to prove that the principle of collective security would work this time.\n\nConsiderations:\n- A “to what extent” question requires an analysis of the degree of, in this case, achievement.\n- A clear answer must first identify the aims. The categories provided are general and can be categorized into more specific aims. A good answer does not need to include all the aims.\n\n**Indicative content (not prescriptive):**\nContainment\n- Success:\n    - South Korea was “saved” from Communism\n    - Canadian Historian David Bercuson explains that Korea was the first effort by a communist state to take over a non-communist neighbor. The fact that this was prevented makes it the first victory of the West in the Cold War.\n    - Japan remained safe and a staunch US ally in the region.\n    - U.S. intervention encouraged the creation of new alliances such as the Southeast Asian Treaty Organization (1954) to contain communism in Asia.\n- Failure:\n    - A peace treaty was never signed. Therefore, the US had to protect the fledgling Republic of South Korea by stationing thousands of troops along the 38th parallel.\n    - After the success at Inchon in 1950, the aim was no longer to defend South Korea but to unite the peninsula; this was not achieved.\n    - US historian John Gaddis sees the war as a precedent for the Vietnam War: the superpowers would get involved in bloody proxy wars.\n    - The war strained relations with the USSR and with China even further\n\nDomestic Political Aims\n- Success:\n    - Early polls showed ¾ of Americans supported assistance to South Korea\n    - Truman was able to show a tough stance on Communism after being accused of having “lost China” (McCarthyism was in full swing)\n- Failure:\n    - In the congressional elections of 1950, the Democrats, Truman`s party, lost their majority in Congress.\n    - Since Truman had not obtained a declaration of war from Congress, he was the only one to blame for the war\n    - The war cost the US $67 billion, which led to inflation. In January 1951, the government introduced a price freeze to combat it.\n    - The war's unpopularity made Truman realize he could not be a candidate in the 1952 election, which a Republican won for the first time after 20 years.\n\nHelp the United Nations in its peacekeeping role\n- Success:\n    - The UN asked Truman to appoint a commander for the United Nations Command. The US contributed 9/10 of the US troops to the fight.\n    - It was the first time one country invaded another, and the UN was able to avoid repeating the mistake of appeasement and implement collective security. 15 nations committed troops to the struggle.\n- Failure:\n    - General MacArthur was named commander of the United Nations Command. Truman insisted he communicate only with Washington and not with the UN authorities, which undermined the UN's authority.\n    - Many UN members felt that crossing the 38th parallel after Inchon was an act of aggression against North Korea and a violation of the UN's peacekeeping mission.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Korean War (1950-1953) was the first proxy war of the Cold War. Initially, it was a purely Korean confrontation between the North and the South, with both seeking to reunify the peninsula under their respective governments. North Korean aggression in 1950 motivated the participation of the UN to defend the Republic of Korea. Even though the war was officially between the two Koreas and the United Nations, with the addition of China at the end of 1950, the United States played a significant role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). President Truman decided to assist the UN in taking a stand in favor of collective security and to present a strong position against the spread of communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). The US was also bound to the words of the 1947 Truman Doctrine, in which it pledged to \"support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or by outside pressures\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The situation in Korea was a clear test of the US's containment pòlicy as established in this Doctrine ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). This essay will analyze the extent to which these two aims were achieved ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nUS participation in the Korean War strengthened the fledgling United Nations' ability to react while at the same time undermining it ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Created after the failure of the League of Nations in part due to its inaction in the face of aggression, the invasion of South Korea was the first test of the UN's peacekeeping capabilities. Truman was determined to uphold the structure of collective security, and for that he knew the US had to participate and not repeat the policies of isolationism and appeasement that had partly led to the Second World War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Thus, when the UN asked Truman to appoint a commander for the United Nations Command, General MacArthur was named to lead the UN forces. The US also contributed 9 out of every 10 troops to the UN forces and considerable resources. These contributions made the war both possible and partly successful. However, the preponderance of US participation and the limited role of the other 15 nations that contributed troops to the UN forces also undermined the authority of the United Nations. Furthermore, Truman ordered MacArthur to communicate only with Washington and not with the UN authorities, further undermining the concept of a joint effort ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This situation led to the UN effort being perceived, instead, as a US effort, with the US determining the goals of the struggle. While to most UN members it was clearly necessary to defend the invaded South Korea, the US incursion into North Korea and the advance to the Chinese border were explicit acts of aggression that were contrary to the spirit of the United Nations. Therefore, even though US resources and men allowed the success of the UN mission, their domination ended up sidelining the United Nations, thus eroding its authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nOn the other hand, the Truman administration achieved its objective of containing communism by participating in the Korean War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). First of all, it prevented the spread of communism to yet another country in Asia by pushing back the North Korean invasion and securing the Republic of Korea as an American ally to this day. This can also be said for Japan, which would have had its development seriously thwarted if both the whole Korean peninsula and China were communist ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). As Canadian historian Bercuson states, “saving” South Korea from communism was the first true Western victory in the Cold War, since the expansion of a communist state over a non-communist neighbor had been successfully blocked. This showed communist leaders in the USSR and China that the US would not allow communist aggression to go unchecked. However, in this assessment, Bercuson ignores the importance of the Berlin Airlift of 1948-1949. In this instance, the Western allies also successfully resisted what can be interpreted as a Soviet incursion into a capitalist country as the USSR was forced to end its blockade of West Berlin ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). Moreover, the success was limited. A formal peace treaty was never signed, forcing the US to maintain tens of thousands of troops along the 38th parallel since South Korea was and is still technically at war with the North ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Furthermore, after the successful Inchon landing in 1950, the US aims expanded from simply defending South Korea to reunifying the peninsula under non-Communist rule, which would have meant a successful offensive move against the spread of communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).  This goal had to be abandoned after China entered the conflict. Moreover, John Gaddis observes that the war set a dangerous precedent for future conflicts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). After Korea, the US would become entangled in bloody, inconclusive proxy wars instead of thinking of diplomatic solutions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Consequently, the US did achieve its immediate containment objectives in the Korean War, but at the same time, it came to see the use of force as a valid way to stop communism.\n\nIn conclusion, the United States mostly achieved the aims it set for itself in the Korean War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).  Containment was upheld in South Korea, and the UN demonstrated its ability to respond to aggression. However, these achievements were limited by the failure to reunify Korea, the burden of an indefinite US military commitment in the region, and the dangerous precedent of proxy wars ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Ultimately, the Korean War would determine the aims the US set for itself in future conflicts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1022","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1022\n\nCompare and contrast the foreign policies of Presidents Kennedy and Johnson.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organisation) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**General Guidance:**\n- The question requires students to provide an account of the similarities and differences in the foreign policies of Kennedy and Johnson.\n    - When using a comparative approach, it is advisable to organize the content in a specific order: first, the similarities, then the differences. Additionally, for each case, identify topics such as similarities in views of communism or perceptions of the “domino theory,” and then discuss differences related to Cuba, intervention in Southeast Asia, and so on.\n- There does not need to be a balance of similarity and difference, but both must be features of the response.\n- Students' opinions or conclusions will be presented clearly and supported by appropriate evidence.\n\n**Possible content and examples:**\n\n**Similarities**\n- Both presidents reaffirmed their commitment to stopping communism in Southeast Asia, especially in Vietnam.\n- The intervention strategy was justified by the “domino effect” doctrine, which argued that the fall of Vietnam would lead to communism spreading across the region.\n- Kennedy and Johnson supported the South Vietnamese government with military and economic aid, including assistance, advisors, and equipment.\n- Both administrations viewed the Vietnam War as a key part of the global effort to oppose Soviet and Chinese influence during the Cold War.\n- Both presidents focused on stopping communism worldwide, not just in Asia, but also in regions such as Latin America and Africa.\n- Each administration emphasized nuclear deterrence and bolstered arms control efforts, including dialogue with the Soviet Union (e.g., the signing of the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty under Kennedy, which Johnson continued to uphold).\n- Both engaged in crisis management during Cold War standoffs—Kennedy with the Cuban Missile Crisis and Johnson with the Dominican Republic intervention—showing a willingness to use force or negotiate to protect U.S. interests in the Americas.\n\n**Differences**\n- Kennedy initially focused on limited intervention by sending military advisors and technical assistance, avoiding, as much as possible, the large-scale deployment of combat troops.\n- During Johnson's presidency, the conflict significantly escalated, including deploying large numbers of U.S. troops and increasing bombings over North Vietnam (Operation Rolling Thunder).\n- Kennedy was more cautious about direct intervention, aiming to avoid getting involved in a prolonged war, while Johnson, after the Gulf of Tonkin incident, obtained Congressional approval to escalate military involvement without a formal declaration of war.\n- Johnson faced growing domestic and international opposition as the conflict escalated, which in turn influenced his foreign policy and the subsequent peace negotiations. In contrast, Kennedy did not encounter the same level of social unrest over the war.\n- Kennedy's administration dealt with the Bay of Pigs invasion and the Cuban Missile Crisis, focusing on isolating and pressuring Cuba, while Johnson paid less attention to Cuba, prioritizing Southeast Asia.\n- Kennedy launched the Alliance for Progress to encourage economic growth and political reform in Latin America; Johnson's approach was less ambitious, relying more on military intervention when needed.\n- Kennedy's policies focused on high-stakes brinkmanship and efforts to establish direct communication with the Soviets, such as creating the Washington-Moscow \"hotline\" after the Missile Crisis. Johnson's administration paid more attention to arms control, including setting the stage for Strategic Arms Limitation Talks (SALT).\n- Kennedy took a balanced approach toward Israel and the Arab nations, while Johnson shifted toward stronger support for Israel, especially after the Six-Day War in 1967.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nDuring the turbulent years of the early Cold War, the foreign policies of Presidents Kennedy (1961-1963) and Johnson (1963-1969) reflected both continuity and change in America's approach to global affairs. Both leaders were committed to containing communism and protecting U.S. interests, especially in Southeast Asia and the Western Hemisphere, but their methods varied significantly ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While Kennedy and Johnson shared the goal of stopping the spread of communism, Kennedy's foreign policy was characterized by cautious, limited intervention and diplomatic engagement. In contrast, Johnson's presidency saw a dramatic escalation of military involvement, especially in Vietnam, and a greater reliance on force, highlighting key differences in the balance between diplomacy and military action in their administrations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nBoth Kennedy and Johnson pursued a policy in Vietnam and Southeast Asia centered on containing communism through a combination of military and economic support for anti-communist governments ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Their administration's involvement in the region was based on the “domino effect” doctrine. To address this perceived threat, both presidents adopted a strategy of supporting and reinforcing the South Vietnamese regime, not only with military hardware and personnel but also by providing economic aid, in hopes of strengthening its stability and legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The threat of regional instability and the potential for a domino effect toward communism played a decisive role in shaping U.S. foreign policy throughout the 1960s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nBeyond Vietnam, Kennedy and Johnson consistently pursued a global strategy of containing communism, using both diplomatic and military measures to safeguard U.S. interests wherever threats emerged ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Their administrations adopted a global containment strategy, aiming to stop the spread of communism wherever it posed a threat, whether in Latin America, Africa, or elsewhere ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Moreover, Kennedy, who was consistently cautious in his approach to nuclear escalation, handled the Cuban Missile Crisis ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$), ultimately securing a peaceful resolution while showing military strength. Johnson, on the other hand, used similar crisis management strategies during the intervention in the Dominican Republic ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$), justifying U.S. army involvement as necessary to prevent a communist takeover in the Western Hemisphere ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Both leaders, therefore, demonstrated a willingness to use force or negotiation as needed, always aiming to protect American interests and stop the spread of communism in the Americas ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nAlthough both Kennedy and Johnson were committed to containing communism, their contrasting strategies in Vietnam reveal key differences in how they balanced military action and diplomatic engagement during the Cold War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Kennedy's approach to Vietnam emphasized caution and limited intervention. He focused on dispatching military advisors and offering technical assistance to the South Vietnamese government, deliberately avoiding the commitment of large-scale U.S. combat forces ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Kennedy's administration aimed to contain communism through strategic support and capacity-building, wary of the dangers of deep military engagement. His goal was to strengthen South Vietnam without dragging the United States into a prolonged, costly war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In contrast, Johnson's presidency marked a significant escalation of the conflict. After the Gulf of Tonkin incident in 1964, Johnson gained Congressional approval, which authorized expanding U.S. involvement without a formal declaration of war. So, the United States sent hundreds of thousands of combat troops and carried out continuous bombing campaigns over North Vietnam—most notably Operation Rolling Thunder ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$). This change reflected Johnson's belief that a much stronger response was necessary to prevent South Vietnam from collapsing and, by extension, to uphold the “domino theory” of communist containment. It was also partially motivated by his desire to end the conflict quickly and focus more on his domestic “Great Society” reforms ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Kennedy remained more restrained, preferring to exert influence through advisory missions and economic aid rather than direct military intervention. He was careful about the risks of escalation and aimed to limit American exposure in Vietnam. Johnson, however, showed a greater willingness to use overwhelming force, believing that military superiority could break the will of North Vietnam and its allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Regarding Johnson, historian Arthur Schlesinger sees pragmatic adaptation but misses Kennedy's vision. In contrast, C. Dale Walton is more critical, emphasizing increased military interventions and neglected reforms. Together, they depict a pragmatic yet controversial legacy, defined by shifting approaches to containment and global transformation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nIn addressing Cold War challenges in the Western Hemisphere, Kennedy and Johnson adopted distinctly different approaches to Cuba and Latin America, reflecting their broader philosophies on containing communism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Kennedy's presidency focused heavily on Cuba and hemispheric issues ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). He dealt with the implementation and failure of the Bay of Pigs invasion and successfully handled the Cuban Missile Crisis, creating direct channels like the Washington-Moscow hotline to avoid nuclear conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Kennedy also launched the Alliance for Progress, a broad initiative aimed at promoting economic growth and political change in Latin America as a means of combating communist influence peacefully. Johnson, however, paid less attention to Cuba, shifting U.S. foreign policy focus toward Southeast Asia and the escalating Vietnam conflict. In Latin America, Johnson's strategy was less ambitious and relied more on military interventions, when necessary ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$), such as in the Dominican Republic ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example}$), rather than on comprehensive development initiatives. Schlesinger and Walton's contrasting views highlight the complexity of Kennedy and Johnson's foreign policies during the Cold War. Schlesinger favors Kennedy's balanced, development-oriented diplomacy, while Walton criticizes its inconsistency and overlooked inequalities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nIn summary, Kennedy and Johnson's Cold War policies were characterized by a strong commitment to containing communism, the practical use of the “domino theory” in Southeast Asia, extensive military and economic support for anti-communist governments, and a willingness to handle crises through a combination of negotiation and, when necessary, military intervention. Kennedy preferred limited engagement and economic efforts, staying cautious of deep military involvement. Johnson, on the other hand, was ready to escalate militarily, dedicating significant American resources to Vietnam and taking a more interventionist approach in different parts of the hemisphere. These differences highlight distinct philosophies regarding the balance between military force and diplomatic and economic solutions for containing communism during the Cold War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1181","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1181\n\nAccording to Source A, what was the main purpose of the United States' involvement in Southeast Asia?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from a transcript of an interview of US Secretary of State Dean Rusk by NBC correspondent Elie Abel, broadcast over nationwide television on August 5, 1964. |\n\n[Source: Office of the Secretary of Defense Vietnam Task Force. (1964, August 24). *Justification of the War, Public Statements, Volume II: D-The Johnson Administration.* United States - Vietnam Relations 1945-1967. Pentagon Papers Part V, Vol II, D. Retrieved October 26, 2025 from https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/research/pentagon-papers/Pentagon-Papers-Part-V-A-Vol-IID.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To promote and preserve a peaceful international order\n- To defend nations in Southeast Asia from external aggression (“pressures from the North”)\n- To uphold international law and protect U.S. rights\n- To demonstrate U.S. credibility and deter further communist aggression\n- To ensure stability and self-determination for Southeast Asian nations\n- To prevent the escalation of communist influence (Domino Theory context)\n- To align with postwar U.S. moral identity.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Rusk, the primary purpose of U.S. involvement in Southeast Asia was to promote a peaceful international order upholding the post-World War II goal of building “a decent world community” where nations could coexist without threatening their neighbours ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHe argued that American intervention aimed to defend Southeast Asian countries from external aggression, particularly communist pressure and interference from North Vietnam ($\\checkmark$).\n\nRusk also justified U.S. military action as a matter of upholding international law and protecting American rights, explaining that attacks on U.S. ships in the Gulf of Tonkin occurred in international waters where the United States had every right to operate ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1182","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1182\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying U.S. foreign policy during the Cold War.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from a transcript of an interview of US Secretary of State Dean Rusk by NBC correspondent Elie Abel, broadcast over nationwide television on August 5, 1964. |\n\n[Source: Office of the Secretary of Defense Vietnam Task Force. (1964, August 24). *Justification of the War, Public Statements, Volume II: D-The Johnson Administration.* United States - Vietnam Relations 1945-1967. Pentagon Papers Part V, Vol II, D. Retrieved October 26, 2025 from https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/research/pentagon-papers/Pentagon-Papers-Part-V-A-Vol-IID.pdf. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of two marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{\\textrm{Value}}}$:\n\nOrigin\n- Senior official directly involved in Cold War decision-making; reflects the official mindset and rationale of U.S. leadership. Rusk's long tenure (Kennedy and Johnson administrations) offers continuity across the broader Cold War containment strategy.\n- Contemporary source that provides immediate insight into policy justification at a critical turning point (the Gulf of Tonkin incident/crisis).\n- Public broadcast makes it useful for studying how the government communicated policy to domestic and international audiences.\n\nPurpose\n- Intended to reassure the public and allies that U.S. actions were defensive and morally justified.\n- Aims to defend the credibility of U.S. commitments in Southeast Asia and to deter perceived communist aggression.\n\nContent\n- Explicitly links Vietnam to a global U.S. mission of building “a decent world community” illustrating idealistic self-perception in U.S. foreign policy.\n- Frames the problem as “pressures from the North,” showing the Cold War binary worldview (aggressor vs defender).\n- Mentions the need for immediate military action to prevent being seen as a “paper tiger,” providing evidence of credibility concerns central to U.S. containment thinking.\n\n$\\textbf{{\\underline{\\textrm{Limitations}}}}$:\n\nOrigin\n- Produced by a highly positioned government official likely reflecting official policy line rather than personal doubt or internal debate.\n- Occurred in a media setting, not a confidential document, limiting candour and depth.\n\nPurpose\n- Designed to justify and defend policy decisions, not to evaluate them critically.\n- Propagandistic tone: aims to shape public opinion and win support for potential escalation.\n\nContent\n- Presents a simplified narrative: North Vietnam as sole aggressor, South Vietnam as passive victim, reflecting ideological bias.\n- Fails to acknowledge U.S. involvement in South Vietnamese politics or covert operations.\n- Offers no discussion of alternative diplomatic options or internal disagreements within the administration.\n- Lacks reference to wider global Cold War complexities (Sino-Soviet split, French colonial legacy).\n- Statements are predictably defensive and moralistic, offering limited empirical detail about actual policy processes.\n- Omits controversial details such as the ambiguity over the Gulf of Tonkin attacks, thus limiting its factual reliability.\n- Purposeful rhetoric of morality (“decent world community”) may mask strategic motives, such as containment and prestige.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin is of value as it comes from a senior U.S. official directly involved in Cold War decision-making. As Secretary of State, Rusk's perspective reflects the official mindset and strategic rationale of American leadership during this period ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).   The source is valuable in its purpose because it was intended to justify U.S. actions in Southeast Asia and explain foreign policy aims to the public, providing insight into how the government sought to frame its involvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nHowever, the origin also limits its value, as it represents the viewpoint of those responsible for shaping and defending policy, rather than offering independent or critical reflection ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).  Furthermore, the content presents a simplified, one-sided account that identifies North Vietnam as the sole aggressor and ignores U.S. interference in South Vietnamese politics, revealing strong ideological bias ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1183","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1183\n\nUsing Source B and your own knowledge, identify **two** criticisms Kerry makes of U.S. Cold War policy and conduct, and discuss how these reflect wider disillusionment with American leadership in the Cold War.\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  John F. Kerry. Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations, 22 April 1971. |\n\n[Source: Kerry, J. F. (1971, April 22). *Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations.* Retrieved October 25, 2025 from: https<nonlink>://voicesofdemocracy.umd.edu/kerry-statement-before-the-senate-committee-speech-text/.Public domain.]\n:::\\","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following criticisms with a discussion of how they reflect wider disillusionment and a conclusion:\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{\\textrm{Criticisms of U.S. Cold War Policy (from the source)}} }$:\n\nThe U.S. betrayed its moral ideals and used soldiers as instruments of policy\n- Kerry says veterans felt “used in the worst fashion by the administration of this country.”\n- Suggests policymakers sacrificed American lives for political or ideological ends rather than defence of genuine national interest.\n- Implies moral hypocrisy with U.S. leaders claiming to fight for freedom while disregarding the human cost.\n- Critiques the dehumanising nature of Cold War realpolitik: individuals subordinated to the abstract goal of “containing communism.”\n\nU.S. misunderstood and misapplied its Cold War containment policy\n- Kerry rejects the framing of Vietnam as communist aggression, describing it instead as “a civil war… an effort by a people who had for years been seeking their liberation from any colonial influence...”\n- Exposes the flawed Cold War assumption that all local conflicts were Soviet- or Chinese-backed.\n- Accuses the U.S. of ideological blindness, imposing the East-West binary on a nationalist struggle.\n- Reflects disillusionment with Cold War thinking that saw every regional dispute through the lens of global ideological competition.\n\nU.S. conduct in Vietnam exposed the moral decay of its Cold War leadership\n- Refers to the contradiction of “destroying villages in order to save them” and the acceptance of “a My Lai.”\n- Condemns the ethical bankruptcy of using violence to defend liberty.\n- Undermines America's claim to moral superiority over the communist bloc.\n- Shows that Cold War rhetoric about protecting freedom masked imperialist or militaristic behaviour.\n- For historians, this illustrates the collapse of the U.S. moral narrative that had underpinned Cold War legitimacy since 1945.\n\nOfficial Cold War rhetoric of freedom and democracy was hypocritical\n- Kerry calls it “the height of criminal hypocrisy” to justify American deaths as sacrifices for freedom.\n- Argues that U.S. actions contradicted its stated Cold War values, promoting democracy abroad while suppressing it through force.\n- Reflects broader 1970s criticism that American global leadership had lost credibility after Vietnam, coups, and covert interventions.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Reflections of wider disillusionment with U.S. Cold War leadership (own knowledge)}}$:\n\nDecline of faith in U.S. moral exceptionalism\n- Many Americans no longer believed the U.S. was a benevolent global power.\n- Kerry's speech echoed wider sentiment after My Lai (1968) and the Pentagon Papers (1971) revealed lies and atrocities.\n- Illustrated how the Vietnam experience shattered Cold War confidence in America as the defender of democracy.\n\nThe emergence of domestic opposition and moral reckoning\n- The testimony was part of the Vietnam Veterans Against the War movement, signalling disillusionment among those who had fought for the Cold War cause.\n- Veterans' opposition carried symbolic power showing that disillusionment had reached the very agents of U.S. foreign policy.\n- Reflected a generational rejection of Cold War militarism and blind patriotism.\n\nCrisis of credibility in Cold War diplomacy\n- The Vietnam failure eroded trust in government as citizens realised leaders had exaggerated threats and manipulated information.\n- The “credibility gap” widened, undermining the idea that the U.S. was a reliable or transparent global leader.\n- Contributed to congressional restrictions such as the War Powers Resolution (1973), showing an institutional response to Cold War overreach.\n\nShift toward restraint and re-evaluation of global leadership\n- The moral and strategic failure in Vietnam encouraged later moves toward détente and limited engagement (Nixon and Carter years).\n- Kerry's criticisms reflected a broader national fatigue with Cold War confrontation and interventionism.\n- His language foreshadowed post-Vietnam foreign policy debates about human rights and realism vs idealism.\n\nLoss of U.S. global prestige and ideological dominance\n- Vietnam damaged America's image as the leader of the “free world.”\n- The anti-war movement and revelations of misconduct weakened U.S. influence among allies and the developing world.\n- The speech represents the symbolic unravelling of Cold War consensus, as both domestic and international audiences questioned U.S. motives and integrity\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, Kerry argues that the U.S. betrayed its moral ideals, using soldiers as instruments of policy rather than defenders of freedom. Veterans, he claims, felt “used in the worst fashion,” as lives were sacrificed for political goals instead of genuine national security ($\\checkmark$). This exposed the moral hypocrisy of a superpower that preached liberty while committing atrocities such as My Lai and using chemical weapons. Such views echoed the public's loss of faith in U.S. moral exceptionalism and growing public anger at government deception, as revealed by the Pentagon Papers ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecond, Kerry contends that the U.S. misunderstood the Vietnam conflict, wrongly framing it as communist aggression rather than a nationalist civil war ($\\checkmark$). This criticism reflected wider disillusionment with Cold War thinking, highlighting an emerging belief that American leaders had imposed broad political assumptions on local struggles, eroding global trust and domestic confidence in U.S. leadership ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOverall, Kerry's criticisms reveal a deep moral and ideological crisis at the heart of U.S. Cold War leadership. By exposing the gap between America's democratic ideals and its actions in Vietnam, he echoed a broader public belief that the Cold War had become hypocritical and dehumanising, eroding the very values it claimed to defend. His speech reflects how, by the early 1970s, many Americans, especially veterans, had lost faith in leaders who justified war in the name of freedom, showing how disillusionment replaced confidence in U.S. global moral authority ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1184","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1184\n\nUsing Sources A, B, and your own knowledge, evaluate the impact of the Vietnam War on the United States' Cold War policy.\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from a transcript of an interview of US Secretary of State Dean Rusk by NBC correspondent Elie Abel, broadcast over nationwide television on August 5, 1964. |\n\n[Source: Office of the Secretary of Defense Vietnam Task Force. (1964, August 24). *Justification of the War, Public Statements, Volume II: D-The Johnson Administration.* United States - Vietnam Relations 1945-1967. Pentagon Papers Part V, Vol II, D. Retrieved October 26, 2025 from https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/research/pentagon-papers/Pentagon-Papers-Part-V-A-Vol-IID.pdf. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  John F. Kerry. Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations, 22 April 1971. |\n\n[Source: Kerry, J. F. (1971, April 22). *Statement before the U.S. Senate Committee on Foreign Relations.* Retrieved October 25, 2025 from: https<nonlink>://voicesofdemocracy.umd.edu/kerry-statement-before-the-senate-committee-speech-text/.Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors |Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|The response is focused on the question| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$) |\n|The response uses the provided sources effectively as evidence and refers to them clearly| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{sources}$) |\n|The response uses accurate and relevant “own knowledge” as evidence to support arguments alongside source knowledge| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{O-K}$)\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{The strongest response will}}$:\n- Integrate both sources (Rusk = official justification; Kerry = moral/political backlash).\n- Demonstrate how Vietnam transformed U.S. Cold War thinking from confident global leadership to strategic restraint.\n- Weigh continuity (containment still central) against change (shift to détente and domestic constraints).\n- Address domestic as well as international impacts (credibility, public opinion, legislative changes, alliances).\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{From the Sources}}$:\n\nSource A: Rusk, 1964 (Official justifications during the War)\n- Rusk presents the U.S. as acting defensively, protecting “peaceful nations” from “pressures from the north.”\n- Reflects the moral and ideological framing of U.S. policy: preserving freedom and order.\n- Immediate justification for the Gulf of Tonkin strikes as deterrence against communist aggression.\n- Emphasis on credibility and avoiding the perception of the U.S. as a “paper tiger.”\n- Broader continuation of Cold War orthodoxy: containment, deterrence, and credibility maintenance.\n\nSource B: Kerry, 1971 (Critical reassessment)\n- Kerry's testimony marks a turning point in public discourse, challenging the justification of intervention.\n- Calls war “the biggest nothing in history”, highlighting futility and moral decline (“destroying villages to save them”).\n- Represents the post-1968 anti-war sentiment and loss of trust in U.S. foreign policy.\n- Rejects the Cold War logic that Southeast Asia mattered strategically to U.S. security.\n- Reflects shift from Cold War idealism to realism and the demand for policy restraint.\n\n$\\textbf{\\underline{Own knowledge}}$:\n\nPre-Vietnam Cold War Policy context\n- Post-WWII U.S. policy rooted in containment (Truman Doctrine, 1947).\nSuccessive administrations viewed conflicts as part of a global struggle against communism.\nThe Domino Theory shaped belief that loss of Vietnam would endanger all of Southeast Asia.\nU.S. credibility became a key Cold War tool after setbacks in China (1949) and Korea (1950-53).\n\nImpact during the war: Policy continuity and escalation\n- Under Kennedy and Johnson, Vietnam became the test case for containment in Asia.\n- Massive escalation (1964-68): U.S. troop deployment peaked at over 500,000.\n- Demonstrated shift toward militarised containment and the use of limited war strategy.\n- Reinforced belief in U.S. global responsibility to resist communism (“credibility gap” if lost).\n- The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution (1964) effectively handed the president open-ended war powers.\n\nErosion of credibility and public confidence\n- As the war dragged on, domestic opposition undermined political consensus on Cold War aims.\n- Growing media coverage (e.g., Tet Offensive, My Lai Massacre) eroded moral legitimacy.\n- The concept of the “credibility gap” emerged between government statements and battlefield realities.\n- Disillusionment led to questioning the moral and strategic basis of U.S. global leadership.\n\nPolicy consequences and shifts post-Vietnam\n- “Vietnamization” (1969-73): Nixon's policy to withdraw U.S. troops while strengthening South Vietnamese forces.\n- Nixon Doctrine (1969): Reinterpreted containment. Allies expected to defend themselves with U.S. aid, not troops.\n- Détente: A pragmatic shift toward negotiation (SALT I, improved relations with China) rather than direct military confrontation.\n- Emergence of the “Vietnam Syndrome”, a public aversion to intervention abroad without clear objectives or exit strategy.\n- Congressional limits on executive power:\n    -  War Powers Act (1973) restricted presidents' ability to commit U.S. forces without congressional approval.\n- Broader decline in moral authority and global prestige of the United States.\n\nLong-Term Implications for U.S. Cold War Policy\n- U.S. foreign policy became more cautious and pragmatic with the emphasis on stability, not ideology.\n- Shift from military containment to diplomacy and proxy support (e.g., in Latin America, Africa, Afghanistan).\n- Questioning of the domino theory and limits of U.S. power.\n- Set the stage for Reagan's later reassertion of U.S. global confidence in the 1980s, but within awareness of Vietnam's lessons.\n\\\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Vietnam War initially reinforced U.S. Cold War policy by deepening the commitment to containment and the need to maintain global credibility ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). From the beginning, American leaders viewed the conflict as a test of the containment doctrine and the United States' position as the leader of the free world. As Source A highlights, Secretary of State Dean Rusk framed U.S. involvement in Southeast Asia as part of a wider mission to build “a decent world community” and to ensure that nations “leave their neighbors alone” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{sources}$). This echoed the moral tone of U.S. foreign policy since 1947, shaped by the Truman Doctrine, and the belief that communist expansion had to be opposed wherever it appeared ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{O-K}$). The Domino Theory added urgency to these concerns, fueling fears that a communist victory in Vietnam could trigger regional collapse across Asia. After the alleged Gulf of Tonkin attacks in 1964, Rusk defended immediate retaliation, reflecting the broader goal of safeguarding American credibility. Congress soon passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, granting President Johnson sweeping authority to escalate U.S. involvement. By 1968, more than 500,000 American troops were in Vietnam ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{O-K}$). The U.S. strategy of militarised containment was meant to deter communism through strength. However, it also revealed contradictions: in fighting for “freedom,” the U.S. found itself backing authoritarian regimes and engaging in a brutal war that alienated allies and divided its own population. Although the intention was to bolster global deterrence, the war increasingly exposed the fragile foundations of America's moral authority and raised doubts about the long-term viability of its interventionist strategy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nOver time, the war's failures and controversies transformed U.S. Cold War policy from idealistic interventionism to a more cautious, pragmatic approach ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). By 1971, as Kerry declared in Source B, many returning veterans saw the war as “the biggest nothing in history” and criticised the government for what they saw as “criminal hypocrisy”  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{sources}$). This reflected a growing sense of disillusionment with the principles behind U.S. containment policy. Events like the Tet Offensive and the My Lai Massacre shattered public confidence and undermined the idea that the U.S. was fighting a just war. Protest movements intensified, and the widening “credibility gap” between government statements and the war's reality forced a reevaluation of policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{O-K}$). Under President Nixon, this shift became more formalised. The strategy of Vietnamization aimed to reduce American troop involvement while still supporting anti-communist governments. At the same time, the Nixon Doctrine signaled a significant change: instead of direct military intervention, the U.S. would focus on providing aid and diplomatic support to allies. This coincided with a broader move toward détente, including key diplomatic openings to the Soviet Union (through SALT I) and China. Domestically, the War Powers Act of 1973 sought to limit presidential war powers, ensuring greater Congressional oversight ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{O-K}$). The psychological and political impact of the war, often referred to as the “Vietnam Syndrome”, left American policymakers wary of future large-scale interventions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nUltimately, the Vietnam War represented both a continuation and a turning point in U.S. Cold War policy. In its early years, it reflected the logic of global containment and the emphasis on credibility that had defined U.S. strategy since the late 1940s. But the prolonged stalemate, moral controversies, and backlash at home compelled a shift toward realism. Military power alone was no longer seen as a reliable tool for achieving ideological goals. By the mid-1970s, U.S. policy had evolved into a more selective, restrained form of engagement. One that emphasised diplomacy, alliances, and caution. This more measured approach would shape American foreign policy for the remainder of the Cold War and beyond ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$)."},{"ref":"HI1185","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1185\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about the nature of the Cold War in Asia?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Vietcong Poster. Title reads: “Glorious victories of the armed forces and people of South Vietnam in 1962 and the first three months of 1963,” c. 1963. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/The%20Cold%20War%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI1185.png\" alt=\"HI1185\">\n\nPartial translation…\n\n19 million people united to struggle against “My Diem” or “America's Diem.”\n\\\nWe fought 19,711 battles\n\\\nWe killed 28,108 enemy including 222 American advisers.\n\\\nWe wounded 26,011 enemy including 102 American advisers.\n\\\nWe captured 5,118 enemy including 4 Americans\n\n[Source: Information Bureau (1963). *Glorious victories of the armed forces and people of South Vietnam in 1962 and the first three months of 1963.* Records of the U.S. Information Agency. Retrieved October 29, 2025 from https://docsteach.org/document/viet-cong-army-propaganda/ . Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n\nFrames the Cold War as a popular, collective struggle:\n- The phrase “19 million people united to struggle against $\\lq$America's Diem' shows that the war is depicted as a mass movement of ordinary Vietnamese, rather than a conflict between great powers.\n- This emphasises national unity and popular participation, implying that the Cold War in Asia was fought through people's revolutions, not just state policies.\n\nFrames the Cold War as anti-imperialist resistance:\n- “America's Diem” links the U.S. directly to the South Vietnamese regime, suggesting foreign control and interference.\n- The image of a Viet Cong fighters against a warship labeled “USA” portrays America as a foreign invader, not a defender of freedom.\n- The language and imagery implies the Cold War in Asia is a continuation of anti-colonial struggles, with the U.S. replacing former European empires as the main adversary.\n\nMilitary success as proof of legitimacy\n- The listing of precise statistics, “We fought 19,711 battles,” “killed 28,108 enemy including 222 American advisers,” “wounded 26,011 enemy including 102 American advisers,” “captured 5,118 enemy including 4 Americans”, conveys that local revolutionary forces were strong and effective.\n- These numbers symbolise that the Vietnamese people were capable of defeating a global superpower, reinforcing an image of asymmetric but righteous resistance.\n\nThe Cold War as both local and international\n- References to “American advisers” highlight direct U.S. involvement, showing that the Cold War in Asia was not abstract but physically fought on Asian soil.\n- This conveys that regional conflicts like Vietnam were proxy arenas of the broader U.S.-Soviet rivalry.\n- The flag-raising imagery symbolises national independence and socialist identity, suggesting the Cold War in Asia was inseparable from anti-colonial nationalism.\n\nThe moral and ideological dimension\n- The triumphant tone of the statistics suggest that communist or revolutionary forces had moral superiority.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe image shows a Vietnamese fighter attacking a battleship marked “USA,” symbolising the Cold War in Asia as a direct, armed confrontation with American imperial power ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe text declares that “19 million people united to struggle against $\\lq$America's Diem'” and that they “fought 19,711 battles,” presenting the conflict as a mass, united struggle for national liberation led by the Vietnamese people ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1233","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1233 \n\nCompare and contrast the application of Eisenhower's New Look policy in **two** countries in the Americas (not including the United States).\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- The command term calls for both similarities and differences in the application of the policy.  A running comparison structure, rather than a structure organized by case study, allows for better analysis, but both can earn marks.\n- There are many examples of the application of New Look in Latin America. Some possible examples include:\n    - **The support of the Batista regime in Cuba:** With US support, Batista controlled “puppet” governments from 1933 until the elections of 1952. Knowing he would lose, he organized a bloodless coup in March 1952. Batista remained in power until the victory of the Cuban Revolution in 1959.\n    - **US support of the Somoza regime in Nicaragua:** The United States provided military assistance and economic support, which helped strengthen the Somoza regime and its National Guard. This allowed the Somoza family to remain in power between 1936 and 1979, and the US to have a reliable anticommunist ally in the region.\n    - **Vice President Nixon's tour of Latin America in 1958:** Vice President Nixon's 1958 goodwill tour of Latin America was intended to improve U.S. relations with the region. The initial motivation was to participate in the assumption ceremony of the newly democratically elected president of Argentina, but it also included visits to dictators like Stroessner of Paraguay. The visit was marked by widespread anti-American protests, particularly in Venezuela, where his motorcade was violently attacked. The tour highlighted deep-seated resentment over U.S. support for dictators and perceived economic exploitation.\n\n**Indicative content for the Bolivian National Revolution (1952-1964) and the coup against President Arbenz in Guatemala (1954):**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Context and reasons for application of the New Look policy}}:$\n- Bolivian National Revolution\n    - Between 1956 and 1958, the US assisted the Revolutionary Nationalist Movement (MNR)  with 78.3 million USD. In exchange, Bolivia was expected to adopt conservative economic policies that favored US interests. With these policies, the BNR abandoned its original nationalist programme and distanced itself from the miners' movement that had originally supported the 1952 coup.\n    - US influence in the way the BNR restructured the Armed Forces determined the Bolivian government's way of dealing with “domestic insurgents”.\n\n- Guatemala\n    - The U.S. successfully secured near-unanimous support for a resolution denouncing communist influence in Guatemala at the Tenth Inter-American Conference of the Organization of American States (OAS) in March 1954.\n    - Telecommunications infrastructure owned by the United Fruit Company was used to broadcast the “Voice of Liberation” radio station run by CIA agents to portray Arbenz as a Soviet agent.\n    - The CIA implemented Operation PBSUCCESS to stage a coup\n    - Around 200 exiled Guatemalans, led by Carlos Castrillo Armas, a member of the far-right National Liberation Movement (MLN) who had also worked with the CIA during the Truman administration, were trained and equipped by the CIA in Honduras and Nicaragua to overthrow the government.\n    - CIA operatives bombed the main cities of Guatemala while forces invaded from both Honduras and El Salvador. Castillo was then installed as an authoritarian leader with the backing of the US and would rule Guatemala until his assassination in 1957.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWhen Eisenhower became president in 1953, he was worried that if the US continued the policy of Containment, it would damage the US economy and hurt the “American Way of Life”. Therefore, he sought to implement a policy that would be more cost-effective in responding to perceived communist aggression. Elaborated by Secretary of State John Foster Dulles, this “New Look” policy relied on diplomatic maneuvering, economic pressure, and covert operations to eliminate perceived threats to national security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This policy was applied in different ways to the governments of the Bolivian National Revolution (BNR), which had taken power in 1952, and of Jacobo Árbenz in Guatemala ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). On paper, it would seem that in both cases, the presence of a nationalist government that had the people's support, as well as a rhetoric of reform against the oligarchical powers of the region, would command the same US response. However, the US treatment of the two countries was distinct ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nIdeological differences determined how the US administration dealt with both governments. While the government of Árbenz in Guatemala viewed the Communist Party as another reformist group, the government of the Bolivian National Revolution (BNR) viewed it as an enemy that should be destroyed ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This in part explains US involvement in the overthrow of Àrbenz and US support for the BNR.\n\nHowever, the difference in the importance of US economic interests in each country better explains the different ways in which the New Look policy was applied ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). While American companies did operate in Bolivia, their actions were minimal. The only notable US-Bolivia commercial relation at the time was the extraction of tin, a strategic resource during World War II. However, by 1952, the US already had a significant stockpile of it. Moreover, the BNR promised to nationalize tin mines but at the same time to compensate American companies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This was not the case in Guatemala, where the United Fruit Company (UFC) owned 40% of Guatemalan land, and much of it was left fallow as a way to limit the supply of bananas and thus control their international price. Árbenz nationalized fallow land to distribute to landless peasants and promised to pay the Company what it calculated to be the land value based on the amount declared by United Fruit Company for tax purposes, which came out to be $\\text{\\textdollar}$1,185,000. The Company claimed that the land was actually worth $\\text{\\textdollar}$19,355,000 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). When Árbenz's administration replied that it would have received the amount it was demanding if it had not cheated on its tax forms, the UFC started a publicity campaign to convince the American public that he was a communist ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).  In a 1953 article by the New York Times, Guatemala was described as “operating under increasingly severe Communist-inspired pressure to rid the country of United States companies” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nAnother significant difference that motivated the varied application of the New Look policy was the personal connections between the UFC and the US administration  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$), which did not exist in Bolivia. For example, CIA director, Allen Dulles, had previously served on the UFC's board ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Aside from the nationalization of US interests, these two countries had few things in common for the US in the 1950s ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$), which helps explain the distinct treatment by the US.\n\nThe US also applied the New Look policy inconsistently in other ways. Since the BNR came to power through a coup in 1952, the US could have chosen to view it as illegitimate. However, the Truman administration recognized this new, but illegal government. Arbenz, on the other hand, was elected president through a democratic process in 1951, so his administration automatically received US recognition. Initially, although the nature of the government was different in both countries, they received the same treatment by the US ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).  However, the US initiated a diplomatic attack on Arbenz at the  1954 conference of the Organization of American States (OAS), where it secured a resolution denouncing communist influence in Guatemala ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). A CIA operation followed which took advantage of the UFC's near-complete control of the communications infrastructure in Guatemala to undermine the government. The CIA coordinated propaganda campaigns, including fictitious radio broadcasts, to demonize Árbenz's government. Spending between 5 and 7 million USD, the US trained Guatemalans led by Colonel Castillo Armas to invade ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). To help, planes piloted by CIA operatives bombed key towns. As a result, Arbenz left the country, and Castillo Armas became dictator. This US-orchestrated coup against a democratic government contrasts with the treatment given to the de facto government of Bolivia. In this case, but with similar results for US interests, the BNR received financial aid to help the new government remain in power. For example, in 1957, 32% of the central government`s budget came from US assistance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). In exchange, Bolivia had to adopt conservative monetary policies like spending cuts and the opening of oil fields to foreign companies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). For example, US advisors were involved in the drafting of the 1955 Petroleum Code ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The US also helped the BNR maintain its control over the country by training and equipping the Bolivian armed forces, enabling them to control civil unrest. By 1964, 20 out of 23 senior officers of the Bolivian army had either attended the U.S. School of the Americas or had visited the United States for training ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$).\n\nIn both cases, the application of the New Look policy achieved the goals of obtaining friendly governments in power with little cost to the US public. While in Guatemala, this came about through a covert operation, in Bolivia, it was achieved with economic influence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Bolivia, the combination of limited economic stakes and the government's anti-communist declarations led Washington to adopt a cooperative approach. In Guatemala, strong corporate interests, the Dulles brothers' connections to UFC, and a campaign designed to convince the American public of communist infiltration prompted a  CIA intervention. The New Look policy and its flexibility allowed the Eisenhower administration to adjust its responses based on its interests and not on the nature of each government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1234","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1234\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The withdrawal of U.S. forces from Vietnam was driven primarily by domestic pressures.” To what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**Guidance for Responses**\n- Candidates should evaluate how far domestic pressures were responsible for the U.S. decision to withdraw from Vietnam. Strong responses will consider a range of domestic pressures, including public opinion, anti-war activism, media influence, political and economic constraints, while also addressing other significant factors, such as military developments in Vietnam and broader Cold War strategic concerns.\n\nCandidates should demonstrate:\n- Understanding of the evolution of both domestic and external pressures from 1965 to 73.\n- How these pressures influenced presidential decisions under Johnson and Nixon.\n- The interaction between conditions in Vietnam and domestic developments.\n- A clear, analytical judgement on whether domestic pressures were the primary factor behind withdrawal.\n- Responses should avoid description and focus on analysis, integration of evidence, and a substantiated conclusion.\n\n**Indicative Content**\n\\\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Domestic Pressures (supporting the statement)}}$\n- Anti-war movement: Teach-ins, escalation of protests after 1965, SDS, Moratorium March 1969, veterans' activism, impact of Kent State and Jackson State shootings.\n- Media influence: Growth of televised war coverage; Walter Cronkite's 1968 broadcast; publication of the My Lai massacre (1969) and the Pentagon Papers (1971).\n- Public opinion and the “credibility gap”: Decline in support following Tet; increasing distrust of official statements; Johnson's approval ratings collapsing.\n- Presidential political pressures: Johnson's decision not to run in 1968; Nixon's need to navigate public unrest while maintaining political credibility.\n- Economic considerations: Inflation, rising budget deficits, impact on Great Society programs, domestic criticism of war spending.\n- Congressional limits on the executive: Repeal of Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, Cooper-Church Amendment, and the 1973 War Powers Act restricting presidential war-making powers.\n- Draft resistance and social division: Evasion, conscientious objectors, and widespread societal tension influencing political decision-making.\n\nDomestic pressures can be used to argue that the war became politically untenable.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Developments in Vietnam (challenging the statement) }}$\n- Military stalemate: Difficulty in defeating the Viet Cong; inability to secure rural areas; attrition strategy proving ineffective.\n- Tet Offensive (1968): Turning point undermining the image of progress; psychological impact on U.S. public and policymakers.\n- ARVN weaknesses: Problems of corruption, leadership failures, poor morale, and limited effectiveness without U.S. support.\n- Limitations of Vietnamization: Initial claims of success contrasted with ARVN failures during the 1972 Easter Offensive.\n- North Vietnamese resilience: Continued logistical support from USSR and China; ability to absorb losses; effective use of the Ho Chi Minh Trail.\n- Paris Peace Talks: Stalled negotiations; reliance on military pressure (bombings of Cambodia/Laos) and diplomatic manoeuvring.\n\nThese factors support arguments that military and political realities in Vietnam itself forced withdrawal.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Broader Cold War Strategic Factors}}$\n- Détente: Nixon and Kissinger's policy of easing tensions with the USSR and China reduced the strategic importance of Vietnam.\n- Triangular diplomacy: Improved U.S. relations with Beijing and Moscow shifted policy priorities away from Southeast Asia.\n- Nixon Doctrine: The expectation that U.S. allies would take greater responsibility for their own defense justified U.S. disengagement.\n- Shift in global priorities: Resources and attention redirected to other Cold War fronts, nuclear arms negotiations, and Middle East policy.\n- Risk of wider conflict: Expansion of the war into Cambodia and Laos threatened instability in the broader region.\n\nThese can be used to argue that withdrawal was part of a larger strategic recalibration.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe withdrawal of U.S. forces from Vietnam resulted from several converging pressures, both domestic and external ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While mass protest, media scrutiny, political costs, and economic strain played a significant role in shaping decisions in Washington, these domestic pressures became influential largely because military realities in Vietnam and shifting Cold War priorities made the war increasingly unsustainable ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$). This essay argues that domestic pressures were crucial but operated alongside failures on the battlefield and strategic recalculations under Nixon and Kissinger ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nDomestic pressures escalated sharply after 1965 and became a central force pushing policymakers toward disengagement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Public opposition intensified as troop levels rose and casualties mounted. The anti-war movement expanded rapidly, from early campus teach-ins to mass demonstrations organized by groups like Students for a Democratic Society (SDS) and the 1969 Moratorium, which involved millions nationwide ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These protests signaled widespread dissatisfaction not limited to student radicals, but increasingly involving veterans, clergy, and middle-class Americans. The 1970 Kent State and Jackson State shootings showed the extent to which the war had destabilized domestic politics, increasing pressure on elected officials. The media amplified these domestic pressures. The Vietnam War was the first conflict broadcasted extensively on television, and graphic footage challenged official claims of progress. Walter Cronkite's influential 1968 broadcast declaring the war unwinnable helped shift public opinion dramatically ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Investigations into the My Lai massacre (1969) and the Pentagon Papers (1971) deepened the “credibility gap” between the government and the public ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This climate of public distrust weakened both Johnson's and Nixon's political authority and made an indefinite military commitment increasingly politically risky ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nPresidential calculations were increasingly shaped by these pressures. Johnson's approval ratings collapsed in 1968, contributing to his decision not to seek re-election. Nixon, though initially supported by a “silent majority,” confronted mounting domestic criticism as the war continued($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Draft resistance, desertion, and widening social divisions placed further strain on policymakers. Economic pressures added to this climate: the war contributed to inflation, budget deficits, and cuts to domestic programs. Many Americans blamed rising prices and declining social spending on the war, increasing the political incentives for withdrawal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Congress also restricted the executive's freedom of action. Between 1970 and 1973, it repealed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, passed the Cooper-Church Amendment limiting military operations in Cambodia, and finally enacted the War Powers Act, which curtailed presidential war-making authority. These developments showed that the war had lost political legitimacy at home and that domestic institutions increasingly constrained U.S. strategy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nHowever, domestic pressures alone cannot explain the decision to withdraw ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Developments within Vietnam itself fundamentally shaped the environment in which domestic opposition grew, and ultimately made withdrawal unavoidable. The Tet Offensive in 1968 was a crucial turning point. Although it was a military defeat for North Vietnam, the coordinated attacks across South Vietnam exposed the weaknesses of U.S. and South Vietnamese intelligence, contradicting official claims that victory was near ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Tet deeply affected U.S. policymakers and contributed directly to Johnson's decision to de-escalate and pursue negotiations. It was, therefore, military failure, and not simply protest at home, that triggered a shift in strategy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). More broadly, the conflict reached a military stalemate by the late 1960s. Despite deploying more than half a million troops, the United States struggled to defeat a highly resilient enemy employing guerrilla tactics. Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN) remained plagued by corruption, poor morale, and weak leadership ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These shortcomings became obvious when ARVN required extensive U.S. airpower to repel the 1972 Easter Offensive. Vietnamization, central to Nixon's policy, failed to create a self-sustaining South Vietnamese military ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The inability to achieve meaningful progress on the ground made continued U.S. engagement increasingly irrational ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nA third factor shaping withdrawal was the broader Cold War context. By the early 1970s, U.S. strategic priorities were shifting ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Nixon and Kissinger's policy of détente sought to reduce tensions with the Soviet Union and China. Ongoing conflict in Vietnam complicated these diplomatic goals and consumed resources needed for other fronts, including the Middle East and arms control negotiations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Triangular diplomacy further reduced the perceived strategic importance of Vietnam in containing communism. The Nixon Doctrine, which emphasized that allies should take more responsibility for their defense, provided an ideological justification for disengagement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Withdrawal was therefore not only a concession to domestic pressure but also part of a broader realignment of U.S. Cold War policy.\n\nIn conclusion, domestic pressures played a major role in driving U.S. withdrawal from Vietnam, but they became decisive largely because military and strategic conditions had already made victory improbable ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$). Public protest, media scrutiny, and political pressures shaped the timing and manner of withdrawal, but the underlying necessity of withdrawal was rooted in the military stalemate and shifting Cold War priorities. Domestic pressures were therefore a critical factor, but not the primary one in isolation; withdrawal resulted from the convergence of domestic opposition, battlefield realities, and broader strategic concerns ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1480","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1480\n\nEvaluate the impact of McCarthyism on the domestic and foreign policies of the United States.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**General Guidance:**\n\nThe command term evaluate requires candidates to assess the extent to which McCarthyism influenced domestic and foreign policy in the United States. Responses should move beyond description and reach a judgment about McCarthyism's significance relative to other forces shaping early Cold War policy. Candidates should distinguish the domestic dimension by analyzing how McCarthyism affected political institutions, civil liberties, and public debate. This includes assessing the influence of investigations, loyalty programs, surveillance, and blacklisting, and determining how far these mechanisms reshaped governance and limited dissent. Candidates should assess the extent to which McCarthyism shaped Cold War the political context within which Cold War decisions were made. This involves examining how domestic accusations of weakness influenced containment, diplomacy and military commitments, and weighing these influences against broader drivers such as geopolitical developments, presidential leadership and long-term strategic priorities. Strong responses will reach a balanced judgment on the overall impact of McCarthyism. They should consider variation across institutions and contexts, recognize where its influence was decisive and where it was limited, and assess its significance relative to the structural Cold War dynamics that also shaped United States policy.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n\n**Domestic Examples**\n- **Hollywood and HUAC**\n    - Congressional investigations into alleged communist influence in Hollywood revealed how McCarthyism operated through public hearings, accusations, and political pressure.\n    - The Hollywood Ten case demonstrated the limits of civil liberties when refusal to testify led to imprisonment and professional punishment.\n    - Blacklisting reshaped media production, narrowing ideological diversity and discouraging criticism of government policy.\n    - Studios and writers adapted content to avoid suspicion, revealing how fear reshaped cultural expression and public debate.\n    - These mechanisms revealed how domestic institutions struggled to balance national security concerns with constitutional protections.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some historians argue that Hollywood’s response reflected preexisting industry conservatism, meaning that McCarthyism intensified, but did not create, ideological control.\n\n- **Loyalty Review Program**\n    - The federal Loyalty Review Program required millions of government employees to undergo loyalty screenings.\n    - Investigations relied on vague criteria, anonymous accusations, and broad definitions of subversive conduct.\n    - The program expanded surveillance, encouraged self-censorship, and contributed to dismissals that eroded civil liberties.\n    - It embedded suspicion in bureaucratic procedures, narrowing acceptable political participation.\n    - It demonstrated how McCarthyism reshaped governance by redefining the boundaries of legitimate dissent.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some scholars contend that the program reflected broader Cold War anxieties rather than McCarthyism alone.\n\n- **Army–McCarthy Hearings**\n    - The hearings revealed that McCarthyism destabilized federal institutions through nationally televised confrontations.\n    - Public exposure of McCarthy’s tactics weakened his credibility and revealed the limits of fear-based politics.\n    - They illustrated how institutional resistance could curb McCarthyist pressure.\n    - They highlighted tensions between national security concerns and political opportunism.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some historians argue that the hearings reflected personal rivalry rather than structural resistance.\n\n**Foreign Policy Examples**\n\n- **State Department Purges**\n    - Accusations led to dismissals and resignations, reducing diplomatic expertise during key Cold War crises.\n    - The purges narrowed policy debate and discouraged nuanced analysis of Asia and Eastern Europe.\n    - They reinforced rigid containment thinking and eroded institutional capacity.\n    - They showed how domestic political pressure could distort the formulation of foreign policy.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some scholars argue that broader Cold War priorities, not McCarthyism alone, drove personnel changes.\n\n- **“Loss of China” and escalation in Asia**\n    - The controversy over China's loss showed how domestic accusations of weakness shaped foreign policy decisions.\n    - Policymakers feared being blamed for another strategic failure, making anti-communist credibility paramount.\n    - This pressure shaped decisions during the Korean War, reinforcing military containment and limiting diplomatic flexibility.\n    - Domestic vulnerability pushed officials toward more rigid interpretations of global communist expansion.\n    - Congressional criticism and media rhetoric amplified the belief that assertive action abroad was necessary to prevent political damage.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some scholars argue that escalation reflected broader containment priorities rather than McCarthyite pressure.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nThe extent to which McCarthyism influenced United States domestic and foreign policy has been widely debated by historians. This essay examines two key examples, Hollywood and the “loss of China,” to assess how far McCarthyite pressures shaped political institutions, civil liberties, public debate, and Cold War decision-making. ($\\checkmark$ focus)While both examples reveal significant effects, they also show that McCarthyism interacted with broader structural forces, including containment strategy, geopolitical developments, and presidential leadership. A balanced assessment requires analyzing the specific mechanisms through which McCarthyism operated and weighing their significance against other drivers of early Cold War policy. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\nMcCarthyism profoundly reshaped domestic political culture, and Hollywood became one of its most visible arenas. Congressional investigations into alleged communist influence in the film industry demonstrated how McCarthyism operated through public hearings, accusations, and political pressure. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The House Un-American Activities Committee (HUAC) used highly public congressional hearings to expose supposed subversive networks, creating a climate in which refusing to cooperate was equated with disloyalty. The Hollywood Ten case revealed the limits of civil liberties when individuals who declined to testify were imprisoned for contempt of Congress and subsequently blacklisted. This episode illustrated how McCarthyism redefined acceptable dissent by linking silence or resistance to accusations of communist sympathy. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nHollywood offers clear examples of how McCarthyism reshaped cultural production. Studios and writers adapted content to avoid suspicion, narrowing ideological diversity and discouraging criticism of government policies. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Blacklisting kept hundreds of actors, screenwriters and directors from working, demonstrating how political fear could restrict professional opportunities and reshape an entire industry. The pressure extended beyond Hollywood into universities, unions and federal agencies, revealing how domestic institutions struggled to balance national security concerns with constitutional protections. These mechanisms show that McCarthyism did not merely punish alleged communists but also transformed the boundaries of public debate by embedding suspicion in everyday professional life. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nHistorians disagree about how fully Hollywood’s experience reflects McCarthyism’s broader domestic impact. Some, such as Ellen Schrecker, argue that the film industry became a symbolic battleground where McCarthyism demonstrated its capacity to distort democratic norms and suppress dissent. Others contend that Hollywood’s response reflected preexisting conservatism within the industry, suggesting that McCarthyism intensified but did not create ideological control. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) This debate highlights the need to evaluate McCarthyism’s influence relative to structural and cultural tendencies that also shaped political expression in the early Cold War.\n\nMcCarthyism also influenced foreign policy, particularly through the political controversy surrounding the “loss of China.” After the Chinese Communist Party triumphed in 1949, accusations of domestic weakness became central to United States political discourse. Policymakers feared being blamed for another strategic failure, making anti-communist credibility a crucial factor in Cold War strategy. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The belief that officials had been insufficiently vigilant in Asia created intense pressure on the Truman administration, reinforcing the idea that assertive action abroad was necessary to avoid political damage at home. This dynamic linked foreign policy decisions directly to domestic vulnerability, demonstrating how McCarthyism shaped perceptions of global communist expansion. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe “loss of China” controversy had significant consequences for United States policy in Asia. Congressional criticism and media rhetoric amplified the belief that diplomatic flexibility risked further strategic setbacks. ($\\checkmark$ examples) During the Korean War, policymakers adopted more rigid interpretations of containment, supporting military intervention to demonstrate resolve and avoid accusations of appeasement. Domestic political fears limited the space for negotiation and encouraged escalation, revealing how McCarthyism could distort foreign policy formulation by prioritizing political credibility over strategic nuance. These pressures contributed to a broader pattern in which Cold War decisions were shaped not only by geopolitical conditions but also by internal accusations of disloyalty. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nHistorians offer contrasting interpretations of the significance of the “loss of China” in shaping United States foreign policy. Some, such as John Lewis Gaddis, argue that domestic political pressures played a decisive role in pushing policymakers toward more aggressive containment strategies. Others contend that escalation in Asia reflected broader structural priorities of the Cold War, suggesting that McCarthyite rhetoric intensified but did not fundamentally redirect strategy. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) This debate underscores the importance of evaluating McCarthyism’s influence relative to long-term strategic concerns that also shaped United States policy.\n\nComparing Hollywood and the “loss of China” demonstrates that McCarthyism exercised fundamentally different forms of influence over domestic and foreign policy. In Hollywood, McCarthyism directly reshaped institutions, civil liberties and cultural production by embedding suspicion in professional practices and narrowing acceptable dissent. These effects were immediate, visible and deeply personal, affecting careers, artistic expression and public debate. In contrast, the “loss of China” controversy influenced foreign policy more indirectly, shaping perceptions of credibility and vulnerability rather than producing specific institutional mechanisms. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe comparison also highlights variation in the significance of McCarthyism relative to other factors. Hollywood shows that McCarthyism could be a primary driver of institutional change, while foreign policy decisions in Asia reveal that it often interacted with broader geopolitical dynamics. Domestic accusations of weakness intensified containment but did not replace structural strategic priorities. This difference is essential for evaluating the extent to which McCarthyism shaped United States policy, showing that its influence was uneven across contexts. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nOverall, McCarthyism had a substantial impact on both domestic and foreign policy, though its significance varied. In Hollywood, it was a major force that redefined dissent, restricted civil liberties, and reshaped cultural production. In foreign policy, it influenced decision-making by amplifying fears of political vulnerability, but its effects were mediated by broader Cold War dynamics. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion) Therefore, McCarthyism was highly influential in shaping domestic institutions and public debate, while its impact on foreign policy, though significant, was less decisive and operated alongside structural geopolitical factors.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n|:--|:-:|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples) |\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives) |\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"},{"ref":"HI1481","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1481\n\nExamine the reasons for, and effects of, Nixon's covert operations in Chile.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**General Guidance:**\n\nThe command term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$examine\" requires candidates to investigate both the reasons for and effects of Nixon's covert operations in Chile, explaining why the United States intervened and analysing the consequences of this intervention for Chile, the United States and the wider Cold War. Responses should move beyond a chronological narrative by examining the interaction of ideological, strategic, political and economic motivations before assessing both the immediate and longer-term effects. The strongest responses will distinguish between short-term and long-term consequences while recognising that historians continue to debate the extent to which US covert operations directly caused the 1973 military coup.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n- **Containment and the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demonstration effect\"**\n    - Nixon and National Security Adviser Henry Kissinger viewed Salvador Allende's election (1970) as a threat to US containment policy in Latin America.\n    - The administration feared that a successful democratically elected Marxist government would encourage similar movements elsewhere in the region (the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demonstration effect\").\n    - US policymakers believed Chile could become another Cuba if Allende consolidated power.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterpoint:}}$ Chile remained a constitutional democracy under Allende, and there is debate over whether his government posed an immediate strategic threat to the United States.\n\n- **Cold War strategic considerations**\n    - The United States sought to prevent increased Soviet and Cuban influence in South America.\n    - Nixon authorised covert action, including Track II, to prevent Allende from taking office and later to destabilise his government.\n    - The failed kidnapping of General René Schneider (1970), who opposed military intervention, illustrated the risks of covert intervention.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterpoint:}}$ Soviet support for Allende remained cautious and limited, suggesting that US policymakers may have exaggerated the communist threat.\n\n- **Economic motivations**\n    - The nationalisation of Chile's copper industry threatened major American investments, including those of Anaconda Copper and Kennecott.\n    - The United States reduced economic assistance and encouraged international financial institutions to restrict loans to Chile.\n    - Nixon reportedly instructed officials to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$make the economy scream\" in order to increase pressure on Allende's government.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterpoint:}}$ while revisionist historians emphasise the protection of American economic interests, most historians argue these concerns reinforced rather than replaced broader Cold War objectives.\n\n- **Effects on Chile**\n    - CIA funding supported opposition parties, newspapers such as El Mercurio, and anti-government organisations, contributing to growing political polarisation.\n    - General Augusto Pinochet overthrew Allende on 11 September 1973.\n    - Military rule suspended democratic institutions, banned political parties and resulted in approximately 3,000 deaths or disappearances, with tens of thousands imprisoned or tortured.\n    - Market-oriented reforms implemented by the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Chicago Boys\" transformed Chile's economy but also increased social inequality.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterpoint:}}$ Chile's political and economic divisions had significant domestic origins, meaning US intervention accelerated rather than solely caused democratic collapse.\n\n- **Effects on the United States and the Cold War**\n    - The Nixon administration initially viewed Allende's removal as a successful example of containment.\n    - Congressional investigations, particularly the Church Committee (1975), exposed CIA activities and increased oversight of US intelligence agencies.\n    - US involvement damaged America's international reputation and fuelled criticism that it had undermined democracy while claiming to defend it.\n    - Chile became one of the defining examples of Cold War interventionism in Latin America.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterpoint:}}$ although Chile demonstrated the global reach of the Cold War, superpower rivalry in Latin America reflected broader ideological and geopolitical competition.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nNixon's covert operations in Chile remain one of the clearest examples of Cold War intervention because they combined ideological rivalry, strategic calculation and covert action to influence the internal politics of another sovereign state. ($\\checkmark$ focus) Although economic interests contributed to American policy, the overriding objective was to prevent a successful democratic socialist government from becoming a model for the rest of Latin America. The consequences extended far beyond Chile itself, reshaping Chilean politics, damaging America's international reputation and influencing later debates over the limits of covert intervention. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nThe principal reason for Nixon's covert operations was the belief that Salvador Allende's presidency threatened the credibility of American containment in the Western Hemisphere. Allende's election in September 1970 represented the first time a Marxist had gained power through democratic elections in Latin America. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Nixon and Henry Kissinger feared not simply that Chile would align more closely with the Soviet Union, but that a successful constitutional path to socialism would encourage similar political movements elsewhere in the region. This so-called $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demonstration effect\" challenged the United States' long-standing objective of limiting communist influence in its traditional sphere of influence. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Consequently, Washington viewed Chile less as an isolated national issue than as a test of whether democratic socialism could succeed during the Cold War.\n\nStrategic concerns reinforced these ideological fears. Although Soviet economic assistance remained relatively limited and Moscow was cautious about committing major resources to Chile, American policymakers believed that Allende's government could eventually strengthen Soviet and Cuban influence in South America. Nixon therefore authorised covert CIA operations, including Track II, to prevent Allende from assuming office and later to undermine his government. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The failed kidnapping of General René Schneider, who opposed unconstitutional military intervention, demonstrated both the determination of American policymakers and the dangers of covert action. However, historians generally agree that the United States did not directly organise or control the military coup of September 1973. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) As Paul Sigmund argues, American intervention significantly influenced events without completely determining their outcome. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nEconomic considerations also shaped Nixon's policy, although they are best understood as reinforcing Cold War priorities rather than replacing them. Allende's nationalisation of Chile's copper industry threatened substantial American investments held by companies such as Anaconda Copper and Kennecott. The United States responded by reducing economic assistance, discouraging international lending and, according to Nixon's own instructions, attempting to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$make the economy scream.\" ($\\checkmark$ examples) Revisionist historians such as Peter Kornbluh argue that protecting American economic interests formed an important motivation for intervention. Nevertheless, the broader evidence suggests that economic pressure was primarily intended to weaken a government perceived as strategically dangerous rather than simply protect corporate profits. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nThe effects of Nixon's covert operations were most profound within Chile itself. ($\\checkmark$ focus) CIA support for opposition political parties, business groups and newspapers such as El Mercurio contributed to increasing political polarisation and undermined confidence in Allende's government. Combined with inflation, shortages, strikes and growing political violence, these pressures created conditions in which military intervention became increasingly likely. On 11 September 1973, General Augusto Pinochet overthrew Allende and established a military dictatorship that lasted until 1990. Democratic institutions were suspended, political parties were banned, and approximately 3,000 people were killed or disappeared while tens of thousands more were imprisoned or tortured. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Pinochet also introduced market-oriented reforms developed by the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Chicago Boys\", which contributed to long-term economic growth but also increased social inequality. However, as Chilean historian Joaquín Fermandois argues, Chile's political crisis cannot be explained solely through American intervention because deep domestic divisions had already weakened the country's democratic system. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) US covert operations therefore acted as an important accelerant rather than the sole cause of democratic collapse. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe consequences also extended well beyond Chile. Initially, Nixon regarded Allende's removal as a successful demonstration of containment in Latin America. However, revelations during the Church Committee investigations in 1975 exposed the extent of CIA involvement and damaged America's moral authority internationally. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The Chilean case became one of the most prominent examples of Cold War interventionism, reinforcing criticism that the United States had undermined democracy while claiming to defend it against communism. Congress responded by increasing oversight of intelligence agencies, placing greater restrictions on future covert operations. More broadly, Chile illustrated that Cold War competition increasingly relied upon covert intervention, economic pressure and political influence rather than direct military confrontation between the superpowers. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nIn conclusion, Nixon's covert operations in Chile were driven primarily by the desire to contain communism and prevent Chile from becoming a successful democratic socialist model for Latin America, while economic interests reinforced these broader strategic objectives. (✓ conclusion) The operations contributed significantly to the destabilisation of Allende's government, but they interacted with profound domestic political and economic tensions rather than independently causing the 1973 coup. Their effects proved far-reaching: Chile experienced nearly two decades of authoritarian rule and widespread human rights abuses, while the United States faced lasting criticism for its willingness to undermine democratic governments in pursuit of Cold War objectives. Ultimately, the Chilean case demonstrates both the global reach of the Cold War and the complex, often unintended, consequences of covert intervention as an instrument of American foreign policy. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n|:--|:-:|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples) |\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives) |\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"},{"ref":"HI1489","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1489\n\nExamine the reasons for Axis defeat in the Second World War.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**General Guidance:**\n\\\nThe command term examine requires candidates to identify and explain the key factors behind Axis defeat. Strong responses should move beyond narrative description and analyze both internal weaknesses and external pressures. Candidates should assess Axis weaknesses, including strategic overextension, flawed planning and inadequate industrial and logistical mobilisation, while comparing these with Allied strengths. Strong responses should compare Axis weaknesses (strategic overextension, planning and logistics) with Allied strengths (industrial production, intelligence, technology and coalition warfare) before reaching a balanced judgement.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n\n- **Eastern Front**\n    - The largest and most destructive theater of the war, creating levels of attrition that Germany could not sustain.\n    - The USSR relocated factories eastward and achieved production levels that exceeded German output.\n    - Stalingrad destroyed Germany's Sixth Army and marked the end of sustained German offensives in the east.\n    - Kursk confirmed the transition to permanent German defensive warfare.\n    - The USSR relocated industry east of the Urals and continued to outproduce Germany in tanks and artillery despite enormous losses.\n    - Harsh climate, long distances, and supply shortages undermined German operations.\n    - Counterargument: Some historians argue that Germany might have stabilized the front had it avoided opening additional theaters and concentrated resources solely against the USSR.\n$\\\\$\n- **Allied industrial superiority**\n    - The United States produced ships, aircraft, and vehicles at unprecedented levels.\n    - Allied industry enabled simultaneous campaigns across Europe and the Pacific.\n    - ontrol of sea lanes and transport capacity ensured continuous supply flows.\n    - Strategic bombing reduced German production and damaged critical infrastructure.\n    - Radar, Ultra intelligence and long-range escort fighters strengthened Allied air and naval superiority.\n    - The Allies could replace losses quickly, while the Axis lacked comparable reserves.\n    - Counterargument: Some scholars argue that industrial superiority alone would not have been decisive without Soviet resistance and effective Allied coordination.\n$\\\\$\n- **Strategic overextension**\n    - Germany invaded the USSR while continuing the war against Britain, Italy struggled in North Africa and the Balkans, and Japan's expansion provoked war with the United States.\n    - The Axis lacked integrated planning and a coherent coalition strategy.\n    - Ideology led to unrealistic objectives and underestimated enemy capabilities.\n    - Counterargument: Some argue that overextension was partly driven by Allied pressure rather than by pure Axis miscalculation.\n$\\\\$\n- **Allied intelligence and technology**\n    - Allied intelligence anticipated Axis movements and disrupted Axis operations.\n    - Technological advances strengthened air defense and naval warfare.\n    - Precision attacks damaged production centers and communication networks.\n    - Counterargument: Some historians view intelligence as important but secondary to material and demographic factors.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe reasons for Axis defeat in the Second World War have been widely debated by historians. This essay argues that the Eastern Front and Allied industrial superiority were the two most significant reasons for Axis defeat because together they exposed Germany's inability to sustain a prolonged global war ($\\checkmark$ focus). While both examples reveal decisive effects, they also show that Axis defeat resulted from the interaction of strategic overextension, flawed planning, and the overwhelming material and logistical advantages of the Allied coalition. A balanced assessment requires analyzing the specific mechanisms through which the Axis lost the initiative and weighing their significance relative to broader geopolitical and economic forces ($\\checkmark$ organization).\n\nThe Eastern Front was the war's most destructive theater and the central arena where Axis defeat unfolded. German strategy underestimated Soviet resilience, industrial capacity, and manpower, leading to a campaign of attrition that Germany could not sustain ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). The Battle of Stalingrad marked a turning point, destroying German offensive capability and demonstrating the limits of Blitzkrieg when confronted with vast distances, harsh climate, and determined resistance. The subsequent defeat at Kursk confirmed the transition to permanent defensive warfare, as the Red Army gained the initiative and began pushing westward. These developments illustrate how the Eastern Front consumed German resources on a scale unmatched in any other theater ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nThe Eastern Front also provides clear examples of how structural disadvantages undermined the Axis war effort. Soviet industrial relocation to the Urals allowed production to continue despite territorial losses, enabling the USSR to outproduce Germany in tanks, aircraft, and artillery ($\\checkmark$ examples). The manpower imbalance further weakened the Axis, as the USSR could replace enormous losses while Germany faced demographic limits and declining recruitment. Logistical collapse compounded these problems, with supply shortages, inadequate transport networks, and extreme weather conditions crippling operations. These mechanisms show that the Eastern Front did not merely challenge German strategy but also exposed fundamental weaknesses in Axis planning and resource mobilization ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nHistorians disagree on how fully the Eastern Front alone explains Axis defeat. Some argue that Germany might have stabilized the front if it had avoided opening additional theaters and concentrated resources exclusively against the USSR. Others contend that the scale of Soviet industrial and demographic capacity made long-term German victory impossible regardless of strategic choices ($\\checkmark$ perspectives). This debate underscores the need to evaluate the Eastern Front alongside other structural factors that shaped the war's outcome.\n\nAllied industrial superiority was another decisive factor in the Axis defeat. The United States produced ships, aircraft, and vehicles at unprecedented levels, enabling the Allies to sustain multi-front operations and replace losses rapidly ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). Industrial strength allowed the Allies to maintain control of the sea lanes, expand air power, and support large-scale offensives in both Europe and the Pacific. Strategic bombing increasingly disrupted German oil production, transport networks and communications, making it harder to sustain the war effort.These developments demonstrate how material superiority gradually eroded Axis operational capacity ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nIndustrial superiority also reshaped the strategic balance by enabling effective coalition warfare. The Allies coordinated production, logistics and technology, integrating resources across the United States, Britain and the USSR ($\\checkmark$ examples). Technological innovation, including radar and improved aircraft, increased efficiency and strengthened air and naval operations. The ability to transport troops and supplies across vast distances ensured that Allied offensives could be sustained over time, while the Axis struggled with shortages, fragmented planning and inadequate coordination. These mechanisms show that industrial superiority was not merely a quantitative advantage but a structural foundation for Allied strategic success ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nHistorians offer contrasting interpretations of the significance of industrial superiority. Richard Overy argues that material dominance alone would not have been decisive without Soviet resistance and effective Allied coordination. Others, including David Edgerton, contend that industrial capacity was the primary determinant of victory, shaping every major theater of the war ($\\checkmark$ perspectives). This debate underscores the importance of evaluating industrial superiority alongside other strategic and geopolitical factors.\n\nComparing the Eastern Front with Allied industrial superiority shows that Axis defeat stemmed from the interplay of internal weaknesses and external pressures. On the Eastern Front, the Axis faced overwhelming attrition, logistical collapse, and a resilient enemy capable of sustained mobilization. These effects were immediate, visible, and structurally decisive, consuming German resources and breaking military momentum. In contrast, industrial superiority operated more broadly, shaping the strategic environment by enabling multi-front operations, technological innovation, and logistical dominance ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nThe comparison also highlights variation in the significance of different factors. The Eastern Front shows that Axis defeat stemmed from structural disadvantages in manpower, production, and logistics, while industrial superiority reveals how Allied coordination and material strength amplified these weaknesses. Strategic overextension and flawed planning further intensified the impact of these factors, demonstrating that Axis defeat cannot be attributed to a single cause but to the cumulative effect of interconnected pressures ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nAxis defeat in the Second World War resulted from a combination of structural weaknesses and overwhelming Allied advantages. The Eastern Front consumed German resources and exposed fundamental limitations in planning and mobilization, while Allied industrial superiority ensured sustained pressure across multiple theaters ($\\checkmark$ conclusion). Therefore, Axis defeat was strongly influenced by the scale of the Eastern Front and the material strength of the Allied coalition, alongside strategic errors and broader geopolitical dynamics that shaped the outcome of the war.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"}]},{"name":"World War II and the Americas","questions":[{"ref":"HI0059","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0059\n\nExamine the extent to which the treatment of people of Japanese descent in the Americas during the Second World War was motivated by security concerns rather than racial prejudice.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\n**Guidance for responses**\n\nCandidates should evaluate whether the treatment of Japanese communities in the Americas (U.S., Canada, and Latin America) during the Second World War was primarily driven by wartime security concerns or by racial prejudice.\n- Responses should assess U.S. relocation and confinement policies, Canadian removal and property confiscation, and Latin American measures such as deportations from Peru.\n- They should consider wartime context (Pearl Harbor, Pacific theatre) as well as pre-existing discrimination, political pressure, and economic rivalry.\n- Strong responses will explore comparisons across regions, highlighting similarities and differences in treatment.\n- Conclusions should weigh whether security threats or racial prejudice better explain the policies, acknowledging the complexity of motivations.\n\n**United States:**\n- Pearl Harbor created panic over sabotage and invasion.\n- Executive Order 9066 (Feb 1942) authorised exclusion zones → 120,000 Japanese Americans relocated.\n- Two-thirds were U.S. citizens (Nisei).\n- Roberts Commission alleged disloyalty; DeWitt declared “A Jap is a Jap.”\n- Munson Report (1941) and later intelligence found no evidence of sabotage.\n- Hawaii (with larger Japanese population) did not undergo mass removal → undermines security rationale.\n- Racial prejudice and economic jealousy (land and farms seized) central factors.\n\n**Canada:**\n- 22,000 Japanese Canadians removed from British Columbia in 1942.\n- Homes, boats, and businesses confiscated and sold.\n- Mackenzie King justified as precaution, but little evidence of disloyalty.\n- Military leaders often opposed mass removal; civilians pushed it.\n- Policies reflected both wartime hysteria and entrenched racism in British Columbia.\n- Japanese Canadians barred from returning to the West Coast until 1949.\n\n**Latin America:**\n- Under U.S. pressure, Peru deported ~1,800 Japanese Peruvians to U.S. camps.\n- Others faced surveillance, property confiscation, restrictions on movement.\n- Brazil and Mexico restricted Japanese schools, media, and movement.\n- Measures motivated partly by hemispheric security coordination, but also by local prejudice and economic rivalry.\n- Peru especially influenced by anti-Japanese resentment due to economic competition in trade and agriculture.\n\n**Racial Prejudice and Long-Term Context:**\n- Anti-Asian laws and sentiment predated WWII:\n    - U.S. Chinese Exclusion Act (1882), Immigration Act (1924).\n    - Canadian anti-Asian legislation, denial of suffrage, Alien Land Laws.\n- Economic jealousy → white farmers and fishermen benefited from dispossession of Japanese property.\n- Propaganda depicted Japanese as “unassimilable,” reinforcing prejudice.\n\n**Legal Dimensions:**\n- U.S. Supreme Court upheld relocation in Korematsu v. U.S. (1944), deferring to “military necessity.”\n- Evidence of security irrelevance suppressed, later exposed by Peter Irons.\n- Canadian government maintained restrictions until after 1945 despite end of war, showing prejudice outlasted security concerns.\n\n**Historiographical Perspectives:**\n- Roger Daniels (Prisoners Without Trial): Policies shaped by racial prejudice and economic opportunism, not evidence of threat.\n- Greg Robinson (By Order of the President): Roosevelt influenced by political pressures and racial stereotypes.\n- David M. Kennedy (Freedom from Fear): Acknowledges security fears but highlights hysteria and lack of evidence.\n- Peter Irons (Justice at War): Legal system legitimised racial prejudice under the guise of security.\n- Ann Gomer Sunahara (The Politics of Racism): Canadian policies reflected long-term racism in British Columbia rather than genuine wartime necessity.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe treatment of people of Japanese descent across the Americas during the Second World War has been one of the most controversial aspects of the wartime home front ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). While governments defended their actions as security measures, the scale and nature of relocation, dispossession, and surveillance suggest that racial prejudice played the more decisive role ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). This essay argues that while security fears following Pearl Harbor created the immediate context for such policies, deep-seated racial discrimination in the United States, Canada, and parts of Latin America better explains the extent and severity of the measures ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nIn the United States, the attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941 triggered widespread fears of sabotage and invasion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Military officials such as General John L. DeWitt insisted that Japanese Americans could not be trusted, claiming “a Jap is a Jap\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).Roosevelt's Executive Order 9066 (Feb. 1942) authorised the removal of around 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry from the West Coast to inland camps, two-thirds of whom were U.S. citizens ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). The justification was national security, reinforced by the Roberts Commission, which claimed—without evidence—that Japanese Americans had aided the Pearl Harbor attack ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, intelligence reports such as the Munson Report had already concluded Japanese Americans posed little threat. The lack of similar measures against German or Italian Americans strongly suggests that security concerns alone do not explain the U.S. response ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nCanada implemented similar policies, though on a smaller scale ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Following Pearl Harbor and Japan's entry into the war, Canadian authorities forcibly removed 22,000 Japanese Canadians from coastal British Columbia in 1942 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Families were relocated inland, their homes, businesses, and fishing boats confiscated and sold by the Custodian of Enemy Property ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Prime Minister Mackenzie King justified this as a necessary wartime precaution, claiming the West Coast was vulnerable to Japanese attack. Yet, as historians such as Ann Sunahara note, there was no evidence of espionage, and many military officials opposed mass removal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Much like the U.S., the Canadian government treated Japanese Canadians not on the basis of individual loyalty but ancestry, reflecting racial prejudice more than actual security concerns.\n\nIn Latin America, U.S. pressure influenced the treatment of Japanese communities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Peru, which had the largest Japanese population in South America, around 1,800 Japanese Peruvians were deported to the United States, where they were placed in camps ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Many others were subject to surveillance, restrictions on movement, and property confiscation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These measures were justified as contributions to hemispheric security, as the U.S. sought to prevent Axis influence in Latin America. However, scholars such as Daniel Masterson emphasise that local hostility toward the Japanese community—resentment of their economic success and longstanding racial discrimination—was a critical factor in Peru's compliance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Brazil and Mexico, restrictions were placed on Japanese-language schools, newspapers, and travel, again reflecting suspicion of disloyalty rooted more in prejudice than concrete threats.\n\nThe common thread across the Americas was the racialised perception of people of Japanese ancestry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Anti-Asian discrimination had deep roots in North America: U.S. immigration laws barred Asian immigrants (Immigration Act 1924), while in Canada, Japanese immigrants had long faced exclusion from land ownership and fishing licenses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$).Economic jealousy also played a role. On the U.S. West Coast, white farmers and businessmen benefited from the removal of Japanese Americans, acquiring land and businesses at cheap prices ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Similarly, in Canada, confiscated fishing boats were redistributed to white competitors ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Roger Daniels argues in Prisoners Without Trial that such economic opportunism, combined with racial prejudice, outweighed genuine security concerns ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nBy contrast, where security threats were arguably greatest, governments did not resort to mass relocation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Hawaii, which had a large Japanese population making up one-third of the territory's residents, experienced martial law and surveillance but no mass relocation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). U.S. authorities concluded that removing the entire Japanese population would be both impractical and economically disastrous ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This undermines the argument that security alone explains relocation on the mainland, as Hawaii faced the real threat of Japanese invasion yet avoided mass removal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe legal and political justifications for treatment further highlight the dominance of prejudice ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In the United States, the Supreme Court upheld relocation in Korematsu v. United States (1944), accepting the government's claim of “military necessity” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Peter Irons later exposed how evidence undermining the security rationale was suppressed, revealing how courts enabled racial prejudice under the cover of security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Canada, Mackenzie King admitted privately in 1944 that Japanese Canadians were not a real threat but still resisted their return to the West Coast until 1949, long after the war had ended ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These decisions show that prejudice and politics, not genuine military necessity, dictated policy.\n\nIn conclusion, the treatment of people of Japanese descent in the Americas during the Second World War was shaped by both security concerns and racial prejudice, but prejudice was the decisive factor ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$). In the United States and Canada, policies of mass removal, confiscation of property, and confinement were implemented without evidence of disloyalty, reflecting long histories of anti-Asian discrimination and economic opportunism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In Latin America, U.S. pressure combined with local prejudice to target Japanese communities, again without clear evidence of security threats ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). As Greg Robinson and Roger Daniels have shown, the internment policies across the hemisphere cannot be explained simply as wartime necessity—they reveal how racial prejudice was given legal and political cover under the guise of national security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0250","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0250\n\n\"Nuclear weapons were necessary for the US to win the war with Japan.\" Provide **three** arguments that support this perspective.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following arguments with its supporting evidence.\n- Nuclear weapons were necessary to avoid American casualties.\n    - American leaders were afraid that an invasion of the Japanese home islands would require similar US casualties.\n- Conventional strategic bombing was not enough to force Japan to surrender.\n    - Conventional bombing against Japanese urban centers had caused huge losses to the Japanese, but it was not enough to cause their surrender.\n- The use of atomic weapons would mean a faster end to the war.\n    - The Japanese emperor surrendered 6 days after the bombing of Nagasaki.\n- The use of atomic weapons would actually mean fewer overall casualties.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMany at the time, including the US high command and President Truman argued that atomic bombs had to be used to end the war as quickly as possible to save American lives ($\\checkmark$). The previous battle for Okinawa, the last stepping stone before reaching the Japanese home islands, had resulted in 65,000 U.S. casualties, or 35% of the American forces deployed. If those numbers held, it was expected an invasion of the Japanese home islands would result in around 250,000 American casualties  ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The death toll for the Japanese of the use of conventional weapons would not be much better ($\\checkmark$). After only 1 air raid in March of 1945 over Tokyo the death toll had been 80.000 Japanese civilians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The US government also feared that the Japanese would not surrender since they had not done so yet despite air raids that had destroyed 50% of Japanese urban areas ($\\checkmark$). In this context, the use of atomic weapons appeared to offer a solution that minimized further loss of life for the Americans while delivering a shock that would make the Japanese surrender. In fact, the Japanese Emperor called for surrender on 15 August, 9 days after the bombing of Hiroshima ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0252","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0252\n\nDefine the Lend-Lease Policy and describe **two** of its features.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**One** mark for definition similar to:\n- The Lend-Lease Act set up a scheme through which the US sent aid to the Allies during the Second World War.\n\nAny **two** of the following features:\n- Immediate payment for war material was not required as the US was “lending” the materials to the Allies.\n- The act that started the policy was passed in March of 1941, before the US officially entered WWII as a belligerent.\n- It was introduced as a result of a conversation Churchill had with Roosevelt warning him that Britain could not keep paying for US war materials and would therefore not be able to continue to keep its stance against the Nazis.\n- The US had Lend-Lease agreements with a number of Allied countries. For example, with the Soviet Union. It ended up supplying it with 18 000 airplanes; 1,500 tanks and more than 500 ships.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Lend-Lease policy was adopted by the US to be able to supply the Allies during WWII ($\\checkmark$). The first act that created the policy was passed before the US entered the war as a belligerent  ($\\checkmark$) but it allowed the British to be able to resist the Nazis without having to pay for American war materials ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0256","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0256\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the decision to use atomic weapons on Japan.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Harry Truman and the Bomb\" by Lee Lacy, Assistant Professor at the US Army Command and General Staff College, published August 5 , 2014.|\n\n[Source: Lacy, L. (2014, August 5). *Harry Truman and the Bomb*. The National Archives - Pieces of History. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://prologue.blogs.archives.gov/2014/08/05/harry-truman-and-the-bomb/. Public domain.]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Months of the War with Japan\" by MacEachin, D. J., former Deputy Director for Intelligence at the US Central Intelligence Agency, published 1998. |\n\n:box[…for all practical purposes the decision on whether to use the nuclear weapon against Japan had already been reached by the time the President arrived in Potsdam. On this point virtually all scholars who have studied the issue seem to concur, however much they may disagree on the motives for its use and whether its use was justified. …\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/Screenshot%202025-01-29%201.17..png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-01-29 1.17.\">Nor should the evidence be construed as indicating that concern over casualties was the only factor exerting critical force on the A-bomb decision. What the evidence does indicate is that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions, (2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam, and before the acquisition of intelligence showing much-larger-than-expected Japanese forces on Kyushu [the southernmost Japanese home island]. Nonetheless, it is certainly plausible that the buildup disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance. …\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/Screenshot%202025-01-29%20at%201.17..png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-01-29 at 1.17.\">For those favoring a bomb-and-blockade approach or even just a postponement of any invasion, Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands. On the other hand, if Japan's surrender did not take place until after the Soviets had been in the Pacific war for some length of time, and if there were no US forces on Japanese territory (because no US invasion had occurred), how could the United States and its Allies acquire the control over occupation that they were seeking? Unless the mere entry by the USSR somehow caused an immediate surrender on the unconditional terms being demanded by Washington, the Potsdam experience was likely to reinforce the tendency among at least some US officials to see any gain resulting from Soviet entry as also carrying a serious potential cost--the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: MacEachin, D. J. (1998, December). *The Final Months of the War With Japan.* CIA's Center for the Study of Intelligence. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/The-Final-Months-of-the-War-With-Japan-Monograph.pdf . Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources agree that one reason to use atomic weapons was to avoid American casualties. Source B: “an amphibious invasion of the Japanese mainland was unthinkable. Neither were the estimated millions of American lives that would be lost if mainland Japan was invaded.”  “Source C: “the evidence does indicate that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions”.\n\nFor contrast, any **two to four** of the following differences:\n- While Source B stresses the unity of the Allies, Source C portrays one of the Allies, the USSR, as a rival. Source B: “He hoped the ultimatum would coincide with a successful test of the atomic bomb to demonstrate the resolve of the Allies to Japan” Source C: “minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage”\n- The sources disagree on who made the decision to use the atomic bomb. Source B states “No doubt the weight of the world was on Truman’s shoulders, and the final decision was not easy. Finally, he concluded it was his decision, alone” while Source C speaks of “US leadership” generally.\n- Besides avoiding American casualties, which is mentioned in both sources as a reason for the use of atomic weapons, Source C also identifies two other reasons “(2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam.”\n- Source B seems to imply that Truman had no other choice but to use atomic weapons saying, “On July 28, Japan announced its intention to continue the war. There was no alternative—Truman had to take action to end the war” while Source C presents another possibility: “Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands.\"\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources agree that one reason to use nuclear weapons was to end the war quickly and avoid further American casualties ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B explains that “an amphibious invasion of the Japanese mainland was unthinkable. Neither were the estimated millions of American lives that would be lost if mainland Japan was invaded” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) and Source C gives a similar motivation when it says: “the evidence does indicate that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also contain many contrasts regarding the decision to drop the atomic bombs on Japan. While Source B stresses Truman´s desire to show the unity of the Allies against Japan by saying that “He hoped the ultimatum would coincide with a successful test of the atomic bomb to demonstrate the resolve of the Allies to Japan,” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) Source C identifies the USSR as a rival of the US saying that the decision had to do with the desire to reduce “the USSR's postwar leverage” and there is not mention of a joint decision regarding the use of the bomb ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference }$$\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThey also disagree on who made the decision ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). While Source B states, “No doubt the weight of the world was on Truman's shoulders, and the final decision was not easy. Finally, he concluded it was his decision, alone.” In contrast, Source C speaks of “US leadership” as a group of many who participated in the decision-making ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nA final difference between the sources is that Source B only talks about a desire to avoid casualties as the motivation behind the use of the atomic bombs, while Source C identifies many other motivations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) such as,  “(2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). This helps explain another difference, the fact that Source B says that Truman had “no alternative,” while Source C explores the possibility of obtaining the surrender of Japan with the entry of the USSR to the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0257","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0257\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the reasons for the use of atomic weapons on Japan?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Months of the War with Japan\" by MacEachin, D. J., former Deputy Director for Intelligence at the US Central Intelligence Agency, published 1998. |\n\n:box[…for all practical purposes the decision on whether to use the nuclear weapon against Japan had already been reached by the time the President arrived in Potsdam. On this point virtually all scholars who have studied the issue seem to concur, however much they may disagree on the motives for its use and whether its use was justified. …\n\\\n\\\nNor should the evidence be construed as indicating that concern over casualties was the only factor exerting critical force on the A-bomb decision. What the evidence does indicate is that the view of the bomb as a potential way to end the war quickly--in the hope of (1) avoiding the need for an invasion with resulting casualties that by any standard would be of intimidating proportions, (2) minimizing the USSR's postwar leverage, and (3) not having to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender--was the driving force in the minds of the US leadership team before Potsdam, and before the acquisition of intelligence showing much-larger-than-expected Japanese forces on Kyushu [the southernmost Japanese home island]. Nonetheless, it is certainly plausible that the buildup disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance. …\n\\\n\\\nFor those favoring a bomb-and-blockade approach or even just a postponement of any invasion, Soviet entry could have been cited as an additional reason why surrender could be obtained without invading the main Japanese islands. On the other hand, if Japan's surrender did not take place until after the Soviets had been in the Pacific war for some length of time, and if there were no US forces on Japanese territory (because no US invasion had occurred), how could the United States and its Allies acquire the control over occupation that they were seeking? Unless the mere entry by the USSR somehow caused an immediate surrender on the unconditional terms being demanded by Washington, the Potsdam experience was likely to reinforce the tendency among at least some US officials to see any gain resulting from Soviet entry as also carrying a serious potential cost--the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n \\\n]\n\n[Source: MacEachin, D. J. (1998, December). *The Final Months of the War With Japan.* CIA's Center for the Study of Intelligence. Retrieved December 9, 2024 from https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/The-Final-Months-of-the-War-With-Japan-Monograph.pdf . Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To avoid casualties\n- To end the war quickly\n- To minimize the USSR's postwar leverage: they wanted to avoid the possible emergence of a Far Eastern version of the Soviet hegemony that was beginning to be imposed on Eastern Europe.\n- To not have to confront debate over concessions on the terms of unconditional surrender\n- Knowledge of military buildup in Kyushu disclosed by early August reinforced the belief that the decision to use the bomb was the path of least resistance.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom the source, we can learn that the atomic weapons were used on Japan because the United States wanted to end the war as quickly as possible ($\\checkmark$). Also, they wanted to avoid American casualties ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, there was the worry that if the USSR participated in the defeat of Japan, it would impose its hegemony over the Far East just like it had done in Eastern Europe ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0292","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0292\n\nDescribe **three** economic impacts of WWII on the United States.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - WWII effectively ended the Great Depression in the United States. While the unemployment rate was 15$\\%$ in 1940, it fell to 2$\\%$ in 1945. \n\n - The Price Administration Office limits the price of 90$\\%$ of products and this results in an average inflation of only 3.5$\\%$\n\n - A system of strict rationing was introduced in 1942 to keep up war production and supply of the Allies` troops\n\n - The US spent \\$341 billion, including $50 billion for lend-lease supplies that went to its allies. \n\n - The US economy emerged from the war as the dominant economy of the world. It held two-thirds of the world's gold supply and produced over 60$\\%$ of the world's industrial output. \n\n - America's economic influence during the war and immediately after it allowed the US to make its allies accept tariff and monetary agreements that opened much of the world, especially the colonial empires of Europe, to American trade and investment.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWWII had a positive impact on the US economy. The necessities of war put an end to the decade-long Great Depression. Evidence of this is the fact that the unemployment rate was 15$\\%$ in 1940, and it fell to $2\\%$ in 1945 ($\\checkmark$). The US also emerged from the war as the dominant economy of the world, producing about 60$\\%$ of the world's industrial output by 1945 ($\\checkmark$). This economic might and the fact that it had assisted its allies economically as well as militarily both during and after the war gave US leaders the possibility of influencing US aid recipients to accept tariff and monetary agreements that opened much of the world, especially the colonial empires of Europe, to American trade and investment ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0293","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0293\n\nExplain the impact of WWII on minorities in the United States.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - Around 1.5 million African Americans moved in search of the jobs that opened up in the war industries. In their new homes, they had substandard housing, and experienced discrimination from neighbouring communities. This occasionally led to race riots such as the one that occurred in Detroit in 1943\n\n - Roosevelt, by decree, prohibited war industries from discriminating when hiring employees. This was the first time race discrimination was outlawed. Thanks to this, while African Americans suffered around 50$\\%$ unemployment in 1940, and usually found work only in low-level positions, by 1945 African Americans made up 8$\\%$ of all war production workers.\n\n - The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) was an organization that pressed for racial equality. It successfully pressured the US government to allow African American men to participate in combat, although in segregated units. \n\n - NAACP membership went from 50,000 members in 1940 to 450,000 in 1946. Many of the new members were returning servicemen determined to fight for their civil rights. \n\n - In 1948, Truman prohibited segregation in the Armed Forces.\n\n - 1942-Executive order to remove people of Japanese descent from the West coast. About 120,000 were sent to internment camps even though ⅔ were American citizens. They were ordered to dispose of their property in very little time=lost their jobs and businesses\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThere was an improvement in the access to equal rights for some minorities in the United States. In great part, this was due to the need to increase their contribution to the war effort. President Roosevelt issued a decree prohibiting war industries from discrimination based on race when hiring employees. This is the first time race discrimination was outlawed in the US. Thanks to this, by 1945, African Americans made up 8$\\%$ of all war production workers which helped them improve their economic situation ($\\checkmark$). Other roles were also opened up for African Americans, most notably in the Armed Forces. The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) played a key role in successfully advocating to convince the government to allow African American men to participate in combat, even if they did so in segregated units ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOther minorities, however, were greatly disadvantaged by the war situation. The clearest example is that of Japanese Americans who lived on the West Coast who, due to an executive order of 1942, were sent to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$internment camps” ($\\checkmark$).  Approximately 120,000 persons, two-thirds of whom were American citizens, were sent to internment camps having to leave their jobs and business, losing their freedom until 1945 and their economic stability for many more years ($\\checkmark$). \n\n$\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"HI0295","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0295\n\nEvaluate the impact of WWII on the role of women in the United States.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. \n\nPositive results: \n\n - Creation of Women's Army Corps (WACs), US Naval Women's Reserve (WAVES), Women's Air Force Service Pilots (WASPs) and Women's Coast Guard Reserve. In total, 200,000 serve in war,\n\n - 6 million enter the workforce, 57$\\%$ more than before WWII\n\n - The absence of husbands made women heads of the family.\n\nNegative results: \n\n - Female stereotypes are reinforced in the Armed Forces despite women's new roles. For example, in 1944 a contest was organized under the title:  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Who is the prettiest in the Armed Forces?”\n\n - They have no way to take care of their children if there are no grandparents/aunts: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$8-hour orphans” the media calls them, criticizing the mothers\n\n - With demobilisation and the smaller demand for goods that characterised the post war years, most women lost the jobs they had done during the war: 2.5 million women stopped working outside the home. \n\n - Women still earned an average of 60$\\%$ less than men.\n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall results of WWII were short lived for the role of women in opening up new possibilities in the economic sphere.  \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative.\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWWII had a considerable impact on the role of women in the United States. On the positive side, 200,000 women were able to join the Armed Forces participating in almost all tasks except combat ($\\checkmark$). Women were even part of the Air Force though the Women's Air Force Service Pilots (WASPs)  that had the job of transporting material and men. The war also made it possible for many women to join the workforce now that more than 10 million men were overseas fighting ($\\checkmark$). In 1945 there were  57$\\%$ more women working outside the home than before WWII.\n\nHowever, most of these advances were lost once the war ended. Female sections of the Armed Forces were no longer needed and in fact, even during the war itself female stereotypes were reinforced despite the new positions allowed to women ($\\checkmark$). For example, in 1944 a beauty contest was organized under the title  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Who is the prettiest in the Armed Forces?” showing that women were still valued for their looks and not for their contribution to the war effort. The same can be said of their role in the job market. After 1945 with demobilisation and the smaller demand for goods that characterised the post war years, most women lost the jobs they had done during the war: 2.5 million women stopped working outside the home ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIn conclusion, WWII allowed more women to enter the job market and to participate in the Armed Forces than ever before. However, once the war ended many doors were shut to women. The Armed Forces became again a place for men and many women lost the jobs they had since it was necessary to accommodate the returning soldiers. The changes to the role of women were therefore short lived.  What cannot be denied is that millions of women were not limited to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$home life” for at least some years and the experiences they had changed the lives of many ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0300","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0300\n\nExamine the extent to which the Second World War improved the position of racial and ethnic minorities in the Americas.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance for responses\n- Candidates should assess the degree to which the war led to improvements (social, economic, political, or cultural) for racial and ethnic minorities across the Americas, and balance this against evidence of continuity or worsening discrimination.\n- Improvements might include new employment opportunities, migration to cities, military service and recognition, political mobilisation, and the beginnings of civil rights activism.\n- Limitations might include: persistent segregation, violence and discrimination, treatment of Japanese Americans and Japanese Canadians, and limited long-term change.\n- Candidates should choose more than one country (United States, Canada, Brazil, Mexico, etc.), and must evaluate both positive changes and enduring inequalities.\n- Strong responses will include comparative analysis across regions.\n- A substantiated conclusion should weigh whether the war represented a turning point or more of a temporary shift.\n\nIndicative Content\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{United States}}$\n\nAfrican Americans:\n- Over 1 million served in segregated units (e.g., Tuskegee Airmen). Military service fostered pride but highlighted inequality.\n- “Double V Campaign” called for victory abroad and at home against racism.\n- Migration from rural South to urban centres for war industries (Second Great Migration).\n- FEPC (Fair Employment Practices Committee, 1941) banned discrimination in defense industries after A. Philip Randolph's threatened march.\n- Gains were limited: segregation persisted, lynching continued, race riots (Detroit 1943).\n\nJapanese Americans:\n- 120,000 forcibly relocated under Executive Order 9066 (U.S. West Coast).\n- Loss of property, harsh conditions in camps → dramatic worsening of position.\n- Military service (442nd Regimental Combat Team) showed loyalty and won recognition, but discrimination remained.\n\nMexican Americans:\n- Bracero Program (1942): provided farm labourers to the U.S. → economic opportunities, but exploitative conditions.\n- Zoot Suit Riots (1943): U.S. servicemen attacked Mexican American youth in Los Angeles, highlighting prejudice.\n- Mixed legacy: some integration into the war economy, but discrimination persisted.\n\nNative Americans:\n- 25,000 served in the armed forces; Navajo Code Talkers became famous.\n- Many migrated to cities for war industries.\n- Limited long-term gains; faced difficulty readjusting postwar.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Canada}}$\n\n- About 3,000 Japanese Canadians were removed from coastal British Columbia, their property confiscated, and they were not allowed to return until 1949. → position worsened.\n- Indigenous peoples enlisted and gained skills, but rights were not significantly improved postwar; some lost their Indian status.\n- Black Canadians served in the forces and gained some visibility, but faced employment discrimination at home.\n\nLatin America\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Brazil}}$:\n- Afro-Brazilians served in the Força Expedicionária Brasileira (FEB) in Italy, challenging racial stereotypes.\n- Urban migration to wartime industries created limited opportunities.\n- Still faced structural racism and limited political inclusion.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Mexico}}$:\n- The Bracero Program (1942-1964) created economic links, but many workers were exploited.\n- Indigenous and rural Mexicans gained some wartime industrial employment, but inequalities persisted.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Other Latin American countries}}$:\n- Some restrictions were placed on Japanese immigrants in Peru, Brazil, and Mexico; deportations from Peru to U.S. camps → worsened the position of Japanese Latin Americans.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Second World War significantly affected racial and ethnic minorities across the Americas, but impacts varied widely ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). For some, such as African Americans and Mexican Americans in the United States, the war opened new opportunities in employment, migration, and military service. Others, such as Japanese Americans and Japanese Canadians, saw their position dramatically worsen through forced relocation and property loss. In Latin America, opportunities were limited, with Afro-Brazilians and Indigenous peoples experiencing only modest changes. This essay will argue that while the war created short-term economic and political openings for some minorities, overall progress was uneven and often overshadowed by persistent discrimination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nIn the United States, African Americans experienced both crucial gains and continued systemic discrimination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). More than one million African Americans served in the armed forces, often in segregated units. The Tuskegee Airmen, an all-Black fighter group, became a symbol of African American skill and patriotism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). On the home front, the “Double V” campaign called for victory against fascism abroad and racism at home, reflecting growing political awareness ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The war economy also facilitated the migration of hundreds of thousands of African Americans from the rural South to urban centres in the North and West, sometimes called the Second Great Migration ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Employment in war industries offered higher wages and opportunities previously unavailable. In 1941, after A. Philip Randolph threatened a March on Washington, Roosevelt issued Executive Order 8802, banning discrimination in defense industries and creating the Fair Employment Practices Committee (FEPC)($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). While these were genuine improvements, segregation persisted in the military and civilian life, while race riots in Detroit in 1943 and continued lynching revealed deep-seated racism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Thus, while the war helped lay the groundwork for the postwar civil rights movement, it did not transform African Americans' position in the short term.\n\nOther U.S. minorities faced mixed experiences ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Japanese Americans endured some of the harshest treatment. After Pearl Harbor, 120,000 were forcibly removed from the West Coast to inland camps under Executive Order 9066 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Two-thirds were U.S. citizens, and most lost homes, farms, and businesses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Although some Japanese Americans served heroically in the military, notably the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, their loyalty did not prevent widespread suspicion and discrimination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). For Mexican Americans, the war years brought new opportunities and new challenges($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Bracero Program, launched in 1942, brought hundreds of thousands of Mexican workers into U.S. agriculture ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). While this provided employment, conditions were often exploitative, with poor wages and little legal protection ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). At the same time, Mexican Americans participated in the armed forces and found new industrial employment in U.S. cities. However, events like the Zoot Suit Riots of 1943 in Los Angeles, when U.S. servicemen attacked Mexican American youth, revealed the persistence of discrimination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Native Americans also contributed: around 25,000 served in the armed forces, including the famous Navajo Code Talkers, while many others migrated to cities for wartime employment ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Yet after the war, many Native veterans returned to reservations facing continued poverty and marginalisation, showing limited long-term improvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn Canada, the experience of minorities similarly showed both continuity and regression ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The most dramatic case was the treatment of Japanese Canadians. In 1942, 22,000 were forcibly removed from the coast of British Columbia, their homes and fishing boats confiscated and sold by the Custodian of Enemy Property ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Families were relocated inland, and many were not permitted to return to the coast until 1949, long after the war had ended ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This represented a clear deterioration in their social and economic position. Indigenous Canadians contributed significantly to the armed forces and gained new skills and experiences, but returned to communities where systemic discrimination remained in place ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Black Canadians served in the military and gained some visibility, yet faced ongoing barriers to equal employment and housing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Thus, for Canada, the war years offered few lasting improvements for minorities, and in some cases, such as Japanese Canadians, conditions worsened ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn Latin America, the impact of the war on minorities was more indirect, yet still notable ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). In Brazil, Afro-Brazilians enlisted in the Força Expedicionária Brasileira (FEB) and fought in Italy, challenging long-held stereotypes about their role in society ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Some also benefited from urban migration as war industries expanded, particularly in Rio de Janeiro and São Paulo ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, these opportunities did not significantly alter Brazil's entrenched racial hierarchy, and social inequality remained stark. In Mexico, the war spurred industrial growth and urbanisation, creating opportunities for Indigenous and rural workers to move into cities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Yet, discrimination persisted, and most rural communities saw little change in living standards. In Peru, Japanese Peruvians faced hostility; under U.S. pressure, around 1,800 were deported to camps in the United States, where they were held for the duration of the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Others had property confiscated or were subject to surveillance and restrictions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). This reflected continuity of prejudice rather than improvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nOverall, the impact of the Second World War on racial and ethnic minorities in the Americas was mixed, with more continuity than change ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). For African Americans and Mexican Americans in the United States, the war created new employment opportunities, fostered migration, and expanded political activism, though discrimination and segregation persisted ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Japanese Americans and Japanese Canadians saw their position deteriorate dramatically through forced relocation and property loss, while in Latin America, Afro-Brazilians, Indigenous peoples, and Japanese communities experienced only limited or negative change ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In conclusion, the war produced some temporary and uneven improvements, particularly in economic and military participation, but it did not fundamentally alter social status; rather, it exposed inequalities and set the stage for postwar struggles for rights and recognition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0322","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0322\n\nEvaluate the impact of WWII on the Mexican economy.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. To respond to an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate” command term, it is valid to say that the whole goal was not reached as something negative. \n\nPositive results:\n\n - US loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million to the Mexican government\n\n - US guaranteed to purchase Mexican silver \n\n - The government encouraged the population to increase its productivity to assist the Allied cause. This led to an 8$\\%$ increase in GDP per year between 1940 and 1945, \n\n - The needs of the war and the war itself encouraged industrialization. Mexico replaced European and American production in Central America and the West Indies. For example, textile exports increased from less than 1$\\%$ of Mexico's total exports in 1939 to 32$\\%$ in 1945-46.\n\n - US assistance led to improved infrastructure (highways and ports) which in turn helped economic development. For example, in December 1942 the US sent a Technical Mission to help rehabilitate Mexico's railway system. \n\n - Thanks to the Bracero Program, which was revived in 1942, Mexico received remittances from the Mexican citizens that were given short term work permits to the US. For example, in 1944, 62,200 Mexican citizens were allowed to work in the US thanks to this program. As part of their contracts, braceros agreed to have ten percent of their wages withheld and placed in a fund controlled by the Mexican government.\n\nNegative results:\n\n - Inflation increased an average 18.5$\\%$ during each of the war years. \n\n - Large government contracts gave the opportunity for increased corruption\n\n - Mexico became dependent on the US market. By 1945 sent 90$\\%$ of its exports to the United States.\n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall economic effects of WWII were positive for Mexico.\n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the negative ones as well. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWorld War II presented significant economic benefits to Mexico. First, the US was willing to assist Mexico's economic development so it could produce the necessary war materials for the war effort. In that light, the US granted the Mexican government a loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million ($\\checkmark$). The higher demand the war generated as well as the government's mobilization of the economic capabilities to support the war led to a significant growth in Mexico's economy. Between 1940 and 1945 it had a sustained 8% average annual GDP growth ($\\checkmark$). A great part of this growth had to do with the industrial sector. As US and European consumer goods production was virtually halted, Mexico stepped in to cover that demand in the markets of Central America and the West Indies. This is evident in the fact that the textile industry was now a key component of Mexico's exports. Whereas in 1939 textile goods were only 1% of total exports, in 1945-1946 they were 32% of the total ($\\checkmark$). \n\nHowever, the war also brought economic challenges. Like in most countries, war leads to inflation. This was also the case in Mexico, where the war years had an average inflation rate of 18.5$\\%$ ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, Mexico became heavily dependent on the U.S. market, with 90% of its exports directed to the United States by 1945 ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, its economic prosperity became tied to the United States.\n\nDespite higher inflation and dependence, the overall economic impact of WWII on Mexico's economy was positive. The important growth in its GDP and the development of its industries outweigh the negative consequences ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0323","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0323\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Argentina's policy of neutrality during most of Second World War had the nation's best interests in mind.” Provide **three** arguments that support this perspective.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with its supporting evidence.  \n\nPossible arguments: \n\n - Argentina had a large population of Italians, plus their descendants. War against the Axis would mean they would have to choose what nation to be loyal to. \n\n     - Given the large population of ethnic Italians, neutrality helped avoid domestic conflict.   \n\n - There was division in the Armed Forces about what position to take. To avoid internal struggles, neutrality was the best option. \n\n     - The Navy wanted to join the Allies while the Army was divided. 10$\\%$ of the Army was pro-Nazi, and 20$\\%$ was pro-Allies. \n\n - Argentina had a long tradition of neutrality in armed conflicts. \n\n     - It had been neutral in First World War and this position had benefited the country's economy by allowing them to maintain economic relations with countries on both sides of the conflict.\n\n - It was easier to assist Argentina`s main trading partner, the UK by being neutral. \n\n     - In 1939, Argentina decided to sell its goods to the United Kingdom on credit at 0% interest for as long as the conflict lasted. Neutrality meant Argentina`s merchant ships would not be military targets. Argentina supplied around 40$\\%$ of the UK's mean consumption during the war.\n\n - Argentina did not want to depend on the United States because the two economies were competitive. The government feared the necessities of war would push Argentina to become dependent on the US. \n\n     - Since 1927, the US had prohibited meat imports from Argentina, one of the country's main export commodities. \n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor most of World War II, Argentina made the controversial decision to remain neutral. For many historians and contemporaries, this was a terrible decision, but for others, it was what suited the nation's best interests. The government wanted to prevent internal divisions and, with that in mind, it had to consider the makeup of the population with mostly immigrant origins and the characteristics of the Armed Forces ($\\checkmark$). With a significant portion of its population—now estimated between 60$\\%$ and 70$\\%$—having Italian ancestry, declaring war against the Axis would have forced many citizens to choose a side in the war ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Argentina accepted dual citizenship, so many in its population were both Italian and Argentinian citizens. Furthermore, divisions within the Armed Forces made neutrality sound policy. While the Navy favored joining the Allies, the Army was split, with 10% supporting the Nazis and 20$\\%$ backing the Allies ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nEconomically, neutrality was also perceived as something positive. In a way, Argentina was a provider of the Allies since the war began. In 1939, the Argentine government decided to sell goods on credit at 0$\\%$ interest to the United Kingdom in light of the difficult situation it was going through. This benefit was only allowed to the UK because it was Argentina's leading trading partner. Neutrality allowed merchant ships with the Argentinian flag to sail the seas without being legal targets of German submarines ($\\checkmark$). Thanks to this, Argentina provided around 40$\\%$ of the UK's meat consumption during the war  ($\\checkmark$ evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0325","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0325\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of World War II on the United States labor movement.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Roosevelt supported trade unions during the Great Depression and, in turn, trade unions did not organize strikes that might hamper the war effort. \n\n - In 1943, Congress passed the Smith-Connally Act (or War Labor Disputes Act) to ensure continued wartime production. It allowed the president to seize productions of wartime industries. Roosevelt would use it to intervene in labor disputes and ensure the production of goods to support the war effort\n\n - With the end of the war and the return of 10 million demobilised soldiers, the national government passed the Taft-Hartley Act. This measure prohibited strikes unless they were first authorized by the government and reserves, clearly limiting the power of trade unions. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nRoosevelt supported the work of trade unions through most of the Great Depression and unions responded in support by pledging not to strike during the war to assist in the final victory ($\\checkmark$). However, in light of a few exceptions, Congress passed the Smith-Connally Act (War Labor Disputes Act) in 1943, granting the president the authority to seize control of industries critical to the war ($\\checkmark$). Roosevelt utilized this power to intervene in labor disputes and maintain the steady production of goods necessary to support the war."},{"ref":"HI0326","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0326\n\nDescribe **three** diplomatic impacts of World War II on Canada.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Relations between the United States and Canada became closer. The Permanent Joint Board on Defense was created in 1940 to formulate a joint strategy for the military defence of North America. \n\n - While it had traditionally been represented by British diplomats (as Canada was part of the Commonwealth) during the war, Canada established formal, independent relations with Brazil, Argentina, and Chile.\n\n - The people of Canada made a tremendous contribution to the war effort which raised Canada's international profile. Almost 10% of the Canadian population had served in the Armed Forces during the war. \n\n - Canada made a significant contribution to the war effort. For example, more than half of its production of war material went to the UK. Because of this contribution, Canada was given a place as one of the founding members of the United Nations after the war ended.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nRelations between the United States and Canada grew closer during World War II. In 1940, a Permanent Joint Board on Defense was set up so that both countries could coordinate their military strategy for North America ($\\checkmark$). The significant contributions of the Canadian people to the war effort, such as the fact that almost 10$\\%$ of the whole population served in the Armed Forces,  improved the country's international profile. Many now saw it as second only to the great superpowers and thus a key global player. Evidence of this is the fact that it would become a founding member of the United Nations ($\\checkmark$). This increased international role of Canada translated itself into more diplomatic representation. Before the war, it had been mainly represented by British diplomats as it was part of the Commonwealth. However, during the war, it established independent diplomatic missions in Brazil, Argentina, and Chile ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0342","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0342\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Nuclear weapons were NOT necessary for the US to win the war with Japan.\" Provide **three** arguments that support this perspective.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following possible arguments with supporting evidence:\n- The US was keen on showing the USSR its superior armament as the alliance created to fight in WWII was turning into the rivalry that would characterize the Cold War\n    - At Potsdam there were already many disagreements with Stalin regarding the new world order. Truman did not want Soviet entry into the war against Japan since that would allow the Soviet Union to increase its its influence in East Asia just like it had done in Eastern Europe\n- The Japanese were willing to negotiate the peace as long as their Emperor was not made to pay the price for Japan's involvement in WWII.\n    - The US demanded an “unconditional surrender” and the Japanese feared this would mean an end to their constitutional monarchy. In fact, after the war, the US allowed the Emperor Hirohito to remain as sovereign, however, it had never clarified this when asking for peace.\n- The USSR had promised to join the war against Japan at the Yalta Conference and that was enough to bring the Japanese to their knees.\n- Japan was virtually defeated by August of 1945. Military advisers told Truman that Japan that the US naval blockade of the home islands meant that its war effort would wane quickly. Thanks to the blockade and the continued conventional bombing, Japan was expected to surrender soon.\n    - The Japanese had sent Stalin a telegram in July 1945 asking for peace.\n- What the US really wanted was vengeance for the attack on Pearl Harbour and for the horrible treatment of Japanese prisoners of war.\n    - Following the liberation of the Allied prisoner-of-war camps in 1944 and 1945, accounts of horrific conditions and barbaric treatment by the Japanese guards fed a desire for vengeance that has started in 1941\n- Making the bomb had cost thousands of hours of research and development as well as billions of dollars. The government had to show the resources had been well spent\n    - The Manhattan Project cost approximately \\$2 billion by 1945 (or over \\$30 billion in 2023 USD)\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSince the dropping of the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, there has been considerable debate on whether these horrific events were really necessary for the US to win the war with Japan. Many arguments support the notion that it was actually not necessary. In fact, many of Truman's military advisers, like General Eisenhower, told him that nuclear weapons were not needed since Japan's surrender was imminent and that it could not hold out much longer due to the US`s blockade of the Japanese home islands ($\\checkmark$). This blockade prevented the empire's soldiers from returning home and accessing the resources necessary to keep up the war effort. In fact, in July of 1945, Japan had already requested the USSR to act as a mediator for peace negotiations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe main issue that prevented a Japanese surrender was the future of Emperor Hirohito, the political and religious leader of the nation. If the US had guaranteed his position, as it did once the war was over, the Japanese government might have been willing to surrender sooner ($\\checkmark$). However, the US insisted on demanding an “unconditional surrender” even in the Potsdam Declaration of July 1945, and this scared the Japanese, who saw their emperor as a living god ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nMany historians point to the fact that the US wanted to use its atomic weapons not so much to end the war but to prove its superiority to the USSR since the end of WWII meant the conclusion of the alliance against the Axis and the start of the rivalry that would characterize the Cold War ($\\checkmark$).  At the Potsdam Conference, disagreements between Truman and Stalin over the post-war world order were already evident. Truman wanted to end the war as quickly as possible so that the Soviets would not have to set foot on Japan, allowing them to extend Soviet influence in East Asia, much like it had in Eastern Europe ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0343","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0343\n\nDescribe the Manhattan Project.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- President Roosevelt assembled the greatest minds in physics and chemistry in 1942 so they would discover how to initiate the chain reaction of nuclear fission and harness it into a weapon,\n- The scientific efforts were directed by Dr Robert Oppenheimer and included scientists from around the world.\nIt employed over 125,000 people and cost about 2 billion dollars.\n- By the summer of 1945 the project test an atomic weapon in New Mexico.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1942, President Roosevelt brought together the greatest minds in physics and chemistry to discover how to initiate a nuclear fission chain reaction and harness it as a weapon ($\\checkmark$). This was a secret endeavor known as the Manhattan Project. Over the years, it employed over 125,000 people and cost approximately 2 billion dollars ($\\checkmark$). By the summer of 1945, the project had successfully tested an atomic weapon in New Mexico, paving the way for the use of atomic bombs against Japan ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0347","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0347\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the impact of the atomic bomb on Nagasaki?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Nagasaki, Japan after the atomic bomb detonation. Photograph taken March 17, 1948. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0347%28A%29.png\" alt=\"HI0347(A)\">\n\n[Source: Unknown photographer. (1948, March 17). *Nagasaki, Japan after the atomic bomb detonation*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 7, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/39147850. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The whole city was destroyed\n\n - The impact of the destruction was felt years after the end of the war\n\n - Civilian buildings were also destroyed \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source A we can learn that the atomic bomb had a long-lasting impact on Nagasaki as this photograph was taken in 1948 and the city is not rebuilt ($\\checkmark$). It is evident from the tori (temple entrance) that even religious buildings were hit, not only military targets ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0348","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0348\n\nWhat, according to Source C, were the reasons for using the atomic bombs on Japan?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Letter from President Harry S. Truman to Samuel Cavert (General Secretary of the Federal Council of the Churches of Christ in America) in response to his call for an end to the use of atomic weapons, August 11, 1945.\n\n[Source: Truman, H. S. (1945, August 11). *Correspondence Between Samuel Cavert and President Harry S. Truman*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 8, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/296762538. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following:\n\n - As revenge for the attack on Pearl Harbour and for Japan's treatment of American prisoners of war\n\n - It is the only way the Japanese will understand they need to surrender\n\n - The Japanese are beasts\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source C, we can learn that, according to Truman, the US had to use atomic bombs against Japan since it was the only thing they would understand ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the President reminds Mr. Cavert of Japan's attack on Pearl Harbour and its treatment of American prisoners of war, showing his desire to avenge these horrors ($\\checkmark$). Truman also refers to the Japanese as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$beasts,” using it as a justification to treat them as such ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0349","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0349\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for a historian studying the reasons for the United States’ choice to use atomic weapons on Japan.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Letter from President Harry S. Truman to Samuel Cavert (General Secretary of the Federal Council of the Churches of Christ in America) in response to his call for an end to the use of atomic weapons, August 11, 1945.\n\n[Source: Truman, H. S. (1945, August 11). *Correspondence Between Samuel Cavert and President Harry S. Truman*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 8, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/296762538. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source contains insight as it was made on August 11, 1945, only a few days after the bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Moreover, it was made by the president of the United States, the man who made the final decision to use the bombs (origin).\n\n - The source mentions some of the reasons why the bombs were dropped, reminding Mr. Cavert of Japan's previous actions to explain that the Japanese only understand violence (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source lacks hindsight. It was made before Japan`s actual surrender, so the president could not know if the bombs would have their intended purpose. The source contains only Truman's point of view (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to justify the decision to use the atomic bombs. It is made in response to a letter criticizing his actions (purpose).\n\n - The source leaves out many other reasons for the use of atomic weapons. It also exaggerates, speaking of the Japanese as beasts and saying, “The only language they seem to understand is the one we have been using to bombard them.” The source also contradicts what Truman himself said in source B (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C`s origin is valuable since it provides insight as it was created on August 11, 1945, just days after the atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Therefore, Truman has very fresh in his mind the reasons why he says he had to use atomic weapons. Truman was also the president of the United States at the time and thus the person with the final authority over the use of the bombs ($\\checkmark$ VO). The content of the source is valuable since he mentions some of the reasons behind the bombings, specifically justifying them by referring to Japan's previous actions and suggesting that the Japanese only understand violence ($\\checkmark$ VC). \n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the reasons for US use of atomic weapons on Japan. First of all, due to its origin, it lacks hindsight as it was produced before Japan's official surrender, meaning the president could not yet know whether the bombings would in fact be $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$understood” by the Japanese ($\\checkmark$ LO). Also, it only presents Truman's point of view as part of a reply to a previous letter by Ms. Cavert, so the information it gives is limited to that conversation. That is why it leaves out many other reasons for the use of atomic weapons, like the fact that he wanted to save American lives. This is a limitation of the content ($\\checkmark$ LC).  The purpose is also limited since he writes in response to a letter criticizing his decision. Therefore, his purpose is to justify his actions, not to explain all his real motivations ($\\checkmark$ LP)."},{"ref":"HI0350","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0350\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about Truman's justification for the use of atomic weapons on Japan.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Letter from President Harry S. Truman to Senator Richard Russell in response to his call for the further use of atomic weapons to destroy Japan, August 9, 1945.\n\n[Source: Truman, H. S. (1945, August 9). *Correspondence Between President Harry S. Truman and Senator Richard Russell*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 8, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/40020053. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Letter from President Harry S. Truman to Samuel Cavert (General Secretary of the Federal Council of the Churches of Christ in America) in response to his call for an end to the use of atomic weapons, August 11, 1945.\n\n[Source: Truman, H. S. (1945, August 11). *Correspondence Between Samuel Cavert and President Harry S. Truman*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 8, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/296762538. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n\nFor comparison, any two to four of the following similarities: \n\n - They both refer to the Japanese as beasts\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$because they are beasts” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you have to deal with a beast” \n\n - Both refer to Japan`s brutality in war \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Japan is a terribly cruel and uncivilized nation in warfare” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unwarranted attack by the Japanese on Pearl Harbor and their murder of our prisoners of war”\n\nFor contrast, any two to three of the following differences:\n\n - The sources differ on whether the US should behave as brutally as Japan\n\n     - In Source B Truman says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ I can't bring myself to believe that, because they are beasts, we should ourselves act in the same manner.” But in Source C he says the opposite: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you have to deal with a beast you have to treat him as a beast”\n\n - The sources differ on the reasons the Japanese will surrender. \n\n     - Source B focuses on the participation of the USSR $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It is my opinion that after the Russians enter into the war the Japanese will very shortly fold up,” while Source C focuses on the use of bombs by the US: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The only language they seem to understand is the one we have been using to bombard them.$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ \n\n - The sources differ in how they refer to the Japanese. While Source B identifies the 'pigheadedness' of the leaders of a nation$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ and recognizes the innocence of Japanese women and children, Source C just refers to the Japanese as a monolithic group. \n\n \\\n\n [6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth Sources B and C reveal similar things about Truman's justification for the use of atomic weapons on Japan. One of the reasons that seem to validate the dropping of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki is the consideration of the Japanese  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$beasts\", which is how they are characterized both in Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$because they are beasts” and in Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you have to deal with a beast” ($\\checkmark$ similarity and evidence ). In both sources, it is clear that they are considered beasts because of their behaviour as Source B says they are a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$terribly cruel and uncivilized nation in warfare,\" while Source B highlights the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unwarranted attack by the Japanese on Pearl Harbor and their murder of our prisoners of war.\" ($\\checkmark$ similarity and evidence). By bringing up this behavior, Truman justifies the use of atomic weapons.\n\n \n\nHowever, despite coming from the pen of the same person, the sources also disagree on the reasons for the use of atomic weapons. A clear contrast concerns how the US should respond to Japanese brutalities ($\\checkmark$ difference). While in Source B Truman can't believe $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$that, because they are beasts, we should ourselves act in the same manner,\" in Source C, he says the opposite by stating, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you have to deal with a beast you have to treat him as a beast.\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence) One of the reasons why they disagree is because they identify different groups of people as responsible for Japanese atrocities ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B identifies the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$'pigheadedness' of the leaders of a nation$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ and recognizes the innocence of Japanese women and children. In this view, the indiscriminate use of atomic weapons would be unjustified due to their impact on civilians. Source C just refers to the Japanese as a singular group and thus views the destruction of entire cities as valid ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Moreover, the sources differ on what they believe will lead to Japan's surrender ($\\checkmark$ difference). Source B suggests that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$after the Russians enter into the war the Japanese will very shortly fold up,\" therefore atomic weapons are not really needed while Source C emphasizes the impact of U.S. bombings, stating, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The only language they seem to understand is the one we have been using to bombard them.\" ($\\checkmark$ evidence)"},{"ref":"HI0351","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0351\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the reasons for NOT using the atomic bombs on Japan?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Letter from President Harry S. Truman to Senator Richard Russell in response to his call for the further use of atomic weapons to destroy Japan, August 9, 1945.\n\n[Source: Truman, H. S. (1945, August 9). *Correspondence Between President Harry S. Truman and Senator Richard Russell*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved February 8, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/40020053. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following:\n\n - Because the US should not act as the Japanese had done\n\n - Because Japan will surrender with the USSR`s entry into the war\n\n - Because Japan`s women and children are not responsible for the stubbornness of their leaders.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, the United States should not use atomic weapons to destroy whole populations of Japan since it should not behave as beastly as the Japanese had done ($\\checkmark$). Truman recognizes that the ones guilty were the stubborn Japanese leaders and not the women and children, who would also suffer the effects of the atomic bombs ($\\checkmark$). He also says it was not really necessary to use them as he thought Japan would surrender once the USSR entered into WWII ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0468","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0468\n\nDescribe Franklin D. Roosevelt's Good Neighbor Policy.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\n**One** mark for a definition similar to:  \n\n - No sovereign state in the region, including the United States, had the right to intervene in the affairs of other sovereign states.\n\nAny **two** of the following additional details:\n\n - It was proposed by the US, under the presidency of Franklin D. Roosevelt,  at the 7th Pan American Conference held in Montevideo in 1933.\n\n - It was a departure from the former US policy of intervening militarily in Latin American countries to protect US interests or those of its citizens.\n\n - As it was put into effect, the US withdrew its marines from Haiti and Nicaragua, and eliminated the Platt Amendment that had limited Cuba`s sovereignty since its independence. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Good Neighbor Policy was declared at the 7th Pan American Conference held in Montevideo in 1933, by President Franklin D. Roosevelt ($\\checkmark$). He proposed that no sovereign state in the region, including the United States itself, had the right to intervene in the affairs of other sovereign states ($\\checkmark$). This was an important shift from the previous U.S. policy of military intervention in Latin American countries to protect American interests ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0469","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0469\n\nExplain why Chile did not break diplomatic relations with the Axis powers after the Rio Conference of 1942.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with evidence:\n\n - For domestic political reasons. \n\n     - Chile was going to have presidential election in 1942 and it did not want to have problems with its influential German population\n\n     - The National Socialist Chilean Movement had gotten almost 5$\\%$ of the votes in the municipal elections of 1938\n\n     The National Socialist Chilean Movement was part of the main opposition coalition, the Alianza Popular Libertadora\n\n - For military reasons\n\n     - Chile feared attacks by Japanese or German ships on its own territories. It did not have the necessary naval power to defend itself and it knew the US could not do so either due to the destruction of its navy at Pearl Harbor. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nChile did not break diplomatic relations with the Axis Powers after the Rio Conference of 1942 due to domestic political reasons. Since presidential elections were in that same year, the government did not want to alienate the influential German population or German origin population in the country ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the National Socialist Chilean Movement, originally inspired by the German Nazi Party, had some popular support. In the local elections of 1938 it had secured almost 5% of the votes, allowing it to become part of the main opposition coalition, the Alianza Popular Libertadora ($\\checkmark$). Additionally,Chile feared attacks by Japanese or German ships on its territories ($\\checkmark$). It knew it lacked the naval power to defend itself and Chile's authorities guessed that the US could not do so either because of the destruction of the US naval base at Pearl Harbor ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0470","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0470\n\nEvaluate the economic effects of the Good Neighbor Policy for the United States.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. To respond to an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate” command term it is valid to say that the whole goal was not reached as something negative. \n\nPositive results:\n\n - The Export-Import Bank was created to lend money to foreign companies that wanted to import goods from the USA. This favored US producers who were hard hit by the Great Depression. Before WWII, the efforts of this bank concentrated on Latin America. \n\n - The US government passed the Reciprocal Trade Agreements Act by which it could negotiate bilateral trade agreements with Latin American countries. This allowed the US to become the main trading partner of almost all Latin American countries.  \n\nNegative results:\n\n - One the goals of the Good Neighbor Policy was to dominate trade in the Americas. This goal was not reached. Argentina did not sign a bilateral trade agreement with the US, choosing instead to sign one with the United Kingdom. \n\n - Many in the US business community felt their interests threatened, as this implied the US government would no longer intervene should the country's domestic policies become less friendly to US businesses. By 1933, the USA held $\\text{\\textdollar}$5 billion in investments in Latin America ($1.5 billion in portfolio, $3.5 billion in direct investment).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall economic effects of the Good Neighbor Policy  were positive for the US.  \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging it did not have the desired results with all the countries of the region..\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Good Neighbor Policy had many positive economic effects for the US. It led to the creation of the Export-Import Bank, which provided loans to Latin American companies looking to import goods from the United States. This helped U.S. producers that were suffering the effects of the Great Depression by opening up new markets for their goods ($\\checkmark$). By 1940 Congress allowed it to lend $\\text{\\textdollar}$700 million to support exports to Latin America. Additionally, the U.S. government passed the Reciprocal Trade Agreements Act, allowing it to negotiate bilateral trade agreements. For example, in 1935, a reciprocal trade agreement placed 90$\\%$ of Brazilian exports on the duty-free list, allowing it to become the fifth largest supplier to the USA. As a result, the United States became the primary trading partner for almost all nations in the region ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, not all aspects of the Good Neighbor Policy were successful. Argentina, for instance, chose not to sign a bilateral trade agreement with the United States and instead aligned itself economically with the United Kingdom. This showed the limitations of the Good Neighbor Policy since not all countries in the region were willing to conform to U.S. trade interests ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, many in the US business community were afraid of losing their investments in Latin America which by 1933 had reached $\\text{\\textdollar}$5 billion ($\\checkmark$). Up to then, they had enjoyed the support of their government in case of conflict with local authorities.\n\nIn conclusion, the economic effects of the Good Neighbor Policy were mostly positive for the United States, as it facilitated trade and strengthened economic relations with most Latin American countries at a time when the nation needed new markets to incentivize a production hard hit by the Great Depression. However, its impact was not uniform across the region, as seen in Argentina's decision to maintain economic ties with Britain. Moreover, the Latin American market was not enough to take the US out of the Depression ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0471","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0471\n\nIdentify the most important agreements reached at the Inter-American Conference for the Maintenance of Peace, Buenos Aires, 1936.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The countries of the continent agreed to meet and decide on a joint action plan in case of a threat to the peace of the hemisphere\n\n - The countries of the continent agreed to remain neutral in case of extra continental conflicts\n\n - The nations condemned intervention by one State in the internal or external affairs of another State\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the end of the 19th century, the countries of the Americas met periodically in a series of Pan American Conferences to discuss issues that affected the region. One such conference was the one held in Buenos Aires in 1936. Some of the more important results of the conference were that the countries of the continent agreed to meet and decide on a joint action plan in case of a threat to the peace of the hemisphere ($\\checkmark$). This was important as tensions were rising in Europe and Asia and there was the threat that they would spill over to the rest of the world. To prevent this, at least in the Americas, the countries of the region agreed to remain neutral in case of an extra continental war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0472","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0472\n\nExplain the motivations behind the policy of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cash and carry.”\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - Many in the US were isolationists and FDR had promised to keep the US out of the war. Congress approved of this policy since it maintained a semblance of neutrality for the United States while helping the Allied Powers. \n\n - It removed the danger of US ships being targets for the Axis powers since the buyers were responsible for transporting (carrying) the purchased materials. This was important since it prevented a Lusitania-like situation from repeating itself and pushing the US into war and the British had naval dominance. \n\n - By the Neutrality Acts of 1935, 1936, 1937 and 1939, the US could not help either side in the war. The “cash and carry” policy respected the law established by those acts while allowing the US to supply the United Kingdom and France with war materials. The policy allowed the US to supply any belligerent country and not take sides as long as it paid in cash for the weapons purchased. However, since Germany was cash-poor, only the French and the British could take significant advantage of US production. \n\n - The US was still experiencing the effects of the Great Depression (17$\\%$ unemployment by 1939) and this policy allowed US production into European markets. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cash and carry” policy helped remove the danger of US ships being targeted by the Axis powers by placing the responsibility for transporting purchased materials on the buyers ($\\checkmark$). This was crucial in preventing a repeat of incidents like the sinking of the Lusitania, which was one of the reasons the US entered WWI ($\\checkmark$). This aspect of the policy was also aimed at helping the Allies while remaining neutral. Because the United Kingdom was still in control of the seas, it was the only one that could take advantage of US production ($\\checkmark$). This is also true of the second element of the policy.  The policy permitted the US to supply any belligerent nation as long as it paid in cash for the war materials purchased. However, since Germany was cash-poor, it was effectively only the French and the British who could benefit from the US's production ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0473","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0473\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about US involvement in WWII in 1940?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cartoon by Clifford Berryman, September 2, 1940. Berryman titled this cartoon “Uncle Sam becomes Democracy's Arsenal,” and he made it for Labor Day. Uncle Sam comments, \"every day will be Labor Day from now on.\" |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0473.jpg\" alt=\"HI0473\">\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1940, September, 2). Uncle Sam becomes Democracy's Arsenal. National Archives NextGen Catalog. Retrieved February 4, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/6012217. Public domain.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The US will switch its industrial production to meet the needs of war\n- The US will make weapons for the democratic nations\n- Since Uncle Sam says, “from now on,” we can infer that the US had not been producing weapons before this time\n- The US will substantially increase weapon production.\n- The weapons will be of good quality as they are tested\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom Source A, we can learn that before 1940 the US was not getting ready to participate in WWII. In the cartoon, Uncle Sam says, “every day will be Labor Day from now on,” so we can infer that the United States had not been producing weapons before this time ($\\checkmark$). The cartoon also suggests that weapons produced by the US will be of good quality, as depicted by the teddy bear testing the weapons ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0474","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0474\n\nHow, according to Source B, was the US participating in WWII by 1941?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech delivered by President Roosevelt at the White House Correspondents' Association Dinner in Washington, March 15, 1941. |\n\n[Source: Roosevelt, F. D. (1941, March 15). Address to the White House Correspondents' Association. Internet Archive. Retrieved February 4, 2025 from https://web.archive.org/web/20161022223216/https://www<noklink>.mtholyoke.edu/acad/intrel/WorldWar2/fdr24.htm. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- It was sending overseas every possible instrument of war that it could spare.\n- It was dedicating its total effort to the war\n- It was building factories to produce war material as quickly as possible as well as the means to transport it.\n- It was training millions of soldiers\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, by 1941 the United States was sending overseas every possible instrument of war which it could spare ($\\checkmark$). It was also producing as much war material as possible as well as the way to transport it ($\\checkmark$). Due to the decision of the American people, the US was not dedicating its total effort to winning the war ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0475","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0475\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying US participation in WWII by 1941.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech delivered by President Roosevelt at the White House Correspondents' Association Dinner in Washington, March 15, 1941. |\n\n[Source: Roosevelt, F. D. (1941, March 15). Address to the White House Correspondents' Association. Internet Archive. Retrieved February 4, 2025 from https://web.archive.org/web/20161022223216/https://www<noklink>.mtholyoke.edu/acad/intrel/WorldWar2/fdr24.htm. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The source contains insight as it was made in 1940 by the president of the United States, a key decision-maker in his country's policies (origin).\n- The source wants to explain how the US is contributing to the fight to save democracy (purpose).\n- The source mentions the initial debate on whether the US should get involved. It also describes the situation in March 1941 when the “Aid-for-Democracies Bill” (aka the Lease-and-Lend Bill) was passed and what the US was doing to contribute as much as possible to the fight (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The source lacks hindsight as it was made in March 1941 and does not know that the US entered the war as a belligerent by the end of the year (origin).\n- The source seeks to persuade the correspondents that the entire US population, 130 million Americans, will give their best to help democracy and that it is the right thing to do. Since this is a speech to journalists, convincing them it is right for the US to become an arsenal of democracy will help him get the message across to the general public (purpose).\n- The source does not provide the perspective of those who disagreed with the president. It also contains some exaggerations, such as implying that all 130 million Americans will do their part. The source also lacks specific information, such as “we started the erection of hundreds of plants and we started the training of millions of men” (content).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B's origin is valuable to a historian studying US participation in WWII by 1941 since it was made in that same year and thus provides contemporary insight. It is also made by the president of the US, a key decision-maker in shaping his country`s foreign policy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose is also valuable as Roosevlet wants to explain how the U.S. is contributing to the fight to save democracy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). With this in mind, the content provides information on the initial debate over U.S. involvement, the situation in March 1941 when the aid-to-democracies bill was passed, and the country's efforts to contribute as much as possible to winning the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. Because it was made in March of 1941 the origin makes it lack hindsight as Roosevelt could not know that the US would enter the war as a belligerent that same year ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose is also limited since, with this speech, Roosevelt wants to persuade the correspondents that the 130 million Americans are going to give their best to help democracy and that it is the right thing to do. Since this is a speech to journalists, convincing them the US should become an arsenal of democracy will help him get the message across to the general public ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). This makes the content limited as it leaves out any mention of the many who still opposed US involvement in the war in any way and it makes Roosevelt exaggerate with the idea that all 130 million Americans would give their total effort to win the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0476","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0476\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about US participation in World War II.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Speech delivered by President Roosevelt at the White House Correspondents' Association Dinner in Washington, March 15, 1941. |\n\n[Source: Roosevelt, F. D. (1941, March 15). Address to the White House Correspondents' Association. Internet Archive. Retrieved February 4, 2025 from https://web.archive.org/web/20161022223216/https://www<noklink>.mtholyoke.edu/acad/intrel/WorldWar2/fdr24.htm. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An introduction to the Lend-Lease Act of 1941 from the US National Archives's *Milestone Documents* feature.|\n\n[Source: The National Archives. (2022, June 8). Lend-Lease Act (1941). The National Archives - Milestone Documents. Retrieved February 4, 2025 from https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/lend-lease-act#:~:text=Passed%20on%20March%2011%2C%201941,defense%20of%20the%20United%20States.%22. Public Domain.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive and no set answer is required.\n\nFor comparison, any **one to five** of the following similarities:\n- They both recognize there was a debate regarding US participation in the war.\n    - Source B: “We have just now engaged in a great debate. It was not limited to the halls of Congress. It was argued in every newspaper, on every wave length-over every cracker barrel in the land”\n    - Source C: “a major foreign policy debate erupted over whether the United States should aid Great Britain or maintain strict neutrality.”\n- Both mention the US will supply weapons to those who are fighting for democracy.\n    - Source B: “The great task of this day, the deep duty which rests upon us, is to move products from the assembly lines of our factories to the battle lines of democracy-Now!”\n    - Source C: FDR  believed the United States should serve as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$great arsenal of democracy”\n- Both mention the desire the help Great Britain specifically\n- Both mention the willingness to help other countries who fight for democracy.\n    - Source B: “There are many more millions in Britain and elsewhere bravely shielding the great flame of democracy from the blackout of barbarism.”\n    - Source C: “supplies needed by any country whose security was vital to the defense of the United States.”\n- Both sources see this position as necessary for the US` defense.\n    - Source B: “It is a total effort and that is the only way to guarantee ultimate safety.”\n    - Source C: “We are buying our own security while we prepare.”\n\nFor contrast, any **one to three** of the following differences:\n- The sources differ on whether American soldiers will fight in the war.\n    - Source B implies that American soldiers will fight in the war: “Beginning a year ago, we started the erection of hundreds of plants and we started the training of millions of men.”\n    - Source C says: “your boys are not going to be sent into any foreign wars”\n- The sources differ on who the US will aid.\n    - Source B focuses on helping democracy: “the democracies of the world will be able to prove that dictatorships cannot win.”\n    - Source C focuses on helping the UK “debate erupted over whether the United States should aid Great Britain”\n- The sources differ on when the US started getting ready for war.\n    - Source B says that the US has been preparing for war since 1940: “Beginning a year ago, we started the erection of hundreds of plants and we started the training of millions of men.”\n    - Source C says it was only after passing the Lend-Lease Act that this was happening “Congress passed the Lend-Lease Act, meeting Great Britain's deep need for supplies and allowing the United States to prepare for war while remaining officially neutral.”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources B and C recognize that there was a debate in the US regarding its participation in WWII ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). In Source B, Roosevelt says, “We have just now engaged in a great debate. It was not limited to the halls of Congress. It was argued in every newspaper, on every wave length-over every cracker barrel in the land” and Source C mentions there was “a major foreign policy debate” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both sources also mention that, once the debate was resolved, the US was committed to supplying weapons to those fighting for democracy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B states that “The great task of this day, the deep duty which rests upon us, is to move products from the assembly lines of our factories to the battle lines of democracy-Now!”. At the same time, Source C mentions that  FDR  “believed the United States should serve as a great arsenal of democracy” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In both sources, the needs of Great Britain are highlighted as well as the fact that the US will come to its aid ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).  Furthermore, both sources say that the US supplying the democracies of the world is essential for its own security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). In Source B Roosevelt recognizes that this effort is “the only way to guarantee ultimate safety\" while in Source C the Secretary of War says “We are buying our own security while we prepare” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the sources also have key contrasts. One important difference concerns whether American soldiers will fight in the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{contrast}$). Roosevelt says in Source B that “we started the training of millions of men.” while in Source C he says “your boys are not going to be sent into any foreign wars” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). If they are not going to be sent to war, why would they be trained? Another key difference has to do with the time the US has started preparing for war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{contrast}$). Speaking in 1941, Roosevelt said  “Beginning a year ago, we started the erection of hundreds of plants and we started the training of millions of men.” In contrast, Source C says it started in 1941 with the passing of the Lend-Lease Act: “Congress passed the Lend-Lease Act, meeting Great Britain's deep need for supplies and allowing the United States to prepare for war while remaining officially neutral” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0587","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0587\n\nContrast the impact of conscription in the United States and Canada during World War II.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - In Canada, the issue of conscription generated a division in public opinion between the English speakers and the Quebecois, while in the US, it was generally less debated by the public, especially after Pearl Harbor. \n\n      - Although 70$\\%$ of Canadians agreed to conscription in the plebiscite organized by the government, in Quebec only 20$\\%$ of voters agreed. \n\n     - While there was some debate about the need for the draft in the US before Pearl Harbor, after it, it largely disappeared. Voluntary enlistment increased such that President Roosevelt had to issue an Executive Order (9279) to slow down enlistments in order to protect the US workforce.\n\n - The impact of conscription in the Canadian war effort was negligible, while in the US, it resulted in most of the combatants. \n\n     - Most Canadian combatants were volunteers. Only after 1944 did the Canadian government have to resort to conscription. Until the surrender of Germany only 2500 conscripts saw battle. In the US only about 40$\\%$ of servicemen were volunteers. The majority of the 10 million strong Armed Forces were draftees. \n\n - The governments dealt differently with those who were conscripted and refused to fight.\n\n     - In Canada, there was a mutiny of soldiers who refused to be sent overseas in Terrace, British Columbia. The mutiny was quickly put down, and those involved were sent to other military bases. In the US, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$conscientious objectors” were allowed to serve as medics or work in the Civilian Public Service. The idea was to keep them out of public view. Members of the African American Nation of Islam refused to serve due to political reasons. They, including their leader Elijah Mohammad, were convicted on charges of draft dodging. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn the war itself, the impact of conscription was quite different ($\\checkmark$). One of the reasons that Prime Minister King was able to order conscription so late in the war was the large number of volunteers that made conscription unnecessary. This changed only after 1944 so, in total, only 2500 Canadian conscripts saw any action before Germany's surrender. In the US, the majority of servicemen, about 60$\\%$, were actually draftees ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBoth countries dealt differently with those who were conscripted but refused to fight ($\\checkmark$). When in Canada there was a mutiny in Terrace, British Columbia ,of soldiers who refused to be sent overseas, it was quickly put dow,n and those involved were sent to other military bases suffering no long-term sanctions. However, in the US, while $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$conscientious objectors” were allowed to serve as medics or work in the Civilian Public Service, this was not so for those who had political reasons to oppose conscription ($\\checkmark$). The most notable case is that of members of the African American Nation of Islam who refused to serve and fight for a country that did not grant them equal rights. They, including their leader Elijah Mohammad, were convicted on charges of draft dodging and sent to prison ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0588","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0588\n\nDescribe the workings of one US government agency that helped organize the economy for the Second World War.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** details of **one** of the following government agencies:\n\n - Office of Production Management (OPM, 1941-1942)\n\n     - Created by Roosevelt in January 1941, before the passage of the Lend-Lease Act and the declaration of war by the US. \n\n     - It aimed to regulate the production and supply of materials necessary for national defense.\n\n     - It was not always successful in avoiding supply chain bottlenecks.\n\n - The War Production Board (WPB, 1942-1945)\n\n     - It replaced the Office of Production Management after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbour. \n\n     - Its job was to organize the production of war materials and ensure war industries had the necessary raw materials as well as to help .\n\n     - It could not always ensure a constant flow of raw materials such as aluminium but it was partly responsible for a great increase in production of war materials. For example, production of aircraft in 1944 was 5 times that of 1941. \n\n - War Manpower Commission (WMC, 1942-1945)\n\n     - It was set up by President Roosevelt in1942 due to tensions between the military and the war industry. \n\n     - Its aim was to balance the manpower needs of agriculture, industry and the armed forces. \n\n     - It was responsible for publicity campaigns aimed at women to convince them to join the war industries or essential civilian services\n\n     - It set up a Training Program to train unskilled workers to cover the shortages caused by those who had enlisted. \n\n     - It was able to train more than 1.5 million workers thus covering the shortage of skilled labor. \n\n - Office of Price Administration (OPA, 1941-1947)\n\n     - It was set up to control prices and rents to avoid inflation\n\n     - It placed maximum prices on 90% of retail food prices and residential rents. \n\n     - It introduced rationing to control demand of scarce goods. It rationed things like tires, gasoline, fuel oil, coal, firewood, nylon, silk, and shoes.\n\n     - Its measures resulted in an average inflation of 3.5% for the years 1942-1946. \n\n - Office of War Mobilization (OWM, 1943-1945)\n\n     - Managed the coordination among various wartime agencies to ensure smooth production.\n\n     - Helped prioritize military contracts and ensure resources were used efficiently.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Office of Price Administration was established to control prices and rents in order to prevent inflation during World War II ($\\checkmark$). It set maximum prices on 90$\\%$ of retail food items and residential rents while also introducing rationing to manage the demand for scarce goods ($\\checkmark$). Thanks to the action of the Office of Price Administration, the average inflation rate was only 3.5$\\%$ between 1942 and 1946 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0589","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0589\n\nCompare and contrast the reasons for Brazil and Mexico's participation in WWII. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ contrast)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\n\\\n\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive and no set answer is required. The answer need not be balanced but it requires at least **four** arguments with their respective evidence. \n\nFor comparison, any **one to three** of the following similarities:\n\n - Initial neutrality\n\n     - Both countries were initially neutral. In the case of Brazil, because it thought its commerce would benefit from a neutral position and, in the case of Mexico, because the government's policy was to remain neutral unless Mexico was attacked directly. \n\n - German sinking of their ships\n\n     - The catalyst for declaring war on the Axis was their ships being sunk by German submarines. Almost 40 Brazilian merchant ships were sunk between 1941-2, resulting in about 1700 dead. In May 1942, the Mexican oil tanker Potrero del Llano was sunk by a German submarine. After the Mexican government demanded an official apology, the German government's response was to torpedo another Mexican ship.  \n\n - Desire to obtain US assistance \n\n     - The USA gave two-thirds of its overall assistance via the Lend-Lease Act to Brazil. In 1942, Varga's government signed an agreement whereby the USA would help to equip the Brazilian army, and Brazil would provide key materials for the war effort, such as iron ore. A reciprocal defense agreement was signed between the US and Mexico in 1941, which allowed the US to use Mexican military bases and, in return, it helped train Mexican air pilots. That same year, Mexico agreed to sell all its strategic minerals to the US and the US agreed to give the Mexican government a loan of $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million. \n\nFor contrast, any **one to three** of the following differences:\n\n- Mexico feared a Japanese attack, while Brazil initially feared one from Argentina. \n\n     - Due to its proximity to Japan, Mexico feared a Japanese attack after Pearl Harbour. On the other hand, Brazil initially did not want to join the war since it did not want to focus on an external threat when its rival, Argentina, could take advantage of the situation. \n\n - Mexico had ideological differences with the Axis, while Brazil's government had many similarities. \n\n     - President Avila Camacho used Nazi ideology to unify the population against the Axis. He explained that Hitler's racial policies would certainly oppress the indigenous and Mestizo populations. On the other hand, Vargas was a dictator who had come to power through a coup in 1930 and was creating a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$New State” in Brazil with many of the same ideas as Fascist Italy. \n\n - The Mexican public was reluctant to get involved in an armed conflict, while a large part of the Brazilian population demanded war \n\n     - After years of civil war, the Mexican population was tired of fighting. On the other hand, Vargas' nationalistic rhetoric and the many ships sunk by German submarines led to popular protests in all major cities demanding a declaration of war against Germany. \n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nBoth Mexico and Brazil initially remained neutral during World War II, though for different reasons ($\\checkmark$ similarity). For Brazil, the decision had to do with the idea that being able to trade with both sides in conflict would benefit its economy. On the other hand, in Mexico, the government had a strict policy of neutrality unless directly attacked. However, both nations entered the war on the side of the Allies in 1942 partly in response to the sinking of their merchant ships by German submarines ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Between 1941 and 1942, nearly 40 Brazilian merchant ships were sunk, while in May 1942, the German navy torpedoed the Mexican oil tanker Potrero del Llano. When the Mexican government demanded an apology, Germany responded by sinking yet another Mexican vessel ($\\checkmark$ evidence). The idea that an entry into WWII would encourage US military and economic assistance also played a part in the declaration of war for both countries ($\\checkmark$ similarity). This idea proved true as Brazil received two-thirds of all Lend-Lease aid allocated to Latin America. At the same time, Mexico signed a reciprocal defense agreement with the U.S. in 1941, allowing American forces access to Mexican military bases in exchange for pilot training and a $\\text{\\textdollar}$40 million loan ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBesides these common grounds, each nation had its own reasons for participating in WWII. In the case of Mexico, there was an ideological component as President Avila Camacho resented the Nazis' racist ideology. On the other hand, Brazil's leader, Getúlio Vargas, had established a dictatorship since 1930 and shared many ideological similarities to Fascist Italy ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence). Support for participation in war was also different. Mexicans were tired of war after 25 years of civil strife and preferred to stay out of the conflict, while in Brazil, the many ships sank by the Germans caused public outrage, and nationalist protests erupted in all major cities, which demanded war against the Axis ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0590","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0590\n\nDescribe Brazilian participation in WWII.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Brazil's navy helped combat German U-boats in the south and central Atlantic Ocean. \n\n - The USA built airfields in Brazil to be used for the war effort. For example, the US air base at Parnamirim was the largest single US air base outside its own territory. From the base, supplies were sent to North Africa or the China-Burma-India theatre. Because of its significant contribution to the war, the air base at Parnamirim was known as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Trampoline to Victory.\"\n\n - Brazil provided key materials for the US war effort, such as iron ore, chrome, manganese, nickel, bauxite, and rubber.\n\n - Brazil was the only Latin American country to send part of its army, 25,000 men, into combat overseas. In 1944, the Brazilian Expeditionary Force (BEF) and the Air Force's First Fighter Group were sent to fight in Italy. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince its entry into the war in 1942, Brazil played an important logistical role in the Allied war effort. The government of Vargas also allowed the US to build airfields in Brazil, including Parnamirim, which became the largest U.S. air base outside the continental United States. This base became known as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Trampoline to Victory\"  due to its importance for transporting supplies to North Africa and the Pacific theater ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, Brazil provided essential raw materials to the US war effort, such as iron ore, chrome, manganese, nickel, bauxite, and rubber ($\\checkmark$). Brazil was also the only Latin American country to deploy troops overseas, sending 25,000 Brazilian Expeditionary Force (BEF) soldiers to fight in Italy in 1944 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0591","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0591\n\nDescribe US participation in WWII.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Initially, the US participated by providing the Allies with supplies through the Cash and Carry policy and then the Lend-Lease policy. For example, the Lend-Lease Act granted the USSR 18,000 airplanes, 1,500 tanks, and more than 500 ships.\n\n - After 1941, the US was responsible for carrying out the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$island hopping” strategy in the Pacific to defeat Japan. \n\n - In both the European theater and the Pacific theater, it was responsible for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$carpet bombing” of major cities. Estimates about damage to urban areas in Japan vary, but many indicate well over 40$\\%$ of urban areas were destroyed with a higher percentage in Tokyo. American troops participated in major battles on the Italian and Pacific fronts and in the Normandy invasion. In all, they sent about 16 million soldiers, 1 million of whom were casualties. \n\n - The US was responsible for effectively ending the war with the use of atomic bombs against Japan. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe United States initially supported the Allies by supplying resources through the Cash and Carry policy and later the Lend-Lease Act. By the Lend-Lease Act, 18,000 airplanes, 1,500 tanks, and over 500 ships were provided to the USSR  ($\\checkmark$). After officially entering the war in December of 1941, American forces were responsible for large-scale $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$carpet bombing\" campaigns in both Europe and the Pacific, with devastating effects. In Tokyo alone it is estimated that around 60$\\%$ of the city was destroyed.  ($\\checkmark$). Ultimately, the U.S. brought the war to an end by forcing Japan to surrender after the use of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0592","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0592\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the treatment of Japanese Americans during WWII?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | During a visit to the Gila River camp, where his family is confined, Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto shows his Purple Heart to his brother, Susumu. October 31, 1943.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0592%28A%29.png\" alt=\"HI0592(A)\">\n\n[Source: No title. (1945, October 31). Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2024 from https://fdrlibrary.org/documents/356632/390886/Japanese+American+Internment+Photographs.pdf/56c62fad-dd2a-4325-9fef-76ca2ff1ffa6.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Whole families were confined to camps\n\n - Even though Japanese Americans were willing to risk their lives for their country, they were still treated as criminals. \n\n - Even harmless and innocent people were confined. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source A, we can learn that whole families were confined to camps, even with their little children, such as the one portrayed in the photograph ($\\checkmark$). We can also learn the unfairness of the situation as, despite the willingness of Japanese Americans to risk their lives for their country, people like Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto, awarded the Purple Heart, were still treated as threats or criminals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0593","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0593\n\nWhat, according to Source C, was the impact of internment on Japanese Americans?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An explanation of Executive Order 9066 published by the National Archives (2022).\n\n[Source: Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (2022, January 24). *Milestone Documents. National Archives*. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift //archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n- They lost personal liberties. \n\n- Due to the speed at which the orders were implemented, many were unable to sell their property quickly. \n\n- They were treated as criminals as the camps were built in isolated places and surrounded by guards. \n\n- They had fewer rights than criminals since they could not even appeal their incarceration.\n\n- Even though they were treated as enemies of the country, some were still drafted into the army. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFrom Source C, we can learn that internment for Japanese Americans was a traumatic experience. Being confined, they lost many of their personal liberties ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, due to the rapid implementation of the order, many were forced to sell their property as quickly as possible, often unable to get a reasonable price ($\\checkmark$). Perhaps the worst consequence was that they were treated as criminals, put in camps built in the middle of nowhere, and surrounded by guards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0594","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0594\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | During a visit to the Gila River camp, where his family is confined, Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto shows his Purple Heart to his brother, Susumu. October 31, 1943.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/594.PNG\" alt=\"594\">\n\n[Source: No title. (1945, October 31). Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2024 from https://fdrlibrary.org/documents/356632/390886/Japanese+American+Internment+Photographs.pdf/56c62fad-dd2a-4325-9fef-76ca2ff1ffa6.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source contains insight as the picture was taken in 1943, a year after the internment of people of Japanese origins started.  (origin).\n\n - The photograph was made to show the reality of what Japanese Americans were living. \n\n - From the source we can learn that even small children were confined and that you could get out of the camps to serve in the army. We also learn that despite the injustice suffered, Japanese Americans stood out for their bravery in combat defending the US (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source lacks hindsight. The photographer could not know how the lives of Japanese Americans would change in the upcoming years (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to criticize the internment camps as it highlights the injustice of a man who has risked his life for his country while his country was depriving his family of their freedom (purpose).\n\n - The source is only one small window into the conditions in which those in the internment camps lived. It portrays only one moment in time for two people. Because it's a photograph, it cannot show the reasons for the establishment of internment camps (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A has a valuable origin since it has insight as the photograph was taken in 1943, a year after the internment of Japanese Americans began ($\\checkmark$ VO). Its purpose is also valuable since it seeks to show the reality of the internment camps, showing that even young children were confined ($\\checkmark$ VP). The source shows that some internees were allowed to leave the camps to serve in the army. From the purple heart earnt by Army Sergeant Kazuo Komoto we can also learn that, despite the injustice they endured, Japanese Americans demonstrated remarkable bravery in combat, defending the United States ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Its origin is limited since it lacks hindsight, as the photographer could not predict how the lives of Japanese Americans would change in the following years ($\\checkmark$ LO). The purpose is limited as it seeks to criticize the internment camps by emphasizing the injustice of a man risking his life for a country that was simultaneously depriving his family of freedom ($\\checkmark$ LP). Moreover, as it is the image of only 2 individuals in one moment in time, the content we learn is limited. For example, it does not provide context regarding the reasons behind the establishment of internment camps ($\\checkmark$ LC)."},{"ref":"HI0595","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0595\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the reasons for Roosevelt's Executive Order confining people of Japanese descent to internment camps.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Paul Sparrow, Director of the FDR Library, in an introduction to the exhibition *Images of Internment* held at the Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum (2017).\n\n[Source: Sparrow, P. M. (2017, February 17). *Images of Internment*. Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2025  from https://fdr.blogs.archives.gov/2017/02/17/images-of-internment/.  Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An explanation of Executive Order 9066 published by the National Archives (2022).\n\n[Source: Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (2022, January 24). *Milestone Documents. National Archives*. Retrieved February 10, 2025 from https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift //archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks):|Labels|\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark$ similarity)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark$ contrast)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark$ evidence)|\n\nThe following material is an indication of what candidates may elect to write about in their responses. It is neither prescriptive nor exhaustive, and no set answer is required.\n\nFor comparison, any **one to four** of the following similarities: \n\n - They both identify racism as a cause of why people of Japanese descent were targeted\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$entrenched and widespread racism of that time” \n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans.” \n\n - Both refer to the attack on Pearl Harbor as the catalyst of the executive order\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The 'dastardly' surprise attack on Pearl Harbor, and Japan's brutal military expansion throughout Asia, enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time.”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the Empire of Japan attacked the United States at the Pearl Harbor Naval Base in Hawaii. The attack launched a rash of fear about national security”\n\n - Both refer to the threat to and fear for national security  \n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The attack launched a rash of fear about national security”\n\n - Both identify the West Coast as the place where demands for internment were especially coming from\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The President was under tremendous pressure from military commanders, members of congress and political and business leaders in California, Oregon and Washington”\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fear about national security, especially on the West Coast.”\n\nFor contrast, any one to two of the following differences:\n\n - The sources differ on who was calling for internment. \n\n     - Source B speaks of military leaders, Congress, newspapers, and the military: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The President was under tremendous pressure from military commanders, members of congress … Leading newspaper columnists demanded action.” \n\n     - Source C only mentions lobbyists from Western states who put pressure on politicians.$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lobbyists from western states, many representing competing economic interests or nativist groups, pressured Congress and the President”\n\n - They differ on the reasons for the internment. \n\n     - Source B mentions racism and military reasons for confinement: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack or even invade the West Coast …The 'dastardly' surprise attack on Pearl Harbor, and Japan's brutal military expansion throughout Asia, enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time.” \n\n     - Source C also adds economic competition as a factor: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$economic competition, distrust over cultural separateness, and long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans.”\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSources B and C give similar reasons for Roosevelt's Executive Order confining people of Japanese descent to internment camps. First, they both identify racism as a factor in the targeting of Japanese Americans ($\\checkmark$ similarity), with Source B referring to the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$entrenched and widespread racism of that time” and Source C describing how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$long-standing anti-Asian racism turned into disaster for Japanese Americans” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Both also mention the attack on Pearl Harbor as the catalyst for Executive Order 9066 (✓ similarity), with Source B emphasizing how the attack $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$enflamed the entrenched and widespread racism of that time,” while Source C states that the attack $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$launched a rash of fear about national security” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). Furthermore, both sources highlight the fear for national security as one of the reasons for the order ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source B mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack”  while Source C says that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the attack launched a rash of fear about national security” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nHowever, the sources differ on what groups were calling for the internment of people of Japanese descent ($\\checkmark$ contrast). Source B mentions pressure from military leaders, Congress and how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$leading newspaper columnists demanded action.” In contrast, Source C highlights how $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lobbyists from western states, many representing competing economic interests or nativist groups, pressured Congress and the President” ($\\checkmark$ evidence). The role of lobbyists helps explain why Source C includes “economic competition, distrust over cultural separateness, and long-standing anti-Asian racism” as reasons for internment, while Source B stays focused on the military aspect, saying,  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There was sincere concern about espionage and sabotage, and fear that the Japanese Empire could attack or even invade the West Coast” ($\\checkmark$ contrast and evidence)."},{"ref":"HI0596","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0596\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII.\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Paul Sparrow, Director of the FDR Library, in an introduction to the exhibition *Images of Internment* held at the Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum (2017).\n\n[Source: Sparrow, P. M. (2017, February 17). *Images of Internment*. Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum. National Archives. Retrieved February 10, 2025  from https://fdr.blogs.archives.gov/2017/02/17/images-of-internment/.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source contains hindsight as it was made in 2017 so the author is able to analyze events detached from the passions of WWII. He is also the Director of the FDR Library so he is likely an expert on FDR's actions.  \n\n - The text seeks to give a short explanation of the internment of Japanese Americans since it is the introduction of an exhibit on the topic that was organized by the Library and Museum (purpose). \n\n - From the source we can learn the reasons why Roosevelt gave the executive order as well as some information on those who supported and those who opposed the measure (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source lacks some insight as it was made by someone who did not live the events and was not privy to the feelings at the time. Moreover, since the author is the Director of the FDR Library and Museum, he is biased in favor of Roosevelt (origin).\n\n - The source seeks to give only a brief introduction so the details given are few. It also wants to portray FDR in a positive light stressing that it was not really his fault that the executive order was given (purpose).\n\n - The source's nature as a brief introduction means it has a limited amount of information about the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Furthermore, it contains some exaggerations regarding Roosevelt calling him $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of our greatest champions of human rights” (content).\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource B is valuable for a historian studying the internment of Japanese Americans during WWII. Since it was created in 2017, the origin contains hindsight and is thus able to analyze events without the emotional intensity of World War II. Additionally, the author is the Director of the FDR Library, so he should be an expert on Roosevelt's actions ($\\checkmark$ VO). The purpose of the source is valuable since it provides a brief explanation of the internment of Japanese Americans, as it is part of an exhibit organized by the Library and Museum ($\\checkmark$ VP). The content allows us to learn some of the reasons Roosevelt issued Executive Order 9066, as well as information on those who supported and opposed the measure ($\\checkmark$ VC).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. The origin is limited since the author did not experience the events firsthand, it lacks insight into the emotions of the time. Furthermore, as he is the Director of the FDR Library, the author has a bias in favor of Roosevelt ($\\checkmark$ LO). This is apparent in the content of the source where the author includes some exaggerations such as calling Roosevelt $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of our greatest champions of human rights” ($\\checkmark$ LC).The purpose is also limited since the brevity required for an introduction to an exhibition necessarily means that a lot of information must be left out. We can also see from the text that the Director of the FDR Library understandably seeks to portray Roosevelt in a positive light, downplaying his responsibility for the executive order ($\\checkmark$ LP)."},{"ref":"HI0804Q1","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0804Q1\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the relationship between Argentina and the U.S. in 1942?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Cartoon by Clifford Berryman, January 24, 1942. It refers to US attempts to convince Argentina of breaking relations with the Axis Power. The cartoon portrays Secretary of State Cordell Hull, Assistant Secretary of State Sumner Welles and a female character labeled as Argentina.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/History%20of%20the%20Americas/World%20War%20II%20and%20the%20Americas/images/HI0804Q1.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0804Q1\">\n\n[Source: Berryman, C. K. (1942, January 24). *Untitled*. National Archives NextGen Catalog. Retrieved June 25, 2025 from  from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/6012250. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- Argentina has not accepted Cordell Hull`s previous U.S. proposals\n\n- The U.S. is interested in getting Argentina on its side\n\n- Cordell Hull thinks Sumner Welles' proposals have a good chance of being accepted\n\n- The relationship is peaceful as U.S. diplomats are trying to convince Argentina to join them. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A suggests that Argentina had not agreed to follow U.S. policy by 1942. We can see this in that Cordell Hull, as Secretary of State, is not directly negotiating with Argentina and says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$they can`t resist my boy Sumner”, implying that there was previous resistance ($\\checkmark$). The Source also suggests that the U.S. is not giving up and is still actively trying to win Argentina over and, to do so, it changes the negotiator ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0804Q5","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0804Q5\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and F reveal about U.S. policies regarding Argentina in 1942. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n\n - Both sources agree that Argentina was not yet convinced to follow U.S. policy. \n\n     - In Source A, the character labeled Argentina is on a balcony, away from the U.S.\n\n     - Source F says the purpose of the measure of freezing funds is to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to coerce Argentina into greater collaboration with the war effort of the United Nations.”\n\n - Both sources agree that the U.S. should persuade Argentina to follow its policy. \n\n     - In Source A the character labeled as Sumner Welles is trying to lure Argentina by singing a serenade and bring it down from the balcony\n\n     - In Source F, Cordell Hull explains the measure put in place saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in order to persuade Argentina of where its vital interests lie”\n\n - In both sources, Cordell Hull oversees U.S. policy toward Argentina.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n\n - While in Source A, the issue concerns only Argentina, in F, Hull recognizes the impact the relationship could have on other countries in the region, such as Chile and the other nations in general. \n\n     - In Source A, only Argentine and U.S. officials are present in the cartoon\n\n     - Source F says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the other American republics would begin to wonder whether we had repudiated our present policy and returned to the days of the Big Stick.”\n\n - While in Source A the issue is being handled by members of the State Department, in Source F, the proposal to freeze funds is from the Board of Economic Warfare \n\n - While in Source A the way to convince Argentina is by giving her a nice song, the measures mentioned in Source F are the opposite of giving. Source F says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the United States, since Rio has withheld delivery of military and naval supplies to Argentina (...) and is furnishing scarce nonmilitary goods(...).”\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSources A and F have some similarities regarding U.S. policies towards Argentina in 1942. In both, we can see that Argentina was not convinced to follow U.S. policy ($\\checkmark\\text{ similarity}$).  In Source A, Argentina is shown as a lady who is physically distant from the United States, who is trying to lure her. Similarly, Source F states that the purpose of freezing funds was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to coerce Argentina into greater collaboration with the war effort of the United Nations.”  ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$). Another similarity is that the U.S. is trying to convince Argentina to align with its policies despite the country`s hesitation ($\\checkmark\\text{ similarity}$). In Source A, the character labeled Sumner Welles tries to lure Argentina by singing a serenade. At the same time, in Source F, Cordell Hull explains that the measure was intended $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to persuade Argentina of where its vital interests lie.”  ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$). Furthermore, in both sources, Cordell Hull is shown as the key figure responsible for handling U.S. policy toward Argentina.\n\nHowever, the sources also contain differences regarding the same topic. Source A focuses only on the relationship between Argentina and U.S. officials, while Source F acknowledges the wider regional impact this relationship could have  ($\\checkmark\\text{ difference}$). Hull notes that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the other American republics would begin to wonder whether we had repudiated our present policy and returned to the days of the Big Stick,” highlighting concerns about U.S. relations with other Latin American nations if Argentina was treated too harshly ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$). Another difference has to do with the way to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$convince” Argentina to align with the U.S.  ($\\checkmark\\text{ difference}$). While in Source A, Welles sings a serenade to Argentina, the measures mentioned in Source F are more forceful saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the United States, since Rio has withheld delivery of military and naval supplies to Argentina (...) and is furnishing scarce nonmilitary goods(...).” ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0804Q6","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0804Q6\n\nEvaluate the impact of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lima Declaration” reached at the 8th Inter-American Conference (Lima, 1938) on the United States' policies in the Americas. \n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than **four** marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. To respond to an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate” command term, it is valid to say that the whole goal was not reached as something negative. \n\n$\\\\$\n\nPositive results: The U.S. obtained an agreement for cooperation in case the troubles in Europe spread to the Americas. \n\n - The nations reiterated their compromise with continental solidarity, promising to help each other in case of need \n\n - In case the peace of the continent was endangered, they agreed to coordinate a response\n\n - The declaration was signed by all Latin American countries\n\n$\\\\$\n\nNegative results: The U.S. did not obtain the unity of action it desired. \n\n - The different republics would act independently as sovereign states\n\n - The U.S. had to give up the idea of creating an American League of Nations.  \n\n - To get this Declaration approved by all 21 nations of the Pan American Union, the U.S. had to agree to another declaration condemning intervention in the domestic or foreign affairs of other states. \n\n \\\n\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the impact of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Lima Declaration” was positive for the U.S..  \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging that it did not have the full desired results.\n\n[1 marks]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe impact of the “Lima Declaration” on the United States' continental policy was positive in that it obtained an agreement from all Latin American countries to cooperate if conflicts in Europe threatened the security of the continent ($\\checkmark$). To achieve cooperation, the nations agreed to have a coordinated response ($\\checkmark$). All 21 nations of the Pan American Union signed this declaration, showing the unanimous support for continental solidarity that the Good Neighbor Policy aimed for ($\\checkmark$). \n\nHowever, the U.S. did not achieve all of its objectives. First of all, it failed to secure a commitment to unified action. The declaration stated that each republic retained its right to act independently as a sovereign state  ($\\checkmark$). Along with this, the U.S. had to abandon its proposal for a permanent inter-American body, or an American League of Nations ($\\checkmark$). To get all the nations to sign this declaration, the U.S. also had to accept a separate declaration that condemned intervention in the domestic or foreign affairs of other states, limiting its ability to influence regional affairs  ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIn conclusion, while the Lima Declaration strengthened the principle of hemispheric cooperation and was a diplomatic win for the U.S., it fell short of achieving the full level of unity that U.S. policymakers had initially hoped for ($\\checkmark\\text{ conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI0804Q8","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0804Q8\n\nExplain how the U.S. responded to the challenges faced by the Good Neighbor Policy up to 1945.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of at least **two** of the below with supporting details, examples and/or explanation:\n\n - Expropriation of U.S. oil companies in Bolivia in 1937 and Mexico in 1938: To put pressure on Bolivia, it was denied loans from the Export-Import Bank, and negotiations lasted over three years. In 1940, with Europe and Asia at war, the U.S.A. was much more willing to concede, and an agreement was reached in which Standard Oil received $\\text\\textdollar$1.5 million for the sale of the company to Bolivia.\n\n - There was a lack of cultural understanding between the U.S. and its Southern neighbors: Roosevelt created the Cultural Relations Division of the State Department, with Latin America as a special focus.. It encouraged educational and technical exchanges. Moreover,  passenger lines to South America were developed that sailed from New York to Argentina, Brazil, and Uruguay to establish cultural understanding. The U.S. media was encouraged to showcase Latin American culture.\n\n - Resistance to join the U.S. war effort after 1941. The U.S. pressured the governments of Argentina and Bolivia into compliance. While it did not militarily intervene in these countries' domestic affairs, it applied considerable diplomatic and economic pressure and supported factions rivaling the established governments of both countries. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBetween 1933, when it was first proclaimed, and 1945, the U.S. faced many challenges in achieving the aims of the Good Neighbor Policy. \n\nOne key problem was the lack of trust and cultural understanding between the U.S. and Latin American nations ($\\checkmark$). To address this, in 1938, President Roosevelt created the Cultural Relations Division of the State Department, with a special focus on Latin America. This division encouraged educational and technical exchanges and promoted Latin American culture in U.S. media. In addition, new passenger lines were developed between New York and South American countries like Argentina, Brazil, and Uruguay ($\\checkmark$). The aim was to increase the connections between the U.S. and South America to improve relations. \n\nAnother issue was presented by the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor, which forced the U.S. to participate in WWII actively. For this, it aimed at hemispheric solidarity and unity of action, but some nations did not want to follow U.S. policy ($\\checkmark$). In the case of Argentina, although the U.S. did not resort to military intervention, it responded by applying significant diplomatic and economic pressure, like freezing Argentinian funds and refusing to sell any strategic resource to the South American nation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0804Q9","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0804Q9\n\nEvaluate the success of the Rio Conference of 1942 from the perspective of the U.S.\n\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. To respond to an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate” command term it is valid to say that the whole goal was not reached as something negative. \n\n$\\\\$\n\nPositive results:\n\n - The Conference recommended the breaking of relations with the Axis Powers after the attack on Pearl Harbor. Most of the countries in the region did so.  \n\n - Assistant Secretary of State Sumner Welles made promises of economic assistance in exchange for continental solidarity in the face of attacks. This was to be done by including Latin American countries in the Lend-Lease policy. \n\n - The hemispheric nations established the Inter-American Defense Board to coordinate the region's defense.  \n\n$\\\\$\n\nNegative results:\n\n - Chile did not break off relations with the Axis Powers until 1943, when concern for Japanese naval strength disappeared. Argentina waited until March 1944 to break off relations with the Axis Powers.  \n\n - The rift between Assistant Secretary of State Sumner Welles` diplomatic approach and Secretary of State Cordell Hull`s desire to pressure the countries in the region to commit to war was made evident. Welles would resign under pressure in 1943.  According to Gellman, the Good Neighbor policy effectively ended with the departure of Sumner Welles.\n\n \\\n\n [4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the overall results of the Conference were positive for the U.S.\n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging it did not have the desired results with all the region's countries.\n\n \\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Rio Conference of 1942 was mostly successful from the point of view of the U.S. in its aim of achieving continental solidarity in its fight against the Axis Powers. \n\nFirstly, the conference recommended breaking diplomatic relations with the Axis Powers and, except for Argentina and Chile, the region's countries did so  ($\\checkmark$). To strengthen ties and achieve the United States' wartime interests, the Inter-American Defense Board was established to coordinate the region's defense. Up to this moment, cooperation on defensive matters was limited to periodic meetings and the individual decisions of the states. With the  Inter-American Defense Board, there was finally a regional organization ($\\checkmark$). \n\nMoreover, Assistant Secretary of State Sumner Welles` promise of economic assistance in exchange for continental solidarity tied the Latin American economies closer to the U.S. economy  ($\\checkmark$). This was done through the inclusion of Latin American countries in the Lend-Lease policy ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the U.S. was not able to accomplish all its goals. Chile delayed breaking relations with the Axis until 1943, and Argentina only did so in March 1944. These delays undermined the goal of unified action against the Axis Powers  ($\\checkmark$). The conference also laid bare internal divisions within the U.S. administration on how to achieve its objectives. Assistant Secretary of State Sumner Welles favored a diplomatic approach based on incentives, while Secretary of State Cordell Hull preferred applying pressure  ($\\checkmark$). This conflict led to Welles's resignation in 1943. \n\nIn conclusion, while the Rio Conference did not achieve total regional alignment and led to important disagreements among U.S. policymakers, it was overall a success from the U.S. perspective. Most countries broke ties with the Axis, regional defense was coordinated, and the U.S. secured broader cooperation in the war effort ($\\checkmark\\text{ conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1000","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1000\n\nCompare and contrast the involvement of Canada and the USA in the Second World War.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\n**Three** aspects can be analyzed to answer this question. \n\n - Diplomatic policies\n\n - Economic contribution\n\n - Military contribution \n\nConsiderations:\n\n - In the exam, it will be up to the candidate to choose which countries to use to answer the question.\n\n - Students should attempt to analyze at least two similarities and two differences between the countries chosen. \n\n - It is important that the information regarding both countries mentioned in the question is balanced and that a running comparison structure is attempted, rather than an isolated discussion of each. \n\nIndicative content (not prescriptive):\n\n - Diplomatic policies: \n\n     - US Neutrality Acts of 1935, 1936, 1937, and 1939. Did not enter the war until the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbour in 1941\n\n     - Canada was the only country in the region to declare war on the Axis Powers in 1939, one week after the British did. \n\n     - Canada declared war on Germany, while the US declared war on Japan, and as a result, Germany declared war on the US.\n\n     - While the US tried to organize a hemispheric united response to the conflict, Canada followed its own policy and ignored the rest of the Americas. \n\n     - Both countries considered the United Kingdom a vital partner and assisted it since 1939. The US and Canada both provided supplies, with Canada also contributing militarily.\n\n     - Both countries played an important role in the diplomatic conferences organized to plan the war. Canada's diplomatic status increased when it hosted the Quebec Conferences, although Prime Minister Mackenzie King was always aware that Roosevelt and Churchill were the leading players, and he remained in the background. The US was the main participant in all the wartime conferences, not only in Quebec. \n\n - Economic contribution \n\n     - The US assisted in the war effort even before it entered the war through its Cash and Carry scheme, the Destroyers for Bases plan, and the Lend-Lease Act. Canada implemented the Mutual Aid programme to assist the Allies with food, raw materials, and munitions from May 1943 until the end of the Second World War.\n\n     - Canada assisted the US in its implementation of Lend-Lease\n\n - Military contribution\n\n     - Canada supported the US and British plans for the development of the war, while the US determined the course of action. \n\n     - Canada's navy was fundamental during the Battle of the Atlantic, and it was the seat of the British Commonwealth Air Training Force, which was instrumental in the bombing campaigns in Europe and in defending Britain from the Luftwaffe.  \n\n     - US navy and Air Force determined the result of the war in the Pacific\n\n     - The Canadian army's first major engagement in Europe, the Battle of Dieppe in 1942, was a failure. \n\n     - Both countries had a meaningful participation in D-Day, 6 June 1944.\n\n     - In the Pacific theater, Canada played an important role only in the 1941 battle for the defense of Hong Kong, while the US was almost completely responsible for the defeat of Japan. \n\n     - Because of the war, the US suffered over 1 million casualties. One million Canadians served in the military during the conflict.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Second World War was a conflict that, in one way or another, involved most of the countries of the world. Two countries that had an impact on the outcome were Canada and the United States. The following essay will compare and contrast the involvement of these nations in the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). To do so, it will refer to why these countries got involved in the war as well as their economic and military contributions to the victory of the Allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). \n\nOne of the ways the US and Canada differed was in the reasons for becoming involved in the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). Canada was the first to declare war on the Axis Powers out of a loyalty to the British Commonwealth of which it was part. Canada was an independent country, however, its official head of state was the British monarch. When the UK declared war on Germany in 1939, there was a two-day debate in the Canadian parliament that determined Canada's entrance into the war. On the other hand the US's military participation came as a response to the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbour in 1941. In this case, the US only declared war on Japan, and the Germans declared war on the US due to their alliance with Japan through the Tripartite Pact.  \n\nHowever, participation in WWII was not limited to the military aspect. The US, like Canada, got involved in the war in 1939 as a way to help out the British. The US's initial participation concerned its economic contribution to the British war effort ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). Through the Cash and Carry scheme of 1939, belligerents could purchase goods from the United States as long as the recipients paid immediately in cash and assumed all risk in transportation using their own ships ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Since the British were the only ones with readily available cash and ships to transport, this was a way to assist them while remaining nominally neutral in the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).  \n\nBoth Canada and the US contributed to the war effort by supplying the Allies with the necessary materials for success ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). When Prime Minister Churchill informed Roosevelt they were out of cash to keep the Cash and Carry scheme going, it was replaced with the Lend-Lease Act. Like Canada's Mutual Aid programme of 1943, the objective was to assist the Allies with food, raw materials, and munitions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Due to the US's production capabilities, its economic impact on the war's outcome was much bigger ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). In fact, Roosevelt called his country “the arsenal of democracy” since without its supplies, the UK and the USSR would have found it impossible to continue the war. Roosevelt also recognized Canada's contributions by calling it the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$aerodrome of democracy\" due to its production of more than 16,000 aircraft ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Although Roosevelt's views are tainted with bias, it is undeniable that US production was able to keep the Allies supplied during the conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). Canada's contributions, though smaller in scale, were vital in supplementing the US, as noted by Roosevelt. \n\nWhen speaking about the military involvement of both nations, it can also be said that the US's role was critical, but that Canada's support was also crucial for Allied victory ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). The Royal Canadian Navy was indispensable in the Battle of the Atlantic, ensuring the safe passage of supplies across the ocean ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). By the war's end in Europe, Canadians escorted 25,343 merchant ships to help the UK continue its war effort and keep morale high during the Blitz ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). On the battlefield, Canada's first major participation was in the battle for the French port of Dieppe in 1942. While it failed in capturing the town, the information gathered was crucial for the proper planning of what would become the D-Day invasion of 1944 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). On the other hand, the US military role was vital in the success of all fronts during the war, except for the Eastern Front in Europe ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$).  This was especially so in the Pacific, where the US determined the war strategy and could be recognized as the leading actor in the defeat of Japan. From the Battle of Midway of 1942, which ended Japanese dominance of the Pacific, to the dropping of the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945,  it can be said that the US played a decisive role in the defeat of Japan ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Canada's only notable participation in the Pacific Theater was in the failed defense of Hong Kong in 1941 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). The different impact of the involvement of both nations had a lot to do with the amount of soldiers each was able to provide ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). While one million Canadian citizens served in the military during the war, the US suffered over one million casualties, with far more total participants. The overall population of each country and the magnitude of their armed forces clearly show that their military involvement would be different ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). \n\nIn conclusion, the involvement of Canada and the USA in the Second World War had many similarities and differences ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). While they declared war at different moments and for different reasons, both got involved initially to help Britain resist the Germans. Due to the size of their populations, it can be said that even though the impact of their involvement was different, their intentions were the same, and they complemented each other in the search for victory ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1001","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1001\n\nEvaluate the economic impact of the Second World War on **two** countries of the Americas.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organization}$)|\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer. |($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$)|\n\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$)$\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)|\n\n**General Guidance**:\n\n - The question requires that candidates consider the economic impact of the Second World War on two countries in the Americas. \n\n - The economic effects can extend beyond the stated time period, but they must be clearly linked to the question. \n\n - Candidates may offer equal coverage of the impact on both countries, or they may prioritize their discussion of either one. However, both aspects will be a feature of the response. A comparative approach may or may not be used. \n\n     - In the case of using a comparative approach, it is advisable to organize it by theme, exploring it first in one of the chosen countries and then in the other.\n\n - Each student should present their opinions or conclusions clearly and support them with appropriate evidence. \n\n**Possible content and examples**:\n\n - Candidates may refer to a wide range of effects of war on domestic economics, including increased GDP, higher employment rates, end of the Depression, higher inflation, industrialization and new industries, changes in trade patterns, changes in the economic role of government, economic integration of markets, and promotion of technology, especially new technologies.\n\n - Answers will vary according to the selected case study. Possible countries could be:\n\n     - Brazil: World War II propelled rapid industrial growth and a substantial GDP increase. Government investment and Allied demand modernized key sectors like steel and infrastructure. U.S. support accelerated urbanization and established Brazil as a regional economic power.\n\n     - Argentina: The wartime economy grew thanks to agricultural exports and foreign reserves. Industrial activity expanded, boosting prosperity in both rural and urban areas. Despite facing inflation and shortages, Argentina emerged from the conflict economically stronger and more diverse.\n\n     - Mexico: Its economy expanded quickly because of rising demand for raw materials and exports. Government collaboration with the United States fueled industrial growth and infrastructure development. This modernization set the stage for prosperity after the war.\n\n     - Canada: Depression ended, leading to quick industrial growth and more jobs. Massive government spending on war production made it a significant manufacturing power. Continued prosperity after the war and stronger economic ties with the U.S. followed.\n\n     - The United States:  The Great Depression ended, leading to economic growth and full employment. Massive government spending and war production transformed the U.S. into a global industrial leader. Postwar prosperity was driven by technological innovation, expanded infrastructure, and the growth of consumer society.\n\n - While other relevant factors, for example, social effects, may be referred to, they would be relevant mainly as context for the issue raised in the question.\n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWorld War II transformed American economies, notably in Latin America, by spurring swift financial growth, industrialization, and social change. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). This essay examines Argentina and Brazil by highlighting GDP growth, employment, industrialization, and government intervention. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ organization}$). The outbreak of war swiftly ended the economic stagnation of the 1930s caused by global conflict, and both countries saw GDP growth during the war years ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$).\n\nArgentina and Brazil experienced notable economic growth and change due to the Second World War. Argentina's GDP grew as war-driven demand for agricultural exports, including meat, wheat, and corn, brought in foreign reserves and boosted domestic consumption ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). Industrial output rose with the export boom, spurring prosperity in rural and urban areas. New meat-packing plants and grain mills sprang up near ports like Buenos Aires and Rosario ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Inflation and shortages posed challenges, but Argentina emerged from the war as a more economically diverse country ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). Brazil's GDP growth was even more pronounced. The Vargas government used the war for economic transformation, with Allied demand for raw materials fueling an export boom ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). Government investment led to rapid industrialization and high growth rates in the early 1940s. The National Steel Company (CSN) in Volta Redonda, funded by Brazil and the U.S., is an example of state-led industrial investment, like modern infrastructure, such as railroads and ports ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$).  The wartime boom also accelerated urbanization as rural populations moved to cities for new opportunities ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nThe war also spurred rapid industrial growth in both countries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). Previously, Argentina relied on agricultural exports, but wartime import disruptions prompted a shift to Import-Substitution Industrialization (ISI). New industries such as chemicals, machinery, textiles, and processed foods emerged with tariff protection and government support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). The government backed projects like the National Fertilizer Factory and Bagó Laboratories to boost domestic production of fertilizers and medicines ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Public-private partnerships and protectionist policies diversified Argentina's industrial sector ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). This period of import substitution and growth in the domestic market encouraged technological innovation and industrial expansion, supporting the emergence of new industries and helping Argentina reduce its dependence on agriculture ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). In Brazil, the Vargas government promoted heavy industry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$), founding the National Steel Company (CSN) in 1941 and laying the groundwork for Petrobras ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ examples}$). Production of durable goods, textiles, chemicals, and armaments grew. Import shortages from Europe and the US forced Brazil to develop its own national industrial sector. Brazil's education system began to support industrial needs with new technical institutes and training programs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). This emphasis on technical education met immediate labor needs, setting the groundwork for Brazil's postwar scientific and technological progress. These steps reflected the Vargas administration;s goal for a modern, self-sufficient industrial economy that shaped Brazil's later growth ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nBoth countries experienced a significant increase in government involvement in the economy, fueled by wartime demands ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ focus}$). The Argentine government increased its involvement through price and wage controls, export management, and investments in infrastructure and industry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). The Argentine state became a business state, as Fernando Devoto argues ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ perspectives}$). The state also expanded its role in labor relations and social welfare. In the early 1940s, Argentina established the National Labor Department to mediate industrial disputes and implement minimum wage standards ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ example}$). This helped maintain stability and reinforced the government's commitment to balancing growth with social welfare. These policies accelerated diversification and established the government as an architect of national development ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$). In Brazil, the state led the economic planning and industrial growth. Strategic investments and the creation of state-owned companies, like National Motor Factory (FNM) in 1942, boosted domestic production ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ example}$). Government policies also promoted public-private collaboration, which set the stage for postwar growth and adaptability to global changes ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).  A key development was the government's introduction of labor laws, minimum wage, and social security programs to protect workers in the industrializing economy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ knowledge}$). These measures improved living conditions, reduced inequality, and helped stabilize the workforce, laying a foundation for social inclusion and shared prosperity ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ analysis}$).\n\nIn summary, World War II was a turning point for Argentina and Brazil, ending their economic stagnation and sparking growth. The war increased GDP and drove significant changes in employment, industry, and state involvement. Argentina's shift to import substitution created a more diverse economy. In Brazil, wartime opportunities fueled heavy industry, infrastructure upgrades, and technical education, making it a regional leader in industrialization. Greater government involvement promoted innovation, expanded social welfare, and developed new sectors. Despite challenges like inflation and shortages, both countries emerged from the war more modernized and prepared for ongoing development ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ conclusion)}$."},{"ref":"HI1231","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1231\n\nDiscuss the reasons behind the United States’ decision to use atomic weapons against Japan in 1945.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nGuidance:\n- The question requires discussing the reasons that the US had to use atomic weapons against Japan in August 1945. It may be considered significant that they ended the Second World War or that the use of nuclear weapons was based on purely military motives to ensure a speedy victory. It may also be motivated by other factors, as shown below.\n- As it is a debatable historical topic, there are different opinions on it, for example, the historiographic perspective that considers the bombings as a military necessity to end the war and save lives, or the other one that interprets them as a politically motivated act.\n\nPossible evidence/examples to explore:\n\n- Desire to end the war quickly and minimize casualties.\n    - Invading Japan was planned (Operation Downfall), but was not carried out because it was projected to be much more costly in terms of casualties, both American and Japanese, with many projections in the hundreds of thousands of American troops and potentially millions of Japanese military and civilian lives. Furthermore, it would have taken much longer than the immediate “shock” of the nuclear bomb, so the bomb would minimize suffering.\n- Demonstrate power to the Soviet Union (early Cold War considerations) and prevent Soviet expansion in Asia.\n    - Had the US invaded, the Soviet Union would most likely have invaded as well, so the nuclear bomb was used to both signal US military and technological supremacy in the early Cold War as well as to prevent any further tension over the control of Japan with Stalin. By ending the war quickly with the nuclear bomb, the USSR would be unable to project power or influence further in Asia.\n- Justification for the high cost of the Manhattan Project.\n    - The Manhattan Project cost roughly $2 billion USD in 1945 dollars ($30-35 billion in modern terms). Some argue that the use of the bomb to end the war was a way of justifying the enormous cost to avoid the domestic political fallout of such a decision.\n- Force an unconditional Japanese surrender.\n    - As of 1943 (Casablanca), the US had committed to the policy of “unconditional surrender.” Japanese leaders still hoped to negotiate some form of terms that would preserve the power of the Emperor and avoid occupation. The US leadership thought the nuclear bomb would break Japan's will and avoid a negotiation that would set a dangerous precedent for future conflicts.\n- Lack of effective alternatives from the perspective of US leadership.\n    - Other alternatives were also considered, such as a “demonstration explosion” to more clearly display the power of the weapon and put pressure on Japan to surrender. Negotiated peace and modifying “conditional” terms of surrender were also possibilities, but both were dismissed because of concerns about the reliability of the bomb and due to previous commitments to “unconditional surrender”. The US felt that if a demonstration explosion failed, it would only serve to embolden Japan's resistance.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945 remain two of the most significant and debated decisions in modern history. The United States' choice to use nuclear weapons was driven by a complex mix of military, political, and strategic factors and has become a significant topic of scholarly debate ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). To fully understand this decision, it is essential to analyze both the immediate reasons behind it and the various interpretations offered by historians ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nU.S. military planners feared that a conventional invasion of the Japanese home islands would lead to catastrophic casualties ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Estimates for Operation Downfall projected between 400,000 and 800,000 American deaths, while millions of Japanese fatalities. The scale of anticipated losses led the U.S. Army to order 370,000 Purple Hearts in advance. Secretary of War Henry Stimson warned President Truman that an invasion could cost “over a million American casualties,” and General Douglas MacArthur predicted more than 100,000 casualties in just the first 90 days($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example/evidence}$). Meanwhile, intelligence revealed that Japan had mobilized civilians and prepared thousands of aircraft for kamikaze attacks, signaling that resistance would be ferocious ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). So, the atomic bomb was a way to prevent this bloodbath. At the same time, the bomb's unmatched destructive power was meant to shock Japan into surrender, demonstrating that resistance was futile ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nStrategic considerations also influenced decision-making ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). For instance, the Soviet Union had declared war on Japan in August 1945 and was preparing to advance into Manchuria and potentially Japan itself($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example/evidence}$). By ending the war quickly, the United States aimed to limit Soviet influence in postwar Asia and strengthen its own geopolitical position. It means President Truman's urgency to use the bomb quickly, not only to compel Japan's surrender but also to signal American military supremacy in the emerging Cold War order ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nAnother factor was the massive investment in the Manhattan Project ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Billions of dollars were poured into the effort ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{example/evidence}$). A great deal of scientific effort was mobilized, with Enrico Fermi leading the first controlled nuclear chain reaction in Chicago in 1942, Richard Feynman contributing to theoretical calculations at Los Alamos, and J. Robert Oppenheimer serving as the overall scientific director. The project also involved vast industrial facilities, such as Oak Ridge in Tennessee for uranium enrichment and Hanford in Washington for plutonium production ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Its successful deployment in Hiroshima and Nagasaki not only demonstrated the bomb's destructive power but also validated the enormous scale of resources and expertise invested in the project ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nWhile these reasons explain why U.S. policymakers acted, historians have presented different viewpoints on whether the bombings were necessary and moral. Richard B. Frank contends that the atomic bombings were crucial in avoiding the large-scale casualties expected from a conventional invasion, and that only the shock of nuclear devastation could force Japan's military leadership to surrender. At the same time, Robert James Maddox argues that Japan was not close to surrendering in August 1945, so the bombings ultimately saved more lives than they took ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). On the other hand, Gar Alperovitz contends that Japan was already weakened and that the real reason for the bombings was to intimidate the Soviet Union, signaling the start of Cold War power struggles. In the same way, Tsuyoshi Hasegawa argues that the Soviet Union's entry into the war against Japan was more pivotal in making Japan surrender than the atomic bombings, because Japan's leaders were more afraid of Soviet occupation than of American nuclear attacks ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nThe United States' decision to use atomic weapons against Japan in 1945 was motivated by a combination of military, political, and strategic factors. The reasons explained reveal the thought process of American leaders at the time. However, this decision cannot be fully understood solely in terms of military necessity. Geopolitical considerations also influenced it and have had a lasting moral effect. Ultimately, the atomic bombings ended World War II but ushered in the nuclear age, raising ongoing questions about the balance between military strategy, political power, and humanitarian responsibility ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1232","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1232\n\nDiscuss the reasons for cooperation and/or neutrality in the Second World War in **two** countries of the Americas (excluding the USA).\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nAny **two** of the following (one example of Cooperation and one of Neutrality):\n\n**Cooperation**\n- Brazil: Axis attacks on Brazilian merchant vessels; strategic alliance with the U.S., providing vital naval and air bases; military contribution with the Brazilian Expeditionary Force; Vargas has economic and political motives, such as modernizing Brazil's economy and securing military aid.\n- Mexico: German submarine attacks with the sinking of Mexican oil tankers; support for the U.S., closing ports to Axis ships, and supplying raw materials and labor to the Allied war effort; military role with the Escuadrón 201 (Mexican Air Force squadron); geopolitical alignment, strengthening Mexico's relationship with the U.S., and securing economic benefits.\n\n**Neutrality**\n- Chile: Governments faced pressure from the U.S. to join the Allies but resisted to maintain autonomy; Chile maintained significant trade with Axis powers, especially Germany and Japan; there was a gradual shift, as Chile broke relations with the Axis in 1943 under U.S. pressure, but only declared war on Japan in April 1945.\n- Argentina: Economic interests related to exports were vital to both Britain and Germany; political divisions between nationalist and pro-Axis sympathies within the military and government slowed alignment with the Allies; sovereignty concerns led to a view of neutrality as a means of resisting U.S. dominance in Latin America; late entry due to declaration of war in March 1945, just before the conflict ended.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMexico's entry into World War II was shaped by direct aggression, strategic necessity, and geopolitical opportunity, while Argentina's stance reflected a complex interplay of economic, political, and sovereignty concerns ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The analysis will therefore examine, in Mexico's case, the impact of German submarine attacks, its subsequent support for the United States, its military role, and its broader geopolitical alignment. In contrast, Argentina's position will be explored through its economic interests, internal political divisions, and sovereignty issues, which together explain its delayed entry into the war. This structure highlights how differing national circumstances produced distinct responses to the global conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{organisation}$).\n\nIn 1942, Mexico was drawn directly into World War II as a result of Germany's aggression ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). German U-boats sank Mexican oil tankers, including Potrero del Llano and Faja de Oro ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). These attacks violated Mexico's sovereignty and threatened its vital oil exports, leaving the government with little choice but to declare war on the Axis ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). The incidents galvanized public opinion, shifting Mexico from neutrality to active cooperation with the Allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). After aligning with the Allies, Mexico closed its ports to Axis ships, severing potential supply routes. It also became a crucial supplier of raw materials, particularly oil, copper, and other strategic resources, to Allied industry ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Additionally, the Bracero Program allowed Mexican laborers to work in U.S. agriculture and on railroads ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$), easing wartime labor shortages and strengthening ties between the two countries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Though modest in scale, Mexico's deployment of a fighter squadron to the Philippines symbolized its commitment to collective security and provided its military with international experience ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Sarah Pruitt argues that Mexico played a valuable role by supplying vital resources and labor, by deploying Escuadrón 201, by strengthening ties with the U.S., and by contributing to the Allied victory ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). In contrast, Juan Carlos Álvarez contends that Mexico's involvement was shaped more by U.S. geopolitical security needs than by autonomous strategy, making its military role largely symbolic and dependent on external pressure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$).\n\nCooperation strengthened Mexico's relationship with the U.S., its dominant neighbor. By siding with the Allies, Mexico secured economic benefits, including modernization of its armed forces and industrial infrastructure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Politically, it positioned itself as a responsible actor in hemispheric defense, thereby gaining prestige and leverage in postwar negotiations ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In sum, Mexico's cooperation was driven by Axis aggression, pragmatic support for the U.S., symbolic military participation, and the pursuit of long-term economic and diplomatic gains ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Although limited compared with that of major powers, its role marked a decisive shift in Latin America's involvement in global conflict ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn contrast to Mexico's cooperation, Argentina remained neutral until very late in the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). Argentina's exports of beef and grain were essential to Britain and Germany ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Maintaining neutrality enabled Argentina to continue trading with multiple markets, ensuring economic stability during the war ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Historian Julio Mutti notes that the decision to remain neutral also had an economic dimension, as Argentina was a major supplier of meat and raw materials to countries such as the United Kingdom ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). For example, Britain relied heavily on Argentine beef to feed its population, while Germany sought grain shipments via neutral intermediaries ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{examples}$). Within Argentina, nationalist factions and pro-Axis sympathizers in the military and government resisted aligning with the Allies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These groups admired aspects of German and Italian authoritarianism and saw cooperation with the U.S. as compromising national sovereignty. This internal division slowed any decisive move toward joining the Allied cause ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Some historians, such as David Rock, frame Argentina's neutrality as a defense of its independence from U.S. influence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{perspectives}$). Washington urged Latin American nations to join the Allies, but Argentina resisted, portraying neutrality as a means to assert autonomy in regional politics. This stance reflected broader skepticism of U.S. dominance in hemispheric affairs ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). Ultimately, Argentina declared war on Germany and Japan in March 1945, only weeks before the war ended ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). This late move was largely symbolic, intended to secure a place in postwar negotiations and avoid exclusion from the newly formed United Nations. By then, Argentina's neutrality had already marked it as one of the last major holdouts in Latin America ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$). In summary, Argentina's neutrality was less about indifference than about striking a balance among economic interests, internal political divisions, and resistance to external pressure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Its late entry underscored the pragmatic nature of its policy, which sought to protect sovereignty and trade while ultimately aligning with the victorious Allies to secure international legitimacy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nIn summary, Mexico cooperated with the Allies primarily due to Axis attacks on its shipping and its strong ties to the United States, which prompted it towards a policy of cooperation with the Allies. In contrast, Argentina remained neutral for much of the war due to domestic politics, economic interests, and a reluctance to fully align with U.S. pressure ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1478","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1478\n\nEvaluate the application and effects of Roosevelt's Good Neighbor policy.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**General Guidance:**\n\nAddress the Good Neighbor policy as a shift in U.S. hemispheric strategy, explaining how Roosevelt replaced military intervention and coercive diplomacy with principles of non-intervention, mutual respect, and economic cooperation. Explain “application” by identifying concrete actions through which the policy was implemented, such as treaty revisions, diplomatic negotiations, and responses to regional crises. Explain “effects” as multi-layered outcomes, assessing changes in sovereignty, diplomatic relations, regional perceptions of the United States, and long-term political or economic consequences. The strongest answers will integrate the two examples to evaluate both the successes and limitations of the policy, showing how its ideals were applied in practice and how its impact varied across different national contexts.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n- Cuba\n    - Roosevelt applied the Good Neighbor policy by abrogating the Platt Amendment in 1934, removing the legal basis for direct U.S. intervention in Cuban affairs.\n    - The cancellation of the amendment increased Cuban sovereignty and improved diplomatic relations after decades of U.S. control over domestic and foreign policy.\n    - Cuban leaders used the new diplomatic space to pursue more autonomous political and economic agendas, strengthening national legitimacy.\n    - Despite improved relations, economic dependence on U.S. markets persisted, limiting the transformative impact of the policy.\n    - The policy reduced overt intervention but did not eliminate U.S. influence, showing both the success and the structural constraints of Roosevelt’s approach.\n    - Cuba became a symbolic example of the Good Neighbor policy’s ability to reshape hemispheric perceptions of the United States.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some historians argue that the policy’s impact in Cuba was overstated because U.S. economic leverage and informal political influence continued to shape Cuban decision-making.\n\n- Mexico\n    - Roosevelt applied the Good Neighbor policy during the 1938 oil expropriation crisis by refusing to use military or coercive pressure despite strong U.S. corporate interests.\n    - The U.S. acceptance of Mexico’s nationalization of foreign oil companies demonstrated a concrete shift from interventionism to respect for sovereignty.\n    - Diplomatic negotiations replaced threats, allowing both governments to reach compensation agreements that preserved stability.\n    - Mexico’s successful defense of its economic autonomy strengthened regional confidence in the Good Neighbor policy.\n    - The crisis improved bilateral relations, reducing historical tensions rooted in earlier U.S. interventions and territorial disputes.\n    - The episode enhanced Roosevelt’s regional prestige, showing that the policy could withstand economic conflict without reverting to force. Long-term cooperation in trade and security reflected the durable effects of the policy and its contribution to hemispheric solidarity.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some scholars note that U.S. acceptance of the expropriation was driven partly by strategic concerns before World War II, suggesting that the policy’s principles were not the only factor shaping Washington’s response.\n\n- Brazil\n    - Roosevelt strengthened diplomatic cooperation with Brazil through high-level visits and cultural diplomacy, showing the Good Neighbor policy’s emphasis on partnership rather than intervention.\n    - The U.S. supported Brazilian industrial development and trade agreements, illustrating the economic dimension of the policy.\n    - Brazil’s alignment with the United States during the late 1930s and early 1940s reflected improved hemispheric relations rooted in non-intervention and mutual respect.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Some historians argue that Brazil’s cooperation was driven more by its own strategic interests and Vargas’s search for external support than by the intrinsic success of the Good Neighbor policy.\n\n- Nicaragua\n    - Roosevelt withdrew U.S. Marines from Nicaragua and ended direct military occupation, demonstrating a clear application of non-intervention principles.\n    - The policy encouraged diplomatic engagement rather than force, allowing Nicaragua to regain greater political autonomy after decades of U.S. involvement.\n    - Improved relations reduced overt conflict and helped stabilize the region in the short term.\n    - $\\underline{\\textrm{Counterargument:}}$ Critics note that U.S. support for the Somoza regime showed that the Good Neighbor policy did not eliminate indirect influence, limiting its effectiveness in promoting genuine sovereignty.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nRoosevelt’s Good Neighbor policy marked a major shift in United States hemispheric strategy in the 1930s. This essay examines Cuba and Mexico to assess how the policy was applied and the effects it produced in each case. ($\\checkmark$ focus) While both examples demonstrate a clear departure from earlier interventionist practices, they also reveal structural constraints, economic dependencies, and strategic considerations that limited the policy’s transformative potential. A balanced assessment requires analyzing both the policy’s practical implementation and the varied outcomes it produced across national contexts. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nThe application of the Good Neighbor policy in Cuba was most evident in the 1934 abrogation of the Platt Amendment. Roosevelt’s decision to cancel the amendment marked a concrete commitment to nonintervention and symbolized a break with decades of U.S. control over Cuban affairs. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) The amendment had allowed the United States to intervene in Cuba’s domestic politics and foreign relations, creating a political environment in which Cuban sovereignty was severely restricted. Its removal therefore signaled a major shift in hemispheric diplomacy. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe cancellation of the Platt Amendment increased Cuban autonomy and improved diplomatic relations. Roosevelt withdrew U.S. Marines and replaced coercive measures with treaty-based cooperation, allowing Cuban leaders to pursue more independent political and economic agendas. These actions demonstrated the practical application of the Good Neighbor policy and helped stabilize relations after years of tension. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The policy therefore reshaped the diplomatic landscape and reduced overt intervention, even if deeper structural issues persisted.\n\nHowever, the policy’s effects in Cuba were not absolute. Economic dependence on U.S. markets continued to constrain Cuban sovereignty, and informal political influence remained strong. These structural conditions meant that the Good Neighbor policy softened U.S. behavior without fully transforming the underlying power dynamics. Foreign intervention had ended, but economic leverage still shaped Cuban decision-making. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Historians such as Louis Pérez argue that the policy’s impact in Cuba was overstated because U.S. influence persisted through economic channels, while others highlight the symbolic importance of ending formal intervention as a meaningful diplomatic achievement. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Taken together, these interpretations suggest that the policy improved relations but did not eliminate asymmetries.\n\nMexico offers a second major example of the Good Neighbor policy’s application and effects. The 1938 oil expropriation crisis tested Roosevelt’s commitment to nonintervention more directly than the Cuban case. When President Lázaro Cárdenas nationalized foreign oil companies, including major U.S. firms, Washington faced intense pressure from business interests to respond forcefully. Roosevelt refused to use military or coercive measures, insisting instead on diplomatic negotiations and respect for Mexican sovereignty. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) This decision demonstrated that the Good Neighbor policy could guide U.S. behavior even when economic interests were threatened.\n\nRoosevelt’s approach in Mexico had significant effects. Mexico successfully defended its economic autonomy and strengthened its national legitimacy. Diplomatic negotiations replaced threats, enabling both governments to reach compensation agreements that preserved stability. The crisis improved bilateral relations and reduced historical tensions rooted in earlier U.S. interventions and territorial disputes. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The episode enhanced Roosevelt’s regional prestige and reinforced the idea that the United States could act as a cooperative partner rather than an imperial power.\n\nYet, as in Cuba, the Mexican case also reveals limitations. Some scholars argue that Roosevelt’s acceptance of the expropriation was influenced not only by Good Neighbor principles but also by strategic concerns before World War II. Maintaining Mexican support and regional stability was essential as global tensions escalated. These interpretations suggest that the policy’s ideals were intertwined with pragmatic calculations and that nonintervention was not the sole factor shaping Washington’s response. ($\\checkmark$ analysis) Historians such as Friedrich Katz emphasize Mexico’s agency and the strength of Cárdenas’s nationalist project, while others highlight Roosevelt’s desire to secure hemispheric unity amid rising global conflict. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nComparing Cuba and Mexico reveals important differences in how the Good Neighbor policy was applied and its effects. In Cuba, the policy addressed a long-standing legal framework that had institutionalized intervention. Its application was clear and symbolic, but its effects were constrained by economic dependence. In Mexico, the policy was tested by a major economic conflict. Its application required resisting domestic pressure and accepting a challenge to U.S. corporate interests. The effects were more transformative, strengthening Mexican sovereignty and improving bilateral relations. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe comparison also shows that the Good Neighbor policy operated differently across historical contexts. In Cuba, the policy reduced overt intervention but did not eliminate informal influence. In Mexico, the policy reinforced national autonomy and improved relations more substantially, demonstrating that nonintervention could succeed even during periods of economic confrontation. These differences are essential to evaluating the policy's overall significance. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nOverall, Roosevelt’s Good Neighbor policy marked a significant reorientation of U.S. relations with Latin America. Its application in Cuba and Mexico demonstrated a commitment to nonintervention and diplomatic cooperation. The effects included greater sovereignty, improved relations, and stronger regional confidence. At the same time, economic dependence and strategic considerations limited the policy’s transformative potential. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion) Therefore, the Good Neighbor policy succeeded in redefining U.S. behavior and improving hemispheric relations, but it did not fully resolve the underlying asymmetries that continued to shape the region.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n|:--|:-:|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples) |\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives) |\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"},{"ref":"HI1479","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 3","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI1479\n\nEvaluate the significance of the US use of atomic weapons against Japan.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**General Guidance:**\n\nThe command term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$evaluate\" requires candidates to make a judgement about the significance of the United States' use of atomic weapons against Japan, weighing its importance against other military, political and diplomatic factors associated with the end of the Second World War. Responses should move beyond describing the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki by assessing both their immediate and long-term significance. The strongest responses will distinguish between significance for Japan, the ending of the Second World War, and the post-war international order, while recognising that historians continue to debate whether the atomic bombings alone were decisive in securing Japan's surrender.\n\n**Possible suitable examples include:**\n- **Immediate military significance**\n    - Hiroshima (6 August 1945) and Nagasaki (9 August 1945) caused unprecedented destruction, demonstrating the devastating power of nuclear weapons.\n    - The bombings destroyed important military and industrial centres while inflicting enormous civilian casualties.\n    - In his surrender broadcast, Emperor Hirohito referred to the \"new and most cruel bomb\", indicating that the atomic attacks influenced his decision to end the war.\n    - Counterpoint: despite Hiroshima, Japan's Supreme War Council remained divided, with War Minister Korechika Anami and other military leaders continuing to favour resistance until after the Soviet declaration of war and Nagasaki.\n\n- **Contribution to Japan's surrender**\n    - The combination of Hiroshima, the Soviet declaration of war (8 August 1945) and Nagasaki rapidly transformed Japan's strategic position.\n    - The atomic bombings strengthened Emperor Hirohito's ability to overrule military hardliners and accept surrender.\n    - Historians such as Richard Frank argue that the bombings were decisive in convincing Japan that continued resistance was impossible.\n    - Counterpoint: historians including Tsuyoshi Hasegawa argue that the Soviet invasion of Manchuria was equally, or even more, significant because it destroyed Japan's hopes of Soviet mediation.\n\n- **Significance for the ending of the Second World War**\n    - Japan surrendered on 15 August 1945 and formally signed the Instrument of Surrender on 2 September 1945.\n    - The bombings helped avoid Operation Downfall, the planned Allied invasion of Japan.\n    - Contemporary Allied planning anticipated hundreds of thousands of Allied casualties and potentially millions of Japanese military and civilian casualties if an invasion became necessary.\n    - Counterpoint: Japan's military position had already become untenable because of conventional bombing, naval blockade, shortages of food and fuel, and the Soviet entry into the war.\n\n- **Significance for international relations and the Cold War**\n    - The bombings marked the beginning of the nuclear age.\n    - They demonstrated overwhelming American military and technological superiority.\n    - The Soviet Union accelerated its atomic programme, successfully testing its own nuclear weapon in 1949.\n    - Nuclear deterrence, mutually assured destruction (MAD) and the arms race became defining features of the Cold War.\n    - Revisionist historians such as Gar Alperovitz argue that the bombings were intended partly to strengthen the US diplomatic position towards the Soviet Union.\n    - Counterpoint: although atomic weapons transformed Cold War diplomacy, ideological rivalry and competing security interests remained the fundamental causes of the Cold War.\n\n- **Humanitarian and ethical significance**\n    - Well over 100,000 people died by the end of 1945, with many more suffering long-term effects from radiation exposure.\n    - The bombings raised enduring questions about civilian casualties, proportionality and the morality of nuclear warfare.\n    - Historians remain divided over whether the bombings were militarily necessary or ethically justified.\n    - Counterpoint: supporters argue that, despite the humanitarian cost, the bombings ultimately saved lives by preventing a prolonged invasion.\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nThe United States' use of atomic weapons against Japan remains one of the most controversial decisions of the Second World War because of both its immediate military impact and its lasting consequences for international relations. ($\\checkmark$ focus) Although the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki accelerated Japan's surrender, historians continue to debate whether they were decisive or whether the Soviet declaration of war played an equally important role. This essay argues that the atomic bombings were highly significant in bringing about Japan's surrender, but their greatest significance lay in inaugurating the nuclear age and fundamentally reshaping international politics. ($\\checkmark$ organization)\n\nThe atomic bombings made a major contribution to Japan's surrender by demonstrating that continued resistance was increasingly hopeless. Hiroshima was devastated on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki three days later, causing destruction on an unprecedented scale. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Emperor Hirohito later referred to the \"new and most cruel bomb\" when explaining Japan's decision to surrender, suggesting that the attacks strengthened his determination to seek peace. However, Hiroshima alone did not immediately end Japanese resistance. Members of the Supreme War Council remained divided, and War Minister Korechika Anami continued to favour fighting despite the devastation. ($\\checkmark$ examples) The bombings therefore strengthened the position of those seeking surrender, but they did not by themselves produce an immediate political consensus. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe significance of the bombings becomes clearer when considered alongside the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August 1945. The rapid Soviet invasion of Manchuria destroyed Japan's final hope that Moscow might mediate a negotiated settlement with the Allies. Historian Tsuyoshi Hasegawa argues that Soviet intervention was at least as significant as the atomic bombings in forcing Japan to surrender, whereas Richard Frank maintains that the unprecedented destructive power of the bombs was the decisive factor. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Rather than viewing these explanations as mutually exclusive, the evidence suggests that Hiroshima, the Soviet invasion and Nagasaki combined to convince both the Emperor and many civilian leaders that further resistance was futile. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe atomic bombings were also significant because they brought the Pacific War to a rapid conclusion. American planners expected that Operation Downfall, the planned invasion of Japan, would result in extremely heavy casualties, with contemporary estimates ranging from hundreds of thousands of Allied casualties to several million Japanese military and civilian casualties. ($\\checkmark$ examples) Japan's surrender on 15 August, formally signed on 2 September 1945, eliminated the need for such an invasion and brought the Second World War to an end. However, Japan's position had already deteriorated because of conventional bombing, naval blockade and severe shortages of resources. Consequently, the bombings accelerated the end of the war but should be viewed as one of several interconnected factors leading to Japan's defeat. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nPerhaps the greatest significance of the atomic bombings lay not in ending the war, but in transforming the post-war international order. ($\\checkmark$ focus) The destruction of Hiroshima and Nagasaki marked the beginning of the nuclear age and demonstrated overwhelming American military and technological superiority. The Soviet Union accelerated its own nuclear programme, successfully testing an atomic bomb in 1949, while nuclear deterrence and the arms race became defining features of the Cold War. Revisionist historian Gar Alperovitz has argued that the bombings were intended partly to strengthen the United States' diplomatic position towards the Soviet Union, although most historians continue to emphasise Japan's defeat as the primary objective. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives) Regardless of this debate, the existence of nuclear weapons fundamentally changed the way international crises were managed throughout the Cold War. ($\\checkmark$ analysis)\n\nThe bombings also possessed profound humanitarian significance that continues to shape historical debate. Well over 100,000 people died by the end of 1945, while many survivors experienced long-term effects from burns and radiation. ($\\checkmark$ knowledge) Critics argue that the attacks constituted an unnecessary use of force against civilian populations, particularly because Japan was already close to defeat. Others maintain that the bombings ultimately saved lives by preventing a prolonged invasion that would likely have produced even greater casualties. These competing interpretations illustrate why the use of atomic weapons remains one of the most contested decisions of the twentieth century. ($\\checkmark$ perspectives)\n\nIn conclusion, the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki were highly significant in ending the Second World War, but their importance extended well beyond Japan's surrender. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion) They strengthened those advocating peace within the Japanese government, hastened the end of the Pacific War and helped avoid an invasion, although they acted alongside the Soviet declaration of war and Japan's deteriorating military position. Their greatest significance, however, was the beginning of the nuclear age, which transformed international relations, reshaped Cold War diplomacy and permanently altered the nature of warfare. ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (13-15):|Labels|\n|:--|:-:|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question.|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples) |\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response evaluates different perspectives, integrating them into the argument.|($\\checkmark$ perspectives) |\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|"}]}]},{"name":"Independence Movements (1800-2000)","subtopics":[{"name":"Challenges & Responses in First 10 Years","questions":[{"ref":"HI0488","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0488\n\nList **three** economic challenges to Cuban Independence in the first ten years following Cuban Independence. \n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Economic dependence on the US \n\n - Fluctuations in the global price of sugar\n\n - Lack of industrial development\n\n - Foreign land ownership\n\n - High unemployment\n\n - Mismanagement of public funds\n\n - Limited infrastructure, which made trade difficult \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe first ten years after Independence, Cuba faced many economic challenges.  One challenge was the dependence of Cuba on the US for trade ($\\checkmark$).   Additionally, much of Cuba was still owned by foreigners ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, there was a lack of industrialisation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0489","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0489\n\nExamine **two** impacts of the US on Cuba's economy in the first ten years of Independence.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following impacts: \n\n - Sugar industry owned by the US\n\n - US land ownership\n\n - US control over resources\n\n - US banking and financial control\n\n - Unemployment \n\n - Labour exploitation\n\n - Lack of industrial development\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\nAny **four** of the following explanations connected to the relevant impact: \n\n - The reliance on sugar as the main crop led to a mono-economy\n\n - The sugar industry was seasonal leaving many Cubans unemployed in the off season with no alternative work\n\n - The US controlled not only land but also the infrastructure needed for trade which further made Cuba dependent on the US\n\n - Profits from the infrastructure and the sale of natural resources did not remain in Cuba for further investment but rather went back to the US\n\n - The US made Cuba reliant on investment from the US since the US controlled the banks\n\n - The Cuban peso was tied to the US dollar giving stability but also allowing the US to control Cuba’s monetary policies\n\n - Many Cuban workers faced horrible working conditions in US owned industry.\n\n - Many US companies preferred to hire foreigners over Cubans for higher positions in the US owned industries\n\n - The Reciprocity Treaty of 1903 gave Cuban sugar preferential access to U.S. markets, but in return, U.S. goods flooded Cuban markets, making it hard for local industries to develop.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne major issue was that the U.S. controlled vast amounts of Cuban land  ($\\checkmark$), including sugar plantations, railroads, and trade infrastructure. This control made Cuba economically dependent on the U.S. since American companies owned the land and controlled the infrastructure needed for trade  ($\\checkmark$). As a result, Cuba had little control over its own economic growth and was vulnerable to U.S. decisions regarding trade and investment ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, profits from the sale of natural resources and infrastructure did not remain in Cuba for reinvestment but instead flowed back to the U.S., preventing economic development ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother key factor was U.S. banking and financial control ($\\checkmark$), which made Cuba reliant on U.S. investment. Since the U.S. controlled the banks, Cuba depended on American loans and financial policies, limiting its ability to establish independent economic strategies ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the Cuban peso was tied to the U.S. dollar, providing stability, but also allowing the U.S. to dictate Cuba's monetary policies ($\\checkmark$). This meant that Cuba's financial system was not truly independent, making it difficult to develop a strong, self-sufficient economy."},{"ref":"HI0490","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0490\n\nOutline **two** key provisions of the Platt Amendment (1901).\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Made Cuba unable to sign treaties with other countries without US approval\n\n - Prevented Cuba from incurring debts to pay $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ordinary revenues”\n\n - Gave the US the right to intervene in Cuban affairs militarily\n\n - Required Cuba to execute sanitation plans to protect the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people and commerce” of Cuba\n\n - Cuba had to give the US land for naval bases, establishing Guantánamo Bay Naval Base in 1903\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Platt Amendment was a law passed by the U.S. Congress in 1901 that established the terms under which the U.S. would end its military occupation of Cuba following the Spanish-American War  ($\\checkmark$). It was later incorporated into the Cuban Constitution of 1901, effectively limiting Cuba's sovereignty  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0491","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0491\n\nExamine the impacts of the Platt Amendment on Cuba following the first ten years of Cuban Independence. \n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following: \n\n - The status as a US $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$protectorate\" restricted Cuba's ability to govern independently \n\n - Created a permanent US military presence in Cuba to enforce political stability\n\n - US Naval bases were built on a permanent $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$lease\" to the US (Guantánamo Bay most significantly)\n\n - Symbolised Cuba’s dependency on the US, which led to social discontent\n\n - Nationalist movements were beginning to form, but had little to no impact on the first ten years of Independence\n\n - Insured a US-dominated economy, which made Cuba reliant on US funds and trade.\n\n - U.S. control over Cuban foreign policy and trade agreements meant that Cuba's economy remained tied to U.S. interests.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne of its significant effects was that it made Cuba a U.S. protectorate, restricting its ability to govern freely ($\\checkmark$).  Although Cuba was technically an independent nation, the U.S. retained the right to intervene in Cuban affairs, which weakened Cuba's ability to establish a stable government without external interference  ($\\checkmark$). Another key impact was that Cuba had to give the U.S. land for naval bases, leading to the establishment of Guantánamo Bay Naval Base in 1903. This granted the U.S. a permanent military presence in Cuba and symbolized the country's lack of full sovereignty  ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, this led to the beginnings of early nationalist movements, which were not yet successful in the first ten years ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0492","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0492\n\nList **three** social challenges Cuba faced in the first ten years of Cuban Independence.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following: \n\n - Despite Afro-Cubans playing a significant role in the wars of independence, they faced widespread discrimination and were largely excluded from political and economic opportunities.\n\n - Many Afro-Cubans were denied government jobs and faced barriers to land ownership and education.\n\n - The Afro-Cuban Rebellion of 1912: the Cuban government violently suppressed demands for racial equality.\n\n - Cuba's economy was heavily reliant on sugar production, which was seasonal, leaving many workers unemployed during the off-season.\n\n - Poor wages and lack of alternative industries led to economic hardship, particularly for rural workers and former soldiers who struggled to find stable employment.\n\n - Many Cubans lived in poverty, while wealth remained concentrated among landowners and foreign investors.\n\n - Corruption and election fraud led to political instability, causing uprisings and protests among dissatisfied groups.\n\n - The U.S. military intervened in 1906-1909, further disrupting Cuban society and limiting the country's ability to govern itself.\n\n - Many Cubans felt their independence was incomplete due to continued U.S. influence and intervention, leading to growing nationalist resentment.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne challenge to Cuba was the discrimination that Afro-Cubans faced even after having contributed greatly to the cause of independence; they were treated less than other Cubans  ($\\checkmark$).  Another difference was that since the economy was based on sugar, which is a seasonal crop, for much of the year, many sugar workers were unemployed  ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, there was widespread corruption among the political elites, and this caused protests and uprisings ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0493","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0493\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the American perspective of Cuba in the early 20th Century?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cover of the sheet music for $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'll See You in C-U-B-A\", a song by Irving Berlin, for the musical revue The Greenwich Village Follies, 1919. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Independence%20Movements%20%281800-2000%29/Challenges%20%26%20Responses%20in%20First%2010%20Years/images/HI0493.jpeg\" alt=\"HI0493\">\n[Source: Music Division, The New York Public Library. (1920). *I'll see you in C-U-B-A.* Retrieved February 15, 2025. https://digitalcollections.nypl.org/items/510d47da-4f9a-a3d9-e040-e00a18064a99. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The illustration presents Cuba as a tropical paradise, reinforcing the idea of Cuba as an exotic getaway.\n- The woman, depicted in bright, flowing clothes and a seductive pose, suggests a romanticized and possibly idealized view of Cuban culture, catering to American fantasies of adventure and leisure.\n- The image reflects how many Americans saw Cuba not as an independent nation but as a tourist escape.\n- The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'll See You in Cuba\" implies that Cuba was a desirable location for Americans, particularly for leisure, business, or escape from restrictions in the U.S.\n- The reference to the Greenwich Village Follies, a popular American revue, suggests that Cuban culture was being commercialized and marketed for American entertainment.\n- The presence of Irving Berlin, a prominent American songwriter, highlights how Cuba was embedded in American popular culture, often depicted as a place for relaxation and enjoyment rather than a fully sovereign nation.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne message is that Cuban culture was being marketed to Americans as a source of entertainment and fantasy ($\\checkmark$).  As the source is an advertisement for a theatre play, aspects of Cuban culture were being commercialized and adapted for American audiences. This reflects how Cuba was embedded in U.S. popular culture, often viewed as a place of leisure ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0494","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0494\n\nBased on the text of Source A, list three things the Platt Amendment prohibits Cuba from doing.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Text of the Platt Amendment, initially introduced in the Army Appropriations Bill of 1901 and approved by the US Congress on May 22, 1903 and signed as a permanent treaty between the United States and Cuba.|\n\n[Source: Adapted from *Platt Amendment* (1903). Milestone Documents. National Archives. Retrieved February 14th, 2025 from https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/platt-amendment. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Cuba could not sign any treaty or agreement that might allow a foreign power to gain control or influence over any part of the island (Clause I).\n- Cuba was forbidden from permitting any foreign country to establish military bases, colonies, or other forms of control on its territory (Clause I).\n- Cuba was prohibited from contracting public debt that exceeded its ability to repay using ordinary revenues after covering government expenses (Clause II).\n- The U.S. had the right to intervene in Cuba’s affairs whenever necessary to preserve Cuban independence, maintain order, and protect life, property, and liberty (Clause III).\n- All acts, laws, and rights established by the U.S. during its occupation of Cuba were permanently validated and could not be overturned (Clause IV).\n- Cuba was required to implement and extend the U.S.-designed sanitation plans to prevent the spread of diseases, protecting both Cuban and U.S. economic interests (Clause V).\n- The sovereignty of the Isle of Pines was left to future negotiation through a treaty, meaning Cuba could not claim it as part of its national territory (Clause VI).\n- Cuba was required to lease or sell land to the U.S. for the establishment of coaling or naval stations(Clause VII).\n- Cuba was obligated to formally incorporate the provisions of the Platt Amendment into a permanent treaty with the U.S.(Clause VIII).\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCuba was not allowed to enter into agreements with other nations that could threaten its independence or allow foreign influence over its territory ($\\checkmark$). Cuba could not take on financial obligations that it could not repay using its regular government income ($\\checkmark$). Cuba was forbidden from allowing any foreign country other than the US  to establish military bases ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0495","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0495\n\nAccording to Source C, what did Marti believe the US’s intentions were for Cuba?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Cuba in 1898” by Jose M. Hernandez. Published by the Library of Congress as a part of the presentation *World of 1898: International Perspectives on the Spanish American War.* |\n\n:box[He [Marti] feared that if Cuba's struggle for independence continued indefinitely without the imminent prospect of success it would create conditions leading to U.S. intervention and ultimately to the annexation of the island. At one point he even came to believe, rightly or wrongly, that there existed an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$iniquitous plan to put pressure on the island and drive it to war so as to fabricate a pretext to intervene in its affairs and with the credit earned as guarantor and mediator keep it as its own.\" For this reason, he thought that Cubans had to achieve a quick victory and then present Washington with their political emancipation as an accomplished fact. Otherwise, they could very well shed their blood merely to exchange one master for another.]\n\n[Source: Hernandez, J.M. (2023, February 28). *Cuba in 1898.* Library of Congress. Retrieved June 10, 2025 from https://guides.loc.gov/world-of-1898/cuba-overview. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The US intended to intervene in Cuba and annex it.\n- The US might use instability in Cuba as a pretext to involve itself in Cuban affairs, claiming to be a mediator or guarantor, to take power in Cuba..\n- That the Spanish power would be replaced by US power\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nJose Marti believed that the US had a deliberate plan to annex Cuba ($\\checkmark$).  He suggested they might try to act as a mediator between Cuba and other powers as a pretext to take control ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0497","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0497\n\nTo what extent did Marti's concerns, summarized in Source C, become a reality according to Source A?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Text of the Platt Amendment, initially introduced in the Army Appropriations Bill of 1901 and approved by the US Congress on May 22, 1903 and signed as a permanent treaty between the United States and Cuba.|\n\n[Source: Adapted from *Platt Amendment* (1903). Milestone Documents. National Archives. Retrieved February 14th, 2025 from https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/platt-amendment. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from “Cuba in 1898” by Jose M. Hernandez. Published by the Library of Congress as a part of the presentation *World of 1898: International Perspectives on the Spanish American War.* |\n\n:box[He [Marti] feared that if Cuba's struggle for independence continued indefinitely without the imminent prospect of success it would create conditions leading to U.S. intervention and ultimately to the annexation of the island. At one point he even came to believe, rightly or wrongly, that there existed an $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$iniquitous plan to put pressure on the island and drive it to war so as to fabricate a pretext to intervene in its affairs and with the credit earned as guarantor and mediator keep it as its own.\" For this reason, he thought that Cubans had to achieve a quick victory and then present Washington with their political emancipation as an accomplished fact. Otherwise, they could very well shed their blood merely to exchange one master for another.]\n\n[Source: Hernandez, J.M. (2023, February 28). *Cuba in 1898.* Library of Congress. Retrieved June 10, 2025 from https://guides.loc.gov/world-of-1898/cuba-overview. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **three** of the following concerns paired with appropriate evidence from Source C:\n- Fear of U.S. intervention if Cuban independence was delayed or uncertain.\n- Suspicion of an \"iniquitous plan\" by the U.S. to engineer a pretext for intervention.\n- Worry that Cuba might exchange one colonial master (Spain) for another (the U.S.).\n- Urgency to win independence quickly and present it as a fait accompli to avoid domination.\n\nPossible evidence from Source C (The Platt Amendment):\n- U.S. retains intervention rights (Clause III):\n “The United States may exercise the right to intervene for the preservation of Cuban independence...”\n- Limits on Cuba's foreign policy and sovereignty (Clause I):\n “Cuba shall never enter into any treaty... with any foreign power...”\n- Requirement to lease naval bases to the U.S. (Clause VII):\n “The government of Cuba will sell or lease to the United States lands necessary for... naval stations...\n- Control over Cuban public debt and finances (Clause II)\n- U.S. oversight of internal Cuban affairs (sanitation, governance, law validation)\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C, the Platt Amendment, legally limited Cuban Independence by limiting its ability to interact with other countries and retaining a right to intervene, which Marti predicted would happen ($\\checkmark$). The Platt Amendment also limited Cuban sovereignty by promising to $\\lq$protect' Cuba, which Marti alluded to as a pretext for controlling Cuba ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, Marti suggested that the US would replace Spain as its colonizer and the control of Cuba outlined in the the Platt Amendment suggested the US would maintain a similar control oer Cuba that Spain did ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0554","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0554\n\nAccording to Source B, what does Taft promise to do concerning Cuba?\n[3 marks]\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  The Provisional Government of Cuba (Gobierno Provisional de Cuba) lasted from September 1906 to February 1909. This period was also referred to as the Second occupation of Cuba. William H. Taft was the Secretary of War of the United States and the Provisional Governor of Cuba in 1906. |\n\n:box[“To the People of Cuba:\\\n\\\nThe failure of [the Cuban] Congress to act on the irrevocable resignation of the President of the Republic of Cuba [Palma] or to elect a successor leaves this country without a government at a time when great disorder prevails, and requires that, pursuant to a request of President Palma, the necessary steps be taken in the name and by the authority of the President of the United States to restore order, protect life and property in the Island of Cuba and islands and keys adjacent thereto, and for this purpose to establish therein a provisional government. The provisional government hereby established by direction and in the name of the President of the United States will be maintained only long enough to restore order and peace and public confidence, and then to hold such elections as may be necessary to determine those persons upon whom the permanent government of the Republic should be devolved. In so far as is consistent with the nature of a provisional government, established under authority of the United States, this will be a Cuban government, conforming, as far as may be, to the constitution of Cuba. The Cuban flag will be hoisted as usual over the Government buildings of the island. All the executive departments and the provincial and municipal governments, including that of the city of Habana, will continue to be administered as under the Cuban Republic. The courts will continue to administer Justice, and all laws not in their nature inapplicable by reason of the temporary and emergent character of the Government will be in force. President Roosevelt has been most anxious to bring about peace under the constitutional government of Cuba and has made every endeavor to avoid the present step. Longer delay, however, would be dangerous. In view of the resignation of the cabinet, until further notice the heads of all departments of the central government will report to me for instructions, including Maj. Gen. Alejandro Rodriguez, in command of the Rural Guard and other regular Government forces, and Gen. Carlos Roloff treasurer of Cuba. Until further notice the civil governors and alcaldes will also report to me for Instructions. I ask all citizens and residents of Cuba to assist in the work of restoring order, tranquility, and public confidence.\\\n\\\nWilliam H. Taft Secretary of War of the United States, Provisional Governor of Cuba, Habana, 29 September 1906.”]\n\n[Source: Taft, W. H. (1906, September 29). *Annual Report of Charles E. Magoon, Provisional Governor of Cuba, to the Secretary of War via Archive.org.* Adapted version retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provisional_Government_of_Cuba. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Set up a temporary, provisional government on the island.\n- Ensure the safety and security of the Cuban population and their property\n- Organize and oversee elections to determine new leadership for a permanent Cuban government\n- To keep the government functioning under the Cuban constitution.\n- Restore peace and stability in Cuba.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSecretary Taft promised to create a provisional government to keep normal government systems functioning ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, Taft promises to restore peace and to secure the safety of the Cuban people ($\\checkmark$). Finally, he promises that the US will help organize elections to establish a new, permanent Cuban government  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0555","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0555\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the limitations of Source C for an historian studying challenges during the first ten years of Cuban Independence.\n[3 marks]\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the book “The Destiny of a Continent” (*El destino de un continente*) by Manuel Ugarte (1875-1951), an Argentinian writer, published in 1923. |\n\n[Source: Ugarte, M. (1923). *The Destiny of a Continent (El destino de un continente)*. Translation by G. Merle. Retrieved June 9th, 2025 from https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015059519978&seq=10. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following, with reference to origin, purpose **and** content:\n- The source was written in 1923, a time of strong US influence in Cuba, which could impact the author's perspective.\n- The author was a known proponent of Latin American independence who wanted Latin America to be free of foreign influence.\n- The author's goal is to highlight the negatives of US control and to encourage pan-hispanism in Central and South America\n- The scenario described in the text is hypothetical, meant to provoke debate or discussion, and therefore does not reflect historical realities.\n- The tone of the text is meant to be persuasive and biased against perceived American imperialism\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C reflects the author's ideological belief that Central and South America should be unified, limiting his perspective ($\\checkmark$). The text is also limited by the time at which it was published (1923), when the US was heavily invested in and involved in Cuba, thus influencing the negative interpretation by the author ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the content of the text is hypothetical, imagining a situation for Cuba which did not exist and therefore is limited in its usefulness to an historian researching challenges to Cuban Independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0556","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0556\n\nContrast what Sources B and C reveal about the challenges Cuba faced post-independence.\n[3 marks]\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The Provisional Government of Cuba (Gobierno Provisional de Cuba) lasted from September 1906 to February 1909. This period was also referred to as the Second occupation of Cuba. William H. Taft was the Secretary of War of the United States and the Provisional Governor of Cuba in 1906. |\n\n:box[“To the People of Cuba:\\\n\\\nThe failure of [the Cuban] Congress to act on the irrevocable resignation of the President of the Republic of Cuba [Palma] or to elect a successor leaves this country without a government at a time when great disorder prevails, and requires that, pursuant to a request of President Palma, the necessary steps be taken in the name and by the authority of the President of the United States to restore order, protect life and property in the Island of Cuba and islands and keys adjacent thereto, and for this purpose to establish therein a provisional government. The provisional government hereby established by direction and in the name of the President of the United States will be maintained only long enough to restore order and peace and public confidence, and then to hold such elections as may be necessary to determine those persons upon whom the permanent government of the Republic should be devolved. In so far as is consistent with the nature of a provisional government, established under authority of the United States, this will be a Cuban government, conforming, as far as may be, to the constitution of Cuba. The Cuban flag will be hoisted as usual over the Government buildings of the island. All the executive departments and the provincial and municipal governments, including that of the city of Habana, will continue to be administered as under the Cuban Republic. The courts will continue to administer Justice, and all laws not in their nature inapplicable by reason of the temporary and emergent character of the Government will be in force. President Roosevelt has been most anxious to bring about peace under the constitutional government of Cuba and has made every endeavor to avoid the present step. Longer delay, however, would be dangerous. In view of the resignation of the cabinet, until further notice the heads of all departments of the central government will report to me for instructions, including Maj. Gen. Alejandro Rodriguez, in command of the Rural Guard and other regular Government forces, and Gen. Carlos Roloff treasurer of Cuba. Until further notice the civil governors and alcaldes will also report to me for Instructions. I ask all citizens and residents of Cuba to assist in the work of restoring order, tranquility, and public confidence.\\\n\\\nWilliam H. Taft Secretary of War of the United States, Provisional Governor of Cuba, Habana, 29 September 1906.”]\n\n[Source: Taft, W. H. (1906, September 29). *Annual Report of Charles E. Magoon, Provisional Governor of Cuba, to the Secretary of War via Archive.org.* Adapted version retrieved June 25, 2025, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provisional_Government_of_Cuba. Public domain.]\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt from the book “The Destiny of a Continent” (*El destino de un continente*) by Manuel Ugarte (1875-1951), an Argentinian writer, published in 1923. |\n\n[Source: Ugarte, M. (1923). *The Destiny of a Continent (El destino de un continente)*. Translation by G. Merle. Retrieved June 9th, 2025 from https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015059519978&seq=10. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Source B focuses on political instability after independence and Cuba's failure to solve its internal political problems, forcing the US to intervene, whereas Source C implies that Cuba never had true sovereignty.\n- Source B focuses on the necessity of US intervention to restore peace in the country and prevent more violence post-independence. In contrast, Source C is more focused on the idea that US military intervention in Cuba was a threat if Cuba defied US will.\n- Source B focuses on making sure that it is visually clear that Cuba maintains its own sovereignty at least on the surface “All the executive departments and the provincial and municipal governments, including that of the city of Habana, will continue to be administered as under the Cuban Republic.” whereas Source C implies that Cuba only had superficial sovereignty to begin with and that it ‘bows’ to American will.\n- Source B focuses on the challenge of restoring peace and stability whereas Source C looks more at political and economic possibilities for Cuba to resolve economic and political challenges to independence.\n- Source B is a primary source from 1902 and states the US response to a the challenge of real political crisis in Cuba and Source C is a secondary source posing several hypotheticals and implications of a US dominated Cuba and the challenges it will bring.\n- Source B is very early on in Cuban Independence whereas Source C has the benefit of almost 20 years of Cuban Independence to reflect upon various challenges to independence.\n- Source B is focused on US military intervention to solve the challenges to peace in an independent Cuba and Source C poses hypothetical help from other foreign powers like France helping to resolve Cuba’s challenges.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B and Source C contrast in their opinions on the challenges posed to independent Cuba. Source B gives us the information that Cuba was allowed to keep its flag and other institutions indicating no challenge to independence whereas Source C implies that Cuba was only independent only superficially ($\\checkmark$). Source B discusses the US maintaining peace and stability militarily in post independent Cuba while Source C focuses more on other foreign powers hypothetically becoming involved to aid an independent Cuba ($\\checkmark$). Source B is written shortly after Cuba became independent and Source C has the advantage of being written twenty years after Cuban Independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0571","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0571\n\nAnalyse the value and limitations of Source D for an historian researching the demographics of Cuba up to 1902.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  This map is from the United States Geological Survey conducted in 1902. It represents the proportion of the $\\lq$coloured' to the total population of Cuba.  |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Independence%20Movements%20%281800-2000%29/Challenges%20%26%20Responses%20in%20First%2010%20Years/images/HI0571.png\" alt=\"HI0571\">\n[Source: Gannett, H. (1902). *A Gazetteer of Cuba*. Page 21. US Library of Congress. Retrieved  June 13, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/item/03005819/. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor value, any **one** of the following:\n- It allows for a regional analysis of the “colored” population Cuba.\n- It was compiled by the US Geological Survey and shows that the US was interested in mapping out the population of Afro-Cubans.\n\nFor limitations, any **one** of the following:\n- The source and date of collection of the information used to create the map are not provided, limiting the utility of the map.\n- The designation “colored” is vague and makes it unclear who is being considered in this category.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable as it helps illustrate the distribution of the Afro-Cuban population of Cuba just at the time of independence ($\\checkmark$). The source is also valuable because it suggests that the US had a particular interest in the racial makeup and distribution of Cuba ($\\checkmark$). However, the source of the data in the map is unclear, and the criteria for what was considered “colored” by the USGS at the time limits the utility of the map itself ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0752","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0752\n\nDescribe **four** immediate problems resulting from the Partition of India in August 1947.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- Indian independence was accompanied by the partition of the country into two independent states - secular India and a Muslim state Pakistan. The borders of the new states, the Radcliffe Line, was only announced two days after independence, causing chaos. 10-15 Million Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs found themselves on the wrong side of the border.\n- Partition resulted in the division of the states of Punjab in the west and Bengal in the east of India. The new state of Pakistan was thus separated by 1600 kms of Indian territory.\n- The announcement of the borders led to desperate migration of people leading to a complete breakdown of law and order and the slaughter of a million people in communal attacks. Infrastructure collapsed under the pressure; trains and roads were overwhelmed, and there was little food, shelter, or sanitation.\n- The resettlement of refugees resulted in a huge financial burden for the new states. Homes, land, and businesses left behind by migrants were often looted or seized.Property disputes and compensation became major administrative burdens.\n- Partition involved dividing the British Indian civil service, military, railways, postal systems, and treasury. The division of assets (e.g., cash reserves, military equipment) created tensions; notably, Gandhi’s insistence that Pakistan receive its agreed share of cash led to political crisis. New governments struggled with a lack of experienced administrators, especially in Pakistan.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe celebrations accompanying the independence of India on August 15, 1947 were quickly drowned in the chaos and slaughter that accompanied the partition of the country in the states of India and Pakistan. The Radcliffe Line that was to form the border between the new states was announced two days after independence. This caused chaos as 10-15 million Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs found themselves on the wrong side of the border ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe announcement led to desperate migration of these  people, resulting in a complete breakdown of law and order, with  a million people  dying in communal attacks. The new states faced a collapse of Infrastructure with  trains and roads being overwhelmed, and migrants facing a scarcity of food, shelter, or sanitation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe new state of Pakistan was separated by 1600 kms of Indian territory as partition resulted in the formation of the new country with the the division of the states of Punjab in the west of India and Bengal in the east of India ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe division of assets including cash reserves and military equipment together with a division of the British Indian civil service, railways and postal systems created further tensions. The new governments, especially in Pakistan, struggled with a lack of experienced administrators, and Gandhi’s insistence that Pakistan receive its agreed share of cash led to a political crisis in India ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0753","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0753\n\nDescribe **three** problems related to initial territorial consolidation faced by the new Indian state.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- At independence, over 560 princely states existed, each with varying degrees of autonomy. The Indian government led efforts to integrate them into the Indian Union through persuasion and, in some cases, force. Most acceded peacefully and rulers of princely states were asked to sign the Instrument of Accession, which formally recognized their state's merger with the Indian Union.\n- Resistance from some large princely states created major issues.\n- The states of Hyderabad and Junagadh had Muslim princes ruling over large Hindu populations. Hyderabad refused to join and were annexed to India by force while Junagadh joined after 91% of the population voted to join India in a plebescite held in 1948.\n- Kashmir was strategically placed with a Hindu prince and a predominantly Muslim population. Initially hesitant to join India, an armed uprising followed by a Pakistani-backed tribal invasion, forced the ruler Hari Singh to seek help from India and sign the Instrument of Accession on October 26, 1947. India sent troops to Kashmir to counter the invasion, marking the beginning of the first Indo-Pakistan War; the start of an ongoing dispute.\n- Pondicherry was handed over by France in 1954. Portugal continued to occupy Goa, which was invaded by the Indian army and united with India in 1961.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe newly independent Indian state was faced with the challenge of incorporating over 560 princely states that existed within the borders of the former British Indian state. These states had varying degrees of autonomy and the Indian government moved to successfully integrate most of them through persuasion and by offering to financially compensate them. These rulers signed the Instrument of Accession formally recognising their merger with the Indian Union ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, there was resistance from the Muslim rulers of Hyderabad and Junagad; states with large Hindu populations, who refused to join the newly independent Indian state. The ruler of Junagad agreed to join in February 1948, following a plebiscite, where 91% of the population voted to join India. The state of Hyderabad was annexed to India by force in September 1948 when negotiations with the Nizam of Hyderabad failed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nKashmir was to prove another challenge as the state was strategically placed and had a predominantly Muslim population. It was ruled by a Hindu prince who was initially resistant to joining India. An armed uprising within the state followed by a Pakistani-backed tribal invasion, forced the ruler Hari Singh to seek help from India and to sign the Instrument of Accession on October 26, 1947. India sent troops to Kashmir to counter the invasion, marking the beginning of the first Indo-Pakistan War; the start of an ongoing dispute that has still not been resolved ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0754","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0754\n\nDescribe the impacts of Communalism on post-independence India.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **five** of the following:\n- The rise of communalism (politicization of religious identities) threatened this principle of secularism adopted in the Indian constitution. Post-independence, groups like the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) and the Bharatiya Jan Sangh (BJS) promoted the idea of India as a Hindu nation.\n- Gandhi was assassinated in January 1948 by Godse, a young Hindu extremist and active supporter of the communalist RSS, opposed to the creation of a secular state in India. The shock of Gandhi's death strengthened the position of secularists in government and helped calm communal tension immediately after independence. In the 1952 general elections, the BJS won only 3% of the vote.\n- The BJS was succeeded by the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) in 1980 as the main Hindu nationalist party and emerged as a powerful force in Indian politics. It promoted the creation of a state modelled on Hindu beliefs and culture. The movement was stridently anti-Muslim and triggered communal riots.\n- In 1992, Hindu extremists demolished a mosque in Ayodhya, claiming it was built on one of the holiest Hindu sites. This resultant violence led to the deaths of 3000 people.\n- The 1998 general elections saw the defeat of the Congress and the emergence of the BJP, riding on the support from increasingly communalised politics, as the largest single party.\n- Rising Hindu nationalism has led to increased insecurity among religious minorities, especially Muslims and Christians. Hindu nationalism has blurred the lines between religion and state, raising questions about minority rights. Hindu nationalist influence has led to attempts to rewrite history textbooks, emphasizing ancient Hindu glories and minimizing contributions from Muslim rulers or other communities.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe rise of communalism (politicization of religious identities) in the post-independence years with groups like the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) and the Bharatiya Jan Sangh (BJS) who promoted the idea of India as a Hindu nation, threatened secular India ($\\checkmark$). The initial impact of this rising communalism resulted in Gandhi's assasination in January 1948 by Godse, a young Hindu extremist and active supporter of the RSS who was opposed to the creation of a secular state in India ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBharatiya Janata Party (BJP) in 1980, succeeded the BJS as the main Hindu nationalist party and soon emerged as a powerful force in Indian politics ($\\checkmark$). It campaigned to promote the creation of a state modelled on Hindu beliefs and culture, with a movement that was stridently anti-Muslim and which frequently triggered communal riots in different parts of India ($\\checkmark$).  As a result of increasing communalism in politics, the BJP emerged as the largest single party in the 1998 general elections after defeating the Congress ($\\checkmark$). Communalism promoted the rise of Hindu nationalism, blurring the lines between religion and state in India as well as raising questions about minority rights."},{"ref":"HI0755","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0755\n\nDescribe **three** responses to economic challenges faced by the newly independent Indian state.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The government adopted a mixed economy model, combining state-led planning with a regulated private sector.\n- India launched a series of Five-Year Plans starting in 1951. The First Five-Year Plan (1951–56) focused on Agricultural development while the Second Five Year Plan emphasised industrialization and public sector development focusing on heavy industries, steel and coal.\n- There was a shift from industry to agriculture in the late 1960s to make India self-sufficient in food production. The $\\lq$Green Revolution' with the use of high-yielding seed varieties, irrigation schemes, and the use of chemical fertilisers led to an impressive increase in agricultural production.\n- Government controls over the economy were relaxed in the 1980s, and India sought to become part of the world capitalist system. This led to an exponential growth of the economy in the 1990s.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe newly independent Indian state adopted a mixed economy model that combined state-led planning along with a regulated private sector, in order to meet the economic challenges they faced ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA series of Five-Year Plans were launched in 1951, the first of which focused on Agricultural development in the period 1951-1956, while the Second Five Year Plan emphasised industrialization and public sector development focusing on heavy industries, steel and coal ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe late 1960s witnessed a shift from industry to agriculture with the $\\lq$Green Revolution'. In order to make India self-sufficient in food production, there was a focus on  the use of high-yielding seed varieties, irrigation schemes and the use of chemical fertilisers, all of which led to an impressive increase in agricultural production ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0756","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0756\n\nExplain **two** responses of the Indian state to the social challenges faced after independence.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- The new government was faced with the challenge of improving access to education as India’s literacy rate was very low at 16%. The government focused on universal primary education. While there was an increase in the number of children attending school, compulsory education for all children was not achieved. Centres of higher education with the establishment of national institutions like institutes of technology and research establishments and universities helped support economic goals of industrialization. By 2010 literacy rates had risen substantially to 61%.\n- Despite the Constitutional guarantee of equality (Articles 14–16) women faced legal, social, and economic discrimination.New laws gave women equal rights in inheritance and ownership of property and greater rights in marriage. Societal attitudes especially in the rural areas proved an obstacle and educational opportunities for girls here lagged far behind that for boys.\n- The entrenched caste system marginalized Dalits and Scheduled Castes/Tribes, denying them education, employment, and social dignity. The 1950 Constitution specifically abolished untouchability. Affirmative action with reservations in education, employment, and legislatures aimed to promote social justice and representation.However social discrimination and caste oppression was still common in rural areas and at times linked to resentment over the preferential treatment of the government policies.\n- Linguistic and Regional Diversity was a challenge as India had over a dozen major languages and strong regional identities. The government responded with reorganization of states on linguistic lines through the States Reorganisation Act (1956). There was the adoption of a three-language formula in education with the inclusion of the state language along with English and Hindi.\n- Health services were another challenge in a country with a population of 350 million, facing regular epidemics of smallpox, plague, cholera and malaria. The government funded health services to train doctors, nurses and build hospitals and clinics. While life expectancy improved, it resulted in rising population growth that put pressure on land and resources. Population control focused on offering incentives for smaller families and a campaign promoting sterilisation which faced considerable resistance. Between 1947 and 2010 average life expectancy had risen to sixty -six years and the population tripled to nearly 1.2 billion people.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne of the main challenges facing the new government in India was improving access to education, as India's literacy rate was very low at 16% ($\\checkmark$). The government focused on both implementing access to universal primary education and ensuring the establishment and expansion of post-secondary technical education. This increased the number of children attending school; however, the goal of compulsory education for all children was not achieved ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Indian state also faced the challenge of ensuring access to health services for all its citizens ($\\checkmark$). The country faced regular epidemics of smallpox, plague, cholera, and malaria with a sizable population of 350 million. Health services were funded by the government to train doctors and nurses, as well as build hospitals and clinics. The results were tangible, with a dramatic improvement in life expectancy; however, the rising population growth put pressure on land and resources. Between 1947 and 2010, the average life expectancy had risen to sixty-six years, and the population tripled to nearly 1.2 billion people ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0757","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0757\n\nAccording to Source B, what were the steps taken by the Indian Government to deal with the influx of refugees following Partition?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Ramchandra Guha, historian, environmentalist, and writer, in his book $\\lq$India after Gandhi: The History of the World's Largest Democracy'. Published by *Pan Books*, 2008. |\n\n[Source: Guha, R..(2008). *India after Gandhi: The History of the World's Largest Democracy.* Pan Books. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://archive.org/details/IndiaAfterGandhiByRamchandraGuha/page/n29/mode/2up. Public Domain Mark 1.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Refugee camps were set up to provide shelter, with tents being set up to house the migrants.\n- The State provided basic staples of rice, flour, and lentils to help the refugees with food.\n- The State also organised a network of Indian and foreign social workers under the umbrella of the United Council for Relief and Welfare.\n- To help the refugees deal with the shock and trauma of dislocation, there were movies, including Disney cartoons shown every evening.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B in order to deal with the influx of refugees following Partition, the Indian government set up camps with tents to house the migrants and to provide shelter ($\\checkmark$). The government also ensured the provision of basic staples of rice, flour, lentils etc. to ensure the refugees were provided with food ($\\checkmark$). To help them deal with and overcome the shock and trauma of the dislocation and slaughter, social work organisations screened movies every evening, including Disney cartoons, which provided a way for them to de-stress ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0758","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0758\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the Indian foreign policy following independence?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  A political cartoon by K.Shankar Pillai titled $\\lq$Devil and the Deep Sea' published in *Shanker's Weekly,* June 25, 1950. Nehru leads the Non-Aligned Movement as Truman and Stalin watch with  concern.|\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Independence%20Movements%20%281800-2000%29/Challenges%20%26%20Responses%20in%20First%2010%20Years/images/HI0758.png\" alt=\"HI0758\">\n[Source: Pillai, S.K. (1950) *Devil and the Deep Sea.* Shanker's Weekly. Retrieved June 13, 2025 from https://archive.org/details/dont-spare-me-shankar. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The new Indian state is faced with a difficult choice with regards to its foreign policy as it faces the pressure of having to align with either of the two major superpowers- neither choice being appealing. This is shown in the caption, $\\lq$Devil and the Deep Sea'.\n- India has embarked on an independent foreign policy choice of Non-Alignment with either of the two major superpowers. This is portrayed by Nehru leading other nations taking a similar stand and holding the flag, $\\lq$No Alignment with Blocs'.\n- Both the major superpowers disapprove of the Indian stand. This is shown by the disapproving and angry glares of Truman and Stalin.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A suggests that India is faced with pressure  from both the USA and the USSR to align its foreign policies with that of either country. This is a difficult choice and not viewed as appealing as conveyed by the caption, $\\lq$Devil and the Deep Sea', signifying the danger from both choices ($\\checkmark$). The source also suggests that India has adopted an independent foreign policy of Non-Alignment with both the superpowers. This message is conveyed by Nehru leading other nations taking a similar stand and holding the flag, $\\lq$No Alignment with Blocs'($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0759","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0759\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and\nlimitations of Source B for an historian studying the impact of the Partition on post-Independence India\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Ramchandra Guha, historian, environmentalist, and writer, in his book $\\lq$India after Gandhi: The History of the World's Largest Democracy'. Published by *Pan Books,* 2008. |\n\n[Source: Guha, R..(2008). *India after Gandhi: The History of the World's Largest Democracy.* Pan Books. Retrieved June 16, 2025 from https://archive.org/details/IndiaAfterGandhiByRamchandraGuha/page/n29/mode/2up. Public Domain Mark 1.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The origin is valuable as the author is a historian specialising in Modern Indian history and trained in the skills of historical analysis. The date of publication is also valuable as the author would have access to documents in archives that historians writing closer to the event would have been unable to access.\n- The purpose of the source is valuable as it offers a comprehensive and accessible account of India's political and social evolution from 1947 and  highlighting how democracy took root in India.\n- The content is of value as it documents the steps taken by the Indian state in the immediate aftermath of the crisis caused by Partition.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- A limitation of the origin is the proximity to the latter events discussed in the book which was published in 2008. This can lead to a reliance on preliminary judgements as the long-term consequences of these events were still unfolding.\n- The source's focus is not just on the impact of Partition on India, as evidenced from its title ‘India After Gandhi: The History of the World's Largest Democracy',  referring to its focus on a broader history of India. This hampers a historian studying the impact of the Partition on post-Independence India.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of Source B is valuable as its author Guha is a well-known historian trained in the skills of historical analysis and specialising in Modern Indian history. The date of publication in 2008 is also valuable as the author would have accessed documents in archives that would not have been available to historians writing closer to the event ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose of the source holds value as it offers a comprehensive account of India's political and social evolution after independence, highlighting how democracy took root in India following the trauma of partition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content also holds value as it details the steps taken by the Indian state in the immediate aftermath of the crisis caused by Partition and records the trauma of people caught up in events beyond their control ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the origin of the source is limited in relation to its discussion of events during the later years following independence, due to the proximity of these events to the publication of the book in 2008. As the long-term consequences of these events were still unfolding, this could lead to a reliance on preliminary judgements in analysis ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$)."},{"ref":"HI0760","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0760\n\nAccording to Source C, what were the main reasons for the adoption of the Five-Year Plan in post-Independence India?\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | L.M. Singhvi, jurist, parliamentarian, scholar, and diplomat, in his book, $\\lq$India: Government and Politics'. Published by *Berkeley, South Asia Project, University of California*, 1956.|\n\n[Source: Singhvi, L.M.(1956). *India: Government and Politics.* Berkeley, South Asia Project, University of California. Retrieved June 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b181209. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- It was adopted to mobilize the country's available resources and raise the living standards of the large population.\n- Ensure that the state implements extensive developmental programs.\n- To ensure improvements to the economy without undermining the democratic institutions set up by the Constitution.\n- To supplement and support economic development, as private capital was limited.\n- To ensure that the electorate's demands, which expected a speedy improvement in its conditions of chronic poverty, are met.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIndia adopted economic planning based on Five-Year Plans, for the government to mobilize and use the country's resources and raise the living standards of its large population ($\\checkmark$). This also ensured the government could implement and spearhead extensive developmental programs in specific areas that would be implemented by the state ($\\checkmark$). It was also felt that government leadership was necessary for economic development, as private capital for investment was limited in newly independent India ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, government guidance was to ensure economic development without undermining the democratic institutions set up by the Constitution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0761","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0761\n\nAccording to Source C, what was the impact of the expansion of the public sector on India's private sector?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | L.M. Singhvi, jurist, parliamentarian, scholar, and diplomat, in his book, $\\lq$India: Government and Politics'. Published by *Berkeley, South Asia Project, University of California*, 1956.|\n\n[Source: Singhvi, L.M.(1956). *India: Government and Politics.* Berkeley, South Asia Project, University of California. Retrieved June 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b181209. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- There was less freedom for private enterprise as the Indian government now exercised a greater measure of regulation over the private sector of the economy at strategic points.\n- The private sector was disadvantaged because the growth and expansion of the public sector had been far more rapid than that of the private sector.\n- The Five-Year Plan emphasised the Indian government's socialistic outlook, which could be detrimental to the growth of the private sector.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source C, due to the expansion of the public sector, India's private sector now found that the State exercised greater regulation, leaving less freedom for the private sector to expand and flourish ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe rapid growth and expansion of the public sector as a result of the Five-Year Plan further disadvantaged the private sector and hampered its economic interests ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1040","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1040\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of the Platt Amendment in Cuba.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Platt Amendment authorized future US intervention should it be felt necessary by the USA.\n- The US was granted two naval bases, including the one at Guantanamo Bay.\n- The Platt Amendment made the Cuban economy heavily dependent on the United States.\n- Cuba was not allowed to make alliances without US approval.\n- Cuba fell under the US sphere of influence by including the Amendment in the Cuban Constitution.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe 1903 Commercial Reciprocity Treaty incorporated the Platt Amendment into law, granting duty reductions on certain goods exchanged between the USA and Cuba. Cuban sugar and tobacco were tied to US markets through preferential tariffs, while US goods entered Cuba at reductions varying from 25 to 40 percent ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Platt Amendment limited Cuban sovereignty by granting the United States rights to intervene in Cuba's affairs. This provision was applied in 1906 when Cuba was occupied by US forces after the collapse of Cuban president Tomás Estrada Palma's government ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Methods Used & Reasons for Success","questions":[{"ref":"HI0225","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0225\n\nExplain **two** reasons why some black people decided to fight for the royalists against the patriots in the Latin American wars of independence.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - They believed the Spanish Crown gave them a better chance to achieve their political ends.\n\n    - A reason is some black people considered the authority of the Spanish Crown conducive to achieve their political ends. In previous years, communities of black freed people, called cimarrones, were working towards their political recognition utilizing their knowledge of the colonial legal framework. \n\n - They believed the king of Spain would better protect them from their creole masters.\n\n     - A reason some enslaved black people pledged loyalty to the king of Spain and opposed revolutionary insurgents is they saw the Crown as a protector from their creole masters. This is true, for example, in areas like Cali and Popayán in New Granada. More evidence of this can be seen in the 1789 slave law issued by Spain, which aimed to improve the living and working conditions of enslaved people. In contrast, Creole slave owners resisted these reforms, leading to the law's suspension in 1794 across much of the Spanish Caribbean.\n\n - They thought the Spanish Crown would grant them greater economic freedoms. \n\n     - Freedom was closely tied to aspirations for land ownership and recognition as members of society with rights. This pattern was evident not just in Popayán, New Granada, and the Caribbean, where enslaved people were often granted land or legal rights as a reward for their involvement in conflicts. In Esmeraldas, New Granada, enslaved workers reached agreements with royalist forces (aligned with the Spanish Crown), allowing them to retain control over mining operations in exchange for paying tribute.\n\n - Spain promised them freedom in exchange for their service.\n\n     - A reason blacks supported the royalist faction was that Spanish authorities promised enslaved black people that if they fought for the royalist armies, they would be freed. This is the case, for example, in Popayán, where the governors promised enslaved people freedom and armed them to fight for the royalist side.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason why some Black people decided to fight for the royalists was that Spanish authorities promised enslaved Black people freedom in exchange for their service in the royalist armies ($\\checkmark$). This was the case, for example, in Popayán, New Granada, where the governor promised freedom to enslaved individuals and armed them to fight for the royalist cause. In such cases, the backlash from their Creole owners against the governor reinforced the belief that the revolutionary (patriot) faction was unlikely to grant them freedom ($\\checkmark$). A second reason was economic. For black people, freedom was closely tied to aspirations for land ownership ($\\checkmark$). This was evident not just only in New Granada, but in the Caribbean, where enslaved people were often granted land as a reward for their involvement in conflicts. In Esmeraldas, New Granada, enslaved workers reached agreements with royalist forces, allowing them to retain control over mining operations in exchange for paying tribute ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0483","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0483\n\nDescribe **two** violent methods used to achieve Cuban Independence. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Both guerrilla and traditional open warfare: \n\n     - Cuban insurgents known as mambises used common guerrilla tactics, including hit-and-run, ambush, and sabotage against Spanish forces. They were noted for their $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$machete charge”.\n\n     - Cuban rebels also used traditional open battles when strategic opportunities arose. This combination allowed rebel forces to disrupt Spanish control across rural areas while avoiding prolonged direct confrontation, which favored the better-equipped colonial army.\n\n - Destruction of Property\n\n     - Cuban Rebels deliberately burned sugar plantations, mills, and railroads, targeting the economic heart of Spanish power in Cuba. \n\n     - The goal was to undermine the profitability of the colony, discourage foreign investment (particularly American), and force Spain to abandon the island.\n\n- Assassinations and Raids\n\n     - Cuban Rebels Cuban rebels carried out assassinations of colonial officials, military officers, and loyalist collaborators.\n\n     - Armed raids on towns, military outposts, and supply routes were also common, allowing rebels to capture weapons, provisions, and disrupt Spanish communications. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOutside the armed rebellions, there was sporadic guerrilla warfare whereby the Cuban rebels used hit-and-run tactics or ambushed Spanish forces ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, there was a clear attempt to destroy property in an effort to economically impact the country. Often, rebels raided towns and supply routes in an effort to disrupt trade ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0484","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0484\n\nExplain **two** impacts of guerrilla tactics in the Cuban War of Independence.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence:\n\n - Maximize mobility \n\n     - Invasion of the West - Led by Máximo Gómez and Antonio Maceo in 1895-96, this campaign moved rebel forces over 1,800 kilometers from eastern to western Cuba, covering nearly the entire island. \n\n     - The rebels moved in small, fast-moving columns on horseback, often with minimal supplies, enabling them to evade larger Spanish forces.\n\n - Strike Unpredictably\n\n     - Rebel fighters launched surprise attacks on Spanish patrols, supply lines, and small garrisons, often melting back into the countryside before reinforcements could arrive.\n\n    - Rebels used mountain passes, dense forests, and sugarcane fields to hide movements and strike from unexpected directions.\n\n    - In places like the Sierra Maestra and Camagüey plains, Cuban forces had a tactical advantage through local knowledge\n\n - Control rural areas\n\n    - The Cuban rebels drew support from rural peasants, former slaves, and small farmers, who provided food, shelter, and intelligence.\n\n    - By burning sugar plantations and mills, the rebels not only damaged Spain's economic interests but also drove Spanish troops out of rural production zones\n\n    - Established hidden trails and supply lines across the countryside, allowing them to move between provinces and coordinate regional offensives\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nGuerrilla tactics allowed rebels to strike unpredictably because they were familiar with the terrain and could use forests and mountain passes ($\\checkmark$). Specifically, knowledge of the Sierra Maestra proved advantageous to rebels in their use of guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the guerrilla tactics allowed the rebels to control the rural areas ($\\checkmark$). The Spanish presence was often more significant in the cities, and the rebels knew the locals who might provide support and unique knowledge that the Spanish could not ascertain ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0485","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0485\n\nDescribe the role and significance of the Cuban Revolutionary Party (PRC) in the Wars for Cuban Independence.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **one** of the following roles with relevant arguments for significance from below:\n\n - Organise Cuban Independence into a single movement\n\n - Unify all Cubans across class, race, and region\n\n - Provide a cohesive national structure\n\n - Coordinate military \n\nFor significance, any **two** of the following: \n\n - Promoted a vision of a democratic, inclusive republic for all Cubans\n\n - Mobilized the Cuban exile community, especially in the United States \n\n - Through the PRC, trusted military leaders like Máximo Gómez and Antonio Maceo were appointed, linking the party's political aims with experienced military leadership.\n\n - Allowed for a cohesive political structure that functioned to lead the movement for independence\n\n - Created an organisation that could function to raise money and support for the movement both inside Cuba and internationally.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Cuban Revolutionary Party's establishment allowed Cuban Independence to be more effectively organised ($\\checkmark$). This was significant in that it allowed for a political structure which could not only lead the movement ($\\checkmark$) but also legitimised the independence movement, enabling the rebels to look for support abroad ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0486","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0486\n\nExplain the effect of **two** non-violent methods used to achieve independence in Cuba.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following methods with supporting discussion:\n\n - The establishment of the Cuban Revolutionary Party (Partido Revolucionario Cubano) in 1892.\n\n     - Helped organize and unify the independence movement\n\n     - Ensured that efforts to support Cuban independence inside and outside of Cuba were coordinated. \n\n     - This diplomatic work ultimately contributed to U.S. intervention in the Spanish-American War (1898), which resulted in Cuba's independence from Spain.\n\n - Propaganda\n\n     - Through writings, speeches, and diplomatic efforts, Martí and others successfully rallied support in the United States and Latin America, influencing public opinion and securing funding for the revolutionary cause.\n\n     - These publications created a strong sense of Cuban identity and convinced more people to join the independence cause.\n\n     - By shaping public opinion, propaganda efforts weakened Spanish influence and increased the momentum for independence.\n\n - Boycotts\n\n    - Calls for boycotting Spanish goods and refusing to cooperate economically with colonial authorities undermined Spain's control over the Cuban economy. \n\n     - By limiting Spain's ability to profit from its colony, economic resistance weakened the Spanish government's financial ability to sustain its rule.\n\n - Public protest\n\n     - Public marches and rallies organized by Cuban nationalists kept the independence movement visible and pressured Spain to consider reforms. \n\n     - Protests helped to maintain morale, spread revolutionary ideas, and increase civilian involvement in the fight for independence.\n\n - Covert underground revolutionary groups\n\n     - Plan uprisings, spread information, and mobilize resistance across Cuba. \n\n     - These networks provided essential coordination for rebel forces, ensuring that independence efforts were organized and sustained over time.\n\n- International financial support\n\n    - Cuban exiles, particularly in the United States, Mexico, and Latin America, played a key role in raising funds and securing weapons for the independence struggle. \n\n - Rise of Cuban Nationalism\n\n     - The promotion of Cuban art, music, and literature strengthened a shared national identity\n\n     - Cultural nationalism inspired people to support independence and reinforced the idea that Cuba should be a sovereign nation, free from colonial control.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne non-violent method contributing to Cuban independence was establishing the Cuban Revolutionary Party (PRC) in the United States ($\\checkmark$). The PRC helped organise the Independence Movement both inside and outside of Cuba, drawing support not only from the US, but also from other Latin American countries that provided funds and donations used to wage war against the Spanish ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, there were calls to boycott Spanish products and goods ($\\checkmark$). This had the intention to undermine Spain's control over the Cuban economy and to weaken Spain in general ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0487","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0487\n\nExplain the role and significance of José Martí to the Cuban Independence Movement. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - Political leadership\n\n     - Founder of the Cuban Revolutionary Party in 1892 which served as the ideological motivation for the Independence movement\n\n     - Promoted Cuban nationalism - wanted a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Cuba for all Cubans\" including Afro-Cubans and the working class\n\n - Writer and propagandist\n\n     - His essays and speeches, like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Nuestra América\" (1891), called on Latin Americans to embrace their identity and resist both colonialism and U.S. imperialism.\n\n     - Through newspapers like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Patria”, he reached both Cuban exiles and readers on the island, denouncing Spanish abuses and arguing for unity, liberty, and justice.\n\n - Military role and martyrdom\n\n     - He returned to Cuba in 1895 to fight despite lack of military experience because he believed it was important to set an example and fight physically, not just ideologically. \n\n     - He died in battle becoming a martyr for the cause\n\n - Secured international support for the movement\n\n     - Martí mobilized Cuban exiles, especially in the United States, raising funds and building revolutionary networks.\n\n     - He wrote for major newspapers abroad which brought international awareness to his cause\n\n     - He traveled throughout Latin America building support for Cuban Independence\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nJose Marti is significant to the Cuban Independence Movement because of his role as the founder of the Cuban Revolutionary Party ($\\checkmark$)  and his writings which were the ideological background to the Independence Movement. His role as a writer not only gave the party an ideological focus but it also helped get international funding for the revolution ($\\checkmark$) by writing for foreign newspapers and traveling extensively throughout Latin America. His role as a martyr came after he returned to Cuba to lead the movement ($\\checkmark$). He felt it was important to physically participate in the struggle which sadly cost him his life but his status as a martyr influenced and inspired the future revolutionaries who achieved independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0547","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0547\n\nDescribe the role and significance of Carlos Manuel de Céspedes in the Cuban War for Independence.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** or more of the following for role:\n- He is considered the father of the Cuban independence\n- He is known for the Grito de Yara - a public call for Cuban Independence.\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following for significance:\n- He freed his slaves and tied the cause for independence with the abolition of slavery.\n- Became a popular leader and revolution figure among Afro-Cubans who played a critical role in independence.\n- He is known as the one who began the Ten Years War which, though it failed to achieve independence, laid the groundwork for future movements.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCarlos Manuel de Céspedes is known as the Father of Cuban independence for his leadership in the revolution and because he publicly called for Cuban Independence ($\\checkmark$). He is also known for freeing his slaves ($\\checkmark$), which was significant because he linked the abolition of slavery with independence in Cuba ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0548","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0548\n\nDescribe the role and significance of Antonio Maceo in the Cuban War of Independence.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** or more of the following for role:\n- He was a military commander for the Ten Years War and later for the Cuban War of Independence.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nAny **two** of the following for significance:\n- He was a brilliant strategist in guerrilla warfare and led the famous Invasion from East to West (1895-1896)\n- Was unhappy with the Pact of Zanjón and issued the Protest of Baraguá, calling for further resistance and full independence of Cuba.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAntonio Maceo was an Afro-Cuban military commander during the Cuban War of Independence ($\\checkmark$). He was known to be an excellent military commander and expert in guerrilla warfare ($\\checkmark$). He also vocally rejected the Pact of Zanjón, which he viewed as an inadequate compromise, and pushed for complete Cuban independence from Spain ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0549","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0549\n\nCompare and contrast the roles and significance of Máximo Gómez and Calixto García in the Cuban Independence Movement.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities with supporting evidence:\n- Both were veterans of all three Wars of Independence, which earned them considerable support and respect, as well as strategic experience and deep knowledge of guerrilla warfare in Cuban terrain.\n- Both were among the most experienced military leaders. They both held high-ranking military positions: Gómez served as a General in Chief, and García was a major General and commander in the East.\n- They were both deeply committed to the cause of independence; Both made personal sacrifices for independence. García was captured and attempted suicide to avoid dishonor; Gómez fought for Cuban freedom, though he was from the Dominican Republic.\n- Both were seen as symbols of the resistance and respected at home in Cuba and internationally. They represented the struggle for independence from Spanish imperialism throughout Latin America.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences with supporting evidence:\n- Different origins: Máximo Gómez was born in the Dominican Republic and fought for Cuban Independence as a foreigner. He saw Cuba's struggle as part of a larger fight against imperialism. Calixto García came from an established Creole-Cuban family with a long history of rebellion. Both his father and grandfather fought against Spanish imperialism.\nContrasting military strategies: Máximo Gómez was known for his scorched earth policy and his determination to economically weaken Spain, whereas Calixto García focused more on strategic battles and guerrilla warfare.\n- Differing roles in diplomacy: Máximo Gómez was focused on military command, while Calixto Garcia was more focused on coordination with the US during the Spanish-American War.\n- Differing relations/perspectives of the US: Máximo Gómez was wary of working with the US, whereas Calixto García advocated for more cooperation with the US, particularly in the future after the war.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMáximo Gómez and Calixto García played significant roles in the Cuban Independence Movement, marked by shared military experience, deep commitment, and widespread respect. Both men were veterans of all three wars for independence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$), which provided them with battlefield experience and intimate knowledge of Cuba’s terrain ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Each held a significant military command ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Gómez became General-in-Chief of the Cuban Liberation Army, while García commanded key forces in eastern Cuba as a major general ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Beyond their tactical war experience, both figures came to represent the Cuban resistance and were admired both within Cuba and by pro-independence supporters across Latin America ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nDespite their shared goals, the two leaders differed in background, strategy, and international engagement. Máximo Gómez, born in the Dominican Republic, was an outsider by nationality ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) and viewed Cuba's independence as part of a broader anti-imperialist/anti-Spanish struggle ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In contrast, Calixto García was Cuban-born, from a Creole family deeply rooted in rebellion ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) as both his father and grandfather had taken part in earlier uprisings against Spain ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Strategically, Gómez favored a scorched-earth approach ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$), destroying sugar plantations to destabilize Spanish power economically ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), whereas García concentrated on targeted guerrilla battles and territorial gains, particularly in eastern Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference and evidence}$). Their relationships with the United States were also different ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Gómez remained cautious of U.S. intentions in Cuba ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), while García actively collaborated with American forces during the Spanish-American War, hoping for support in the island’s transition to independence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0550","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0550\n\nDiscuss the significance of Tomas Estrada Palma's contributions to the Cuban Independence Movement.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following arguments supporting the significance of his contributions:\n- Unity and continuity: Palma took over leadership of the Cuban Revolutionary Party after Martí died in 1895, which helped keep unity and continuity in the independence movement.\n- Organisation of various peoples: Palma was able to unify the various factions of the Cuban population (class, race, and religious differences)  under the cause of independence.\n- Coordination and organisation: Palma mobilized the Cuban exiles in the United States, which helped coordinate fundraising from abroad and build relationships with key American political figures.\n- Appointment of good military leadership: Palma was able to link strong military leaders (Gomez and Garcia) to the Cuban Revolutionary Party, and they were instrumental in helping win independence.\n- International relationships: Palma maintained a good relationship with the US, which led to the Spanish-American War, which ended Spain's colonial rule in Cuba.\n\nAny **two** of the following counterclaims rejecting the significance of his contributions:\n- Failure to achieve Cuban sovereignty: Palma governed under the Platt Amendment, which allowed U.S. intervention in Cuban affairs. His acceptance of U.S. control over Cuban policies and economy contradicted the revolutionary vision of full independence.\n- Foreign influence: His presidency set a precedent for foreign influence in Cuban politics, undermining long-term nationalist goals.\n- Distance from the people: Palma, based in the United States during most of the war, meant he was not on the ground fighting in Cuba, which many who were fighting resented.\n- Perception as a bureaucrat: Many Cubans, especially Afro-Cubans and rural rebels, viewed him as more of a bureaucratic figure than a revolutionary leader.\n- Favored the elites: His policies were seen to be advantageous to the Cuban elite as they aligned closely with the U.S. rather than radical reform or social inclusion.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSome of Palma's contributions were significant in Cuban Independence. He took over leadership of the Cuban Revolutionary Party after Marti's death, which helped maintain consistency and leadership of the independence movement ($\\checkmark$). Palma also maintained a good relationship with the United States, which later led to the US's involvement in helping end Spanish colonialism and the subsequent Cuban Independence ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, some of Palma's contributions were less than significant.  As a result of the Platt Amendment, which allowed for US intervention, Cuba was unable to achieve absolute independence ($\\checkmark$). Although Palma's relationship with the US helped achieve independence, it became a liability in his leadership immediately after independence ($\\checkmark$).  He became the first president of Cuba, but he did not have the support of many who fought on the ground for independence."},{"ref":"HI0552","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0552\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about Spain's reasons for strongly resisting Cuban Independence?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the article “John O’Brien: Captain Dynamite of the Cuban War for Independence, 1868-1898” by Herminio Portell-Vilá (1901-1992), a former professor at the University of Havana, published in the academic journal *Irish Migration Studies in Latin America* in March of 2010. |\n\n:box[The revolutionary effort of Cubans to achieve their independence was prolonged for much more time than should have been necessary, and it required far more heroism and greater sacrifices than that of any other people of the Americas. During the course of nearly one hundred years, Cuba was in continuous political tumult, marked by both smaller, abortive conspiracies and larger-scale attempts to throw off the Spanish colonial yoke. Because Spain had lost the majority of its New World dominions during the first third of the nineteenth century, it had learned its lesson well and went on to concentrate all remaining resources in Cuba, along with an intensified hatred of republicanism and all that was American. With jealous zeal, the Spanish Crown's most characteristic strategy was to keep Cubans out of the armed services and to render it impossible for their sons to become sailors. The imperial government dared not manufacture any guns, sabres, or munitions on the island itself; all weaponry had to be brought to Cuba from abroad through official Spanish channels. Its aim was to maintain domination over a docile and disarmed population. Cubans should be kept unfamiliar with the technical knowledge of warfare and lack access to such maritime communication as would be indispensable for any successful rebellion. This imperial monopoly over the trade in arms and explosives characterized a regime of externally-controlled defense that left the average Cuban to his own devices when personally threatened or when contemplating a rebellion. For example, the machete was a key tool for agricultural workers, but in the hands of the *mambises* [a colloquial name for Cuban revolutionaries], it could be turned into a terrible weapon.]\n\n[Source: Portell Vilá, Herminio. (2010, March). John O'Brien: *Captain Dynamite of the Cuban War for Independence, 1868-1898.*  Irish Migration Studies in Latin America, Volume 7, No. 3. Retrieved May 7, 2025 from https://www.irlandeses.org/1003portellvila.htm.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following or similar:\n- The Spanish government feared both republicanism and American influence, associating Cuban independence with the spread of these ideas.\n- Cuba was Spain's last major colony in the Americas, and it held strategic, economic, and symbolic importance.\n- Spain had learned its lesson through the loss of their other colonies in the region and therefore spent more time and effort ensuring it kept Cuba.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A suggests that Spain wanted to prohibit Cuban Independence because they hated/feared the ideological basis of republicanism ($\\checkmark$). More importantly, Spain had already lost most of their colonies in the early 19th Century and was afraid to lose prestige by losing more ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0553","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0553\n\nAccording to Source A, what did Spain do to maintain control of Cuba?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Herminio Portell-Vilá (1901-1992) was a professor at the University of Havana, where he once taught a young Fidel Castro.   He left the country for Miami shortly after the Revolution, where he worked for the Voice of America, was Director of the American Security Council, and continued to write his historical studies. |\n\n:box[The revolutionary effort of Cubans to achieve their independence was prolonged for much more time than should have been necessary, and it required far more heroism and greater sacrifices than that of any other people of the Americas. During the course of nearly one hundred years, Cuba was in continuous political tumult, marked by both smaller, abortive conspiracies and larger-scale attempts to throw off the Spanish colonial yoke. Because Spain had lost the majority of its New World dominions during the first third of the nineteenth century, it had learned its lesson well and went on to concentrate all remaining resources in Cuba, along with an intensified hatred of republicanism and all that was American. With jealous zeal, the Spanish Crown’s most characteristic strategy was to keep Cubans out of the armed services and to render it impossible for their sons to become sailors. The imperial government dared not manufacture any guns, sabres, or munitions on the island itself; all weaponry had to be brought to Cuba from abroad through official Spanish channels. Its aim was to maintain domination over a docile and disarmed population. Cubans should be kept unfamiliar with the technical knowledge of warfare and lack access to such maritime communication as would be indispensable for any successful rebellion. This imperial monopoly over the trade in arms and explosives characterized a regime of externally-controlled defense that left the average Cuban to his own devices when personally threatened or when contemplating a rebellion. For example, the machete was a key tool for agricultural workers, but in the hands of the *mambises* [a colloquial name for Cuban revolutionaries], it could be turned into a terrible weapon.]\n\n[Source: Portell Vilá, Herminio. (2010, March). John O'Brien: *Captain Dynamite of the Cuban War for Independence, 1868-1898.*  Irish Migration Studies in Latin America, Volume 7, No. 3. Retrieved May 7, 2025 from https://www.irlandeses.org/1003portellvila.htm.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Spain developed a strong hostility toward republican ideas and American influence, which it actively resisted in Cuba\n- The Spanish Crown deliberately excluded Cubans from military service and naval opportunities to keep them inexperienced in warfare\n- Spain maintained strict control over weapons manufacturing and importation—no arms were produced in Cuba, and all had to come through Spanish channels\n- The Cuban population was deliberately kept unarmed and unfamiliar with military technology to prevent effective rebellion\n- Spain limited Cubans' access to maritime communication, further hindering coordination and the spread of revolutionary ideas\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSpain attempted to prohibit Cuban independence by squashing ideologies and influences that were viewed as threats, such as republicanism and the influence of the US ($\\checkmark$). They also excluded Cubans from any military service or experience to keep them ignorant of warfare ($\\checkmark$).  Lastly, Spain imported all weapons so that Cubans would not know how to make them or use them effectively ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0701","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0701\n\nDescribe the methods used by Gandhi to resist British rule during the Non-Cooperation Campaign of 1920-22 and explain their impact.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor methods, the following or similar:\n\n - Gandhi supported nonviolent resistance based on the concept of Satyagraha, or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$soul force\", which states that ordinary people can use peaceful means to fight for justice. \n\n - This involved a boycott of British schools, universities, law courts, and British goods. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor impacts, any **four** of the following:\n\n - Thousands of students left schools and colleges to attend $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$national schools\" set up outside the government system.\n\n - Protestors picketed shops that sold British textiles and made bonfires of foreign clothes. \n\n - The visit of the Prince of Wales to India in 1921 was met with empty streets and official receptions held for him were boycotted by Indians . \n\n - The British arrested 20,000 protestors.\n\n - The protests turned violent in Bombay resulting in the death of demonstrators. A violent mob in the village of Chauri Chaura killed 22 Indian policemen in February 1922, by setting the police station alight. \n\n - This led Gandhi to call off the Non-Cooperation Campaign as he felt the people were not ready to apply the tactics of Satyagraha as intended. \n\n - Gandhi was arrested, charged with sedition and sentenced to six years in prison. He was released after two years for health reasons. He now abstained from political activity till 1929 and focused instead on fighting for greater rights for those adversely impacted by the caste system in India. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Non-Cooperation Movement, launched by Gandhi in 1920 to resist British rule, was based on non-violence. This method relied on Gandhi's teaching that ordinary people could use peaceful means to bring about change, including the boycott of British schools, universities, law courts, and goods ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe Non-Cooperation Campaign involved the boycott of shops that sold British textiles, with protestors making bonfires of British cloth and having an adverse economic impact on British businessmen in India ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIndians conveyed their displeasure with British rule during the visit of the Prince of Wales to India in 1921. The Prince was greeted with empty streets and Indians boycotted official receptions held for him, in a bid to take their message of non-cooperation crystal clear to the British government. In addition, the boycott was seen as a humiliation for the British Empire ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe protests turned violent in Bombay and resulted in the deaths of people demonstrating. The British used force against the protestors and arrested 20,000 Indians.  Violence also broke out in the village of Chauri Chaura, where a mob set fire to the police station, killing 22 Indian policemen ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe violence at Chauri Chaura led Gandhi to call off the Non-Cooperation movement as he felt people were not ready to apply the principles of non-violence as required for Satyagraha. Gandhi was arrested and sentenced to six years in prison on the charges of sedition ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0702","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0702\n\nDescribe the methods of the Civil Disobedience Campaign launched by the Indian National Congress between 1930 and 1934 and explain their impacts.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor methods, the following or similar:\n\n - The lack of response from the British government to Gandhi's list of demands to the Viceroy which included the abolition of the salt tax and the government's monopoly on the production and sale of salt prompted Gandhi to launch his second satyagraha campaign with the Salt March in March 1930. \n\n - Gandhi and a select group of 78 volunteers walked from his ashram in Ahmedabad to Dandi on the Gujarat coast. A journey of 400 kms covered by local and international press,where they were joined by thousands on the way. \n\n - The symbolic action of picking up dried salt from the beach was a public breach of law and followed by people in other parts of the country where the boycott extended to alcohol and foreign cloth. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor impacts, any four of the following:\n\n - Gandhi was arrested before the next step of the campaign; the march to the Dharasana salt works near Dandi. The march to Dharasana in May 1930 resulted in nonviolent protestors being brutally attacked by Indian soldiers under British command and turned public opinion on the world stage against British rule in India. \n\n - Gandhi's arrest led to nationwide strikes and riots resulting in the arrest of 90,000 people and many deaths. This further damaged the credibility of the British ability to govern India. \n\n - Britain was alarmed by the economic impact of the campaign as foreign cloth imports had halved, there was reduced income from taxes on salt and alcohol and loss of land revenue. \n\n - In March 1931, Gandhi and the British authorities reached an agreement and signed the Gandhi-Irwin Pact, with Gandhi suspending the Civil Disobedience campaign. \n\n - Gandhi calls for a resumption of the campaign following the lack of progress at the Second Round Table Conference in London. However, the campaign failed to regain its earlier momentum as a result of increasingly repressive measures used by the British along with Gandhi's arrest on his arrival in India in January 1932. The campaign was formally called off in April 1934. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nGandhi launched the Civil Disobedience Campaign following a lack of response from the British government to his list of demands which included the abolition of the tax on salt along with the government's monopoly on the production and sale of salt. His second satyagraha campaign began with the Salt March in March 1930, when Gandhi and a group of 78 volunteers walked 400 kms from his ashram in Ahmedabad to Dandi on the Gujarat coast and proceeded to symbolically break the law prohibiting the production of salt. This was to be the start of an India wide campaign of opposition to British rule that extended to the boycott of alcohol and foreign cloth ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe British arrested Gandhi before the next step of the campaign - the march to the Dharasana salt works near Dandi. The march itself went ahead in May 1930 and the brutal attacks by Indian soldiers under British command, turned public opinion on the world stage against British rule in India ($\\checkmark$). \n\nGandhi's arrest led to nationwide strikes and riots put down by the British with force and mass arrests of 90000 Indians. These disturbances and deaths called into question the British ability to govern India ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe economic impact of the campaign resulted in reduced income for the British as foreign cloth imports were halved and the British were alarmed by  the reduced income from taxes on salt and alcohol along with the loss of land revenue ($\\checkmark$). \n\nGandhi suspended the Civil Disobedience Movement in March 1931 following an agreement with the British and the signing of the Gandhi-Irwin Pact. The campaign was formally called off in April 1934, following Gandhi's arrest on his arrival in India following the failure of the Second Round Table Conference held in London ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0703","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0703\n\nOutline the reasons for and impact of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Quit India\" Movement on the Indian struggle for independence. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor reasons, any **two** of the following:\n\n - In September 1939, Viceroy Linlithgow of India declared that India was at war with Germany without consulting Indian politicians. The provincial Congress governments resigned in protest. \n\n - The British Prime Minister, Churchill sent the Cripps Mission to India in March 1942 to negotiate with the nationalist leaders in an attempt at conciliation, as the Japanese threatened India's eastern borders. Cripps made a commitment to grant India independence after the war was over. Congress rejected the offer that Gandhi referred to as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a post-dated cheque on a failing bank\". \n\n - In August 1942, the Congress adopted the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Quit India\" resolution drafted by Gandhi and called for a mass campaign of civil disobedience to force Britain to leave India. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\nFor impacts, any **three** of the following:\n\n - The British imprisoned Congress leaders including Gandhi and Nehru. Closed Congress offices and printing press. \n\n - In the absence of the major Congress leaders, the movement continued and included many acts of violence. Strikes by factory workers, attacks on police stations, rural uprisings and acts of sabotage that targeted railway lines and bridges all occurred during the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$August Revolution\". \n\n - The British suppression was ruthless, with the imposition of martial law in areas, machine guns used on demonstrators and entire villages destroyed. 1000 people were killed, 3000 wounded and 100000 Indians detained without trial. \n\n - While the uprising was controlled in six weeks, the demonstration of grass-roots activism played an important role in the British decision to leave India at the end of the war.  \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Quit India Movement was launched by the Indian National Congress in August 1942 and called for a mass campaign of civil disobedience to force Britain to leave India ($\\checkmark$). This followed the declaration by the Viceroy in September 1939 of Indian participation in the Second World War, which was made without consulting Indian politicians. The provincial Congress governments resigned in protest and declared their lack of support for the British war effort ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe British responded by arresting Congress leaders, including Gandhi and Nehru. They also closed Congress offices and the printing press. In the absence of the major Congress leaders, the movement continued and included acts of violence with strikes by factory workers, attacks on police stations, rural uprisings and acts of sabotage that targeted railway lines and bridges that all occurred during the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$August Revolution\" ($\\checkmark$). The British responded with the imposition of martial law, detaining Indians without trial, using machine guns on demonstrators, and destroying entire villages ($\\checkmark$). \n\nDespite the British bringing the Quit India movement under control in six weeks, it played an important role in their eventual decision to leave India at the end of the war. The mass demonstrations that continued despite the imprisonment of major leaders were a clear indication of grassroots activism that would now make it impossible for the British to suppress the nationalist movement and retain control of the country. This would prove an important consideration in their eventual decision to withdraw from the country in 1947 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0704.5","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0704.5\n\nDescribe **four** impacts of religious divisions within the Nationalist movement.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n - The Viceroy's decision to declare Indian involvement in the Second World War, resulted in the resignation of the provincial Congress governments, which the League declared a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$day of deliverance\". The League also declared its  support for the British war effort. \n\n - In March 1940,the League passed the Lahore resolution calling for creating a separate Muslim state. Jinnah in his speech, stated that Hindus and Muslims were two distinct and irreconcilable communities as justification for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Nation\" theory. \n\n - Jinnah accepted the British offer of delayed independence in return for support during the war, put forward by the Cripps Mission in 1942. He however demanded a two-state solution after independence . \n\n - At the Simla Conference arranged by the British in June 1945, to discuss the future of post-war India, the Viceroy Wavell proposed an equal representation of Hindus and Muslims on the Executive Council to function as an interim government and plan future constitutional developments. Jinnah's insistence that all Muslim members had to be from the League, resulted in the failure of the Conference.  \n\n - In the 1946 elections for the central and provincial assemblies, the Congress won 90$\\%$ of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$open seats\" in the central legislature and a majority in 8 of the 11 provinces. The Muslim League won all 30 seats in the central legislature, reserved for Muslim voters and 442 of the 500 reserved seats in the provincial legislatures. This strengthened Jinnah's claim that the Muslim League represented all Muslims in India. \n\n - The proposals put forward by the Cabinet Mission in March 1946 to facilitate the transfer of power failed after Muslim League withdrew its initial acceptance of the proposals, due to disagreement with the Congress stand which viewed the proposals as transitional rather than fixed. \n\n - The League reiterated its call for the creation of Pakistan as a separate state and Jinnah called for $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Direct Action\" in support of the League's demand for partition. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Direct Action Day\" on August 16, 1946 resulted in rioting and widespread communal violence that spread from Calcutta to other parts of India. The $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Great Calcutta Killing\" resulted in 5000 deaths and 20,000 injured. The inclusion of members of the Muslim League in the interim government failed to stop the violence.\n\n - The Congress reluctantly came to accept that partition was the only viable solution. The last Viceroy of India, Lord Mountbatten, arrived in February 1947 to oversee the transfer of power by June 1948. He brought this forward to August 1947. The partition resulted in violence and a refugee crisis with more than 5,000,000 Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs fleeing from their homes. \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe religious divisions within the Nationalist movement was apparent when the Muslim League declared a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$day of deliverance\", on the resignation of Provincial Congress governments protesting against the unilateral declaration of the Viceroy of Indian participation in the Second World War.The League also declared its  support for the British war effort ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThese divisions were further reinforced when in March 1940,the League passed the Lahore resolution for the creation of a separate Muslim state. The $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Two Nation\" theory was put forward by Jinnah in his speech, stating that Hindus and Muslims were two distinct and irreconcilable communities  ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe acceptance by Jinnah and the Muslim League of the British offer of delayed independence in return for support during the war, proposed by the Cripps Mission in 1942 further highlighted the differences with the Congress rejection of these proposals. The Muslim League however demanded a two-state solution after independence in return for its support ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe League's stand at the Simla Conference arranged by the British in June 1945, to discuss the future of post-war India, prevented any resolution of the widening divisions between the parties. Jinnah's insistence that all Muslim members on the Executive Council of the Viceroy, that was to function as an interim government and plan future constitutional developments, had to be only from the League, resulted in the failure of the Conference ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0704","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0704\n\nExplain **two** reasons for growing religious divisions within the Nationalist movement.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - The nationalists' protests following the Partition of Bengal in 1905 unnerved Muslims in Bengal due to the appeals to Hindu nationalism made by some protestors. Representatives of the Muslim community met the Viceroy in 1906 and the Simla Deputation and stressed the view that the Muslims as a distinct community needed separate representation.\n\n - In December 1906, the Muslim League was formed with only Muslim membership, as distinct from the Congress, which included people of all religions, including Muslims. The aim of the Muslim League was to secure constitutional protection for Muslims with separate electorates and reserved seats. This was dramatically opposed to the Congress's stand of all-Indian representation in legislatures. \n\n - The Morley-Minto Reforms of 1909 provided for Indian representation in central and provincial legislative councils and introduced separate electorates for Muslims and reserved seats. This introduced the concept of religious divisions in the polity of Indian nationalism and consolidated the idea of religious divisions. \n\n - The 1927 rejection by the Congress of the offer made by the Muslim League to end its support for separate electorates in exchange for an agreement that Muslims could fill one-third of the seats on the Central Legislative Council furthered religious divisions. This stand by the Congress went against their earlier agreement with the Muslim League in the Lucknow Pact of 1916, where the Congress had agreed that Muslims would have a fixed proportion of seats in the future Indian parliament and extra seats in areas where Muslims were in a minority. This caused anger and distrust from the Muslim League. \n\n - In 1930, the first proposal for a Muslim $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$homeland\" that would be part of United India was made by Urdu poet and philosopher Muhammad Iqbal at a session of the Muslim League. In 1933, Chaudhri Rahmat Ali, an Indian student in Cambridge, put forward a plan for a federation of ten Muslim states called Pakistan. These actions by the Muslim League furthered the Muslim League's program to establish a separate identity unlike other parties like the Congress or the Communist Party that included all religions, including Hindus and Muslims. \n\n - The Muslim League fared badly in the 1937 provincial elections, with the Congress winning 70$\\%$of the popular vote. The Muslim League won barely 5% of the Muslim vote.  The Muslim League leader Jinnah proposed that the League could form part of coalition governments in provinces with a large Muslim minority. Congress refused Jinnah's offer. While the Congress did appoint its own Muslim members to provincial governments, the rejection of Jinnah's proposal had angered the League. Jinnah now aware of the lack of support from most Muslims worked to increase Muslim support for the League using the slogan, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Islam is in danger\". \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Partition of Bengal in 1905 witnessed the initial start of growing religious divisions that impacted the Nationalist movement and led to the eventual partition of India in 1947 ($\\checkmark$). The appeals to Hindu nationalism by a section of nationalists protesting the partition unnerved Muslims in Bengal. Representatives of the Muslim community met the Viceroy in 1906, and this Simla Deputation put forward the view in the meeting that the Muslims as a distinct community needed separate representation. In December, the Muslim League was formed in Dhaka with an aim to secure constitutional protection for Muslims with separate electorates and reserved seats ($\\checkmark$). \n\nReligious divisions within the Nationalist movement were further exacerbated when, in 1927, the Congress, as a result of pressure by Hindu nationalists, rejected the Muslim League's offer to end its support for separate electorates in exchange for an agreement that Muslims could fill one-third of the seats on the Central Legislative Council ($\\checkmark$). This increased tension between the two groups as it went against the earlier Lucknow Pact of 1916 between the two parties, in which the Congress had agreed that Muslims would have a fixed proportion of seats in the future Indian parliament and extra seats in areas where Muslims were in a minority. This widened the gulf between the two parties and ensured the retention of separate electorates ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0705","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0705\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Violent methods were essential to achieving independence between 1800-2000.” Discuss with reference to **two** states, each chosen from a different region.\n\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nA high-scoring response should: \n\n - Clearly address the **methods** used to achieve independence in each state. \n\n - Analyse the **impact** and **effectiveness** of **violent methods** in the achievement of independence in relation to the **impact** and **effectiveness** of **other methods** that led to independence in each state. \n\n - Arrive at a logical **conclusion supported by evidence**, weighing the importance of the different methods that eventually contributed to independence in each state. \n\nPossible suitable examples include: \n\n - **Ben Bella and Algeria**:\n\n     - The struggle for Algerian independence from French colonial rule was violent and traumatic. Algeria was considered an integral part of France and the settler population ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$pied-noirs\") enjoyed full citizenship rights, while the indigenous Muslim majority was heavily discriminated against.Political reforms and non-violent nationalist efforts had limited success before 1954.FLN (National Liberation Front) launched the Algerian War of Independence in 1954.Violence became a tool to both confront French authority and unify Algerians under the FLN's and Ben Bella's leadership.While violence was central to the final achievement of independence,diplomatic efforts, including negotiations leading to the Evian Accords, were ultimately needed to finalize independence. \n\n - **José Martí and Cuba**: \n\n     - The struggle for Cuban independence from Spanish colonial rule in the late 19th century involved a complex mix of armed resistance, political activism, and foreign intervention. Violent methods were central to the Cuban independence movement, and Martí, though primarily a thinker and organizer, saw armed struggle as a necessary path to liberation. The Cuban War of Independence (1895-1898) that  Martí helped plan relied on widespread and sustained violence to challenge Spanish control. However, final independence came only after U.S. intervention, suggesting that while violence was essential to initiating and sustaining the fight, it was not sufficient alone to secure complete sovereignty. In Martí's case, violence was a necessary means, but not the only factor shaping Cuba's path to independence.\n\n - **Ho Chi Minh and Vietnam**: \n\n     - The Vietnamese struggle for independence in the 20th century was prolonged and violent.Ho Chi Minh recognized that colonial powers would not concede power through peaceful negotiation and committed to a long-term armed struggle. From the defeat of the French in 1954 to the eventual withdrawal of the Americans in 1975, violent resistance, through both conventional and guerrilla warfare, was indispensable. While diplomacy as in the Geneva Conference, ideology that helped unify different groups, and political strategy played key roles, it was ultimately the military success of Vietnamese forces that secured full independence and national unification.\n\n - **Kolokotronis and Greece**: \n\n     - The Greek War of Independence (1821-1829) marked the end of four centuries of Ottoman rule, with the creation of an independent Greek state. Violent methods were essential in initiating and sustaining the fight for independence. The military leadership of Theodoros Kolokotronis and the victories he achieved on the battlefield were critical in undermining Ottoman control and asserting Greek nationalist claims. While diplomatic and international support from Britain, France, and Russia helped finalize independence with the Treaty of Constantinople (1832), it was the armed struggle that made this support possible. Therefore, violence was not just present, but indispensable to Greece's independence.\n\n - **Nehru, Gandhi and India**: \n\n     - India's independence movement under the leadership of Mohandas Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru was based on a movement rooted in non-violence; the constant threat of violence did play a critical supporting role and helped pressure the British, especially in the final years. The threat of unrest, mutiny, and revolutionary violence contributed to Britain's decision to leave,together with the rise of anti-colonialism and the economic exhaustion of Britain, following the Second World War. While violence was not the main method, it was a significant factor in making non-violent resistance more effective and credible. \n\n - **Collins, de Valera and Ireland**: \n\n     - Ireland's struggle for independence from British rule in the early 20th century involved both political negotiation and violent resistance. Éamon de Valera and Michael Collins played different roles in this struggle. While de Valera provided important political leadership and helped articulate the Republican vision, it was Collins' violent guerrilla campaign that broke British control and forced negotiation. The Easter Rising sparked nationalist sentiment, but it was sustained violence during the War of Independence that made continued British rule unfeasible. While politics legitimized the movement, violence was the critical instrument that brought independence. \n\n\\\n\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIndia and Ireland, both achieved independence from British colonial rule through a combination of political negotiation, mass mobilisation, and armed resistance ($\\checkmark$ examples). While India's independence is widely associated with the non-violent philosophy of Gandhi and the political leadership of  Nehru, Ireland's struggle was marked by armed resistance, especially under the direction of Collins and the Irish Republican Army (IRA). In Ireland, violent methods were crucial in achieving independence, while in India, independence was achieved primarily through non-violent mass resistance, though the threat of violence played an accelerating role ($\\checkmark$ focus). \n\nThe use of violence played a role in both independence movements. Ireland's independence struggle highlights the critical role of violent methods in undermining colonial rule, while in India, the latter years of the Indian independence movement also saw pockets of violent resistance that influenced the British decision to grant India independence ($\\checkmark$ focus). Violence erupted in Ireland following the British introduction of the Home Rule Bill in 1912, which the Irish Nationalists felt fell far short of complete Irish independence. Armed insurrection became a central strategy for Irish nationalists with the Easter Rising of 1916 and the proclamation of the Irish Republic ($\\checkmark$ examples). The British response with the use of force and execution of the leaders consolidated public demand for full independence and laid the foundation for future armed campaigns. Clashes between the IRA and the British forces continued during the Irish War of Independence (1919-1921), with the use of violent methods by Collins and the Irish Republican Army (IRA). This included targeted assassinations and ambushes ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). The British deployment of the Black and Tans and the Auxiliary Division to suppress the violence and unrest was marked by brutality and further alienated the Irish population and increased support for the Republicans ($\\checkmark$ analysis). \n\nIn India, violent resistance also impacted the British decision to grant India independence ($\\checkmark$ focus). Revolutionary groups carried out political assassinations and bombings. Subhas Chandra Bose and the Indian National Army (INA) launched campaigns that, despite being militarily unsuccessful, put pressure on the British with the post-war INA trials (1945-46) ($\\checkmark$ examples). The rising tide of nationalism and the perceived injustice of the trials created a powerful wave of resentment within the Royal Indian Navy, culminating in the mutiny of 1946  and signalled a breakdown in the loyalty of Indian armed forces ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). While these efforts did not lead directly to independence, they inspired sections of the youth and maintained pressure on the British government. In addition, the threat of violence from the growing conflict between Hindus and Muslims linked to the demand for a separate Muslim homeland, accelerated British withdrawal, as colonial authorities feared that prolonged occupation would lead to a civil war that they would need to deal with ($\\checkmark$ analysis). \n\nIn Ireland, violence was not only present but essential in achieving independence, as political concessions from Britain were only made under pressure from armed insurrection and domestic instability ($\\checkmark$ focus). The Indian Independence movement, however, was based mainly on non-violent resistance under the leadership of Gandhi, as the Indian National Congress spearheaded mass civil disobedience movements that directly challenged British rule ($\\checkmark$ focus). Gandhi's Satyagraha (truth-force) philosophy emphasized moral persuasion and non-violent protest. Campaigns such as the Non-Cooperation Movement (1920-22), the Salt March (1930), and the Quit India Movement (1942) mobilized millions of Indians across social, religious, and caste lines ($\\checkmark$ knowledge). These movements severely disrupted British administration and helped delegitimize colonial rule domestically and internationally. While the movements demonstrated the potential of unified civil resistance, they also revealed the fragility of colonial authority when denied mass cooperation ($\\checkmark$ analysis).\n\nNon-violent political strategy was hence the dominant and most effective element in the Indian independence movement, as the decades-long nationalist movement had already laid the groundwork for self-rule. The sustained, moral force of mass non-violence, combined with Britain's weakened post-WWII position, proved decisive. In Ireland, the failure of peaceful negotiation and the success of guerrilla warfare under Collins suggest that violence was essential to forcing British withdrawal. Political concessions in Ireland only followed military pressure and sustained unrest. Therefore, in the Irish case, violent methods were essential, while in the Indian case, they were influential but not indispensable ($\\checkmark$ analysis). Independence was ultimately achieved in both nations, but by different routes shaped by local conditions and strategic choices ($\\checkmark$ conclusion)."},{"ref":"HI0706","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0706\n\nAccording to Source B, what was the impact of Gandhi's campaign against the British government's monopoly of salt?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Kumar Goshal, author, journalist, and lecturer on Asian and African affairs, in his book, $\\lq$The People of India'. Published by *Sheridan House* (New York), 1944. |\n\n[Source: Goshal, K.. (1944). *The People of India.* Sheridan House (New York). Retrieved April 18, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015027043804. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The people enthusiastically responded by boycotting and picketing legal salt stores, and manufacturing illegal salt. There were strikes, mass demonstrations and refusal to pay rent. Women played an important part in these demonstrations.\n- The armory in Chittagong was raided by a crowd of young people.\n- Towns such as Peshawar and Sholapur were controlled solely by the people for many days.\n- The Hindu soldiers of the 18th Garhwali Rifles refused orders to fire on a crowd of predominantly Muslim agitators in Peshawar and Peshawar remained under the control of the people from April 25th to May 4th as the British withdrew all military police from the city.\n- Gandhi was  arrested on May 5th and the Congress and its subsidiary organizations were declared illegal. This increased public protests and demonstrations.\n- The Viceroy issued a number of ordinances that created a martial law like situation and the boycott impacted British business.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nGandhi's campaign against the British government's monopoly of salt manufacturing and sale found huge support from the people who proceeded to boycott and picket stores selling salt and also proceeded to manufacture salt themselves; an act that was illegal. Women were a major part of these demonstrations ($\\checkmark$). The British also faced the threat of mutiny among the Indian soldiers in their army when Hindu soldiers of the 18th Garhwali Rifles refused orders to fire on a predominantly Muslim crowd of agitators. The British lost control of the city and withdrew all military police, leaving the city to be controlled by the people from April 25th to May 4th ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the source suggests that after the British arrested Gandhi and declared the Congress and its subsidiary organisations illegal, public protests and demonstrations increased ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0708","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0708\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and\nlimitations of Source C for an historian studying the impact of the Second World War on the Nationalist movement in India.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kate L. Mitchell, member of the Institute of Pacific Relations, an international organisation for the study of events in the Far East and the magazine Amerasia, a journal of Far Eastern Affairs, in her book $\\lq$India Without Fable, a 1942 survey'. Published by *A. A. Knopf* (New York), 1942. |\n\nSource: Mitchell, K. L. (1942). *India without fable, a 1942 survey.* A. A. Knopf (New York).\nRetrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b83906. Public domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The author was a member of the Institute of Pacific Relations and the magazine Amerasia, both of which focused on an academic study of matters in the Far East, which included India. The date of the publication in 1942 during the Second World War, is also of value as the events leading to independence in 1947 are now gathering force in India.\n- The purpose of the source, $\\lq$India Without Fable, a 1942 survey' is to provide a clear, unfiltered analysis of the state of the country and of the nationalist movement in 1942 following the outbreak of WWII.\n- The content analyses the impact of the British actions in India on the nationalist movement, following the outbreak of the Second World War.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- As the source was published in 1942, it cannot provide insight into or an analysis of the later events that contributed to Indian independence in 1947.\n- The source's focus is not just on the independence movement during the Second World War, as evidenced from its title A combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\nBoth values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of two marks.\nOrigin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The author was a member of the Institute of Pacific Relations and the magazine Amerasia, both of which focused on an academic study of matters in the Far East, which included India. The date of the publication in 1942 during the Second World War, is also of value as the events leading to independence in 1947 are now gathering force in India.\n- The purpose of the source, $\\lq$India Without Fable, a 1942 survey' is to provide a clear, unfiltered analysis of the state of the country and of the nationalist movement in 1942 following the outbreak of WWII.\n- The content analyses the impact of the British actions in India on the nationalist movement, following the outbreak of the Second World War.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- As the source was published in 1942, it cannot provide insight into or an analysis of the later events that contributed to Indian independence in 1947.\n- The source's focus is not just on the independence movement during the Second World War, as evidenced from its title $\\lq$India Without Fable', referring to its focus on a broader history of India.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe origin of the source is valuable as the author is a member of an academic organisation, the Institute of Pacific Relations. The publication date, 1942, also gives the source value as it captures a record of the events that would lead India to independence in 1947 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose of the source is also valuable as it aims to provide a clear, critical analysis of the situation in the country and of the nationalist movement in 1942 following the outbreak of the Second World War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content of the source also provides value as it provides specific examples of British actions in India that impacted the Nationalist movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nOne limitation of the origin of the source is that, because it was published in 1942, it cannot provide an analysis of the later crucial events that contributed to Indian independence in 1947 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). One limitation of the source is that its focus is on a broader history of India and thus may lack some detail with regards to the independence movement during the Second World War ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0709","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0709\n\nDescribe the political tensions generated in India as a result of the Second World War according to Source C.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Kate L. Mitchell, member of the Institute of Pacific Relations, an international organisation for the study of events in the Far East and the magazine Amerasia, a journal of Far Eastern Affairs, in her book $\\lq$India Without Fable, a 1942 survey'. Published by *A. A. Knopf* (New York), 1942. |\n\n[Source: Mitchell, K. L. (1942). *India without fable, a 1942 survey.* A. A. Knopf (New York).\nRetrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b83906. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- The British government's actions that declared India a belligerent nation in the Second World War and the The Defense of India Ordinance of September 3, 1939 that allowed the British government to rule by decree, prohibit meetings and to arrest without warrant created opposition and political tensions in India.\n- The Congress protested against these British actions while the Muslim League gave assurances of cooperation in the British war effort, provided that Moslem interests were guaranteed against encroachment by the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hindu majority\".\n- The British used the differences in the Nationalist Movement as an excuse for deferring any concrete concessions, on the grounds that Congress and the League must first arrive at a solution of their differences.\n- Following the White Paper issued by the Viceroy Linlithgow, which implicitly accepted the League's claim to speak for the Moslems of India, the Congress asked their Provincial Ministers to resign as they felt cooperation with Great Britain would be an endorsement of their imperialist policy.\n- Jinnah at the annual meeting of the Muslim League put forward his $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Pakistan” plan for the partition of India into Moslem and non-Moslem states. Much of northern India would be separated into a separate Muslim region including the Northwest Frontier Province (92% Moslem), Baluchistan (87%), the Punjab (56%), the State of Jimmu and Kashmir (77%), and Bengal (55%).\n\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe British declaration of India as a belligerent nation at the outbreak of the Second World War without the consultation of Indian representatives created opposition and raised political tensions. The British government tightened its hold over India with the passing of the Defense of India Ordinance of September 3, 1939 that gave them the power to rule by decree,to prohibit meetings and to arrest without warrant, in an attempt to prevent any opposition to their actions ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Congress refused to accept the decision taken to bring the nation into the war, without the consultation of Indian representatives  and protested against this.  The Muslim League put forward an assurance of cooperation with the British war effort, provided the British ensured that Moslem interests were guaranteed against encroachment by the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hindu majority\". This move heightened the tensions between the two parties ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Viceroy Linlithgow issued the White Paper  which implicitly accepted the League's claim to speak for all the Moslems of India leading the Congress to ask their Provincial Ministers to resign on the grounds that any cooperation with Great Britain would be an endorsement of their imperialist policy ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBolstered by British implicit support, Jinnah and the Muslim League, put forward a $\\lq$Pakistan' plan for the partition of India into Moslem and non-Moslem states. The Moslem regions included much of northern India with the Northwest Frontier Province (92% Moslem), Baluchistan (87%), the Punjab (56%), the State of Jimmu and Kashmir (77%), and Bengal (55%). This furthered the political tensions between the two major nationalist parties ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0710","marks":"15","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# HI0710\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of responses to social and economic challenges during the first ten years of independence in **one** state.\n[15 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband (13-15):||\n|:--|:--|\n|Response is clearly focused on the question, showing a high degree of awareness of both the demands and implications of the question|($\\checkmark$ focus)|\n|Response is well organized.|($\\checkmark$ organization) |\n|Response includes accurate and relevant knowledge and concepts (change, continuity, cause, consequence, perspectives and significance) and the examples chosen are used in their proper historical context.|($\\checkmark$ knowledge)|\n|Response uses the chosen examples to effectively support the analysis and evaluation and makes effective comparisons or links when appropriate.|($\\checkmark$ examples)|\n|Response includes clear, coherent critical analysis and evaluation of perspectives which are integrated effectively into the answer.|($\\checkmark$ analysis)|\n|Response argues to a well-substantiated conclusion that is consistent with the evidence and examples provided.|($\\checkmark$ conclusion)|\n\nThe strongest responses should:\n- Clearly indicate the social and economic challenges faced by the newly independent state.\n- Analyse the steps taken to respond to the challenges and evaluate their effectiveness from multiple perspectives (when relevant).\n- Arrive at a logical conclusion supported by the evidence presented.\n\nPossible suitable examples include:\n- Algeria:\n    - The responses to Algeria's social and economic challenges in the first decade of independence were mixed. The government made bold and ambitious moves, particularly in key nationalised sectors, such as oil and gas, education, building heavy industry, literacy campaigns and expansion of schools and universities, urban housing projects, and free public healthcare. However, it faced significant implementation problems.  While some foundations for long-term development were laid, the period was also marked by missed opportunities, uneven progress, and a failure to address the broader population's needs fully.\n- Cuba:\n    - The revolutionary government, led by Fidel Castro, attempted sweeping changes to overcome deep-rooted social and economic inequalities inherited from decades of dictatorship and U.S. economic dominance. Cuba achieved remarkable progress by redistributing land to peasants and cooperatives, nationalizing most major industries, implementing an intensive literacy campaign, free healthcare, building clinics and hospitals, and emphasizing social equality, women's rights, and racial inclusion. These responses were effective in raising the standard of living for many Cubans. However, economic policies, especially nationalization and planning, while ideologically driven, were less successful in producing sustainable growth. Over-reliance on the Soviet Union and poor execution of ambitious development plans limited long-term economic resilience.\n- Vietnam:\n    - After the fall of Saigon and reunification, the first ten years of Vietnam's post-1975 independence (1975-1985) were a period of ambitious socialist transformation, massive social restructuring, and significant economic hardship. The country faced enormous challenges: rebuilding a war-torn country, integrating the North and South, managing economic isolation, and addressing widespread poverty and underdevelopment. The government nationalised industries and collectivized agriculture, adopting Soviet-style central planning. It expanded literacy campaigns and ideological training and introduced free basic healthcare. Vietnam's responses to social and economic challenges were largely ineffective in achieving their intended goals. These failures ultimately forced the Communist Party to reconsider its approach, leading to the launch of the Đổi Mới (Renovation) reforms in 1986, marking a shift toward a market-oriented economy.\n- Greece:\n    - The first ten years of independence in Greece from 1830 to 1840  saw the newly established state face profound difficulties along with its attempts at national reconstruction. The early Greek state attempted to rebuild through taxation, foreign loans, and limited agricultural reform. The government promised to distribute former Ottoman lands (national lands or ethniké gé) to landless peasants and veterans. Efforts were made to revive trade, especially in maritime sectors, and to rebuild port cities like Nafplio and Athens. The monarchy also began building an educational system and promoting the Greek language and classical heritage to foster a national identity. These responses were partially effective but ultimately limited by external dependency, weak institutions, and internal divisions. While some foundational steps were taken, particularly in education and administration, economic recovery was slow.\n- India:\n    - The first ten years of India's independence (1947-1957) were marked by immense social and economic challenges linked to colonial rule, partition-related violence, and mass displacement. The government implemented sweeping reforms, prioritizing refugee relief, rebuilding trade links, and infrastructure. India adopted a planned economy, launching the First Five-Year Plan (1951-1956), focusing on agriculture, irrigation, and infrastructure to lay the foundation for development. The Constitution of 1950 outlawed untouchability and provided affirmative action for marginalized sections of society. The government promoted universal primary education, expanded health services, and launched campaigns against malaria and smallpox.  India's responses to social and economic challenges in the first decade of independence were largely effective, though problems like poverty, inequality, and bureaucratic inefficiency persisted.\n- Pakistan:\n    - The first ten years of Pakistan's independence (1947-1957) were marked by acute social and economic challenges stemming from Partition, weak institutional structures, and an unstable political environment. The government worked to stabilize the economy by setting up its own currency and a central bank and initiating trade negotiations with India and other countries. It focused on increasing agricultural productivity through canal-based irrigation, land redistribution, and agricultural reforms. Efforts were made to expand primary and secondary education, and to Islamize the curriculum in line with national identity. Pakistan's responses were partially effective. The state succeeded in some areas, such as refugee resettlement and creating basic institutions, but economic development was slow, social services remained underdeveloped, and political instability severely hampered progress. The failure to integrate East and West Pakistan equitably would have long-term consequences for the country's stability and cohesion.\n\\\n[15 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nExtraordinary challenges and significant transformation marked the first decade of India's independence. Emerging from nearly two centuries of British colonial rule, India faced the trauma of Partition, mass displacement, communal violence, economic stagnation, and entrenched social inequalities. The Indian government, led by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru and the Indian National Congress, launched a series of responses rooted in planning, state-led development, and secular democracy. These initiatives contributed to India's emergence as a stable and democratic nation-state, initiating economic development in the first ten years post-independence, though the country still struggled to achieve social equality and alleviate poverty ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$).\n\nOne of the most immediate and severe crises confronting the Indian state in 1947 was the fallout of Partition. The division of British India into India and Pakistan led to the displacement of an estimated ten to fifteen million people, as well as large-scale communal violence that resulted in the deaths of over a million individuals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Indian government responded by establishing refugee camps, creating the Ministry of Rehabilitation, and facilitating the resettlement of displaced populations. Efforts were made to provide housing, employment, and compensation, especially in northern regions such as Punjab and Delhi, which absorbed a large share of the incoming refugees ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Although the scale of the crisis overwhelmed administrative capacity, and conditions in many camps were harsh, the state's intervention ultimately stabilized the situation within a few years. While not without flaws, these measures prevented a prolonged humanitarian catastrophe and helped integrate refugees into independent India's social and economic fabric ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nSocial reform was another critical area in which the Indian state attempted transformative change ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Constitution of India, adopted in 1950, enshrined a vision of equality, justice, and secularism. It abolished untouchability, outlawed discrimination based on caste or religion, and granted fundamental rights to all citizens. The government also introduced affirmative action policies, reserving seats in educational institutions and public sector jobs for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes. These constitutional and legal reforms were pivotal in reorienting Indian society toward egalitarian ideals, even though implementation remained uneven . Efforts were made to increase the number of children attending school, but the aim of compulsory education for all was not achieved. Funding to improve health care services saw a sharp reduction in epidemics and raised the average life expectancy, resulting in rising population growth that put pressure on land and resources. In the realm of gender equality, the Nehru administration pushed through the Hindu Code Bills (1955-56), which reformed Hindu personal law by giving women greater rights in marriage, inheritance, and divorce ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). These laws were hotly contested and faced strong opposition from conservative groups, yet their eventual passage was a landmark in Indian legislative history. However, the impact of these social reforms was constrained by persistent societal conservatism and the slow pace of change, particularly in rural areas. While societal change was gradual, the legal provisions set a precedent for future social justice initiatives ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe issue of language posed a significant challenge to the new government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The Indian constitution recognised fourteen major languages and made Hindi and English the official languages. The constitutional provision to alter state boundaries set the stage for demands by speakers of different languages. Andhra Pradesh was created from the area formerly part of Tamil Nadu due to a violent campaign by the Telugu-speaking Andhras of southern India. Protests over language also led to the division of the state of Bombay to satisfy the demands of Gujarati and Marathi speakers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). Violent opposition to the official use of Hindi, particularly by Tamil speakers in southern India, resulted in the continued use of English. Language divisions continued to prove challenging to successive governments ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe Indian government also embarked on an ambitious economic development strategy to combat poverty, underdevelopment, and unemployment. India adopted a planned economy model inspired by Soviet-style central planning, adapted to democratic conditions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{focus}$). The First Five-Year Plan, launched in 1951, prioritised agriculture, irrigation, rural development, and infrastructure. It aimed to increase food production and lay the groundwork for future industrialisation. The plan proved largely successful in its goals: agricultural output increased significantly, particularly in food grains, and large-scale infrastructure projects such as the Bhakra Nangal Dam were initiated. India's national income grew annually at 3.6 percent, surpassing the plan's target of 2.1 percent ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{knowledge}$). However, the plan's industrial progress was limited, and structural issues such as land inequality, underemployment, and regional disparities persisted. Although these limitations were acknowledged, the plan nonetheless marked an important first step in state-led development and laid a foundation for future economic planning ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{analysis}$).\n\nThe responses of the Indian state to the social and economic challenges of its first decade of independence were largely effective, though uneven. The government succeeded in stabilising the immediate post-Partition crisis, launching a credible and functional development plan and initiating key social reforms. However, many of these efforts faced limitations due to resource constraints, societal resistance, and institutional weaknesses. While structural problems such as caste inequality, poverty, and underdevelopment could not be resolved within a decade, the foundational work of this period was crucial in shaping India's long-term trajectory as a democratic, secular, and developmental state ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{conclusion}$)."},{"ref":"HI1039","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1039\n\nList **two** terms of the Treaty of Paris of 1898.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Ended the Spanish-American War.\n- Spain gave up its claim to Cuba.\n- Cuba became independent but would be occupied by the USA.\n- Spain gave up Puerto Rico to the USA.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Spanish were required to withdraw from Cuba ($\\checkmark$), while another allowed for the temporary occupation of Cuba by the US ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Origins & Rise to Independence","questions":[{"ref":"HI0224","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0224\n\nExplain **four** reasons why the following statement is inaccurate: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$New Granadians fought from 1810 to 1819 against the Spanish for their Independence.”\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **four** of the following:\n\n - In 1810 independence was declared in Bogotá, the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, but different cities declared their independence at different moments. \n\n - In the patriots faction, who fought against Spanish rule, there were also combatants from other countries such as England and France. \n\n - The act of independence of July 20, 1810, rejected French-dominated Spain but maintained allegiance to the king of Spain.\n\n - No cohesive New Granadian identity existed, and the declaration in Bogotá did not represent all regions.\n\n - Some regions, like Pasto and Santa Marta, supported Spanish rule rather than independence.\n\n - Indigenous people, Black people, mestizos, and Creoles fought on both sides, often switching allegiances based on personal interests.\n\n - In 1819 independence from Spain was gained, but in the center of New Granada, not the peripheral regions, continuing in wars until 1823.\n\n \\\n\n [4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe statement is inaccurate as it does not reflect the complex process of independence in the Viceroyalty of New Granada. To begin, although on July 20, 1810—known as the Grito de Independencia ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the cry for independence”)—an act was signed by Creoles in Bogotá, the capital of New Granada, declaring independence from Spain, this act specifically repudiated French-dominated Spain while maintaining fidelity to the king of Spain ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, at the time, a cohesive New Granadian national identity did not yet exist. As a result, the declaration in Bogotá did not necessarily represent other cities and regions, many of which had declared their own independence from Spain already or did so afterwards, such as Cartagena, Tunja, and El Socorro, among others ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, some regions and cities supported Spanish rule over New Granada; in other words, there were New Granadians who were royalists, such as those in Pasto and Santa Marta ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, Indigenous people, Black people, mestizos, and even Creoles born in New Granada fought on both the patriot and royalist sides, often switching allegiances based on economic and political interests ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0227","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0227\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the socioeconomic structure during the time of the Latin American Wars of Independence?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Drawing and its caption found in the book $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$*América: Historia de su Colonización, Dominación e Independencia*. In English $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$America: The History of Its Colonization, Domination, and Independence\", written by Jose Coroleu, correspondent of the Royal Academy of History. Published in Barcelona, in 1896. The caption reads $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Types of Peruvian people and attire in 1820”.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Independence%20Movements%20%281800-2000%29/Origins%20%26%20Rise%20to%20Independence/images/HI0227%28A%29.png\" alt=\"HI0227(A)\">\n\n[Source: Coroleu, J. (1896). *América: Historia de su Colonización, Dominación e Independencia*. Montaner y Simón Editores. Retrieved November 15, 2024 from https://bvpb.mcu.es/iberoamerica/es/consulta/resultados_ocr.do?id=131869&tipoResultados=BIB&posicion=1&forma=ficha. CC-BY.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n- The race-based system remained largely unchanged during or after the wars of independence.\n\n- Creoles retained positions of power in the military and the church.\n\n- The economic, religious, and military spheres were still predominantly male domains.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFirstly, the source suggests that the colonial race-based system did not change significantly during or after the years of the Latin American wars of independence, at least in Perú. This is indicated by the caption of the image, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Types of Peruvian people,” which already highlights a division of social groups that had existed under Spanish rule as well. This idea is reinforced by the image itself, where individuals considered by the white hegemony at the time to belong to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$inferior races” (Indigenous, castas, and Black people), positioned at the center, are depicted with distinct social roles, as reflected in their attire ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, the source suggests that Creoles (white people of Spanish descent born in the Americas) maintained positions of power in the military and the church. This is evidenced by the attire of the priest on the left and the two high-ranking military figures on the right, who are represented as white people ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0478","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0478\n\nExplain how the Saint-Domingue Slave Rebellion (Haiti, 1791) delayed Cuban independence.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following impacts with relevant explanation from below:\n\n - The rebellion in Haiti frightened the Cuban plantation owners. \n\n - The Spanish authorities tightened control over the enslaved population.\n\n - The sugar plantations were expanded in Cuba. \n\n - Slavery was expanded in Cuba by the Spanish authorities.\n\n - Cuban elites/plantation owners became even wealthier.\n\nAny **two** of the following explanations to support each impact:\n\n - Spain increased its military presence in Cuba to cope with more enslaved people and quell any possible rebellions.\n\n - The sugar industry in Saint-Domingue was destroyed, which opened up the opportunity for Cuban plantations to expand their sugar industry.\n\n - Fear of slave rebellions and protection of the Spanish authorities made the Cuban elites even less likely to support a rebellion against the Spanish authorities.\n\n - Expanded slavery in Cuba created a paradoxical situation - more slaves created more fear in the Cuban elite, but also made them very rich.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFirst, the destruction of Saint-Domingue's economy as a result of the rebellion created an opportunity for Cuba to expand its sugar industry, leading to a surge in plantation profits ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the Cuban elite became even wealthier and had little incentive to support a rebellion against Spanish rule ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, after the rebellion, Spain increased its military presence in Cuba to control the growing enslaved population and prevent uprisings similar to the one in Haiti ($\\checkmark$). This reinforced Spanish dominance and delayed any significant movement toward Cuban independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0479","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0479\n\nIdentify **three** impacts of the Saint-Domingue Slave Rebellion (Haiti, 1791) on Cuban independence.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Created a sugar boom in Cuba to replace lost output from Saint-Domingue.\n\n -Increased fear of slave rebellions caused Spain to tighten its grip in Cuba.\n\n - Expanded/reinforced slavery in Cuba to support increased sugar production.\n\n - Pushed some wealthy white refugees from Saint-Domingue to Cuba who brought money, technical knowledge about sugar production, and anti-rebellion ideologies.\n\n - Served as inspiration for future rebellions in the Caribbean.\n\n - Pushed Cuban elites towards loyalty to Spain for protection out of fear of a rebellion \n\n - Made both the elites and the colonial government resistant to liberal and revolutionary ideas until later in the 19th century.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBecause of the rebellion, sugar production in Saint-Domingue declined, causing colonial powers to seek sugar elsewhere, and Cuba was able to fulfil this need ($\\checkmark$).  As a result, slavery was significantly increased in Cuba to support new production levels ($\\checkmark$). This also made Spain double their control over the island and pushed any Cuban elites towards Spain out of fear of a rebellion in Cuba ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0480","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0480\n\nExplain in detail **two** ways Spain economically exploited Cuba in the late-19th and early-20th centuries.\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting detailed explanation: \n\n - Spain held a monopoly over Cuban trade\n\n     - Spain imposed the mercantilist system, which restricted Cuba to trade only with Spain and its approved merchants.\n\n     - Cuban ports were closed to most foreign ships; this limited access to competitive markets.\n\n     - Spain retained tight control over import-export licenses, often favoring peninsulares merchants (Spanish born merchants living in the Spanish colonial territories)\n\n - Spain placed heavy taxes and financial controls over Cuba\n\n     - Spain imposed royal monopolies on products like tobacco and salt,forcing Cubans to buy and sell through the state\n\n     - The $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$situado” system required Cuban tax revenues to be sent to other parts of the empire, such as Florida and Venezuela, rather than being reinvested locally\n\n     - Cuba's growing economy was heavily taxed to fund Spain's military and administrative presence on the island \n\n - Cuba's industry was dominated by sugar which Spain controlled\n\n     - By the mid-19th century, Cuba had become the world's largest sugar producer controlled by Spain\n\n     - The economy became deeply dependent on slave labor.\n\n     - The expansion of sugar was driven by the Haitian Revolution, which collapsed Haiti's sugar economy, opening space for Cuba to dominate the global market.\n\n - Spain restricted industry and manufacturing in Cuba\n\n     - Spain discouraged industrial development in Cuba to prevent competition with Spanish goods.\n\n     - Laws restricted the growth of textile mills, ironworks, and shipbuilding, forcing Cuba to import manufactured goods from Spain\n\n     - Even modest proposals to industrialize the sugar industry with modern machinery faced bureaucratic hurdles or were blocked by colonial authorities.\n\n - Spain exploited Cuba's land ownership by owning most of the land in Cuba\n\n     - Large tracts of fertile land were owned by peninsulares (Spaniards born in Spain), who were favored in land grants and legal disputes\n\n     - Many Creole (Cuban-born) elites were marginalized from high-ranking offices or denied land unless fully loyal to Spain\n\n     - Spanish-owned latifundios (large estates) dominated the countryside, and land was often cultivated by enslaved Africans, not smallholders\n\n     - Land ownership patterns reinforced a rigid racial and class hierarchy, and fueled resentment among Creoles seeking autonomy or reform\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSpain maintained a colonial economic system in Cuba, holding a monopoly on trade ($\\checkmark$) and forcing Cubans to trade only with Spain or with Spanish-approved merchants ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, any new licences to trade would be given to peninsular merchants who were Spanish-born rather than Cuban-born criollos ($\\checkmark$). \n\nIn addition to controlling trade, Spain also maintained control over the land through large estates called latifundios ($\\checkmark$). Production on these estates was maintained through slave labor, not through Cuban farmers, helping the Spanish maintain control ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, it was virtually impossible for the Creole (Cuban-born elites) to buy land or become wealthy landowners, as they were often denied the right to purchase land in favor of the Spanish-born elite ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0481","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0481\n\nDescribe **two** economic impacts the abolition of slavery in 1886 had on Cuba.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following: \n\n - Plantations were forced to hire wage labour, mostly rehiring their former slaves\n\n - These newly freed slaves often became tenant farmers/sharecroppers, but were still reliant on the plantation owner economically.\n\n - The cost of production increased because owners now had to pay wages, provide housing, or offer contracts.\n\n - Some plantation owners began to import workers from other countries, most commonly China, to save money on paying wages to former slaves.\n\n - The sudden increase of new immigrant workers created unrest in the countryside, which led to social unrest that had the potential to disrupt the economy.\n\n - As opposed to decreasing the size of plantations, it increased them as many smaller plantation owners sold their estates to foreigners (mainly US investors or other Spanish-owned plantations), which led to larger sugar-producing estates.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe abolition of slavery disrupted Cuba's plantation economy, forcing a transition to wage labor, which had consequences for former slaves because they were often rehired and became tenant farmers still dependent on the landowner for income ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, many plantation owners could not economically survive having to pay wages, so they sold their land to foreign investors, thus creating even more extensive plantations in the hands of non-Cubans ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0482","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0482\n\nDescribe the impact of the Ten Years' War (1868-1878) on Cuban Independence.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\nEconomic impact: \n\n - Thousands of plantations were burned\n\n     - Trade routes were disrupted\n\n     - Cuba's economic recovery remained under Spain's exploitative control\n\n - Political impact:\n\n     - The failure of the war to secure independence fueled further discontent with Spanish rule\n\n     - Politicised a new generation of Cubans\n\n     - Gave an opportunity for future leaders to learn from the mistakes\n\n - Social impact:\n\n     - Cuba's economic recovery remained under Spain's exploitative control\n\n     - Increased the involvement of Afro-Cubans as some enslaved people were promised freedom in exchange for military service, and thousands joined the rebel forces\n\n     - Brought an awareness of the potential power the Afro-Cubans could have in helping Creole Cubans seek independence from Spain\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe prolonged conflict devastated Cuba's economy as many sugar plantations were burned in the course of the war  ($\\checkmark$).  The Ten Years War was unsuccessful and the issues which prompted the war remained; however, it gave future leaders the opportunity to learn from their mistakes ($\\checkmark$). The devastation of the economy led to more social unrest which would allow for a second attempt at independence which would integrate a broader base of opposition as during the Ten Years War, some enslaved people were offered freedom if they took up arms with rebel forces. While this led to more social and racial unrest, it also showed future leaders the  potential impact the enslaved peoples could have on a Cuban Independence Movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0551","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0551\n\nIn what ways did tension over race and class impact the Cuban independence movement?\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following tensions with supporting explanation:\n- Most white Creole elites did not embrace social change although the push for independence was initially led by wealthy white Cuban-born elites (Creoles)\n    - Carlos Manuel de Céspedes, who sought political autonomy, was reluctant to challenge the existing racial and class hierarchy which made unity difficult.\n- Afro-Cuban participation was large but devalued by other Cubans.\n    - While Afro-Cubans made up a large portion of the rebel forces, they were often excluded from high-level leadership roles, reflecting ongoing racial inequality within the movement which led to resentment and internal conflict within the movement.\n- Antoinio Maceo's participation focused heavily on the inequality of the participation of Afro-Cubans which added to the tensions\n    - Maceo, an Afro-Cuban general, also highlighted the broader lack of racial equality which again led to resentment and internal conflict.\n- Resistance to embrace change by elites (mostly Creoles)\n    - Elites in the leadership hesitated to support the redistribution of land, fearing it would upset the social hierarchy and economic system and were therefore often against the independence movement or for more US or international elite participation in the movement\n- Resistance to the abolition of slavery\n    - Many land-owning elite leaders were worried that the abolition of slavery would upset the economic system which made them unlikely to work with the rebels seeking independence.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThere were significant tensions over race and class which made the independence movement difficult. There was a difference between Creoles who were often for independence ($\\checkmark$) but did not want to challenge class and social differences which led to an inability to work with other members of the independence movement ($\\checkmark$).  Antonio Maceo was an Afro-Cuban who became very prominent and he brought a clear focus on the inequality of Afro-Cubans ($\\checkmark$) which made working together with other factions difficult as the Afro-Cubans were angry at the lack of recognition for their participation ($\\checkmark$).  Lastly, there was on the part of some elites a resistance to changing the economic system by reorganising land ($\\checkmark$) as this meant that the social hierarchy might be upset and therefore this fear made working together as a unit with the working class or the Afro-Cubans more difficult. Many elites moved towards favoring the participation of the US to achieve independence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0572","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0572\n\nDescribe the significance of the Revolt of the Comuneros in 1781 to the independence movement of New Granada (Colombia).\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The Revolt of the Comuneros (1781) was one of the first significant uprisings against Spanish rule in New Granada. It showed that people could unite and fight against unfair taxes and colonial control, inspiring future independence movements.\n\n - A source of inspiration when leaders like José Antonio Galán became symbols of resistance, inspiring revolutionary figures such as Simón Bolívar and Antonio Nariño.\n\n - As the Comuneros' revolt ended in betrayal when Spanish authorities revoked concessions and violently repressed the rebels, this event deepened colonial resentment, reinforcing the belief that true freedom could only come through complete independence from Spain.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Comuneros Rebellion of 1781 was one of the earliest large-scale challenges to Spanish authority in New Granada. It showed that diverse groups could oppose unjust taxes and colonial abuses, revealing the fragility of Spanish control and planting the seeds for future independence struggles. ($\\checkmark$) Although the rebellion failed, José Antonio Galán's actions encouraged future leaders like Simón Bolívar and Antonio Nariño and helped grow the belief that real freedom was only possible through total independence from Spain. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0573","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0573\n\nDescribe **two** short-term and **two** long-term results of the Revolt of the Comuneros in 1781.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** from each of the following:\n\n - Short term:\n\n    - The revolt was ultimately defeated, and the Spanish maintained control over New Granada.\n\n     - Brutal repression, as many Comuneros, including José Antonio Galán, were executed, and indigenous and mestizo rebels faced severe punishment.\n\n     - Spanish reforms were finally established, even though Spain initially refused to meet demands, and some minor tax reductions were later implemented to appease the population.\n\n - Long term:\n\n     - It exposed deep divisions between the Spanish Crown and its colonies. It simultaneously united the various classes of people, creoles, mestizos, and indigenous, against Spanish authority. \n\n     - The memory of the revolt fueled Creole nationalism, contributing to the general unrest. This memory of organized rebellion stayed alive, influencing the political thinking of Creole elites by proving that popular action could challenge Spanish authority.\n\n     - It inspired future revolutionary leaders, including Simón Bolívar, by demonstrating the power of mass resistance.The Comuneros' failure also taught the new leaders that stronger unity and clearer political goals were needed, shifting the struggle from short-term demands to a full independence movement.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn the short term, Spanish authorities responded with harsh repression, executing many Comuneros, including José Antonio Galán, and severely punishing indigenous and mestizo rebels to discourage future uprisings. ($\\checkmark$) However, after initially ignoring the rebels' demands, Spain later introduced small tax reductions and reforms to calm the growing unrest in the colony. ($\\checkmark$) \n\nIn the long term, the revolt inspired future leaders like Simón Bolívar, proving that people could come together to resist Spanish rule and fight for independence. ($\\checkmark$) Another long-term consequence was the exposure of the divide between the Spanish authorities and their colonies, thus driving greater cohesion and collaboration among various social classes to resist Spanish control. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0574","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0574\n\nIdentify and explain **two** political causes of the Colombian independence movement.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n\n - Bourbon Reforms: \n\n     - The Spanish Crown's centralization policies limited colonial autonomy and created resentment among Creoles.\n\n - Lack of Representation:\n\n     - The colonies had no representation in the Spanish Cortes (parliament or representative assembly), leading to demands for self-governance.\n\n- Napoleonic Invasion of Spain in 1808: \n\n     - The capture of King Ferdinand VII led to a power vacuum, prompting the colonies to question their allegiance to Spain.\n\n - Influence of the American and French Revolutions: \n\n     - These revolutions inspired ideas of liberty, equality, and republicanism among Creoles.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nTwo major political causes of Colombia's independence movement were the Bourbon Reforms and the lack of colonial representation in the Spanish *Cortes*. ($\\checkmark\\,\\checkmark$) The Bourbon Reforms, introduced by the Spanish Crown, aimed to centralize power and increase royal control over the colonies, reducing local governments' autonomy and preventing Creoles from holding high-ranking political positions. This created growing frustration among Creole elites, who felt excluded from decision-making despite their economic and social influence. ($\\checkmark$) At the same time, the colonies had no representation in the Spanish *Cortes*, meaning they had no voice in laws and policies that directly affected them. This exclusion fueled resentment and strengthened demands for self-governance, as many colonial leaders began to question their loyalty to Spain and seek greater political independence. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0575","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0575\n\nDescribe **two** goals of the *juntas* created in Bogota and Cartagena in 1810.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following goals:\n\n - Replace Spanish officials: the *juntas* aimed to remove Spanish-born authorities and replace them with local Creole leaders.\n\n - Protect the rights of Ferdinand VII: at first, the leaders of the *juntas* claimed loyalty to the king, who was currently imprisoned by Napoleon, to justify their actions and avoid being seen as rebels.\n\n - Gain political autonomy: they sought to govern themselves locally without interference from Spanish colonial rule.\n\n - Defend Creole interests: the *juntas* wanted to protect the political and economic privileges of the Creole elite.\n\n\\\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe *juntas* formed in Bogotá and Cartagena in 1810 mainly wanted to govern themselves and make local decisions without Spanish colonial officials' interference. Their goal was to have more control over political and economic matters in New Granada. ($\\checkmark$) They also wanted to protect the Creole elite's interests, ensuring that important positions and privileges stayed in the hands of American-born Spaniards rather than Spanish-born authorities. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0576","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0576\n\nCompare and contrast the Cartagena and Bogotá uprisings in 1810. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)|\n\nFor **comparison**, any of the following **similarities**:\n\n - Both rejected Spanish colonial authority and set up local juntas.\n\n - Both first claimed loyalty to King Ferdinand VII to avoid being seen as rebels.\n\n - Both inspired other provinces in New Granada to form their own juntas.\n\n - Both movements sought political autonomy, which later moved toward full independence.\n\nFor **contrast**, any of the following **differences**:\n\n - In Bogotá, the uprising started with a planned argument (the Flower Vase Incident), but in Cartagena, leaders acted directly without planning a public fight.\n\n - Creoles in Bogotá were more careful and talked with Spanish officials, while in Cartagena they quickly removed Spanish leaders without much discussion.\n\n - Bogotá's leaders stayed loyal to the Spanish king longer, but Cartagena moved faster and declared independence in 1811.\n\n - Arguments between different political groups happened sooner in Bogotá, while in Cartagena, the leaders stayed united at first.\n\n - In Bogotá, crowds of people helped push for change, but in Cartagena, the leaders made the decisions without needing big protests.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nIn 1810, growing tensions in New Granada between Creoles and Spanish authorities led to uprisings in Bogotá and Cartagena marking a regional shift towards emancipation. \n\nThe uprisings were alike in that both encouraged other provinces in New Granada to form their own juntas and start breaking away from Spanish control. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) For example,after Cartagena, the creation of similar juntas all over the viceroyalty were Cali on July 3, Pamplona the next day, and Socorro on July 10. Meanwhile, after Bogotá's events on July 20, cities such as Tunja, Socorro, and Popayán soon followed by establishing their own juntas in the following weeks. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) \n\nFurthermore, in both cases, the first goal was to get more local power, by replacing Spanish-born officials with American-born leaders while still claiming loyalty to King Ferdinand VII, who was a prisoner of Napoleon at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). However, as political debates intensified and it became clear that Spain might reassert its authority, both cities gradually shifted from seeking autonomy to demanding full independence. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)\n\nHowever, there were some notable differences between the uprisings as well. In Bogotá, the uprising began with a planned incident or trigger, called the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Flower Vase Incident” (florero de Llorente), to get people angry and start protests. ($\\checkmark\\,\\text{evidence}$) In contrast, the uprising in Cartagena was more organic in that leaders acted immediately without creating a public fight.($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference and evidence}$) Also, in Bogotá, large crowds of citizens gathered in the main square and were key in putting pressure on the viceroy, showing the importance of public involvement in the uprising, whereas in Cartagena, the process was more controlled by the elite, who made the key decisions without needing support from mass protests. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference and evidence}$)"},{"ref":"HI0577","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0577\n\nWhat, according to Source A, were the main threats to those pursuing independence from Spain?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from a supplement to Issue 5 (1811) of “La Bagatela,” a political criticism newspaper founded by Antonio Nariño, a politician, soldier, and hero of Independence. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from *La Bagatela - Suplemento al número 5.* (1811). Biblioteca Virtual de la Republica Retrieved May 11, 2025 from https://babel.banrepcultural.org/digital/collection/p17054coll26/id/1507. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Spanish deception with promises: Spain uses false promises of equality to pacify colonies and keep them under control using words such as $\\lq$fraternity' or $\\lq$integral parts'\n- Hypocrisy about liberty and independence: Spain allows freedom at home but calls it treason in America, showing double standards where Liberty in Europe is a virtue though a crime in the Americas.\n- Internal corruption and ambition: it shows the  worry that Spanish offers of honors, jobs, and titles would corrupt them so it warns against Americans accepting titles or rewards from Spain, as this weakens the independence cause and encourages betrayal\n- Violence and broken trust: the massacre in Quito shows Spain's violent repression and broken promises, warning colonists that trusting Spain is dangerous.\n- Religious manipulation: it accuses clergy members and elites of being co-opted by Spain to legitimize colonial rule using religious arguments like the Bull of Alexander VI, that is invoked to justify Spanish dominance.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A shows that the patriots feared many threats. First, there is the danger of Spanish lies and false promises. The text says the words “fraternity,” “equality,” “integral parts” are nothing more than snares laid for your credulity.” This means Spain used nice words to trick the colonies into trusting them, but they never planned to give real freedom ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecond, there is hypocrisy about liberty. The author explains that “in the former, the words $\\lq$liberty' and $\\lq$independence' are virtues; in the latter, they are $\\lq$insurrection' and $\\lq$crime.’” This shows Spain treated freedom as good in Spain but as a crime in America, so equality was impossible ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, there is the threat of violence and broken trust. The text warns: “Keep your gaze turned ever toward Quito: let the bloody scene of August 2 constantly remind you of the trust you should place in the promises of your former masters.” This refers to the massacre in Quito, showing that Spain kills those who resist and cannot be trusted ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0578","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0578\n\nWith reference to Source A, who was the intended audience, and why might it have been printed in Santa Fe de Bogota?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from a supplement to Issue 5 (1811) of “La Bagatela,” a political criticism newspaper founded by Antonio Nariño, a politician, soldier, and hero of Independence. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from *La Bagatela - Suplemento al número 5.* (1811). Biblioteca Virtual de la Republica Retrieved May 11, 2025 from https://babel.banrepcultural.org/digital/collection/p17054coll26/id/1507. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of each of the following:\n- Intended audience:\n    - Creole-born elites and educated urban readers who could access and spread printed pamphlets\n    - Provincial leaders in New Granada sympathetic to independence\n- Printing in Santa Fe:\n    - Santa Fe de Bogotá was the administrative and political capital of New Granada\n    - It housed the main printing press and a network for distributing revolutionary literature across the provinces\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A was aimed at Creole leaders and other educated city residents who could read and share political leaflets ($\\checkmark$). It was printed in Santa Fe de Bogotá because that city served as New Granada's political center ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0579","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0579\n\nWhat is the message conveyed by Source A?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from a supplement to Issue 5 (1811) of “La Bagatela,” a political criticism newspaper founded by Antonio Nariño, a politician, soldier, and hero of Independence. |\n\n[Source: Adapted from *La Bagatela - Suplemento al número 5.* (1811). Biblioteca Virtual de la Republica Retrieved May 11, 2025 from https://babel.banrepcultural.org/digital/collection/p17054coll26/id/1507. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The colonies should remain vigilant and united because the next phase of Spanish domination would be subtle manipulation, not military violence.\n- Colonial citizens should distrust Spanish promises\n- Spanish policies held a double standard between European Spain and colonial Spain.\n- There is some collaboration between the Catholic clergy in the Americas and the Spanish government, and thus, certain church figures should not be trusted.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source conveys a powerful message: do not be deceived by words or rewards, remember past atrocities, and stay together in the fight for independence ($\\checkmark$). Another message in the source is that some church figures should not be trusted because they are collaborating with the elites to help maintain Spanish domination of the colonies ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0619","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0619\n\nOutline the initial aims and makeup of the Indian National Congress founded in 1885.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of the following:\n- The Indian National Congress was a movement by educated Indians to ensure greater political representation.\n- The founding members were all university-educated, English-speaking, and initially saw themselves as a bridge between the Indian masses and the colonial power.\n- The Congress called for greater Indian representation in the legislative councils, easier access to the Indian Civil Service, and less expenditure on the army.\n- Membership consisted of both Hindus and Muslims, and the organisation felt the interests of the Indian nation were primary rather than a focus on caste or religion.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Indian National Congress, founded in 1885, initially asked for greater representation of Indians in the Legislative Councils ($\\checkmark$). They also wanted Indians to have easier access to positions in the Indian Civil Service and less expenditure spent on maintaining the army. They aimed to represent the interests of the entire nation rather than any particular group ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0620","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0620\n\nIdentify the reasons for religious tension between the Hindus and Muslims as a result of the Partition of Bengal in 1905.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The British viceroy's 1905 partition of Bengal created an eastern province with a Muslim majority and a western province with a majority of Bihari and Oriya-speaking Hindus. Bengali-speaking Hindus saw this as an attempt by the British to weaken the strong nationalist movement in the province.\n- An anti-partition movement was organised by different groups that included groups that appealed to Hindu nationalism.\n- The opposition to the partition was viewed with apprehension by the Muslims of Bengal, who believed the partition would benefit them, and support for the Muslim League increased.\n- In October 1906, Muslim community representatives met with Lord Minto, the Viceroy at Simla. This $\\lq$Simla Deputation' received assurances from Minto that the political interests of Muslims would be safeguarded in any future constitutional reforms introduced.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe partition of Bengal in 1905 created a split between Bengali Hindus and Bengali Muslims. The Bengali Hindus viewed the establishment of a Muslim-dominated eastern province as an attempt to weaken the strong nationalist movement that was centered in Bengal ($\\checkmark$). The anti-partition agitation created apprehension amongst the Bengali Muslims due to the strong emphasis on Hindu nationalism and served to further religious tensions ($\\checkmark$). Apprehension amongst the Muslims resulted in increased support for the Muslim League, and the division between the two religious groups increased when the Viceroy, Lord Minto, met with representatives of the Muslim community in 1906 and granted an assurance that the political interests of the Muslim community would be safeguarded when issuing future constitutional reforms ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0621","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0621\n\nDescribe **three** ways India's involvement in the First World War influenced the growth of Indian Nationalism.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Most nationalist leaders supported Britain’s war effort and viewed substantial Indian involvement as an opportunity to press for greater independence. The leaders hoped that the considerable Indian support for Britain in the war would result in Britain conceding to their demand for self-government and working towards a Dominion status.\n- The war caused a conflict of loyalties for Muslims in India as Britain was fighting the Ottoman Empire, whose Sultan was regarded as the Caliph of Sunni Islam, to which most Indian Muslims belonged. This resulted in opposition to the British amongst the Muslim community.\n- Heavy wartime taxation and increased recruitment of soldiers caused dissatisfaction as the war continued. The war resulted in rising food prices and fuel shortages. The failure of the monsoons in large areas in 1918 resulted in drought, causing food shortages and famine. This coincided with the spread of the Spanish flu brought by the returning troops, which killed 12 to 13 million people within a year. These were factors that increased anger against continued British control.\n- Indians realised how dependent Britain had been on their help to secure victory, as one and a half million Indians served in the British army. This was the largest volunteer army for the British Empire that fought on the Western Front in Gallipoli, Palestine, and North and East Africa. This increased their confidence that Britain would now work to meet the demands of the Indian nationalist movement for self-government to retain continued control of India.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIndia's involvement in the First World War in support of the British war effort,  despite having the backing of most Indian nationalists, caused a conflict for Indian Muslims. As Britain was now fighting the Ottoman Empire, whose Sultan was the Caliph or leader of the Sunni sect of Islam to which most Indian Muslims belonged, opposition amongst Indian Muslims grew and fueled the nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$).\n\nDissatisfaction among Indians increased as the war progressed due to heavy taxation imposed by the British as well as, at times, forced recruitment of soldiers. This anger and opposition to the British served to strengthen the nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIndian nationalists were further strengthened with the realisation that Britain's eventual victory relied, to a large extent, on Indian contribution. One and a half million Indians had served in the British army as the largest volunteer force and played a crucial role in the battles on the Western Front, Gallipoli, Palestine and North and East Africa. British dependence on Indian resources gave the nationalist movement confidence that they could now press for their demands to be accepted as they hoped the British would satisfy nationalist demands in order to retain control over the country ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0622","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0622\n\nExplain **two** steps Britain took to retain control of India following the First World War.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- In August 1917, Edwin Montagu, the new Secretary of State, together with Lord Chelmsford, the Viceroy, announced the British government's intention to encourage the gradual development of self-governing institutions for the progressive realisation of $\\lq$responsible government' in India. This was done to calm the nationalist agitation calling for increased self-government and ensure continued control of India.\n- The British government passed the Rowlatt Acts of March 1919 based on the recommendations of the Rowlatt Commission that stated in its report that $\\lq$an under-manned police force was bound to be overwhelmed once wartime legislation lapsed'.  The Rowlatt Acts provided for continuing wartime controls over political activities, including detention without trial, censorship, and house arrest. Normal judicial procedures, such as trial by jury, were abolished for political offences. This gave the British continued extraordinary powers and allowed them to bypass normal legal channels to quell any nationalist opposition.\n- The Montagu-Chelmsford report, published in July 1918, proposed a system of $\\lq$dyarchy' (dual government) where the British-appointed central government in Delhi would control key areas of government such as foreign policy, defence, and taxation. Indians only had advisory power here. The elected provincial governments would only control education, health, and economic development. The right to vote based on property and income qualifications extended only to 1.5 million of India's total population of 300 million. With the right to decision-making retained in British hands, Indians lacked any real influence on matters that influenced the entire country. In addition, the restricted right to vote strengthened British control as the upper classes were less inclined to push for drastic change.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAt the end of the First World War, Britain attempted to retain control of India with the promise of greater self-governance to calm the rising demands of Indian nationalists. This was outlined in the Montagu-Chelmsford report published in July 1918. The report proposed a system of dual government ($\\checkmark$) in which the British-appointed central government based in Delhi held control of key government elements such as defence, taxation, and foreign policy. In contrast, the elected provincial governments controlled the provinces' education, health, and economic development. The provincial governments were to be elected, and the right to vote was based on property and income qualifications, restricting the vote to 1.5 million of the total population of 300 million ($\\checkmark$).\n\nBefore the British Parliament passed the Montague-Chelmsford reforms as the Government of India Act in November 1919, the British passed the Rowlatt Acts in March 1919 ($\\checkmark$). This was done to retain control using force, as the Rowlatt Commission highlighted the dangers of an under-manned police force in India, which it said was bound to be overwhelmed once wartime legislation lapsed. The Act allowed the British to continue wartime controls over political activities such as detention without trial, censorship, and house arrest. It also set aside normal judicial procedures, such as trial by jury for political offences  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0623","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0623\n\nDescribe **two** causes of the Amritsar Massacre of 1919.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Rowlatt Acts alienated a wide section of society, including politically moderate Indians, who saw this as an insult after India’s contribution to the war effort.\n- The Rowlatt Acts became a major source of widespread opposition, with protest marches in major cities accompanied by violence and disorder.\nIn Amritsar, there were incidents of uncontrolled unrest. Banks and buildings were torched, and Europeans attacked.\n- The governor of the Punjab province sent troops commanded by General Reginald Dyer to control the situation. Dyer issued prohibitory orders on all public meetings and imposed a curfew, which he proceeded to implement using force. These actions increased anger and caused opposition and protest amongst the people, increasing tension in the lead-up to the massacre.\n- On 13th April 1919, which was Baisakhi, an important Sikh festival, a large gathering of people from the surrounding villages gathered at Jallianwala Bagh in the center of Amritsar to protest against the Rowlatt Act and the arrest of pro-independence activists Saifuddin Kitchlew and Satyapal. The Bagh (a had only one entrance/exit and was enclosed on the other three sides.  Dyer ordered troops to open fire on the unarmed crowd, killing 379 people and wounding 1200. Dyer and the troops then departed, leaving the wounded and dead, many of whom were women and children.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Amritsar Massacre of 1919 was a pivotal point of the nationalist movement. The passing of the Rowlatt Acts in 1919, following the considerable Indian contribution to the First World War and the promise of the Montagu-Chelmsford reforms, was met with widespread outrage and anger all across India. This opposition was expressed in protest marches accompanied by violence and disorder in many major cities, including Amritsar ($\\checkmark$\n).\n\nThe Governor of Punjab ordered General Reginald Dyer to control the situation, who proceeded to issue prohibitory orders on public meetings and imposed a curfew on the area. General Dyer, on April 13, 1919, decided to order troops to fire on a large gathering of people at Jallianwala Bagh, which had only one exit, resulting in the massacre of 379 people, primarily women and children. His actions and the fact that Dyer and the troops left the dead and wounded unattended resulted in outrage and anger that fuelled the nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0624","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0624\n\nAccording to Source B, what were the reasons for opposition to the Partition of Bengal in 1905?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Bipin Chandra Pal, Indian nationalist, journalist and social reformer in his book, $\\lq$The Spirit of Indian Nationalism'. Published by *The Hind Nationalist Agency* (London), 1910. |\n\n:box[On the 16th of October, 1905, the old Province of Bengal was divided into two administrative units by Lord Curzon. The old unit comprised three provinces, Bengal proper, Behar, and Orissa. It was under one Lieutenant-Governor, who was assisted in legislative work by a Legislative Council, and in revenue work by a Board of Revenue, composed of the senior members of the Civil Service. The plea for a redistribution of the administrative area was that the burden of responsibility had become too heavy for one Lieutenant-Governor.\\\n\\\nBut why should a mere redistribution of administrative area be so strongly resented by the people? The reason of it is that the people of Bengal saw in it an attempt to weaken the solidarity of the Bengalee-speaking people, and, by depriving Calcutta of the support of Eastern Bengal, to curb and cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal.[...]the most strong objection, was that it struck at the sense of unity of the Bengalee race. The Eastern and Western districts had always formed an integral social, literary and commercial, as well as an integral political and administrative unit. This redistribution destroyed to some extent, or at least sought to destroy, this sense of national unity. It was interpreted as a strategic move, and the interpretation came from the general  policy of Lord Curzon, whose one aim was, it seemed to the thinking and intelligent classes in the country, to kill the growing political power of the educated classes.\\\n\\\nThe proposal for thus dividing Bengal at once called forth an agitation, the like of which had never been seen before in British India. The classes and the masses stood shoulder to shoulder to protest against what they all regarded as a wanton outrage on their cherished sentiments. Mass meetings were held in every part of the province, and people came to these in their thousands and tens of thousands. Petitions were sent to the Government. The Hindus and the Mahomedans both joined together to support these. The protest, especially from Eastern Bengal, was absolutely unanimous.[...]\\\n\\\nWhen the final decision of the Government of India on the Partition of Bengal was published, people saw that their suspicions of the scheme were fully justified. In this document, Lord Curzon gave a new reason for it; and this was to institute a special Mahomedan Province in British India. In the eastern districts of Bengal there is a very great preponderance of Mahomedan populations. But still there never had been any Hindu-Mahomedan disturbance there within living memory. The two communities had always lived together in peace and amity.\n]\n\n[Source: Pal, B.P. (1910). *The Spirit of Indian Nationalism.* The Hind Nationalist Agency (London). Retrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/umn.31951p009866515. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The people of the province viewed the partition of Bengal as an attempt to weaken the solidarity of the Bengalee-speaking people, Hindus and Muslims.\n- It was seen as an attempt to cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal by depriving Calcutta of the support of Eastern Bengal.\n- The partition destroyed the sense of national unity of the Bengalis by disrupting the integral social, literary, commercial, political and administrative unit long established between the Eastern and Western districts.\n- The aim of the partition seemed to be focused on destroying the growing political power of the educated classes.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source B, opposition to the partition of Bengal resulted from the fact that the people of Bengal saw it as an attempt to weaken the solidarity of the Bengalee-speaking people of different religions ($\\checkmark$). It was also seen as an attempt to destroy the political influence of Bengal in the nationalist movement by depriving the major center, Calcutta, of the support of Eastern Bengal ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, the partition would destroy the peaceful integration of the Eastern and Western districts of Bengal that long formed one united social, literary, commercial, and political unit ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0625","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0625\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the constitutional reforms passed by the British?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A political cartoon by David Low titled $\\lq$Sit-Down Strike In India', March 31, 1937. The howdah on the back of the elephant has the words, $\\lq$CONSTITUTION. Front seats strictly reserved', in which an occupant asks, $\\lq$Gad sir, call a taxi!'. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Independence%20Movements%20%281800-2000%29/Origins%20%26%20Rise%20to%20Independence/images/sit%20down%20strike%20in%20india.PNG\" alt=\"sit down strike in india\">\n[Source: Low, D. (1936, January 20). *Low Again.* Internet Archive. Retrieved March 13, 2025 from\nhttps://archive.org/details/371201-low-again-1930s-political-satirical-cartoons.\n CC-BY-ND 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Source A suggests the constitutional reforms passed by the British do not result in any real devolution of power to the Indians. This is apparent from the writing on howdah which states, $\\lq$CONSTITUTION. Front seats strictly reserved' and the howdah itself being occupied by British individuals.\n- The source also suggests that the Congress party is not inclined to support the constitutional reforms. This is indicated by the elephant which represents the Congress refusing to carry the British passengers.\n- The source further suggests that opposition to the constitutional reforms in India is through strikes. This is indicated by the caption that reads $\\lq$sit-down strikes in India' and by the depiction of Congress leader Nehru who is shown as a mahout unwilling to ask or persuade the elephant to move.\n- The source also suggests that there are some amongst the British who are not inclined to struggle with the opposition to British rule in India and would want to exit and leave the problems behind. This is indicated by the blurb with one of the British passengers asking, $\\lq$Gad sir, call a taxi'.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A suggests that the British did not intend to empower Indians with the right to self-government through the constitutional reforms they had passed. This is apparent from the words on the howdah in which only the British are seated, which states, $\\lq$CONSTITUTION. Front seats strictly reserved' ($\\checkmark$). The Source also suggests that the constitutional reforms will not be supported by the Congress Party in India. This is conveyed by the elephant representing the Congress, refusing to carry the British passengers ($\\checkmark$). The source further suggests that the constitutional reforms will be opposed in India through strikes and protests. This is highlighted by the caption of the cartoon that reads, $\\lq$sit-down strike in India' and by the depiction of Congress leader Nehru, who is shown as a mahout unwilling to ask or persuade the elephant to move along with Gandhi who is shown disinterested, sitting and reading in a tree ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0626","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0626\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source B for an historian studying the impact of the Partition of Bengal on the Nationalist movement in India.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Bipin Chandra Pal, Indian nationalist, journalist and social reformer in his book, $\\lq$The Spirit of Indian Nationalism'. Published by *The Hind Nationalist Agency* (London), 1910. |\n\n:box[On the 16th of October, 1905, the old Province of Bengal was divided into two administrative units by Lord Curzon. The old unit comprised three provinces, Bengal proper, Behar, and Orissa. It was under one Lieutenant-Governor, who was assisted in legislative work by a Legislative Council, and in revenue work by a Board of Revenue, composed of the senior members of the Civil Service. The plea for a redistribution of the administrative area was that the burden of responsibility had become too heavy for one Lieutenant-Governor.\\\n\\\nBut why should a mere redistribution of administrative area be so strongly resented by the people? The reason of it is that the people of Bengal saw in it an attempt to weaken the solidarity of the Bengalee-speaking people, and, by depriving Calcutta of the support of Eastern Bengal, to curb and cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal.[...]the most strong objection, was that it struck at the sense of unity of the Bengalee race. The Eastern and Western districts had always formed an integral social, literary and commercial, as well as an integral political and administrative unit. This redistribution destroyed to some extent, or at least sought to destroy, this sense of national unity. It was interpreted as a strategic move, and the interpretation came from the general  policy of Lord Curzon, whose one aim was, it seemed to the thinking and intelligent classes in the country, to kill the growing political power of the educated classes.\\\n\\\nThe proposal for thus dividing Bengal at once called forth an agitation, the like of which had never been seen before in British India. The classes and the masses stood shoulder to shoulder to protest against what they all regarded as a wanton outrage on their cherished sentiments. Mass meetings were held in every part of the province, and people came to these in their thousands and tens of thousands. Petitions were sent to the Government. The Hindus and the Mahomedans both joined together to support these. The protest, especially from Eastern Bengal, was absolutely unanimous.[...]\\\n\\\nWhen the final decision of the Government of India on the Partition of Bengal was published, people saw that their suspicions of the scheme were fully justified. In this document, Lord Curzon gave a new reason for it; and this was to institute a special Mahomedan Province in British India. In the eastern districts of Bengal there is a very great preponderance of Mahomedan populations. But still there never had been any Hindu-Mahomedan disturbance there within living memory. The two communities had always lived together in peace and amity.\n]\n\n[Source: Pal, B.P. (1910). *The Spirit of Indian Nationalism.* The Hind Nationalist Agency (London). Retrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/umn.31951p009866515. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- The author was a prominent Indian nationalist and journalist suggesting access to people who shaped the events of the partition. Another value is the date of publication in 1910, where the events and impact of the partition is still recent.\n- The purpose of the source is to provide an in-depth focus on the factors driving the spirit of Indian nationalism.\n- The content analyses the reasons for and people's reactions to the partition.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- Because the source was published in 1910 it cannot provide insight into the long-term impact of the Partition of Bengal on the growth of the Nationalist movement.\n- The source's purpose has limitations, as the author's nationalist leanings tend to influence the viewpoints presented in the source.\n- The author was part of a triumvirate of assertive nationalists in British India in the early 20th century, from 1906 to 1918 referred to a Lal Bal Pal, who advocated the Swadeshi movement during the anti-Partition agitation in Bengal.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable with reference to the origin as the author was a well-known Indian nationalist and also a journalist. In both these roles, he would have gained an insider view on events that shaped the nationalist movement along with the factors impacting the partition of Bengal. The date of publication of the source in 1910 also holds value as the reaction to the partition is still recent ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  The purpose of the source also holds value as its focus on $\\lq$the spirit of Indian nationalism', aims to provide an indepth dive into the factors shaping Indian nationalism ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). Finally the content of the source provides value as it provides an in-depth analysis of the reasons for partition and the reactions of people to this event ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nThe source also has some limitations with reference to the origin as it was published in 1910 and so fails to provide an analysis of the long-term impact of the partition of Bengal on the growth of the nationalist movement and its contribution to the factors that shaped the eventual independence of India ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Further the source is also limited with reference to its purpose as the author's nationalist leanings tend to influence the viewpoints presented in the source ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI0627","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0627\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and C reveal about the Partition of Bengal.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Bipin Chandra Pal, Indian nationalist, journalist and social reformer in his book, $\\lq$The Spirit of Indian Nationalism'. Published by *The Hind Nationalist Agency* (London), 1910. |\n\n:box[On the 16th of October, 1905, the old Province of Bengal was divided into two administrative units by Lord Curzon. The old unit comprised three provinces, Bengal proper, Behar, and Orissa. It was under one Lieutenant-Governor, who was assisted in legislative work by a Legislative Council, and in revenue work by a Board of Revenue, composed of the senior members of the Civil Service. The plea for a redistribution of the administrative area was that the burden of responsibility had become too heavy for one Lieutenant-Governor.\\\n\\\nBut why should a mere redistribution of administrative area be so strongly resented by the people? The reason of it is that the people of Bengal saw in it an attempt to weaken the solidarity of the Bengalee-speaking people, and, by depriving Calcutta of the support of Eastern Bengal, to curb and cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal.[...]the most strong objection, was that it struck at the sense of unity of the Bengalee race. The Eastern and Western districts had always formed an integral social, literary and commercial, as well as an integral political and administrative unit. This redistribution destroyed to some extent, or at least sought to destroy, this sense of national unity. It was interpreted as a strategic move, and the interpretation came from the general  policy of Lord Curzon, whose one aim was, it seemed to the thinking and intelligent classes in the country, to kill the growing political power of the educated classes.\\\n\\\nThe proposal for thus dividing Bengal at once called forth an agitation, the like of which had never been seen before in British India. The classes and the masses stood shoulder to shoulder to protest against what they all regarded as a wanton outrage on their cherished sentiments. Mass meetings were held in every part of the province, and people came to these in their thousands and tens of thousands. Petitions were sent to the Government. The Hindus and the Mahomedans both joined together to support these. The protest, especially from Eastern Bengal, was absolutely unanimous.[...]\\\n\\\nWhen the final decision of the Government of India on the Partition of Bengal was published, people saw that their suspicions of the scheme were fully justified. In this document, Lord Curzon gave a new reason for it; and this was to institute a special Mahomedan Province in British India. In the eastern districts of Bengal there is a very great preponderance of Mahomedan populations. But still there never had been any Hindu-Mahomedan disturbance there within living memory. The two communities had always lived together in peace and amity.\n]\n\n[Source: Pal, B.P. (1910). *The Spirit of Indian Nationalism.* The Hind Nationalist Agency (London). Retrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/umn.31951p009866515. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Kate L. Mitchell, member of Institute of Pacific Relations and the magazine Amerasia, in her book $\\lq$India without fable, a 1942 survey'. Published by *A. A. Knopf* (New York), 1942. |\n\n[Source: Mitchell, K. L. (1942). *India without fable, a 1942 survey.* A. A. Knopf (New York). Retrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b83906. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources state that the partition of Bengal resulted in intense opposition and a wave of nationalist opposition.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$The proposal for thus dividing Bengal at once called forth an agitation, the like of which had never been seen before in British India.'\n    - Source C:  $\\lq$1905 marked the beginning of the first wave of nationalist activity in India.'\n- Both sources state that Bengal was a strong centre of the nationalist movement.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$to curb and cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal'\n    - Source C: $\\lq$Bengal was the center of revolutionary political activity in India.'\n- Both sources state that the aim of the partition was to create an eastern province with a Muslim majority.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$this was to institute a special Mahomedan Province in British India'\n    - Source C: $\\lq$decision to create a new province in Eastern Bengal in which the Moslems. would form a clear majority of the population, was deeply resented.'\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source B mentions the opposition to the partition came from both Hindus and the Muslims unlike Source C which states the opposition was led by the Hindus.\n    - Source B: $\\lq$Petitions were sent to the Government. The Hindus and the Mahomedans both joined together to support these.'\n    - Source C: $\\lq$was deeply resented by the educated middle class (predominantly Hindu) on the grounds that it was designed to weaken political activity and subject nationalist Hindus to Moslem control.'\n- The sources also differ in their discussion regarding the nature of the agitation e.g. violence versus petitions and meetings.\n    - Source C: $\\lq$In protest, a boycott of foreign goods was proclaimed on August 7, 1905, and there was a rapid spread of revolutionary and terrorist activity in Bengal.'\n    - Source B: $\\lq$Mass meetings were held in every part of the province, and... Petitions were sent to the Government.\n- Source C mentions that the plan for partition was devised by Lord Curzon but the implementation was left to his successor Lord Minto, while Source B only credits Lord Curzon with the partition of Bengal.\n    - Source C: $\\lq$the Partition of Bengal, a plan devised by Lord Curzon and carried out under his successor, Lord Minto.'\n    - Source B: $\\lq$the old Province of Bengal was divided into two administrative units by Lord Curzon.'\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWhen comparing Sources B and C, both sources state  that the partition of Bengal resulted in a wave of nationalist opposition and aroused  intense opposition ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source B states $\\lq$The proposal for thus dividing Bengal at once called forth an agitation, the like of which had never been seen before in British India while Source C mentions, $\\lq$1905 marked the beginning of the first wave of nationalist activity in India' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both Sources also state that Bengal was a major centre of the nationalist movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). This is evident in Source B where the partition was said $\\lq$to curb and cripple the increasing political strength and influence of Bengal' which is also reflected in Source C  that states, $\\lq$Bengal was the center of revolutionary political activity in India' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth Sources also have points of contrast. Source B mentions that both Hindus and Muslims opposed partition unlike Source C which states the opposition was led by the Hindus ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). This is evident in Source B which states, $\\lq$Petitions were sent to the Government. The Hindus and the Mahomedans both joined together to support these' while Source C states the partition, $\\lq$was deeply resented by the educated middle class (predominantly Hindu) on the grounds that it was designed to weaken political activity and subject nationalist Hindus to Moslem control' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both sources differ in their discussion regarding the nature of the agitation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source B mentions its revolutionary nature, $\\lq$In protest, a boycott of foreign goods was proclaimed on August 7, 1905, and there was a rapid spread of revolutionary and terrorist activity in Bengal.' while Source C emphasises its moderate and disciplined nature, $\\lq$Mass meetings were held in every part of the province, and... Petitions were sent to the Government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Finally, in contrast, Source C mentions that the plan for partition was devised by Lord Curzon but the implementation was left to his successor Lord Minto, while Source B only credits Lord Curzon with the partition of Bengal. This is evident in Source C which states, 'the Partition of Bengal, a plan devised by Lord Curzon and carried out under his successor, Lord Minto.' while Source B only mentions,\n$\\lq$the old Province of Bengal was divided into two administrative units by Lord Curzon' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0628","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0628\n\nWhat, according to Source C, was the impact of the Partition of Bengal on the Congress Party?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Kate L. Mitchell, member of Institute of Pacific Relations and the magazine Amerasia, in her book $\\lq$India without fable, a 1942 survey'. Published by *A. A. Knopf* (New York), 1942. |\n\n[Source: Mitchell, K. L. (1942). *India without fable, a 1942 survey.* A. A. Knopf  (New York). Retrieved March 15, 2025 from https://hdl.handle.net/2027/uc1.$b83906. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The partition led the Congress, dominated by the Extremists, to adopt a four-point program in the 1906 Calcutta session. The program emphasised Swaraj or self-governance, support of the boycott protests against the partition, and support for Swadeshi or the promotion of indigenous industries and national education.\n- The differing reactions to the partition led to a formal split in the Congress at the 1907 Surat Session, with the Extremists leaving the organisation. The Moderates emphasised the path of gradual political reform leading to parliamentary government, and the Extremists asked for violent revolutionary action to achieve complete independence. The Moderates, led by Gokhale, prevailed over the Extremists led by Tilak.\n- The opposition to the partition led to Tilak being sentenced to imprisonment in 1908 for six years, along with many Extremist leaders who were either jailed or deported.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Partition of Bengal led the Indian National Congress to adopt a more extreme, four-point program in 1906 which emphasised self-governance, support of the boycotts, the promotion of indigenous industries and a national education system ($\\checkmark$). The Partition also led to a formal split in Congress between the Extremists and the Moderates ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0711","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0711\n\nExplain **two** impacts of the Amritsar Massacre of 1919 on the Nationalist movement in India. [4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Increase in British suppression of dissent:\n    - The massacre was followed by public flogging and punishments such as the ‘Crawling Order’, which forced any Indian that passed down the street where a British woman missionary had been attacked, to crawl on their stomachs.\n- Conflicting official British viewpoints resulting in a decline of British legitimacy:\n    - The Hunter Commission, appointed by the British government, censured Dyer's actions, forcing him to resign from the army. However, British officials in India and London expressed approval, and the House of Lords passed a motion approving his actions. This increased the anger in India and strengthened the nationalist movement because it was seen as a disregard for Indian lives.\n- Increasing Nationalist support for complete independence\n    - The news of the massacre and the British reaction to it caused widespread anger and support for the Congress, which demanded a call for an end to British rule rather than just self-governance. One of the Congress leaders who condemned the massacre was Mohandas Gandhi, who now emerged as a dominant figure in the nationalist movement.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Amritsar Massacre of 1919 was followed by an increased British suppression, particularly in Punjab. The British resorted to public floggings and other harsh punishments that aimed to humiliate, such as the $\\lq$Crawling Act', that forced Indians to crawl on the stomachs to get to their homes located on a street that had witnessed an attack on a British woman. This served to stiffen resistance against British rule and further strengthened the Nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe massacre caused widespread revulsion not just in India but also in Britain. This led the British government to appoint the Hunter Commission that proceeded to censure General Dyer as a result of which he was forced to resign from the British army. However, the General found support amongst some British officials both in India and London including the House of Lords that passed a motion approving his actions; a signal of Britain's intentions to continue to retain control of India ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0712","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0712\n\nDescribe **three** reasons for the reversal of the Partition of Bengal in 1911.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The anti-partition movement protestors organised a boycott of British goods, which became known as the Swadeshi (self-sufficiency) movement. This resulted in a 25% drop of British imports into India and helped expand Indian industries.\n- Confrontation over Bengal radicalised parts of the Congress, and a revolutionary wing called the New Party emerged under the leadership of Bal Gangadhar Tilak, which urged active opposition to British rule.\n- The Morley-Minto reforms were implemented in the Indian Councils Act of 1909, by which Indians could elect representatives to sit on the Viceroy's council. The provincial councils were enlarged and given more power, and two Indians were nominated to serve on the Council for India in London, which advised the Secretary of State.\n- The 1910 elections for the central and provincial legislative councils gave the Muslims separate representation with separate electorates and reserved seats. Economic and educational qualifications determined the right to vote for all Indians.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe reversal of the Partition of Bengal in 1911 resulted from increasing opposition, which had economic and political repercussions that threatened to weaken British rule.\n\nThe reversal of the Partition of Bengal was a result of the adverse economic impact on Britain from the boycott of British goods in India along with the promotion of the Swadeshi or self-sufficiency movement.  The anti-partition movement organised a boycott of British goods and spread the message of self-sufficiency to encourage the use of Indian goods. The Swadeshi (self-sufficiency) movement impacted Britain's economy adversely as it resulted in a 25% drop in British imports into India and helped expand Indian industries. The movement also revealed the power of economic boycotts, which soon became a cornerstone of the Indian National Congress's policy ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother reason for the reversal of the partition was the British fear of destabilisation of their control in India from the increasing use of more violent revolutionary tactics. The protests against the partition led to the emergence of leaders in the Indian National Congress who supported the use of such revolutionary tactics against British rule. A revolutionary wing of the Congress led them, called the New Party, which emerged under the leadership of Bal Gangadhar Tilak, who urged active opposition to British rule ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, the reversal of the Partition was prompted by British fears of losing control over India's army. British attempts to crush the protests resulted in greater opposition with fears that Indian soldiers in the British army in Punjab were about to go on strike. The threat of destabilisation of British rule prompted the Secretary of State for India, John Morley, and the Viceroy Minto to implement concessions to retain British control of the subcontinent. The Morley-Minto reforms or the Indian Councils Act of 1909 permitted Indian elected representatives to sit on the Viceroy's council, the enlarged provincial councils were given more power, and two Indians were nominated to serve on the Council for India in London, who advised the Secretary of State. Finally, the partition was reversed in 1911 and the capital of British India moved from Calcutta to Delhi ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0713","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0713\n\nExplain the impacts of **two** actions taken by the British government in India between 1918 and 1939 on the Nationalist Movement.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{Government of India Act 1919}}$ introduced a form of $\\lq$dyarchy' (dual government), where some power over local affairs in agriculture, education, and health was given to Indian legislatures in the provinces, while the British retained firm control over the central government, police, justice system and retained the right to veto legislation. The Act also extended the right to vote for provincial legislatures to 10% of the adult male population based on property qualifications, and separate electorates for minorities were established.\n    - The initial reaction from the moderate section of the nationalists was cooperation with the British regime, which seemed to be taking steps towards self-government. However, with the release of the Hunter Report into the Amritsar Massacre of 1919, together with the realisation that the British retained control as a result of the right to veto legislation, nationalist attitudes hardened with Gandhi and the Congress leaders urging voters to boycott the first elections of November 1920.\n    - It reinforced separate identities and created divisions, encouraging communalism and further perpetuating a policy of $\\lq$divide and rule'.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{The Simon Commission}}$ appointed in 1927, recommended further constitutional reforms. The commission did not include Indians.\n    - Members of the Commission faced angry public demonstrations in India, and political organisations, including the Indian National Congress and Muslim League, refused to give evidence before it.\n    - In 1928, a series of All-Parties Conferences released the $\\lq$Nehru Report,' calling for dominion status with full self-government for India and a federal constitution.\n    - Attempting to avoid a showdown between the nationalists and the British authorities, the Viceroy, Lord Irwin, published the $\\lq$Irwin Declaration' in October 1929. It confirmed British support for dominion status and proposed a Round Table conference in London to draw up a constitutional framework for India.\n    - The $\\lq$Nehru Report', together with the ‘Irwin Declaration', established the irrelevance of any report produced by the Simon Commission.\n- $\\underline{\\textrm{The Government of India Act 1935}}$ was a set of constitutional reforms that gave elected Indian ministers in the provinces more control. However, Britain retained control through emergency powers that could be imposed whenever necessary. The system of $\\lq$dyarchy' was abolished, and the provisional governments controlled everything except defense and foreign affairs. The electorate was separated into religious, racial, and $\\lq$special interest' constituencies.\n    - Both the Congress and the League condemned the measures as inadequate but decided to participate in the provincial elections held in 1937.\n    - Congress emerged with a landslide victory with 70% of the popular vote. The Muslim League got barely 5% of the total Muslim vote.\n    - The Congress turned down Jinnah, the leader of the Muslim League's offer of cooperation in provinces with large Muslim minorities, which caused the League to strengthen its efforts to gain a mass following.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Government of India Act of 1919 formalised the reforms outlined in the Montagu-Chelmsford report of 1918 and aimed to calm the opposition to British rule resulting from the Amritsar Massacre. It introduced a system of $\\lq$dyarchy' (dual government), with some power over local affairs in agriculture, education, and health given to the provincial Indian legislatures. In contrast, the central government remained under British control ($\\checkmark$). The moderate nationalists initially viewed the Act as evidence that the British government was taking steps towards self-government ($\\checkmark$). However, nationalist attitudes hardened with the realisation that the British ultimately retained control due to the power to veto legislation. This together with the release of the Hunter Report into the Amritsar Massacre of 1919 prompted Gandhi and the Congress leaders to urge voters to boycott the first elections of November 1920 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nContinuing protests led Britain to take further action and appoint the Simon Commission in 1927. The Commission visited India to explore the possibility of  further constitutional reforms but the visit caused anger as it failed to include any Indians ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the Commission faced angry public demonstrations in India, and political organisations, including the Congress and Muslim League, refused to meet the members of the Commission ($\\checkmark$). The mandate of the Commission to recommend constitutional reforms was further undermined by the decision taken in 1928 by all the Indian political parties to demand full self-government, rather than agree to a few reforms.   This demand was outlined in the release of the Nehru Report following a series of All-Parties Conferences in 1928, where dominion status with full self-government for India and a federal constitution was highlighted. The increasing conflict between the nationalists and the British authorities resulting from the Nehru Report led to the publication by the Viceroy Lord Irwin of the $\\lq$Irwin Declaration' in 1929.  The Declaration now confirmed British support for the Indian demand for dominian status.  The agitation against the Simon Commission by the nationalists also led the Viceroy Lord Irwin to propose a Round Table conference in London to draw up a constitutional framework for India, furthering the goal of the nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0714","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0714\n\nDescribe **three** impacts of the Second World War on India's independence.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny of **three** of the following:\n- The British viceroy committed India to fight on the Allied side at the start of the Second World War, without consulting the Indian Legislative Council. This emphasised India's subservience as a colony and strengthened the resolve of the nationalist movement to aim for total independence.\n- In 1941, the British Prime Minister Winston Churchill after signing the Atlantic Charter which supported the rights of all people to self-determination, told the British parliament this provision did not apply to India. This further outraged Indian nationalists.\n- Churchill changed his stance as a result of sweeping Japanese victories in Asia. Under pressure from the US government  and recognising the need for support from Indian leaders, Churchill sent Stafford Cripps to India in 1942 to negotiate with Indian nationalist leaders. The proposals put forward by Cripps included the commitment to grant India independence after the war was over. However,the mission failed as the Congress was unhappy at proposals that would allow states to opt out of an united India and Churchill was unwilling to make any real meaningful concessions.\n- In 1943, two to four million people died in the Great Bengal famine, caused by loss of rice imports from Japanese occupied Burma and by the British administrative decision to divert food from the Bengal countryside to feed the military in Europe,despite being aware of the famine conditions. The famine was also aggravated by the $\\lq$scorched earth' policy implemented by the British to stall Japanese advance into Bengal. This resulted in the destruction of 20000 boats, depriving the region of transporting food or fishing along with the destruction of bridges across rivers.\n- India's contribution to the allied victory consisted of Indian soldiers fighting in North Africa, Italy and Burma. The Indian economy was also a significant contributor to the defeat of the Axis Powers. The Jamshedpur steel complex became the largest producer of steel in the British Empire. Bombay became a major center for light engineering and manufacturing of pharmaceuticals and chemicals.\n- The war transformed India's economic relationship with Britain. India had been in debt to Britain prior to the war. The war forced Britain to borrow heavily from India and by 1945 Britain owed India large sums of money. Britain emerged victorious from the Second World War but with her economy on the brink of collapse. It became apparent that she was not in a position to maintain her global empire and by 1945, it was time for Britain to make good its promises of independence and negotiate with nationalist leaders.\n- The election of a Labour government in 1945 hastened Britain's determination to reach a settlement with Indian nationalist leaders as they were anxious to focus on domestic reforms in Britain and the creation of a welfare state.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBritain's entry into the Second World War gave a boost to the Indian Independence Movement, as the war economically weakened Britain and defeats at the hands of the Japanese in South East Asia dented its aura of invincibility which catalyzed the resolve of the nationalist movement to work towards independence ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIndian commitment to fight in the war was made without consulting the Indian Legislative Council which emphasised India's subservience as a colony. This, together with the stand taken by Churchill in the British parliament that the Atlantic Charter and the right of self-determination of all people did not apply to India, angered the Indians and motivated the  nationalist movement ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe war also strengthened the Indian economy while weakening the British economy. The Jamshedpur steel complex became the largest producer of steel in the British Empire and Bombay a major center for light engineering and manufacture of pharmaceuticals and chemicals. Britain was now forced to borrow heavily from India during the war and, by 1945, Britain owed India large sums of money leaving it in no position to maintain its global empire thus ensuring the inevitability of Indian independence ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"Rights & Protest","subtopics":[{"name":"Apartheid South Africa","questions":[{"ref":"HI0036","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0036\n\nDefine the terms “petty apartheid” and “grand apartheid” and give examples of each.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAny combination of following to define “petty apartheid” for **one** mark:\n-   Everyday, small-scale segregation and discrimination that affected the daily lives of non-white South Africans.\n-   Racial separation in public spaces and services.\n\nAny of the following examples to illustrate “petty apartheid” for **one** mark:\n-   Separate facilities and amenities such as buses, public restrooms, schools, hospitals, beaches, and even park benches.\n\nAny combination of the following to define “grand apartheid” for **one** mark:\n-   Large-scale, systematic policies that structured South African society along racial lines\n-   Policies that created separate political and economic systems for white and non-white South Africans.\n\nAny of the following examples to illustrate “grand apartheid” for **one** mark:\n-   The creation of the Bantustans (homelands) where black South Africans were forced to live, often in underdeveloped and remote areas.\n-   Laws that restricted land ownership (rural or urban), employment opportunities, and movement/migration based on race.\n-   Naming and description of specific legislation including The Population Registration Act, the Group Areas Act, and/or the Bantu Authorities Act.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPetty apartheid refers to the day-to-day forms of segregation experienced by black South Africans ($\\checkmark$) including restrictions on use of public restrooms, beaches or parks ($\\checkmark$).\n\nGrand apartheid created entirely different systems for non-white South Africans at a large scale ($\\checkmark$). The South African government created legislation like the Population Registration Act which classified South Africans racially and then was used to determine what parts of society they could access including which kinds of jobs they were allowed to work in ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0037","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0037\n\nCompare and contrast the roles and significance of the African National Congress (ANC) and the South African Communist Party (SACP) in the anti-Apartheid movement in South Africa. [6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any **two to four** of the following similarities:\n-   Both organisations played a critical role in the actions that would lead to the eventual collapse of the apartheid regime in South Africa.\n-   Both the ANC and the SACP were banned by the South African government because of the threat they presented to its all-white leadership.\n-   After being banned, both parties continued to work underground and often in alliance with each other against the apartheid regime.\n-   Both parties produced significant leaders in the anti-apartheid movement including Nelson Mandela (ANC), Albert Luthuli (ANC), Dr. Yusuf Dadoo (SACP) and Moses Kotane (SACP).\n-   Both parties played a substantial role in the formation and actions taken by Umkhonto we Sizwe (the “MK”), the armed, militant wing of the ANC and both participated in sabotage, guerrilla warfare and other armed resistance against the South African government.\n-   Both parties drew significant international support in terms of money, materials and philosophical backing.\n\nFor contrast, any **two to four** of the following differences:\n-   While the ANC advocated for some socialist ideals/goals including policies to reduce inequality and nationalise certain industries, its primary political ideology was based in African nationalism and focused on the liberation of non-white South Africans. The SACP’s ideology was primarily a class-based, anti-capitalist struggle for both white and non-white working class South Africans.\n-   The ANC drew its membership from a variety of political and economic perspectives including black nationalists, white liberals, socialists and religious leaders while the SACPs membership was more limited as it drew from a narrower ideological group committed more to Marxist-Leninst principles.\n-   While both groups drew significant international support, the ANC’s support tended to come from the capitalist, democratic Western World (especially Great Britain) while the SACP’s was drawn more from the Soviet Union and its sphere of influence.\n-   Because the SACP was banned and forced underground much earlier than the ANC, its role in the anti-apartheid struggle tended to be more covert, acting more through other larger, less-restricted organisations including, but not limited to the ANC.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nBoth the ANC and SACP played a significant role in the struggle against apartheid and shared a common goal of dismantling the white-dominated apartheid government in South Africa ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). As a result of the threat they posed to the South African government, both parties were also effectively banned ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). The CPSA, the predecessor to the SACP, voluntarily disbanded in 1950 in the face of the Suppression of Communism Act and reformed in 1953 as the SACP. The ANC was banned in 1960 in the aftermath of the Sharpeville massacre ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nAfter the banning of the ANC, both the ANC and SACP continued to work in an underground alliance to form the MK, the armed militant wing of the ANC which would carry out various forms of resistance including sabotage, bombings of civilian facilities and guerrilla tactics ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nWhile both organisations worked in alliance for many years, they still maintained some significant differences. One major difference was the size and membership base ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). The ANC’s membership included a variety of people from a variety of ideological perspectives appealing to African/black nationalists and white liberals and while the SACP’s narrow ideological origins in communism/socialism tended to limit its membership and appeal ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth groups drew significant international support ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$), but from entirely different sources ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). The ANC’s international support tended to come from Western nations and particularly Great Britain while the SACP’s support, both financial and material, came primarily from the Soviet Union including the training of MK members ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0038","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0038\n\nDescribe the purpose of the Freedom Charter.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of the following:\n- A statement of principles and goals for the various groups opposing apartheid.\n- A document that set the ideological foundation for a future democratic South Africa.\n- The document written at the Congress of the People that created the vision and ideals for ending apartheid and creating a more democratic and equal South Africa.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Freedom Charter was the document that set out a series of ideals and goals for South Africa's future including equal rights for white and non-white South Africans ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0039","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0039\n\nExplain the role and significance of Albert Luthuli in the anti-Apartheid movement.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following (or minor variation) for role:\n\n- Leader (President-General) of the African National Congress beginning in 1952 who emphasised the concept of Ubuntu and non-violent resistance in his anti-apartheid activism.\n\nAny **two** of the following (or combination of the following) for significance:\n\n- Public supporter and participant in the Defiance Campaign in June of 1952 which helped dramatically increase overall membership of the ANC which represented the first large-scale, multiracial anti-apartheid resistance.\n- Rooted his resistance and philosophy of non-violence in his Christianity, creating a wider audience and appeal for the ANC and anti-apartheid movement.\n- Nobel Peace Prize winner who helped draw significant international attention to the anti-apartheid struggle in South Africa.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAlbert Luthuli was the national leader of the African National Congress (ANC) beginning in 1952 until his death in 1967 ($\\checkmark$). He was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1960 for his deep commitment to nonviolence in the anti-apartheid movement ($\\checkmark$). He led the ANC during a time where its membership was growing dramatically after growth in popularity during the Defiance Campaign in 1952 and was a renowned orator who inspired many subsequent anti-apartheid activism ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0040","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0040\n\nDescribe **one** of the events leading to the Sharpeville Massacre in 1960.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following (or a combination of them):\n- A common form of protest was the burning of “pass books” (or “dompas”) which were used to control movement and employment of Black South Africans and thus were a point of great resentment.\n- A large group (5000-10000) of anti-apartheid demonstrators gathered at the police station in Sharpeville to protest the enforcement of “pass laws” by intentionally not carrying their “pass books” as was required by law.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nA large group of demonstrators gathered at the police station to protest the use and enforcement of “pass books” which controlled various aspects of Black South African’s lives. The demonstrators were intentionally not carrying their pass books which was required by the law. The protest escalated, though it is unclear why, and led to the policy opening fire on the demonstrators ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0093","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0093\n\nWith reference to Source A, explain the impact of the Defiance Campaign on the resistance to apartheid in South Africa. \n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | This extract from a reputable online history site focusing on South African history, SA History Online, explores the impact of the Defiance Campaign on a national and international scale. |\n\n[Source:  No author. No date. Defiance Campaign 1952. Retrieved September 30, 2024 from https://www.sahistory.org.za/article/defiance-campaign-1952. CC BY-SA.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following: \n\n- Increased international attention. The campaign drew international recognition, with the United Nations acknowledging South Africa's racial policy as an international issue. This led to the establishment of a UN Commission to investigate apartheid.\n\n- Mass mobilisation and jailings. More than 8,000 people were jailed for defying apartheid laws during the campaign, showing the extent of public resistance and willingness to challenge the regime.\n\n- Growth in ANC membership .The African National Congress (ANC) saw a significant rise in membership\n\n- Change in ANC strategy.\\\n\nShifted from a more moderate approach to a more militant stance in its approach resisting apartheid\n\n- Non-racial cooperation and organisational development.\\\n\nThe Defiance Campaign marked the beginning of non-racial cooperation, strengthened with Congress Alliance in 1954, demonstrating the growing organisational power of African leadership.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source A, The Defiance Campaign $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$won UN recognition” and brought international attention to the apartheid regime, leading the United Nations to establish a commission to investigate the situation ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, as a result of the campaign, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$more than 8,000 people went to jail for defying apartheid laws”, demonstrating the growing commitment and willingness of South Africans to challenge the system through mass mobilisation ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the African National Congress (ANC) experienced a significant rise in membership during the campaign, rising by $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$tens of thousands” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q2","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0228Q2\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about opposition to apartheid policies?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Photo taken at an anti-apartheid protest in The Hague, The Netherlands. The banners read: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Against Apartheid,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One Race of Men,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One People Humanity,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Equal rights for black and white,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Black, white, together one,” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Luns, protest” (a reference to Joseph Luns, the Dutch Minister of Foreign Affairs at the time).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Apartheid%20South%20Africa/images/HI0228Q2.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0228Q2\">\n\n[Source: Behrens, H. (1960, April 2). *Protestdemonstratie tegen apartheidspolitiek van Zuid Afrika in Den Haag (A demonstration against the apartheid policy of South Africa in The Hague)*. Fotocollecie Anefo. Retrieved April 23, 2025 from  https://picryl.com/media/protestdemonstratie-tegen-apartheidspolitiek-van-zuid-afrika-in-den-haag-de-42c34c. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - That the anti-apartheid struggle had an international element\n\n - That many people in the Netherlands/Holland were outspoken about opposition to apartheid\n\n - That one of the primary messages of the protest was equal rights among races\n\n - That the protestors wanted Joseph Luns, the Dutch Minister of Foreign Affairs at the time, to take action\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C suggests that the opposition to apartheid extended outside of the country as well, as this protest is taking place in The Hague, Netherlands ($\\checkmark$). The source also suggests that the protestors felt it was the responsibility of their own government to take action. This is shown by the signs that read $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Luns, protest!” in reference to Joseph Luns, the Dutch Minister of Foreign Affairs ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q3","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0228Q3\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for an historian studying the anti-apartheid movement.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Nelson Mandela's statement at the opening of the defense case in the Rivonia Trial (Pretoria Supreme Court, April 20, 1964).\n\n:box[I have already mentioned that I was one of the persons who helped to form Umkhonto. I, and the others who started the organization, did so for two reasons. Firstly, we believed that as a result of Government policy, violence by the African people had become inevitable, and that unless responsible leadership was given to canalize [direct] and control the feelings of our people, there would be outbreaks of terrorism which would produce an intensity of bitterness and hostility between the various races of this country which is not produced even by war. Secondly, we felt that without violence there would be no way open to the African people to succeed in their struggle against the principle of white supremacy. All lawful modes of expressing opposition to this principle had been closed by legislation, and we were placed in a position in which we had either to accept a permanent state of inferiority, or to defy the Government. We chose to defy the law. We first broke the law in a way which avoided any recourse to violence; when this form was legislated against, and then the Government resorted to a show of force to crush opposition to its policies, only then did we decide to answer violence with violence.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nBut the violence which we chose to adopt was not terrorism. We who formed Umkhonto were all members of the African National Congress, and had behind us the ANC tradition of non-violence and negotiation as a means of solving political disputes. We believe that South Africa belongs to all the people who live in it, and not to one group, be it black or white. We did not want an interracial war, and tried to avoid it to the last minute. If the Court is in doubt about this, it will be seen that the whole history of our organization bears out what I have said, and what I will subsequently say, when I describe the tactics which Umkhonto decided to adopt. (...)]\n\n[Source: Mandela, N. (1964, April 20). *I Am Prepared to Die*. Pretoria Supreme Court. Retrieved March 11, 2025 from https://law2.umkc.edu/faculty/projects/ftrials/mandela/mandelaspeech.html. Public domain. ]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source provides insight as a firsthand account from Nelson Mandela, a significant anti-apartheid activist and a founder of Umkhonto we Sizwe.\n\n - The source provides details about the motivations for the strategic shift from non-violence to violence in anti-apartheid resistance.\n\n - The content of the source sheds light on the details of the more controlled form of violence the MK pursued in the broader struggle.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source is from Mandela's courtroom testimony after his arrest in 1964 and thus cannot provide further insight into the anti-apartheid movement after that date.\n\n - Mandela seeks to justify the turn to violence and downplay the accusations of terrorism, thus framing the decision as necessary and omitting details that might contradict that framing.\n\n - The source lacks the perspective of other anti-apartheid groups with both more and less violent methods of resistance or the international response to the decision. It also does not fully address the consequences of violent resistance.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A is valuable to an historian studying the anti-apartheid movement because it represents a contemporary accounting of Nelson Mandela's perspective as the most prominent anti-apartheid leader ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$)\n\n. The purpose of the source is also valuable to the study of the anti-apartheid movement because Mandela seeks to explicitly state the reasoning for the shift from non-violence to violence in the resistance and the tactics that the MK used ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nOne limitation of the source's origin, however, is that it comes from 1964 just before Mandela was imprisoned and thus cannot provide insight into the continuation of the anti-apartheid movement after his (and many other prominent leaders' arrests) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). Furthermore, as this statement is made at the beginning of a defense trial, its purpose is somewhat limited as Mandela seeks to justify the turn to violence and reframe their actions not as terrorism, but rather as necessity, downplaying the consequences of their methods ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Finally, the source's content is limited as it only provides the perspective of Mandela, a leader of the ANC/MK and cannot include the perspectives or actions of other anti-apartheid organizations, especially those that chose to maintain non-violent resistance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q4","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0228Q4\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources B and D reveal about international responses to apartheid.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |Excerpt from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$uMkhonto weSizwe (MK) in exile” published in the academic website *South African History Online* in 2019.\n\n:box[Initially\\, South African liberation movements were committed to a policy of non-violence and constructive engagement with the government. The increasing use of security forces to brutally suppress resistance and torture those [who] fought against the government's racially biased [policies] convinced the ANC that [the] military option could succeed where non-violence had failed. Consequently, the armed wing of the ANC uMkhonto weSizwe (MK) was formed in 1961 jointly by the ANC and SACP to spearhead the armed struggle.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nA positive development for the ANC was an increasing tide of Independent African states in the 1960s. Coupled with the growing influence of Pan Africanism and African nationalism in the foreign policy of these states. Most of them were committed to the liberation of Africa as a whole from colonial rule and apartheid. Thus, they supported the liberation struggle through the institutions such as the Organisation of African Unity. The ANC was supported by a number of African states which hosted ANC leaders fleeing from increasing repression by the Apartheid state. In some countries they were allowed to stay as [refugees] but not allowed to establish military bases while in others they could establish military training camps. From these countries MK operated and co-ordinated the liberation struggle abroad and in South Africa.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThough committed to the principle of supporting liberation movements in African colonies, efforts of some African countries were limited by their economic weakness and dependence on their former colonial masters. Aware of this weakness, South Africa adopted what it called a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$new policy\" to entice African states into closer co-operation and friendly relations with her in return for financial aid, mainly in the form of technical aid. This was on condition that they did not support the ANC and MK activities within their borders. In instances where the desired outcomes by the South African government were not met, a deliberate policy of aggression was adopted. For instance the South African government embarked a deliberate policy of destabilisation by funding armed groups and assassination of political activists. Neighbouring countries like Botswana, Lesotho, Mozambique and Swaziland were especially vulnerable.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nDespite these threats these countries and others such as Tanzania, Angola, Zambia and Zimbabwe played an important role in supporting the South African liberation struggle. Furthermore, the MK campaign abroad extended to other countries in Europe and the Soviet Union where MK cadres received military training.]\n\n[Source: *uMkhonto weSizwe (MK) in exile*. (2019, April 4). South African History Online. Retrieved April 23, 2025 from  https://sahistory.org.za/article/umkhonto-wesizwe-mk-exile. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from United Nations Security Council Resolution 134, published April 1, 1960. \n\n[Source: The UN Security Council. (1960, April 1). *Resolution 134 (1960)* / [adopted by the Security Council at its 856th meeting], of 1 April 1960. Retrieved April 23, 2025 from https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112105?ln=en&v=pdf#files. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities: \n\n - Both sources highlight that the issue of apartheid was significant internationally\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$[African countries] supported the liberation struggle through the institutions such as the Organisation of African Unity”\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Having considered the complaint of twenty-nine Member States…”\n\n - Both sources openly criticize the apartheid regime/government of South Africa\n\n     -Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Most of them were committed to the liberation of Africa as a whole from colonial rule and apartheid.”\n\n     - Source D:  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Calls upon the Government of the Union of South Africa to initiate measures aimed at bringing about racial harmony based on equality in order to ensure that the present situation does not continue or recur, and to abandon its policies of apartheid and racial discrimination”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n\n - Source B discusses the practical/material support provided to anti-apartheid groups by international allies, while Source D reveals the diplomatic resistance to apartheid from the UN\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$African states which hosted ANC leaders fleeing from increasing repression by the Apartheid state” and in some countries “they could establish military training camps”.\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the General Assembly calling upon it to revise its policies and bring them into conformity with its obligations and responsibilities under the Charter of the United Nations”\n\n - Source B mentions the role of specific countries, while Source D only mentions Security Council members who signed the resolution (and those who abstained).\n\n     - Source B: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Botswana, Lesotho, Mozambique and Swaziland” … $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$these countries and others such as Tanzania, Angola, Zambia and Zimbabwe” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the Soviet Union where MK cadres received military training.”\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Adopted at the 856th meeting by 9 votes to none, with 2 abstentions (France, United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland).”\n\n - Source B simply describes the role of other countries in the support of the armed/violent resistance while Source D does not and, instead, only condemns the South African government's violent response to protests.\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$large-scale killings of unarmed and peaceful demonstrators” and “Deplores that the recent disturbances in the Union of South Africa should have led to the loss of life of so many Africans and extends to the families of the victims its deepest sympathies.”\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSources B and D agree that there was a wide, significant international response to the apartheid government and its policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$)\n\n. Source B describes support for the anti-apartheid movement from the Organisation of African Unity and Source D mentioned that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$twenty-nine [UN] Member States” signed the resolution condemning the actions of the apartheid government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, while the sources both mention the importance of international responses, they differ in the examples of these responses they emphasize with Source B primarily discussing the material and training support provided to the ANC by African allies while Source D only represents a diplomatic condemnation of the regime and its actions ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source B says that the ANC's African allies “hosted ANC leaders fleeing from increasing repression” and, in some cases, allowed the ANC to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$establish military training camps ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Source D, in contrast, is the official language of the UN's condemnation of the Sharpeville massacre saying that is “deplores the policies and actions” taken by the South African government, calling upon it to change its behavior in alignment with the “principles of the [UN] Charter” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nWhile both sources suggest a broad condemnation by the international community ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$), Source B specifies the countries like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Tanzania, Angola, Zambia and Zimbabwe” that provided material support $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$liberation struggle” and “the Soviet Union where MK cadres received military training ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source D simply mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$twenty-nine Member States” who signed the resolution and that the resolution was adopted by nine of the eleven members of the UN Security Council at the time (and specifically names France and the UK/Northern Ireland as having abstained from the vote) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q5","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0228Q5\n\nDescribe **two** examples of increasing repression and/or violence by the government of South Africa between 1960 and 1965.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The Sharpeville Massacre (1960) and other examples of state violence/repression\n\n     - March 21, 1960 - police opened fire on a crowd of initially unarmed protestors outside of Sharpeville Police Station in a suburb of Johannesburg. Reports vary about the numbers killed and injured, but most reports are at least 60 and at least 180, respectively.\n\n     - The Pondoland Rebellion/Revolts (began as early as 1951, but escalated in June 1960) is another notable example of increasing state violence/repression.\n\n - The Unlawful Organizations Act (1960)\n\n     - April 1960 - the South African government declared both the Pan Africanist Congress (PAC) and African National Congress (ANC) illegal, banning them. Shortly after, the Indemnity Act of 1961 protected the government and police from any legal action being taken against them connected to the violence of the Sharpeville Massacre.\n\n - The Sabotage Act (General Law Amendment Act No. 76 of 1962)\n\n     - June 1962 - lessened the burden of proof on those accused of sabotage and presumed guilty (needed to prove their innocence) and widened the definition of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sabotage” to include strikes and other crimes typically seen as petty, such as writing graffiti/messages on the walls of buildings. The law also implemented a minimum sentence of five years and a maximum sentence of execution by hanging.\n\n - The 90-Day Detention Law (General Law Amendment Act, number 37 of 1963)\n\n     - May 1963 - allowed police to detain people accused of political crimes almost indefinitely without access to an attorney as the initial 90-day detention could be immediately re-applied upon release. This law also added an element known as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Sobukwe Clause” that would allow retroactive application of the law to individuals who had already been arrested/accused prior to the law’s existence. \n\n - The Rivonia Trial, life sentences for ANC leadership (1963-1964)\n\n     - October 1963-June 1964 - signalled a shift in the seriousness of the consequences for anti-apartheid action. While previous non-violent direct action campaigns had led to the arrests and detention of activists, the outcome of the Rivonia Trial for many of the accused was life imprisonment.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe most notable example of increasing violence by the South African government between 1960 and 1965 was the Sharpeville Massacre ($\\checkmark$). In March of 1960, police officers opened fire on a large, unarmed gathering of protestors outside the Shapeville Police Station, killing at least 60 people and injuring many more ($\\checkmark$). To make matters worse, in the heightened tensions after the massacre, the South African government pushed through even more repressive legislation targeting the anti-apartheid movement ($\\checkmark$). One notable example of this increasing repression was the Unlawful Organizations Act of 1960, which banned both the African National Congress (ANC) and Pan Africanist Congress (PAC,) who were forced to continue their work underground ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q6","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0228Q6\n\nDescribe **two** examples of increasing repression and/or violence by the anti-apartheid movement between 1960 and 1965.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Formation of uMkhonto weSizwe (aka MK or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Spear of the Nation”), 1961\n\n     - As a direct response to the government's actions at Sharpeville, the ANC formed a military wing, the MK, led by Nelson Mandela. It announced in June 1961 that it would begin a sabotage campaign if the government did not agree to reforms and began that campaign in late 1961. The organization also began the planning of a broader campaign known as “Operation Mayibuye” which was never carried out.\n\n - Sabotage campaigns (1961-1963)\n\n     - Beginning with the bombing of an electrical substation in New Brighton, Eastern Cape, nearly 200 acts of sabotage are recorded between October 1961 and July 1963. No deaths or injuries are recorded as these attacks were focused on “hard targets” and intended to destroy government property/infrastructure rather than individuals. Attacks were typically carried out at times when these places would be unoccupied to avoid loss of life intentionally.  \n\n - Formation of Poqo (1961)\n\n    - The Pan Africanist Congress (PAC) formed its armed wing, Poqo, in response to state violence but took a far more radical approach by targeting white civilians, police officers, and police informants. The organization carried out many targeted attacks in both the Western Cape (Paarl and Langa) and Transkei (Eastern Cape). After a violent assault in Paarl (1962) and the murder of a family camping near the Bashee River (1963), the government carried out mass arrests and executions of Poqo members. This, combined with a lack of mass support for its violent methods, largely destroyed the organization.  \n\n - International military training (early 1960s)\n\n     - Many ANC members traveled abroad for military training in the early 1960s in various places, including the Soviet Union, China, Egypt, Algeria and Tanzania. Notable examples from the ANC include: \n\n     - Chris Hani - received military training in the Soviet Union and served in the Zimbabwean War of Liberation. \n\n     - Joe Modise - trained in the Soviet Union, former Czechoslovakia and Cuba. He also lived in Tanzania and trained MK members there while also establishing bases in Angola and Zambia as well.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth of the major anti-apartheid groups, the ANC and the PAC formed their military wings between 1960 and 1965 ($\\checkmark$ $\\checkmark$). The ANC formed the MK and began sabotage tactics against the apartheid government, and the PAC formed Poqo, which carried out targeted violence against both civilians and police officers ($\\checkmark$). While it was never carried out, the MK began planning a major campaign of resistance known as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Operation Mayibuye” and sent ANC and MK members overseas for military training in preparation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0228Q7","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0228Q7\n\nExplain the significance of the Sharpeville Massacre to the anti-apartheid movement.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Marked the shift from peaceful, non-violent protest to armed struggle\n\n     - In response to the events in Sharpeville, both the African National Congress (ANC) and Pan Africanist Congress (PAC) adopted an armed struggle creating uMkhonto weSizwe and Poqo, respectively.\n\n - Led to government crackdowns and further repression\n\n     - The South African government declared a state of emergency and implemented mass arrests of anti-apartheid leadership. Major anti-apartheid groups including the ANC and PAC were banned, forcing them underground. The South African government implemented even more repressive laws including the Sabotage Act and the 90-day Retention Law.  This response from the government largely suppressed the broader anti-apartheid movement until the 1970s, essentially destroying the PAC.\n\n - Increased international attention and anti-apartheid activism\n\n     - International outcry resulted in increased diplomatic pressure on the South African government. The United Nations passed a resolution condemning the South African government’s actions. Originally known as the “Boycott Movement” in the UK, the organization rebranded as the Anti-Apartheid Movement (AAM) and expanded the scope of its work. Anti-apartheid activism also grew in other countries through economic sanctions, arms embargoes, cutting diplomatic ties and grassroots domestic protest campaigns \n\n - Inspired a new generation of domestic anti-apartheid activism\n\n    - While the initial crackdown on and repression of anti-apartheid activism after Sharpeville initially had a dampening effect on the movement, the massacre served as a point of inspiration for continued activism.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nOne reason the Sharpeville Massacre was significant in the anti-apartheid movement was because it became the catalyst for increased resistance for many activists ($\\checkmark$). More specifically, it helped inspire the birth of the military wing of the African National Congress, uMkhonte weSizwe, which signaled the transition toward violent resistance ($\\checkmark$). \n\nHowever, in response, the massacre also sparked an uptick in violent, repressive responses from the government as they became concerned such protests would continue and threaten their control ($\\checkmark$). In the short time following the massacre, the ANC and Pan Africanist Congress (PAC) were both banned and more restrictive laws were implemented, such as the 90-day Retention Law, which allowed the government to jail and hold arrested activists almost indefinitely ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0253","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0253\n\nWhat, according to Source A, was the importance of the Congress of the People in the context of the anti-apartheid movement?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the book $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Prize\" by Norman Levy and published in 2013. Levy opposed Bantu Education and promoted alternative learning for black students during apartheid. He was a member of the Congress of the People, and also contributed to founding the Congress of Democrats, a white anti-apartheid organization, also integrated by Rusty Bernstein.\n\n:box[\n\n(…) the idea of a Congress of the People (COP) at which the delegates would adopt a Freedom Charter which would assert the demand for civil rights for all South Africans, seemed to be the appropriate next step. (…) The leadership believed that this was an objective (...) about which $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all levels of the congress membership were passionate”. That this assessment was accurate was to some extent manifested in (…) the language of the campaign. This was essentially the genius of Rusty Bernstein who (…) reached out to the masses with a quality of prose that matched the expectations of the occasion. Accordingly, the Freedom Charter helped to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mobilize the people up and down the land and awaken an echo \\[for freedom\\] in their hearts”. (…)\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThis excitement was captured in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call to the Congress of the People”, a passionate document in the format of a leaflet, drafted (…) by Rusty Bernstein. (…) Rusty was obviously touched by the emotions that the idea of the COP had aroused. He was not by nature overtly passionate (…). $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call” (…) was an exception, an indication of the extent to which Bernstein was personally inspired by the egalitarian idealism of the action. Its stirring message reached out $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to brothers without land and children without schooling”; to the farmers in the reserves; to the miners $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in the dark shafts and cold compounds, far from their families”; and to the farm workers and workers in the factories. It called on them to speak of long hours, housing and pass laws, of taxes and of cattle-culling and of famine, ending with the repetitive refrain:\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Let us speak together, all of us together - African and European, Indian and Coloured. Voter and voteless. Privileged and rightless. The happy and the homeless. Let us speak of freedom.”\n\n]\n\n[Source: Levy, N. (2015, June 18). *The Final Prize* by Norman Levy. South African History Online. Retrieved August 2, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/freedomcharter-norman-levy. CC.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The Congress of the People was convened to draft the Freedom Charter, demanding civil rights and equality for all South Africans.\n\n - Its members were passionately committed to the anti-apartheid cause.\n\n - The campaign mobilized diverse groups across South Africa, instilling hope and encouraging collective action for freedom.\n\n - The movement sought unity across all races to fight for equality.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to source A, the Congress of the People was a crucial moment in the anti-apartheid movement because it asserted $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the demand for civil rights for all South Africans” and united diverse groups around a common goal: the Freedom Charter. This document, which called for civil rights and equality for all South Africans, helped strengthen the anti-apartheid movement. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nCommitted to the anti-apartheid cause, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all levels of the congress membership were passionate” in working together across diverse backgrounds and races to amplify the voices of the marginalized. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThis union of different social groups sparked hope for meaningful change among South Africans, fostering a sense of solidarity and shared commitment that revitalized the movement $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to speak of long hours, housing and pass laws, of taxes and cattle-culling and of famine,\" as noted in source A, which means fighting for equality. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0259","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0259\n\nWhat does Source D suggest about the ideals of the apartheid resistance movement in the 1950s? \n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from the 1956 Freedom Charter. \n\n[Source: South African History Online. (November 4, 2013). *Notes on meeting with Mr. Sam Nujoma*. Retrieved January 26, 2025 from https://sahistory.org.za/archive/note-meeting-mr-sam-nujoma-1983. CC-BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - The extract suggests the apartheid resistance movement in the 1950s was grounded in the belief that South Africa should belong equally to all its people, regardless of race.\n\n - The extract demonstrates a strong commitment to democratic principles, stating that legitimate governance must be based on the collective will of all citizens.\n\n - The extract emphasises unity between black and white South Africans, with a shared pledge to work together towards achieving democratic reforms.\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe extract suggests that the apartheid resistance movement in the 1950s was founded on the principle of non-racialism, declaring that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$South Africa belongs to all who live in it, black and white\" ($\\checkmark$). It reflects a commitment to equality and democracy, asserting that governance must be \"based on the will of all the people\" ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0260","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0260\n\nDefine $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$petty apartheid' and describe **two** examples of how it was enforced legally.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor definition, **any** combination of the following: \n\n - Petty apartheid refers to the system of racial segregation in South Africa during apartheid (1948-1994) that regulated everyday life.\n\n - Petty apartheid was the system of racial segregation that focused on the separation of races in public spaces, transportation, schools, hospitals, and other facilities. \n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor examples, any **two** of the following laws and their descriptions: \n\n - Separate Amenities Act which mandated separate and unequal facilities for different races.\n\n - The Group Areas Act which divided residential and business districts by race \n\n - The Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act and/or the Immorality Act which outlawed interracial relationships \n\n - The Pass Laws which required black citizens to carry passbooks to restrict their movement in areas designated for white people \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nPetty apartheid refers to the racial segregation laws and practices in South Africa during the apartheid era (1948-1994) that dictated the everyday lives of citizens ($\\checkmark$). One example of how petty apartheid was enforced is the Reservation of Separate Amenities Act. This law mandated that all public spaces and facilities, such as beaches, buses and benches, be segregated by race ($\\checkmark$). Another example was the Group Areas Act, which segregated both residential and business districts by race ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0261","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0261\n\nIdentify **two** reasons why the ANC split in 1959.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny two of the following: \n\n - Dissatisfaction with ANC leadership under Albert Luthuli, who was seen by some as too moderate\n\n - Critics within the ANC emphasised African nationalism, advocating for a more radical, militant and African-centered approach to resistance and for a departure from non-racial principles\n\n - Some members of the ANC believed the ANC was overly influenced by communism and too focused on alliances with non-African groups, such as Indians and whites.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFirstly, some members, including leaders like Robert Sobukwe, were dissatisfied with the leadership of Albert Luthuli, viewing him as too moderate in his approach to opposing apartheid ($\\checkmark$). Second, critics within the ANC emphasised African nationalism and an African-centered resistance including more radical, militant action as well as rejecting the ANC's non-racial principles ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0262","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0262\n\nDescribe the Defiance Campaign and discuss why it can be seen as both a success and failure. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor description, the following: \n\n - The Defiance Campaign (1952) was a mass protest movement led by the African National Congress (ANC) and its allies which aimed to challenge apartheid laws through nonviolent civil disobedience by deliberately breaking discriminatory laws, such as pass laws and curfews.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor successes and failures, a combination of five of the following that includes at least one success and one failure: \n\n - Success:\n\n     - The campaign significantly increased membership in the ANC, from approximately 7,000 to over 100,000.\n\n     - It united people across racial and ethnic lines, creating solidarity among oppressed groups, such as Black, Indian and “Coloured” South Africans \n\n     - The campaign attracted international attention, highlighting the injustices of apartheid to a global audience, and partially contributing to the UN General Assembly formally recognising apartheid as a threat to international peace. \n\n     - It set the stage for the anti-apartheid movement's future support from global organisations like the United Nations.\n\n     - The Defiance Campaign established nonviolent resistance as a powerful tool, inspiring future movements, such as the Freedom Charter in 1955.\n\n - Failure: \n\n    - The campaign failed to force the apartheid government to repeal any major discriminatory laws, as the government responded with repression rather than reform.\n\n     - The government introduced harsher penalties for civil disobedience and passed stricter laws to punish protesters more severely.\n\n     - The campaign primarily involved urban areas and had limited impact on rural South Africans, who were equally affected by apartheid policies. \n\n\\\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Defiance Campaign of 1952 was a coordinated protest effort spearheaded by the African National Congress (ANC) in collaboration with allied groups. Its primary objective was to challenge apartheid policies through peaceful nonviolent resistance, deliberately violating discriminatory regulations such as pass laws, curfews, and segregation measures ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe campaign boasted notable achievements. It successfully expanded ANC membership, surging from approximately 7,000 to over 100,000, illustrating its effectiveness in mobilising support on a national scale ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, it united diverse racial and ethnic communities, including Black, Indian, and \"Coloured\" South Africans,instilling a shared sense of purpose among oppressed groups ($\\checkmark$). It also gained international attention, exposing apartheid's injustices to the world, and paved the way for global organisations like the United Nations to recognise apartheid as a threat to peace ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the campaign was not without shortcomings. It did not compel the apartheid government to repeal any significant laws; instead, the authorities intensified their oppression by enacting stricter regulations and imposing harsher punishments on protestors ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the movement primarily affected urban areas, leaving many rural South Africans, who faced similar hardships, largely untouched by its effort ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0263","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0263\n\nDefine $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$non-racialism” and explain its relevance in the context of the anti-apartheid struggle in the 1950s.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor definition, the following: \n\n - Non-racialism refers to the principle of rejecting racial discrimination and advocating for equality and unity among all racial groups.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any of the following: \n\n - In the context of the 1950s anti-apartheid struggle, it was a key ideology of the African National Congress (ANC) and its allies, emphasising that South Africa belonged to all who lived in it, regardless of race.\n\n - Promoted collaboration between racial groups to oppose apartheid.\n\n - Central to documents such as the Freedom Charter (1955), which declared a vision of an inclusive and democratic South Africa.\n\n\\\n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nNon-racialism refers to belief in eliminating racial discrimination and promoting equality and unity among people of all racial backgrounds ($\\checkmark$). In the context of the 1950s anti-apartheid struggle, non-racialism was a central principle espoused by African National Congress (ANC) and its allies, who called for South Africa to be a shared nation for all inhabitants, irrespective of race ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0264","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0264\n\nIdentify and explain **two** ways the Freedom Charter was significant in the anti-apartheid struggle.\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor identification, any **two** of the following: \n\n - It provided a unifying vision\n\n - It became a symbol of resistance\n\n - It challenged the legitimacy of apartheid\n\n - It encouraged international support \n\nFor explanation, any **two** of the following:\n\n \n\n - *Unifying vision*: The Freedom Charter articulated a shared vision for a non-racial, democratic South Africa, rallying various resistance groups under a common cause.\n\n - *Symbol of resistance*: The Charter served as an enduring symbol of the anti-apartheid movement, inspiring future initiatives like the drafting of the 1996 Constitution.\n\n - *Challenged legitimacy of apartheid*:  By declaring that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$South Africa belongs to all who live in it, black and white,\" the Charter rejected the racial segregation at the heart of apartheid.\n\n - *Encouraged global support*:  The principles of equality and democracy outlined in the Charter resonated with international audiences, increasing support for the movement.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Freedom Charter was significant because it provided a unifying vision for the anti-apartheid struggle ($\\checkmark$). It declared that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$South Africa belongs to all who live in it, black and white,\" emphasising non-racialism and bringing together groups like the African National Congress (ANC) and the South African Indian Congress (SAIC) under a common goal ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the Charter encouraged international support by aligning the anti-apartheid movement with global ideals of democracy and human rights ($\\checkmark$), which attracted sympathy and backing from organizations such as the United Nations and international solidarity networks ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0265","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0265\n\nWhat, according to Source A, was the rationale behind apartheid? \n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract is from a speech titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Case for Apartheid” made by A.L. Geyer, apartheid supporter and South African High Commissioner in Great Britain, to the Rotary Club in London in 1953. \n\n[Source: Geyer, A.L. (1953, August 19). *The Case for Apartheid*. Internet Modern History Sourcebook. Retrieved January 19, 2025 from https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1953geyer.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Apartheid is described as a means of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$self-preservation\" for white South Africans, portrayed as a natural and necessary urge.\n\n - The source claims that apartheid would enable the Bantu to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$develop fully as a separate people\" in their designated areas.\n\n - Whites are justified in maintaining political power because the Bantu are deemed $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$immature\" and not ready to govern.\n\n - The source references the Dutch Reformed Church's statement that no people would accept being excluded indefinitely from decision-making, implying a moral obligation to gradually give the Bantu limited autonomy.\n\n - The immediate aim is to enforce racial separation outside Bantu areas and improve living standards for the Bantu.\n\n - The long-term aim is to develop Bantu areas agriculturally and industrially, making them the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$national home\" for the Bantu, where they could eventually gain autonomy.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\nAccording to Source A, the rationale behind apartheid was framed as a policy of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$self-preservation\" for white South Africans ($\\checkmark$). The source also claims that apartheid would enable the Bantu people to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$develop fully as a separate people\" in their designated areas, where they would progressively gain greater autonomy ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, it emphasises that political power should remain with whites due to the perceived $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$immaturity\" of the “Bantu population,” suggesting that apartheid was in their \"best interest\" ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0266","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0266\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for an historian studying apartheid policies.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract is from a speech titled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Case for Apartheid” made by A.L. Geyer, apartheid supporter and South African High Commissioner in Great Britain, to the Rotary Club in London in 1953. \n\n[Source: Geyer, A.L. (1953, August 19). *The Case for Apartheid*. Internet Modern History Sourcebook. Retrieved January 19, 2025 from https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1953geyer.asp. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n\n - The origin of the source provides a firsthand perspective of an apartheid supporter, giving insight into the mindset, justifications and rhetoric of proponents of apartheid in the 1950s\n\n - The purpose of the source is to defend apartheid policies,  revealing how apartheid supporters rationalised their policies and attempted to gain international acceptance.\n\n - The content of the source reveals the apartheid government’s immediate goals for racial segregation and paternalistic view toward black South Africans as \"immature\" and in need of guidance by the white minority.\n\n - The content reveals that the apartheid government used the Dutch Reformed Church's perspective that black South Africans would rise up against the system unless they received some autonomy as a justification for the homelands policy, which could be presented as a degree of that autonomy.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following: \n\n - The origin of the source is a representative of the apartheid government in Britain, and therefore the source is unlikely to be critical of the National Party regime\n\n - The purpose of the source is to defend apartheid, persuade an international audience of its benefits, and gain sympathy therefore it downplays the regime's harsh realities.\n\n - The content of the source fails to acknowledge the perspective and experiences of oppression and exploitation faced by black, coloured and Indian South Africans.  \n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe origin of the source, a speech by the South African High Commissioner in Britain, A.L. Geyer, is valuable as it provides insight into the views and justifications of apartheid supporters in the 1950s, reflecting their rhetoric and ideology ($\\checkmark$ VO). However, as a propaganda tool created by an apartheid supporter, it is inherently one-sided and fails to provide the perspectives of those who opposed the system ($\\checkmark$ LO). The purpose of the speech, aimed at defending apartheid to an international audience, is valuable because it shows how the South African government framed apartheid as a policy of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$self-preservation\" to sanitise its image abroad ($\\checkmark$ VP). A value of the content is that it provides examples of the justifications used for apartheid policies, the homelands policies in particular ($\\checkmark$ VC)."},{"ref":"HI0267","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0267\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the nature of resistance to apartheid in the 1950s? \n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The photograph below shows all the accused at the Treason Trial. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Apartheid%20South%20Africa/images/hi0267.jpg\" alt=\"hi0267\">\n\n[Source: Weinberg, E. (1956, December 1). *Treason Trial*. Originally found in Mayibuye Archive. Retrieved January 18, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TreasonTrial1956.jpg.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Resistance to apartheid was collective and organised, as shown by the large group of individuals accused during the Treason Trial, united in their opposition to apartheid policies.\n\n - The movement was diverse and inclusive, with participants of different races and genders working together to challenge apartheid, reflecting the principle of non-racialism.\n\n - The scale of the opposition is evident, with a significant number of individuals involved, suggesting widespread participation in anti-apartheid activities.\n\n - The photo portrays a sense of unity and solidarity among those resisting apartheid, as they are pictured together, smiling and defiant in the face of prosecution.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFirstly, the image suggests that resistance to apartheid in the 1950s was united and organised across racial and gender lines. This is evident in the photograph, which shows a large, diverse group of individuals—including men and women of different racial backgrounds—gathered together in solidarity during the Treason Trial of 1956 ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, the image also suggests that there was a strong sense of solidarity amongst the accused, who are photographed smiling together in spite of the fact that they are facing prosecution ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0268","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0268\n\nWhat does Source C suggest about the actions of the police during the Sharpeville Massacre?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract comes from an article written by a journalist, Humphrey Tyler, who was present during the Sharpeville Massacre in 1960. He was the assistant editor of the anti-apartheid publication Drum magazine at the time of the shooting. \n\n[Source: Tyler, H. (1960, March 21) *Eyewitness accounts of the Sharpeville Massacre 1960*. Retrieved January 18, 2025 from https://sahistory.org.za/article/eyewitness-accounts-sharpeville-massacre-1960. CC-BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe following:\n\n - The actions of the police were excessive and unjustified, challenging the police's claim of self-defence by highlighting the imbalance between the number of police who were injured by stones and the number of unarmed protestors who were shot by police. \n\n - The source suggests that police actions were brutal, as they fired $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$without warning” and continued shooting until “there was no living thing” in the compound\n\n - The source also suggests that the police force was dishonest in recounting its version of the events, with the journalist questioning the claim that the crowd was armed by reporting that he saw no weapons, and just personal belongings amongst the bodies. \n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C suggests that the actions of the police during the Sharpeville Massacre were excessive and unjustified, contradicting the police's claim that they were acting in self-defence with the assertion that only three policemen had been injured with stones, while many more unarmed protestors had been shot. ($\\checkmark$\n\n). The source states that the police fired without warning and continued shooting until $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$there was no living thing\" in the compound, implying a deliberate and brutal attack ($\\checkmark$\n\n). It also challenges the police's claim of self-defence by highlighting that only three officers were hit by stones while over 200 protestors were shot, many of whom were unarmed ($\\checkmark$ )."},{"ref":"HI0269","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0269\n\nWith reference to origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the events at the Sharpeville Massacre. \n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The following extract comes from an article written by a journalist, Humphrey Tyler, who was present during the Sharpeville Massacre in 1960. He was the assistant editor of the anti-apartheid publication Drum magazine at the time of the shooting. \n\n[Source: Tyler, H. (March 21, 1960) *Eyewitness accounts of the Sharpeville Massacre 1960*. Retrieved January 18, 2025 from https://sahistory.org.za/article/eyewitness-accounts-sharpeville-massacre-1960. CC-BY-SA 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following: \n\n - The source is a firsthand account from an observer at the Sharpeville Massacre, providing direct observations that add credibility to its claims.\n\n - Its purpose is to document the events and challenge the police's official narrative, making it valuable for offering a critical perspective on police actions.\n\n - The content includes specific details, such as the lack of warning shots, the sustained shooting, and the absence of weapons, which provide explicit evidence of the events.\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:: \n\n - The observer's personal biases as an anti-apartheid journalist could influence their interpretation.\n\n - The intent to discredit the police may also lead to an unbalanced perspective, exaggerating the peacefulness of the crowd or minimizing any potential threat.\n\n - The source only reflects the author's viewpoint and does not account for the police's perspective or the broader context of the protest, limiting its comprehensiveness.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource C is valuable for a historian studying the Sharpeville Massacre because the origin is a firsthand account of an observer present at the scene ($\\checkmark$ VO). The content provides detailed observations, such as the lack of weapons, the absence of warning shots, and the disproportionate force used, which highlight the excessive violence of the police ($\\checkmark$ VC). However, the source has limitations. The purpose is to discredit the police, which could lead to selective reporting ($\\checkmark$ LP), and the content only offers one perspective, omitting the police's view or events leading up to the massacre ($\\checkmark$ LC).\n\n$\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"HI0327","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0327\n\nDescribe **two** strategies Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK) used in apartheid resistance.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- As an armed resistance group, MK initially focused on sabotaging government infrastructure, targeting power plants and transportation networks (1960s).\n- Later (1970s-1980s), the MK used ambushes and bomb attacks against military and police targets.\n- Operated underground and relied on secret cells, including in other countries.\n- Built a network of safe houses and secret training camps, both within South Africa and in neighbouring countries.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTo achieve its goals, MK initially depended on tactics focused on sabotage, targeting government infrastructure, power plants, and transportation networks to avoid loss of life ($\\checkmark$). The organization operated underground, utilizing secret cells to execute its missions and evade government crackdowns through the use of safe houses ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0328","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0328\n\nDescribe **two** impacts of the Inkatha Freedom Party (IFP) on the anti-apartheid movement.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Advocated for the rights and culture of the Zulu people.\n- Provided an alternative political voice for Zulu nationalism.\n- Played a significant role in the negotiations that led to the end of apartheid.\n- Served as a moderating force, advocating for federalism and regional autonomy.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe IFP played a crucial role in advocating for the political and cultural rights of the Zulu people, as well as in preserving and promoting Zulu cultural heritage and identity. It significantly contributed to the recognition of cultural diversity within South Africa's evolving democratic landscape ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe IFP emerged as a major political party and played a significant role in the multiparty negotiations that led to the end of apartheid. Its involvement ensured that the concerns of Zulu nationalists and regional interests were represented in the transition process, helping shape the new democratic framework ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0404","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0404\n\nDistinguish between the terms $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$petty apartheid” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$grand apartheid”.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny of the following distinguishing characteristics:\n\n- Petty apartheid segregated public facilities on a small scale, while grand apartheid created large-scale, political, and territorial segregation.\n\n- Petty apartheid focused on limiting day-to-day social interactions between races, while grand apartheid enforced physical separation by creating designated areas for different races.\n\n- Petty apartheid created separate public facilities, including buses, restaurants, schools, hospitals, beaches, and restrooms while grand apartheid created the Bantustans or black “homelands” across South Africa.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nPetty apartheid and grand apartheid were both components of South Africa's racial segregation system but differed in scope. Petty apartheid focused on enforcing racial separation in everyday life, restricting interactions in public spaces such as schools, transport, and restaurants through laws that segregated facilities and services ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, grand apartheid was a broader, structural system designed to enforce long-term racial segregation by controlling land ownership, political rights, and residency, primarily through laws like the Group Areas Act and the establishment of Bantustans ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0405","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0405\n\nDescribe **two** key elements of South Africa's Population Registration Act of 1950. \n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - It established a legal racial classification system in South Africa.\n\n - It used various physical and social factors to classify a person's race.\n\n - It was used to control access to different aspects of society based on race.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Population Registration Act created a legal framework for racial classification in South Africa ($\\checkmark$). It used physical features like skin color and social features like type of employment to determine an individual's racial classification ($\\checkmark$), which then dictated other aspects of that individual's life."},{"ref":"HI0406","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0406\n\nWith reference to content, analyze a value and limitation of Source A for an historian studying Grand Apartheid politics in South Africa.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Forced Removals: Summary” in Overcoming Apartheid published by Michigan State University. |\n\n:box[From 1960 to 1983, the apartheid government forcibly moved 3.5 million black South Africans in one of the largest mass removals of people in modern history. There were several political and economic reasons for these removals. First, during the 1950s and 1960s, large-scale removals of Africans, Indians, and Coloureds were carried out to implement the Group Areas Act, which mandated residential segregation throughout the country. More than 860,000 people were forced to move in order to divide and control racially-separate communities at a time of growing organized resistance to apartheid in urban areas; the removals also worked to the economic detriment of Indian shop owners. Sophiatown in Johannesburg (1955-63) and District Six in Cape Town (beginning in 1968) were among the vibrant multi-racial communities that were destroyed by government bulldozers when these areas were declared $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$white.\" Blacks were forcibly removed to distant segregated townships, sometimes 30 kilometers (19 miles) from places of employment in the central cities. In Cape Town, many informal settlements were destroyed. In one incident over four days in 1985, Africans resisted being moved from Crossroads to the new government-run Khayelitsha township farther [sic] away; 18 people were killed and 230 were injured.]\n\n[Source: *Forced Removals: Summary.* (No date). Overcoming Apartheid - Michigan State University. Accessed August 12, 2024 from https://overcomingapartheid.msu.edu/multimedia.php?kid=163-582-18. No known usage restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor **value**, the following:\n\n - The source gives relevant, specific details about the impacts of Grand Apartheid policies on African, Indian, and Coloured communities.\n\nFor **limitation**, the following:\n\n - The source lacks detail about the motivation for the forced removals under Grand Apartheid.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSource A's content provides value because it provides specific details about the forced removals under Grand Apartheid, such as the specific communities and numbers of people they affected ($\\checkmark$). However, despite the details provided, the source's content lacks information about what motivated these removals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0407","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0407\n\nWhat, according to Source B, were the impacts of the Defiance Campaign in 1952?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Defiance Campaign 1952” published by South African History Online (SAHO): Towards a People's History, a South African non-profit that partners with various universities to publish articles about South African history. |\n\n[Source: *Defiance Campaign 1952*. (No date). South African History Online: Towards a People's History. Accessed August 16, 2024 from https://sahistory.org.za/article/defiance-campaign-1952. CC-BY 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Anti-apartheid activists defied apartheid laws in various parts of South Africa.\n\n - The South African government arrested many activists for minor offenses such as entering areas without permits, staying out past curfew, or using facilities labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Europeans Only”.\n\n - Repercussions for activists included short-term imprisonment, fines, whippings, and, in some cases, acquittal. \n\n - The campaigns made the South African government concerned about increasing resistance.\n\n - Some national leaders were arrested and charged under the Suppression of Communism Act and sentenced to longer terms.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source B, the Defiance Campaign organized resistance to apartheid laws across South Africa, including many major urban areas ($\\checkmark$). The South African government initially sentenced participants for minor crimes, even acquitting some ($\\checkmark$). However, as the campaign grew, the government became more concerned and sentenced national organizers like Nelson Mandela to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$statutory communism” according to the Suppression of Communism Act ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0408","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0408\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources C and D reveal about the significance of the 1952 Defiance Campaign. \n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Defiance Campaign 1952” published by South African History Online (SAHO): Towards a People's History, a South African non-profit that partners with various universities to publish articles about South African history.|\n\n[Source: *Defiance Campaign 1952*. (No date). South African History Online: Towards a People's History. Accessed August 16, 2024 from https://sahistory.org.za/article/defiance-campaign-1952. CC-BY 4.0]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the article $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Defiance Campaign: Summary” in *Overcoming Apartheid* published by Michigan State University.| \n\n:box[The Defiance Campaign in 1952 was the first large-scale, multi-racial political mobilization against apartheid laws under a common leadership - by the African National Congress, South African Indian Congress, and the Coloured People's Congress. More than 8,000 trained volunteers went to jail for $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$defying unjust laws,” laws that had grown worse since the National Party came to power in 1948. Volunteers were jailed for failing to carry passes, violating the curfew on Africans, and entering locations and public facilities designated for one race only.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nIn early 1953, the government imposing [sic] stiff penalties for protesting discriminatory laws, including heavy fines and prison sentences of up to five years. It then enacted the Public Safety Act, allowing declaration of a State of Emergency to override existing laws and oversight by courts. Although the Defiance Campaign did not achieve its goals, it demonstrated large-scale and growing opposition to apartheid. Furthermore, the use of non-violent civil disobedience was part of an important international tradition - from the independence movement in Indian two decades before to sit-ins and other non-violent protests in the United States civil rights movement more than a decade later.\n\n]\n\n[Source: *The Defiance Campaign: Summary.* (No Date). Overcoming Apartheid - Michigan State University. Accessed August 16, 2024 from https://overcomingapartheid.msu.edu/multimedia.php?kid=163-582-21. No known usage restrictions]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n\\\n\nFor **comparison**, any **two** to **three** of the following similarities:\n\n - Both sources mention the scale of the arrests of participants.\n\n    - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$reacted with the arrest of national leaders of the campaign in Johannesburg and the Eastern Cape” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$more than 8 000 people went to jail for defying apartheid laws”\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$More than 8,000 trained volunteers went to jail for defying unjust laws.”\n\n - Both sources agree that the campaign did not achieve its primary goals, it was significant in other ways.\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Although the campaign did not achieve the desired aim of overturning the apartheid laws it was successful in a number of other respects”\n\n     - Souce D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Although the Defiance Campaign did not achieve its goals it demonstrated large-scale and growing opposition to apartheid.”\n\n - Both sources agree the campaign was the first major example of cooperation between different races against apartheid.\n\n     - Source C: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This period marked the beginning of non-racial co-operation in the resistance to apartheid”\n\n     - Source D: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$was the first large-scale, multi-racial political mobilization against apartheid laws”\n\nFor contrast, any **one** to **two** of the following differences:\n\n - The sources emphasize/mention some different significant outcomes of the campaign:\n\n     - Source C mentions the following: the campaign moved $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the ANC from moderation to militancy”, increased ANC membership, brought the attention of the United Nations, making South Africa's racial policies an “international issue,” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demonstrated the potential power of African leadership and its organisational skill and discipline.” \n\n     - Source D describes the campaign as another example of the “international tradition” of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$non-violent civil disobedience” and mentions that the campaign influenced the passage of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Public Safety Act.”\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSources C and D largely agree about the significance of the 1952 Defiance Campaign in South Africa.\n\nBoth sources agree that the campaign did not achieve its overarching goal of overturning apartheid ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Source C says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the campaign did not achieve the desired aim of overturning the apartheid laws” and Source D simply says the campaign $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$did not achieve its goals” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nHowever, both sources also agree that the campaign was still significant in other ways ($\\checkmark$ similarity). Both sources agree that the campaign was the first notable example of cooperative resistance to apartheid between race-based organizations. Source C says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$this period marked the beginning of non-racial co-operation in the resistance to apartheid” while Source D says the campaign was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the first large-scale, multi-racial political mobilization against apartheid laws under a common leadership” ($\\checkmark$ evidence).\n\nFrom there, however, they diverge in their emphasis on the campaign's significance, emphasizing different successes ($\\checkmark$difference ). Source C mentions two major impacts on the African National Congress including its increased membership from the campaign and that it signalled a move $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$from moderation to militancy” ($\\checkmark$ evidence) in the organization while Source D does not mention this. Instead, Source D emphasizes the campaign's significance as an example of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$international tradition” of “non-violent civil disobedience” along with the Indian independence movement and the US civil rights movement ($\\checkmark$ evidence). \n\nSource D also mentions that the campaign drove an increased legal response from the South African government ($\\checkmark$difference ) with the passage of the Public Safety Act, which would allow it to declare a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$State of Emergency to override existing laws and oversight by courts” ($\\checkmark$ evidence) while Source C does not mention this."},{"ref":"HI0806","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0806\n\nDefine the term “pass laws” and explain why they were the focus of the protest actions during apartheid.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor the definition of “pass laws,” the following or similar:\n- The pass laws restricted where black South Africans could live, work, and travel. Black South Africans had to carry a pass book which employers had to sign giving them permission to be in urban areas\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any **three** of the following:\n- Pass laws were seen as dehumanizing as they reduced black South Africans to carrying identification documents at all times or face arrest\n- The enforcement of pass laws resulted in frequent arrests and harassment by police, creating widespread anger among the black population\n- Pass laws were a visible and tangible symbol of apartheid oppression that ordinary people encountered regularly in their daily lives\n- The PAC specifically chose to target pass laws as they believed this would mobilize mass support due to the universal hatred of the system\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe pass laws were introduced to limit black South Africans' access to whites-only urban areas. They required black South Africans to carry a pass book which gave them permission to work in these areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\nPass laws were chosen as the focus of protest because they controlled where black South Africans could live and work, creating daily restrictions that led to deep resentment ($\\checkmark$). The constant police enforcement of these laws led to regular arrests and harassment, generating widespread anger, resentment, and humiliation amongst black South Africans ($\\checkmark$). The PAC strategically selected pass laws as their target because they recognized that the universal hatred of this system could lead to massive popular participation in the protest ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0807","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0807\n\nDiscuss the perspectives around the ANC's decision to adopt the armed struggle in 1961.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nStudents should provide a discussion that considers both perspectives on the ANC's decision. To achieve full marks, students should include perspectives from both sides, including at least one point from each side, but a minimum of four points in total:\n\nPerspective supporting the decision to adopt an armed struggle:\n- Decades of non-violent resistance (including the Defiance Campaign and the 1956 Women's anti-pass protest) had failed to achieve meaningful change, proving peaceful methods were ineffective against the apartheid government\n- The government's increasingly repressive policies throughout the 1950s had closed all legitimate political space for opposition\n- The government's violent response to peaceful protests (for example, at Cato Manor in 1959 and Sharpeville in 1960) demonstrated it was willing to use lethal force against civilians\n- The banning of the ANC and other political organizations in 1960 eliminated legal avenues for political activity, making underground resistance an inevitable option\n\nPerspectives questioning the decision to adopt an armed struggle:\n- The ANC had a history of non-violent protest and had built a reputation and following based on the principle of non-violent resistance. In fact, it was just three months before the launch of Umkhonto we Sizwe that the ANC’s President-General was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize based on this non-violent history\n- Although the armed struggle aimed to avoid human casualties, there was a risk of escalating violence and potentially harming innocent civilians\n- Non-violent resistance had not been fully exhausted, as other forms of peaceful protest could have been attempted\n- The decision to adopt an armed struggle led to the government becoming more repressive, with increased security legislation including the Sabotage Act (1962) and the General Laws Amendment Act (1963), which gave police more power to arrest and detain opposition and imposed the death penalty for acts deemed as treason\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe ANC's decision to adopt armed struggle in 1961 can be understood from multiple perspectives. The adoption of the armed struggle came after decades of unsuccessful peaceful protest. Large protest actions, like the 1956 Defiance Campaign, had been unable to end apartheid laws ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the government's violent response to peaceful demonstrations, notably the Sharpeville massacre in 1960, where 69 unarmed protesters were killed, and the banning of the ANC shortly thereafter, limited the options for effective peaceful protest ($\\checkmark$).\n\nOn the other hand, the decision to adopt an armed struggle directly resulted in the government passing laws like the General Laws Amendment Act in 1963, which allowed for wider police powers to arrest opponents, further weakening resistance ($\\checkmark$). The adoption of the armed struggle also ran counter to the image and philosophy that the ANC had cultivated since its inception, which had even led to the ANC's President-General being awarded the Nobel Peace Prize just three months before the start of the armed struggle ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0808","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0808\n\nIdentify **two** consequences of the Rivonia Trial for the anti-apartheid movement.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The imprisonment of key ANC leaders, including Nelson Mandela, Walter Sisulu, and Govan Mbeki, for life sentences\n- The effective decapitation of the ANC's leadership structure and organizational capacity within South Africa\n- The forced exile of the remaining ANC leadership and operations to other African countries\n- The creation of martyrs and symbols of resistance that inspired future anti-apartheid activism\n- The international attention and sympathy generated for the anti-apartheid cause through media coverage of the trial\n- The weakening of Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK) as an armed resistance organization due to the loss of key leaders\n- The shift of anti-apartheid resistance leadership to younger activists and external organizations\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Rivonia Trial resulted in the imprisonment of key ANC leaders, including Nelson Mandela, for life sentences, effectively removing the organization's leadership ($\\checkmark$). The trial forced the remaining ANC leadership into exile in other African countries, weakening the organization's domestic operations ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0809","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0809\n\nWhat, according to Source A, were the consequences of government policies on black South Africans?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extracts from a letter calling for the repeal of repressive legislation and threatening a “defiance” campaign from ANC leaders Dr. J. S. Moroka and W. M. Sisulu to Prime Minister D. F. Malan. Sent January 21, 1952. |\n\n[Source: Moroka, J.S., Sisulu W. M. (1952, January 21). *EXCHANGE OF CORRESPONDENCE BETWEEN THE AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS, THE SOUTH AFRICAN INDIAN CONGRESS, AND THE PRIME MINISTER OF THE UNION OF SOUTH AFRICA.* African National Congress. Retrieved May 30, 2025 from https://web.archive.org/web/20070714142402/http://www.anc.org.za/ancdocs/history/struggles/correspondence.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Gradual worsening of the social, economic and political position of the black South Africans\n- Increase in racial bitterness and tension in South African society\n- Black South Africans were deprived of fundamental human rights enjoyed in democratic communities\n- They were insulted and degraded through discriminatory legislation\n- Opposition organizations were being suppressed\n- Economic position of the people was being eroded\n- Creation of a reservoir of cheap labour for farms and gold mines\n- Black South Africans were being prevented from obtaining full nationhood\n- Humiliation of black South Africans in various ways\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source A, government policies caused the social, economic, and political conditions for black South Africans to steadily deteriorate ($\\checkmark$). The letter also speaks about increased racial tensions and hostility across the country ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the source states that black South Africans faced discrimination and were denied basic rights that would typically be guaranteed in democratic societies ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0810","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0810\n\nUsing evidence from Source A, analyze how the ANC justifies its planned civil disobedience campaign.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extracts from a letter calling for the repeal of repressive legislation and threatening a “defiance” campaign from ANC leaders Dr. J. S. Moroka and W. M. Sisulu to Prime Minister D. F. Malan. Sent January 21, 1952. |\n\n[Source: Moroka, J.S., Sisulu W. M. (1952, January 21). *EXCHANGE OF CORRESPONDENCE BETWEEN THE AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS, THE SOUTH AFRICAN INDIAN CONGRESS, AND THE PRIME MINISTER OF THE UNION OF SOUTH AFRICA.* African National Congress. Retrieved May 30, 2025 from https://web.archive.org/web/20070714142402/http://www.anc.org.za/ancdocs/history/struggles/correspondence.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following ways:\n- The ANC emphasizes its long history of peaceful, constitutional attempts (40 years since 1912) to portray itself as patient and having exhausted all legal options before resorting to civil disobedience.\n- The letter stresses the ANC's repeated offers of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cooperation\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$desire for peace, harmony and friendship\" to present themselves as the responsible party seeking dialogue. At the same time, the government is portrayed as uncompromising.\n- The ANC provides a specific deadline (February 29, 1952) and clear warning of consequences to demonstrate that it is acting reasonably by giving the government a final chance to avoid conflict.\n- The letter explicitly states the struggle is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not directed against any race or national group but against unjust laws\" to present their actions as targeting discriminatory legislation rather than being motivated by racial hatred.\n- The ANC references support from $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all enlightened and honest men and women, black and white, in our country and across the seas\" to suggest their cause has broad moral backing and legitimacy.\n- The letter blames the government for creating $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$tension and crises\" rather than anti-apartheid leaders, positioning the ANC as responding to government provocation rather than initiating conflict.\n- The ANC presents its actions as being $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in the interest of all\" sections of the population, suggesting that its campaign serves the greater good rather than narrow interests.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource A presents the ANC as reasonable and legitimate by emphasizing their extensive history of using $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$constitutional methods\" over $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$forty years\" to demonstrate they had exhausted all legal avenues before considering civil disobedience ($\\checkmark$). The letter also presents their approach as legitimate by highlighting their repeated demonstrations of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$willingness and readiness to cooperate with the Government\" and their $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sincere desire for peace, harmony and friendship,\" positioning the ANC as the responsible party seeking dialogue, while the government rejected these efforts ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the ANC presents their planned campaign as reasonable by providing the government with a specific ultimatum and deadline of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$29th day of February, 1952\" to repeal discriminatory acts, showing they were giving a final opportunity for peaceful resolution rather than acting hastily ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the letter presents the campaign as legitimate by explicitly stating that their struggle is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not directed against any race or national group but against unjust laws,\" demonstrating that their actions target discriminatory legislation rather than being motivated by racial animosity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0811","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0811\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the nature of protests against pass laws in 1956?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Women demonstrate against pass laws, 1956. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Apartheid%20South%20Africa/images/HI0811.jpg\" alt=\"HI0811\">\n[Source: *File:Collectie Fotocollectiie Afdrukken ANEFO Rousel,* fotonummer 157-0188, Bestanddeelnr 157-0188.jpg. (1956). ANEFO. Retrieved May 30, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collectie_Fotocollectiie_Afdrukken_ANEFO_Rousel,_fotonummer_157-0188,_Bestanddeelnr_157-0188.jpg. CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The protests were specifically directed at the extension of pass laws to women, as evidenced by the signs mentioning \"women\" and the fact that all protesters visible are women.\n- The protests were peaceful and non-threatening, as shown by the presence of a baby, smiling women, and the absence of any weapons or aggressive behavior.\n- The protests were organized and planned, as demonstrated by the prepared signs with consistent messaging and coordinated slogans.\n- Pass laws were viewed very negatively by protesters, who equated them with slavery, as shown by the sign $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$WITH PASSES WE ARE SLAVES.\"\n- The protests involved ordinary community members rather than just political activists, as evidenced by the participation of women of different ages, including mothers with children.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B suggests that the protests against pass laws in 1956 were peaceful and non-violent ($\\checkmark$), as evidenced by the presence of women smiling and a mother carrying her baby, showing they did not expect or intend confrontation. The source also suggests the protests were well-organized ($\\checkmark$), as demonstrated by the prepared signs with clear, consistent messages opposing the pass laws. Finally, Source B suggests that black South African women felt that pass laws made them feel like “slaves” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0997","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0997\n\nWhat, according to source B, were the proposals of the Freedom Charter to achieve equality for all races and national groups?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source B** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An excerpt adapted from $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*The Freedom Charter,”* signed in Kliptown, South Africa on June 26, 1955.\n\n[ Source: Congress of the People. (1955, June 26). *The Freedom Charter*. Retrieved August 15, 2025, https://anc1912.org.za/the-freedom-charter-2/ Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Equal rights, including for all racial groups and in all sectors of society.\n\n - Equal opportunities for all people without discrimination of race, religion, or beliefs.\n\n - Socioeconomic equality for all sectors and social classes.\n\n - Educational equality for all children, regardless of their national group, race, or religion.\n\n - Political equality for all inhabitants of South Africa, without discrimination based on race, colour, or sex. \n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nTo achieve equal rights, the Freedom Charter proposes that all laws that discriminate based on race or belief are to be repealed, ensuring equality before the law. Besides, the Charter shall guarantee equal human rights, that is, the right to speak, publish, preach, worship, etc., irrespective of race or belief.  ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThe Freedom Charter guarantees educational equality by declaring that education shall be free, compulsory, universal, and equal for all children, without discrimination. It emphasizes that education aims to instill a love of culture, honor for liberty and peace, and to end adult illiteracy through a mass state education plan. By making education accessible and equitable for everyone, regardless of race or background, the Charter seeks to ensure that all individuals have the opportunity to learn and thrive equally. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0998","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0998\n\nWhat, according to Source A, was the importance of the participation of Rusty Bernstein in the Congress of the People?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the book $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$The Final Prize\" by Norman Levy and published in 2013. Levy opposed Bantu Education and promoted alternative learning for black students during apartheid. He was a member of the Congress of the People, and also contributed to founding the Congress of Democrats, a white anti-apartheid organization, also integrated by Rusty Bernstein. \n\n:box[\n\n(…) the idea of a Congress of the People (COP) at which the delegates would adopt a Freedom Charter which would assert the demand for civil rights for all South Africans, seemed to be the appropriate next step. (…) The leadership believed that this was an objective (...) about which $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all levels of the congress membership were passionate”. That this assessment was accurate was to some extent manifested in (…) the language of the campaign. This was essentially the genius of Rusty Bernstein who (…) reached out to the masses with a quality of prose that matched the expectations of the occasion. Accordingly, the Freedom Charter helped to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mobilize the people up and down the land and awaken an echo \\[for freedom\\] in their hearts”. (…)\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThis excitement was captured in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call to the Congress of the People”, a passionate document in the format of a leaflet, drafted (…) by Rusty Bernstein. (…) Rusty was obviously touched by the emotions that the idea of the COP had aroused. He was not by nature overtly passionate (…). $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call” (…) was an exception, an indication of the extent to which Bernstein was personally inspired by the egalitarian idealism of the action. Its stirring message reached out $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to brothers without land and children without schooling”; to the farmers in the reserves; to the miners $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in the dark shafts and cold compounds, far from their families”; and to the farm workers and workers in the factories. It called on them to speak of long hours, housing and pass laws, of taxes and of cattle-culling and of famine, ending with the repetitive refrain:\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Let us speak together, all of us together - African and European, Indian and Coloured. Voter and voteless. Privileged and rightless. The happy and the homeless. Let us speak of freedom.”\n\n]\n\n[Source: Levy\\, N. (2015, June 18). *The Final Prize by Norman Levy. South African History Online.* Retrieved August 2, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/freedomcharter-norman-levy. CC.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - Rusty Bernstein's participation signified a commitment to inclusivity and unity across racial lines.\n\n - Bernstein played a key role, as a white leader, in uniting South Africans for a common cause.\n\n - He authored influential material that inspired support and collective action against apartheid.\n\n - Bernstein's leadership and rhetoric helped transform the Freedom Charter into a symbol of hope and solidarity.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to source A, Rusty Bernstein, as part of a racial minority group, played a key role in uniting South Africans of all races, calling to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$African and European, Indian and Coloured” and demonstrating that the movement should go beyond racial profiling to focus on the shared goal of fighting for the freedoms and rights everyone deserved equally. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nIn his $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call to the Congress of the People,\" Bernstein used persuasive language that connected with all social and racial groups. His ability to emphasize the struggles of the marginalized and to call for collective action with his refrain $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Let us speak together, all of us together. Let us speak of freedom” made the Congress of the People a powerful force in the fight for racial unity against apartheid. ($\\checkmark$)\n\nThrough his leadership skills and unifying rhetoric -as can be seen in source A-, Rusty Bernstein played a vital role in turning the Freedom Charter into a document that symbolized hope for all South Africans, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mobilized the people up and down the land”, awakening $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$an echo for freedom in their hearts”. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0999","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0999\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyze the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the anti-apartheid resistance.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source A** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from the book $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$*The Final Prize\"* by Norman Levy and published in 2013. Levy opposed Bantu Education and promoted alternative learning for black students during apartheid. He was a member of the Congress of the People, and also contributed to founding the Congress of Democrats, a white anti-apartheid organization, also integrated by Rusty Bernstein. \n\n:box[\n\n(…) the idea of a Congress of the People (COP) at which the delegates would adopt a Freedom Charter which would assert the demand for civil rights for all South Africans, seemed to be the appropriate next step. (…) The leadership believed that this was an objective (...) about which $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all levels of the congress membership were passionate”. That this assessment was accurate was to some extent manifested in (…) the language of the campaign. This was essentially the genius of Rusty Bernstein who (…) reached out to the masses with a quality of prose that matched the expectations of the occasion. Accordingly, the Freedom Charter helped to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mobilize the people up and down the land and awaken an echo \\[for freedom\\] in their hearts”. (…)\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThis excitement was captured in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call to the Congress of the People”, a passionate document in the format of a leaflet, drafted (…) by Rusty Bernstein. (…) Rusty was obviously touched by the emotions that the idea of the COP had aroused. He was not by nature overtly passionate (…). $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Call” (…) was an exception, an indication of the extent to which Bernstein was personally inspired by the egalitarian idealism of the action. Its stirring message reached out $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to brothers without land and children without schooling”; to the farmers in the reserves; to the miners $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in the dark shafts and cold compounds, far from their families”; and to the farm workers and workers in the factories. It called on them to speak of long hours, housing and pass laws, of taxes and of cattle-culling and of famine, ending with the repetitive refrain:\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Let us speak together, all of us together - African and European, Indian and Coloured. Voter and voteless. Privileged and rightless. The happy and the homeless. Let us speak of freedom.”\n\n]\n\n[Source: Levy\\, N. (2015, June 18). *The Final Prize by Norman Levy. South African History Online.* Retrieved August 2, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/freedomcharter-norman-levy. CC.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor **value**, any of the following: \n\n - Norman Levy was a member of the Congress of the People, and also contributed to founding the Congress of Democrats, a white anti-apartheid organization. \n\n - He writes to highlight Rusty Bernstein's role in drafting the Freedom Charter, an essential document in the anti-apartheid resistance movement. \n\n - Levy gives testimony of the events leading up to and during the Congress of the People, as part of the resistance to apartheid. \n\nFor **limitations**, any of the following:\n\n - Source A represents only Norman Levy's perspective in the movement. \n\n - The fact that Levy and Bernstein are both part of the same white anti-apartheid organization may influence his attempt to convince the reader regarding Bernstein's leading role.\n\n - Source A only highlights Bernstein's role, but it does not mention other anti-apartheid movement leaders who were also important.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nNorman Levy, a member of the Congress of the People and the Congress of Democrats, writes as a witness to the events he describes. His account constitutes a firsthand source, giving historians direct access to the facts related to these crucial events in the anti-apartheid resistance. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ VO}$) The purpose of Levy's account is to highlight Rusty Bernstein's contribution to the resistance to apartheid. This allows historians to delve into the roles and motivations of a key figure in the movement. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ VP}$) The source's content describes the events leading up to and during the Congress. For historians, this source provides valuable details and a narrative that reflects the atmosphere, decisions, and significance of these critical events, which marked a turning point in the resistance movement against apartheid. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ VC}$)\n\nOn the other hand, as for the source's origin, since this is an account from the perspective of Norman Levy, who participated in the anti-apartheid movement, it may be subject to personal bias, selective memory, or a particular retrospective interpretation that somehow modifies the facts. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ LO}$) Levy's focus on highlighting Rusty Bernstein's role may lead him to overlook other contributions or different leaders' perspectives in the resistance movement. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ LP}$) Furthermore, the source content, taken from a chapter in Levy's book, presents only part of the story and may omit important details or broader context surrounding Congress and the whole anti-apartheid resistance. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{ LC}$)"},{"ref":"HI1008","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1008\n\nDescribe the role of the Federation of South African Women (FEDSAW) during apartheid.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Founded in 1954 in Johannesburg as part of the Congress Alliance.\n- Brought together women of all races to fight for equality and social justice.\n- Organised opposition to apartheid laws, especially the pass laws for women.\n- Provided leadership through figures such as Lilian Ngoyi or Helen Joseph\n- Mobilised grassroots campaigns in townships and trade unions, linking local struggles to national ones.\n- Led the 20,000-strong Women's March on Pretoria in 1956, one of the largest demonstrations in South African history.\n- Adopted the slogan “You strike a woman, you strike a rock” to symbolise women's strength in resistance.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Federation of South African Women played a key role by bringing together women of all races to resist apartheid laws, especially the pass laws ($\\checkmark$), and by leading the 1956 Women's March of 20,000 women in Pretoria, which became one of the most powerful demonstrations against the regime ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1009","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1009\n\nExplain the significance of the Women's March on Pretoria in 1956\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Mass protest: Around 20,000 women marched peacefully against pass laws, one of the largest demonstrations in South African history.\n- Unity: Demonstrated solidarity across racial and class lines, showing the potential of mass non-racial resistance.\n- Women's leadership: Highlighted the role of women such as Lilian Ngoyi, Rahima Moosa, Sophia Williams-De Bruyn, and Helen Joseph.\n- Challenged stereotypes: Undermined the view that women were passive or politically uninvolved.\n- National symbol: Became an iconic moment of resistance, remembered annually as Women's Day (9 August).\n- Grassroots power: Reflected successful local organising by the Federation of South African Women (FEDSAW).\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Women's March of 1956 was significant because it showed unity across racial and class lines. Women from different communities came together to protest the pass laws, proving that non-racial cooperation was possible in the fight against apartheid ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt also challenged stereotypes that women were passive in politics. By taking a leading role in such a large protest, women demonstrated that they could be powerful organisers and leaders in the resistance movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1010","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1010\n\nDescribe **three** ways women's organisations strengthened grassroots resistance in the 1950s\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Mass mobilisation and protest:\n    - The Federation of South African Women (FEDSAW) united women across racial and class lines.\n    - Organised the 1956 Women's March, with 20,000 women protesting against pass laws, one of the largest demonstrations of the era.\n- Local and community organising:\n    - Women worked street by street in townships, linking local struggles to national campaigns.\n    - Mobilised through trade unions and neighbourhood networks.\n- Broadening the anti-apartheid struggle:\n    - Framed resistance as both a fight for racial equality and for gender rights, giving the movement wider legitimacy.\n    - Challenged stereotypes that women were passive or politically uninvolved.\n- Support structures and alliances:\n    - The Black Sash, led mainly by white women, supported grassroots struggles with vigils, petitions, and legal aid centres.\n    - Provided moral visibility and practical assistance for Africans targeted by apartheid laws.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWomen played a central role by organising at grassroots level, connecting people in their streets and workplaces so that local actions could strengthen larger campaigns ($\\checkmark$). They also reshaped the resistance, insisting it included women's rights and overturning the belief that women should stay out of politics ($\\checkmark$). Meanwhile, the Black Sash, an organisation for liberal white women, showed solidarity through vigils and petitions and gave Black South Africans legal advice, offering both moral pressure on the state and concrete assistance to those under apartheid laws ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1011","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1011\n\nExplain the contributions of **two** female leaders in the anti-apartheid movement during the 1950s and 1960s.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following (or others not mentioned):\n- Winnie Mandela: Active in the, she mobilised women at the grassroots and became an enduring symbol of resistance, especially after Mandela's imprisonment, showing women's central role in sustaining the struggle.\n- Ruth First was an investigative journalist who exposed apartheid abuses and gave voice to the oppressed. She was active in the SACP, supported the Freedom Charter, and was charged in the 1956 Treason Trial. In 1963 she was detained under the 90-Day Detention Law. As an academic scholar, she linked her research to activism, raising awareness both inside South Africa and internationally.\n- Lilian Ngoyi: first woman elected to the ANC's National Executive Committee who helped bridge grassroots movements with national political organizations. Also a leader in FEDSAW.\n- Helen Joseph: helped draft the Freedom Charter (1955) and played a key role in FEDSAW.\n- Other FEDSAW leaders (e.g., Sophia Williams-De Bruyn, Rahima Moosa): Organised the 1956 Women's March, uniting 20,000 women of all races in a landmark act of defiance.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe leadership of Winnie Mandela showed the importance of women in sustaining the anti-apartheid struggle. She organised through the ANC Women's League, encouraging women to join resistance campaigns ($\\checkmark$),\nand later stood as a public symbol of resilience when Nelson Mandela was jailed, proving that women could lead from the front ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSimilarly, Ruth First offered intellectual and activist leadership. Through her role as a journalist for New Age and as an academic, she revealed the injustices of apartheid, supported the Freedom Charter, and helped broaden the movement's vision ($\\checkmark$). Her involvement in the 1956 Treason Trial and later detention in 1963 showed how central she was to the struggle ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1012","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1012\n\nExplain **two** ways apartheid laws affected the economic and social lives of Africans between 1948 and 1964.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nGuidelines:\n- Must include  a combination of **both** social and economic effects **and** mention the relevant law.\n- **One** mark is awarded for each law and **one** mark for accurate explanation of  effect.\n\n**Economic impact laws:**\n- Pass Laws: Restricted African movement into cities; limited access to jobs.\n- Job Colour Bar (reinforced by labour regulations): Reserved skilled, better-paid jobs for whites.\n- Bantu Education Act (1953): Legally restricted Africans to inferior education, preparing them only for menial labour.\n- Group Areas Act (1950): Forced removals made commuting costly and disrupted economic stability\n\n**Social impact laws:**\n- Population Registration Act (1950): Classified all South Africans by race, forming the basis of discrimination.\n- Group Areas Act (1950): Segregated residential areas, breaking up communities and families.\n- Bantu Education Act (1953): Also reinforced social inequality by denying equal schooling.\n- Petty Apartheid Laws: Enforced segregation in public spaces (schools, hospitals, transport, shops, benches).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTwo key apartheid laws that shaped Africans' lives were the Pass Laws ($\\checkmark$)  and the Group Areas Act (1950) ($\\checkmark$). The Pass Laws restricted Africans from freely entering cities, and as a result they were blocked from better-paid urban jobs. This meant many families stayed trapped in poverty, showing how the law mainly benefited white employers by controlling cheap labour ($\\checkmark$). The Group Areas Act of 1950 forced people into segregated townships far from their workplaces. Because of this, families and communities were broken apart, and workers had to spend more time and money travelling. Therefore, this law had both an economic and social impact, making daily life much harder ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1013","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1013\n\nAccording to Source A, what problems were caused by the pass laws?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An extract from a speech delivered by Dora Tamana in Langa Township on January 4, 1953, during a protest against the new pass laws. Tamana was a member of the ANC Women's League and later the Federation of South African Women (FSAW). |\n\n[Source: South African History Online. (2015, May 13). Found in the article *The 1956 Women's March in Pretoria.* South African History Online. Retrieved February 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/1956-womens-march-pretoria  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Unemployment\n- Risk of imprisonment for the women\n- Lack of accommodation\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source A, the pass system restricted people's ability to work, as Tamana explains that “we have seen unemployment… because of passes” ($\\checkmark$). It also disrupted family life, since the source states that passes led to “families broken” and even the risk of mothers being jailed “for a small technical offence - not having a pass,” leaving their children without care ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1014","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1014\n\nWith reference to the origin, purpose and content, assess the value of source A for a historian studying resistance to apartheid pass laws.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An extract from a speech delivered by Dora Tamana in Langa Township on January 4, 1953, during a protest against the new pass laws. Tamana was a member of the ANC Women’s League and later the Federation of South African Women (FSAW). |\n\n[Source: South African History Online. (2015, May 13). Found in the article *The 1956 Women's March in Pretoria.* South African History Online. Retrieved February 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/1956-womens-march-pretoria  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nValues:\n- Because the source is a speech by Dora Tamana, an ANC Women's League member later active in the Federation of South African Women, it provides a direct voice from a female leader involved in early resistance to apartheid pass laws.\n- The purpose of the speech, to mobilise African women and men in Langa township in 1953, shows how activists tried to inspire solidarity and direct action against the new pass regulations.\n- The content of the speech, which describes unemployment, lack of housing, family break-ups, and imprisonment caused by the passes, also demonstrates how women framed the laws as destructive to family life.\n\nLimitations:\n- Because the source is a public political speech, it is inherently biased towards the opposition and does not provide a balanced perspective.\n- The purpose was to inspire resistance, so it emphasises only the negative effects of the passes without acknowledging any government justification or wider social context.\n- The content reflects one leader's passionate appeal, but may not capture the diversity of experiences among African women, nor the views of men or other community groups.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThis source is valuable because its origin is a speech by Dora Tamana at a protest in Langa in 1953, showing the views of a woman leader in the ANC Women's League who was actively resisting and organizing resistance to the pass laws at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). Tamana says, “we women will never carry these passes,” suggesting that the laws were impacting all sectors of society and inspiring resistance within them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content highlights the damage of pass laws, mentioning “unemployment, lack of accommodation and families broken,” which helps us understand why women opposed them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$). However, as a rally speech, it is one-sided and leaves out the government's perspective ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$)."},{"ref":"HI1015","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1015\n\nUsing the information in Source B, explain how apartheid laws affected the Church's ability to provide welfare and services.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from the 1972 SPRO-CAS Church Commission Report, edited by Peter Randall, which analysed how apartheid laws restricted the work of Christian churches in South Africa. |\n\n[Source: Randall, P. (Ed.). (1972). *Apartheid and the Church: Report of the Church Commission of the Study Project on Christianity in Apartheid Society (SPRO-CAS Publication No. 8).* SPRO-CAS / Christian Institute of Southern Africa, pp.12,13. Retrieved September 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/sites/default/files/archive-files3/rep19720000.037.052.008.pdf Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Apartheid created material need (poverty, broken families, lack of accommodation), increasing demands on Churches.\n- Racial separation made it difficult for white congregations to see or help African communities.\n- Laws restricted Church access to resettlement camps, prisons, and detainees.\n- Welfare organisations faced government pressure to stop multi-racial work, threatening subsidies.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source explains that apartheid laws increased the Church's welfare burden by creating “breakdown of African family life” through the migrant labour system and “poverty of Africans in resettlement camps” ($\\checkmark$). These conditions meant there were “great material and spiritual need,” so the Church had to devote more “manpower and money” to helping people, making its welfare role much harder ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt also shows that the state directly limited the Church's work by restricting access to those who needed help. The report mentions that the Church had “difficulty in obtaining access to these resettlement camps” and that it was “denied access to detainees” held under security laws ($\\checkmark$). This meant the Church could not easily reach prisoners or people in camps, severely reducing its ability to provide service where it was most needed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1016","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1016\n\nAccording to Source C, what were the main differences between Afrikaans Churches and English-speaking Churches in their approach to Black participation?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | This source is taken from the 1972 SPRO-CAS Church Commission Report, which examined how apartheid shaped church membership and racial divisions. It highlights why many Black Christians left mainstream churches and turned to independent African churches. |\n\n[Source: Randall, P. (Ed.). (1972). *Apartheid and the Church: Report of the Church Commission of the Study Project on Christianity in Apartheid Society (SPRO-CAS Publication No. 8).* SPRO-CAS / Christian Institute of Southern Africa, p. 33. Retrieved September 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/sites/default/files/archive-files3/rep19720000.037.052.008.pdf  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Afrikaans Churches created structures for separate Black participation\n- English-speaking Churches failed to implement integration for Black members\n- Afrikaans Churches had few breakaways, but many Blacks left English Churches for independent churches.\n- Afrikaans Churches allowed some African culture, while English Churches offered almost none.\n- Both struggled to attract Black members, but for different reasons: separation vs. unequal treatment.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source C, Afrikaans Churches “provided the structures for blacks to have… separate independence,” creating segregated but organised spaces for Black worship, while English-speaking Churches “failed to implement their official policy of integration” and did not give Black members equal participation, keeping Black members in unequal, subordinate roles ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe source also says there were “fewer separatist churches and sects splitting off” from Afrikaans Churches, but many Black Christians left English-speaking Churches to join independent churches where “self-respect and Africanisation” were priorities ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt also notes that Afrikaans Churches allowed “some degree of self-expression in their own cultural forms,” so African music, language or ritual could appear in a limited way, while English-speaking Churches offered almost no space for African culture and remained strongly European in style ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1017","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1017\n\nUsing Source B and C, identify two ways churches attempted to promote interracial fellowship under apartheid.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | An extract from the 1972 SPRO-CAS Church Commission Report, edited by Peter Randall, which analysed how apartheid laws restricted the work of Christian churches in South Africa. |\n\n[Source: Randall, P. (Ed.). (1972). *Apartheid and the Church: Report of the Church Commission of the Study Project on Christianity in Apartheid Society (SPRO-CAS Publication No. 8).* SPRO-CAS / Christian Institute of Southern Africa, pp.12,13. Retrieved September 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/sites/default/files/archive-files3/rep19720000.037.052.008.pdf Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | This source is taken from the 1972 SPRO-CAS Church Commission Report, which examined how apartheid shaped church membership and racial divisions. It highlights why many Black Christians left mainstream churches and turned to independent African churches. |\n\n[Source: Randall, P. (Ed.). (1972). *Apartheid and the Church: Report of the Church Commission of the Study Project on Christianity in Apartheid Society (SPRO-CAS Publication No. 8).* SPRO-CAS / Christian Institute of Southern Africa, p. 33. Retrieved September 10, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/sites/default/files/archive-files3/rep19720000.037.052.008.pdf  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Churches had an official policy of integration and tried to include all races in one fellowship.\n- English-speaking Churches attempted to give shared worship spaces to both white and Black members.\n- Churches tried to offer multi-racial welfare and service programmes.\n- Some Churches attempted to maintain interracial contact despite apartheid's restrictions.\n- Welfare organisations tried to remain multi-racial in structure and membership.\n- Churches aimed to minister to people of different races as part of Christian unity.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource B shows that Churches tried to build interracial fellowship by preserving “inter-racial contact”, even though apartheid reduced these chances and made such interaction rare ($\\checkmark$). Source C also explains that English-speaking Churches attempted unity through their “official policy of integration,” but this effort failed because Black members were not given equal participation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1096","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1096\n\nDefine the concept of the Bantustan in apartheid South Africa.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following (or a combination of them):\n- “Bantu” means “people” in Bantu languages, and “stan” means “land” in the Persian language.\n- It refers to the homelands of Black South Africans that were established by the all-white government of South Africa.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe concept of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Bantustan\" originated in the late 1940s, combining the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Bantu,\" which means $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people\" in various Bantu languages, with the Persian suffix $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$-stan,\" meaning $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$land.\" It refers to the designated areas for Black South Africans (Bantu-speaking peoples) within South Africa's system of separate development ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1097","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1097\n\nDescribe Henrik Verwoerd's role and significance in apartheid South Africa.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following for the **role**:\n- Between 1950 and 58, Hendrik Verwoerd served as Minister of Education, creating a separate and inferior education system for non-white students.\n- As prime minister (1958-1966), Verwoerd promoted apartheid as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$policy of separate development.\"\n\nAny **two** of the following (or a combination of the following) for his **significance:**\n- Key figure in implementing $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$grand apartheid” using education to reinforce the Black South African students' subordinate status to whites.\n- Official establisher of the concept of Bantustan and main responsible for its lasting effects.\n- Principal architect of the compelled allocation of each African (Bantu) group to poor land territories (Bantustans).\n- He reserved cities, industrial zones, and mineral-rich lands for whites.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBetween 1950 and 1958, Hendrik Verwoerd served as Minister of Education and created a separate and inferior education system for non-white students; later, as prime minister from 1958 to 1966, he promoted apartheid as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$policy of separate development\" ($\\checkmark$).\n\nVerwoerd was the official establisher of the Bantustan concept and played a key role in implementing $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$grand apartheid,\" using education to reinforce the subordinate status of Black South African students to whites, leaving lasting effects on the country ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAs the principal architect of the forced allocation of each African (Bantu) group to impoverished land territories known as Bantustans, Verwoerd also ensured that cities, industrial zones, and mineral-rich areas were reserved exclusively for whites. His leadership solidified apartheid, enforcing racial segregation and increasing economic inequality in South Africa. His legacy is characterized by the systematic marginalization of Black South Africans with enduring social and economic consequences ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1098","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1098\n\nAnalyze the role of the Group Areas Act (1954) and the Native Resettlement Act (1954) in the implementation of “grand apartheid” measures.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of the following:\n- Reshaping South African society ensured that whites maintained unquestioned power.\n- Assigned racial groups to distinct residential and commercial areas in urban regions as part of a system of urban apartheid (Group Areas Act).\n- Exclude people of color from living in the most developed areas, which were restricted to Whites (Group Areas Act).\n- Allowed the removal of Black people from areas within and near the magisterial district of Johannesburg by the South African government. (Native Resettlement Act).\n- The forced removals under the Group Areas Act and Native Resettlement Act uprooted non-white communities, relocating them to deprived areas. This led to enduring social dislocation and persistent poverty.\n- These laws provided the legal foundation for the apartheid government to systematically enforce spatial segregation in cities, setting a precedent for further discriminatory legislation and policies throughout the apartheid era.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Group Areas Act and the Native Resettlement Act of 1954 are considered part of the “grand apartheid” measures because they were designed to fundamentally reshape South African society by enforcing strict racial segregation. The Group Areas Act assigned racial groups to separate residential and business districts in urban areas, creating a system of urban apartheid that ensured whites would maintain unquestioned power in the most developed and economically prosperous locations ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Natives Resettlement Act allowed the government to forcibly remove Black South Africans, enabling the displacement of these communities and strengthening white control over urban centers. These laws formalized urban apartheid, systematically marginalizing and disenfranchising non-white populations, and supporting the goal of maintaining white dominance, reflecting the core aims of “grand apartheid” ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the implementation of the Group Areas Act and the Natives Resettlement Act had profound social and economic effects. Entire communities were uprooted, often with little to no compensation, and relocated to areas with fewer resources, limited infrastructure, and poor access to employment opportunities. The forced removals also caused overcrowding and poverty in the designated non-white areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThese laws provided the legal basis for the apartheid government to systematically plan and carry out the spatial segregation of South African cities, establishing a precedent for additional discriminatory laws and policies throughout the apartheid era ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1099","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1099\n\nOutline the South African government's goals in implementing the Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959 aimed to establish $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Separate Development\" by creating autonomous homelands for Black South Africans.\n- The government planned to reclassify Black South Africans as citizens of these homelands, stripping them of their rights as South African citizens.\n- The Act created homelands as self-governing regions, which could eventually become formally independent states.\n- Precedent for The Black Homelands Citizenship Act of 1970, which granted all Black South Africans citizenship of the homelands, even if they lived in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$White South Africa.\"\n- Then reinforced by the Bantu Homelands Constitution Act of 1971, which established a framework for developing the homelands toward complete independence.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959 aimed to create distinct, self-governing regions for different racial groups by creating independent homeland territories for Black South Africans, known as Bantustans ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe government sought to reclassify Black South Africans as citizens of these Bantustans instead of South Africa itself, effectively stripping them of their remaining rights as South African citizens and increasing racial segregation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThis policy aimed to reduce the power of the Black population in South Africa and further entrench disenfranchisement. The Act established the plan called $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Separate Development,\" which allowed the so-called $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$homeland territories” to be set up as autonomous regions and, eventually, as nominally fully independent states. Bantustans were encouraged to choose independence, which would greatly decrease the number of Black citizens in South Africa ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1100","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1100\n\nDescribe the significance of the Transkei Bantustan to apartheid South Africa.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Transkei was the first Bantustan established under South Africa's apartheid policy of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate development\" and served as a trial model.\n- The Transkei Territorial Authority was established in 1959; its territory was defined in 1962 and officially established in 1963.\n- It was the largest Bantustan, covering 42,000 km², with a population of 4.75 million, and it was assigned to the Xhosa ethnic group.\n- In 1976, after the Soweto uprising, Transkei declared nominal independence and established its own government led by Kaiser Matanzima.\n- International recognition was very limited; only Uruguay, then under military rule, acknowledged Transkei's independence.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nTranskei is significant as the first Bantustan created under South Africa's apartheid policy of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate development.\" The government intended Bantustans to be designated homelands for Black South Africans, thereby enforcing racial segregation and restricting Black political rights. Transkei served as a test case for this system ($\\checkmark$). Covering 42,000 km², Transkei was the largest and most populous of the Bantustans, with a population of 4.75 million. The South African government specifically designated Transkei for the Xhosa ethnic group, aiming to isolate them from areas set aside for whites and other groups. This separation was meant to reinforce the apartheid ideology ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn 1976, following the Soweto uprising, Transkei declared nominal independence. It established its own government, led by Kaiser Matanzima, but remained economically reliant on South Africa, and its autonomy was largely symbolic. Transkei's existence highlights the failures and injustices of the Bantustan system, which was used to justify excluding Black South Africans from citizenship and rights, helping the white minority maintain power ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1101","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1101\n\nDescribe **two** of the racial/ethnic definitions used during South Africa's apartheid policies.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Blacks: Largest group. Segregated. Removed from their homes.\n- Whites: Ruling minority. Held power and privileges.\n- Coloureds: Mixed race. Also segregated. With sub-classifications.\n- Indians: Originally from India. Also segregated and classified.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Colored community, made up of people of mixed race, also faced segregation and discrimination during apartheid. Within this group, additional subcategories were created, such as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Cape Coloured,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Cape Malay,” $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Griqua,” and “Other Coloured,” showing the government's effort to divide and control the population through strict racial classifications ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIndians, originally from India, were part of the groups classified under apartheid. Like other racially designated groups, they faced segregation and the denial of many rights. The government also labeled people of Asian descent with terms like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Indian,\" $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Chinese,\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Other Asiatic,\" subjecting them to similar discriminatory policies and restrictions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1102","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1102\n\nDescribe the strategies used by the South African National Party to win the 1948 elections and secure long-term power.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- The National Party won the 1948 election by manipulating electoral laws.\n- Only white citizens were permitted to vote, which contributed to the National Party's victory.\n- Starting in 1948, the National Party established a repressive system of racial segregation, stripping rights from most Black, Coloured, and Asian populations.\n- Encouraged messaging that made the white population feel afraid or threatened by the black population.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn the 1948 general elections, the radical National Party (NP) won by forming a coalition with the Afrikaner Party, led by Protestant pastor Daniel François Malan, who opposed more liberal candidates ($\\checkmark$). Their victory was achieved through the manipulation of the electoral law, which gave them a majority despite receiving fewer votes than their rival, the United Party ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the NP used political propaganda to instill fear in the white population, strengthening support for their apartheid proposals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1103","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1103\n\nExplain the goals/objectives of Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK).\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** (or a combination) of the following:\n- Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK), the armed wing of the African National Congress (ANC), aimed to fight against apartheid and end white minority rule in South Africa.\n- Its primary objective was to achieve full political rights and equality for all South Africans.\n- MK targeted government installations for sabotage to disrupt their function while intentionally avoiding human casualties.\n- As the conflict intensified, a gradual shift toward guerrilla warfare tactics began to emerge.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nUmkhonto we Sizwe (MK), established as the armed wing of the African National Congress (ANC), primarily aimed to fight apartheid and end white minority rule in South Africa ($\\checkmark$). Its main goal was to secure full political rights and equality for all South Africans, regardless of race, and to end the system of apartheid and white minority rule through resistance ($\\checkmark$). MK initially targeted government installations and symbolic sites for sabotage, to avoid unnecessary loss of life while substantially disrupting the apartheid government ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1104","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1104\n\nIdentify **one** key figure of the Black Consciousness Movement and explain its contributions to the anti-apartheid movement.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor identify, any of the following:\n- Steve Biko - leader and founder of BCM\n- Mamphela Ramphele - worked with the South African Students' Organisation (SASO) and the Black Community Programs (BCP) for black community development\n- Barney Pityana - founding member of SASO and black theologian\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any combination of the following:\n- Promoted black pride, self-reliance, and psychological liberation from apartheid oppression.\n- Mobilized students and youth, inspiring resistance and raising awareness of the injustices of apartheid.\n- Promoted unity among all oppressed groups in South Africa who were labeled “non-white” under apartheid laws.\n- Helped mobilize young people for key events such as the Soweto Uprising in 1976, drawing global attention.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe key figure of the Black Consciousness Movement (BCM) was Steve Biko, its most prominent leader and founder ($\\checkmark$). The BCM's main achievements included promoting black pride, self-reliance, and psychological liberation from apartheid oppression ($\\checkmark$). It mobilized students and youth, inspiring resistance and raising awareness about the injustices of apartheid ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the BCM encouraged the formation of grassroots organizations and community projects, which empowered local communities to challenge apartheid policies and fostered a sense of unity and purpose among oppressed South Africans ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1105","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1105\n\nDescribe **three** challenges faced by South African resistance movements in their efforts to combat apartheid.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- The apartheid government used harsh repressive measures.\n- Activists and their families were often subject to harassment, imprisonment without trial, and even torture or death.\n- Many resistance groups struggled with limited resources and had to operate underground.\n- There were ideological and strategic differences among various groups, which sometimes led to divisions within the anti-apartheid movement.\n- International isolation and the state's propaganda also hindered their efforts.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSouth African resistance movements against apartheid encountered many major obstacles. The apartheid government employed harsh repressive actions, such as banning groups, detaining leaders, and using violence to suppress dissent ($\\checkmark$). Many resistance groups faced limited resources and had to operate underground, which made communication and coordination difficult ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, ideological and strategic differences existed among groups, such as the African National Congress (ANC), the Pan-Africanist Congress (PAC), and the United Democratic Front (UDF), which sometimes led to divisions within the broader anti-apartheid movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1106","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1106\n\nWhat regulations, according to Source A, did the Population Registration Act establish in apartheid South Africa?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from The Population Registration Act (Act No. 30 of 1950). The act was published and came into effect on July 7, 1950. |\n\n[Source: Parliament of South Africa (1950, June). *Population Registration Act.* wikisource.org. Retrieved October 31, 2025, from https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Population_Registration_Act_1950. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** (or a combination) of the following:\n- Every person listed in the register had to be classified by the Director.\n- Authorities further categorized people of color and natives.\n- The Act allowed for classifications to be changed if found to be incorrect.\n- This classification system enforced racial categorization.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to this source, the Population Registration Act regulated that every person whose name was included in the register had to be classified by the Director as a white person, a coloured person, or a native ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, people of colour and natives were to be classified according to their specific ethnic or other group, as defined by the authorities ($\\checkmark$). The Act also allowed for the classification to be changed if it was deemed incorrect, after providing notice and an opportunity to be heard ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1107","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1107\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about racial violence in apartheid South Africa?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Reproduction of a road sign which appears on the barbed-wire fence of a farm facing the Johannesburg-Verreeniging Road in Johannesburg. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Apartheid%20South%20Africa/images/HI1107.png\" alt=\"HI1107\">\n[Source: Wikimedia Commons. (1952, July). *Road sign in Johannesburg.* Retrieved November 6, 2025, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reproduction_of_a_road_sign_which_appears_on_the_barbed-wire_fence_of_a_farm_facing_the_Johannesburg-Verreeniging_Road_in_Johannesburg.jpg. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** or a combination of the following:\n- The sign explicitly threatens violence against $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Natives, Indians & Coloureds,\" showing how brutality was used as a tool of racial control.\n- The language is dehumanizing, depicting targeted groups as intruders whose lives are considered disposable.\n- The fact that such signs could be publicly displayed indicates that White landowners had the right to use deadly force without consequences.\n- The sign serves as both a literal and symbolic barrier, enforcing segregation through fear.\n- This shows that apartheid was about creating fear and forcing submission.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThis source shows how racial violence was normalized during apartheid in South Africa by highlighting explicit threats of brutality like shooting on sight and releasing savage dogs against marginalized groups such as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Natives, Indians & Coloureds\" ($\\checkmark$). The dehumanizing language on the sign portrays these communities as unwanted intruders whose lives are considered disposable, reflecting an ideology that denied non-White South Africans any legal or moral protection ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1108","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1108\n\nAccording to Source C, what impacts did detention without trial have on Africans during apartheid?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  “The many faces of apartheid repression” is an article whose content was taken from the book A Crime Against Humanity - Analysing the Repression of the Apartheid State, edited by Max Coleman (Cape Town). |\n\n[Source: South African History Online. (2013, July 26). *The Many Faces of Apartheid Repression.* Retrieved November 1, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/many-faces-apartheid-repression Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** or a combination of the following:\n- Detention for interrogation or 'preventive' reasons was common since the 1960s, often without access to courts, family, or other necessary support.\n- Detainees could be held in solitary confinement for nearly indefinite periods.\n- Isolation promoted abuses, and there were ongoing allegations of torture and assault.\n- There was a continuous series of deaths in custody, many of them officially classified as suicides.\n- Since 1960, more than 75,000 people have been detained without trial.\n- These practices led to severe physical, psychological, and social consequences for African people.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to Source C, detention without trial allowed for detention for interrogation or $\\lq$preventive' reasons, without access to courts, lawyers, family, or friends ($\\checkmark$). As a result, this system kept many activists in solitary confinement for nearly indefinite periods, sometimes lasting up to 32 months, promoting abuse, torture, and assault on detainees ($\\checkmark$). Source C also says that many deaths occurred in custody, with 69 reported between 1963 and 1988, many of which were suicides ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1109","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1109\n\nDescribe **three** similarities between Sources A and D about racial classification in apartheid in South Africa.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from The Population Registration Act (Act No. 30 of 1950). The act was published and came into effect on July 7, 1950. |\n\n[Source: Parliament of South Africa (1950, June). *Population Registration Act.* wikisource.org. Retrieved October 31, 2025, from https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Population_Registration_Act_1950. Public Domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Statement of the National Party of South Africa on its “Policy on Color,” March 29, 1948, two months before a general election. It outlines the ideological justification for the subsequent apartheid legislation. |\n\n[Source: South African History Online. (2012, June 1). *Statement of the National Party of South Africa on its Policy on Color.* Retrieved October 30, 2025, from\nhttps://sahistory.org.za/archive/statement-national-party-south-africa-march-29-1948\nPublic Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** or a combination of the following:\n- Both sources are central to the establishment of apartheid in South Africa. Source A provides the legal framework, and Source D outlines the ideological justification.\n- Both emphasize dividing the population into racial categories (white, coloured, native/non-European).\n- Both prioritize protecting the white population as a distinct and dominant group.\n- Both advocate for the separation of racial groups rather than integration. Source A does this through administrative classification; Source D through the political ideology of “separate development.”\n- Both give power to officials: Authority figures decide how groups are defined and managed.\n- Both reject the idea of racial equality.\n- Both present apartheid as a system that supposedly benefits all racial groups by preserving their “character” and “future.”\n- Both sources see apartheid as essential for the future stability of South Africa. Source A establishes the legal infrastructure, and Source D provides the ideological rationale before the law's enactment.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth Source A and Source D highlight the division of the South African population into separate racial groups. Each source emphasizes the separation of whites, coloureds, and natives/non-Europeans as a key principle, making racial classification the basis of apartheid. This similarity points to their shared view of a society built on strict racial boundaries rather than integration ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother clear similarity is their support for the separation of racial groups. Source A enforces this through administrative classification, and Source D promotes it through the ideology of “separate development” ($\\checkmark$). Despite different approaches, both sources agree on the same goal: keeping racial communities apart. This shared stance shows their united effort to make apartheid not just a legal system but also a political philosophy guiding their actions ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, both sources depict apartheid as essential for South Africa's future stability. Source A provides the legal framework for enforcing the system, and Source D offers the ideological reasoning that justifies it ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1110","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1110\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source A for a historian studying the racial classification of apartheid's legislation.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from The Population Registration Act (Act No. 30 of 1950). The act was published and came into effect on July 7, 1950. |\n\n[Source: Parliament of South Africa (1950, June). *Population Registration Act.* wikisource.org. Retrieved October 31, 2025, from https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Population_Registration_Act_1950. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- As a primary source, the Population Registration Act is a piece of legislation enacted by the apartheid-era South African government in 1950.\n- The Act aimed to categorize every South African into rigid racial groups with further subdivisions for “coloured” and “native” groups.\n- Section 5 illustrates how race was not self-identified but imposed by the state, often arbitrarily and bureaucratically.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- As an official apartheid-era law, the source reflects the perspective and intentions of the ruling National Party. It does not offer a neutral or critical view of the legislation.\n- The Act was designed to enforce racial categorization, not to justify or explain the broader apartheid system. It omits the ideological motivations behind racial segregation and the political goals of the regime.\n- The language is formal and administrative, which may obscure the social consequences of the law. It focuses on procedures rather than outcomes.\n- The Act does not explain why these classifications were deemed necessary or how they would be used to enforce other apartheid laws.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs a piece of official legislation enacted by the apartheid-era South African government in 1950, the Population Registration Act serves as a direct primary source. Its origin provides historians with unfiltered insight into the legal mechanisms used by the National Party to institutionalize racial segregation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  The Act's explicit aim to classify all South Africans into racial categories such as “white,” “coloured,” or “native,” reveals the foundational role of racial categorization in the apartheid system. The further subdivisions within “coloured” and “native” groups underscore the regime's intent to fragment society along ethnic lines ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).  Section 5, which outlines how racial identity was determined by the state rather than self-identified, highlights the arbitrary and bureaucratic nature of apartheid enforcement, providing evidence of how racial identity was imposed by law, rather than based on lived experience or self-perception ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nAs an official government document, the Act reflects the ideology and intentions of the ruling National Party. It does not offer a critical or oppositional view, nor does it acknowledge the human cost or moral implications of its policies ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).  The Act is concerned solely with the mechanics of racial classification. It does not explain the broader ideological motivations behind apartheid or how this classification system was used to enforce other discriminatory laws, such as those governing land ownership, education, or movement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1192","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1192\n\nWhat, according to Source N, are the benefits of having separate white and Bantu national territories in South Africa?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source N** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Hendrik Verwoerd's Senate speech advocating apartheid and the Bantustans, titled “They will become the conquerors of white South Africa.” Cape Town, South Africa, 1959. |\n\n:box[If that will be the result, if, through the capabilities of the Bantu, it happens that here in South Africa there will be a White state, a big and strong White nation, along with various Bantu national units and areas (or states, if you like), how is that different from what we have in Europe? (…) Why is it so terrible that there are various nations and territories in South Africa, and even neighbouring states? These states can nevertheless have a bond, the bond of common interest. (…)  The ideal is to retain political independence while maintaining economic interdependence.\\\n\\\nNow I ask further: if such a division isn't possible, if having separate territories isn't a viable solution to political goals, what other option remains? The United Party [the opposition] has repeatedly stated: \"Nothing but a common South Africa is possible, a multiracial country, even if numerically the Bantustans outnumber whites three or four times.\" I emphasize, honestly and in the best interests of the white people of South Africa, that I prefer a secure white state in South Africa, regardless of what happens in other areas, rather than my people being absorbed into an integrated state where the Bantustans eventually dominate. A Bantustan for all of South Africa is the unavoidable result of the United Party's policy. Therefore, characterizing partition and subdivision as an undesirable pattern is completely nonsense, because under both policies, there will be black areas. Under the apartheid policy, the white man will at least control his own area, no matter the difficulties and how hard it may be. At least he has a chance to protect himself, something he wouldn't have in a multiracially controlled state.\n]\n\n[Source: Verwoerd, H. (1959, May 20). *They will become the conquerors of white South Africa.* Speakola. Retrieved October 11, 2025, from https<nolinks>://speakola.com/political/hendrik-verwoerd-apartheid-senate-speech-1959. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Separate territories allow each group to maintain political independence.\n- Economic connections and cooperation can be maintained even with political separation.\n- Separation provides a safe space for the white population to prevent being “absorbed” into an integrated country.\n- Compares this separation to examples of nations in Europe, suggesting that distinct national territories are normal and natural, rather than problematic.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to this source, the advantages of separate white and Bantu national units or territories in South Africa include allowing each group to maintain its own political independence while remaining economically interconnected ($\\checkmark$). Verwoerd also suggests that this arrangement would not be fundamentally different from the existence of various European nations, pointing to their successful coexistence and collaboration based on shared interests ($\\checkmark$). Finally, he argues that such separation offers the white population a secure area under their own control, giving them a chance to protect themselves from potential domination in a multiracial state where they might be outnumbered ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1193","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1193\n\nWhat does Source M suggest about the apartheid system in South Africa?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source M** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Map of the ten bantustans in South Africa at the end of the apartheid period (~1994), just before they were reincorporated into South Africa. By 1981, Transkei, Bophuthatswana, Venda and Ciskei all had nominal “independence.”|\n\n*Note:* The Bantu Authorities Act of 1951 helped establish these “homelands,” but was built on earlier laws such as the Natives Land Act of 1913 and subsequently modified by later laws such as the Bantu Homelands Citizenship Act (1970) and Bantu Homelands Constitution Act (1971), as well as others which modified their borders and legal rights.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Apartheid%20South%20Africa/images/HI1193.png\" alt=\"HI1193\">\n[Source: Htonl. (2013, May 8). *File: Bantustans in South Africa.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bantustans_in_South_Africa.svg. CC-BY-SA 3.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- Large-scale segregation and forced removals, as well as how legislation disrupted the social and economic life of non-white communities.\n- The creation of ten bantustans conveys apartheid's territorial strategy, transforming segregation into a system of geographic isolation and control.\n- Fragmented and disconnected “homelands” suggest a reinforced marginalization, deliberately dividing Black South African communities to legitimize exclusionary policies.\n- The organization of the homelands on the map suggested that smaller areas were reserved for a larger proportion of the population.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe map suggests that the apartheid system was characterized by large-scale segregation imposed on non-white populations. This is shown by the relatively large reserves at the periphery of the national territory, which were reserved for black ethnic groups in South Africa, such as the Venda, Zulu, and Ndebele peoples ($\\checkmark$). The map also suggests that the apartheid system created these “homelands” to be isolated and fragmented, as shown by their scattered and disconnected arrangement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1194","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1194\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose, and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source O for a historian studying the significance of Bantustans.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source O** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Nelson Mandela's article on Verwoerd's proposal of Bantu self-government, published in Liberation magazine N° 36.. |\n\n:box[There is no sovereignty. No autonomy. No democracy. No self-government. Nothing but a crude, empty fraud, to bluff the people, and to serve as a pretext for accumulating yet more hardships and injustices upon the African people. Politically, the talk about self-government for the reserves is a trick. Economically, it is an absurdity.\\\n\\\nThe few scattered African reserves in the Union, comprising approximately 13 percent of the least desirable land, represent the last remnants of land ownership left to the African people in their original ancestral home. After the depredations of generations of Europeans, the outrageous Land Acts from 1913 onwards had turned the once free and independent Tswana, Sotho, Xhosa, Zulu, and other peasant farmers of this country into a nation of landless outcasts and roving beggars, humble \"work-seekers\" on the mines and the farms where yesterday they had been masters of the land. And now the new White masters of the country generously \"presented\" the few miserable areas that were left to remain as reservoirs and breeding-grounds for black labor. These are the reserves.\\\n\\\nThe facts are that the reserves are congested, distressed areas, completely unable to sustain their present populations. People are on the verge of starvation. The White Paper [Verwoerd's proposal] discusses teaching Africans about soil conservation and agriculture. No amount of agricultural instruction will ever enable 13 percent of the land to sustain 66 percent of the population.]\n\n[Source: Mandela, N. (1959, May). *Verwoerd's Grim Plot.* South African History Online. Retrieved October 11, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/archive/verwoerds-grim-plot-nelson-mandela. CC-BY.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose, and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation, or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- Written by Nelson Mandela, offering a firsthand political critique of apartheid and Bantustan policies.\n- Aims to expose Bantustans as unjust and mobilize resistance through passionate, accusatory language.\n- Describes harsh conditions like poverty, land scarcity, and forced labor migration.\n- Links apartheid land laws to colonial exploitation and African liberation struggles.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- The source is politically charged and reflects Mandela's personal stance rather than a neutral viewpoint.\n- It overlooks diverse African perspectives, especially those from rural communities and traditional leaders.\n- Emotive language may obscure practical details about how Bantustans were administered.\n- Lacks empirical data and official records, limiting its value for policy or quantitative analysis.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWritten by Nelson Mandela, the source's origin is valuable because it represents a firsthand political critique of apartheid ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). Mandela seeks to expose the injustices of the Bantustan policies by explicitly addressing their impacts on black South Africans, describing poverty, land scarcity, and the forced migration of laborers, painting a grim picture of life under systemic oppression ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content of the source links these conditions to the broader legacy of colonial exploitation, framing apartheid land laws as a continuation of imperialist control and situating the struggle against Bantustans within the larger context of African liberation movements ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nBecause the source's explicit purpose is to critique the Bantustan system, it cannot be considered an objective account of its significance ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). The emotive language used by Mandela further evidences this. Though effective in conveying urgency and injustice, it can obscure practical details about the actual administration of Bantustans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1195","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1195\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources O and P reveal about the impacts of the Bantustan system.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source O** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Nelson Mandela's article on Verwoerd's proposal of Bantu self-government, published in Liberation magazine N° 36.. |\n\n:box[There is no sovereignty. No autonomy. No democracy. No self-government. Nothing but a crude, empty fraud, to bluff the people, and to serve as a pretext for accumulating yet more hardships and injustices upon the African people. Politically, the talk about self-government for the reserves is a trick. Economically, it is an absurdity.\\\n\\\nThe few scattered African reserves in the Union, comprising approximately 13 percent of the least desirable land, represent the last remnants of land ownership left to the African people in their original ancestral home. After the depredations of generations of Europeans, the outrageous Land Acts from 1913 onwards had turned the once free and independent Tswana, Sotho, Xhosa, Zulu, and other peasant farmers of this country into a nation of landless outcasts and roving beggars, humble \"work-seekers\" on the mines and the farms where yesterday they had been masters of the land. And now the new White masters of the country generously \"presented\" the few miserable areas that were left to remain as reservoirs and breeding-grounds for black labor. These are the reserves.\\\n\\\nThe facts are that the reserves are congested, distressed areas, completely unable to sustain their present populations. People are on the verge of starvation. The White Paper [Verwoerd¨s proposal] discusses teaching Africans about soil conservation and agriculture. No amount of agricultural instruction will ever enable 13 percent of the land to sustain 66 percent of the population.]\n\n[Source: Mandela, N. (1959, May). *Verwoerd's Grim Plot.* South African History Online. Retrieved October 11, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/archive/verwoerds-grim-plot-nelson-mandela. CC-BY.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source P** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An excerpt from the article titled “Forced Removals in South Africa,” published in 2016 by South African History Online. |\n\n[Source: South African History Online. (2016, May 25). *Forced Removals in South Africa.* Retrieved October 22, 2025, from https://sahistory.org.za/article/forced-removals-south-africa CC-BY.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both state that forced removals and displacement stripped Africans of their fundamental rights.\n- Both sources show how the system disrupted African life and identity.\n- Both express that African ethnic groups became impoverished after losing their land, causing trauma and loss of their communities.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Source O focuses more on the political and economic impacts, while P focuses more on the social consequences.\n- Source O focuses on the immediate impacts of the implementation of the system, while Source P, written much later, explores the long-term consequences of the Bantustan system\n- Source O critiques the Bantustan system's impacts, while Source P takes a more neutral stance, emphasizing its practical consequences.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources reveal the damaging impacts of the Bantustan system ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). In Source O, Mandela says the system created “no sovereignty. No autonomy. No democracy. No self-government” and was a “crude, empty fraud” that only served to further white domination in South Africa. Source P says black “communities… were destroyed” while “White people made enormous profits” from the system ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, their specific emphases on the damage to black communities differ ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). In Source O, Mandela focuses primarily on economic and political disenfranchisement, saying black populations were forced to become \"work-seekers\" rather than land owners, and that they now simply supplied “black labor” rather than holding real economic power ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In contrast, Source P only briefly mentions economic impacts ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$), describing the homelands as “under-resourced and overwhelmed” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) and instead focuses on the social and cultural destruction of black communities, saying the system gave “little opportunity [for black South Africans] to re-establish their former relationships” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nFinally, the sources differ in the tone of their analyses ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source O, in the immediate wake of the policy and written by someone seeing the impacts directly, takes a passionate and condemnatory tone that reveals the moral implications of the system saying sarcastically that the Bantustands were “generously $\\lq$presented'” by “the new White masters of the country,” and that they were “miserable areas” which simply served as “breeding-grounds for black labor.” Source P, as an academic source, takes a more neutral tone, describing the practical impacts of the system without explicitly condemning or criticizing it ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"HI0392","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0392\n\nEvaluate the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement of the 1950s\n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nSince the command term is “evaluate”, the response must contain both positive and negative results to reach more than four marks. To reach full marks, there must be a conclusion. \n\nPositive results: \n\n - It was responsible for taking legal steps to overturn laws that promoted racial segregation. For example, Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1954) seemed to remove all constitutional sanctions for racial segregation by overturning Plessy v. Ferguson.\n\n - It helped other groups even if it did not agree with their methods. For example, it posted bail for hundreds of Freedom Riders and gave legal and financial aid to students arrested in sit-ins. \n\n - It was the initial driving force behind the Montgomery bus boycott and Little Rock Nine in 1957.\n\nNegative results: \n\n - The strategy of using the court system was slow, so other leaders of the movement, such as Martin Luther King, wanted direct action and mass mobilization. This disagreement over tactics led to a division of the movement with the creation of the SCLC and the . \n\n - The NAACP's victories in the courtrooms did not always translate into real change for the people. For example, acceptance of the Brown ruling varied. In the urban South, desegregation was introduced relatively quickly, but schools remained segregated in the Deep South, especially in rural areas. \n\n - It could not always act freely in the states of the Deep South. For example, after helping to organize the Montgomery Bus Boycott, Alabama barred the NAACP from working in the state.\n\n[5 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n\n - Responses are likely to conclude that the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s was positive \n\n - The strongest responses will mention the overall positive results while acknowledging the reasons why they were limited. \n\n[1 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) had an important role in the civil rights movement during the 1950s. Its main tactic was to fight against segregation in the courts, and this proved successful in landmark cases like Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1954). With its ruling, the Supreme Court decision overturned Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), thus removing constitutional support for segregation in education. This legal triumph was a turning point in the fight for civil rights, setting a precedent that segregation was inherently unequal ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, although it was not its main focus, the NAACP participated in other types of actions during the 1950s that contributed to the civil rights movement. For example, the NAACP initiated events like the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1956 and the integration of Little Rock Central High School in 1957 ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the impact of the NAACP on the civil rights movement during the 1950s was also limited. The slow pace of change through the court system frustrated many, leading to disagreements over tactics. Leaders like Martin Luther King Jr. called for direct action and mass mobilization, resulting in the formation of organizations such as the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, a legal victory did not always mean things improved for people in real life. For example, while the Brown decision called for the desegregation of schools, its implementation varied widely. In some urban areas, schools complied relatively quickly, while some districts in the Deep South remained segregated for many more years ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, local authorities often restricted the NAACP's ability to act. For example, after its involvement in organizing the Montgomery Bus Boycott, Alabama prohibited the organization from operating within the state ($\\checkmark$). \n\nThe NAACP's contributions to the civil rights movement in the 1950s were significant, as its litigation tactic successfully attacked institutional racism and inspired broader activism. However, its achievements were limited by the slow pace of change, internal divisions within the movement, and resistance in regions like the Deep South ($\\checkmark$conclusion )."},{"ref":"HI0393","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0393\n\nProvide **two** arguments to justify the following statement: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Malcolm X was a controversial figure of the civil rights movement.”\n\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nArguments need evidence to back them up. **Two** of the following arguments with supporting evidence: \n\n - He was accused of incentivizing the use of violence. In the televised documentary $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Hate that Hate Produced” (1959), Malcolm X said blacks should defend themselves $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$by any means necessary.\"\n\n - His relationship with the Nation of Islam (NOI) ended badly. Even though it was a small religious sect until Malcolm X became its spokesman, he was suspended in 1963 and left the organization in 1964. In February 1965, Malcolm was assassinated by NOI gunmen. \n\n - He made controversial remarks after Kennedy's assassination. When asked by reporters to comment on the assassination of President Kennedy, Malcolm X said that it was a case of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$chickens coming home to roost\" and added that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$chickens coming home to roost never did make me sad; they've always made me glad.\" For his comments, he was suspended from preaching by the NOI.\n\n - It was said by many that his influence actually set back the civil rights movement. Time Magazine wrote an article after his death describing him as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a disaster to the civil rights movement,” and Martin Luther King said of him, \"urging Negroes to arm themselves and to prepare to engage in violence, as he has done, can reap nothing but grief.\"\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nToday, Martin Luther King and Malcolm X are remembered as some of the most influential figures of the civil rights movement.  However, at the time, Malcolm X was considered a very controversial figure. As part of the Black Power movement, he rejected the peaceful protests of other organizations ($\\checkmark$) and called national attention to himself by stating in the televised documentary $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Hate that Hate Produced” (1959) that African Americans should defend themselves “by any means necessary” ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, the Nation of Islam, of whom he was the main spokesman, ended up suspending his preaching ($\\checkmark$). Malcolm X disagreed with some of the behaviour and beliefs of the leader of the NOI, Elijah Muhannad, and he left the organization in 1964. In February 1965, Malcolm was assassinated by NOI gunmen ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0394","marks":"30","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0394\n\nIdentify three central beliefs of the members of the Nation of Islam about the issue of racial segregation in the United States.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny three of the following:\n\n - Elijah Muhammad was a prophet of God (Allah)\n\n - African Americans should aim for separation, not integration, with the white Americans\n\n - Elijah Muhammad preached that white people are evil \n\n - Divine intervention would resolve race problems\n\n - Was against the the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$turn the other cheek\" philosophy of Christianity as a perpetuation of enslavement\n\n - Stressed racial pride and economic self-help as a way to improve the lives of African Americans\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Nation of Islam was an organization that mixed ideas of black nationalism with elements of Islam. Some of its beliefs included the idea that its leader, Elijah Muhammad was a prophet of Allah (God)($\\checkmark$). Elijah Muhammad preached that African Americans should aim for separation instead of integration ($\\checkmark$) since he considered white people as evil ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0395","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0395\n\nDescribe the Montgomery bus boycott (1955-1956).\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The objective was to end segregation on buses in Montgomery, Alabama, the state capital.\n\n - It was initially organized by the local branch of the NAACP, and then by the newly created Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), led by Martin Luther King. \n\n - It started just after the arrest of Rosa Parks, secretary of the local branch of the NAACP, for refusing to give up her seat to a white passenger on a crowded bus. \n\n - Most of the African American community in Montgomery, roughly 50,000 people, refused to use buses until their demands were met. \n\n - As a result, Montgomery buses were desegregated after the results of the federal court case Browder v. Gayle took effect,\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe main goal of the Montgomery bus boycott was to end racial segregation on Montgomery's public buses in Alabama ($\\checkmark$). The boycott was initially organized by the local NAACP branch but gained strength under the organization of the newly established Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), headed by Martin Luther King Jr. ($\\checkmark$). Partly as a result of the boycott, Montgomery buses were desegregated although it took the decision of the federal courts with the case Browder v. Gayle for desegregation to officially take effect ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0396","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0396\n\nDescribe the Freedom Rides of 1961.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following (or a combination):\n\n - Small, racially integrated groups of activists travelled the South testing Supreme Court rulings in favor of desegregation on interstate transport and interstate bus facilities.\n\n - Initially organized by CORE (Congress of Racial Equality - an interracial American organization established by James Farmer in 1942 to improve race relations and end discriminatory policies through direct-action projects) but eventually included the participation of the  SCLC and SNCC as well\n\n - The Freedom Rides generated the violent response of white racism\n\n - They led Attorney General Robert Kennedy and the Interstate Commerce Commission to enforce the Supreme Court rulings on desegregated interstate travel in November 1961\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Freedom Rides of 1961 were a movement that consisted of small, racially integrated groups of students who traveled on buses throughout the Southern United States to test Supreme Court rulings in favor of desegregation on interstate transport and bus facilities ($\\checkmark$). It was initially organized by the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), but eventually had the support of other organizations like the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) and the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0397","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0397\n\nExplain the significance of the Montgomery bus boycott for the civil rights movement. \n\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n\n - It demonstrated the power of a community using direct but non-violent action. \n\n     - Most of the 50.000 members of the African American community of Montgomery participated in the boycott for more than a year. \n\n - It demonstrated the importance of the Churches in the fight for equality.\n\n     - Martin Luther King Jr.  was the minister of the Dexter Avenue Baptist Church which served as the backbone of the organization of the boycott. \n\n - This form of protest served as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$model\" for other grass-roots demonstrations. \n\n     - There were similar successful bus boycotts in 20 Southern cities. For example, the Tallahassee Bus Boycott of 1956. \n\n - It led to more Northern white support and more co-operation between Northern and Southern blacks.\n\n - It showed the economic importance of African Americans.\n\n     - White business men began to work against segregation because the boycott cost businesses around \\$1 million in sales since shoppers could not get downtown for lack of transportation. \n\n - It brought King, with his powerful rhetoric, to the forefront of the movement. In 1957 he helped to establish a new organization, the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC).\n\n - It showed the effectiveness of the NAACP strategy of working through the US legal system. \n\n     - It was only after the Supreme Court backed the federal district court in Browder v. Gayle (1956) that buses were desegregated. \n\n - Historiography debate:\\\n\n    - David Garrow (1994) says the Montgomery Bus Boycott marked the start of the civil rights movement. \n\n    - In contrast, Mark Newman (2004) says it $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$did not spark a mass movement\".\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Montgomery Bus Boycott that took place for almost a year in 1955-1956 was highly significant for the civil rights movement. One of the main reasons for this was that it showed the importance of unity of the community to achieve success in direct and non-violent action ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$). In Montgomery, almost all 50,000 members of the African American community participated in the boycott of the city buses ($\\checkmark$). Due to this, the boycott was able to prove the economic importance of the African American community since the boycott led to an estimated \\$1 million in lost sales since people could not go downtown to shop ($\\checkmark$). This economic power forced many white businessmen to start opposing segregation ($\\checkmark$). \n\nAccording to historian David Garrow (1994), the Montgomery Bus Boycott was one of the most important events of the civil rights movement as he considers it marked the start of the movement ($\\checkmark$). However, Mark Newman (2004) concludes it was not so significant since it $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$did not spark a mass movement\" as the next big mass action event was many years later, with the Freedom Rides of 1961 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0398","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0398\n\nWhat, according to the source, were the reasons for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An introduction about Lyndon B. Johnson's speech to Congress titled “The American Promise” (1965). With this speech, he called for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Published on the website of the U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany. |\n\n[Source: *U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany.* (n.d.). Lyndon B. Johnson from “Basic Readings in US Democracy.” Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://usa.usembassy.de/etexts/democrac/40.htm. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Though the 1964 Civil Rights Act called for an end to state discrimination in voter registration, the Justice Department did not have the means to enforce it, so more measures were necessary.\n- In March, Martin Luther King organized a march on Selma, Alabama, to dramatize the need for a voting rights bill. The violence used by Alabama officials on the peaceful protesters generated a national crisis with tens of thousands of volunteers heading south to join King.\n- Johnson delivered a powerful speech to Congress that called for a respect of American democracy, urging it to pass the bill.\n- Both the public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills led to the passing of the Voting Rights Act\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAccording to the source, the passing of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 was because the provisions of the previous Civil Rights Act of 1964 did not give enough power to the federal government to make sure ending discrimination in voter registration was achieved ($\\checkmark$). Due to this failure, Martin Luther King Jr. organized a march on Selma, Alabama to show the need for a voting rights bill. The violent response to the march by white racists led to a national crisis and showed the need for further federal government action ($\\checkmark$). Johnson was able to convince Congress to pass the Voting Rights Act thanks to a speech he gave appealing to the need to respect American democracy ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0399","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0399\n\nWhat does the source suggest about the NAACP?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Poster by the National Association For The Advancement Of Colored People, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$People is what the NAACP is about.\" Created between 1965 and 1980. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Unassigned/images/HI0399.jpg\" alt=\"HI0399\">\n\n[Source: National Association for the Advancement Of Colored People (NAACP). (Between 1965 and 1980). *People is what the NAACP is about*. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/item/2015648062/.CC-BY 4.0.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It is a nationwide organization\n- It represents the people of the country\n- It needs more support\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFrom the source we can learn that the NAACP was a nationwide organization since we can see in the poster the map of the continental United States ($\\checkmark$). We can also learn that the NAACP needed more members since the poster calls for people to “join the NAACP” ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0400","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0400\n\nWhat does the source suggest about the March on Selma?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Photograph by Stanley Wofson for the newspaper New York World Telegram & Sun, March 1965. This Photograph shows an African American man carrying a child on his shoulders with a placard telling President Johnson to go to Selma, Alabama. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Unassigned/images/presjohnson%20selma.jpg\" alt=\"presjohnson selma\">\n[Source: Wofson, S. (March, 1965). *President Johnson go to Selma now!* World Telegram & Sun. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2005685852/. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- It required the president`s help\n- The situation was urgent as it calls for the president's action “now!”\n- It was peaceful as there were young children participating\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source suggests that marchers at Selma were requesting the president's help as we see one of them carrying a poster that says “President Johnson go to Selma now!”($\\checkmark$). From the inclusion of the word “now!” in the phrase, we can also infer that the situation is urgent ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0401","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0401\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of the source for an historian studying the March on Selma of 1965.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Photograph by Stanley Wofson for the newspaper New York World Telegram & Sun, March 1965. This Photograph shows an African American man carrying a child on his shoulders with a placard telling President Johnson to go to Selma, Alabama. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Unassigned/images/presjohnson%20selma%20-%20opcvl.jpg\" alt=\"presjohnson selma - opcvl\">\n[Source: Wofson, S. (March, 1965). *President Johnson go to Selma now!* World Telegram & Sun. Retrieved January 12, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2005685852/. No known copyright restrictions.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\nOrigin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any combination of the following:\n- The source provides insight as it was made in 1965 by a photographer who was present at the march on Selma (origin).\n- The source seeks to show what was going on during the March on Selma since the photograph was published in the newspaper The New York World Telegram & Sun (purpose).\n- We can learn from the source that the March was organized with peaceful intentions as the man portrayed is carrying a small child. We also can see that, at least this individual, was calling for Johnson's presence (content).\n\nFor limitations, any combination of the following:\n- The source has a limited perspective of what the photographer was pointing at when he took the picture. The picture was probably taken at the beginning of the march before the situation turned violent (origin).\n- The source seeks to convey the urgency of Johnson's actions by only focusing on this particular participant's sign (purpose).\n- The source portrays a very limited angle of the march and leaves out other demands of the people who were there as well as the impact of the March itself (content).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source is valuable in its origin since it has insight into the situation at Selma as the photograph was taken in 1965 ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The purpose is also valuable since the photographer wanted to document what was happening at the March for the newspaper The New York World Telegram & Sun ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). The content of the source is valuable as we can learn that the march was organized with peaceful intentions, as evidenced by the man depicted carrying a small child. Additionally, the photograph shows that at least one individual was calling for President Johnson's involvement ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations for a historian studying the March on Selma of 1965. First, its origin is limited since it only shows the perspective the photographer chose to focus on. Moreover, the photograph was obviously taken before the situation turned violent, so it has limited information on the whole development of the March ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). The purpose is limited since the photograph seeks to emphasize the need for President Johnson to act by concentrating solely on one character’s sign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Since the picture provides a narrow view of the march, its content is limited since the other demands of the marchers are left out, as is the overall impact of the March itself ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0402","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0402\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and B reveal about the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  An introduction about Lyndon B. Johnson's speech to Congress titled “The American Promise” (1965). With this speech, he called for the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Published on the website of the U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany. |\n\n[Source: *U.S. Diplomatic Mission to Germany.* (n.d.). Lyndon B. Johnson from “Basic Readings in US Democracy.” Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://usa.usembassy.de/etexts/democrac/40.htm. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from the online textbook $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$US History\" by P. Scott Corbett, Volker Janssen, John M. Lund, Todd Pfannestiel, Sylvie Waskiewicz, and Paul Vickery, 2015.|\n\n:box[Protecting African Americans' right to vote was as important as ending racial inequality in the United States. In January 1964, the Twenty-Fourth Amendment, prohibiting the imposition of poll taxes on voters, was finally ratified. Poverty would no longer serve as an obstacle to voting. Other impediments remained, however. Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance, and protests against this interference often met with violence. On March 7, 1965, a planned protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into “Bloody Sunday” when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas. Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-02-08%20t%2014.2.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-02-08 t 14.2\">Deeply disturbed by the violence in Alabama and the refusal of Governor George Wallace to address it, Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause. His proposal, the Voting Rights Act of 1965, prohibited states and local governments from passing laws that discriminated against voters on the basis of race. Literacy tests and other barriers to voting that had kept ethnic minorities from the polls were thus outlawed. Following the passage of the act, a quarter of a million African Americans registered to vote, and by 1967, the majority of African Americans had done so. Johnson's final piece of civil rights legislation was the Civil Rights Act of 1968, which prohibited discrimination in housing on the basis of race, color, national origin, or religion. \\\n]\n\n[Source: Corbett, P.S., Janssen, V., Lund, J.M., Pfannestiel, T., Waskiewicz, S., and Vickery, P. (2015). *US History.*  Retrieved January 10, 2025 from https://openstax.org/books/us-history/pages/29-2-lyndon-johnson-and-the-great-society. CC-BY-SA.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources agree that there was white resistance to the voter registration of Africans Americans\n    - Source C “the continuing resistance of southern leaders kept black registration low”\n    - Source D “Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance”\n- During the March on Selma, government employees attacked the marchers.\n    - Source C “protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into “Bloody Sunday” when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas”\n    - Source D “Alabama officials brutally attacked the marchers”\n- The violence at Selma was broadcast on TV to the whole nation.\n    - Source C “Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.”\n    - Source D “police violence, shown on national television, sickened the country”\n- President Johnson was instrumental in the passage of legislation that would allow African Americans to effectively be able to register to vote.\n    - Source C “The combination of public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills brought Congress to pass the voting rights bill on August 5, 1965.”\n    - Source D “Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause.”\n- The crisis at Selma was one of the reasons that motivated Johnson's actions.\n    - Source C “In the midst of the crisis, the president delivered the following message to Congress.”\n    - Source D “Deeply disturbed by the violence in Alabama and the refusal of Governor George Wallace to address it Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause”\n- Voter registration of African Americans greatly increased after the passing of the bill.\n    - Source C “In Mississippi, the state with the worst voting registration record, enrollment of black voters jumped from 6 to 44 percent in three years.”\n    - Source D “ Following the passage of the act, a quarter of a million African Americans registered to vote”\n- The March on Selma was a planned event.\n    - Source C “Martin Luther King led a march on Selma, Alabama, to dramatize the need for a voting rights bill.”\n    - Source D “a planned protest march from Selma, Alabama”\n- The new law increased the involvement of the federal government in the local registration process.\n    - Source C “The new law, known either as the Civil Rights Act of 1965 or as the Voting Rights Act of 1965, brought an unprecedented federal intrusion into local affairs,”\n    - Source D “Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause”\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- While Source C credits Johnson as well as civil rights workers and leaders, Source D only gives credit to Jonson for the passing of the Voting Rights Act.\n    - Source C “The credit belongs not only to the civil rights workers who put their bodies and their lives on the line, and to the civil rights leaders like Martin Luther King Jr., who aroused public consciousness, but in large measure to Lyndon Johnson.”\n    - Source D “His proposal, the Voting Rights Act of 1965, prohibited states and local governments from passing laws that discriminated against voters on the basis of race. Literacy tests and other barriers to voting that had kept ethnic minorities from the polls were thus outlawed.”\n- While Source C highlights the violence at Selma was shown nationwide and to the rest of the world while Source D only mentions national television.\n    - Source C “Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation and in newspapers around the world.”\n    - Source D “police violence, shown on national television”\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C and D share many similar ideas regarding the passing of the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Both say that there was significant white resistance to the voter registration of African Americans ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C notes that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the continuing resistance of southern leaders kept black registration low,\" while Source D states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Attempts to register southern African American voters encountered White resistance\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The sources also agree that this resistance was evident during the March on Selma where government employees violently attacked the marchers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C describes how the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$protest march from Selma, Alabama, to the state capitol in Montgomery, turned into $\\lq$Bloody Sunday' when marchers crossing the Edmund Pettus Bridge encountered a cordon of state police, wielding batons and tear gas,” and Source D similarly states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Alabama officials brutally attacked the marchers\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The two sources also agree on the fact that the violence at Selma was seen by the whole nation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C explains, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Images of White brutality appeared on television screens throughout the nation\" while Source D emphasizes that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$police violence, shown on national television, sickened the country\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe two sources also agree on the fact that President Johnson played a crucial role in the passage of legislation that allowed African Americans to register to vote effectively ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). According to Source C, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\nthe combination of public revulsion over southern white violence and Johnson's political skills brought Congress to pass the voting rights bill on August 5, 1965.\" Similarly, Source D highlights that \"Johnson introduced a bill in Congress that would remove obstacles for African American voters and lend federal support to their cause\" ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). However, the two sources differ in the others they credit for passage of the bill beyond the president ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source C says that along with LBJ, “the credit belongs … to the civil rights workers who put their bodies and their lives on the line, and to the civil rights leaders like Martin Luther King Jr., who aroused public consciousness.” In contrast, Source D only recognizes the impact of Johnson's “proposal”  as having put an end to local laws that “discriminated against voters on the basis of race” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0700","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0700\n\nWith reference to the expressions “RACIAL ACCORD” and “EXTREMISTS” in Source B, describe **two** characteristics of the US civil rights movement in the 1960s.\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B**  $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Cartoon titled “A Flower Tries to Grow in Birmingham” by Jim Berryman published in 1963. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/Unassigned/images/HI0700.JPEG\" alt=\"HI0700\">\n[Source: Berryman, J. (1963, May 14). *A Flower Tries to Grow in Birmingham*. National Archives Catalog. Retrieved April 7, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/5743297. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following alongside own knowledge of historical context:\n- Racial accord may be understood as the initial progress of the US civil rights movement toward political equality.\n- Racial accord may be associated with growing societal pressure from non-violent protests.\n- Extremists may refer to the limited reach of legislative decisions beyond state-level.\n- Extremists may reflect the persistent violence exerted by US authorities or organizations in an attempt to hinder civil rights entitlements for African American people.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nAs seen in the growing flower in the cartoon, the engagement in non-violent demonstrations led by peaceful leaders such as Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. reflected initial progress toward pro-civil rights legislation for African-American people and wider social reach advocating for political equality ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAlthough peace initiatives initially gained traction in the legislature through anti-segregation agreements at the subnational level, persistent wider social violent actions such as riots and bombs by extremist groups further challenged the civil rights movement's aims ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0949","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0949\n\nExplain **two** methods used to disenfranchise African American voters in the South before 1965.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with explanation:\n- Literacy tests: Required potential voters to pass reading or civics tests that were often unfair and selectively administered.\n- Poll taxes: Imposed fees to vote, disproportionately affecting poor African Americans.\n- Grandfather clauses: Allowed those whose grandfathers could vote before the Civil War to bypass restrictions, thus excluding Black citizens.\n- Voter intimidation: Threats, violence, and job loss used to discourage voter registration or participation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirst, the implementation of literacy tests required individuals to demonstrate reading and comprehension skills before being allowed to vote ($\\checkmark$). In practice, the tests were applied selectively and unfairly, with white voters often being exempted or given easier versions, while Black voters faced complex or intentionally misleading questions. This created a significant barrier to voter registration ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother method was the use of poll taxes, which required citizens to pay a fee in order to cast a ballot ($\\checkmark$). These taxes disproportionately affected African Americans, who were more likely to live in poverty due to systemic economic discrimination. Since many could not afford to pay the tax, they were effectively excluded from voting ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0952","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0952\n\nExplain **three** key ways the federal government responded to resistance to school desegregation.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Judicial actions and court rulings\n    - Brown v. Board of Education II (1955): Follow-up to the original 1954 Brown decision. Directed schools to desegregate “with all deliberate speed.” Gave federal courts ongoing oversight of desegregation, though the vague language allowed Southern states to delay compliance.\n    - Court enforcement of desegregation orders: Federal courts issued injunctions and orders against school boards refusing to comply. Judges could hold local officials in contempt of court for noncompliance.\n- Executive actions and federal intervention\n    - Little Rock, Arkansas (1957): President Dwight D. Eisenhower deployed the 101st Airborne Division to escort the Little Rock Nine into Central High School after the Arkansas governor used the National Guard to block integration. Marked the first time since Reconstruction that federal troops were used to enforce African American civil rights.\n    - Department of Justice involvement: The DOJ increasingly intervened in desegregation cases, filing lawsuits and supporting civil rights plaintiffs. Used amicus curiae ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$friend of the court\") briefs to support integration.\n- Legislation\n    - Civil Rights Act of 1964: Title IV: Gave the federal government authority to file suits to desegregate public schools. Title VI: Allowed the government to withhold federal funds from school districts practising discrimination. Created the Office for Civil Rights (OCR) within the Department of Health, Education, and Welfare to monitor school compliance.\n    - Elementary and Secondary Education Act (1965): Provided federal funding to schools but tied it to compliance with desegregation mandates. Used financial incentives to encourage local school districts to implement integration.\n- Monitoring and compliance mechanisms\n    - Federal oversight of school districts: The federal government, often through the courts or the OCR, monitored desegregation plans and ensured districts made $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$good faith\" efforts to comply. Required submission of desegregation plans and regular progress reports.\n- Public statements and pressure\n    - Presidents (especially Kennedy and Johnson) used the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$bully pulpit” to support civil rights and encourage compliance with desegregation: JFK sent federal marshals to enforce court orders (e.g., University of Mississippi, 1962). LBJ pushed through legislation and linked civil rights to national unity and moral responsibility.\n    - Brown II (1955) ruling mandated that desegregation occur $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$with all deliberate speed,\" although the term was vague.\n    - Eisenhower's intervention in Little Rock (1957): Sent federal troops to enforce integration of Central High School.\n    - Civil Rights Act of 1964: Gave the federal government power to withhold funds from segregated schools.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne major way the federal government responded was through judicial action ($\\checkmark$). After the Supreme Court's decision in Brown v. Board of Education (1954), federal courts continued to oversee desegregation cases and issued orders to force school districts to comply. In Brown II (1955), the Court instructed schools to integrate with \"all deliberate speed,\" and federal judges later used their authority to hold districts accountable :span($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, the federal government used executive power to enforce desegregation when state authorities refused to comply ($\\checkmark$). For example, in 1957, President Eisenhower sent federal troops to Little Rock, Arkansas, to protect nine African American students entering Central High School, directly confronting resistance from the state governor :span($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, federal legislation played a critical role :span($\\checkmark$). The Civil Rights Act of 1964 gave the federal government the power to cut funding to segregated schools and enabled the Department of Justice to bring legal action against non compliant districts. This increased pressure on local authorities to end school segregation and comply with federal law ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0953","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0953\n\nDefine redlining and explain **three** ways it was used to legitimise racial discrimination between 1954 and 1965.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor define:\n- Redlining was a form of racial discrimination in which banks and other financial institutions refused to offer services such as home loans, mortgages, or insurance to people living in certain areas, primarily because those neighbourhoods were inhabited by racial or ethnic minorities.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation, any **three** of the following:\n- Institutionalised housing segregation: Redlining provided a bureaucratic and financial justification for keeping African Americans confined to segregated, under-resourced neighbourhoods. Banks and federal programs refused to offer loans to Black families in redlined zones, while making loans easily available to white families in “green” or “desirable” areas.\n- Blocked access to suburbanisation: As white Americans moved to newly developed, federally subsidised suburbs, African Americans were excluded due to redlining and racially restrictive covenants. This deepened economic and social divides by denying Black families access to the same housing opportunities, wealth accumulation, and education systems.\n- Reinforced cycles of poverty: By cutting off investment in Black neighbourhoods, redlining led to urban decay, lower property values, and a lack of access to public services like quality schools, healthcare, and infrastructure. African Americans were effectively trapped in impoverished communities, unable to accumulate home equity or escape economic hardship.\n- Racial discrimination under the guise of “risk management”: Redlining framed racism as objective economic policy, legitimising racial segregation as a matter of sound business rather than overt prejudice. This provided a veneer of legality and professionalism to racial discrimination, making it harder to challenge.\n- Undermined desegregation efforts: Even after Brown v. Board of Education (1954) began to challenge school segregation, redlining reinforced residential segregation, which in turn perpetuated de facto school segregation. This limited the impact of legal victories by keeping communities and their schools racially divided.\n- Contributed to long-term wealth disparities: Homeownership is a major avenue for building wealth in the U.S.; by denying loans, redlining systematically excluded African Americans from wealth-building. These disparities have had long-lasting effects that persisted well beyond the official end of Jim Crow laws.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nRedlining was a discriminatory practice in which banks and federal housing agencies marked certain neighbourhoods, usually those with large African American populations, as “high-risk” areas on residential maps. While it appeared to be an economic assessment, redlining was in fact based on racial composition rather than financial risk, and it served to reinforce segregation and inequality ($\\checkmark$).\n\nRedlining legitimised racial discrimination by institutionalising residential segregation, preventing African American families from moving into white neighbourhoods or accessing loans to buy homes outside of redlined zones ($\\checkmark$). Second, it restricted economic mobility by cutting off opportunities for Black Americans to build wealth through homeownership, an option that remained available to most white families during the postwar suburban expansion ($\\checkmark$). Third, redlining helped justify unequal access to public services, including education, by ensuring that Black communities remained underfunded and isolated. These policies framed racial exclusion as a matter of financial prudence, masking systemic racism behind the language of economic risk ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0954","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0954\n\nIdentify **three** forms of nonviolent protest used during the Civil Rights Movement between 1954 and 1965.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Bus boycotts - e.g., Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955-56)\n- Sit-ins - e.g., Greensboro sit-ins (1960)\n- Freedom Rides - to test desegregation of interstate buses (1961)\n- Marches and demonstrations - e.g., March on Washington (1963), Selma to Montgomery marches (1965)\n- Voter registration drives - e.g., Mississippi Freedom Summer (1964)\n- Legal challenges - e.g., NAACP-supported court cases like Brown v. Board of Education (1954)\n- Use of media/publicity - strategic use of television, newspapers, and photographs to raise awareness\n- Economic boycotts - e.g., boycotting segregated businesses\n- Church-led activism and prayer pilgrimages - often coordinated by leaders like MLK\n- Non-violent civil disobedience - refusing to obey unjust laws peacefully\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, bus boycotts, most famously seen in the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1955-56, placed economic pressure on the bus system and contributed to the Supreme Court ruling that bus segregation was unconstitutional ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, sit-ins such as the Greensboro sit-in of 1960 challenged the legality and morality of segregation in public places ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, Freedom Rides drew national attention to the continued defiance of federal laws and helped prompt stricter enforcement of desegregation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0955","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0955\n\nExplain **three** ways the Student Non-Violent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) contributed to the Civil Rights Movement from 1960 to 1965.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Grassroots activism. Focused on empowering local communities, particularly young Black activists in the South.\n- Sit-ins. Helped coordinate and expand sit-ins after the Greensboro Four protest in 1960.\n- Freedom Rides. Participated in the 1961 Freedom Rides alongside CORE to challenge segregation on interstate buses.\n- Voter registration drives. Led campaigns in the Deep South, especially in Mississippi (e.g., 1964 Mississippi Freedom Summer).\n- Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party (MFDP). Helped organise this alternative political party to challenge the exclusion of Black voters in Mississippi.\n- March on Washington (1963). SNCC leader John Lewis gave a speech (moderated by other leaders for tone) representing youth activism.\n- Selma Voting Campaign (1965). Organised efforts in Selma prior to SCLC's involvement, showing its early leadership.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nIn 1961, SNCC members joined the Freedom Rides, risking violence and arrest to challenge the illegal segregation of interstate bus travel in the South ($\\checkmark$). These actions drew national attention to ongoing racial injustice and helped push federal enforcement of desegregation laws ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe SNCC also played a leading role in voter registration efforts, particularly in areas where white resistance was particularly extreme ($\\checkmark$).Its most notable campaign was the Mississippi Freedom Summer (1964), where volunteers worked to register Black voters and establish schools and community centres, despite threats and violence ($\\checkmark$).\n\nFinally, SNCC helped form the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party (MFDP) to challenge the all-white Mississippi delegation at the 1964 Democratic National Convention ($\\checkmark$). This effort highlighted the exclusion of Black citizens from political representation and pressured the national Democratic Party to confront racism in its ranks ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0956","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0956\n\nIdentify and explain **three** main provisions of the Civil Rights Act of 1964.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** with explanation:\n- Title II - Desegregation of Public Facilities. Outlawed segregation in public places (e.g., restaurants, hotels, theatres, parks).\n- Title III - Desegregation of Public Facilities by Government Agencies. Authorised the federal government to enforce desegregation in public facilities owned or operated by state and local governments.\n- Title IV - Desegregation of Public Schools. Directed the federal government to assist with the desegregation of schools, including legal support and funding.\n- Title VI - Federal Funds Cut for Discrimination. Banned discrimination by government agencies and organisations that received federal funding; violators could lose funding.\n- Title VII - Equal Employment Opportunity. Prohibited employment discrimination on the basis of race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin and established the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) to investigate complaints.\n- Voting rights enforcement (while strengthened further in the Voting Rights Act of 1965, the Civil Rights Act provided some federal oversight for voting discrimination, such as prohibiting unequal application of voter registration requirements).\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, the Act outlawed segregation in public places, including restaurants, hotels, theatres, and transportation terminals ($\\checkmark$). This meant that African Americans could no longer be legally denied service or access based on their race, helping to dismantle Jim Crow laws across the South ($\\checkmark$).\n\nSecondly, it banned employment discrimination based on race, colour, religion, sex, or national origin ($\\checkmark$). This was enforced through the creation of the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC), which investigated cases of unfair hiring and workplace practices ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThirdly, the Act barred discrimination by any institution receiving federal funds, including public schools and hospitals ($\\checkmark$). This allowed the federal government to withhold funds from segregated institutions, putting pressure on local authorities to comply with desegregation laws ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0957","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0957\n\nCompare and contrast the strategies and beliefs of Martin Luther King Jr and Malcolm X in achieving civil rights for African Americans.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3),\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Committed to Black empowerment and dignity. Both wanted to end white oppression and improve the social, political, and economic conditions of African Americans.\n- Challenged systemic racism. Each condemned the systemic discrimination that African Americans faced in housing, education, and employment.\n- Influential orators. Both used powerful speeches to inspire their followers and influence public opinion.\n- Supported Black pride and self-worth. Especially in later years, both emphasised African American identity and self-respect.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Philosophy of non-violence vs. self-defence\n    - King: Advocated non-violent civil disobedience, inspired by Gandhi and Christian theology.\n    - Malcolm X: Originally promoted self-defence “by any means necessary”, rejecting non-violence if attacked.\n- Views on integration\n    - King believed in racial integration and cooperation between races.\n    - Malcolm X (early): Advocated for Black separatism, believing that integration was impossible in a racist society.\n- Relationship with the U.S. political system\n    - King: Worked within the system, lobbying politicians and pushing for federal civil rights legislation (e.g., Civil Rights Act, Voting Rights Act).\n    - Malcolm X: Distrusted the system, viewing it as fundamentally corrupt; often rejected appealing to white institutions for justice.\n- Evolution over time\n    - Malcolm X's views evolved after 1964; he softened his stance on working with white allies and began advocating for global human rights.\n    - King also became more radical in his later years, focusing on economic justice and opposing the Vietnam War.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nFirstly, both leaders utilised powerful public speeches to raise awareness and inspire their followers, thereby becoming national symbols of the civil rights movement ($\\checkmark$). Secondly, each sought to empower Black communities and condemned systemic racism in American society, although they approached the issue differently ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, there were important differences in their strategies and beliefs. King believed in nonviolent protest, using peaceful marches and civil disobedience to push for legal reforms such as the Civil Rights Act of 1964. In contrast, Malcolm X initially argued that African Americans should defend their rights “by any means necessary,” including violence if provoked ($\\checkmark$). Also, King welcomed white support and believed interracial cooperation was essential for progress. He worked closely with white activists and religious leaders and emphasised the shared moral responsibility of all Americans to fight racism. However, Malcolm X rejected alliances with white liberals or civil rights groups, believing that African Americans should build their independent power without interference. He viewed white involvement as potentially condescending or untrustworthy. However, this view evolved later in his life after his 1964 pilgrimage to Mecca, where he expressed greater openness to working with people of all races who supported human rights ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0958","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0958\n\nAnalyse **two** ways the Freedom Rides (1961) were significant in challenging segregation in the American South.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** with analysis.\n- Tested federal enforcement of desegregation rulings: Exposed the federal government's failure to uphold the law in Southern states and pressured the Kennedy administration to intervene and uphold civil rights rulings.\n- Exposed violent resistance in the South: Media coverage of attacks (e.g., firebombing in Anniston, beatings in Birmingham) shocked national and international audiences.\n- Increased federal involvement: Resulted in the Interstate Commerce Commission (ICC) issuing regulations (Nov 1961) banning segregation in interstate transport facilities.\n- Galvanised the Civil Rights Movement: Inspired greater activism and cooperation among groups (CORE, SNCC, SCLC) and proved young activists could take the lead in high-risk direct action.\n- Highlighted the courage of civil rights activists: Demonstrated the power of nonviolent protest even in the face of extreme danger, and raised the profile of student activism and nonviolent resistance.\n- Deepened North-South divide on civil rights: Caused tension between Southern local authorities and the federal government and forced national attention on the persistence of de facto segregation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Freedom Rides were significant because they directly challenged the federal government to enforce its desegregation rulings. Although the Supreme Court had ruled that segregation in interstate travel was unconstitutional, Southern states ignored these decisions ($\\checkmark$). When Freedom Riders were violently attacked in places like Anniston and Birmingham, it exposed the federal government's reluctance to act. This public pressure eventually led the Interstate Commerce Commission to issue regulations banning segregation in interstate bus terminals, making the Freedom Rides a turning point in federal enforcement of civil rights law ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThe Freedom Rides were also significant for the way they galvanised the Civil Rights Movement ($\\checkmark$). The bravery of the young Black and white activists, many of whom were students, inspired a new wave of grassroots protest. Groups like the SNCC gained momentum, and the rides demonstrated that strategic, nonviolent action, even in the face of violent opposition, could bring national attention to the issue of injustice. This helped expand the movement beyond courtrooms and into direct action campaigns across the South ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0959","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0959\n\nTo what extent does Source A explain the reasons for the Civil Rights Act of 1964?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Radio Coverage of President Lyndon Johnson's Remarks upon Signing the Civil Rights Act of 1964. |\n\n[Source: Johnson, L. B. (1964). *The Civil Rights Act of 1964: A Long Struggle for Freedom.* Retrieved July 13, 2025 from https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-rights-act/multimedia/johnson-signing-remarks.html. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both support from the Source and other factors must be addressed in the response.\n\nPoints from the source:\n- Moral imperative: Johnson appeals to morality, stating that “morality forbids” the continuation of racial discrimination.\n- Constitutional foundation: He cites the U.S. Constitution as a basis for equality; “Our Constitution… forbids it.”\n- Legacy of American values: Reference to the Declaration of Independence and belief that “all men are created equal.”\n- Historical injustice: Acknowledges that millions are denied rights “not because of their own failures, but because of the colour of their skin.”\n- Bipartisan political support: Johnson references the overwhelming support in Congress, showing political will for change.\n- Continuity with Kennedy's agenda: The bill was initially proposed by JFK, reinforcing a commitment to civil rights reform.\n\nPoints from Own Knowledge:\n- Grassroots activism: Civil rights campaigns such as the Birmingham Campaign (1963) and Freedom Summer (1964) increased public support and pressure on politicians.\n- Violent backlash and media exposure: Events like the Birmingham church bombing (1963) and police brutality against peaceful protesters drew national attention and sympathy.\n- International Cold War pressure: The U.S. faced global criticism for racial inequality, which undermined its image as a defender of democracy.\n- March on Washington (1963): Demonstrated mass support for civil rights and influenced legislative priorities.\n- Southern resistance to desegregation: Highlighted the need for strong federal legislation to override state inaction or obstruction.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nConcluding remark:\n- Good conclusions will likely argue that the source material only addresses a limited scope of the factors that influenced the passage of the bill.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne clear reason Johnson outlines in the speech is the moral obligation to end racial discrimination. He states, “Morality forbids it,” arguing that denying rights based on skin colour is fundamentally unjust. This moral framing positioned the legislation as not merely political, but a necessary ethical correction ($\\checkmark$). He also references the U.S. Constitution as a legal and ideological foundation for the Act, stating that “our Constitution... forbids it.” This demonstrates an appeal to shared national values and legal equality, strengthening the legitimacy of the legislation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the speech does not fully acknowledge the role of grassroots activism, which was instrumental in prompting federal action. Events like the Freedom Summer of 1964, which sought to register Black voters in Mississippi, exposed the violence and institutional racism still present in the South ($\\checkmark$). The murder of three civil rights workers during that campaign shocked the nation and increased pressure for reform. Additionally, media coverage of brutality, such as that seen in Birmingham in 1963, where police attacked peaceful demonstrators, particularly children, drew national and international condemnation. This helped build public and political support for comprehensive legislation ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Johnson's speech effectively highlights the moral and constitutional justifications for the Act, it underrepresents the critical role of civil rights activism and public outrage in driving the legislation forward. Therefore, the source provides a partial explanation of the reasons behind the Civil Rights Act of 1964 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0960","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0960\n\nTo what extent does Source B support the argument that federal action was necessary to ensure civil rights progress?\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | President John F. Kennedy's Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, June 11). *Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963.* Retrieved July 12, 2025 from https://www.jfklibrary.org/archives/other-resources/john-f-kennedy-speeches/civil-rights-radio-and-television-report-19630611. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both support for federal action and other important factors must be addressed in the response.\n\nPoints from the Source supporting federal action:\n- Persistent inequality despite existing rights: The source details severe racial disparities in education, income, and life expectancy, suggesting local/state efforts had failed.\n- Need for federal enforcement: References to military presence (e.g., “without having to be backed up by troops”) imply that only federal intervention could guarantee access to education.\n- Civil rights tied to national values: JFK frames civil rights as central to American identity and international credibility (e.g., “we do not ask for whites only” in Vietnam), this suggests national responsibility.\n- Failure of voluntary compliance: The fact that demonstrations were still necessary implies that federal law, rather than local goodwill, was essential.\n- Moral imperative from the presidency: As the highest executive office, JFK's public stance signals the need for top-down intervention.\n\nPoints suggesting other important factors:\n- Grassroots activism: The speech acknowledges public demonstrations, suggesting the impact of movements like sit-ins and Freedom Rides.\n- Media coverage: While not directly mentioned, JFK's timing aligns with intense media attention on Birmingham and other flashpoints.\n- Economic and social change: The rise of a Black middle class and urban migration shifted the political landscape.\n- International pressure: Cold War context (referenced in speech) created pressure for the U.S. to address domestic racial injustice for its global image.\n- Judicial progress: Earlier court cases like Brown v. Board of Education and Boynton v. Virginia were vital judicial steps outside of federal legislation.\n- Local and state variation: Some areas (e.g., in the North) had made more progress through local reforms, suggesting federal action wasn't the sole pathway.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nKennedy cites stark disparities in education, income, and employment, framing these as evidence of enduring racial injustice. His remark that it “ought to be possible... to attend any public institution… without having to be backed up by troops” directly implies that local governments and institutions could not be relied on to uphold equal rights, therefore requiring federal intervention ($\\checkmark$). Moreover, Kennedy connects civil rights to America's global reputation, referencing Vietnam and West Berlin to argue that the country cannot advocate freedom abroad while denying it at home. This alignment with national interests further justifies federal responsibility ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, the speech also indirectly acknowledges the role of grassroots movements. The reference to demonstrations in the street reflects the pressure placed on the government by civil rights activists, especially those involved in the Birmingham Campaign and the upcoming Freedom Summer ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, judicial rulings such as Brown v. Board of Education and Boynton v. Virginia had already begun to erode legal segregation before federal civil rights legislation was passed ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while the source supports the idea that federal action was vital to achieving civil rights progress, it also implies that activism, court decisions, and international dynamics were important contributing factors. Federal action may have been decisive, but it was not the only force driving change ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0961","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0961\n\nEvaluate the extent to which Source C represents typical attitudes of Southern white leaders in 1963.\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with supporting evidence and a conclusion. Both typical and atypical attitudes must be addressed in the response.\n\nWays the speech represents attitudes of many Southern white leaders:\n- Commitment to segregation: Wallace's demand for “segregation now… segregation forever” echoes the positions of many Southern governors, legislators, and law enforcement officials.\n- Use of Confederate legacy: Invoking Jefferson Davis and “the Anglo-Saxon Southland” reflects common appeals to Southern identity and the “Lost Cause” narrative.\n- States' rights rhetoric: The speech frames federal civil rights legislation as “tyranny,” a view widely shared among white Southern elites who saw desegregation as federal overreach.\n- Political expedience: Many white leaders maintained segregation to gain political support among white voters who opposed integration.\n- Opposition to civil rights demonstrations: Like Wallace, many officials resisted civil rights campaigns with legislative, rhetorical, or violent means (e.g., Ross Barnett in Mississippi, Lester Maddox in Georgia).\n\nWays the speech does not fully represent all Southern white leaders:\n- Extremity of language: Wallace's explicit and theatrical rhetoric went further than many white leaders who maintained segregation more subtly or pragmatically.\n- Internal division: Some Southern white leaders were beginning to quietly moderate their stance by 1963 due to national pressure (e.g., Tennessee's Governor Buford Ellington cautiously cooperated with federal law).\n- Private reluctance: Some leaders may have privately opposed civil rights but avoided the kind of defiant posturing Wallace embraced in public.\n- Fear of federal consequences: Certain officials complied with court rulings or federal mandates not out of agreement but to avoid federal intervention (e.g., some school boards in Virginia).\n- Urban vs rural divide: White political leaders in more urbanised Southern areas were sometimes more pragmatic about gradual integration due to economic pressures.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nWallace's declaration of “segregation now, segregation tomorrow, segregation forever” captures the deep resistance to racial integration that dominated Southern political life. His speech aligns with the views of other prominent figures such as Mississippi's Governor Ross Barnett, who similarly resisted desegregation, including standing in the way of James Meredith's enrollment at the University of Mississippi ($\\checkmark$). The speech’s appeal to “freedom-loving blood” and the Confederacy also mirrors the cultural and ideological defences many leaders used to justify maintaining segregation and opposing federal mandates ($\\checkmark$).\n\nHowever, not all Southern leaders were as extreme in tone or as openly defiant. Some, like Arkansas Governor Orval Faubus, had begun to moderate their language under federal pressure by 1963, even if they still resisted integration ($\\checkmark$). Others complied with court rulings reluctantly, without the fiery appeals to Southern heritage that Wallace embraced. Furthermore, Wallace's theatrical delivery and explicit invocation of white supremacy were not universally adopted, especially by leaders in more economically progressive areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIn conclusion, while Wallace's speech was emblematic of mainstream white Southern political resistance to civil rights, its intensity and symbolism represent the most vocal and hardline segment rather than a complete picture of Southern leadership attitudes in 1963 ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0962","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0962\n\nCompare and contrast the views expressed on civil rights in Sources B and C.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | President John F. Kennedy's Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, June 11). *Radio and Television Report to the American People on Civil Rights, June 11, 1963.* Retrieved July 12, 2025 from https://www.jfklibrary.org/archives/other-resources/john-f-kennedy-speeches/civil-rights-radio-and-television-report-19630611. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Themes of freedom and American identity: Both reference freedom, heritage, and what it means to be American, but they define these ideals in very different ways.\n- Strong emotional appeals: Both use rhetoric and emotive language to persuade their audiences, Wallace appeals to Southern pride, Kennedy to moral responsibility and national unity.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- View of Segregation\n    - Wallace defends it as a permanent, positive good (“segregation now, tomorrow, forever”).\n    - Kennedy condemns segregation as morally wrong and contrary to American values, calling for legislation to end it.\n- Role of Government\n    - Wallace positions the federal government as a “tyranny” imposing its will on the South.\n     -Kennedy presents the federal government as a protector of rights, upholding the Constitution for all citizens.\n- Tone and Vision of National Unity\n    - Wallace invokes division, portraying the South as culturally and politically distinct.\n    - Kennedy seeks unity, arguing that all Americans, regardless of race, should enjoy the same rights and opportunities.\n- Intended Audience and Purpose\n    - Wallace addresses a Southern, white segregationist base to solidify political power.\n    - Kennedy speaks to the entire nation, urging moral progress and legislative reform.\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nA point of similarity is that both leaders refer to core American ideals, such as freedom and heritage, to support their arguments ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Wallace invokes the “Cradle of the Confederacy” and the “Anglo-Saxon Southland” to stir up Southern pride, while Kennedy appeals to the Declaration of Independence and American moral values, stating that the nation was founded on the principle that “all men are created equal” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Additionally, both use emotionally charged language to rally their audiences ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Wallace's dramatic phrasing, “draw the line in the dust”, mirrors the passionate tone of Kennedy's call for equal rights and national conscience ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nHowever, the two speeches fundamentally differ in their messages ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Wallace passionately defends segregation, calling for its continuation “forever” as a symbol of Southern resistance to federal authority ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). In contrast, Kennedy opposes segregation, declaring it unjust and emphasising the need for civil rights legislation to ensure equality. Furthermore, Wallace depicts the federal government as an oppressive force threatening Southern traditions. In contrast, Kennedy argues that it is the duty of the federal government to enforce constitutional rights for all citizens ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$)."},{"ref":"HI0963","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0963\n\nIdentify **two** historical references in Source C and explain their significance to Wallace's message.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace. Delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama, January 14, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Wallace, G. (1963, January 14). *Inaugural address of Governor George Wallace*, which was delivered at the Capitol in Montgomery, Alabama. Retrieved July 13, 2024 from https://digital.archives.alabama.gov/cdm/%20singleitem/collection/voices/id/2952/re.  Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with a conclusion. Each point requires an explanation and discussion of significance.\n\nHistorical reference 1: Jefferson Davis\n- President of the Confederate States of America during the Civil War (1861-1865).\n- Why Wallace references him:\n    - To align himself with the Confederacy's legacy of Southern resistance to federal authority.\n    - To evoke Southern pride and reinforce the image of segregation as a heritage issue, not a moral failing.\n    - To legitimise defiance of federal civil rights laws by invoking a well-known figure who also rejected federal control.\n- Significance:\n    - Sets a tone of rebellion and continuity; Wallace presents himself as a modern heir to Davis's cause.\n    - Suggests that resisting civil rights is a defence of tradition rather than extremism.\n\nHistorical Reference 2: “Cradle of the Confederacy”\n- What it refers to: Montgomery, Alabama, where the Confederacy was founded and its first capital in 1861.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To anchor his speech in a location symbolic of Southern defiance and white supremacy.\n    - To invoke nostalgia for a romanticised version of the Old South.\n- Significance:\n    - Embeds his political position in a place deeply associated with slaveholding and segregationist values.\n    - Strengthens the idea that Alabama, and the South in general, has a special mission to resist federal changes.\n\nHistorical Reference 3: “The Great Anglo-Saxon Southland”\n- What it refers to: A racialized phrase used by Wallace to describe the cultural identity of the South.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To appeal to a white, Protestant conception of Southern heritage and cultural superiority.\n    - Reinforces the idea that segregation is not just political, but deeply rooted in racial and cultural identity.\n- Significance:\n    - Frames the civil rights movement as a threat to the racial and cultural order of the South.\n    - Highlights how white identity was embedded in political resistance to integration.\n\nHistorical Reference 4: “Tyranny that clanks its chains upon the South”\n- What it refers to: An allusion to Revolutionary War-era language about British oppression.\n- Why Wallace uses it:\n    - To liken the federal government's push for desegregation to a foreign tyranny infringing on Southern “freedom.”\n    - Portrays the South as a victim of oppressive outside control, echoing the Confederate and Revolutionary tradition of resistance.\n- Significance:\n    - Helps cast federal civil rights enforcement as morally illegitimate and reminiscent of colonial oppression.\n    - Adds emotional and historical weight to the argument against federal intervention, appealing to patriotic Southern sentiment.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne reference is to Jefferson Davis, the President of the Confederacy ($\\checkmark$). Wallace states that he is standing “where once Jefferson Davis stood,” signalling to his audience that he sees himself as the successor to Davis's legacy of resisting federal authority ($\\checkmark$).  By invoking Davis, Wallace attempts to legitimise his opposition to civil rights as part of a proud Southern tradition rather than as political extremism ($\\checkmark$).\n\nA second reference is to Montgomery, Alabama, described as the “Cradle of the Confederacy”($\\checkmark$).  This phrase evokes the founding of the Confederacy and reinforces the idea that Alabama has a special historical role in defending states' rights and white supremacy ($\\checkmark$).  By anchoring his speech in this symbolic location, Wallace aligns the modern civil rights struggle with the Civil War and frames federal desegregation efforts as a continuation of historical tyranny ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0994","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0994\n\nDescribe the role of **two** prominent leaders of the Civil Rights Movement in the United States.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Martin Luther King Jr. - Baptist minister, activist, and important leader in the nonviolent protest movement for racial equality.\n\n - Rosa Parks was an activist who played a crucial role in sparking the Montgomery Bus Boycott.\n\n - Malcolm X - A Muslim leader advocating for Black empowerment and self-defense.\n\n - John Lewis - Leader of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) and later a U.S. Congressman.\n\n - Bayard Rustin - An essential organizer and advisor. One of the strategists behind the 1963 March on Washington.\n\n - Ella Baker - Organizer and leader, pivotal in SNCC and other organizations.\n\n - Thurgood Marshall - First Black Supreme Court Justice and important NAACP lawyer in cases like Brown v. Board of Education.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThurgood Marshall was a lawyer, a Supreme Court Justice, and a prominent civil rights advocate. He was also notable for being the first African American to serve on the Supreme Court. He led the NAACP Legal Defense and Education Fund. He won important Supreme Court cases, including the landmark Brown v. Board of Education, which declared racial segregation in public schools unconstitutional.  ($\\checkmark$)\n\nElla Baker was a prominent African-American civil rights activist, notable for her low-profile organizing work, which focused on empowering African-American communities through education and training.  ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI1623","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1623\n\nDefine the term Black Power and provide **two** examples.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **one** of the following to define “Black Power” with **two** supporting examples:\n- **Black supremacy**\n    - Some supporters believed Black Power meant reversing historical racial domination, with Black people ruling over White people.\n    - This was a minority and controversial interpretation.\n- **Black working-class revolution**\n    - Black workers in Detroit's automobile industry saw Black Power as a movement for economic justice and workers' rights.\n    - The League of Revolutionary Black Workers organised Black workers and pushed for greater representation and better work conditions.\n- **Black pride and dignity**\n    - Although initially critical of the slogan, Martin Luther King Jr. later argued that Black Power could represent racial pride, self-respect, and empowerment.\n    - He emphasised the need for Black Americans to gain confidence, dignity, and a sense of belonging.\n- **Economic empowerment**\n    - Nathan Wright and later Richard Nixon interpreted Black Power as increasing Black ownership of businesses and economic resources.\n    - They believed economic strength would lead to greater opportunities and influence.\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBlack Power was a movement that encouraged African Americans to gain political, economic, and social power, while promoting racial pride, self-respect, and independence ($\\checkmark$). Rather than having a single meaning, it was understood in different ways by different groups. One example is that Black workers in Detroit's automobile industry viewed Black Power as a struggle for economic justice and workers' rights, leading them to form the League of Revolutionary Black Workers to fight workplace discrimination and improve conditions ($\\checkmark$). Martin Luther King Jr. later interpreted Black Power as a way of developing Black pride, dignity, and a sense of belonging among African Americans. Thus, Black Power was both a call for racial empowerment and a demand for greater equality and opportunity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1624","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1624\n\nCompare and contrast the roles and significance of Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X in the U.S. Civil Rights Movement.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence**.\n\nFor **comparison**, any **two or more** of the following **similarities**:\n- **Political ideas:**\n    - Both wanted to improve the lives and rights of Black Americans. Both had large numbers of followers and were influential leaders.\n- **Influence:**\n    - Both were powerful and inspiring speakers. Both believed in the importance of organisations, preaching, and publications to spread their ideas.\n- **Later Developments and Legacy:**\n    - Both were involved in influential civil rights organisations. Their views changed over time, making them more similar in their later years. Both were controversial figures and were assassinated.\n\nFor **contrast**, any **two or more** of the following **differences**:\n- **Main Political Goal:**\n    - King sought integration of Black and White Americans, while Malcolm X initially supported separatism and Black self-determination.\n- **Political Focus:**\n    - King focused mainly on ending segregation in the South, while Malcolm X focused on problems in urban ghettos in the North.\n- **Political Views:**\n    - King increasingly supported economic equality and social justice in his later years, while Malcolm X emphasised Black nationalism and later moved toward a broader human rights approach.\n- **Methodology and Approach to Violence:**\n    - King promoted non-violent protest, while Malcolm X accepted self-defence and justified violence when necessary for protection.\n- **Attitude Towards White Americans:**\n    - King worked towards cooperation and integration with White Americans, while Malcolm X was initially more critical and distrustful of White Americans, though he became less antagonistic later in life.\n- **Achievements and Legacy:**\n    - King is generally seen as achieving more concrete civil rights successes, including helping secure the Voting Rights Act of 1965, while Malcolm X had a major impact on Black pride and Black consciousness but achieved fewer legislative changes.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nMartin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X had several similarities. Both aimed to improve the lives and rights of Black Americans and became influential leaders with large followings ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Both were powerful speakers who used important civil rights organisations to reach out to people to spread their ideas. Martin Luther King Jr. utilised and collaborated with the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC), which he helped found, and the National Association for the Advancement of Coloured People (NAACP). Malcolm X was associated with the Nation of Islam (NOI) and the Organization of Afro-American Unity (OAAU) ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Their views evolved over time, making them more similar in their later years ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Both men were controversial figures and were eventually assassinated ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$).\n\nHowever, there were also significant differences between them. King believed in integration and sought equality between Black and White Americans, whereas Malcolm X initially supported separatism and Black self-determination ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). King focused mainly on ending segregation in the South, while Malcolm X concentrated on the problems faced by Black people in northern ghettos ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). King promoted non-violent protest, but Malcolm X accepted self-defence and the use of violence when necessary ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Furthermore, King encouraged cooperation with White Americans, whereas Malcolm X was initially more critical and distrustful of them ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Finally, King achieved major legislative successes, including helping secure the Voting Rights Act of 1965, while Malcolm X's greatest impact was promoting Black pride and Black consciousness ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\n$\\\\$\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |"},{"ref":"HI1625","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1625\n\nExplain **two** political impacts of the Voting Rights Act (1965) on American society.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **two** of the following with supporting explanation:\n- Enfranchisement of Black Americans in the South\n    - Removed barriers to voting by ending literacy tests and poll taxes, making it easier for African Americans to register and vote.\n    - Greatly increased Black voter registration in the South, with Mississippi's registration rate rising to 59% by 1968 and the gap between Black and White voter registration narrowing to just 7% by 1980.\n- The rise of Black political power\n    - Increased Black political representation, as the number of African Americans elected to office in the South increased six-fold between 1965 and 1969 and doubled again by 1980.\n- Party realignment\n    - Shifted American political alignments dramatically, with the South transitioning away from the Democratic Party and towards the Republican Party. The transformation reshaped both parties' ideological identities and formed the foundation of US politics to today.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne impact of the Voting Rights Act (1965) was that it removed barriers to voting by abolishing literacy tests and poll taxes, making it easier for African Americans to vote ($\\checkmark$). Another impact was that Black voter registration increased significantly across the South, with Mississippi reaching 59% registration by 1968 ($\\checkmark$). A further impact was the increase in Black political representation, as the number of African Americans elected to office in the South increased six-fold between 1965 and 1969 ($\\checkmark$). The Civil Rights Movement reshaped American political loyalties, as many white Southern voters moved from the Democratic to the Republican Party. This shift contributed to the parties’ modern ideological identities shaping American politics even in modern times ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"US Civil Rights Movement","questions":[{"ref":"HI0046Q3","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0046Q3\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of the source for an historian studying the Freedom Summer.\n[4 marks]\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  The Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), a key organisation in the Freedom Summer, writing in a contemporary publication about the Freedom Summer in an article titled “Three CORE Members murdered in Mississippi”. Published on their own website in 2021.|\n\n:box[The organisation of the Mississippi Freedom Party (MFDP) was a major focus of the summer program. More than 80,000 Mississippians joined the new party, which elected a slate of sixty-eight delegates to the national Democratic Party convention in Atlantic City. The MFDP delegation challenged the seating of the delegates representing Mississippi's all white Democratic Party. While the effort failed, it drew national attention, particularly through the dramatic televised appeal of MFDP delegate Fannie Lou Hamer. The MFDP challenge also lead to a ban on racially discriminatory delegations at future conventions.<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/US%20Civil%20Rights%20Movement/images/Screeshot%202-01-14%20t%2012.04.37.png\" alt=\"Screeshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\"> Freedom Summer officials also established 30 \"Freedom Schools\" in towns throughout Mississippi to address the racial inequalities in Mississippi's educational system. Mississippi's black schools were invariably poorly funded, and teachers had to use hand-me-down textbooks that offered a racist slant on American history. Many of the white college students were assigned to teach in these schools, whose curriculum included black history, the philosophy of the Civil Rights Movement, and leadership development in addition to remedial instruction in reading and arithmetic. The Freedom Schools had hoped to draw at least 1000 students that first summer, and ended up with 3000. The schools became a model for future social programs like Head Start, as well as alternative educational institutions. <img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/US%20Civil%20Rights%20Movement/images/Screenshot%202-01-14%20t%2012.04.37.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2-01-14 t 12.04.37\">But despite the internal divisions, Freedom Summer left a positive legacy. The well-publicised voter registration drives brought national attention to the subject of black disenfranchisement, and this eventually led to the 1965 Voting Rights Act, federal legislation that among other things outlawed the tactics Southern states had used to prevent blacks from voting. Freedom Summer also instilled among African Americans a new consciousness and a new confidence in political action. As Fannie Lou Hamer later said, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Before the 1964 project there were people that wanted change, but they hadn't dared to come out. After 1964 people began moving. To me it's one of the greatest things that ever happened in Mississippi.\"]\n\n[Source: *Three CORE Members murdered in Mississippi*. No date. The Congress of Racial Equality. Retrieved September 16, 2024 from https://www.thecongressofracialequality.org/freedom-summer.html.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nValue:\n- Because the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE) (origin) was a participating organisation in the Freedom Summer, they provide a first hand perspective on the event.\n- The source is secondary and thus has the benefit of hindsight to assess the outcomes of the Freedom Summer.\n- The purpose of the source is to highlight both the successes and failures of the Freedom Summer, thus providing a degree of balance.\n- The content of the source provides a detailed account of the goals and outcomes of the Freedom Summer.\n\nLimitations:\n- Because CORE was a key organisation in the Freedom Summer, there is likely to be some overemphasis on its role in the events.\n- The title of the source “Three CORE Members murdered in Mississippi” implies a purpose and content, but the specific excerpt provides no information on these murders.\n- The content of the source briefly mentions some of the failures of the Freedom Summer, but tends to frame those positively as well (e.g. “While the effort failed, it drew national attention”).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nOne value of the source for an historian studying the Freedom Summer is that the article originates from the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE) who was an important participant in the Freedom Summer. ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$) However, this could also be considered a limitation of the source as they may overemphasise their own role in the campaign ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).\n\nAnother value of the source lies in its purpose which is to share both successes and failures of the Freedom Summer which provides a balanced understanding of the event ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$). To achieve that purpose, the content of the source describes those successes and failures specifically including the failure of the MFDP to unseat the all-white Democratic delegation and the success of the Freedom Schools in addressing some of the educational inequalities experienced by Black residents in Mississippi ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VC}$).\n\nFinally, one limitation of the content of the source that may imply some bias by the author, as mentioned previously, is that even the failures it describes are framed positively ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$). Specifically, “despite the internal divisions, Freedom Summer left a positive legacy” and “While the [MFDP] effort failed, it drew national attention”."},{"ref":"HI0046Q4","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0046Q4\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources C and D reveal about the impacts of the Freedom Summer. [6 marks]\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Eddie Wong, political activist and cultural worker (photography, film and writing), writing in an article titled “How Mississippi Freedom Summer 1964 Can Inspire Us in 2024”. Published in *Convergence Magazine* (2024). |\n\n[Source: Wong, Eddie (2024, January 12). *How Mississippi Freedom Summer 1964 Can Inspire Us in 2024*. Convergence Magazine. Retrieved September 16, 2024 from https://convergencemag.com/articles/how-mississippi-freedom-summer-1964-can-inspire-us-in-2024/. Usage requested September 16, 2024.]\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | The Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), a key organisation in the Freedom Summer, writing in a contemporary publication about the Freedom Summer in an article titled “Three CORE Members murdered in Mississippi”. Published on their own website in 2021. |\n\n[Source: No author. No date. *Three CORE Members murdered in Mississippi*. The Congress of Racial Equality. Retrieved September 16, 2024 from https://www.thecongressofracialequality.org/freedom-summer.html.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\nPoints of comparison and contrast must **both** be included to reach the 5-6 markband.\n\nFor **comparison**, any combination of the following similarities:\n-   Both sources mention the creation of the MFDP and its goal to challenge the all-white Democratic Party of Mississippi at the Democratic National Convention.\n-   Both sources mention the creation of the “Freedom Schools”, the reason they were needed, their goals and their connection to the Head Start program.\n-   Both sources mention voter registration as a focus of the campaign.\n-   Both sources mention the national attention drawn by the campaign.\n\nFor **contrast**, any combination of the following differences:\n-   Source D mentions both the successes and failures of the MFDP whereas Source C simply mentions its creation.\n-   Sources C and D disagree on the quantities of Freedom Schools and their student numbers. Source C says that 40 Freedom Schools served 2000 students whereas Source D says that 30 Freedom Schools served 3000 students.\n-   Source C cites specific quantities of attempts to register voters (17000) and their frequent refusal whereas Source D simply states that the drives drew attention to black disenfranchisement.\n-   Source D mentions the role the Freedom Summer played in the passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 whereas Source C makes no mention of it.\n-   Source C mentions the violent backlash against the Freedom Summer whereas Source D makes no mention of it beyond its title.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nBoth Sources C and D mention the creation of Freedom Schools and voter registration campaigns as significant impacts of the Freedom Summer ($\\checkmark \\text{similarity}$). About Freedom Schools, Source C mentions that “40 Freedom Schools… served 2000 students, including adults who sought literacy education” and Source D says that the Freedom Schools offered “black history, the philosophy of the Civil Rights Movement and leadership development in addition to remedial instruction in reading and arithmetic.” ($\\checkmark \\text{evidence}$) One minor contrast on this point is that the sources disagree about the quantities of Freedom Schools and their number of students ($\\checkmark \\text{difference}$). Source C says that 40 Freedom Schools served 2000 students whereas Source D says that 30 Freedom Schools served 3000 students ($\\checkmark \\text{evidence}$).\n\nBoth Sources C and D mention the creation of the MFDP to challenge the traditional, all-white Democratic Party of Mississippi ($\\checkmark \\text{similarity}$), but Source D also goes further to discuss the successes and failures of the MFDP whereas Source C only mentions its creation ($\\checkmark \\text{difference}$).\n\nFinally, two major contrasts between Sources C and D is their discussion of the overall outcomes of the Freedom Summer and their emphasis on the violent backlash ($\\checkmark \\text{difference}$). Source D credits the Freedom Summer in the eventual passage of the Voting Rights Act in 1965 while Source C makes no mention of this ($\\checkmark \\text{evidence}$). On the contrary, Source C explicitly discusses the violent response by the local community to the Freedom Summer while Source D makes no clear comment on this besides its title “Three CORE members murdered in Mississippi.” ($\\checkmark \\text{difference and \\checkmark\\ evidence}$ )"},{"ref":"HI0046Q5","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0046Q5\n\nDescribe the role of the Council of Federated Organizations (COFO) in the Freedom Summer of 1964.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **two** of the following:\n- It coordinated the work of various civil rights groups in Mississippi including the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC), the Congress of Racial Equality (CORE), and the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP).\n- It coordinated the volunteers working to register Black voters in Mississippi during Freedom Summer.\n- It established Freedom Schools which offered alternative education to Black students by teaching subjects not typically taught in segregated schools and to encourage political participation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nCOFO coordinated the work of other civil rights groups during the Freedom Summer including SNCC and the NAACP ($\\checkmark$). As the primary organiser of the Freedom Summer, COFO helped establish Freedom Schools and lead Black voter registration campaigns ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0046Q6","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0046Q6\n\nDescribe the significance of President Lyndon B Johnson to the civil rights movement in the United States.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n- President Johnson signed key civil rights legislation including the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\n- Johnson created a broader domestic legislative agenda known as the \"Great Society\" which aimed to reduce poverty and inequality, which disproportionately affected African Americans.\n- Key pieces of legislation within the Great Society which addressed social and racial inequality  included: the laws focused on the “War on Poverty” such as the Economic Opportunity Act of 1964, Medicare and Medicaid (1965) and the Food Stamp Act (1964) as well as the Elementary and Secondary Education Act (1965) and the Higher Education Act (1965).\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPresident Johnson pushed for and signed the two most important pieces of civil rights legislation of the period, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 ($\\checkmark$). Along with those two key laws, Johnson also worked to implement his Great Society reforms ($\\checkmark$) which included the creation of Medicaid to address the health inequalities for people living in poverty in the US ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0046Q7","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0046Q7\n\nExplain the significance of the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party (MFDP) in the context of the Freedom Summer.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n- The MFDP was founded during the Freedom Summer as an integrated party that challenged the dominance of the all-white Mississippi Democratic Party.\n- The MFDP was an organisational platform for significant civil rights leaders, especially Fannie Lou Hamer who was the Vice Chairwoman of the party and spoke from personal experience about the challenges African Americans faced in Mississippi, drawing national attention to the issues.\n- The MFDP drew national attention to racial injustice in Mississippi when it attempted to challenge the legitimacy of the Mississippi Democratic Party during the Democratic National Convention of 1964.\n- The MFDP helped create pressure on the federal government to pass reforms, especially the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe MFDP was a racially integrated party created during Freedom Summer to challenge the authority of the traditional Mississippi Democratic Party which was all-white ($\\checkmark$). Specifically, the MFDP created a 68-member delegation which attended the 1964 Democratic National Convention and attempted to be recognised as the legitimate representative delegation from Mississippi instead of the all-white party ($\\checkmark$). One of its leaders, Fannie Lou Hamer, drew national attention when she spoke about the discrimination she had faced when trying to register to vote in Mississippi ($\\checkmark$). However, the MFDP’s attempt to unseat the all-white delegation ultimately failed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0449","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0449\n\nDescribe the significance of the Brown v. Board of Education decision of 1954.\n\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **three** of the following:\n\n - Overturned the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate but equal” doctrine enshrined by the 1896 Plessy v Ferguson Supreme Court decision on the grounds that it was a violation of the Equal Protection clause of the 14th amendment\n\n - Outlawed segregation in public schools in the US, aiming to ensure black students had the same educational opportunities as white students\n\n - Signalled a major victory for the US Civil Rights movement and catalyzed further actions to fight for racial equality in the US\n\n - Provided a legal basis to challenge other forms of institutional racism and segregation in the United States using the 14th amendment\n\n - Argued that desegregation was not just a legal, educational or practical issue, but a moral one as well, citing the negative social impacts of segregated schools on black children\n\n - Sparked resistance to the decision in communities across the US including notable examples such as the challenge integration faced in Little Rock, Arkansas in 1957\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka, Kansas US Supreme Court decision of 1954 ended the long-standing concept of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate but equal” facilities in the United States that had been established nearly 60 years early by the Plessy v. Ferguson Supreme Court decision in 1896 ($\\checkmark$). The decision served as a significant boon to the broader Civil Rights movement and encouraged further legal challenges to segregation such as the NAACP's work to desegregate public transportation through the Browder v. Gayle decision in 1956 ($\\checkmark$). However, this decision was highly controversial and also sparked substantial resistance such as Virginia Senator Harry F. Byrd's $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$massive resistance” laws that sought to prevent the integration of Virginia's public schools ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0450","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0450\n\nDefine the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jim Crow law” and provide **two** examples.\n\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nFor $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$define,\" the following:\n\n - State and/or local laws common in the southern United States in the late 19th and early 20th centuries which mandated and enforced racial segregation in a variety of aspects of life.\n\nFor $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$example,\" any combination of the following OR other valid examples:\n\n - Public facilities and transportation including schools, railroads, buses/bus stations, hospitals, waiting rooms, parks, restrooms and water fountains\n\n - Privately owned restaurants - states and localities made it illegal for restaurants to serve black and white customers in the same spaces\n\n - Voting access and political participation including unequal application of literacy tests, disproportionate impacts of poll taxes and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$grandfather clauses” that outlawed voting unless the individual was a descendant of an eligible voter\n\n - Marriage and family including restrictions on interracial marriage and adoption\n\n - Employment restrictions that prevented blacks and whites from working in the same facilities or parts of the same facilities (e.g. segregated hospitals and/hospital wards)\n\n - Entertainment services including theaters that maintained separate seating areas for black and white patrons\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nJim Crow laws were laws in southern states of the US that required segregation between races in many different day-to-day activities ($\\checkmark$). \n\nOne common example of Jim Crow laws was segregated transportation systems including city buses, interstate buses and railroads (as well as their waiting rooms). Buses were often internally segregated with black and white areas while railroads had separate cars for black and white passengers ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAnother example of Jim Crow laws were those that prevented full political participation by black residents such as poll taxes. While outwardly they were applied equally as black and white residents were both required to pay the fee, they disproportionately limited black voter participation because, on average, black residents tended to have much lower incomes than white residents ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0451","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0451\n\nDescribe the concept of the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Great Migration” in the 20th-century United States.\n\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of the following:\n\n - The large-scale movement of African Americans from the southern US to the northern US and Midwest\n\n - African Americans moving away from the South for better economic opportunities\n\n - African Americans moving away from the South to escape racial violence and discrimination\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe Great Migration was the mass movement of African Americans out of the southern United States and into other regions, especially cities in the Midwest (e.g. Chicago, Illinois) and Northeast (e.g. Philadelphia, Pennsylvania) ($\\checkmark$). Many of these migrants were motivated to move by higher paying jobs in factories of major cities compared to their wages working on farms in the South ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0452","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0452\n\nWhat does Source A suggest about the Great Migration between 1940 and 1970?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n| **Source A** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | A thematic map from 2015 showing the changes in black population in US cities resulting from the Second Great Migration from 1940-1970. |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/US%20Civil%20Rights%20Movement/images/GreatMigration1910to1970-UrbanPopulation%20cropped.PNG\" alt=\"GreatMigration1910to1970-UrbanPopulation cropped\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from: Bjork-James, Carwil (2015, February 22). *File\\:GreatMigration1910to1970-UrbanPopulation.png*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 8, 2024 from https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/GreatMigration1910to1970-UrbanPopulation.png. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny combination of **two** of the following or other valid observations shown on the map:\n\n - Large numbers of African Americans left the southern US.\n\n - It increased the African American population in many western, Midwestern and northeastern cities of the US increased substantially (10 percent or more).\n\n - Some southern cities still maintained large populations of African Americans or even grew their populations as well (e.g. New Orleans, LA).\n\n - Many southern cities lost substantial percentages (10 percent or more) of their black populations (e.g. Jacksonville, FL)\n\n - Most of the western interior of the United States was not impacted by the Great Migration\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe map suggests that the Great Migration between 1940 and 1970 caused significant increases, 10$\\%$ or more, in the black population of many northern cities in the United States such as New York City, NY ($\\checkmark$). The map also suggests that many of those migrants came from southern cities as many of them had substantial decreases in their black populations, though there are exceptions like Atlanta, GA whose black population increased during this period ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0721","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0721\n\nWhat, according to Source M, were the grounds for the Powell amendments?\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source M** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Public of delivery by Congressman Adam Clayton Powell Jr. before the House of Representatives in 1956. His address revolves around the reasons for his initiative to amend the conditions on government funding of schools that did not desegregate education.\n\n:box[\n\nWith the executive branch of Government now indicating that it will $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$discharge whatever obligations are imposed upon it whether by Congressional action\" it is now obvious that the Powell amendments to the Federal aid to school construction bill are mandatory.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSeven objections have been raised to my amendments: \n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n…\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nFourth. \\[Presidential Candidate\\] Adlai Stevenson has come out against the amendment because he believes in moderation and gradualism. You cannot apply the principle of moderation and gradualism to any legislation that is a crash legislation which will be over within 4 years. I ask this question of Mr. Stevenson and his crowd: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$How can new Jim Crow schools built in States that are defying by legislative action the Supreme Court help even gradualism?”\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nFifth. The Christian Science Monitor \\[newspaper\\] has pointed out that my amendments are punitive. This they are not. In the first place, I am not forcing integration upon any school district. If they do not want to integrate, that is a matter to be taken up in other ways. But if they do want to integrate, then, and only then, can they receive Federal funds. My amendments are not punitive because my second amendment would hold the accumulated funds for the whole 4 years of the bill until any district now segregated does integrate during the life of the Kelley bill or the McConnell substitute.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSixth. It has been indicated that this would even hurt Negro children. I am happy to announce that every single editor of every single Negro newspaper in this country, monthly, weekly, and daily, who has replied to my letter, has indicated 100 percent backing of the Powell amendments.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSeventh. That the sentiment of the American people is not in favor of such amendments. This is totally erroneous. I have just conducted a quick questionnaire of the Members of the House of Representatives and the answers to these questionnaires mailed to every Member of the House are now running more than 2 to 1 in favor of the Powell amendments.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Powell, A. C. (1956, February 16). *Federal Aid to School Construction.* Congressional Record, p. 2731. Retrieved July 9, 2025 from https://congress.gov/bound-congressional-record/1956/02/16/house-section. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **three** of the following:\n\n - The executive branch of the government supported the autonomy of the legislative processes.\n\n - The amendments allowed flexibility for school districts transitioning to desegregation measures.\n\n - African Amerrican newspapers expressed their firm support of the amendments.\n\n - A great majority of members of the Congress supported Mr. Powell's initiative to amend the bill.\n\n\\\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAccording to Source M, the Powell amendments were supported by Negro social groups as evidenced in the endorsement of associated news outlets. ($\\checkmark$) The President of the USA guaranteed the autonomy of the decisions made in Congress regarding the amendments proposed by Powell, thus encouraging the amendments. ($\\checkmark$) Finally, most of the House of Representatives supported the Powell amendments. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0722","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0722\n\nWhat does Source N suggest about the challenges to the education system in the US in the 1950s?\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source N** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Cartoon by Jim Berryman about the unsuccessful outcome in the US House of Representatives of President Eisenhower's bill to build schools with federal resources.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/US%20Civil%20Rights%20Movement/images/7-07-1956_B-082_46_Jim_Berryman_PR.JPEG\" alt=\"7-07-1956_B-082_46_Jim_Berryman_PR\">\n\n[Source: Berryman, J. (1956, July 7). *Smothered*. National Archives. Retrieved July 9, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/5743224. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nAny **two** of the following:\n\n - Legal and legislative issues were obstacles to improvements to the US education system.\n\n - Education should be at the centre of the legislative processes in the late 1950s.\n\n - The education system was struggling to meet the needs of students in the USA.\n\n\\\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nThe references to literacy and numeracy, misspelled as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$readin' ”, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ritin' ” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rithmatic”, in the image portray education as both an area of US society that needs improvement and an important issue that the US Congress should prioritise during the late 1950s. ($\\checkmark$) Further, the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$smothered” in the title and the layout of documents on top of schoolbooks suggest that the wider sociopolitical system did not allow for progress in the education system. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"HI0723","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0723\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source M for an historian studying desegration legislation in the US Civil Rights era.\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source M** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Public of delivery by Congressman Adam Clayton Powell Jr. before the House of Representatives in 1956. His address revolves around the reasons for his initiative to amend the conditions on government funding of schools that did not desegregate education.\n\n:box[\n\nWith the executive branch of Government now indicating that it will $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$discharge whatever obligations are imposed upon it whether by Congressional action\" it is now obvious that the Powell amendments to the Federal aid to school construction bill are mandatory.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSeven objections have been raised to my amendments: \n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n…\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nFourth. \\[Presidential Candidate\\] Adlai Stevenson has come out against the amendment because he believes in moderation and gradualism. You cannot apply the principle of moderation and gradualism to any legislation that is a crash legislation which will be over within 4 years. I ask this question of Mr. Stevenson and his crowd: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$How can new Jim Crow schools built in States that are defying by legislative action the Supreme Court help even gradualism?”\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nFifth. The Christian Science Monitor \\[newspaper\\] has pointed out that my amendments are punitive. This they are not. In the first place, I am not forcing integration upon any school district. If they do not want to integrate, that is a matter to be taken up in other ways. But if they do want to integrate, then, and only then, can they receive Federal funds. My amendments are not punitive because my second amendment would hold the accumulated funds for the whole 4 years of the bill until any district now segregated does integrate during the life of the Kelley bill or the McConnell substitute.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSixth. It has been indicated that this would even hurt Negro children. I am happy to announce that every single editor of every single Negro newspaper in this country, monthly, weekly, and daily, who has replied to my letter, has indicated 100 percent backing of the Powell amendments.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nSeventh. That the sentiment of the American people is not in favor of such amendments. This is totally erroneous. I have just conducted a quick questionnaire of the Members of the House of Representatives and the answers to these questionnaires mailed to every Member of the House are now running more than 2 to 1 in favor of the Powell amendments.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Powell, A. C. (1956, February 16). *Federal Aid to School Construction.* Congressional Record, p. 2731. Retrieved July 9, 2025 from https://congress.gov/bound-congressional-record/1956/02/16/house-section. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n\\\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of two marks.\n\n - Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full four marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n\n - Powell is a member of Congress and it is a direct account of congressional proceedings.\n\n - The purpose is to justify the desegration amendments based on legal precedents.\n\n - Provides an overview of the legal background of the reasons why the proposed amendments should be considered.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n\n - The formal, congressional setting where the legislative process was taking place does not allow tracking of the desegregation provisions on the ground.\n\n - Offers a one-sided perspective in support of the amendments and  interpretation of legal precedents to justify them.\n\n - Only addresses the debate in Congress and does not indicate whether or not the amendments were passed or had any impact.\n\n\\\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nWith reference to its origin, the source is valuable because Adam Powell's role as a congressman and the setting of a congressional hearing provides a firsthand insight into the legislative processes on desegregation in education ($\\checkmark\\text{ VO}$). However, it should be noted that it is also limited because the accounts of the process mainly take place in the formal setting of the Congress, thereby disregarding the effects of and reactions to the legislative decisions in local places ($\\checkmark\\text{ LO}$).\n\nIn terms of its purpose, the source is valuable because Powell present both the legal basis for the amendments and their feasibility for implementation as desegration in education might proceed after their passage ($\\checkmark\\text{ VP}$). Nonetheless, the source content is limited because the Powell amendments are only proposed to one bill, which does allow for a well-rounded study of wider legislative processes on education and the source content gives no indication as to whether or not the amendments passed and were made into law ($\\checkmark\\text{ LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI0724","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI0724\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources O and P reveal about the perspectives on school desegregation.\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source O** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from *Brown vs Board of Education* ruling by the US Supreme Court, 1954.\n\n:box[\n\n[Black children] had been systematically denied admission to schools attended by white children under laws requiring or permitting segregation according to race. This segregation was alleged to deprive the plaintiffs of the equal protection of the laws under the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n…\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nThe plaintiffs contend that segregated public schools are not $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal\" and cannot be made $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal,” and that hence they are deprived of the equal protection of the laws. Because of the obvious importance of the question presented, the Court took jurisdiction. Argument was heard in the 1952 Term, and reargument was heard this Term on certain questions propounded by the Court.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nReargument was largely devoted to the circumstances surrounding the adoption of the Fourteenth Amendment in 1868. It covered exhaustively consideration of the Amendment in Congress, ratification by the states, then existing practices in racial segregation, and the views of proponents and opponents of the Amendment. This discussion and our own investigation convince us that, although these sources cast some light, it is not enough to resolve the problem with which we are faced. At best, they are inconclusive. The most avid proponents of the post-War Amendments undoubtedly intended them to remove all legal distinctions among $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all persons born or naturalized in the United States.” Their opponents, just as certainly, were antagonistic to both the letter and the spirit of the Amendments and wished them to have the most limited effect. …\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nAn additional reason for the inconclusive nature of the Amendment's history, with respect to segregated schools, is the status of public education at the time. In the South, the movement toward free common schools, supported by general taxation, had not yet taken hold. Education of white children was largely in the hands of private groups. Education of Negroes was almost nonexistent, and practically all of the race were illiterate. In fact, any education of Negroes was forbidden by law in some states.\n\n]\n\n[Source: Adapted from *Opinion, Brown v. Board of Education*. (1954, May 17). National Archives. Retrieved July 7, 2025 from https://catalog.archives.gov/id/1656510. Public domain.]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | |\n\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n\n|**Source P** $\\\\$ $\\hspace{5em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Excerpt from the $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$Declaration of Constitutional Principles”, also known as the $\\textit{\\textquotedblleft}$Southern Manifesto,” presented by U.S. Representative from Virginia Howard Smith and signed by various US congresspeople from the South against the implications of the Brown vs Board of Education ruling by the Supreme Court. Presented to the US House of Representatives in March of 1956.\n\n[Source: Adapted from Smith, H. (1956, March 12). *Declaration of Constitutional Principles.* Congressional Record. Retrieved July 9, 2025 from https://congress.gov/bound-congressional-record/1956/03/12/house-section. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n|Includes clear points of comparison (similarity)|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$)|\n\n|Includes clear points of contrast (difference)|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{contrast}$)|\n\n|Provides evidence from the texts to validate arguments|($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)|\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n\n - Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four marks**.\n\n - Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n\n - The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear evidence from the texts.\n\nFor **comparison**, any of the following similarities:\n\n - Both sources show the importance of the Fourteenth Amendment to both the arguments of both pro- and anti-integration perspectives..\n\n - Both sources agree that segregated education was to be taken as a historical fact, regardless of debates surrounding legal provisions and judicial decisions.\n\n - Both sources suggest that the Supreme Court played a significant role in establishing provisions on racial segregation in the education system.\n\nFor **contrast**, any of the following differences:\n\n - While Source O calls for a revision of the scope of the Fourteenth Amendment on education matters, arguing that segregated schools are not (and cannot be made) equal. In contrast, Source P stresses that the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate but equal” doctrine is still legitimate according to the court's interpretation of Plessy.\n\n - Source O supports the nationwide decision against the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate but equal” doctrine in public schools due to historical racial inequalities in the South, whereas Source P maintains that states' autonomy should be guaranteed and that the court's decision was overstepping and illegitimate.\n\n\\\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n\\\n\nBoth sources agree that the 14th Amendment was central to the debate about integration in education ($\\checkmark\\text{ similarity}$). Source O says that the debate in the courts was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$devoted to the circumstances surrounding the adoption of the Fourteenth Amendment in 1868.” Similarly, Source P says that conversations about the 14th Amendment $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$clearly show that there was no intent that it should affect the systems of education maintained by the States” ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$). Both sources also acknowledge the long tradition of segregated schools in the United States ($\\checkmark\\text{ similarity}$). Source O points out that schools serving black children were $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$almost nonexistent” or even “forbidden by law in some states” while Source P points out that the application of the “constitutional doctrine of separate but equal schools began in the North-not in the South” ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$).\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nHowever, the sources differ with reference to the scope of the Supreme Court's jurisdiction on school desegregation matters and their precise interpretations of the Fourteenth Amendment ($\\checkmark\\text{ difference}$). While Source O argues that the Supreme Court should revise the application of the Fourteenth Amendment because “segregated public schools are not $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal\" and cannot be made $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$equal.” Source P maintains that its provisions were clear in that there “was no intent” for it to influence the education system in the US ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$). \n\n\\\n\n\\\n\nFinally, Source O argued that the court is fulfilling its duties according to the constitution, while Source P suggests it is abusing its power ($\\checkmark\\text{ difference}$). Source O mentions the “obvious importance of the question presented” and its constitutional responsibility to rule on the Equal Protection Clause, while Source P denounces the ruling as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$clear abuse of judicial power” ($\\checkmark\\text{ evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI0950","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0950\n\nAnalyse the role of the Ku Klux Klan (KKK) in maintaining white supremacy between 1954 and 1965.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- Engaged in terrorist acts (bombings, lynchings, beatings) targeting civil rights activists and Black communities.\n- Sought to intimidate African Americans and white allies from participating in desegregation or voting rights efforts.\n- Worked to suppress civil rights progress through fear, often with local law enforcement complicity.\n- Had a resurgence in the 1950s and 60s in response to the Civil Rights Movement.\n- Created a climate of fear that reinforced systemic racism.\n- Held positions of power in local, state, and federal governments, allowing it to influence legislation.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Ku Klux Klan played a strategic role in reinforcing the social and political dominance of white Americans by acting as an unofficial enforcer of segregation and racial hierarchy ($\\checkmark$). Through acts of terror such as lynchings, bombings, and intimidation, the KKK aimed to deter African Americans from asserting their civil rights, especially in areas like voting, education, and protest ($\\checkmark$). This not only maintained the status quo of white supremacy but also undermined the legal efforts of the civil rights movement, making it dangerous for individuals to pursue equality ($\\checkmark$). The Klan's actions contributed to a climate of fear that sustained systemic racism, even as federal laws and court rulings began to challenge segregation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI0951","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI0951\n\nEvaluate the social and economic effects of Jim Crow laws on African Americans in the 1950s.\n[5 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following with an evaluation. Responses may include two points for each side (2/2), or be weighted (3/1 or 1/3), as long as both social and economic effects are addressed.\n\nSocial effects:\n- Legally enforced racial segregation in all public facilities.\n- Systemic humiliation and exclusion from mainstream society.\n- Barriers to quality education, health care, and public services.\n\nEconomic effects:\n- Limited access to skilled jobs or unionized labor.\n- African Americans were often confined to menial, low-paying work.\n- Denied equal access to home loans, property ownership, and economic mobility (e.g., redlining).\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\nEvaluation:\n- Jim Crow laws entrenched a racial caste system, reinforcing white supremacy.\n- Although challenged by legal and grassroots activism, the legacy persisted beyond formal repeal.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\\\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSocially, the Jim Crow Laws created a system of exclusion and humiliation, where African Americans were denied access to the same facilities as white citizens and forced into a separate and inferior public existence ($\\checkmark$). This legalised inequality reinforced the notion of Black inferiority and discouraged social mobility ($\\checkmark$).\n\nEconomically, Jim Crow laws limited African Americans’ opportunities for advancement. They were routinely excluded from skilled jobs and often confined to low-paying, insecure employment ($\\checkmark$). Discriminatory housing policies and redlining practices restricted where African Americans could live and blocked access to home ownership, one of the main pathways to building wealth ($\\checkmark$).\n\nWhile these laws were increasingly challenged by legal activism and grassroots protest in the 1950s, their effects remained deeply entrenched. Overall, Jim Crow laws played a central role in preserving systemic racism and ensuring that African Americans remained socially and economically disadvantaged, even as the civil rights movement began to gain momentum ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1081","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1081\n\nSummarize the provisions of the 13th, 14th, and 15th amendments to the US Constitution.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- The 13th Amendment abolished slavery.\n- The 14th Amendment established citizenship for all people born or naturalized in the US (including former slaves) and guaranteed equal protection of law and due process.\n- The 15th Amendment prohibited the denial of the right to vote based on race, color, or previous “condition of servitude,” functionally granting the right to vote to African Americans and former slaves\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe 13th Amendment abolished slavery in the USA ($\\checkmark$). The 14th Amendment provided for “equal protection” of the law and defined citizenship at a national level ($\\checkmark$). The 15th Amendment provided for voting rights and removed colour, race, or previous enslavement as a barrier to voting ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1082","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1082\n\nIdentify the doctrine established by the *Plessy vs Ferguson* Supreme Court decision of 1896 and explain its significance.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor identify, the following or similar:\n- It established the legality of racial segregation based on the doctrine of $\\lq$separate but equal' in the United States.\n\\\n[1 marks]\n\nFor explanation of significance, any **two** of the following:\n- Gave legal approval for the establishment and continuation of Jim Crow laws in the southern US.\n- Set the precedent for broader, legalized racial segregation at various levels of social, economic, and political life in the US, which would stand until 1954.\n- Sparked some local resistance in New Orleans (where Homer Plessy was from) via the Citizens' Committee of New Orleans.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Plessy vs Ferguson decision of 1896 was a key moment in civil rights in the USA as it firmly established the doctrine of $\\lq$separate but equal' which allowed facilities to be racially segregated as long as they were “equal” ($\\checkmark$).\n\nThis led to Jim Crow laws, like those that established racially segregated public facilities, having legal approval and continuing to be used in the southern US ($\\checkmark$). These segregated facilities, such as schools, resulted in very different outcomes and opportunities for black Americans, further widening the socioeconomic gap ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1083","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1083\n\nOutline how African American citizens in the South were still economically restricted even after the end of slavery.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Landowners retained complete control over production, rents and wages and deliberately kept African-American citizens in debt\n- African-Americans were arrested for minor crimes such as vagrancy and given a high fine or imprisonment\n- Prisons rented out African-American labour to private companies at a cheap rate so that companies profited from the virtual slavery that was created\n- Sharecropping resulting in chronic debt and dependence\n- Violence and intimidation by the KKK\n- Segregated schools hindering education and advancement\n- Poll taxes\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThough black citizens had technically been granted equal rights, landowners in the South retained control over production and rents which enabled them to keep black citizens in debt ($\\checkmark$). The justice system was used to further entrench debt and servitude by arresting blacks for minor crimes like vagrancy and giving them the choice of a high fine or imprisonment ($\\checkmark$). Black prisoners were then used as a form of cheap labour, rented out by the prisons to profit private companies and producing a system akin to slavery ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1084","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1084\n\nExplain how voting rights for African Americans were limited in Southern United States after the Civil War.\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following plus the corresponding evidence/explanation:\n- The grandfather clause was created to allow poor whites to vote despite other restrictions that had been created.\n    - The clause stated that anyone whose grandfather or great-grandfather had been eligible to vote was also able to vote. This ensured that whites could continue to vote, but blacks were excluded.\n- Widespread tampering with votes.\n    - African-American votes were marginalised by the stuffing of ballot boxes or the removal of African-American votes. At times, African-American votes were even recast into boxes of white candidates.\n- Implementation of poll taxes\n    - The poll tax ensured that poor African-American citizens could not actually place their vote, as they were unable to afford the tax\n- Implementation of literacy tests\n    - As many African-American citizens remained illiterate the introduction of a literacy test excluded them from the vote. Biased voting officials also discounted African-American citizens who could read from voting.\n- The actions of the US Supreme Court hindered African-American voting rights\n    - The decision of the Supreme Court in the US v. Reese decision of 1875 limited the federal government’s ability to enforce proper voting practices in states\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPractices were introduced designed to limit African-American voting rights, such as poll taxes ($\\checkmark$). These had to be paid for a person to be eligible to place their vote, and as many African-American citizens remained impoverished, this was an effective hindrance to their voting ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, literacy tests were introduced for citizens to be able to vote ($\\checkmark$). This hindered African-American citizens, as around half were illiterate, and even those who could read were often denied the right to vote by biased electoral officials ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the introduction of a Grandfather Clause limited African American voting rights ($\\checkmark$). This clause gave the right to vote to anyone whose grandfather or great-grandfather had had the right to vote, which enabled poor whites to bypass restrictions against voting that might have applied to them (such as literacy tests), whilst keeping African Americans unable to vote ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1085","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1085\n\nAnalyse the significance of the Little Rock Nine (1957).\n[6 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following plus its corresponding evidence:\n- The Little Rock Nine put defiance of Jim Crow practices into action\n    - The bravery of the students to go to school in the face of intimidation was a clear sign of the strength of resistance within the Civil Rights movement\n- The youth and gender of the Little Rock Nine showed the widespread appeal of the Civil Rights movement\n    - Carlotta Walls was just 14 years old at the time and the age of the group ranged from 14 to 16, including both boys and girls.\n- The fierce resistance to the Little Rock Nine displayed the levels of racism that the Civil Rights movement had to face\n    - The shocking images of racist insults being hurled at young children walking to school made it clear for all to see how much hatred and oppression African-Americans still faced\n- The national media attention gave publicity to the Civil Rights movement\n    - As the publicity around the Little Rock Nine grew and televised images were broadcast, people around the USA had the dramatic tension of the Civil Rights movement beamed into their living rooms\n- The decision of the President to send in troops to escort students revealed the conflict between the Southern states and the federal government\n    - When President Eisenhower was forced to send in troops to keep the peace, it became clear that federal government action and involvement in the Civil Rights movement would be necessary and could not be avoided.\n- Signalled the first major test of the *Brown v Board of Education* decision.\n    - Demonstrated that Supreme Court rulings alone were not adequate to change local behaviors and the enforcement for these rulings was lacking.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Little Rock Nine was a highly significant moment in the Civil Rights movement as their determination was an example of putting non-violent theory into action ($\\checkmark$). The bravery of the students in resolutely attending school in the face of such racism showed the strength of the movement ($\\checkmark$). The youth and gender of the students showed the wide appeal of the movement ($\\checkmark$). The youngest student was just 14 at the time and the group included both boys and girls ranging from 14 to 16 years old ($\\checkmark$). The national media attention given to the incident publicised the cause of the movement ($\\checkmark$). As televised images of the students and their racist opponents were beamed across the nation, Americans saw the dramatic tension at the heart of the Civil Rights movement for themselves which increased support for its goals ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1086","marks":"2","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1086\n\nExplain the significance of the murders of James Chaney, Michael Schwerner, and Andrew Goodman to the Freedom Summer.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- The murders became a symbol of sacrifices made by civil rights activists and drove further activism.\n- The murders exposed the brutality of racism in the South.\n- The murders drew national media attention and therefore increased visibility of the reality for Black Americans in the South.\n- Because of increased visibility, the federal government became more involved, taking greater action and interest in state and local activities during the Civil Rights movement.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe murder of the three activists was a terrible blow to the morale of the movement as its brutality showed the reality of racism in the south ($\\checkmark$). This was a significant blow to planning for Freedom Summer as they had been given the role of finding housing for volunteers and churches to be used as schools and showed the sacrifices required by civil rights activists ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1087","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1087\n\nDescribe the Birmingham Campaign of 1963 and explain its significance.\n[3 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nFor **describe**, the following or similar:\n- It was a campaign of non-violent protest led by the SCLC (Southern Christian Leadership Conference) designed to achieve progress in the fight for civil rights and equality. Actions included sit-ins, kneel-ins, and marches culminating in the Children's Crusade.\n[1 marks]\n\nFor **explanation of significance**, any **two** of the following:\n- The Children's Crusade effectively utilized children and young people to highlight the generational aspect of the fight for civil rights. It was significant for the media to see that youth, as well as better-known older campaigners, were hungry for change\n- The media attention garnered by the campaign further publicised the cause of the Civil Rights Movement. This was significant as getting their cause into the public gaze was one of the goals of the movement.\n- As one of the most segregated cities in the country, progress achieved by the campaign had a significant impact in reducing inequality and segregation for the people of Birmingham.\n- Represented a turning point that indicated the potential effectiveness of nonviolent direct action campaigns.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Birmingham campaign of 1963 was a model of nonviolent protest to bring about greater equality that was led by the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) and drew media attention to the civil rights cause ($\\checkmark$).\n\nIt was significant because it took place in an extremely segregated city, so that any progress made was highly impactful for the people who faced daily discrimination there ($\\checkmark$). It was also significant as it was widely reported in the media, which helped publicise the movement and its goals across the nation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1088","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1088\n\nOutline **four** reasons why Birmingham was targeted as a place for civil rights activism in 1963.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **four** of the following:\n- Though the city's population was 40% black, there were no black police officers, firefights, sales clerks, bus drivers or store cashiers\n- Only 10% of the city's black population was registered to vote in 1960\n- Jobs available to African Americans were limited to manual labour, yard work or work in black neighbourhoods\n- The unemployment rate for blacks was more than twice that for whites\n- The average income for black employees in the city was half that for white employees\n- Racial segregation of public and commercial facilities was legally required and rigidly enforced\n- 50 unsolved racially motivated bombings between 1945 and 1962 had earned the city the nickname Bombingham\n- Economic deprivation as the city shifted from white to blue collar work meant that white workers were afraid of black workers taking their limited employment opportunities if equal employment rights were enforced\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBirmingham was an extremely segregated city and an ideal place for civil rights activism, as according to voter registration in 1960, only 10% of the city's African American population could vote ($\\checkmark$). The city was suffering from economic challenges as white collar work declined and white citizens were afraid that African Americans might take their jobs  ($\\checkmark$). The unemployment rate was twice as high for African Americans as for whites in the city, so there was an apparent injustice that could be tackled ($\\checkmark$). The city followed a strong policy of racial segregation in all public and commercial facilities ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1089","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1089\n\nDescribe **two** reasons the arrest of Dr Martin Luther King Jr. in Birmingham in 1963 was significant.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- King's arrest was significant as it demonstrated his willingness to break the law for the cause of civil rights and helped pressure the federal government to act.\n- His arrest was captured on camera, making it significant as it helped publicize the movement's goals and the need for change.\n- While in prison, Dr King wrote “A Letter from Birmingham Jail,” which clearly explained his rationale for action and became an essential piece of literature in the civil rights movement.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe arrest of Dr King in Birmingham in 1963 was significant as it was captured on camera and drew national attention to the goals of the civil rights movement and the need for change ($\\checkmark$). During his imprisonment in solitary confinement, Dr King wrote “A Letter from Birmingham Jail” which explained in detail his rationale for action and became a celebrated and important piece of writing within the civil rights movement ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1090","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# HI1090\n\nExplain the significance of the Sunday School Bombing (1963, aka 16th Street Baptist Church bombing) to the civil rights movement in the US.\n[4 marks]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following with its corresponding evidence:\n- Dr Martin Luther King Jr., a key figure in the Civil Rights movement used the event to bring national attention to the cause of the Civil Rights Movement\n    - Dr Martin Luther King Jr. called on the President and federal government to intervene, raising the event to a tragedy with national implications\n- The President involved himself in the crisis\n    - President Kennedy made a national address in the aftermath of the crisis and then met with community leaders on all sides to try and bring reconciliation\n- The age and gender of the victims drew national sympathy\n    - As four young girls were killed in the bombing, public sympathy was heightened and awakened by the bombing in a way that had not been so pronounced for other violent acts in Birmingham.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Sunday School bombing was an event that drew national attention to Birmingham due to its victims ($\\checkmark$). Four young girls were killed in the bombing which created a level of national sympathy and awareness that was particularly pronounced ($\\checkmark$). Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. who was a prominent activist used the event to bring awareness of the need for progress in the civil rights cause ($\\checkmark$). He emphasised the nature of the tragedy as an event with national significance and helped draw President Kennedy to take action to bring justice and peace ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1091","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1091\n\nAccording to Source D, what was the purpose of the Inter-Citizens Committee?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Extract from a letter sent to President Kennedy by Reverend C. Herbert Oliver detailing the violence against the Negro community that has taken place in Birmingham, 1963. |\n\n[Source: Oliver, C. H. (1963, September 19). *HU: 2: ST 1 (Alabama): General, 1963: 1 July-9 December JFKWHCSF-0367-008-p0008.* JFK Library Retrieved October 14, 2025 from https://www.jfklibrary.org/asset-viewer/archives/jfkwhcsf-0367-008#?image_identifier=JFKWHCSF-0367-008-p0008. Public domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- To document cases of police brutality\n- To secure remedial justice for victims of injustice\n- To assist the victims of brutality\n- To awaken the nation to the scope of injustice\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource D states that the purpose of the Inter-Citizens Committee was to ensure that cases of police brutality went on the record ($\\checkmark$).  Secondly, Source D mentions that the Committee existed to support those who had suffered police brutality ($\\checkmark$). Lastly, it states that the wider purpose of the Committee and its work was to create awareness across the nation of how stark the realities of injustice and police brutality were in the area ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1093","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1093\n\nAccording to Source C, what actions did President Kennedy take in response to the bombing of the 16th Street Baptist Church?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Statement made by President J.F. Kennedy expressing outrage and calling for peace and unity in the wake of the Sunday School bombing in Birmingham, 1963 |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, September 16). *Statement to the Nation.* University of California, Santa Barbara. The American Presidency Project.. Retrieved October 14, 2025 from https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-the-sunday-bombing-birmingham. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nThe following:\n- President Kennendy sent Assistant Attorney General Burke Marshall to Birmingham in person to lend assistance to the investigation.\n- President Kennedy ensured that bomb specialists provided by the FBI were sent to aid in looking for those responsible for the crime.\n- He publicly condemned the attack while appealing for peace and unity.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nPresident Kennedy announces that the Assistant Attorney General will personally support the investigation in Birmingham through his presence there ($\\checkmark$). President Kennedy also states that specially trained FBI officers who are experts in bombs will support the investigation into the Sunday School bombing ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1094","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1094\n\nCompare and contrast the responses to the Sunday School bombing presented in Sources C and D.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\n- Responses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\n- The strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, the following:\n- Both President Kennedy and Rev. Oliver express grief at the bombing\n- Both President Kennedy and Rev. Oliver state that actions are needed now to bring peace and justice in the aftermath\n\nFor contrast, any **two** of the following:\n- Source C states that restraint is needed, whilst Source D makes no mention of restraint and instead calls for urgent action\n- Source D states that the feeling of safety has completely disappeared for the Negro community, whereas Source C makes no mention of security and safety disappearing\n- Source D states that the bombing is part of a wider context of police brutality and violence, whereas Source C does not mention police brutality at all\n- Source C states that law enforcement is there to support investigation and prosecution, whereas Source D states that law enforcement officers have inflicted further injuries since the bombing took place\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSources C and D are similar in that they both express sadness at the bombing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source C states that it is written out of $\\lq$outrage and grief' whilst Source D states that it is written $\\lq$out of deep sorrow' ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both leaders also state that actions are necessary to restore calm and security as well as call on citizens' participation($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source D states that it seeks $\\lq$remedial justice' and to $\\lq$awaken the nation' whilst Source C calls on every citizen to $\\lq$join in this effort' for domestic peace and security ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nIn contrast, the sources differ in their representation of the impacts of the bombing on the black community in Birmingham ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source D states that $\\lq$the feeling of safety has completely disappeared from the Negro community,', whilst Source C makes no mention of this ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The sources also diverge in their commentary about the role of law enforcement in the aftermath of the bombing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source C mentions that law enforcement is there to help, whilst Source D includes a reference to injuries inflicted by law enforcement officers ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Source C also notes that FBI $\\lq$bomb specialists' are present to help, whilst Source D makes no mention of this and instead states that there have been $\\lq$death and injuries' inflicted by policemen even after the bombing ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."},{"ref":"HI1095","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1095\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the response to the Sunday School bombing in Birmingham, 1963.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Statement made by President J.F. Kennedy expressing outrage and calling for peace and unity in the wake of the Sunday School bombing in Birmingham, 1963 |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1963, September 16). *Statement to the Nation.* University of California, Santa Barbara. The American Presidency Project.. Retrieved October 14, 2025 from https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-the-sunday-bombing-birmingham. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nA combination of the below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\nFor value, any of the following:\n- As the source was written in 1963, it offers a primary source and insight from the time\n- The purpose of the source is to address the nation and therefore it offers substantial value as a source of insight to the impact of the bombing\n- The source focuses on the actions taken by law enforcement and provides useful details of concrete steps that were made.\n\nFor limitations, any of the following:\n- The source was produced in the immediate aftermath of the bombing and does not therefore include the more thorough response after meeting with those involved locally.\n- Kennedy's purpose is to provide a calming response to a national crisis and therefore the response may be overly positive in tone.\n- The content of the source does not address the wider context of police brutality in Birmingham\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nSource C is valuable to historians studying the response to the Sunday School bombing as its origin is from the time, providing direct contemprary insight into the events ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$). The source is also valuable due to its purpose, as it is designed to address the nation and therefore conveys important information about how the nation reacted to the tragedy ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nSource C is also limited in terms of its origin, though, as it is from the immediate aftermath of the bombing and does therefore not include any reference to meetings with community leaders that took place before or after ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$). It is also limited in that its purpose was to reassure the nation, so it may have left out important information in order to take on a calming and reassuring tone ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LP}$). Lastly, it is also limited in terms of its content as it makes no mention of the wider issue of policy brutality at the time ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1306","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1306\n\nWhat, according to Source D, were the responses to the Freedom Riders' non-violent protest by local authorities?\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source D** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Excerpt from an article titled “Freedom Riders pose a problem for the South.” Published in *The Daily News*, Jackson, Mississippi, 28 May 1961 (Associated Press). |\n\n[Source: The Associated Press. (28 May 1961). *Freedom Riders Pose Problem for the South.* The Daily News, Jackson, Mississippi. Accessed via Trove. Retrieved January 24, 2026) https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/103082711. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **three** of the following:\n- Immediate and organised arrests of Freedom Riders (“quick,” “orderly,” “efficient”).\n- A pre-planned system to detain riders on arrival (police wagon waiting).\n- A refusal to change tactics, showing entrenched opposition to desegregation.\n- A cycle of arrests and imprisonment, including transfers to Parchman penitentiary.\n- A “stalemate,” indicating that protest did not shift state behaviour.\n- A determination by white authorities to preserve segregation, despite the non-violent nature of the protest.\n- A coordinated, large-scale effort to contain or suppress the movement.\n- Mississippi authorities adopted a different enforcement tactic compared to the violent mob attacks in Alabama. Instead of mob violence, Mississippi used swift, systematic arrests to contain the Riders.\n- This suggests segregationists in Mississippi preferred legalistic suppression to uncontrolled violence. However, this was not a softening of resistance, just a different method of maintaining segregation.\n\\\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe repeated arrests and the description of a “stalemate” suggest that Mississippi authorities responded to the Freedom Riders' non-violent actions with consistent and organised resistance, rather than attempts to negotiate or change segregation policies ($\\checkmark$). The article also describes how riders were “quickly” and “efficiently” arrested each time they arrived, implying a systematic effort to neutralise the protest ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the source also implies that segregationist authorities in Mississippi relied on efficient legal suppression, mass arrests, rather than mob violence, suggesting a different tactical approach but not a reduction in opposition ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1307","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1307\n\nWhat does Source B suggest about the resistance faced by the Freedom Riders during their non-violent protest in Alabama?\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source B** | $\\hspace{3em}$ | Freedom Riders watch as bus burns just outside of Anniston, Alabama.  |\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20History%20HL/Rights%20%26%20Protest/US%20Civil%20Rights%20Movement/images/HI1307.jpg\" alt=\"HI1307\">\n[Source: National Park Service. (1961). *Freedom Riders watch as bus burns outside Anniston, Alabama.* NPGallery Digital Asset Management System. Retrieved January 18, 2026 from https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/f35c833e96e7439e9aa2c71e6a8ecfee. Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\nAny **two** of the following:\n- The Freedom Riders faced violent resistance, shown by the burning bus.\n- They encountered physical danger, including smoke/fire, implying an attack.\n- The group appears distressed or disoriented, suggesting sudden or severe hostility.\n- The Riders were unprotected, with no visible law enforcement assisting them.\n\\\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe Freedom Riders faced violent and dangerous resistance, shown by the burning bus and riders' escape from the smoke ($\\checkmark$). It also indicates that they were left vulnerable, as no authorities appear to protect them, highlighting that their non-violent protest was met with serious hostility ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"HI1308","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1308\n\nWith reference to its origin, purpose and content, analyse the value and limitations of Source C for an historian studying the 1961 Freedom Rides.\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Statement by the President John F. Kennedy concerning Interference With the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Freedom Riders\" in Alabama, 1961. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1961, May 20). *Statement by the President concerning interference with the “Freedom Riders” in Alabama.* The American Presidency Project. Retrieved January 18, 2026  from https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-concerning-interference-with-the-freedom-riders-alabama Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\\\n**A combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:**\n- Both values and limitations must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **two** marks.\n- Origin, purpose and content must all be referenced at some point in the response (either as a value, limitation or both) to receive the full **four** marks.\n\n**For value, any of the following:**\n\nOrigin (President John F. Kennedy, 1961)\n- Statement issued by the sitting U.S. President gives insight into official federal attitudes during the Freedom Rides.\n- Contemporary source created during the events, making it valuable as a first-hand governmental response.\n- Reflects the perspective of the highest level of federal authority, useful for understanding national policy-making.\n\nPurpose (public appeal for order, federal reassurance)\n- Intended to calm public tensions and assert federal responsibility, showing how the government sought to manage the crisis.\n- Reveals federal attempts to pressure state officials into preventing violence, showing the dynamic between federal and state powers.\n- Provides insight into how the administration wanted its actions perceived domestically and internationally during the Cold War.\n- Helps historians understand what the federal government was publicly willing to say, indicating political priorities and sensitivities.\n\nContent (description of disorder, call for restraint)\n- Acknowledges “outbreaks of violence,” showing recognition of the dangers faced by Freedom Riders.\n- Demonstrates federal concern and the limits of local enforcement, highlighting systemic challenges to civil rights.\n- Shows the government urging both sides not to “provoke further outbreaks,” indicating the tension between maintaining order and supporting civil rights activism.\n- Provides evidence of the moderate, cautious federal stance, useful for analysing federal reluctance and gradual intervention.\n\n**For limitations, any of the following:**\n\nOrigin (official government position)\n- Represents the executive branch only, not the views of activists, local authorities, or segregationist groups.\n- As a public statement, it reflects politically calculated language, limiting its frankness about federal anxieties or shortcomings.\n\nPurpose (to manage crisis and maintain stability)\n- Intended to reassure rather than reveal internal debates or failures, so it may downplay federal inaction.\n- Purposefully avoids blaming the federal government, making it less reliable for understanding federal reluctance before intervention.\n- Crafted to avoid alienating Southern states, so it may soften criticism of segregationist authorities.\n\nContent (selective and constrained)\n- Focuses on “law and order” rather than the moral dimensions of segregation or the broader goals of the Freedom Riders.\n- Does not acknowledge the systemic nature of Southern resistance, so the depth of the challenge is understated.\n- Omits reference to violence by segregationist mobs or police complicity, limiting its usefulness for understanding local hostility.\n- Provides no insight into activist motivations, strategy, or lived experience.\n- Presents the federal government as acting decisively (“take all necessary steps”), which historians know was partially rhetorical at this point.\n\\\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nThe source has value because its origin as a public statement by President John F. Kennedy in May 1961 provides a contemporary federal perspective on the Freedom Rides during the crisis itself ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VO}$).  Its purpose, to calm tensions and urge state authorities to prevent further violence, reveals how the federal government publicly framed events as issues of “law and order,” which is useful for understanding how Washington sought to manage the situation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{VP}$).\n\nHowever, the source also has limitations. The origin as an official presidential statement means it reflects a politically cautious and diplomatic stance, not the full range of debates within the administration ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LO}$).  Furthermore, the content is selective, focusing on appeals for restraint rather than describing the severity of mob violence or police complicity, which restricts its usefulness for understanding the full extent of the challenges faced by non-violent protesters ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{LC}$)."},{"ref":"HI1309","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SBQ","body":"# HI1309\n\nCompare and contrast what Sources A and C reveal about the challenges for non-violent protests during the Freedom Rides.\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source A** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  James Farmer, statement reflecting on the strategy behind the Freedom Rides (1961), reproduced on the Civil Rights Movement Veterans Archive. |\n\n[Source: Farmer, J. (1961). Originally published in Voices of Freedom: *An Oral History of the Civil Rights Movement 1950-1980 (Hampton and Fayer).* Reproduced in the timeline Civil Rights Movement History, 1950-1970. Retrieved January 18, 2026 from https://www.crmvet.org/tim/timhis61.htm#1961-11]\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n|**Source C** | $\\hspace{3em}$ |  Statement by the President John F. Kennedy concerning Interference With the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Freedom Riders\" in Alabama, 1961. |\n\n[Source: Kennedy, J. F. (1961, May 20). *Statement by the President concerning interference with the “Freedom Riders” in Alabama.* The American Presidency Project. Retrieved January 18, 2026  from https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-concerning-interference-with-the-freedom-riders-alabama Public Domain.]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n|Top Markband Descriptors (5-6 marks)|Labels |\n| :-- | :-- |\n|Includes clear points of comparison| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$) |\n|Includes clear points of contrast| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$) |\n|Provides evidence from the text (SBQ) or own knowledge (SAQ) to validate arguments| ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$) |\n\n$\\\\$\n\nA combination of the ideas below that meets the following guidelines:\n- Both similarities and differences must be addressed. If only one is addressed, award a maximum of **four** marks.\nResponses that only describe the content of the source can achieve a maximum of **two** marks.\nThe strongest answers will identify and develop at least **two** similarities and at least **two** differences, both supported by clear **evidence** from the texts.\n\nFor comparison, any of the following similarities:\n- Both sources acknowledge violence as a major challenge facing non-violent protesters.\n- Both recognise failures of state and local authorities to protect Freedom Riders.\n- Both show that the Freedom Rides exposed profound resistance to desegregation in the South.\n- Both indicate that non-violent action faced significant risk and opposition.\n\nFor contrast, any of the following differences:\n- Farmer (Source A) stresses political inaction and federal reluctance; Kennedy (Source C) stresses federal concern and action within legal limits.\n- Source A reveals a strategy to force federal enforcement; Source C urges restraint to avoid escalation.\n- Source A presents challenges as rooted in systemic Southern segregation laws; Source C frames them as problems of public disorder and local responsibility.\n- Source A emphasises non-violent protest as a deliberate confrontation to expose injustice; Source C views protest as something that may “provoke” unrest.\n- Source A focuses on activist frustration with federal hesitation; Source C highlights federal efforts and appeals to state officials to act.\n\\\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\\\nBoth sources show that non-violent protest faced serious obstacles in the South ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A reveals that despite Supreme Court rulings, “state laws… and local ordinances” continued to mandate segregation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), creating a structural challenge for protest. Source C similarly shows conditions that hindered non-violent activism, describing a “situation… of deepest concern” marked by unrest and threats of violence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). Both also indicate failures by Southern authorities to protect the Riders ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{similarity}$). Source A highlights that local officials upheld segregationist rules and activists expected “racists of the South to create a crisis” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), while Source C calls on state leaders to “prevent any further outbreaks of violence,” implying that they had not done so ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$).\n\nThe sources differ in how they characterise the role of the federal government ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A stresses federal reluctance, suggesting that Washington's failure to enforce the law was “because of politics” and fear of Southern reprisals ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), portraying federal inaction as a challenge activists had to overcome. Source C, by contrast, presents the government as engaged and responsible, with the President having “instructed the Justice Department to take all necessary steps” ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$). The sources also differ in interpreting the risks of protest ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{difference}$). Source A frames non-violent action as a strategic necessity designed to compel federal intervention despite expected violence ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$), whereas Source C warns that protest actions might “provoke further outbreaks,” suggesting federal concern about escalation ($\\checkmark\\, \\text{evidence}$)."}]}]}]};
